const COMMENTARY = {"1 Chronicles 12:16–18": {"title": "\"We are yours, O David\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David is not king yet. He is a fugitive in a stronghold, and armed men keep arriving — some from Saul's own tribe, which is exactly the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you come to me in peace, to help me, then my heart will be knit to yours. But if you come to betray me…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts it to them straight, and leaves the judging to God rather than interrogating them or turning them away. A hunted man cannot afford either paranoia or naivety, and David does something in between: he names both possibilities out loud and waits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Spirit comes on Amasai, and what comes out is not a speech but a chant, three beats of *peace*:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "Peace, peace to you, and peace to all who help you, for your God is your help."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not offer terms or negotiate a position. He declares whose side they are on and blesses the man they are joining — and the blessing is grounded not in David's prospects but in God being his help."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David does with it.** He welcomed them and *made them leaders of his raiding bands* — command, straight away, to men who had been potential assassins ten minutes earlier. The chapter is a long list of people who came over to David before he had anything to give them, and the writer is keeping count on purpose."}]}, "1 Chronicles 12:23–37": {"title": "\"Men who understood the times\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A muster roll, tribe by tribe, and one entry in it is not counted the same way as the others."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the list is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The armed men who came to David at Hebron **to turn Saul's kingdom over to him**, just as the LORD had said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul is dead. David has been king over Judah alone for seven years, and this is the moment the other tribes come across."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler counts them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the others are described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every tribe gets a number and a military note:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Judah — *carrying shield and spear*: 6,800", "Simeon — *brave warriors ready for war*: 7,100", "Zebulun — *seasoned soldiers with every weapon*: 50,000", "Asher — *seasoned for war*: 40,000", "and 120,000 from beyond the Jordan, *armed with every kind of weapon*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly three hundred and forty thousand men in total, described by their weapons and their readiness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then Issachar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From Issachar, **men who understood the times and knew what Israel should do**: 200 chiefs, with all their kinsmen under their command.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two hundred. It is the smallest number of leaders in the entire list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they are the only tribe whose contribution is not military. No shields, no spears, no seasoning for battle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They knew what time it was, and what to do about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Understood the times** — the Hebrew is discernment of the season, the moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Knew what Israel should do** — knowledge that issues in action. Not analysis for its own sake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plenty of people can read a situation. The verse credits them with both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Issachar was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's blessing over him was the saddest of the twelve: *a strong donkey, resting among the sheepfolds… he will bend his shoulder to the load and submit to hard labour.* See the note on Genesis 49:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tribe that put its shoulder under a burden it did not have to carry produced the men who could read a season."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Zebulun's line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Standing by David with **undivided hearts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *not of a double heart*. Fifty thousand of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Benjamin's"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Saul's own kinsmen: 3,000, **though most of them had stayed loyal to the house of Saul until then**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler is honest about it. Three thousand out of Saul's own tribe, and he notes that the rest had not come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All these men of war, who could keep ranks, came to Hebron with a loyal heart, to make David king over all Israel… and there was joy in Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they *were with David three days, eating and drinking, for their brethren had prepared for them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A three-day feast, with food sent down from as far as Naphtali on donkeys, camels, mules and oxen."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David did not know how to read.** *When spring came around, the season when kings ride out to war… **but David stayed behind in Jerusalem**.* One sentence, and the chapter that follows is Bathsheba. See the note on 2 Samuel 11:1."}]}, "1 Chronicles 28:11–12": {"title": "The plans handed over", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David is not allowed to build the temple, so he does everything except build it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he hands over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David handed Solomon **the plans** — the design for the porch, the buildings, the storerooms, the upper rooms, the inner rooms, and **the room for the mercy seat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *plans* is **tabnith** — a pattern, a model, an architectural drawing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word used of what Moses was shown on the mountain: *see that you make all things according to the **pattern** shown you on the mountain.* See the note on Exodus 25:31–40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both buildings were copied from something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave him the plan for everything **the Spirit had laid on his heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew reads *the pattern of all that he had by the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David worked out the specification, and the chapter says where the specification came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the list, and then the verses following: the courses of priests, the weight of gold for each lampstand, the weight of silver for each table, the gold for the forks and basins by weight, the refined gold for the altar of incense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left to Solomon's judgement. The design is complete down to the cutlery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All of this,\" David said, \"**the LORD made clear to me in writing, His own hand upon me** — every detail of the plan, made plain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the LORD made me understand in writing by His hand upon me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote it down, and says the understanding was given while he wrote."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same combination as Moses at Sinai — a pattern shown, and a man producing the drawing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he could not build"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter: *\"You shall not build a house for My name, **because you have been a man of war** and have shed blood.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is refused the job and not refused the involvement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gathered the materials: gold, silver, bronze, iron, timber, onyx, marble — and then gave his own private treasure on top of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because I have set my affection on the house of my God, I have given to the house of my God, **over and above all that I have prepared for the holy house, my own special treasure**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man builds a house he will never see, out of money he did not have to give, to a design he wrote down and handed to somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says to Solomon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Be strong and of good courage, and do it; **do not fear nor be dismayed**, for the LORD God — my God — will be with you. **He will not leave you nor forsake you**, until you have finished all the work.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he told the assembly.** *All things come from You, and of Your own we have given You.* He raised the largest building fund in Israel's history and then said nothing in it had been theirs to begin with."}]}, "1 Chronicles 29:12": {"title": "\"Wealth and honor come from You\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old king prays in front of the nation after the largest offering in Israel's history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David has assembled everything for the temple he will not live to build — gold, silver, bronze, iron, timber, onyx, marble — and given his own treasure on top of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the leaders gave, and the people, *and they rejoiced, for they offered willingly, because with a loyal heart they offered willingly to the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This prayer follows immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yours, O LORD, is the greatness, the power and the glory, the victory and the majesty; for all that is in heaven and in earth is Yours.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five nouns, and then a claim about everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Wealth and honor come from You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **osher** — riches — and **kavod**, glory, weight, honour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it holding an inventory of what has just been donated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You rule over everything**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two in His hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**In Your hand is power and might**; in Your hand **it lies to make great and to give strength to all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to make great and to give strength*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verbs are causative. Whatever greatness or strength anybody has, the sentence puts the source somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **who am I, and who are my people, that we should be able to offer so willingly**? For **all things come from You, and of Your own we have given You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence the prayer turns on. The gift was made out of what had been received."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes further: *for **we are aliens and pilgrims before You**, as were all our fathers; our days on earth are as a shadow, and without hope.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by the king of Israel at the height of the kingdom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for himself. *Give my son Solomon a loyal heart, to keep Your commandments… and to build the temple for which I have made provision.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David had already said about the temple"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The house to be built for the LORD must be exceedingly magnificent… **now for my house I have prepared with all my might**\"* — and he lists three thousand talents of gold from his own possession, *over and above all that I have prepared for the holy house*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not asking the people for what he had not given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the phrase turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon, asked in a dream what he wants, asks for an understanding heart to judge the people. And God's answer: *I have also given you what you have not asked, **both riches and honour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two words, given to the son of the man who prayed this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David the son of Jesse reigned over all Israel. And the time that he reigned was forty years… **and he died in a good old age, full of days and riches and honour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler closes the account with the two words from the prayer."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When it was said.** Not in poverty. Standing in front of the largest voluntary offering the nation ever gave, with the inventory in front of him, David says the wealth came from somewhere else and that the giving was out of what was received."}]}, "1 Chronicles 4:9–10": {"title": "The prayer of Jabez", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine chapters of genealogy, and the record stops for two verses over one man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tucked into the long roll of names.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1 Chronicles opens with Adam and runs through nine chapters of lists. Names, fathers, sons, clans, with almost no story attached to any of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Jabez, and then straight back to the list. Nothing before or after connects to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about him first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jabez was **more honored than his brothers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nikbad** — from **kabed**, heavy, weighty. The same root as glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verdict is given before the prayer. The chronicler states it and then explains it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His mother had named him Jabez — **which sounds like \"pain\"** — because, she said, **\"I gave birth to him in pain.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Yabets**, and **otseb** is pain, sorrow, toil. The letters are rearranged, which Hebrew naming does often."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She named him after the worst day of her life, and he carried it every time anyone called him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jabez **would not let his name have the last word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else was in his family, his name was a sentence pronounced over him at birth by somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lifted his voice to the God of Israel and prayed, **bold and plain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four requests, and he does not soften any of them:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"Oh, that You would **bless me — really bless me!**\"* — the Hebrew doubles the verb, *blessing You would bless*, the intensive form.", "*\"**Enlarge my borders**, widen the space of my life.\"* — territory. He asks for more ground.", "*\"Let **Your hand be on me**, near me, holding me.\"*", "*\"And **keep me from harm**, so that I will be **free from pain**.\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last word of his prayer is the word in his name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he asks the enlarging from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not go and take it. He asks the God of Israel, out loud, for more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And God… **granted him exactly what he asked. Every word of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *God brought about what he requested*. No negotiation, no partial answer, no rebuke for asking about land and safety."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler was writing for people who had come back from exile to a ruined city, rereading their family lists to work out who they were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that he puts a man whose name meant pain, who asked for enlargement, and got it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for honoured"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root turns up over Abram coming out of Egypt — *very heavy in livestock, silver and gold*. See the note on Genesis 13:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weight, in Hebrew, is what honour is made of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two verses do not contain.** Any condition. He is not told to qualify, and nothing is required of him first. He asked, and the next clause is that God gave it."}]}, "1 Corinthians 1:18–25": {"title": "The message of the cross", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the word his critics were using and keeps it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two audiences"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The message of the cross is **foolishness to those who are perishing**, but to us who are being saved **it is the power of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *foolishness* is **moria** — the root of *moron*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both participles are present continuous: those who **are perishing**, those who **are being saved**. Paul describes two directions of travel rather than two finished states."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What crucifixion meant to them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cicero called it *the most cruel and disgusting penalty* and said the very word should be kept away from Roman citizens. It was reserved for slaves, rebels and the lowest criminals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Preaching a crucified Saviour in that world was not a difficult doctrine. It was an absurdity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will destroy the wisdom of the wise**, and the cleverness of the clever I will set aside.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 29, spoken about counsellors advising Judah to trust Egypt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is **the wise one**? Where is **the scribe**? Where is **the debater of this present age**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Greek philosopher, a Jewish scholar, and a professional arguer. Between them they cover every kind of authority in Corinth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What did not work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since, **in the wisdom of God**, the world **through its own wisdom did not come to know God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in the wisdom of God* — the arrangement was deliberate. God set it up so that this route would not arrive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He chose instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It **pleased God to save those who believe through the foolishness of the message we announce**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *the message* is **kerygma** — the thing proclaimed, the herald's announcement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul calls his own preaching foolish, and says God preferred it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two demands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Jews **ask for a sign** and the Greeks **go searching for wisdom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are reasonable requests. Both had been offered honestly for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is preached instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But we proclaim **Christ crucified** — **a stumbling block to the Jews and foolishness to the Greeks**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *stumbling block* is **skandalon** — the trigger of a trap, the thing that trips you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not adjust the message for either audience. He names the offence it gives to each and preaches it unchanged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **to those who are called**, both Jews and Greeks, **Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two things they were asking for — power and wisdom — are named, and both are located in the person they found offensive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the foolishness of God is wiser than men**, and **the weakness of God is stronger than men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul concedes the terms and then measures with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue that the cross is really quite clever. He grants it looks foolish and weak, and says God at His most foolish outdoes men at their wisest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the church itself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not many wise according to the flesh, not many mighty, not many noble, are called. **But God has chosen the foolish things of the world to put to shame the wise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The congregation is the same argument in miniature."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The strategy in the passage.** Paul never softens the offence. He states exactly what each audience objects to, agrees that it looks that way, and says God chose it on purpose."}]}, "1 Corinthians 1:26–31": {"title": "\"Look at your own calling\" — the argument from the congregation", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes his case by telling the Corinthians to look around the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence he points at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not many of you were wise by the reckoning of the flesh, not many were powerful, not many were nobly born.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not insulting them. He is presenting them as data. If the message spread by being intellectually fashionable or socially connected, this congregation would look different than it does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the precision: *not many* — not *none*. Corinth had some people of standing, and Paul does not overstate his case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four things God chose"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["what the world calls **foolish**", "what the world calls **weak**", "the **low and the despised**", "**the things that count for nothing at all**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last one escalates past the others — literally *the things that are not*, brought in to nullify *the things that are*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the stated purpose is entirely negative: *so that no human being might boast in His presence*. The selection criteria were designed to rule something out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God chooses to work with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The principle is not confined to people. It runs through the whole Old Testament as a method:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["a **tent peg and a hammer** against nine hundred iron chariots (Judges 4)", "a **nursing lamb** on an altar while a Philistine army closed in (1 Samuel 7)", "a **shepherd's sling** against a man in full armour", "**jars and torches**, three hundred men, no swords drawn (Judges 7)"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in that list looks like a weapon. Every one of them is what God chose, and the choosing is the point — *so that no human being might boast in His presence.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A piece of bread and a small cup belong on that list. It is not that they are adequate despite being small; small is the specification. See the notes on Judges 4:17–24 and 1 Samuel 7:7–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Christ is said to have become"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wisdom from God — and everything that puts us right with God, and everything that makes us holy, and everything that sets us free.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, and every one of them is the sort of thing a person would otherwise be trying to achieve. Paul does not say Christ helps with them. He says Christ **became** them, for us."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The quotation at the end.** *Let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord* — from Jeremiah, where the context is a warning against boasting in wisdom, strength or riches. The three things Jeremiah names line up almost exactly with the three Paul has just said the Corinthians did not have much of. He is not improvising the argument; he is landing on a text."}]}, "1 Corinthians 10:1–5": {"title": "\"That Rock was Christ\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists five things every one of them had, and then says most of them died in the desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he repeats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "***All*** — five times in four verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All our fathers were **under the cloud**, and **all** of them passed through the sea, and **all** of them were baptized into Moses, and **all** of them ate the same spiritual food, and **all** of them drank the same spiritual drink.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody was left out of anything on the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose fathers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our fathers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to a mostly Gentile church in Corinth and calls Israel's wilderness generation **our** fathers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cloud and the sea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Baptized into Moses **in the cloud and in the sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pillar over them and the water either side. Paul reads the crossing as a baptism — they went in one thing and came out another, following a leader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The food"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The same **spiritual food**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The manna, six mornings a week for forty years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The drink"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The same **spiritual drink** — for they drank from **a spiritual Rock that went with them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rock at Rephidim and the rock at Kadesh, thirty-eight years apart. See the notes on Exodus 17:4–7 and Numbers 20:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the Rock **went with them** — the Greek is *the accompanying Rock*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **that Rock was Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states it flatly, in the past tense, about an object in the Sinai desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the strongest identification of an Old Testament thing with Christ that Paul makes anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And yet **with most of them God was not well pleased**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *with the majority of them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the adults who came out of Egypt, two entered Canaan: Caleb and Joshua."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For they were **scattered and left across the wilderness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they were strewn about* — **katestrothesan**, laid low, spread over the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Paul is saying it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *now these things became **our examples**, to the intent that we should not lust after evil things as they also lusted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *examples* is **typoi** — types, patterns, the impression left by a stamp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *let him who thinks he stands **take heed lest he fall**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth was a confident church. Paul had spent the previous chapter on his own discipline — *lest, when I have preached to others, I myself should become disqualified.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being argued"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the provision was inadequate. Paul says twice that it was **the same** for all of them, and calls it spiritual both times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No temptation has overtaken you except such as is common to man; but **God is faithful, who will not allow you to be tempted beyond what you are able**, but with the temptation will also make the way of escape.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted verse in the chapter comes directly after the wilderness graves."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one clause everything hangs on.** *That Rock was Christ.* The people who drank from it are the same people Paul says were scattered across the desert, and he puts both facts in one paragraph."}]}, "1 Corinthians 10:11–13": {"title": "\"The way of escape\" — and what it is escape from", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most quoted promises in the New Testament, sitting immediately after a list of Israel's worst failures in the desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just run through the golden calf, the immorality at Peor, the grumbling and the snakes. Then: *all these things happened to them as examples, and they were written down as instruction for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for examples is **typoi** — patterns, impressions, the mark a die leaves. Paul reads Israel's history as a shape that repeats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning that comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the one who thinks he is standing take care that he does not fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the sentence the famous promise is attached to, and it is a warning against confidence, not an encouragement to it. The order matters: watch yourself, **then** the assurance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised, precisely"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the testing is *common to everyone* — not unique, not a sign of being singled out", "God is faithful, and will not allow testing beyond what you are able to bear", "with the testing He *will also make the way of escape*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase is worth reading closely: the escape is **so that you are able to bear it**. Not a door out of the situation — a way through it that does not break you. Paul's own thorn, which he asked three times to have removed and did not, is the same shape."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"more than you can bear.\"** The verse is often quoted about suffering in general — God will not give you more than you can handle. Paul is talking about *temptation and testing*, and elsewhere he says flatly that he was burdened beyond his strength, so that he would rely on God who raises the dead. The comfort here is real but specific: no one is ever cornered into sin with no way out."}]}, "1 Corinthians 10:31–33": {"title": "\"Whether you eat or drink\" — the end of a long argument about dinner", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous line is the conclusion of three chapters about meat, and knowing that changes how it reads."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth's markets sold meat that had been sacrificed in pagan temples. Some Christians ate it without a thought; others could not, and were distressed by those who did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's answer runs long, and it never resolves into a rule about the meat. It resolves into a question about people: what does eating this do to the person next to me?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three groups"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give no offence to Jews, or to Greeks, or to the church of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the whole social map — the synagogue, the pagan city, and his own congregation — and asks them to consider all three. Not a private conscience question; a public one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The standard he sets himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not seeking my own profit but the profit of the many, so that they may be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *profit* is a plain commercial one — advantage, benefit, what is in it for me. Paul says he runs the calculation for other people rather than for himself, and he says it as a description of how he already lives, not as advice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"do everything for the glory of God.\"** Lifted out, it becomes a general piety about doing your best. In place it is a tiebreaker for a specific and genuinely difficult question — when two Christians disagree about something permitted, and there is no rule to settle it, decide by what serves the other person. The very next line of the letter is *follow my example, as I follow Christ's*."}]}, "1 Corinthians 11:20–22": {"title": "Not the Lord's Supper", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul says something startling about a meeting that thought it was taking communion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So when you assemble together in one place, **it is not the Lord's Supper that you eat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *you are taking it badly*. It is not that meal at all. Whatever they were doing, the name did not apply to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each one goes ahead with his own supper, and one is left hungry while another is drunk.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Corinthian gathering was a full meal. Wealthier members arrived earlier — they were not working — and ate what they had brought. Slaves and labourers came when they could get away, to nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *goes ahead* is about taking yours first, before the others."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not have houses to eat and drink in? Or do you **despise the church of God**, and **shame those who have nothing**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not accuse them of bad theology about the bread. He accuses them of humiliating poor people at the table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *shame* is to put to shame, to disgrace — a public thing done to somebody's face."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In this I do not praise you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has praised them a few lines earlier for holding to what he passed on. Here he withdraws it, in the same formula, so the reversal is unmissable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famous words come next — *the Lord Jesus, on the night He was betrayed, took bread.* Paul recites the institution **as the correction** to what he has just described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses on, *not discerning the body* — which in this context is a gathering that cannot see the people it is eating with. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:27–30."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The point of the whole passage.** A meal about a body given for everyone was being eaten in a way that divided the room by income. Paul's remedy at the end is two words long: *wait for one another.*"}]}, "1 Corinthians 11:23–26": {"title": "Received from the Lord", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The oldest written account of the Lord's Supper anywhere — earlier than any of the four Gospels — and Paul says where he got it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I received from the Lord\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I **received from the Lord** what I also delivered to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul was not in the upper room. He was not a disciple then, and he does not say Peter told him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the Lord did. The same man tells the Corinthians in his next letter about being caught up to the third heaven and hearing words no one is permitted to repeat. Whatever else Paul received, he received this — and he passed on this one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Broken for you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take, eat: this is My body, **which is broken for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither Matthew nor Mark has this phrase. Luke has *given for you*. Paul has **broken**, and he adds the two words that make it personal: *for you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only place in the New Testament where the breaking of the bread is stated as being for the eater's sake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53 says *by His stripes we are healed* in the same breath as *He was wounded for our transgressions*. Two provisions in one passage, and two elements on one table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In remembrance of Me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice — once over the bread, once over the cup. Repetition in Paul is never accidental."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **anamnēsis** is stronger than a fond recollection; the Septuagint uses it for the memorial portion of an offering, the part that brought a thing to mind before God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The new covenant in My blood\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a renewal of the old. A different covenant, and Jeremiah 31 had promised it: *I will forgive their iniquity and remember their sin no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old covenant's terms were *if you obey*. The new one's terms are the blood it is named after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You proclaim the Lord's death until He comes\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For as often as you eat this bread and drink this cup, you **proclaim** the Lord's death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katangellō** — to announce publicly, the word used for preaching. Eating is a form of announcement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the announcement is aimed at more than the people in the room. Something is declared every time the bread is taken."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until He comes.* The instruction has an end date, which is also the only date in it that has not yet arrived."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage is answering.** Paul does not recite the institution as a liturgy lesson. He recites it because the Corinthians were shaming poor people at that table. The most quoted communion passage in Scripture was written as a correction. See the notes on 1 Corinthians 11:20–22 and 11:27–30."}]}, "1 Corinthians 11:27–30": {"title": "Discerning the body", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives a reason for weakness and sickness in the Corinthian church, and the reason he gives is not sin in general. It is one specific failure: *not discerning the Lord's body.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Diakrino** — to distinguish, to tell one thing apart from another. Not *deserve*. Not *feel worthy of*. To recognise what is in front of you and see what it is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the failure is a failure of recognition. Something was being handled without being seen for what it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What there was to recognise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just quoted the words of institution, four verses earlier: *this is My body, which is for you.* The bread is named as the body given on their behalf."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what was going unrecognised at Corinth — people eating in a hurry, some drunk, the wealthy shaming the poor, and the bread on the table treated as nothing in particular. Paul's whole complaint is that they were not seeing what they were holding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things to tell apart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Diakrino** does not only mean to notice. It means to **separate** one thing from another — to draw a line between two items and treat them differently."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There are two things on that table, and Paul has just named what each one is:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the cup** — *the new covenant in My blood*, and Matthew adds *for the forgiveness of sins*", "**the bread** — *My body, which is broken for you*, and Matthew's own Gospel says of that body, *He took our infirmities and bore our diseases*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "To take the cup for forgiveness and swallow the bread as though it were nothing in particular is to separate what He handed over together. The blood is not doubted; the body is passed over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the failure named in the verse, and Paul attaches a consequence to it in the next line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same word, on the road to Jerusalem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus uses **diakrino** in Mark 11, the morning after the fig tree: *whoever says to this mountain, be removed, and **does not doubt** in his heart…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word rendered *doubt* is this word. Doubting, in that sentence, is being divided in yourself — saying one thing and holding another, split down the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mountain and the fig tree are not two different kinds of work; a man who divides them will not move either. Neither are the body and the blood. See the note on Mark 11:20–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In an unworthy way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is an adverb. It describes **how** they ate, not who they were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That distinction is the difference between a table and a tribunal. Paul is not saying only worthy people may come — that would empty the room, and it would contradict what the bread means. He is saying there is a way of coming to it that misses the point of it entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not staying away. Two verses later: *wait for one another.* And in the same paragraph, *let a man examine himself, and so let him eat* — the examining leads to eating, not away from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer to eating badly is eating properly, with the body recognised."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line, and what it implies.** *For this reason many among you are weak and sick.* Paul states a link between failing to discern the body and physical weakness — which means the reverse is worth taking seriously too. This is a table where the body broken *for you* is set in front of you, and Paul plainly thought that recognising it mattered for more than the soul."}]}, "1 Corinthians 11:31–34": {"title": "Three judgments in two verses", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*If we judged ourselves, we would not be judged. But when we are judged, we are chastened by the Lord.* Three judgment words in two lines — and in the Greek they are not all the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first one is a different verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we **judged** ourselves…* — **diakrino**. To distinguish, to separate, to tell one thing apart from another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same verb Paul used two verses earlier in *not **discerning** the Lord's body*, and the same one Jesus used in Mark 11 for the man who *does not doubt* — is not divided — in his heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the first clause is not about interrogating yourself for sin. It is the verb of telling things apart, applied to yourself at that table: judging yourself healed because of a body broken for you, and forgiven because of blood poured out for you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second and third are another verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…we would not be **judged**. But when we are **judged**, we are chastened by the Lord.* — **krino**, both times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different Greek verbs, rendered by one English word, in a single sentence. The distinction is invisible in translation and it is the whole hinge of the passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And Paul says what the second word means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not leave it to be guessed. He defines it in the same breath:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **chastened** by the Lord, **so that we would not be condemned along with the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chastening and the condemning are set against each other, not stacked together. Whatever *krino* is doing here, Paul's own clause rules out its being sentence and condemnation — that is what the world is under, and he says so explicitly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Chastened\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paideuo**, from **pais**, a child. It is the vocabulary of raising a son — training, instruction, correction inside a family."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 12 uses the same word at length: *the Lord disciplines the one He loves… no discipline seems pleasant at the time, but afterward it yields the peaceable fruit of righteousness.* Correction, and a stated harvest. See the note on Hebrews 12:5–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And condemnation is off the table already"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…so that we would not be condemned along with the world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John 3 settles who is where: *whoever believes in Him is not condemned… he who does not believe is condemned already.* The condemnation is the world's condition, and it is described as a thing already in place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes to keep the Corinthians clear of that, not to put them under it. See the note on John 3:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The practical instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you come together to eat, **wait for one another**. If anyone is hungry, let him eat at home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After all of it — the warnings, the weakness, the sleeping — the remedy is five words long, and it is about manners at a shared table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is told to stay away."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *And the rest I will set in order when I come.* Paul closes the subject the way a man closes a letter, not the way a judge closes a case."}]}, "1 Corinthians 12:29–31": {"title": "Seven questions expecting no", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks seven questions in a row, and in Greek each is framed to expect the answer *no*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are all apostles? Are all prophets? Are all teachers? Are all workers of miracles? Do all have gifts of healings? Do all speak with tongues? Do all interpret?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The construction is unambiguous — he is not wondering. He is closing off the idea that every believer should exhibit every gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole argument of the chapter: a body is made of parts that are not the same, and an eye that resented not being a hand would be misunderstanding what a body is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Earnestly desire the best gifts\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after saying not everyone has everything, he tells them to want the better ones — a plural imperative, addressed to the church as a body rather than to individuals shopping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *best* is defined two chapters later, in his own terms: what builds the church up. In chapter 14 he ranks prophecy above tongues in a gathering for exactly that reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A still more excellent way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence that ends the chapter, and it is the doorway into chapter 13 — the passage about love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the structure people miss when 1 Corinthians 13 gets read at weddings. It is not a standalone poem; it is Paul's answer to a church quarrelling about which gifts made a person important."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How chapter 13 opens.** *If I speak in the tongues of men and of angels but have not love, I am a noisy gong.* He lists the very gifts they were competing over and prices each one at nothing without the thing he is about to describe. See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:1–5, where he picks the argument back up: *pursue love*, and then desire the gifts."}]}, "1 Corinthians 12:4–7": {"title": "\"The same Spirit\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three sentences built the same way, and the repetition is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Different kinds of **gifts**, but the **same Spirit**.* *Different ways of **serving**, but the **same Lord**.* *Different things being **done**, but it is the **same God** who works all of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times: variety on one side, sameness on the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the sameness is named as Spirit, Lord, and God — Paul writes a Trinitarian sentence without pausing to explain it, in the middle of a practical passage about church meetings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is saying it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth was ranking each other by which gift they had. The whole chapter is written against that, and the answer he gives is not to level the gifts but to point at their source."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Different — and Paul never suggests they should be the same. He simply removes the grounds for comparison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To each one\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **each one** the manifestation of the Spirit is given.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to the impressive ones, or the mature ones. Each one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *manifestation* is a showing, a making visible. Something invisible becoming apparent through a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the good of everyone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *toward what is profitable* — for the common advantage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the criterion Paul applies all through chapters 12 to 14. Not what a gift does for the person who has it. What it is worth to the room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again in chapter 14: *let all things be done for edification*, and *since you are zealous for spiritual gifts, seek to excel to the edifying of the church.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses following list them: a word of wisdom, a word of knowledge, faith, healings, the working of miracles, prophecy, discerning of spirits, tongues, and the interpretation of tongues."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after the list: *but one and the same Spirit works all these things, **distributing to each one individually as He wills**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distribution is His decision, not an achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The body"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the chapter is the body — the foot saying it is not a hand, the ear saying it is not an eye, and the eye unable to say to the hand *I have no need of you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those members of the body which seem to be weaker are necessary.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes straight after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 13. *Though I speak with the tongues of men and of angels, but have not love, I have become sounding brass.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists the gifts, describes the body, and then interrupts himself for a chapter on love before returning to the subject."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that opens the chapter.** *Now concerning spiritual gifts, brethren, **I do not want you to be ignorant**.* Paul's reason for the whole discussion is that they did not understand what they had."}]}, "1 Corinthians 12:8–11": {"title": "\"Just as He wills\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine items in a list, and the sentence that closes it is about who decides."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nine"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**A word of wisdom**", "**A word of knowledge**", "**Faith**", "**Gifts of healings**", "**The working of miracles**", "**Prophecy**", "**The discerning of spirits**", "**Different kinds of tongues**", "**The interpretation of tongues**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two that are words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two are **a word** of wisdom and **a word** of knowledge — the Greek is **logos**, a saying, an utterance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not wisdom as a quality, and not knowledge as a store of learning. A word of it, for an occasion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faith as a gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is in the list, which is worth noticing, because every believer is said to have faith elsewhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This one is listed alongside healings and miracles rather than alongside salvation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both plurals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gifts of **healings**.* Both nouns are plural in the Greek — **charismata iamaton**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a healing gift held by a person, in Paul's phrasing. Gifts, plural, of healings, plural."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul repeats the source clause four times in three verses:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*through **the Spirit**… according to **the same Spirit**… by **the same Spirit**… by **the one Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing to a church that was ranking itself by which gift a person had. The repetition is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for gifts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Charisma** — from **charis**, grace. A gift of grace, something given without being earned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same root for salvation, for celibacy, and for these."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **one and the same Spirit works all of these**, distributing to each one **individually** just as **He wills**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three claims in one sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**One and the same Spirit** — the ninth repetition of the point.", "**To each one individually** — the Greek is *to each his own*. Nobody has the set.", "**Just as He wills** — the Greek is **kathos bouletai**, according to His own deliberate choice."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distribution is not by request, merit, seniority or hunger. Paul closes the list by locating the decision entirely with the Spirit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says three verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There are **diversities of gifts, but the same Spirit**. There are **differences of ministries, but the same Lord**. And there are **diversities of activities, but it is the same God** who works all in all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three sets, and each is attributed to a different person of the Trinity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the manifestation of the Spirit is given to each one for the profit of all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *for what is advantageous* — **sumphero**, to bring together, to benefit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated before the list, and it governs it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into the body — one body, many members, the eye that cannot say to the hand *I have no need of you*, and the parts that seem weaker being necessary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, after *earnestly desire the best gifts*: *and yet I show you a more excellent way* — and the whole of chapter 13."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the nine have in common.** Not one of them is described as produced, cultivated, or achieved. Paul lists them, names one Spirit four times, and ends on the Spirit's own will."}]}, "1 Corinthians 13:4–8": {"title": "The way of love", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Fifteen short statements, and every one of them is a verb in the Greek."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a poem about romance. Paul is in the middle of an argument with a church that was ranking itself by which gift a person had, and this is the answer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 12 ends *and yet I show you a more excellent way*. Chapter 14 begins *pursue love*. This sits between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grammar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "English turns them into adjectives — love is patient, love is kind. The Greek has verbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Love **waits**, love **acts kindly**, love **does not envy**. It is a description of conduct, not of temperament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two positives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love **waits without complaining**. Love **is kind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the first is **makrothumei** — long-tempered, slow to burn. It is used of God's patience with people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only two of the fifteen are positive at the start. The next eight are all negatives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eight things it does not do"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Is not jealous** — **zeloi**, the same root as *zeal*", "**Does not show itself off** — **perpereuetai**, a rare word for boasting; it occurs once in the Bible", "**Is not full of itself** — **phusioutai**, puffed up, inflated. Paul uses it six times in this letter and almost nowhere else; it was Corinth's particular problem", "**Is never rude** — **aschemonei**, behaving indecently", "**Is not out for what it can get** — *does not seek its own*", "**Is not quick to take offense** — **paroxunetai**, provoked, stirred", "**Keeps no ledger of wrongs** — **logizetai**, the accounting word. Love does not enter it in the book", "**Takes no pleasure in anything wrong**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ledger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love **keeps no ledger of wrongs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is the one Paul uses eleven times in Romans 4 for God crediting righteousness and not imputing sin. See the note on Romans 4:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same bookkeeping word, applied to what love does with an offence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four \"keeps on\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love **bears up under anything**. Love **keeps on trusting**. Love **keeps on hoping**. Love **outlasts everything**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first and last are the same Greek root twice — **stegei** (covers, roofs over) and **hupomenei** (remains under)."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love **never fails**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oudepote piptei** — never falls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What does fail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **prophecies — they will come to an end; tongues — they will fall silent; knowledge — it will be finished with**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three gifts Corinth was competing over, and Paul lists all three as temporary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument. Not that the gifts are worthless — he has just spent a chapter saying they come from one Spirit — but that they have an end date and this does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For we know in part and we prophesy in part. But when that which is perfect has come, then that which is in part will be done away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For now we see in a mirror, dimly, but then face to face.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three that remain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And now abide **faith, hope, love**, these three; but **the greatest of these is love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith and hope also end — you do not need either when the thing is in front of you. Paul calls love greatest and then, in the next sentence, tells them to chase it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter sits.** Between a list of nine spiritual gifts and the instructions for using them in a meeting. It is not an interlude. It is the standard the other two chapters are measured against."}]}, "1 Corinthians 14:1–5": {"title": "\"Pursue love\" — the verb, and what it is set against", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 13 has just finished — faith, hope and love, and the greatest of these is love. Then, without a break, this: *pursue love.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two different verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses one word for love and a different one for the gifts, and the pair is deliberate:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**pursue** love — *dioko*, to chase, to hunt down, to run after. The word used of persecutors pursuing their target.", "**desire earnestly** the spiritual gifts — a strong word for wanting, but not the same as running someone down."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Love is the thing you chase. The gifts are things you want. Nobody reading this in Corinth could have missed which one Paul put in the hunting verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure he applies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this is judged by one question: does it build up the church?"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the one who speaks in a tongue *builds himself up*", "the one who prophesies *builds up the church*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not dismissive — he says he wishes they all spoke in tongues, and elsewhere that he does so more than any of them. His argument is about the gathering: in a room full of people, the thing that everyone can understand does more good than the thing only one person understands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"builds up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Oikodomeo** — from **oikos**, house, and **demo**, to build. To construct a house, or to repair one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it in this chapter more than anywhere else in his letters. It governs everything he says about a meeting: *let all things be done for edification.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the speaking is to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who speaks in a tongue **does not speak to men but to God**, for no one understands him; however, in the spirit he speaks mysteries.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states the direction before he states the ranking. The addressee is God."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"prophesy\" means here.** Paul defines it in the passage rather than leaving it to be assumed: speaking *to build them up, to urge them on, and to comfort them*. That is a wider and more ordinary thing than predicting the future, and all three of its stated purposes are aimed at other people."}]}, "1 Corinthians 14:13–19": {"title": "\"My spirit prays\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul describes two things that can be going on at once, and says he does both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I pray in a tongue, **my spirit prays, but my understanding is unfruitful**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *understanding* is **nous** — the mind, the reasoning faculty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Unfruitful** is **akarpos** — bearing no fruit. Paul is not saying the praying is unfruitful. He says the mind is not the part producing anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does not conclude"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not conclude that it should stop. The next line is the resolution:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is it then?** I will pray with the spirit, **and I will pray with the understanding also**; I will sing with the spirit, **and I will sing with the understanding also**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, two pairs, and the connector is *also* rather than *instead*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore let the one who speaks in a tongue **pray that he may interpret**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Interpretation is itself something to be prayed for. Paul treats it as available on request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters in a room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Otherwise, if you bless with the spirit, **how will the person sitting there** who does not follow any of it **say the 'Amen'** to your thanksgiving?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the one who fills the place of the uninformed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Amen is the congregation's part. It was the response in the synagogue and it is what Paul is protecting — a person cannot agree with what they cannot follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession he makes in the same breath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **you indeed give thanks well enough**, but the other person is not built up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not withdraw the first half. The thanksgiving is fine. The problem named is one of usefulness to somebody else in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I thank my God that I speak with tongues more than you all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to the church that was doing it most, and he outdoes them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody would guess it from Acts or from any of his letters, which is the point of him having to say it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yet **in the church** I would rather speak **five words with my understanding**, so that I might **teach others also**, than **ten thousand words in a tongue**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five against ten thousand — a ratio of one to two thousand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the whole comparison is qualified at the front: **in the church**. Paul is ranking the two for a gathering, not in general."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The governing rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter states it repeatedly: *let all things be done for **edification**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Oikodome** — house-building. Paul's whole test in this chapter is whether something builds anybody up, and he applies it to a meeting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says four verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who speaks in a tongue **edifies himself**, but he who prophesies edifies the church.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states both as facts and then spends the chapter on which one belongs in a public gathering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, desire earnestly to prophesy, **and do not forbid to speak with tongues**. Let all things be done decently and in order.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both clauses, in the same sentence, as his closing instruction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul never does in the chapter.** Argue that the thing is not real, not from God, or not for them. His entire concern is the person sitting in the seat who cannot say Amen to what they did not follow."}]}, "1 Corinthians 14:20–25": {"title": "\"A sign, not to those who believe\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Isaiah at them and then walks two visitors into an imaginary meeting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be children in understanding**; in malice be babes, **but in understanding be mature**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things to be childlike about and one not to be. The Greek for *malice* is **kakia** — badness, ill-will."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Be an infant about that. Be grown up about thinking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**With men of other tongues and other lips I will speak to this people, and even so they will not hear Me**,\" says the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 28. In its own setting it was a threat: since Judah would not listen to plain Hebrew from the prophets, they would hear Assyrian — the language of the invading army. See the note on Isaiah 28:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Foreign speech in the ears of the people of God, in that passage, meant judgment had arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion drawn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So then, **tongues are for a sign, not to those who believe but to unbelievers**; while **prophesying is not for unbelievers but for those who believe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are counter-intuitive and Paul states them flatly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first scenario"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If the whole church comes together in one place and **everyone speaks with tongues**, and there come in those who are **uninformed or unbelievers**, will they not say **that you are out of your minds?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mainesthe** — you are raving, mad."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a sign, in Isaiah's sense. The visitor concludes something, and what they conclude is that these people are unreachable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second scenario"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But if all prophesy**, and an unbeliever or an uninformed person comes in.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things happen to him, in order:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*He is **convinced by all***", "*he is **convicted by all***", "*and so **the secrets of his heart are made plain***", "*and **falling down on his face he will worship God***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *and report that **God is truly among you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Elencho** — to expose, to convict, to bring to light. It is the word John uses of the Spirit: *He will convict the world of sin.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What prophecy is in this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul defined it four verses in: *he who prophesies speaks **edification and exhortation and comfort** to men.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not primarily prediction. Building, urging, comforting — in a language the room understands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the visitor does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the speaker. He falls down and reports that God is there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with all of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses after this set out the order for a meeting: two or three at most, one at a time, and let one interpret. And if no interpreter, *let him keep silent in church, and let him speak to himself and to God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *let all things be done decently and in order.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The rule underneath.** Paul is asking one question the whole way through — what does the person who walked in off the street take away from it. Everything in the chapter is arranged around that visitor."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:12–19": {"title": "If Christ is not risen", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the position he is arguing against and follows it all the way down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem in Corinth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How is it that **some among you are saying there is no resurrection of the dead?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that Christ did not rise. That the dead in general are not raised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Greek thought had no difficulty with the soul surviving; a body coming back was the offensive part. Paul had been laughed off the Areopagus in Athens over exactly this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If there is **no resurrection of the dead**, then **Christ Himself has not been raised**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He runs their principle backwards. A general rule with no exceptions cannot have one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They wanted to keep the resurrection of Christ and drop the rest. Paul says the two stand or fall together, and he says it twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each consequence follows from the last:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Our **preaching is empty*** — the Greek is **kenon**, hollow, containing nothing.", "*And **your faith is empty** as well.*", "*We are found to be **false witnesses about God***, because we testified that He raised Him.", "*Your faith is **futile***, and *you are **still in your sins***.", "*Those who have **fallen asleep in Christ have perished***."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for futile"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mataia** — useless, pointless, without effect. It is a different word from the *kenon* of verse 14: that one means empty of content, this one means empty of result."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Still in your sins\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clause carries the theology of the chapter. If the resurrection did not happen, the death did not settle anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it in Romans the other way round: *delivered up because of our offences, and **raised because of our justification**.* See the note on Romans 4:23–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And those who have **fallen asleep in Christ have perished**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He includes the people they had buried. If he is wrong, the ones who died believing are simply gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If our hope in Christ belongs to this life only, then **there is no one on earth more to be pitied than we are**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *most pitiable of all men*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say Christians would still be better off, or that the ethics would remain worth having. He says they would be the most wretched people alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had been beaten five times, stoned once, shipwrecked three times, and was writing from Ephesus where he says he *fought with beasts*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The life only made sense on one condition, and he says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But now **Christ is risen from the dead, and has become the firstfruits** of those who have fallen asleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aparche** — the first sheaf brought in, which is not a sample but a guarantee. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:23–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witnesses he had already listed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cephas, the twelve, over five hundred at once — *of whom the greater part remain to the present, but some have fallen asleep* — then James, then all the apostles, and last of all himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names people who could be asked."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul refuses to do.** Salvage anything. He takes the denial, applies it consistently, and lists everything that goes with it — including the people already buried."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:23–28": {"title": "The last enemy", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is arguing for the resurrection against people who had decided it was nonsense, and here he sets out the order of events."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The last enemy that will be destroyed is death\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in that sentence are easy to miss."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Enemy.** Death is not described as natural, or as a doorway, or as part of the design. It is on a list of hostile powers being put down. The Bible's usual tenderness about death — falling asleep, departing — never softens into calling it a friend."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Last.** It goes, but it goes at the end. Everything else is subdued first. That is a straight answer to the question of why death is still here if it has been defeated: Paul says the defeat is real and the removal is scheduled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ the firstfruits, and afterward those who are Christ's at His coming.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Firstfruits is a harvest word — the first sheaf brought in, which is not a sample but a guarantee. The rest of the field is coming, and it will be the same crop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument of the whole chapter compressed into a phrase: what happened to Him is what happens to His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He must reign until\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **He must reign until** He has put all enemies under His feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reign is present tense and the subduing is in progress. Paul is not describing a future coronation — he is describing what is happening now, between the resurrection and the last enemy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Psalm 110 again: *sit at My right hand **until** I make Your enemies Your footstool.* Both verses put the reigning and the subduing in the same window, and in both the one reigning is seated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the putting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **He has put** all things under His feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Psalm 8 and then stops to clarify the grammar: *when He says all things are put under Him, it is evident that He who put all things under Him is excepted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He interrupts his own argument to make sure nobody misreads a pronoun."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The strange ending.** Paul says that when everything has been put under Christ, the Son Himself will be subject to the Father, *so that God may be all in all.* It reads oddly against everything else the New Testament says about Christ's glory — and it is Paul's way of saying that the goal of the whole operation is not a rearranged universe with a new authority in charge, but God filling everything, with nothing left out and nothing left over."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:3–8": {"title": "What he handed on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The oldest summary of the Christian message in existence, and Paul says he did not write it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The transmission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I **handed on** to you, first of all, **the very thing I myself received**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paredoka** and **parelabon** — the technical pair for passing on a fixed tradition. Rabbinic teaching used the same vocabulary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is quoting. He says so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was converted within a few years of the crucifixion, so what he is repeating predates his own conversion — which puts this formula within a handful of years of the event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"First of all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *among the first things*, in order of importance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each begins with *that*, which is how a creed is set out:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*That **Christ died for our sins** according to the Scriptures*", "*That **He was buried***", "*That **He rose again the third day** according to the Scriptures*", "*That **He was seen***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the four carry *according to the Scriptures*. The death and the resurrection are both attached to the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The burial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That He was buried.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A short clause doing a specific job. A buried man is a dead man, and the tomb is a location somebody could have gone to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list of witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cephas, and then **the twelve**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter first. The Gospels give the women first, which is why the omission is noticeable — Paul is listing official witnesses in a legal sense, and a woman's testimony carried no weight in that world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After that He was seen by **more than five hundred brothers all at one time**, and **most of them are still here with us to this present day**, though some have fallen asleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That clause is an invitation. Most of them can be asked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After that He was seen by **James**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Lord's brother, who did not believe during His life. See the note on Acts 1:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then by **all the apostles**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **last of all He was seen by me too**, as by **a child born at the wrong time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ektroma** — a miscarriage, an untimely birth. It is a harsh word, and Paul chose it about himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse explains: *for I am the least of the apostles, who am not worthy to be called an apostle, **because I persecuted the church of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is on the list, and he says how he got there. See the note on Acts 9:3–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse after that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **by the grace of God I am what I am**, and His grace toward me was not in vain; but I laboured more abundantly than they all, **yet not I, but the grace of God which was with me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole chapter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some in Corinth were saying there is no resurrection of the dead. Paul sets out the tradition first, and then follows their premise all the way down. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:12–19."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does not claim.** Authorship. He opens the most important paragraph he ever wrote by saying he is passing on what he was handed — and then puts himself at the end of the witness list with the ugliest word available."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:50–52": {"title": "A mystery and a trumpet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent a chapter arguing that the dead are raised. Here he deals with the people who are not dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Flesh and blood **cannot inherit the kingdom of God**, and **what decays cannot inherit what never decays**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phthora** (corruption, decay) against **aphtharsia** (incorruption)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the body is bad. That it is the wrong material for the destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen, **I am telling you a mystery**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **musterion** — not a puzzle, but something previously undisclosed and now told."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it of things that could not have been worked out and had to be revealed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mystery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not all of us will fall asleep, **but all of us will be changed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and the second is the universal one. Dying is optional in the sentence; changing is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Fall asleep** is the New Testament's usual word for a believer's death. Paul uses it throughout this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **a moment**, in **the twinkling of an eye**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *moment* is **atomos** — indivisible, uncuttable. The word behind *atom*. The smallest unit of time there is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The twinkling of an eye** — **rhipe**, a flick, a dart. It is used of the beat of a wing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The signal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At **the last trumpet**. For **the trumpet will sound**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Trumpets in Israel called the assembly, moved the camp, and announced the new moon and the Day of Atonement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same in Thessalonians: *the Lord Himself will descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of an archangel, and with the trumpet of God.* See the note on 1 Thessalonians 4:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two groups"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the dead will be raised beyond the reach of decay**, and **we will be changed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says *we*. He wrote as a man who expected to be in the second group."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For this corruptible must put on incorruption, and this mortal must put on immortality.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *put on* is **endusasthai** — to get dressed, to put on clothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same verb elsewhere of putting on Christ, and of the armour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The taunt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**O Death, where is your sting? O Hades, where is your victory?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Hosea, and turns a prophet's line into a mockery of the thing being quoted about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sting of death is sin, and the strength of sin is the law. **But thanks be to God, who gives us the victory** through our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, my beloved brethren, **be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord**, knowing that your labour is not in vain in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-seven verses on the resurrection, and the application is to keep working."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the next sentence in the letter is *now concerning the collection*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 16:1–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is promised to everybody.** Not that nobody dies. Paul says plainly that some will and some will not, and then says all of them are changed — in a unit of time he describes as indivisible."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:56–57": {"title": "\"The strength of sin is the law\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two short clauses in the middle of Paul's chapter on resurrection, and the second one is startling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **sting of death is sin**, and the **strength of sin is the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things linked backwards: death gets its sting from sin, and sin gets its power from the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Remove the last link and the whole chain goes slack."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Strength\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunamis** — power, the word behind dynamite. Not *the measure of sin* or *the definition of sin*. Its power source."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same thing at length in Romans 7: *I would not have known covetousness unless the law had said, \"You shall not covet.\" But sin, taking opportunity by the commandment, produced in me all manner of evil desire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *the commandment which was to bring life, I found to bring death.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which does not make the law bad"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul defends it in the same breath: *the law is holy, and the commandment holy and just and good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fault is not in the statute. It is in what a prohibition does when it meets the old nature — a wall to push against, a line to notice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin is not weakened by being told to stop. It is armed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this is in a chapter about resurrection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just quoted Hosea: *O Death, where is your sting? O Hades, where is your victory?* It is a taunt, thrown at a defeated enemy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he explains what made the sting sharp in the first place — and the two clauses are there to show that the whole apparatus has been dismantled at the root, not merely at the grave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **thanks be to God, who gives us the victory** through our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *who helps us achieve* it. Gives — present tense, continuous in the Greek."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The victory is handed over, and the passage names where it came from and does not name any contribution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, my beloved brethren, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, knowing that **your labour is not in vain** in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one command in the paragraph comes after the victory has been given, not before it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chain is broken.** *You are not under law but under grace* — Romans 6:14, and the promise attached to it is *sin shall not have dominion over you.* Paul puts the two in one sentence, in that order. See the note on Romans 6:12–14."}]}, "1 Corinthians 16:1–4": {"title": "On the first day of the week", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just finished the great resurrection chapter, and the next sentence is about money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 15 ends *therefore, my beloved brethren, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, knowing that your labour is not in vain in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *now about the collection.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul puts the two next to each other with nothing in between."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction is not new"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do **just as I directed the churches of Galatia** to do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is repeating an arrangement already running elsewhere. Corinth is being brought into a scheme, not given a personal appeal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **the first day of the week**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the earliest references to Christians organising anything around Sunday — this letter predates the Gospels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the day of the resurrection, the day the Emmaus road happened, the day the disciples were gathered when He came through the door, and the day Troas met to break bread. See the note on Acts 20:7–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four parts of the instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **each one of you** **set something aside**, **storing it up** **in keeping with how well he has done**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Each one** — nobody exempt, nobody named.", "**Set aside** — the Greek is *put by himself*, at home, personally.", "**Storing it up** — accumulating, not a single gesture.", "**In keeping with how well he has done** — the Greek is *as he may prosper*."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "No sum is stated. No percentage is stated. The measure is each person's own account of how the week went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why weekly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **there will be no collections to make when I come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not want to arrive and start fundraising. He wants it already done, so that his visit is about something else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It also means nobody gives under the pressure of an apostle standing in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who carries it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whomever **you approve by your letters** I will send to carry your gift to Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They choose the couriers, not him, and the appointment is in writing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul keeps his own hands off the money. He says why in 2 Corinthians: *avoiding this, that anyone should blame us in this lavish gift… providing honourable things, not only in the sight of the Lord, but also in the sight of men.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And if it is fitting that I go as well\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He treats his own involvement as optional and puts it last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it was going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jerusalem — the same relief effort that runs across four of Paul's letters, from Gentile churches to the Jewish believers in Judea. The famine had come as Agabus said it would. See the note on Acts 11:27–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 15: the dead are raised, death is swallowed up, your labour is not in vain. Chapter 16: put something aside on Sunday."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The resurrection argument ends in a practical arrangement about a collection."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is missing from the instruction.** Any figure. Paul, who could simply have set one, tells each person to weigh their own week and put something by."}]}, "1 Corinthians 2:1–5": {"title": "\"In weakness, and in fear\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul describes his own arrival in Corinth, and every detail is a disqualification."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did not bring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I did not come with **grand speech or impressive wisdom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huperoche logou e sophias** — superiority of word or wisdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth was a Greek city that paid for rhetoric. A travelling speaker was judged on delivery, and Paul's opponents there later said his *bodily presence is weak, and his speech contemptible*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The decision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **I made up my mind to know nothing** while I was among you **except Jesus Christ, and Him crucified**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekrina** — I judged, I decided. It was a deliberate narrowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**And Him crucified** — the perfect participle: crucified and standing crucified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just spent a chapter on why: *we proclaim Christ crucified — a stumbling block to the Jews and foolishness to the Greeks.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:18–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he had come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Athens. Acts 17 has him on the Areopagus quoting Greek poets, and the response was that some mocked and some said *we will hear you again on this matter*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *after these things Paul departed from Athens and went to Corinth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three states"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I was with you **in weakness**, and **in fear**, and **in much trembling**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **astheneia**, **phobos**, and **tromos polys**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not describing humility as a posture. He says he was frightened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts confirms it: in Corinth the Lord spoke to him in a vision — *do not be afraid, but speak, and do not keep silent; for I am with you, and no one will attack you to hurt you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not say that to a man who is not afraid."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the preaching was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My speaking and my preaching were **not made of the persuasive words of man's wisdom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word for *persuasive* is rare and may have been coined here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But were **a showing of the Spirit and of power**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apodeixis** — a demonstration, a proof. It was a technical term in logic for a conclusive argument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rejects one kind of proof and claims another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **your faith might not rest on the wisdom of men but on the power of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *rest on* is *be in*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names the purpose of his own weakness: a faith built on a persuasive speaker stands on the speaker."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Corinth was like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter is written to a church with factions, a lawsuit, a case of immorality worse than the pagans', drunkenness at the Lord's table, and an argument about which gift ranked highest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That congregation came out of eighteen months of preaching by a frightened man who had decided to talk about one thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the message next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But we speak the wisdom of God in a mystery, the hidden wisdom which God ordained before the ages for our glory, **which none of the rulers of this age knew; for had they known, they would not have crucified the Lord of glory**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he lists as his qualifications.** No rhetoric, no impressive wisdom, one subject, weakness, fear and much trembling — and he says he arranged it that way on purpose."}]}, "1 Corinthians 3:16–17": {"title": "\"That temple is what you are\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks a question he clearly thinks they should not need asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Do you not know\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the phrase ten times in this letter. It is not gentle — it means *you already have this information.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **the temple of God**, and **the Spirit of God makes His home in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *temple* is **naos** — not the whole temple complex but the inner sanctuary, the holy place itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the courts. The room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who \"you\" is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plural, throughout. Paul is addressing the church at Corinth as a body — *you all, together, are the sanctuary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He makes the individual point elsewhere in the same letter: *your body is the temple of the Holy Spirit who is in you.* Both are true, and this one is corporate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which church he says it to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth. The letter's own list: division into factions, a man sleeping with his father's wife, believers suing each other, drunkenness at the Lord's table, chaos in the meetings, and some of them denying the resurrection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul calls that congregation the sanctuary of God, in chapter 3, before correcting any of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone **ruins** the temple of God, **God will ruin him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Greek verb both times — **phtheiro**, to corrupt, to destroy, to spoil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What you do to it is what happens to you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ruining is, in context"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is about the divisions: *I am of Paul… I am of Apollos.* And about building on the foundation with gold and silver or with wood, hay and straw."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing that ruins the temple in this passage is factionalism — men splitting a congregation around their favourite teacher."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the temple of God is **holy**, and **that temple is what you are**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek puts the emphasis at the end: *which you are.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this replaced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon's temple took seven years, a hundred and eighty thousand men, cedar, gold, and stone cut silently in a quarry. See the note on 1 Kings 6:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Solomon's own verdict at the dedication: *heaven and the heaven of heavens cannot contain You. How much less this temple which I have built!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells a fractious church in Greece that they are the room."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jesus said about the building.** *\"Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up.\"* They thought He meant the forty-six-year construction project they were standing in. *But He was speaking of the temple of His body.*"}]}, "1 Corinthians 3:5–9": {"title": "Two men, one field, and the verb that settles the argument", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Corinthians had split into fan clubs — some for Paul, some for Apollos. Paul answers with farming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tenses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I planted, Apollos watered, but God is the One who made it grow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek the first two verbs are aorist — single, finished actions. Somebody turned up, did a job, and left. The third is imperfect: **kept on giving the growth**, continuous, still going."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument is in the grammar. Two men each did a discrete piece of work. One party has been doing the thing that actually matters, the whole time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Neither one is anything\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a hard sentence about himself, and Paul means it as a corrective to people building identities out of which preacher they preferred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But he does not leave them worthless: *each will receive his own reward according to his own labour*. Not according to results. According to labour — which is the only part a planter or waterer controls."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *We are fellow workers who belong to God; you are God's field, God's building.* Paul flips the metaphor mid-sentence, from farm to construction, and he will run with the building for the next several verses. Both images have the same point: the workers are staff, and the property is somebody else's."}]}, "1 Corinthians 5:1–2": {"title": "A grief not yet felt", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul names a situation in one sentence and then turns, immediately, to the congregation's reaction to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being reported"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immorality of a kind **not found even among the nations around you** — that a man is living with his father's wife.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman law itself forbade this; Cicero calls a comparable case an almost unheard-of crime. Paul's measure is not the synagogue but the pagan city, and Corinth was not a strict place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His father's wife*, not *his mother* — the phrase indicates a stepmother, and Leviticus 18 names it directly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **you** are swollen with pride over it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is the one Paul has been using through the whole letter for the Corinthians' inflation — puffed up, blown out like a bellows. He has used it in chapter 4 twice already."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses in, and the subject has changed from the man to the church."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What should have been there instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Should you not rather have been **broken with grief**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is the one used of mourning the dead. Paul does not ask for outrage, disgust, or a statement. He asks for a funeral."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notice the order in his sentence: *so that the man… would be taken out from among you.* The mourning comes first, and the action follows from it. A church that had grieved would have known what to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were proud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's whole argument gives the answer. Corinth prized wisdom, freedom, and being beyond the reach of small rules. A man doing this and remaining in fellowship was, in that atmosphere, a demonstration of how free they were."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does not do.** He does not write to the man. The letter is addressed to the church, the pride is the church's, and the correction lands there. See the notes on 1 Corinthians 5:3–5 and 1 Corinthians 5:9–11."}]}, "1 Corinthians 5:3–5": {"title": "\"So that the spirit may be saved\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of Paul's severest paragraphs, and the last clause tells you what the whole thing is for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Present in spirit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though I am absent in body I am **present with you in spirit**, and I have already reached my judgment, as though I were standing there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same thing to the Colossians — *absent in the flesh, with you in spirit* — and there it is warm approval of a church holding its line. Here it is a verdict."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase does both, which suggests it means something more than a fond thought at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When you are gathered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, **when you are gathered together** — my spirit with you, and the power of our Lord Jesus Christ with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things are named as present at the meeting: the church, Paul's spirit, and *the power of our Lord Jesus Christ*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a gathering in the name, with the power, and Paul is careful to say so before he says what is to be done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Handed over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Such a one is to be handed over to Satan for the destruction of the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *handed over* is the same verb used of Jesus being delivered up. It means to place into someone's hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The flesh** here is set against **the spirit** in the next clause, and Paul's usual use of *sarx* is not the body but the whole self as it operates apart from God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…**so that the spirit may be saved** in the day of the Lord Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the sentence bends toward this. The severity is instrumental. Paul is not describing a punishment with an end in itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What actually happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If 2 Corinthians 2 refers to the same man — and most readers have taken it that way — the sequel is Paul urging the church to *forgive and comfort him*, in case he is *swallowed up with too much sorrow*, and to *reaffirm your love for him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The discipline worked, and Paul's next concern is that it not go too far."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is really about.** Not the offender. Paul's anger in verse 2 is at the congregation: *and you are proud!* A church that boasted of its tolerance is the target, and the sentence in verse 5 is the shock administered to it."}]}, "1 Corinthians 5:9–11": {"title": "The letter they misread", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul had written to Corinth before, and they had taken his instruction and turned it exactly the wrong way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The earlier letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I wrote to you in my letter not to keep company with people who are sexually immoral.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A letter we do not have. This is one of the places where the New Testament refers plainly to correspondence that was not kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did not mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I did not mean the immoral of this world **at all** — otherwise you would have to leave the world altogether.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had read it as a rule about outsiders. Paul says the opposite was intended, and his reason is practical to the point of dryness: you would have to leave the planet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no instruction anywhere in Paul to withdraw from unbelievers. The separation he asks for runs in the other direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not to keep company with anyone **who is called a brother** and is…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The qualifier is the hinge of the paragraph. The list that follows applies inside the church, to someone claiming the name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six items, and they are not sorted by how shocking they are:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["sexually immoral", "**greedy**", "an idolater", "**someone who tears people down with his words**", "a drunkard", "a swindler"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A reviler sits between an idolater and a drunkard. Paul puts the sins of the mouth and the wallet in the same sentence as the sin that started the chapter, without ranking them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given that chapter 5 began with the church being *swollen with pride*, the inclusion of the reviler is not accidental."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not even to sit down and eat\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The table was where fellowship was expressed. In Corinth it was also where the Lord's Supper was eaten."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Withholding the meal is the specific action named — and read against chapter 11, where the rich shamed the poor at that same table, the instruction is not about keeping the meal pure. It is about not letting the meal say something untrue."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter ends.** *God judges those outside.* Paul draws the line explicitly: the church's business is the church. The verse most often used to police the world is, in its own context, a refusal to do that."}]}, "1 Corinthians 6:18–20": {"title": "\"You are not your own\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives one instruction about running away and then three reasons about ownership."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Flee** sexual immorality.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pheugo** — run. Not resist, not manage, not stand firm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same word for idolatry, and Joseph is the model — *he left the garment in her hand and fled outside.* See the note on Genesis 39:7–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There are things the New Testament says to stand against and things it says to run from, and Paul is clear which this is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distinction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Every other sin a person commits is **outside the body**, but the one who sins sexually **sins against his own body**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's own argument, and readers have long noticed it will not survive a pedantic reading — gluttony and drunkenness reach the body too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not writing a taxonomy. He is saying this one goes somewhere the others do not, and his reason is in the verses either side: *the two shall become one flesh.* See the note on Genesis 2:21–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The temple"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do you not know that **your body is the temple of the Holy Spirit** who is in you, **whom you have from God**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **naos** — the inner sanctuary, not the courts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this one is singular. Three chapters earlier Paul said the same of the church collectively; here he says it of a person. See the note on 1 Corinthians 3:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whom you have from God* — the Spirit is described as a possession received rather than an achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ownership"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And that you are not your own?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, and they are the argument of the whole passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth's slogan, quoted by Paul twice in this chapter, was *all things are lawful for me.* This is his answer to it: the question is not what is permitted but whose you are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You were bought at a price.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a market word — purchased, the way you buy something at a stall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not name the sum here. He does elsewhere: *not with corruptible things, like silver or gold… but with the precious blood of Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **glorify God in your body** and in your spirit, which are God's.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note where it lands. Not *avoid* — glorify. The instruction is positive and it is about the body specifically."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Corinth was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city had a reputation across the empire; *to Corinthianise* was a Greek verb for living immorally."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had just listed what some of them had been — and then: *and such were some of you. But you were washed, but you were sanctified, but you were justified.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, all past tense, before any of this instruction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the argument starts.** *Do you not know?* — six times in this one chapter. Paul's remedy for the Corinthians' behaviour is repeatedly to tell them what is already true of them."}]}, "1 Corinthians 9:7–10": {"title": "Soldier, vineyard, flock", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul argues a right at length and then, in the same chapter, declines to use it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who ever serves as a **soldier at his own expense**? Who **plants a vineyard** and does not eat its fruit? Who **tends a flock** and does not drink of the milk?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three trades, three self-evident answers. Nobody funds their own army service, plants a vineyard for someone else's grapes, or shepherds without milk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The principle is so ordinary that Paul does not argue it — he just asks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Am I saying these things **only as a man would**? **Does the law not say the same**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He will not rest the case on common sense. He goes to Moses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ox walking in circles over the threshing floor, with grain under its feet all day, and the law forbids tying its mouth shut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is it **oxen that God is concerned about**, or does He say it entirely for our sake?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A striking piece of interpretation. Paul takes an agricultural regulation and says the point of it was never really the ox."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For our sake it was written, that **the one who ploughs ought to plough in hope**, and the one who threshes in hope of sharing in the harvest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever does the work should have grounds to expect a share of what the work produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul concludes from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we have sown spiritual things for you, is it a great thing if we reap your material things?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses later: *the Lord has commanded that those who preach the gospel should live from the gospel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He establishes the right about as thoroughly as it can be established."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **I have used none of these things**… For it would be better for me to die than that anyone should make my boasting void.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had the right and would not take it, and the reason he gives is *lest we hinder the gospel of Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Corinth he made tents. In Thessalonica he *worked night and day, that we might not be a burden.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both things at once"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul holds two positions without embarrassment: that ministers ought to be supported, and that he personally would not be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He argues the first for other people's sake and declines it for the gospel's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy gets the same principle as instruction: *the elders who lead well should be counted worthy of a double portion of honour… the worker has earned his pay.* See the note on 1 Timothy 5:17–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says he is doing instead.** *What is my reward then? That when I preach the gospel, I may present the gospel of Christ **without charge**.* The pay he wanted was the pleasure of not charging."}]}, "1 John 2:16–17": {"title": "The three cravings, and where they were first used", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John lists three things and says none of them come from the Father. The list is not arbitrary — it has been used before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the craving of the body**", "**the craving of the eyes**", "**the bragging about what a man has**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now set that beside Genesis 3, where Eve looks at the tree:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the fruit seemed **good to eat*** — the body", "***pleasing to the eye*** — the eyes", "***desirable for the wisdom it promised*** — the standing it would give her"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same three, in the same order, at the first temptation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And in the wilderness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three temptations of Christ run the same way. Turn the stones into bread — the body. All the kingdoms of the world shown to Him — the eyes. Throw Yourself down and let the angels catch You — the display, the proving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One test taken in a garden with everything available, and one in a desert with nothing. The first was failed and the second was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says about the shelf life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The world is on its way out, and everything it makes you crave along with it. But whoever does what God wants stays forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is present continuous — the world **is passing**, in progress, already under way. He is not predicting an end so much as describing something currently draining away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is not between pleasure and misery. It is between what is going and what stays."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it is not a list of forbidden things.** John does not name any object — no food, no possession, no specific pleasure. Every item on his list is an appetite pointed a particular direction. The problem he identifies is where the craving comes *from*, not what it happens to land on."}]}, "1 John 2:20–21": {"title": "\"There is an anointing on you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John is writing about people who had left the church claiming superior knowledge, and this is his answer to the readers who stayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chrisma** — an anointing, and the noun is built on the same root as **Christos**, the Anointed One. John is making the connection deliberately: the Anointed has anointed them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Israel the oil went on prophets, priests and kings. John says the ordinary believers reading his letter have it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you know all things.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is not a claim to omniscience — the next verse makes that clear: *I have not written to you because you do not know the truth, but because you **do** know it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not adding to their information. He is telling them they already have what the departing teachers claimed to be selling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses just before name the problem: people had gone out from among them, teaching that Jesus was not the Christ. The whole passage is about a congregation being told they were missing something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's response is not a counter-argument. It is: you have the anointing, and you know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No lie ever comes out of the truth\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "A short, blunt line, and it is the test he gives them. Truth does not generate lies. If a teaching leads away from what they already knew about Christ, its source is settled by that fact alone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it goes.** A few verses on: *the anointing you received from Him remains in you… as it has taught you, remain in Him.* John does not tell them to seek a fresh experience. He tells them what they have stays, and to stay in the One who gave it."}]}, "1 John 2:26–27": {"title": "\"That anointing stays right in you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John says why he wrote the paragraph, and then tells them they did not need it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have written this to you about **the people who are trying to lead you off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **planonton** — deceiving, leading astray. The root of *planet* — the wandering star."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has been about them: *they went out from us, but they were not of us*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **that anointing from Him stays right in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chrisma** — an anointing, the oil itself. It occurs three times in the New Testament, all in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Christos** — Christ, the Anointed One — comes from the same root. The anointing and the Anointed share the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The permanence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stays right in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **menei** — abides, remains. It is John's favourite verb; he uses it more than sixty times across his Gospel and letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **nobody has to teach you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is a strange sentence to find in the middle of a letter written to teach them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just spent two chapters instructing them, and warns them about false teachers in the verse before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is not against teaching. It is against needing a class of people to mediate what they already have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the anointing does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His anointing **teaches you about everything**, and **it is true, and there is no lie in it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims: it teaches, and it does not lie."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The false teachers are the contrast. John has just called their message a lie: *who is a liar but he who denies that Jesus is the Christ?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **just as it has taught you — you will stay in Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be read as a statement or an imperative. Either way the verb is **meno** again — the same word as the anointing's abiding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It abides in them; they abide in Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he said seven verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you have an anointing from the Holy One, and you know all things**.* See the note on 1 John 2:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice in one chapter, which is how John signals something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said about the same thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But the Helper, the Holy Spirit, whom the Father will send in My name, **He will teach you all things**, and bring to your remembrance all things that I said to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"When He, the Spirit of truth, has come, **He will guide you into all truth**.\"* See the note on John 16:12–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John was in the room when both sentences were spoken, and he is writing decades later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter keeps doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Testing. John's letters are full of *by this we know* — five or six times over. He does not tell them to trust a feeling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tests are doctrinal and practical: what they say about Christ, and whether they love each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My little children.* He uses it seven times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An old man, the last of the twelve, writing to congregations he had watched people walk out of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The odd sentence.** *Nobody has to teach you* — written by a teacher, in a letter, four verses after warning them about teachers. What he is removing is the middleman, not the instruction."}]}, "1 John 3:16": {"title": "\"Not talk, but what we do\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John gives the definition of a word everyone thought they already knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **how we have come to know the love of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egnokamen** — perfect tense, we have come to know and know still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not describing a feeling arrived at. He is pointing at an event and saying that is where the knowledge comes from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **gave His own life away for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *He laid down His life* — **tithemi**, to put down, to place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the verb Jesus uses of Himself in John's Gospel: *no one takes it from Me. **I lay it down of My own accord**.* See the note on John 10:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same writer, the same word, decades apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John refuses to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Define love by how it feels. His letters use the word more than any other book in the New Testament and he never once describes an emotion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, he points at the cross or at an action."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **we ought to give our own lives away for the brothers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **opheilomen** — we are indebted, we owe it. It is a financial word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But whoever has this world's goods, and **sees his brother in need**, and **shuts up his heart from him**, how does the love of God abide in him?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John immediately scales it down from dying to a coat and a meal, which is the harder test."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse it turns on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My little children, **let us not love in word or in tongue, but in deed and in truth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not in word, neither in tongue*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "3:16 in both — John 3:16 and 1 John 3:16, the same author, the same subject, from the two sides:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God so loved the world that He gave His only begotten Son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave His own life away for us; and we ought to give our own lives away for the brothers.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other definition in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In this is love, **not that we loved God, but that He loved us**, and sent His Son to be the propitiation for our sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John insists on the direction every time he defines it. See the note on 1 John 4:8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And by this **we know that we are of the truth**, and shall assure our hearts before Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For if our heart condemns us, **God is greater than our heart**, and knows all things.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That last sentence comes two verses later, and it is the letter's answer to a heart that will not settle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disciple who leaned on His chest at the supper and stood at the cross, and who was given a mother from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw the laying down."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he does with the word.** Refuses to let it float. John says *this is how we know what love is* and then points at one afternoon — and the very next sentence is about a coat."}]}, "1 John 4:16": {"title": "\"We have put our weight on it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs about the love of God, and the second one is what makes the verse usable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Known, and trusted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have come to know the love God has for us, and we have put our weight on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are perfect tense — arrived at, and standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is knowledge: having come to see it. The second is the ordinary word for believing or trusting, and the Illumination renders it physically, which is what trust means when it is real. Not *we agree with it*. We have leaned on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A person can know something is load-bearing and still stand somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Love is what God is\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says it twice in this chapter, and it is the only sentence of its kind in Scripture — no other quality is described this way. Not *God is loving*, which would be an attribute among others."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else John says about God in this letter — light, truth, righteousness — is held together by it rather than competing with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Staying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever stays in love stays in God, and God stays in him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **meno**, John's great word: abide, remain, stay put. It runs through his Gospel — abide in Me, and I in you — and all through this letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not describe an achievement. It describes not leaving."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it is for.** The next verse says the purpose: *so that we can stand bold on the day of judgment* — and then, *there is no fear in love; perfect love throws fear outside.* The knowing and the leaning are what put the fear out. See the note on 1 John 4:17."}]}, "1 John 4:17": {"title": "\"What He is, that is what we are, in this world\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the boldest sentences in the New Testament, and it is easy to read past because of how quietly John drops it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because what He is, that is what we are, **in this world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what we will be. Not what we are working toward. The tense is present and the location is named — here, now, in this world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not say we are like Him in some respects. He says *as He is, so are we*. The comparison is unqualified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is given as the reason for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we can stand bold on the day of judgment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **parresia** — freedom of speech, the confidence of someone who has the right to be in the room. John uses it four times in this letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the ground of that boldness is not the reader's record. It is that their standing and Christ's are the same standing. If He is accepted, so are they — because it is the same acceptance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sentence starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is how love comes to full growth with us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole thing is about love being brought to completion. And the very next verse says what that maturity does: *there is no fear in love; perfect love throws fear outside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sequence runs — love matured, boldness at judgment, fear thrown out. Not by working on the fear, but by knowing what you are in Christ."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it is such a strong statement.** John has just spent the chapter saying God is love, that He sent His Son, and that we love because He first loved us. This verse is the conclusion of all of it: the love that came down did not stop at rescuing us. It made us, in this world, what He is."}]}, "1 John 4:18": {"title": "\"Love grown full drives fear out the door\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse about two things that cannot occupy the same space."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love has **no fear** in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *there is no fear in love*. Not that love overpowers fear — that fear is not in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love **grown full drives fear out the door**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Teleia agape** — love brought to completion, matured, finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **ballo exo** — to throw outside. It is a physical, unceremonious word; the same one used of casting out demons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fear is not persuaded to leave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What fear brings with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fear brings **torment** with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kolasis** — punishment, correction, penalty. It is the word used in Matthew 25 for *everlasting punishment*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the fear John has in view is not nervousness. It is the fear of being punished, and he says it carries its own punishment inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who is afraid **has not come to full growth in love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *does not love enough*, and not *is a bad Christian*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that he has not been perfected — completed — in love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the whole letter has been clear about whose love that is: *we love Him **because He first loved us***, in the very next verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The growing up is in the receiving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verse before it says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love has been perfected among us in this: that **we may have boldness in the day of judgement**; because **as He is, so are we in this world**.* See the note on 1 John 4:17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the context. Boldness on the day everybody is afraid of, and the reason given is a present-tense comparison: *as He is* — not as He was, and not as we will be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say fear is a sin, or tell anybody to stop being afraid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He describes it as an indicator — the way a thermometer reports rather than accuses — and points at the remedy, which is not an effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other fear in Scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom.* Proverbs uses a different Hebrew idea — awe, reverence, the response to size."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is talking about the fear that expects a blow."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the letter keeps saying about the order.** *We love Him because He first loved us.* Every command in 1 John about loving comes after a statement about being loved, and this verse names the growing-up as the thing that removes the fear."}]}, "1 John 4:19": {"title": "\"We love Him because He loved us first\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six words in Greek, and the argument of the whole letter is in the last one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Protos** — first, prior, before. It is doing all the work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is not saying God loved us more, or better. He is saying He loved us **earlier** — before there was anything to respond to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the order matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read backwards, the verse becomes an instruction: love God, and He will love you. That is the reverse of what John spends the chapter arguing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In this is love, not that we loved God, but that He loved us and sent His Son.* He says it four verses earlier in almost the same words, ruling out the wrong order before he states the right one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it makes love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A response. Not something worked up, and not a duty discharged — the reaction to something that already happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why John can say elsewhere that His commands are not burdensome. A command to love, given to someone who has been loved first, is not the same weight as a command given cold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter has just been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is no fear in love; perfect love throws fear outside.* And: *what He is, that is what we are, in this world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This verse is the summary of both. The fear goes because the love was there before we arrived, and it did not wait to see how we would turn out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What John does with it next.** He goes straight to the brother you can see: *if anyone says \"I love God\" and hates his brother, he is a liar.* The love that came first is expected to run through and land on somebody. See the note on 1 John 4:16."}]}, "1 John 4:4": {"title": "\"The One in you is bigger\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and every clause in it is a completed fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You belong to God, **little children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's habitual address — **teknia**, small children. He uses it seven times in this short letter, and he was an old man when he wrote it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *belong to God* is *you are of God* — out of Him, sourced in Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has already happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **you have beaten them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense: you have overcome, and stand having overcome."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *you will*, not *you can*. It is reported as done, and it is said to people John has just been warning about false prophets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who \"them\" are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *many false prophets have gone out into the world* — and John gives a test, *every spirit that confesses that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the overcoming is of a specific thing: teaching that denies the incarnation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the One in you is bigger than the one in the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a straightforward comparative — greater."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two beings are named and located: one **in you**, one **in the world**. The comparison is not between you and the world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the believer's strength enters the sentence. The victory is credited to a difference in size between two others, and the believer's only contribution is being the place one of them is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter does with this elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the victory that has overcome the world — **our faith**.* And: *who is he who overcomes the world, but he who believes that Jesus is the Son of God?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same perfect tense, the same completed overcoming, and the mechanism named as believing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten verses later: *there is **no fear in love**, but perfect love casts out fear, because fear involves torment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *we love Him **because He first loved us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that says the One in you is greater also says the whole thing started with being loved first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letter's habit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John states facts about his readers before he asks anything of them. *You are of God. You have overcome. You know the truth. Your sins are forgiven. You have an anointing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commands come after the indicatives, every time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who wrote it.** The disciple who leaned on His chest at supper, watched Him die, ran to the tomb, and lived long enough to be the last one left. *That which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have looked upon, and **our hands have handled**.*"}]}, "1 John 4:8": {"title": "\"Love is what God is\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the second half of it is unlike anything else said about God in Scripture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **love is what God is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *ho theos agape estin* — God is love. No article on *love*: not *God is the love*, and not *God is loving*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a statement of what He consists of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence does not reverse. John is not saying that love is God, or that wherever there is affection there is deity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is saying that if you took away the loving there would be no God left to describe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other two of the same shape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John writes three sentences like this and they are the only ones of their kind:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*God is **light**, and in Him is no darkness at all.*", "*God is **spirit**, and those who worship Him must worship in spirit and truth.*", "*God is **love**.*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not attributes He has. Categories of what He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The negative half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever does not love **has never known God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is aorist — *did not come to know*. Not *has stopped knowing*; never started."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not argue it. He states the test and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he says it twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight verses later: *God is love, and he who abides in love abides in God, and God in him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it, explains it for eight verses, and says it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he puts in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In this the love of God was manifested toward us, that God has **sent His only begotten Son** into the world, that we might live through Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In this is love, **not that we loved God, but that He loved us**, and sent His Son to be the propitiation for our sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John will not let the word stay abstract. Every time he uses it he attaches it to an event with a date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he insists on the direction: not our love reaching up, but His arriving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition he refuses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never defines love by feeling. The nearest thing to a definition in his letters is *by this we know love, because He laid down His life for us.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it means for fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **no fear in love; but perfect love casts out fear**, because fear involves torment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses later, and it is the practical conclusion he draws from *God is love*. See the note on 1 John 4:18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who wrote it.** The man who called himself *the disciple whom Jesus loved* — his own description of himself, five times, in his own Gospel. He is not writing a definition from the outside."}]}, "1 Kings 1:5–10": {"title": "\"His father had never once crossed him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prince crowns himself, and the narrator stops mid-story to explain why."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His father had **never once crossed him**, never asked, \"**Why do you do this?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *never grieved him at any time by saying*. Not once, in his whole life, had David asked him a question about his conduct."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts it in as the explanation. It is not an aside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Adonijah began to **strut and swell** with ambition. \"**I will be king**,\" he told himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not consult David, who is still alive. He does not consult the prophet. He says it to himself and then acts on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He got himself **chariots and horsemen, and fifty men to run ahead of him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trappings first. He arranges the appearance of a king before there is anything behind it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he learned it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was a handsome man, **born just after Absalom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Absalom did exactly the same thing — *Absalom prepared chariots and horses, and fifty men to run before him* — and it ended with him hanging in an oak tree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David watched one son do it and did not ask the next one a single question."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Absalom's rebellion also had a history. Amnon violated Tamar, and *when King David heard of all these things, he was very angry* — and did nothing. Two years later Absalom killed Amnon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three sons, one pattern, and the same silence behind each of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who joined him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joab, the commander of the army, and **Abiathar the priest***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The army and the priesthood. Adonijah had the two institutions that mattered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Zadok the priest, Benaiah, **Nathan the prophet**, Shimei, Rei, and David's mighty men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nathan is the man who came to David after Bathsheba with *you are the man.* He is on the other side, and it is Nathan who goes to Bathsheba and gets Solomon crowned before the day is out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guest list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He invited all his royal brothers and all the king's men of Judah. **But he did not invite Nathan the prophet, or Benaiah, or the mighty men, or his brother Solomon.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knew exactly who would say no. The omissions are a complete list of everyone with the standing to stop him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage suggests David should have been harsh with him. David was never harsh with anybody in his family, and that is the problem the verse names."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never asked. *Why do you do this?* — one question, and it was never put."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it ends.** Solomon lets him live: *if he proves himself a worthy man, not one hair of his shall fall to the earth.* Adonijah then asks for Abishag, the girl who had nursed David — a claim on the throne by another route — and Solomon has him executed. The strutting outlives the reprieve."}]}, "1 Kings 12:6–11": {"title": "Two sets of advisers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A new king asks the same question twice and gets two answers. The kingdom splits over which one he takes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you will be a servant to them today — if you answer them kindly — they will be your servants forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old men who had stood before Solomon give him a piece of counsel that is almost a paradox: serve them today and they are yours for life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It costs one conversation. The return they promise is permanent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My little finger is thicker than my father's waist… My father disciplined you with whips? I'll use whips with iron barbs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men he grew up with tell him to escalate. Their advice is not strategy; it is posture — a way of appearing strong to people who had asked for relief."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read what the people actually wanted. Solomon's building programme had been paid for in forced labour, and the request was *lighten the load your father put on us, and we will serve you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were not rebelling. They were offering loyalty in exchange for relief. Both sets of advisers understood that; they simply disagreed about whether to take the offer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rehoboam takes the harsher counsel and ten tribes walk away that day. The kingdom never reunites. Every king after this, north and south, lives inside the consequences of one afternoon's advice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The detail about who he asked.** He went to the old men first — which was right — and then went and asked again. Getting counsel and taking counsel are two different acts, and a man who keeps asking until he hears what he wanted is not being advised. See the note on Proverbs 11:8–14 on many counsellors, and what the surrounding verses assume about their quality."}]}, "1 Kings 14:25–28": {"title": "Bronze shields for gold", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five years after Solomon dies, everything he made in gold walks out of the country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the fifth year** of King Rehoboam.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon reigned forty years. His son loses the treasury in five."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Shishak took"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **stripped the treasures** from the temple of the LORD and from the royal palace, taking **everything** — even **all the gold shields** Solomon had made.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before records what those shields were: two hundred large shields of beaten gold, six hundred shekels each, and three hundred smaller ones — hung in the House of the Forest of Lebanon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were ceremonial. Nobody fought with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The replacement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So King Rehoboam **made bronze shields to replace them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same number, same shape, same purpose, same place in the procession."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bronze looks like gold at a distance, especially polished, especially in a corridor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail that gives it away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whenever the king went to the temple of the LORD, **the guards carried the shields, and afterward they returned them to the guardroom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gold ones hung in the House of the Forest. Nobody had to lock those away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bronze ones go back into a locked room after every use."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not put a guard on a thing that is worth what it appears to be worth. The security arrangements are the confession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says it: *Judah did evil in the sight of the LORD… they also built for themselves high places, sacred pillars, and wooden images.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And 2 Chronicles adds the reason Shishak got as far as he did: *they had transgressed against the LORD* — and, when the king and princes humbled themselves, *they shall be his servants, that they may distinguish My service from the service of the kingdoms of the nations.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other shield in Scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul's, on Gilboa: *the shield of Saul, **no longer anointed with oil**.* See the note on 2 Samuel 1:17–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shield had to be oiled to keep the leather supple and turn a blade. An unoiled shield is a shield in name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the one Paul names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Above all of it, **take up the shield, which is faith**, and with it you will be able to put out all the burning arrows of the evil one.* See the note on Ephesians 6:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's is the full-body **thureos**, the door-shaped shield — and it is the only piece of the armour that is described as putting fires out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was never replaced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gold did not come back. Every later reference in Kings and Chronicles to the temple treasury is to what somebody added, or what somebody else carried off."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Solomon had said would happen.** *If you or your sons at all turn from following Me… then I will cut off Israel from the land which I have given them.* God said it to him at the dedication, and the reign after his is where the gold starts moving."}]}, "1 Kings 17:1": {"title": "\"The One I stand before\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elijah arrives in the narrative with no introduction — no father named, no calling scene, no commission recorded. One sentence and he is in front of the king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As surely as the LORD lives — the God of Israel, **the One I stand before and serve**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his whole credential. Not *the prophet of Israel*, not a title or an office. A description of where he stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew *to stand before* is the language of a servant attending a king in his throne room — the phrase used of courtiers waiting on Solomon. Elijah is saying he is in attendance somewhere else, and speaking from there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the weather"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There will be no dew and no rain in these years, not a drop, **until I say the word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things a farming nation could not live without, and the shutting of both is put on his word. Baal was the storm god — the one whose whole portfolio was rain and fertility."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the drought is not a general judgment. It is aimed precisely at the thing Baal was supposed to supply, in a country that had gone over to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the authority came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is the one who tells us what happened before the sentence: *he prayed hard that the rain would stop, and it stopped.* The declaration in front of Ahab is the outcome of praying, not a substitute for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prayed it first, then announced it, and then lived on a brook and ate what ravens brought him for the duration."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"Elijah\" means \"my God is the LORD.\"** In a kingdom that had spent a generation deciding otherwise, the man's own name is the argument. And on Carmel the nation shouts it back — *the LORD, He is God* — without necessarily noticing whose name they are saying."}]}, "1 Kings 17:2–7": {"title": "The brook dried up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is fed twice a day by birds for the better part of a year, and then the water stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Leave here. Turn east** and go **hide yourself** by the Kerith Ravine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three commands, and the third is the odd one. Elijah has just walked into Ahab's throne room and shut the sky. The next thing he is told is to disappear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *Kerith* comes from a root meaning *to cut off*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two provisions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You'll **drink from the brook**, and **I've told the ravens** to bring you food there.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water from the ground and food from the air."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ravens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **orev**. Ravens are on the unclean list in Leviticus, and they are birds that scavenge and do not share — Job asks *who provides food for the raven?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are also the birds that feed their own young badly. The psalm says God *gives to the beast his food, and to the young ravens which cry.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "These ones carry food past their own nests to a man in a ravine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the meat came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and meat, twice a day, in a famine. Nobody in Israel was eating meat twice a day that year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not say where it came from and Elijah does not ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Elijah **did exactly what the LORD said**. He went and settled by the Kerith Ravine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he went and did according to the word of the LORD*. It is the same formula used of him at Zarephath and on Carmel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rhythm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bread and meat **in the morning**, bread and meat **again in the evening**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice daily, at the hours of the morning and evening sacrifice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enough for the day and no more. The same arrangement as manna, which could not be stored."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **after a while the brook dried up**, because no rain had fallen on the land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He watched it go down. A brook does not stop in a morning — it narrows, slows, becomes pools, then mud, then stones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it dried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because no rain had fallen on the land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The drought was the one Elijah had called for. He prayed it and then drank from a stream that it emptied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What did not happen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No new instruction while the water was falling. The word comes in the next verse, after the brook is gone: *arise, go to Zarephath.* See the note on 1 Kings 17:8–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next place is a Gentile town in Jezebel's home territory, and the next provider is a widow with a handful of flour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ravens, then a widow, then the whole nation on Carmel. Each provision is stranger than the last and each one ends before the next is named."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was told to do at the brook.** Hide. Not preach, not organise, not intercede. The most public prophet in the Old Testament spends the first stretch of his ministry sitting by a stream watching birds arrive."}]}, "1 Kings 17:8–16": {"title": "\"Make a little loaf for me first\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A starving widow with one meal left is asked to give it away before she eats it, and what happens next is one of the plainest pictures of provision in the Bible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A handful of flour in a jar and a little oil in a jug.* She is gathering sticks to bake it, and she says out loud what the plan is after that: *we'll eat it, and then we'll die.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not being dramatic. That is arithmetic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make a little loaf for me **first**, and then make something for yourself and your son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is doing all the work. Not instead — first. The prophet is not asking her to go without; he is asking her to change the order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the request comes with *don't be afraid* in front of it, and a promise behind it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The jar of flour will not run out, and the jug of oil will not run dry, until the day the LORD sends rain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a barn filled overnight. A jar that does not empty. The supply is daily, and it lasts as long as the drought does — provision measured to the length of the need."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So she went and did what Elijah said.* No argument recorded. A foreign widow in Sidon — Jezebel's own country, outside the covenant — takes an Israelite prophet's word and bakes the last of her flour for him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there was food every day.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Jesus mentions.** Preaching in Nazareth, He says there were many widows in Israel in Elijah's day, and the prophet was sent to none of them — only to this one, in Sidon. It is one of the sentences that made His hometown want to throw Him off a cliff. See the note on Luke 4:16–21."}]}, "1 Kings 18:1–6": {"title": "Obadiah in the palace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The word comes after three years, and the chapter pauses to tell you who else was in Israel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wait"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A long time passed — **into the third year of the drought**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years by the brook and at Zarephath. James gives the figure as three years and six months, and Jesus uses the same number in Nazareth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go, show yourself to Ahab**. I'm about to send rain on the land.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Show yourself — the opposite of the command that opened chapter 17, which was *hide yourself*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the rain is announced before the contest on Carmel has happened. God tells Elijah the outcome first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The severity"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By now **the famine in Samaria was severe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samaria is the capital. The famine has reached the palace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ahab's man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ahab had called for **Obadiah, the man in charge of his palace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew title is *over the house* — the highest office under the king, the royal steward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means **servant of the LORD**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parenthesis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*(Obadiah was a man who **feared the LORD deeply**. When Queen Jezebel had been **killing off the prophets of the LORD**, Obadiah had **taken a hundred of them and hidden them in two caves, fifty in each**, and **kept them supplied with bread and water**.)*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The historian stops the narrative to put this on record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred men, in two caves, fed through a famine, by the chief of staff of the household that was hunting them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The risk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jezebel was in the same palace. Obadiah was requisitioning bread and water during a drought, for men who were officially dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elijah did not know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters later, Elijah says twice that he is the only one left. See the note on 1 Kings 19:9–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred prophets were alive in two caves, kept there by a man in Ahab's own house, and the historian has already told the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The errand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let's **go through the land, to every spring and every valley**. Maybe we'll find enough grass to **keep the horses and mules alive** so we don't lose all the animals.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the king's priority in the third year of a national famine. The horses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahab is out looking for grass. Elijah is on the road to find him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The division"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **split the land between them** to search it — Ahab going one way, Obadiah another.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is how Obadiah, not Ahab, is the one who meets Elijah — and the meeting terrifies him. He is convinced the Spirit will carry Elijah off and Ahab will kill him for a false report."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I your servant have feared the LORD from my youth. Was it not reported to my lord what I did… how I hid a hundred men of the LORD's prophets?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only person in the chapter who has to say his own résumé out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Elijah's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As the LORD of hosts lives, before whom I stand, **I will surely present myself to him today**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter puts side by side.** A king searching for grass to keep his horses, and his steward keeping a hundred prophets alive in caves. The famine reached both of them and they spent it differently."}]}, "1 Kings 18:17–24": {"title": "\"How long will you go limping between two opinions?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three and a half years of drought end with a confrontation, and it opens with an accusation and a correction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who troubled Israel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So it's you — the man who's brought all this trouble on Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I haven't troubled Israel. You have.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahab blames the prophet who announced the drought. Elijah puts it back on the man whose house abandoned the commands and went after the Baals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word Ahab uses is a strong one — the same root used of Achan, whose theft brought disaster on the whole camp. Elijah does not soften it; he redirects it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question to the people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How long will you go limping between two opinions?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is the one used a few verses later of the prophets of Baal **dancing** around their altar. Israel is doing the same hop from foot to foot, and the word is chosen deliberately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had not rejected the LORD outright. They were keeping both, and Elijah's charge is that this is not a position at all — it is a limp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people said nothing — not a word.* Nobody answers him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The terms of the test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two bulls, two altars, no fire lit by anyone. *The God who answers by fire — He is God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a fair test and the crowd says so. Baal was the god of storm and lightning; fire from the sky was precisely his advertised speciality. Elijah lets them choose their bull first and gives them the whole morning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"I am the only prophet of the LORD left.\"** It is how he sees it, and it is not quite true — Obadiah has a hundred hidden in caves, and the same chapter has just said so. God corrects the same thought a chapter later with the seven thousand who never bowed. Elijah's courage is real, and so is his sense of being alone in it."}]}, "1 Kings 18:25–29": {"title": "\"No voice. No answer.\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Half a day of noise, and the historian keeps returning to the same three words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You go first, since there are so many of you**. Choose your bull and prepare it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred and fifty of them against one. Elijah gives them first pick, the first turn, and the whole morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then call on your god — **but light no fire**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire rule of the contest, and both sides are bound by it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They called on Baal **from morning until noon**. \"**O Baal, answer us!**\" they shouted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Baal was the storm god — the one who was supposed to bring rain and lightning. Fire from the sky was precisely his department, and Israel had been in drought for three years under his management."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **there was no voice. No one answered**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dancing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **danced around the altar** they had made.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a limping, hobbling dance — the same verb Elijah used on the crowd earlier in the chapter: *how long will you **falter** between two opinions?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had accused Israel of limping between two positions, and now the prophets of Baal limp around their altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mockery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At noon Elijah began to mock them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four suggestions, and each one is worse for Baal than the last:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"**Surely he's a god!**\"*", "*\"Maybe he's **deep in thought**, or **busy**, or **off on a journey**.\"*", "*\"Maybe he's **asleep** and needs to be woken up.\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of *busy* is a euphemism — he has withdrawn, he is occupied privately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah is not arguing theology. He is asking practical questions about where the god has got to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escalation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they **shouted louder** and, in their custom, **cut themselves with swords and spears until the blood ran**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Self-laceration was standard Canaanite practice and the law forbade it: *you shall not make any cuttings in your flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "More volume, then more blood. When nothing answers, the only thing left is to raise the cost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The afternoon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Noon passed, and they **raved on into the afternoon**, all the way to **the time of the evening offering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six more hours. And the hour it ends at is the hour of the sacrifice in Jerusalem — the hour Elijah then steps forward and prays. See the note on 1 Kings 18:30–39."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **there was nothing. No voice. No answer. No one paying attention**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The historian says it twice in five verses, and the second time he adds a third clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *no one paying attention* is *none regarded* — nobody there to notice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it took to answer Elijah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One prayer, sixty-three words in the Hebrew, and no shouting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And before it he drenched the altar with twelve barrels of water in the third year of a drought."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two halves of the day measure.** From morning to evening on one side, and the time it takes to say one prayer on the other. The historian gives four hundred and fifty men five verses and Elijah's request three."}]}, "1 Kings 18:30–39": {"title": "Twelve stones, four jars three times, and one prayer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The contest on Carmel, and every detail Elijah arranges is aimed at leaving no other explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The twelve stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took twelve stones, one for each of the tribes descended from Jacob — the man to whom the word of the LORD had come, \"Israel shall be your name.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The northern kingdom had been split from Judah for generations. Elijah rebuilds the altar as though the nation were whole, and the writer stops the narrative to mention the renaming at the Jabbok."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not addressing ten tribes. He is addressing Israel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four jars, poured over the offering and the wood. Then *do it again*. Then *a third time*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve jars in total, in the middle of a drought, on a mountain — water that must have been carried up. It fills the trench."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every drop is there to remove an excuse. No hidden coals, no dry tinder, no sleight of hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After the prophets of Baal had spent a day shouting, dancing and cutting themselves, Elijah prays about sixty words:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*Let it be known today that You are God in Israel, that I am Your servant, and that I have done all these things at Your word. Answer me, LORD, answer me, so these people will know that You, O LORD, are God — and that You are turning their hearts back to You.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three requests, and all three are about **them**: that they would know who God is, and that their hearts are being turned back. Nothing is asked for the prophet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it came to pass, **at the time of the offering of the evening sacrifice**, that Elijah the prophet came near.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three in the afternoon — the hour the lamb was killed in Jerusalem, in a kingdom that had not sent anyone to Jerusalem in generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophets of Baal had been shouting since morning and finished at exactly that hour. See the note on 1 Kings 18:25–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The altar he repaired"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He repaired **the altar of the LORD that was broken down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There had been one on that mountain. Somebody had pulled it down, and Elijah does not build a new one — he puts the old one back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the fire of the LORD fell. It consumed the offering, the wood, the stones, the very dust — and licked up all the water in the trench.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not just the sacrifice. The stones and the dust and the water — the fire takes everything, including the things fire does not normally take."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the people say.** *The LORD — He is God! The LORD — He is God!* In Hebrew that is *YHWH hu ha-Elohim*, twice. And it is the meaning of the prophet's own name — Eliyahu, *my God is the LORD*. The nation ends the day shouting his name back at him without knowing it."}]}, "1 Kings 18:41–46": {"title": "\"I hear the sound of a heavy rain\" — before there is a cloud", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elijah announces rain to the king before anything has changed in the sky, and then goes up the mountain to pray it in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go on up — eat and drink, for **I hear the sound of a heavy rain coming**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no rain. There has been none for three and a half years, and the sky is clear. He says he hears it anyway, tells the king to go and eat, and only then climbs Carmel to pray."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The announcement comes before the answer, and the praying comes after the announcement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He bent down to the ground and put his face between his knees.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a dignified stance. The man who had just called fire out of heaven in front of a nation folds himself onto the ground and stays there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Seven times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go look toward the sea\"… \"There's nothing\"… Seven times Elijah said, \"Go back and look again.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six times the report comes back empty. He does not adjust the announcement or conclude he misheard. He sends the servant again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what finally arrives is *a cloud as small as a man's hand* — the least impressive possible confirmation, and enough. He immediately sends word to Ahab to get down the mountain before the rain stops him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to him afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The power of the LORD came on Elijah — he tucked his cloak into his belt and ran ahead of Ahab's chariot all the way to Jezreel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly seventeen miles, on foot, ahead of a horse-drawn chariot, by a man who had been awake since before dawn and had fasted through a contest and a slaughter. The chapter records it in one sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was praying for.** God had already told him, at the start of the chapter, *I will send rain on the land.* The promise was given first; Elijah announced it, then prayed it down, then sent seven times for the evidence. The praying was not to persuade God of something. It was to bring in what had already been said."}]}, "1 Kings 19:1–8": {"title": "Under the broom tree", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The day after the greatest day of his life, he asks to die — and what he is given is sleep and a hot meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ahab told Jezebel **everything Elijah had done**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fire, the water, the four hundred and fifty, the rain. Ahab saw all of it and reports it to his wife as news."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The threat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**May the gods deal with me, and worse, if by this time tomorrow I haven't made your life like the life of one of them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A messenger, not an arrest party. She had the power to send soldiers and sent words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And she gives him a day's notice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had just done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outrun a chariot from Carmel to Jezreel with the hand of the LORD on him — about seventeen miles, on foot, after a full day on the mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elijah **was afraid, and he ran for his life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew can be read *he saw* or *he was afraid*; the older versions have *when he saw*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who stood in front of a nation and four hundred and fifty prophets runs from one message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How far"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came to **Beersheba in Judah**, **left his servant there**, and went **a day's journey into the wilderness alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beersheba is at the far southern end of Judah — about a hundred miles, and out of Jezebel's jurisdiction entirely. He was safe when he got there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he leaves the last person with him and walks another day into the desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came to **a solitary broom tree**, sat down under it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **rothem** — a desert shrub with thin foliage. It gives some shade and not much."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only shelter for miles and it is barely shelter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **prayed that he might die**. \"**I've had enough, LORD**,\" he said. \"**Take my life. I'm no better than my ancestors**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it is enough now*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks God to kill him, a day's walk from a woman who had offered to do it for free."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I'm no better than my ancestors* — his complaint is that he has not turned out to be more than the men before him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a rebuke, not a correction, not a reminder of yesterday."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **lay down under the tree and fell asleep**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first touch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And suddenly **an angel touched him** and said, \"**Get up and eat**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He looked, and there by his head was **a warm loaf of bread, baked over hot coals, and a jug of water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and water at his head while he slept. The same two things Obadiah carried to a hundred prophets in caves. See the note on 1 Kings 18:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Baked on coals, so somebody had made a fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He ate and drank and **lay back down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes back to sleep, and nothing is said about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second touch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The angel of the LORD came **a second time**, touched him, and said, \"**Get up and eat. The journey is too much for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the way is too great for you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given for the second meal is the distance ahead, which Elijah has not been told about yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strengthened by that food, he traveled **forty days and forty nights** until he reached **Horeb, the mountain of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty days on two meals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Horeb is Sinai — where Moses saw the bush, and where the law was given. Elijah walks to the place the covenant started."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of what he was given.** Sleep, food, sleep, food. Then, and only then, a journey and a conversation. Nothing was said to him about Jezebel, Carmel, or the prayer under the tree."}]}, "1 Kings 19:9–18": {"title": "A low, gentle whisper", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The same question is asked twice, the same answer is given twice, and in between the mountain comes apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What are you doing here, Elijah?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an accusation and not a summons. A question, and it is the first thing said to him after forty days of walking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have been very zealous** for the LORD God Almighty. The Israelites have **abandoned Your covenant, torn down Your altars, and killed Your prophets** with the sword. **I'm the only one left** — and now they're trying to kill me too.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four complaints and a conclusion. Every fact in it is true except the last one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go out and stand on the mountain** in the presence of the LORD, for **the LORD is about to pass by**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same mountain, and nearly the same words, as Moses in the cleft of the rock: *while My glory passes by, I will put you in the cleft of the rock.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A great and powerful **wind** tore through the mountains and shattered the rocks — **but the LORD was not in the wind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After the wind came an **earthquake** — **but the LORD was not in the earthquake**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After the earthquake came a **fire** — **but the LORD was not in the fire**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times the clause repeats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is how God had shown up before. Sinai had all three when the law was given, in this exact place. Elijah had watched fire fall on Carmel eight verses ago."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is shown the whole repertoire and told God is not in any of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And after the fire came **a sound. A low, gentle whisper**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qol demamah daqqah** — a voice of thin silence. **Daq** is what is used of something ground fine, and **demamah** is stillness, hush."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Elijah heard it, he **pulled his cloak over his face**, went out, and stood at the mouth of the cave.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wind, the earthquake and the fire did not move him out of the cave. The whisper did — and he covered his face for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses hid his face at the bush."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then a voice came to him: \"**What are you doing here, Elijah?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Identical. Nothing has been explained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Word for word the same complaint, minus one clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not talked out of it. He says it twice and God lets him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is given instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go back the way you came**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three anointings: **Hazael** over Aram, **Jehu** over Israel, and **Elisha** to take his place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked to die and is given work — including, in the third item, a successor. The request under the broom tree is answered in a way, and by a man rather than a grave. See the note on 1 Kings 19:1–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the three, Elijah personally does only the last: he finds Elisha ploughing with twelve yoke of oxen and throws his cloak on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven thousand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yet **I have kept seven thousand in Israel** — every knee that has **not bowed to Baal**, every mouth that has **not kissed him**. **You are not alone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have left*, *I have reserved*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah said it twice — *I'm the only one left* — and the correction comes at the end, after the anointings, not before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been there through the whole drought. Obadiah's hundred prophets were among them, in two caves, being fed. See the note on 1 Kings 18:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 11: *\"LORD, they have killed Your prophets… and I alone am left.\" But what does the divine response say to him? \"**I have reserved for Myself seven thousand men who have not bowed the knee to Baal**.\" Even so then, at this present time there is a remnant **according to the election of grace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the answer under the mountain and calls it grace."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was never said to him.** No rebuke for running, no reference to Jezebel, no mention of Carmel. He was fed, allowed to sleep, asked a question, allowed to answer it twice, and then told he was not the only one."}]}, "1 Kings 20:22–30a": {"title": "\"A god of the hills and not of the valleys\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A defeated army works out what went wrong, and gets it exactly backwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The theory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Their gods are gods of the hills** — that's why they beat us. But if we fight them **on the plains**, we'll surely be stronger.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a reasonable conclusion by the standards of the time. Gods were local. Each had territory, and you fought accordingly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had lost in the hills, so they book the rematch on flat ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reorganisation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Replace the kings with regular commanders, raise another army as big as the one you lost, **horse for horse and chariot for chariot**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing wrong with the military reasoning either. They rebuild to exact specifications and change the command structure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every adjustment they make is competent, and the premise underneath all of it is wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Israel looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Camped in front of the vast Aramean force, the Israelites looked like **two little flocks of goats**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is two little flocks of kids — young goats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the writer's image, not a boast from either side, and it is the most vivid line in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because the Arameans think the LORD is a god of the hills and not of the valleys**, I will hand this whole vast army over to you, and **you will know that I am the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given for the victory is not Israel's righteousness — and Ahab is one of the worst kings in the book, married to Jezebel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the sentence the Arameans said out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God answers a piece of bad theology by demonstrating the opposite in the place they chose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You will know that I am the LORD\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in the chapter — once at the first battle in the hills, once here in the valley."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same purpose given for both terrains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Seven days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **seven days the two camps faced each other**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A week of standing in the valley looking at an army that outnumbers you, before anything happens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word had already been given on day one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred thousand foot soldiers in one day, and a wall falling on twenty-seven thousand more at Aphek."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahab. The man who *did more to provoke the LORD God of Israel to anger than all the kings of Israel before him*, according to the chapter before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God sends him a prophet with a battle plan, twice, and delivers him both times — and the stated reason is never Ahab."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Ahab does with the victory.** Makes a treaty with Ben-Hadad and lets him go, and a prophet tells him *because you have let slip out of your hand a man whom I appointed to utter destruction, your life shall go for his life.* He wins in the valley and loses it at the negotiating table."}]}, "1 Kings 3:5–14": {"title": "A listening heart, and everything he did not ask for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God offers a blank cheque, and the interest of the story is entirely in what Solomon does with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he actually asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The English usually says *an understanding heart*. The Hebrew is **lev shomea** — a **listening** heart. A heart that hears."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a striking thing for a king to request. Not brilliance, not judgement in the abstract, but the capacity to take in what is being said to him — by God and by the people he is about to govern."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he gets there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the run-up. He talks about David, then says two things about himself:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*I am only a child* — he was a grown man with a throne", "*I do not know how to go out or come in*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request comes out of an admission, and the admission comes before the ask. Then the reason: *who on his own could ever shepherd this great people of Yours?* He is not asking for wisdom to be impressive. He is asking because he has looked at the job and knows he cannot do it."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you asked for this — not long life for yourself, not riches, not the death of your enemies…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God lists the things Solomon did not ask for, which tells you what the offer was understood to include. And then: *what you did not even ask for, I give you too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The riches and honour arrive as an unrequested extra. They are not the reward for a clever request; they come because of what the request revealed about the man making it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conditional at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if you walk in My ways… I will give you long life as well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Worth reading precisely. The wisdom is given outright, no condition. The riches and honour are given outright. Only the long life carries an *if* — and Solomon's own story, which turns badly in his later years, is the reason that detail is in the text."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What this passage is not.** It is not a formula for getting more by asking for the right thing. God's pleasure is in what the request showed about Solomon's estimate of himself and the size of the task. The story records a man who knew he was out of his depth, and the whole answer follows from that."}]}, "1 Kings 6:14–18": {"title": "\"Not a single stone in sight\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most expensive stonework in Israel's history, and none of it could be seen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great, costly stones, dressed square at the quarry* — chapter 5, and the finest masonry money could buy, cut and finished in the dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What went over it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **lined the inside walls with boards of cedar** — from the floor of the house up to the rafters of the ceiling.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every internal surface panelled. Then the floors in cypress, then the whole interior overlaid with gold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All cedar, **not a single stone in sight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the writer's own summary, and it is the point of the paragraph."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stones were quarried, hauled, squared and set with enormous care, and then covered so completely that nobody entering the building would ever see one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The work on them was not for display."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Cedar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cedar of Lebanon is close-grained, aromatic, and resistant to rot and insects. It was the prestige timber of the ancient world, shipped down the coast in rafts by Hiram's men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not decay, and it does not admit what usually gets into wood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The carving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Carved with **gourds and open flowers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the gold — which is applied in the verses following — there is carving nobody would ever see either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gourds and open flowers, hidden under metal, in a room only priests entered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inner room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At the far end of the house he built off twenty cubits with boards of cedar, from floor to rafters, and set that space apart as **the inner sanctuary, the Most Holy Place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A perfect cube, twenty by twenty by twenty, entered once a year. See the note on Hebrews 9:6–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in the walls"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stone, from a quarry, cut to fit somewhere it had never been."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cedar over the stone. Gold over the cedar. And carving in between that the gold covered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Layer on layer, and the visible surface is the last one applied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the letters take it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You also, as living stones, are being built up a spiritual house* — Peter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *in Him the whole building, being fitted together, grows into a holy temple in the Lord, in whom you also are being built together for a dwelling place of God in the Spirit.* See the notes on 1 Peter 2:4–5 and Ephesians 2:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stones, quarried out of the dark, covered in something that does not rot and something that does not tarnish."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Solomon said at the dedication.** *But will God indeed dwell on the earth? Behold, heaven and the heaven of heavens cannot contain You. **How much less this temple which I have built!*** Seven years of cedar and gold, and the man who paid for it says it will not do."}]}, "1 Kings 6:6–8": {"title": "\"No hammer, no chisel, no iron tool\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The largest building project in Israel's history, and the site was silent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The stone was **dressed and finished at the quarry before it ever reached the house** — so that **no hammer, no chisel, no iron tool of any kind was heard in the house** while it was going up. **Not a sound.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every stone was cut, squared and finished miles away, underground, and carried up already fitted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that requires"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The measurements had to be exact before anything was moved. A stone dressed in the dark of a quarry has to arrive and sit down into a place prepared for it, with no adjustment possible on site."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no trimming at the wall. It fits or it does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the stones came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Eighty thousand who **cut stone up in the hill country**… at the king's command they **quarried great, costly stones, dressing them square**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shaping happened in the quarry — Jerusalem's stone was cut from tunnels under the city, in the dark, out of sight of the building."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The noise was all somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty thousand in shifts to Lebanon, seventy thousand carrying loads, eighty thousand quarrying, and thirty-three hundred supervisors."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nearly two hundred thousand men, and the site was quiet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "No iron"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The specification is not incidental. When Israel built an altar, the law said *you shall not build it of hewn stone, for if you use your tool on it, you have profaned it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Iron on stone, in that law, spoiled it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ledges"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He set **narrow ledges all around the outside** of the house, so the supporting beams would rest on those ledges **and not be driven into the walls of the house itself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The side rooms lean on the temple and nothing is driven into it. The main structure is not pierced to carry the additions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **stairway wound up** to the middle story, and from the middle to the third.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lulim** — a spiral, a winding stair. You could not see the top from the bottom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody on it climbs in a straight line. The way up is round the outside of the house, and at every point on it the wall is between the climber and where he is going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the door was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The doorway to the lowest side chamber was **on the south side** of the house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One entrance, at the bottom, on one side. You did not choose your route into the chambers — there was a single door and a single stair, and it went the way it went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three storeys"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five cubits, six cubits, seven cubits — each floor wider than the one below it, because the beams rested on ledges cut further back in the wall as it rose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The higher you went, the more room there was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter takes this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You also, **as living stones**, are being built up a spiritual house, a holy priesthood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And of Christ: *a living stone, rejected indeed by men, but chosen by God and precious.* See the note on 1 Peter 2:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul builds the same picture: *in whom the whole building, being fitted together, grows into a holy temple in the Lord.* See the note on Ephesians 2:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stones in Solomon's temple were finished in the dark and set in place without a sound."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How long it took.** Seven years, from Ziv in the fourth year to Bul in the eleventh. And Solomon spent thirteen years on his own house. The book records both figures without comment."}]}, "1 Kings 7:23–26": {"title": "The Sea on twelve oxen", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A bronze basin so large the writer calls it a sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The size"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ten cubits across from rim to rim, five cubits deep, and thirty cubits around… **it held two thousand baths of water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly fifteen feet across, seven and a half deep, and something in the order of ten to twelve thousand gallons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was cast in bronze, a handbreadth thick, in one piece."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it replaced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle had a bronze laver, made from the mirrors of the women who served at the door, for the priests to wash their hands and feet before serving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon's version is the same item at enormous scale. Chronicles says plainly what it was for: *the Sea was for the priests to wash in.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The twelve oxen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It rested on **twelve bronze oxen — three facing north, three west, three south, three east** — all their hindquarters turned inward, the Sea riding on their backs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four groups of three, facing outward in every direction, and the whole weight of the water on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve is the number of the tribes, and the same arrangement as the camp in the wilderness — three tribes on each side of the tabernacle, facing out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order they are listed in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "North, west, south, east."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not the order anybody would use to describe a circle if they were going clockwise. It is the reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why oxen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ox is the working animal of the Old Testament, and the law protected it in the middle of its labour: *do not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes that law twice and applies it to those who preach and teach — *is it oxen God is concerned about, or does He say it entirely for our sake?* See the notes on 1 Corinthians 9:7–10 and 1 Timothy 5:17–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else the twelve are, they are carriers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The washing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ also loved the church and gave Himself up for her, so that He might make her holy, **washing her clean with water through the word**.* See the note on Ephesians 5:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The water in Ephesians is the word, and Paul says so in the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lily"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its rim curved out **like the rim of a cup, like the bloom of a lily**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A vessel holding twelve thousand gallons of bronze-cast water, and the writer records that the edge was shaped like a flower."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz cut the panels off the stands and *removed the Sea from the bronze oxen that were under it, and put it on a pavement of stones.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Ahaz whose stairway the shadow went back on. See the note on 2 Kings 20:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when Babylon came: *the bronze Sea that was in the house of the LORD, the Chaldeans broke in pieces, and carried all the bronze to Babylon.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Revelation puts in front of the throne.** *Before the throne there was **a sea of glass, like crystal**.* Solomon's held twelve thousand gallons for priests who needed washing. John's is glass — still, and not needed."}]}, "1 Peter 1:13": {"title": "Where Peter tells them to put their hope", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This verse is the hinge of Peter's letter. Everything before it is what God has done; everything after it is how to live. And the joint between the two is a single instruction that most readings skip over to get to the commands that follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three pictures, and only one of them is the command"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**get your mind stripped down and ready to move** — the Greek is one word, literally *gird up the loins of your mind*. A man in a long robe pulled it through his belt to run or work. Nobody had to explain it.", "**keep a clear head** — the word for not being drunk, used of staying alert.", "**pin your hope … on the kindness that arrives the day Jesus is revealed**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two are participles. The third is the actual imperative. In Greek that distinction is unmistakable: the sentence is *hope*, and the other two describe the posture of someone doing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the command in this verse is not \"try harder\" or \"stay sober.\" It is **hope**, and hope aimed at one specific thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he tells them to hope in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in an improved version of themselves. Not in surviving the pressure they were under — and they were under real pressure; that is why the letter exists."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them to put their hope on *the kindness that arrives* — grace, being brought to them, at the revealing of Jesus Christ. It is spoken of as something in transit, on its way, arriving."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All of it, nothing held back\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is an adverb in the sentence — *teleios*, completely, without reserve. It can attach to either the clear head or the hoping, and translators split over it. Either way the force is the same: nothing partial. Peter is not asking for hope with a fallback plan behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a hard instruction for people being pressed, which is exactly who he is writing to. The alternative he is ruling out is not despair. It is hedging — hoping in grace while quietly keeping a second account open in your own effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it comes exactly here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what precedes it: an inheritance kept in heaven, a salvation ready to be revealed, prophets and angels leaning in to look at it. Peter spends twelve verses on what has been done and is coming. *So* — get your mind ready, and put your hope there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is not decorative. The commands in the rest of the letter are addressed to people who have already been told where to look."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the holiness that follows.** Three verses later comes *be holy, for I am holy*, and it is often quoted on its own as the letter's demand. In Peter's sentence it stands downstream of this one: hope fixed on grace first, and holiness described as what children of that Father look like."}]}, "1 Peter 1:24–25": {"title": "Grass dries out; the word stands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes Isaiah and then attaches the quotation to the sermon they had heard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 40 — the chapter that opens *comfort, comfort My people*, written to exiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The voice in the wilderness, the valleys raised, the mountains made low, and then this: *all flesh is grass, and all its loveliness is like the flower of the field.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **people are grass**. Everything splendid about us is **the little flower on the grass**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the tree, not the crop. Grass, and the flower on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grass **dries out**. The flower **drops off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and both are ordinary. Isaiah's setting explains the speed: *the grass withers, the flower fades, **because the breath of the LORD blows upon it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that climate the hot east wind off the desert could finish a field of wildflowers in a day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being compared to grass"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything splendid about us.* The Greek is **doxa** — glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not human weakness. Human glory. The best of it is the part that falls off first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **what the Lord says stands, and keeps standing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **menei eis ton aiona** — remains into the age."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And that is **the message that was announced to you as good news**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter closes the loop. The eternal word Isaiah was talking about is the one his readers heard preached — the Greek is **euangelisthen**, evangelised to you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not leave it as a general statement about Scripture's durability. He points at the specific message they received."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is saying it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *having been **born again**, not of corruptible seed but **incorruptible, through the word of God** which lives and abides forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs on the seed. What a thing is born from determines how long it lasts, and Peter says theirs was imperishable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pilgrims of the Dispersion* — scattered believers in five Roman provinces, under pressure, and the letter keeps returning to *the fiery trial* and *suffering wrongfully*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The grass image lands differently for people whose situation is precarious."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other side of the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who according to His abundant mercy has begotten us again to **a living hope**… to **an inheritance incorruptible and undefiled and that does not fade away**, reserved in heaven for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Does not fade away** — the Greek is **amaranton**, unfading. It is a flower word, chosen deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Amos fits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A famine of hearing the words of the LORD. See the note on Amos 8:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says the word stands. Amos describes what it is like when people cannot find it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Peter does with an old quotation.** He cites a poem about human transience and then says the permanent thing in it is what they were told when somebody preached to them."}]}, "1 Peter 1:4–5": {"title": "A fortune with your name on it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three Greek adjectives, all built the same way, describing what cannot happen to what is being kept for you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nothing can rot it, nothing can dirty it, nothing can drain the colour out of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each word in Greek begins with an *a-* prefix — the negative, like our *un-*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**aphthartos** — not perishable. It cannot decay from inside.", "**amiantos** — not defiled. Nothing can spoil it from outside.", "**amarantos** — not fading. It will not lose its colour with time."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter stacks three negatives because he is ruling out every way anything has ever been lost: rot, contamination, and the slow bleaching of age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third word is where we get *amaranth*, the flower the ancients said never withered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is put away in heaven, waiting for you.* The verb is perfect passive — already reserved, and standing reserved. Someone else did the putting away, and it is still there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And who is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God's power stands guard over you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the second half, and it is the one people miss. The inheritance is guarded in heaven; the heir is guarded on earth. Peter uses a military word — garrisoned, protected as a city is protected."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both ends of the arrangement are held by someone other than the person inheriting."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who he was writing to.** Scattered believers about to face real persecution — the letter goes on at length about suffering. This is what he opens with: everything you were born into is out of reach of anything that is about to happen to you."}]}, "1 Peter 2:24": {"title": "\"He hauled our sins up onto the wood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes Isaiah 53 and changes the tense, and the change is the whole point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He hauled our sins up onto the wood Himself, in His own body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anaphero** — to carry up, to bear upward. It is the word used in the Greek Old Testament of a priest carrying a sacrifice **up** onto the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, writing about a man nailed to a raised beam, chooses the altar word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In His own body\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is precise about the location of the transaction. Not in principle, not representatively — in a body, on a piece of wood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word he uses for the cross is *xylon*, timber, the same word the Greek Old Testament uses in the line about anyone hung on a tree being under a curse. Paul quotes that verse in Galatians for the same reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Isaiah, in the past tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were healed by his wounds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah wrote *we are healed* — a prophet describing something ahead of him. Peter writes **were**, aorist: done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the difference the cross makes to the sentence. Isaiah is looking forward; Peter is looking back at an accomplished thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we would be dead to sin and alive for what is right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purpose clause is worth keeping attached. Peter does not present the bearing of sins as an end in itself but as the ground of a life lived differently — and the two verbs are both about a state, not an effort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who he was writing to.** The paragraph is addressed to household slaves being treated unjustly, and the verses just before hold up Christ's response to suffering as the pattern: He did not retaliate, but *entrusted Himself to Him who judges justly.* The healing sentence arrives in a passage about people with no power, and Peter tells them what was carried for them."}]}, "1 Peter 2:4–5": {"title": "A house made out of people", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter, whose name means a stone, writes about stones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Keep coming to Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a present participle — continuous. Not *come once*; keep coming."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same posture the whole passage assumes: something already true, being returned to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stone that is alive"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is a stone, and **He is alive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two words do not belong together. A stone is the standard image for what does not move, does not grow, and is not alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter puts them side by side without explaining, and then does it again to his readers: *and you are stones too, **alive the way He is**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rejected and chosen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People took one look and wanted nothing to do with Him — but God chose Him, and to God He is worth everything.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verdicts on the same stone, and Peter reports both. The builders' assessment was not a mistake about the stone's quality; it was simply theirs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is quoting Psalm 118 — *the stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone* — the psalm sung at the close of Passover, on the night of the bread and the cup."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God is doing with the stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is **stacking you up** into a house He lives in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passive. The stones are not building anything; they are being built."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the house is not a place people visit. It is where He lives, and it is made out of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Priests carrying the Spirit's own sacrifices"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **the priests in it**, set apart, and the sacrifices you carry in are the Spirit's own — and through Jesus Christ God is glad to take them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things worth slowing down on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Priests.** Not a class within the house. The stones and the priests are the same people. Under the old covenant one tribe served and everybody else waited outside a curtain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Spirit's own.** The offerings are called *spiritual sacrifices* — they originate on the other side. The priest carries in what he was given to carry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through Jesus Christ God is glad to take them.* The acceptability is located in the phrase *through Jesus Christ*, and not in the quality of the offering."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The building materials.** No stone in this house was quarried square. Peter's readers were *scattered strangers*, refugees in five provinces, and he tells them they are load-bearing."}]}, "1 Peter 2:9": {"title": "\"But look at you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four titles handed out in one verse, and every one of them was Israel's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's readers are *pilgrims of the Dispersion* — refugees scattered across five provinces of Asia Minor, most of them poor, some of them slaves, and under pressure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To those people: *God picked you out.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**God picked you out** — a chosen generation, **eklektos**", "**priests in a King's house** — a royal priesthood", "**a nation set apart** — a holy nation", "**a people He keeps for Himself** — a people for His own possession"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every phrase comes from Exodus 19, spoken at Sinai: *you shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation.* See the note on Exodus 19:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter takes the sentence God said to Israel at the mountain and says it to Gentile believers in Pontus and Cappadocia."}, {"k": "h", "t": "King and priest together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A royal priesthood** — the two offices in one phrase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Israel they were kept strictly apart. Uzziah was a king who walked into the sanctuary with a censer and came out a leper. See the note on 2 Chronicles 26:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Melchizedek held both — *king of Salem, priest of God Most High* — and Zechariah was told to put a crown on a high priest's head and say *He shall be a priest on his throne.* See the notes on Genesis 14:17–20 and Zechariah 6:9–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That combination, forbidden to everybody in the old covenant, is what Peter calls his readers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there is a job in it: **say out loud how good He is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aretas** — excellencies, virtues, the qualities that make something what it is. The verb is to publish abroad, to tell out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priestly work named here is not sacrifice or mediation. It is speech about what He is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two states"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were **in the dark**, and He called you, and **the light you walked into is more than you can take in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *His marvellous light* — wonderful, astonishing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the movement is His: *He called you.* Nobody in the verse found their own way out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before, and after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before: *a stone of stumbling and a rock of offence* — the same stone that is precious to those who believe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "After: *who once were not a people but are now the people of God, who had not obtained mercy but now have obtained mercy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is quoting Hosea. *Lo-Ammi*, not My people, reversed — the same passage Paul uses in Romans 9. See the notes on Hosea 1:8–9 and Romans 9:25–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Peter had called them a few verses earlier.** *Living stones, being built up a spiritual house, a holy priesthood.* The house and the priests are the same people, and the stones were not quarried square. See the note on 1 Peter 2:4–5."}]}, "1 Peter 3:10": {"title": "\"Anyone who wants to see good days\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is quoting a psalm, and he changes almost nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 34: *who is the man who desires life, and loves many days, that he may see good? Keep your tongue from evil, and your lips from speaking deceit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter lifts four verses of it into the middle of a letter about suffering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm's own setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title says David wrote it *when he pretended madness before Abimelech, who drove him away, and he departed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was on the run from Saul, had fled to enemy territory, was recognised, and escaped by scratching at the doors and letting spit run down his beard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he wrote a psalm about wanting good days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wanting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who **wants to enjoy life and see good days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse begins with a desire, not a duty. It is addressed to whoever wants this — and takes the wanting as reasonable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Days, plural"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Trouble in this psalm is singular and days are plural. The psalm says *many are the afflictions of the righteous, but the LORD delivers him out of them all* — and the delivering is what the whole psalm is about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shut your mouth on the rotten word, and **do not let a lie past your lips**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two instructions and both are about the mouth. Nothing about circumstances, nothing about effort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the psalm is *keep your tongue from evil* — **natsar**, to guard, as a sentry guards a gate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in Peter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let him **turn away from evil and do good**; let him **seek peace and pursue it**. For the eyes of the LORD are on the righteous, and His ears are open to their prayers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Turn, do, seek, pursue. And then the reason: He is watching and listening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Peter needed it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter is written to *the pilgrims of the Dispersion* — believers scattered across five Roman provinces, and the letter is full of *fiery trial*, *suffering wrongfully*, and *when you do good and suffer for it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that Peter puts a quotation about wanting good days, and does not qualify it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is around it in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately before: *not returning evil for evil or reviling for reviling, but on the contrary **blessing**, knowing that you were called to this, **that you may inherit a blessing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mouth again, and the blessing named as the inheritance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, taste and see that the LORD is good; blessed is the man who trusts in Him. The young lions lack and suffer hunger, but those who seek the LORD shall not lack any good thing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's own mouth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man quoting *do not let a lie past your lips* had denied three times with an oath in a courtyard, and had been restored by a charcoal fire on a beach. See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse offers.** Not survival, not endurance, not compensation later. Life enjoyed and good days seen — and the condition attached is about what a man says."}]}, "1 Peter 3:6": {"title": "\"Will not let any scare rattle you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter reaches back to Genesis for an example, and the sentence he ends on is about fear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sarah did it — she **did what Abraham said and called him her master**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kurion** — lord, master."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is quoting one word from one verse. Genesis 18: Sarah laughs behind the tent door and says to herself *after I have grown old, shall I have pleasure, **my lord** being old also?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is an aside, in her own head, and Peter builds on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she was doing when she said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laughing at the promise. And then denying she had laughed, and being told *no, but you did laugh*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter picks the sentence out of that scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who her daughters are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **her daughters when you do what is right** and **will not let any scare rattle you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *doing good and not being afraid with any terror*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two conditions, and the second is the one with the weight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fear clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Will not let **any scare rattle you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me phoboumenai medemian ptoesin** — not fearing any intimidation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ptoesis** occurs once in the New Testament. It means a fluttering, a startling — the motion of a bird put up out of cover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is not writing about courage in the abstract. He is writing to women in the Roman world, some of them married to unbelieving husbands, in an era when a wife's religion was supposed to be her husband's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this address exactly that: *wives, likewise, be submissive to your own husbands, that **even if some do not obey the word, they, without a word, may be won by the conduct of their wives**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the husbands being right. The paragraph assumes some of them are not obeying the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The adornment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let your adornment be merely outward — arranging the hair, wearing gold, or putting on fine apparel — rather **let it be the hidden person of the heart**, with the incorruptible beauty of **a gentle and quiet spirit**, which is **very precious in the sight of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *very precious* is **poluteles** — of great cost. It is the word used of the alabaster flask of ointment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Husbands, likewise, dwell with them **with understanding**, giving honour to the wife… **that your prayers may not be hindered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to knowledge*. And the sanction attached is about prayer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says to everybody a few verses on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **do not be afraid of their threats, nor be troubled**. But sanctify the Lord God in your hearts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Isaiah 8 there, and the same fear-word runs through the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for fear in this letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses it both ways. *Do not be afraid* of threats — and *conduct yourselves throughout the time of your sojourning here **in fear***, meaning reverence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not confuse them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the whole passage sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between instructions to servants under harsh masters and the passage about suffering for doing good, with Christ's example in the middle: *who, when He was reviled, did not revile in return.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What makes somebody Sarah's daughter, in Peter's account.** Doing right, and not letting a fright put you up out of cover — and he takes the example from a woman caught laughing at the promise."}]}, "1 Peter 3:7": {"title": "\"So that nothing gets in the way of your prayers\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse to husbands, and it ends with a consequence nobody expects."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Your turn\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just spent six verses on wives. This verse is the answering half, and it is shorter — but everything in it is a command and there is no let-out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Live with her thoughtfully"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Live with her **thoughtfully, with real understanding**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to knowledge* — **gnosis**. To dwell with her knowledgeably."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It assumes study. A man is expected to know this particular woman: what she is carrying, what she is like, what things cost her. Not women in general."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Give her honour\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Time** — the word for worth, value, price. It is the noun used of the price paid for something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To honour her is to treat her as expensive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As the weaker of the two\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the weaker vessel*, and **skeuos** is a vessel — a container, and in that world often a valuable one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is between two vessels. Both are vessels; one is described as weaker, and the instruction that follows is to handle it with more care, not less respect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the reasoning you apply to the fine china rather than the tin mug."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The equality clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And as someone who comes into **the grace of life right alongside you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Greek: **sunkleronomos** — co-heir, joint inheritor. The same word Paul uses of believers being *joint heirs with Christ*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever *weaker vessel* means, Peter rules out the obvious misreading in the very next clause. She inherits everything he inherits, equally, and *alongside* is his word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*— **so that nothing gets in the way of your prayers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to cut into, to hinder — it was used of breaking up a road to stop an army advancing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter attaches a man's prayer life to how he treats his wife, and puts it in the same sentence rather than a separate paragraph."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no equivalent warning to the wives in the passage. It is aimed at him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole passage is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is writing to scattered believers under pressure, and the household section runs servants, wives, husbands. Every part of it is about voluntary honour given where the world of that time gave none."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The husband is the one told his access to God is on the line."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul says in his version.** *Husbands, love your wives, just as Christ also loved the church and gave Himself for her.* Two apostles, two letters, and neither gives a husband anything to require — only things to give. See the note on Ephesians 5:22–24."}]}, "1 Peter 4:15": {"title": "The kind of suffering that is your own fault", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is writing to Christians under real persecution, and he stops to draw one distinction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just make sure none of you is suffering for being **a murderer, a thief, a crook, or someone who cannot stay out of other people's business**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three crimes and then a fourth item that is not a crime at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind the last one is **allotriepiskopos** — a word that appears nowhere else in Greek literature, and Peter may have coined it. It is built from *belonging to another* and *overseer*: a person who supervises what is not theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is on that list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts a busybody beside a murderer and a thief. It is startling, and it is not a slip — the sentence is short and every item in it was chosen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Interfering in another person's affairs produces suffering, and Peter classes the suffering it produces with the suffering a thief brings on himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is separating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *if you are insulted for the name of Christ, you are blessed.* The verse after: *but if anyone suffers as a Christian, let him not be ashamed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So there are two kinds of trouble a believer can be in, and Peter will not let them be confused. One is because of Christ. The other is because of you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And a third kind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither category covers Job."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not suffering for the name, and he was not suffering for anything he had done — chapter 1 says so and God says so out loud. His friends spent twenty-five chapters insisting it had to be the second kind, because their system had no room for anything else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's distinction is real and it does not exhaust the possibilities. A man can be in the ashes for neither reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter tells them to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let those who suffer according to the will of God **commit their souls to Him in doing good, as to a faithful Creator**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *commit* is a banking word — to deposit something with someone for safekeeping. It is the verb Jesus used on the cross: *into Your hands I commit My spirit.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name he gives God in that sentence.** *A faithful Creator* — the only place in the New Testament God is called that. Peter reaches for the word for the One who made the thing, writing to people whose lives are being taken apart."}]}, "1 Peter 5:10": {"title": "\"Your little while\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs at the end of the sentence, and every one of them is God's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the God all grace comes from**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the God of all grace*. Peter has used the word grace throughout the letter — *the grace that was to come to you*, *stewards of the manifold grace of God*, *God gives grace to the humble*, four verses back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names Him by it here, at the point where he is describing suffering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who **called us into His forever glory** through Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The destination is stated before the trouble is mentioned. Glory, and it is called **eternal** — set against the *little while* in the next clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Through Christ Jesus.** Peter puts the route in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After you have suffered **your little while**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oligon** — a little, a small amount. It can mean brief or slight; here it is set against *eternal*, so the sense is duration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not say it is small in itself. He says it is small next to the other thing in the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has said it before in the letter: *though now for **a little while**, if need be, you have been grieved by various trials.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Will **put you back together**, **hold you up**, **make you strong**, and **set you on rock**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Katartizo** — to mend, to repair, to set a bone, to refit a ship. It is the word used of the disciples *mending their nets*, and of restoring a fallen brother in Galatians.", "**Sterizo** — to fix firmly, to establish. It is the word Jesus used to Peter: *when you have turned back, **strengthen** your brothers.* See the note on Luke 22:31–34.", "**Sthenoo** — to make strong, to fill with strength. It occurs once in the New Testament, here.", "**Themelioo** — to lay a foundation, to ground."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four different repairs, and the tense is future indicative. Peter states them as facts, not possibilities."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All four verbs have the same subject: **the God of all grace**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the sentence is asked of the reader. Verses 8 and 9 gave the instructions — be sober, watch, resist, stand. This verse has none."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What restoration means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first verb is the one used for a broken bone or a torn net. Peter assumes damage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not promise that nothing will break. He promises who does the mending."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doxology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him be the glory and the dominion forever and ever. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter finishes the argument and then stops arguing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who had collapsed completely and been put back together on a beach over breakfast. He knows the word for mending nets because he had mended them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the sentence.** Grace first, glory second, the suffering third and called little, and then four things God does. The suffering is the shortest item in the verse."}]}, "1 Peter 5:8": {"title": "A lion's roar in his throat", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two short commands and then a description."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep your head clear. **Stay awake**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nepho** — to be sober, unintoxicated, clear-minded — and **gregoreo**, to be awake, to keep watch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are single words in the Greek and both are imperatives. Nothing about fighting yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who was told *watch and pray* in Gethsemane and fell asleep three times, and who was told at the supper table that Satan had asked for him. See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same verb here that Jesus used on him in the garden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The enemy named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have an enemy — **the devil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **antidikos**, adversary — a legal word, the opposing party in a lawsuit. And **diabolos**, the slanderer, the one who throws accusations across."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both words come out of a courtroom rather than a battlefield."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is out **walking around**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peripateo** — going about. It is what the accuser says he has been doing in Job: *from going to and fro on the earth, and from walking back and forth on it.* See the note on Job 1:6–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The roar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **a lion's roar in his throat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *as a roaring lion*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A lion that roars is a lion announcing itself. Roaring is what a lion does when it is not currently in a position to bite — the sound travels five miles, and it is meant to be heard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is *as*. Peter says he is **like** one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hunting for **somebody to eat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *seeking whom he may devour*. **Seeking** — he is looking, which means he has not found."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else is called a lion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Lion of the tribe of Judah**, the Root of David, has prevailed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Revelation's answer to the announcement: John turns to look at the Lion and sees *a Lamb as though it had been slain.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not run and not argue. The next verse: *stand up to him. **Plant your feet in the faith**.* See the note on 1 Peter 5:9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Resist, in the Greek, is **anthistemi** — to set yourself against, to hold ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cast **all your care** upon Him, for He cares for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter puts the anxiety verse and the lion verse back to back, in that order. The watchfulness he asks for is not the same thing as worry, and he removes the worry first."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being described.** A prowler, making noise, looking for somebody who is not paying attention. Peter's instruction is to be sober and awake — and then, in the next breath, to stand still."}]}, "1 Peter 5:9": {"title": "\"Plant your feet in the faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The answer to the lion in the verse before, and it is a standing instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stand up to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anthistemi** — from *anti*, against, and *histemi*, to stand. To set yourself opposite something and not move."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James has the same word and adds the outcome: *resist the devil and he will flee from you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in either verse is about advancing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the feet go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Plant your feet **in the faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *steadfast in the faith* — **stereos**, solid, hard, firm. It is used of solid food as against milk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ground is named. Not in resolve, not in experience. In the faith."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows the resisting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not describe a fight. He gives a piece of information instead, and it is about other people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knowledge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And know this: **your brothers and sisters all over the world are being put through the very same suffering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the same sufferings are being accomplished in your brotherhood in the world*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is writing to scattered believers in five Roman provinces who cannot see each other. The isolation is the point — each congregation thinks it is the only one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them what is happening elsewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The very same\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta auta** — the same things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not similar. The same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Right to the end of it\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek phrase carries the sense of being completed, brought to its full measure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is a limit and it is being reached — which is the argument the next verse takes up: *after you have suffered **your little while**.* See the note on 1 Peter 5:10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for brotherhood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Adelphotes** — the brotherhood, as a single body. Peter uses it twice in this letter and it appears nowhere else in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter has already told them about suffering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beloved, **do not think it strange** concerning the fiery trial which is to try you, **as though some strange thing happened to you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same instruction, earlier in the letter. The strangeness is what he keeps removing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter learned to stand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been sifted, and had been told in advance that he would be, and had been told *when you have turned back, strengthen your brothers.* See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This letter is that assignment being carried out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is offered against the lion.** Solid ground, and the knowledge that the same thing is happening to everybody else. Peter thought the second one was worth putting in the same sentence as the first."}]}, "1 Samuel 15:7–9": {"title": "What Saul kept", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Saul wins the battle and loses the kingdom, and the difference is a list of what he decided to keep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sorting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They kept the best of the sheep and cattle, the fat calves and lambs — everything that was good. These they were unwilling to destroy. But whatever was weak or worthless, they devoted to destruction.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He obeyed the command exactly, on everything he did not want."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator lays it out as a sorting exercise: good things kept, poor things destroyed. Obedience applied to the portion where obedience cost nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Agag"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took Agag, king of the Amalekites, alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king is the other thing kept. And that detail runs a long way — Haman, in the book of Esther, is called an Agagite, and he comes within a decree of destroying the Jewish people centuries later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What Saul kept alive turned up again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read on and it is the excuses that undo him. He tells Samuel he *has* performed the LORD's command; when the bleating of sheep gives him away, he says the people took them, and then that it was to sacrifice to the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel's answer is the line the chapter is remembered for: *to obey is better than sacrifice.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for the sparing.** *They were unwilling* — the Hebrew is about not being willing, refusing. It is not that Saul lacked the opportunity or the means. Everything in the sentence turns on what he wanted to keep."}]}, "1 Samuel 16:1–3": {"title": "\"I'll show you what to do\" — guidance given one step at a time", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Samuel is sent on the most consequential errand of his life and is not told how it ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is given"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["go to Bethlehem", "to a man named Jesse", "I have already picked one of his sons"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, when Samuel raises a genuine danger — Saul will kill him if he hears — God does not rebuke the fear. He gives him something practical to do about it: take a heifer, say you have come to sacrifice, invite Jesse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is not given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A name. Samuel arrives knowing the family and not the son, which is why the scene that follows works the way it does — seven brothers paraded past, each one looking more like a king than the last, and the answer is a boy nobody thought to call in from the field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I'll show you what to do.* The instruction is deliberately incomplete, and the missing piece is promised for when he gets there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that opens it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How long are you going to grieve over Saul? I have set him aside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds harsh until you notice what it is not. Samuel's grief is real and God does not call it foolish — He calls time on it, and the reason is that a replacement has already been chosen. *I've already picked one of his sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of the whole scene: the old arrangement has failed, the mourning is genuine, and the next thing is already decided before the prophet has filled his horn. Samuel is not being told to feel less. He is being told to fill the horn and go, because there is somewhere to go."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line that gets quoted from later in the chapter.** *People look at the outside; the LORD looks at the heart* — it arrives while Samuel is standing in front of the eldest brother, impressed. Even the prophet on the errand needed telling. The correction is not aimed at a crowd of shallow people; it is aimed at the man who had been sent, and who had already made up his mind."}]}, "1 Samuel 16:11–13": {"title": "\"We won't sit down until he's here\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven sons have passed and none of them is it, so Samuel asks a question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Are these all the sons you have?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has to ask. Nobody volunteers the information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There's still the youngest**,\" Jesse said. \"**But he's out in the fields, keeping the sheep**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **haqqaton** — the smallest, the youngest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *but* is Jesse's. A prophet has come to town, a sacrifice has been called, seven sons have been paraded, and the eighth was not thought worth fetching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is looking after the animals — which in a family with eight sons is the job given to the one who counts for least."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Send for him. We won't sit down until he's here**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *we will not sit around until he comes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole ceremony stops. Nobody eats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **ruddy and bright-eyed, with a fine face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *ruddy, with beautiful eyes, and good to look at*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the joke of the chapter. God has just told Samuel not to look at the outside, and then the one He picks is the handsome one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point was never that appearance disqualifies. It was that it does not decide."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD said, \"**This is the one. Get up and anoint him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel had to be told. He had asked for the boy without knowing, and he waits for the sentence before pouring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anointing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Samuel took the horn of oil and anointed him **right there in front of his brothers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In front of the seven who were called first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chapter says the family understood what it meant. Samuel had told them he came to sacrifice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And from that day on, **the Spirit of the LORD rushed upon David and stayed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsalach** — rushed, came mightily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**And stayed.** The next verse says the opposite of Saul: *but the Spirit of the LORD departed from Saul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, back to back, and the chapter turns on the contrast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Samuel did next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Samuel **went home to Ramah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No instruction, no training, no schedule. He anoints a teenager and leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David goes back to the sheep, and the next thing recorded of him is carrying bread and cheese to his brothers at the front. See the note on 1 Samuel 16:20–23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long the wait was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Years — through Goliath, Saul's spear, the wilderness, Ziklag, and Saul's death. Roughly fifteen by most reckonings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is anointed here and crowned in 2 Samuel 5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the choosing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God has chosen the foolish things of the world to put to shame the wise; and God has chosen the weak things of the world to put to shame the things which are mighty.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:26–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What had to happen first.** Somebody had to ask. Seven sons were presented and the eighth was in a field — and the prophet stopped the whole proceeding and refused to sit down until he came in."}]}, "1 Samuel 16:20–23": {"title": "David plays for Saul", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The newly anointed king's first job is playing music for the man he is going to replace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel has come to Bethlehem, worked through seven sons, asked *are all the young men here?*, and anointed the one who was out with the sheep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Spirit of the LORD came upon David from that day forward.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the next verse: *but the Spirit of the LORD departed from Saul.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesse sent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesse loaded a donkey with **bread, a skin of wine, and a young goat**, and sent them to Saul **with David his son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A gift to a king, and a boy alongside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesse does not know what Samuel did — or if he does, he sends him anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The post"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David came to Saul and **entered his service**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul felt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Saul **loved him dearly** and made him **one of his armor-bearers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *loved him greatly*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul later throws a spear at him twice, hunts him across the wilderness for years, and kills eighty-five priests for helping him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator records the loving first, without irony."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Saul sent word to Jesse: \"**Let David stay with me, for I'm pleased with him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for he has found favour in my eyes* — **chen**, grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word used over Noah, Joseph in Potiphar's house, and Esther before the king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trouble"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whenever **the harmful spirit came over Saul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 14 says where it came from: *the Spirit of the LORD departed from Saul, and a distressing spirit from the LORD troubled him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator says it plainly and does not explain it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David would **take the harp and play**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kinnor** — a lyre, played by hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Saul would **find relief, the darkness would lift, and the tormenting spirit would leave him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *Saul was refreshed and was well*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said, sung or prayed in the account. A boy plays and it lifts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David was doing beforehand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Keeping sheep, which is where Samuel found him, and where he learned the instrument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalms attributed to him run to seventy-three, and the one everybody knows opens *the LORD is my shepherd*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul's servants had said about him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A skilful player on the harp, a mighty man of valour, a man of war, prudent in speech, and handsome; **and the LORD is with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five qualities and then the last clause, which is the one that matters and the one they could not have arranged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goliath, in the following chapter — and Saul does not recognise him. *Whose son is this youth?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two accounts sit awkwardly together and the narrator lets them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first thing the anointed king does.** Carries a donkey-load of bread and wine to the palace and plays the lyre for a man in the dark — and 1 Samuel says the darkness lifted every time."}]}, "1 Samuel 16:6–7": {"title": "\"The LORD looks at the heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The prophet gets it wrong on the first candidate, and is corrected in his own head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is looking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Samuel took one look at Eliab, the oldest, and **thought**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *said* — but it is interior. Nothing is spoken aloud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he concluded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Surely this is the one** — the LORD's anointed is standing right here.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel is the last judge of Israel, the man who heard a voice in the night as a boy, and who had anointed Saul."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a genuine prophet, and his first read is wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't look at how handsome he is, or how tall he stands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The height matters because of who the last king was. Saul was chosen partly for it: *from his shoulders upward he was taller than any of the people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel is applying the criteria that produced Saul."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have not chosen him**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD does not see the way people see**. People look at **the outside — the face, the height, the polish**. But **the LORD looks at the heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *man looks at the eyes* — what is visible — *but the LORD looks at the heart*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has already established"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: God tells Samuel *how long will you mourn for Saul, seeing I have rejected him from reigning over Israel? **Fill your horn with oil, and go**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel is afraid — *if Saul hears it, he will kill me* — and is told to bring a heifer and say he has come to sacrifice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven sons pass by. Abinadab, Shammah, and four more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Samuel said to Jesse, \"**The LORD has not chosen these**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the question that breaks it open: *are all the young men here?* See the note on 1 Samuel 16:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was not invited"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eighth son was not brought to the sacrifice. His own father left him with the sheep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men in the room applied the same criteria Samuel had, and Samuel had to ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sentence is quoted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Man looks at the outward appearance, but the LORD looks at the heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It becomes one of the most quoted lines in the Old Testament, and it is spoken as a correction to a prophet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Eliab does later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the valley of Elah, when David asks about Goliath, Eliab is the brother who turns on him: *why did you come down here? And with whom have you left those few sheep in the wilderness? **I know your pride and the insolence of your heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's own theme, in the mouth of the man it was first applied to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who is corrected.** Not the family. The prophet — silently, in his own thought, before he had said a word out loud."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:28–30": {"title": "\"Can't I even ask a question?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first opposition David meets is not the giant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **Eliab, David's oldest brother**, heard David talking with the men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The brother Samuel had looked at first and been told *I have not chosen him*. See the note on 1 Samuel 16:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliab was in the room when the oil went on his youngest brother's head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **burned with anger**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *his anger was kindled*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four accusations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why did you even come down here?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **who's watching those few sheep in the wilderness?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I know how proud you are, and how wicked your heart is**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You only came to watch the battle**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one is aimed lower than the last: his presence, his job, his character, his motive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Those few sheep** — the Hebrew is *those few sheep*. It is contempt for the work and for the size of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David had been sent by their father with bread and cheese, on their father's instructions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The irony in the accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know how proud you are, and how wicked your heart is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before has just established the standard: *the LORD does not see the way people see… the LORD looks at the heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliab makes a confident claim about a heart, and he is the man that sentence was spoken over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "David's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What have I done now?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what have I done now?* — the *now* implies a history."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Can't I even ask a question?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek and Hebrew both come out as a mild protest. He does not defend himself and does not raise it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **turned to someone else and asked the same thing**, and **got the same answer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He steps around it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question he was asking — *what shall be done for the man who kills this Philistine?* — he asks again, of the next man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue with his brother, does not appeal to the anointing, and does not leave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been told about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing that he could use here. The anointing had happened *in front of his brothers* and Samuel had gone home to Ramah without explaining anything. See the note on 1 Samuel 16:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul hears about it, sends for him, and the exchange about the lion and the bear follows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the armour, and the five stones from the brook, and the giant's contempt. See the note on 1 Samuel 17:41–44."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sneers in one chapter — one from his brother and one from the Philistine — and David answers the second one at length and the first one in six words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Eliab turns up later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chronicles lists his daughter as married into the royal line, and 2 Chronicles 11 names her as the mother of Rehoboam's wife."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator never mentions the quarrel again."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the opposition.** The brother comes before the giant, and the accusation is about the heart — which is the exact category the previous chapter said only God can see."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:34–37": {"title": "The lion and the bear", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David's argument for being allowed to fight Goliath is not about courage. It is a work history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he offers as his credentials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When a lion or a bear came and carried off a sheep from the flock, I went after it, struck it down, and rescued the sheep from its mouth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody saw this. There was no army watching, no king, no reward. A boy alone with sheep, going after a predator to recover one animal that belonged to his father."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire résumé, and it is enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he went after them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to prove anything. Because the sheep was his father's, and a shepherd who lets a lion keep it is not a shepherd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail is precise — *rescued the sheep from its mouth*, and when the animal turned on him, he took it by the fur and killed it. Whoever preserved this account preserved the specifics."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion he draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD who rescued me from the paw of the lion and the paw of the bear will rescue me from the hand of this Philistine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reasoning runs backwards from what has already happened. He is not claiming to be strong; he is naming who did the rescuing last time and expecting the same person to do it again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the change of word: paw, paw, and then **hand**. Goliath is put in the same category as the animals."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go, and the LORD be with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king with no better plan sends a shepherd. And the thing that persuaded him was not a display of strength — it was an account of what God had already done where nobody was looking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the hidden work goes.** Everything David uses in the valley he learned on the hills: the sling he had used on predators, the refusal of armour he had never worn, and the conviction that the LORD rescues. The lion and the bear are not preparation for the giant in some general sense. They are the reason he knows what he knows."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:41–44": {"title": "\"Am I a dog?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The giant's only recorded speech to David, and it is four sentences of contempt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The approach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Philistine **came steadily closer**, his **shield-bearer walking ahead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already described the equipment: a bronze helmet, a coat of mail weighing five thousand shekels, bronze greaves, a bronze javelin, and a spear whose *staff was like a weaver's beam*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a man in front of him carrying the shield, because the armour is too heavy to carry a shield as well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The look"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he **looked David over** and saw **only a boy, ruddy and handsome**, he **sneered at him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he disdained him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ruddy and handsome** is the same description Samuel was given at the anointing. See the note on 1 Samuel 16:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing that marked him out to the prophet is the thing that makes the giant laugh."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Am I a dog**,\" he said, \"**that you come at me with sticks?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David had a staff, a sling, and five stones from the brook."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plural — *sticks* — is contempt for the whole equipment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dog** was the strongest insult in that culture, and it is the giant applying it to himself as a measure of the offence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cursing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **cursed David by his gods**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The threat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come here**, and **I'll give your flesh to the birds and the wild animals**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verses, and they are the reply the chapter is built for:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You come to me with a sword, with a spear, and with a javelin. But I come to you in the name of the LORD of hosts**, the God of the armies of Israel, whom you have defied.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This day the LORD will deliver you into my hand**… and all this assembly shall know that **the LORD does not save with sword and spear; for the battle is the LORD's**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the threat is returned, expanded: *I will give the carcasses of the camp of the Philistines this day to the birds of the air and the wild beasts.* See the note on 1 Samuel 17:45–47."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the armies had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty days. Goliath came out morning and evening and shouted, and *when Saul and all Israel heard those words, they were dismayed and greatly afraid*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had answered for nearly six weeks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was in the camp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul, who was *taller than any of the people from his shoulders upward* — the one man physically closest to the challenge — offering rewards to anyone else who would go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David had already told Saul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your servant has killed both lion and bear… **the LORD, who delivered me from the paw of the lion and from the paw of the bear, He will deliver me** from the hand of this Philistine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He argues from a record nobody witnessed, made while looking after sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The armour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul put his own armour on him and David took it off: *I cannot walk with these, for I have not tested them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes out in what he came in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the giant assessed.** Correctly, as far as it went — a boy with a stick. The reply David gives does not dispute the inventory; it names a different party to the fight."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:45–47": {"title": "\"The battle is the LORD's\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A boy with a sling makes a speech before he makes a shot, and the speech is longer than the fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two inventories"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You come at me with **sword and spear and javelin**, but I come at you **in the name of the LORD Almighty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goliath's equipment has been listed in detail earlier in the chapter — a bronze helmet, a coat of mail weighing five thousand shekels, bronze greaves, a bronze javelin, a spear whose shaft was like a weaver's beam, and a shield-bearer walking ahead of him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's list has one item on it, and it is a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he calls God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD Almighty, **the God of the armies of Israel** — the very One you have defied.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**LORD of hosts** — Yahweh Sabaoth, the God of armies. The title is chosen for the occasion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the offence is relocated. Forty days of Goliath shouting at Israel, and David identifies who was actually being insulted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says before it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This very day** the LORD will hand you over to me. **I'll strike you down and cut off your head.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past-tense certainty about a future event, spoken in the open, to the man's face, before a stone has left the pouch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes further — *the bodies of the whole Philistine army I'll give to the birds* — announcing not just a duel won but an army routed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So the whole earth will know there is a God in Israel.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated purpose is not David's survival or Israel's border. It is what everybody watching will conclude."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD does not save with sword or spear.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by a man who is about to use a sling and a stone — which are also not nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not despising the weapon. He is naming what the weapon is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For the battle is the LORD's**, and He will give all of you into our hands.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the battle belongs to the LORD*. It is a statement of ownership."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jehoshaphat is told the same thing in almost the same words centuries later: *the battle is not yours, but God's* — and he sends singers out in front of the army. See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:16–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had already told Saul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lion and the bear. *Your servant has killed both lion and bear, and this uncircumcised Philistine will be like one of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had a record nobody had seen, built in a field with nobody watching, and it is what he was standing on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David ran toward.** *When the Philistine arose and came and drew near to meet David, **David hurried and ran toward the army** to meet the Philistine.* He did not wait to be approached, and the text notes that he ran."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:48–51": {"title": "\"David broke into a run\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The whole fight takes one verse, and the detail worth noticing is the direction David is moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He ran at him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Goliath moved in to attack, David **broke into a run toward** the battle line to meet him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty days the army had stood still while the giant shouted. The first thing that changes is a shepherd running the wrong way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He closes the distance himself. A sling needs room, and he gives up room to shorten it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weapons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David won with a sling and a stone. Without a sword in his hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator says it twice, which is how Hebrew underlines. He had refused Saul's armour a few verses earlier — it was untested and he could not walk in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sling was not a toy. Judges mentions seven hundred left-handed Benjaminites who could sling a stone at a hair and not miss. It was the ranged weapon of a man with no armour, and David had used it on lions and bears where nobody was watching. See the note on 1 Samuel 17:34–37."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forehead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He struck the Philistine on the forehead. The stone sank in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one place the champion was not covered. Verse 5 describes the bronze helmet, the coat of scale armour weighing about fifty kilos, greaves, a javelin — and a gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sword"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then David ran and stood over him, drew Goliath's own sword from its sheath, and cut off his head with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He finishes with the giant's own weapon. The thing everyone had been terrified of becomes the instrument of the ending."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David had already said.** *The battle is the LORD's, and He will give all of you into our hands.* He announced the outcome to Goliath's face before he picked up a stone, and the account records the running, the sling and the sword as the working out of a sentence already spoken."}]}, "1 Samuel 18:1–4": {"title": "The covenant Jonathan made, and what he took off to make it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The heir to the throne meets the man who will take it from him, and gives him his clothes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When David finished speaking with Saul.* The conversation immediately after Goliath — David is holding the giant's head, and he is a shepherd nobody had heard of that morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan is the crown prince, a seasoned soldier who had won his own famous victory a few chapters earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he gave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took off the robe he was wearing and gave it to David, along with his tunic, and even his sword, his bow, and his belt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five items, and they are not gifts of affection. They are the marks of his position:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the robe** — the outer garment of a king's son", "**the tunic** — what was under it", "**sword, bow, belt** — his weapons and his rank"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He hands over the visible evidence of being next in line, to the man who is replacing him, on the day they meet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jonathan made a covenant with David, because he loved him as himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Jonathan who initiates it. David is not recorded as asking, and has nothing to offer in return."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That covenant is the reason David goes looking for a survivor of Saul's house years later, and the reason Mephibosheth gets a seat at the table. See the note on 2 Samuel 9:5–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan knew where this went. Later he says it plainly to David: *you will be king over Israel, and I will be second to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was never second to anyone. He died at Gilboa beside his father, and David wrote the lament."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for the knitting.** *Something knit Jonathan's soul to David's.* The Hebrew verb is used of tying or binding fast — the same root used later of Jonathan's love for him. Whatever else is in this friendship, the text describes it as a bond made rather than a feeling that arrived."}]}, "1 Samuel 2:1–10": {"title": "Hannah's song", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman hands over the child she prayed for and sings about the whole world turning over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has just left Samuel at Shiloh — *as long as he lives he shall be lent to the LORD* — and the song is what she says walking away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The child is about three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barren, and mocked for it. *Her rival also provoked her severely, to make her miserable… year by year.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eli had watched her lips moving at the tabernacle door and assumed she was drunk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My heart overflows with joy in the LORD**. In Him **my strength is lifted high**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my horn is exalted* — the horn of an animal, the standard image for strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word appears at the start and at the very end of the song, and Hannah is the first person in Scripture to use it that way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the song is not about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her son. He is not mentioned once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She sings about God reversing things, and the personal deliverance is one line of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The theology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD is a God who knows, and He weighs every deed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *by Him actions are weighed*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven pairs, and each turns over:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The **bows of the mighty are shattered**, but **those who stumbled are dressed in strength**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The **full-bellied hire themselves out for bread**, but **the hungry hunger no more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The **barren woman gives birth to seven**, while **the mother of many withers away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That third one is hers, and she puts it in the middle of a list rather than at the head of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD **brings death, and He brings life**. He **brings down to the grave, and He raises up**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD **makes poor, and He makes rich**. He **humbles, and He lifts high**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both sides of each pair are attributed to Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ash heap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He **raises the poor from the dust**. He **lifts the needy from the ash heap**, to **seat them among princes**, to hand them **a throne of honor**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 113 uses the same lines, and so does Mary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He gives strength to **His king** and lifts high the horn of **His anointed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **meshicho** — His Messiah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel has no king. There will not be one for decades, and the son she has just handed over will anoint the first two."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hannah sings about a king before the office exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the song goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary's Magnificat is built on it, line by line: *He has put down the mighty from their thrones, and exalted the lowly. He has filled the hungry with good things, and the rich He has sent away empty.* See the note on Luke 1:39–45."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thousand years apart, two women who had been told they would have a son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eli's sons and the three-pronged fork. The chronicler alternates between the two households, and after every account of the corruption comes a line about the boy. See the note on 1 Samuel 2:12–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does afterward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moreover his mother used to make him **a little robe, and bring it to him year by year** when she came up with her husband.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, once a year, for the rest of his childhood."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the song.** The child. She has just given him away and sings ten verses about God turning the order of things over — and the personal reversal is one line in the middle."}]}, "1 Samuel 2:12–17": {"title": "The three-pronged fork", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The corruption is described in procedural detail, which is what makes it damning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Eli's sons were **scoundrels**. They had **no regard for the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bene beliyaal** — sons of worthlessness. The phrase is used of the worst men in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**No regard for the LORD** — the Hebrew is *they did not know the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the priests at Shiloh, where the tabernacle stood. Their father is the high priest and the judge of Israel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The custom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here was **their custom** with the people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the priests' custom with the people*. It had become the practice, not an occasional abuse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fork"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priest's servant would show up **while the meat was still boiling**, holding **a three-pronged fork**. He'd **jab it into the pot or kettle or pan**, and **whatever the fork brought up, the priest took**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four vessels named. The detail is procedural, and that is the point — somebody set this up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus assigned the priests specific portions: the breast and the right thigh, waved and heaved. This is a fork in a pot, taking whatever comes out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it was done to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That's how they treated **all the Israelites who came to Shiloh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every pilgrim, every time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The worse half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Worse — **even before the fat was burned to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fat belonged to God. Leviticus is explicit and repeats it: *all the fat is the LORD's… you shall eat neither fat nor blood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were taking their portion before God's portion went on the fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The demand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Hand over some meat for the priest to roast. **He won't take boiled meat from you — only raw**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They want it uncooked so they can roast it their own way. It is a preference."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The threat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if the man said, \"**Let the fat be burned first**, then take what you want,\" the servant would answer, \"**No. Give it now, or I'll take it by force**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The worshipper is the one arguing for the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the sin of these young men was very great in the LORD's eyes**, because they were **treating the LORD's offering with contempt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they despised the offering of the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is not theft. It is contempt for what was being brought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **men abhorred the offering of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next clause of the passage, and it is the real damage. People stopped wanting to come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter alternates. After every account of the sons: *but Samuel ministered before the LORD, even as a child, wearing a linen ephod.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after the warning Eli gives them: *and the boy Samuel kept growing, both in size and in favour — with the LORD and with people.* See the note on 1 Samuel 2:22–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses almost the same sentence of Jesus at twelve. See the note on Luke 2:51–52."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both sons died on the same day at Aphek, the ark was captured, and Eli fell backward off his seat and broke his neck at the news."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His daughter-in-law, dying in labour, named the child **Ichabod** — *the glory has departed*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What made it great in God's eyes.** Not the amount taken. The fork went into the pot before the fat went on the fire — and the men bringing the sacrifices were the ones objecting."}]}, "1 Samuel 2:22–26": {"title": "A father's warning ignored", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eli confronts his sons, and the chapter ends by cutting to a boy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Eli was **very old** now. And he **kept hearing** about everything his sons were doing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he heard* — repeatedly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the high priest and the judge of Israel. He knows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything his sons were doing to **all Israel**, and how they **slept with the women who served at the entrance to the tent of meeting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fork in the sacrificial pot was chapter 2's first charge. See the note on 1 Samuel 2:12–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the second, and it is inside the tabernacle precinct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confrontation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why are you doing these things?** I hear from all these people about your evil ways.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks a question and cites public report."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reproach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No, my sons.** This is **no good report** I'm hearing — **you're leading the LORD's people into sin**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it is no good report that I hear*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the real damage: not what they take, but what it does to everybody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If a man sins against another man, **God may mediate for him**. But **if a man sins against the LORD, who can plead his case?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the judge shall judge him*, and then: who shall intercede?"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a genuine theological question, and the Old Testament never answers it — Job asks the same thing: *there is no umpire between us, no one to lay his hand on us both.* See the note on Job 9:25–35."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer is centuries away: *there is **one Mediator** between God and men, the Man Christ Jesus*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Eli did not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything. God's charge against him comes two chapters on, through the boy Samuel:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will judge his house forever for the iniquity which he knows, because **his sons made themselves vile, and he did not restrain them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And through the man of God in this chapter: *why do you **honour your sons more than Me**, by making yourselves fat with the best of all the offerings?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Making yourselves** — plural. Eli was eating it too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He talked and did not act, and he ate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But his sons **would not listen to their father**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the hardest clause in the chapter: *for **it was the LORD's will to put them to death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *because the LORD desired to kill them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator states it and does not explain it. Scripture does this occasionally — Pharaoh's hardened heart, the lying spirit in Ahab's prophets — and offers no commentary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cut"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the boy Samuel kept growing, both in size and in favour — with the LORD and with people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter alternates between the two households throughout, and this is the last line of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses almost the same sentence twice: of John, and of Jesus at twelve — *and Jesus increased in wisdom and stature, and in favour with God and men*. See the note on Luke 2:51–52."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famous line opens chapter 3: *and the word of the LORD was rare in those days; there was no widespread revelation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then a voice in the night, three times, and Eli — who could not restrain his own sons — telling the boy what to say: *speak, LORD, for Your servant hears.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old man gives the right instruction to somebody else's child."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the warning lacked.** Not clarity. Eli says exactly what is wrong and asks a question nobody in the Old Testament can answer — and the chapter's charge against him, delivered later, is that he did not restrain them."}]}, "1 Samuel 25:2–3": {"title": "Nabal and Abigail", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two people introduced in one verse, and the writer sets them against each other word for word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His name was **Nabal**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nabal** means fool. Not a nickname the chapter awards him — his own wife says it later: *as his name is, so is he. Nabal is his name, and folly is with him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **nabal** is not the word for a simpleton. It is the moral fool of Proverbs and the Psalms — *the fool has said in his heart, there is no God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **very rich** — three thousand sheep and a thousand goats.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer gives the figures. He is a substantial man, and shearing time was the year's payday."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She was a woman of **good sense and lovely to look at**, but the man was **harsh and mean** in his dealings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for her good sense is **sekel** — insight, prudence, the wisdom that produces good outcomes. It is the same root as the word in Joshua 1:8, where meditating on the book produces *good success*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her sense is listed **before** her looks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And his two adjectives are hard and evil in his doings. The contrast is deliberate and total."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David had protected Nabal's shepherds in the wilderness — *they were a wall to us both by night and day*, one of the servants says. At shearing, David asks for provision and is insulted: *who is David? Who is the son of Jesse?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David straps on his sword and takes four hundred men to kill every male in the household."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abigail loads two hundred loaves, two skins of wine, five dressed sheep, roasted grain, raisins and figs, and rides out to meet him without telling her husband."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speech"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the longest by a woman in the Old Testament, and it works. She takes the blame herself, feeds four hundred men, and then tells David what he is about to throw away:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When the LORD has done for my lord all the good He has promised, and made you ruler over Israel, then you will have **no cause for grief, no burden on your conscience** for shedding blood without cause.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She argues from his future rather than his anger. See the note on 1 Samuel 25:29–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed is your advice**, and blessed are you, because you have kept me this day from coming to bloodshed and from avenging myself with my own hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He blesses the counsel before he blesses the woman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What became of them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nabal held a feast *like the feast of a king* that same night, drunk. She told him in the morning, *and his heart died within him, and he became like a stone*, and ten days later he was dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David sent for Abigail and married her."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The house he was from.** *He was of the house of Caleb* — the man who at eighty-five asked for the mountain with the giants on it. The blood was there; the spirit was not. See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}]}, "1 Samuel 25:29–31": {"title": "\"The bundle of the living\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman standing in a ravine in front of four hundred armed men talks a future king out of a massacre."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture she gives him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The life of my lord will be **bound safe in the bundle of the living**, kept by the LORD your God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *bound in the bundle of life*. The image is of valuables tied up in a cloth and carried — the way a traveller secured what he could not afford to lose, by keeping it on his person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase entered Jewish liturgy and is still cut into gravestones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the picture for his enemies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But the lives of your enemies He will **sling away like a stone from a sling**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is talking to the man who killed Goliath with a sling. She takes the weapon from the best day of his life and hands it to God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two images in one sentence — one for what God keeps and one for what God discards — and she picked both for this listener."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she argues from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When the LORD has done for my lord **all the good He has promised**, and made you **ruler over Israel**…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David is a fugitive living in caves. Saul is on the throne. She speaks of the kingdom as settled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had crowned him. She had heard the reports, worked out what he was, and addresses him accordingly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thing she is protecting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"…then you will have **no cause for grief, no burden on your conscience** for shedding blood without cause or for taking vengeance yourself.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is her whole case. Not *spare us* — she barely mentions her household."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She argues that a man on his way to a throne cannot afford to arrive at it with this on him. The Hebrew is *no stumbling of heart*, no obstacle in the conscience."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is defending his future from his present temper."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And when the LORD deals well with my lord, **remember your servant**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One line, at the very end, and it is the same request the thief makes on the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Blessed is the LORD God of Israel, who sent you this day to meet me. **Blessed is your advice**, and blessed are you, because you have kept me this day from coming to bloodshed and from **avenging myself with my own hand**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He blesses God, then the counsel, then her. And he names precisely what he was about to do: avenge himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had spared Saul twice by then, refusing to touch the LORD's anointed. He was minutes from slaughtering a household over an insult about food."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wisdom in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter introduced her as *a woman of good sense* — **sekel**, insight that produces good outcomes. This is what it looks like in operation: right words, right man, right moment, at considerable personal risk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on 1 Samuel 25:2–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she did not do.** Tell her husband. *But she did not tell Nabal her husband.* The chapter records it without comment, and then records that when she did tell him, in the morning, when he was sober, his heart died within him."}]}, "1 Samuel 3:1–3": {"title": "\"The lamp of God had not yet gone out\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of scene-setting, and every detail in them is about light failing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word was rare"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In those days **the word of the LORD was rare; visions rarely broke through**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *precious* — scarce because valuable, the way a rare metal is precious."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the end of the period of the judges. Eli's sons are stealing from the offerings and sleeping with the women at the door of the tabernacle, and the last line of the book of Judges is *everyone did what was right in his own eyes.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eli's eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His eyes had grown so weak he could **barely see**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Physical, and the narrative means it both ways — the chapter before has the old priest unable to tell the difference between a woman praying and a woman drunk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lamp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **lamp of God had not yet gone out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The golden lampstand, in the holy place. The priests were charged to keep it burning: *Aaron shall tend it from evening until morning before the LORD continually.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Had not yet gone out* is a strange way to report it. Not *was burning* — the writer tells you it was close."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the last hours before dawn, the oil is low, and the light is nearly gone. That is the state of the priesthood and the nation in one clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the boy is lying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Samuel was lying down in the temple of the LORD, **where the ark of God was**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A child asleep on the floor beside the ark, under a lamp that is going out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who hears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the priest. The boy — who *did not yet know the LORD, nor was the word of the LORD yet revealed to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times he runs to Eli, and it takes Eli three tries to work out what is happening. Then the old man, whatever else is true of him, tells the child exactly what to say: *Speak, LORD, for Your servant hears.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to the lamp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next chapter: the ark is captured, Eli's sons are killed, Eli falls backwards and breaks his neck, and a woman in labour names her child **Ichabod** — *the glory has departed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp went out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what happens to the boy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD was with him and **let none of his words fall to the ground**. And all Israel from Dan to Beersheba knew that Samuel had been established as a prophet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word that was rare became plentiful, and it came through the child who was sleeping under the failing light."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God said in that first message.** A judgement on Eli's house. The first word ever given to Samuel was one he had to deliver to the man who raised him, and Eli's answer was *it is the LORD; let Him do what seems good to Him.*"}]}, "1 Samuel 6:19–21": {"title": "\"Who can stand before the LORD, this holy God?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The ark comes home after seven months among the Philistines, and the welcome ends badly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God struck down some of the men of Beth-shemesh because they looked into the ark of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They opened it. The lid — the mercy seat, the place God had said He would speak from — was lifted, and what was under it was the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers had laid down that even the Levites who carried the holy things were not to look at them, on pain of death. These were farmers in a wheat field who took the covering off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew reads oddly — seventy men and fifty thousand — and the figure has long been discussed, since Beth-shemesh was a small town. Some manuscripts and early witnesses have only the seventy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text as it stands records both, and the mourning that followed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who can stand before the LORD, this holy God? Where can the ark go from here?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the right question, asked too late. And their answer is to send it somewhere else — the ark goes on to Kiriath-jearim, where it stays twenty years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the chapter asks how a person might come near safely. They ask who will take it off their hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage is actually about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The uncovered law kills. That is the plainest reading of it, and it is the same point the New Testament makes in different words — *the letter kills, but the Spirit gives life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What made the ark approachable on the Day of Atonement was blood on the lid and smoke over it. Take the covering away and what is left is holiness with nothing between."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read with Exodus 25:17–22.** God had said He would meet them *above the mercy seat*. Not at the tablets — above them, over the covering. Beth-shemesh is what happens when the covering comes off, and it is why the arrangement was given in the first place."}]}, "1 Samuel 7:7–11": {"title": "A nursing lamb and the thunder", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An army is coming, and the one thing Israel does about it is kill a very small lamb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Samuel offered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Samuel took a **nursing lamb** and offered it **whole** as a burnt offering to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a bull, not a ram, not the seven bullocks that a national emergency might seem to call for. A lamb still on its mother's milk — the smallest offering a man could bring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *whole* — the Hebrew is the word for entire, offered complete, nothing kept back and nothing eaten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While Samuel was offering the sacrifice, the Philistines drew near to attack.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator puts the two clauses in the same sentence on purpose. The enemy is closing while the lamb is on the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is armed. Nobody has taken a position. Israel is at worship with an army walking toward them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who fought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But that day the **LORD thundered** with a mighty voice against the Philistines and threw them into such confusion that they were routed before Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *confusion* is the panic-word — the same one used at the Red Sea and against the Canaanite kings under Joshua. It is a specific act of God recorded in a specific verb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel's part comes second: *the men of Israel rushed out… and chased them.* They pursue a battle that has already been won over an altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The small thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The size of the lamb and the size of the deliverance are not proportioned to each other, and the chapter does not apologise for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the offering did was proclaim a death. Everything after the thunder is Israel walking into ground the sacrifice had already taken. See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:26–31 on what God chooses to confound the strong with."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Samuel set up afterwards.** A stone, named **Ebenezer** — *thus far the LORD has helped us.* A rock in a field, with no power in it at all, put there so that people walking past would have to remember what happened. The chapter ends with a memorial as small as the lamb it commemorates."}]}, "1 Samuel 8:1–3": {"title": "\"They were nothing like their father\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses that set up everything that follows — the demand for a king, the warning about what a king will do, and the whole monarchy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The decision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Samuel had grown old, and so he handed the reins of Israel to his sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the book of Judges established succession by inheritance. Judges were raised up individually; the office did not pass from father to son. Samuel makes his sons judges anyway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not editorialise. It records the appointment and then records the result."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They chased after money, took bribes, and bent the truth to line their own pockets.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bribery and perverted justice are exactly what the law forbids of judges, in almost these words — Deuteronomy warns that a bribe blinds the eyes of the wise. Samuel's sons are doing the specific thing the office exists to prevent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The echo nobody in the story mentions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel had grown up in the house of Eli, whose own sons were corrupt, and whose failure to restrain them was the reason his house was cut off. Samuel himself delivered that message as a boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he appoints his own sons, and they turn out the same way. The book puts the two households in parallel and never comments on it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it triggers.** The elders come to Samuel and ask for a king *like all the other nations*, and they cite his sons as the reason. Their complaint is legitimate. What they ask for as the remedy is the thing God says amounts to rejecting Him — and He tells Samuel to give it to them anyway, with a warning attached."}]}, "1 Samuel 9:6–10": {"title": "A quarter shekel and some lost donkeys", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first king of Israel is out looking for livestock when he stumbles into the appointment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul's father has lost some donkeys and sent him with a servant to find them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have been through the hill country of Ephraim, Shalisha, Shaalim and Benjamin, and found nothing. Saul is ready to turn back — *lest my father cease caring about the donkeys and become worried about us*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose idea it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the servant had an idea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Saul's. The servant's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The suggestion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Wait — there's **a man of God in this town**. People hold him in high honor; **everything he says comes true**. Let's go to him. **Maybe he can tell us which way to go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They want directions to some donkeys."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man is Samuel — prophet, judge, the last of the old order, and the one who will anoint Israel's first two kings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither of them names him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But if we go,\" Saul said, \"**what would we bring him? Our food is gone, and we've got nothing to offer** a man of God. **What do we have?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Custom expected a gift. Saul checks his pockets and finds nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The solution"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look — **I've got a quarter shekel of silver right here**. I'll give it to the man of God, and he'll tell us the way.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servant has it. A quarter shekel was a small coin — enough to be an offering and not much else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole meeting happens because a servant had loose change."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The narrator's aside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*(In those days, if a man wanted to inquire of God, he'd say, \"**Let's go to the seer**\" — for **what we now call a prophet used to be called a seer**.)*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ro'eh** — one who sees — against **nabi**, prophet, one who speaks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator interrupts his own story to explain a word that had gone out of use by the time he wrote."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older title is about the eyes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The decision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Good idea,\" Saul said. \"**Let's go**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Samuel already knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the LORD had told Samuel in his ear **the day before Saul came**, saying, \"Tomorrow about this time I will send you a man from the land of Benjamin, and **you shall anoint him commander over My people Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-four hours' notice, and a meal already prepared with a portion set aside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Samuel says about the donkeys"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for **your donkeys that were lost three days ago, do not be anxious about them, for they have been found**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He deals with the errand in one sentence and then: *\"And on whom is all the desire of Israel? Is it not on you and on all your father's house?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Saul's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Am I not a Benjamite, **of the smallest of the tribes of Israel**, and my family the least of all the families of the tribe of Benjamin? **Why then do you speak like this to me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benjamin had nearly been wiped out at the end of Judges. He is not being falsely modest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A meal, a bed on the roof, and then early in the morning at the edge of town: *Samuel took a flask of oil and poured it on his head, and kissed him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul goes home and tells his uncle about the donkeys. *But about the matter of the kingdom, he did not tell him.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was looking for.** Donkeys. He arrives because a servant remembered a seer lived nearby and happened to have a quarter shekel — and the narrator stops the story to explain that a prophet used to be called somebody who sees."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 1:3–5": {"title": "Faith, love, hope — and what each one does", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's opening thanksgiving, and each of the three is given a verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three with their nouns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How your **faith goes to work**, how your **love keeps laboring on**, how your **hope in our Lord Jesus Christ holds steady and patient** through everything.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is three genitive pairs:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Ergou tes pisteos** — the work of faith", "**Kopou tes agapes** — the labour of love", "**Hupomones tes elpidos** — the endurance of hope"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's triad appears throughout his letters — *faith, hope and love, these three* — and here each is attached to something it produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three words"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Ergon** — work, what is done", "**Kopos** — labour to weariness, toil that costs", "**Hupomone** — remaining under, holding position"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is stronger than the first, and the third is not activity at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which goes with which"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith works. Love is the one that wears you out. Hope is what stays under the weight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not shuffle them; the pairings are the same when he repeats the triad in the same letter: *putting on the breastplate of faith and love, and as a helmet the hope of salvation.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **all of it lived out in the sight of our God and Father**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Dear friends, **loved by God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a perfect passive participle — having been loved and standing loved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We know that **He chose you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eklogen** — election."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says he knows it, and then gives his evidence — which is the striking part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **when we brought you the good news it did not arrive as words and nothing more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not in word only*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue from their sincerity, their decision, or their conduct. He argues from what the preaching was like when it landed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things it came with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It came **with power**, and **with the Holy Spirit**, and **with a deep and settled certainty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last is **plerophoria polle** — much full assurance. Full-carrying, complete conviction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half of the evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **you saw for yourselves the kind of men we were while we were with you**, and **it was for your sake that we were that way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The conduct of the messengers is offered as part of it, and the reason given for the conduct is the readers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long he was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts 17: three sabbaths in the synagogue, then a mob, then Jason arrested and made to post bond, then Paul and Silas sent away by night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weeks. And what he says about the church that came out of it is in the next verses: *the word of the Lord rang out from you… your trust in God has gone out everywhere.* See the note on 1 Thessalonians 1:8–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you became followers of us and of the Lord, **having received the word in much affliction, with joy of the Holy Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Affliction and joy in the same clause."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul offers as proof of election.** Not the converts' performance. The manner in which the message arrived — power, the Spirit, and full assurance — and the fact that they saw what the messengers were like."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 1:8–10": {"title": "The word that travelled out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes to a church he had to leave in a hurry, and tells them their reputation arrived before he could."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sound"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The word of the Lord **rang out from you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exechetai** — from the root behind *echo*. It is used of a trumpet sounding, or thunder rolling out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How far"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **not only through Macedonia and Achaia**; your trust in God has **gone out everywhere**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Macedonia and Achaia are the two Roman provinces of Greece. Then Paul widens it to *every place*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thessalonica sat on the Via Egnatia, the main road from the Adriatic to Byzantium, and it had a busy port. What was said there travelled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul's position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **there is nothing left for us to say about it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He arrives somewhere to talk about the Thessalonians and finds the story already told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is reporting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People **tell us themselves** what kind of welcome we found among you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Strangers are telling Paul about his own visit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And how you **turned away from your idols** and **turned to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One movement with two directions, and Paul puts them in that order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *turned* is **epestrepsate** — the ordinary word for turning round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To serve **the God who is living and real**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the living and true God* — **alethinos**, genuine as against a copy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to people who had just stopped serving statues."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things they do now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **serve** the God who is living and real, and to **wait for His Son from heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Serving and waiting, together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both Thessalonian letters are dominated by the return of Christ — every chapter of the first one ends on it — and Paul had been with them only a few weeks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Son described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Son He **raised from the dead**, Jesus, who **rescues us from the wrath that is coming**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *rescues* is **rhuomenon** — present participle, the one who is rescuing. It is the same verb Paul uses of his own deliverance in Asia. See the note on 2 Corinthians 1:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What their welcome had cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts 17: three sabbaths in the synagogue, then a mob, then Jason's house attacked, then Jason dragged before the city officials and made to post bond, and Paul and Silas sent away by night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weeks, and then an evacuation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You became followers of us and of the Lord, **having received the word in much affliction, with joy of the Holy Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about them at the top of the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Remembering without ceasing **your work of faith, labour of love, and patience of hope**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three, in that order, with a genitive attached to each."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What travelled.** Not a report of Paul's preaching. Paul says what went out was their faith — and that he keeps meeting people who tell him about it before he can open his mouth."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 4:13–15": {"title": "Grief with something left in it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The earliest surviving Christian letter, written to people who had a specific worry: friends of theirs had died before the Lord came back, and they did not know what that meant for them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell them to stop grieving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that your sorrow is not the sorrow of people who have nothing left to hope for.* The comparison is not between grief and no grief. It is between two kinds of grief — one with a floor under it, one without."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That distinction has held up better than any attempt to talk Christians out of mourning. Paul assumes they will weep. He is dealing with what they weep into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Fallen asleep\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it three times in three verses. It was already a common euphemism, but in Christian use it acquired a specific edge, because sleep is the one state everybody expects to end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not being delicate about death — the next paragraph is trumpets and shouting and the dead rising. He is choosing a word whose whole force depends on waking up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument in one line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We believe that Jesus died and rose again; and in the same way, God will bring with Him those who have fallen asleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything rests on the parallel. Not on a general hope of the soul's survival — on one event that happened, and the claim that the same thing happens to those who belong to Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The anxiety he is answering.** *Those of us who are still alive… will not go on ahead of the ones who have fallen asleep.* They seem to have feared their dead would miss it, or come second. Paul's correction is almost blunt: the dead in Christ are not at a disadvantage. If anything, they go first."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 4:16–18": {"title": "What the passage is actually for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the most argued-over paragraph about the Lord's return, and Paul ends it with an instruction that tells you what he wrote it for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three sounds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With a shout, with the voice of the archangel, with the trumpet of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing hidden or quiet. The word for shout is a **command** shouted aloud — used of an officer to troops, or a captain to rowers. Whatever else this is, it is not secret."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Caught up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **harpazo** means to seize, to snatch away by force. It is used of the Spirit carrying Philip off, and of a child snatched up to God in Revelation. The Latin translation of it, *rapturo*, is where the English word rapture comes from — which is why that word is not in most English Bibles: it came in through Latin, not from a missing Greek term."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is not a gentle ascent. It is being grabbed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order Paul gives"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the Lord comes down", "the dead in Christ rise **first**", "then the living are caught up **together with them**", "and so we will be with the Lord always"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is still answering the question from the verses before: their dead are not left behind. The sequence exists to settle that worry, and *together* is the word carrying it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line is the point.** *Comfort one another with these words.* Not \"chart these events,\" not \"work out the timing.\" Paul wrote this to be said to grieving people, and the destination of the whole passage is the plainest thing in it: *we will be with the Lord always.* Everything else in the paragraph is the transport."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 5:4–8": {"title": "The helmet, and what it is made of", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just described the day coming like a thief, and here is what he says about that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the thief surprises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you, brothers, are not living in the dark**, so that the day should come upon you the way a thief comes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thief image belongs to the previous paragraph, and Paul immediately says it does not apply to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thief surprises people who are asleep in the dark. His readers are described as neither."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of you is **a child of the light, a child of the day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew idiom — *son of* — meaning belonging to, characterised by."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We do not belong to the night, and we do not belong to the darkness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated as fact before anything is asked. Paul's habit everywhere: the identity first, then the instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **let us not drowse** along with everyone else; **let us stay awake, and keep our heads clear**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *keep our heads clear* is **nepho** — to be sober, unintoxicated, and by extension clear-minded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same word: *be sober, be vigilant, because your adversary the devil walks about like a roaring lion.* See the note on 1 Peter 5:8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The observation about night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sleepers do their sleeping at night, and those who drink themselves senseless do it at night — **but we belong to the day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A plain observation about how people behave, used as an argument about identity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The armour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Put on **faith and love as armour across the heart**, and **the hope of salvation as a helmet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pieces only. Paul gives the full set in Ephesians 6; here he names two, and both are placed over things that need protecting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The breastplate** covers the heart — faith and love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The helmet** covers the head — hope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the helmet is made of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hope. Specifically *the hope of salvation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The head is where the arguments happen, and Paul's protection for it is not a technique for managing thoughts. It is an expectation about the outcome."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"salvation\" means here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verse says: *for God did not appoint us to wrath, but to obtain salvation through our Lord Jesus Christ.* See the note on 1 Thessalonians 5:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Soteria** — the New Testament's wide word, covering rescue, healing, wholeness, preservation. The same root used of a woman made well and a sinking man pulled out of the water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the line after that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who died for us, that **whether we wake or sleep, we should live together with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just spent a paragraph on waking and sleeping, and then says the outcome is the same either way."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul says the readers already are.** *Not in darkness. Children of light. Not appointed to wrath.* Three things stated flatly in five verses, and the helmet is what keeps them in the head."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 5:9–11": {"title": "\"God never marked us out for wrath\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent a chapter and a half on the Lord's return, and this is the conclusion he draws from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appointment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God never marked us out for wrath. He marked us out to come into salvation through our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about setting or appointing — placing someone in a category. Paul denies one appointment and states another, and both are God's doing rather than the reader's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Thessalonians had been anxious. The letter has already reassured them about their dead; now it settles the other question, which is what is coming for the living."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Awake or asleep\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that awake or asleep, we are alive with Him, and alive together.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has used *asleep* for the Christian dead all through chapter 4. Here he uses both words in one clause and says the outcome is identical."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether the Lord finds a believer alive or already buried makes no difference to the result — *alive with Him, and alive together.* It is the same reassurance he gave earlier, compressed to a phrase."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he tells them to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So keep encouraging one another, and keep building one another up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The practical instruction attached to the second coming is not preparation, not calculation, and not vigilance about dates. It is to speak to each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said the same at the end of chapter 4 — *comfort one another with these words*. Twice, and both times the teaching about the return is handed over as material for encouraging somebody else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last clause.** *Which is exactly what you are already doing.* Paul adds it almost in passing, and it is characteristic — he tells a church to do the thing and then notes that they are doing it. The instruction arrives as confirmation rather than correction."}]}, "1 Timothy 4:11–13": {"title": "\"Do not let anyone dismiss you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's instruction to a young man left running a difficult church."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give these instructions, and teach them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parangelle** — a military word, to pass on an order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let anyone **dismiss you because you are young**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kataphroneito** — think down on, despise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Young** — **neotes** could cover anyone up to about forty in that culture. Timothy had been travelling with Paul for over a decade and was still the junior man in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What replaces the credential"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let them see in you instead **the pattern of what a believer is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tupos** — a stamp, a mould, the impression a die leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer to being dismissed is not an argument about authority."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **what you say**, in **the way you live**, in **love**, in **spirit**, in **faith**, in **purity**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two are outward — speech and conduct. Four are inward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three public duties"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until I come, give your attention to:*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The reading of the Scriptures aloud** — the Greek is **te anagnosei**, the public reading. It was the synagogue practice carried into the church: a passage read out to a congregation where almost nobody owned a copy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Calling people up to what is right** — **paraklesei**, exhortation, the same root as the word for the Helper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Teaching** — **didaskalia**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read, exhort, teach. That is the whole job description."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Timothy was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ephesus. Paul had left him there *that you may charge some that they teach no other doctrine* — a church he had spent three years in, and had warned that *from among your own selves men will rise up*. See the note on Acts 20:28–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not neglect **the gift that is in you**, which was given to you by prophecy with the laying on of the hands of the eldership.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Meditate on these things; **give yourself entirely to them**, so that **your progress may be evident to all**.* See the note on 1 Timothy 4:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *progress* is **prokope** — a pioneering word, cutting a way forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter has already dealt with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Myths and endless genealogies, people wanting to be teachers of the law *understanding neither what they say nor the things which they affirm*, and — in the chapter directly before this — men *forbidding to marry and commanding to abstain from foods*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's answer to that: *every creature of God is good, and nothing is to be refused if it is received with thanksgiving*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Years later, in his last: *but you — hold your ground in what you learned and came to be sure of.* See the note on 2 Timothy 3:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *stir up the gift of God which is in you through the laying on of my hands. For God has not given us a spirit of fear, but of power and of love and of a sound mind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which suggests the timidity was real and persistent."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is offered against being written off.** Not seniority and not volume. A pattern visible in six things — and three public duties: read it out, urge them, teach."}]}, "1 Timothy 4:14–16": {"title": "What \"meditate\" meant, and what \"saved\" covers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Take these things to heart, our text says — and that is the right feel for it. Underneath sits a Greek word with more packed into it than any one English phrase can carry, so here is what is in there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "It is the Psalm 1 word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is meletao. It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses in Psalm 1 — the man whose delight is in the law, and who meditates in it day and night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And look at what Paul said two verses earlier: give your attention to the reading of the Scriptures aloud. Then he says this. He is telling a young minister to do with these things what the blessed man of Psalm 1 does with the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "It is not emptying your mind. It is filling it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Behind the Greek stands a Hebrew word, hagah. It does not mean sitting still and going blank. It means muttering, murmuring, going over something under your breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Hebrew word gets used for a lion growling over its food, and for a dove moaning. It is a noise word. Biblical meditation is closer to chewing something over out loud than it is to silence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And it also means practice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In ordinary Greek, meletao is what a public speaker does when he drills a speech — going over it until it is in him and he no longer has to reach for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So both are in the one word: turn it over, and let it become yours. Say it until it lives in you."}, {"k": "note", "t": "One thing not to push too far. The Greek word for meditate does cover quiet reflection as well — the noisy, muttering sense comes from the Hebrew word behind the Psalm, not from the Greek on its own. The claim that holds is this: biblical meditation means filling your mind with something and going over it, not emptying your mind. That much is solid, and it is enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why \"profiting\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is prokope. It comes from a verb meaning to cut forward — the way someone hacks a path through undergrowth ahead of everyone else. It is not money quietly accumulating. It is ground gained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is saying: stay in this, and people will be able to see the path opening up in front of you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And now the word this block ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at how it finishes: you will be the saving of yourself and of everyone who listens to you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is sozo."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sozo means to save, to rescue, to heal, to make whole, to preserve, to bring safely through. Complete salvation and complete deliverance — in any and every sense a person could need it.** That is not a devotional stretch. That is the size of the word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In English, saved has narrowed until it means one thing: getting out of hell. The Greek word never narrowed like that, and it never had to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "A picture of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Think of a man pulled out of the sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The English word stops at the moment he stops sinking. He did not drown. That is saved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But the rescue is not finished there, and no one who has actually been rescued thinks it is. He is hauled over the side. The water is got out of him. Somebody wraps him in something dry, puts hot food in his hands, and sits with him until he stops shaking. He is carried to shore. He is taken home. In the morning he wakes up in his own bed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sozo is the whole of that. Not the instant he stopped going under — everything from the water to the front door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same word does all of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word the disciples shout when the boat is going down in the storm and they wake Jesus: save us, we are perishing. Nobody in that boat is thinking about the afterlife. They mean get us out of this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used when a blind beggar receives his sight. It is the word used of the woman who had been ill for twelve years, when she touches Him and the bleeding stops. It is the word James uses for a sick man raised up when the church prays over him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the word for the rescue of a life, finally and for ever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word, wide enough to hold all of it — because what God does with a person was never divided into compartments in the first place."}]}, "1 Timothy 5:17–18": {"title": "\"Double portion of honour\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells Timothy how to treat the men who lead, and the word he uses does two jobs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The elders who **lead well** should be counted worthy of **a double portion of honour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *honour* is **time** — and it means both esteem and price. It is the same noun Peter uses in *give her honour* to husbands, and it is the ordinary word for the cost of a thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's proof text settles which sense he has in mind here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two quotations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain**\"* — Deuteronomy 25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The worker has earned his pay**\"* — and this one is a saying of Jesus, from Luke 10, when He sent out the seventy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Moses and Jesus in one breath and calls both of them *the Scripture*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel of Luke was recent, and Paul is already citing it alongside the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ox, again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He used the same verse in 1 Corinthians 9, with the same application, and there he spelled out the logic: *is it oxen God is concerned about, or does He say it entirely for our sake?* See the note on 1 Corinthians 9:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is an animal working with food under its feet all day. The law forbids tying its mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Especially those who preach and teach\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **most of all those who wear themselves out** in preaching and teaching.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to labour to exhaustion — the word Paul uses of his own work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He distinguishes within the group: elders who lead well get the double portion, and those who wear themselves out teaching get it most."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds this in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before are about widows — who to support, who not to, at length. The verses after are about accusations against an elder: *do not receive an accusation against an elder except from two or three witnesses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the passage is a run of practical instructions about money and reputation inside a church, and this one sits in the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul's own practice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He took none of it. He argued the right thoroughly in Corinth and then made tents, *that we might not be a burden to any of you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He legislates generosity toward others and declines it for himself, and both are in his letters without any sense of contradiction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse right after.** *Do not receive an accusation against an elder except from two or three witnesses.* Pay them properly and protect them from casual accusation — Paul puts the two instructions side by side, and both are about not making a hard job harder."}]}, "1 Timothy 6:11–12": {"title": "Run away, chase after, take hold", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives a young man three verbs and one title."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you, **man of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anthrope theou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the standing Old Testament title for a prophet — Moses, Samuel, Elijah, Elisha. Paul uses it of Timothy, twice in the two letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Run away from all of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pheuge** — flee. The root of *fugitive*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**All of it** — what he has just described: men who *suppose that godliness is a means of gain*, and the love of money as a root everything grows out of. See the note on 1 Timothy 6:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's counsel there is not to resist. It is to leave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same about immorality — *flee sexual immorality* — and about idolatry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Chase after what is right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dioke** — pursue, hunt down. The word used of persecutors chasing their target."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same verb of love: *pursue love*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Run from one thing, run after another. Both are running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **what is right**, after **a life turned toward God**, after **faith**, **love**, **endurance**, **gentleness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six in the Greek: righteousness, godliness, faith, love, patience, meekness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last is **praupathian** — gentleness of spirit. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fight **the good fight that faith fights**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agonizou ton kalon agona tes pisteos** — a triple play on the same root. **Agon** is a contest, a struggle in the arena; the root of *agony*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a battle. A contest, with a prize at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take hold of **the life that lasts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epilabou** — seize, grasp firmly. The same verb used of Jesus taking the sinking Peter by the hand, and of Him taking the man with dropsy by the arm. See the note on Luke 14:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The life **you were called into**, the life **you spoke for so well in front of many witnesses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses about the past. He was called, and he made a public confession."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The taking hold is of something already given and already professed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I urge you in the sight of God **who gives life to all things**, and before Christ Jesus **who witnessed the good confession before Pontius Pilate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy's confession before witnesses, and Christ's before Pilate, in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Timothy was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A young man Paul had picked up at Lystra, son of a Jewish mother and a Greek father, left in charge at Ephesus — the city where Paul had warned that *savage wolves will come in among you*. See the note on Acts 20:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second letter tells him *let no one despise your youth*, and *stir up the gift of God which is in you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Command those who are rich in this present age **not to be haughty, nor to trust in uncertain riches but in the living God, who gives us richly all things to enjoy**.* See the note on 1 Timothy 6:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter warns about wanting money and ends by telling the wealthy to enjoy what they have and give it away."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The four verbs.** Flee, pursue, contend, take hold. Three of them are about motion, and the last one is about something that was already handed over."}]}, "1 Timothy 6:17–19": {"title": "What to tell the rich", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives Timothy instructions for the wealthy people in his congregation, and they are not the ones anybody expects."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not told to stop being rich, to divest, or to feel guilty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are also not congratulated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two warnings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not to **carry themselves high**, and not to **set their hope on riches**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pride, and misplaced confidence. Both are about the inside of a person rather than a bank balance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since **there is nothing certain about riches**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the uncertainty of wealth. Paul states it as a plain fact about the asset class."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What to hope in instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But on **the living God, who pours out on us everything we have and means us to enjoy it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *who gives us richly all things to enjoy* — **eis apolausin**, for enjoyment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That clause is doing a lot of work. Paul, in a passage warning about money, says God supplies things generously and intends them to be enjoyed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no asceticism in the instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four commands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tell them to **do good**, to be **rich in the kind of works that count**, to be **openhanded** and **quick to share**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rich in works — Paul uses the wealth vocabulary for something other than money in the same sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Openhanded** and **ready to share** are two different Greek words, and both are about willingness rather than amount."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The investment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In that way they will be **laying by a good foundation for the age that is coming**, so that they may **take hold of the life that lasts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a banking metaphor — storing up, laying away treasure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says it more sharply: *lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten verses earlier is the famous one: *the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not money — the love of it. And Paul distinguishes carefully: *those who **desire to be rich** fall into temptation and a snare.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses the ones who *desire to be rich* first, and then, separately, those who already are. Two different groups, two different instructions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now godliness with **contentment** is great gain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **autarkeia** — sufficiency, having enough. The same word Paul uses of himself: *I have learned, in whatever state I am, to be content.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says just before the contentment line.** *We brought nothing into this world, and it is certain we can carry nothing out.* The argument for contentment is logistical."}]}, "1 Timothy 6:9–10": {"title": "\"A root, and every kind of evil grows up out of it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most misquoted sentence in the New Testament, and the wording is precise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verse says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the love of money** is a root, and every kind of evil grows up out of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **philarguria** — literally *silver-love*. One word, and it is the subject of the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not money. The love of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A root\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek has no definite article: *a root of all kinds of evil*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not claiming this is the single origin of everything wrong in the world. He is saying it is a root, and that a great variety grows off it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Men **set on getting rich**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *those who are determined to be rich* — **boulomai**, wanting, resolving, setting the will on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is describing an aim, not a bank balance. Two verses earlier he was talking about contentment; a few verses later he addresses *those who are rich in this present age* and does not tell them to stop being rich. He tells them to be generous and ready to give. See the note on 1 Timothy 6:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rich are given instructions. The ones determined to get rich are given a warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Walk **straight into the bait and the trap**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peirasmos** (testing, enticement) and **pagis** (a snare, a bird trap)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A snare works by having something in it worth walking toward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And into **a whole crowd of cravings that make no sense and do no good**, the kind that **drag a person down into ruin and destruction**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words for the ending — **olethros** and **apoleia**. Paul doubles it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two consequences"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some have **reached out for it** and been **led away from the faith**, and have **run themselves through with sorrow upon sorrow**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Led away from the faith** — the Greek is *wandered away*, drifted off course.", "**Run themselves through** — **peripeiro**, to impale, to pierce all the way through. A violent word, and it is reflexive. They did it to themselves."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is not saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not saying money is evil, that the poor are safer, or that wealth disqualifies anybody. He says none of those things in the passage, and eight verses later he tells Timothy that God *gives us richly all things to enjoy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The object of the warning is a craving, and the injury described is self-inflicted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The context"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens on false teachers *who suppose that godliness is a means of gain* — men using the ministry to get money."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is who Paul is writing about. The warning has a specific target in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he offers instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **godliness with contentment is great gain**. For we brought nothing into this world, and it is certain we can carry nothing out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the profit word — **porismos**, gain — and applies it to contentment."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause people drop.** *The love of.* Two words, and without them the sentence says something Paul does not say anywhere in his letters."}]}, "2 Chronicles 18:1–3": {"title": "\"I am as you are\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A good king ties himself to a bad one, and the chapter opens by telling you exactly how it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Jehoshaphat had great riches and honour, **and he joined himself to Ahab by marriage**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prosperity and the alliance are in the same breath. Jehoshaphat was one of Judah's better kings — the previous chapter has him removing idols and sending teachers out with the law through every town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The marriage was almost certainly a sensible piece of statecraft: his son to Ahab's daughter, two kingdoms at peace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The visit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some years later he went down to visit Ahab in Samaria. Ahab slaughtered many sheep and cattle for him… and he urged him to march up against Ramoth Gilead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *urged* is a strong word — enticed, persuaded, worked on him. The banquet came first and the request came after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am as you are, and my people as your people; we will join you in the war.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He agrees before asking a single question. Only afterwards does he think to say *first seek the counsel of the LORD* — and when four hundred prophets say go, and one says otherwise, and Ahab admits he hates that one for never prophesying anything good, Jehoshaphat still goes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already given his word at the dinner."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it ends for him.** He survives, but a prophet meets him on the way home: *should you help the wicked and love those who hate the LORD?* And years later the marriage he made brings Ahab's daughter Athaliah into Judah's royal house, where she murders almost the entire line of David. The alliance outlived the battle by a generation."}]}, "2 Chronicles 18:28–34": {"title": "\"Jehoshaphat cried out, and the LORD helped him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two kings go into a battle they were warned about. One of them has a plan, and it involves the other one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ahab's arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will disguise myself and go into battle, but you wear your royal robes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks his ally to stay identifiable while he becomes invisible. And the reader already knows what Ahab does not: the king of Aram has ordered his chariot commanders to fight nobody *except the king of Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the disguise sends the entire enemy strike force at the man in the robes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to the man in the robes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the chariot commanders saw Jehoshaphat, they thought, \"This is the king of Israel,\" and they turned to attack him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is surrounded, in the wrong battle, wearing the wrong clothes, because he agreed to an alliance at a banquet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jehoshaphat cried out, and the LORD helped him. God drew them away from him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three short clauses. He was in a mess entirely of his own making, in a war he had been told not to enter, and when he shouted, God moved the army off him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No rebuke recorded at that moment. No conditions. He cried out and was helped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But someone drew his bow at random and struck the king of Israel between the sections of his armour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shot fired at nobody in particular, finding the one gap in a disguised man's armour. Everything Ahab had arranged to make himself safe was undone by an arrow with no aim."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chronicles reports it without comment: *at random*, and then a king propped up in his chariot until sunset, dying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Two men, one battle.** Both were where they should not have been. One called out and was pulled clear; the other had a plan and did not survive the afternoon. The chapter puts the two outcomes side by side and leaves them there."}]}, "2 Chronicles 20:10–12": {"title": "\"We do not know what to do\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A king finishes a public prayer with an admission."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three nations are marching on Judah. Jehoshaphat *set himself to seek the LORD, and proclaimed a fast throughout all Judah*, and the whole country came to Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prays standing in the temple court in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The history he recites"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Here are the men of **Ammon and Moab and Mount Seir**. **You would not let Israel invade them** when they came out of Egypt, so **Israel turned away from them and did not destroy them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ammon and Moab are Lot's descendants; Mount Seir is Esau's country."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 2 records the instruction: *do not provoke them… I have given Mount Seir to Esau as his possession.* See the note on Deuteronomy 2:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had gone round them because they were told to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Look how they repay us** — coming to drive us out of the land You gave us to inherit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not arguing that Judah deserves rescue. He is pointing out that the nations they spared are the ones invading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"O our God, **will You not judge them?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three admissions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We have no power** against this great multitude that is coming against us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We do not know what to do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But our eyes are upon You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *no power* is *no strength*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses, and the first two are concessions. He does not claim capability and does not claim a plan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a king is not supposed to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jehoshaphat is standing in front of the army, the elders, the women and the children, saying he does not know what to do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse notes who was in the room: *all Judah, **with their little ones, their wives, and their children**, stood before the LORD.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole prayer pivots on the last clause. The Hebrew is *upon You are our eyes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is offered. The prayer ends on a direction of gaze."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, in the middle of the assembly, **the Spirit of the LORD came on Jahaziel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Levite of the sons of Asaph — a musician's family — speaks. See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid or dismayed** because of this great multitude, for **the battle is not yours, but God's**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will not need to fight in this battle. Position yourselves, stand still and see the salvation of the LORD** with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence Moses gave at the sea: *stand still, and see the salvation of the LORD.* See the note on Exodus 14:10–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Appointed singers to go out in front of the army, praising *the beauty of holiness* and singing *praise the LORD, for His mercy endures forever* — the refrain of Psalm 136. See the note on Psalms 136:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they began to sing and to praise, **the LORD set ambushes** against the people… and they were defeated.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah arrives to find the field already settled, and spends three days collecting the spoil."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the prayer ends.** Not with a strategy or a claim. Two admissions — no power, and no idea — and then a sentence about where they are looking."}]}, "2 Chronicles 20:13–15": {"title": "\"The battle is not yours\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The answer arrives while everybody is still standing there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was present"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All Judah stood there before the LORD, with **their little ones, their wives, and their children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler names them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An invading army is coming and the nation has brought its infants to a prayer meeting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the word comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, **in the middle of the assembly**, the Spirit of the LORD came on **Jahaziel son of Zechariah — a Levite of the sons of Asaph**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the king, not a priest, and not a prophet by office."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The sons of Asaph** were the temple musicians — David's appointed singers. Twelve psalms carry Asaph's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who speaks is from the choir."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His genealogy runs four generations deep in the verse, which is the chronicler's way of saying he was nobody in particular."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Listen, **all Judah, and you people of Jerusalem, and you, King Jehoshaphat**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king is named last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid or dismayed** because of this great multitude.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew pair — *fear not, neither be dismayed* — is the same one Moses gave Joshua, and Joshua gave his commanders with their feet on five kings' necks. See the note on Joshua 10:22–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **the battle is not yours, but God's**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the battle is not yours but God's*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It answers the prayer's exact words. Jehoshaphat had said *we have no power against this great multitude*; the reply concedes the point and reassigns the fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are told to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses after: *\"Tomorrow go down against them… **You will not need to fight in this battle. Position yourselves, stand still and see the salvation of the LORD** with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four instructions and three of them are about not moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jehoshaphat did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jehoshaphat **bowed his head with his face to the ground**, and all Judah and the inhabitants of Jerusalem **bowed before the LORD, worshipping**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the Levites stood up *to praise the LORD God of Israel with voices loud and high*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Believe in the LORD your God, and you shall be established; believe His prophets, and you shall prosper**.\"* See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:16–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the singers were appointed *to go out before the army*, singing *praise the LORD, for His mercy endures forever*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now when they began to sing and to praise, **the LORD set ambushes** against the people of Ammon, Moab, and Mount Seir… and they were defeated.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three invading nations turned on each other. Judah arrived at the lookout and found *dead bodies fallen on the earth. No one had escaped.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they collected"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They found among them **an abundance of valuables on the dead bodies, and precious jewellery**, which they stripped off for themselves, **more than they could carry away**. And they were **three days gathering the spoil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days to pick up what fell in a battle they did not fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name of the valley"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the fourth day they assembled in **the Valley of Berachah**, for there they blessed the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Berachah** — blessing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the word came through.** A man from the temple choir, in the middle of the crowd, with his genealogy given so nobody mistakes him for somebody important — and what he says is that the fight belongs to somebody else."}]}, "2 Chronicles 20:16–19": {"title": "Singers in front of the army", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three armies are coming and Judah is told the battle is not theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moab, Ammon and Mount Seir, marching on Jehoshaphat together. His own prayer sums it up: *we have no power against this great multitude that is coming against us, nor do we know what to do — but our eyes are on You.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He admits all three: no strength, no plan, no idea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will not even need to fight this battle.** Take your positions, stand still, and watch the salvation of the LORD on your behalf.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same instruction Moses gave at the Red Sea, word for word in places: *stand still and see the salvation of the LORD.* See the note on Exodus 14:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three commands, and all three are about stopping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And yet they go out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Tomorrow, **go out to face them**, and the LORD will be with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not told to stay home. They have to march to the battlefield of a battle they will not fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jehoshaphat bowed with his face to the ground, and all Judah and the people of Jerusalem fell down before the LORD to worship Him. Then the Levites… **stood up to praise the LORD with a very loud voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole nation on the floor, and then a choir at full volume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of march"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next morning Jehoshaphat did something no commander does. *He appointed those who should sing to the LORD, and who should praise the beauty of holiness, **as they went out before the army**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Singers in front. Unarmed men, out ahead of the soldiers, in the tribe whose name means praise. See the note on Genesis 49:8–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they sang"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Praise the LORD, for His mercy endures forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six words. It is the refrain of Psalm 136, repeated twenty-six times in that psalm, and it is what Judah took into a valley with three armies in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the enemy. Nothing about the danger. One line about His mercy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **when they began to sing and to praise**, the LORD set ambushes against the people of Ammon, Moab and Mount Seir, and they were defeated.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When they began. The timing is in the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the time Judah reached the lookout point there was nothing standing. It took them three days to carry away what was left."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the valley was renamed.** *Beracah* — blessing. They assembled there on the fourth day to bless the LORD, and the name stuck. A place named for what happened in it, by people who had not raised a weapon."}]}, "2 Chronicles 20:20–21": {"title": "Singers out in front", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An army marches out to a battle it has been told it will not fight, with the choir leading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Early in the morning they rose and went out into **the wilderness of Tekoa**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tekoa is Amos's home town, south of Bethlehem, and the road out of it drops toward the Dead Sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three armies are coming up that way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Listen to me, Judah and people of Jerusalem. **Trust in the LORD your God, and you will be held firm. Trust His prophets, and you will succeed.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and the Hebrew has a play on them that does not survive translation: *ha'aminu… te'amenu* — believe, and you will be made firm. The same root, twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half is the unusual one. He tells them to believe the prophets, and the word that had come was *you will not need to fight this battle.* See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:16–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consultation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, **after talking it over with the people**, he appointed singers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew notes that he consulted. It was not a royal decree issued over their heads."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he appointed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Singers for the LORD, to praise Him **in the splendour of holiness**, **as they marched out ahead of the army**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In front. Unarmed men, at the head of the column, between the soldiers and three armies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody does this. The front rank is where the shields go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they sang"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give thanks to the LORD, for His love endures forever.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six words. The refrain of Psalm 136, repeated twenty-six times in that psalm, and it is what Judah carried into a valley."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the enemy. Nothing about the danger. One line about His **chesed** — the covenant word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it worked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **when they began to sing and to praise**, the LORD set ambushes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the next verse, and the timing is stated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days of collecting spoil, more than they could carry away — and a valley renamed **Beracah**, blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "With a king who prayed *we have no power against this great multitude that is coming against us, nor do we know what to do — **but our eyes are upon You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He admitted no strength, no plan and no idea, in public, in front of the whole assembly with their wives and children standing there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the word had been.** *You will not even need to fight this battle. Take your positions, stand still, and watch the salvation of the LORD.* The same instruction Moses gave at the sea — *stand still and see*. See the note on Exodus 14:10–14."}]}, "2 Chronicles 26:1–5": {"title": "\"As long as he sought the LORD\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-two years on the throne, and the chronicler puts the whole reign under one condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sixteen years old"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a boy when they make him king. His father Amaziah had been murdered by conspirators, and the people take the son and put him on the throne."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in his position was earned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man beside him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He set himself to seek God **in the days of Zechariah, who taught him to fear God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the prophet who wrote the book — an otherwise unknown man, named here only, and named because Uzziah's seeking is tied to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A young king with someone teaching him. The chronicler thought that worth recording."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **as long as he sought the LORD**, God gave him success.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *gave him success* is to make prosper, to cause to advance — the same verb used of Joseph in Potiphar's house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter goes on to list what that looked like: victories over the Philistines, tribute from the Ammonites, towers built in the desert, cisterns dug, vineyards in the hills, an army of over three hundred thousand, and engines of war on the walls of Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And his fame spread far, for he was marvellously helped, till he was strong.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Till he was strong\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "That clause is the pivot of the whole chapter, and it is doing two jobs. It marks how far the help went — and it marks where the trouble starts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *but when he had grown strong, his heart became proud, and it led to his ruin.* See the note on 2 Chronicles 26:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What lifted him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the east, not the west, not the desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is God who judges. He brings one down and lifts another up.* The psalm says promotion has one source, and this chapter is a fifty-two-year illustration of it — including the end. See the note on Psalms 75:6–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter's phrase lands elsewhere in Scripture.** *In the year that King Uzziah died, I saw the Lord.* Isaiah dates his vision of the throne by the death of this man — the king who went into the sanctuary he had no business in, and the prophet who saw the true throne the year that king was buried."}]}, "2 Chronicles 26:16–18": {"title": "Strength turned to pride", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence that ends a fifty-two-year reign is eleven words long."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hinge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **when he had grown strong**, his heart became proud, and it led to his ruin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strength was God's doing. The chapter has just said so twice — *God gave him success*, *he was marvellously helped, till he was strong.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the strength is what he stumbled over. The Hebrew for *led to his ruin* is to corrupt, to spoil — the same root used of the earth being corrupt before the flood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not stop being helped and then get proud. He got proud because of how far the help had gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What pride made him do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went into the temple of the LORD **to burn incense** on the altar of incense.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not idolatry. Not a foreign god. He walks into the house of the LORD to worship the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offence is not what he did. It is who he was when he did it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line he crossed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **not for you**, Uzziah, to burn incense to the LORD, but **for the priests, the sons of Aaron**, who are consecrated to do so.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two offices, and God had kept them apart the whole way through Israel's history. Saul lost the kingdom for sacrificing; the sons of Korah died for claiming the censer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Uzziah had been given a throne, an army, towers, cisterns and international fame. What he had not been given was a censer, and that is the one thing he reached for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eighty men"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Azariah the priest went in after him, and with him **eighty priests of the LORD, courageous men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler calls them courageous, and they needed to be. They walked into the sanctuary to stand in front of a king at the height of his power and tell him to leave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they told him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Leave the sanctuary, for you have been unfaithful, and **there will be no honour for you from the LORD God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had come in to add to his honour. He is told, standing there with the censer in his hand, that this will bring him none."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Only one man ever held both offices.** A king on a throne who is also a priest — Melchizedek in Genesis, and then the promise in Zechariah, *He shall be a priest upon His throne.* Uzziah reached for it and was struck. It belonged to Somebody. See the note on 2 Chronicles 26:19–21."}]}, "2 Chronicles 26:19–21": {"title": "The leprosy at the altar", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The censer is still in his hand when it happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Uzziah **flew into a rage**. The censer was in his hand… and **while he raged at the priests**, leprosy broke out on his forehead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is corrected and his answer is fury. The chronicler puts the anger and the leprosy in the same sentence, in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it appeared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Leprosy **on his forehead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The forehead is where the high priest wore the gold plate engraved *HOLINESS TO THE LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man standing where only the priest could stand, and the mark comes out on the one place the priest's holiness was displayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he was standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There before the priests, in the house of the LORD, **beside the altar of incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The location is spelled out. Not later, not at home, not on the way out. At the altar, in the middle of the act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two directions at once"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **rushed him out**, and **he himself hurried to leave**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priests were pushing and he was already going. A leper could not remain in that house and he knew it before anyone had to explain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because the LORD had struck him.* The chronicler names who did it and does not soften it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So King Uzziah was a leper **until the day he died**. He lived in a separate house, a leper, **cut off from the house of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-two years on the throne and the last of them spent in isolation, with his son running the kingdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cut off from the house of the LORD* — the one place he had forced his way into is the one place he can never enter again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the reign"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*as long as he sought the LORD, God gave him success*", "*he was marvellously helped, till he was strong*", "*when he had grown strong, his heart became proud*", "*the LORD had struck him*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sought, helped, strong, proud, struck. The chronicler lays it out in order so nobody can miss which step led to which."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Isaiah saw the year this man died.** *In the year that King Uzziah died, I saw the Lord, high and lifted up, and His train filled the temple.* A king who forced his way into the sanctuary is buried, and a prophet is shown the One whose throne it actually is — with a live coal from the altar carried to his mouth by a seraph, because no one takes it for himself."}]}, "2 Chronicles 29:12–17": {"title": "Sixteen days", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler names fourteen men and then counts the days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rising"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the Levites arose**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The response to Hezekiah's speech is one verb, and then a list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen men, and the chronicler groups them by family:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Kohathites** — Mahath and Joel", "**Merari** — Kish and Azariah", "**Gershonites** — Joah and Eden", "**Elizaphan** — Shimri and Jeuel", "**Asaph** — Zechariah and Mattaniah", "**Heman** — Jehuel and Shimei", "**Jeduthun** — Shemaiah and Uzziel"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first four groups are the working divisions of Levi. The last three — Asaph, Heman and Jeduthun — are the musicians, the three families David appointed over the song."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The singers turn out for the clearing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kohath was Korah's branch, the one that carried the holy things. See the note on Numbers 16:1–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **gathered their brothers, consecrated themselves**, and went in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order Hezekiah gave, carried out in the order he gave it: themselves, then the building."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The authority"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As **the king had commanded**, according to **the words of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. The chronicler puts the royal order and the divine word in the same clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The division of labour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **priests** went into **the inner part** of the house of the LORD to cleanse it, and they **brought out to the courtyard** all the unclean things they found.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Levites** took them and **carried them out to the Kidron Valley**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A chain. The priests go where only priests may go, bring things as far as the court, and the Levites take them the rest of the way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody does the whole distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Kidron — the ravine between the temple hill and the Mount of Olives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is where Asa burned his grandmother's idol, where Josiah burned the Asherah and beat it to dust, and where the blood from the temple sacrifices drained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus crossed it on the way to Gethsemane."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They began the consecration on **the first day of the first month**, and on **the eighth day** they came to **the porch** of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **eight more days** they consecrated the house, and on **the sixteenth day** of the first month **they finished**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight days to get from the inner house out to the porch. Eight days on the rest. Sixteen in all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the count tells you"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler could have said *they cleansed the temple*. He gives the calendar instead, twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It took over two weeks, and the first eight days produced no visible progress beyond the porch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Passover had to be moved. Chapter 30 opens with Hezekiah keeping it in the second month rather than the first — the law's provision for those who were unclean or on a journey — *for they could not keep it at the regular time, because a sufficient number of priests had not consecrated themselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cleaning ran past the feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text says *all the unclean things they found in the temple* without listing them. Ahaz's Damascus altar, the dismantled lavers, whatever had accumulated behind sixteen years of shut doors."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape of it.** A speech, a list of names, and a date at each end. The chronicler gives more space to the calendar than to the contents, and the number he lands on is sixteen."}]}, "2 Chronicles 29:3–5": {"title": "\"In the first month of the first year\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A new king's first recorded act, and it is about a set of doors."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the first month of the first year of his reign**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hezekiah is twenty-five. His father Ahaz had spent sixteen years importing Assyrian altars, cutting up the temple furniture, and finally shutting the building."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The son does not wait. The chronicler dates it to the first month."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doors"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **opened the doors of the house of the LORD and repaired them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His father had *shut up the doors of the house of the LORD*, in the previous chapter, in the same words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first act of the reign is the exact reversal of the last act of the previous one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Repaired** — the Hebrew is **chazaq**, to strengthen, to make firm. The same root as his own name, **Chizqiyahu**: *the LORD strengthens*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he calls"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He brought in **the priests and the Levites** and gathered them **in the square on the east**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The east side — the side the glory left by in Ezekiel's vision, and the side it returns by. See the note on Ezekiel 10:18–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Listen to me, Levites. **Consecrate yourselves now, and consecrate the house** of the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Themselves first, then the building. He does not send them into a defiled temple unprepared."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *consecrate* is **qadash** — to set apart, to make holy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Carry out the filth from the holy place**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **niddah** — a strong word for uncleanness, used in Leviticus for ceremonial defilement and in the prophets for what is repulsive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Carry out.** It has to be picked up and physically removed. Nothing about the instruction is symbolic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz's imports. He had seen an altar in Damascus, sent the plans to the priest, had a copy built, and moved the bronze altar aside to make room. He cut up the bases of the lavers, took down the sea from off the bronze oxen, and closed the doors."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters of demolition, and this is the clearing up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the whole operation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doors opened, staff called, staff consecrated, building consecrated, filth removed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the burnt offering, then the Levites with the instruments of David, then the song, and then the Passover in chapter 30 — kept a month late because there were not enough priests ready in time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hezekiah says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them why the doors had been shut, and does not spare his own father. See the note on 2 Chronicles 29:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict on him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did what was right in the sight of the LORD, according to all that his father David had done.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *his father Ahaz*. The chronicler skips a generation to find the comparison."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the reign starts.** Not with an army, a treaty, or a building programme. A young king opens a door his father closed and tells the Levites to carry the rubbish out."}]}, "2 Chronicles 29:6–9": {"title": "\"As you see with your own eyes\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The young king explains to the Levites why the building is in the state it is in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is talking about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **our fathers** were unfaithful and did evil in the eyes of the LORD our God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Our** fathers. He includes himself, standing there as the son of the man who did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz was his father. Hezekiah does not name him and does not exempt himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They **forsook Him**, **turned their faces away** from the dwelling of the LORD, and **turned their backs** on Him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forsook, turned away, turned the back. The chronicler stacks them, and each one is a movement of the body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did to the building"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They also **shut the doors of the porch** and **put out the lamps**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two acts, both by hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamps were the seven-branched lampstand, which was to burn *from evening until morning before the LORD* — a continual statute, forever, in Leviticus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody put it out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They **did not burn incense** or **offer burnt offerings** in the holy place to the God of Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The incense on the golden altar and the offering on the bronze one. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both daily. Both stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **the anger of the LORD came upon Judah and Jerusalem**, and He made them **an object of terror, of horror, and of scorn**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for what the nation became to the countries around it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As you see with your own eyes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not arguing from theory. He points at the room they are standing in and the state of the country outside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz's reign had brought invasion from Aram, from Israel, and finally from the Assyrians he had paid to protect him — *Tiglath-Pileser king of Assyria came to him and distressed him, and did not assist him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cost, stated plainly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is why our fathers have **fallen by the sword**, and **our sons and daughters and wives are in captivity**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the families in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not blame the Levites, the previous priesthood, or the political situation. He gives one cause and traces the whole list of effects to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he does not stop there — the next verse begins *now it is in my heart to make a covenant with the LORD God of Israel.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the diagnosis goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Straight into work. The Levites rise, consecrate themselves, and start carrying things out to the Kidron Valley the same day. See the note on 2 Chronicles 29:12–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows the cleansing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offerings, the Levites with cymbals and harps *according to the commandment of David*, the song of the LORD beginning, and the whole assembly worshipping while the burnt offering burned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *Hezekiah rejoiced, and all the people, that God had prepared the people, **for the thing came about suddenly**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he opens.** By naming what went wrong, in the first person plural, in front of the men who are about to do the work. Nobody is told the temple got that way by accident."}]}, "2 Chronicles 3:8–9": {"title": "Six hundred talents of gold", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler gives the weight of the gold in the holiest room, and then the weight of the nails."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its length ran **the full width of the house, twenty cubits, and twenty cubits across** as well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A perfect square in plan, and 1 Kings adds the third dimension — twenty high. A cube, about thirty feet each way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation describes the new Jerusalem the same way: *its length, breadth and height are equal.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gold"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He overlaid it with fine gold — **six hundred talents of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A talent is somewhere around seventy-five pounds. Six hundred of them is upward of twenty tons of gold, in one room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nails"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The nails alone weighed **fifty shekels of gold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler weighs the nails."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the smallest item in the building account, and he records it because the point of the whole passage is that nothing in that room was ordinary — down to the fastenings holding the sheets to the wall."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verses: two cherubim, made and overlaid with gold, with a combined wingspan of twenty cubits — wing tip to wall on both sides, and the inner wings meeting in the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They stood on their feet, and **they faced inward**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler notes which way they were looking, and it is the one detail 1 Kings does not give."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cherubim over the ark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Separate from these, and much smaller: the two beaten out of the mercy seat itself, facing each other with their faces toward it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There I will meet with you, and I will speak with you **from above the mercy seat, from between the two cherubim**.\"* See the note on Exodus 25:17–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where cherubim appear first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the gate of Eden, with a flaming sword, keeping the way to the tree of life. See the note on Genesis 3:20–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are guards at the beginning of the Bible, and by Exodus they are hammered into the lid of a box where God says He will meet somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Solomon said about the room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But will God indeed dwell on the earth? Behold, heaven and the heaven of heavens cannot contain You. **How much less this temple which I have built!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty tons of gold, gold nails, and the man who commissioned it says at the dedication that it will not do."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is in front of the throne in Revelation.** Not a cube of gold. *A sea of glass, like crystal* — and around the throne, four living creatures full of eyes, who *do not rest day or night, saying, \"Holy, holy, holy.\"*"}]}, "2 Corinthians 1:15–20": {"title": "Where the Amen actually goes", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the verse this app opens on, and it is usually quoted from halfway through. The half that gets left off is the reason Paul wrote it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He is defending a change of travel plans"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is genuinely what starts it. Paul said he would visit, then did not, and somebody in Corinth used it to suggest he was unreliable — a man whose yes meant nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer runs: *do I make my plans the way anyone makes them, so that with me Yes, yes and No, no come out of the same mouth in the same breath?* Then, instead of defending his diary, he moves straight to the character of the message he preached. My travel arrangements are not the point, he says. What we brought you was never *Yes and No at once*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this — the most quoted sentence in the letter — is Paul arguing from God's consistency, not his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"However many promises God has made\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is deliberately open-ended. Not *some*. Not *the important ones*. However many there are, they all find their Yes in one place: **in Him.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which locates them. The promises are not free-floating guarantees attached to circumstances, and they are not conditional on the reader's steadiness — the whole argument began with Paul's own unsteadiness. They are held in Christ, and that is why they can be described with a word as flat as *always*."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the Amen ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here is the part almost always dropped:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*and in Him the Amen, spoken through us to the glory of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amen is a Hebrew word meaning firm, settled, so be it. It is what a congregation says back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's picture has both halves: God says Yes in Christ, and the Amen comes back *through us*. The promises are answered by God, and then answered again by the people who trust them. Nobody has to generate the Yes. What is left for a person to do is agree with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a very different act from working to secure the promise, and it is why the sentence ends where it does — *to the glory of God*. The praise goes to the one who said Yes, not to the one who managed to believe it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters that this began with a broken plan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's point lands harder because of where it started. A man whose word had just been questioned uses the moment to say: whatever you think of my reliability, God's Yes does not run on the same terms."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"a second helping of grace.\"** Paul's phrase for the visit he had planned. He thought of turning up in person as another portion of grace for them. It is a small thing, but it is the vocabulary of a man who thought of grace as something you get more of, not something you use up."}]}, "2 Corinthians 1:8–11": {"title": "\"He is pulling us out still\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells them how bad it got, and then uses one verb three times in three tenses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disclosure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We would rather you knew, brothers and sisters, about **the trouble that came on us in Asia**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He chooses to tell them. He never says what it was — the riot at Ephesus, an illness, a sentence, a shipwreck; the letters do not specify."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he describes is the weight rather than the event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How far past"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The weight of it was **far past anything our strength could carry**, so far past it that **we gave up on life itself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kath' huperbolen** — beyond all measure, hyperbolically. He piles the intensifiers up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gave up on life** is *we despaired even of living*. The word is **exaporeomai** — to be utterly without a way out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the man who wrote *I can do all things through Christ who strengthens me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Deep inside ourselves we had already **heard the verdict read out: death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *we had the sentence of death in ourselves* — **apokrima**, a legal ruling, the official answer of a court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had stopped expecting to survive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But that was so we would **stop resting on ourselves** and **rest instead on the God who raises the dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the purpose and it is a transfer of weight, from one place to another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The God who raises the dead** is a deliberate choice of title. A man who has heard a death sentence in himself needs a God described that way and no other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three tenses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **pulled us out** of so deadly a danger, and **He is pulling us out still**, and it is on Him that our hope is set, **that He will go on pulling us out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past, present, future — one verb, **rhuomai**, to drag out of danger, to rescue by force."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul argues from what has happened to what will. The past deliverance is the evidence he uses for the next one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they can do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While **you work alongside us by praying for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunupourgeo** — to work together underneath, to lend a hand from below. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks the Corinthians — the church that questioned his credentials, tolerated immorality and split into factions — to hold him up by praying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose of the many"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the gift given to us through the prayers of many people** may have **many people giving thanks** for it on our behalf.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Many pray, one is delivered, many give thanks. Paul builds the circle deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the letter opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five verses earlier: *the Father of mercies and God of all comfort, who comforts us in all our tribulation, **that we may be able to comfort those who are in any trouble**, with the comfort with which we ourselves are comforted by God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for comfort appears ten times in five verses."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he did with the worst thing that happened to him.** Wrote it down and sent it to a church that had been criticising him — including the part where he gave up."}]}, "2 Corinthians 10:3–6": {"title": "The fortresses are arguments", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Military language from start to finish, and Paul names the target so there is no guessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is defending himself against critics at Corinth who said his letters were weighty but his presence was unimpressive. The whole chapter is about that quarrel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the warfare he describes is not abstract spiritual combat. It is what he is doing in the letter — dealing with what people were saying and thinking about the gospel and about him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What gets pulled down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We pull down **arguments**, and every high thing that lifts itself up against the knowledge of God.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**arguments** — reasonings, calculations, the conclusions people reach", "**every high thing** — the word for a rampart or a proud elevation, something raised up"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fortresses in this passage are made of ideas. That is what the weapons are for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Take every thought prisoner\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image completes the siege: the walls come down, and then the occupants are led out as captives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And bring it into the obedience of Christ.* The destination is not destruction — it is submission. A captured thought is brought somewhere and put to work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weapons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not the weapons of the flesh at all; through God they have power to pull down fortresses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not list them here, and the omission is deliberate; the point is what they are not. In Ephesians he does list them, and the offensive one is the word of God."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse people skip.** *We are holding ourselves ready to punish every act of disobedience, once your own obedience has been brought to the full.* Paul deals with his own house first. The order matters — the readiness to address others waits on the readiness of the people he is writing to."}]}, "2 Corinthians 12:1–4": {"title": "Visions and revelations", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is being forced into boasting by people in Corinth who boast well, and he tells the greatest thing that ever happened to him in the third person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I know a man in Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is talking about himself. He says so a few verses later — *lest I should be exalted above measure by the abundance of the revelations* — but he will not put it in the first person while he is describing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distance in the grammar is the point of the paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Fourteen years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fourteen years ago.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not mentioned it. Not in Galatians, not in Romans, not in either Thessalonian letter. Fourteen years of preaching and letter-writing and never once bringing it up, and it comes out now only because the Corinthians have pushed him into a boasting contest he did not want."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"God knows\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whether he was in the body I do not know, or out of the body I do not know; **God knows**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice. The most remarkable experience in his life, and the thing he insists on is what he is not able to tell you about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third heaven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Paradise** in the second sentence appears to be the same place named twice — Paul restates the vision rather than describing a second stage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is Persian in origin: a walled garden, a royal park. The Greek Old Testament uses it for Eden, and Jesus uses it to the thief on the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Words beyond all speaking, words that no human being is permitted to say aloud.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **arrheta** — unspoken, unspeakable — and then a second clause: it is *not permitted*. Both that they cannot be put into words and that he is not allowed to try."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the content is withheld. Whatever Paul was shown, the church did not get it here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did pass on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He kept that to himself for fourteen years. What he did hand over, he introduces in the other letter with the same claim to have got it directly: *I received from the Lord what I also delivered to you* — the bread and the cup."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of everything he was given, that is the piece he made sure everybody had. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:23–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes immediately after.** The thorn. *Lest I should be exalted above measure.* Paul places the highest experience and the standing affliction in the same paragraph, and the sentence that ties them together is *My grace is sufficient for you.*"}]}, "2 Corinthians 12:8–10": {"title": "\"My grace is enough for you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks three times and gets an answer that is not the one he asked for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Three times I begged the Lord about this, **that it might leave me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for removal. He was specific, and he was refused."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times is also what happened in Gethsemane: *He went away again a second time and prayed… so He left them, went away again, and prayed the third time.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A thorn in the flesh* — the Greek is a stake, a splinter — and *a messenger of Satan to buffet me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says where it came from: a messenger of Satan. He does not call it God's gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he says what it was doing: the Greek for *buffet* is to strike with the fist, repeatedly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What it was exactly, nobody knows. Every candidate has been proposed and the text does not say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My grace is enough for you, for My strength is made perfect in weakness.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is present tense and continuous — *My grace is sufficient*, and goes on being."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Made perfect* is **teleitai** — brought to its completion, brought to full expression. Not that weakness improves anything about the strength. That weakness is where it comes fully into view."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with the refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I will **boast all the more gladly** in my weaknesses, so that **the power of Christ may come to rest upon me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *rest upon* is **episkenoo** — to pitch a tent over, to tabernacle upon. It is the verb behind the Word *dwelling* among us in John 1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for the thorn to leave. He got a tent pitched over him instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Weaknesses, insults, hardships, persecutions, times when everything is pressed tight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five, and every one of them is something happening to him rather than something he arranged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I take pleasure in* them — and the phrase attached is *for the sake of Christ*, which is the only thing that makes the sentence anything other than strange."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **when I am weak, then I am strong**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not despite. **When.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "With the third heaven, and *words beyond all speaking* he was not permitted to repeat, fourteen years earlier and never mentioned until now. See the note on 2 Corinthians 12:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thorn is introduced in the same paragraph as the revelation, and Paul links them: *lest I should be exalted above measure.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greatest experience of his life and the standing affliction sit on the same page, and the sentence between them is *My grace is enough for you.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he had already said about it.** *We have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellence of the power may be of God and not of us.* The clay pot is not an apology in this letter. It is the argument."}]}, "2 Corinthians 2:14–17": {"title": "Led in His victory parade", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for a Roman street scene his readers had all watched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **always leads us along in His victory parade** in Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thriambeuo** — to lead in a triumph. The Roman triumph was a specific event: a victorious general processing through the city, with his army behind him and the captives walking in the column."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priests swung censers the whole way, so the route was full of smoke and scent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul puts himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the procession, behind the general."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls himself **doulos** — a slave of Christ — at the top of most of his letters, and the picture here is consistent with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Always\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in every place*, *at all times*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He writes it three verses after saying he had no rest in his spirit because he could not find Titus, and one chapter before describing the trouble in Asia where he despaired of life. See the note on 2 Corinthians 1:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fragrance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And through us **spreads the fragrance of knowing Him everywhere we go**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **osme** — the smell itself, the thing carried on the air."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not see a triumph before you smell it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What we are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **to God we are the fragrance of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The audience named is God. The scent goes up to Him first, before it does anything to anybody in the street."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek here changes to **euodia** — the same word the Greek Old Testament uses for the *sweet aroma* of an accepted sacrifice, over Noah's altar and throughout Leviticus. See the note on Genesis 8:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both crowds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Both among **those who are being saved** and among **those who are perishing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same smell reaches both. Paul does not describe two different fragrances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two effects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the one **a smell of death that ends in death**, to the other **a breath of life that opens into life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *death unto death* and *life unto life*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a triumph the same incense meant two different things depending on which part of the column you were in — the returning soldiers, or the prisoners walking to execution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **who is equal to any of this?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops mid-argument and asks it. He answers it two chapters later: *our sufficiency is from God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **not like so many, hawking the word of God for profit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kapeleuo** — to be a retailer, a huckster; it carried the sense of a trader who waters the wine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**So many** — Paul says there were plenty of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We speak **in Christ, with sincerity**, as men **sent from God** and **standing in the sight of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four descriptions, and two of them are about who is watching."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the scent comes from.** Not from the man carrying it. In a triumph the smoke belongs to the procession, and the people lining the street smell the victory of somebody who was not them."}]}, "2 Corinthians 3:12–15": {"title": "The veil at the reading", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes one detail out of Exodus 34 and builds a whole argument on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since we have **a hope like that**, we speak with **complete openness, nothing held back and nothing veiled**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parrhesia** — freedom of speech, the right to say anything in a place where you belong. It is the same word Hebrews uses about entering the Holiest. See the note on Hebrews 10:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hope in question is the one just described: a glory that stays, against a glory that was going out. See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:7–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **not like Moses**, who would **put a veil over his face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 34 records the veil. It does not say why."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul's reading of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that Israel **could not keep looking at the end of what was passing away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the end of that which was being abolished*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's account is that the veil was not there because the glory was too bright. It was there so nobody would watch it fade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shining came from forty days on the mountain, and it wore off — and Moses put the veil on afterward, between the times he went back in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The transfer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **their minds had gone hard**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eporothe** — petrified, calloused, made stony."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The veil moves from Moses' face to their understanding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **to this day, when the old covenant is read aloud, that same veil is still lying there, unlifted**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is describing a synagogue service he had sat in a thousand times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scroll is read, correctly, in Hebrew, by people who honour it — and he says something is over it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is removed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **it is in Christ that it is done away with**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the same verb as the fading — **katargeitai**, abolished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The veil and the glory it hid are removed by the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restatement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yes, **to this day, whenever Moses is read, a veil lies over their heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice in three verses, and the second time it moves from the mind to the heart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is not saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the text being defective. He has just called the ministry of death **glorious**, and quotes the Old Testament constantly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem in his description is at the reader's end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What lifts it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *nevertheless **when one turns to the Lord, the veil is taken away**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a middle voice — *when it turns*. And the turning is toward a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But we all, **with unveiled face**, beholding as in a mirror the glory of the Lord, are being transformed into the same image **from glory to glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' face faded and was covered. Paul says the readers' faces are uncovered and going the other way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the veil in the temple went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom.* See the note on Matthew 27:51–53."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two veils, both removed, and Paul's argument here is about the one nobody could see."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The detail everything rests on.** A cloth over a face in Exodus 34, and Paul's reading of why it was there — not to protect the onlookers from the brightness, but to keep them from seeing it go out."}]}, "2 Corinthians 3:16–18": {"title": "From glory to glory", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of Paul's long comparison between the two covenants, and it finishes with a mirror."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The veil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But whenever anyone **turns to the Lord, the veil is taken away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent the chapter on Moses coming down the mountain with his face shining, and putting a veil over it — and Paul says why, which Exodus does not: *so that the children of Israel could not look steadily at the end of what was passing away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The glory was fading, and the veil hid the fading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The removal is not something the reader performs. *Whenever anyone turns.* The veil comes off in the turning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Freedom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the Lord is the Spirit, and where the Spirit of the Lord is, **there is freedom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eleutheria** — liberty, the state of a free person as against a slave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mirror"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of us, **with faces unveiled, gazing at the glory of the Lord as though into a mirror**, are being changed into that same likeness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once. Looking at Him, and seeing it in a mirror."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had to veil his face because of what looking at God had done to it. Paul says every face is unveiled now, and what you see when you look is being reproduced in you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are **being changed** into that same likeness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metamorphoo** — the word behind metamorphosis, and the word Matthew and Mark use of the transfiguration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is passive, and it is present continuous. Something is being done to you, and it is not finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the change comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **all of it comes from the Spirit of the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul closes the sentence by removing the reader from the mechanism entirely. Not by effort, not by discipline, not by resolve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You look, and you are changed, and the changing is His work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From glory to glory\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from failure to glory. From one degree of it to the next."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *from glory into glory* — a movement between two of the same thing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the beholding is of.** Not yourself in the mirror. *The glory of the Lord.* The chapter after this says where that glory is located: *the light of the knowledge of the glory of God **in the face of Jesus Christ**.*"}]}, "2 Corinthians 3:4–6": {"title": "\"The letter kills\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul disqualifies himself twice before saying what he is qualified for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he denies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not that we are **equal to anything in ourselves**, as though we could claim any of it as coming from us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hikanos** — sufficient, adequate, up to the job."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing to a church that has been comparing him unfavourably with more impressive teachers, and his answer is to concede the point entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what he affirms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever we are equal to **comes from God**, who has made us equal to the work of serving a new covenant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word, three times in two verses. Not sufficient — sufficiency is from God — made sufficient."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two covenants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not of the letter but of the Spirit. For **the letter kills, and the Spirit gives life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The letter* is **gramma** — the written character, the thing engraved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What follows in the chapter identifies exactly what he means: *the ministry of death, **written and engraved on stones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only one thing in Scripture was written and engraved on stones. Ten commandments, by the finger of God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two ministries, compared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does the comparison himself over the next few verses, and he is not shy about it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the ministry of **death**… was glorious* — glorious, and he calls it that", "*the ministry of the **Spirit** is more glorious*", "*the ministry of **condemnation** had glory*", "*the ministry of **righteousness** exceeds much more in glory*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never says the first was bad. He says it had glory — Moses' face shone from it. And then he says the glory was *passing away*, and that Moses veiled his face so nobody would watch it fade. See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:7–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two Greek words he sets against each other are **katargoumenon**, being abolished, and **menon**, remaining. Temporary and permanent — and the temporary one is the one that came with a face too bright to look at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day it was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses came down the mountain with the tablets and three thousand men died at the hands of the Levites. See the note on Exodus 32:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at Pentecost, when the Spirit came, Peter preached and *about three thousand souls were added to them.* See the note on Acts 2:40–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two mountains, two givings, and the same number counted on each day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not permit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not arguing that behaviour stops mattering. He is arguing about what produces it, and Galatians answers the question directly: *walk in the Spirit, and you shall not fulfil the lust of the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fruit list ends with *against such there is no law* — nothing on it needs legislating. See the note on Galatians 5:22–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is engraved now.** *You are our epistle, written in our hearts, known and read by all men… written not with ink but by the Spirit of the living God, not on tablets of stone but on tablets of flesh, that is, of the heart.* Paul says it two verses before this one. Same finger, different surface."}]}, "2 Corinthians 3:7–11": {"title": "Glory that was fading", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul concedes everything about the first arrangement and then makes the concession his argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names he gives the two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never calls them law and grace here. He calls them ministries:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**The ministry that dealt in death**", "**The ministry that handed down condemnation**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "against"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**The ministry of the Spirit**", "**The ministry that hands us righteousness**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two administrations, each with a job description."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The medium"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Chiseled **letter by letter into stone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written by the finger of God, on the hardest available surface. See the note on Exodus 31:18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul concedes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Arrived **in such glory** that the people of Israel **could not hold their eyes on the face of Moses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not diminish it. Exodus 34 records that Moses came down not knowing his face shone, and *they were afraid to come near him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A glory that was **already fading**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a present participle — being done away with, in the process of being abolished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the shine on Moses' face was on its way out while it was still bright. He returns to it in the next paragraph: Moses put a veil on *so that the children of Israel could not look steadily at the end of what was passing away*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two comparisons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **the ministry that dealt in death** had that glory, **will the ministry of the Spirit not come with far more?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **the ministry that handed down condemnation** had glory, **how much more does the ministry that hands us righteousness overflow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are arguments from lesser to greater, and both start by granting the lesser its glory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blunt line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In fact, **what once shone with glory has no glory left at all, set beside a glory so far beyond it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *has not been glorified in this respect*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that it was never glorious. That next to the other one there is nothing left of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison Paul is reaching for is a lamp in daylight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If what was **on its way out** came with glory, much more glorious is **what stays**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **to katargoumenon** (being abolished) against **to menon** (the remaining)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Temporary and permanent, and the permanent one is the second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was written on stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ten commandments, and Paul calls their delivery a ministry of death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not attacking the law. Romans has him calling it *holy, and just, and good*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is describing what it was appointed to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, since we have such hope, **we use great boldness of speech**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *we all, with unveiled face, beholding as in a mirror the glory of the Lord, **are being transformed into the same image from glory to glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' face faded. Paul says the readers' is going the other way."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does with the argument.** He grants the old one everything — glory, divine authorship, a face too bright to look at — and then says that glory was already going out while the crowd was covering their eyes."}]}, "2 Corinthians 4:13–15": {"title": "The same spirit of faith", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul names something he shares with a psalmist who lived a thousand years before him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have **the same spirit of faith** as the one who wrote, \"**I believed, and so I spoke**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is quoting Psalm 116. And he does not say *the same faith* — he says the same **spirit** of faith, as though it were a disposition, a way of operating, that both men were working out of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Believed**, then **spoke**. The speaking comes out of the believing, not the other way round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We too believe, and so we speak.* Paul repeats the pattern about himself in the same sentence, which is the whole point of the quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalmist was in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 116 is not a victory song written afterwards. Read the line Paul is quoting in its own setting:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I believed, even when I said, \"**I am badly shaken**.\" In my alarm I said, \"Every person is a liar.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He believed and he said he was shaken, in one breath. The psalm has him with *the pains of death* around him and *the sorrows of Sheol*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the spirit of faith Paul is claiming is not one that denies the state it is in. The psalmist says both things — I am badly shaken, and I believed. See the note on Psalms 116:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul was in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same chapter, a few verses earlier: *hard pressed on every side… perplexed… persecuted… struck down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And his own list later in the letter: beaten five times with thirty-nine lashes, three times with rods, once stoned, three shipwrecks, a night and a day in the open sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the man saying *we too believe, and so we speak.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he believes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that **the One who raised up the Lord Jesus will raise us up as well**, and stand us in His presence together with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The content of the faith is a resurrection — one that has happened, and one that will."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's confidence is not about the next few weeks. It reaches past the outcome of whatever is currently pressing on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of it is **for your sake**, so that the grace reaching more and more people may make **thanksgiving overflow**, to the glory of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He turns even his own beatings outward. The chapter has already said it: *death is working in us, but life in you.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verses on either side.** Before: *we have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellence of the power may be of God and not of us.* After: *though our outward man is perishing, yet the inward man is being renewed day by day.* The spirit of faith sits between a clay pot and a body wearing out, and speaks anyway."}]}, "2 Corinthians 4:16–18": {"title": "The scales Paul is using", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*This trouble of ours is light, and it lasts a moment.* Coming from most people that sentence would be worthless. Two chapters later Paul lists what he means by trouble: beatings, imprisonments, shipwreck, a night and a day in open water, hunger, cold, and being let down a wall in a basket to escape assassination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has earned the sentence, which is the only reason it can be read."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two pairs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage runs on opposites, and they are stacked:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**outer person** wearing away — **inner person** being made new, *day after day*", "**light** and **a moment** — **weight** and **endless**", "**what can be seen** — **what cannot**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle pair is a deliberate joke in Hebrew terms. The word for glory, *kavod*, comes from a root meaning heavy. Paul sets a light affliction against a weight of glory: the very thing that feels heaviest is the lighter side of the scale."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Day after day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The renewal is not described as a single experience but as something happening on a daily basis, in parallel with the wearing away. Both processes run at once, and Paul does not pretend the first one stops."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That honesty is what makes the paragraph usable by someone in a hospital chair. Nothing here says the outer wearing away is imaginary or will reverse."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"We keep our eyes not on what can be seen.\"** The verb is about aiming attention deliberately — the word behind our *scope*. It is not denial of the visible; Paul has just catalogued it. It is a decision about which set of facts gets to set the terms."}]}, "2 Corinthians 4:3–4": {"title": "Veiled to those perishing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul picks the veil back up, and this time it is over somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 3 ended with Moses' veil, the hardened minds, and the covering that is still there when the old covenant is read aloud. See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:12–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *therefore, since we have this ministry… **we have renounced the hidden things of shame**, not walking in craftiness nor handling the word of God deceitfully.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just said his own preaching hides nothing. This paragraph answers the obvious objection — then why does anybody miss it?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **if the good news we bring is veiled at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He allows the possibility rather than denying it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the veil is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is veiled **among those who are perishing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not over the message. Over an audience."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a present participle — *those who are perishing*, in progress. Paul used the same pair in the first letter: *the message of the cross is foolishness to **those who are perishing**, but to us who **are being saved** it is the power of God.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:18–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whose minds **the god of this present age has blinded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **etuphlosen** — blinded, from the word for blind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The god of this age** — Paul's phrase, used once. John's Gospel has *the ruler of this world*, three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blinding is described as an act by an agent, and the organ is the mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being blocked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the light streaming from the good news of the glory of Christ** will not dawn on them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **augasai** — to shine upon, to dawn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is prevented is not information arriving. It is light dawning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Christ is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ, **who is the image of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eikon** — image, likeness, exact representation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it in Colossians: *He is the image of the invisible God*. And Hebrews: *the express image of His person*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says he does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *for **we do not preach ourselves, but Christ Jesus the Lord**, and ourselves your bondservants for Jesus' sake.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer to the blindness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is the God who commanded light to shine out of darkness who has **shone in our hearts** to give **the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for Genesis 1 — *let there be light* — and says the same command was issued into a heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The remedy for a blinding is a creation act, and it is described in exactly those terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vessel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **we have this treasure in earthen vessels**, that the excellence of the power may be of God and not of us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses later, and it is one of the sharpest sentences in his letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hard-pressed on every side, yet not crushed; perplexed, but not in despair; persecuted, but not forsaken; struck down, but not destroyed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four pairs, and the whole chapter ends *the things which are seen are temporary, but the things which are not seen are eternal.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 4:16–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where Paul locates the difficulty.** Not in the message and not in the preaching — he has just said he hides nothing. In an audience, and in something done to their minds — and the remedy he names is the sentence God spoke over the dark on the first day."}]}, "2 Corinthians 4:8–12": {"title": "Pressed but not crushed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four pairs, and the second half of each one is what makes the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **pressed hard from every direction, but never squeezed into a corner with no way out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thlibomenoi** (pressed, squeezed) against **stenochoroumenoi** (confined in a narrow space). Two words from the same field, and the second is denied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **at a loss, but never at a loss for hope**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a deliberate pun: **aporoumenoi** but not **exaporoumenoi** — perplexed but not utterly perplexed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had used the second word about himself one chapter earlier, about Asia: *we despaired even of living*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 1:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **hunted, but never left on our own**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Diokomenoi** — pursued, persecuted — but not **egkataleipomenoi**, abandoned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **knocked to the ground, but never finished off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kataballomenoi** — thrown down — but not **apollumenoi**, destroyed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is granted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the first half of each pair. Paul concedes the pressure, the confusion, the pursuit and the falling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list is not a denial that any of it happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The carrying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wherever we go we **carry in our body the dying of the Lord Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nekrosin** — the putting to death, the process of dying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the life of Jesus may be seen in our body too**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives the reason for the whole list, and it is display."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restatement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For we who are alive are **handed over to death again and again for Jesus' sake**, so that **the life of Jesus may be seen in this mortal flesh of ours**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice, and the second version specifies **mortal flesh** — the ordinary, dying body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **death is at work in us, and life in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **energeitai** — is operating, is at work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the strangest sentences Paul wrote. The two clauses are set against each other and he does not soften the trade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had listed elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In labours more abundant, in stripes above measure, in prisons more frequently, in deaths often. From the Jews five times I received forty stripes minus one. Three times I was beaten with rods, once I was stoned, three times I was shipwrecked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four pairs are not rhetorical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **we have this treasure in earthen vessels**, that the excellence of the power may be of God and not of us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Clay pots, and the whole point is that nobody credits the container."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore we do not lose heart. Even though our outward man is perishing, yet **the inward man is being renewed day by day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For our light affliction, which is but for a moment, is working for us **a far more exceeding and eternal weight of glory**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 4:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Light affliction** — written by the man who had just listed the stripes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for the glory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Baros** — weight. Paul weighs one against the other, and calls his own list light."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What each pair does.** Concedes the first thing and denies the second. Pressed, yes. Cornered, no. Paul never argues that the pressure is imaginary — he argues about where it stops."}]}, "2 Corinthians 5:16–17": {"title": "A whole new creation", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops assessing people by what can be seen, and gives the reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The new measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From this moment on we **do not size anyone up by what the flesh can see**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to the flesh* — by outward appearance, by natural reckoning: background, record, credentials, family, what a man looks like he is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Even about Christ"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even if we did once size up Christ that way, **we do not know Him that way any longer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had. He had assessed Jesus of Nazareth by the visible facts — a carpenter's son from a bad village, executed as a criminal — and drawn the obvious conclusion, and gone to Damascus with letters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is admitting his own worst misjudgement as the example."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So if anyone is in Christ — **a whole new creation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no verb in the Greek. It reads *if anyone in Christ, new creation* — the words land without a connective, which is why translators supply one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ktisis** is the creation word. Paul does not say improved, reformed, forgiven, or repaired. New — **kainos**, new in kind rather than merely recent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The old things **are gone**; look, all things have **become new**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are gone* is a completed action, standing done. *Have become new* likewise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is in progress and neither is future."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that reaches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old things being gone is stated without a list of exceptions — which is Paul's habit whenever he describes what happened at the cross. In Colossians the record against us was *wiped out… nailed to the cross*; in Romans, *there is therefore now no condemnation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever came down the line before — a family's history, a father's diagnosis, a past that had a claim — the sentence puts it under *the old things are gone.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole thing is located there. Not *if anyone tries hard* — *if anyone is in Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Paul's most-used phrase, some two hundred occurrences across his letters, and it is where every benefit in his theology sits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now all things are of God, who has reconciled us to Himself through Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the sentence the chapter is famous for: *He made Him who knew no sin to be sin for us, that we might become the righteousness of God in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The new creation is not a self-improvement claim. It is the result of an exchange described eleven verses later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul had been.** *A blasphemer, a persecutor, and an insolent man* — his own description, in his own letter. The man who wrote *the old things are gone* had more old things than most."}]}, "2 Corinthians 5:18–19": {"title": "\"Refusing to hold their sins against them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul states what God did, and then what He has put into our hands, and the two are the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did the reconciling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of it comes from God, who has brought us back to Himself through Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction is fixed and it is worth noticing. In ordinary usage the offended party is the one who needs to be appeased — you reconcile the person you have wronged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul never says God was reconciled to us. He says God reconciled **us** to Himself. The move came from the side that had been sinned against."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"God was in Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not God sending an agent at a distance. Paul puts Him in the person doing the work — which is why the next clause can be so absolute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bookkeeping term"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Refusing to hold their sins against them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **logizomai** — to count, to reckon, to enter in a ledger. It is the same word Paul uses in Romans 4 for righteousness being credited to Abraham."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the claim is accounting language: the sins are not being entered. Not overlooked in a vague sense — not posted to the account."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bringing **the world** back to Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a narrow group. Paul then says the message of that homecoming has been placed in us, and two verses later he is pleading with people to be reconciled — so the reconciling accomplished and the appeal to receive it are both in the passage, without contradiction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"the ministry of reconciliation\" actually is.** Paul defines it in the same breath: God has *placed in us the message of that homecoming.* The work handed over is not to reconcile anybody — that is done. It is to carry the news that it has been."}]}, "2 Corinthians 5:20–21": {"title": "The great exchange", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and it is built as a swap with nothing left over on either side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He made Him who never knew sin **to be sin** for us, so that in Him we might become **the righteousness of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *treated as a sinner*, and not *made righteous-ish*. Paul writes both sides at full strength:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| Him | us | |---|---| | never knew sin | had nothing else | | **made to be sin** | **made the righteousness of God** |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever weight the first half carries, the second carries the same. If He was genuinely made sin, then we are genuinely made righteousness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Never knew sin\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about knowing by experience. It is not that He avoided it successfully — He had no acquaintance with it at all. That is what made the transfer possible: there was nothing of His own on His side of the ledger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The location matters and Paul is exact about it. The righteousness is not manufactured in us and inspected. It is **in Him** — held in the same place the blessings of Ephesians 1 are held."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means it is as secure as He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence before it is the appeal: *we come as ambassadors on behalf of Christ… come back to God.* The exchange is the reason the appeal can be made at all. An ambassador carries terms he did not set, and these are the terms."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"become.\"** Not *have* righteousness, as a possession that could be mislaid. Paul says we **become** it — the noun, not the adjective. It is the strongest way the sentence could have been put."}]}, "2 Corinthians 6:1–2": {"title": "\"Right now is the day of rescue\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes one appeal, and it is not about behaviour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Working alongside Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunergountes** — working together with. Paul describes his own ministry as a partnership."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before ends chapter 5: *we are ambassadors for Christ, as though God were pleading through us: **be reconciled to God**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appeal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We ask you as well **not to take the grace of God and let it come to nothing in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not to receive the grace of God in vain* — **kenos**, empty, hollow, to no purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is possible to receive something and get nothing out of it. That is the danger named, and it is the only thing Paul asks in this sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *do not lose it*. Do not let it be empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**At the time of My favour I listened to you, and on the day of rescue I came to your help.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 49, from a passage about the Servant — spoken to Him, about a moment when a request would be heard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul's comment on it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look — **right now is the time of favour. Right now is the day of rescue.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes a prophecy about a future acceptable time and says it has arrived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word he uses is **kairos** — not clock time but the right moment, the season when a thing is possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right now… right now.* Paul says it twice in one breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same urgency Isaiah has at the end of the free invitation: *seek the LORD **while He may be found**, call upon Him **while He is near**.* See the note on Isaiah 55:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both passages hold two things at once — the offer costs nothing, and the moment is now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul then gives one of the great lists in his letters, of what the ministry cost:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In much patience, in tribulations, in needs, in distresses, in stripes, in imprisonments, in tumults, in labours, in sleeplessness, in fastings…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the paradoxes: *as unknown, and yet well known; as dying, and behold we live; as sorrowful, yet always rejoicing; **as poor, yet making many rich**; as having nothing, and yet possessing all things.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks at the end of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O Corinthians! **We have spoken openly to you, our heart is wide open**… Now in return for the same — I speak as to children — **you also be open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage is a man asking a difficult church to stop being closed toward him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"in vain\" would look like.** Not losing the grace. Standing in it and getting nothing from it — which is why the chapter's ask is about an open heart rather than better conduct."}]}, "2 Corinthians 8:9": {"title": "The exchange, in one sentence", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is in the middle of organising a collection for the poor in Jerusalem, and he stops to give the reason. It turns out to be the whole gospel in a line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The structure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though He was rich, for your sakes He became poor, so that you, through His poverty, might become rich.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four movements, and they cross over:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| Him | you | |---|---| | was rich | were poor | | **became poor** | **become rich** |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left in the middle. What was His went one way; what was ours went the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You know the grace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul opens with *for you know* — he is not teaching them something new. He is reminding a church that had gone cold on a promised collection what grace had already cost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **charis**, and here it is applied to an act rather than an attitude: the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ *is* the exchange he then describes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never orders them to give. The next verse says he is not commanding, and a few lines on comes the sentence about God loving a cheerful giver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Instead he sets one act beside another: Christ emptied Himself for you, so finish what you started for them. Generosity is argued from generosity received, not from obligation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the riches are.** Paul does not spell it out here, which is why the verse travels so well. Read alongside Ephesians 1 — every blessing, already given, in the heavenly places — and the wealth being described is not narrow. What He gave up was real; what came back the other way is described in the same unqualified terms."}]}, "2 Corinthians 9:6–7": {"title": "\"A cheerful giver\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is organising a collection for the poor in Jerusalem, and he refuses to apply any pressure to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The farming image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who **sows sparingly** will reap sparingly too, and the one who **sows generously** will reap generously too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *generously* is *with blessings* — literally, he who sows upon blessings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for agriculture rather than commerce. A farmer's seed is not spent; it is placed. And nobody who has ever planted expects the harvest to match what went into the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The freedom clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let each one give **as he has settled it in his own heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to purpose, to decide beforehand. It is his own decision, made privately, in advance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No amount is named anywhere in these two chapters. Paul never sets a figure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things ruled out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not with **a grudge** and not **because he is made to**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Grudgingly** — the Greek is *out of grief*, with regret."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Of necessity** — under compulsion, because someone applied pressure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are excluded, and between them they cover most of what motivates religious giving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is wanted instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is a **cheerful giver** that God loves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hilaros** — the root of the English *hilarious*. Cheerful, merry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is quoting Proverbs 22:8 in the Greek Old Testament, and it is the disposition rather than the sum that is named as loved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Paul ran the collection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the two chapters and watch what he does not do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not command. *I speak not by commandment* — chapter 8, in so many words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them the Macedonians gave *beyond their ability* out of *deep poverty*, and that they *begged us urgently* for the privilege — and then says he is only telling them this *to test the sincerity of your love*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *if there is first a willing mind, it is accepted according to what one has, and not according to what he does not have.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts the whole thing on willingness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For you know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that **though He was rich, yet for your sakes He became poor**, that you through His poverty might become rich.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is chapter 8, verse 9, and it is the only leverage Paul uses in the entire appeal. See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he promises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Further down the same chapter: *God is able to make all grace abound toward you, that you, always having all sufficiency in all things, may have an abundance for every good work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sufficiency for yourself and abundance for the work — and the Greek for *sufficiency* is **autarkeia**, contentment, having enough."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the money was for.** Not a building or a programme. A famine relief fund, carried by hand from Greek churches to Jewish believers in Jerusalem — people most of the donors would never meet."}]}, "2 Kings 2:11–12": {"title": "\"My father, my father\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He goes up mid-conversation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As they walked on, **talking together**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No ceremony, no farewell speech, no last instruction. They are walking and talking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The separation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suddenly **a chariot of fire and horses of fire swept between them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *separated the two of them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chariot's job in the sentence is to divide them. It is not recorded that Elijah rode in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Elijah went up to heaven in a whirlwind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whirlwind, not the chariot. The chapter says so at the top as well: *when the LORD was about to take up Elijah into heaven **by a whirlwind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **se'arah** — a storm wind, a tempest. It is the word for what God answers Job out of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shout"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elisha saw it and cried out, \"**My father! My father! The chariots and horsemen of Israel!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in one breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My father** — he had asked for a double portion, the inheritance of a firstborn son. The word he shouts at the moment it is granted is the word for the relation it depends on. See the note on 2 Kings 2:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The chariots and horsemen of Israel** — a military title. He is calling the departing prophet the nation's actual defence, in a kingdom that had a standing army and chariotry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the words come back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha is on his own deathbed, decades later, and King Joash comes and weeps over him and says the identical sentence: *\"My father, my father, the chariots of Israel and their horsemen!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words Elisha shouted are shouted over Elisha."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition met"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then **he saw him no more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw, which was the condition. And then it was over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **caught hold of his own clothes and tore them in two**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mourning gesture — and the last thing he does before picking up the mantle that fell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tears his own garment and puts on his father's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had asked for and what he got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked to inherit. He gets a shout, a torn coat, and a cloak on the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he walks to the Jordan alone and finds the water still parts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other man who did not die"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enoch. *And Enoch walked with God; and he was not, for God took him.* See the note on Hebrews 11:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men in the whole Old Testament, and Elijah turns up again on a mountain with Moses, talking with Jesus about His departure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elijah left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A cloak. That is the whole estate."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chariot did.** It came between them, and the man it came for went up in something else. Elisha shouts about the chariots and horsemen while watching the one he calls father go into a storm."}]}, "2 Kings 2:9–10": {"title": "A double portion", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old prophet offers his successor anything, and the answer is about inheritance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they had crossed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah had rolled up his mantle, struck the Jordan, and the water divided. They walked over on dry ground — the same river, the same way Israel came in under Joshua, going the other direction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha has followed him from Gilgal to Bethel to Jericho to the Jordan, refusing three times to be left behind: *as the LORD lives, and as your soul lives, I will not leave you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Ask me — what can I do for you before I am taken from you?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah knows he is going. So do the sons of the prophets at every stop, who kept telling Elisha so and being told *yes, I know; keep silent*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Please, let me **inherit a double portion of your spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *let a double portion of your spirit be upon me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a double portion was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not twice as much as Elijah had. It is the inheritance law from Deuteronomy: the firstborn receives *a double portion of all that he has* — two shares where the other sons get one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha is not asking to be greater than Elijah. He is asking to be treated as the heir."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word he uses is the language of a son claiming a father's estate — and when the chariot comes he shouts *my father, my father*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have asked a hard thing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you have made hard to ask*. Elijah does not refuse it and does not grant it. It is not his to give."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Still — **if you see me as I am taken from you, it will be yours. But if not, it will not**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One condition, and it is about looking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not about worthiness, service, or preparation. Whether his eyes are on the man at the moment he goes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had already done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Followed, refused three separations, and walked through a divided river. The condition attached to the request is the same thing he had been doing all day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw it. *Elisha saw it and cried out.* See the note on 2 Kings 2:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The miracles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old count is that Elijah's recorded miracles number eight and Elisha's sixteen — which readers have long noticed lines up with the request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scripture never makes the tally itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha did first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Picked up the mantle that fell, went back to the Jordan, struck the water and said *where is the LORD God of Elijah?* — and it parted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He crossed back into the country alone, and the sons of the prophets watching from Jericho said *the spirit of Elijah rests on Elisha.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asked for.** Not power and not a position. The share of a firstborn son in his father's house — and the only condition was to keep watching until the end."}]}, "2 Kings 20:1–7": {"title": "\"I have heard your prayer, I have seen your tears\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A sentence of death is delivered by a prophet and reversed before he can get out of the building."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Put your house in order, for you are going to die; you will not recover.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a warning or a diagnosis. It is delivered as the word of the LORD, with no conditions attached and no *unless*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hezekiah does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hezekiah **turned his face to the wall** and prayed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue with Isaiah or ask for a second opinion. He turns away from the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Remember, LORD, how **I have walked before You faithfully and with a whole heart**, and have done what is good in Your eyes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He pleads his record. It is the only argument he makes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it was a genuine record — Hezekiah tore down the high places, broke the sacred pillars, and smashed the bronze serpent when Israel started burning incense to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he wept — bitter, heavy tears**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he wept a great weeping*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaiah **had not even left the middle court** when the word of the LORD came to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet is still inside the palace complex. He delivered a death sentence and is sent back up the stairs with the opposite message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says He responded to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have heard your prayer, I have seen your tears.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things, and the second is not speech."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tears are named separately, as though they were their own petition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is added"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will add fifteen years to your life.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A number, given in advance. Not *you will live* — a figure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And two more things: *on the third day you will go up to the house of the LORD*, and the city delivered from Assyria."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For My own sake and for the sake of David My servant.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reasons given are not Hezekiah's record, which is what he had argued from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The figs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Bring a lump of figs.**\" They brought it and laid it on the boil, and Hezekiah recovered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A poultice. Isaiah has just announced a miraculous reversal and then calls for a fruit compress."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account puts the promise and the poultice in consecutive sentences without any apparent tension between them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hezekiah did with the fifteen years.** Manasseh, the worst king Judah ever had, was born three years into them. And Hezekiah showed the Babylonian envoys everything in his treasury, and was told that all of it would one day be carried to Babylon — to which he answered *the word of the LORD is good*, because *there will be peace and truth in my days.*"}]}, "2 Kings 20:8–11": {"title": "The shadow that went back", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A dying king asks for proof and is offered a choice about which direction time should go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is the sign** that the LORD will heal me?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already been told he would live and given a number of years. He asks for a sign anyway, and nobody in the passage rebukes him for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah simply provides one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Shall the shadow go forward ten steps, or back ten steps?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two options, and the prophet lets the king pick."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hezekiah chooses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It's easy for the shadow to go forward ten steps** — no, let it **go back** ten steps.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rules out the easy one himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shadow moves forward all day without anybody's help. Forward is what shadows do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for the one that cannot happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Isaiah the prophet **called on the LORD**, and the LORD **drew the shadow back the ten steps** it had already gone down on the stairway of Ahaz.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the steps of Ahaz* — a stairway built by his father, evidently functioning as a sundial, with the shadow marking the hours down the stairs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz was the king who shut the temple doors and sacrificed his own son. His stairway is where the sign is given to the son who reopened them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ten steps"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten of the marks the shadow had already covered. Time already spent, given back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chronicler notes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "2 Chronicles adds one line about the whole episode: *God withdrew from him, in order to test him, that He might know all that was in his heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And 2 Chronicles also records who was watching — *the envoys of the princes of Babylon… sent to inquire about **the wonder that was done in the land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Babylonians came to ask about the shadow. Astronomy was their profession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other long day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sun stood still, and the moon stopped… **there has never been a day like it**, before or since — a day when the LORD listened to the voice of a man.* See the note on Joshua 10:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua's day was lengthened in the middle of a battle. Hezekiah's was reversed at a sickbed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah's own account adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 38 records Hezekiah's writing after his recovery — a psalm, in the first person:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I said, \"In the prime of my life I shall go to the gates of Sheol\"… **like a weaver I have cut off my life**… O LORD, by these things men live… **You have lovingly delivered my soul from the pit of corruption**, for You have cast all my sins behind Your back.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing he says he will do with the years.** *The living, the living man, he shall praise You, as I do this day; the father shall make known Your truth to the children.* Hezekiah's stated plan for the added time is telling his children."}]}, "2 Kings 4:1–7": {"title": "The widow's oil", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman about to lose her children asks for help, and is asked a question back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she has lost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your servant **my husband is dead** — and you know he honoured the LORD. But now **a creditor has come to take my two boys as his slaves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the law a debtor's children could be taken in payment until the jubilee. It was legal. She has no case to make."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her husband was one of the company of prophets, and she says he feared the LORD. Being godly had not exempted him from dying in debt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can I help you? Tell me — what do you have in the house?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha does not produce anything. He asks what is already there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your servant has **nothing there at all** — **except a small jar of oil**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She says nothing first, and then corrects herself. The oil is so small it did not count as having something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a flask of anointing oil — the amount you would use once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instructions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Ask **all your neighbours for empty jars** — and **don't ask for just a few**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing she has to do is go and borrow emptiness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a public act. Every neighbour is going to know something is happening, and she has to explain herself at every door — or not explain, and look stranger still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't ask for just a few.**\"* The only limit in the story is set here, by her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Behind a closed door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then go inside, **shut the door behind you and your sons**, and start pouring.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gathering is public and the pouring is private. Nobody watches the miracle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha is not in the room either. He does not come, does not pray over it, and does not touch the oil."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What stops it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the jars were full, she said to her son, \"Bring me another.\" \"**There isn't one left**,\" he told her. **And then the oil stopped.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when the jar ran dry. When the containers ran out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination puts the point in the last line: *grace kept flowing until every borrowed jar was full.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go, **sell the oil and pay your debt**. **You and your sons can live on what is left**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things, in that order. The debt cleared, and an income after it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell her to give it away or bring it to him. She keeps the surplus and lives on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she started with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dead husband, two boys about to be taken, no money, and one flask of oil she had not thought worth mentioning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other widow with a jar.** At Zarephath, in Elijah's day — a handful of flour and a little oil, and she was gathering sticks to make a last meal before she and her son died. *The bin of flour was not used up, nor did the jar of oil run dry.* The prophets keep meeting women at the end of their supplies."}]}, "2 Kings 4:18–25": {"title": "\"It's all right\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A child dies at noon and his mother says one sentence to everyone she meets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The field"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The boy grew. One day he **went out to his father among the harvesters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The son she had been promised and had not asked for. See the note on 2 Kings 4:8–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The collapse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suddenly he cried, \"**My head! My head!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, repeated. Sunstroke is the usual reading; the text does not say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Carry him to his mother**,\" the father told a servant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not go himself. The harvest is on and he sends the boy back with a servant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the account suggests he understands how serious it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The boy **sat on her lap until noon — and then he died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew gives the hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did with the body"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **carried him up and laid him on the bed of the man of God**, **shut the door on him**, and **went out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three actions, and none of them is what a mother would ordinarily do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not call the household, does not wail, and does not send for the burial party."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She puts him on the prophet's bed — in the room she built, on the furniture she chose — and closes the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says to her husband"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Send me one of the servants and a donkey**, so I can hurry to the man of God and come back.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not tell him the boy is dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why go to him today?** It's **not the New Moon or the Sabbath**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which tells you the local custom: you visited a prophet on holy days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is puzzled by the schedule, not alarmed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It's all right**,\" she said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shalom** — peace, it is well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She says it three times in the chapter. Here to her husband, then to Gehazi sent to meet her on the road — *is it well with you? Is it well with your husband? Is it well with the child?* and she answers **shalom** — and only when she reaches Elisha does anything else come out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She will not say it to anyone but him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ride"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **saddled the donkey** and told her servant, \"**Lead on. Don't slow down for me unless I tell you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *do not slacken the pace for me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shunem to Carmel is about fifteen miles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens at the mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha sees her coming and sends Gehazi. She goes past him, comes to Elisha and *caught him by the feet*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gehazi tries to push her away, and Elisha says: *let her alone, for **her soul is in deep distress**, and **the LORD has hidden it from me, and has not told me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not know. The prophet says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says at last"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Did I ask a son of my lord? Did I not say, Do not deceive me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only complaint she makes in the chapter, and it is the sentence she had said when the promise was given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gehazi is sent ahead with the staff and nothing happens. Elisha comes himself, shuts the door on the two of them, prays, and stretches himself on the child. See the note on 2 Kings 4:32–37."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the child sneezed seven times, and the child opened his eyes.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she says to everyone on the road.** *Shalom.* A dead boy is on a bed behind a shut door and she tells her husband and the prophet's servant that it is well — and says nothing else until she has hold of Elisha's feet."}]}, "2 Kings 4:32–37": {"title": "A son raised", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elisha raises the Shunammite's boy, and every step of it is worth counting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it took"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["he **shut the door** on the two of them", "he **prayed** to the LORD", "he **climbed onto the bed and lay over the boy** — mouth to mouth, eyes to eyes, hands to hands", "the child's body **grew warm**", "he **got up and walked back and forth** across the room", "he **bent over him once more**", "the boy **sneezed seven times** and opened his eyes"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven movements. A closed door, a prayer, physical contact along the whole length of the body, a pause, and a second attempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet has to give himself to it — literally lie on the boy and cover him, limb for limb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Take your son\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elisha said, \"**Take your son**.\" She came in, fell at his feet, and bowed to the ground. Then she took her son and went out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has to come in and collect him. And she goes to the floor first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shunem, in the Jezreel valley. The name means double resting place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A few miles away over the ridge of Mount Moreh — whose name means teacher — is Nain, which means pleasant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two towns in the same valley, either side of one hill, and a boy raised from the dead in each of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Nain there is no closed door, no prayer, no stretching over the body, no pacing, no second attempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Young man, I say to you, get up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, spoken in the open air, in front of two crowds. He does not shut anybody out and He does not ask God for anything — He addresses the boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end: *Jesus **gave him back to his mother**.* Elisha said *take your son.* Jesus carried him over. See the note on Luke 7:11–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had asked for a double portion of Elijah's spirit and had received it. He is the greatest of the prophets by the count of his miracles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this is what the best of them had to do to raise one boy."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the woman had said on the way.** Her son had died on her lap that morning, and she saddled a donkey, rode to Carmel, and when Gehazi asked if all was well she answered *it is well.* She said it to Gehazi and she said it to her husband before she left. She had not told anybody the boy was dead."}]}, "2 Kings 4:8–10": {"title": "The room on the roof", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman notices something about a passing prophet and builds him a room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The town"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One day Elisha was passing through **Shunem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A village in the Jezreel valley, on the route between Carmel and the Jordan. Elisha walked it regularly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The woman"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where **a well-to-do woman** lived.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ishah gedolah** — a great woman. It can mean wealthy, prominent, or of standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is never named. Through four chapters she is *the Shunammite*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **urged him to stay for a meal**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *she constrained him* — pressed him to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And after that, **whenever he came by, he would stop there to eat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One meal became a habit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her reasoning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She said to her husband, \"**I know that this man who often passes our way is a holy man of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She works it out herself. Nobody tells her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The husband is not the one who notices, and he does not object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let's **make a small room on the roof** for him and put **a bed in it, a table, a chair, and a lamp**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four items. The Hebrew lists them one by one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A bed to sleep, a table to work, a chair to sit, and a lamp to see. It is the minimum furniture for a life, and she thought through each piece."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **whenever he comes to us, he'll have a place to stay**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for an occasion. For every time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha sends Gehazi to ask what can be done for her — *would you like to be spoken for to the king or to the commander of the army?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her answer: *I dwell among my own people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She wants nothing. It is Gehazi who observes that she has no son and her husband is old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"About this time next year you shall embrace a son.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And her reply: *\"No, my lord. **Man of God, do not lie to your maidservant**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had not asked and did not want the hope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy grows, goes out to the harvesters, cries *my head, my head*, and dies on her lap at noon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She carries him up to the roof room, lays him on the prophet's bed, shuts the door, and rides to Carmel. See the note on 2 Kings 4:18–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she puts him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the bed she built."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says on the road"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To her husband: *it is well.* To Gehazi, sent to meet her: *it is well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only when she reaches Elisha and takes hold of his feet does she say anything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the story ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he called Gehazi and said, \"Call this Shunammite woman.\" So he called her. And when she came in to him, he said, \"**Pick up your son**.\" So she went in, fell at his feet, bowed to the ground, **picked up her son, and went out**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last we hear of her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 8: Elisha warns her of a seven-year famine, she leaves for Philistine country, and comes back to petition the king for her house and land — arriving at the exact moment Gehazi is telling the king the story of her son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She gets everything back, *and all the proceeds of the field from the day that she left the land until now*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she built.** Four pieces of furniture in a room on a roof, for a man who came through occasionally — and it is the bed she lays her dead son on years later."}]}, "2 Kings 5:14": {"title": "Flesh like a little child's", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A general with an army behind him ends up standing in a muddy river doing something undignified seven times, and comes out with the skin of a child."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naaman arrived with horses, chariots, ten talents of silver, six thousand shekels of gold and ten changes of clothes — a fortune, and a letter from his king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha did not even come outside. He sent a servant with a message: go and wash in the Jordan seven times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naaman's fury is recorded in detail. He had expected a ceremony. He had better rivers at home — and he names them, Abana and Pharpar, clean water against Jordan's brown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What actually healed him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination puts it plainly: *it was not his greatness that healed him, nor his gold, nor the rivers he preferred. It was the goodness of God, freely given, and received at last like a child.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he brought was useless. The one thing required was to go down into the water he thought beneath him, the full seven times, and come up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Clean and fresh as the flesh of a little child\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is exactly that — his flesh returned like the flesh of a small boy. Not merely cured. Made new."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is a nice detail in who talked him into it. His own servants — the people with no standing at all — reason with him: if the prophet had asked something great, you would have done it. Why not this?"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The gift he tried to give afterwards.** Naaman goes back and offers the whole treasure, and Elisha refuses every coin of it. The healing was free at both ends: nothing bought it, and nothing was accepted for it. Gehazi chasing after the money in the next paragraph, and catching the leprosy, is the chapter's own comment on that."}]}, "2 Kings 6:15": {"title": "\"What shall we do?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A servant goes out early and finds the town surrounded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What led to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king of Aram had been raiding Israel, and every ambush failed because Elisha kept telling Israel's king where the camps were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramean king assumed a traitor, and was told: *Elisha, the prophet who is in Israel, **tells the king of Israel the words that you speak in your bedroom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His response was to send an army after one man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the **servant of the man of God rose early** the next morning and went out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes out first, before Elisha."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There it was — **an army with horses and chariots ringed all around the city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the city was surrounded*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before says the Aramean king *sent horses and chariots and a great army* by night, and they came and surrounded Dothan while everyone slept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about his report is wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Oh, my master**,\" the servant said, \"**what shall we do?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *alas, my master*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men in a town with an army round it, and one of them is a prophet with no army at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *\"**Do not fear, for those who are with us are more than those who are with them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is, on the visible evidence, absurd. There are two of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Elisha prayed, and said, \"**LORD, I pray, open his eyes that he may see**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not pray for the army to leave, or for an escape, or for the servant's courage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prays about eyes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD opened the eyes of the young man, and he saw. And behold, **the mountain was full of horses and chariots of fire all around Elisha**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been there the whole time. Nothing was added; something was shown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha prays again, and this time for the opposite: *strike this people, I pray, with blindness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he walks into the middle of the blinded army and says *this is not the way, nor is this the city; follow me* — and leads them into Samaria."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king of Israel asks *shall I kill them?* twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You shall not kill them… **set food and water before them**, that they may eat and drink and go to their master.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he prepared a great feast for them; and after they ate and drank, he sent them away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An army that came to arrest him is fed and released."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the bands of Syrian raiders **came no more into the land of Israel**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chariots turn up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha's own master had gone up in one: *suddenly a chariot of fire and horses of fire swept between them*. See the note on 2 Kings 2:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what Elisha shouted then — *the chariots and horsemen of Israel!* — is what the servant is now shown a hillside of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servant was not told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To be braver. The prophet's whole response to the panic is one prayer about sight."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What changed on the hill.** Not the odds. The Aramean army was there and stayed there. One man saw what else was on the ground, and the prayer that got him there was about eyes."}]}, "2 Kings 6:16": {"title": "\"More than those with them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, spoken to a terrified servant who has just done the arithmetic correctly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servant saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: the king of Syria has sent *horses and chariots and a great army* to Dothan by night, *and they surrounded the city.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servant gets up early, goes out, and there it is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Alas, my master! **What shall we do?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His count is right. Two men, one town, and an army round it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't be afraid.** Those who are with us are **more than those who are with them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha does not dispute the servant's figures. He adds a column the servant did not know about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha prays"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"LORD, I pray, **open his eyes that he may see**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *remove the army*. Not *get us out*. Open his eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet's request is about the servant's perception, and nothing about the situation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was there the whole time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD opened the eyes of the young man, and he saw. And behold, **the mountain was full of horses and chariots of fire all around Elisha**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It had been there before the prayer. The praying changed the seeing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha was not asking for reinforcements. He could already see them, which is why he was calm at dawn with an army outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha prays a second time — *strike this people with blindness* — and then walks the entire Syrian army into Samaria and asks the king of Israel to feed them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Set food and water before them, that they may eat and drink and go to their master.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he prepared a great feast for them; and after they ate and drank, he sent them away.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the bands of Syrian raiders **came no more** into the land of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The campaign ends not with a slaughter but with a meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the army was there at all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha had been telling the king of Israel where the Syrian ambushes were, repeatedly — *the prophet who is in Israel tells the king of Israel the words that you speak in your bedroom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The army was sent to capture one man who knew too much."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other prayer in the chapter.** A borrowed axe head falls into the Jordan and a student cries out *alas, master, it was borrowed.* Elisha cuts a stick, throws it in, and the iron floats. Chariots of fire and a lost tool in the same chapter, and the book gives both the same attention."}]}, "2 Peter 1:1–2": {"title": "\"Worth every bit as much as ours\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter opens his last letter by levelling the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Simon Peter here.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both names. The one he was born with and the one he was given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the old form **Symeon** in the Greek, which is how James addresses him at the council in Acts 15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **belong to Jesus Christ**, and He is the one who **sent me out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **doulos** — a slave — and **apostolos**, one sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Slave first, apostle second. He puts the ownership before the office."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You who were **handed the same trust we were**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lachousin** — to obtain by lot. It carries the sense of something allotted, not achieved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **in your hands it is worth every bit as much as it is in ours**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **isotimon** — of equal value, of the same honour. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a commercial and civic word, used of foreigners granted the same rights as citizens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter — an eyewitness, one of the three on the mountain, the man who preached at Pentecost — tells readers he has never met that their faith is worth exactly what his is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have it **because God is righteous, and because Jesus Christ our Savior is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by the righteousness of our God and Saviour Jesus Christ*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The equality is grounded in God's fairness, not in their attainment. It would be unjust for the standing to differ, because it does not come from the holder in either case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May **grace and peace keep arriving, more of it each time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plethuntheie** — be multiplied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same greeting in his first letter. Grace and peace, in that order, which is the order every New Testament letter uses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As you **come to know God, and Jesus our Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epignosis** — full knowledge. It is the theme word of the whole letter; Peter uses it four times in the first chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The multiplying is attached to the knowing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His divine power has given to us **all things that pertain to life and godliness**, through the knowledge of Him who called us by glory and virtue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the list of seven things to add. See the note on 2 Peter 1:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The supply is stated before anything is asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that shortly **I must put off my tent**, just as our Lord Jesus Christ showed me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus had told him at the lakeside how he would die."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is a man setting down what he wants remembered."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the first sentence establishes.** That the reader's faith is not a lesser edition of the writer's. Peter says it in the opening line, before any instruction, and grounds it in God's righteousness rather than theirs."}]}, "2 Peter 1:19": {"title": "A lamp in a pitch-black room", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just described the mountain — the voice, the brightness, the *this is My beloved Son*. He was standing there. And then he says something surprising about what is more reliable than that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Firmer now than ever\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the word the prophets spoke **stands firmer now than ever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a comparative: *more sure*, more confirmed. Peter is comparing the prophetic word to the transfiguration he has just recounted as an eyewitness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not doubt what he saw. He says the written word is the steadier of the two things to stand on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Picture a lamp lit in a pitch-black room — that is what it is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word behind *pitch-black* is **auchmēros**: literally dry, parched, squalid, and so murky. It appears nowhere else in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp is not lighting a pleasant evening. It is the one light in a dry dark place, and the passage takes for granted that the place is dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Until"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You watch it until the morning breaks through and the star that carries the day comes up **inside you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things happen at the end of the sentence, and they are different in kind:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the day dawns** — outside, the whole sky", "**the morning star rises in your hearts** — inside, personal"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Phōsphoros* is the light-bearer, the planet that rises just before sunrise. In Revelation, Jesus calls Himself *the bright morning star*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp is for the meantime. The star is the thing the lamp was pointing at all along."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that follows.** Peter's next sentence is his reason: no prophecy of Scripture came about by the prophet's own working out of it. The lamp is trustworthy in the dark because of where it was lit."}]}, "2 Peter 1:5–7": {"title": "Set one thing next to another", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven items in a chain, and the chain is fastened at one end before Peter says anything about adding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On top of **all that**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *for this very reason*. Everything in the list is a response to what has just been stated:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His divine power has given to us **all things that pertain to life and godliness**… exceedingly great and precious promises, that through these you may be **partakers of the divine nature**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The supply is announced before the work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set goodness down beside it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epichoregeo** — to supply, to furnish. It comes from the **choregos**, the wealthy citizen in Athens who paid for the chorus in a play: costumes, training, the whole production, out of his own pocket."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word carries lavishness, and Peter has just used the same root of God in verse 3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is already there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have **trust already**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is not on the list of things to add. It is the thing everything else is set beside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Goodness** — **arete**, moral excellence, a thing performing what it is for", "**Understanding** — **gnosis**, knowledge", "**A firm hand on your own appetites** — **enkrateia**, self-control", "**Staying power to keep going when it costs you** — **hupomone**, remaining under", "**A life bent toward God** — **eusebeia**, godliness", "**Real warmth toward the family** — **philadelphia**, brotherly affection", "**Love** — **agape**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each item is set beside the one before it. The Greek preposition is *in* — supply in your faith, virtue; in virtue, knowledge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a ladder of stages, with one finished before the next begins. Peter is describing things laid alongside each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it starts and ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith at one end, love at the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's ordering is the same: *faith working through love*, and *the greatest of these is love*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The effort"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not sit still. **Put everything you have into this**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **spouden pasan pareisenenkantes** — bringing in all diligence alongside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter asks for real exertion, and he asks for it on the strength of a supply he has already said was given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words for family and love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Philadelphia** and **agape** are listed separately and in that order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warmth toward the household comes second-to-last, and love is set beside even that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the list is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next two verses give the reason, and it is about sight. See the note on 2 Peter 1:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, in what he says is his last letter — *knowing that shortly I must put off my tent, just as our Lord Jesus Christ showed me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For if you do these things **you will never stumble**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order Peter uses.** God's supply first, stated in the fullest terms available. Then *for this very reason*, and then a list of things to bring alongside a faith the reader is told they already have."}]}, "2 Peter 1:8–9": {"title": "What grows and what goes blind", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two sentences, and the second one traces a problem back to something forgotten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If those things are in you, and **there is more of them all the time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *being in you and abounding* — both present participles, both continuous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is not describing a completed set. He is describing something increasing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two negatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will keep you from **standing around useless** and from **coming up empty**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Useless** — **argos**, from *a-* and *ergon*: without work, idle. It is the word for the labourers standing in the marketplace all day in the parable.", "**Coming up empty** — **akarpos**, unfruitful."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter states the benefit as the absence of two failures."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the fruitfulness is located"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the knowing of our Lord Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epignosis** — full knowledge, knowledge that has landed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in ministry, in achievement, or in general. In the knowing of a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **whoever is missing them cannot see**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tuphlos** — blind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is **squinting at what sits right in front of his face and nothing past it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **muopazon** — the root of *myopic*. Short-sighted, screwing up the eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says blind and then immediately narrows it: not sightless, near-sighted. He can see what is close."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he has **forgotten that his old sins were washed off of him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lethen labon** — having received forgetfulness. The root behind *Lethe*, the river of forgetting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the cause Peter lands on. Not weak will, not bad teaching, not lack of effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was forgotten"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That his old sins were washed off of him.* The Greek is **katharismos** — cleansing, purification. It is the word used of ritual purification, and of what Hezekiah's Levites did to the temple. See the note on 2 Chronicles 29:12–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A past event, done to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just given a list of seven things to add. See the note on 2 Peter 1:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he explains what their absence means, and the explanation runs backward — the missing list is a symptom, and the thing underneath it is a forgotten cleansing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, be even more diligent to **make your call and election sure**, for if you do these things you will never stumble.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *I will not be negligent to **remind you always of these things**, though you know them and are established in the present truth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's stated method against the forgetting is repetition. He says it three times in four verses: *remind you*, *stir you up by reminding you*, *that you may be mindful*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is in a hurry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that shortly I must put off my tent, just as our Lord Jesus Christ showed me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing what he wants remembered after he is gone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the near-sighted man can see.** What is in front of him. The thing Peter says he has lost sight of is behind him — a washing that already happened."}]}, "2 Peter 2:4–6": {"title": "\"He did not let the ancient world off either\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter builds an argument out of three historical cases, and every one of them is treated as fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the angels who sinned** — thrown down, chained, kept for judgement", "**the ancient world** — the flood, with eight kept alive", "**Sodom and Gomorrah** — burned to ash"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, in chronological order, and in each one the same two things happen: judgement falls, and somebody is preserved through it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He threw them down into **the pit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tartaroo** — a verb built from *Tartarus*, and it appears nowhere else in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek thought Tartarus was the deepest place, below Hades, where the Titans were confined. Peter borrows the word and uses it once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He handed them over to **chains and to the dark**, and there they are being kept until the judgement.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jude says the same in his own words: *angels who would not stay in the place that was theirs; they walked out of their own home. He has them now in chains that will never break.* See the note on Jude 1:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two letters, independently, describing angels held in confinement for something specific, and both put it next to Sodom and the flood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eight"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He kept **eight alive, Noah among them**, a man who never stopped saying what was right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *a preacher of righteousness* — a herald."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the only place in Scripture we are told Noah preached. Genesis records the building and not a word of the speaking, and Peter supplies it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and twenty years of hammering, and a man telling anyone who came by what it was for. Eight got in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ash"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He burned Sodom and Gomorrah down **until there was nothing left but ash**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is to reduce to ashes — **tephroo**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the purpose: *He made of them **the specimen** of what is coming.* The word is **hupodeigma**, a pattern, an exhibit, something set out to be looked at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is answering scoffers who say *where is the promise of His coming? Everything goes on as it always has.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His reply is a list of three times it did not. And the whole passage is one long conditional, completed in verse 9: *then the Lord knows how to deliver the godly out of temptations, and to reserve the unjust under punishment for the day of judgement.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three cases are evidence for a rescue, not only for a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same comparison turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus used it Himself: *as it was in the days of Noah… and likewise as it was in the days of Lot.* Same two examples, same order."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word Peter uses for Lot.** *Righteous Lot, who was oppressed by the filthy conduct of the wicked… **tormented his righteous soul** from day to day.* Genesis gives Lot a poor account of himself, and Peter calls him righteous three times in two verses."}]}, "2 Peter 3:18": {"title": "Keep growing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last sentence Peter wrote."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep growing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **auxanete** — grow, increase. It is the ordinary word for a plant or a child."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present imperative: keep at it, continuously."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two directions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Deeper into **grace**, and deeper into **knowing Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ*, with one article covering both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not say grow in holiness, in discipline or in service. Two things, and both are about what is received."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gnosis** — and Peter has used the fuller form **epignosis** four times in the letter's first chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the theme of the whole letter: *grace and peace be multiplied to you **in the knowledge of God***. See the note on 2 Peter 1:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"instead\" answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *beware lest you also **fall from your own steadfastness**, being led away with the error of the wicked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter names the danger and then, instead of a defensive instruction, gives a growth one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The counter to being carried off is not to brace. It is to grow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doxology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The glory is His, **now and on into the day that has no end**. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *both now and to the day of eternity* — a phrase that appears once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is unusual about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doxologies in the New Testament are usually addressed to God the Father. This one is to Christ, and Peter closes his last letter with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scoffers asking *where is the promise of His coming?*, the answer that *one day is with the Lord as a thousand years*, and: *the Lord is not slack concerning His promise… but is longsuffering toward us, **not willing that any should perish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the day of the Lord coming as a thief, and the elements melting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the question: *since all these things will be dissolved, **what manner of persons ought you to be**?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about Paul two verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As also **our beloved brother Paul**, according to the wisdom given to him, has written to you… in which are **some things hard to understand**, which untaught and unstable people twist to their own destruction, **as they do also the rest of the Scriptures**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter calls Paul's letters Scripture, admits parts are difficult, and says people distort them. All in one sentence, and it is the only place in the New Testament where one apostle comments on another's writing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he was writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that shortly **I must put off my tent**, just as our Lord Jesus Christ showed me. Moreover I will be careful to ensure that **you always have a reminder of these things after my decease**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is a man setting down what he wants left behind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**His last instruction.** Not to hold the line. To keep growing — and the two things named are grace and knowing a person."}]}, "2 Peter 3:3–4": {"title": "\"Where is the promise of His coming?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter tells his readers what they will hear, and then gives the argument in the mockers' own words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prediction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the last days scoffers will come, scoffing and following their own evil desires.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not treat the objection as a surprise to be defended against. He treats its arrival as evidence — the mocking is itself one of the things that was said would happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is the promise of His coming? Ever since our ancestors died, everything goes on exactly as it has since the beginning of creation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a decent argument, and it is still made. Two parts:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the delay** — the people who first heard the promise have died", "**the uniformity** — nothing ever changes; the world runs on as it always has"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is disputing the facts. Peter's answer is that the second point is false as history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he answers it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He points at the flood. The world *did* change once, catastrophically, and the people who assumed continuity were wrong then too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the two answers that follow: God's reckoning of time is not ours, and the delay is deliberate patience. See the note on 2 Peter 3:9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says they are doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They deliberately forget.* The Greek is about it escaping their notice on purpose — a chosen blindness rather than an oversight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Peter's diagnosis of the argument. Not that the scoffers lack information. That the information is available and being stepped around."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The scale he sets it in.** The chapter runs from creation, through the flood, to the elements melting and a new heavens and new earth. The mockers' claim that everything continues unchanged is answered by a history in which it has already not, and a future in which it will not again."}]}, "2 Peter 3:8": {"title": "\"A single day is the same size as a thousand years\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse about how time looks from the other side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The framing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Dear friends, **do not let this one thing slip past you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter flags it. He is about to say something he expects to be skated over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the Lord, **a single day is the same size as a thousand years, and a thousand years is no longer than a day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both directions, and that is the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read only the first half and it says God is slow. Read both and it says the comparison does not apply — a day can hold a millennium and a millennium can pass like a day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not on the scale. That is the claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 90, Moses' psalm: *a thousand years in Your sight are like yesterday when it is past, and like a watch in the night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oldest psalm in the book, written by a man leading a nation through a forty-year detour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter is answering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scoffers, three verses earlier: *\"**Where is the promise of His coming?** For since the fathers fell asleep, all things continue as they were from the beginning of creation.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their argument is from elapsed time. Nothing has happened, therefore nothing will."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's reply is that they are measuring with the wrong instrument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the reason for the delay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verse: *the Lord is not slack concerning His promise, as some count slackness, **but is longsuffering toward us, not willing that any should perish** but that all should come to repentance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The waiting is not indifference or inability. It is named as patience with a purpose, and the purpose is people. See the note on 2 Peter 3:9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter has already used as evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By the word of God the heavens were of old, and the earth standing out of water and in the water, by which the world that then existed **perished, being flooded with water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scoffers say nothing ever changes. Peter points at the flood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is building to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The day of the Lord will come as a thief in the night… **but we, according to His promise, look for new heavens and a new earth in which righteousness dwells**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And his conclusion is practical: *what manner of persons ought you to be in holy conduct… looking for and hastening the coming of the day of God.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse is not for.** Peter is not offering a formula for dating anything — he opens the chapter saying the day comes like a thief, unannounced. The verse removes a calculation rather than supplying one."}]}, "2 Peter 3:9": {"title": "What looks like slowness", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is answering scoffers who have noticed that the promised return has not happened, and he reframes the delay completely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation he is answering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before it have people asking where the promise of His coming is, since everything carries on as it always has."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not argue about the timetable. He changes the category the delay belongs to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord is not dragging His feet over what He promised, whatever some people call it. He is being patient with us. He does not want anyone destroyed. He wants every last one to turn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word behind *patient* is **makrothumeo** — long-tempered, slow to anger, the quality of someone who could act and does not yet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is being read as inability or indifference is a decision being held open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He wants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two statements, one negative and one positive, and both are unrestricted:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**not wanting** anyone to be destroyed", "**wanting** every last one to come to repentance"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter puts God's desire on record in the plainest possible terms, in a chapter otherwise full of fire and dissolving elements."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the delay is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the answer to the scoffers. The time that looks like absence is the time being given — and Peter says so again five verses on: *consider the patience of our Lord as salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The waiting is not neutral. It is rescue in progress."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse before it.** *With the Lord a day is like a thousand years, and a thousand years like a day.* Peter answers the timing question twice — once by saying God's reckoning of time is not ours, and once by saying the delay is deliberate mercy. He does not pick between them."}]}, "2 Samuel 1:17–27": {"title": "The Song of the Bow", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David writes a lament for the man who hunted him for years, and orders it taught to the nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **ordered that the people of Judah be taught it** — the Song of the Bow, written down in the Book of Jashar.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a private grief. He has it learned by heart and archived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Book of Jashar is cited once more in Scripture, for Joshua's long day, and has not survived. See the note on Joshua 10:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Oh, how the mighty have fallen!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times — at the start, in the middle, and as the last line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he will not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't tell it in Gath.** Don't spread the news in the streets of Ashkelon.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gath is Goliath's town, and the place David had lived as a fugitive when Saul drove him out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had every reason to want the news carried there. He asks for silence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Mountains of Gilboa — may no dew fall on you**, no rain touch your slopes, no fields yield an offering.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He curses the ground for taking them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shield"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For there the shield of the mighty was thrown down, **the shield of Saul, no longer anointed with oil**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leather shields were rubbed with oil to keep them supple and turn a blade. An unoiled shield cracks, and stops being a shield."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever David means by it, he chose that image for Saul's end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about them both"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Saul and Jonathan — **beloved and beautiful in life, and in death they were not divided**. Swifter than eagles, stronger than lions.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul had thrown a spear at his own son over David. David's song puts them together and calls them undivided."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he credits Saul with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Daughters of Israel, weep for Saul**, who dressed you in scarlet and fine things, who put ornaments of gold on your clothing.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He finds something to praise, and it is Saul's provision for the women of Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is not one line of criticism in the whole lament. Not the spears, not the cave, not the years in the wilderness, not Nob."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jonathan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I grieve for you, Jonathan, my brother. **You were so dear to me. Your love to me was wonderful, deeper than the love of women.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant between them is in 1 Samuel 18 — Jonathan gave David his robe, his armour, his sword, his bow and his belt, which is to say his claim on the throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Oh, how the mighty have fallen, and **the weapons of war are lost**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He ends on the weapons rather than the men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the Amalekite fits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with a man arriving at David's camp with Saul's crown, claiming to have finished him off, expecting a reward. See the note on 2 Samuel 1:5–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David tears his clothes, mourns until evening, has the man executed for laying a hand on the LORD's anointed — and then writes this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he had said twice while Saul was alive.** *The LORD forbid that I should stretch out my hand against the LORD's anointed.* He had him in a cave and in a camp and did not touch him, and the song is consistent with the two occasions he had the chance."}]}, "2 Samuel 1:5–10": {"title": "The Amalekite who came to collect a reward", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A young man arrives at David's camp with Saul's crown in his hands and a story he has clearly rehearsed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he says he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*An Amalekite.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the detail the chapter is built on. Saul had been commanded to destroy the Amalekites and had spared what he chose to spare. Now, at the end of his life, it is an Amalekite standing over him — and an Amalekite who arrives to profit from his death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing kept back came back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The story he tells"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says Saul was leaning on his spear, called him over, and asked to be finished off, and that he did it and took the crown and the armlet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account does not match what the previous chapter recorded, where Saul fell on his own sword after his armour-bearer refused. Most readers conclude the Amalekite found the body and invented a role for himself, expecting a reward from the man Saul had hunted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he misjudged"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He read David the way the world reads: an enemy is dead, the throne is open, and the man who brings news of it will be paid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's response is to tear his clothes, mourn until evening, and then execute him for laying a hand on the LORD's anointed. Then he writes a lament for the man who had spent years trying to kill him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pattern the chapter draws.** What Saul was told to deal with and did not, stood over him at the end and took the crown off his head. The narrative makes no comment. It simply gives the man's nationality in the fifth verse and lets the reader remember chapter 15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Amalek was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy describes the nation by its method: it came at Israel's back and cut down the stragglers *when you were weary and worn out*. It hunts what is already tired."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul was told to blot it out. He kept the king and the best of the livestock — what looked useful — and destroyed the rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is spared because it still seems worth something does not stay where it was put. It stands over you at the end and takes the crown off your head, and four hundred years later it signs a decree in Persia with a king's ring on its hand. See the notes on Deuteronomy 25:17–19 and Esther 3:1–4."}]}, "2 Samuel 11:1": {"title": "\"But David stayed behind\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse of scene-setting, and everything that follows is in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The season"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When spring came around, **the season when kings ride out to war**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *at the return of the year, at the time when kings go out*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Campaigns were fought in spring — after the rains, when the roads were passable and there was fodder for the animals. It was the calendar, and everybody knew it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David **sent Joab** out with his officers and the whole army of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sends. The army goes, the siege is laid, the war is prosecuted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The machinery works without him, which is part of the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **David stayed behind in Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six words in the Hebrew, and the writer puts them at the end of the verse where they cannot be missed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about it is illegal. A king delegating a siege is not a crime, and David had fought enough wars."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives the season first, then the fact that everyone else went, then the exception."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole verse exists to place David in the wrong location at the right time of year, and then the next verse opens: *and it happened one evening that David arose from his bed and walked on the roof.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chapter it introduces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bathsheba, Uriah, the letter carried by the man it condemned, and a child who dies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nathan's parable comes in the next chapter, and God's own summary of the charge is about Uriah rather than Bathsheba: *you have killed Uriah the Hittite with the sword… now therefore the sword shall never depart from your house.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other man in the story"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Uriah, offered leave and a night at home, sleeps at the palace door instead:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The ark and Israel and Judah are dwelling in tents… **shall I then go to my house to eat and drink and lie with my wife?** As you live, and as your soul lives, I will not do this thing.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The foreign soldier will not go home while the army is in the field. The king of Israel had not gone out with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sons of Issachar had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Men who **understood the times and knew what Israel should do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two hundred of them came to Hebron to make David king. See the note on 1 Chronicles 12:23–37."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any judgement. The writer records where the army was and where the king was, and leaves it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How much rests on one clause.** The rest of David's reign runs downhill from this chapter — Amnon, Tamar, Absalom, the flight from Jerusalem — and the narrative traces all of it back to a spring he did not go out."}]}, "2 Samuel 19:31–40": {"title": "Barzillai turns it down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man who had kept a king alive is offered a place at his table, and lists reasons why not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was he who had **kept the king supplied** while David stayed at Mahanaim.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David was in flight from Absalom, with an army and no supply line. Chapter 17 gives the inventory Barzillai brought out: beds, basins, wheat, barley, flour, parched grain, beans, lentils, honey, curds, sheep and cheese."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For they said, \"The people are hungry and weary and thirsty in the wilderness.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He backed the true king when the throne was in doubt and it was dangerous to do so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cross over with me. Come to Jerusalem, and I will care for you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Open-ended, from a restored king, to the man who had fed him. There is no condition on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How many years do I have left…? **I am eighty now.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then a run of questions, all in the negative:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"Can I still **tell good from bad**?\"*", "*\"Can I **taste** what I eat and drink?\"*", "*\"Can I still **hear** the voices of singers, men and women?\"*", "*\"Why should your servant be **a burden** to my lord the king?\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judgement, taste, hearing — he inventories what he has lost, and concludes from the list that the invitation is not for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he wants to go instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let me go back home, and let me die in my own town, near the graves of my father and mother.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks to go back and be buried where his parents are. That is his stated ambition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he sends instead of himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But here is **your servant Kimham** — let him cross over with my lord the king.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most readers take Kimham for his son. The place at the table gets filled; Barzillai is not in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He crosses *a little way over the Jordan*, takes the king's kiss and blessing, and goes home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Set beside Caleb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caleb, at eighty-five, is five years older than Barzillai here, and this is what he says:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am as strong this day as on the day Moses sent me; just as my strength was then, so now is my strength for war… **give me this mountain**\" — the one with the giants and the fortified cities on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two old men, five years apart. One counts what he can no longer taste; the other asks for the hill country. See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue. *The king kissed Barzillai and blessed him, and Barzillai went home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is taken away from him, and nothing is forced on him. The offer stood and he declined it, and the record is kind to him throughout — David remembers him on his deathbed and tells Solomon to show kindness to his sons."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he does not say.** He never doubts David or the kingdom. His reasons are all about himself — his age, his senses, his usefulness. The invitation was not conditional on any of them."}]}, "2 Samuel 9:5–8": {"title": "Mephibosheth at the king's table", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A king sends for the last surviving heir of the family he replaced, and the man arrives certain he is going to die."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan's son, Saul's grandson. Crippled in both feet since he was five, when his nurse dropped him fleeing the news that his father and grandfather had been killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the political logic of that world he was a dead man — a rival claimant living on borrowed time. That is why he had been living in **Lo Debar**, a name that means roughly *no pasture*, or *nothing there*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who is your servant, that you would even notice a dead dog like me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is trembling, face to the ground. He does not ask for anything. He does not argue that he is harmless or that he deserves better."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls himself a dead dog."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David gives him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things, and none of them are requested:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**don't be afraid**", "**every piece of land that belonged to your grandfather Saul**", "**you yourself will always eat at my table**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land is restored, and the seat is permanent. The chapter ends by saying he ate at the king's table like one of the king's sons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am going to show you kindness **for the sake of your father Jonathan**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason has nothing to do with Mephibosheth. It is a covenant made years earlier between David and Jonathan — see 1 Samuel 18:1–4 — and the kindness runs to him on the strength of somebody else's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not being rewarded. He is being kept."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last verse of the chapter.** *And he was lame in both feet.* Second Samuel mentions it three times, and the final time is the very last line — after the land, the table, and the sons. The condition never changed. His place at the table was not conditional on it."}]}, "2 Thessalonians 2:13–14": {"title": "Chosen from the beginning", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "After two chapters about the man of lawlessness, Paul turns and thanks God for the people reading it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **we owe God thanks for you always**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *we are bound to give thanks*. He uses the same construction at the start of the letter — an obligation rather than an inclination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most alarming passage in either Thessalonian letter: the falling away, the man of sin sitting in the temple of God, the lawless one whom the Lord will consume with the breath of His mouth, and *strong delusion* sent on those who did not receive the love of the truth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, without transition: **but we owe God thanks for you**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brothers and sisters **loved by the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a perfect passive participle — *having been loved*, and standing loved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The choosing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **God chose you from the beginning to be saved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **heileto** — took for Himself, selected."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**From the beginning** — some manuscripts read *as firstfruits*; either way the point is that the choosing predates them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Spirit setting you apart** for Himself, and **you believing the truth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sanctification by the Spirit, and belief of the truth. Paul puts God's action and the person's response in one clause without ranking them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is what **He called you to**, and **He called you through the good news we brought**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eternal choice and the specific sermon are the same event described twice. Paul does not treat the preaching as incidental to the choosing — it is the means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *good news* is **euangelion**, and *we brought* makes it concrete: Paul, Silas and Timothy, in a synagogue in Thessalonica, for three sabbaths, before a mob drove them out of town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that you would come to **possess the glory of our Lord Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *for the obtaining of the glory*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to see it. To obtain it. Paul writes the same thing to Rome: *whom He justified, these He also glorified* — in the past tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Thessalonians were like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had been with them a matter of weeks. The church was young, poor, under pressure from its own countrymen, and confused enough about the return of Christ that both letters have to address it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them they were chosen from the beginning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, **stand fast and hold the traditions which you were taught**, whether by word or our epistle.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then a prayer: *now may our Lord Jesus Christ Himself, and our God and Father, who has loved us and given us everlasting consolation and good hope by grace, **comfort your hearts and establish you in every good word and work**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where Paul puts the paragraph.** Directly after the darkest passage he ever wrote about the future. The answer he gives to it is not information — it is a list of what is already true of the people who were frightened."}]}, "2 Thessalonians 3:10–12": {"title": "\"Unwilling\" is the whole word", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This verse gets quoted at people in hard circumstances more often than almost any other, and usually with one word quietly dropped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that is always dropped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who is **unwilling** to work should not eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek does not say *does not work*. It says *does not want to*. Paul is describing a decision, not a situation. Everyone who cannot find work, cannot work, or has run out of options is outside the sentence entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was actually writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next line names them: people in that church who were *drifting, doing no work at all, and yet somehow finding the hours to busy themselves in everyone else's affairs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The likeliest cause is in the letter itself. Some of them had concluded the Lord's return was imminent and had downed tools to wait. Paul is not scolding the poor. He is dealing with a specific enthusiasm that had turned into idleness — and, as he notes drily, into meddling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he tells them to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Settle down, do your own work without all the noise, and eat food you have earned.* Then, immediately after, he tells the rest of the church not to grow weary in doing good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both instructions in the same breath: work quietly, and keep being generous."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How Paul argues it.** He appeals to his own habits first — he paid his way in Thessalonica rather than take support, though he says outright he had the right to. He is asking of them something he had already done in front of them."}]}, "2 Thessalonians 3:3–5": {"title": "\"But the Lord is faithful\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just asked for prayer because *not all have faith*, and then turns the word around."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But the Lord is faithful.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *pray… that we may be delivered from unreasonable and wicked men, **for not all have faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names a shortage of faith in people and answers it with the faithfulness of the Lord. Same root, opposite subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same in 2 Timothy: *if we are faithless, **He remains faithful**; He cannot deny Himself.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things He does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He is the one who will **make you steady on your feet**, and He is the one who will **stand guard over you and keep you clear of evil**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the first is **sterizo** — to establish, to set firmly, to prop up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is to guard, the word for a garrison keeping watch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs are His and neither is asked of the readers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is confident about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And where you are concerned, **we are confident in the Lord**: you are doing what we told you, and you will go on doing it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note where the confidence is placed. Not *we are confident in you* — **in the Lord**, about you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's expectation of their perseverance rests on somebody other than them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"May the Lord **steer your hearts straight into the love of God**, and straight into **the patience that waits for Christ**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to make straight, to direct — the same verb used of guiding a ship."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two destinations: God's love, and patience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The patience that waits for Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the patience of Christ*, and it has been read both ways — the endurance Christ Himself had, and the endurance that waits for Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either reading is at home in this letter. The Thessalonians had got themselves in a state about the Lord's return; some had stopped working over it, which is what the rest of the chapter is about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is dealing with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Persecution, and confusion about the second coming, and people who had quit their jobs to wait for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's answer to the last of those is the plainest sentence in the letter: *if anyone will not work, neither shall he eat.* See the note on 2 Thessalonians 3:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He writes about being guarded and steadied, and then tells them to go back to work."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How Paul signs it.** *The salutation of Paul with my own hand, which is a sign in every epistle; so I write.* Somebody had been sending the Thessalonians forged letters about the day of the Lord, and Paul started signing his own."}]}, "2 Timothy 1:6–7": {"title": "Fan the gift into flame", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last surviving letter, written from a Roman prison to a younger man who appears to be losing his nerve."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is already there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God **placed a gift inside you** the day I laid my hands on you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense, and settled. Nothing in the passage asks Timothy to obtain anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole instruction assumes the gift is present and has been present for years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Fan it back up into open flame\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anazopureo** — *ana* (again) + *zoos* (alive) + *pur* (fire). To rekindle; to stir a fire back up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not fan an ashtray. The verb takes for granted that there are coals in there, banked and covered, still hot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy is not told to light something. He is told to stir something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Was never a small, cowering thing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What God breathed into us was never a small, cowering thing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek noun is **deilia** — cowardice, timidity. It appears nowhere else in the New Testament, and it is not a neutral word; the Greeks used it as the opposite of courage, a fault rather than a temperament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say Timothy is not afraid. He says where the fear did not come from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three things it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave us **power**, and He gave us **love**, and He gave us **a mind that stays clear and steady**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**power** — *dunamis*, the plain word for ability and force", "**love** — *agape*", "**a clear and steady mind** — **sophronismos**, a word about soundness of mind, self-command, a head that is in one piece"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is the one that gets flattened in translation. It is built from the words for *safe* and *mind*: a mind that is intact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists it as something given, alongside power and love — not as a disposition Timothy has to manufacture under pressure."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul asks for at the end of the letter.** His cloak, the books, and especially the parchments. A man writing about power, love and a sound mind from a cell, and asking for a coat because winter is coming."}]}, "2 Timothy 3:14–15": {"title": "\"Ever since you were a small child\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last letter, and this is what he tells a young man to do when everything else is coming apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is emphatic — *su de*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What comes before it: a list of what the last days will be like — *lovers of themselves, lovers of money, boasters, proud, blasphemers, disobedient to parents* — eighteen items, and then *evil men and impostors will grow worse and worse*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against all of it: *but you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hold your ground **in what you learned and came to be sure of**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mene** — remain, abide, stay put."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not advance, and not defend. Stay."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **you know the ones you learned it from**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is plural — *from whom you learned*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names them at the start of the letter: *the genuine faith that is in you, which dwelt first in **your grandmother Lois and your mother Eunice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two women, named, three generations back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts adds the detail: Timothy's father was Greek. The Scripture came down the maternal line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you know that **ever since you were a small child you have been at home in the sacred writings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apo brephous** — from infancy. **Brephos** is the word for an unborn or newborn child; Luke uses it of John leaping in the womb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he learned"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **sacred writings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hiera grammata** — the standard Jewish term for the Hebrew Scriptures."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy learned the Old Testament as a small boy, in a Greek town, from a Jewish mother and grandmother, years before Paul arrived in Lystra."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they can do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they are able to **make you wise for the salvation that comes through faith in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sophisai** — to make wise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clause is careful. The writings make wise *for* salvation, and the salvation comes *through faith in Christ Jesus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is describing what the Old Testament did for a boy who met Christ later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse everybody knows:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All Scripture is **given by inspiration of God**, and is profitable for doctrine, for reproof, for correction, for instruction in righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **theopneustos** — God-breathed. One word, and it appears once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last. He is in a cold cell — *bring the cloak that I left with Carpus* — most of his companions have gone, and *only Luke is with me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith.* See the note on 2 Timothy 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks Timothy to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be diligent to come to me quickly… **come before winter**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether he arrived is not recorded."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul tells him to fall back on.** Not new arguments. What he learned, and who he learned it from — two women who taught him the Scriptures before he could walk."}]}, "2 Timothy 3:16–17": {"title": "Breathed out by God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's clearest statement about Scripture, written from a cell, in his last letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every Scripture is **breathed out by God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **theopneustos** — from *theos* (God) and *pneo* (to breathe). It appears once in the New Testament, here, and Paul may have coined it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the direction. Not that God breathed *into* the writings, but that they were breathed *out* — the Scripture is the exhalation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 2 has God breathing into a man's nostrils and the man becoming a living soul. The same picture, applied to a text."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Every\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be read *all Scripture* or *every Scripture*. Either way there is no subset — no clause exempting the genealogies, the census lists, the building specifications, or the parts nobody preaches from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is useful for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, in sequence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**teaching** — showing what is true", "**showing us where we have gone wrong** — the Greek is conviction, exposure", "**setting us straight again** — restoration, putting back upright", "**training us in the life that is right** — the word is *paideia*, the whole business of raising a child"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle two are worth the order they are in. The exposure comes before the correction, and the correction is described as being set back on your feet rather than knocked off them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that the one who belongs to God may be **complete, fully equipped** for every good work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *complete* is **artios** — fitted, in working order. And *fully equipped* is the same root intensified: outfitted, supplied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the vocabulary of a ship rigged for a voyage or a workman with his tools."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which Scripture Paul meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier: *from childhood you have known **the Holy Scriptures**, which are able to make you wise for salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy's childhood Bible was the Old Testament. Paul says those writings can make a man wise for salvation through faith in Christ Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How much weight a single word can carry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does it himself in Galatians 3: *it does not say, \"and to seeds,\" as though to many, but as of one — **and to your Seed**, who is Christ.* An argument on a singular noun. See the note on Galatians 3:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus did the same to the Sadducees, on a present-tense verb: *I **am** the God of Abraham* — therefore *He is not the God of the dead, but of the living.* See the note on Matthew 22:23–33."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of them read Scripture as though the individual words were load-bearing, and both won the argument that way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul was when he wrote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bring the cloak that I left with Carpus at Troas — and the books, especially the parchments.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same letter, two chapters later. A man in a Roman cell asking for his coat and his scrolls."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that follows the famous one about the last days.** Paul lists the difficult times ahead — *lovers of themselves, lovers of money, boasters* — and his instruction to Timothy about it is *but you must continue in the things you have learned.* The remedy offered for a bad age is a book."}]}, "2 Timothy 4:6–8": {"title": "Three finished things, and a crown that is not only his", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last words we have from Paul, written from a Roman cell with no expectation of release."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Poured out like an offering\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is a drink offering — wine poured out beside a sacrifice, the last act of a ritual and the smallest part of it. Paul does not compare himself to the sacrifice. He is the libation poured alongside one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The hour of my going is here* — and the word for going is **analusis**, the noun of the verb he used in Philippians for casting off. Same picture, years later, with the ship now actually leaving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three perfects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have fought the good fight. I have finished the race. I have kept the faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three are in the perfect tense — done, and the result stands. Note what he does not claim: he does not say he won, or that he did it well. He says he fought, he finished, he kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle one is not about coming first. The Greek is simply completing the course."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Laid aside and kept for me* — a verb meaning reserved, put away for someone. It is already in storage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the line that stops it being about Paul:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "and not to me only, but to every last one who has loved His appearing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condition for the crown, stated by a man about to be executed, is not achievement or office or martyrdom. It is having loved the fact that He is coming."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read on to verse 9.** Immediately after this — the crown, the finished race, the settled peace — Paul writes *do your best to come to me quickly*, and later asks for his cloak and his books. The grandeur and the cold cell are three sentences apart, and both are in the letter because both were true."}]}, "3 John 1:2": {"title": "\"The way your soul is already doing well\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A single verse from a letter of fourteen, written by an old man to a friend named Gaius."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he prays"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*More than anything I hope things go well for you and that your health holds up, the way your soul is already doing well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things in one sentence: that it goes well with him generally, that his body holds up, and that both would keep pace with a soul that is already thriving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb behind *go well* is **euodoo** — literally to have a good road, a prosperous journey. It is used of travelling without obstruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure John uses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The way your soul is already doing well.* The soul is the benchmark, and it is in good order — that much John states as fact rather than wish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the prayer is not for prosperity in the abstract. It is that the outward life would come up level with the inward one, which John already knows is flourishing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he was confident about the soul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse says why: travellers had come and reported that Gaius was walking in the truth, and John says nothing gives him greater joy. Then the letter commends him for how he treated strangers and missionaries passing through — at his own cost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gaius was a generous man, and John prays for his health and his road in the same breath as praising it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In all things\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peri panton** — concerning all things, in every respect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not narrow it. He prays it over the whole of the man's life and then names health separately, so the health is not the whole of what *all things* means."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The apostle who leaned on His chest at the supper, writing in old age to a private individual. It is a personal letter — the shortest book in the New Testament — and this is the sentence he opens with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls it what he wishes *above all things*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What kind of sentence it is.** It sits where a first-century letter always put a wish for the reader's health — the standard opening courtesy of the era. John takes that ordinary form and fills it with something else: a prayer that the body and the circumstances would match the state of the soul. He evidently thought the three belonged together."}]}, "Acts 1:12–14": {"title": "The room they went back to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He has just gone up out of sight, and Luke's next sentence is about the walk home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the hill called the Mount of Olives, which is close to the city, **a Sabbath day's walk away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly two thousand cubits — a little over half a mile. Luke measures it in the unit a Jewish reader would use."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ascension happened close enough to the city to walk back from before lunch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They went up to **the upstairs room** where they were staying.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek has the definite article — **the** upper room. Luke assumes his reader knows which one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is where the supper was eaten, and it is where they are again. See the note on Mark 14:12–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter and James and John and Andrew, Philip and Thomas, Bartholomew and Matthew, James the son of Alphaeus, Simon the Zealot, and Judas the brother of James.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven. Luke lists every one by name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order has changed from the Gospel lists — Peter, James and John are moved to the front, and Judas Iscariot is simply gone. No comment is made about the absence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of them **stayed together, praying with one heart and one mind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **homothumadon** — of one accord, one passion. Luke uses it repeatedly in the early chapters of Acts and almost nowhere else in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are waiting, because they were told to: *wait for the Promise of the Father.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The women"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Along with **the women**, and **Mary the mother of Jesus**, and **His brothers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, who opened his Gospel with Mary, mentions her here for the last time — in a prayer meeting, named among the others, with no special place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The brothers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And His brothers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In John's Gospel: *even His brothers did not believe in Him.* They mocked Him at the Feast of Tabernacles and told Him to go show Himself to the world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between that verse and this one, the resurrection happened, and Paul records one detail: *then He was seen by James.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James goes on to lead the church in Jerusalem and write a letter that opens *James, a bondservant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ* — without mentioning that they grew up in the same house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How many"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *the number of names together was about **a hundred and twenty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what was left, in one room, of three years of public ministry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in that room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten days later: *suddenly there came a sound from heaven as of a rushing mighty wind, and it filled the whole house where they were sitting.* See the note on Acts 2:1–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they had been told to do.** Not to organise, not to plan, not to preach yet. *Tarry in the city of Jerusalem until you are endued with power from on high.* Luke describes ten days of exactly that, and lists everyone who was there."}]}, "Acts 1:9–11": {"title": "\"This same Jesus\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They watched Him go, and two men had to tell them to stop looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While they were **looking straight at Him**, He was lifted up, and a cloud took Him out of their sight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is careful about this — they were watching. It happened in daylight, in front of eleven men, on a hillside outside Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cloud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not weather. The cloud in Scripture is the visible sign of the presence — the pillar in the wilderness, the cloud that filled the tabernacle and then the temple, the bright cloud at the transfiguration."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Standing there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were **still standing there staring into the sky** as He went.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it twice, and the two men in white say it back to them as a question: *why are you standing here looking into the sky?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody rebukes them for grief. They are simply moved along — there is a room in Jerusalem to go to and ten days to wait."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This same Jesus**, who has been taken from you into heaven, will come back **in the same way** you have watched Him go.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves matter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**This same Jesus.** Not a different one, not an altered one. The Man they had walked with for three years — the one who wept at a grave, cooked breakfast on a beach, touched a leper, and would not break a bruised reed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is who went up, and that is who is coming back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the same way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bodily, visibly, and to the same place. Zechariah says it: *in that day His feet will stand on the Mount of Olives* — which is precisely the hill they were standing on when they were told this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was doing as He left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's Gospel gives the detail Acts leaves out: *He lifted up His hands and blessed them. And it came to pass, while He blessed them, He was parted from them and carried up into heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His hands were up and He was in the middle of a blessing. The blessing was never finished — He went up mid-sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He is now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has taken His seat at the right hand of God's throne.* Every priest under the old covenant stood, because the work was never done. He sat down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He *always lives to make intercession for them* — the same Man, doing that, now."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last thing recorded of the disciples in that chapter.** *They returned to Jerusalem with great joy.* He had just left, and Luke says joy. They had been told He was coming back exactly as He went."}]}, "Acts 10:1–8": {"title": "A Roman officer at prayer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that opens the church to the world starts with a soldier of the occupying army."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In Caesarea there was a man named **Cornelius, a centurion of what was called the Italian Regiment**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caesarea was the Roman administrative capital of Judea — Pilate's seat, and the garrison town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A centurion commanded eighty to a hundred men. Every centurion named in the New Testament comes off well: the one at Capernaum, the one at the cross, and the one who kept Paul alive on the ship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke says about him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **devout**, and **he and his whole household feared God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**God-fearer** was a recognised category — a Gentile who worshipped the God of Israel, attended the synagogue, and had not taken circumcision."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **gave generously to the people** and **prayed to God continually**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Alms and prayer. Both were Jewish devotional practice, and he did them as an outsider."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*About **three in the afternoon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ninth hour — the hour of the evening sacrifice, and the hour Peter and John went up to the temple in chapter 3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman officer is keeping Jewish prayer hours in a garrison town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **saw plainly in a vision an angel of God** coming in to him and saying, \"**Cornelius**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says *plainly* — the Greek is *manifestly*, openly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **stared at the angel, frightened**, and said, \"**What is it, Lord?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same **kurie** that can mean *sir*. He does not know what he is speaking to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the angel says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your prayers and your gifts to the poor have gone up before God, and He has remembered them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *have ascended for a memorial before God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The language is sacrificial — the phrase used of the grain offering's memorial portion burned on the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prayers and alms from a Gentile soldier, described as an offering God has kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the angel does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Preach. It gives an address."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now **send men to Joppa and bring back a man called Simon, who is also named Peter**. He is staying with **Simon the tanner, whose house is by the sea**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An angel appears and its message is a street direction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man is to be told by a man. Peter says the same thing at the council later: *God made a choice among us: that **by my mouth** the Gentiles should hear the message.* See the note on Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tanner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has already told the reader where Peter is: *Peter stayed on in Joppa for some time with a tanner named Simon.* See the note on Acts 9:36–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tanner was ceremonially unclean by trade, and tanneries were built outside towns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man about to be told *what God has cleansed, you must not call unholy* is already lodging with one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He will tell you what you ought to do**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Cornelius did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He called **two of his household servants and a devout soldier** from among those who attended him. **He explained everything to them** and sent them to Joppa.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty miles down the coast. He tells them the whole thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens the next day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter goes up on the roof to pray at noon, and sees a sheet. See the note on Acts 10:9–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two visions are a day apart and neither man knows about the other."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the angel is for.** Not the message. It gives a name, a town, a host and a street — and says the man in the house will do the telling."}]}, "Acts 10:17–20": {"title": "Three men at the gate", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The interpretation of the vision knocks on the door while he is still working on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state he is in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While Peter was **still turning the vision over in his mind, wondering what it could mean**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dieporei** — thoroughly perplexed, at a complete loss."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not understand it. Luke says so twice in four verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sheet full of animals has not yet produced a conclusion about people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening downstairs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The men Cornelius had sent **had asked their way to Simon's house and were standing at the gate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had walked thirty miles from Caesarea. Luke has been running the two scenes in parallel since the chapter opened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question they ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They called out, asking **whether Simon, also called Peter, was staying there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exact name the angel gave Cornelius. See the note on Acts 10:1–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter was still thinking about the vision when **the Spirit said to him**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four parts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look, **three men are asking for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Information he could not have had. He is on the roof."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, go downstairs**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **go with them without hesitating**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meden diakrinomenos** — making no distinction, doubting nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root Peter uses at the council: *He made **no distinction at all** between us and them.* See the note on Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because I have sent them**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim in the last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men were sent by Cornelius, who was told by an angel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Spirit says *I have sent them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sequence of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An angel to a Roman at three in the afternoon. A sheet to a Jew at noon the next day. Three men at a gate. And a sentence from the Spirit joining them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four separate movements, none of which the human parties arranged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Peter went down to the men and said, \"Yes, I am he whom you seek. **For what reason have you come?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks. He still does not know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They tell him about Cornelius — *a just man, one who fears God and has a good reputation among all the nation of the Jews* — and that an angel told him to send."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he invited them in and lodged them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jew gives lodging to two Gentile servants and a Roman soldier, in a tanner's house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first thing that changes, and it happens before he leaves Joppa."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who goes with him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And some brethren from Joppa accompanied him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six of them, as he says later in Jerusalem — witnesses he brought deliberately. See the note on Acts 11:4–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the vision meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He works it out on the road, and states it on arrival: *God has shown me that I should call no one unholy or unclean.* See the note on Acts 10:24–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sheet had animals in it. The conclusion is about people, and Peter reaches it somewhere between Joppa and Caesarea."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When the answer arrives.** Not in the vision. Peter is still perplexed when three men turn up at the gate — and the Spirit's instruction is simply to go with them without making distinctions."}]}, "Acts 10:24–29": {"title": "\"I am only a man myself\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter walks into a Roman officer's house and finds a congregation waiting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The preparation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cornelius **was expecting them** and **had called together his relatives and close friends**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told to send for a man who *will tell you what you ought to do*, and he assembled an audience for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the angel's message said to invite anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Peter came in, Cornelius met him, **fell at his feet and bowed low**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman centurion on the floor in front of a Galilean fisherman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Peter **lifted him up** and said, \"**Stand up. I am only a man myself**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *I myself am also a man*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul and Barnabas say the same at Lystra when the crowd tries to sacrifice to them: *we also are men with the same nature as you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the angel in Revelation, twice, when John falls down: *see that you do not do that. I am your fellow servant.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter states first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You know that it is **against our law for a Jewish man to associate with a foreigner or to visit him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **athemiton** — unlawful, taboo."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was custom rather than written Torah — the Old Testament nowhere forbids it, and Jews did trade and live among Gentiles. But the practice was real and everybody in the room knew it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter names the wall out loud before crossing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But God has shown me that I should call no one unholy or unclean**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **medena** — no man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sheet had animals in it. This is Peter's interpretation, and it is about people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He worked it out on the road from Joppa. See the note on Acts 10:9–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That is why **I came without objection when I was sent for**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anantirretos** — without contradiction, without raising an argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **may I ask why you sent for me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has walked thirty miles and still does not know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Cornelius answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole story of the vision, and then: *\"**Now therefore, we are all present before God, to hear all the things commanded you by God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the best sentences in Acts, and it comes from a Gentile soldier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**In truth I perceive that God shows no partiality**.\"* See the note on Acts 10:34–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosopolemptes** — a receiver of faces, one who judges by appearance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What interrupts him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While Peter was still speaking these words, **the Holy Spirit fell upon all those who heard the word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mid-sermon. Peter reports it that way in Jerusalem: *as I began to speak, the Holy Spirit fell on them just as He did on us at the beginning.* See the note on Acts 11:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had been baptised, circumcised or instructed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter asks the six men who came with him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Can anyone forbid water**, that these should not be baptised **who have received the Holy Spirit just as we have**?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is put on trial in Jerusalem for it in the next chapter — not for preaching to Gentiles, but for eating with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the wall goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For He Himself is our peace, who has made both one, and **has broken down the middle wall of separation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, in Ephesians, writing about the same barrier."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Peter says on the doorstep.** Names the custom he is breaking, states what God showed him, and then asks why he was invited — because he still does not know."}]}, "Acts 10:34–43": {"title": "\"He went about doing good\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter's sermon in a Roman officer's house, and the summary of Jesus' ministry in it is the shortest in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I see now, truly, that God shows no favouritism.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says *I see now* — he had not seen it before that week. It took a vision on a roof, three men at the gate, and a walk to Caesarea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is vivid: God is *no taker of faces*, no respecter of appearances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How God anointed Jesus of Nazareth with the Holy Spirit and with power, and how He went about **doing good** and **healing everyone under the oppression of the devil**, because God was with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence for three years. Two verbs: doing good, and healing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is speaking to people who had heard the reports — *you know what has happened all through Judea.* He does not need to persuade them it occurred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the healing is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone under the oppression of the devil.* The word means being tyrannised, held down by force."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter characterises sickness there as an oppression being lifted, and healing as release rather than reward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Because God was with Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given for the whole ministry. Peter puts the anointing, the power, and the presence together and hangs everything on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of the prophets bears witness to Him, that through His name **everyone who believes** in Him receives forgiveness of sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last sentence before the Spirit interrupts him. The word *everyone* is what he is saying when it happens — in a Gentile house, to people he would not have entered a room with a week earlier. See the note on Acts 10:44–48."}]}, "Acts 10:44–48": {"title": "\"Peter was still speaking\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The moment the church stopped being a Jewish movement, and it happens before the sermon finishes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter was still speaking when the Holy Spirit fell on everyone who was listening.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is cut off mid-sentence. Nothing has been required of these people — no baptism, no circumcision, no probation, no instruction on keeping the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells us what Peter had just said: *everyone who believes in Him receives forgiveness of sins through His name.* The Spirit falls on that sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was astounded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The believers of the circumcision who had come with Peter were astounded that the gift of the Holy Spirit had been poured out on the Gentiles as well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the Gentiles. The Jewish Christians who had travelled with him — men who had needed a vision on a rooftop and a direct instruction to walk into that house at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They believed in Jesus and were still astonished at this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Can anyone refuse water to keep these people from being baptized, when they have received the Holy Spirit just as we did?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The logic runs backwards from what God has already done. The evidence came first; the church's practice has to catch up with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the argument Peter will use again in Jerusalem when he is called to account for eating with Gentiles — *if God gave them the same gift, who was I to think I could stand in God's way?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Afterward they asked him to stay on with them a few days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jewish apostle stays in a Roman officer's house. In the culture of the time that sentence is nearly as significant as the Spirit falling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter opens.** Cornelius is described as devout, God-fearing, generous and prayerful — and none of it saved him; an angel still had to send for Peter. Luke gives him every religious credential available and then has the gospel arrive anyway, unearned."}]}, "Acts 10:9–16": {"title": "\"What God has cleansed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter goes up to pray and comes down with the whole Gentile mission."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The next day, **as they were on their way and coming near the city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three men from Caesarea are a mile off. Luke synchronises the two scenes so the reader can see the arrangement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter went up on **the flat roof to pray**. It was about **noon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roofs were living space. Noon was one of the three Jewish prayer hours."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **grew hungry and wanted something to eat**, and **while the meal was being prepared** he fell into a trance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the physical detail. The vision arrives in a hungry man waiting for lunch, and it is about food."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sheet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Something like **a great sheet coming down, let down to the earth by its four corners**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four corners — the four quarters of the earth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All kinds of **four-footed animals of the earth, and wild beasts, and creeping things, and birds of the air**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The categories of Leviticus 11, clean and unclean together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, Peter. Kill and eat**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Never, Lord. I have never eaten anything unholy or unclean**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **medamos** — by no means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses Him as Lord and refuses in the same breath. He had done it before — *depart from me, for I am a sinful man*, and *You shall never wash my feet*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His record is clean and he says so. Peter is not being disobedient; he is being observant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What God has cleansed, you must not call unholy**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekatharisen** — has purified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The issue is not what the animals are. It is who has ruled on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This happened **three times**, and then the sheet was taken up again into heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who denied three times and was asked three times on the beach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Food. Peter says so himself when he gets to Caesarea: *God has shown me that **I should call no one unholy or unclean**.* See the note on Acts 10:24–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sheet had animals in it and the application is people. Peter works that out on the journey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While Peter was **still turning the vision over in his mind**.* See the note on Acts 10:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not understand it immediately. The interpretation arrives in the form of three men at the gate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter tells the story twice more — to the church in Jerusalem when he is put on trial for eating with Gentiles, and again in substance at the council. See the notes on Acts 11:4–14 and Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells the Cornelius episode three times in his book. Nothing else gets that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark's Gospel says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thus He declared all foods clean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds that clause himself, in a parenthesis, in the passage about what defiles a man — and Mark is traditionally held to have written down Peter's preaching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter had been in the room when it was said, and had not drawn the conclusion."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is corrected.** Not his obedience. Peter's answer is accurate about the law and accurate about his own record — and what he is told is that a ruling has been made somewhere else."}]}, "Acts 11:15–18": {"title": "\"Who was I to stand in God's way?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is on trial in Jerusalem for eating with Gentiles, and his defence is a report of something he did not do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The believers in Judea had heard that Gentiles had received the word, and when Peter came up to Jerusalem *those of the circumcision contended with him, saying, \"You went in to uncircumcised men and ate with them!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is about a meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells the whole story again from the beginning — the sheet, the voice, the three men at the door, the journey to Caesarea. Luke records the entire Cornelius account twice, in chapter 10 and again here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As I began to speak**, the Holy Spirit fell on them **just as He did on us at the beginning**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was interrupted. His sermon in chapter 10 stops mid-flow — Luke says *while Peter was still speaking these words*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had been decided about these people. Nobody had been baptised, circumcised, or instructed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he remembered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And I remembered what the Lord had said: '**John baptized with water, but you will be baptized with the Holy Spirit**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Words spoken to him personally before the ascension, and he recognises the event as the one they described."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The logic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So if **God gave them the same gift He gave us** when we believed on the Lord Jesus Christ, **who was I to stand in God's way?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses. The premise is the identical gift; the conclusion is a question about his own authority to object."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *who was I, able to withstand God?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is conceded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **same gift**. Peter does not describe a lesser or preliminary version."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again at the council years later: *God, who knows the heart, testified for them **by giving them the Holy Spirit just as He gave Him to us**. He made **no distinction at all**.* See the note on Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they heard this, **they had nothing more to say**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they kept silent*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they praised God. \"So then,\" they said, \"**God has given the Gentiles too a change of mind that leads to life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A change of mind** — **metanoia**, the same word Paul uses to the Ephesian elders. See the note on Acts 20:17–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is described as **given**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it settles and what it does not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It settles the Cornelius case. It does not settle the question — Acts 15 has the whole church convening on the same issue, and Galatians 2 has Paul confronting Peter for withdrawing from Gentile tables at Antioch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Peter who said *who was I to stand in God's way* got up and moved to another table when men came from James."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports the argument here; Paul reports the relapse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Straight to Antioch, where men from Cyprus and Cyrene *spoke to the Hellenists, preaching the Lord Jesus*, and *a great number believed and turned to the Lord*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barnabas is sent to look into it, goes to Tarsus to find Saul, and the two of them teach there for a year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the disciples were first called Christians in Antioch.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What his defence rests on.** Not a decision he made or a policy he adopted. Something that happened while he was still talking, which he says was the same thing that had happened to them."}]}, "Acts 11:27–30": {"title": "Bread sent ahead of the famine", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A famine is announced before it arrives, and the church's response is to give money away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The messenger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of them named **Agabus** stood up and **indicated by the Spirit** that a great famine was coming over the whole world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Agabus** comes from a root meaning *locust*. A man named for the insect that eats a harvest stands up to announce a shortage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He appears twice in Acts and both times with a warning — here, and later at Caesarea, binding his own hands and feet with Paul's belt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confirmation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which **happened in the days of Claudius Caesar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke writes this after the fact and says so. Roman sources record serious grain shortages under Claudius, and Judea was hit hard in the mid-forties."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophecy is dated and checked in the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was warned"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Antioch. A Gentile church, a long way north, outside the famine's worst."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are told about a hardship that is going to fall on somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with the warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the disciples, **each according to what he had**, determined to **send help** to the brothers living in Judea.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not stockpile. Told that a famine is coming, they send their supplies south."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Each according to what he had\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *as each was prospered*. Nobody is assessed a fixed amount and nobody is told what anyone else gave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they sent it to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Living in Judea* — the mother church in Jerusalem, the people who had been suspicious of Antioch's existence in the first place. Peter had to explain himself in the previous chapter for eating with Gentiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gentile church sends money to the Jewish one that questioned it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who carried it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sending it to the elders by the hands of **Barnabas and Saul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barnabas is the man who sold a field and laid the money at the apostles' feet in chapter 4, and who vouched for Saul when nobody in Jerusalem would meet him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul is on his first recorded assignment for the church, and it is a delivery job."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Antioch was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses earlier: *the disciples were **first called Christians** in Antioch.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is given in this chapter, and the first thing the people carrying it do is this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of events.** The warning came first, then the collection, then the famine. Luke sets it down in that sequence — the relief was already on the road before anyone in Judea was hungry."}]}, "Acts 11:4–14": {"title": "Peter tells it from the beginning", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Put on trial for eating with Gentiles, Peter's defence is to tell the story again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *those of the circumcision contended with him, saying, \"**You went in to uncircumcised men and ate with them**!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The accusation is about a meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Peter **began at the beginning and laid it all out for them in order**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathexes** — in sequence. It is the word Luke uses in his Gospel's preface about his own method: *to write to you an orderly account*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not argue. He narrates."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells the Cornelius story twice — chapter 10 as it happens, chapter 11 as Peter reports it — which is unusual in a book this compressed, and it is how Luke marks what matters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I was in the city of Joppa, **praying**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same town Jonah sailed from to avoid going to Gentiles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sheet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Something like **a great sheet coming down out of heaven, let down by its four corners**, and **it came right to me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four corners — the four quarters of the earth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**It came right to me** — Peter adds that detail in the retelling. It did not appear near him; it arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Four-footed animals of the earth, and wild beasts, and creeping things, and birds of the air**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every category in Leviticus 11, clean and unclean together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, Peter. Kill and eat**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Never, Lord. Nothing unholy or unclean has ever passed my lips**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by no means* — the strong double negative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses Him as Lord and then refuses. He had done the same in the boat — *depart from me, for I am a sinful man* — and at the foot-washing: *You shall never wash my feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His record is clean and he says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What God has cleansed, you must not call unholy**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *cleansed* is **ekatharisen** — purified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The issue is not what the animals are. It is who has ruled on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This happened three times**, and then it was all drawn back up into heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is the man who denied three times and was asked three times on the beach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**At that very moment** three men, sent to me from Caesarea, arrived at the house.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vision ends and there is knocking at the door. The interpretation walks up the street."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The Spirit told me to **go with them without hesitating**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meden diakrinomenon** — making no distinction, doubting nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root as the word Peter uses at the council years later: *He made **no distinction at all** between us and them.* See the note on Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **these six brothers went with me too**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter brought corroboration to Jerusalem. Six men, standing there while he speaks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Cornelius had been told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will **speak words to you by which you and all your household will be saved**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An angel appeared to Cornelius and did not preach to him. It told him where to send for a man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As I began to speak, **the Holy Spirit fell on them just as He did on us at the beginning**.\"* See the note on Acts 11:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the room goes quiet."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he defends himself.** By telling it in order, with six witnesses in the room, and letting the sequence do the arguing — a vision he refused, an instruction he could not answer, and three men at the door before it was over."}]}, "Acts 13:13–15": {"title": "\"If you have any word of encouragement\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of travel notes, and two things in them matter later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change of name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul and his companions set sail from Paphos.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has said *Barnabas and Saul* through the whole account until Cyprus. From here on it is **Paul**, and Barnabas comes second."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The switch happens in the verse about the sorcerer — *then Saul, who is also called Paul* — and the order of the names never goes back. See the note on Acts 13:9–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The departure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There **John left them and went back to Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John Mark. Luke gives no reason."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It becomes the sharpest recorded disagreement between two believers in the New Testament: at the start of the second journey Barnabas wants to take him and Paul refuses, *because he had departed from them in Pamphylia and had not gone with them to the work*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the contention became so sharp that they parted from one another.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barnabas takes Mark to Cyprus; Paul takes Silas through Syria."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Decades later, in Paul's last letter: *Get Mark and bring him with you, for **he is useful to me for ministry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Mark writes a Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The synagogue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the Sabbath day they **went into the synagogue and sat down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's method throughout Acts. He goes to the synagogue first in every city, and he says why in this very chapter: *it was necessary that the word of God be spoken to you first.* See the note on Acts 13:44–47."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of service"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **the reading of the law and the prophets**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two readings — a portion from the Torah and one from the Prophets. The pattern is the same one Luke describes in Nazareth, where Jesus was handed Isaiah. See the note on Luke 4:16–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rulers of the synagogue sent word to them: \"**Brothers, if you have any word of encouragement for the people, please speak**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logos parakleseos** — a word of exhortation, of comfort. It was the standard term for the sermon after the readings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Visiting teachers were routinely asked. Nobody in the room has any idea what they are about to hear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul stands, motions with his hand, and delivers the longest of his recorded sermons — Israel's history from Egypt to David, then the resurrection, then the two sentences the whole thing was built toward. See the note on Acts 13:38–39."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the invitation cost them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the next sabbath nearly the whole city was outside the building, the leaders were contradicting him, and Paul and Barnabas were expelled from the district."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it starts.** A polite formula at the end of a normal service — *if you have any word of encouragement* — offered to two travellers who had just sat down at the back."}]}, "Acts 13:34–37": {"title": "\"Served God's purpose in his own generation\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is preaching in a synagogue in Antioch, and he uses David to make an argument that turns on a single observable fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm says *You will not let Your Holy One see decay*. Paul's point is simple and almost forensic:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["David said it", "David died, was buried, and **did see decay** — his tomb was a known location", "therefore he was not talking about himself"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the One God raised up saw no decay at all.* The psalm had to be about somebody else, and Paul says who."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about David"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After he had served God's purpose in his own generation, [he] fell asleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a remarkable summary of a life that filled several books. Not \"after he had won his wars\" or \"after he had built the kingdom.\" He served God's purpose **in his own generation** — the phrase locates the whole thing inside a lifetime, with a start and a finish, and treats the finish as unremarkable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David is not diminished by it. He is placed: a man who did what was his to do, in the years he had, and then slept."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two halves do together.** Paul's whole case rests on the difference — David's work ended and his body decayed; the other did not. But the way he says it about David is not dismissive. Serving God's purpose in your own generation is offered as a good and sufficient description of a life, by a man arguing that only one person ever escaped the ending."}]}, "Acts 13:38–39": {"title": "\"Everything the law could never set you right from\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The conclusion of Paul's first recorded sermon, and it is the sentence the rest of his letters unpack."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Antioch of Pisidia, in a synagogue, after the reading of the law and the prophets, at the invitation of the rulers. See the note on Acts 13:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just walked them through Israel's history — Egypt, the wilderness, the judges, Samuel, Saul, David — and arrived at the resurrection with three quotations from the Psalms and Isaiah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So let it be known to you, **brothers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is speaking to Jews and God-fearing Gentiles in their own building, on their own sabbath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **through this One that forgiveness of sins is announced to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kataggelletai** — proclaimed, announced publicly. It is a herald's word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not offered on terms. Announced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And by Him **everyone who believes is set right from everything the law of Moses could never set you right from**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **dikaioutai** — justified, declared in the right. It is the word the whole of Romans and Galatians turns on, and this is the first time Paul is recorded using it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence has two halves and the second one is a concession about the first system."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say the law justified nobody. He says there were things it could not reach, and that from **everything** it could not reach, everyone who believes is set right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Everyone who believes\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pas ho pisteuon** — the same construction he will use in Romans: *the righteousness of God through faith in Jesus Christ, to all and on all **who believe**. For there is no difference.* See the note on Romans 3:21–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Beware therefore, lest what has been spoken in the prophets come upon you: '**Behold, you despisers, marvel and perish!** For I work a work in your days, a work which you will by no means believe, though one were to declare it to you.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Habakkuk, and he ends the sermon on a warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the Jews had gone out of the synagogue, **the Gentiles begged them to speak these same words to them the next Sabbath**.* See the note on Acts 13:42–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the following week nearly the whole city turned up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul urged them to do in the meantime"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Talked with them and **urged them to continue in the grace of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Luke's summary of the follow-up conversation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same argument goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Galatians, to churches Paul planted on this same journey: *knowing that a man is not justified by the works of the law but by faith in Jesus Christ… for **by the works of the law no flesh shall be justified**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb, same conclusion, written to the region he was standing in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is announced and what is not.** Forgiveness is proclaimed, not offered on conditions. And the justifying is stated as covering the ground the older system never could — which is the whole of Paul's later argument, in one sentence, in a synagogue."}]}, "Acts 13:42–43": {"title": "\"Continue in the grace of God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The immediate reaction to Paul's first sermon, and the instruction he gives afterward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who asks for more"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the Jews had gone out of the synagogue, **the Gentiles begged them to speak these same words to them the next Sabbath**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parekaloun** — begged, urgently requested."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**These same words.** Not more, and not something adapted. The same ones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gentiles in the building — God-fearers who attended synagogue without becoming proselytes. Paul had addressed them directly in the sermon: *men of Israel, **and you who fear God***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been sitting at the edge of Israel's worship for years, and the sentence about being *set right from everything the law of Moses could never set you right from* was addressed to them too. See the note on Acts 13:38–39."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when the gathering broke up, **many of the Jews and of the devout converts to Judaism followed Paul and Barnabas**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both groups. Luke is careful to record that Jews responded as well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Devout converts** — the Greek is **proselytoi**, full converts who had taken on circumcision and the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conversation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **talked with them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proslalountes** — kept talking to them. It continued after the service."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **urged them to continue in the grace of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epimenein te chariti tou theou** — to remain in, to stay put in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Luke's one-line summary of the follow-up teaching, and it is worth noticing what it is not. Not *be careful now*, not a list of next steps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Continue** — the same verb Paul uses in Colossians: *if indeed you continue in the faith, grounded and steadfast.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Luke uses for the message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Charis** — grace. Luke uses it of the message repeatedly in Acts: *the word of His grace*, *the gospel of the grace of God*, and the church at Antioch commending Barnabas and Paul *to the grace of God for the work*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's own summary of his commission, to the Ephesian elders, uses the same phrase: *the ministry I received from the Lord Jesus — to testify to **the good news of the grace of God**.* See the note on Acts 20:22–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The following Sabbath **nearly the whole city gathered** to hear the word of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And with the crowd came the opposition. See the note on Acts 13:44–47."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records the same shape in city after city: a synagogue, a hearing, a large response, and then trouble from the same building."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they were told to do.** Not to add anything, and not to guard against the message. To stay in it — and Luke gives that as the whole of the follow-up instruction."}]}, "Acts 13:44–47": {"title": "\"Nearly the whole city\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second sabbath in Antioch of Pisidia, and the crowd is the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turnout"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The following Sabbath **nearly the whole city gathered to hear the word of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sermon, one week's word of mouth. See the note on Acts 13:42–43."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **when the Jews saw the crowds, they were filled with jealousy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zelou** — zeal, jealousy. The trigger Luke names is the size of the crowd, not the content."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had invited him to speak the week before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **contradicted what Paul was saying and spoke against it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **antelegon** — spoke against — and **blasphemountes**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Paul and Barnabas **spoke out boldly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parresiasamenoi** — from **parrhesia**, freedom of speech, the right to say anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It was **necessary that the word of God be spoken to you first**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states the priority as an obligation. He does it in every city — synagogue first — and he writes it into Romans: *to the Jew first and also to the Greek.* See the note on Romans 1:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The judgment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But since you **push it away and judge yourselves unworthy of eternal life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *push away* is **apotheisthe** — to thrust from oneself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Judge yourselves unworthy.** Paul does not pronounce them unworthy. He says the verdict is self-issued, and the word is **krinete** — you judge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look — **we are turning to the Gentiles**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The authority"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For this is what the Lord has commanded us: '**I have made you a light for the nations, so that you may carry salvation to the ends of the earth**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 49, spoken to the Servant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul applies it to himself and Barnabas — *the Lord has commanded **us***. He reads the Servant's commission as one he is inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simeon had quoted the same passage over the infant in the temple: *a light to bring revelation to the Gentiles, and the glory of Your people Israel.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now when the Gentiles heard this, **they were glad and glorified the word of the Lord**. And **the word of the Lord was being spread throughout all the region**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the Jews stirred up the devout and prominent women and the chief men of the city, raised up persecution against Paul and Barnabas, **and expelled them from their region**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But they shook off the dust from their feet against them, and came to Iconium. **And the disciples were filled with joy and with the Holy Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke ends the episode on the disciples' joy rather than on the expulsion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It repeats through Acts — Iconium, Lystra, Thessalonica, Berea, Corinth. Paul says it again at Corinth: *your blood be upon your own heads; I am clean. From now on I will go to the Gentiles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he still goes to the synagogue in the next city."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What turned them.** Not the sermon — they had asked for it and heard it out. Luke names the crowd. The following week nearly the whole city came, and that is the verse the jealousy is attached to."}]}, "Acts 13:9–12": {"title": "Cyprus and the sorcerer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first miracle of Paul's ministry is a judgment, and it happens in front of a Roman governor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change of name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Saul, who is also called Paul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has used *Saul* since chapter 7. From this verse on it is Paul, for the rest of the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both were his from birth — a Hebrew name and a Roman one, which was ordinary for a Jew with citizenship. Luke switches at the point the mission turns toward the Gentile world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And from here the pair is *Paul and Barnabas* rather than *Barnabas and Saul*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is opposing him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Elymas** the sorcerer, also called **Bar-Jesus** — son of Joshua. Luke calls him *a Jewish false prophet*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is attached to the household of Sergius Paulus, the proconsul, who had sent for Barnabas and Saul because he *sought to hear the word of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elymas *withstood them, seeking to turn the proconsul away from the faith*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The look"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Filled with the Holy Spirit, **looked straight at him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **atenisas** — to gaze intently, to fix the eyes. Luke uses it repeatedly: Stephen looking into heaven, the disciples watching the ascension, the crowd staring at Peter and John."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are **full of every kind of deceit and every kind of trickery**. You are **a son of the devil**, an **enemy of everything that is right**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *son of the devil* is **huie diabolou** — set against his own name, Bar-Jesus, son of Joshua."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Will you never stop **twisting the straight paths of the Lord**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *making crooked the straight ways*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is from Isaiah, quoted of John the Baptist: *make His paths straight*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look now — **the hand of the Lord is on you. You will be blind, unable even to see the sun, for a time**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**For a time** — the Greek is **achri kairou**, until a season. The blindness is given an end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had been struck blind himself on the Damascus road, and had spent three days without sight before Ananias came. See the note on Acts 9:3–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He pronounces on another man the thing that had happened to him, and includes the time limit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately **a mist and a darkness came over him**, and he **went groping about, looking for someone to lead him by the hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *mist* is **achlus**, a medical term for a clouding of the eye. Luke the physician uses it once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Looking for someone to lead him by the hand* — the Greek is **cheiragogous**, hand-leaders. It is the same word used of Paul being led into Damascus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the proconsul saw what had happened, **he believed, astonished at the teaching of the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says he was astonished at the **teaching**, not at the blindness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sergius Paulus is described as *an intelligent man*, and an inscription naming a Sergius Paulus has been found on Cyprus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they go next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Straight to the mainland, where John Mark leaves them and Paul preaches in a synagogue in Antioch of Pisidia. See the note on Acts 13:13–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the page.** A false prophet with a Jewish name blocking a Roman's hearing, and the first thing recorded of Paul under that name is a temporary blindness — the same one he was given himself."}]}, "Acts 15:6–9": {"title": "\"Cleansing their hearts by faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first church council, and the first speech is Peter's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being decided"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men had come down from Judea to Antioch teaching *unless you are circumcised according to the custom of Moses, you cannot be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul and Barnabas had *no small dissension and dispute* with them, and the matter was sent up to Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The procedure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The apostles and elders **came together to look into the matter**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both groups. It is a deliberation, not a ruling handed down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The debate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **a long discussion**, Peter stood up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *much disputing*. Luke does not summarise it, and he does not pretend it was brief or comfortable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter appeals to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Brothers, you know that **in the early days God made a choice among us**: that **by my mouth the Gentiles should hear the message** of the good news and believe.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Cornelius episode, roughly ten years earlier. He had already had to explain it once in Jerusalem. See the note on Acts 11:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The choosing is God's, and Peter describes himself as the mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **God, who knows the heart**, testified for them **by giving them the Holy Spirit just as He gave Him to us**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *who knows the heart* is **kardiognostes** — heart-knower. It appears twice in the New Testament, both times in Acts, both times about God choosing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evidence Peter brings is not an argument. It is something God did, and he says God is the one who testified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Just as He gave Him to us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is exact. Peter does not allow a two-tier arrangement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distinction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He made no distinction at all between us and them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *he discriminated nothing between*. It is the same verb Peter used of himself in chapter 11 — *the Spirit told me to go with them, **doubting nothing**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cleansing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cleansing their hearts by faith**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katharisas** — purified. It is the ritual word, the one for making something fit to approach God with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in the clause: what was cleansed, and how."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Their hearts** — not their diet, their calendar, or their bodies. The dispute was about an external mark, and Peter answers about an internal cleaning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**By faith** — the mechanism, stated in two words, with nothing else in the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now therefore, **why do you test God** by putting a yoke on the neck of the disciples **which neither our fathers nor we were able to bear?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the sentence the council turns on:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But we believe that **through the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ we shall be saved in the same manner as they**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is reversed. Not *they are saved like us*. **We** are saved like **them**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then all the multitude kept silent and listened to Barnabas and Paul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James then speaks, quotes Amos, and the letter goes out to Antioch with four requests and no circumcision."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What settles it.** Not a text and not a precedent. Peter reports that God gave the same Spirit and cleansed the same organ by the same means — and the room goes quiet."}]}, "Acts 16:14–15": {"title": "\"The Lord opened her heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first convert in Europe is a businesswoman at a prayer meeting by a river."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A woman named **Lydia**, a **seller of purple cloth** from the city of Thyatira, and she **worshipped God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Purple dye was the most expensive commodity in the ancient world — extracted a drop at a time from a shellfish, and reserved by law in some places for royalty. A dealer in it was a wealthy woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is from Thyatira in Asia Minor, and she is in Philippi, running a business abroad."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were at a river"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philippi had no synagogue. Ten Jewish men were required to form one, and the city evidently did not have them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So Paul goes down to the riverside on the Sabbath *where prayer was customarily made*, and finds a group of women."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gospel enters Europe at an informal women's prayer meeting outside the city gate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She was listening, and **the Lord opened her heart** to pay attention to what Paul was saying.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once, and Luke puts them side by side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She was listening — that is her. The Lord opened her heart — that is Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *opened* is the word used of opening a door, and elsewhere in Luke of opening the Scriptures on the Emmaus road and of the disciples' eyes being opened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul preached. Somebody else did the opening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her household"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **she and her household** had been baptised.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not just her. The Greek is her house — family, servants, dependents. The whole establishment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase turns up at the end of the same chapter, over the jailer: *believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and you will be saved — you and your household.* See the note on Acts 16:29–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two households in one chapter, at opposite ends of Philippi's social order: a purple merchant and a prison officer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did with her house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you have judged me faithful** to the Lord, come into my house and stay.\" And **she prevailed on us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She makes it a question of whether they think she is genuine, which is not a request anyone can easily refuse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *prevailed* is to compel, to force — the same verb the Emmaus two used on Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What her house became"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The church at Philippi. Paul's warmest letter is addressed to it, and the last lines of this chapter have him and Silas going back to *the house of Lydia* after the earthquake, to encourage the brethren, before leaving the city."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the money went.** Philippi is the church that sent Paul support again and again — *even in Thessalonica you sent aid once and again for my necessities* — and the one he thanks in Philippians for a gift carried by Epaphroditus. The church that funded him started in a wealthy woman's front room."}]}, "Acts 16:29–31": {"title": "\"You and your household\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prison officer at midnight, with a sword already in his hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had done that day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was the man who took Paul and Silas after the beating, *put them into the inner prison, and fastened their feet in the stocks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The inner prison is the deepest cell. The stocks were a punishment device as much as a restraint — the legs forced apart and held."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did that to two men whose backs had just been laid open with rods, and then went to bed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What woke him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the prisoners were listening to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the earthquake, the doors open, everyone's chains loose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He drew his sword to kill himself, because under Roman practice a jailer answered with his own life for escaped prisoners. Paul stopped him: *do yourself no harm, for we are all here.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody ran. That is the detail that brings him in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Trembling, he **fell down before Paul and Silas**… \"Sirs, **what must I do to be saved?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is on the floor in front of two men he had put in the stocks a few hours earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and you will be saved** — you and your household.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight words to the question. No conditions, no probation, no list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *make restitution*. Not *release us first*. Not *stop being a jailer*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And your household\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase used over Lydia at the start of the chapter. See the note on Acts 16:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse says they *spoke the word of the Lord to him and to all who were in his house* — the household hears it too, and then *he and all his family were baptised immediately*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did before the night was over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took them the same hour of the night and **washed their stripes**… and he set food before them, and **rejoiced, believing in God with all his household**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He washed the wounds he had caused, in the dark, and then fed them at his own table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the washing and the baptism in the same sentence: *he washed their stripes, and immediately he and all his family were baptised.* Water both ways, in the same hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two households, one chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wealthy purple merchant at a riverside prayer meeting, and a Roman jailer at midnight. The top and the bottom of that city, in one chapter, and both times Luke says *and her household*, *and his household*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Philippi cost them.** Beaten with rods, illegally — they were Roman citizens, and Paul says so the next morning. The magistrates who ordered it come down in person to ask them to leave quietly. Paul goes back to Lydia's house first."}]}, "Acts 16:6–8": {"title": "Guidance that arrives as a closed door", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three sentences, and almost nothing happens in them. Paul tries to go one way and cannot. He tries another and cannot. He ends up somewhere he was not aiming for. It is one of the most useful passages in the New Testament about being led, precisely because so little of it looks like leading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Spirit actually does here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the verbs:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**kept** from speaking the word in Asia", "**would not allow** them into Bithynia", "so they **passed by** Mysia and came down to Troas"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a word of positive instruction. No voice, no vision, no verse. The Spirit's whole contribution in these three sentences is to stop them, twice, and the narrative does not say how — Luke, who elsewhere reports angels and trances and voices in detail, does not tell us."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were being stopped from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the part worth sitting with. They were not being stopped from sin. They were being stopped from **preaching the gospel in Asia**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was nothing wrong with the plan. It was a good plan, made by good men, for the best possible reason. It was simply not the one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That should adjust how a closed door gets read. A blocked way is not proof that the intention was bad, and the passage never suggests Paul had made a mistake in wanting to go."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Troas is not a destination in this story; it is where you end up when you have run out of directions. And it is there — after two refusals and a journey that looks like wandering — that the vision of the man from Macedonia comes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order matters. The clear guidance arrives *last*, and it arrives to men who had kept moving while they only had the unclear kind. If they had stopped at the first refusal and waited for something positive, they would not have been at Troas to receive it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not license"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It would be easy to turn this into a rule: every obstacle is God redirecting you. Acts will not support that. In the same book Paul is stoned, jailed, shipwrecked and opposed at length, and none of those are read as the Spirit's redirection — he goes back to several of the cities he was driven out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference is not in the size of the obstacle. Luke simply tells us, in these two cases, whose hand it was. Most of the time nobody gets told, which is why the next verse matters as much as these: they were still walking when the answer came."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the word \"kept.\"** *Koluo* is an ordinary word for hindering or preventing — used of stopping someone crossing a street or forbidding an action. Luke does not dress it up. Whatever the experience was, he reports it as plainly as a road being shut."}]}, "Acts 17:22–25": {"title": "The altar to an unknown God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul in front of the Athenian council, and he opens with something he found on a walk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul stood up **in the middle of the Areopagus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mars Hill — the council that oversaw religion and morals in Athens. It had brought Socrates to trial four centuries earlier on a charge of introducing new gods."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is there on the same charge in substance: *he seems to be a proclaimer of foreign gods*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *while Paul waited for them at Athens, **his spirit was provoked within him** when he saw that the city was given over to idols.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paroxuneto** — the sharp-stirring word, the root of *paroxysm*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Men of Athens, I see that **in every way you are an unusually god-fearing people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **deisidaimonesterous** — a word that can be read as *very religious* or as *superstitious*, and Paul may have chosen it because it can carry both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He begins with an observation about them rather than an accusation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As I was walking through your city and **looking carefully at the things you worship**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had studied the shrines before speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The find"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I even found an altar with this inscription: **TO THE UNKNOWN GOD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Greek writers record altars in Athens *to unknown gods*, raised to cover any deity who had been overlooked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The One you worship without knowing Him** — that is **the One I am telling you about**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *your gods are wrong*. He takes their own admission of ignorance and fills it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *what you worship in ignorance, this I proclaim to you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The God who **made the world and everything in it**, being **Lord of heaven and earth**, **does not live in temples built by hands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing below the Parthenon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen had said the same at his trial — *the Most High does not dwell in temples made with hands* — and Paul had been holding the coats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Nor is He **served by human hands as though He needed anything**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosdeomenos** — as though needing something in addition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole ancient sacrificial economy assumed the gods needed feeding. Paul removes the premise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Since **it is He who gives to everyone life and breath and all things**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing goes up. Everything comes down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the speech goes on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes two Greek poets — *in Him we live and move and have our being*, and *we are also His offspring* — and argues from them that God cannot be an image of gold or silver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No Old Testament quotation in the whole address, which is unlike every other sermon in Acts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Truly, these times of ignorance God overlooked, but **now commands all men everywhere to repent**, because He has appointed a day on which He will judge the world in righteousness by the Man whom He has ordained. He has given assurance of this to all **by raising Him from the dead**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they heard of the resurrection of the dead, **some mocked**, while others said, \"We will hear you again on this matter.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*However, **some men joined him and believed**, among them Dionysius the Areopagite, a woman named Damaris, and others with them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A member of the council, and a named woman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he went next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After these things Paul departed from Athens and went to Corinth* — where he says he arrived *in weakness, and in fear, and in much trembling*, determined to know nothing but Christ crucified. See the note on 1 Corinthians 2:1–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he starts from.** An inscription admitting ignorance. Paul does not argue them out of their religion — he points at the one altar in the city that was left blank and says he has come to fill it in."}]}, "Acts 2:1–4": {"title": "Wind, fire, and languages", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three images in four verses, and every one of them had been used before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A sound from heaven like a great rushing wind, and it filled the whole house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is careful: a **sound like** wind. Nothing is described as blowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In both Hebrew and Greek the word for spirit is also the word for wind and breath. Ezekiel's valley of dry bones is raised when the breath comes; Jesus told Nicodemus the wind blows where it wishes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What looked like tongues of fire, separating and coming to rest on each one of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fire had marked God's presence before — the bush, the pillar, the mountain, the altar on Carmel. Always one fire, in one place, that the people stood back from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it divides, and a piece of it settles on every individual in the room. Nobody is standing at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The languages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Began to speak in other languages, as the Spirit gave them the words.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd outside is the point: fifteen nations are listed in the next paragraph, and each hears its own tongue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babel scattered people by confusing their language. This gathers people by overcoming it. Luke does not draw the comparison; he just lists the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sermon goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter stands up and explains it from Joel — *in the last days I will pour out my Spirit on all people* — and adds the detail from Psalm 16 about the Holy One not seeing decay."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This Jesus God has raised up, and we are all witnesses of it… He has poured out what you are seeing and hearing.* The wind and the fire are presented as evidence of an ascension. See the note on Acts 2:32–33."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The day it happened.** Pentecost was the feast of firstfruits of the wheat harvest, fifty days after Passover — and by tradition, the day the law was given at Sinai. Three thousand died the day the law came down at Sinai. Three thousand were added the day the Spirit came down here."}]}, "Acts 2:14–16": {"title": "\"It is only nine in the morning\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first Christian sermon opens by dealing with a heckle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who stands up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Peter stood up with the eleven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven, so Matthias has been added. Luke has just recorded the casting of lots."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**With the eleven** — Peter speaks, and the other eleven stand with him. It is presented as the group's statement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who denied three times in a courtyard fifty days earlier, in the same city, addressing the crowd that includes people who were there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told it would happen and told what to do afterward: *when you have turned back, strengthen your brothers.* See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The voice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **raised his voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes it. Three thousand respond by the end, so the crowd is large."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Men of Judea, and **all of you staying here in Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The visitors from fifteen countries listed a few verses earlier. See the note on Acts 2:9–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let me tell you what this is**. Listen carefully to my words.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *let this be known to you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He begins by taking responsibility for explaining an event nobody in the street can account for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heckle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some in the crowd had said *\"They are full of new wine.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**These men are not drunk, as you suppose — it is only nine in the morning**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the third hour of the day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Devout Jews fasted until the morning sacrifice on a feast day. Nobody had eaten, let alone been drinking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the plainest possible reply, and it is the entire rebuttal. He does not argue about the nature of the experience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pivot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No, this is what was spoken of through the prophet Joel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not explain the phenomenon. He identifies it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel 2, at length: *\"I will pour out of My Spirit on all flesh; your sons and your daughters shall prophesy… **and whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes the whole passage, including the wonders in heaven and the blood and fire and smoke, and ends on the last line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sermon goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three Old Testament passages, in order: Joel, then Psalm 16 (*You will not leave my soul in Hades*), then Psalm 110 (*The LORD said to my Lord, \"Sit at My right hand\"*). See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost the whole sermon is quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Him, being delivered by the determined purpose and foreknowledge of God, you have taken by lawless hands, have crucified, and put to death**; whom God raised up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves in one sentence — God's purpose and their hands, neither cancelling the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now when they heard this, **they were cut to the heart**, and said to Peter and the rest of the apostles, \"**Men and brethren, what shall we do?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And that day about three thousand were added."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the first sermon begins.** Not with a text and not with an appeal. With a man pointing at a clock."}]}, "Acts 2:17–18": {"title": "\"In the last days\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes Joel, and the small change he makes is the whole claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel wrote *and it shall come to pass **afterward***. See the note on Joel 2:28–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says *and it will happen **in the last days**, says God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names which afterward. The era Joel pointed at is the one they are standing in, and Peter says so in the first line of the quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pouring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **pour out My Spirit on all people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekcheo** — to pour out, empty. The same verb the Greek Old Testament uses in Joel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**All flesh.** In front of him is a crowd from fifteen nations. See the note on Acts 2:9–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four groups"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your **sons and your daughters** will prophesy, your **young men** will see visions, and your **old men** will dream dreams.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter reverses Joel's order of the last two — Joel has old men dreaming first. Neither order changes the point: both ends of a life are included."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The servants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yes, and on **My servants — the men and the women both** — in those days I will pour out My Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel says *the servants*. Peter says **My** servants, twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The addition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they will prophesy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That clause is not in Joel. Peter adds it to the second group."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel had said the sons and daughters would prophesy; Peter extends it to the slaves as well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is quoting it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some in the crowd had said *they are full of new wine*, and Peter answered with a clock — *it is only nine in the morning*. See the note on Acts 2:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this. He does not explain the phenomenon; he identifies it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he quotes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of Joel, unabridged: *wonders in heaven above and signs in the earth beneath: blood and fire and vapour of smoke. The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the coming of the great and awesome day of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not stop at the convenient part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he stops"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it shall come to pass that **whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last line of the quotation and the hinge of the sermon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the same line in Romans 10 and draws the same conclusion: *there is no distinction between Jew and Greek, for the same Lord over all is rich to all who call upon Him.* See the note on Romans 10:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sermon does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three Old Testament passages: Joel, then Psalm 16 on the resurrection, then Psalm 110 on the seating. See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost the whole sermon is quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then those who gladly received his word were baptized; and that day about **three thousand souls** were added to them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other three thousand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The day the law was given at Sinai, three thousand fell. See the note on Exodus 32:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two givings, and the same number counted on each day."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two words Peter changes.** Joel's *afterward* becomes *in the last days*, and *the servants* becomes *My servants*. He is dating the prophecy and naming who the slaves belong to."}]}, "Acts 2:32–33": {"title": "\"He has poured out what you are seeing and hearing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is explaining a phenomenon, and his explanation runs backwards from the noise in the street to a throne nobody present can see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*This Jesus God has raised up, and we are all witnesses of it*", "*lifted up to the right hand of God*", "*having received from the Father the promised Holy Spirit*", "*He has poured out what you are seeing and hearing*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four links: resurrection, ascension, receiving, pouring. Peter builds the whole argument to explain a sound and a sight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd can check"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What you are seeing and hearing.* They are not being asked to accept an invisible claim. Something audible and visible is happening in front of them, and Peter says: here is what it proves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evidence for the ascension, in his argument, is the Spirit's arrival. Nobody watched Jesus sit down at the right hand of God — but this is what it looks like from below."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Having received… He has poured out\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Son receives from the Father and gives to the people. Peter puts all three in one sentence without explanation, and the church spent centuries working out what he had said in passing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it mattered that day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd's first suggestion was that these people were drunk at nine in the morning. Peter's answer is not a defence of behaviour. It is a claim about location: the man you crucified is at God's right hand, and this is Him acting from there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the hearers do next.** *They were cut to the heart* and asked what to do — and Peter's answer includes *the promise is for you and your children and all who are far off.* The pouring out described here is presented as available to the crowd, not just to the hundred and twenty in the room."}]}, "Acts 2:40–41": {"title": "Three thousand added", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first day of the church, and Luke gives the number."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter kept saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said much more than this, and he kept urging them, \"Be rescued from this crooked generation.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells us the sermon we have is a summary — there was much more. And the plea he records is passive: *be rescued*, be saved. Not save yourselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd's own question had been *what shall we do?*, and the answer they were given was to turn and be baptised, and to receive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That day about three thousand people were added to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The figure is not chosen at random by history. Exodus 32 records three thousand falling on the day the law was given at Sinai — and Pentecost was, by long tradition, the anniversary of that giving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same feast, same number, and the verb reversed. Three thousand fell there; three thousand were **added** here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not comment. He was writing for people who knew the story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Added\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is the ordinary one for putting something to an existing total, and Luke will use it repeatedly through the early chapters — the Lord added to their number daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in Acts is described as joining. They are added, and the one doing the adding is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the day cost them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said about qualifications. They had, seven weeks earlier, been part of the city that called for a crucifixion — Peter says so to their faces two paragraphs before this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The response to that sermon was three thousand baptisms."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows immediately.** The famous four: the apostles' teaching, the fellowship, the breaking of bread, and the prayers. The number is not left as a headline; Luke goes straight on to what those three thousand then did every day."}]}, "Acts 2:46–47": {"title": "\"From house to house\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's picture of what the first weeks actually looked like, and it is domestic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two places"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Day after day they were together **in the temple courts**… and they broke bread **from house to house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, and they are different activities. The temple was public, Jewish, and enormous — the courts held tens of thousands. The houses were small."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The teaching and the praise happened in the open; the meals happened where people lived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Day after day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not weekly. Luke says it twice in two verses — daily in the temple, and the Lord adding to their number daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the shape of the early church was, it was not a Sunday event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Breaking bread"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase covers an ordinary meal and carries the memory of the Last Supper, and Luke has just used it a few verses earlier as one of the four things they devoted themselves to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not separate the two. They *ate their meals with glad and generous hearts* — the eating and the remembering are described in one sentence, at somebody's table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words for how"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**glad** — the Greek is a strong word for rejoicing, exultation", "**generous** — literally *simplicity* of heart, the same root as the word behind God giving *with both hands* in James 1:5. Single-minded, without a hidden second motive."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how Luke describes the atmosphere: glad and unguarded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Praising God and finding favour with all the people. And the Lord added to the church daily.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The favour is with outsiders, and the adding is God's doing. Neither is presented as the goal of the meals — both are reported as what followed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the picture.** No building, no programme, no name for the movement yet. A temple courtyard, other people's houses, and a shared table every day."}]}, "Acts 2:9–11": {"title": "The list of countries", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke stops the narrative to write out a table of nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the list is there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could have written *people from everywhere*. Instead he gives fifteen entries by name, and the naming is the point — every one of them heard their own language."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The geography"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list moves roughly in a circle, east to west and back:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Parthians, Medes, Elamites** — the far east, beyond the empire, in what is now Iran.", "**Mesopotamia** — where Abraham came from.", "**Judea** — the middle, and the odd one on the list.", "**Cappadocia, Pontus, Asia, Phrygia, Pamphylia** — Asia Minor, and every one of them turns up later in Acts or the letters. Peter addresses his first letter to four of them.", "**Egypt, Libya toward Cyrene** — North Africa. Simon who carried the cross was from Cyrene.", "**Visitors from Rome** — the capital, *both Jews and converts to the Jewish faith*.", "**Cretans and Arabs** — the last two, and the least expected."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jews of the diaspora, in Jerusalem for the feast, plus proselytes — Gentiles who had come into Judaism."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most of them would have spoken Greek or Aramaic well enough to get by. The languages they hear are the ones from home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We are hearing them tell the wonderful works of God** in our own tongues!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta megaleia tou theou** — the great things, the magnificent deeds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the list reports hearing a sermon, an instruction or an appeal. What they hear is the works of God being told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *tell* is the ordinary word for speaking. The content is God's own acts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's sermon comes afterward, in Aramaic or Greek, and it is a separate thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babel is the other passage where languages multiply in one event, and there the result is that nobody understands anybody and the people scatter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the languages multiply and everybody understands, and the people gather."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke never mentions Babel. He writes out the table of nations and leaves it there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The feast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pentecost — **Shavuot**, fifty days after Passover, the wheat harvest, and the feast at which two loaves *baked with leaven* were waved before the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jewish tradition also counted it as the anniversary of the giving of the law at Sinai."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two crowds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were all amazed and perplexed, saying to one another, \"Whatever could this mean?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Others mocking said, \"They are full of new wine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both reactions are recorded in the next verses, from the same event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three thousand were added that day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Sinai, on the day the law was given and the calf was made, three thousand fell. See the note on Exodus 32:25–29."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the list is for.** Luke is a careful writer and he spends three verses on place names. Every one is a claim that a specific person heard the great things of God in the language they grew up speaking."}]}, "Acts 20:17–21": {"title": "\"I kept back nothing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is in a hurry to reach Jerusalem, so he sends for the elders rather than going to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **Miletus** he sent to **Ephesus** and called for the elders of the church.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty miles each way. He is skipping Ephesus deliberately — Luke says *he was hurrying to be at Jerusalem, if possible, on the Day of Pentecost* — and the elders walk to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the only speech in Acts addressed to a Christian congregation. Every other one is to a crowd, a court, or a synagogue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The record"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You know how I lived among you the whole time, from the first day I set foot in Asia**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He appeals to what they saw. He had been in Ephesus about three years, longer than anywhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Serving the Lord **with a humble heart**, **with tears**, and **through the trials that came to me by the plots of the Jews**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He mentions the tears three times in this speech — here, in verse 31 about warning them night and day, and at the end when they weep on his neck."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I kept back nothing that would help you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupostello** — to draw back, to shrink from, to withhold. It was used of furling a sail and of a person keeping quiet out of fear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again in verse 27: *I have not shunned to declare to you the whole counsel of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he taught"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But told it to you and taught you **publicly and from house to house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two settings. The hall of Tyrannus, where Luke says he reasoned daily for two years, and the houses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he taught"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Testifying to both Jews and Greeks**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same message to both, which was not obvious in that city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"About **a change of mind toward God**, and **faith toward our Lord Jesus Christ**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He compresses three years of teaching into two items."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A change of mind** — the Greek is **metanoia**, from *meta*, change, and *nous*, mind. It is a word about what a person thinks, and the direction is given: *toward God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Faith toward our Lord Jesus Christ** — the second half, and the object is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And see, now I go bound in the spirit to Jerusalem, **not knowing the things that will happen to me** there, except that the Holy Spirit testifies in every city, saying that **chains and tribulations await me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *\"**But none of these things move me**; nor do I count my life dear to myself, so that I may finish my race with joy.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the meeting ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He knelt down and prayed with them all. Then they all wept freely, and **fell on Paul's neck and kissed him**, sorrowing most of all for the words which he spoke, that **they would see his face no more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke was there — the passage is in the *we* sections — and this is one of the few scenes in Acts he describes as an eyewitness to the emotion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one saying of Jesus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul closes with a line found nowhere in the Gospels: *\"**It is more blessed to give than to receive**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts preserves it and nothing else does."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the speech is.** A man laying out three years of work in front of the people who watched him do it, and inviting them to check. The summary of the content is two phrases long."}]}, "Acts 20:22–24": {"title": "\"None of these things move me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of his farewell to the Ephesian elders, Paul says where he is going and what he expects there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The compulsion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am going to Jerusalem **under a compulsion in the spirit I cannot shake**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dedemenos to pneumati** — bound in the spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word for being in chains. He is describing himself as already tied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ignorance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Not knowing what will happen to me there**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing he does know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Except that **the Holy Spirit testifies to me in every city that chains and hardships are waiting for me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "City after city, the same message."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records one instance in the next chapter: Agabus takes Paul's belt at Caesarea, binds his own hands and feet, and says *so shall the Jews at Jerusalem bind the man who owns this belt*. And the whole company begs him not to go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer there: *what do you mean by weeping and breaking my heart? For I am ready not only to be bound, but also to die at Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **none of these things move me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *I make my life of no account* — the older versions have *none of these things move me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The valuation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nor do I count my life dear to myself**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **timian** — precious, valuable. He declines to price it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that I may finish my course with joy**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dromon** — a race course, a track. Paul uses it of himself three times, and the last is in his final letter: *I have fought the good fight, **I have finished the race**, I have kept the faith.* See the note on 2 Timothy 4:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**With joy.** He puts the two in the same clause — a course he expects chains at the end of, finished with joy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the ministry I received from the Lord Jesus** — to testify to **the good news of the grace of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names his own commission in six words, and the noun at the centre of it is grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the outcome, the outcome's fairness, or a request for it to change."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been told what is waiting. He states it and keeps going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He reached Jerusalem, was seized in the temple, nearly beaten to death, rescued by Roman troops, and stopped a flogging by asking one legal question. See the note on Acts 22:25–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then two years in Caesarea, an appeal to Caesar, a shipwreck, and Rome — where Acts leaves him *preaching the kingdom of God and teaching the things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ **with all confidence, no one forbidding him***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He got to Rome in chains, as promised, and Luke's last word about it is *no one forbidding him*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was told and what he did.** Chains and hardships, confirmed in every city he passed through — and he says the only thing he wants is to finish the course and deliver the message he was given."}]}, "Acts 20:28–31": {"title": "\"Purchased with His own blood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The charge Paul leaves with the Ephesian elders, and the order of it is deliberate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Keep watch over yourselves** and over all the flock.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Themselves first. The Greek is **prosechete** — pay attention to, hold your mind on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same order as Hezekiah's instruction to the Levites: consecrate yourselves, and then the house. See the note on 2 Chronicles 29:3–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who appointed them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In which **the Holy Spirit has made you overseers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **episkopous** — overseers, bishops."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses three words for the same men in one sentence: **presbuteroi** (elders, in verse 17), **episkopoi** (overseers), and the verb **poimainein**, to shepherd. In the New Testament they are one office."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say the church appointed them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To **shepherd the church of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Poimainein** — to tend, to pasture. It is what Jesus told Peter to do on the beach: *tend My sheep*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Which **He purchased with His own blood**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **periepoiesato** — acquired, obtained for oneself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is one of the strongest statements in the New Testament about who died: the church of **God**, purchased with **His own** blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some manuscripts read *the church of the Lord*; the difficulty is in the text and has been noted since the early centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason for the watching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I know that **after I am gone, savage wolves will come in among you**, and they **will not spare the flock**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lukoi bareis** — heavy, grievous wolves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus had used the image: *beware of false prophets, who come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravenous wolves*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The worse half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Even from among your own selves men will rise up**, saying twisted things in order to **draw the disciples away after them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not only from outside. Paul tells the men in front of him that some of them are the problem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Saying twisted things** — the Greek is **diestrammena**, distorted, turned aside. The same root Paul used of Elymas *twisting the straight paths of the Lord*. See the note on Acts 13:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**After them** — the object of the drawing is named, and it is the teachers themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **stay awake**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gregoreite** — the word Jesus used in Gethsemane, and Peter uses in his letter about the lion. See the note on 1 Peter 5:8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The record"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And remember that for **three years, night and day, I did not stop warning every one of you with tears**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years — the longest Paul stayed anywhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Every one of you** — the Greek is *each one*, individually."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**With tears** — the third mention of them in this speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he leaves them with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *and now, brethren, **I commend you to God and to the word of His grace**, which is able to build you up and to give you an inheritance.* See the note on Acts 20:32–35."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wolves are coming, and what he hands them is a word."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the danger is located.** Outside and inside, and Paul spends more of the warning on the second. He is speaking to the overseers, and he tells them some of the men in the room will be the ones drawing people away."}]}, "Acts 20:32–35": {"title": "The word of His grace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last words to the Ephesian elders, and he hands them over to something rather than somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The handover"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now, brothers, **I commit you to God and to the word of His grace**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paratithemai** — to deposit, to entrust, as you would leave valuables with someone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is leaving and knows he will not be back — he has just told them *you all… will see my face no more*. See the note on Acts 20:17–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not appoint a successor or leave instructions. He deposits them with two things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word can do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Which is able to build you up** and to **give you an inheritance among all those who are set apart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs attached to the word:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Build up** — **oikodomesai**, house-building. The same word Paul uses throughout 1 Corinthians 14 about a meeting. See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:1–5.", "**Give an inheritance** — the word is credited with doing the giving."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wanted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have wanted no one's silver or gold or clothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Clothing is on the list because in that world cloth was wealth. Achan's confession begins with a Babylonian garment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You yourselves know that **these hands provided for my own needs** and for those who were with me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is speaking to men who had watched him do it. Paul was a tentmaker, and Acts 18 has him working with Aquila and Priscilla in Corinth at the same trade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not only his own needs — *and for those who were with me*. He funded the team."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In everything I showed you that **by working like this we must help the weak**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **antilambanesthai** — to take hold of in support. The same root Paul uses in Romans 8 of the Spirit taking hold of the other end of the load. See the note on Romans 8:26–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He worked so that he could give."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **remember the words of the Lord Jesus**, how He said, '**It is more blessed to give than to receive**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not in any Gospel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts preserves it and nothing else does — one sentence of Jesus that survived only because Paul quoted it to a group of elders on a beach at Miletus, and Luke was listening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*More blessed **to give** than **to receive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a straight comparative. Both are blessed; one more so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when he had said these things, **he knelt down and prayed with them all**. Then they all wept freely, and **fell on Paul's neck and kissed him**, sorrowing most of all for the words which he spoke, that they would see his face no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they accompanied him to the ship.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke was there — the passage is in the *we* sections — and this is one of the few scenes in Acts where he records the emotion of a farewell."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he leaves them.** Not a structure and not a name. God, and the word of His grace — and he says outright that the word is the thing capable of building them and giving them the inheritance."}]}, "Acts 20:7–12": {"title": "The young man in the window", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke was there for this one — the *we* in the passage is his — and he tells it with a doctor's eye and a slight glint of humour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the first day of the week, when the disciples came together to break bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the earliest indications of Christians meeting on Sunday, and of what they met for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There were many lamps burning in the upper room.* Luke mentions it because it matters: many oil lamps in a crowded upper room, at night, burning the air. A young man in a window is looking for cooler air."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul talking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was leaving the next day, and he kept on talking until midnight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not defend him. The word he uses for the sinking sleep is a medical term — being borne down, overcome by degrees. Eutychus does not doze off carelessly; he is gradually overwhelmed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He fell from the third floor, and **they picked him up dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, a physician, states it flatly. Not *as though dead*. Dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul went down, bent over him, and took him in his arms.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The posture is Elijah's over the widow's son, and Elisha's over the Shunammite's boy — stretching himself on the child. Paul repeats an old gesture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be alarmed. His life is in him.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he went back up, broke bread and ate, and talked with them a long while, until daybreak, and then he left.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He resumes. The meeting continues, the bread is broken, and the preaching runs till dawn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy is mentioned once more, in the last line: *they brought the young man home alive, and were greatly comforted.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "His name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Eutychus** — from *eu*, well, and *tuche*, fortune. It means **good fortune**, **fortunate**, **lucky**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a common slave name in the Greek world; owners gave it the way a person might name a child Prosper. Luke, who names very few of the people in these travel sections, names this one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A boy called Good Fortune falls asleep, drops three floors, and is picked up dead. Then he is carried home alive, and the name holds after all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three floors"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke marks the height twice — the lamps in the upper room, and *he fell from the third floor*. He wants the reader to know how far it was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sleep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek moves through two words. First **katapheromenos** — being borne down, sinking by degrees, the medical term for going under. Then **hupnos**, deep sleep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is a doctor describing a descent in stages, and then a fall."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Luke leaves out.** Any comment. No sermon on the meaning of it, no rebuke of the boy, no reflection on the length of the sermon. A death, a raising, and then breakfast — reported by a man who was in the room."}]}, "Acts 22:22–24": {"title": "The word they stopped at", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke marks the exact syllable where a crowd turns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is standing on the steps of the Antonia fortress, having just been dragged out of the temple and beaten. He asked the commander for permission to speak, motioned with his hand, and addressed the mob in Hebrew — at which *they kept all the more silent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He then told them his whole story: Tarsus, Gamaliel, the persecution, the road, the light, Ananias."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They had **listened to him up to that word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it precisely. The whole testimony was heard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word was the last one of the previous verse: *\"Depart, for I will send you far from here **to the Gentiles**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they shouted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get this man off the earth** — he should not be allowed to live!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *away with such a fellow from the earth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what a crowd in the same city had shouted about somebody else, in front of Pilate, a little over twenty years earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shouting and **throwing off their coats** and **flinging dust into the air**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both gestures are Middle Eastern expressions of outrage. Dust thrown upward is what you do when you cannot reach the person you want to stone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the coats: at a stoning the witnesses laid their garments down. Paul had held them once, at Stephen's — and he had said so in this very speech, four verses earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is watching the coats come off again, and he described the scene from memory to the people now doing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commander's response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The commander ordered Paul **brought into the barracks** and said he should be **examined under the lash**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Claudius Lysias does not speak Hebrew. He has heard a man address a mob in a language he does not know and watched the mob try to kill him for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lash"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mastix** — the **flagellum**, the scourge with weighted leather thongs. It was an interrogation instrument, not a punishment: the theory was that a man under it would say anything true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that he could **find out why they were shouting** against him like this.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He wants information, and torture is the standard method for getting it out of a non-citizen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Is it lawful for you to flog a Roman citizen who has not even been condemned?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He waits until they are tying him up, and then asks a question. See the note on Acts 22:25–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Roman law said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Lex Porcia and the Lex Julia forbade scourging a citizen, and forbade binding one without trial. Claiming citizenship falsely was a capital offence, so a claim was generally believed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had let this happen to him before without objecting — at Philippi he was beaten with rods and only mentioned his citizenship the next morning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How far the speech got.** Everything — the arrest warrants, the light, the voice, the blindness, Ananias — was heard in silence. The sentence that broke it was about who the message was going to."}]}, "Acts 22:25–26": {"title": "The question asked while they were tying him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He does not shout, argue or resist. He asks a legal question of the man nearest him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As they were **tying him up for it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The straps are already on. Luke puts the sentence in the middle of the preparation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he speaks to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul said **to the centurion standing there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to the commander who gave the order, and not to the soldiers doing the work. To the officer supervising — the man legally responsible for what is about to happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is it lawful for you to flog a Roman citizen who has not even been condemned?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a claim. A question about the law, put to a man whose career depends on the answer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two illegalities are named in one sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**A Roman citizen** — the Lex Porcia forbade scourging one.", "**Who has not even been condemned** — **akatakriton**, uncondemned. No trial, no verdict, no sentence."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not appeal to his innocence, his mission, or his God. He cites Roman statute."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had every opportunity to refuse this on other grounds and used the one that would work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The centurion's reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the centurion heard that, he **went to the commander and reported it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately, and he leaves the prisoner where he is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be careful what you are about to do**, because this man is a Roman.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A junior officer warning his superior. The Greek is *see what you are about to do* — watch yourself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The penalties were real. Scourging a citizen could end a commander's career, and worse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the claim was believed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Falsely claiming citizenship was a capital offence. That made an assertion of it generally credible — nobody said it lightly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Paul uses about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Rhomaios** — a Roman. He was a Jew of Tarsus, a Pharisee trained under Gamaliel, and a citizen of the empire, and he uses each identity where it counts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had opened the speech before the mob in Hebrew and told them *I am a Jew, born in Tarsus of Cilicia*. Twenty minutes later he tells a centurion he is a Roman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one time he did not use it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Philippi he and Silas were *beaten with rods* and thrown into the inner prison, and he said nothing about citizenship until the next morning — when he used it to make the magistrates come and escort them out personally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commander comes and asks him directly, and finds out he was born to it while the commander himself had to buy it. See the note on Acts 22:27–29."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the question achieved.** He was still bound when he asked it, and the flogging never happened. Luke's account moves from the straps going on to the men stepping away in the space of four verses."}]}, "Acts 22:27–29": {"title": "\"But I was born with it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short exchange between a prisoner and the officer holding him, and the ranks reverse inside three sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commander comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The commander came and said to him, \"**Tell me, are you a Roman?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He comes down himself. He does not send for Paul, and Paul is still tied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Claudius Lysias is a **chiliarch**, commander of a thousand men, the senior Roman officer in Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said, \"**Yes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word. Luke gives him nothing else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost the commander"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It cost me a large sum of money** to buy this citizenship.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *with a great sum I obtained this citizenship*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Citizenship was for sale under Claudius, and the trade was notorious — the emperor's freedmen and his wife were known to be selling it. The commander's name, **Claudius** Lysias, is almost certainly the emperor's name taken at purchase, which was the custom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a Greek who bought his way in, and he mentions the price."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul's reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Paul said, \"**But I was born with it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *but I was even born so*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six words, and they settle the question of rank. Inherited citizenship outranked purchased citizenship socially and absolutely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man in chains has more standing than the man who put him in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Paul had it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke never explains. Tarsus was a free city but that did not confer citizenship; someone in the family must have been granted or bought it a generation or more earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul mentions it three times in Acts and never in a letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the men who were about to examine him **stepped away from him at once**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is they *departed from him*. The straps are dropped where they stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commander's own state"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the commander himself was afraid** when he realized that **he had put a Roman in chains**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the flogging he nearly ordered. The binding, which had already happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Merely chaining a citizen without trial was an offence. He is frightened of what he has already done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He keeps him — but the next morning he releases him from the chains and convenes the Sanhedrin to find out the charge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when a plot to ambush Paul is discovered, this same commander sends him to Caesarea under escort: two hundred soldiers, seventy horsemen, two hundred spearmen, at nine o'clock at night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Roman army moves one prisoner out of Jerusalem with four hundred and seventy men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letter he writes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This man was seized by the Jews and was about to be killed by them. Coming with the troops I rescued him, **having learned that he was a Roman**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rearranges the order of events slightly in his own favour. Luke includes the letter anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the citizenship takes him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two years later, in front of Festus: *I appeal to Caesar.* And the appeal is what puts him on a ship to Rome."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told he would go: *as you have testified for Me in Jerusalem, so you must also bear witness at Rome.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it was used for.** Paul does not use the citizenship to get out. He uses it to stop a flogging, and then again to appeal — and the appeal sends him further into custody, all the way to the capital."}]}, "Acts 23:1–3": {"title": "\"You whitewashed wall\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul in front of the council, and the exchange goes badly within two sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brothers, I have lived my life before God with a completely clear conscience up to this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses the Sanhedrin as equals — *brothers*, not *rulers* — and claims a clear conscience, which is not a modest thing to say to a court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is struck on the mouth for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is going to strike you, you whitewashed wall. You sit there to judge me according to the law, and you break the law by ordering me to be struck?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The legal point is exact. Striking an unconvicted defendant was illegal, and Paul names the contradiction precisely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The insult is also exact. **Whitewashed wall** — clean on the outside, structurally unsound; the same image Jesus used of whitewashed tombs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bystanders tell him he is reviling God's high priest, and Paul answers: *I did not know, brothers, that he was the high priest* — and then quotes Exodus about not speaking evil of a ruler of your people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not defend the outburst. He retracts it on the spot, on the basis of a text."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Luke includes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not have to. This is Paul at his least impressive — losing his temper, and then discovering he had insulted the wrong man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports his hero's failures the way the Gospels report Peter's."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whether he really did not know.** Readers have wondered for centuries: poor eyesight, a long absence from Jerusalem, or an irregular seating arrangement. The text does not say. What it does record is a man who spoke first, was corrected, and immediately took it back — which is the more useful half of the story."}]}, "Acts 26:4–8": {"title": "The wolf in the evening", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last recorded defence, before a king, and he turns it into something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is in the room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Agrippa, Bernice, the governor Festus, the commanders and the prominent men of Caesarea, *with great pomp*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul, in chains, given permission to speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about his past"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They could testify, if they were willing, that **I lived as a Pharisee, by the strictest party of our religion**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften it. Elsewhere he puts it harder: *I persecuted this Way to the death, binding and delivering into prisons both men and women.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benjamin's blessing was one line: *in the morning he devours the prey, and in the evening he divides the spoil.* Paul names his own tribe twice in his letters, and the morning is what Luke describes with the word used of an animal mangling a carcass. See the note on Genesis 49:27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says he is on trial for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now I stand here on trial **for the hope of the promise God made to our fathers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for a crime, a riot, or a sect. For a hope, and he says it is the hope *our twelve tribes* are serving God night and day to see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts himself on the same side as his accusers and says the disagreement is about whether it has happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks the king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why should it be judged something incredible by any of you that God raises the dead?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner asking the court a question. It is not a defence — nothing in it is about his innocence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with the hearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them about the road to Damascus: the light above the brightness of the sun, the voice, and the commission — *to open their eyes, to turn them from darkness to light… that they may receive forgiveness of sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Festus interrupts him: *Paul, you are beside yourself! Much learning is driving you mad.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then Agrippa: *you almost persuade me to become a Christian.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I would to God that not only you, but also all who hear me today, might become both almost and altogether such as I am — **except for these chains**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is in irons, and he wishes the whole room were as he is. The only thing he excludes is the chains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the evening dividing the spoil. Everything he took in the morning he is handing out, from a prisoner's dock, to the men who are trying him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Agrippa concluded.** *This man might have been set free if he had not appealed to Caesar.* He appealed, and it took him to Rome — which is where he wrote the letters. Philippians, Ephesians, Colossians and Philemon come out of that appeal."}]}, "Acts 27:21–26": {"title": "The angel who stood by me", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner stands up in a sinking ship and tells the crew what is going to happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen days in a storm. Luke, who is aboard, describes it: they had thrown the cargo over, then the ship's tackle with their own hands, and *when neither sun nor stars appeared for many days, and no small tempest beat on us, all hope that we would be saved was finally given up*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they had **gone a long time without food**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not fasting by choice. Nobody could cook or keep anything down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who stands up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul **stood up among them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner in chains, on his way to trial in Rome, addressing the crew, the soldiers and the centurion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reproach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Men, **you should have listened to me** and not put out from Crete.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had warned them: *I perceive that this voyage will end with disaster and much loss.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the centurion was more persuaded by the helmsman and the owner of the ship than by the things spoken by Paul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which was the reasonable decision. A tentmaker's opinion against the captain's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He mentions it once and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But now I urge you: take heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Not one life among you will be lost — only the ship**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two hundred and seventy-six people aboard, by Luke's count."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The source"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Last night an angel of the God whose I am and whom I serve stood beside me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses about ownership and service, in a sentence to a shipful of pagans."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the angel said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid, Paul. You must stand before Caesar**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told this in Jerusalem two years earlier: *as you have testified for Me in Jerusalem, so you must also bear witness at Rome.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And look, **God has granted you the lives of all who are sailing with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kecharistai** — from **charis**, grace. Has graciously given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two hundred and seventy-five other people are described as given to Paul."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **take heart**, men. **I trust God that it will turn out exactly as I was told**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *take heart* twice in six verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **we are going to run aground on some island**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not promise a smooth ending. The ship is lost and they hit land somewhere unspecified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens later that night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sailors try to escape in the lifeboat under pretence of laying anchors, and Paul tells the centurion: *unless these men stay in the ship, **you cannot be saved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soldiers cut the ropes and let the boat fall away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are true in the chapter: God has granted the lives, and the men have to stay aboard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The breakfast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And while day was coming on, Paul implored them all to take food… **and he took bread and gave thanks to God in the presence of them all**, and when he had broken it he began to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they were all encouraged, and also took food themselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says grace over a meal in a wrecking ship, and everybody eats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so it was that **they all escaped safely to land**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they land"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Malta. See the note on Acts 28:1–6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who is giving the orders.** A prisoner, after a fortnight of storm, on the strength of a night visit — and Luke, who was on the ship, records that the count came out exactly as he said."}]}, "Acts 27:9–12": {"title": "The prisoner who was right, and the vote that went the other way", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A ship's council, a prisoner's opinion, and a decision made the sensible way that turns out to be wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was in the room"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the pilot** — the professional navigator", "**the ship's owner** — the money", "**the centurion** — the authority, and the man who had to decide", "**Paul** — a prisoner in chains, with no standing whatsoever"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes that the centurion listened to the first two rather than the last, and that *most of them were in favour* of sailing. Expertise, ownership and the majority all lined up on one side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the timing mattered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sailing had now become dangerous because the Fast was already over* — the Day of Atonement, which puts this in late September or October. Ancient sailing in that sea effectively shut down for the winter, and everyone aboard knew it. The question was not whether it was risky but whether they could make one short hop to a better harbour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a reasonable call. It was also the wrong one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage does not do.** It never says God told Paul. It says *Paul warned them* and reports what he could see. Later in the chapter an angel does speak to him, and Luke says so plainly — he distinguishes the two. This part of the story is a man's judgement being right while the experts were wrong, and Luke lets that stand without dressing it up as prophecy."}]}, "Acts 28:1–6": {"title": "The fire on Melita", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two hundred and seventy-six people come ashore, and the first thing that happens is a bonfire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The island"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Once we were safe, we learned that the island was called **Melita**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Malta. The name may come from a Phoenician root for refuge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**We** — Luke is there. This is one of the eyewitness sections."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The welcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people there showed us **unusual kindness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **philanthropian** — the root of *philanthropy*, love of humankind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke calls them **barbaroi** — the standard Greek word for anyone who did not speak Greek. It carried no moral weight; it meant *foreign*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **built a fire and welcomed us all**, because **it had begun to rain and it was cold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Late autumn, after fourteen days at sea and a swim ashore."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul had **gathered an armful of sticks**, and as he laid them on the fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An apostle picking up firewood, with a Roman guard, after a shipwreck."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes it because of what comes next, and it is also just what Paul did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The snake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **viper driven out by the heat fastened itself on his hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathepsen** — fastened, took hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the islanders concluded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This man must be a murderer. He escaped the sea, but justice will not let him live**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek names her: **he Dike** — Justice, a goddess in Greek religion, daughter of Zeus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their theology is coherent. A man survives a shipwreck and is bitten within the hour; something is clearly pursuing him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul **shook the creature off into the fire and suffered no harm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apotinaxas** — shook off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No comment, no sermon, and no reaction recorded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wait"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They kept **waiting for him to swell up or suddenly fall down dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the two expected symptoms, and he is a doctor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But after **waiting a long time** and seeing that **nothing out of the ordinary happened to him**, they **changed their minds and said he was a god**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metabalomenoi** — turned round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From murderer to deity in one paragraph, on the same evidence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened before at Lystra"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd called Paul and Barnabas Zeus and Hermes and tried to sacrifice to them — and minutes later stoned Paul and dragged him out of the city for dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records both swings without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief man of the island, Publius, whose father was *sick of a fever and dysentery* — Luke's terms — and Paul prays and lays hands on him and he is healed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So when this was done, the rest of those on the island who had diseases also came and were healed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months on Malta."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark's ending says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They will take up serpents; and if they drink anything deadly, it will by no means hurt them.\"* See the note on Mark 16:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not connect them. He reports what happened at a bonfire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rome, in his own rented house, *preaching the kingdom of God and teaching the things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ with all confidence, **no one forbidding him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last sentence of Acts."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the islanders got right and wrong.** Both times they reasoned from the same event. A man bitten must be guilty; a man unharmed must be divine — and in between, Paul shook it into the fire and went on stacking wood."}]}, "Acts 3:9–11": {"title": "\"They recognized him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The crowd's reaction, and the thing Luke keeps in the frame is that everybody knew the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they see"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the people saw him **walking and praising God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once. He had gone into the temple *walking, leaping, and praising God* two verses earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The recognition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they recognized him** — this was **the man who used to sit begging at the Beautiful Gate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they knew him thoroughly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was a fixture. Luke has already said he was *carried* there **daily**, and Peter's sermon a chapter later notes he was *over forty years old*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone entering that gate had walked past him for decades."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no question about identity. Nobody in the courtyard has to be persuaded that this is the same man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Sanhedrin says as much the next day: *what shall we do to these men? For, indeed, that a notable miracle has been done through them is evident to all who dwell in Jerusalem, and **we cannot deny it**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Beautiful Gate — the entrance from the court of the Gentiles into the temple proper. Josephus describes a great bronze gate on the east side that took twenty men to close."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was placed at the door of the building every day and never went in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they were **filled with wonder and amazement** at what had happened to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thambos** and **ekstasis** — astonishment, and being put out of one's place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the man does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the healed man kept hold of Peter and John**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is imperfect: he was holding on to them, continuously, and would not let go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An hour earlier he had asked them for money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Silver and gold I do not have, but what I do have I give you: **in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, rise up and walk**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *he took him by the right hand and lifted him up, and immediately his feet and ankle bones received strength.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the crowd runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the people came running to them in **the covered walkway called Solomon's Colonnade**, utterly astonished.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same portico Jesus had been walking in at the Feast of Dedication. See the note on John 10:22–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters later the whole church is meeting there: *they were all with one accord in **Solomon's Porch**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter does with the crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Turns them away from himself in the first sentence:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Men of Israel, **why do you marvel at this? Or why look so intently at us, as though by our own power or godliness we had made this man walk?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *His name, through faith in His name, has made this man strong.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it costs them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That evening Peter and John are arrested, and spend the night in custody. Five thousand men had believed by then."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why Luke keeps the man in the shot.** He is standing next to Peter throughout the sermon, holding on. When the council convenes the next morning the record notes that *the man who had been healed was standing with them* — and there was nothing to say."}]}, "Acts 4:32–35": {"title": "Everything held in common", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's second summary of the young church, and every clause in it is worth slowing down over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "One heart and soul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The whole company of believers was **one in heart and soul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is literally *of one heart and one soul* — a phrase used in Greek literature of the closest friendships, applied here to thousands of people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said about property"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not one of them said that anything he owned was **his alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is precise. He does not say they had no possessions. He says nobody used the word *mine* about what he had."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The change is in the pronoun before it is in the ledger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Great power, great grace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And with **great power** the apostles kept giving their witness to the resurrection of the Lord Jesus, and **great grace** was on them all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two adjectives are the same Greek word, and Luke uses it twice in one sentence — great power on the preaching, great grace on everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The power was on the apostles. The grace was on all of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not a needy person among them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is an echo. Deuteronomy 15 promised Israel *there shall be no poor among you* if they kept the covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports it as an accomplished fact in Jerusalem, and the mechanism he describes is not a law. It is people selling things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "As he had need"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was distributed to each one as he had need.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not equally. According to need — which requires somebody to know each person's situation. The distribution assumes a community small enough to be known in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What this paragraph is doing where it is.** It follows the prayer of chapter 4, where the place was shaken and they spoke the word with boldness. Luke's order is: they pray, the room shakes, and then nobody owns anything alone. He puts the economics downstream of the prayer meeting."}]}, "Acts 5:1–6": {"title": "A gift and a lie", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest story in Acts, and Luke reports it without a word of explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was not required"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Before you sold it, was it not your own? And once it was sold, was the money not yours to do with as you pleased?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter states it twice, plainly. The land was theirs. The money was theirs. Nobody had commanded them to sell anything, and no percentage was owed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That removes the obvious reading. This is not about how much they gave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why has Satan filled your heart, so that you would **lie to the Holy Spirit** and keep back part of the price?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *keep back* is **nosphizomai** — to embezzle, to set aside for yourself out of something already dedicated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses for Achan, who kept back devoted things from Jericho and hid them under his tent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was lied to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have not lied to men. You have lied to God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says *the Holy Spirit* in one line and *God* in the next, about the same act. It is one of the clearest statements in Scripture of who the Spirit is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pretence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4 has just described Barnabas doing exactly this — selling a field, bringing the money, laying it at the apostles' feet. Luke names him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ananias performs the same scene with a portion, and presents it as the whole. The sin is not the withholding. It is the performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke records afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A deep fear came over everyone who heard of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **phobos**, and Luke repeats it a few verses later after Sapphira, extending it to *the whole church*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He offers no commentary, no softening, and no moral. The young men wrap him and carry him out, and the narrative moves on to signs and wonders in the next paragraph."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this sits in Acts.** Immediately before it, a community where *there was not a needy person among them*. Immediately after it, crowds bringing the sick into the streets. The story is set between two descriptions of extraordinary grace, and Luke placed it there."}]}, "Acts 5:12–16": {"title": "\"Every one of them was healed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's summary of what the early weeks looked like, and he does not hedge the numbers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People even carried the sick out into the streets and laid them on cots and mats… Crowds came in from the towns around Jerusalem too, bringing the sick and those troubled by unclean spirits, and **every one of them was healed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses the same phrase Matthew used of the evening at Peter's house: all of them. He is a careful writer — he says elsewhere that he investigated everything closely — and this is what he reports."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shadow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hoping that as Peter came by, at least his shadow might fall across some of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records what the people hoped. He does not say the shadow healed anybody, and no apostle is described as teaching it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the detail conveys is the expectation in the city at that moment. People were carrying relatives into the street on the chance of being in the right place when Peter walked past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reactions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one else dared to attach himself to them, though the people held them in high regard. And still more were added to the Lord, crowds of men and women alike.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both at once — a wariness about joining, and steady growth. Luke has just recorded the deaths of Ananias and Sapphira, and *great fear came on the whole church*. He puts the fear and the crowds in the same paragraph without resolving the tension."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they met"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Solomon's Colonnade* — the covered walkway on the east side of the temple courts. They were meeting in public, at the temple, in the open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two paragraphs later the apostles are arrested there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Luke says was happening among them.** *The believers all gathered with one heart.* The healings, the growth and the unity are reported in one sentence, and Luke keeps making that connection through the early chapters — see also Acts 2:40–41."}]}, "Acts 5:17–21": {"title": "\"During the night an angel opened the doors\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The apostles are locked up, let out overnight, and sent straight back to the place they were arrested."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were arrested"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Filled with **indignation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zelos** — jealousy, zeal. Luke names the motive, and it is not doctrine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this describe crowds coming from the surrounding towns with the sick, and *they were all healed*. The temple authorities are watching their standing evaporate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **during the night** an angel of the Lord opened the doors of the jail.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No earthquake, no drama recorded. Doors opened, men brought out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports the rescue in half a sentence and spends the rest of the paragraph on what they were told to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the angel says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go, stand in the temple, and tell the people all the words of this life.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *go home*, not *leave the city*, not *lie low*. Go back to the temple courts — the most public place in Jerusalem, where they were picked up — and preach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rescue is not from the trouble. It is back into it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All the words of this life\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *this life* — a striking phrase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been arrested for talking about a resurrection, and the message is described by the angel as **life**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hearing that, they went into the temple **at daybreak** and began to teach.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not wait or discuss it. They are teaching by first light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comedy of the next scene"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Meanwhile the high priest convenes the entire Sanhedrin, sends to the prison for the prisoners, and the officers come back with:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We found the prison shut securely, and the guards standing outside before the doors; but when we had opened them, we found no one inside!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doors were locked. The guards were at their posts. Nobody had left, and nobody was there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then somebody comes in and says: *\"Look, the men whom you put in prison are standing in the temple and teaching the people!\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say when re-arrested"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We ought to obey God rather than men.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Gamaliel's advice to the council: *if this plan is of men, it will come to nothing; but if it is of God, you cannot overthrow it — lest you even be found to fight against God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How they leave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They departed from the presence of the council, **rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer shame for His name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just been beaten."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they did next.** *Daily in the temple, and in every house, they did not cease teaching and preaching Jesus as the Christ.* The angel's instruction was obeyed permanently."}]}, "Acts 7:54–60": {"title": "The one time He is seen standing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports a detail here that he does not report anywhere else, and the whole scene turns on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sitting, everywhere but here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament describes the risen Christ at the right hand of God more than a dozen times, and almost always **seated** — Hebrews makes a point of it, because a seated priest is one whose work is finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen, with the court screaming at him, looks up and sees Jesus **standing**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it twice in two verses, once as narration and once in Stephen's own words. He is not being careless with a posture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Stephen says as he dies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sentences, and both of them are Jesus' own words from the cross with one word changed:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Lord Jesus, receive my spirit* — Jesus had said *Father, into Your hands I commit my spirit*", "*Lord, do not hold this sin against them* — Jesus had said *Father, forgive them*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen prays to Jesus what Jesus prayed to the Father. For the first Christian to die, that is a considerable thing to have on record, and Luke simply lets it stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He fell asleep\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has just described a stoning in unflinching detail, and he ends it with the gentlest verb available. It became the ordinary Christian word for a believer's death — the same one Paul uses in Thessalonians — and it did not mean the death was mild. It meant it was not final."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The coats at the young man's feet.** Luke names him: Saul. He will not mention it again for chapters, but it is here, in the first paragraph about a Christian being killed, that Paul enters the story — holding the coats."}]}, "Acts 8:26–29": {"title": "The desert road", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Philip is in the middle of a revival and is sent to an empty road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before this: Philip in Samaria, *and the multitudes with one accord heeded the things spoken by Philip… and there was great joy in that city.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A city-wide response, and he is told to leave it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up and go south**, to the road that runs down from Jerusalem to Gaza.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No reason given. No name, no destination, no explanation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Luke adds his own note: ***It is a desert road.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is sent from a crowd to a place with nobody on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So he **got up and went**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words. No question is recorded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is on the road"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man from Ethiopia, **a court official of Candace** the queen of the Ethiopians, **in charge of all her treasury**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The finance minister of a kingdom, in a chariot, on the way home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Candace** is a title rather than a name — the queen mother of the Nubian kingdom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had **come to Jerusalem to worship** and was now on his way home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A journey of some fifteen hundred miles, each way, to worship at a temple that would not have let him past the outer court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a God-fearer from the ends of the known world, going home with a scroll."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is doing in the chariot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sitting in his chariot and **reading aloud from the prophet Isaiah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reading aloud was normal in the ancient world — texts were read out even in private."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A scroll of Isaiah was an enormous expense. He had bought one, and he is reading it in a moving vehicle in a desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit said to Philip, \"**Go over to that chariot and stay near it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel gave the road. The Spirit gives the chariot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Guidance arrives in two stages, and Philip had to act on the first before he got the second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Philip **ran** up alongside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He runs, beside a moving chariot, and hears what is being read."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage the man had reached is Isaiah 53 — *led like a sheep to the slaughter*. See the note on Acts 8:30–35."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it ends.** *The Spirit of the Lord caught Philip away… but Philip was found at Azotus.* Luke reports it flatly, and the next thing recorded is that he kept preaching through every city until he came to Caesarea. Twenty years later he is still there, with four daughters who prophesied, hosting Paul."}]}, "Acts 8:30–35": {"title": "\"Unless someone guides me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man is reading the right passage and cannot get into it, and somebody arrives running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you understand what you are reading?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philip does not open with an announcement. He asks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek has a play on it — *ginoskeis ha anaginoskeis*, do you know what you are reading — and it survives in the English *do you get what you have got*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can I, unless someone guides me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No defensiveness. A treasury official, in his own chariot, on his own road, says he cannot do it alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he *invited Philip to come up and sit with him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was reading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53:7–8, quoted in full:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was led like a sheep to the slaughter, and like a lamb silent before the one who shears it, **so He does not open His mouth**. In His humiliation His justice was taken away. And who can tell of His generation? For **His life is taken from the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the whole scroll, he is at that page."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question underneath the question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Tell me, please, **who is the prophet speaking about? Himself, or someone else?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the right question, and it is the one Jewish readers had argued about for centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has no idea who the answer is, and he knows the passage is about somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Philip does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Philip opened his mouth, and **beginning from that very Scripture** he told him the good news about Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He starts where the man already was. He does not go back to Genesis or forward to a different text."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *beginning from this Scripture* — that verse, that scroll, that sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two men, one road"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philip left a revival in Samaria for an empty desert road, because he was told to. See the note on Acts 8:26–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Ethiopian had travelled fifteen hundred miles to worship at a temple that would not let him past the outer court, and was going home with a scroll he could not understand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both journeys converge on one chariot at one moment, and neither man arranged it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look, here is water. **What is there to keep me from being baptized?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a desert. See the note on Acts 8:36–38."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Which verse he had not reached.** Isaiah 53 goes on: *He was **wounded for our transgressions**, He was **bruised for our iniquities**… and by His stripes we are healed.* Whatever Philip said, that was a few lines further down the same column. See the note on Isaiah 53:4–6."}]}, "Acts 8:36–38": {"title": "\"Look, here is water\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man in a desert finds water and asks the only question left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who spots it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As they went on down the road **they came to some water, and the official said, \"Look, here is water.\"***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Philip. The Ethiopian."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke had already told us the road was a desert one. The man notices water in a place that does not have much, and knows immediately what it is for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is there to keep me from being baptized?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every honest answer available in the first century would have been *a great deal*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a Gentile, from a foreign court, and — as an official of that kind — a eunuch. Deuteronomy 23 barred a eunuch from the assembly of the LORD outright."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had travelled fifteen hundred miles to a temple that would not let him past the outer court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks what is in the way, and Philip does not list anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you believe with all your heart, you may.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"I believe that Jesus Christ is the Son of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire examination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah says about him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been reading Isaiah 53. Three chapters further on, in the same scroll, is this:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let the eunuch say, \"Here I am, a dry tree.\" For thus says the LORD: **to the eunuchs who keep My Sabbaths… I will give them a place and a name better than that of sons and daughters. I will give them an everlasting name that shall not be cut off.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 56, addressed to exactly his condition, written centuries earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stopping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **gave the order to stop the chariot**, and the two of them went down into the water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A senior official halts his own convoy in a desert to be baptised by a man who ran up alongside an hour earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they came up out of the water, the Spirit of the Lord caught Philip away, and the official saw him no more, **and went on his way rejoicing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philip vanishes. The man is left standing in a desert with wet clothes, no teacher, and a scroll — and Luke's note is that he went on rejoicing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the gospel had reached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts 1:8 set the programme: *Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philip had just done Samaria. This man is going home to the southern edge of the known world."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that keeps some manuscripts apart.** Verse 37 — *if you believe with all your heart, you may* — is absent from the oldest Greek manuscripts and present in many later ones. Whatever its history, the confession it records is the only requirement anyone in the passage names."}]}, "Acts 9:10–14": {"title": "Ananias is sent", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The man who has to knock on the door, and he argues."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was **a disciple in Damascus named Ananias**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is all Luke gives. He appears in this chapter, is mentioned in Paul's own retelling as *a devout man according to the law, having a good testimony with all the Jews who dwelt there*, and is never heard of again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Lord spoke to him **in a vision**. \"**Ananias**,\" He said. \"**Here I am, Lord**,\" he answered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew equivalent is **hinneni** — Abraham's word at the mountain, Moses' at the bush, Samuel's in the night. See the note on Genesis 22:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Get up and go to **the street called Straight**, and ask at **the house of Judas** for a man **from Tarsus named Saul**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A street, a householder, a hometown and a name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Street Called Straight ran east to west through Damascus and still does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the address with the precision of a man who checked it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He is praying at this moment**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days blind, eating and drinking nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other vision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **he has seen in a vision a man named Ananias coming in and laying hands on him so that he might see again**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two visions again, as with Cornelius and Peter — and again neither man knows about the other's until told. See the note on Acts 10:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul has already seen Ananias arrive. Ananias is being asked to complete a picture that has been shown to somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Lord, I have heard from many people about this man** and **all the harm he has done to Your holy ones in Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kaka** — evils."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not exaggerating. Luke has recorded it: *Saul made havoc of the church, entering every house, and dragging off men and women, committing them to prison.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **he is here with authority from the chief priests to arrest everyone who calls on Your name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warrants are in the house on Straight Street."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ananias would be one of the names on them. *Everyone who calls on Your name* is a description of himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is being sent to lay hands on the man who came to arrest him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go, for he is a chosen vessel of Mine** to bear My name before Gentiles, kings, and the children of Israel. **For I will show him how many things he must suffer for My name's sake**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a reassurance about safety. An assignment for the other man, and a note about what it will cost him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Ananias does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Ananias went his way and entered the house; and **laying his hands on him he said, \"Brother Saul…\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Brother.** The first word out of his mouth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"…the Lord Jesus, who appeared to you on the road as you came, has sent me that you may receive your sight and be filled with the Holy Spirit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately there fell from his eyes something like scales, and he received his sight at once.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul remembers him saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In his own account years later, Ananias adds: *the God of our fathers has chosen you that you should know His will… **and now why are you waiting? Arise and be baptised**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Ananias is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who let Paul into the church. Everything Paul wrote came through a door somebody had to be argued into opening."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he is not given.** Any assurance for himself. He raises the objection — the man has warrants and my name is on them — and the answer is about what the other man is for."}]}, "Acts 9:3–6": {"title": "Light on the Damascus road", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses, and everything in Paul's life afterward comes out of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *Saul, still **breathing threats and murder** against the disciples of the Lord, went to the high priest and asked letters from him to the synagogues of Damascus, so that if he found any who were of the Way, he might bring them bound to Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is on official business with warrants in his bag. Damascus is a hundred and fifty miles from Jerusalem — a week's walk, undertaken deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The light"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suddenly **a light out of heaven blazed all around him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells it twice more in Acts, and each time adds a detail: *at midday*, and *brighter than the sun*, and *shining around me and those who journeyed with me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Midday, and it outshone the sun."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **fell to the ground**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Saul, Saul**, why are you persecuting Me?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubled name — *Abraham, Abraham*, *Moses, Moses*, *Samuel, Samuel*, *Martha, Martha*, *Simon, Simon*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time in Scripture it precedes something serious, and every time it is warm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why are you **persecuting Me**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *why are you persecuting them*. Paul had never met Jesus and had been arresting people in houses in Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The identification is the whole of what he is told, and his letters never stop working it out: *you are the body of Christ*, and *if one member suffers, all the members suffer with it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who are You, Lord?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **kurie** can be simply *sir*. He does not yet know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the few genuinely open questions in Scripture, asked by a man face down in the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In his own account to Agrippa he adds *Jesus **of Nazareth***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The goads"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is hard for you to **kick against the goads**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A goad was a pointed stick used to drive oxen. An animal that kicked back drove it in further."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase was a Greek proverb — it appears in Euripides — and Paul, from Tarsus, would have known it as a piece of ordinary speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Trembling and astonished, he said, \"**Lord, what do You want me to do?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second use of *Lord* in the passage, and the man asking is the one who had letters in his bag an hour earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up and go into the city, and you will be told what you must do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not told what the assignment is. He is told to go and wait to be told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days without sight, eating and drinking nothing, in a house on Straight Street — and then Ananias, who had to be argued into going: *Lord, I have heard from many about this man, how much harm he has done to Your saints.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer: *Go, for **he is a chosen vessel of Mine** to bear My name before Gentiles, kings, and the children of Israel. For **I will show him how many things he must suffer** for My name's sake.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Paul describes it later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Last of all He was seen by me too, **as by a child born at the wrong time**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:3–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *I am the least of the apostles, who am not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never stops mentioning it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was given on the road.** A name, a question, and an instruction to go and be told. The commission itself was delivered days later, through a frightened man who did not want to knock on the door."}]}, "Acts 9:36–43": {"title": "\"Tabitha, get up\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only woman in the New Testament called a disciple, and the room is full of the coats she made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In Joppa there was **a disciple named Tabitha, which in Greek is Dorcas**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mathetria** — the feminine of disciple. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tabitha** is Aramaic and **Dorcas** is Greek, and both mean *gazelle*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives both, which tells you Joppa was a mixed town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her life was **full of good works and acts of mercy to the poor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *full of good works and almsdeeds which she did*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The death"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In those days she **fell ill and died**, and after they had **washed her** they laid her in **an upstairs room**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The washing is the preparation for burial. In that climate burial followed within a day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They wash her and then stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the disciples heard that Peter was there they sent two men to him, urging him, \"**Please come to us without delay**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lydda is about ten miles from Joppa. They send two men and do not say why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the message asks for anything specific. They simply want him there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he arrived they took him to the upstairs room, and **all the widows stood around him weeping, showing him the tunics and other clothes Dorcas had made while she was still with them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *displaying* — holding them up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are wearing the evidence. Every woman in the room has something she made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Widows in that world had no income and no legal standing, and the early church's first administrative crisis was about their daily distribution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter **sent them all outside**, and he **knelt down and prayed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two actions, in that order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had watched Jesus do exactly this in Jairus's house: *He put them all outside, and took her by the hand.* See the note on Luke 8:49–56."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter was one of the three allowed to stay in that room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he turned toward the body and said, \"**Tabitha, get up**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic would have been **Tabitha koum**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records what Jesus said to the girl: **Talitha koum** — *little girl, get up*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One letter apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **opened her eyes**, and when she saw Peter she **sat up**. **He gave her his hand and helped her to her feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, a physician, gives it in stages: eyes, sitting, standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The presentation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then he called the believers and the widows and **presented her to them alive**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Word of it spread through all of Joppa, and **many believed in the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter **stayed on in Joppa for some time with a tanner named Simon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tanner worked with animal skins and was ceremonially unclean by trade. Observant Jews kept their distance, and tanneries were built outside towns by the sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter lodges with one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the address the men from Caesarea come to in the next chapter, after the sheet and the voice. See the note on Acts 11:4–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The staying is a small detail and Luke puts it there deliberately — the man about to be told *what God has cleansed, you must not call unholy* is already living in a tanner's house."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the widows brought.** Not an argument. They held up the clothes and cried, and Peter put them all outside before he prayed."}]}, "Acts 9:7–9": {"title": "Three days without sight", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The aftermath on the road, and Luke gives the details in the order they happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The men with him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The men traveling with him **stood there speechless. They heard the voice but saw no one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's account here and Paul's own retellings differ on the detail — chapter 22 has them *seeing the light but not hearing the voice of Him who spoke to me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The likeliest reading is that they heard a sound without hearing words. The Greek allows it, and the point in both accounts is the same: something happened that they registered and could not receive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are witnesses to an event they cannot interpret."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escort"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were there to help arrest people. Saul had letters from the high priest and a party to execute them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Getting up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Saul got up from the ground, and **when he opened his eyes he could see nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had fallen at the light. Luke notes that he opened his eyes deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **they took him by the hand and led him into Damascus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **cheiragogountes** — hand-leading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He set out at the head of a party with warrants and arrives being led like a child."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same word himself of Elymas the sorcerer, struck blind at Paphos, *groping about, looking for someone to lead him by the hand*. See the note on Acts 13:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul pronounces on another man what had happened to him — and includes the time limit that he had been given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **three days he was without sight**, and **he neither ate nor drank**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives all three: blind, no food, no water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days in a house on Straight Street, in the city he had come to raid, with nothing to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Lord tells Ananias: *\"**he is praying at this moment**.\"* See the note on Acts 9:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"he has seen in a vision a man named Ananias coming in and laying hands on him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is blind and being shown something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he lost in those days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he had been. His letters put it this way: *what things were gain to me, these I have counted loss for Christ.* See the note on Philippians 3:2–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seven credentials he lists there were all intact on the road out of Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What ended it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ananias, a man who had to be argued into coming, laying hands on him and calling him **brother**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately there fell from his eyes **something like scales**, and he received his sight at once.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lepides** — flakes, scales. Luke, a physician, chooses the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And immediately he **preached the Christ in the synagogues, that He is the Son of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then all who heard were amazed, and said, \"**Is this not he who destroyed those who called on this name in Jerusalem?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three days elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern is not commented on in the text. Luke reports the count and moves on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the escort saw.** Enough to stand still and say nothing. They heard something and saw nobody — and then took the man who had been leading them and walked him into the city by the hand."}]}, "Amos 8:11–14": {"title": "A famine for the words of the LORD", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The prophet names a shortage of an unusual commodity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos was a herdsman from Tekoa and a tender of sycamore figs, not a professional prophet — he says so himself: *I was no prophet, nor was I a son of a prophet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is preaching in the northern kingdom during a boom. Amos 6 describes ivory beds, lambs from the flock, wine by the bowlful."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The days are coming,\" declares the Lord GOD, \"when **I will send a famine over the land**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Famine is one of the standard judgments. He then withdraws the usual meaning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Not a hunger for bread, nor a thirst for water**, but **a hunger to hear the words of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The searching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"People will **stagger from sea to sea** and **wander from north to east**, **searching everywhere for the word of the LORD**, and **they will not find it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stagger* is the verb for reeling, wandering like a drunk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three directions and no result. The last clause is the whole point: not that nobody looks, but that looking does not work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction they do not go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "North and east, and sea to sea — and not south. Jerusalem, the temple, and the place Amos himself had come from are south."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What made it a famine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that God stopped speaking. Amos 2 has already recorded the complaint: *I raised up some of your sons as prophets… but you gave the Nazirites wine to drink, and commanded the prophets saying, \"Do not prophesy!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in chapter 7 the priest of Bethel tells Amos to leave: *go, flee to the land of Judah… but never again prophesy at Bethel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famine is the granting of a request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who faints"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The **lovely young women and the strong young men** will faint from thirst.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strongest and the healthiest. A famine takes them last, which is how you know how bad it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had been swearing by"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Those who **swear by the shame of Samaria**, who say, '**As surely as your god lives, Dan**,' and, '**As surely as the way of Beersheba lives**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three shrines. Dan had one of Jeroboam's golden calves; Beersheba was a pilgrimage site in the far south."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not irreligious. They have three functioning centres of worship and a famine of the word at the same time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Amos had already said about their services"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I hate, I despise your feast days… Take away from Me the noise of your songs… **But let justice run down like water**, and righteousness like a mighty stream.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament says stands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grass dries out. The flower drops off. **But what the Lord says stands, and keeps standing**.* See the note on 1 Peter 1:24–25."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being taken away.** Not the buildings, the songs, or the pilgrimages. Those stay. What goes is the word — and Amos says people will cross the country looking for it."}]}, "Amos 9:11–12": {"title": "The fallen shelter of David", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last page of the harshest of the prophets, and it turns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has come before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight and a half chapters of judgement. Amos was a shepherd and a dresser of sycamore figs, not a professional prophet, and the book is unrelenting: *I hate, I despise your feast days… take away from Me the noise of your songs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five visions of destruction, and then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In that day I will **raise up David's fallen shelter**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sukkah** — a booth, a hut, the temporary shelter Israel lived in during the Feast of Tabernacles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the house of David, and not the throne. A **shelter**, and a fallen one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dynasty is described at its lowest — not a palace but a lean-to, and that collapsed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **raise up**… I will **mend** its broken walls… **set its ruins right**… **build it as it was in the days of old**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is His. Nothing in the sentence is asked of anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So they may possess what is left of Edom and all the nations that bear My name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Edom — Esau's people, Israel's oldest enemy. And *all the nations that bear My name*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gentiles, named in the promise, in the last paragraph of Amos."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts 15, the Jerusalem council. The question is whether Gentiles have to be circumcised and keep the law to belong."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter reports what happened at Cornelius's house. Paul and Barnabas describe the miracles among the nations. And then James stands up and quotes this passage:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**After this I will return and will rebuild the tabernacle of David, which has fallen down**… so that the rest of mankind may seek the LORD, even all the Gentiles who are called by My name.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His conclusion: *therefore I judge that we should not trouble those from among the Gentiles who are turning to God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse that settled the first great church controversy is the second-to-last paragraph of Amos."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the tabernacle of David was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the tabernacle of Moses. David pitched a tent for the ark in Jerusalem and left it there, with singers and musicians before it continually — no veil, no sacrifice system, and access that the Mosaic arrangement did not allow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It stood for about forty years, until Solomon's temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The days are coming** when the plowman will overtake the reaper.\"* See the note on Amos 9:13–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the book ends.** *\"I will plant them in their own land, and **never again will they be uprooted**,\" says the LORD your God.* Nine chapters of demolition, and the last word is planting."}]}, "Amos 9:13–15": {"title": "\"The plowman will overtake the reaper\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A picture of a farming year that will not fit into a year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The days are coming when **the plowman will overtake the reaper**, and **the one who treads the grapes the one who plants the seed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In an ordinary season these are months apart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You reap in spring, thresh, and then wait until the autumn rains soften the ground before you can plough again. You harvest grapes in late summer and sow after them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos describes a harvest so long that the man ploughing for next year catches up with the man still bringing in this one — and a vintage still being trodden when the next sowing starts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seasons overlap and stop being a queue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**New wine will drip from the mountains** and flow down every hill.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that the mountains will drip sweet wine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Vineyards were terraced on hillsides and the presses cut into the rock. The picture is of so much fruit that the hills themselves run with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel says the same thing in almost the same words: *the mountains shall drip with new wine, the hills shall flow with milk.* See the note on Joel 2:25–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four things restored"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **rebuild the ruined cities** and live in them. They will **plant vineyards and drink their wine**. They will **make gardens and eat their fruit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point of each clause is who gets the benefit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the exact reversal of the curse in Deuteronomy 28: *you shall build a house and not dwell in it; you shall plant a vineyard and not gather its grapes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the same promise as Psalm 128 — *you will eat the fruit of your labour.* See the note on Psalms 128:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The planting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will plant them in their own land**, and never again will they be uprooted from the land I have given them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gardener language is applied to the people. They plant vineyards; He plants them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Directly after the promise to raise up David's fallen shelter — the verse James quoted at the Jerusalem council to settle whether Gentiles could come in without the law. See the note on Amos 9:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The acceleration follows the rebuilding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book was before this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos was a shepherd from Tekoa, and the book is nine chapters of judgement on Israel, Judah and six surrounding nations, with five visions of ruin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Woe to you who are at ease in Zion.\"* *\"I hate, I despise your feast days.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the last five verses."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last three words.** *Says the LORD **your God**.* The possessive is the final word of a book that spent nine chapters describing what He was going to tear down."}]}, "Colossians 1:15–20": {"title": "\"In Him all of it holds together\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Probably an early hymn, quoted by Paul, and it is the highest sustained statement about Christ in his letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Firstborn of all creation\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase has been misread for centuries as making Him part of what was created — and the next sentence rules that out: *in Him everything was created.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Prototokos** in the Old Testament is a title of rank, not chronology. God calls Israel His firstborn, and David — the youngest of eight sons — is called firstborn in Psalm 89. It means the one with the rights of the heir."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prepositions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hymn turns on four small words, and they cover everything:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["created **in** Him", "created **through** Him", "created **for** Him", "and He is **before** all things"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the line that holds the passage together: *in Him all of it holds together.* The verb means to stand together, to cohere. The universe is described as continuously held rather than wound up and released."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two firstborns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is *firstborn of all creation* and *firstborn from among the dead*. The hymn puts the two in parallel — first place in the old creation and in the new one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the stated purpose: *so that in everything He might hold the first place.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through Him He should bring everything back into peace with Himself, having made that peace by the blood of His cross.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After thrones, lordships, rulers and authorities, after all things visible and invisible held together — the hymn ends at a cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood is what the cosmic language was building toward, which is what keeps the passage from being an abstraction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it lands next.** *And you, who once were alienated and enemies in your mind… He has now reconciled.* Having described the whole universe being brought back, Paul turns immediately to the readers and applies it to them personally. See the note on 2 Corinthians 5:18–19."}]}, "Colossians 1:9–12": {"title": "The chain of a prayer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of Paul's two great prayers, and it runs as a chain — each link producing the next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The links"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**filled with the knowledge of what He wants**, with wisdom and spiritual understanding", "*so that* you **walk in a way worthy of the Lord**, pleasing Him through and through", "**bearing fruit in every good work** and **growing in the knowledge of God**", "**strengthened with every strength** His glorious power supplies", "ready for **endurance and patience** — *and all of it with joy*", "**giving thanks to the Father**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "It begins with knowing and ends with thanking. Everything in between follows from the first link."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not strength. **Knowledge of what God wants** — and the word for knowledge here is the intensified form, full or thorough knowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's prayers consistently start here. He treats seeing rightly as the thing that produces walking rightly, rather than the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strength, and what it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strengthened with every strength that His own glorious power supplies, ready for endurance of every kind and for patience that does not run out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enormous language — glorious power, every strength — and then the purpose lands somewhere unexpected. Not for miracles or conquest. For **endurance and patience**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last three words change the tone completely: *and all of it with joy.* Not grim survival. Staying power that is glad."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last link"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Giving thanks to the Father, who has made us fit to take our share of what belongs to His holy people in the light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about qualifying someone, making them sufficient. It is past tense and God is the subject: the fitness was conferred, not developed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the prayer goes next.** Straight into one of the highest passages about Christ in the New Testament — the image of the invisible God, in whom all things hold together. Paul prays for knowledge and then immediately supplies some."}]}, "Colossians 2:11–15": {"title": "Nailed to the cross", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul runs five verbs together and every one of them is God's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The circumcision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In Him you were also circumcised, with **a circumcision no human hand performed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **acheiropoietos** — not made with hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Colossae was being pressed toward Jewish observance. Paul does not argue about the knife; he says the thing it stood for has already happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Buried and raised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were **buried with Him** in baptism, and in that same baptism **you were raised with Him too**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both in one sentence, and both past tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **trusting what God was at work doing** — the God who raised Him from the dead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *through faith in the working of God*. The faith is in what God did, not in what the believer manages."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you, who were **dead in your trespasses** and in the uncircumcision of your flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dead. Not weakened, not struggling. Paul's diagnosis leaves nothing for the patient to contribute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **made alive together with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One Greek word — **suzoopoieo**, to make alive together with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forgiveness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Forgiving you **every trespass**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **charizomai** — from **charis**, grace. To give freely, to grant as a favour. It is not the courtroom word for acquittal; it is the word for a gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Every trespass** — all of them, in one clause, with no accounting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The note"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **erased the written note that stood against us**, with all its demands set down in our disfavor.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **cheirographon** — a handwritten document, specifically an IOU. A debtor wrote it out in his own hand as an acknowledgement of what he owed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Erased** is **exaleipho** — to wipe out, to sponge off. Ancient ink sat on the surface of papyrus and could be washed away, leaving the sheet blank."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Removed and nailed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **removed it from between us**, **nailing it to the cross**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two more verbs. Taken out of the way, and then fixed to the beam."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge sheet is put where the body went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **stripped the rulers and authorities of their weapons**, **put them on open display**, and **led them in triumph** in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The triumph image again — the Roman victory procession, the general in front and the defeated walking behind. Paul uses it in 2 Corinthians of the believers. See the note on 2 Corinthians 2:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the ones in the column are the powers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the display happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In it* — in the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The place that looked like the defeat is named as the parade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is arguing against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses on either side: *let no one judge you in food or in drink, or regarding a festival or a new moon or sabbaths*, and *why do you subject yourselves to regulations — do not touch, do not taste, do not handle?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list of what God did is his answer to a list of what the Colossians were being told to do."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whose hands are in the passage.** Not the reader's. Circumcised without hands, buried, raised, made alive, forgiven, the note erased and nailed up — and the only thing asked of anybody is trusting what God was at work doing."}]}, "Colossians 2:4–7": {"title": "Keep on walking in Him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to a church he has never met, and the thing he is guarding them against is not immorality. It is persuasive speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Talk you around\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I say this so that no one may **talk you around** with words that sound persuasive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is a reckoning word — to miscount, to defraud someone by faulty arithmetic, and so to beguile with plausible reasoning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The danger named is not error that sounds wrong. It is error that adds up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Good order and firm faith"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am glad to see the good order among you and how firm your faith in Christ stands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both nouns are military. **Taxis** is a rank drawn up in formation; **stereōma** is a solid front, a bulwark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, writing from prison to people he has never seen, describes them as a line that is holding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as you **received** Christ Jesus the Lord, **keep on walking** in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and the second is present tense — continuous. However you got in is how you go on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You received Him — you did not achieve Him, negotiate Him, or qualify for Him. Walk the same way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four participles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then four pictures stacked in one sentence, and they do not come from one world:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**rooted in Him** — agricultural, and the tense is a completed act; the root went down once", "**being built up in Him** — architectural, and this one is ongoing", "**made firm in the faith exactly as you were taught** — legal, the word used of a contract being confirmed", "**overflowing with thanksgiving** — a river over its banks"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rooted is done. Built up is happening. That order is deliberate."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter goes on to name.** Philosophy and empty deceit, human tradition, food rules, festivals, new moons, sabbaths, angel worship. All of it answered by one sentence: *in Him all the fullness of the Deity lives in bodily form, and you have been made full in Him.*"}]}, "Colossians 2:8–10": {"title": "\"In Him you have been made complete\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul warns against something and then, instead of arguing point by point, states a fact that makes the alternative unnecessary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take care that no one carries you off as his prize.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is used of kidnapping — carrying someone off as plunder. Not persuading; taking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what does the carrying: *philosophy and hollow deceit… traditions handed down by men… the elementary lessons the world teaches.* Colossae was being sold a package of rules, visions and observances as an upgrade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fullness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For in Him all the fullness of what God is lives in bodily form.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pleroma** — fullness, the whole content. The word was in use among the teachers Paul is opposing, and he takes it off them: the fullness is not spread across a hierarchy of spiritual beings. It is in Christ, and it is *bodily*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that ends the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in Him **you have been made complete**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek uses the same root. The fullness is in Him; you have been filled full in Him. Perfect tense — done, and standing done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why the warning works. Anyone offering an addition is offering to add to something already complete. Nothing on the market can be a next step from here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who is the head over every ruler and every authority.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The powers the false teaching was anxious about — angelic hierarchies, elemental spirits — are put under the same head the reader is joined to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does not do.** He never refutes the philosophy in detail. He states what Christ is and what the reader has in Him, and lets the offer collapse under the comparison. It is the same move as Ephesians 1: prayer that they would see what they already have, rather than instruction on getting more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before this one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning does not stand on its own. Paul has just written *just as you received Christ Jesus the Lord, keep on walking in Him* — rooted, built up, made firm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what makes the kidnapping possible to resist. A man who is rooted can be approached by a persuasive teacher and stay where he is. See the note on Colossians 2:4–7."}]}, "Colossians 3:15–17": {"title": "Peace as the umpire", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Let the peace of God have the deciding voice in your hearts.* The English has to spread out to say what the Greek says in one word, and that word is where this verse gets interesting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word is from the stadium"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Brabeuo.** A *brabeus* was the judge at the games — the official who watched the race and said who had won, whose call settled the matter. The verb means to act as that official: to arbitrate, to decide, to rule on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So Paul is not saying *feel peaceful*. He is saying: give peace the umpire's whistle. Let it be the thing that makes the call inside you when two courses of action are competing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a far more practical instruction than it usually gets treated as. An umpire is not an atmosphere. An umpire is a decision-maker."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being umpired"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The context is a chapter about how believers live together — putting off anger and malice, putting on compassion, kindness, humility, patience, bearing with one another and forgiving. Then this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it was into that peace you were called, in one body.* The peace is not first a private calm; it is the peace they were called into *together*. So a good deal of what this umpire is ruling on is how a person deals with other people, and the verse right after it is about the word of Christ living in them richly enough to teach and correct one another."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Reading it for guidance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This verse is used constantly as a way of finding God's will, and that use is not foreign to it. Paul does put peace in the position of the one who decides."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things keep it honest. First, it says *the peace of God*, not the absence of nerves — the same peace Jesus described as not the kind the world hands out. Someone can feel nervous and still be at peace, and can feel enthusiastic and have no peace at all. Second, it sits in a sentence about the body, and Paul immediately surrounds it with the word of Christ dwelling richly and with people teaching one another. Peace umpires; it does not umpire alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And be thankful people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul attaches this to the end of the sentence, and it is easy to read as a pleasantry. In the paragraph it is structural: he says it here, again two lines later with the singing, and a third time at the close — *giving thanks through Him to God the Father*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in three verses. Whatever else the umpired life looks like, Paul seems to think it is audibly grateful."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"whatever you do, in word or in work.\"** The paragraph ends by collapsing the distinction between religious activity and ordinary work — everything, in the name of the Lord Jesus. That is where a chapter about how to live together ends up: not with a special category of holy tasks, but with the whole of a life brought under one name."}]}, "Colossians 3:22–25": {"title": "The wages nobody expected", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to household slaves, which is worth saying plainly rather than softening into \"servants.\" These were people with no legal standing and no prospect of wages at all — and the sentence he lands on would have been startling in that room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase that does the work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that the Lord Himself will hand you the inheritance as your wages.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words are being deliberately jammed together. An **inheritance** is what a son receives because of who he is. **Wages** are what a worker earns. A slave could expect neither."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says they will be handed the first *as* the second. The thing they could never earn is given to them in the form of the thing they never got."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Work at it from the soul\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is literally *out of the soul*, and it is set against work that only happens while someone is watching — the phrase behind it means eye-service, work done for the eye."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is not to work harder. It is to change who the work is aimed at: *as for the Lord and not for people*. That transfer is what makes a hidden task and a visible one worth the same."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line at the end.** *There is no playing of favorites* — and the very next verse turns to the masters and tells them they have a Master in heaven. In a world that ran on rank, Paul puts both parties under the same eye and tells the ones on the bottom that their unwitnessed work is seen."}]}, "Colossians 4:5–6": {"title": "\"With salt in them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses near the end of the letter, about talking to people who do not believe any of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is in view"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Live wisely toward **those who are outside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hoi exo** — those outside, the standard early-church term for people not in the congregation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes it from a Roman prison, to a church he had never visited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **buy up every hour you are given**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exagorazomenoi ton kairon** — redeeming the time. **Kairos** is not clock time; it is the opportune moment, the window."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Buy up* is a market word: purchase it out, take it off the shelf before somebody else does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same phrase in Ephesians and adds *because the days are evil*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speech"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let your words **always be gracious**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in grace* — **en chariti**. Not merely polite. The quality of the message is to be the quality of the delivery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The salt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **salt in them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Greek and Roman writers used salt as a metaphor for wit — speech with some bite, not insipid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it carries the Old Testament sense as well: salt was required on every offering, and it was called the salt of the covenant. See the note on Leviticus 2:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Salt preserves and it makes thirsty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **you know the answer that fits each person you speak with**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *how you ought to answer each one*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Each one.** Not a formula to be repeated at everybody. The answer is fitted to the person asking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the point of the salt: seasoning is measured against what it is going into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell them to start conversations, to argue, or to prepare a case."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter frames it the same way: *always be ready to give a defence to everyone who **asks** you a reason for the hope that is in you, with meekness and fear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both apostles describe answering rather than initiating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul asks for in the same paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses earlier: *pray for us, that God would **open to us a door for the word**, to speak the mystery of Christ, **for which I am also in chains** — that I may make it manifest, as I ought to speak.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for a door and for the right words, about himself, immediately before telling them the same two things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Colossians spends three chapters on Christ — *in Him dwells all the fullness of the Godhead bodily*, *hidden with Christ in God*, *He is all and in all*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the practical section, and this is nearly the last item in it: how to talk to the neighbours."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being asked for.** Not a script. Grace in the words, salt in them, and the awareness that the person in front of you is one person rather than a category."}]}, "Daniel 1:17": {"title": "\"And to Daniel He gave more still\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is the hinge of the whole book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the four are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel, Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah — renamed Belteshazzar, Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego by the chief of the eunuchs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judean nobility, deported as teenagers, selected for looks and aptitude and put into a three-year Babylonian training programme."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were being taught"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The language and literature of the Chaldeans* — astronomy, astrology, divination, mathematics, and the mythology of Babylon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The syllabus of a pagan court, taught by the wise men Daniel later has to compete with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before this verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refusal of the king's food, the ten-day test on vegetables and water, and the result: *at the end of ten days their features appeared better and fatter in flesh than all the young men who ate the portion of the king's delicacies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had asked rather than demanded — *Daniel requested of the chief of the eunuchs* — and God had given him *favour and goodwill* with the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To these four young men **God gave knowledge and skill in all learning and wisdom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew names three: **madda** (knowledge), **haskel** (skill, insight) and **chokmah** (wisdom)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It covers the whole curriculum, including the parts of it that were pagan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not say they excelled by working harder. It says God gave it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The addition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **to Daniel He gave more still: understanding in every kind of vision and dream**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chazon** — the vision word — and **chalomot**, dreams."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the four gets something the other three do not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole book runs on it. Nebuchadnezzar's statue in chapter 2, the tree in chapter 4, the writing on the wall in chapter 5, the ram and goat in chapter 8, the seventy weeks in chapter 9, and the visions of chapters 10 to 12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel is the only man in the Old Testament who both receives visions and interprets other people's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about it later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing in front of Nebuchadnezzar, asked how he did it:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This mystery was not opened to me because I have more wisdom than anyone living**.\"* See the note on Daniel 2:29–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He declines the credit at the moment it is offered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result of the training"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in all matters of wisdom and understanding about which the king examined them, **he found them ten times better** than all the magicians and astrologers who were in all his realm.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were examined by the king and outperformed the professionals."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Joseph stands beside him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh's assessment of Joseph is nearly identical: *can we find anyone like this man, one in whom the Spirit of God lives?* See the note on Genesis 41:37–45."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two Hebrews in foreign courts, both raised by interpreting a king's dream, both given a new name and a wife by the crown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long Daniel lasted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Daniel continued **until the first year of King Cyrus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deported under Nebuchadnezzar, still in office when Babylon fell to Persia — roughly seventy years, through four kings and two empires."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse divides.** All four are given the whole curriculum. One is given something extra, and the extra is the reason there is a book."}]}, "Daniel 10:12–13": {"title": "Twenty-one days", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The messenger explains why he is late."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens: *in those days I, Daniel, was mourning three full weeks. I ate no pleasant food, no meat or wine came into my mouth, nor did I anoint myself at all, till three whole weeks were fulfilled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-one days of fasting, by an old man in Persia."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid, Daniel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just been told *O Daniel, man greatly beloved* — the same description Gabriel gave him in chapter 9. See the note on Daniel 9:20–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**From the first day** you set your heart to understand, and to humble yourself before your God, **your words were heard**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Day one. The prayer registered immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have come because of your words**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The visit is caused by the praying. Not permitted by it — caused."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The delay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **the prince of the kingdom of Persia stood against me for twenty-one days**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *withstood me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer was heard on day one and the answer arrived on day twenty-one. Daniel had spent the whole of it fasting, with no indication anything was happening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chapter suggests his praying was deficient, and the messenger does not suggest it either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prince of Persia"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Cyrus. The messenger goes on to say *I will return to fight with the prince of Persia; and when I have gone out, indeed the prince of Greece will come*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kings do not follow kings that way, and empires do. The chapter treats these as authorities standing behind nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The help"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **Michael, one of the chief princes, came to help me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Michael appears three times in Daniel and twice in the New Testament — in Jude, disputing over the body of Moses, and in Revelation 12, at war."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel 12 calls him *the great prince who stands watch over the sons of your people*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel could not see"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any of it. He fasted three weeks and got nothing, and then was told what had been going on the whole time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never asks about it and is never told to pray differently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was in the middle of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 10 opens the last vision of the book — the long account of the kings of the north and south that runs to the end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The messenger says: *now I have come to make you understand what will happen to your people in the latter days.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state Daniel was in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I retained no strength… my comeliness was turned in me into corruption, and I retained no strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is touched three times in the chapter and told twice not to be afraid. *A hand touched me, which made me tremble on my knees.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the categories"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We do not wrestle against flesh and blood, but against **principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this age**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And immediately after listing the armour: *praying always with all prayer and supplication in the Spirit.* See the note on Ephesians 6:14–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the messenger says about the prayer.** That it was heard on the first day, and that he was dispatched because of it. The twenty-one days were not the wait for an answer — they were the journey."}]}, "Daniel 2:19": {"title": "\"Then Daniel blessed the God of heaven\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the first thing he does with the answer is stop and sing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nebuchadnezzar has dreamed something and demanded that his wise men tell him both the dream and its meaning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They protest that no king has ever asked such a thing, *and there is no other who can tell it to the king except the gods, whose dwelling is not with flesh*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king orders every wise man in Babylon killed. Daniel and his three friends are on the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel did first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Daniel went in and asked the king **to give him time**, that he might tell the king the interpretation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for an extension on a death sentence, from a king who has just executed his own advisers for stalling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Daniel went to his house, and **made the decision known to Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah, his companions**, that they might **seek mercies from the God of heaven concerning this secret**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not pray alone. He goes and gets three men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their Babylonian names are the famous ones — Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego — and here they are used as their own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **in the night, in a vision, the mystery was opened to Daniel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is **raz** — a secret, a mystery. It is a Persian loan-word, and it is the term the whole chapter turns on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Opened** — the Aramaic is *revealed*, uncovered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had prayed in the evening and he was asleep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Daniel **blessed the God of heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before he goes to Arioch, before he goes to the king, before he tells his three friends, and before anyone is saved from execution."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soonest possible action would have been to run."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the blessing says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verses 20 to 23, and it is one of the finest prayers in the Old Testament:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Blessed be the name of God forever and ever, for **wisdom and might are His**. And **He changes the times and the seasons; He removes kings and raises up kings**. He gives wisdom to the wise and knowledge to those who have understanding.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He reveals deep and secret things; He knows what is in the darkness, and light dwells with Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about himself in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I thank You and praise You, O God of my fathers; **You have given me wisdom and might, and have now made known to me what we asked of You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**What we asked** — plural. He credits the three men who prayed with him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says to Arioch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not destroy the wise men of Babylon**; take me before the king.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first use he makes of it is to stop the execution of the astrologers who could not do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says to the king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This mystery was not opened to me because I have more wisdom than anyone living**.\"* See the note on Daniel 2:29–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the dream was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A statue of gold, silver, bronze, iron and clay, struck by a stone *cut out without hands* which became a mountain and filled the whole earth."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the paragraph.** The mystery is revealed in the night, and the next recorded act is a blessing — before the king is told, before anyone is saved, and before the man is out of danger himself."}]}, "Daniel 2:29–30": {"title": "\"Not because I have more wisdom\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Standing in front of the most powerful man alive, with the answer in his hand, Daniel gives away the credit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before Nebuchadnezzar, after every wise man in Babylon has failed and the execution order has been issued."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just told the king: *there is a God in heaven who reveals secrets, and He has made known to King Nebuchadnezzar what will be in the latter days.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the king was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As you were **lying there**, O king, your thoughts turned to **what would come after this**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is *your thoughts came into your mind upon your bed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel tells the king what he had been thinking about before he fell asleep. Nobody could have known it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An emperor lying awake wondering what happens after him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who answered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the One who uncovers mysteries showed you what will be**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is *the revealer of secrets*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel has already used the title twice in the chapter. It is his name for God through the whole book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disclaimer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for me, **this mystery was not opened to me because I have more wisdom than anyone living**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is holding the one thing the king wants, and the sentence he chooses is a denial of personal capacity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been assessed as *ten times better than all the magicians and astrologers in all his realm*, and had every ground to say so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reasons he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It was opened **so that the king would know its meaning**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And so that **you would understand the thoughts of your own heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both purposes are about the king. Neither is about Daniel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is the more surprising: the dream was given so a pagan emperor would understand what was in his own head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Daniel He gave more still: understanding in every kind of vision and dream.* See the note on Daniel 1:17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has the gift and refuses to be the reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the dream was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A statue: head of gold, chest and arms of silver, belly and thighs of bronze, legs of iron, feet of iron mixed with clay."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You watched while **a stone was cut out without hands**, which struck the image on its feet… and the stone that struck the image **became a great mountain and filled the whole earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Without hands** — the same construction Paul uses of the circumcision *no human hand performed*. See the note on Colossians 2:11–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interpretation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are this head of gold**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then four kingdoms, and: *in the days of these kings the God of heaven will set up a kingdom which shall never be destroyed.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king's reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then King Nebuchadnezzar fell on his face, prostrate before Daniel… and said, \"**Truly your God is the God of gods, the Lord of kings, and a revealer of secrets**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel's own title for God, in the king's mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel does with the promotion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is made ruler over the province of Babylon and chief of the wise men — and immediately *Daniel petitioned the king, and he set Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego over the affairs of the province of Babylon*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing he does with the office is get his three friends appointed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says at the moment of maximum leverage.** That it was not his wisdom, and that the whole thing was arranged so a king would understand his own thoughts."}]}, "Daniel 2:31–33": {"title": "The great statue", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A pagan king dreams the whole future of the world, and a Jewish captive tells him what he dreamed before interpreting it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel had to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nebuchadnezzar demanded that his wise men tell him the dream itself, not just its meaning — on pain of death for all of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the description in these verses is Daniel telling the king what was in the king's own head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The metals, top to bottom"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**head** — fine gold", "**chest and arms** — silver", "**belly and thighs** — bronze", "**legs** — iron", "**feet** — part iron, part clay"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel names the first outright: *you are that head of gold.* Babylon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, in his own words, *another kingdom inferior to yours*, and another, and a fourth as strong as iron that breaks everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of travel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each metal down the statue is **less valuable** and **harder**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gold to silver to bronze to iron to iron mixed with baked clay. Worth decreasing, strength increasing, and then the last one is strong and brittle at the same time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Empires get tougher and cheaper as the statue goes down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is standing on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its **feet** part iron and part clay.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything above rests on the weakest joint in it. Iron and fired clay do not bind — Daniel says so a few verses later: *they will not hold together, just as iron does not mix with clay.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole weight of world history, in this dream, is balanced on a mixture that cannot adhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the king saw it as"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Huge it was, **blazing with a bright and fearsome light**, and the look of it was **terrifying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how the kingdoms of men look from a throne — enormous, blazing, and frightening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel's God shows the same thing to Daniel as a beast-vision in chapter 7. The king saw a magnificent statue. The prophet saw animals."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the dream yet.** A stone, cut without hands, which strikes the feet and grinds the whole statue to chaff, and then becomes a mountain filling the earth. That is the next part of what Nebuchadnezzar saw, and it is the part the dream was given for. See the note on Daniel 2:44–45."}]}, "Daniel 2:44–45": {"title": "The stone cut without hands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The statue is enormous, blazing and terrifying, and what destroys it is a rock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not by human hands\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the stone you saw, **cut from the mountain but not by human hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one detail Daniel repeats. It is what distinguishes the stone from everything else in the dream — every empire in the statue was quarried, shaped and raised by men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody made this one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it hits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stone strikes the **feet** — the iron and clay, the weakest joint, the part everything else is standing on. And the whole statue comes down at once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Broke to pieces the iron, the bronze, the clay, the silver, and the gold.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the order Daniel lists them in: from the feet up to the head. It collapses from the bottom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to the pieces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this describe it: the fragments become *like chaff on a summer threshing floor*, and the wind carries them off so that no trace of them is found."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not defeated and occupied. Gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The kingdom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the days of those kings, **the God of heaven will set up a kingdom that will never be destroyed**. It will not be left to another people… and it will stand forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things said about it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**never destroyed** — unlike every kingdom in the statue", "**not left to another people** — no succession, no handover; the whole statue is a chain of handovers", "**stand forever**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the stone becomes a mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The stone that struck the statue became a great mountain and filled the whole earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It arrives small enough to be called a stone and ends up filling everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who this was said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Babylonian king, by a captive, about the end of Babylon and everything that would follow it — and Nebuchadnezzar's response is to fall on his face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Truly your God is the God of gods and the Lord of kings.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The great God has made known to the king **what is yet to come**. The dream is true, and its meaning is sure.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel signs off on it. Two clauses, both about reliability."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the stone is called elsewhere.** *The stone the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone* — Psalm 118, and quoted by Jesus of Himself the week He died, and by Peter in Acts 4. A stone the builders looked at and discarded; a stone no builder cut."}]}, "Daniel 6:23": {"title": "\"No wound was found on him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it gives the reason at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king's night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the king rejoiced greatly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Darius had spent the night fasting, refusing entertainment, unable to sleep, and had gone to the den *very early in the morning, with a lamenting voice.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who signed the decree is the one who could not rest. Daniel had a better night than the king did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lifting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave the order to **lift Daniel out of the den**. So Daniel was lifted up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice. He goes down by being thrown and comes up by being lifted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **no wound was found on him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is that no injury was discovered. It is an inspection — they looked him over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that he survived, or was mauled but recovered. Nothing on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **he had trusted in his God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole explanation the text offers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because he was brave, or innocent, or valuable to the empire — all of which were true. Because he trusted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews puts him in the roll of faith by his act rather than his name: *who through faith… **stopped the mouths of lions**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had done to get there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The decree forbade petitions to anyone but the king for thirty days, on pain of the den."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Daniel knew that the writing was signed, he went home. And in his upper room, with his windows open toward Jerusalem, **he knelt down on his knees three times that day, and prayed and gave thanks before his God, as was his custom since early days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not increase his praying or make a demonstration of it. He did not reduce it either. *As was his custom.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How old he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel was taken to Babylon as a youth in the first deportation. By the reign of Darius the Mede he is an old man — most reckonings put him in his eighties."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixty years in that court, under a succession of empires, and *they could find no charge or fault, because he was faithful; nor was there any error or fault found in him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His enemies say it themselves: *we shall not find any charge against this Daniel unless we find it against him concerning the law of his God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Darius, at dawn, at the mouth of the den: *\"Daniel, servant of the living God, has your God, whom you serve continually, been able to deliver you from the lions?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer from the dark: *\"O king, live forever! **My God sent His angel and shut the lions' mouths**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the king wrote afterwards.** A decree to every people and nation and language: *that in every dominion of my kingdom men must tremble and fear before the God of Daniel. For He is the living God… He delivers and rescues.* A night in a pit turned into an empire-wide proclamation."}]}, "Daniel 7:1–3": {"title": "\"When he woke, he wrote it all down\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book turns over here. For six chapters Daniel has interpreted other men's dreams; from this one on, he has his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the **first year that Belshazzar ruled Babylon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which places it before chapter 5 — the writing on the wall, on the night Belshazzar died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book is not in chronological order. Chapters 7 and 8 belong before 5 and 6, and Daniel has arranged the material by kind rather than by date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 2 to 7 are in Aramaic — the language of the empire. Chapters 1 and 8 to 12 are in Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose dream it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Daniel had **a dream**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "For six chapters he has been the man who explains. Now he is the one who needs explaining to — and inside the vision he asks: *I came near to one of those who stood by, and asked him the truth of all this.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Visions moved through his mind **as he lay on his bed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An old man asleep in his own room. Nothing about the setting is remarkable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he woke, **he wrote it all down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is *he wrote the dream, telling the main facts*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk had been told to do exactly this: *write the vision, carve it clear on tablets*. See the note on Habakkuk 2:2–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is where **the account begins**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book says so about itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the night I looked, and I saw **the four winds of heaven churning the great sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four winds — from every direction at once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sea in Hebrew imagery is the place of chaos, the thing God set boundaries on at creation. Revelation ends with *there was no more sea*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **up out of that sea came four huge beasts, each one unlike the others**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A lion with eagle's wings, a bear with three ribs in its mouth, a leopard with four heads and four wings, and a fourth *dreadful and terrible, exceedingly strong* with iron teeth and ten horns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same four empires as the statue in chapter 2 — and where Nebuchadnezzar saw gold, silver, bronze and iron, Daniel sees animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king dreams of a gleaming statue. A prophet dreams of what comes out of the sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thrones set in place, the Ancient of Days seated, *His garment white as snow*, and *a thousand thousands ministered to Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *behold, **One like the Son of Man, coming with the clouds of heaven**… to Him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom, that all peoples, nations, and languages should serve Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Son of Man** is the title Jesus uses of Himself more than any other, and it comes from here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes this verse at His trial, which is what the high priest tears his clothes over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it did to Daniel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, Daniel, was grieved in my spirit within my body, and **the visions of my head troubled me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end: *my thoughts greatly troubled me, and my countenance changed; **but I kept the matter in my heart**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not explain it or publish it. He writes it down and keeps it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The turn in the book.** The interpreter becomes the one who needs interpreting — and the first thing he does on waking is reach for something to write with."}]}, "Daniel 9:1–2": {"title": "He found it in the books", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man in Babylon is reading, and what he reads sends him to his knees for the rest of the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the **first year of Darius** son of Ahasuerus, a Mede by descent, who was made ruler over the kingdom of the Babylonians.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babylon has just fallen. The handwriting on the wall was last chapter; Belshazzar was killed that night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel was a teenager when he was deported. He is in his eighties now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, Daniel, **understood from the Scriptures**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I understood by the books*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not having a vision. He is reading, and the visions in this book come to a man who was already in the text."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was reading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the word the LORD had given to Jeremiah the prophet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah's letter to the exiles — the one with *I know the thoughts that I think toward you* in it — is addressed to Daniel's generation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah was alive within Daniel's lifetime. This is nearly contemporary writing being treated as Scripture, in Scripture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seventy years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That the desolation of Jerusalem was to last **seventy years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah 25 and 29 both give the figure. Daniel does the arithmetic and works out that the term is nearly up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *then I set my face toward the Lord God to make request by prayer and supplications, with fasting, sackcloth and ashes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just discovered that the thing is promised, dated and about to happen — and that is what makes him pray."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the prayer is like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve verses, and almost all of it is confession — *we have sinned and committed iniquity, we have done wickedly and rebelled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says **we** throughout. Daniel is the one man in the book with nothing recorded against him, and he puts himself inside the sentence every time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is asked on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not because of our righteous deeds, but **because of Your great mercy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states the ground of the request explicitly, and it is not the record of the people he is praying for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While I was still speaking and praying… **Gabriel** came to me in swift flight.* See the note on Daniel 9:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reply was dispatched before the prayer was finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened in history"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cyrus's decree, in Ezra 1, releases the exiles to go home and rebuild the house — *that the word of the LORD by the mouth of Jeremiah might be fulfilled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezra names the same prophecy Daniel was reading."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he did with a promise that was certain.** He prayed about it — for three weeks, in sackcloth. The prophecy's certainty is the reason given in the text for the praying, not an alternative to it."}]}, "Daniel 9:20–21": {"title": "\"While I was still speaking\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The answer arrives before the prayer is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**While I was still speaking** and praying… **while I was still in prayer**, Gabriel… came to me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel says it at the start of the sentence and again at the end. He wants the timing on record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had done the same thing three verses earlier — *while I was speaking, praying, and confessing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was praying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Confessing **my sin and the sin of my people Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He includes himself. Nothing in the book is charged against Daniel — even his enemies had to invent a law about prayer to get at him — and he says *my sin*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was asking for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Laying **my plea** before the LORD my God **for His holy mountain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for himself, not for release, not for his position under the new Persian administration. For the temple hill, seven hundred miles away, in ruins."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "***Gabriel**, the one I had seen in the vision before.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means *man of God* or *God is my strength*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He appears four times in Scripture and every appearance is a message: twice to Daniel here, then to Zechariah in the temple about John, then to Mary in Nazareth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel who explains Israel's timetable to Daniel is the one sent to announce its fulfilment five hundred years later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Swift flight\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *being caused to fly swiftly*, or *in weariness of flight* — the commentators divide over it. Either way it is a word about speed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*About the time of the **evening offering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three in the afternoon. The hour of the lamb on the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There has been no temple, no altar and no evening offering for sixty-eight years. There is nothing in Jerusalem to make it at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel keeps the hours anyway, and the angel arrives on one of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also the hour the veil tore."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is told first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before the message, the reason: *at the beginning of your supplications the command went out, and I have come to tell you, **for you are greatly beloved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At the beginning* — the reply was issued when he started, and the flight is what took the twenty-one days in the following chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the message was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seventy weeks — seventy sevens decreed on his people, with the Messiah cut off in the middle of it, and a date attached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked about seventy years and was given seventy sevens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been reading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah, and the seventy years of desolation. See the note on Daniel 9:1–2."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was said about him.** *Greatly beloved* — the Hebrew is *precious, treasured*. Gabriel says it to him three times across two chapters, to a civil servant in a pagan administration praying toward a demolished city."}]}, "Daniel 9:24": {"title": "Seventy sevens", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Daniel had been reading Jeremiah, counting the seventy years of the exile. The answer he gets back is about seventy of something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seventy **sevens** are decreed for your people and your holy city.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shabuim* — sevens, weeks. Seventy sevens is four hundred and ninety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel had been counting years; the answer comes back in the same currency, and Israel already reckoned in sevens — a sabbath year every seventh, a jubilee after seven sevens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose clock it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Decreed for **your people** and **your holy city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel and Jerusalem. The measure is not running on the world in general."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six things it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1. *to **finish the transgression*** 2. *to **put an end to sin*** 3. *to **make atonement** for wickedness* 4. *to **bring in everlasting righteousness*** 5. *to **seal up vision and prophecy*** 6. *to **anoint the Most Holy***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is something Israel does. All six are things done for and to them, and the third one names the mechanism: atonement — the Hebrew is **kaphar**, to cover, the word behind the Day of Atonement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everlasting* righteousness. Not a righteousness that has to be maintained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The division"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses that follow split the four hundred and ninety: seven sevens, then sixty-two sevens, then one seven left over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the going forth of the command to restore and build Jerusalem until Messiah the Prince* — sixty-nine sevens. Four hundred and eighty-three years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, in verse 26, something happens that is not in the arithmetic: *the Anointed One will be cut off and will have nothing.* See the note on Daniel 9:26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixty-nine sevens run out at the cutting off. The seventieth is described in verse 27 and has not been described as beginning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the sixty-ninth and the seventieth there is a space, and the church age is in it. The clock in this prophecy is Israel's, and it stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Daniel was praying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's first half is one of the great prayers of Scripture, and it is confession — *we have sinned, we have done wickedly* — for a nation, by a man with nothing personally to confess."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel arrives *at the time of the evening sacrifice*, in a city with no temple and no sacrifice for seventy years, and tells him he is *greatly beloved*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the answer is bigger than the question.** Daniel asked about seventy years. He was given seventy sevens, and the six things listed are not about getting a people home from Babylon. They are about finishing sin and bringing in a righteousness that does not end."}]}, "Daniel 9:26": {"title": "\"Cut off, and will have nothing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most extraordinary sentence in the prophets, and it is put in five words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it falls in the count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After the sixty-two sevens…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven sevens and sixty-two sevens make sixty-nine — four hundred and eighty-three years from the command to restore and rebuild Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Anointed One"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Anointed One** will be cut off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Mashiach** — Messiah. Daniel names Him by title, and the previous verse has already used it: *until Messiah the Prince.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Cut off\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **karath** is the ordinary word for executing a covenant-breaker, and it is also the verb used for making a covenant — *to cut* a covenant, from the animals divided in Genesis 15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And will have nothing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clause everything turns on. The older rendering is *but not for Himself*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is cut off, and gets nothing out of it. There is no throne at the end of the sixty-nine sevens, no kingdom, no crown, no gain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah had said the same thing in his own words: *He was cut off from the land of the living; **for the transgressions of My people** He was stricken.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people of **the prince who is to come** will destroy the city and the sanctuary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written in Babylon, in the sixth century BC, about a city that at that moment lay in ruins and had not yet been rebuilt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Titus and the Roman legions did it in AD 70 — the city and the temple, and not one stone left on another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus said the same, weeping, on the same hill: *they will not leave one stone standing on another in you, because you did not recognise the time when God came to you.* See the note on Luke 19:41–44."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Messiah is cut off **first**. The city and the sanctuary are destroyed **after**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel's sequence requires the Messiah to come and be killed before AD 70. The temple He had to come to was gone within forty years of the crucifixion, and the genealogies that would prove anyone's descent from David burned with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Its end will come like a flood\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is an overwhelming torrent. And then: *war will continue to the end, and desolations are decreed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence does not stop at AD 70. It runs on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in this verse.** The seventieth seven. Verse 27 describes it — a covenant made and broken in the middle of the week — and verse 26 stands between the sixty-ninth and it, describing a cutting off, a destroyed city, and a long stretch of war and desolation. The gap is where the sentence puts it. See the note on Daniel 9:24."}]}, "Deuteronomy 1:26–33": {"title": "\"The LORD hates us\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses recalls the day the nation refused, and he quotes what they said in their tents."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **you would not go up**. You **rebelled against the command** of the LORD your God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kadesh, in the second year. The twelve spies had come back; two said go, ten said no."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said in private"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **grumbled in your tents** and said.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you murmured in your tents*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses reports the tent conversation, which is not in the Numbers account. Forty years on, in his last sermon, he tells them what they said to each other when they thought nobody was listening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD hates us**. That's why He brought us out of Egypt — to hand us over to the Amorites and destroy us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sinah** — hatred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched the plagues, the sea, the pillar, the manna. Their conclusion about the motive was that He hated them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report they believed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They told us the people are **bigger and taller** than we are, the **cities are huge with walls up to the sky**, and they even saw **giants — the sons of the Anakim**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item is from the ten spies' report. Numbers has it: *we were like grasshoppers in our own sight, and so we were in their sight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in it was untrue. The walls were high and the Anakim were there — Joshua and Caleb never disputed the facts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses said at the time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't be terrified. Don't be afraid of them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD your God, who **goes ahead of you**, **He Himself will fight for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence he gave them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Just as you saw Him do **in Egypt right before your eyes**, and **in this wilderness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things they had personally watched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You saw how the LORD your God **carried you, the way a father carries his son**, along the whole road you traveled until you reached this place.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nasa** — to lift and bear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A father carrying a son is not a picture of a God who hates. Moses puts it directly against the sentence they said in their tents."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yet even after all that, **you did not trust the LORD your God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you did not believe*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses names the failure, and it is not cowardice or bad intelligence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had been doing the whole time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The One who **went ahead of you on the journey, searching out a place for you to camp**, in **fire by night and in a cloud by day**, to show you the way to walk.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *searching out* is **tur** — the same verb used of the twelve spies searching out the land."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God had been scouting ahead the whole way. They sent twelve men to do the job that was already being done, and believed the report."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of circling Mount Seir. See the note on Deuteronomy 2:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And of that period Moses says in the same book: *these forty years the LORD your God has been with you, and **you have lacked nothing**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is listening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The children of the people who said it. Everyone over twenty that day is dead except Caleb and Joshua."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they got wrong.** Not the height of the walls. Moses does not correct a single fact in their report — he answers the sentence about who was bringing them out and why."}]}, "Deuteronomy 11:10–12": {"title": "Not like Egypt", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses describes the land they are entering by telling them what it is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Egypt's agriculture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where you planted your seed and watered it by foot, like a garden of vegetables.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt had no rainfall to speak of. Farming there meant the Nile, irrigation channels, and lifting water — either by foot-driven wheels or by opening and closing channels with your feet in the mud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was reliable and it was relentless. The water was there, and getting it onto the field was entirely your own labour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The land ahead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A land of hills and valleys, drinking in the rain from heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hills cannot be irrigated from a river. Everything depends on rain, which nobody can dig for, schedule, or manufacture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From a farmer's point of view this is a downgrade in security. Moses presents it as an upgrade — and the reason follows immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A land the LORD your God cares for. The eyes of the LORD your God are on it all the time, from the first of the year to the last.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *cares for* is a verb about seeking out and attending to. And *the eyes of the LORD are on it* — continuously, the whole year, without a season off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two descriptions belong together: a land whose water comes from above is a land where attention has to come from above too. What looks like less control is described as more care."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this lands in the New Testament.** Egypt's watering by foot is effort applied to a channel you dug yourself; the hill country is a place you cannot irrigate and must receive. It is the same distinction Paul keeps drawing between working for a wage and receiving a promise — see the note on Romans 4:13–15."}]}, "Deuteronomy 11:13–15": {"title": "The early rain and the late", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two rains, named separately, because in that country they did two different things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the farmer's year looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land had a long dry summer with no rainfall at all. Everything depended on two seasons of rain at opposite ends of the growing year:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the early rain** — autumn, October to November. It softened ground baked hard all summer so a plough could break it and seed could go in.", "**the late rain** — spring, March to April. It came just before harvest and filled out the grain in the ear."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Without the first there was no planting. Without the second the crop came up thin and the harvest was poor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why both are promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will send rain on your land in its season, the early rain and the late rain, so you may gather your grain, your new wine, and your oil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise covers the whole cycle, beginning to end. God undertakes to start the year and to finish it — and the three crops named are exactly the three the land was known for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So you may gather\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rain is God's; the gathering is theirs. Nobody in this passage is promised a harvest that appears without a plough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of it: He supplies what no one can manufacture, and they do the work that rain makes possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you truly listen… loving the LORD your God and serving Him with all your heart and all your soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Covenant terms, stated plainly. And notice what the listening is described as — loving and serving, not merely complying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the whole image matters.** These verses come three lines after Moses says the land is not like Egypt, where you watered by foot from a river. See the note on Deuteronomy 11:10–12. The two rains are the reason: a country you cannot irrigate is a country where the harvest is asked for rather than engineered."}]}, "Deuteronomy 11:18–21": {"title": "\"As many as the days the heavens are above the earth\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Where the words are to be kept, and the promise attached to keeping them there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four places"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let these words settle into your **heart** and your **soul**. Tie them on your **hand**… bind them on your **forehead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Inward first, then outward. Hebrew thought does not separate them: what is on the hand governs what you do, what is between the eyes governs what you look at, and both are downstream of the heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Observant Jews still take the outer instruction literally — the small boxes worn on the arm and the head, and the case on the doorpost, come from this passage and its twin in chapter 6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Talk of them when you sit at home and when you walk on the road, when you lie down and when you rise up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two locations and two moments — indoors and out, last thing and first thing. Between them they cover the day, which is the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here describes a lesson. It describes conversation, in the ordinary places, at the ordinary hours."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being taught"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teach them to your children.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction about parents' hearts arrives first and the children second. What gets passed on is what has already settled in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that your days and your children's days may be many in the land… as many as the days the heavens are above the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is a measurement that cannot be measured. As long as there is a sky over the ground — that is the span being described."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it is attached to.** These verses come immediately after the promise of the early and late rain, and immediately before *every place where you set your foot will be yours.* The words in the heart, the rain in its season, and the ground underfoot are one passage."}]}, "Deuteronomy 11:26–28": {"title": "\"Today I am setting before you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses lays two options in front of a nation and tells them to choose. It is the clearest statement of the covenant's terms in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look — today I am setting before you a blessing and a curse. The blessing, if you obey… the curse, if you will not obey.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no ambiguity in it. Blessing is attached to obedience; curse to disobedience. The whole arrangement runs on the word **if**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the Sinai covenant was: a conditional agreement, freely entered into, with both outcomes stated in advance and in detail. Deuteronomy 28 spells out both lists at length."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it assumes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer is genuine and the choice is real. Moses is not describing a trap — he is putting the actual terms in front of people about to enter the land, and he tells them to write them on their doorframes and teach them to their children."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is unfair. Everything depends on a performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the New Testament takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes this covenant structure and then says something startling about it: *Christ redeemed us from the curse of the law, having become a curse for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two columns did not disappear. He took one of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why a Christian reads this passage from a different position than the crowd standing at the Jordan. The if-clause has been answered by somebody else's obedience — which is the argument of Romans 5:18–19."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How Moses puts it later.** In chapter 30 he sets out the same choice and adds two words that are not here: *therefore choose life.* He does not leave it balanced. Having laid out both, he tells them which one to take."}]}, "Deuteronomy 14:22–27": {"title": "What the tithe was spent on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the tithe most people have never read, and it is a feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the presence of the LORD your God, at the place He chooses… **eat the tithe** of your grain, new wine, and oil, and the firstborn of your herds and flocks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tithe is brought to the sanctuary and then eaten — by the man who brought it, with his household."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not handed over. Eaten, in God's presence, as a meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that you may learn to hold the LORD your God in awe always.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated purpose of the exercise is not funding. It is that he would learn something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The travel clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But if the place is too far, and the journey is more than you can carry **because the LORD has blessed you so richly**…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A problem of prosperity: a man does well enough that a tenth of it will not fit on the cart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then trade the tithe for silver, tie up the money, and take it with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Convert it, carry the cash, buy again at the other end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he buys with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There, **spend it on whatever your heart desires** — cattle, sheep, wine, strong drink, **anything you long for** — and eat it there in the presence of the LORD your God, and **celebrate**, you and your household.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read that twice. It is a command to spend a tenth of the year's income on whatever he most wants, and enjoy it in God's presence with his family."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Strong drink is named specifically. *Anything you long for* is the phrase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is to throw a party."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the one condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **do not neglect the Levite in your towns**, for he has no portion or inheritance of his own.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one who has nothing is brought to the same table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three different tithes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reading Deuteronomy alongside Numbers 18, the Old Testament describes more than one:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the Levites' tithe** — Numbers 18, supporting the tribe with no land", "**this one** — the festival tithe, eaten by the giver at the sanctuary", "**the third-year tithe** — stored in the towns for the Levite, the foreigner, the fatherless and the widow. See the note on Deuteronomy 14:28–29."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only one of the three funds ministry. One is a feast, and one is for the poor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nowhere in this passage is the tithe described as a payment, a debt, or the price of anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that you may learn to hold the LORD your God in awe.* That is the reason given, and the mechanism for learning it is a good dinner."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Israel was leaving behind.** They had spent four hundred years in a country where everything they produced belonged to somebody else. The first thing the law tells them to do with a tenth of the harvest is eat it in front of God and enjoy themselves."}]}, "Deuteronomy 14:28–29": {"title": "The third-year tithe", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Every third year the tenth does not go to the sanctuary at all. It stays in town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gather a tenth of that year's produce and **store it up in your towns**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not carried to the place God chose. Left locally, where the people who need it are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four groups, and the passage names them in order:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the Levite**, *who has no land of his own*", "**the foreigner**", "**the fatherless**", "**the widow**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four categories of person in Israel with no inheritance and no legal means of supporting themselves. A man without land in an agricultural economy had nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law names them together repeatedly — *the fatherless and the widow, and the stranger within your gates* — and builds provision for them into the calendar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Eat their fill\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May come and **eat their fill**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to be satisfied, to eat to satiety. Not a ration and not a token."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that the LORD your God may bless you in all the work of your hands.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessing is stated as the consequence, and it is on the work rather than on the tithe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the three tithes cover"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reading the law together, the tenth does three different jobs:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Numbers 18** — support for the tribe with no land", "**Deuteronomy 14:22–27** — a feast, eaten by the giver, at the sanctuary. See the note on that passage.", "**this one** — the poor, every third year"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the three funds ministry. One is a celebration. One is welfare."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anybody arguing about the tithe from the Old Testament is arguing about a system that fed the widow and paid for a party."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same instinct all through the law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gleaning: *when you reap the harvest of your land, you shall not wholly reap the corners of your field… you shall leave them for the poor and the stranger.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sabbath year: whatever grows of itself is for *the poor of your people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The jubilee: land returns, debts release."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of it is charity in the modern sense. It is written into how the economy runs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it turns up in the New Testament.** *Pure and undefiled religion before God and the Father is this: **to visit orphans and widows in their trouble**, and to keep oneself unspotted from the world.* James picks two of the four groups from this passage and calls attending to them the definition of religion."}]}, "Deuteronomy 2:1–7": {"title": "\"You have gone around this mountain long enough\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses recounts the years nobody talks about, and the summary at the end of it is generous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The circling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **many days we wandered around the hill country of Seir**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *we compassed mount Seir many days*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the bulk of the forty years, and Deuteronomy gives it one sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 33 lists the camps — about forty of them — and says almost nothing about any."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have gone around this mountain long enough. Now turn north**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *rav lakem* — enough for you, sufficient."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The circling was not indefinite. It ended with a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they are about to pass"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are about to pass through the territory of **your relatives, the descendants of Esau**, who live in Seir.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's brother's family, four centuries on. See the note on Genesis 27:38–40, where Isaac tells Esau his dwelling will be away from the dew of heaven — and Seir is exactly that country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not provoke them**, because **I will not give you any of their land — not even enough to set your foot on**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *not so much as a foot breadth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel is marching to take a country, and is told there is a border they may not cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have given Mount Seir to Esau as his possession**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau, who sold the birthright and was blessed second, has a land grant from God, and Israel is not permitted to touch it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy says the same about Moab and Ammon, Lot's descendants, in the same chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they must do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You may **buy food from them with money**… and **buy water from them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They pay. An army of that size could have taken what it wanted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **the LORD your God has blessed you in everything your hands have done**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said about the wandering years, and about a people who had been walking in circles as a consequence of refusing to go in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He has **watched over your journey through this great wilderness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**These forty years the LORD your God has been with you, and you have lacked nothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you have not lacked a thing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 8 adds the detail: *your garments did not wear out on you, nor did your foot swell, these forty years.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the people said about the same period"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why did you drag us up out of Egypt into this terrible place?\"* See the note on Numbers 20:2–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same forty years, described by the people inside them and by Moses looking back, and the two accounts do not resemble each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Moses is standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the plains of Moab, in the eleventh month of the fortieth year, weeks from his own death, addressing a generation who were children when it started."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verdict on the wasted years.** Blessed in everything, watched over, and lacking nothing. Moses says it about the period the nation spent going in circles."}]}, "Deuteronomy 21:15–17": {"title": "The firstborn's right", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A law about inheritance, written for a situation the book of Genesis is full of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a man has **two wives, one loved and one unloved**, and both bear him sons, and **the firstborn is the son of the unloved wife**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *one beloved and one hated* — **senuah**, which in legal usage means the less-favoured rather than the despised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose story this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's. Two wives, one loved and one not — *Jacob loved Rachel*, and *when the LORD saw that Leah was unloved, He opened her womb.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leah's firstborn was Reuben. Rachel's was Joseph."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is written about the exact case in Genesis 29, and Moses is writing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **may not treat the son of the loved wife as the firstborn** in place of the true firstborn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Preference is not allowed to redraw the inheritance. The law removes the father's discretion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The requirement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He must **recognize the firstborn**… and **give him a double share of everything he owns**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **pi shenayim** — literally *a mouth of two*, two portions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The idiom uses **peh**, mouth, for a share. The same word is the edge of a blade, and the teeth of Isaiah's threshing sledge. See the note on Isaiah 41:14–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That son is **the first sign of his father's strength**; the right of the firstborn belongs to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the beginning of his strength* — **reshith ono**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a statement about what the child represents rather than about his character."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened in Jacob's family"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did exactly what the law forbids. Reuben lost the birthright — 1 Chronicles says so outright: *Reuben was the firstborn, but because he defiled his father's bed, **his birthright was given to the sons of Joseph***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Joseph received a double portion in the most literal sense: his two sons, Ephraim and Manasseh, were each counted as a tribe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve tribes, thirteen names, because one man got two shares."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Elisha uses the phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Please, **let me inherit a double portion of your spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same idiom. He is not asking to be twice the prophet Elijah was; he is asking to be treated as the heir. See the note on 2 Kings 2:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah's answer — *you have asked a hard thing* — makes sense in that light. It was not his to allot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha shouted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My father! My father!**\"* — at the moment the chariot came. See the note on 2 Kings 2:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for the relation the double portion depends on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament calls Christ"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Prototokos** — firstborn. *The firstborn among many brethren*, *the firstborn from the dead*, *the firstborn over all creation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And of the readers: *the church of the firstborn who are registered in heaven.* See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the law protects.** Not the favourite. It takes the father's preference out of the calculation entirely and fixes the double share to birth order."}]}, "Deuteronomy 25:1–3": {"title": "Forty, but no more", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A rule about corporal punishment, and the reason given for the limit is about dignity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The court"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When two men have a dispute, they are to **bring it to court**, and the judges will decide, **clearing the innocent and condemning the guilty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *justifying the righteous and condemning the wicked* — **tsadaq** and **rasha**, the two verbs Proverbs pairs. See the note on Proverbs 17:15–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the definition of a working court, stated in one line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the guilty one deserves a beating, the judge shall **make him lie down and be flogged in his presence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**In his presence** — the judge who passed the sentence has to watch it carried out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *before his face*. There is no delegating it to somewhere out of sight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The number of lashes **fitting his offense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *according to his fault, by number*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proportion is required, and the total is to be counted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ceiling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He may give **forty, but no more**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To go beyond that would **degrade your brother before your eyes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *lest your brother be dishonoured before you* — **niqlah**, made light of, held in contempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words matter in that sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Your brother.** He has just been convicted and sentenced, and the law still calls him that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Before your eyes.** The concern is what watching it would do — to him, and to the people watching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Jews did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rabbinic practice reduced it to thirty-nine, so that a miscount could not push the total over the limit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul records receiving it five times: *from the Jews five times I received **forty stripes minus one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two hundred lashes, by his own count."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus received"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman scourging had no limit. The **flagellum** was leather thongs weighted with bone or metal, and the number was left to the soldiers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospels record the fact in a single word and describe nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah says about the appearance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His visage was **marred more than any man**, and His form more than the sons of men.* See the note on Isaiah 52:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *disfigured*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Hebrew for a blow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Makkah** — a stroke, a wound, a plague. It is the word in this passage for the beating, and the same word runs through the curses of Deuteronomy 28 for the diseases. See the note on Deuteronomy 28:58–68."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for a lash and the word for a plague are the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall **not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it twice about paying those who preach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law puts a limit on beating a convicted man and a prohibition on starving a working animal in consecutive verses."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the ceiling protects.** Not the guilty man's comfort — the beating happens. The reason Moses gives is that past a point it stops being a sentence and starts being degradation, in front of witnesses."}]}, "Deuteronomy 25:17–19": {"title": "Remember Amalek", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses about one nation, and the instruction opens and closes with the same command about memory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How they fought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How they **met you on the road** and **cut down all the stragglers at the rear**, when you were **weary and worn out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a battle line. Amalek came at the back, where the exhausted were — the old, the sick, the ones carrying children, whoever could not keep the pace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the method of a predator, not an army. You attack what is already struggling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When they came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had just come out of Egypt. They had no land, no army, no experience, and they were *weary and worn out* from the road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek picked the moment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"They had no fear of God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing said about their character, and it is the explanation for the rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Amalek is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is Esau's grandson — the flesh's line, born of the man who sold a birthright for a bowl of stew and wanted the blessing without the covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek is self-effort: the constant, wearying pressure to get it done yourself, to strive, to keep up, to secure your own position because if you do not do it nobody will. It comes at you hardest when you are already tired, and it takes the stragglers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why it has to be blotted out and not managed. Something that hunts the weary cannot be kept on a leash."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was commanded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall **blot out the memory of Amalek** from under heaven. **Do not forget**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves are held together on purpose: erase the memory of them, and do not forget to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was to be done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the LORD your God gives you **rest** from all your enemies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not while fighting. From rest. The command was given for the moment of settled security — which is exactly when a man decides the old enemy is no longer worth dealing with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul was sent to do it and spared Agag, the Amalekite king, and the best of the livestock — *but everything despised and worthless, that they utterly destroyed.* He kept what looked useful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It cost him the kingdom. An Amalekite is the man standing over him claiming to have finished him on Gilboa. And four hundred years later *Haman the Agagite* has a signet ring and a decree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is spared because it seems useful comes back with authority. See the notes on 2 Samuel 1:5–10 and Esther 3:1–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first battle and the raised hands.** Exodus 17 records the fight, and Israel prevailed only while Moses' hands were held up — by Aaron and Hur, because his own arms gave out. Then the altar built and named *The LORD is my banner*, and the words *the LORD will have war with Amalek from generation to generation.* The one enemy that is never beaten by effort was beaten under raised hands somebody else was holding."}]}, "Deuteronomy 28:1–6": {"title": "\"Every one of these blessings will catch up with you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The blessings of the covenant, and the whole passage hangs on the first word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If** you will truly listen to the LORD your God and **carefully live out all His commands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles the verb — *if listening you will listen*. And *all* is in the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the old covenant stated in its own terms. Everything after it depends on the clause in front of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Every one of these blessings will come and **catch up with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nasag** — to overtake, to reach, to pursue and come up with. It is the word for an army catching a fleeing enemy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessings are described as chasing the person down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb turns up in the curses later in the chapter, about the other list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed **in the city**, and blessed **in the field**. Blessed **the children you bear**, the crops of your ground, and the young of your herds and flocks. Blessed **your basket and your kneading bowl**. Blessed **when you come in**, and blessed **when you go out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Town and country. Family, farm and flock. The basket you carry it in and the bowl you mix it in. Arriving and leaving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a deliberately total list — the two places you can be, the three things you can produce, the two containers you own, and the two directions you can travel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left outside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The kneading bowl"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The smallest item on the list, and it is in a national covenant document."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever this chapter is, it is not written at a distance from ordinary life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verses 1 to 14 are the blessings. Verses 15 to 68 are the curses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen verses of one and fifty-four of the other, and the second list begins *if you do not obey.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who rests on the works of the law is under a curse, for it is written: **cursed is everyone who does not continue in all the things written in the book of the law, to do them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is quoting the sister passage in Deuteronomy 27, and the killing word is *all* — the same *all* as in verse 1 here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And his answer: *Christ has bought us out from under the curse of the law **by becoming a curse for us**.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last of the blessings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD will open to you **His good treasure**, the heavens, to give the rain to your land in its season… **you shall lend to many nations, but you shall not borrow**.\"* See the note on Deuteronomy 28:7–14."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is doing structurally.** Six tribes stood on Mount Gerizim to pronounce the blessings and six on Mount Ebal for the curses, with the ark in the valley between. The blessings in this chapter were shouted across a valley by half a nation to the other half."}]}, "Deuteronomy 28:20–24": {"title": "Bronze above and iron below", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Part of the long half of the chapter, and the images in it are agricultural and exact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 28 gives fourteen verses of blessing and fifty-four of curse. See the note on Deuteronomy 28:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses reads out both sides of the agreement to a nation standing at the border, before anyone has crossed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD will send **confusion and frustration on everything you put your hand to**, until you are wiped out and ruined.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *cursing, confusion and rebuke* — three words for the same thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All because **you turned your back on Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *because of the wickedness of your doings, whereby you have forsaken Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated once and not repeated. The chapter names one cause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plagues"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will make **plague after plague cling to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *cling* is **dabaq** — to stick fast, to adhere. The word used of a man cleaving to his wife."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will strike you with **wasting disease, with fever and burning heat, with drought and blight and mildew** that will **chase you down to your grave**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven conditions, and three of them are crop diseases rather than human ones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Blight** and **mildew** are what happens to standing grain — the first from a hot east wind, the second from too much damp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sky and the ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The sky above you will turn to bronze** and **the ground beneath you to iron**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two metals, and the direction of each is the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bronze does not let water through. Iron does not let a plough in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sealed sky and an unworkable floor — nothing coming down and nothing going in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD will **turn your rain to dust and powder**, drifting down from the sky until you are destroyed.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not no rain. Rain replaced by what falls in a dust storm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **khamsin** off the desert carries fine dust that settles on everything, and the image would have been familiar to every farmer listening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing version of the same clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen verses earlier: *\"The LORD will **open to you His good treasure, the heavens, to give the rain to your land in its season**, and to bless all the work of your hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew calls the sky a treasury."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bronze in one column and an opened treasury in the other, and the same sky in both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elijah did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shut the sky for three and a half years, and then prayed it open. See the note on 1 Kings 17:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James notes that he was *a man with a nature like ours*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the curses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ has **redeemed us from the curse of the law**, having become a curse for us — for it is written, \"Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree.\"* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *redeemed* is **exagorazo** — bought out of the market."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Back to Egypt in ships, offering themselves as slaves, and nobody buying. See the note on Deuteronomy 28:58–68."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two metals.** Bronze overhead and iron underfoot. A farmer hears the whole sentence at once: nothing arriving from above, and nothing possible below."}]}, "Deuteronomy 28:58–68": {"title": "Struck down and scattered", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the longest list of curses in Scripture, and it is read out before anybody has crossed the river."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proportion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 28 gives fourteen verses of blessing and fifty-four of curse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is not describing what will happen. He is setting out the terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you will **not carefully keep every word of this law** written in this book, and will not **stand in awe of this glorious and awesome name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Every word.** The chapter's terms are total, which is the clause Paul quotes in Galatians: *cursed is everyone who does not continue in **all things** which are written in the book of the law, to do them.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plagues"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He will send **terrible and lasting plagues** on you and your children, **severe and unrelenting sickness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *plagues* is **makkah** — a blow, a stroke, a wound. The same word used for the lashes in the flogging law. See the note on Deuteronomy 25:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diseases of Egypt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will bring back on you **all the diseases of Egypt you dreaded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched them for four hundred years. The blessing chapter had promised the opposite: *the LORD will keep every sickness far from you.* See the note on Deuteronomy 7:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The open clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **every sickness and plague not even written in this book**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list does not close. Whatever is not named is included."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You who were once **as many as the stars** will be left few in number.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise to Abraham, run backwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **just as the LORD once delighted to do you good and multiply you, so He will let you be uprooted**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scattering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will scatter you **among all the peoples, from one end of the earth to the other**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Among those nations you will find **no rest, no place for the sole of your foot**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew echoes Noah's dove, which found no rest for the sole of her foot and came back to the ark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inner state"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There the LORD will give you **a trembling heart, failing eyes, and a soul worn thin with despair**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your life will **hang in doubt** before you; night and day you will be afraid.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the morning you will say, '**If only it were evening!**' and in the evening, '**If only it were morning!**'*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most exact description of dread in the Old Testament, and it is in a legal document."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD will send you **back to Egypt in ships**, by **the very road He promised you would never travel again**; and there you will **offer yourselves for sale to your enemies as slaves — and no one will even buy you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends with the exodus reversed: back to Egypt, by sea, offering themselves as slaves, and unsaleable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing lower is available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ has **redeemed us from the curse of the law**, having become a curse for us — for it is written, \"Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *redeemed* is **exagorazo** — to buy out of the market."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That the blessing of Abraham might come upon the Gentiles in Christ Jesus.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last curse and the cross"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 21, four chapters earlier: *he who is hanged is accursed of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes that verse and applies it to the crucifixion. The document that lists the curses also contains the sentence he uses to say where they went."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is.** Not a prediction and not a threat. Moses reads out both sides of an agreement to a nation standing at the border, and gives fifty-four verses to one side of it."}]}, "Deuteronomy 28:7–14": {"title": "\"The head and never the tail\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The blessing list of the covenant, and it is as detailed and generous as anything in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["enemies who come at you one way and scatter seven", "blessing on the barns and on everything you set your hand to", "established as His own holy people, so that the nations see it", "overflow of children, livestock, harvest", "*the heavens, His rich storehouse* opened for rain in season", "lending to many nations and borrowing from none", "**the head and never the tail; always on top and never underneath**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The images are agricultural and national — a farming people who wanted rain, children, animals and safety, being told they would have all four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you listen to His commands and hold to them, never turning aside to run after other gods.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is stated at the end of the passage and it governs everything before it. This is the covenant made at Sinai: a real offer, with real terms, and the curses in the same chapter are the other half of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the chapter whole and the proportions are sobering — fourteen verses of blessing, and over fifty of curse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How a Christian stands to this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul deals with this covenant directly. *Christ redeemed us from the curse of the law, having become a curse for us* — and he says why: *so that the blessing of Abraham might come to the Gentiles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say the blessings were withdrawn. He says the curse was taken by somebody else, and the blessing let out to people who were never under Sinai at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the difference between reading this list as terms to meet and reading it as a description of what a covenant-keeping God is like toward His people."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"The head and not the tail.\"** The Hebrew image is about position in a line — leading rather than trailing. In context it belongs with lending rather than borrowing: a nation that others come to, rather than one that depends on them."}]}, "Deuteronomy 32:1–4": {"title": "\"Let my teaching fall like rain\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening of Moses' last song, and he asks for his words to behave like weather."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four kinds of falling water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let my teaching fall like **rain**, let my words settle like the **dew**, like gentle **showers** on new grass, like **soft rain** on the tender green.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four terms, and every one is gentle. Nothing here is a downpour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice he specifies what it lands on: *new grass* and *the tender green* — the most fragile stage of a plant's life, when heavy rain does damage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Teaching, in this image, is measured to what can take it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen, you heavens… hear, O earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses calls sky and ground as witnesses, which is the formal language of a covenant lawsuit. The song that follows is not gentle at all — it is a long account of a people who will forget, and the consequences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the soft rain of the opening is the manner, not the content."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he proclaims"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will proclaim the name of the LORD. Oh, tell of the greatness of our God!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before a word of the history, he states the subject. The song is about Israel's failure and it is announced as being about God's name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Rock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is the Rock — His works are perfect, and all His ways are just. A faithful God who does no wrong, upright and true is He.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first time God is called the Rock, and Moses uses it repeatedly through the song. Five statements in one verse, and each is about consistency: perfect, just, faithful, without wrong, upright."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is establishing the standard the rest of the song will measure the people against."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When it was sung.** Moses is about to climb Nebo and die. This song was given to be taught to the children so that when everything he predicted came true, the words would already be in their mouths — a lesson planted before it was needed."}]}, "Deuteronomy 32:19–25": {"title": "\"I will hide My face\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Song of Moses, taught to Israel before they entered the land, and it tells them in advance what will happen and what it will look like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is about to die. He has not yet let them cross the Jordan, and God tells him to write a song and teach it to the people so that *when many troubles come upon them, this song will testify against them, for it will not be forgotten.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a song about the future, given to be memorised before any of it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will hide My face** from them,\" He said, \"and see what their end will be.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence, and it is the sentence the whole exile is described by."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I will destroy them*. Not *I will leave them*. **Hide My face** — the Hebrew is to conceal, to cover over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What hiding is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the song is the proof. Read on past this passage and it goes to *the LORD will vindicate His people and have compassion on His servants… He will avenge the blood of His servants… and make atonement for His land and His people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The face is hidden and He is still working. That is the season Hosea named *Lo-Ammi*, and it is the season the book of Esther is set inside — no name of God in ten chapters, and His hand on every one of them. See the notes on Hosea 1:8–9 and Esther 1:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mirror"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They made Me jealous with **what is no god**… so I will make them jealous with **what is no people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves are built to match. *No-god* answers *no-people*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul picks up exactly this line in Romans 10 and applies it to the Gentiles being brought in — the *no-people* who become *sons of the living God*. See the note on Romans 9:25–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Children who have no faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is children in whom there is no **emun** — no faithfulness, no steadiness, from the root behind *amen*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not children who did wrong. Children with nothing dependable in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the song is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD saw it and turned away, **grieved by His sons and daughters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is grieved and they are still called sons and daughters. The relationship is named in the same verse as the turning away."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the song ends.** Not with the arrows. *Rejoice, O nations, with His people, for He will avenge the blood of His servants… and provide atonement for His land and His people.* Moses taught them the whole thing, and the last note in it is atonement."}]}, "Deuteronomy 33:24–25": {"title": "\"May your strength match all your days\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses' last words, blessing the tribes one by one. Asher's blessing ends with a line that has outlived its context by a long way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let your gates be barred with iron and bronze, and may your strength match all your days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is famously compressed — four words, roughly *as your days, your strength*. There is no verb in it, which is why translations differ: *as thy days, so shall thy strength be*, or *your strength will equal your days*, or *may your strength match all your days*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sense everyone agrees on is a ratio. Whatever the days require, the strength is measured to match."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why iron and bronze"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asher's territory was the northern coastal strip — fertile, rich in olive oil, and exposed. *Let him bathe his feet in oil* is about the olive presses; the barred gates are about the border."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessing pairs prosperity with security, and then adds strength for the days themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How to read it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a blessing spoken over a tribe by a dying man, not a promise of physical vigour to individuals. Read as it stands, it says the supply is matched to the demand — not stockpiled in advance, and not exhausted before the days are."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verses after it.** Moses ends the whole blessing with *the eternal God is your refuge, and underneath are the everlasting arms* — and then he climbs Nebo and dies. The last recorded thing he does is bless people who are going somewhere he is not."}]}, "Deuteronomy 34:5–8": {"title": "A hundred and twenty, and a grave nobody found", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last paragraph about Moses, and it records three things nobody else in the Bible gets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He was buried by God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He buried him in the valley in Moab… but to this day no one knows where his grave is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject of that verb is the LORD. Moses climbs a mountain alone, dies alone, and is buried by God — no funeral party, no family plot, no marker."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason is not given. Jude preserves a strange fragment about a dispute over the body, and the plainest guess is the simplest: a known grave of Moses would have become a shrine within a generation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The physical detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses was a hundred and twenty years old when he died, yet his eyes were not weak and his strength had not left him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer goes out of his way to say Moses was not failing. He did not die of old age in the ordinary sense; he died because his part was finished and the river was somebody else's to cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That detail sits in scripture alongside Psalm 90 — Moses' own psalm, with its seventy years, eighty if we are strong. The man who wrote that line lived to 120 undimmed. The Bible keeps both on the page."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Thirty days.** Israel wept for Moses for a month, the same span they later gave Aaron. Then the book ends by saying no prophet has risen since like him, whom the LORD knew face to face — and the next book opens with God telling Joshua that Moses is dead and it is time to cross. Both the grief and the moving on are given their room."}]}, "Deuteronomy 34:9": {"title": "The hands that were laid on him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, near the last page of the Torah, about what transferred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses has died on Nebo, shown the whole land from Gilead to the Western Sea, and *He buried him in a valley in the land of Moab, opposite Beth Peor, **but no one knows his grave to this day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then thirty days of weeping, and then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The filling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **Joshua son of Nun was filled with the spirit of wisdom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ruach chokmah** — spirit of wisdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase used of Bezalel, who built the tabernacle. See the note on Exodus 31:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **Moses had laid his hands on him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for Moses had laid his hands upon him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse gives a cause, and it is a gesture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the laying on happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 27. Moses had asked God for a successor — *let the LORD set a man over the congregation… **that the congregation of the LORD may not be like sheep which have no shepherd***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the instruction: *take Joshua the son of Nun with you, a man in whom is the Spirit, and **lay your hand on him**… **and you shall give some of your authority to him**, that all the congregation of the children of Israel may be obedient.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some of your authority.* The Hebrew is *of your honour*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the order: Joshua is already described as *a man in whom is the Spirit* before the hands go on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the Israelites listened to him** and did what the LORD had commanded Moses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purpose stated in Numbers — that the congregation would obey — is recorded as having happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Joshua was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' attendant for forty years. He had led the fight against Amalek, gone partway up Sinai, stayed in the tent of meeting when Moses left it, and been one of the two spies who said go. See the notes on Exodus 17:8–13 and Numbers 14:5–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **since then there has not arisen in Israel a prophet like Moses, whom the LORD knew face to face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Torah ends by saying the succession was not a replacement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua gets the wisdom and the authority. The face-to-face is not transferred, and Numbers 12 had already marked it as unique. See the note on Numbers 12:4–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the gesture goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul to Timothy: *do not neglect **the gift that is in you, which was given to you by prophecy with the laying on of the hands of the eldership**.* See the note on 1 Timothy 4:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the second letter: *stir up the gift of God which is in you **through the laying on of my hands***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same act, and Paul attributes the gift to it twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joshua is told at the start of his own book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Moses my servant is gone. So now it's your turn**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**The way I was with Moses, that's how I will be with you**.\"* See the note on Joshua 1:1–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse attributes it to.** Not experience, and not the forty years. Two hands on a head — and the nation listened to him afterward."}]}, "Deuteronomy 6:10–12": {"title": "\"Wells you did not dig\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses lists what they are about to inherit, and every item has the same clause attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great and beautiful **cities you did not build**, houses **filled with every good thing you did not fill**, **wells you did not dig**, vineyards and olive trees **you did not plant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four possessions, four denials."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody else laid every stone, stocked every larder, cut every cistern and planted every tree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters that they are wells"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Digging a cistern out of limestone by hand is the work of months. Planting an olive grove is a fifteen-year investment before the first serious crop — you plant olives for your grandchildren."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They walk into the finished version of other people's decades."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The danger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **when you have eaten your fill, then be careful**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when you are hungry. When you are full."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning is attached to the point of satisfaction, and Deuteronomy makes it repeatedly — *when you have eaten and are full, and have built beautiful houses and dwell in them… beware that you do not forget.* See the note on Deuteronomy 8:17–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What forgetting means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not forget the LORD, the One who brought you out of Egypt, out of the house of slavery**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *forget* is not a memory lapse. It is ceasing to reckon with something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the description He gives of Himself is not *the One who gave you these cities*. It is the One who brought them out of slavery — the earlier act, the one nobody could have earned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had done for any of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. They had walked, and complained, and buried a generation in a desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land is described as a gift from beginning to end, and the risk named is that possession will feel like achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after the Shema — *hear, O Israel: the LORD our God, the LORD is one. You shall love the LORD your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the instruction to teach it to children, talk about it sitting and walking and lying down and rising up, bind it on the hand, write it on the doorposts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this: and when you get the cities you did not build, do not forget."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only remembering. There is no work in the passage."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other thing Moses tells them to say.** When your son asks what the testimonies mean, *you shall say to your son: \"**We were slaves of Pharaoh in Egypt, and the LORD brought us out**.\"* The answer to every question about the law is a piece of history, told in the first person plural, by people who were not there."}]}, "Deuteronomy 7:12–15": {"title": "\"He will keep every sickness far from you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A long sentence of blessings, and the first word of it is *if*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you **listen** to these rulings, and **keep** them, and **live them out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, and the Hebrew of the first is *because you hear*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy is Moses' last set of sermons, delivered on the plains of Moab to a generation about to cross. The book is full of these conditional constructions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God keeps"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD your God will **keep His covenant and His steadfast love** with you, **just as He swore to your fathers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chesed** — covenant loyalty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the ground given is not their obedience but an older oath: *as He swore to your fathers*. See the note on Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **love you** and **bless you** and **multiply you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is multiplied"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **fruit of your body** and the **fruit of your ground**, your **grain**, your **new wine**, your **oil**, the **young of your herds** and the **lambs of your flocks**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Children, crops and livestock — the whole economy of an agricultural people, itemised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Grain, new wine and oil** is the standard triad of a land under blessing, and the pair Isaac gave Jacob. See the note on Genesis 27:28–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be **blessed above all peoples**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two absences"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*None among you, **man or woman, will be childless**, and **none of your livestock barren**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barrenness is the recurring grief of the patriarchal narratives — Sarah, Rebekah, Rachel, and later Hannah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sickness clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD will **keep every sickness far from you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the LORD will take away from you all sickness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The named diseases"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **not lay on you any of the terrible diseases of Egypt that you knew**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**That you knew** — they had watched them. Egypt's medical papyri record its endemic conditions, and Israel had lived there four hundred years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 15 had already promised it, with a name attached: *I will put none of the diseases on you which I have brought on the Egyptians. For **I am the LORD who heals you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they go instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But He will **lay them on all who hate you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of passage this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the blessing half of a pair. Deuteronomy 28 sets out both at length — fourteen verses of blessing and fifty-four of curse — and Paul quotes the curse side in Galatians: *cursed is everyone who does not continue in all things which are written in the book of the law.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The terms are total in both directions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD did not set His love on you nor choose you because you were more in number than any other people, **for you were the least of all peoples**; but **because the LORD loves you**, and because He would keep the oath which He swore to your fathers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses removes the obvious reason and gives no other than the loving itself."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter promises.** Children, harvest, herds and health, itemised and without hedging — and Moses grounds the whole list in an oath sworn to men who had been dead four hundred years."}]}, "Deuteronomy 8:11–16": {"title": "Do not forget in the good times", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The danger Moses names is not poverty. It is a full stomach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be careful **not to forget the LORD your God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb for forgetting runs through the chapter. It appears three times, and it is the chapter's whole subject."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list of conditions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you have **eaten your fill**, and **built fine houses** to live in, and your **herds and flocks have grown large**, and your **silver and gold have multiplied**, and **everything you own has increased**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five clauses, and every one of them is a blessing. Nothing on the list is a sin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the exact things promised a few chapters earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The moment of danger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **watch your heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not during the wilderness. After the houses are built."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let it **swell with pride**, so that **you forget the LORD your God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *lest your heart be lifted up*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pride first, forgetting second. Moses gives the order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is forgotten"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who **brought you out of Egypt, out of the house of slavery**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The identity given is the rescue. Forgetting Him means forgetting what state they were found in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wilderness, described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He led you through **that vast and terrible wilderness**, with its **venomous snakes and scorpions**, its **thirsty ground where there was no water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses does not romanticise it. He calls it terrible and lists what was in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God did in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **brought water for you out of the hard rock**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rock at Rephidim and the rock at Kadesh. See the notes on Exodus 17:4–7 and Numbers 20:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **fed you in the wilderness with manna your fathers had never known**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is a thing they could have arranged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Humbling you and testing you, **all for your good in the end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to do you good at your latter end*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses names the purpose of the hardship as their benefit, and puts it at the end of the sentence rather than the middle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then you say in your heart, \"**My power and the might of my hand have gained me this wealth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you shall **remember the LORD your God, for it is He who gives you power to get wealth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both sentences are in the same paragraph. The forgetting produces a specific claim, and the remedy is a specific correction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the earlier part of the chapter says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He humbled you, allowed you to hunger, and fed you with manna… **that He might make you know that man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus quotes that verse in the wilderness, to the first temptation, about bread."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other warning like it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great and good cities which you did not build, houses full of all good things which you did not fill… **then beware, lest you forget the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua uses the same list when he stands the nation at Shechem. See the note on Joshua 24:11–13."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is dangerous in the chapter.** Not the snakes and not the thirst. Moses gets through those in one verse and spends the rest of the paragraph on the houses."}]}, "Deuteronomy 8:17–18": {"title": "\"He gives you the power to gain wealth\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses names the sentence a prosperous man will say to himself, and answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence he warns about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not say in your heart, \"**My own power and the strength of my own hand have made me all this wealth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note where it is said — *in your heart*. Nobody announces this. It is the quiet conclusion a successful man reaches privately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my power and the might of my hand*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When the danger arrives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the verses before it. This is a warning for the good times, not the hard ones:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you have eaten and are full, and have built beautiful houses and dwell in them; and when your herds and flocks multiply, and your silver and gold are multiplied… **when your heart is lifted up, and you forget the LORD your God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter locates the risk precisely: after the wilderness, not during it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Remember the LORD your God — **for it is He who gives you the power to gain wealth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *He gives you wealth*. He gives the **power to get** it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **koach** — strength, ability, capacity. The energy, the wit, the health, the opportunity, the instinct for the thing: all of it named as issued."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the man's mistake is not in noticing that he worked hard. He did. The mistake is in where he thinks the working came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He does it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that He may keep the covenant He swore to your fathers**, as it is today.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purpose clause is startling. Not so that they could be comfortable — so that a promise made to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob would stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wealth is instrumental. It is covenant-keeping, in the form of a good harvest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says about the wilderness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He humbled you, allowed you to hunger, and fed you with manna… **that He might make you know that man shall not live by bread alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of a nation being taught that the food came from somewhere. Then the land, the houses, the herds — and the warning that the lesson is easier to forget at a full table than at an empty one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your garments did not wear out on you, nor did your foot swell, these forty years.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative ending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses spells out where the *my own hand* sentence goes: *if you by any means forget the LORD your God… you shall surely perish.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Proverbs adds.** *The blessing of the LORD makes one rich, **and He adds no sorrow with it**.* Two claims — where it comes from, and what does not come attached. See the note on Proverbs 10:22–27."}]}, "Deuteronomy 8:2–5": {"title": "\"Your clothes did not wear out\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses tells them what forty years in a desert was for, and the answer is not punishment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Remember the whole long way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Remember **the whole long way** the LORD your God **led you** through the wilderness these forty years.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Led you.* Not *allowed you to wander*. The route was chosen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three purposes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He led you there **to humble you** and **to test you**, **to see what was truly in your heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *test* is to prove, to assay — what you do to metal to find out what is in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The testing is not to inform God. It is to bring what is inside a person out where it can be seen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He humbled you. **He let you grow hungry.** Then He fed you with manna.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is stated plainly, and the hunger comes first. It is not an oversight being corrected; it is the first half of a two-part lesson."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bread you had never known and neither had your fathers.* The name **manna** comes from their own question — *man hu*, what is it?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was teaching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teaching you that **a person does not live by bread alone, but by every word that comes from the mouth of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the verse Jesus quoted in the wilderness, after forty days without food, when He was told to turn stones into bread."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had forty years and failed the lesson repeatedly. He had forty days and answered out of the book they were given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Deuteronomy three times in that encounter, and all three come from this stretch of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail nobody would invent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In all those forty years **your clothes did not wear out on you, and your feet did not swell**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of walking in a desert. Sandals, robes, and the feet inside them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses says it as a matter of record to people who had lived it. Nehemiah repeats it centuries later in a prayer: *forty years You sustained them in the wilderness; they lacked nothing; their clothes did not wear out and their feet did not swell.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interpretation given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Know it in your heart: **as a father disciplines the son he loves**, so the LORD your God was disciplining you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yasar**, the same root as *musar* in Proverbs 3 — instruction, the whole business of bringing a child up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 12 makes the identical argument in Greek with **paideia**, and quotes Proverbs to do it. See the notes on Proverbs 3:9–12 and Hebrews 12:5–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years in a desert, described by Moses as a father raising a son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD your God is bringing you into a good land* — and then the warning about what a full stomach does to the memory: *when you have eaten and are full… beware that you do not forget.* See the note on Deuteronomy 8:17–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they were not told at the time.** Any of this. The explanation comes forty years later, from a man about to die, to their children. Nobody eating manna in year three knew what it was for."}]}, "Deuteronomy 8:6–9": {"title": "\"A land where you will lack nothing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses describes the country ahead in the concrete terms of somebody who has been living on manna for forty years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Streams and springs, water welling up from valleys and hills; wheat and barley, vines and fig trees and pomegranates; olive oil and honey; bread without going without; stones of iron, and hills you can dig copper from.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven crops — the same seven the rabbis later called the species of the land — plus water, bread, and metal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a poetic abundance. It is an inventory: food, drink, and the raw material for tools and weapons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two lines that frame it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before: *keep the commands of the LORD your God — walk in His ways and hold Him in awe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After, a few verses on: *when you have eaten and are satisfied, praise the LORD your God for the good land He has given you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The abundance sits between an instruction and a thanksgiving. Moses is not worried about their poverty. He is worried about what full barns do to memory — the very next warning is *be careful that you do not forget.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The danger he names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You may say to yourself, \"My power and the strength of my hands have produced this wealth for me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the risk this chapter exists to address, and it comes with the remedy: *remember the LORD your God, for it is He who gives you the ability to produce wealth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the ability is unreal. That its source is elsewhere."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the manna comes first.** The chapter opens with forty years of being humbled, hungry, and fed with something they had never seen — *to teach you that man does not live on bread alone.* The lesson in the desert is what makes the land safe to enter. Jesus quotes that line in His own wilderness. See the note on Matthew 4:1–4."}]}, "Deuteronomy 9:9–14": {"title": "Forty days, and a calf at the bottom", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses retells the worst thing that happened to him, forty years later, to a generation that was not there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forty days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I stayed on the mountain **forty days and forty nights, eating no bread and drinking no water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a fast he chose. He was in the cloud on Sinai and did not eat or drink for the whole of it — which is beyond what a body does unaided."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it twice in this chapter. Forty days for the tablets, and then another forty on his face after the calf."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The two stone tablets, **written with the finger of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in the passage. The only thing in Scripture described as written by God's own finger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, go down from here quickly**, for your people, the ones you brought out of Egypt, have corrupted themselves.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is handed the tablets and told in the same breath to go down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the pronouns: *your people, the ones **you** brought out of Egypt.* God says it that way to Moses; Moses says it back the other way in his prayer — *Your people, whom You brought out.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"They have turned so quickly\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they have turned aside quickly out of the way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six weeks. They had heard the voice out of the fire and agreed to everything — *all that the LORD has spoken we will do* — and they lasted six weeks. See the note on Exodus 19:7–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let Me alone**, so that I may destroy them… **and I will make you into a nation mightier and greater than they are**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same offer made to Moses that was made to nobody else: start over with you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He turns it down and spends the next forty days face down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did with the tablets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses after this passage: *I took the two tablets and threw them out of my two hands and broke them before your eyes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant was broken before it reached the bottom of the hill. Moses' act simply made visible what had already happened up the mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is telling them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's purpose is stated at the top: *do not think in your heart… \"Because of my righteousness the LORD has brought me in to possess this land.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is telling them the calf story to stop them believing they earned the country they are about to walk into."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Understand that the LORD your God is not giving you this good land to possess because of your righteousness, for **you are a stiff-necked people**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forty days that answer these"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus in the wilderness, forty days without food, and the first thing said to Him is *if You are the Son of God, command that these stones become bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers out of this book — *man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God* — quoting Deuteronomy 8, one chapter earlier. See the note on Deuteronomy 8:2–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How Moses ends the chapter.** *So I fell down before the LORD forty days and forty nights… because the LORD had said He would destroy you. Therefore I prayed.* Eighty days on that mountain without food, and he spends half of them arguing for people who had just replaced him with a calf."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 2:24–26": {"title": "\"The task of gathering and piling up\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first place in Ecclesiastes where the Preacher stops complaining and says something plain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he has been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters of trying everything. Wisdom, pleasure, wine, houses, vineyards, gardens, pools, servants, herds, silver and gold, singers, every delight — *whatever my eyes desired I did not keep from them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *I hated life… I hated all my labour, because I must leave it to the man who comes after me, and who knows whether he will be wise or a fool?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is nothing better for a person than to **eat and drink and find joy in his work**. And this, I saw, is **from the hand of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread, a drink, and satisfaction in the day's work. That is the whole list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second sentence is what makes it more than resignation — he names where it comes from. Ordinary contentment is described as a gift handed over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Apart from Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **apart from Him, who can eat, who can find any real enjoyment?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not who can afford to eat. Who can eat and get anything out of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just proved by experiment that a man can own everything in that list and enjoy none of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the one who **pleases Him**, God gives **wisdom and knowledge and joy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of the three is money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But to the one who misses the mark, He gives **the task of gathering and piling up, only to hand it over** to the one who pleases God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to gather and to heap up*. It is described as an assignment — a **task** given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the collecting. Somebody else does the keeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs says the same thing twice: *the wealth of the sinner is stored up for the righteous*, and *whoever swells his wealth by interest gathers it for one who is kind to the poor.* See the note on Proverbs 28:7–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"This too is vapour\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This too is vapour, a chasing after the wind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **hebel** — breath, vapour, what you can see on a cold morning and cannot hold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He applies it to the gathering, not to the gift. The eating and drinking and joy are *from the hand of God*; the heaping up is wind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the book ends.** *Fear God and keep His commandments, for this is the whole duty of man.* Twelve chapters of a man with unlimited means testing every avenue, and the conclusion is two clauses long."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 3:11": {"title": "\"Eternity in the human heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it explains why nothing under the sun has ever been enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Directly after the famous list — *a time to be born and a time to die, a time to plant and a time to pluck up, a time to weep and a time to laugh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen pairs, and then this verse is the Preacher's comment on all of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Beautiful in its time\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has made **everything beautiful in its time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **yapheh** is beautiful, fitting, appropriate. Each thing suits the moment it belongs to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The weeping and the laughing are both on the list, and both are called beautiful in their season."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause the verse is known for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has also **set eternity in the human heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **olam** — the age, the far distance, perpetuity. The same word used of God's everlasting covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something with no horizon has been placed inside a creature who lives seventy years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the Preacher's diagnosis of the human problem, and it is not a complaint about sin. It is a description of design."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **no one can fully grasp what God has done from beginning to end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The capacity to want the whole thing, and no capacity to see it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the tension the entire book runs on. Twelve chapters of a man with unlimited means testing every avenue — wisdom, pleasure, building, wealth, work — and finding each one *vapour*, because it is finite and he is not built for finite."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the experiments failed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because the things were bad. He says elsewhere that eating your bread with joy and enjoying your work are *from the hand of God.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 2:24–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They failed because he was measuring them against something with no edge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other half of the same observation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God made humankind **upright**, but we have gone looking for **endless clever schemes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes 7:29, and it is the Preacher's other conclusion about people: made straight, and inventive about not staying that way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the Preacher lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever God does, it shall be forever. Nothing can be added to it, and nothing taken from it. God does it, that men should fear before Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the very next verse. The thing that is permanent is what God does, and the eternity in the heart is what recognises it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Augustine made of this verse.** *You have made us for Yourself, and our heart is restless until it rests in You.* Sixteen centuries of readers have taken the restlessness in this line as the point of it rather than the problem with it."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 4:9–12": {"title": "A cord of three strands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four short arguments for company, from the book that spends most of its time on futility."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens on oppression — *I looked again at all the acts of oppression under the sun, and I saw the tears of the oppressed, and they had no comforter*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then a man Solomon describes a few verses earlier: *there is one alone, without companion; he has neither son nor brother. Yet there is no end to all his labours… and he never asks, \"For whom do I toil?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This paragraph is the answer to that man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Two are better than one, because they have **a good reward for their labor**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If one falls down, **his friend can lift him up**. But **pity the one who falls and has no one to help him rise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *woe to him who is alone when he falls*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Falling is assumed. The variable is who is nearby."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if two lie down together, **they keep each other warm**, but **how can one be warm alone?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nights in the Judean hills get cold, and a traveller's cloak was also his blanket. Two men sharing them survive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though one may be overpowered, **two can stand their ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *if one prevails against him, two shall withstand him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **a cord of three strands is not quickly broken**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the threefold cord*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument has been about pairs for three verses, and the last line adds one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rope is stronger than the sum of its strands because of how it is laid — three twisted together bear more than three side by side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line's afterlife"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is read at weddings constantly, and the chapter is not about marriage. Ecclesiastes is talking about work, cold nights, muggings and the man with no brother."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The application is wider than the usual use of it, not narrower."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the book has come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Vanity of vanities, all is vanity.* Thirty-eight uses of **hevel** — vapour, breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the conclusions Solomon keeps reaching: nothing new under the sun, the wise man dies like the fool, and *I hated life*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that, this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What else the book grants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go. **Eat your bread with joy**, and drink your wine with a glad heart, for **God has already approved what you do**.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 9:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The concessions in Ecclesiastes are always concrete — bread, wine, a companion, work you can see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us hear the conclusion of the whole matter: **Fear God and keep His commandments**, for this is man's all.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with the principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sent them out **two by two**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *where two or three are gathered together in My name, I am there in the midst of them.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one the paragraph is written against.** A man with no son and no brother, working without end and never asking who for. Four arguments follow, and the last one adds a third strand to a book that had been counting to one."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 8:2–4": {"title": "\"His word carries power\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Practical advice about standing in front of somebody who can do whatever he likes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep the king's command, I say, **for you swore it before God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *because of the oath of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Loyalty to the throne is grounded in an oath sworn in God's name, which makes it a matter between the subject and God rather than only between the subject and the king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The court manners"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **rush to leave his presence**, and do not **take up a bad cause**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leaving abruptly was an insult; a Persian or Egyptian court had elaborate rules about withdrawal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A bad cause** — the Hebrew is *an evil word*, or *an evil matter*. Do not attach yourself to a losing case in front of a man who cannot be appealed against."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the king does as he pleases**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated without approval or complaint. Ecclesiastes is describing how it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His **word carries power**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the word of a king is authority* — **shilton**, dominion, mastery, the right to act."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king's speech is not a request or an opinion. It is the thing itself: the word and the event are the same object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question nobody asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **no one can say to him, \"What are you doing?\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same clause is used of God in Job — *who can say to Him, \"What are You doing?\"* — and in Daniel, of the Most High: *no one can restrain His hand or say to Him, \"What have You done?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes applies to a human throne the phrase reserved elsewhere for the throne above it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words of the Preacher, the son of David, king in Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king, writing about what it is like to stand in front of a king. He knew both sides of the audience chamber."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the book goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after: *he who keeps his command will experience nothing harmful, and a wise man's heart discerns both time and judgment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, four verses on: *no one has power over the spirit to retain the spirit, and no one has power in the day of death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word for power. The king's word rules everything except that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament says about the readers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him who loved us and washed us from our sins in His own blood, **and has made us kings and priests** to His God and Father.* See the note on Revelation 1:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other king in the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed are you, O land, when your king is the son of nobles, and your princes feast at the proper time — for strength and not for drunkenness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes has opinions about what makes a throne worth serving. It just does not pretend that a bad one has less authority."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse is about.** Not obedience for its own sake. A word spoken from a throne is an act — and Ecclesiastes borrows the language it uses of God to say so."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 9:11–12": {"title": "\"Time and chance meet them all\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five things that ought to be reliable, and the Preacher says none of them is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The race is **not always won by the swift**, nor the battle by the strong. Bread does not always go to the wise, nor riches to the clever, nor favour to the skilled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Speed, strength, wisdom, cleverness, skill. Five advantages, and in each case the expected outcome is denied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not saying these are worthless. He is saying they do not settle it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Time and chance meet them all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *chance* is **pega** — an occurrence, a happening, something that befalls."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Its close relative **qarah** is the word Abraham's servant used at the well: *give me a happening today* — asking God for the right occurrence. See the note on Genesis 24:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same idea from two directions. The Preacher says outcomes are not controlled by the qualifications; the servant asks the One who is not limited by them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes is written *under the sun* — the phrase appears nearly thirty times. It is a deliberate experiment in reasoning about life from the visible evidence alone, and this is one of its findings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From under the sun, the fastest man losing the race looks like chance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Like fish caught in a cruel net, like birds caught in a snare, the children of earth are trapped in an evil time **when it falls on them without warning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one knows his hour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The images are of creatures going about their business with no idea a net exists. It is not cruelty in the passage — it is unforeseeability."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it rules out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any scheme for guaranteeing an outcome by being good enough at something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Preacher has already tried that in chapters 1 and 2 — wisdom, pleasure, building, wealth, every avenue a man with unlimited means could take — and this verse is part of the report."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's own advice, a few verses earlier: *go, eat your bread with joy, and drink your wine with a merry heart… live joyfully with the wife whom you love… whatever your hand finds to do, do it with your might.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not paralysis. Work hard, eat well, love your wife — and hold the outcome loosely, because you were never the one holding it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the book ends.** *Fear God and keep His commandments, for this is the whole duty of man.* Twelve chapters reasoning from under the sun, and the conclusion looks up."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 9:7–9": {"title": "\"God has already approved\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The bleakest book in the Bible stops to tell somebody to enjoy their dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **go**. **Eat your bread with joy**, and **drink your wine with a glad heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three imperatives, and none of them is religious."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **God has already approved what you do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *God has already accepted your works*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The approval is in the past tense and stated before anything is asked. The eating follows it rather than earning it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clothes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let your clothes be **white, always**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "White linen in that climate was what you wore to a feast. Ordinary work clothes were undyed and dark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is to dress for a celebration as the normal condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **never let the oil run dry on your head**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Oil on the head was for guests and for festivity. A host anointed a visitor's head — which is the courtesy Simon the Pharisee omitted. See the note on Luke 7:44–50."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are things you do on a good day, and both are commanded as a standing state."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The marriage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Enjoy life with **the one you love**, through **all the fleeting days God has given you** under the sun, **all your fleeting days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hevel** — vapour, breath, mist. It is the book's key word, used thirty-eight times, and usually translated *vanity*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon repeats it twice in one verse here, and both times attached to the instruction to enjoy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sentence is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's argument is that life is short, unpredictable, and cannot be mastered. He does not resolve that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He draws a different conclusion from it: since you cannot secure the days, receive them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the living know that they will die… **also their love, their hatred, and their envy have now perished**; nevermore will they have a share in anything done under the sun.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the *so go* follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever your hand finds to do, **do it with your might**; for there is no work or device or knowledge or wisdom in the grave where you are going.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book calls it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **your portion** in life, and in the labour you pour out under the sun.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **cheleq** — a share, an allotted piece. The surveying word, the one the Levites were given about God Himself. See the note on Numbers 18:8–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How often the book says this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven times, in almost the same words: *there is nothing better for a man than that he should eat and drink, and that his soul should enjoy good in his labour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And each time it adds: *this also, I saw, was from the hand of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the book ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us hear the conclusion of the whole matter: **Fear God and keep His commandments**, for this is man's all.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What licenses the bread and the wine.** Not that the days are secure — the book says repeatedly that they are vapour. One clause: *God has already approved*, in the past tense, before the eating starts."}]}, "Ephesians 1:15–19": {"title": "What Paul prays for people who already have everything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just spent twelve verses listing what these believers possess. Then he prays — and he does not ask for a single additional thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of wisdom and of unveiling, in coming to know Him — that the eyes of your understanding would be flooded with light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word behind *unveiling* is **apokalypsis**, the uncovering of something already there. Not new information. The removal of whatever is in the way of seeing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the eyes of your understanding* is literally *the eyes of your heart* — the seeing organ Paul is worried about is not the intellect alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things he wants them to see"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**what the hope is that He called you into**", "**how glorious and rich a share He has among His holy people**", "**how far past measuring His power is toward us who believe**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second one is worth reading twice. The Greek most naturally means **His** inheritance in the saints — God's own share, and the people are it. Paul has already said in verse 11 that we have obtained an inheritance; now he prays they would grasp that they *are* one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer is for perception, not supply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole shape of it. Everything in chapter 1 has already been given — chosen, adopted, redeemed, forgiven, sealed. Paul's response is to pray that they would know it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same thing in chapter 3, where the prayer is that they would have power to grasp the dimensions of a love that surpasses knowledge."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The measure of the power.** *Power in step with the working of His mighty strength* — and then the next verse says what strength that was: the force that raised Christ and seated Him above every authority. Paul's answer to \"how much power is available\" is to point at the resurrection. See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}]}, "Ephesians 1:20–23": {"title": "How far above, and what it has to do with you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is praying that they would know the power at work in them, and to describe its size he points at one event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure he uses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That was the strength He put to work in Christ when He raised Him from the dead and seated Him at His own right hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The resurrection is offered as the unit of measurement. Not a general statement that God is powerful — the specific force applied on one morning, named as the same force now at work toward believers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The height"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*High above every ruler and every authority and power and lordship, and above every name that can be named, not only in this present age but in the age still to come.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul empties the vocabulary. Four categories of authority, then *every name that can be named*, then both ages — this one and the next."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is deliberately leaving nothing outside the sentence. Whatever it is, whatever it is called, whenever it exists, it is under."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything so far is about Christ's exaltation. Then:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*And He put everything under His feet, and gave Him, over all things, **as head to the church, which is His body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The elevation is not held privately. He is given as head *to the church* — and a head and a body are not in two locations. Paul will say it outright two chapters on: seated together with Him in the heavenly places."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Where the head is seated is where the body is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Under His feet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He put **everything under His feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is quoted from Psalm 8 — *You have put all things under his feet* — which was written about man, not about the Messiah. Adam's commission, given back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the posture Psalm 110 describes from the other side: *sit at My right hand until I make Your enemies **a footstool** under Your feet.* See the note on Mark 12:35–37."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A footstool is what a seated man rests on. The picture is not a struggle in progress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the feet are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the head is seated and the enemies are under His feet, then the part of the body that touches them is the part furthest from the head — and it is still His body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's closing line in the letter says the same thing from the ground: *the God of peace will crush Satan **under your feet** shortly* is Romans, and Ephesians 6 has the church standing, not advancing. See the note on Ephesians 6:13–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"The fullness of the One who fills everything.\"** Paul calls the church that, which is an extraordinary thing to call a scattered collection of first-century congregations with the problems his letters keep addressing. He does it anyway."}]}, "Ephesians 1:3–6": {"title": "Everything already given, and where it is kept", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening of the longest sentence in the New Testament, and it begins with a claim in the past tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Has blessed us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *will bless*. The verb is aorist — done, complete, a settled fact by the time Paul writes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the extent is total: *with every blessing the Spirit has to give*. Nothing is being held in reserve pending performance. Whatever the Christian life turns out to involve, Paul's starting position is that the giving has already happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Up where heaven is* — in the heavenly places. Twice more in this letter Paul uses the phrase, and it is not a description of somewhere distant. It is where Christ is seated, and where he later says believers are seated with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the blessings are located in the same place as the person they are held in. That is the force of the phrase Paul repeats through this whole passage: **in Him**, in Christ, in the One He loves. Eleven times in twelve verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Before the world was ever laid down\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The choosing is dated earlier than creation, and the purpose is stated: *so that we would stand before Him holy, without a single flaw in us, in love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things about that. It is the goal of the choosing, not its condition. And the last two words attach either to the standing or to the choosing itself — Greek allows both, and either way love is in the sentence rather than obligation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The reason given.** *This was simply what pleased Him, and what He wanted.* Paul offers no cause outside God's own good pleasure, and then says the point of the whole thing is *the praise of the shining beauty of His grace*. When he comes to explain why any of it happened, he does not point at anything in us."}]}, "Ephesians 1:7–10": {"title": "Bought out, and the word for it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is one long sentence into this letter and shows no sign of stopping. In Greek, verses 3 to 14 are a single unbroken sentence — the longest in the New Testament — and this is the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word behind \"bought out of our captivity\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Apolutrosis.** The root is *lutron*, a ransom — the price paid to free a prisoner of war or to buy a slave out of ownership. The prefix makes it a release accomplished by that payment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a commercial word being used about people, and it carries a specific picture: someone was held, a price was named, the price was paid, and the person walked out. Paul says where the price came from in the same breath — *by His blood*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Poured over us without measure\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase for grace here is extravagant even by Paul's standards: the *sheer wealth* of His grace, and a verb meaning to make something overflow, to have more than enough of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not describing a sufficiency. He is describing a surplus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the surplus is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It brought with it every kind of wisdom and clear-eyed good sense.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is an unexpected turn. The overflow of grace does not arrive as feeling — it arrives as understanding, and specifically as being let in on something: *He let us in on the secret of what He wants.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the sentence is going.** The purpose Paul names is enormous: that everything, in heaven and on earth, would be gathered up under one head in Christ. The Greek verb is from arithmetic — adding a column of figures and writing the total at the top. All of it, summed up, in Him. That is the end the ransom was paid toward, and Paul reaches it without once pausing for a full stop."}]}, "Ephesians 2:17–19": {"title": "\"No longer guests in someone else's country\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul announces peace twice, to two groups who had nothing in common."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came and announced the good news of peace to **you who were far away**, and peace **to those who were near**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Far away is the Gentiles. Near is Israel — who had the covenants, the law, the promises and the temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same message is preached to both, and the same peace is announced. Neither group gets a different arrangement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is quoting Isaiah 57: *peace, peace to him who is far off and to him who is near.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The access"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For through Him **we both have our way in to the Father, by one Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *way in* is **prosagoge** — access, introduction, the word for being presented at court by somebody with standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One access, one Spirit, one Father, and *we both*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three denials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So now you are **no longer strangers**, **no longer guests in someone else's country**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Xenoi** — foreigners, outsiders."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Paroikoi** — resident aliens. People living somewhere legally and permanently who will never be citizens of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That was the exact status of a God-fearing Gentile in relation to Israel: welcome as far as the outer court, and no further."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two affirmations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You hold **the same citizenship as all God's holy people**, and **you belong to the household of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Citizenship, and family. The first is a legal standing; the second is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You can be naturalised into a country. Nobody is naturalised into a household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the wall was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter: *He… has broken down the middle wall of separation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the temple there was a literal one — a barrier in the outer court with notices in Greek and Latin warning that any foreigner passing it was responsible for his own death. Two of those inscriptions have been dug up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, who had been accused of taking a Gentile past it and was in prison partly because of the riot that followed, writes that it has been demolished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the paragraph goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verses:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets, **Jesus Christ Himself being the chief cornerstone**, in whom the whole building, being fitted together, grows into a **holy temple** in the Lord, in whom you also are **being built together for a dwelling place of God** in the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Citizens, then family, then masonry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the stones in Solomon's temple were cut and finished in the quarry so that the site was silent. See the notes on 1 Kings 6:6–8 and 1 Peter 2:4–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they had been eleven verses earlier.** *Without Christ, aliens from the commonwealth of Israel, strangers from the covenants of promise, **having no hope and without God in the world**.* Five negatives in one sentence, and then *but now in Christ Jesus you who once were far off have been brought near by the blood of Christ.*"}]}, "Ephesians 2:4–7": {"title": "\"But God\" — and three verbs with \"together\" in them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two of the most consequential words in the New Testament, and then three verbs that all say the same thing about location."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"But God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence before this is a description of the dead: following the course of this world, by nature children of wrath. Then the turn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say *but they tried harder*, or *but God saw potential*. He says *But God — so rich in mercy, and loving us with a love so great*, and the reason given for everything that follows is entirely on His side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one in Greek is a compound built with *syn* — together with:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**made us alive together** with Christ", "**raised us up together** with Him", "**seated us together** with Him"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not alongside as spectators. The same three things that happened to Christ — resurrection, ascension, enthronement — described as having happened to us with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And all three are aorist: completed. Not a process under way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Even while we were dead\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The timing is stated twice for emphasis. The making-alive happened while the condition was death, not after any improvement. Dead men do not cooperate with their own resurrection, which is Paul's point — hence the parenthesis he cannot help inserting: *by grace you have been saved.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that in the ages still to come He could show the overflowing wealth of His grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purpose is exhibition. We are the evidence God intends to display, and the thing being displayed is the size of His kindness — with the ages to come as the audience."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where \"up where heaven is\" leads.** Paul has already put Christ there in chapter 1, seated far above every ruler and authority. Now the same location is given to the church, because a head and a body are not in two places. See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}]}, "Ephesians 2:8–10": {"title": "\"We are what He has made\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and the third one is the reason the first two are not the end of the subject."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is **by grace you have been saved, through faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *have been saved* is a perfect passive participle — **sesosmenoi**. Done, and standing done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**By grace** is the means; **through faith** is the channel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is the gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **this did not come from you** — it is **the gift of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek *touto* is neuter, and neither *grace* nor *faith* is neuter. Grammatically it points at the whole arrangement rather than at one word in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have argued about this for centuries, and the neuter is what makes it arguable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **not the result of anything you have done**, so **no one has a thing to boast about**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not of works, lest anyone should boast*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul removes the boast by name. He does the same in Romans: *where is boasting then? It is excluded.* See the note on Romans 3:21–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens: *and you He made alive, who were **dead in trespasses and sins**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dead. That is the diagnosis the three verses answer, and it is why the passage has no human verb in it until the last line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in between: *but God, who is rich in mercy, because of His great love with which He loved us… **made us alive together with Christ**.* See the note on Ephesians 2:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **we are what He has made**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poiema** — a thing made, a work of craft. It is the root of *poem*, and it occurs twice in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just spent two verses saying salvation is not a work. Then he calls the reader a work — God's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What we were made for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Created in Christ Jesus **for good works**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very thing excluded in verse 9 reappears in verse 10 on the other side of the equation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not works that produce the making. Works the making produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When they were arranged"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which God **prepared long beforehand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proetoimasen** — prepared in advance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The works were ready before the worker."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is left to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **we would walk in them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peripatesomen** — walk about, go about our lives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not achieve them, build them, or generate them. Walk in ones already laid out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore remember that **you, once Gentiles in the flesh**… were **without Christ, aliens from the commonwealth of Israel, and strangers from the covenants of promise, having no hope and without God in the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But now in Christ Jesus you who once were far off have been **brought near by the blood of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three tenses of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dead. Made alive and seated. Walking in prepared works."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul keeps the order and never lets the third one become the cause of the first two."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word that resolves it.** *Poiema.* Not the result of anything you have done — and then, in the next line, a thing made, for works that were laid out in advance to walk in."}]}, "Ephesians 3:20–21": {"title": "Far beyond anything we can picture", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The doxology that closes the first half of the letter, and Paul piles the words up because one will not do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer just before it: that they would be *strengthened with might through His Spirit in the inner man*, that Christ would dwell in their hearts by faith, and that they would *know the love of Christ which passes knowledge*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this, as the amen on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two measures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him who can do **far beyond anything we ask**, far beyond **anything we can even picture**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huperekperissou** — a triple compound Paul appears to have built himself: *huper* (beyond) + *ek* (out of) + *perissos* (abundant)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beyond, out of, abundantly. He stacks three intensifiers on one word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things exceeded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Asking** and **thinking**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is the larger. A person can ask only for what they have thought of, so imagination is the wider boundary — and Paul says the doing is past that too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And can do it **lavishly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *exceedingly abundantly above*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure of the power"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*According to **the power already at work inside us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **energeo** — the root of *energy*. Present participle: currently operating."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not measure the promise by God's power in the abstract. He measures it by what is already running in the reader."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has already named that power in chapter 1: *the exceeding greatness of His power toward us who believe… **which He worked in Christ when He raised Him from the dead**.* See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The resurrection is the unit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the glory goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him be the glory **in the church, in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two locations, and one of them is a collection of first-century congregations with the problems Paul's letters keep addressing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names them anyway, and puts them first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The duration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through **every generation, on and on, forever and ever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *unto all the generations of the age of the ages* — the most extended formula for eternity in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not economising anywhere in the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4: *I, therefore, the prisoner of the Lord, beseech you to **walk worthy of the calling** with which you were called, with all lowliness and gentleness, with longsuffering, bearing with one another in love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doxology ends and the practical half of the letter begins in the next breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man in Roman custody. He calls himself *the prisoner of Christ Jesus for you Gentiles* at the start of the same chapter, and tells them *do not lose heart at my tribulations for you, which is your glory.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The measure Paul gives.** Not what God could do. What is already at work inside the reader — and he says the doing runs past what they would think to ask for."}]}, "Ephesians 4:1–3": {"title": "\"Guard the oneness the Spirit gives you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hinge of the letter. Three chapters of what God has done, and then the first instruction — and it is about how to treat each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not being asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Working hard to **guard** the oneness the Spirit gives you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to create it. Not to achieve it. The unity already exists — it is the Spirit's, and it is given. The job is to keep it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That distinction changes the work entirely. Nobody is being asked to manufacture agreement. They are being asked not to break something that is already there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four qualities"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never above anyone, gentle in your handling of people, patient, putting up with one another because you love one another.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**never above anyone** — the Greek word for lowliness of mind. In classical Greek it was an insult, a term for a grovelling nature. The New Testament picks it up and makes it a virtue.", "**gentle** — the word for strength under control, used of a broken horse", "**patient** — long-tempered, the same word used of God in 2 Peter 3:9", "**putting up with one another** — bearing with, enduring"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is a quality you only need when someone is being difficult. This is not a description of people who naturally get along."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The tie of peace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a bond, a ligament — the thing that holds a joint together. Paul uses the same image later in the chapter for the body being knit together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peace is not the absence of friction here. It is the connective tissue."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"I, the Lord's prisoner.\"** Paul opens the practical half of his letter by naming where he is writing from. The man asking them to be patient with each other is in chains, and he mentions it once, without complaint, as his credential for asking."}]}, "Ephesians 4:28–29": {"title": "\"Leaves grace behind in them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells a thief what to do instead, and the answer is not simply *stop*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three stages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the one who has been stealing **steal no more**. Let him **work instead**, doing something good with his own hands, **so that he has something to share with anyone in need**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stop, work, give. The instruction does not end at the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who stole is not sent to neutrality. He is sent all the way over to the other side — from taking, past earning, to supplying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mouth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let **one rotten word** come out of your mouth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sapros** — rotten, putrid. It is the word used of bad fish and spoiled fruit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *unkind* or *coarse*. Decayed — something that has gone off and should not be handed to anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What to say instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Say only what **builds someone up where they need building**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *for edification of the need* — for the building up that this particular person, at this moment, actually requires."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It assumes you have noticed what the need is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that what they hear **leaves grace behind in them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *that it may give grace to the hearers* — **charis**, the ordinary New Testament word for grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the test Paul applies to speech. Not whether it was true, or deserved, or accurate about the person. Whether grace was left behind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace by definition is not earned. Words that only credit people with what they have coming are not doing this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse straight after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **do not grieve the Holy Spirit of God**, by whom you were sealed for the day of redemption.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul attaches that immediately to the paragraph about speech. The one thing named in Ephesians as grieving the Spirit is what comes out of a mouth toward another person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage is a list of exchanges, each one with a positive on the other side:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["put off lying → **speak the truth** to your neighbour", "be angry → **do not let the sun go down** on it", "stop stealing → **work**, and then **share**", "no rotten word → words that **build** and **give grace**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them stops at the prohibition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let all bitterness, wrath, anger, clamour and evil speaking be put away from you, with all malice. **And be kind to one another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, just as God in Christ forgave you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard is not fairness. It is *just as* — the way you were treated."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sealing.** *By whom you were **sealed** for the day of redemption.* Paul warns about grieving the Spirit in the same breath as saying He is the seal. The grieving is real, and it is not a threat to the sealing — a seal is not something you can be sad out of."}]}, "Ephesians 4:30–32": {"title": "\"Just as God, in Christ, forgave you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses: a warning, a clear-out, and a standard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **do not bring sorrow to the Holy Spirit of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lupeite** — to grieve, to cause pain. It is the ordinary word for making somebody sad."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *do not anger*. Grief is what you cause someone who is attached to you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says the same of the wilderness generation: *they rebelled and **grieved His Holy Spirit***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Spirit is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit who **sealed you and marked you as God's own** for the day when **He sets you free for good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **esphragisthete** — sealed. A seal in that world marked ownership and guaranteed that something would arrive intact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already used it twice in the letter: *you were sealed with the Holy Spirit of promise, who is the guarantee of our inheritance.* See the note on Ephesians 1:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**For the day** — the sealing points forward. The warning sits inside a statement of security."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Put it all away from you: **bitterness, rage, anger, shouting, and words that tear a person's name down** — **every last trace of spite** along with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six items, and the Greek runs from the inside out:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Pikria** — bitterness, the settled kind", "**Thumos** — rage, a flare", "**Orge** — anger, the slower-burning sort", "**Krauge** — clamour, shouting", "**Blasphemia** — slander, speech that damages a name", "**Kakia** — malice, ill-will, and Paul adds *with all*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two are internal, the middle two come out of the mouth, and the last is the root the rest grow from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three positives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be **kind** to one another instead, **soft-hearted**, **forgiving** one another.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Chrestoi** — kind, useful, good to be around", "**Eusplanchnoi** — from **splanchna**, the guts. Tender-hearted, moved in the inward parts. The same root as the compassion Jesus felt looking at the crowds. See the note on Matthew 9:35–38.", "**Charizomenoi** — from **charis**, grace. To forgive as a gift, to cancel freely"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as **God, in Christ, forgave you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *so that* God will forgive. **Just as** He already did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is aorist — a completed act. The forgiving named as the standard is in the past tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it the same way in Colossians: *forgiving one another… **even as Christ forgave you**, so you also must do.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb Paul chooses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Charizomai**, not the courtroom word. It is what you do with a debt you have decided to hand over rather than collect — the verb Luke uses of the two debtors, and Paul uses of God cancelling the note nailed to the cross. See the notes on Luke 7:40–43 and Colossians 2:11–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **be imitators of God, as dear children**. And **walk in love**, as Christ also has loved us and given Himself for us.* See the note on Ephesians 5:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter break hides the connection. The forgiving is the last item before the imitating, and the imitating is the *therefore*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the standard is set.** Not at a level of effort. Paul names an act that has already happened to the reader and says the forgiving is to match it."}]}, "Ephesians 5:1–2": {"title": "The way dearly loved children copy their father", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks for something impossible and then names the two words that make it ordinary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **become imitators of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mimetai** — the root of *mimic*. It occurs here about God and nowhere else in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks people to imitate God. It is the largest instruction in his letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualifier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The way **dearly loved children copy their own father**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tekna agapeta** — beloved children."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what makes it possible. Imitation of a father by a small child is not an achievement; it is what happens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody teaches a toddler to copy. It follows from being in the house and being loved there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oun** — therefore."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It looks back at the end of chapter 4: *and be kind to one another, tenderhearted, **forgiving one another, just as God in Christ forgave you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The imitating is already under way in the sentence before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The walk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **walk in love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peripateite** — the ordinary word for going about, the one Paul uses throughout Ephesians for how a life is conducted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The same love **with which Christ loved us and gave Himself up on our behalf**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not love in general. A specific act, named, with a date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paredoken** — handed over, delivered up. It is the word used of Judas handing Him over, and the word Paul uses in Romans: *He who did not spare His own Son, but **delivered Him up** for us all.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*An **offering and a sacrifice** to God, **a fragrance sweet to Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words from Leviticus — **prosphora** and **thusia** — and then the phrase that runs through the whole sacrificial system."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A sweet-smelling aroma** is the Greek Old Testament's standard rendering of the Hebrew **reyach nichoach**, the restful smell that goes up from an accepted offering. It appears first over Noah's altar. See the note on Genesis 8:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offering was *on our behalf*, and the fragrance was *to God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction of the two clauses is different, and both are in the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same word of a gift of money sent to him in prison — *a fragrance of sweetness, a sacrifice that is welcomed, a delight to God*. See the note on Philippians 4:17–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But fornication and all uncleanness or covetousness, **let it not even be named among you**, as is fitting for saints.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul goes straight from the sweet aroma to a list of what should not be around. The two verses are the standard the list is measured by."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter has already said about the love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That you, being rooted and grounded in love, may be able to comprehend… **the width and length and depth and height** — to know the love of Christ which passes knowledge.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the doxology. See the note on Ephesians 3:20–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the instruction is not crushing.** *The way dearly loved children copy their own father.* Paul puts the impossible command and the reason it is natural in the same sentence."}]}, "Ephesians 5:15–17": {"title": "Buying back the time", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three short clauses, and the middle one is a market transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **watch carefully how you walk**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **blepete akribos** — look precisely, examine closely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**How you walk** — Paul's standing word for how a life is conducted. He uses it six times in this letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not as fools walk **but as the wise do**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purchase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Buying back **the time you are given**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exagorazomenoi ton kairon** — redeeming the time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Exagorazo** is a market word: to buy out, to purchase entirely. It is the same verb Paul uses of Christ redeeming us from the curse of the law. See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kairos**, not **chronos**. Not clock time — the opportune moment, the window while it is open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of buying something off the shelf before somebody else does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the days are evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives one reason and it is not encouraging."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing from Roman custody, and the letter mentions his chains three times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For this reason **do not be foolish, but understand what the will of the Lord is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **suniete** — comprehend, put together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say *find out*. The knowing is described as understanding rather than discovering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And do not be drunk with wine, in which is dissipation; but **be filled with the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a present passive imperative — *be being filled*, continuously."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *speaking to one another in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs, singing and making melody in your heart to the Lord, **giving thanks always for all things**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The buying back of time is followed immediately by singing and thanks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul says it again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Live wisely toward those who are outside, and **buy up every hour you are given**.* See the note on Colossians 4:5–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost identical, and there the context is conversations with outsiders."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both letters were written from the same imprisonment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul means by the days being evil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter says it in chapter 6: *we do not wrestle against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this age*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *take up the whole armour of God, that you may be able to withstand in the evil day*. See the note on Ephesians 6:10–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about efficiency. The Greek is about seizing an opportunity, not about managing a schedule."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter has come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore be imitators of God, as dear children. And walk in love.* See the note on Ephesians 5:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the whole practical half of the letter runs from chapter 4: *walk worthy of the calling with which you were called*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being bought.** Not more hours. **Kairos** — the moment while it is available — and Paul's stated reason for the urgency is that the days are bad."}]}, "Ephesians 5:22–24": {"title": "\"He Himself is the Saviour of the body\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses that get quoted on their own, and almost everything about them depends on the sentence before and the twenty-five verses after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb is not in the Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wives, submit to your own husbands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no verb in verse 22 in the oldest manuscripts. It is carried over from the previous sentence — *submitting to one another in the fear of God* — which is addressed to everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph begins with mutual submission and then works out what it looks like in a household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As to the Lord\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The measure is set immediately. Not *because he has earned it*, and not *however he asks for it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Head"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the husband is **head** of the wife, as Christ also is **head of the church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is what fills the word. Whatever headship means here, it means what Christ's headship means, and Paul spends the next twelve verses saying what that is:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Husbands, love your wives, **just as Christ also loved the church and gave Himself for her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern set for the husband is a man on a cross. Nothing in the passage authorises anything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*— and **He Himself is the Saviour of the body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul breaks his own comparison to say it. He has just lined up husband/wife with Christ/church, and then he stops and notes the point at which the parallel does not run: Christ is the Saviour of the body, and no husband is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The body.** Not the soul, not the spirit. Paul is talking about the church as a body and he says outright that Christ saves it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same thing a few verses later: *no one ever hated his own flesh, but nourishes and cherishes it, **just as the Lord does the church** — for we are members of His body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nourishes and cherishes.** The second Greek word is used of a bird warming her young under her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole passage is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is a great mystery, but I speak concerning **Christ and the church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Paul's own summary, and it comes at the end. The marriage was never the subject. It is the picture, and he says which way the picture points."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the husband is not given.** No authority to command, correct, or require anything. Every instruction in the passage addressed to him is about what he gives: love her, give yourself for her, nourish her, cherish her, love her as your own body. The list is one-directional."}]}, "Ephesians 5:25–27": {"title": "\"Washing her clean with water through the word\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The instruction to husbands is one line. The rest is about what Christ did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Husbands, **love your wives, just as Christ also loved the church and gave Himself up for her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard is not reciprocity, deserving, or a fair division of effort. It is a man on a cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything addressed to the husband in this passage is about what he gives. Nothing in it authorises him to require anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three purpose clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each begins *so that*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*so that He might **make her holy**, washing her clean*", "*so that He might **present her to Himself** a church in all her glory*", "and the state she is in when presented"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is His action, and the church is the object throughout."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The washing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Washing her clean **with water through the word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *word* here is **rhema** — the spoken word, the thing said, rather than **logos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pictures are usually heard in it: the bath a bride took before a wedding in that world, and baptism."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the cleansing agent is named. Not effort, not time, not penance — water, and the word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says the same to the disciples: *you are already clean **because of the word** which I have spoken to you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is presented as"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In all her glory, **with no stain on her and no crease and nothing else of the kind**, but **holy and flawless**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not having spot or wrinkle or any such thing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Spot** is a mark from outside. **Wrinkle** is what age does from inside. Neither is present."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Flawless** is **amomos** — the sacrificial word, without blemish, the term for an animal fit for the altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the presenting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He might present her **to Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He washes her, and He is also the one she is presented to. There is no third party in the transaction and no inspector."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage is really about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says so himself a few verses later: *this is a great mystery, **but I speak concerning Christ and the church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The marriage is the illustration. He names the subject at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Husbands, **live with her thoughtfully, with real understanding**… so that nothing gets in the way of your prayers.* See the note on 1 Peter 3:7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two apostles, two letters, and neither gives a husband anything to demand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the paragraph begins.** *Submitting to one another in the fear of God* — a mutual instruction, addressed to everybody, and the verse the whole household section hangs from. See the note on Ephesians 5:22–24."}]}, "Ephesians 5:28–30": {"title": "\"Of His flesh and of His bones\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing about marriage and says something about the church that goes further than the illustration needed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one ever hated his own flesh; **he feeds it and tends it warmly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *tends it warmly* is **thalpo** — to keep warm, and it is used of a bird brooding over her young in the nest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's case to husbands is not built on duty. It is built on what a man already does for his own body without being asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*— exactly as **the Lord does the church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two verbs, applied to Him. He feeds it and He broods over it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause that goes furthest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **we are parts of His body, of His flesh and of His bones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul could have stopped at *His body*. He does not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is Adam's, word for word, when he first saw Eve: *this is now **bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam said it about a woman taken out of his side while he slept. Paul says it about the church, and two verses later he quotes the rest of that sentence — *for this reason a man shall leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and the two shall become one flesh* — and adds: *this is a great mystery, but I speak concerning Christ and the church.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the union is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an arrangement, an alliance, or a membership. Paul uses the language of one body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who is joined to the Lord is one spirit with Him*, he says in the other letter. And in this one: *God raised us up together, and made us sit together in the heavenly places in Christ Jesus* — the tense is past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means about the body"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The head is in one place and the body is not sick in a way the head is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever is true of Him is what the passage says is true of the parts. *As He is, so are we in this world* — 1 John says it about now, not later. See the note on 1 John 4:17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the instruction lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Husbands ought to love their own wives as their own bodies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything addressed to the husband in this passage is about what he gives: love her, give yourself for her, feed her, keep her warm."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was taken from His side.** Adam's bride came out of an opened side while he slept. On the cross, a soldier *pierced His side with a spear, and immediately blood and water came out.* John records it and then insists on it: *he who has seen it has borne witness, and his testimony is true.*"}]}, "Ephesians 6:1–4": {"title": "The first commandment with a promise attached", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the fifth commandment and adds a note about it — *which is the first commandment with a promise attached to it* — and the aside is worth as much as the quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is pointing out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the ten, this is the first that comes with an outcome named: *so that it may go well with you and you may live a long life on the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not inventing the promise; he is drawing attention to the fact that it is there. In a list mostly of prohibitions, one command arrives with a stated benefit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How to read the promise honestly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a promise in the shape a promise takes in the Old Testament — a general truth about how a society and a life tend to run, not a personal guarantee. The same Bible records godly people who honoured their parents and died young."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as it was written, it is saying that a people who hold the generations together last, and a people who do not, do not. Everyone who has watched a family either work or come apart knows the observation is sound."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line to fathers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not stir your children up to anger.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul turns and addresses the party with all the power, which in a first-century household was a genuinely radical thing to do. The word describes provoking someone into exasperation — the slow kind, built up over time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the alternative he gives is not passivity: *raise them in the training and correction of the Lord*. Not less involvement. Involvement of a different sort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"obey\" and \"honour.\"** Paul uses both, and they are not the same. Obeying belongs to children in a household. Honouring is what the commandment asks, and it has no expiry date — it is what an adult still owes an elderly parent long after obedience has stopped being the relevant word."}]}, "Ephesians 6:10–13": {"title": "\"Having done everything, to stand\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last section, and the verb he keeps returning to is not what people expect from a passage about warfare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he repeats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Stand.** Four times in four verses: stand against the schemes, stand your ground, and — having done everything — *stand*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not advance, not conquer, not take. The posture being described is holding a position that has already been won, which is consistent with everything in the first three chapters: seated with Christ, above every ruler and authority."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You defend ground you already hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Be strong in the Lord\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is passive — *be strengthened*. It is not an exhortation to summon resolve. And the strength named is *the strength of His might*, the same phrase Paul used in chapter 1 of the power that raised Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the fight is with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not against flesh and blood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul rules out people first. Whatever the Ephesians were facing — and the city was famous for its magic and its temple of Artemis — he tells them the opposition is not the human beings in front of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four terms follow: ruling powers, authorities, world-rulers of this present darkness, spiritual armies of wickedness in the heavenly places. He does not define them, and no one since has been able to sort them into a tidy hierarchy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The whole armour of God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice. It is **God's own** armour — Isaiah describes the LORD putting on righteousness as a breastplate and a helmet of salvation, and Paul is handing the same equipment to the church."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing on the list is manufactured by the wearer."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is in the list.** Truth, righteousness, the gospel of peace, faith, salvation, the word of God — and then prayer. Every item is something already given in the first half of the letter. Chapter 6 is chapters 1 to 3, put on."}]}, "Ephesians 6:14–17": {"title": "The whole armour", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six pieces, and Paul names what each one is made of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **stand**, then.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fourth time in five verses. *Stand against, withstand, having done all, stand, stand therefore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in this passage is about advancing. The whole armour is issued for holding ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The belt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Truth fastened **around your waist like a belt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soldier's belt held everything else — the scabbard hung from it, and the tunic was tucked into it to free the legs. Nothing else works if it is loose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The breastplate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Righteousness worn **over your chest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has it first: *He put on righteousness as a breastplate.* It is God's armour before it is ours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And whose righteousness it is has been settled earlier in the letter: *the gift of righteousness*, *accepted in the Beloved*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The feet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your feet fitted with **the readiness that the good news of peace gives**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman military sandals were studded with hobnails, for grip."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hetoimasia** — preparedness, a firm footing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The odd part is what it is made of: *the gospel of peace*. Peace, on the feet, in a passage about a battle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shield"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Above all of it, take up the shield, which is **faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Thureos** — from *thura*, a door. The full-length Roman shield, four feet by two and a half, covering the whole man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the small round buckler. This one you get behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With it you will be able to **put out all the burning arrows** of the evil one.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Arrows wrapped in pitch-soaked tow and lit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Roman shield was wood covered in leather, and the leather was soaked in water before a battle so that a flaming arrow sticking in it went out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the shield extinguishes them. It does not deflect them; it takes them and quenches them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The helmet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The helmet, which is **salvation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah again: *a helmet of salvation on His head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's other version: *the hope of salvation as a helmet.* See the note on 1 Thessalonians 5:4–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sword"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the sword the Spirit gives, which is the word of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **machaira** — the short stabbing sword, not the long broadsword. It was close-quarters equipment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *word* here is **rhema** — the spoken word, the specific saying, rather than **logos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what Jesus used in the wilderness. Three times: *it is written.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not covered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is issued for the back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Put on the whole armour of God, that you may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.* And the passage before all of it: *be strong in the Lord and **in the power of His might**.* See the note on Ephesians 6:10–13."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows the armour.** *Praying always with all prayer and supplication in the Spirit… and for me, that utterance may be given to me.* Paul finishes the armour passage by asking a church to pray for his own courage — *that I may speak boldly, as I ought to speak.* He was in chains when he wrote it."}]}, "Esther 1:1–4": {"title": "The king who ruled the world", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens on the most powerful man on earth, and God is not mentioned. He will not be mentioned anywhere in the ten chapters that follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no name of God in Esther. No prayer is recorded, no temple, no sacrifice, no prophet, no miracle, no angel, no law quoted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That absence is the book. It is the story of a people God had told, through Hosea, *you are not My people* — and it is set in exactly that season, when His face was hidden and His hand was not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not absent from the story. He is not named in it. There is a difference, and the whole of Esther is written to show it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The empire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From India all the way to Cush, a hundred and twenty-seven provinces.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahasuerus is Xerxes. The reach described is the largest empire the world had seen to that point, and the writer measures it in provinces rather than miles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Jews are somewhere inside it, scattered, with no land, no king and no temple to go to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "A hundred and eighty days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For a long while — a full **hundred and eighty days** — he laid out before them the wealth of his glorious kingdom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six months of display. The verb is to show, to make visible; the whole of it is about being seen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then a further seven-day feast for everybody in Susa, which the next verses describe down to the marble pillars and the silver couches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the world looks like at the start"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every visible thing belongs to Ahasuerus. The wealth is his, the law is his, the army is his, and by the end of chapter 3 the life of every Jew in the empire is his to sign away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where the book starts, and it starts there deliberately. The reader is meant to look at all of it and ask the question the whole book answers."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name hidden in the text.** Four times in the Hebrew of Esther the letters of the name **YHWH** stand at the start of four consecutive words, spelled out forwards twice and backwards twice. And once, in Esther 7:5, the letters of **I AM** — *ehyeh* — are formed the same way, in the sentence where the king demands to know who has dared to do this thing. The name is in the book. It is written the way He was working: present, and not announced."}]}, "Esther 1:16–20": {"title": "A small refusal, a great fear", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most powerful court in the world panics over one woman not coming to dinner, and legislates."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escalation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Queen Vashti has not only wronged the king, but **every official and every people in all the provinces**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Memucan takes a private embarrassment and makes it an imperial crisis in one sentence. A hundred and twenty-seven provinces are now involved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were afraid of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Word of what the queen has done will get out to all the women, and they will look down on their own husbands.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fear is of a story travelling. Not of Vashti — of what people will say about her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There will be no end of contempt and quarreling.\"* The men who ruled from India to Cush are worried about arguments at home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let him send out a royal decree, written into the laws of Persia and Media **so it cannot be revoked**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The irrevocability of Persian law is introduced here, casually, in chapter 1. It will matter enormously in chapter 8, when a death sentence on a whole people cannot be cancelled either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer plants it early, in a trivial context, so that the reader already knows the rule when everything depends on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that makes room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let the king give her royal place **to another woman better than she**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Memucan does not know what he has just said. He is drafting a decree about household order, and the clause he adds at the end opens the throne of Persia."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four chapters later a Jewish girl sits in that place, and the empire's entire Jewish population is alive because of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody prays. Nobody consults a prophet. Nobody mentions God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A drunk king, a proud queen, and a nervous adviser between them arrange the one thing that will be needed. See the note on Esther 1:9–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line of the decree.** *Then every wife will honor her husband, from the greatest to the least.* An empire-wide law was passed to make women respectful, and the historical result of it, so far as the book records, is that the Jews were not exterminated."}]}, "Esther 1:9–12": {"title": "Queen Vashti's own feast", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A queen says no, and a vacancy opens that will save a nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seventh day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the seventh day, when the king's heart was merry with wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer tells us the state he was in before he tells us what he ordered. Six days of drinking are behind the command."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wanted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To bring Queen Vashti before him wearing her royal crown. He wanted to **display her beauty** to the people and the officials.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb as the six months of showing off the kingdom's wealth. She is the last item in the display — the treasury, the army, the palace, and then his wife."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Seven names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mehuman, Biztha, Harbona, Bigtha, Abagtha, Zethar, and Carkas.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer lists all seven eunuchs, and no reader has ever needed to know their names. He does the same with the seven princes a few verses later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book keeps its eye on detail like this, on court procedure and paperwork and who was standing where. That is the level at which the whole rescue is going to happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Would not come\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Hebrew, and the empire tilts on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the text explains her. No reason is given, no speech recorded. She simply does not come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the refusal produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king was furious, and the anger smoldered in him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Out of that anger comes the decree, out of the decree comes the vacancy, and out of the vacancy comes a Jewish orphan on the throne of Persia four years before Haman writes his letters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in chapter 1 is trying to save anybody. A queen refuses a drunk man's summons and a place is made ready that will be needed later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the book is doing here.** God is not named and nothing miraculous happens. A woman's pride, a king's temper and a courtier's advice do all the work — and every one of them is moving a piece into position. He is ruling through it while none of them know His name."}]}, "Esther 2:15–16": {"title": "The girl who asked for nothing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence in this passage is doing quiet work, and it is easy to read past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What every other woman did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already explained the arrangement: each young woman went in to the king once, and could take with her whatever she asked for from the harem. One night, one chance, and an open request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You can imagine what most of them took."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Esther took"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She asked for nothing except what Hegai, the king's eunuch and keeper of the women, suggested.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hegai had been running that household for years. He knew the king. Esther, with one opportunity and permission to ask for anything, asked the man who actually knew what he was talking about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not passivity — she is a woman in an impossible position playing the one card available with unusual coolness. It is judgement."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**A book that never mentions God.** Esther is the only book in the Bible in which God's name does not appear, and moments like this are why readers have always felt Him in it anyway: nothing miraculous happens, and yet the details keep landing right. The story is told entirely at ground level — a favour here, a sleepless night there — and the shape only becomes visible from the far end of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word the chapter keeps using"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Esther **obtained favour** in the sight of all who looked at her.* The Hebrew is **chen** — grace, unearned favour, the same word used of Noah finding grace in the eyes of the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter uses it about her again and again. She wins the position without qualifications: no family, no wealth, no nation to speak for her, an orphan and a captive. What she has is favour, and the writer says so every time she advances."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what carries her in chapter 2 and it is what saves her life in chapter 5, when a golden sceptre is held out to a woman who came in unsummoned. See the note on Esther 5:1–2."}]}, "Esther 2:5–7": {"title": "A Jew in the fortress, a girl in his care", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two people are introduced, and everything the book needs is in their description."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The genealogy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mordecai son of Jair, son of Shimei, son of **Kish**, a **Benjamite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kish, a Benjamite, is the name of Saul's father. The writer is pointing at something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul was the Benjamite king commanded to destroy Amalek, and he did not. Haman is introduced two chapters later as *the Agagite* — Agag being the Amalekite king Saul spared."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A descendant of Kish and a descendant of Agag, in the same court, four hundred years later. The unfinished business of 1 Samuel 15 is the plot of this book. See the note on Deuteronomy 25:17–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Carried away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His family had been carried away from Jerusalem among the captives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mordecai is in Susa because his people lost their land. He is not there by ambition. He is there because of the exile, in the middle of the season Hosea called *not My people*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mordecai was raising **Hadassah, that is, Esther**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hadassah** is Hebrew — the myrtle. **Esther** is the Persian name she is known by in the palace, and it is the name on the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The myrtle is a fragrant shrub that gives off its scent most strongly when it is crushed, and Isaiah 55 puts it in a promise: *instead of the brier shall come up the myrtle tree.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her Hebrew name is hidden under a foreign one, in a book where God's name is hidden under an ordinary story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"She had neither father nor mother\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in three lines. The writer wants it registered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The girl who will stand between a nation and its extermination is an orphan in a foreign city, with no standing, no family, and no claim on anybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mordecai had taken her as his own daughter.* The Hebrew is literally *took her for a daughter*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is already in place.** Before Haman is promoted, before the decree is written, before anyone knows there is a danger — a Benjamite of Kish's line is in the fortress and the girl in his house is beautiful. The pieces are set out before the game starts."}]}, "Esther 3:1–4": {"title": "Haman lifted high", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The villain is introduced with a family name, and the family name is the whole argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The Agagite\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Haman son of Hammedatha **the Agagite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Agag was the king of Amalek whom Saul was ordered to destroy and chose to spare — 1 Samuel 15, the disobedience that cost Saul the kingdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek was the nation that attacked Israel from behind in the wilderness, picking off the tired and the stragglers, and God said He would have war with them from generation to generation. See the note on Deuteronomy 25:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four centuries after Saul let Agag live, Agag's descendant is second in the Persian empire and signing a death warrant for every Jew alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not put to death does not stay small. It comes back with an office and a signet ring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promotion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*King Ahasuerus promoted Haman… raising him higher than all the other officials.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No reason is given. Haman has done nothing in the book. Mordecai, by contrast, has just saved the king's life in the previous four verses — and got nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts those two facts side by side without comment. It looks like the wrong man rising, and for three chapters it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Mordecai would not bow\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole court bows. It has been commanded. Mordecai stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives one reason, and it is the only reason in the book: *he had told them he was a Jew.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue, campaign, or explain further. He is asked *day after day* and *he would not listen.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servants were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they told Haman, **to see whether Mordecai's reason would stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They report him in order to watch what happens. That is the sentence that lights the fuse — a small piece of court malice, and an empire-wide massacre is drafted because of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**A Benjamite and an Amalekite.** Mordecai has already been introduced as *son of Kish, a Benjamite* — Saul's line. The old war Saul left unfinished is standing in a Persian gateway, and this time the Benjamite does not bow. See the note on Esther 2:5–7."}]}, "Esther 3:12–15": {"title": "Letters sealed with the king's ring", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The genocide is not a mob. It is paperwork, properly filed, in every language of the empire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The machinery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king's scribes were called… to each province in **its own script** and to each people in **its own language**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Translated for every people so that nobody could plead they had not understood. The empire's whole administrative apparatus is turned on a single ethnic group."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sealed with the king's ring.* Under Persian law that made it irrevocable — the rule the reader was taught back in chapter 1, when it was only about a queen not coming to dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ordering that all Jews be **destroyed, killed, and wiped out**, young and old, little children and women, all in a single day — and their goods taken as plunder.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The piling-up is deliberate. And the phrase *young and old, little children and women* is Amalek's signature: the people at the back, the ones who cannot run."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plunder clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their goods taken as plunder.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman is buying the empire's cooperation. Anyone who kills a Jew keeps the house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hold this clause in mind — in chapter 9, when the Jews defend themselves and win, the writer says three separate times that *they did not lay a hand on the plunder.* They were entitled to it under the counter-decree and they left it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eleven months"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lot had fallen on the thirteenth of Adar. The letters go out in the first month, Nisan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every Jew in the world now has a date on which they are legally to be killed, and eleven months to look at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the king and Haman sat down to drink**, while the city of Susa reeled in confusion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men with a glass, and a city outside the window not knowing what has hit it. The writer sets the two clauses in one line and lets them stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the low point of the book. There is no God named, no prophet, no army, no appeal — a sealed law that cannot be revoked, and the men who wrote it are drinking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the reader knows and Susa does not.** Nisan is the month of Passover. The decree ordering the destruction of the Jews was written on the day before the feast that commemorates their deliverance."}]}, "Esther 3:5–6": {"title": "A rage that would not stop at one man", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and a private insult becomes a policy of extermination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Too small a thing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It seemed **too small a thing** to lay hands on Mordecai alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Killing the man who offended him is beneath the scale of Haman's anger. He wants proportion, and the proportion he settles on is a nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is contemptuous — it despised in his eyes. Murder was not enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What made it possible"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They had told him **who Mordecai's people were**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servants' report in the verses before did this. Mordecai's answer had been *I am a Jew*, and that single fact converts one man's stubbornness into a target the size of an empire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Amalek doing what Amalek does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman is *the Agagite*, and this is Amalek's method exactly as Deuteronomy describes it: not a declared war on soldiers, but an attack on a whole people including *young and old, little children and women* — the phrase in the decree nine verses later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek attacked the stragglers at the back. Haman writes them into the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To wipe out **all the Jews, every one of them**, throughout the whole kingdom of Ahasuerus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and twenty-seven provinces. There was nowhere in the known world a Jew could go that this decree would not reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had no land, no king, no army and no temple. There was no earthly protection anywhere in the picture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God is still not mentioned. Nobody prays in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reader who knows the end of the book knows what is already true while Haman is planning: a Jewish girl has been queen of Persia for four years, and a king's insomnia is coming."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The date they cast lots for.** Haman throws the *pur* — the lot — to pick the day, and it falls on the thirteenth of Adar, eleven months away. The one thing he leaves to chance gives his enemies a year to live. Proverbs says the lot is cast into the lap, but its every decision is from the LORD."}]}, "Esther 4:12–14": {"title": "For such a time as this", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mordecai's answer is the theological centre of a book that never mentions God, and he manages to say everything without using the name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Relief and deliverance will arise from another place\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you stay silent now, **relief and deliverance for the Jews will arise from another place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is completely certain the rescue is coming. That is not in doubt anywhere in his sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *perhaps we will be saved*. He says the deliverance will arrive, with or without her — and he does not name where from. *Another place* is as close as this book comes to saying the name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question on the table is never whether the Jews will be delivered. It is whether Esther will be in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You and your father's house will perish\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one who stands to lose is the one who stays out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had just sent word that going in unsummoned carries a death sentence unless the golden sceptre is held out. Mordecai answers that the danger she is weighing is not the real danger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Who knows whether you have come to the palace for such a time as this?\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every step that put her in that room was somebody else's doing. A queen's refusal, a drunk king's temper, an adviser's decree, a eunuch's advice, a beauty contest she did not enter herself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mordecai looks at that chain and reads it correctly: she did not arrive there by accident, and the position was given for a reason that is only now becoming visible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who knows* — he does not claim a revelation. There is no prophet in this book. He is reading providence from the shape of it, which is what the whole book asks its reader to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her answer is three verses later: gather the Jews, fast three days, *and if I perish, I perish.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes in. And the position that had been prepared for her turns out to be the place the whole nation is saved from."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Mordecai is not saying.** He is not telling her the outcome depends on her. He has already ruled that out — the deliverance is coming from another place. He is telling her there is a part with her name on it, and that she can miss it. See the note on Esther 5:1–2."}]}, "Esther 5:1–2": {"title": "The golden sceptre", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and they carry the weight of the whole book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the **third day** Esther put on her royal robes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days of fasting are behind this sentence. She had asked for them, and she comes out of them on the third day and walks in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she wore"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her **royal robes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sackcloth. She had been in mourning clothes at the end of chapter 4 — the Hebrew here is literally *she put on royalty*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She approaches him as a queen, in what belongs to her position, not as a petitioner in rags."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The law she is breaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had spelled it out herself two chapters earlier: anyone who goes into the inner court unsummoned is put to death, *unless the king holds out the golden sceptre.* She had not been called for thirty days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has no right to be standing there. That is the fact the scene begins with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the king saw Queen Esther standing in the court, **she won his favour**, and he **held out to her** the golden sceptre.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *she obtained grace in his eyes* — **chen**, the same word the book has used over her from the beginning, unearned favour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She did not earn the sceptre by three days of fasting and she did not argue her way to it. It was extended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who moves first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sceptre goes out before she says anything. She has not stated her business, made her case, or explained why she came."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *so Esther came near and **touched the top of the sceptre**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She approaches because it was held out, and what she touches is the thing that spared her. The order in those two verses is the order the whole book runs on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was on the other side of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What is your request? It shall be given you, **up to half the kingdom**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the next sentence. The law said death; the sceptre said half an empire."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the throne came from.** She is standing in a room she only occupies because Vashti refused to come when she was summoned. One woman would not come when she was called; another came when she was not called, and lived. See the note on Esther 1:9–12."}]}, "Esther 5:9–13": {"title": "One man at the gate spoils everything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Haman has everything, and the writer lets him list it out loud so the last sentence can land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inventory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He boasted to them of his **glorious riches**, his **many sons**, and **every way the king had promoted him**… \"And besides all that, **Queen Esther invited no one but me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wealth, sons, rank, and a private banquet with the king and queen. In that world there was nothing else to want."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He counts it in front of an audience he has assembled for the purpose — his wife and his friends, called in to hear it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that undoes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet all of this means nothing to me** as long as I see Mordecai the Jew sitting at the king's gate.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is stronger than the English can quite carry: all of it is worth nothing to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man, sitting down, not moving. That is the entire offence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mordecai did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mordecai **did not rise or so much as tremble** before him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not insult Haman. He did not speak. He sat still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer adds *or so much as tremble* — Haman is not even given the compliment of fear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Still, he held himself in\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman restrains himself in public and goes home to say it. The rage is not managed; it is stored."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the end of the same conversation his wife and friends have proposed the gallows, and *the thing pleased Haman, and he had the gallows made.* Fifty cubits — seventy-five feet, higher than anything needed for the job, built to be seen across the city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is standing when he says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just come from Esther's first banquet, and he has been invited back tomorrow. He thinks the invitation is an honour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a trap that has already closed. The reader can see it; Haman is at home counting his sons."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the boast cost him.** He builds a gallows that night for a man he will be leading through the streets on a horse by tomorrow afternoon, and he will hang on it himself before the second banquet is over. See the notes on Esther 6:10–11 and Esther 7:7–10."}]}, "Esther 6:1–3": {"title": "The night the king could not sleep", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turning point of the book is a man who cannot get to sleep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hinge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That night **sleep would not come to the king**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is it. No angel, no dream, no prophet, no plague. The most powerful man in the world lies awake, and from that one ordinary thing everything reverses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is impersonal — sleep fled from the king. The writer does not say who took it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Count what has to line up on this one night:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Haman is at that moment building a gallows for Mordecai", "the king cannot sleep", "of everything he could call for, he calls for the **chronicles**", "of the whole reign's records, they read the page about **Mordecai**", "the reader is told *nothing has been done for him at all*", "and Haman walks into the court at daybreak to ask for Mordecai's death"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of those is unremarkable on its own. Any one of them going differently and the book ends another way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the record was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mordecai had uncovered the plot of Bigthana and Teresh back in chapter 2, four years earlier, and it was written down and forgotten."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That failure to reward him looked at the time like an injustice. It was a debt being held until the night it would do the most work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Nothing has been done for him at all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servants' answer, and it is the sentence the king cannot leave alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not send for a treasurer. His next question is *who is in the court?* — and Haman has just arrived, early, to ask permission to hang the man the king is about to honour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is showing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no miracle in Esther 6. There is insomnia, an administrative record, and bad timing for one man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how the whole book works: nothing supernatural on the surface, and every detail landing exactly where it has to. He is not named in the chapter. He is all over it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Haman was doing while the king lay awake.** Standing in his own courtyard watching a seventy-five-foot gallows go up. Two men in one city that night — one building, one unable to sleep — and only one of them thought he was in control of the morning."}]}, "Esther 6:10–11": {"title": "The parade he never imagined", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Haman describes, in loving detail, the honours he wants for himself, and then has to carry every one of them out for the man he came to hang."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had just designed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king had asked what should be done for the man he delights to honour, and Haman — *whom would the king delight to honour more than me?* — designed it himself: the king's own robe, the king's own horse, the crown on its head, and a noble to lead it through the city square shouting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was writing his own coronation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"For Mordecai the Jew\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do all of this for **Mordecai the Jew**, who sits at the king's gate.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king names him with the two things Haman hates most: his name, and his nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who sits at the king's gate* — the very posture that started the whole rage. See the note on Esther 5:9–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Leave out nothing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Leave out nothing** of everything you have described.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item. The king will not let him trim it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman had built the list for maximum glory, and now the length of it is the length of his own humiliation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had come to ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing in that court at daybreak for one reason: to get permission to hang Mordecai on a gallows already built and waiting outside his house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never gets the sentence out. The king speaks first, and by the time Haman is allowed to answer he is holding a robe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Haman **took** the robe and the horse, and he **dressed** Mordecai, and he **led** him through the city square… **calling out** before him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, all his, all performed on the man he meant to kill. He dressed him with his own hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is what is done for the man the king delights to honour!\"* — shouted, by Haman, in the street, all afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No one has intervened. No prophet has spoken, no plague has fallen, nobody has prayed on the page."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sleepless night, an old record, and a man's own vanity handing him the exact opposite of what he came for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for it.** *Whoever digs a pit will fall into it, and a stone will come back on the one who starts it rolling.* Haman's own gallows, his own robe, his own parade. Every instrument in this book turns around in the hand that made it."}]}, "Esther 6:12–14": {"title": "Home in grief", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men go home from the same parade, and the writer records both journeys in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Mordecai goes back to work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Afterward **Mordecai returned to the king's gate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Robe off, horse gone, back to his seat. Nothing is recorded of what he said or felt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not asked for the parade, had not campaigned for it, and does not appear to have adjusted his life because of it. He goes back to the gate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Haman goes home"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Haman hurried home, **his head covered**, mourning and shaken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Covering the head was the posture of grief and shame. In 2 Samuel it is what David does going up the Mount of Olives, and what the mourners do for the dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was leading a parade an hour ago."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The advisers change their tune"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same wife and the same friends who suggested the gallows the previous evening now say this:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If Mordecai, before whom you have **begun to fall**, is **one of the Jewish people**, you will not overcome him. You will surely fall before him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They give a reason, and the reason is his nation. Pagan advisers in a Persian house state the principle the book never states outright — that there is something about this people you cannot win against."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They cannot name what they are describing. They can see it working."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Begun to fall\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has the verb twice: you have *begun to fall* before him, you will *surely fall*. Once it starts, they say, it does not stop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened yet except a robe and a horse. There has been no trial, no accusation, and Haman is still the second man in the empire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are reading the direction of things off one afternoon, and they are right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were still speaking when the king's messengers arrived, **hurrying Haman away** to the banquet Esther had prepared.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not get to finish the conversation. He is taken, mid-sentence, to the room where he will be exposed and sentenced within the hour."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pace of the chapter.** Everything in Esther 6 happens in a single day, and the verbs are all urgent — *hurry*, *hastened*, *hurrying away*. Eleven months of waiting under a death decree, and then one day in which the whole thing turns over."}]}, "Esther 7:3–4": {"title": "The night she finally asked", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two banquets, an invitation held back for a day, and then the request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king had asked her twice what she wanted, and twice offered up to half his kingdom. Both times she asked only that he and Haman come to dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In between the two banquets: a sleepless night for the king, the royal records read aloud, Mordecai's unrewarded loyalty discovered, and Haman paraded through the streets leading the horse of the man he meant to hang."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of that happens if she asks on the first night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How she asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I have found favour in your eyes, my king, and if it pleases the king, let my own life be given me as my petition, and my people as my request.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She asks for her own life first. Until this moment the king does not know she is Jewish — the decree he sealed includes her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She puts herself inside the sentence before she mentions anybody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about slavery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Had we only been sold as slaves, men and women, I would have kept silent, for such a trouble would not be worth disturbing the king.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A deliberate piece of restraint. She tells a Persian king that she would not have troubled him over slavery — which makes clear that what she is troubling him about is worse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To be destroyed, to be killed, to be wiped away.* Three, and they are the exact three from Haman's decree, quoted back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not describing the danger in her own words. She is reading his own paperwork to him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The book that never names God.** Nothing miraculous happens in Esther. A queen waits a day, a king cannot sleep, a record is read at the right hour. Only from the far end does the shape become visible — which is the whole method of the book. See the note on Esther 2:15–16."}]}, "Esther 7:5–6": {"title": "Who would dare", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The king's question, and the answer that ends Haman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who is he? Where is the man** who has dared in his heart to do such a thing?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king does not know. He signed the decree himself — sealed it with his own ring — and never asked which people it was for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is furious at a crime he authorised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name written into the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the Hebrew of this verse, the first letters of four consecutive words spell out **EHYEH** — *I AM*. The name God gave Moses at the bush: *I AM WHO I AM.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It stands in the king's demand to know who has dared to do such a thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the fourth and last of the hidden names in Esther. The letters of **YHWH** are formed the same way four times elsewhere in the book — twice reading forwards and twice reading backwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is never printed in this book. It is built into the text, in the places where the story turns, and it is written in the one sentence where the question being asked out loud is *who is behind this?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The man, the enemy, this wicked Haman.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three phrases, and no verb between them. In Hebrew it lands like three blows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She names him in front of the king, at the king's table, with Haman sitting at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had waited for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two banquets, two days. She had the sceptre and the offer of half the kingdom on the first day and asked only for another dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delay put Haman in the room on the night after his humiliation, at the exact hour the king's mind had already turned against him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Haman stood frozen with terror\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he was terrified before the king *and the queen*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not known she was a Jew. Nobody in the palace had — Mordecai had told her not to say. The decree he wrote and sealed included the woman sitting opposite him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the king still does not know.** He never learns that God is in this. He goes to his grave believing he lost his temper twice and settled a matter of court intrigue. The book lets him. The name is in the text and not in his mouth."}]}, "Esther 7:7–10": {"title": "The gallows he built", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Everything Haman made turns around and is used on him, item by item, in the space of four verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pleading with the woman he sentenced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Haman stayed behind to **plead with Queen Esther for his life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who wrote a decree to kill every Jew in the empire is on his knees to a Jewish woman asking her to spare him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not know that is what he is doing until the sentence before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The couch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As the king came back from the garden… there was Haman, **fallen across the couch** where Esther reclined.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is begging. The king walks in and reads it as an assault."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Will he even assault the queen while I am in the house?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing Haman did not do is the thing he is accused of, and it is the accusation that finishes him. He had built his whole career on the reading people put on appearances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"They covered Haman's face\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And as the words left the king's mouth, they covered Haman's face.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Covering the face was what was done to a condemned man. No trial, no verdict pronounced — the servants read the room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had gone home the day before with *his head covered* in shame. Now other hands do it, and it means execution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Harbona"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Harbona**, one of the king's servants, said, \"Look — the gallows Haman made for Mordecai… stands beside Haman's own house, fifty cubits high.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Harbona is named back in chapter 1, one of the seven eunuchs who were sent to fetch Vashti. The writer listed all seven names then, and it looked like nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks once in the whole book, and it is this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gallows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy-five feet, built the night before, standing beside his own house so the whole city could see it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Hang him on it.\" So they hanged Haman on the gallows he had built for Mordecai.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He chose the height. He chose the location. He supplied the instrument, and the only thing he got wrong was who would be on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it costs him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next chapter opens with his estate given to Esther, his signet ring given to Mordecai, and his household put under the man he meant to kill."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he had listed to his friends — the riches, the rank, the sons — goes across to the other side of the ledger."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last clause.** *And the king's fury was stilled.* The same phrase closes chapter 2 after his rage at Vashti. Twice in this book a king's temper decides everything, and both times it lands where it had to for the Jews to live."}]}, "Esther 8:1–2": {"title": "\"Esther's house, Mordecai's ring\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The transfer happens in a single day, and it is total."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"That same day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a process. The morning Haman was still second in the empire; by evening his estate belongs to the woman he sentenced to death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*King Ahasuerus gave Queen Esther **the household of Haman, the enemy of the Jews**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer will not let his name stand alone. He is *the enemy of the Jews* here and in five other places — the title follows him to the end of the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he boasted about to his friends — the glorious riches, the great house — is signed over to a Jewish orphan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"For Esther had told him what he was to her\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The secret comes out at last. Mordecai had told her at the beginning not to reveal her people or her family, and she had kept it for years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She tells the king now, and the man at the gate walks into the royal presence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king took off his signet ring, **the one he had taken back from Haman**, and gave it to Mordecai.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same ring. Not a new one — the writer says where it came from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That ring sealed the decree ordering the death of every Jew in the empire. It is now on the hand of a Jew, and the next thing it will seal is the decree that lets them live."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instrument of the sentence becomes the instrument of the rescue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Esther sets him over it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Esther set Mordecai over the household of Haman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She receives it and immediately puts him in charge of it. Two verses, and the whole apparatus of the enemy is in the hands of the two people it was built against."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What has not changed.** The first decree still stands. Persian law cannot be revoked, and the thirteenth of Adar is still in the calendar — Haman is dead and the sentence he wrote is not. That is the problem chapter 8 has to solve, and it is not solved by cancelling anything. See the note on Esther 8:9–14."}]}, "Esther 8:15–17": {"title": "The city rejoiced", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The man who sat at the gate in sackcloth comes out of the palace in blue and white, and a city that had been reeling shouts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is wearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Royal robes of **blue and white**, a great **crown of gold**, and a mantle of fine **linen and purple**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blue and white are the colours of the Persian royal house, and they are also the colours of the tabernacle hangings. Fine linen and purple are priestly cloth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four chapters earlier the same man was in sackcloth and ashes in the middle of the city, crying out with a loud and bitter cry, and could not pass the gate because no one in sackcloth was allowed in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the city of Susa shouted and was glad**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last time Susa was described, in chapter 3, it *reeled in confusion* while the king and Haman sat down to drink."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same city, same writer, same word placement — and the reversal is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four words for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the Jews there was **light and gladness, joy and honour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Light** first. It is not the obvious word in a list about rejoicing, and it is the word chosen to lead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had had no prophet, no temple, no king, and no name of God spoken over them for a generation. What arrives is light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Everywhere the decree reached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In every province and every city, wherever the king's word and his decree arrived, there was gladness and joy among the Jews, feasting and a holiday.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gladness travels at the speed of the couriers. It is the arrival of the letter that produces it — not the thirteenth of Adar, which is still eight months off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened yet. They are celebrating a word that has been sent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **many of the peoples of the land became Jews**, for the fear of the Jews had fallen upon them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outsiders join. The Hebrew is a rare verb — they *judaised*, made themselves Jews."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people who were *not My people*, hidden and scattered, are suddenly the thing the empire wants to belong to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**From the gate to the palace.** Mordecai never asked for any of it. He would not bow, he sat still, and he told his cousin the deliverance would come from another place whatever she did. The robe found him at the gate. See the notes on Esther 6:10–11 and Esther 4:12–14."}]}, "Esther 8:9–14": {"title": "The second decree", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first decree cannot be revoked. So a second one is written, and it does not cancel the first — it answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Persian law, once sealed with the king's ring, stands. The king says so himself in the verse before: *what is written in the king's name and sealed with the king's ring, no one can revoke.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order to destroy every Jew on the thirteenth of Adar is still law. Haman is dead; his decree is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By these letters the king granted the Jews in every city **the right to gather and defend their lives**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an amnesty. A counter-decree, of equal force, issued through the same machinery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Written the same way, deliberately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set the two side by side and the writer's care is obvious. Every element of Haman's decree in chapter 3 is repeated here:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the king's scribes summoned", "to a hundred and twenty-seven provinces, India to Cush", "each province in its own script, each people in its own language", "written in the name of the king, sealed with the king's signet ring", "sent by couriers, hurried", "for the same single day — the thirteenth of Adar", "with the same clause about *their little ones and their women*", "and the same right to take *the goods of their enemies as plunder*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Word for word, structure for structure. The same ring, the same road, the same date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one difference is who is holding the pen. See the note on Esther 3:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The right to stand\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to stand for their life — to make a stand. It is defensive language throughout."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not told to go on the offensive on any other day. One day, the day already fixed against them, and on that day they may stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eight months' notice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letters go out in Sivan, the third month. The day is in Adar, the twelfth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman's lot fell on a date eleven months away, and that delay is what gives the second decree time to reach India."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plunder they did not take"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The right to take the enemy's goods is granted here in so many words. Chapter 9 then reports, three separate times, that *they did not lay a hand on the plunder.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was theirs by law and they left it. Whatever the day was about, it was not about that."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What one signet ring did twice.** The same ring sealed the sentence and the deliverance. Nothing about the empire changed, nothing about the law changed, and the king never learned whose hand was on any of it."}]}, "Exodus 1:15–22": {"title": "The midwives who feared God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two women are named in this passage. The king of Egypt is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who gets a name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Hebrew midwives, **Shiphrah and Puah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most powerful man in the world is *the king of Egypt* and *Pharaoh*, a title, never a name. Two midwives are recorded by name and have been read out loud for three thousand years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's judgement about who mattered is built into the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were to **put the newborn Hebrew boys to death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boys only. It is the same shape as Haman's decree — an administrative solution to a people who are increasing, carried out through the ordinary machinery of the state."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek at the back of the column; Pharaoh at the birthstool. The attack is always on what is weakest and cannot resist."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the midwives **feared God**, and they refused to do it; they let the boys live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not compassion, courage or ethics. It is fear of God, and the writer names it as the cause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice what Deuteronomy says about Amalek: *they had no fear of God.* The same phrase, on the opposite side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the king demanded why, they answered that the Hebrew mothers were strong and gave birth before they could arrive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They lie to Pharaoh's face. The text records it without a word of criticism, and the next sentence is about God being kind to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **God was kind to the midwives**, and because they honoured Him, **He gave them families of their own**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two women who spent their lives delivering other women's babies were given households of their own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reward matches the work exactly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escalation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At last Pharaoh ordered **all his people** that every newborn Hebrew boy was to be cast into the Nile.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The private instruction failed, so it becomes public policy. Every Egyptian is now deputised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Nile — the river Egypt worshipped, the source of everything they had — is made the instrument."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes of it.** *Still the people kept increasing.* Said in the middle of the chapter and true at the end of it. And the very next paragraph has a Levite woman putting her son into the Nile in a basket, into the hands of Pharaoh's own daughter. See the note on Exodus 2:1–10."}]}, "Exodus 10:21–29": {"title": "Darkness you could feel, and light where Israel lived", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The ninth plague is the strangest of them, and the last one before the death of the firstborn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Darkness that could be touched"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew phrase is unusual — a darkness that may be *felt*, groped at. Not night. Something with substance to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For three days *no one could see anyone else, and no one moved from where he was*. Egypt stops. Nobody travels, nobody works, nobody visits. A whole civilisation sits still in the dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The separation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But all the Israelites had light where they lived.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plagues have been dividing Egypt from Goshen for several chapters now — the flies, the livestock, the hail — and the text keeps saying so plainly. Here the division is at its most visual: one people cannot see their hand, and a few miles away the lamps are on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this plague, and why now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt's chief deity was the sun. The plague before this stripped the land bare; this one puts out the god. And it comes immediately before the night when the firstborn dies and Israel walks out — a rehearsal in the dark for what is coming."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last exchange.** Pharaoh tells Moses to get out and never show his face again, and Moses agrees: *you have spoken well; I will never see your face again.* It is the end of the negotiations that ran through the whole cycle — the compromises about who could go and what they could take are over, and the next thing in the book is the Passover."}]}, "Exodus 10:7–11": {"title": "The negotiation over the children", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh has stopped saying no. That is what makes this passage worth reading slowly — the refusals have become offers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go, worship the LORD your God. But tell me — who exactly is going?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not blocking the worship any more. He is negotiating the guest list, which is a different and more effective tactic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Moses' answer leaves nothing out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We will go with our young and our old, our sons and daughters, our flocks and herds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four categories, and he names them deliberately: both ends of the generations, both sexes of the children, and the livelihood. There is nothing left in Egypt to come back for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Pharaoh draws the line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only the men may go.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The children are the sticking point, and Pharaoh knows exactly what he is doing. A generation of men can go and worship in the desert and still leave everything behind that would make the departure permanent. Keep the children and you keep the future."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The rest of the pattern.** This is the third of four attempted compromises across the chapters: worship inside Egypt; do not go far; leave the children; and finally, leave the livestock. Each one concedes more than the last, and each keeps hold of something. Moses' answer to the fourth is the one that ends it — *not a hoof will be left behind.*"}]}, "Exodus 11:1–3": {"title": "The wages paid on the way out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of instruction between the darkness and the last plague, and the instruction is about asking the neighbours for silver."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that is usually mistranslated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Older versions have Israel *borrow* from the Egyptians, which made generations of readers uneasy — it sounds like taking things under false pretences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is simply *ask*. There is no promise of return in it. What follows is a request made openly, and granted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is not theft"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of unpaid labour stand behind it. The law God gives later requires that a freed slave not be sent away empty-handed but supplied generously from the flock and the threshing floor — and that is exactly the shape of what happens here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel leaves as freed labourers being paid off, not as looters. Psalm 105 says it plainly: *He brought His people out with silver and gold.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The LORD had already softened the Egyptians\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The favour is not attributed to Israel's charm or to Egyptian generosity. The text says God had gone ahead of the request and changed how these people saw their slaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then a line about Moses himself: *a great man in that land, respected by Pharaoh's officials and by the people alike*. The man who had fled Egypt as a wanted murderer is, by the end, the most respected person in it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the silver was for.** Some of it ends up in the tabernacle a few chapters later — gold, silver, bronze, blue and purple yarn, brought as freewill offerings. And some of it ends up in the golden calf. The same wealth, out of the same pockets, going two directions."}]}, "Exodus 12:1–13": {"title": "\"When I see the blood\" — who the sign was for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most important sentence in the passage is the one that says who the mark was for, and it is not the person inside the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"When I see the blood\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *when you see it*. Not *when the destroyer sees it*. The seeing is God's, and the text says it twice, once at the start of the promise and once at the end — the second time as a plain repetition, as though the point were too important to risk being missed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the protection never depended on the confidence of the family behind the door. A household could have spent that night terrified and the blood would have done what blood does. The sign was addressed outward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is ruled out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage states the negative itself: *you are not spared by your goodness or your strength. You are spared because the blood is there, and I see it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had done nothing to deserve exemption; the plagues had fallen on Egypt and their own record over the following forty years is not flattering. Nothing about the household's quality is in view. Only whether the lamb had died and the blood was where it could be seen."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The details worth noticing"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**a lamb for a household** — and if a household is too small, share with the neighbour. It is reckoned by how many can eat, not by how much sin needs covering", "**without flaw**, and kept four days before it is killed — long enough to be looked over", "**eat it dressed to travel** — sandals on, staff in hand. They were to eat the meal in the posture of people already leaving", "**leave nothing until morning** — the whole lamb is consumed that night"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the tenth day of this month they shall take to them every man a lamb… and you shall keep it up **until the fourteenth day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four days in the house before it is killed. Long enough for a family to look it over, and for any blemish to show."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus rode into Jerusalem on the tenth of Nisan, went into the temple, and turned over the tables. From that morning to the Passover He is examined by one delegation after another — the chief priests and elders, the Pharisees and Herodians, the Sadducees, a lawyer — and each goes away with nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Pilate: *I find no fault in Him.* Herod finds none. See the note on Matthew 21:23–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The month starts here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This month is now the first of months for you, the very start of your year.* Before a single Israelite has walked out of Egypt, God resets the calendar. The year now begins at the lamb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a strange administrative detail to include in a rescue narrative, and it tells you how the event was to be reckoned: not one night among others, but the point everything is counted from."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word Passover.** *Pesach* — to pass over, and possibly also to hover or to shield. Both senses have been argued from the same root, and Isaiah uses the word of the LORD protecting Jerusalem like a bird over its nest. Either way the action belongs to God at the door, not to the people behind it."}]}, "Exodus 12:21–28": {"title": "\"He will not let the destroyer enter\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses passes the instructions to the elders, and the practical detail is what stands out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hyssop"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take **a bunch of hyssop**, dip it in the blood in the basin, and **touch the top and both sides of your doorframe** with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hyssop is a small bushy herb, common and worth nothing. It was the applicator — you could not do it with your hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same plant is used for cleansing a leper, and for the red heifer. David asks for it: *purge me with hyssop, and I shall be clean.* See the note on Psalms 51:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the cross: *they filled a sponge with sour wine, put it on hyssop, and put it to His mouth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three sides"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lintel and two doorposts. Top, left and right."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing on the threshold. Nobody was to walk on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction that is easy to miss"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **not one of you is to step outside his door until morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood is on the outside of the house. The safety is on the inside of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They spend the night indoors, eating, dressed to travel, while something goes through Egypt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who sees the mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When **He sees the blood** on the frame, He will pass over that door.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when you see it. The mark is addressed outward, and the seeing is God's. See the note on Exodus 12:1–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destroyer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **not let the destroyer enter** to strike your homes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew **mashchith** — the destroyer, the one who ruins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse describes God passing over the door and, in the same clause, not permitting something else through it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children's question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And when your children ask you, '**What does this ceremony mean to you?**' — tell them…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction assumes the rite will outlive the night and that children will ask about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That question is still asked at the Passover table — *ma nishtanah*, why is this night different — and it comes from this verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer they are given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is the Passover sacrifice to the LORD, **who passed over the houses** of the Israelites in Egypt, **and spared our homes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer is a piece of history, told in the first person plural, by people who were not there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the people did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the people **bowed low and worshiped**. Then the Israelites went and **did exactly as the LORD had commanded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No questions recorded. It is the first time in the book they simply do it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was outside that night.** The Egyptians had also been through nine plagues, and Exodus notes that by this point *the Egyptians urged the people to hurry out of the land.* The same night that emptied Egypt's houses filled Israel's road. See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}]}, "Exodus 12:33–36": {"title": "The wealth of Egypt", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They went in as guests, spent four centuries as slaves, and walked out carrying the treasury."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was doing the hurrying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Egyptians urged the people to hurry out** of the land, for they said, \"We will all die.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt is pushing them toward the door. Nobody has to negotiate a release any more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dough"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people took their dough **before the yeast was added**, wrapping their kneading bowls in their cloaks and carrying them on their shoulders.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where unleavened bread comes from. Not a symbol first and a practicality second — they left before it could rise, and the feast keeps the memory of the speed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Just as Moses had told them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **just as Moses had told them**, the Israelites asked the Egyptians for articles of silver and gold and for clothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was instructed in advance, in chapter 11, and before that it was promised to Abram four hundred years earlier: *afterward your people will come out with great wealth.* See the note on Genesis 15:12–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody improvised this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *asked* is the ordinary verb for making a request. And the Egyptians *gave them whatever they asked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the settlement of an account. Four hundred years of building store-cities — Pithom and Raamses — with no wages paid, and on one morning the books are balanced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose doing it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **LORD had made the Egyptians so favourable** toward His people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *gave the people favour in the eyes of the Egyptians* — **chen**, grace, the same word used over Esther in a Persian palace and over Noah before the flood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Israelites did not persuade anybody. They asked, and the disposition of a nation had already been changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some of it went into a golden calf a few chapters later. Most of it went into the tabernacle — the gold of the ark, the lampstand, the mercy seat, the boards overlaid, the silver sockets, the linen and the blue and purple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt's wages built the tent God met them in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm sums it up.** *He brought His people out with silver and gold, and not one of their tribes stumbled.* Wealthy and well, in one line — the morning after the lamb. See the note on Psalms 105:37–38."}]}, "Exodus 13:17–22": {"title": "The roundabout way", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first navigational decision of the exodus, and the reason given is about what they could take."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The road not taken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God **did not lead them along the road through Philistine country**, though it was **the shorter way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The coast road — the Via Maris — ran from Egypt up through Gaza into Canaan. It was the trade route and the army route, and it was about ten days' walk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses notes that it was shorter. The text does not pretend the long way was more efficient."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For God said, \"**If they face war, they may lose heart and turn back to Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *lest the people change their minds when they see war*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The short road was garrisoned by Egyptian forts and then by Philistine cities. They would have hit an army in the first week."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the reason is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the danger. Their readiness for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The decision is made about people who have been slaves for four hundred years and have never fought anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The route"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So God led the people **the roundabout way, through the wilderness toward the Red Sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a verb for turning aside, going about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The long way ends at a sea with an army behind them — which is where the deliverance happens, and where Exodus 14 says *the Egyptians whom you see today, you shall see again no more forever*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Israelites went up out of Egypt **ready for battle**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in orderly ranks*, or *armed*. They were not defenceless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were equipped and God routed them around the fight anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses **carried the bones of Joseph** with him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For Joseph had made the Israelites swear an oath: \"**God will surely come to you — carry my bones up with you from here**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years earlier, the second man in Egypt gave instructions about a coffin. See the note on Genesis 50:15–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody kept it, moved it, and knew where it was on the night they left. Joshua buries it at Shechem at the end of the conquest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guide"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And here is the wonder of it: **the LORD Himself went ahead of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By day in **a pillar of cloud** to lead the way, and by night in **a pillar of fire** to give light — **so they could travel by day or by night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two forms, one presence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cloud is shade in a desert and the fire is light in the dark. Each is what the hour needs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The constancy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Neither the pillar of cloud by day nor the pillar of fire by night **ever left its place in front of the people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses says it as a summary, and it holds for forty years — Nehemiah still mentions it centuries later: *You did not forsake them… the pillar of cloud did not depart from them by day.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Deuteronomy says about the forty years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went ahead of you on the journey, **searching out a place for you to camp**, in fire by night and in a cloud by day, **to show you the way to walk**.* See the note on Deuteronomy 1:26–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shortest route was available, known, and rejected — and the sentence Moses records is about the people's hearts rather than the road's condition."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the long way.** Not because the short one was blocked. Moses says God looked at what was on it and at who was walking, and turned them."}]}, "Exodus 14:10–14": {"title": "Stand still and see", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Water in front, an army behind, and Moses tells them to stop moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God had brought them there. Two chapters back He had told Moses to have them turn and camp by the sea — deliberately, in a place with no exit, *for Pharaoh will say, they are wandering in confusion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trap was arranged. They were exactly where they had been led."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Were there no graves in Egypt**, that you brought us to the wilderness to die?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is bitter and it is sarcastic. And then: *\"Better to serve them than to die in the desert.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Within days of coming out, they are asking to go back in. The mind had not left Egypt when the body did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had just done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were terrified **and cried out to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text records both. The terror and the crying out are in the same sentence, and neither is criticised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four instructions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be afraid. **Stand still**, and **watch** the salvation the LORD will work for you today… The **LORD will fight for you**. You need only to **be still**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is a command to stop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stand still* is to take a position and hold it — plant yourself. And *be still* is the verb for silence: hold your peace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are given nothing to do. Not a weapon, not a formation, not a plan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"These Egyptians you see today, **you will never see again**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rescue arrives at the last possible hour, with the chariots visible on the horizon. It could have come at any point in the previous three days. It comes when they can see the dust."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sea does not open until they are standing on the shore of it with nowhere else to be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line the Illumination adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The battle is not yours to win. God will fight, and you may rest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what Moses has just said, put plainly. And it is the whole difference between this passage and every strategy a frightened person reaches for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says to Moses next.** *Why are you crying out to Me? Tell the Israelites to go forward.* The stillness is not passivity — it is that the fighting is His. When the word comes to move, they move into a road that was not there a minute earlier."}]}, "Exodus 14:15–18": {"title": "\"Why are you crying out to Me?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Israel is trapped between an army and a sea, Moses is praying, and God tells him to stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why are you crying out to Me? Tell the Israelites to move forward.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chapter suggests Moses was praying wrongly. He had just told the people to stand still and see the salvation of the LORD, which is a fine thing to say."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But there was a word already given. Back at the burning bush, God had said He was bringing them out. The situation had not changed the instruction — it had only made it look impossible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is told to do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lift up your staff, stretch out your hand over the sea, and divide it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The staff is the one that had already turned into a snake, already struck the Nile, already brought hail. He is not being handed something new."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer is not rebuked so much as redirected: there is a time to ask, and a time to pick up what you were already given and use it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the dividing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction says *divide it* — spoken to Moses. Two verses on, the account says the LORD drove the sea back with a strong east wind all night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are true, and neither cancels the other. The man stretches out his hand; God moves the water. That is the pattern the whole book runs on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why God says He is doing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will gain honour through Pharaoh and all his army… Then the Egyptians will know that I am the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not only Israel's rescue. God names His own honour and Egypt's knowledge as the point — the rescue is real, and it is also a demonstration aimed at people watching."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line just before it.** *The LORD will fight for you; you need only to be still.* Moses says it, and God's next words are *tell them to move forward*. Both are in the same passage, four verses apart. Stillness and forward movement are not opposites here — the stillness is about who does the fighting."}]}, "Exodus 16:13–15": {"title": "\"What is it?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A nation wakes up to find breakfast on the ground and has no word for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dew first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the morning **a layer of dew lay all around** the camp. **When the dew lifted**, there on the surface of the desert lay something fine and flaky.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes wrapped. The dew is underneath and above it — Numbers adds *when the dew fell on the camp in the night, the manna fell on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was never in contact with the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Something **fine and flaky, delicate as frost** on the ground.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 11 adds the rest: *like coriander seed, pale like resin*, and it *tasted like something baked with oil.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Israelites looked at it and said to one another, \"**What is it?**\" — for they had **no idea what it was**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **man hu**. The question became the noun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For forty years they ate something whose name was a question, because nothing in their experience had a word for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Moses' answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is the bread the LORD has given you to eat.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not explain what it is either. He says where it came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rules attached to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the chapter is the arrangement, and every part of it is unusual:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**enough for one day** — gather a day's portion each day", "**it bred worms** if kept overnight, and stank", "**except on the sixth day**, when a double portion kept perfectly for the Sabbath", "*he who gathered much had nothing left over, and he who gathered little had no lack*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "That last line is the strangest. Whatever a man collected, it came out to an omer when it was measured."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it in 2 Corinthians 8, about the collection for Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it had to be daily"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing you could not do with manna was store it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A nation of former slaves, who had spent four hundred years with nothing secured, was fed by something that would not keep — every day, for forty years, and it stopped the day they ate the produce of Canaan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it was kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One omer of it was put in a golden pot and placed in the ark, beside Aaron's rod that budded and the tablets of the covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only manna that did not rot is the jar that went into the holiest place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Moses did not give you the bread from heaven**, but My Father gives you the true bread from heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"Your fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, and are dead.\"* See the note on John 6:47–51."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Revelation promises.** *To him who overcomes I will give some of the **hidden manna** to eat.* The jar in the ark, offered to the reader."}]}, "Exodus 16:31–34": {"title": "A jar to remember", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The bread is described, and then a sample of it is put away where nobody will eat it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Israel named it **manna**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **man hu** — *what is it?* Exodus says so earlier in the chapter: *when the children of Israel saw it, they said to one another, \"What is it?\" For they did not know what it was.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is the question they asked, and it stuck."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **white, like coriander seed**, and it **tasted like wafers made with honey**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers adds: *its taste was like the taste of pastry prepared with oil*, and that they ground it in mills, beat it in mortars, cooked it in pans and made cakes of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two descriptions of the same thing, and both are about how good it was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Keep an omer of it for the generations to come**, so they can **see the bread I fed you in the wilderness when I brought you out of Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An **omer** — about two litres, and the daily ration per person named earlier in the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One day's food, kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is strange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has just established that manna could not be kept. Anyone who left it overnight found it *full of worms and stank* — except on the sabbath eve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one jar is an exception to the rule the chapter spends twenty verses establishing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take a jar, put an omer of manna in it, and **set it before the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Aaron set it **before the Testimony**, to be preserved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Testimony is the ark with the tablets in it. The jar goes into the holiest place in Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews lists the contents: *the ark of the covenant… in which were **the golden pot that had the manna**, Aaron's rod that budded, and the tablets of the covenant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three objects: bread, a dead stick that flowered, and stone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why those three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each is a record of something the nation got wrong. The manna is the complaining in the wilderness, the rod is Korah's rebellion, and the tablets are what was broken over the calf."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark holds the evidence, and the lid over it is the mercy seat, with blood on it once a year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, and are dead. **This is the bread which comes down from heaven, that one may eat of it and not die**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**I am the living bread** which came down from heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the discourse that emptied the room. See the note on John 6:60–65."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Revelation says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To him who overcomes I will give some of **the hidden manna** to eat.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The jar in the ark, described as hidden — and offered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they complained about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 11: *\"**we remember the fish which we ate freely in Egypt**, the cucumbers, the melons, the leeks, the onions, and the garlic. But now our whole being is dried up; **there is nothing at all except this manna**.\"* See the note on Numbers 11:4–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same food, twenty chapters apart, described as honey wafers and as nothing at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the jar is.** A day's ration of bread that could not normally last a night, kept in a box in the holiest room in the nation — so that people who never ate it could see what their fathers were fed."}]}, "Exodus 17:4–7": {"title": "\"I myself will stand there before you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first time the rock is struck, and the detail everyone reads past is where God says He will be standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses cried out to the LORD, \"What am I to do with these people? **They're almost ready to stone me!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rephidim. No water, and the people have moved from complaining to picking up rocks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is told to take"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Carry in your hand **the staff you struck the Nile with**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not any staff. The one that turned a river to blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus makes the identification, so the reader knows what kind of instrument is about to be used."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where God will be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I myself will stand there before you, on the rock at Horeb**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *behold, I stand before you there upon the rock*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rock about to be struck is the place God says He will be standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is told to bring the rod of judgment and swing it at a rock with God on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Strike the rock, and water will pour out** for the people to drink.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *strike* is **nakah** — to hit, to smite, to strike down. It is the verb used of the plagues."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take **some of the elders of Israel** with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Moses did it, **right in front of the elders**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Named observers, so it could be attested."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Water came — **for thirsty people who had nothing to give in return**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people had just threatened to stone him. Nothing in the passage is offered, promised or repented of before the water arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He named the place **Massah and Meribah — Testing and Quarreling**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both names stick. Psalm 95 uses them as the standing example of a hardened heart, and Hebrews builds two chapters on the psalm. See the notes on Psalms 95:8–11 and Hebrews 4:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is the LORD among us or not?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what Exodus says they were really asking, and it is a strange question from people standing under a cloud that had led them there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-eight years later, at Kadesh, the same problem and a different instruction: *speak to the rock*. See the note on Numbers 20:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses struck it twice instead, and was told he would not enter the land. See the note on Numbers 20:12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says the rock was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They drank from a spiritual Rock that went with them, and **that Rock was Christ**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out of the pierced side"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of the soldiers pierced His side with a spear, and **at once blood and water came out**.* See the note on John 19:31–34."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause to notice.** *I will stand there before you, on the rock.* The thing being struck is the place God said He would be."}]}, "Exodus 17:8–13": {"title": "Hands held up until sundown", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Israel's first battle after the exodus, and it is won on a hilltop rather than in the valley."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Amalekites came and attacked Israel **at Rephidim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means *resting places*, or places of refreshment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just come through the sea and been given water out of a rock, and the attack lands at the place named for rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Amalek was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau's grandson, and Deuteronomy describes the method: they *met you on the road and cut down all the stragglers at the rear, when you were weary and worn out.* See the note on Deuteronomy 25:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They attack from behind, at the point of exhaustion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two assignments"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Choose some men and go out to fight** Amalek. **Tomorrow I will stand on top of the hill** with the staff of God in my hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua in the valley with a sword. Moses on the hill with a staff."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are given, and the passage is about the relationship between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correlation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As long as Moses **held his hands up, Israel prevailed**; but whenever he **let his hands down, Amalek prevailed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua's fighting is not what varies. The men, the swords and the tactics stay the same all day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only variable in the account is a pair of arms on a hill a mile away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to Moses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Moses' arms **grew tired**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is heavy. He is eighty, and it is an all-day battle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man holding up the staff of God cannot hold up his own arms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stone and the two men"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They took a stone and put it under him, and **he sat on it**, while **Aaron and Hur held up his hands — one on each side** — so his hands **stayed steady until the sun went down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three details, and each one matters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sits. The posture changes from standing to seated, and the account does not treat that as a failure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men hold the hands up. Not his own strength, and not one helper — one on each side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *stayed steady* — the Hebrew is **emunah**, the word for faithfulness, steadiness. It is the root of *amen*, and it is used here of a pair of hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Joshua **overcame the Amalekite army** with the sword.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are true. The sword did the work in the valley, and the hands on the hill decided it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses builds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses built an altar and called its name **The-LORD-Is-My-Banner**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew **Yahweh-Nissi**. A banner is what an army rallies to and fights under."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *the LORD will have war with Amalek from generation to generation.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thing that cannot be won by effort"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek is the one enemy the Old Testament says will be fought in every generation. It is not defeated once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul was told to finish it and did not, and an Amalekite is standing over him on Gilboa. Haman the Agagite is signing a decree centuries later. See the notes on 2 Samuel 1:5–10 and Esther 3:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one enemy that is never beaten by effort is beaten here under raised hands somebody else was holding."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the day required.** A young man fighting, an old man seated on a stone, and two friends who stood there from morning until sundown holding his arms. Exodus names all four."}]}, "Exodus 19:7–9": {"title": "\"We will do everything the LORD has said\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A nation makes a promise, and the whole shape of the next fifteen hundred years turns on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God had just offered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *you have seen what I did to the Egyptians, and how **I bore you on eagles' wings and brought you to Myself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the account of everything up to this point. Grace, from the blood on the doorposts to the sea, the manna, and the water out of the rock. Nothing had been required of them and nothing had been withheld."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And all the people answered **together, as one**: \"**We will do everything the LORD has said.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is emphatic — *all that the LORD has spoken we will do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They say it before they have heard what it is. The commandments are not given until chapter 20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They said the same again after Sinai — *all the words which the LORD has said we will do* — and a third time when the covenant was ratified with blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Six weeks later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A golden calf, while Moses was still up the mountain collecting the tablets. See the note on Deuteronomy 9:9–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What changed at that moment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before this exchange, God dealt with them on the basis of a promise made to Abraham. After it, on the basis of an agreement they proposed to keep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The arrangement they accepted is the one Paul describes: *the man who does those things shall live by them.* See the note on Romans 10:5–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cloud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am coming to you in a dense cloud**, so that the people will hear Me speaking with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a thick cloud, a heavy darkness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what follows in the chapter is a mountain fenced off, with a boundary and a death penalty for touching it, thunder and lightning and a trumpet blast that grew louder and louder, and a mountain shaking and smoking like a furnace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the people trembled.* They ask Moses to speak instead: *let not God speak with us, lest we die.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Moses **told the LORD what the people had said**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He carries their answer up. The verse says it twice — he brought their words back — and the repetition is the writer's way of marking the moment as decisive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Hebrews sets it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have not come to the mountain that may be touched and that burned with fire, and to blackness and darkness and tempest… **But you have come to Mount Zion**, to the city of the living God… to Jesus the Mediator of the new covenant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two mountains, and Hebrews says Christians are standing at the other one. See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was on the mountain before the law.** *I bore you on eagles' wings and brought you to Myself.* That sentence was already true, without any promise from them, and it stayed true — the covenant they proposed did not create the relationship, and breaking it did not end it."}]}, "Exodus 2:1–10": {"title": "Drawn from the water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The decree said put him in the Nile. She put him in the Nile."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Obedience of a kind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh's order was that every Hebrew boy be cast into the river. His mother hides the child three months, and when she cannot hide him any longer she does exactly what the law required — and seals him first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The basket"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A basket of **papyrus**, sealed with **tar and pitch**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word for the basket is **tebah**. It occurs in only one other place in the whole Bible: Noah's ark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the ordinary word for a box or a chest. The word for the vessel that carried one family through the water while everything else went under, used again for a container the length of a baby."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *sealed with tar and pitch* is what Noah was told to do to his — *cover it inside and out with pitch.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she put it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Among the reeds along the bank of the Nile.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for reeds is **suph** — the same word in *Yam Suph*, the Sea of Reeds, which is the sea that will open for this child eighty years later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is drawn out of the reeds, and he brings a nation out through the reeds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who comes down to the river"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Pharaoh's daughter** came down to bathe at the river.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of everyone in Egypt who could have found that basket, it is found by the daughter of the man who signed the order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her heart went out to him.* And she names the problem out loud: *\"This is one of the Hebrew children.\"* She knows precisely what she is looking at and takes him anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sister"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The baby's sister **stepped forward**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Miriam has been standing at a distance watching. She waits for the right moment and speaks to a princess."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Shall I go and find a Hebrew woman to nurse the child for you?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the mother got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take this child and nurse him for me, **and I will pay you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is handed back her own son, with the protection of the palace over him, and Egypt pays her wages to raise him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had put him in the river because the law said so. She gets him back, legally, salaried, and safe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She named him **Moses**, saying, \"I drew him out of the water.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Egyptian princess gives him a Hebrew name, or one that works in both languages. And it becomes the name of a man whose whole life is drawing people out of water."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the deliverance arrived.** No angel, no plague, no army. A basket, a princess taking a bath, a girl brave enough to speak, and a river the empire worshipped. God is named in this chapter only at the very end, when He hears the groaning — and by then He has already had the deliverer raised in Pharaoh's own house for forty years."}]}, "Exodus 20:18–21": {"title": "The people stood far off", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The law has just been spoken, and the first recorded response of the nation is to back away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they experienced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the people saw **the thunder and lightning**, heard **the trumpet**, and saw **the mountain wrapped in smoke**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they saw the voices* — the same word for thunder and for voice. They saw the sound."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **trembled and stood far off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they moved and stood at a distance*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters earlier they had been told to wash their clothes and be ready on the third day. They came, and then went backward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You speak to us, and we will listen. But don't let God speak to us, or we will die**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask for a middleman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the origin of the office of the prophet — Moses recalls it in Deuteronomy, and God answers it there: *I will raise up for them a Prophet like you from among their brethren, and will put My words in His mouth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had just heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ten commandments, spoken aloud by God to the whole nation. It is the only time in Scripture He addresses an entire people directly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked Him not to do it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Moses' answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't be afraid**. God has come **to test you**, so that **the fear of him will keep you from sinning**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two kinds of fear in one sentence, and Moses distinguishes them: do not be afraid, so that the fear of Him would stay with you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew uses the same root twice — *do not fear*, *that His fear may be before your faces*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two directions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the people stayed at a distance**, while **Moses drew near** to the thick darkness where God was.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, two movements, opposite ways."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *thick darkness* is **araphel** — heavy cloud, gloom. It is the word used of the cloud on Sinai and of the darkness God is said to dwell in: *He made darkness His secret place.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses walked into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing the nation was certain would kill them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Hebrews takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have not come to the mountain that may be touched and that burned with fire, and to blackness and darkness and tempest… **For they could not endure what was commanded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you have come to Mount Zion… to God the Judge of all, and to Jesus the Mediator of the new covenant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two mountains, and the letter says which one its readers are standing at. See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the veil later did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle formalised the distance the people asked for here — a curtain, a court, a priest, one man once a year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Matthew records the curtain torn from top to bottom."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was on offer and what was asked for.** God spoke to the whole nation once, and the nation asked Him to stop and send somebody instead. Moses went in."}]}, "Exodus 20:3": {"title": "The first word", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the ten, and it is the one every other commandment sits on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the LORD your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That sentence is verse 2, and Jewish tradition counts it as the first of the ten words — a statement rather than a command."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rescue is announced before anything is asked. The commands follow an introduction of who is speaking and what He has already done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall have **no other gods before me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **al panai** — before My face, in My presence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *ahead of Me in rank*. In My sight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Illumination adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **no rival stand where I stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **the God who came for you**; keep your heart turned toward me alone.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else in the list depends on it. Murder, theft, adultery and false witness are recognisable wrongs in any society; this one is the claim that makes the list a covenant rather than a legal code."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **ten words** — Hebrew **aseret hadevarim**, which is where *Decalogue* comes from. Exodus and Deuteronomy both call them words rather than laws."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who heard them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole nation, spoken aloud. It is the only time in Scripture God addresses an entire people directly, and their response was to ask Him to stop. See the note on Exodus 20:18–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Two tablets of stone, **written by the very finger of God**.* See the note on Exodus 31:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they were broken before they reached the camp, because of what the nation was doing at the foot of the mountain while the writing was happening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the nation had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All that the LORD has spoken **we will do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said before they knew what would be spoken. See the note on Exodus 19:7–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus puts the first commandment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asked which is the greatest, He does not quote Exodus 20. He quotes Deuteronomy 6:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You shall love the LORD your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind**. This is the first and great commandment.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same content, stated as love rather than prohibition. See the note on Matthew 22:34–40."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rich young ruler was asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus listed five commandments to him — do not commit adultery, do not murder, do not steal, do not bear false witness, honour your parents."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All five from the second table. The first table was not recited, and the one thing he was told he lacked was about what he was holding on to. See the note on Luke 18:18–23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul locates the problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **exchanged the truth of God for the lie**, and worshipped and served the creature rather than the Creator.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts it at the head of his indictment in Romans 1, in the same position it holds here."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sentence is about.** Not a ranking. The Hebrew is *before My face* — and the verse before it says who the face belongs to and what He had just done."}]}, "Exodus 21:1": {"title": "Rules set before them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse of heading, and it introduces the oldest legal code in Scripture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are **the rulings God gave Moses to set before the people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mishpatim** — judgments, decisions. The word comes from the root for judging, and it means case law: rulings on situations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The block that follows is called *the book of the covenant* in chapter 24, and it runs from here to the end of chapter 23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Directly after the ten commandments and the people's request that God stop speaking to them. See the note on Exodus 20:18–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ten were spoken to the whole nation. These are given to Moses to set before them, which is the arrangement the people asked for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To set before them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *which you shall place before them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not imposed from a distance. Laid out in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of law it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost all of it is casuistic — *if a man does this, then that*. It reads like a collection of decided cases rather than a list of principles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ten commandments are apodictic: *you shall not*. These are the working-out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing after this verse is the Hebrew servant who may go free in the seventh year and chooses not to — the man whose ear is pierced at the doorpost. See the note on Exodus 21:2–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: injuries, an ox that gores, a pit left uncovered, theft, fire in a field, borrowed property, a garment taken in pledge, bribes, the sabbath year, the sabbath day, and three annual feasts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Oxen, pits, fires and coats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that says about the code"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first case in it is a slave being offered his freedom, and the last chapter of it is about resting the land so the poor can eat and resting the household so the animals and the foreigner can breathe. See the notes on Exodus 23:10–11 and Exodus 23:12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bookends are both about people with no power."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word *plain*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination calls them *plain, fair guidelines for how they were to live justly with one another*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The horizontal direction is right — the ten commandments open toward God and this block is almost entirely about the neighbour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens at the end of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 24: Moses reads the book of the covenant in the hearing of the people, and they say *all that the LORD has said we will do, and be obedient.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then blood is thrown on the people and on the altar: *this is the blood of the covenant which the LORD has made with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews cites that moment: *not even the first covenant was dedicated without blood.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ox-and-pit laws became"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus argued from them twice on sabbath days — *which one of you, having a donkey or an ox that has fallen into a pit, will not immediately pull him out?* See the note on Luke 14:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The case law about a pit in Exodus 21 is what He reaches for when the lawyers cannot answer him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the heading says the code is for.** Not ceremony and not devotion. Rulings, set in front of the people, about how they were to deal with each other."}]}, "Exodus 21:2–6": {"title": "The servant who would not go free", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first civil law given after the ten commandments, and it is about setting somebody free."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is to serve **six years**, and in the seventh year **he goes free, owing nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he goes out free *for nothing* — no redemption price, no debt carried over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very first case law in Israel's legal code is a limit on how long a man can be owned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complication"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If his master **gave him a wife** and she bore him sons or daughters, **the wife and children stay with the master**, and he goes out by himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the hard clause, and it is the one the whole passage turns on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Freedom is available, and it is available alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The choice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the servant says plainly, \"**I love my master, my wife, and my children; I do not want to go free**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three loves named, and the master is first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he says it plainly — the verb implies a declaration, said out loud, so that everyone hears it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ceremony"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His master brings him **before God**, brings him **to the door or the doorpost**, and **pierces his ear with an awl**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three elements: God as witness, a doorway, and a permanent mark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ear is pierced against the door of the house he is choosing to stay in. He is literally fastened, for a moment, to the place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it makes him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From then on the servant belongs to that household **for life, by his own glad choice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **bondservant** — the Hebrew and Greek both distinguish this from a slave. He was free, and chose not to be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul opens his letters *Paul, a bondservant of Jesus Christ.* So do James, Peter and Jude. It is a title they claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm picks it up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sacrifice and offering You did not desire. **My ears You have opened**… Then I said, **see, I have come**… **I delight to do Your will**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 40, and the Hebrew is *ears You have dug for me* — the piercing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes it and applies it to Christ coming into the world: *a body You have prepared for Me… behold, I have come to do Your will, O God.* See the note on Psalms 40:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two loves in the picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servant stays for the master and for the family he would otherwise leave behind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For even the Son of Man **did not come to be served, but to serve**, and to give His life a ransom for many.\"* See the note on Mark 10:41–45."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the law says anything about a servant's hands being marked. It is the ear — the part that hears an order."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the marks ended up.** *Behold My hands and My feet… reach your finger here, and look at My hands; and reach your hand here, and put it into My side.* The permanent marks of the one who would not go out free."}]}, "Exodus 21:28–32": {"title": "Thirty shekels of silver", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A law about a dangerous animal, and the last figure in it turns up again at a betrayal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If an ox gores a man or woman to death, **the ox is to be stoned** and its meat not eaten, and **the owner is cleared**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The animal is executed and the owner goes free. Nobody could have foreseen it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The meat is not eaten — the animal is not to be salvaged as property."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **if the ox had a habit of goring** and its owner **had been warned yet did not keep it penned**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two conditions: a known pattern, and a warning already given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the ox is stoned and its owner too is to be put to death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The negligence is treated as capital."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ransom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **a ransom is set for him**, he may **pay whatever is required to redeem his life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kopher** — a ransom, a covering price. It shares a root with **kaphar**, to atone, and with **kapporeth**, the mercy seat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A life is forfeit and a payment is accepted instead. That is the only place in the law where a capital sentence can be commuted for money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The equality clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The same ruling holds whether the ox gores **a son or a daughter**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if the ox gores **a manservant or a maidservant**, the owner pays **thirty shekels of silver** to the servant's master, and the ox is stoned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A slave was somebody's property, so the compensation goes to the owner and the figure is fixed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty shekels is the valuation of a slave in this law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the figure turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah 11, in the strange passage where the prophet acts out a rejected shepherd and asks for his wages:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they weighed out for my wages **thirty pieces of silver**. And the LORD said to me, \"**Throw it to the potter**\" — that princely price they set on me. So I took the thirty pieces of silver and **threw them into the house of the LORD for the potter**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tone is contempt. It is what you pay for a slave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then one of the twelve, called Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests and said, \"**What are you willing to give me if I deliver Him to you?**\" And they counted out to him **thirty pieces of silver**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And afterward: *he threw down the pieces of silver in the temple and departed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests would not put it in the treasury *because it is the price of blood*, and bought **the potter's field** with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes Zechariah's line and says it was fulfilled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the price says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the room negotiated. The chief priests named the sum, and the sum was the statutory compensation for a dead slave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Philippians says about the form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who, being in the form of God… **made Himself of no reputation, taking the form of a bondservant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **doulos** — a slave."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The figure in the law.** Thirty shekels, for a servant killed by somebody else's animal, paid to the owner. It is the only price in the Torah for a human life, and it is the one that was counted out in the temple."}]}, "Exodus 21:33–34": {"title": "The pit somebody left open", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses of ordinary liability law, and Jesus argues from them twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a man **opens a pit, or digs one and does not cover it**, and **an ox or a donkey falls in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cisterns were cut into rock across that country to hold winter rain. An uncovered one is an invisible hole in the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law names two acts — opening an existing pit and digging a new one — and treats them the same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the fault is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the digging. Pits were necessary; every household needed water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fault is in leaving it open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The liability"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **owner of the pit must make it good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall make it good* — **shalam**, from the root of **shalom**. To make whole, to complete, to restore what is missing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Restitution in this law is described as putting something back to wholeness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The settlement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **pays the animal's owner in silver**, and **the dead animal becomes his**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves. He pays full value and takes the carcass, so the loss transfers entirely and the injured party is not left holding a dead ox."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the surrounding law is like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter runs on cases like this: a goring ox, an ox that has gored before, an ox killed by another ox, a pit, a fire in a field, a borrowed animal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Farm accidents, with rulings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If your donkey, or your ox, fell into a well on the Sabbath**, which one of you would leave him down there? **You would have him out that very moment**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to a room full of lawyers who had just watched Him heal a man on the sabbath and could not answer whether it was allowed. See the note on Luke 14:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in Matthew: *\"What man is there among you who has one sheep, and **if it falls into a pit on the Sabbath, will not lay hold of it and lift it out?** Of how much more value then is a man than a sheep?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument's shape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes a piece of livestock law they all knew and applies it upward: *of how much more value then is a man*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their own practice on the day in question is the premise. Nobody in the room disputed that they would pull the animal out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two animals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ox and the donkey appear together throughout the code — in this law, in the sabbath command, in the rule about a neighbour's beast fallen under its load."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the working animals of a household. See the note on Exodus 23:12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the pit law assumes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That somebody made the hole. The law does not deal with an animal that wandered off and died; it deals with a hazard left uncovered by a person who knew it was there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the ruling requires.** Full value in silver, and the carcass taken off the owner's hands. The Hebrew for making it good is the word for making whole — and it is the law Jesus reaches for when they will not answer him."}]}, "Exodus 22:7–9": {"title": "The one God declares guilty repays double", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A small piece of civil law about stolen property, and the word *double* appears twice in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book of the covenant — the block of case law that follows the ten commandments. The surrounding rules are about oxen, wells, borrowed animals, and fire spreading through a field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ordinary disputes, decided in ordinary courts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a man **entrusts his neighbor with money or goods for safekeeping**, and **they are stolen** from that neighbor's house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bailment. Something is left with somebody for keeping and disappears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "If the thief is found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then if the thief is found, **he repays double**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shenayim** — twofold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Restitution in the law is nearly always more than the loss. Elsewhere in the same chapter it runs to four and five times for an ox or a sheep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The principle is that theft costs the thief more than it cost the owner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "If he is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **owner of the house is brought before God** to swear he did not lay hands on his neighbor's property.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *brought to the judges*, and the word is **elohim** — the same word for God, used of those who judge in His name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oath settles it when the evidence cannot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The general rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In every case of dispute — over **an ox, a donkey, a sheep, clothing, or any lost thing that one claims is his**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *that one claims is his* is *of which one says, \"This is it\"* — a man pointing at an object and saying it belongs to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The procedure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Both parties **bring their case before God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither side is presumed. Both come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the one God declares guilty repays his neighbor double**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *declares guilty* is **rasha** — to condemn, to pronounce wicked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb appears twice in the passage: once as what the court does, and once naming the party it lands on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ruling produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody is condemned, and the other party ends up with twice what he lost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two outcomes are the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where doubling turns up in the prophets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead of your shame you shall have **double honour**, and instead of confusion they shall rejoice in their portion. Therefore in their land **they shall possess double**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 61, in the chapter Jesus read in Nazareth. See the note on Isaiah 61:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Zechariah: *return to the stronghold, you prisoners of hope. Even today I declare that **I will restore double** to you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job received"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Indeed the LORD **gave Job twice as much as he had before**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book counts it: fourteen thousand sheep against seven, six thousand camels against three, and the same doubling through the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the accuser stands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah 3 has Joshua the high priest in filthy garments *and Satan standing at his right hand to oppose him*, and the LORD saying *the LORD rebuke you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *oppose* is **satan** — to accuse, to stand against in court."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Proverbs says about the reverse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Clearing the guilty and condemning the just, **the LORD hates them both alike**.* See the note on Proverbs 17:15–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both errors, equally."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape of the law.** Two parties come, God decides, and the party He condemns pays twice. The restoration and the condemnation are one ruling."}]}, "Exodus 23:10–11": {"title": "Rest for the land", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The shortest statement of the sabbath year, and the reason attached to it is about somebody else's stomach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of the book of the covenant — the block of case law in Exodus 21 to 23, given straight after the ten commandments."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The laws around it are about oxen, wells, borrowed property, bribes and lawsuits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For six years **sow your land and gather its harvest**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seventh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But in the seventh year **let it rest and lie fallow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has two verbs — **shamat**, to release, to let drop, and **natash**, to abandon, to leave alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shamat** is the root of **shemittah**, the name the sabbath year still carries. It is also the word in Deuteronomy 15 for the release of debts in the same year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Letting the field go and letting the debt go are the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the poor among your people may eat from it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus gives the theological account — the land keeps a Sabbath to the LORD. See the note on Leviticus 25:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus gives the practical one, and it is about the people with no field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **whatever they leave, the wild animals may have**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is: the owner does not touch it, the poor eat, and what is left goes to the animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The owner is the only party in the sentence who gets nothing that year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The extension"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do the same with **your vineyard and your olive grove**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Vines and olives, not just grain. Both are long-term crops and both need pruning; leaving them a year costs more than leaving a field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Six days you shall do your work, and **on the seventh day you shall rest, that your ox and your donkey may rest, and the son of your female servant and the stranger may be refreshed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same shape at the scale of a week — and again the reason given is other people and animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land gets one year in seven; the household gets one day in seven."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is unusual in the ancient world"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fallowing itself was known — leaving ground to recover is ordinary agriculture. What is unusual is fixing it to a calendar, releasing the produce to the public, and grounding it in a command rather than in soil science."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "2 Chronicles counts the exile as the sabbaths the land was owed: *as long as she lay desolate she kept Sabbath, to fulfil seventy years.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy years of exile, one for each sabbath year not kept — which puts the neglect at four hundred and ninety years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the release goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 15, the same seventh year: *every creditor who has lent anything to his neighbour **shall release it**… **there shall be no poor among you**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two clauses.** Let it rest, and the poor eat. Exodus puts the fallow year and the food bank in one sentence and does not separate them."}]}, "Exodus 23:12": {"title": "\"So your ox and your donkey may rest\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sabbath commandment restated, and this version gives the reason in terms of somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after the sabbath year — six years of sowing and a seventh of letting the land go, so the poor may eat. See the note on Exodus 23:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same shape at two scales: one year in seven for the ground, one day in seven for the household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Six days do your work, but **on the seventh day rest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shabath** — to cease, to stop. The word is about stopping rather than about resting up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **your ox and your donkey may rest**, and **the son of your maidservant and the foreigner may be refreshed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four parties named, and the householder is not one of them."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**The ox and the donkey** — the working animals", "**The son of your maidservant** — a household slave born in the house", "**The foreigner** — the resident alien, with no legal standing"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list runs from livestock to the people with the least claim on the household. Every one of them is somebody who could not have taken a day off on their own account."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for refreshed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **naphash** — from **nephesh**, the soul, the breath. To take breath, to be re-souled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is used three times in the Old Testament. One of them is of God: *on the seventh day He rested and **was refreshed**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason in the other version"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 20 grounds the sabbath in creation: *for in six days the LORD made the heavens and the earth… therefore the LORD blessed the Sabbath day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 5 grounds it in the exodus: *remember that you were a slave in the land of Egypt… **therefore** the LORD your God commanded you to keep the Sabbath day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this one grounds it in the ox and the slave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three statements of the same command with three different reasons attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Illumination adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This rest is a gift, meant to **breathe life back into everyone under your care**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not framed as an obligation discharged. Nothing in this verse is about worship, assembly or sacrifice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a labour law, and the beneficiaries are the ones with no power to claim it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said about the day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He healed on it repeatedly, arguing each time from what people would do for an animal on that day — an ox led to water, a donkey pulled out of a well. See the notes on Luke 13:10–17 and Luke 14:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is arguing from this verse. The animals are in the commandment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with the rest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There remains therefore **a Sabbath rest for the people of God**. Anyone who has entered His rest has stopped working himself, the way God stopped when His own work was done.* See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is missing from the verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The owner. He is the one being commanded and the only one whose benefit is not mentioned."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the command protects.** Not the householder's leisure. Two animals, a slave's son, and a foreigner — the four in the house who could not have stopped without being told to."}]}, "Exodus 25:10–16": {"title": "Wood and gold, and poles that never come out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first thing God tells Moses to build is not the tent. It is the box that goes inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Start with the ark.* Before the table, the lampstand, the curtains, the courtyard — the instructions begin at the innermost point and work outward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The building starts where God will sit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two materials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Acacia wood** — the hard, durable timber of the desert, the only usable wood they had out there. And **pure gold**, inside and out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wood from the wilderness where they were, and gold, which in Scripture is the metal of what is divine and imperishable. The wood is not replaced by the gold or hidden by it. Both are in the finished object, inseparably."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have long seen the two natures of Christ in that combination, and the instruction to overlay *inside and out* is part of why: gold on the visible surface and gold on the surface nobody would ever see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The poles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Slide them through the rings on the sides so the ark can be lifted and carried. Leave the poles in the rings — **they never come out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An odd, specific rule. The poles are not to be removed even when the ark is at rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The practical effect is that the ark is permanently ready to move. Israel was about to spend forty years going where the cloud went, and the piece of furniture God sits over is built to travel at any hour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had to prepare it. It was always packed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What went inside.** The Testimony — the tablets. Hebrews adds two more items that ended up in there: the jar of manna and Aaron's staff that budded. The bread He supplied, the priesthood He chose, and the law they broke, in one box, under a lid of mercy."}]}, "Exodus 25:17–22": {"title": "The mercy seat, and the place He chose to speak from", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God gives the design for the ark and then tells Moses where He will be found."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the lid was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kapporet** — from the verb to cover, to atone. It was not a decorative top. The lid *was* the place of atonement, which is why English has long called it the mercy seat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pure gold, hammered from one piece with the cherubim — not assembled. The angels and the covering are the same object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Inside put the Testimony I'll give you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stone tablets. The commandments Israel had already broken before Moses got down the mountain lay inside the box, and the mercy seat lay over them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the arrangement: the law underneath, the covering above, and the blood sprinkled on the covering once a year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the cherubim are looking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Facing each other, their eyes turned down toward the cover.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last time cherubim appeared they were at the gate of Eden with a flaming sword, guarding the way back. Here they are bent over a lid, looking down at the place where blood is applied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same creatures, different posture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that is the point of the whole passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And this is where I'll meet you. From above that mercy seat, from the space between the two cherubim… Right there — that's where I choose to draw near and be heard.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from the altar where the sacrifices burned, and not from the tablets. From the space above the covering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God names the address at which He will speak, and it is the one place in the tabernacle where mercy sits on top of the broken law."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul calls Jesus.** In Romans 3 he uses the same word — *hilasterion*, the term the Greek Old Testament uses for this lid. God set Him forth publicly as the mercy seat. What was hidden behind a curtain and visited once a year is the thing Paul says was displayed in the open."}]}, "Exodus 25:23–30": {"title": "The table of the Presence", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A dining table in the holy place, and the food on it is never described as being for God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is made of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A table of **acacia wood**… **overlay it with pure gold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wood inside, gold outside — the same construction as the ark. The desert timber that does not rot, wrapped in the metal that does not tarnish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Build **a rim around it a handbreadth wide**, with its own gold molding on top of that.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A raised lip, about four inches, running round the edge, with a border on top of that as well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is there so nothing on the table can slide off. Whatever is set on it stays on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The poles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Four gold rings… **to hold the poles for carrying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The table was made to travel. Every piece of tabernacle furniture has rings and poles, because the whole thing moved whenever the cloud lifted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bread"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And on this table, **always — day after day — keep the bread of the Presence set out before Me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *bread of the face* — bread of the Presence, before His face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve loaves, arranged in two rows, replaced every Sabbath, with frankincense set alongside. One loaf for each tribe, including the ones that would later be lost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Always.* The table was never bare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus says: *it shall be Aaron's and his sons', and they shall eat it in a holy place.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bread sits before God for a week and is then eaten by the priests. Nothing is burned; nothing is destroyed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a meal in God's presence, with God's people at the table, in the room next to the ark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up in the Gospels"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David and his men ate it when they were hungry and had nothing — *he entered the house of God and ate the showbread, which was not lawful for him to eat, nor for those who were with him, but only for the priests.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus brings that incident up Himself, to defend His disciples for picking grain on the Sabbath, and adds: *the Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He called Himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am the bread of life.* And: *I am the living bread which came down from heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The table with bread on it, permanently, before God's face, in a room lit only by the lampstand. See the note on Exodus 25:31–40."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern in that room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things stood in the holy place: the lampstand, the table of bread, and the altar of incense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Light, food, and prayer — and a curtain, with the ark on the other side of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jesus said He was living on.** *My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me, and to finish His work.* Said in Samaria, to disciples who had gone to buy lunch, while a woman ran back to the town."}]}, "Exodus 25:31–40": {"title": "The golden lampstand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only source of light in the holy place, and it is described as one object rather than seven."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Hammered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A lampstand of pure gold, **hammered out** — base, shaft, cups… **all of one solid piece**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **miqshah** — beaten work. It was not cast in a mould or assembled from parts. A talent of solid gold, worked with a hammer until it became this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Buds and branches and shaft — **all one seamless piece**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stress on its oneness is repeated three times in the passage. Six branches coming out of a central shaft, and the whole thing is one piece of metal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "No wood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else in the tabernacle is acacia wood overlaid with gold — the ark, the table, the altar. The lampstand alone is gold all the way through, with no other substance in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Almond blossoms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cups shaped like **almond blossoms**, buds and petals.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-two of them in total, up the shaft and along the branches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The almond is the first tree in that country to flower, before its own leaves and before anything else. Jeremiah's first vision is an almond branch, and God tells him *I am watching over My word to perform it* — a play on the Hebrew, since the word for almond is *shaqed*, the watcher."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Aaron's rod that budded was an almond rod. It was dead wood that blossomed overnight, and it was kept in the ark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The light goes forward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set them up so their **light spills forward**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that the lamps give light *over against* the lampstand — the flames are angled to illuminate what is in front, not the stand itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstand exists to light the room and the table of showbread opposite it, not to be looked at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in that room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were no windows in the holy place. No natural light reached it at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever a priest did in there — the bread, the incense — he did by this light and no other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The talent of gold"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Use **a full talent of pure gold** for the lampstand and all its tools.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Around seventy-five pounds of gold, worked by hand into one object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I turned around… and there they were: **seven lampstands, all of them gold**. In among them stood **a figure like the Son of man**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation 1. Seven separate lampstands now — the seven churches — and He is standing in the middle of them. See the note on Revelation 1:12–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one that was beaten out of a single piece has become seven, with Him among them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line of the instructions.** *Be sure you make it all **to the pattern you were shown on the mountain**.* Said twice in this chapter. Hebrews picks it up: the priests serve *the copy and shadow of the heavenly things*. Moses was shown something and told to reproduce it."}]}, "Exodus 26:15–25": {"title": "The frames and the silver sockets", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The walls of the tabernacle, and what they stand on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are made of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Upright frames of **acacia wood** to hold the tabernacle upright.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acacia is the desert tree — hard, thorny, slow-growing, and it does not rot. It was the only usable timber in that wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each frame is *ten cubits tall and a cubit and a half wide* — about fifteen feet high and just over two across."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elsewhere in the chapter they are overlaid with gold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pegs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **two pegs at the bottom set side by side** for joining — every frame made the same way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is two hands, two tenons. Each board has exactly two, and they are what hold it in place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they stand in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Resting on **forty silver bases — two bases under each frame, one for each peg**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not driven into the sand. Every board sits in its own pair of silver sockets, and nothing touches the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ninety-six sockets in total, one under each tenon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the silver came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 30 and 38 give it. Every man twenty and over paid a half-shekel at the census:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The rich shall not give more and the poor shall not give less** than half a shekel, when you give an offering to the LORD, **to make atonement for yourselves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is called *the atonement money*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Exodus 38 records what was done with it: *the hundred talents of silver were for casting the sockets of the sanctuary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The foundation the whole tent stands on is redemption money, at a flat rate, from every man alike."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The equal price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rich paid the same as the poor. Nobody had a larger share of the foundation, and nobody had a smaller one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same idea in his own vocabulary: *you were not redeemed with corruptible things, like silver or gold… but with the precious blood of Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The frames, and what they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Desert wood, cut, squared, covered in gold, standing upright in silver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the timber is impressive on its own. It is what it is standing in that holds it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty boards on the south, twenty on the north, and eight at the back — with the two corner frames doubled and fitted into a single ring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty-eight frames, held by ninety-six sockets, carrying the curtains that made the one place on earth God said He would meet people."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it was all designed to hold up.** Ten curtains of fine linen, blue, purple and scarlet, with cherubim worked into them — and behind the veil, the ark. The boards are structure. Nobody was meant to be looking at them."}]}, "Exodus 28:36–38": {"title": "The gold plate on the forehead", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last item in the high priest's clothing, and it exists to solve a problem nobody usually thinks about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make a plate of pure gold and engrave on it, as on a seal: HOLY TO THE LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Worn on the forehead, tied with a blue cord to the front of the turban. The first thing anyone saw when the high priest faced them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem it addresses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That way Aaron carries the weight of any guilt tied to the sacred gifts the Israelites set apart, whatever their holy offerings may be.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read that again. There was guilt attached to **the holy offerings themselves** — the gifts people brought, the worship they gave. Even the best thing Israel brought God needed something to cover it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a startling admission built into the system. Not only their sins, but their sacrifices, required a bearer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who carried it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron. On his forehead. Continuously — *keep it always on his forehead*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest wore the guilt of the nation's worship and the words HOLY TO THE LORD in the same place, at the same time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the people and their gifts are welcomed before the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is about acceptance — being received with favour. The plate was not decoration. It was the mechanism by which imperfect worship from imperfect people was made welcome."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it matters for prayer.** Hebrews argues at length that we now have a great high priest who has gone into heaven itself and appears before God on our account. What Aaron wore on a gold plate, the letter says Christ *is* — which means the acceptance of what we bring never depended on the quality of the bringing. See the notes on Hebrews 9:23–24 and Hebrews 4:14–16."}]}, "Exodus 3:13–15": {"title": "\"I AM\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses asks for a name and gets one that is a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They ask me, 'What is his name?' **What shall I tell them?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A reasonable question in that world. Every nation had gods with names, and a name was what you had to know in order to call on one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I AM WHO I AM.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ehyeh asher ehyeh** — the verb *to be*, first person, twice, with *who* between them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a description of anything. It is not tied to a place, a function, a mountain, a season or an element, which is what every other name in the ancient world was tied to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the short form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Say to the Israelites: **'I AM has sent me to you.'**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes His own sentence and gives Moses one word out of it to carry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the third form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Say to the Israelites, '**the LORD**, the God of your fathers — the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob — has sent me to you.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The LORD**, in capitals, is **YHWH**, and it is built from the same verb. The name and the sentence are the same root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He attaches it immediately to three men: Abraham, Isaac, Jacob. The name that is pure existence is given to Moses fastened to a family."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"This is My name forever\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The name you are to remember Me by **from generation to generation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew for *remember* here is the memorial word — the name is given as something to be kept in mind, generation after generation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the name turns up hidden"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the Hebrew of Esther 7:5 — the verse where the king demands *who is he, where is the man who has dared in his heart to do such a thing?* — the letters of **EHYEH**, *I AM*, stand at the start of four consecutive words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name given at a burning bush is written into a Persian banquet hall, in the sentence asking who is behind all this, in a book that never says God once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **YHWH** is formed the same way four times elsewhere in that book, twice forwards and twice backwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said it was His name forever. It is in the one book where nobody speaks it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jesus does with it.** *Before Abraham was, I AM.* They picked up stones, because they understood exactly which sentence He had just used. And in the garden, when the soldiers say they are looking for Jesus of Nazareth and He answers *I am* — they drew back and fell to the ground. See the note on Esther 7:5–6."}]}, "Exodus 3:16–18": {"title": "A word for the elders", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses is given the message and told, in advance, what the response will be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The errand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go, gather the elders of Israel together**, and tell them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Pharaoh first. The elders."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The credentials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD, the God of your fathers, has appeared to me — the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three names. The elders would know them; the God of the burning bush is being identified by the family He had dealt with four hundred years earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had asked for this: *when they say to me, \"What is His name?\" what shall I say to them?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And had been given **I AM WHO I AM** — and then this, which is the version for the message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have watched over you**, and **I have seen what is being done to you in Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *watched over* is **paqad** — to visit, to attend to, to take account of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used of God visiting Sarah, and of Joseph's promise: *God will surely visit you, and bring you out of this land*. See the note on Genesis 50:15–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph used it four hundred years earlier. Moses is now told to use it, and the elders would have recognised it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **I promise to bring you up out of your misery in Egypt into the land**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Up out of**, and **into**. Both halves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six nations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Canaanites, the Hittites, the Amorites, the Perizzites, the Hivites, and the Jebusites.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list is given so nobody thinks the land is empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A land flowing with milk and honey**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase enters Scripture here and is used about twenty times afterward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Milk means pasture; honey means flowers. It is a description of a country that supports grazing and growing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guarantee about the response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The elders will listen to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Told in advance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' next line, in chapter 4, is *but suppose they will not believe me* — and God gives him three signs anyway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 4 records what actually happened: *so the people believed; and when they heard that the LORD had visited the children of Israel and that He had looked on their affliction, then they bowed their heads and worshipped.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Visited** — the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request to Pharaoh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now please **let us take a three-day journey into the wilderness** to offer sacrifices to the LORD our God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A limited, reasonable ask. It is what Moses is instructed to say, and it is the same request repeated through the plagues."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh's answers narrow it each time: go and sacrifice in the land; go but not far; go but leave the children; go but leave the flocks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **I am sure that the king of Egypt will not let you go, no, not even by a mighty hand**. So I will stretch out My hand and strike Egypt.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told the elders will listen and that Pharaoh will not, before either happens."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses is given.** A message, a name from four centuries earlier, and the outcome of both audiences in advance — that the elders will hear him and the king will not."}]}, "Exodus 3:7–10": {"title": "\"I have seen. I have heard. I know. I have come down.\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of silence, and then four verbs in a row."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have seen it — I have truly seen the misery of my people… I have heard them crying out… **and I know their pain**. So I have come down to rescue them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seen, heard, known, come down. The Hebrew doubles the first — *seeing I have seen* — which is how that language says *thoroughly*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is the one to sit with. **I know their pain.** Not *I am aware of the situation*. The verb is the ordinary word for knowing by experience, the same one used of intimacy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was doing during the silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chapter suggests He had been absent. The claim is the opposite: He had been watching the whole time, and the seeing is recounted before any action is described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That matters for a people who had been in Egypt four centuries and would reasonably have concluded otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is bringing them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of that land into a good land, a wide-open land, a land flowing with milk and honey.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *wide-open* is spacious, broad — a word used elsewhere of being brought out into a large place after being hemmed in. Milk and honey is the shorthand for a land that produces without being forced: pasture rich enough for herds, and wildflowers enough for bees."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not describe it as adequate. He describes it as generous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now go. I am sending you to Pharaoh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs of divine action, and then the fifth verb is Moses'. The rescue God has just described in the first person arrives through a reluctant eighty-year-old with a stick."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not offered.** Nowhere is Egypt made comfortable. The answer to the misery is not improved conditions in the place they were in — it is out, and then up into somewhere else entirely."}]}, "Exodus 30:34–38": {"title": "The holy incense", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A recipe with four ingredients, a rule about proportions, and a prohibition on making it for yourself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take fragrant spices — **gum resin, onycha, and galbanum** — along with **pure frankincense**.\"*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Gum resin** — the Hebrew is **nataph**, *a drop*. It is the sap that bleeds out of a cut in the bark and hardens. The word for dropping is used of speech in the Song of Solomon and of the prophets' oracles.", "**Onycha** — **shecheleth**. Taken from a shellfish, and it gives off its scent only when crushed and burned.", "**Galbanum** — **chelbenah**, from **cheleb**, fat — the word used for the choicest portion, the part of the sacrifice that belonged to God. On its own galbanum is bitter and unpleasant. In the blend it fixes the other scents so they last.", "**Frankincense** — **lebonah**, from the root for *white*. The only one specified as **pure**, and the one that is already fragrant without being burned."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three of the four have to be wounded, crushed or burned to give anything off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proportions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All **in equal amounts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *part for part shall it be*. No ingredient dominates, including the bitter one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The maker"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blend them into a fragrant incense, **the work of a skilled perfumer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *rokeach* — a compounder of ointments, a professional."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The salt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seasoned with **salt**, pure and holy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Salt goes into it, as it goes into every grain offering. See the note on Leviticus 2:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Grind some of it into **fine powder** and set it **in front of the Testimony in the Tent of Meeting, where I'll meet with you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Testimony is the ark with the tablets in it. The incense stands directly in front of the place God said He would meet them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The golden altar of incense stood immediately outside the veil, and the smoke went over it into the Most Holy Place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Most holy\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Treat it as **most holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *holy of holies* — the same phrase as the innermost room. Of the things made for the tabernacle, only a few carry it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Never make incense with this formula for your own use**… Anyone who makes any like it **just to enjoy the scent** is to be **cut off from his people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *to enjoy the scent* is *to smell it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It could not be duplicated, sold, worn, or kept at home. There was one blend and one place to burn it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who burned it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest, morning and evening, at the golden altar. Uzziah the king tried it and came out of the temple a leper. Two of Aaron's sons offered fire that was not from the altar and died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And two hundred and fifty men stood at the tent door with censers, and fire came out. See the note on Numbers 16:16–35."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same substance, in the wrong hand, kills."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The four creatures and the twenty-four elders… every one of them had a harp, and **a gold bowl full of burning incense — and the incense is the prayers of God's people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Revelation 5:7–8, and on Psalms 141:1–2, where David asks for exactly this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the recipe is.** Four things that mostly stink or do nothing until they are crushed and set on fire, in equal parts, blended by an expert — and the result is what stands in front of the place God said He would meet them."}]}, "Exodus 31:1–5": {"title": "Bezalel, chosen by name", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first man in Scripture said to be filled with the Spirit of God is a craftsman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The naming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look — **I've picked a man out by name**: **Bezalel, son of Uri, grandson of Hur, from the tribe of Judah**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have called by name*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three generations given, and the tribe. He is a specific person, not a category."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hur** is likely the man who held up Moses' other arm at Rephidim while Aaron held the first. See the note on Exodus 17:8–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bezalel** means *in the shadow of God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The filling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I've filled him with My own Spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have filled him with the Spirit of God* — **ruach Elohim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the first time the phrase is used of a person in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a prophet, a priest or a king. A man who works metal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the filling consists of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"With **wisdom, understanding, and skill for every kind of craft**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew names three: **chokmah** (wisdom), **tebunah** (understanding), and **daath** (knowledge) — and then *in all manner of workmanship*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three nouns Proverbs uses of how the world was made: *the LORD by wisdom founded the earth, by understanding He established the heavens; by His knowledge the depths were broken up.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He can **dream up beautiful designs** and work them in **gold, silver, and bronze**. He can **cut and set precious stones**, **carve wood**, and **turn his hand to any art at all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *dream up designs* is **lachshov machashavot** — to devise devices, to think out designs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three metals, stonecutting, and woodwork. Four trades in one man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle. The ark, the mercy seat with the cherubim, the table, the lampstand, the altars, the laver, and the priest's garments — everything in the tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 25 to 31 are the specification. Bezalel is the one who executes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And I have appointed with him **Aholiab the son of Ahisamach, of the tribe of Dan**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah and Dan — the first tribe and one of the last. The pairing is deliberate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the workforce"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **in the hearts of all who are gifted artisans I have put wisdom**, that they may make all that I have commanded you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody working on the tent is described the same way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the offering came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The materials had been given: gold, silver, bronze, blue and purple and scarlet thread, fine linen, goats' hair, ram skins, acacia wood, oil, spices and onyx."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All of it out of Egypt, from the morning they left. See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one problem in the project"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people brought too much. *So Moses gave a commandment… \"**Let neither man nor woman do any more work for the offering of the sanctuary**.\" And the people were restrained from bringing, for the material they had was sufficient — and more than enough.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sabbath. Immediately after the craftsman's commission comes *surely My Sabbaths you shall keep* — and then the two tablets, written by the finger of God. See the note on Exodus 31:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Work, rest, and the writing, in that order, at the end of the chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who is filled first.** Not a prophet. A man named to his grandfather, from Judah, who could cut stone and carve wood and beat gold — and the text says God filled him with His own Spirit to do it."}]}, "Exodus 31:18": {"title": "Written by the finger of God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it closes forty days on a mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The moment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when the LORD **finished speaking** with Moses on Mount Sinai, He gave him **the two tablets of the covenant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything from chapter 20 to here — the law, the ordinances, the tabernacle pattern, the priesthood, the Sabbath — has been spoken. This verse ends it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tablets of **stone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not scroll, not clay. The hardest available writing surface, and the one you cannot amend."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The finger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Written by **the very finger of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *written with the finger of God* — the same phrase the magicians of Egypt used when they gave up: *this is the finger of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses it too: *if I cast out demons **with the finger of God**, surely the kingdom of God has come upon you* — where Matthew's parallel has *by the Spirit of God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two tablets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The usual picture is a division of subject — God on one, neighbour on the other. Ancient treaty practice suggests something else: two copies of the same document, one for each party, both kept in the shrine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both tablets went into the ark. Neither party took theirs home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening at the foot of the mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *when the people saw that Moses delayed coming down… they said, \"Make us gods that shall go before us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "While the finger of God is writing, Aaron is taking the earrings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses comes down, sees the calf, and *cast the tablets out of his hands and broke them at the foot of the mountain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The document written by God's own finger did not survive the walk down. See the note on Exodus 32:15–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second set"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cut two tablets of stone like the first ones, **and I will write on them** the words that were on the first tablets which you broke.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This time Moses does the cutting and God does the writing. And these are the ones that went into the ark and lasted."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the writing moves to.** *I will put My laws inside their hearts, and I will write them on their minds.* Same author, same verb, different surface — and a surface that does not shatter on the way down a hill. See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}]}, "Exodus 32:25–29": {"title": "Three thousand, at the giving of the law", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The day the tablets came down the mountain, and what happened at the bottom of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses saw that the people were running wild — Aaron had let them get out of control.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been at Sinai for weeks. Moses was on the mountain receiving the law; the camp had made a calf and was holding a festival for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *running wild* suggests being let loose, unrestrained — and the text puts the responsibility on Aaron for permitting it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line drawn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever is for the LORD, come to me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A call to choose sides, in the open, in front of everyone. The Levites come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What follows is grim and the text does not soften it: swords through the camp, gate to gate, and about three thousand dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three thousand, on the day the law was given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That figure appears once more in Scripture, and the setting is deliberate: Pentecost — by tradition the anniversary of Sinai — when Peter preaches and *about three thousand people were added* to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same festival, same number, opposite direction. Luke was writing for readers who knew Exodus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today you have been set apart to the LORD… And so a blessing has come to you this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tribe that answered the call becomes the priestly tribe. It is the origin of Levi's role in Israel, and the passage records both the cost and the appointment in the same sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How to hold this passage.** It is a hard chapter and it should not be smoothed over. What can be said is what the New Testament says: the law came through Moses, and grace and truth came through Jesus Christ — and the two three-thousands are the plainest illustration in Scripture of what each of them produced on its opening day."}]}, "Exodus 32:30–35": {"title": "\"Perhaps I can make atonement\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The greatest intercession in the Old Testament, and it does not work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he uses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But now I will go up to the LORD. **Perhaps** I can make atonement for what you have done.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Perhaps.** He does not know whether it can be done, and he says so before he climbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That single word is the distance between Moses and the One he is a picture of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he offers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And yet — **if you would only forgive their sin**. But **if not, then blot me out of the book you have written**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence breaks off mid-thought in the Hebrew — *if You will forgive their sin—* and then nothing. He cannot finish it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the offer: erase me instead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same instinct Paul had: *I could wish that I myself were accursed from Christ for my brethren, my countrymen according to the flesh.* Both men offer, and neither is able."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever has sinned against Me, that one I will blot out of My book.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer is declined. A man cannot substitute for another man's sin; God says so plainly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is willing and not qualified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is given instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now go, lead the people to the place I told you of. **My angel will go before you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An angel rather than His presence, and Moses will spend the next chapter arguing about exactly that: *if Your Presence does not go with us, do not bring us up from here.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD struck the people because of what they had done with the calf.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends there. No resolution, no forgiveness pronounced, a plague, and a deferred reckoning: *when the day of reckoning comes, I will hold them to account.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison the sermon is built on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses came down from forty days on the mountain to a golden calf, broke the tablets, went back up with a *perhaps*, and came down again with a face so bright he had to veil it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus came out of forty days in the wilderness and *returned in the power of the Spirit*, and went straight to a synagogue in Nazareth to read Isaiah 61 out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses ended his forty days with a plague on the people. Jesus ended His forty days after the resurrection with His hands raised in blessing, and *while He blessed them, He was parted from them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses could not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Perhaps.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What was said from the cross was **tetelestai** — it is finished. One word, and no conditional in it. See the note on John 19:28–30."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses got right.** He stood between God and a people who deserved nothing, and offered himself. The offer was refused because he was not the one who could make it — but the shape of it is exactly right, and somebody else made it later and was accepted."}]}, "Exodus 34:5–7": {"title": "\"He called out His own name\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses had asked to see His glory. This is what he got."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asked for and what came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Show me Your glory* — chapter 33. And the answer was *I will make all My goodness pass before you, and I will proclaim the name of the LORD before you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The glory turns out to be a name spoken out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the proclaiming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD came down in the cloud and stood there with Moses, and **He called out His own name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He introduces Himself. This is God's own description of God, and it is the passage the rest of the Old Testament quotes back to Him — David, Nehemiah, Joel, Jonah and the psalmists all cite it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD, the LORD — a God **tender and kind, gracious and full of compassion, slow to anger, overflowing with steadfast love and faithfulness**. He keeps His mercy for thousands upon thousands, **forgiving wrongdoing and rebellion and sin**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six or seven adjectives before anything else, and every one of them is mercy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Overflowing with steadfast love* is **rav chesed** — abundant in covenant loyalty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Forgiving wrongdoing and rebellion and sin* — three Hebrew words covering three categories: crookedness, deliberate revolt, and missing the mark. All three are named as forgiven."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet He does not sweep guilt aside** as if it were nothing, and the weight of a father's sin can fall upon children and grandchildren, to the third and the fourth generation.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is emphatic — *by no means clearing the guilty*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is merciful and He does not pretend the debt is not there. Both are in one self-description, and the old covenant had no way to hold them together. A holy God cannot simply overlook it, and this verse says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the new covenant says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will be merciful to their unrighteousness, and **their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 8, quoting Jeremiah 31. See the note on Hebrews 8:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not overlooked, and not swept aside. Remembered no more — which is a different thing again, and it required somebody to satisfy the clause in Exodus 34 rather than delete it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the cross did. *Whom God set forth as a propitiation… to demonstrate His righteousness, **that He might be just and the justifier** of the one who has faith in Jesus.* Paul writes that sentence to answer exactly this verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was spoken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is holding two blank stone tablets, cut by himself, to replace the ones he smashed. The golden calf was six weeks ago."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God proclaims His mercy standing over the rubble of the first covenant."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses does next.** *He made haste and bowed his head toward the earth and worshipped.* No question, no discussion. He heard the name and went down."}]}, "Exodus 4:10–12": {"title": "\"Who gave man his mouth?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses' fourth objection, and the one that is usually read as modesty. It is not quite that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have never been a man of words, not before and not since you have spoken to your servant. I am slow of speech and slow of tongue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *heavy of mouth and heavy of tongue*. Whatever the difficulty was — a speech impediment, a lost fluency in Egyptian after forty years with sheep — he presents it as a fixed fact about himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the aside: *not since you have spoken to your servant*. He is pointing out that the burning bush has not changed anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "God's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who gave man his mouth? Who makes him deaf or mute? Who gives him sight or makes him blind? Is it not I, the LORD?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four questions, and none of them deny the disability. God does not say Moses is mistaken about his speech; He asks who made the mouth in question."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a large and difficult claim, and the passage does not soften it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now go. I myself will be with your mouth, and I will teach you what to say.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, both first person. Not *your speech will improve* — I will be with it, and I will teach you."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it ends.** Moses objects a fifth time anyway — *please send someone else* — and this time God's anger is recorded. Aaron is appointed as the spokesman. The concession is real, and so is the displeasure, and Exodus reports both without resolving them. Moses, notably, does most of the talking from chapter 5 onward."}]}, "Ezekiel 10:18–22": {"title": "The glory leaves by stages", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most reluctant departure in Scripture, recorded in stages by a man watching from Babylon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Ezekiel is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in Jerusalem. He is by the Kebar River among the exiles, and the temple scenes in chapters 8 to 11 are shown to him in vision."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is watching a building he cannot see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the glory of the LORD **moved out from the threshold of the temple** and came to rest **above the cherubim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It had already moved once — chapter 9 has it going up from the cherub *to the threshold of the house*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Threshold. Then above the cherubim. Then:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They stopped **at the entrance of the east gate** of the LORD's house, and the glory of the God of Israel was above them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the next chapter it goes further: *the glory of the LORD went up from the midst of the city and stood on the mountain **which is on the east side of the city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four pauses on the way out. It stops each time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the east gate matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Mount of Olives is east of the temple. That is where the glory settles when it goes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the hill Jesus wept over the city from, and the hill of the ascension. And Ezekiel 40 has the glory returning *from the way of the east*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in the temple by then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 8 lists what Ezekiel had been shown inside: an image of jealousy at the north gate, seventy elders burning incense to creatures carved on the walls, women weeping for Tammuz, and twenty-five men at the door of the temple with their backs to it, worshipping the sun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The glory leaves a building that has been filled with other things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The recognition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These were the living creatures I had seen beneath the God of Israel **by the Kebar River**, and **I knew now that they were cherubim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had described them in chapter 1 without a name for them. Standing in the temple in vision, he recognises them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cherubim were carved into the veil, embroidered on the curtains, and hammered in gold on the mercy seat. He had grown up with the images and now sees the originals."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail he repeats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each had **four faces and four wings**, and under their wings **the form of human hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hands under the wings — Ezekiel notices it twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each one **moved straight ahead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it in chapter 1 as well: *they did not turn when they went.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the departure is hesitant in its motion, however many times it stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Ezekiel does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not comment. Chapters 8 to 11 are almost entirely description, and the evaluation is left to the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the glory had come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle at its dedication, and Solomon's temple at its dedication, when *the priests could not continue ministering because of the cloud*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel watches the same glory go back out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it leaves.** Not in an instant. Threshold, cherubim, east gate, the mountain east of the city — and the prophet who records the way out is the one given the vision of it coming back the same way."}]}, "Ezekiel 18:19–20": {"title": "\"The soul who sins is the one who will die\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel is answering a proverb the exiles kept repeating, and the answer is one of the clearest statements of individual responsibility in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proverb he is dismantling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter: *the parents eat sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a complaint. The exiles were saying they were suffering for their ancestors' sins — which had the convenient effect of making their own conduct beside the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God tells them to stop using it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ruling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The child will not share the guilt of the parent, nor the parent the guilt of the child. The righteousness of the righteous will be credited to him, and the wickedness of the wicked will be charged against him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two accounting words — **credited** and **charged**. Each account stands alone; nothing transfers between them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the principle, and Paul later uses the same vocabulary when he says righteousness is credited to Abraham through faith."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The soul who sins is the one who will die\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated twice in the chapter. It is a sentence about death being earned rather than inherited from a parent's record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read alongside Romans, where death entered through one man and life comes through one Man, the two are addressing different questions: Ezekiel is answering people blaming their fathers, Paul is describing what Adam and Christ each transferred to those in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole point of this argument is the invitation at the end of it: *turn and live… why will you die, house of Israel?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The individual accounting is not stated to frighten. It is stated so that nobody can say their outcome was fixed by somebody else's file — see the note on Ezekiel 33:10–11, where the same chapter's conclusion comes with an oath."}]}, "Ezekiel 33:10–11": {"title": "\"I take no pleasure in the death of the wicked\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God answers a sentence His people are saying to each other, and the answer comes with an oath attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our sins and failures weigh us down. We are wasting away because of them. How then can we live?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is not defiance. It is despair — people who have concluded the account is too far gone to be worth addressing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel is prophesying to exiles. The city has fallen; the worst has already happened. Their question is whether there is any point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As surely as I live, declares the Lord GOD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God swears by Himself, which He does rarely. Whatever follows is being underlined as hard as language allows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I take no pleasure in the death of the wicked. My joy is that the wicked turn from their ways and live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves, negative and positive. The Hebrew for *pleasure* is about delight or desire — He is not describing reluctance to punish. He is denying any appetite for it at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the positive is stronger than mere permission: His *joy* is in the turning and the living."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turn back, turn from your evil ways. Why will you die, house of Israel?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It ends on a question, and the question is almost bewildered. Given that the door is open and He has just sworn He wants them through it — why?"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it turns up again.** Peter says God *does not want anyone destroyed; He wants every last one to turn*, and Paul says God desires all people to be saved. The New Testament keeps making the same claim, and this is the oath underneath it. See the note on 2 Peter 3:9."}]}, "Ezekiel 34:25–27": {"title": "\"There will be showers of blessing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The phrase everybody knows, in a chapter about shepherds who fed themselves instead of the sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel 34 is God's charge against Israel's leaders: you eat the fat, you clothe yourselves with the wool, you slaughter the fattened animals, and you do not feed the flock. The weak were not strengthened, the sick not healed, the lost not sought."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *I Myself will search for My sheep.* God takes the job over personally, and these verses describe the country afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant of peace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make with them a covenant of peace and rid the land of wild beasts, so that they may live safely in the wilderness and sleep in the forests.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Safety in the two places nobody was safe. The wilderness and the forest were where you were most exposed — and the promise is that they will sleep there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The showers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will send down showers in their season. There will be showers of blessing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is literally *showers of blessing*, and Ezekiel keeps the two rains framework of Deuteronomy — *in their season*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note where the blessing is aimed: *I will make them and the places around My hill a blessing.* The people are made into one before the rain is mentioned. The showers land on ground that has already been described as a blessing to its neighbours."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose stated at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they will know that I am the LORD, when I break the bars of their yoke and rescue them from the hands of those who enslaved them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not primarily so the crops come in. So that they know who did it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the shepherd turns out to be.** The chapter goes on: *I will place over them one shepherd, My servant David, and he will tend them.* Written centuries after David died. Jesus picks the whole chapter up in John 10 — the good shepherd, the sheep who were scattered, the hirelings who ran. See the note on John 10:7–10."}]}, "Ezekiel 36:33–36": {"title": "\"Like the garden of Eden\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God describes what a ruined country is going to look like, and the order of events is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the day I cleanse you from all your sins, I will settle people in your towns again, and the ruins will be rebuilt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cleansing is stated before the rebuilding, and the rebuilding is dated from it: **on the day** I cleanse you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already said how — *I will sprinkle clean water on you… I will give you a new heart and put a new spirit in you.* The restoration of the land follows the restoration of the people, not the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison the neighbours make"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People will say, \"This land that was ruined has become like the garden of Eden.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The observation is put in the mouths of outsiders. God does not describe the country that way Himself; He says that is what will be said about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eden is the highest comparison available in Hebrew — the garden before anything went wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the towns were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rubble, deserted and torn down*, and now *walled and full of life*. Ezekiel is writing to people who had watched Jerusalem fall and had walked away from the smoke."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To them, this is not poetry about an abstract future. It is their own streets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why God says He does it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the nations left around you will know that I, the LORD, have rebuilt what was torn down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated audience is the surrounding nations. Earlier in the chapter He says it plainly: this is not for Israel's sake but for the sake of His name, which they had profaned among the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, the LORD, have spoken, and I will do it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and the second guarantees the first. Ezekiel ends the promise the way Isaiah ends his — with the fact that it was said out loud by someone who finishes what He says. See the note on Isaiah 55:10–11."}]}, "Ezekiel 44:28–31": {"title": "\"I am their inheritance\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The tribe that got no land is told what it got instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for their inheritance, **I am their inheritance**. You are to give them no possession in Israel. **I am their possession**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice, in two forms, in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every other tribe received territory when the land was divided. Levi received towns scattered among the others and no ground of their own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua puts it the same way: *the LORD God of Israel was their inheritance.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they lived on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They will eat the grain offering, the sin offering, and the guilt offering, and **every devoted thing in Israel will be theirs**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their income was what came to the altar. If the nation stopped bringing, they stopped eating — which is exactly what happened in Nehemiah's day, and the Levites went back to their fields."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The firstfruits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The best of all the firstfruits** of everything… will belong to the priests.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Reshith** again — the first and the choicest. The same word as Proverbs 3:9. See the note on Proverbs 3:9–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dough"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are to give the priests **the first of your dough**, so that **a blessing may rest on your house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction goes back to Numbers 15 — a cake from the first of the kneading trough, given as a heave offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the smallest possible offering. Not a herd, not a harvest — a piece of the dough before the bread is baked, from an ordinary kitchen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason attached is domestic: *that a blessing may rest on your house.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul takes that idea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the firstfruit is holy, the lump is also holy; and if the root is holy, so are the branches.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 11:16, and he is using the dough offering as an image. A portion given sanctifies the whole batch it came from. See the note on Romans 11:13–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose priesthood this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is about *the sons of Zadok*, who *kept charge of My sanctuary when the children of Israel went astray from Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel's temple vision assigns the closest service to the family that stayed at the post when everybody else drifted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the priests could not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last verse is a food regulation — nothing that died of itself or was torn by beasts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And earlier in the chapter: no wine when they enter the inner court, linen only, no wool, *for they shall not clothe themselves with anything that causes sweat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No sweat in the sanctuary. Whatever else the service was, it was not to be laboured at."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the tribe with nothing ended up holding.** The tabernacle, the ark, the law, the teaching of it, and the altar. No field anywhere, and everything that mattered passed through their hands."}]}, "Ezekiel 47:1–2": {"title": "Water from under the threshold", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel has been shown a temple in enormous detail for eight chapters. Then he is brought back to the front door and shown water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Water flowing out **from under the threshold of the house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from a spring, a well or a river. From under the doorstep of the building itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The source is the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Toward the east, **for the house faced east**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "East is the direction of the entrance, and the direction of the Dead Sea — which the chapter goes on to say the water heals. *The waters shall be healed… and everything will live wherever the river goes.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail he adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The water was coming down **from beneath the south side of the house, south of the altar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He locates it precisely. It passes the altar on its way out, and it comes from the side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burnt offering was killed *on the north side of the altar*. The water runs out past the south side. See the note on Leviticus 1:10–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Trickling\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there **the water was trickling out** from the south side.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a small flow — seeping, oozing. At the threshold it is barely anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What follows in the chapter is the measuring: a thousand cubits, and it is ankle deep. Another thousand, knee deep. Another thousand, up to the waist. Another thousand, and *a river that could not be crossed, for the water was too deep, water in which one must swim.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four measurements, and the further it gets from the door the deeper it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Out of his innermost being will flow rivers of living water**.\" He was speaking about the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus said it standing up in the temple courts at the Feast of Tabernacles, on the day the water-pouring ceremony did not happen. See the note on John 7:37–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel's water comes from under the threshold of the house. John's comes from inside a person — and Paul says *you are the temple of God, and the Spirit of God dwells in you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the river ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Along the bank of the river, on this side and that, will grow all kinds of trees for food. Their leaves will not wither, and their fruit will not fail. They will bear fruit every month… **their fruit will be for food, and their leaves for medicine**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation 22 closes the Bible with the same picture: a pure river of water of life, proceeding from the throne, with the tree of life on either side, *and the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the man with the measuring line does.** He walks Ezekiel out into it four times and asks him each time how deep it is. The prophet is not shown the river from the bank. He is put in it, up to the waist, and then told to swim."}]}, "Ezekiel 9:3–4": {"title": "The mark on the forehead", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Before anything happens to the city, one man is sent through it with a pen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The glory moves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **the glory of the God of Israel had risen from over the cherub** where it had rested and **moved to the threshold** of the house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel tracks this through the whole book — the glory lifts from between the cherubim, goes to the threshold, then to the east gate, then to the mountain east of the city, and leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It moves slowly, and by stages, and it is the saddest sequence in the prophets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it has taken the first step."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man with the writing case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man **clothed in linen** who had **the writing case at his side**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six men had come in with weapons. This one has neither armour nor a weapon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Linen is priestly cloth. The writing case is a scribe's — an inkhorn worn at the belt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Pass through the midst of the city**… and **set a mark upon the foreheads**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes first. Before the six with the weapons are told to do anything, this one goes through and marks people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word is **tav** — the last letter of the Hebrew alphabet, and it is used here as the name of the mark itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the old paleo-Hebrew script, that letter was written as a cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who gets it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The people who **sigh and grieve over all the abominations** that are done within it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and both are about grief."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the people who did nothing wrong. Not the ones who protested, or organised, or left. The ones who were still grieved by what they were living in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever they had or had not done, that is what the mark is set on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The six are told to begin, *and do not let your eye spare, nor have any pity* — and then: *\"but **do not come near anyone on whom is the mark**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is explicit, and the mark is the only thing distinguishing anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it begins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Begin at My sanctuary.**\" So they began with the elders who were before the temple.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The judgement starts at the building and with the leadership."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says the same thing: *the time has come for judgement to begin at the house of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Ezekiel does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I fell on my face and cried out, \"Ah, Lord GOD! **Will You destroy all the remnant of Israel** in pouring out Your fury on Jerusalem?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only one in the vision who intercedes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the mark turns up again.** Revelation 7: *do not harm the earth, the sea, or the trees **till we have sealed the servants of our God on their foreheads**.* Same order — the sealing first, and then the rest."}]}, "Ezra 2:1–2": {"title": "The long road back", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A list of names, and one of them is worth stopping on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the list is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are the people of the province who came up out of captivity… now returning to Jerusalem and Judah, **each one to his own town**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy years after Nebuchadnezzar took them, Cyrus the Persian issues a decree and they are allowed to go home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not everybody went. That is the fact behind the whole book of Esther. Many Jews had built lives in Babylon and Persia across three generations and stayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Each one to his own town\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The towns had been empty for seventy years. Most of the returners had never seen them; they are going to places they know only from their grandparents."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came with Zerubbabel, together with Jeshua, Nehemiah, Seraiah, Reelaiah, **Mordecai**, Bilshan…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether or not this is the man in the palace at Susa, the name is here in the list of those who went up — and it tells you the road home was open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Jews of Esther's book are in Persia by choice. Susa is where they had settled when Jerusalem was available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that matters for Esther"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esther is the story of the people who did not go back, in the season Hosea called *not My people* — no temple, no land, no prophet, and God's name never spoken in the whole of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He is at work in every chapter of it anyway. That is the thing the book was written to show: they were out of the land, out of the covenant blessings, out of His visible dealings — and not out of His hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tally"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the tally of the men of the people of Israel, **counted by their families**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that follows is fifty-eight verses of numbers, family by family, village by village. Down to the singers, the gatekeepers, and the temple servants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody counted, and wrote it down, and it was kept."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they came back to.** Rubble, and a foundation. When the older men who had seen the first temple saw the new foundation laid, Ezra says they wept aloud while everyone else shouted for joy, and nobody could tell the weeping from the shouting."}]}, "Ezra 6:13–15": {"title": "\"They built and they finished\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A building project that had been stopped for sixteen years gets finished in four, and Ezra names why."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The foundation was laid, and then the local officials wrote to the Persian court and the work was halted by decree. It stayed halted through the rest of Cyrus's reign and into Darius's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Tattenai the governor asked the question that reopened it — *who authorised this?* — and the search of the archives at Ecbatana turned up Cyrus's original decree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the officials did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Tattenai and Shethar-bozenai and their companions **did exactly what King Darius had sent, and they did it carefully**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men who tried to stop it are now enforcing it. Darius's reply had ordered them not only to leave the Jews alone but to fund the work out of the provincial taxes, and to supply the sacrifices daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The opposition became the budget."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reasons given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The work went well in their hands, **carried along by the words of Haggai the prophet and Zechariah son of Iddo**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is that they prospered *through the prophesying* of these two men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezra names the preaching as the cause. Not the decree — that came later, and the prophets started in the second year of Darius, before the ruling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai's message was *consider your ways* and *I am with you, says the LORD.* Zechariah's, to Zerubbabel, was *not by might nor by power, but by My Spirit* and *grace, grace to it.* See the note on Zechariah 4:6–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They preached, and men who had given up picked up their tools."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The double authorship"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as **the God of Israel had commanded**, and as **Cyrus and Darius and Artaxerxes** king of Persia had decreed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezra puts God's command and three pagan emperors' decrees in one sentence, as though they were one instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The house was finished on **the third day of the month Adar, in the sixth year of the reign of King Darius**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezra dates it precisely. Roughly twenty years after the foundation was laid, four years after the preaching started."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old men who had seen Solomon's temple wept when the foundation went down, because this one was so much smaller."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai had addressed that directly: *who is left among you who saw this temple in its former glory? And how do you see it now? Is it not in your eyes as nothing?* — and then: *the glory of this latter temple shall be greater than the former.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dedication, a hundred bulls, two hundred rams, four hundred lambs, and twelve male goats *according to the number of the tribes of Israel* — twelve, though only two tribes had come home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then they kept the Passover."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Which building it became.** This is the temple Herod later enlarged, and the one Jesus walked into and cleared. *The glory of this latter temple shall be greater than the former* — the greater glory turned out to be a Person standing in it."}]}, "Galatians 1:15–16": {"title": "\"Set me apart from my mother's womb\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul dates his calling to before he had done anything at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God, who had **set me apart from my mother's womb** and called me **through His grace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from the Damascus road. From the womb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *set apart* is **aphorizo** — to mark off, to separate for a purpose. It is the same root as *Pharisee*, which is what he had been, and he uses it here of what God had done long before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is excluded by the dating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he had listed a few verses earlier: *I advanced in Judaism beyond many of my contemporaries, being more exceedingly zealous for the traditions of my fathers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And everything on the other side of it — *I persecuted the church of God beyond measure and tried to destroy it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither the achievements nor the atrocities are the reason. He was set apart before either existed, and the mechanism named is **grace**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else was told this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah: *before I formed you in the womb I knew you; before you were born I sanctified you; I ordained you a prophet to the nations.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah: *the LORD has called Me from the womb; from the body of My mother He has named Me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Was pleased\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Was **pleased** to reveal His Son in me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eudokeo** — to take good pleasure in, to think it good. It is the word the Father uses at the baptism: *in whom I am well pleased.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The revealing was not a rescue operation forced on Him. It pleased Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *to me*. Paul says the Son was revealed **in** him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that I might **announce Him among the nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The calling and the assignment arrive together. He is not given a revelation and then left to find a use for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the moment that happened, **I did not sit down and talk it over with flesh and blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not go up to Jerusalem to the apostles. He went to Arabia, and came back to Damascus, and it was three years before he saw Peter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole point of the passage is where his gospel came from: *I neither received it from man, nor was I taught it, but it came through the revelation of Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he had been on the way to do.** Carrying letters to Damascus to arrest people. The commission he was actually given that morning was *to open their eyes, to turn them from darkness to light* — and he had been set apart for it before he was born."}]}, "Galatians 1:6–7": {"title": "Astonished so soon", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul opens the letter without the thing he always opens letters with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every other letter of Paul's begins with thanksgiving. *I thank my God always concerning you*, *we give thanks to God always for you all*, *I thank my God upon every remembrance of you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even Corinth, with its factions and its lawsuits and its immorality, gets one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Galatia gets this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **astonished**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thaumazo** — to marvel, to be amazed. It was a standard opening in first-century letters, but used to express pleasure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the polite formula and turns it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At **how quickly** you are walking away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tacheos** — soon, hastily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that they left, but how fast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are leaving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Walking away from **the One who called you in the grace of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from a doctrine. From a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say they are deserting the gospel first. He says they are deserting the one who called them, and the calling is described as *in grace*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Metatithesthe** — to transfer, to change sides. It was used of soldiers deserting and of a person changing political party."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is present tense. It is happening while he writes; it is not finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are going to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Walking off toward **a different good news** — **which is not another good news at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses two different Greek words in one sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Heteron** — another of a different kind.", "**Allo** — another of the same kind."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **different** gospel, which is not **another** gospel.* Different in kind, and not a second one of the same species."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is one, and this is not a variant of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is behind it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There are simply **certain people unsettling you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tarassontes** — agitating, stirring up, throwing into confusion. It is the word for water being troubled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not name them. Later he calls them *false brethren secretly brought in*, and *he who troubles you shall bear his judgment, whoever he is.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they want"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What they want is to **twist the good news of Christ into something else**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metastrepsai** — to turn about, to reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the twist was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Circumcision. The letter names it repeatedly, and the council in Acts 15 dealt with the same teaching: *unless you are circumcised according to the custom of Moses, you cannot be saved.* See the note on Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing immoral was being proposed. An addition was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But even if we, or an angel from heaven, preach any other gospel to you than what we have preached to you, **let him be accursed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then he says it a second time, in the next verse, in case it was read as heat: *as we have said before, so now I say again.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the letter lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **for freedom that Christ has made us free**. Stand fast therefore, and do not be entangled again with a yoke of bondage.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What alarms him.** Not the arrival of teachers and not an argument being lost. The word in the sentence is *quickly* — and what they are described as leaving is a person, not a position."}]}, "Galatians 1:8–9": {"title": "\"Let him be accursed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the hardest thing in his letters, and then says it a second time in case it read as temper."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses after the opening. He has skipped the thanksgiving entirely — the only letter of his that does — and gone straight to *I am astonished at how quickly you are walking away*. See the note on Galatians 1:6–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first party named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But even if **we ourselves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He starts with himself and his own team."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rule he is about to state binds the man stating it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or **an angel out of heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The highest possible authority short of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had received his gospel *by the revelation of Jesus Christ* — he says so in the next paragraph — and he still puts an angel under the rule."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later in the same letter: *Satan himself transforms himself into an angel of light.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Were to announce to you **any good news other than the good news we announced to you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **par' ho** — alongside, contrary to, beside what we preached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *the opposite of*. Beside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Galatian teachers were not denying Christ. They were adding circumcision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let him be **accursed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anathema** — devoted to destruction, handed over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses for what was under the ban at Jericho."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have said it before and **I say it now again**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the whole thing twice, and the second time he changes one word: *if anyone announces to you any good news other than **the one you received**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "First time: what we preached. Second time: what you received."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He shifts the standard from the messenger to the message as it landed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why say it twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because it reads as anger the first time. Repeating it makes it a considered position rather than an outburst."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same thing at the end of the letter with the summary: *for in Christ Jesus neither circumcision nor uncircumcision avails anything, but a new creation.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For do I now persuade men, or God? Or **do I seek to please men?** For if I still pleased men, I would not be a bondservant of Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the answer to the obvious reaction. He is aware of how it sounds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's own summary of the alternative: *you have become estranged from Christ, you who attempt to be justified by law; you have fallen from grace.* See the note on Galatians 5:2–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letter's tone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O foolish Galatians! Who has bewitched you?* And: *I am afraid for you, lest I have laboured for you in vain.* And: *I could wish to be present with you now and to change my tone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle of it, the paragraph about arriving ill and being welcomed like an angel of God. See the note on Galatians 4:12–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the rule binds first.** Himself. Paul puts his own name at the head of the sentence, then an angel — and then says the whole thing again so nobody can put it down to heat."}]}, "Galatians 2:19–20": {"title": "The faith of the Son of God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*The life I now live in the flesh, I live by the faith **of** the Son of God, who loved me and gave Himself up for me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how the older English versions render it, and it is the more literal reading of the Greek. It is worth knowing why, because it changes what the verse asks of you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pistis Christou** — *faith of Christ*. The genitive most naturally reads as belonging to Him: His faith, His faithfulness. Rendering it *faith in the Son of God* requires taking the genitive as an object instead, which is possible but is the less obvious reading of the words as they stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it fits the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what Paul has just said, one clause earlier:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*It is no longer I who live — Christ lives in me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has already removed himself as the subject. To then say that the life is sustained by the strength of his own believing would put him straight back at the centre of a sentence he has just moved himself out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as *the faith of the Son of God*, the whole thing runs one direction: I died, He lives in me, and the faith I live by is His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does to a struggling reader"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It relocates the pressure. If the Christian life runs on the quality of my trust, then every flat morning is a threat. If it runs on His faithfulness, then my believing is a response to something already holding, not the thing doing the holding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's language elsewhere runs the same way — *faithful is He who calls you, who also will do it*, and *if we are faithless, He remains faithful, for He cannot deny Himself.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The four words at the end.** *Who loved me and gave Himself up for me.* Paul writes it singular, which he almost never does. In the middle of the most combative letter he wrote, the argument narrows to one man and the love aimed at him — which is the same move the whole verse makes."}]}, "Galatians 3:1–5": {"title": "One question for you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops arguing and asks a question he says he only needs one answer to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Galatians, **what has come over you?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *O foolish Galatians* — **anoetoi**, without understanding, not thinking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names them by their region and calls them senseless, twice in five verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spell"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who has **put you under a spell**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ebaskanen** — bewitched, from the word for the evil eye."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not saying it literally. He is saying nothing else explains it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had seen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You, **before whose very eyes Jesus Christ was set out plainly**, right there among you, **as the crucified One**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proegraphe** — placarded, posted up publicly, the way a public notice was displayed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tense of *crucified* is perfect: crucified and standing crucified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of them were in Jerusalem. What was placarded in front of them was the preaching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **only one thing I want to learn from you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sets aside the whole argument and reduces it to a single test."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Did you **receive the Spirit by doing the works of the law, or by hearing and believing**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by works of law or by the hearing of faith*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a question about their own experience, and they know the answer. Something happened when they believed, and it did not happen because they had kept anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not argue from Scripture here. He argues from what they remember."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **began in the Spirit** — are you now **to be brought to completion by the flesh**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *brought to completion* is **epiteleisthe** — to finish off, to perfect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is about the second half of the process, not the first. Nobody in Galatia was denying how it started."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The suffering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Did you go through **so much suffering for nothing** — **if it really was for nothing**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts 13 and 14 record what happened when Paul was in that region: driven out of Antioch, threatened at Iconium, and stoned and left for dead at Lystra."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The converts had paid for this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**If it really was for nothing** — Paul adds the clause and then leaves it. He does not finish the thought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, the One who **supplies the Spirit to you and works miracles among you** — does He do it **because you keep the works of the law, or because you heard and believed**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks it a second time, in the present tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not only how it started — how it is still running. He says miracles are currently happening among them, and asks what the supply is connected to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *supplies* is **epichoregeo**, the word for the wealthy citizen who paid for the whole chorus out of his own pocket. Peter uses it of God and then of the reader. See the note on 2 Peter 1:5–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham, believing God, counted as righteousness — four hundred and thirty years before the law. See the note on Galatians 3:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul answers his own question with a case older than the thing in dispute."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The evidence he calls.** Not a text. Their own memory of what happened when they heard, and what is still happening among them now."}]}, "Galatians 3:10–14": {"title": "\"By becoming a curse for us\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the law against itself and then says what was done about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is under a curse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who **rests on the works of the law** is under a curse.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not everyone who breaks it. Everyone who rests on it — who stands on their own keeping of it as their position before God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cursed is everyone who does not continue in ALL the things** written in the book of the law, to do them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 27, the words Israel shouted *Amen* to from Mount Ebal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The killing word is *all*. Not most, not the important ones, not on balance. Everything, continuously, without a gap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James puts the same thing another way: *whoever keeps the whole law and yet stumbles in one point is guilty of all.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two systems, side by side"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul sets the operating principles next to each other:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the one who is right with God **will live by faith*** — Habakkuk", "*the law is not of faith; its word is rather, **the person who does these things will live by them*** — Leviticus 18"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "One says believe. The other says do. They are not two routes to the same place; they run on incompatible currency."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Christ did with the curse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ has **bought us out from under the curse** of the law **by becoming a curse for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *bought us out* is **exagorazo** — to purchase out of the market, the word used of buying a slave in order to free him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He did not lift the curse off. He took the position: *becoming a curse for us.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proof text"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 21. The body of an executed criminal hung on a tree was under God's curse and had to be taken down before nightfall so the land would not be defiled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for the one verse in the law that puts a curse on a hanged man, and says that is the transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two purpose clauses, and both are given:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the blessing of Abraham** might come to the nations in Christ Jesus, and so that through faith we might **receive the promise of the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The curse was taken so the blessing could go somewhere it had never been — *to the nations.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the blessing of Abraham is the one credited in Genesis 15:6, before there was any law to keep. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul asks them at the top of the chapter.** *Did you receive the Spirit by the works of the law, or by the hearing of faith?* He does not argue the theology first. He asks them to remember how it started."}]}, "Galatians 3:15–18": {"title": "\"Not seeds, but Seed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul argues from contract law, and then from a singular noun."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The everyday example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let me speak **the way people speak in ordinary matters**. Even a covenant made between human beings, once it has been **ratified**, is not set aside by anyone, and **nothing is added to it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He starts with what anybody in that world knew about wills and contracts. Once witnessed and sealed, a document stands. You cannot append to it later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The singular"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The promises were spoken to Abraham **and to his Seed**. It does not say, **\"and to seeds,\" as though to many**, but as of one: **and to your Seed, who is Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole weight is on a grammatical number."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **zera** is a collective — like the English *offspring*, it can mean one descendant or a nation. Paul knows this; he uses it collectively himself four verses later: *if you are Christ's, then you are Abraham's seed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His point is not that the collective reading is impossible. It is that the singular is available, and that it is the one that resolves the promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law, which came **four hundred and thirty years afterward**, cannot cancel a covenant that was **confirmed beforehand** by God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument in one line. The promise is older."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A later document does not annul an earlier ratified one, and Paul has just established that from ordinary practice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So as to leave the promise **with nothing in it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to make void, to nullify."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If keeping the law were the condition, the promise would have been quietly converted into a contract with terms — and the terms would be the thing that mattered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two systems"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **if the inheritance comes by the law, then it no longer comes by promise**; but **God gave it to Abraham by a promise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul treats them as mutually exclusive. Not two routes to the same place — two different kinds of thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An inheritance and a wage cannot be the same transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No law, no tabernacle, no priesthood, no commandments. A promise, a night sky, and a covenant cut while he was asleep. See the notes on Genesis 15:6 and Genesis 15:12–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law was for, then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks it himself in the next verse: *what purpose then does the law serve?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer: *it was added because of transgressions, till the Seed should come to whom the promise was made… **the law was our tutor to bring us to Christ**, that we might be justified by faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A guardian escorting a child somewhere, and the escort's job ends on arrival."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How closely Paul reads.** A tense settled the resurrection for Jesus against the Sadducees; a singular settles the inheritance for Paul against the Judaisers. Both arguments would collapse if the words were approximate. See the note on Matthew 22:23–33."}]}, "Galatians 3:19–22": {"title": "Why the law was given", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks the question his own argument forces, and answers it in four parts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What purpose, then, does the law serve?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just proved that the promise to Abraham came four hundred and thirty years before Sinai, and that a later document cannot annul an earlier covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which leaves the obvious problem: then why was it given at all?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **added because of transgressions**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosetethe** — added, placed alongside. Not the main thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Because of transgressions** — the phrase is compressed and Paul unpacks it elsewhere: *by the law is the knowledge of sin*, and *I would not have known sin except through the law*. See the note on Romans 7:7–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The term"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until **the Seed should come to whom the promise had been made**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An expiry date, built into the answer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already argued about that word: *He does not say, \"And to seeds,\" as of many, but as of one, \"And to your Seed,\" who is Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it was delivered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was put in place **through angels, by the hand of a mediator**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two intermediaries. Stephen says the same at his trial — *you who have received the law by the direction of angels* — and Hebrews: *the word spoken through angels proved steadfast*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument from arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **a mediator is not a mediator for one party alone, but God is one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the densest sentences Paul ever wrote, and the point is structural: a mediator implies two sides. The law is an agreement with terms on both ends."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise to Abraham had one party. God swore it by Himself, and Abraham was asleep when the covenant was cut. See the note on Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The denial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is the law, then, **set against the promises of God? Certainly not**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me genoito** — Paul's strongest negative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just spent a chapter setting them in opposition and refuses the conclusion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession he will not make"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **if a law had been given that could give life, righteousness really would have come by the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Conditional, contrary to fact. If such a law existed, Paul says he would say so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem is not that the route is forbidden. It is that it does not go anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Scripture did instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the Scripture shut everything up together under sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunekleisen** — locked up together, enclosed on all sides. It is used of a net closing on fish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything, together. Paul says the same in Romans: *God has committed them all to disobedience, that He might have mercy on all.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the promise, by faith in Jesus Christ, might be given to those who believe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shutting-up has a stated purpose, and the purpose is the giving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But before faith came, we were kept under guard by the law, **kept for the faith which would afterward be revealed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore the law was **our tutor to bring us to Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paidagogos** — not a teacher, but the household slave who walked a boy to school and kept him out of trouble on the way. The job ended when the boy came of age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul carries the image straight into the next chapter. See the note on Galatians 4:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul refuses to say.** That the law is against the promises. He says it was added, that it had a term, that it came through two intermediaries, and that it could not give life — and then denies the conclusion anybody would draw."}]}, "Galatians 3:23–25": {"title": "Kept until faith came", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's word for what the law was, and it is a job title from a Greek household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The custody"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before this faith came, we were **kept under guard by the law, shut in and waiting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs. The Greek is **ephrouroumetha** — held in a garrison, guarded — and **sunkleiomenoi**, shut up together, enclosed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb he used four verses earlier: *the Scripture shut everything up together under sin*. See the note on Galatians 3:19–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was waiting for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the faith that was going to be revealed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The custody has a terminus built into it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the law was **our guardian**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paidagogos**, and it does not mean teacher."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **paidagogos** was a household slave who walked a boy to school, carried his things, kept him from trouble on the road, and handed him over at the door. He taught nothing. His job was to get the child there and keep him safe on the way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The post ended when the boy came of age."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keeping us and **leading us to Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *unto Christ* — the destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That we might be **made right by faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not made right by the guardian. The guardian delivers the boy to the place where it happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **now that faith has come, we are no longer under a guardian**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *we are no longer under a paidagogos*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the guardian was bad, or failed, or was dismissed for cause. The child arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is not saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That the law was cruel. A **paidagogos** was strict by design and the household paid for one because the road was dangerous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's argument is entirely about the term of the appointment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **you are all sons of God through faith in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huioi** — sons, adults, not **nepioi**, infants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *there is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither slave nor free, there is neither male nor female; for **you are all one in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture continued"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next chapter takes the same household further: an heir who owns everything and is indistinguishable from a slave until the day his father set. See the note on Galatians 4:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Galatians were being told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To take on circumcision and the calendar. Paul's answer is that they are proposing to hire the escort back after arriving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it plainly at the end of chapter 4: *how is it that you turn again to the weak and beggarly elements, to which you desire again to be in bondage?*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the job was.** Not to teach and not to make anybody righteous. To keep a child safe and get him to the door — and Paul's whole point is that the appointment had an end date written into it."}]}, "Galatians 3:26–29": {"title": "\"Then you are Abraham's seed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent three chapters arguing that the promise to Abraham came before the law and does not run through it. Here is where the argument lands on the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three divisions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither slave nor free, there is neither male nor female.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those were the three great dividing lines of the ancient world — race, status, sex. A common Jewish morning prayer of the period thanked God for not making the speaker a Gentile, a slave, or a woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the same three categories and says that in Christ they do not determine standing. He is not abolishing the differences; he is saying they no longer decide who inherits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Clothed yourselves with Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is putting on a garment. In the Roman world a boy coming of age exchanged his child's toga for the *toga virilis* — the moment he became a legal adult with rights of inheritance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just used exactly that picture in the verses before: an heir who is a child is no different from a slave until the appointed time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if you belong to Christ, then you are Abraham's seed, and heirs according to the promise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *like* Abraham's seed. Paul has already argued that the promise was made to Abraham and to his *seed*, singular, and that the seed is Christ. So the route runs: Christ is the heir; you belong to Christ; therefore the promise is yours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The inheritance arrives by belonging, not by descent and not by performance."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is inherited.** In Genesis it was land, descendants and blessing for all nations. Paul has already named the piece he is most interested in three verses earlier — *so that the blessing of Abraham might come to the Gentiles*, and the promise of the Spirit received through faith. The inheritance he is arguing for is not smaller than Genesis. It is wider."}]}, "Galatians 3:6–9": {"title": "Blessed together with Abraham", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul settles the Galatian question by going back four hundred and thirty years before the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The precedent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is just as it was with Abraham: **he believed God, and it was counted to him as righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 15:6, quoted here and in Romans 4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's argument is chronological. The verse comes before circumcision in Genesis 17, and centuries before Sinai."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Counted** — the Greek is **logizomai**, the accounting term. It is entered on a ledger by somebody else. See the note on Romans 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Understand, then, that **it is the people of faith who are the sons of Abraham**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *those of faith* — **hoi ek pisteos**, those out of faith."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not those of descent, and not those of observance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the sentence the whole letter turns on. The Galatians were being told they needed to become descendants of Abraham by circumcision; Paul says the descent is already established by the same thing Abraham had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Scripture that sees ahead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the Scripture, seeing ahead of time** that God would set the nations right by faith, **announced the good news to Abraham long before it came**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proeuengelisato** — pre-evangelised. Paul says the gospel was preached to Abraham."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He personifies Scripture as the one doing the foreseeing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was announced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**In you all the nations shall be blessed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From Genesis 12, and repeated in the oath after Moriah. See the note on Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reads *all the nations* as the point. The Gentiles were in the promise from the first sentence of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, **those who are of faith are blessed together with Abraham, who believed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *blessed **with** believing Abraham* — **sun**, alongside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not blessed like him. Blessed with him, in the same act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For as many as are **of the works of the law are under the curse**; for it is written, \"Cursed is everyone who does not continue in all things which are written in the book of the law, to do them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Deuteronomy, and the sting is in **all things**. The law's own terms are total."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *Christ has redeemed us from the curse of the law, **having become a curse for us** — for it is written, \"Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the letter exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men had come to Galatia teaching that Gentile converts must be circumcised. Paul opens the letter without his usual thanksgiving — *I marvel that you are turning away so soon* — and this chapter begins *O foolish Galatians! Who has bewitched you?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse the argument lands on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **the blessing of Abraham might come upon the Gentiles in Christ Jesus**, that we might receive the promise of the Spirit through faith.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the seed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He does not say, \"And to seeds,\" as of many, but as of one, \"And to your Seed,\" **who is Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rests an argument on a singular noun."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order Paul insists on.** Faith counted as righteousness first, then circumcision, then four hundred and thirty years, then the law. He is not arguing about which is better — he is arguing about which came first, and what that makes the other one."}]}, "Galatians 4:1–3": {"title": "The heir and the slave", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul draws a picture from a wealthy household and says it is a picture of the reader's own past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As long as **the heir is a child**, he is **no different from a slave**, though **everything belongs to him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses, and the third is the one that makes it strange."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The estate is legally his. Nothing is in doubt about the ownership."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **child**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nepios** — an infant, literally *non-speaking*. Paul uses it of the immature: *when I was a child, I spoke as a child*, and of those still on milk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of Hebrews uses the same word of readers who should be teaching by now. See the note on Hebrews 5:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No different from a slave.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not worse than a slave and not better. In terms of what he can actually do with the property, indistinguishable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The supervision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is **under guardians and stewards**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words: **epitropous**, guardians of the person, and **oikonomous**, managers of the property."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman law had exactly this arrangement. A minor heir had both, and neither answered to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until **the day his father has appointed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prothesmias** — a set term, a legally fixed date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father sets it. Not the boy, not the guardians, and not the calendar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The application"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **so it was with us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul turns the picture on his readers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were under"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When we were children, we were **held as slaves under the elemental things of the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **stoicheia tou kosmou** — the elements, the rudiments. It is the word for the letters of the alphabet, and for the basic constituents of a thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it again at the end of the chapter, about the Galatians turning back: *how is it that you turn again to **the weak and beggarly elements**, to which you desire again to be in bondage? **You observe days and months and seasons and years**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **when the fullness of the time had come, God sent forth His Son**, born of a woman, born under the law, **to redeem those who were under the law**, that we might **receive the adoption as sons**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The appointed day arrived. See the note on Galatians 4:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for adoption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Huiothesia** — from **huios**, son, and **tithemi**, to place. The placing of a son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Roman law it was a legal act that transferred a person into a family with full inheritance rights, and it could not be undone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proof"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And because you are sons, **God has sent forth the Spirit of His Son into your hearts, crying out, \"Abba, Father!\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the Spirit is sent **because** they are sons, not in order to make them so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore you are **no longer a slave but a son**, and if a son, **then an heir of God through Christ**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the illustration does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That the child was mistreated, or that the guardians were cruel, or that the estate was ever at risk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole difference in the picture is the date the father set."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was always true and what changed.** The estate belonged to him the entire time. What changed is that he came of age — and Paul says the arrangement before that was indistinguishable from slavery."}]}, "Galatians 4:12–16": {"title": "The welcome you gave me", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops arguing for five verses and talks about the first time he came."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brothers, **I am begging you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **deomai** — to plead out of need. The tone changes completely from the chapters either side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Become like me, **for I became like you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had described what that cost in the first letter to Corinth: *to those who are under the law, as under the law… to those who are without law, as without law… **I have become all things to all men***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clearance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have **not wronged me at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He removes any suggestion that this is personal grievance before saying anything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he stopped there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You know that it was **because of a weakness of the flesh** that I preached the good news to you **the first time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **astheneian tes sarkos** — bodily weakness, infirmity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not planned to preach in Galatia. Something physical stopped him there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the New Testament says what it was. Paul mentions *a thorn in the flesh* in 2 Corinthians and does not identify that either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the trial that was in my flesh **you did not treat with contempt**, and you **did not turn away from me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *treat with contempt* is **exoutheneo** — to reject as worthless. The other verb is literally *you did not spit* — a gesture used against illness and the evil eye in that world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the condition was, it was visible and it was the kind of thing people reacted to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The welcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You welcomed me **as an angel of God, as Christ Jesus Himself**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where, then, is **the blessing you spoke of in those days**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *where is your blessedness* — the happiness they had at the time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I can bear you witness that, **had it been possible, you would have torn out your own eyes and handed them to me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail has made readers wonder about his eyes ever since. He ends this letter *see with what large letters I have written to you with my own hand*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination keeps the sentence as it stands and offers no diagnosis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **have I become your enemy now, because I tell you the truth**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aletheuon** — telling the truth to you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften anything in the letter. He asks whether the telling is what changed things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the allegory of the two women and the sentence about freedom. See the notes on Galatians 4:21–27 and Galatians 4:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hardest argument in his letters has a personal paragraph inserted in the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says two verses on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My little children, for whom **I labour in birth again until Christ is formed in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **odino** — the word for labour pains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, writing as a mother."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he reminds them of.** Not his credentials. That he arrived ill, that they did not recoil, and that they were happy — and then he asks what happened to that."}]}, "Galatians 4:21–27": {"title": "Two women and two covenants", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes a family story out of Genesis and says it was always about two systems."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tell me, **you who want to live under the law: do you not listen to what the law itself says?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is about to argue against law-keeping from the law itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two sons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham had **two sons, one by the slave woman and one by the free woman**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael and Isaac. Hagar and Sarah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The difference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The son of the slave woman was **born in the ordinary way of the flesh**, and the son of the free woman was **born through the promise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael was conceived by Abram and Hagar on Sarai's suggestion, when the couple decided the promise needed help. He was born to a man of eighty-six by ordinary means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac was born to a man of a hundred and a woman of ninety past childbearing. Hebrews says of Abraham *as good as dead*. See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's line is between what a couple can manage and what only a promise produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These things **carry a deeper meaning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **allegoroumena** — spoken allegorically."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not denying the history. He treats Genesis as an account of things that happened and as carrying a second sense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **two women are two covenants**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Hagar** — *the one from **Mount Sinai**, bearing children into slavery*", "**Sarah** — unnamed here, but *the Jerusalem above is free*"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The geography"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For this Hagar is **Mount Sinai in Arabia**, and she **answers to the Jerusalem that now is**, and she is **in slavery along with her children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *answers to* is **sustoichei** — stands in the same column, corresponds in a list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul aligns Sinai with the current Jerusalem — the city with the functioning temple, the priesthood and the sacrifices."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a startling thing to write to Jewish readers, and it is the sharpest sentence in the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the Jerusalem above is free**, and she is **the mother of us all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews uses the same phrase: *you have come to Mount Zion and to the city of the living God, **the heavenly Jerusalem***. See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two mountains again: Sinai and Zion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice, **barren woman**, you who never bore a child; **break out and shout aloud**, you who were never in labor — for **the desolate woman has many more children than she who has a husband**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54, the chapter directly after the suffering servant. See the note on Isaiah 54:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it because it is about a woman with no children being told to prepare for a crowd — which is Sarah's situation, and which he says is the reader's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which side the Galatians were choosing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were being told to take on circumcision. Paul's argument places that on the Hagar side of the table — the side that produced a son by ordinary means because somebody could not wait."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now we, brothers, are **children of promise, just as Isaac was**.* See the note on Galatians 4:28–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does with the story.** Reads it as two ways of getting a son — one a couple can arrange and one only a promise produces — and puts the current temple city in the first column."}]}, "Galatians 4:28–31": {"title": "Children of the free woman", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul applies the allegory, and the application has a hard edge on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the readers are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now we, brothers, are **children of promise, just as Isaac was**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not children of Abraham in general. He picks the son and names him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac's distinction is not his character. It is how he came to exist. See the note on Galatians 4:21–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The friction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But as **the one born in the ordinary way of the flesh persecuted** in those days **the one born according to the Spirit**, so it is now.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Genesis account is short about it: at the weaning feast Sarah saw Ishmael *mocking*. See the note on Genesis 21:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **metsacheq** — playing, laughing, mocking. It is the same root as Isaac's own name, which means *laughter*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reads it as persecution, and says the pattern is still running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So it is now\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Galatian churches were being pressured by teachers insisting on circumcision. Paul had been driven out of Antioch, stoned at Lystra, and hounded from city to city on that first journey."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He treats the current pressure as the old one continuing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **what does the Scripture say? Cast out the slave woman and her son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 21, and the words are Sarah's. Paul quotes a family quarrel as Scripture speaking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He drops one word: Sarah said *cast out this bondwoman **and her son**, for the son of this bondwoman shall not be heir with my son, **namely with Isaac***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the son of the slave woman shall never be heir together with the son of the free woman**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point is inheritance, not affection. Genesis records that Abraham loved Ishmael and that the demand *cut Abraham deeply*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two cannot share the estate. That is the whole argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Genesis does with Ishmael"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God tells Abraham to do what Sarah says, and then: *also of the son of the bondwoman I will make a nation, because he is your seed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the desert, when the water runs out, *God heard the voice of the lad* — and Hagar is shown a well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael is provided for. He is not made the heir."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, brothers, **we are not children of the slave woman but of the free**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated as a fact about the readers, not as a goal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **for freedom that Christ has made us free**. Stand fast therefore, and **do not be entangled again with a yoke of bondage**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter break hides it. The famous sentence of Galatians 5 is the *therefore* of this paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being cast out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not people. Paul is arguing about a system — Sinai in the Hagar column, and the reader in the other one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's own summary: *you have become estranged from Christ, you who attempt to be justified by law; you have fallen from grace.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the two cannot both stay.** Not because one son was worse. Because an estate has one heir, and Paul says the two ways of getting a son cannot share it."}]}, "Galatians 4:4–7": {"title": "\"Born under the law\" — and the four steps out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul compresses the whole gospel into two sentences, and the logic runs in a chain where each link depends on the one before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**God sent forth His Son** — born of a woman, born under the law", "**to redeem those who were under the law**", "**so that we might receive the adoption as sons**", "**and because you are sons, God has sent forth the Spirit of His Son**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sendings, and the second follows the first. The Spirit is not sent to make sons; He is sent *because* they are sons. Paul is careful about the order, and it is the opposite of how the sequence is often felt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Born under the law\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christ is described as entering the exact position the people He came for were in. Not visiting it — born into it, under the same obligation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how the redemption works in Paul's argument: someone had to be genuinely inside the arrangement to buy anybody out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of His Son into your hearts, crying out, Abba, Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice who is doing the crying here. In Romans 8 it is *we* who cry Abba; here it is the Spirit. Paul says it both ways within two letters, which suggests he did not think of them as different events."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word *Abba* is Aramaic, left untranslated in a Greek letter to Galatia. See the note on Romans 8:15–17 for what it carries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are no longer a slave but a son; and if a son, then an heir of God through Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Slave, son, heir — three words, and the movement between them is the whole letter in miniature. Paul is writing to people being persuaded to go back under law, and his argument is not that it is wrong so much as that it is *backwards*: it is walking out of the family and asking for a job."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"The fullness of the time.\"** Paul's phrase for the timing. Not that the world was at its best, but that a particular moment had filled up — a Roman peace, Greek as a common language, roads, and a scattered Jewish diaspora with synagogues in every port. He treats the arrangement of an era as deliberate."}]}, "Galatians 5:1": {"title": "Stand firm in freedom", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and it is the hinge of the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter division hides it. This is the *therefore* of the allegory that ends chapter 4 — Hagar and Sarah, the slave woman cast out, and *we are not children of the slave woman but of the free*. See the note on Galatians 4:28–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So stand firm **in the freedom by which Christ has set us free**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *for freedom Christ has made us free* — **te eleutheria**, in the dative, which can be read as the purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Freedom is named as the point of the setting free, not as a by-product of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stand firm.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **stekete** — stand fast, hold your ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it in Ephesians of the armour — *having done all, to stand* — and in Philippians: *stand fast in the Lord*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is to be advanced or won. The instruction is to remain where they were put."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is already done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By which **Christ has set us free**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is aorist. It happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not asking them to get free. He is telling free people not to go back in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **do not let yourselves be harnessed again into a yoke of slavery**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zugo douleias** — a yoke of bondage. A yoke is the crossbar laid over working animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Again** — the Greek is **palin**. They had been under one and had come out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the yoke was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Circumcision and the calendar. Paul says so in the next verse: *if you become circumcised, Christ will profit you nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter used the same image at the council in Jerusalem: *why do you test God by putting a yoke on the neck of the disciples which neither our fathers nor we were able to bear?* See the note on Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other yoke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take **My yoke** upon you and learn from Me… **for My yoke is easy and My burden is light**.\"* See the note on Matthew 11:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two yokes in the New Testament. One is described as slavery and one as rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have become estranged from Christ, you who attempt to be justified by law; **you have fallen from grace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exepesate** — fallen out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's own definition of the phrase is in the clause before it: *you who attempt to be justified by law*. See the note on Galatians 5:2–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the freedom is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **you were called to liberty; only do not use liberty as an opportunity for the flesh**, but through love serve one another.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *opportunity* is **aphorme** — the bridgehead word, the same one he used of sin using the commandment as a launching point. See the note on Romans 7:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names both ditches in the same chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **all the law is fulfilled in one word**, even in this: \"**You shall love your neighbour as yourself**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape of the instruction.** Not go, not fight, not achieve. Stand — and the only thing named as a danger is being put back under something they had already come out from."}]}, "Galatians 5:16–18": {"title": "\"If the Spirit is leading you\" — what Paul concludes from it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses that get quoted for the fight and almost never for the conclusion. Paul describes a genuine conflict, and then draws from it a conclusion nobody expects."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Walk by the Spirit, and you will not carry out what the flesh craves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is worth noticing. The instruction is *walk by the Spirit* — a positive, forward thing. The not-carrying-out is presented as the **result**, not as a second command to be worked at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say fight the craving and then walk. He says walk, and the other follows. In Greek the second half is emphatic — a strong negative, closer to *you certainly will not*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fight is real"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The flesh sets its desire against the Spirit, and the Spirit against the flesh; the two of them stand opposed to each other, so that you do not do the things you want to do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No spiritual life is being sold here as effortless. Paul describes two settled opposing pulls, and he says plainly that they get in the way of what a person means to do. Anyone who has tried to live this knows the sentence is accurate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But notice which two things are opposed. Not the believer against sin. The Spirit against the flesh. The believer is the field this happens in, not one of the two combatants — which is a large part of why the instruction is *walk*, not *win*."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion he draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here is the sentence that gets left off:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "But if the Spirit is leading you, **you are not under the law.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given everything before it, you would expect the conclusion to be about victory, or about proving something. Instead Paul uses the reality of the Spirit's leading to settle a question about **jurisdiction**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole letter is about people being pushed back under law-keeping. So Paul's argument runs: you are being led by the Spirit, therefore that is not where you live. He treats being led as evidence of a legal standing, not as a performance to be assessed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that matters for guidance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "People bring this verse to decisions — am I being led? Paul brings it to identity. He does not use it to make anyone anxious about whether their leading is good enough; he uses it to tell them what their being led already proves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the same move he makes in Romans 8, where *led by the Spirit* is immediately followed by adoption, Abba, and inheritance. Both times, leading is raised as evidence of sonship rather than as a test of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"the things you want to do.\"** The line is often read as defeat — I cannot do what I want. In context Paul is describing why a person cannot simply execute their good intentions by willpower, which is exactly the argument for walking by the Spirit rather than trying harder. It is a diagnosis, and the prescription is in the first line of the paragraph."}]}, "Galatians 5:19–21": {"title": "The works of the flesh", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists them, and the list is longer and more ordinary than people expect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The works of the flesh are **out in the open, plain for anyone to see**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phanera** — manifest, evident."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No detection is required. They surface."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "***Works** of the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plural, and it is set against **fruit** of the Spirit in the next paragraph, which is singular. Paul chose both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Works are produced; fruit grows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen items, and they fall into groups:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sexual** — *adultery, sexual immorality, uncleanness, indecency*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Religious** — *idolatry, sorcery*. The Greek for sorcery is **pharmakeia**, the root of *pharmacy* — it covered drugs and potions as well as spells."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Social** — *hatred, quarrels, jealousy, outbursts of wrath, selfish ambition, dissensions, factions, envy*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Violent and excessive** — *murders, drunkenness, wild parties*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proportion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight of the seventeen are relational. Quarrels, jealousy, ambition, dissensions, factions and envy are the ones a church would recognise in itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to congregations tearing at each other over circumcision, and he had already told them: *if you bite and devour one another, beware lest you be consumed by one another.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The catch-all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **anything else of the same kind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list is open. He is describing a category, not compiling a code."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am telling you about these **ahead of time, just as I told you before**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it in person and is saying it again in writing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wording of the verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That those who **make a practice of such things** will not inherit the kingdom of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prassontes** — a present participle, continuous. Those who go on doing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It describes a habit rather than an incident, and Paul is careful with the tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control**. Against such there is no law.* See the note on Galatians 5:22–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Fruit** is singular in the Greek — one fruit with nine facets, not nine separate achievements."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction that governs both lists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses before the works: *I say then: **walk in the Spirit, and you shall not fulfil the lust of the flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after the fruit: *if we live in the Spirit, **let us also walk in the Spirit**.* See the note on Galatians 5:25–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lists are bracketed by the same instruction, twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does not offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A method for suppressing the first list. The chapter gives one instruction, and the fruit is described as growing rather than being assembled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last two items of the fruit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gentleness, self-control.* Both are on the second list, and neither is described as produced by effort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the list is mostly made of.** Not the spectacular sins. Eight of the seventeen are things that happen between people in a room — and Paul was writing to churches doing exactly that to each other."}]}, "Galatians 5:2–4": {"title": "What \"fallen away from grace\" is actually describing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Few verses have frightened more people than this one, and nearly always for the opposite of the reason Paul wrote it. Read quickly, \"you have fallen away from grace\" sounds like a warning that grace can run out — that a person can sin past the edge of it and be dropped. Read in the sentence Paul actually built, it says something closer to the reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Look at who he is describing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names them in the same breath: *you who are being made right by the law*. That is the person in view. Not someone who has sinned badly — someone who has decided to earn their standing with God by keeping rules. The whole letter aims at that one move."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is addressed to the person who has failed. It is addressed to the person who has started keeping score."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two pictures underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words carry the verse, and both are stranger in Greek than they sound in English."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**cut off** — the word means to be left with nothing to do, put out of work, made idle. Not cut in half.", "**fallen away** — a word used of a ship blown off its course, and of a flower dropping off its stem."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Put together, the picture is not of grace pulling back from a person. It is of a person stepping off the ground they were standing on. Christ has not run out of power; He has been *given nothing to do* by someone who decided to handle their standing themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says two lines later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes straight on: *we, through the Spirit, wait with longing for the righteousness we hope for* — and he says **we**. Having just written the hardest sentence in the letter, he puts himself back in the same boat as the people he wrote it to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not telling them what they have permanently become. He is telling them what their position costs them while they hold it, which is a different thing. That is why the same chapter opens with *Christ has set us free*. A person cannot stand on two grounds at once, and the ground they were reaching for cannot hold anyone up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this matters practically"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If grace could be forfeited by sinning, the remedy would be to sin less. Paul's remedy is the opposite: stop trying to be made right by what you do. The fall he describes is not downward, into failure. It is sideways, out of the only thing that was holding anyone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "A word about *grace* itself. In Greek it is not leniency, and it is not a mood God happens to be in. It runs from favour freely given through to the working of that favour in a person's life. So \"fallen away from grace\" is not \"fallen out of God's good books.\" It is nearer to having stepped out of the current that was doing the work."}]}, "Galatians 5:22–24": {"title": "The fruit the Spirit grows", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine words, and the noun in front of them is singular."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Fruit, not fruits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **fruit** the Spirit grows is…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One fruit with nine characteristics, not nine items to be collected separately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And set it against the phrase Paul used eight verses earlier: *the **works** of the flesh are evident.* Works, plural, and manufactured. Fruit, singular, and grown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody grows fruit. A tree does, and what the farmer does is stay attached to the soil and the season."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose fruit it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fruit **the Spirit** grows.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The genitive is His. It is not the believer's fruit produced with the Spirit's assistance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus said the same in the vine passage: *apart from Me you can do nothing*, and the branch's whole job is to remain. See the note on John 15:5–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Love, joy, peace** — Godward, and the first three."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Patience, kindness, goodness** — toward other people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Faithfulness, gentleness, self-control** — the inward three, and it is worth noticing where self-control lands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is last, and it is fruit. Not the root of the list but the ninth thing grown out of it. The one quality most people try to start with is the one Paul puts at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **no law standing against things like these**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent five chapters arguing against putting believers under law, and here is his answer to the obvious objection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing on that list needs legislating. No statute was ever passed against joy. Produce this and the law has no business with you — not because it was lowered, but because it has been outgrown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it another way in Romans: *love is the fulfilment of the law.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the flesh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who belong to Christ **have crucified** the flesh with its passions and its cravings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense — done, and standing done. Not *should crucify*, and not *are crucifying daily*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reports it as an accomplished fact about everyone who belongs to Christ, the same way he reports it in Romans 6: *our old man was crucified with Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is only one, and it is four verses earlier: *walk in the Spirit, and you shall not fulfil the lust of the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A promise attached to a direction of travel. Nothing in the paragraph tells anybody to work on the list."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul feared for them.** *You have become estranged from Christ, you who attempt to be justified by law; you have fallen from grace.* That is the letter's charge — not immorality. The Galatians' problem was that they had started trying, and Paul's remedy is a fruit that grows."}]}, "Galatians 5:25–26": {"title": "Keep in step", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two sentences at the end of the fruit passage, and the second one is oddly small after everything that came before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The premise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the Spirit is our life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a condition being tested. Paul is stating what is already true of the people he is writing to, and building the instruction on top of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then let us **keep in step** with the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **stoicheo** — a military word, to march in line, to keep rank. It is not the word Paul used earlier in the chapter for *walk in the Spirit* (**peripateo**, the ordinary word for going about)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This one is about following a lead you did not set."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Life first, then the walk. The stepping does not produce the life; it follows it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole letter is built against the reverse order. See the note on Galatians 3:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is warned against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **not become full of ourselves**, **provoking** one another, **envying** one another.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three items, and they run in sequence: conceit, then provocation, then envy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *full of ourselves* is **kenodoxos** — empty glory. *Provoking* is **prokaleo**, to challenge, to call somebody out to fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why these three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just listed the works of the flesh and the fruit of the Spirit — nine of each, in the verses immediately before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having named adultery, idolatry and murder on one side, he ends the passage on conceit and rivalry between believers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The small things are the ones the churches in Galatia were actually doing to each other: *if you bite and devour one another, beware lest you be consumed by one another.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fruit is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love, joy, peace, longsuffering, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Fruit** is singular in the Greek. One fruit with nine facets — not nine separate achievements to be worked up one at a time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against such there is no law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brethren, if a man is overtaken in any trespass, you who are spiritual **restore such a one in a spirit of gentleness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *restore* is **katartizo** — the word for setting a broken bone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first thing Paul asks of people who are keeping in step. See the note on Galatians 6:1–2."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the marching image gives you.** In a line, nobody sets their own pace and nobody is out in front. Conceit and rivalry are simply what breaking rank looks like."}]}, "Genesis 1:26–31": {"title": "\"Let us make human beings in our own image\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sixth day, and the account changes register for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Let us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything before this is *let there be*. Here God speaks about the work rather than commanding it into existence — a deliberation, in the plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christian readers have long heard the Trinity in it, and the New Testament supports the direction: all things were made through the Son, and the Spirit was hovering over the waters in verse 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the image of God He created them; male and female He created them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said three times in one verse, which is how Hebrew emphasises. And the second clause is part of the definition, not an afterthought — the image is carried by humanity as male and female together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for image is used elsewhere of a statue set up to represent a ruler in a province he could not visit in person. That is the job description: a representation, placed where the king is not visibly present."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have children, and multiply, and fill the earth, and tend it. I am placing it all in your care.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older translations say *subdue* and *have dominion*, and the words are strong. But they are attached to the image — this is rule exercised on behalf of someone else, by people made to look like Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Very good\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then God looked over everything He had made — and it was not merely good, it was very good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refrain through the chapter has been *good*, five times. Only at the end, with humanity in it, does the assessment change."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter never says.** Nothing here is earned and nothing is conditional. The blessing, the food, the care of the earth and the image itself are all given before a single human being has done anything at all."}]}, "Genesis 1:3–5": {"title": "\"Let there be light\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first recorded action of God toward a dark world is that He spoke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was looking at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *the earth was without form and void, and darkness was on the face of the deep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the situation. Formless, empty, dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then God **spoke**. \"Let there be light.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not repair the darkness, examine it, or comment on how dark it was. He said what He wanted to be there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is three words: *yehi or* — and it is answered by two: *vayehi or.* Light was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"At once, effortlessly, simply because He said so\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no interval in the sentence. The saying and the being are the same moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews puts it this way: *the worlds were framed by the word of God, so that the things which are seen were not made of things which are visible.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Light before the sun"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sun, moon and stars are not made until the fourth day. There is light on the first day and no light source in the sky for three more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever this light is, it does not come from a lamp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"God looked at the light and loved what He saw\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing called **good** in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He *drew a line between the light and the darkness*. He separated them rather than mixing them or managing the balance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the pattern is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God is made in no one's image; man is made in His. And the first thing recorded of Him is that He faced a dark, empty, formless situation and said what He wanted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what He could see. What He wanted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs puts the same thing about us: *death and life are in the power of the tongue.* See the note on Proverbs 18:17–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Evening, then morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Evening came, then morning — the first day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew day runs from dark to light, not light to dark. Every one of the six days is counted that way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction is always toward the morning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the New Testament puts this verse.** *For it is the God who commanded light to shine out of darkness who has shone in our hearts, to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ.* Paul quotes day one and says the same command was spoken over a person."}]}, "Genesis 12:1–3": {"title": "The call built on promise", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Everything after Babel starts here, with one man and a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things to leave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Leave your country, your people, and your father's household**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, and they narrow as they go — nation, clan, then the household he was raised in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last is the hardest and it is named last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go to the land I will show you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a land I have shown you. **Will** show."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews notes it: *he went out, **not knowing where he was going**.* See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is given a direction and no destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Count the promises:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**I will make you into a great nation**", "**I will bless you**", "**I will make your name great**", "**you will be a blessing**", "**I will bless those who bless you**", "**whoever curses you I will curse**", "**through you all the families of the earth will be blessed**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven, and six of them begin with *I will*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will make **your name great**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babel is the chapter immediately before this. *Come, let us build ourselves a city, and a tower whose top is in the heavens; and **let us make a name for ourselves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters, two projects, and the same phrase. One group tries to build a name and is scattered; one man is given one and told to walk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Through you all the families of the earth will be blessed.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nations were just divided in chapter 10 and scattered in chapter 11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing said about the man God calls out of them is that they will all be blessed through him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul calls this line *the gospel preached beforehand to Abraham* — *in you all the nations shall be blessed.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No law, no covenant sign, no Scripture, no priest and no tabernacle. A sentence, and a road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abram departed as the LORD had spoken to him* — the next verse — *and Abram was seventy-five years old.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Illumination's note on it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A call built entirely on promise: God asking Abram to walk, and **God Himself carrying the outcome**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the seven is contingent on his performance. The only thing asked is the leaving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of Genesis 12 is Abram going down to Egypt in a famine and telling a king his wife was his sister. See the note on Genesis 12:10–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promises stand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the blessing and cursing clause turns up.** *I will bless those who bless you, and whoever curses you I will curse.* Balaam is hired to curse Israel and can only bless them, and quotes this line back at Balak: *blessed is he who blesses you, and cursed is he who curses you.*"}]}, "Genesis 12:10–20": {"title": "Abram goes down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with the greatest promise in the Old Testament and closes with the man who received it lying to a foreign king about his wife."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Went down\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram **went down** to Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is geographical — Egypt is lower ground — and Genesis uses it that way consistently. But the direction is the whole shape of the story."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set it beside Isaiah 2: *Come, let us **go up** to the mountain of the LORD.* Up is toward the house of God. Down is toward Egypt, and Abram was not told to go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no word from the LORD anywhere in this paragraph. That is the loudest thing in it. The chapter before is *the LORD had said to Abram*; here, nothing — he sees a famine and he moves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Say you are my sister, **so that I will be treated well for your sake** and my life will be spared because of you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He works out the arithmetic before he crosses the border. His wife is the price and his safety is the return."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is half telling the truth — Sarai was his half-sister — which is what makes it usable. It is the kind of lie that can be defended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he gained"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pharaoh treated Abram well for her sake, and Abram acquired sheep and cattle, donkeys, male and female servants, and **camels**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plan worked. He came out of Egypt richer than he went in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Male and female servants.** The next chapter has Lot's herdsmen quarrelling with Abram's because their possessions were too great for the land to hold them. And a few chapters later a woman appears in the household with a name and a country: *Hagar, her Egyptian maidservant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he picked up in Egypt he took home with him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who protected the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the LORD struck Pharaoh and his household with serious diseases because of Sarai, Abram's wife.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram does not fix this. He does not pray, confess, or intervene. God moves directly on Pharaoh's house to get Sarai back out of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise of a son through her was already given, and the promise is defended by the One who made it while the man who received it is silent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What have you done to me? Why didn't you tell me she was your wife?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pagan king rebukes the friend of God, and every word of the rebuke is just. Abram has no reply recorded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram went **up** from Egypt… to the place where his tent had been at the beginning… to the place of the altar he had made there at first, and there Abram called on the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first line of chapter 13. The way back is up, to the old tent site, to the altar he had already built."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not said.** Not one sentence of condemnation from God. The next time He speaks to Abram, in chapter 13, it is to enlarge the promise: *lift up your eyes and look… all the land you see I will give to you.* The detour is recorded in full, and it does not cost him the covenant."}]}, "Genesis 13:1–4": {"title": "Back to the altar he had built", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He goes back the way he came, and stops at the exact spot he left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Up from Egypt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abram **went up from Egypt** to the Negev, with his wife and everything he had.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is always *up* from Egypt and *down* into it, regardless of the map. The direction of travel in Scripture is theological before it is geographical."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he is coming up from is the episode where he called Sarai his sister and Pharaoh took her into the palace. See the note on Genesis 12:10–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he came out with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram had become **very wealthy in livestock, silver, and gold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kabed** — heavy. The word for glory shares the root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He arrived in Egypt driven by famine, told a half-truth that cost him his wife, was found out by a pagan king and escorted to the border — and he leaves heavy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is taken back off him. Verse 2 sits there without a word of comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Everything he had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With his wife and **everything he had**, and Lot went with him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarai is back. Lot is still with him — and the wealth in this verse is what makes them part company nine verses later, because *the land was not able to support them dwelling together.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The return route"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the Negev he journeyed **from place to place** until he came back to Bethel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *by his journeys* — the same stages, in reverse. He retraces the trip."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **the spot between Bethel and Ai** where he **had first pitched his tent and built an altar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethel means *house of God*. Ai means *ruin*. He pitches between them, as he had before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses records the location twice — in chapter 12 and again here — with the same wording, so the reader knows it is the same ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There Abram **called on the name of the LORD once more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes back to the altar and picks up where he stopped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no sacrifice recorded, no confession, no vow. He calls on the name, which is what he had been doing before he went down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No rebuke. God does not appear in chapter 13 until Lot has gone, and when He does the first words are *lift up your eyes now and look.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses tells the Egypt story plainly and then tells this one plainly, and leaves the two side by side."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the altar was for.** He built one at Shechem when the land was promised, and one here. He builds another at Hebron at the end of the chapter. The altars mark the route, and this one was still standing when he came back to it."}]}, "Genesis 13:14–18": {"title": "\"Look around you, from where you are\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God speaks to Abram at the moment he has just given away the best land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **Lot had gone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram had offered his nephew first choice: *if you take the left, then I will go to the right*. Lot *lifted his eyes and saw all the plain of Jordan, that it was well watered everywhere* and took it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram is left with the hill country, and the narrator adds a note about where Lot went: *the men of Sodom were exceedingly wicked and sinful against the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God speaks after the choice has been made and cannot be revisited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Look around you, from where you are**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *lift up your eyes now and look from the place where you are*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**From where you are.** Not from a better vantage point, and not after moving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lot had lifted his eyes and chosen. Abram is told to lift his and look at what is left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four directions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To the **north and south**, the **east and west**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**All the land you see I will give to you and your offspring forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of it, including the plain Lot took."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offspring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will make your offspring **like the dust of the earth** — **if anyone could count the dust, then your descendants could be counted**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dust here; stars in chapter 15, when he is taken outside at night; sand on the seashore in chapter 22, after the mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three images, each attached to a different moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dust is what is underfoot — and God had said in the previous chapter that Abram would walk this land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The walking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go, walk through the length and breadth of the land**, for **I am giving it to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present participle: *I am giving*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told to walk over ground he does not own, on the strength of a sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction runs through Joshua: *every place your foot touches, I am handing over to you*. See the note on Joshua 1:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How much he owned when he died"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One field with a cave in it, at Hebron, bought for four hundred shekels of silver to bury Sarah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen says it at his trial: *and God gave him **no inheritance in it, not even enough to set his foot on**. But even when Abraham had no child, He promised to give it to him for a possession.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abram **moved his tent** and went to live near **the great trees of Mamre at Hebron**, and there **he built an altar to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He moves, and builds. Genesis marks his route by altars — Shechem, Bethel, and now Hebron."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Hebron is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Machpelah is there. The cave he later buys, where Sarah, Abraham, Isaac, Rebekah, Leah and Jacob are all buried."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He pitches his tent beside the only piece of the country his family would ever hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says about the tents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith he dwelt in the land of promise as in a foreign country, **dwelling in tents**… for he waited for the city which has foundations, whose builder and maker is God.* See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When it is said.** After the choice went the other way. Lot lifted his eyes and picked the watered plain; Abram is told to lift his and look at everything from where he is standing."}]}, "Genesis 14:17–20": {"title": "Melchizedek brings out bread and wine", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A priest-king appears in three verses, blesses Abram, and vanishes from the narrative entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two kings come out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **king of Sodom** came out to meet him… And **Melchizedek king of Salem** brought out bread and wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both arrive at the same moment, and Abram deals with both. One offers him goods; one offers him bread and wine and a blessing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He accepts from one and refuses the other. See the note on Genesis 14:21–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Melchizedek** — *melek tsedeq*, king of righteousness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Salem** — peace. Hebrews makes both points: *first being translated king of righteousness, and then also king of Salem, meaning king of peace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Salem is the older name of Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bread and wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a sacrifice, though he is called *priest of God Most High*. There is no altar and no animal in the passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and wine, carried out to a man coming back from a battle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was priest of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "***El Elyon***, God Most High — and he uses the title twice more in his blessing: *Creator of heaven and earth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a Canaanite king in the middle of a chapter full of pagan kings, and he knows God by name. Abram accepts the blessing without hesitation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed be Abram** by God Most High… and **blessed be God Most High**, who delivered your enemies into your hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He blesses the man and then blesses God, and the second half tells Abram who won the battle he had just fought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tenth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Abram gave him **a tenth of everything**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first tithe in Scripture, and everything about the setting matters:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Four hundred and thirty years before the law", "No commandment existed, no tabernacle, no priesthood to fund", "Abram is not an Israelite; there is no Israel", "Nobody asked him for it"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had just been blessed, and he gave a tenth of the spoils to the man who blessed him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for tithe is **maaser**, and inside it sits **asar** — the root behind **osher**, wealth, and **ashir**, rich."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenth and the riches are built on the same three letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and wine first. Then the blessing. Then the tenth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is asked for and nothing is negotiated. Melchizedek arrives carrying something, says two blessings, and Abram responds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now think how big a figure this must be. **Abraham himself, the father of the whole family**, handed him a tenth of what he had taken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs on rank: the lesser is blessed by the greater. Melchizedek blessed Abraham and received from him, so Melchizedek is greater — and Levi, still in Abraham's body, paid through him. See the notes on Hebrews 7:4–5 and Hebrews 7:9–10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the record leaves out.** No father, no mother, no genealogy, no birth, no death. Hebrews notices all four omissions and says he is *made like the Son of God, remaining a priest continually.* Three verses in Genesis, one line in Psalm 110, and two chapters of Hebrews built on them."}]}, "Genesis 14:21–24": {"title": "\"Not even a thread\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Abram turns down a fortune in a single sentence, and gives his reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give me the people and keep the goods for yourself.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the war conventions of the time everything was Abram's — he had won it. The king of Sodom is asking for a favour and calling it a deal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a fortune, offered legitimately, with no strings visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have raised my hand to the LORD, God Most High, Creator of heaven and earth, and sworn an oath.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already sworn — the Hebrew is past tense. This was decided before the king opened his mouth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notice the title he uses: *God Most High, Creator of heaven and earth* — word for word what Melchizedek had just called Him in the blessing three verses earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the priest's language and swears by it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will accept nothing belonging to you, **not even a thread or the strap of a sandal**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two most worthless items he can name. A loose thread and a sandal lace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that you can never say, 'I made Abram rich.'**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole of it. Not that the money was dirty, or that he did not need it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He would not have anyone else able to claim credit for his prosperity — and Sodom in particular."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did take"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will take nothing but **what my men have eaten** and **the share belonging to the men who went with me** — Aner, Eshcol, and Mamre. **Let them have their share**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refuses on his own behalf and refuses to impose the decision on anyone else. His allies are Amorites; they get their portion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The conviction is his, and he does not legislate it for other people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two transactions, in order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Within the same hour:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**gave** a tenth to the priest of God Most High", "**refused** everything from the king of Sodom"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gave to one and would take nothing from the other, and the two decisions are the same decision seen from both ends: about who he wanted to be indebted to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verse — Genesis 15:1:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be afraid, Abram. **I am your shield, your exceedingly great reward.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He turned down a reward and was told who his reward was. The Hebrew for *great reward* is the word for wages."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happened to the goods he refused.** Sodom is destroyed five chapters later, and Lot — who went back to live in it — comes out with nothing but his daughters and the clothes he stands in. Abram, who took not a thread of it, dies *an old man and full of years*, rich in livestock, silver and gold."}]}, "Genesis 15:1": {"title": "\"I Myself am your shield\" — what Abram had just turned down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*After all this* — three words that make the verse. To feel it you have to know what just happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram had just done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 14: he rescues Lot by attacking a coalition of kings at night with 318 men. On the way back he meets Melchizedek, gives him a tenth of everything, and then the king of Sodom offers him all the recovered goods."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram refuses. He will not take *a thread or a sandal strap*, so that no one can ever say the king of Sodom made him rich."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So he has just fought a war, given away a tenth, and declined a fortune. Then night falls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Don't be afraid, Abram. I Myself am your shield, your protector. And your reward will be very great.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves answer something specific:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**shield** — he has just made permanent enemies of four kings", "**reward** — he has just refused payment"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for reward is a plain one for wages or spoils. God uses the vocabulary of the thing Abram walked away from and applies it to Himself."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Abram says next.** He does not thank God. He says: what can You give me, since I remain childless? The great promise lands on a man whose actual complaint is elsewhere entirely — and God takes him outside to look at the stars rather than rebuking him for it."}]}, "Genesis 15:12–16": {"title": "\"Afterward they will come out with great wealth\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Abram is asleep, and God tells him four hundred years of his family's history before any of it exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The darkness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A deep sleep fell over Abram, and **a thick, terrifying darkness** settled on him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *deep sleep* is **tardemah** — the same word used of Adam when the woman was taken out of his side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is unconscious for the covenant. What follows in the chapter is a smoking firepot and a burning torch passing between the divided animals, and only one party walks the path. Abram is asleep for the whole thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four hundred years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your descendants will live as strangers in a land not their own. **They will be enslaved and mistreated for four hundred years**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told in advance. The slavery is not a surprise God responds to later; it is announced to Abram while Isaac is not yet born."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the sentence after it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But I will judge the nation that enslaves them, and afterward your people will come out with great wealth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Great wealth** — the Hebrew is a large substance, heavy possessions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of unpaid work, and God says at the start how the account will be settled. What happened on the night of the exodus was not a windfall; it was the back wages, promised centuries before the first brick was laid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus records it plainly: *the LORD had made the Egyptians so favourable toward His people that they gave them whatever they asked. And so Israel walked out carrying the wealth of Egypt.* See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in Abram's life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters earlier, Abram had come back from rescuing Lot, met Melchizedek, and *gave him a tenth of everything.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refused a penny of the king of Sodom's — *I will not take a thread or a sandal-strap, lest you should say, \"I have made Abram rich.\"* He tithed to the priest and turned down the king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, in chapter 15, God says *I am your shield, your **exceedingly great reward*** — and lays out the four hundred years and the great wealth on the far side of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You will go to your ancestors in peace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of a prophecy about slavery, one line addressed to the man himself: *you will be buried in a good old age.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not going to see any of it. He is told anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Amorites"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In the fourth generation your descendants will return here, **for the wrongdoing of the Amorites is not yet complete**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason for the delay is not about Israel at all. Another people is being given four centuries."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who walked between the pieces.** In that culture both parties to a covenant passed between the divided animals, invoking the same fate on themselves if they broke it. Abram was asleep. Only the fire went through, which means the covenant was cut on one signature."}]}, "Genesis 15:4–5": {"title": "Count the stars", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God takes a childless old man outside at night and gives him something to look at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No. That man will not be your heir.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram had proposed Eliezer of Damascus, his steward. It was the sensible legal arrangement of that world for a man with no son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God refuses the workaround. *A son from your own body — he will be your heir.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Outside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And God **led him outside into the night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is taken out of the tent. Whatever conversation was happening indoors, it continues under the sky."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look up. **Count the stars**, if you can even number them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is **saphar** — and it carries both senses at once. To count, and to recount: to tell, to declare, to narrate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the root behind **sepher**, a book or scroll, and **sopher**, a scribe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the instruction has two readings sitting in one word: number them, and read what they tell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the heavens are said to be doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 19 puts it plainly: *the heavens **declare** the glory of God, and the firmament **shows His handiwork**. Day unto day utters speech, and night unto night **reveals knowledge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *there is no speech nor language where their voice is not heard. Their line has gone out through all the earth, and their words to the end of the world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sky is described as speech going out to every nation, with no language barrier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The story told there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account runs from the virgin to the lion — from **Virgo**, the woman with the seed, to **Leo**, the lion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 3:15, spoken in the garden: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel* — the seed of the woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation 5:5: *the **Lion** out of Judah's tribe, the Root of David, has won.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first promise and the final victory, and the whole of it written overhead where Abram was told to look. See the notes on Genesis 3:14–15 and Revelation 5:5–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he believed the LORD, and He counted it to him as righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the very next verse — the sentence Romans and Galatians are built on. It follows a night sky and nothing else. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sand, later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Genesis 22, after Isaac on the mountain, God adds the second image: *as the stars of the heaven and as the sand which is on the seashore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two innumerable things, and both given as a picture before there was one child."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How long he had to hold it.** Isaac is born in chapter 21. Between this night and that morning is roughly a quarter of a century, and a stretch of it he spent being called *father of a multitude* with no son by his wife. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}]}, "Genesis 15:6": {"title": "Credited as righteousness", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine words in English, and three New Testament books are built on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Abram **believed** God, and God **counted it to him as righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *believed* is **aman** — to lean on, to trust, to find something firm enough to put weight on. It is the root of **amen**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was believing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before it: God takes him outside at night and says *look now toward heaven, and count the stars if you are able to number them. So shall your descendants be.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram is seventy-five, childless, with a barren wife. There is nothing to inspect except the sky and the promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Counted\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chashab** — to reckon, to impute, to enter into an account. Paul's Greek for it is **logizomai**, the bookkeeping word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness was **credited** to him. Not produced by him, not earned, not demonstrated. Entered on his account by the One keeping the books."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram had not done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was no law. Sinai is four hundred and thirty years away — no commandments, no tabernacle, no priesthood, no sacrifice system, no circumcision yet either. Paul makes a great deal of that last one: the declaration came *while he was still uncircumcised*, so that he could be the father of everyone who believes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not built anything, kept anything, or performed anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He believed, and the entry was made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram did afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Went down to Egypt and told a king his wife was his sister. Took Hagar because the promise seemed slow. Laughed on his face when God said Sarah would have a son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The entry stood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three books that quote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul in Romans 4, to prove righteousness is credited rather than earned. Paul again in Galatians 3, to prove the blessing of Abraham comes by faith. And James 2, from the other side, on what a living faith looks like."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three arguments, one verse, and all three treat it as the founding precedent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God calls him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The offspring of **Abraham My friend**.* Isaiah says it, and 2 Chronicles and James say it too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only man in Scripture God calls His friend, and the friendship is dated from a night when he had nothing but a sky full of stars and a sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter does next.** The covenant is cut — animals divided, and Abram put into a deep sleep while a smoking firepot and a burning torch pass between the pieces alone. He was asleep for the signing. See the note on Genesis 15:12–16."}]}, "Genesis 17:1–2": {"title": "\"I am God Almighty\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Thirteen years of silence end with a new name for God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The age"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Abram was **ninety-nine years old**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael was born when Abram was eighty-six, at the end of chapter 16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between that chapter and this one, Genesis records nothing. Thirteen years, and no appearance, no word, and no conversation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The silence begins after the attempt to produce the heir by arrangement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am God Almighty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **El Shaddai**, and this is the first time it appears in Scripture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The derivation is uncertain and has been argued over for centuries — the older sense may be *the mountain one*, or connect to a root for sufficiency."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It becomes the patriarchs' name for God. Exodus says so explicitly: *I appeared to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob, as **God Almighty**, but by My name LORD I was not known to them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man of ninety-nine is about to be told he will have a son by a wife of ninety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name given at that moment is the one about sufficiency."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Walk before Me, and be blameless**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tamim** — complete, whole, without blemish. It is the word used of a sacrificial animal, and of Noah: *Noah was a just man, **perfect** in his generations*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Walk before Me** — the Hebrew is *walk to My face*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enoch and Noah *walked with*. Abram is told to walk before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **make My covenant between Me and you**, and **I will multiply you greatly**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *multiply* is doubled — *multiply you exceedingly*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Abram **fell on his face**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The names change. **Abram** — exalted father — becomes **Abraham**, father of a multitude. **Sarai** becomes **Sarah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both new names have a **hei** inserted, the letter of the divine name, and Jewish commentary has noted it for a very long time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The laugh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Abraham **fell on his face and laughed**, and said in his heart, \"Shall a child be born to a man who is a hundred years old? And shall Sarah, who is ninety years old, bear a child?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**Oh, that Ishmael might live before You!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has a son already and would rather the promise attached to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No, Sarah your wife shall bear you a son**, and you shall call his name **Isaac**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yitzhaq** — *he laughs*. The name is given in the same conversation as the laughing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarah laughs too, one chapter later, behind a tent door, and denies it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is required in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Circumcision — *the sign of the covenant*, given here and not before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes the chronology his whole argument: Genesis 15 has Abram counted righteous by believing; Genesis 17 has the sign. Fourteen years apart, in that order. See the note on Romans 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says about the pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith **Sarah herself also received strength to conceive seed**, and she bore a child when she was past the age, because she judged Him faithful who had promised.* See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the name means in that room.** A man of ninety-nine, thirteen years after trying to arrange the heir himself, is told the name **El Shaddai** — and then told the son is coming through the ninety-year-old."}]}, "Genesis 17:15–16": {"title": "Sarai becomes Sarah", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The wife gets a new name too, and hers is the more improbable of the two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for Sarai your wife, **you are no longer to call her Sarai; her name will be Sarah**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference in Hebrew is one letter. **Sarai** to **Sarah** — and the letter added is *hey*, which is also the letter added into Abram to make Abraham."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same letter, out of God's own name, put into both of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sarah** is princess, noblewoman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was true of her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ninety years old. Barren her entire life — the very first thing Genesis records about her, back in chapter 11, is *Sarai was barren; she had no child.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is her introduction in Scripture, and it stood for sixty-five years of narrative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says over her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will bless her**, and I will give you a son by her. **I will bless her so that she will become nations; kings of peoples will come from her.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will bless her* — twice in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nations.** **Kings of peoples.** Said of a woman who had never carried a child."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who has to say the name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham does. *You are no longer to call her Sarai.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So for the year before Isaac, a man of ninety-nine called *father of a multitude* was addressing his ninety-year-old barren wife as *princess*, and the whole household heard both names every day. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham did in the next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Abraham fell on his face **and laughed**, and said in his heart, \"Shall a child be born to a man who is a hundred years old? And shall Sarah, who is ninety years old, bear a child?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He laughs, face down, doing the arithmetic."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarah laughs too, a chapter later, behind the tent door. And the boy is named **Isaac** — *he laughs*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament says about her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith Sarah herself also received strength to conceive… because she judged Him faithful who had promised.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The laughing is not mentioned. The reckoning is. See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter holds her up as an example to wives, calling her a woman of *a gentle and quiet spirit* — which is not the phrase most readers would have chosen for the woman who threw Hagar out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name in the name.** The letter added to both of them, *hey*, appears twice in the covenant name YHWH. Whatever else the renaming did, it put something of His own name into theirs before either promise came true."}]}, "Genesis 17:3–8": {"title": "Abram becomes Abraham", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God changes a man's name to a claim that is not yet true, and makes him answer to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ninety-nine years old. Twenty-four years since the promise was first made. One son, Ishmael, by a servant girl, and none by Sarah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram fell facedown.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Abram** — exalted father. That name was already a stretch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Abraham** — *ab hamon*, father of a multitude."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No longer will you be called Abram; your name will be Abraham, **for I have made you the father of many nations**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **have made** you.* Past tense, completed, spoken to a childless man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I will make you*, though the promises around it are future — *nations will come from you, kings will be among your descendants*. This one clause is put in the past, and it is the clause the name is built on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes exactly this line in Romans 4 as his example of *the God who calls things that do not exist as though they already do.* See the note on Romans 4:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him to carry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every person in that camp — servants, herdsmen, his wife, the neighbours he traded with — now had to call him *father of a multitude*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For a year. Isaac is not born until chapter 21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man of ninety-nine, with no child by his wife, introducing himself by that name for a full year. Every use of it was either a joke or an act of faith, and Genesis records no complaint from him about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sarai too"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A few verses on: *As for Sarai your wife, you shall not call her name Sarai, but **Sarah** shall be her name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sarah** means princess. *And I will bless her, and she shall be a mother of nations; kings of peoples shall be from her.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ninety years old, barren her whole life, and renamed princess and mother of nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"everlasting\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "It appears three times in these six verses — an **everlasting** covenant, an **everlasting** possession, and *I will be their God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **olam** — perpetual, without a stated end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham fell facedown **and laughed**.* That is verse 17, and Genesis reports it without softening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He laughs, Sarah laughs in the next chapter, and the boy is named **Isaac** — *he laughs*. God took the laughter and made it the child's name."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not asked of him here.** Nothing. The whole passage is *I will* — I will make, I will keep, I will give, I will be their God. Circumcision comes in the next paragraph as the sign of a covenant already made, not a condition of getting one."}]}, "Genesis 2:1–3": {"title": "The seventh day", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first thing declared holy in the Bible is not a place or a person. It is a day, and it is a day off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It was finished\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so it was finished — the heavens, the earth, and everything in them, **down to the smallest detail**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *all the host of them*, and the verb is completion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same idea is spoken from the cross: **tetelestai**, it is finished. See the note on John 19:28–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He rested"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On that seventh day He rested. **Not because He was weary, but because the work was whole and good.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says it outright: *the everlasting God, the LORD, the Creator of the ends of the earth, **neither faints nor is weary**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest is not recovery. It is what you do when a thing is done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was blessed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So God **blessed the seventh day and set it apart as holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first use of the word **holy** in Scripture, and it is applied to a day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had blessed the creatures on day five and the man on day six. Now He blesses time itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What man's first day was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam was made on the sixth day, near the end of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the first full day of his existence was the seventh — the one God had already finished everything on and rested through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He began in a completed world, with nothing to do, on a day that was already blessed. Everything he ate had been grown before he arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing from this day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the six days before and each closes the same way: *and there was evening, and there was morning — the first day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seventh has no evening and no morning recorded. The formula stops."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews builds on that: *there remains therefore a rest for the people of God. For he who has entered His rest has himself also ceased from his works, as God did from His.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for rested is **shabath** — to cease, to stop. It is the root of **Sabbath**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not mean to relax. It means to stop working because the work is over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said about the day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when He healed on it and was challenged: *\"My Father has been working until now, and I have been working.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the pattern lands in the letters.** *For we who have believed do enter that rest… let us therefore be diligent to enter that rest.* Hebrews 4, and the effort named in it is the effort of stopping."}]}, "Genesis 2:15–17": {"title": "How much of the garden was permitted", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first command God gives a human being is usually remembered as a prohibition. Read the sentence and the proportions are the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Count the words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Eat freely from any tree in the garden — all of it is yours to enjoy. Only one tree is not for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Hebrew the permission is emphatic — the verb is doubled, a construction that intensifies: *eating you shall eat*. Every tree. Then one exception."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination's phrase for it is *a generous word*, and that is a fair reading of the grammar. The restriction exists inside an abundance, and it is a fraction of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at stake in the one tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The tree of the knowledge of good and evil.* Not a magic fruit. The name describes what taking it would mean: deciding for yourself what counts as good and what counts as evil, rather than receiving that from God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the serpent's pitch in the next chapter is exactly that — *you will be like God, knowing good and evil.* He is not offering something the tree does not offer. He is recommending it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"On the day you do, you will surely die.\"** They ate, and did not drop dead that afternoon, and readers have puzzled over it ever since. The Hebrew phrase can carry the sense of a sentence passed rather than an execution carried out — and the chapter that follows describes exactly that: exile, hard ground, a guarded gate, and a life now running toward dust. Something did die that day, and the rest of the Bible is about what it cost to undo."}]}, "Genesis 2:21–25": {"title": "The first marriage", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only thing in the creation account God called *not good*, and what He did about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The deep sleep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD God caused **a deep sleep** to fall over the man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tardemah** — the same word used of Abram in Genesis 15, when the covenant was cut and only the fire walked between the pieces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam is unconscious for the making of his bride. He contributes nothing but the material, and he was asleep while it was taken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was taken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God took **a part of his side** and closed the place up again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **tsela** is usually rendered rib, and it is the ordinary word for a side — the side of the ark, the side of the tabernacle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is made from the side, not from the head or the feet, and God does the fashioning. The verb is the one used of a builder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And He brought her to the man\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "God performs the introduction. The first marriage has a witness and a giver, and they are the same Person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Adam says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**At last** — this one is **bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew opens with an exclamation — *this time!* He had just named every animal and found *no helper suitable for him*, which is what makes the *at last* land."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes this exact phrase and applies it to the church: *we are parts of His body, **of His flesh and of His bones**.* See the note on Ephesians 5:28–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is why a man **leaves** his father and mother and **holds fast** to his wife, and the two **become one flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leave, hold fast, become one. In that order, and Jesus quotes the whole sentence in Matthew 19 as the definition, then adds *what God has joined together, let not man separate.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hold fast** is **dabaq** — to cling, to be glued to. It is used of skin sticking to bone, and of Ruth clinging to Naomi."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *one flesh* is not a metaphor for cooperation. The Hebrew is one flesh."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who is joined to a harlot is one body with her, for \"the two,\" He says, \"shall become one flesh.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for this verse to explain why sexual union outside marriage is not a minor thing. On his reading it does the same joining regardless of whether the covenant is there — which is his whole argument for why it is not casual."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were both naked, and **they felt no shame** — nothing to hide, nothing to fear, completely at home with each other.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two people fully known and entirely unafraid. That state lasts exactly one chapter, and the first thing the fall produces is *I was afraid, because I was naked, and I hid myself.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was not good.** *It is not good that man should be alone.* The only negative verdict in a chapter of *very good*, and it was pronounced over a man in a garden, in daily contact with God, with meaningful work and no sin. He still needed her."}]}, "Genesis 2:4–7": {"title": "Dust and breath", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two ingredients, and the account is careful about which one came from where."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD God **stooped down and formed** a man from the dust of the ground.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yatsar** — the potter's word. It is used of a craftsman working clay, and it implies hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else in the creation account is spoken into being. *Let there be light*, and light was. This one is made by hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dust"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the dust of the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Adamah** is ground, soil. **Adam** is the man. The name and the material are the same word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham says it to God: *I who am but dust and ashes.* And Psalm 103: *He knows our frame; He remembers that we are dust.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The breath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He **breathed into his nostrils the breath of life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Face to face. Whatever this is, it is not done at a distance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **neshamah** is breath, and it is used almost exclusively of God's breath and of human life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two make"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the man became **a living soul** — dust and breath, earth and God, joined together.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nephesh chayyah** — a living being. The whole man is the soul; the account does not describe a soul being installed in a body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dust on its own is dirt. Breath on its own is not a man. The person is the join."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was not there yet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No fields yet green, no plants yet sprung up, for the LORD God had **not sent rain**, and there was **no one to work the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two absences named, and only one of them is about the man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead a mist rose up from the earth and watered the whole surface of the soil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ground is watered before there is anybody to farm it. He arrives into a place already provided for. See the note on Genesis 2:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same breathing turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had said this, **He breathed on them**, and said to them, \"Receive the Holy Spirit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John 20, in a locked room on the evening of the resurrection. The Greek verb **emphusao** occurs once in the New Testament, and once in the Greek Old Testament — here, in Genesis 2:7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word for the first man and the new creation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And where it goes back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the dust will return to the earth as it was, and the spirit will return to God who gave it.* Ecclesiastes 12, taking the two components apart again."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does with the verse.** *\"The first man Adam became a living being.\" **The last Adam became a life-giving spirit.*** He quotes Genesis 2:7 and then sets a second man beside it — one who received life, and one who gives it."}]}, "Genesis 20:17–18": {"title": "The first prayer for healing in the Bible", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*So Abraham prayed to God, and God healed Abimelech.* It is the first time anyone in scripture prays for someone else's healing, and the circumstances are uncomfortable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is praying for whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham had just told a foreign king that Sarah was his sister — the same lie he had told in Egypt — and Abimelech had taken her into his household on the strength of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the man praying is the man whose deception caused the crisis. The man being prayed for is the injured party who behaved better throughout: God Himself says Abimelech acted *with a clear conscience and clean hands*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter God tells Abimelech to return the woman, and adds: *he is a prophet, and he will pray for you, and you will live*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is not that Abraham deserves to be heard. It is simply what is to happen. God assigns the intercession to the person in the wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD had closed up every womb in Abimelech's household.* The reason for the healing is only explained after it is granted — the writer holds it back to the last line of the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Abraham, who has been childless for decades and is one chapter away from Isaac, prays for another household's fertility and is answered."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage will not support.** It is not a model of a righteous man whose prayers were effective because of his conduct. Genesis records the lie in full, without excusing it, and then records the prayer being answered. Both things happened, and the text is not embarrassed by either."}]}, "Genesis 21:1–2": {"title": "\"Right on time, at the very season God had spoken of\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-five years after the promise, two verses record its arrival, and they are almost entirely made of repetition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repeated phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD came to Sarah, just as He had said He would. He did for her exactly what He had promised… right on time, at the very season God had spoken of.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in two verses: as He said, as He promised, at the appointed time. The Hebrew is even more insistent than the English."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer is not being redundant. He is making the only point that matters about a promise delayed by a quarter of a century — it did not arrive early, and it did not fail."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had happened in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The intervening chapters are not a story of patient waiting. Abraham laughed at the promise. Sarah laughed at it. They tried to arrange it themselves through Hagar, with consequences that run for the rest of Genesis. Twice Abraham passed his wife off as his sister to save himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of that is edited out, and none of it changes the timetable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac means *he laughs*. The child is named after the reaction the promise provoked, and Sarah says so herself in the verses following: *God has brought me laughter, and everyone who hears about this will laugh with me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joke told at God's expense becomes the boy's name."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line the Illumination adds.** *Nothing was too late. Nothing was forgotten.* It is a translator's summary rather than a separate clause in the Hebrew, and it is a fair rendering of what the triple repetition is doing. The passage exists to say that the delay was not neglect."}]}, "Genesis 21:8–11": {"title": "The feast and the demand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A party for a small boy, and it ends a household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The occasion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The child grew and was **weaned**, and on that day Abraham **threw a great feast**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weaning in that world came at two or three, and it was a milestone worth marking — the child had survived the most dangerous years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham is about a hundred and three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Sarah saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Sarah noticed **Ishmael — the son Hagar the Egyptian had borne to Abraham — mocking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **metsacheq** — playing, laughing, making sport."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same root as **Yitzhaq**, Isaac, which means *he laughs*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is genuinely ambiguous and translators divide over it. Genesis does not say what he was doing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reads it as persecution: *as the one born in the ordinary way of the flesh persecuted the one born according to the Spirit.* See the note on Galatians 4:28–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the text names them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ishmael — **the son Hagar the Egyptian had borne to Abraham**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator gives the full lineage in the middle of the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael is about seventeen at this point. He had been the only son for thirteen years before Isaac was born."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The demand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Send this slave woman and her son away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarah does not use either name. *This slave woman*, and *her son*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That boy **will never share the inheritance with my son, with Isaac**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall not be heir with my son*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The issue named is the estate. Paul quotes this clause as Scripture speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Abraham"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words **cut Abraham deeply**, for **Ishmael was his son too, and he loved him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the matter was very grievous in Abraham's sight because of his son*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records the father's grief and does not minimise it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *do not let it be displeasing in your sight because of the lad and because of your bondwoman. **Whatever Sarah has said to you, listen to her voice**, for in Isaac your seed shall be called.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *yet I will also make a nation of the son of the bondwoman, **because he is your seed***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. The demand is upheld and Ishmael is provided for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened in the desert"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and a skin of water, and a boy under a bush with his mother sitting a bowshot away because she could not watch him die."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **God heard the voice of the lad**… and God opened her eyes, and she saw a well of water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name **Ishmael** means *God hears*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham had asked for years earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Oh, that Ishmael might live before You!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's answer then: *Sarah your wife shall bear you a son, and you shall call his name Isaac… **and as for Ishmael, I have heard you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sons, one estate, and a father who loved both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reads it as two covenants. See the note on Galatians 4:21–27."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the day was.** A weaning feast — a celebration that a child had come through. Genesis puts the demand that broke the family on the same afternoon."}]}, "Genesis 22:1–2": {"title": "\"Every word landed heavier than the last\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening of the hardest chapter in Genesis, and the reader is told at the start what Abraham is not told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the reader knows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sometime later, **God tested Abraham**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nissah** — to test, to prove, to put to the trial."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses tells us in the first line. Abraham has no such sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James says God tempts nobody toward evil; this is the other word. See the note on James 1:13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He called to him, \"**Abraham**.\" And Abraham answered, \"**Here I am**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hinneni** — here I am, at your disposal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it three times in this chapter: to God here, to Isaac on the way up, and to the voice that stops him. See the note on Genesis 22:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take your son — your only son, Isaac, the one you love**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four, and each narrows:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Your son**", "**Your only son** — the Hebrew is **yachid**, only, unique. Ishmael has been sent away one chapter earlier. See the note on Genesis 21:8–11.", "**Isaac** — the name means *he laughs*", "**The one you love** — and this is the first time the word **ahav**, love, appears in the Bible"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first occurrence of love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jewish tradition notes it: the word for love enters Scripture here, in the sentence asking a father to give up the son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destination"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go to the land of Moriah**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "2 Chronicles: *Solomon began to build the house of the LORD at Jerusalem **on Mount Moriah***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same ridge, and about a three-day walk from Beersheba — which is what Genesis says it took."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There, **on a mountain I will show you**, offer him up to me as a burnt offering.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A mountain I will show you** — the same construction as the original call: *get out of your country… **to a land that I will show you***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years earlier, the same open ending."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not explained"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything. No reason is given, no assurance offered, and no alternative mentioned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line Moses adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every word landed heavier than the last: **your son, your only son, the one you love**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abraham **rose early in the morning**, saddled his donkey, split the wood, and went.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis gives no conversation with Sarah and no night of wrestling. It gives an itinerary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he said at the foot of the hill"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Stay here with the donkey; the lad and I will go yonder and worship, **and we will come back to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plural. Hebrews explains it: *he had worked it out that God could raise the dead.* See the note on Hebrews 11:17–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he said on the way up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac: *\"Behold, the fire and the wood, but **where is the lamb** for a burnt offering?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *\"My son, **God will provide for Himself the lamb**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was on the mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A ram caught in a thicket by its horns, behind him, already there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the place **Jehovah Jireh** — *the LORD will provide* — and Moses adds the saying still current when he wrote: *in the Mount of the LORD it shall be provided.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An oath, sworn by God on Himself, because there was nobody greater. See the note on Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word that enters Scripture here.** Love. Genesis has covered creation, a flood, Babel, and three generations of a family without using it — and the first time it appears, it is in the sentence about the son on the mountain."}]}, "Genesis 22:11–12": {"title": "The knife never fell", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hand is already up. Everything in these two verses is about timing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the angel of the LORD **called to him from heaven** — \"**Abraham! Abraham!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubled name. It is how the urgent moments in Scripture open — *Moses, Moses* at the bush; *Samuel, Samuel* in the night; *Simon, Simon* at the supper table. See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Here I am**,\" he answered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hinneni** — the same word he gave when God first called him at the top of the chapter, and the same word he gave Isaac on the way up when the boy asked where the lamb was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in one chapter. He answers the same way to God, to his son, and to the voice that stops him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the answer the Son gives in Hebrews: *So I said, Here I am… I have come to do what You want.* See the note on Hebrews 10:5–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not lay a hand on the boy**,\" the voice said. \"**Do nothing to him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, in two forms. The prohibition is absolute and immediate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is now known"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Now I know** that you honor God, for you have **not held back your son, your only son**, from me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *honor God* is *you are one who fears God* — **yare Elohim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Have not held back** is **chasak** — to withhold, to spare, to keep back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same idea about God in Romans: *He who **did not spare** His own Son, but delivered Him up for us all.* The one phrase Abraham is commended for is the one turned back on God Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Your son, your only son\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says it three times — in the command, here, and again in the oath at the end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael was alive. Isaac is the only son of the promise, the one the covenant runs through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The knife never fell.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses does not say what Isaac did, or what was said on the way down, or whether they spoke at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was already there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses on, Abraham looks up and there is a ram caught in a thicket by its horns — behind him, already on the mountain, while he was building the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the place **Jehovah Jireh**: *the LORD will provide.* And Moses adds the saying that was still current when he wrote: *in the Mount of the LORD it shall be provided.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the mountain is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The land of Moriah.* 2 Chronicles: *Solomon began to build the house of the LORD at Jerusalem on Mount Moriah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same ridge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham had worked out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews says he had reckoned that God could raise the dead — which is why he told the servants *we will come back to you*. See the note on Hebrews 11:17–22."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What stopped it.** A voice, at the last possible second, with the hand in the air. The chapter that opens *take now your son* closes with a substitute already caught in a bush."}]}, "Genesis 22:15–18": {"title": "\"I swear by myself\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The voice comes a second time, and this one is an oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The angel of the LORD called to Abraham from heaven **a second time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first stopped the knife. See the note on Genesis 22:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This one comes after the ram has been found in the thicket and offered, and after the place has been named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I swear by myself**, declares the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only time in Genesis God does this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has nothing higher to swear by, so He stakes Himself. Hebrews makes the whole argument out of that one clause. See the note on Hebrews 6:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because you have done this** and **have not held back your son, your only son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase as verse 12, repeated word for word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Have not held back** — the Hebrew is **chasak**, to withhold, to spare."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul turns it around in Romans: *He who **did not spare** His own Son, but delivered Him up for us all.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will surely bless you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles the verb — *blessing I will bless you*. Hebrews carries it straight over into Greek. See the note on Hebrews 6:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two measures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will make your descendants **as many as the stars in the sky** and **as the sand on the seashore**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both had been used before — the stars in Genesis 15, when Abram was taken outside at night, and the dust of the earth in Genesis 13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here both, together, at the end of the hardest thing he ever did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gates"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your descendants will **possess the gates of their enemies**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gate was where a city's business was done and where it was defended. To hold the gate is to hold the city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **through your offspring all nations on earth will be blessed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the clause that reaches past Israel. It was in the first call, in Genesis 12, and it is repeated here at the end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reads it precisely: *He does not say, \"And to seeds,\" as of many, but as of one, \"And to your Seed,\" **who is Christ**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it rests on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because you have obeyed my voice**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated twice in the paragraph — *because you have done this*, and *because you have obeyed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant was given free in Genesis 12 and 15, and Genesis 22 attaches an oath to it, after a mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the mountain was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The land of Moriah.* 2 Chronicles: *Solomon began to build the house of the LORD at Jerusalem **on Mount Moriah**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name Abraham gave the place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Jehovah Jireh** — *the LORD will provide*. And Moses adds the saying still current when he wrote: *in the Mount of the LORD it shall be provided.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Future tense, in a place where provision had just happened."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is sworn and what it is sworn on.** Descendants, gates, and blessing for all nations — and God puts His own name behind it, on a hill, next to an altar with a ram on it."}]}, "Genesis 24:10–11": {"title": "Ten camels, loaded", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*The servant took ten of his master's camels and set out, with all kinds of good things from his master in his hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The number is not incidental. Genesis counts things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ten, and what it carries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten camels, each loaded, standing for the whole of what Abraham had to send. The servant is not carrying a sample. He is carrying his master's substance out on an errand, and every good thing in that caravan came from the man who sent him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham is the man who had already given a tenth to Melchizedek and refused to take a thread from the king of Sodom. The wealth on those camels is what a man has when he has handled his blessing that way — held loosely, given first, received back with interest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the wealth was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not display. Every item on those animals was going to be given away — to Rebekah, to her mother, to her brother, before the servant would even eat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of the whole chapter. The blessing goes out ahead of the servant, arrives before he explains himself, and secures the bride for the son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture the chapter draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christian readers have long seen the outline here and it is hard to miss: a father sends a trusted servant to find a bride for his son, and the servant goes loaded with the father's own riches to give away on the way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The camels are how the errand is funded. Ten of them, fully laden, is Genesis saying the sending was not done by halves."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Rebekah does with them.** She waters all ten — somewhere between fifty and a hundred litres, hauled up by hand for animals belonging to a stranger. The chapter establishes her character by what she was willing to do for a caravan she had nothing to gain from, and then puts her at the head of the promise."}]}, "Genesis 24:12–14": {"title": "The prayer at the well", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A servant with ten camels and no idea what he is doing prays the most specific prayer in Genesis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose prayer it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Abraham's. Not Isaac's. An unnamed senior servant, sent five hundred miles to find a wife for a man he works for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the one praying, and the chapter gives more space to him than to anybody else in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Give me success today\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qarah** — to happen, to meet, to fall out. It is the word for occurrence, for something coming to pass."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking for a **happening**. Not for strength, or wisdom, or a sign in the sky — for the right thing to occur."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root gives the word Ruth's story uses when *she happened to come to the part of the field belonging to Boaz* — an accident, reported by a narrator who plainly does not think it was one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And show kindness to my master\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chesed** — the covenant word, loyal love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks twice, at the start and the end of the prayer, and both times the kindness he wants is for Abraham rather than himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test he proposes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When I say to a girl, 'Please lower your jar so I may drink,' and she says, '**Drink, and I'll water your camels too**' — let her be the one.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what he is screening for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Giving a stranger a drink was ordinary courtesy. Watering his camels was not. Ten camels after a long journey drink something in the order of two hundred litres, drawn by hand, one jar at a time, from a well with steps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is an hour or more of hard physical work, offered unasked, to a foreigner she has never met, with no expectation of anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not testing for beauty, family or wealth. He is testing for a particular kind of generosity — the sort that volunteers work nobody would have asked for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before he had finished speaking* — the next verse — Rebekah comes out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And she says it. *Drink, my lord… I will draw water for your camels also, until they have finished drinking.* Then *she hurried, and emptied her pitcher into the trough, and ran again to the well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator gives her three verbs of haste."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man, wondering at her, remained silent, to know whether the LORD had made his journey prosperous or not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He watches. He does not announce anything until the camels are done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the gold ring and the two bracelets, and: *I bowed my head and worshipped the LORD… who had led me in the way of truth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says about the leading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I being in the way, **the LORD led me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His own summary, said twice. He was already moving when the leading happened."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the prayer.** Any request to be shown the future, or to be given certainty in advance. He asks for a thing to occur and then keeps his mouth shut and watches to see whether it did."}]}, "Genesis 24:66–67": {"title": "Isaac was comforted", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last two verses of the longest chapter in Genesis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixty-seven verses, all about one errand: a servant sent four hundred miles to find a wife for Isaac."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis gives more space to this than to the creation. And it tells the story twice — once as it happens, and once as the servant repeats it to Rebekah's family."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the servant **told Isaac everything he had done**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servant is never named in the chapter. He is *the eldest servant of his house, who ruled over all that he had*, and every step of it is his."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had prayed at the well for a specific sign — a woman who would offer to water ten camels — and *before he had finished speaking*, Rebekah came out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaac was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses earlier: *and Isaac **went out to meditate in the field in the evening**; and he lifted his eyes and looked, and there, the camels were coming.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *meditate* is a rare word and translations divide over it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Isaac **brought her into the tent of his mother Sarah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarah had died in chapter 23 at a hundred and twenty-seven. Abraham bought the cave of Machpelah to bury her — the only piece of Canaan the family ever owned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tent has been standing empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he married Rebekah. She became his wife, and he loved her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew runs: he took her, she became his wife, and he loved her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Love is third in the sentence. It is the second occurrence of **ahav** in Genesis; the first is Abraham's for Isaac on the mountain. See the note on Genesis 22:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Isaac **was comforted after the loss of his mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayinnachem** — was comforted, consoled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years have passed since chapter 23. Genesis says nothing about the intervening grief and closes the chapter by saying it ended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Isaac in this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does almost nothing. He is not consulted, does not travel, and does not choose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A father sends, a servant prays, a woman agrees to go, and the son walks out into a field in the evening and looks up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rebekah's decision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they called Rebekah and said to her, \"**Will you go with this man?**\" And she said, \"**I will go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Hebrew. She is asked directly and answers for herself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter began"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD had blessed Abraham in all things.* And the oath: *put your hand under my thigh… that you will not take a wife for my son from the daughters of the Canaanites.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same gesture Jacob later asks of Joseph about his burial. See the note on Genesis 47:27–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servant says he was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I being on the way, the LORD led me** to the house of my master's brethren.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice, and bows and worships in the middle of a stranger's courtyard."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter ends.** Not with a wedding scene. With a man taking a woman into his dead mother's tent, and one word about grief finally lifting."}]}, "Genesis 25:27–28": {"title": "Two boys, two parents", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses of family description, and everything that goes wrong for the next twenty years is in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two men"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Esau became **a skilled hunter, a man of the open country**, while Jacob was **a quiet man, staying among the tents**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *quiet* is **tam** — complete, sound, whole. It is the same word used of Job: *that man was blameless and upright*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not the word for timid. It carries the sense of settled, of a man without a divided purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Staying among the tents is what a herdsman does. Jacob is with the flocks and the household; Esau is out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac loved Esau, **because he had a taste for wild game**, but Rebekah loved Jacob.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses gives the reason for one and not the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac's reason is his stomach. It is stated as flatly as that, and it will be the exact mechanism of the deception in chapter 27 — a bowl of savoury food and a father who could no longer see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebekah's reason is not given. What she had been told is: *two nations are in your womb… and **the older shall serve the younger**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was already said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before either boy was born, at the oracle. Paul quotes it in Romans and adds *for the children not yet being born, nor having done any good or evil, that the purpose of God according to election might stand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The younger was named as the heir before either of them had a personality."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six verses later, Esau comes in from the field exhausted and sells the birthright for a bowl of red stew."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thus Esau **despised** his birthright* — the Hebrew is to hold in contempt, to treat as worthless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews calls him *a profane person, who for one morsel of food sold his birthright.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parenting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each parent has a favourite and they do not have the same one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It produces a wife coaching one son to deceive a blind husband, a father blessing the wrong boy, a brother planning a murder, and a mother sending her favourite four hundred miles away — where he is deceived by his uncle over a wife, and then works twenty years for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses reports the whole thing and never once says it was a good idea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Esau** — *hairy*. **Jacob** — **Yaaqov**, from the heel he came out holding; it becomes the word for supplanter, one who takes another's place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau uses it as an accusation in chapter 27: *is he not rightly named Jacob?* See the note on Genesis 27:34–37."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty years later, at the Jabbok, wrestling until daybreak, and the name is changed: *your name shall no longer be called Jacob, but Israel.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two verses set up.** A father who loves by appetite and a mother who loves by the promise. Everything in chapter 27 runs down the lines drawn here."}]}, "Genesis 25:29–34": {"title": "\"So Esau treated his birthright as though it were nothing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The narrator waits until the last line to say what the story was about, and then says it flatly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Esau treated his birthright as though it were nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *Jacob cheated him*. Genesis is perfectly willing to record Jacob's manipulation elsewhere and does not spare him — but the summary of this scene lands on Esau, and on his estimate of what he was holding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not condemned for being hungry. He is condemned for the exchange rate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a birthright was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The firstborn's portion: a double share of the inheritance, the headship of the family, and in this family the covenant line — the promise made to Abraham and passed to Isaac."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what was on one side of the trade. On the other side: lentils."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Esau ate and drank, then got up and left.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs in a row, with no pause between them. The Hebrew reads as a rapid sequence — he did not linger, did not reflect, did not appear to think anything significant had occurred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The transaction cost him nothing at the moment he made it. That is precisely what the writer wants you to see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I'm about to die\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, I'm about to die. What good is a birthright to me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not about to die. He had come in from hunting, tired and hungry, and talked himself into an emergency — and used the emergency to justify letting go of the long thing for the immediate one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the New Testament does with him.** Hebrews 12 calls him godless and ties him to the warning about missing God's grace — *who traded away what was his by birth for a single meal.* The word there is about a person who treats sacred things as common. Esau's failure is presented as a failure of valuation. See the note on Hebrews 12:12–17."}]}, "Genesis 26:1–2": {"title": "\"Do not go down to Egypt\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A famine, and a father's example, and a different instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A famine in the land — **different from the earlier one in Abraham's time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis makes the reader think of it. In that one, Abram *went down to Egypt to stay awhile, for the famine was severe* — with no word from God anywhere in the passage, and it ended in a lie about his wife and a rebuke from a pagan king. See the note on Genesis 12:10–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac is standing in the same situation his father handled badly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac went to **Abimelech, king of the Philistines, in Gerar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gerar is on the way to Egypt, not in it. He has started moving in the familiar direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not go down to Egypt. Live in the land I tell you to live in.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two commands, and the second one does not name the place. He is told to stay and to be told later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is a hard word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt had the Nile. When the rains failed in Canaan, Egypt still had a river and granaries, and everybody in that region knew it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told to stay in the country with the famine in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Stay in this land, and **I will be with you and bless you**… I will perform the oath which I swore to Abraham your father.\"* See the note on Genesis 26:3–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise is attached to the staying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac sowed in that land, and **reaped in the same year a hundredfold**; and the LORD blessed him.* See the note on Genesis 26:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He plants a crop in a famine and gets a hundred times back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Philistines end up envying him and stopping his wells, and Abimelech asks him to leave because *you are much mightier than we.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing Isaac repeated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He told Abimelech that Rebekah was his sister — the same lie his father had told, in the same town, to a king of the same name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records the obedience and the inherited fault in the same chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaac is otherwise known for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Very little. He is the quietest of the three patriarchs — no journey like Abraham's, no wrestling like Jacob's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He dug his father's wells again, after the Philistines had filled them in, *and he called them by the names which his father had called them.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is really about.** Staying put. Abraham went down and came back up. Isaac is told not to go, sows in the famine, and is the only patriarch who never leaves the land."}]}, "Genesis 26:12–14": {"title": "A hundredfold in a famine year", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and the timing is everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When he planted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with a famine — *besides the one in Abraham's time* — and God telling Isaac not to go down to Egypt but to stay put."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sowing happens in a year when the land was not producing, in a country belonging to somebody else, by a man who had just been told not to go where the food was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harvest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac planted crops in that land, and that same year he reaped a hundredfold, because the LORD blessed him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundredfold was extraordinary in ordinary conditions — Jesus uses it as the top end of the good-soil yield in the parable of the sower. In a famine it is absurd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the writer gives the reason in the same sentence, without elaboration: *because the LORD blessed him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"sowed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is plain agriculture: he put seed in the ground. There is no ritual described, no vow, no negotiation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What preceded it is in the verses just before — God's promise to be with him and bless him if he stayed. Isaac stayed, and then he planted, which is what a man does with land he intends to remain on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His wealth kept increasing until he became very wealthy… so many flocks and herds and servants that the Philistines grew envious of him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter does not stop there, and it is worth reading on: the envy leads to his wells being stopped up, and to Abimelech asking him to leave. The blessing brought friction with the neighbours almost immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three famines in Genesis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses puts the same test in front of three generations, and the chapter itself makes the comparison — *besides the one in Abraham's time*."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Abram** went down to Egypt, and came back up heavy with livestock, silver and gold. See the notes on Genesis 12:10–20 and Genesis 13:1–4.", "**Isaac** was told not to go down, stayed in Gerar, and reaped a hundredfold in the famine year.", "**Jacob** heard there was grain in Egypt and sent his sons, and the man selling it was the son he had buried in his mind twenty years earlier. See the note on Genesis 41:53–57."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three famines, three different routes, and provision in every one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Isaac does about the wells.** He digs them again, is driven off, digs again, is driven off again, and digs a third — and names it Rehoboth, *room*, saying the LORD has made room for us. The prosperity in this chapter is not frictionless. It is a man who keeps digging."}]}, "Genesis 26:3–5": {"title": "The promise repeated to the second generation", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only chapter in Genesis where God speaks to Isaac at length, and what He says is almost entirely a repetition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stay in this land, and I will be with you and bless you… I will make your descendants as many as the stars in the sky, and I will give them all these lands, and through your offspring all nations on earth will be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item was said to Abraham first. Isaac receives no new terms — he receives his father's covenant restated in his own hearing, with his own name on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because Abraham obeyed me and kept my charge, my commands, my decrees, and my instructions.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the striking part. The reason offered to Isaac is his father's obedience, not his own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac is at this point a middle-aged man whose chief recorded acts are being nearly sacrificed, receiving a wife arranged for him, and — in the very next paragraph — lying about that wife exactly as his father had twice done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He inherits a promise anchored in somebody else's faithfulness. That is the shape of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stay in this land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One command, and it runs against the obvious response to famine. Abraham had gone down to Egypt in a famine and it went badly. Isaac is told to stay in the place of scarcity, with a promise attached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hundredfold harvest a few verses later happens in the land he was told not to leave."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the New Testament puts the reader.** *If you belong to Christ, then you are Abraham's seed, and heirs according to the promise.* Paul makes the same move Genesis does here — an inheritance received on the strength of somebody else's faithfulness. See the note on Galatians 3:26–29."}]}, "Genesis 27:24–27": {"title": "\"The smell of a field the LORD has blessed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A blind old man checks four times and is fooled by the fifth sense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four checks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac tests everything he can."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hearing** — *the voice is Jacob's voice.* He gets it right and does not act on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Touch** — Rebekah had put the goatskins on Jacob's hands and neck, and *the hands are the hands of Esau.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Taste** — *bring me some of your game to eat.* Two kids from the flock, cooked the way Isaac liked venison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Speech** — *\"Are you really my son Esau?\" \"I am.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four tests, and Jacob passes them with a lie, a costume and his mother's cooking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The kiss"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come here, my son, and kiss me.**\" So he went and kissed him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fifth test, and Isaac may not have known he was running one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What settled it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **when Isaac caught the scent of his clothes**, he blessed him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebekah had put *the choice clothes of her elder son Esau, which were with her in the house*, on Jacob."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau's clothes — a hunter's, worn outdoors, in the open country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Ah, **the smell of my son is like the smell of a field the LORD has blessed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what tips it. Not evidence, not proof — a smell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the blessing that follows is the one that mattered: *the dew of heaven, the fatness of the earth, plenty of grain and wine… cursed be everyone who curses you, and blessed be those who bless you.* See the note on Genesis 27:28–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was on him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His own hands, wrapped in animal skin. His own voice. And a garment belonging to the firstborn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac blesses what he can smell, and what he can smell is the elder brother's coat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau's cry when he finds out: *\"Bless me — me also, O my father!\"* And *he cried with an exceedingly great and bitter cry.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob runs that night, and is away twenty years. He never sees his mother again; Rebekah's death is not recorded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews uses Esau as a warning, and notes that he *found no place for repentance, though he sought it diligently with tears.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaac had been told before the boys were born"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The older shall serve the younger.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebekah had it from the LORD during the pregnancy. The blessing went where it had been said it would go, and the family tore itself apart getting it there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaac does afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He trembles violently — *Isaac trembled exceedingly* — and then says: *I have blessed him, **and indeed he shall be blessed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not withdraw it. Whatever a blessing was in that world, once said it stood."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing nobody in the chapter comes out of well.** Isaac favours Esau and plans to bless him privately. Rebekah eavesdrops and organises the deception. Jacob lies to his father's face and uses God's name doing it. Esau had already sold the birthright for a bowl of stew. Genesis reports all four without a word of comment."}]}, "Genesis 27:28–29": {"title": "The blessing spoken over the wrong son", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Isaac blesses the son in front of him, believing he is blessing the other one, and the words cannot be taken back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May God give you the dew of heaven and the richness of the earth — an abundance of grain and new wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dew** first. In a land with no summer rainfall, dew was what kept vines and crops alive through the dry months — moisture arriving overnight from nowhere anyone could arrange."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then grain and wine, the staples of the covenant blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the two elements come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two things Melchizedek carried out to Abram after the battle of the kings. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They run through the Old Testament as the sign of a land under blessing — *grain, new wine and oil* — and they are what Isaac says he has already given when Esau comes in too late: *I have **sustained him with grain and new wine**.* See the note on Genesis 27:34–37."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the words did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A spoken blessing in that culture was an act rather than a wish. Once it left the mouth it stood — which is why the entire chapter is a race to get an old blind man to say something, and why nothing can be done afterward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May nations serve you… be lord over your brothers… may those who curse you be cursed, and those who bless you be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line is the promise made to Abraham, handed down. Isaac is passing on the covenant, not merely wishing his son well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The deception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob is wearing Esau's clothes with goatskins on his hands and neck, and he lies twice — *I am Esau your firstborn*, and then *the LORD your God gave me success*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis does not hide any of it, and Jacob spends twenty years in exile as a consequence, deceived in turn by Laban with a substituted older sibling. See the note on Genesis 29:21–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens when Isaac finds out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac trembled violently* — and then says something remarkable: *I blessed him, and indeed he will be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not revoke it. Not because he could not have tried, but because he recognises he has been overruled. The blessing had already been assigned before either boy was born: *the older will serve the younger.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Esau gets.** A blessing of a kind, and it is mostly an absence: away from the richness of the earth, living by the sword, serving his brother. The chapter that opened with a father's plan ends with the plan overturned and nobody in the family happy — and the covenant intact."}]}, "Genesis 27:34–37": {"title": "\"What is there left that I can do for you?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The blessing has already gone, and this is the sound of finding out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He let out **a loud and bitter cry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a great and exceedingly bitter cry*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews looks back at it: *he found no place for repentance, **though he sought it diligently with tears**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Bless me — me too, my father!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the birthright, which he had already sold for stew. The blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Isaac's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your brother came deceitfully and took your blessing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac names it accurately. He does not defend Jacob and does not soften it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the chapter has already recorded his reaction on discovering it: *Isaac **trembled exceedingly**.* Then, in the same breath: *I have blessed him — **and indeed he shall be blessed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not take it back. Hebrews lists that as faith. See the note on Hebrews 11:17–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Esau's complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Isn't he rightly named Jacob?** This is **the second time** he has taken advantage of me. **He took my birthright, and now he has taken my blessing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yaaqov** — heel-grabber, supplanter. Esau turns the name into a verb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His accounting is half right. The birthright he sold himself, in chapter 25, for a bowl of red stew, and Genesis says he *despised* it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Haven't you saved even one blessing for me?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was given away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have made him **lord over you**, and I have given him **all his relatives as servants**, and I have **sustained him with grain and new wine**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three items, and Isaac lists them as things already handed over:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Lordship** over the brother", "**The household** as servants", "**Grain and new wine** — the sustaining"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *sustained* is **samak** — to lean on, to uphold, to support. It is the word for the hand laid on the head of a sacrifice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grain and the wine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two elements Melchizedek carried out to Abram at the end of a battle. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **what is there left that I can do for you, my son?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a real question, and there is no answer to it. The blessing was one thing, and it had been spoken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it could not be recalled"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A spoken blessing in that culture was an act, not a wish. Once said, it stood — which is why the whole chapter turns on getting the words out of the old man's mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Esau gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A different word entirely, and it is mostly a description of a hard country. See the note on Genesis 27:38–40."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Isaac says he has done.** Made him lord, given him the household, sustained him with grain and wine. Not *I intended to* — the verbs are finished, and the man in front of him is the one they were meant for."}]}, "Genesis 27:38–40": {"title": "\"Away from the dew of heaven\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Esau asks a second time, and what he gets back is the first blessing with the key word reversed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you have only one blessing**, my father? Bless me too, my father!\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Esau wept aloud**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *Esau lifted up his voice and wept*. It is the third description of his crying in five verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob had been given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"May God give you **of the dew of heaven, of the fatness of the earth**, and plenty of **grain and new wine**.\"* See the note on Genesis 27:28–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things: dew, fatness, grain and wine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Esau is given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your dwelling will be **away from the richness of the earth, away from the dew of heaven above**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two nouns, in reverse order, with one preposition changed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew preposition **min** can mean *of* or *away from* — Jacob got *of* the dew and *of* the fatness; Esau's line uses the same word the other way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a lesser blessing. It is the first one with the direction turned round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that country dew was not decorative. Through the rainless summer, the night moisture off the Mediterranean was what kept crops alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Edom is the country south-east of the Dead Sea — rock, cliffs, and very little of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sword"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will live by the sword**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau's descendants become Edom, and the Old Testament records the relationship: refusing Israel passage in Numbers, subdued by David, in revolt under Joram, condemned in Obadiah for standing by while Jerusalem fell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod the Great was an Idumean — an Edomite — and he is the king on the throne when Jacob's line produces the Messiah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The service"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **you will serve your brother**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oracle before they were born: *the older shall serve the younger.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing given to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But when you grow restless, you will throw his yoke from off your neck**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *grow restless* is to become restive, to roam, to break loose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It happened. 2 Kings records it: *in his days Edom revolted from under the hand of Judah, and made a king over themselves.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in either blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither son is given the covenant in this chapter. That comes two chapters later, at Bethel, when Jacob is a fugitive sleeping on a stone: *in you and in your seed all the families of the earth shall be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac blesses him again before he leaves, and this time it is explicit — *the blessing of Abraham*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Esau does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The days of mourning for my father are at hand; then I will kill my brother Jacob**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebekah hears of it and sends Jacob to Haran, telling Isaac it is about finding a wife. She says *stay with him a few days*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She never sees him again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they meet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty years later, Jacob crossing back with his family, terrified, having sent droves of livestock ahead — and *Esau ran to meet him, and embraced him, and fell on his neck and kissed him, and they wept.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the two blessings differ.** Not in the nouns. Dew and fatness are in both — and the whole difference in Esau's is which side of them he lives on."}]}, "Genesis 28:10–15": {"title": "Jacob's stairway", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man running for his life falls asleep on a rock and is given the whole covenant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jacob **left Beersheba** and traveled toward Haran.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is running. He has just cheated his brother out of the blessing, and Esau has promised to kill him as soon as their father is buried."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is alone, with nothing recorded in his hands, on his first night away from home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pillow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took **one of the stones lying there, put it under his head**, and lay down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the accommodation. A stone, on open ground, *because the sun had set* — not because he chose the spot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **stairway**, its foot set on the earth and **its top reaching all the way into heaven** — and the angels of God **going up and coming down** on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the direction. **Up** first, then down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They start from the earth. Whatever the traffic is, it begins where Jacob is lying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is at the top"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there, **above it all, stood the LORD Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stairway is not the point of the vision. The Person at the top is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a word about what Jacob has just done. No mention of Esau, the stolen blessing, the lie to his father, or the goatskins on his arms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Instead: *I am the LORD, the God of Abraham your father, and the God of Isaac* — and then the covenant, handed to him."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the ground you are lying on — I will give it*", "*your descendants will be like the dust of the earth*", "*through you and your family, **every family on earth will be blessed***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land, the seed, and the blessing to the nations — the three promises made to Abraham, given to a runaway on his first night out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four \"I will\"s"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look — **I am with you**. **I will keep you** wherever you go, and **I will bring you back** again. **I will not leave you**. I will not stop until I have done all I have promised.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clause is His action. Nothing is asked of Jacob in the entire speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Surely the LORD is in this place, and I did not know it… **How awesome is this place!** This is none other than the house of God, and this is the gate of heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names it **Bethel**, house of God. And then he makes a bargain — *if God will be with me… then the LORD shall be my God* — which is Jacob all over: given everything unconditionally, and responding with terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I tell you the truth: from now on you will see heaven standing wide open, and **the angels of God going up and coming down on the Son of Man**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to Nathanael, in John 1, and it is a deliberate quotation of this passage. See the note on John 1:47–51."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob saw a stairway between earth and heaven. Jesus says He is it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The stone.** He set it up as a pillar and poured oil on it. Years later, coming back the other way with two wives, eleven sons and a limp, God tells him *arise, go up to Bethel, and dwell there* — back to the rock he slept on when he had nothing."}]}, "Genesis 28:16–19": {"title": "\"Surely the LORD is in this place\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man wakes up on a rock and discovers where he had been sleeping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Surely the LORD is in this place — and I never knew it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are true and the second is the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It had been true all night. He had chosen the spot because the sun set, not because it was anything. See the note on Genesis 28:10–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The awe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **holy awe** came over him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he was afraid — the fear that comes with realising what you have been standing next to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he calls it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How full of wonder this place is.** This is nothing less than **the house of God**. This is **the very gate of heaven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two names for it, and neither is what it looked like. It was open ground with stones on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**House of God** — a building, on a site with no building. **Gate of heaven** — a doorway, in a field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took **the stone he had rested his head on**, and he set it up as a pillar, and he **poured oil over the top of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pillow becomes a pillar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same stone, moved from horizontal to vertical, and marked with oil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the first anointing with oil in Scripture, and it is performed by a man on the run, on a rock, alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the oil came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis does not say, and he had left home in a hurry with nothing recorded in his hands. Oil was ordinary in that world — carried for lamps, for cooking, for skin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses what he had on a stone he had slept on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He named that place **Bethel** — the house of God — though the town had **once been called Luz**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Luz** means almond, or a turning aside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The town had a name already. He renames it after what he had not known was there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jacob made a vow: \"If God will be with me, and keep me in this way… and give me bread to eat and clothing to put on, so that I come back to my father's house in peace, **then the LORD shall be my God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given the whole covenant unconditionally in his sleep, he wakes up and negotiates."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Jacob throughout, and Genesis records it without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty years later, God tells him: *arise, go up to Bethel and dwell there, and make an altar there to God, who appeared to you when you fled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes back with two wives, eleven sons, a limp and a new name, to the rock he slept on when he had nothing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jesus does with the vision.** *\"You will see heaven standing wide open, and the angels of God going up and coming down **on the Son of Man**.\"* Said to Nathanael, in John 1. Jacob called the place the gate of heaven. Jesus says He is the stairway. See the note on John 1:47–51."}]}, "Genesis 29:21–25": {"title": "\"When morning came — there was Leah\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four words in this passage do more work than the rest of the chapter, and in Hebrew they land as bluntly as they do here: *when morning came — there was Leah.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob had already done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To feel this you have to remember the last time a father, a firstborn and a disguise appeared together in this family. Jacob had gone in to his blind father wearing his brother's clothes with goat's hair on his hands, and when Isaac asked who he was, he said: *I am Esau your firstborn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now Jacob is the one in the dark, and it is a firstborn substituted for a younger sibling that undoes him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laban even says it out loud a few verses later — *it is not done here to give the younger before the firstborn*. It is the exact thing Jacob did, spoken back to him as local custom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What have you done to me? Didn't I serve you for Rachel? Why have you deceived me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word for *deceived* is the one used of Jacob in the earlier story. He is asking Laban the question his brother had every right to ask him. The narrator does not comment. He does not need to."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the story is not saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a lesson that God pays people back — the chapter never says so, and what follows is not punishment but a long, tangled family in which God keeps working anyway. Leah, married by trickery and unloved, becomes the mother of Judah, and the line runs through her to David and to Christ. Not through Rachel, the one Jacob chose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some things are only visible from the other side of them. Jacob had a name for what his brother did to him and no name for what he had done himself, until he woke up on the receiving end of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the use of this passage: it puts a man in the position he once put someone else in, and it lets him hear his own words come back in another man's mouth. Nothing is explained to him. He simply sees it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On Leah.** She is the person this is being done *to*, and the story rarely gets told from where she is standing. Handed over, unwanted the next morning, and told for years that she was not the one. The chapter that follows names her sons and you can hear it in every name. And it is her son Judah, not Rachel's, whose line ends up carrying the promise. The Bible keeps doing this, and it is worth noticing every time."}]}, "Genesis 3:1–5": {"title": "The serpent's question", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five verses, and every move in them is about what God is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the serpent was **more crafty than any creature the LORD God had made**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **arum** — shrewd, prudent, subtle. Proverbs uses it approvingly of the prudent man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is a pun in the Hebrew: the previous verse ends *they were both naked* — **arummim** — and this one opens **arum**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came to the woman with **a question that twisted things**: \"**Did God really say** you must not eat from **any tree** in the garden?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *has God indeed said*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two distortions in one sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["It is a **question** rather than a claim, so nothing is asserted and everything is suggested.", "**Any tree** — the actual command was one tree out of the whole garden. He inverts the proportion."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Of every tree of the garden you may freely eat**; but of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil you shall not eat.\"* See the note on Genesis 2:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles the permission: *eating you may eat*. It is emphatic generosity, with one exception."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The serpent quotes the exception and drops the generosity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We may eat from the trees — only not from the tree in the middle. God said we must not eat it **or even touch it**, or we will die.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three changes:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Freely eat** becomes simply *we may eat* — the generosity flattens.", "**Or even touch it** is not in the command. She adds a restriction.", "The Hebrew of *you shall surely die* — the doubled form — becomes *lest we die*."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The permission shrinks, the prohibition grows, and the certainty weakens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contradiction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will not die**,\" the serpent said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first flat denial in Scripture, and it is a direct negation of the sentence in Genesis 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The motive supplied"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God knows** that the moment you eat it, **your eyes will open, and you will be like God, knowing good and evil**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The move is not to deny the command. It is to explain the motive behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The suggestion is that the restriction exists to keep something back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was already true"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 1: *God created man **in His own image**; in the image of God He created him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were already like Him. The offer is of something they had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was true in the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your eyes will be opened.* And the next verse: *then the eyes of both of them were opened.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The serpent's factual claim was accurate. What they saw was that they were naked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the pattern repeats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the wilderness, three temptations, and two of them open *if You are the Son of God* — which is the same move, aimed at what had just been said out loud at the Jordan: *this is My beloved Son*. See the note on Matthew 3:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers all three with Scripture, quoted accurately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But I fear, lest somehow, **as the serpent deceived Eve by his craftiness**, so your minds may be corrupted from the simplicity that is in Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word — **panourgia**, craftiness."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is attacked first.** Not the rule. The opening question misquotes the generosity, and the closing sentence supplies a motive — and by the end the only thing in dispute is what kind of God gave the command."}]}, "Genesis 3:14–15": {"title": "The first promise, spoken to the serpent", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first announcement of a rescue in the Bible is not addressed to the people being rescued. It is spoken to the enemy, over their heads."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being talked to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God curses the serpent, and in the middle of the sentence describes a future conflict — and its outcome. Adam and Eve are standing there listening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not consulted, promised anything directly, or asked to do anything. They overhear their own hope while being sentenced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Her seed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Between your offspring and hers. Her seed will crush your head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is unusual. Descent in Hebrew genealogy runs through the man, and Scripture speaks of the seed of Abraham, of David, of Israel. The seed **of a woman** is not how anyone talked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christian readers have seen a virgin birth signalled from the first pages because of it, and Paul's phrasing in Galatians follows the same track: *God sent forth His Son, born of a woman.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two wounds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her seed will crush your head, and you will strike his heel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both parties are injured, and the injuries are not equal. A crushed head ends the fight. A struck heel is real damage that heals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything from here to the cross is the working out of that asymmetry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the promise sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even here, in the wreckage, God spoke a promise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before the curse on the ground, before the exile from the garden, before the garments of skin. The first thing God says after the fall is that it will not stand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the wound cost.** *You will strike his heel* is not a small thing. Whatever the crushing of the head would take, the promise made in Genesis 3 already includes the injury to the one doing it. See the note on Isaiah 53:4–6."}]}, "Genesis 3:17–19": {"title": "Thorns, sweat, and the ground", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence on the man, and what is cursed in it is not the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is cursed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The ground is cursed** because of you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Adam. The serpent is cursed in verse 14 and the ground is cursed here, and the man is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *cursed is the adamah for your sake* — and **adamah** is the ground he was made out of and named after. See the note on Genesis 2:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"In **toil** you will eat from it all the days of your life\"*", "*\"It will bring you **thorns and thistles**\"*", "*\"By **the sweat of your brow** you will eat your bread\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Toil, thorns, and sweat. Work itself was not the curse — he had been given the garden *to tend and keep it* before any of this. What changed is what work costs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *toil* is **itstsabon**, painful labour, and it is the same word used of the woman's pain a few verses earlier. They get the same noun."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Thorns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **qots** appears here for the first time in Scripture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the visible mark of the curse on the ground — what grows without being planted, in soil that used to produce a garden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A cohort of Roman soldiers twisted a circle of them and pressed it onto His head. See the note on Matthew 27:27–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sweat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"By the sweat of your brow you will eat your bread.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in a garden on the Mount of Olives: *His sweat became like great drops of blood **falling to the ground**.* See the note on Luke 22:39–46."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sweat of a man's face, and the cursed ground receiving it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Until you return to the ground\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For out of it you were taken. **Dust you are, and to dust you will return.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account closes the loop it opened. He was formed from dust and breathed into; now the dust part is named as the destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any statement that God has finished with him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses later God makes them tunics of skin and clothes them — the first death in Scripture, and it is an animal killed to cover two people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And before the sentence on the man, in verse 15, the promise to the serpent: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.* See the note on Genesis 3:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The remedy is announced before the sentence is passed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the ground is dealt with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ has redeemed us from the curse of the law, having become a curse for us* — Galatians 3, and the proof text Paul uses is *cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thorns on His head, sweat on the ground, and a tree."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Lamech named his son for.** *This one will **comfort us** concerning our work and the toil of our hands, **because of the ground which the LORD has cursed**.* He called him Noah, which means rest — and Genesis says he was the man who found grace. See the note on Genesis 6:5–8."}]}, "Genesis 3:20–24": {"title": "Garments of skin, and the sword at the gate", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three things happen in these five verses, and they do not obviously belong together: a name, a set of clothes, and an expulsion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man named his wife Eve, because she would become the mother of all who live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it immediately after being told he will return to dust. Standing under a death sentence, Adam names his wife **Life**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only thing he has to go on is the promise about her seed, three verses earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clothing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD God, tender even in judgment, made garments of animal skin and clothed them Himself — covering what they could not cover on their own.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had made coverings out of fig leaves. Those are not mentioned again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's replacement required an animal to die — the first death in Scripture, and it happens so that two guilty people can be dressed. He does not hand them the skins. He clothes them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The expulsion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He drove the man out, and east of the garden He stationed cherubim and a flaming sword.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated reason is the tree of life: *he must not reach out and take also from the tree of life and live forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as pure punishment that is bleak. Read as it stands, it is a mercy — immortality in a fallen condition would have locked the ruin in permanently. The gate is shut so that the situation stays fixable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cherubim, with a sword, guarding the way back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next time cherubim appear in Scripture they are hammered out of gold at either end of the mercy seat, wings over it, looking down at the place where the blood goes. Same creatures. The sword is gone, and the way is open."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line of the chapter.** *To guard the way to the tree of life.* Not to abolish it — to guard the road. Revelation ends with that tree in the middle of the city, its leaves for the healing of the nations, and no sword mentioned anywhere."}]}, "Genesis 3:6–7": {"title": "\"Their eyes were opened — but not the way the serpent said\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The serpent had promised that their eyes would be opened and they would be like God, knowing good and evil. The eyes did open. Everything else was a lie."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things she saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fruit seemed good to eat, pleasing to the eye, and desirable for the wisdom it promised.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three appraisals, made in order, before a hand moves. The tree had been there the whole time; what changed was the looking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's list of what the world offers runs the same three ways — the craving of the body, the craving of the eyes, the bragging about what a man has. See the note on 1 John 2:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Who was with her\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She took some and ate, and gave some to her husband, **who was with her**, and he ate too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words that are easy to skim. Adam was not away in another part of the garden. He was standing there through the entire conversation, and said nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the opened eyes actually showed them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suddenly they saw their own nakedness, and they felt exposed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise was knowledge of good and evil. The delivery was self-consciousness — the first thing the new sight produced was shame about themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been naked in the previous chapter and it is specifically noted that they felt no shame. Nothing about their bodies changed. What changed was what they could see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fig leaves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they sewed fig leaves together and made coverings for themselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first human response to guilt is a covering they make. It does not last — before the chapter ends God makes them garments of skin, which required a death they did not perform."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was actually lost.** Not innocence in the abstract. Read on: the sound of God walking in the garden, and two people hiding from it. The tree cost them the walk."}]}, "Genesis 3:8–13": {"title": "\"Where are you?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God's first recorded words after the fall are a question, and He already knows the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sound"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They heard the sound of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail implies a habit. This was not the first evening He had walked there — what is new is that nobody comes out to meet Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where are you?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an inquiry. It is the opening of a conversation with people who have hidden, and it invites them to say where they have got to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every question in this passage works the same way: *who told you that you were naked?* and *what have you done?* God asks; He does not announce."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Adam actually admits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I heard You in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked, so I hid.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice what is in the answer and what is missing. Fear, nakedness, hiding — all true. The eating is not mentioned until God names it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, when it is named: *the woman You gave me — she gave me the fruit, and I ate.* Two people blamed in one sentence, and one of them is God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The woman's answer is shorter and does the same: *the serpent deceived me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody says *I did it*. That is the whole exchange, and it is the first conversation between God and guilty people in the Bible."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does anyway.** The curse, then the promise about the woman's seed, and then — before the exile from the garden — *the LORD God made garments of skin for Adam and his wife, and clothed them.* They had made coverings out of leaves. He replaced them with something that required a death, and dressed them Himself."}]}, "Genesis 32:22–26": {"title": "\"I will not let You go unless You bless me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jacob sends everyone he loves across a river and stays behind, and something happens in the dark that changes his name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Alone, on purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jacob was left alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just sent his wives, his children and everything he owns across the ford. Esau is coming with four hundred men and Jacob has spent the evening arranging gifts to go ahead of him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he empties the camp of everybody and stays on the wrong side of the water by himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wrestling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man wrestled with him until the breaking of day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text calls him a man. Hosea, looking back, calls him the angel; Jacob will say he has seen God face to face and names the place accordingly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the man saw that he could not overpower Jacob* — which is a strange sentence about God, until you notice how it is resolved. One touch to the hip socket and the match is over. The strength was never the question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grip"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let me go, for the day is breaking.\" But Jacob said, \"I will not let You go unless You bless me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is now injured and cannot win, and he holds on anyway. The wrestling turns into clinging at the exact moment he stops being able to fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asked his name — the same question his father asked him in the dark years earlier, when he answered *I am Esau your firstborn*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This time he says Jacob: heel-grabber, supplanter. He tells the truth about himself, and is immediately given a new name."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he took away.** A blessing, a name, and a limp. Genesis says the sun rose on him as he passed Penuel, *and he was limping because of his hip*. The injury does not get healed in the story. He walks into the meeting with Esau newly named and permanently marked."}]}, "Genesis 35:16–20": {"title": "Ben-oni and Benjamin", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman dies naming her son, and her husband changes the name before she is buried."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They journeyed on **from Bethel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethel is where Jacob had just built an altar and been renamed Israel a second time. The family is on the road south."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The labour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rachel went into labor — and it was **a hard, bitter labor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *she had hard labour*, and the word is repeated in the next verse for emphasis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Years earlier: *give me children, or else I die.* And when Joseph was born: *the LORD shall add to me another son* — which is what **Joseph** means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She got the second son and it cost her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The midwife"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid; you have another son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last words spoken to her are the answer to the prayer she had prayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her name for him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With her last breath she named him **Ben-oni, \"son of my sorrow.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **oni** is sorrow, trouble, affliction. The same root as the name Jabez carried. See the note on 1 Chronicles 4:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She names him after what it cost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His father's name for him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **his father called him Benjamin, \"son of my right hand.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ben-yamin.** The right hand is the place of strength and of favour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob does not argue with what she saw. He gives the boy a different name and it is the one that stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last name in Scripture is given by the father."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The burial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Rachel died and was **buried on the road to Ephrath, that is, Bethlehem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the family tomb at Machpelah, where Sarah, Abraham, Isaac, Rebekah and Leah are. She is buried at the roadside where she died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ephrath is Bethlehem.** The narrator adds it so nobody misses where."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pillar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jacob set up a pillar over her grave — **the marker of Rachel's tomb, standing there to this day**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the tomb turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah: *a voice was heard in Ramah, lamentation and bitter weeping, **Rachel weeping for her children**, refusing to be comforted, because they are no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Matthew quotes it — over Bethlehem, after Herod's soldiers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob says about it at the end of his life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty years later, blessing Joseph's sons in Egypt: *but as for me, when I came from Padan, Rachel died beside me… and **I buried her there on the way to Ephrath, that is, Bethlehem***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is blind and dying and still mentions the roadside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Benjamin goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He becomes the youngest of the twelve, the one Jacob will not let out of his sight, and the one the whole of the Joseph reunion turns on. See the notes on Genesis 43:1–7 and Genesis 44:1–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul was of the tribe of Benjamin, and says so twice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two names.** One given by a dying woman out of what it cost her, and one given by the father over the same child — and Genesis records both and then only ever uses the second."}]}, "Genesis 39:1–6": {"title": "\"But the LORD was with Joseph\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The lowest point of his life so far, and the chapter's second word is *but*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph had been **taken down to Egypt**, and Potiphar… **bought him** from the Ishmaelites.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen years old, stripped of the coat, thrown in a pit by his brothers, sold to a caravan, resold in a foreign market to a man who ran executions for Pharaoh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in that sentence is done to him. He is the object of every verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the LORD was with Joseph**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clause appears four times in this chapter — twice here, and twice again after he is put in prison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses says it at the top and at the bottom, and never in between explains how it looked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **everything went well for him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he was a man who prospers* — **tsalach**, to advance, to succeed, to push through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said of a slave. He is still owned; the word is used anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Potiphar could see"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His Egyptian master, **who saw that the LORD was with him** and **made everything he did prosper in his hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pagan officer of Pharaoh's court works out that a Hebrew slave's God is the reason his house is running well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is recorded of Joseph preaching. Potiphar drew the conclusion from the results."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promotion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph **found favor** in his master's eyes and became **his personal servant**, and Potiphar **put him in charge of his household** and **entrusted everything** he owned to his care.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four stages in one verse. Favour, then personal attendance, then charge of the house, then everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Favour** is **chen** — grace. The same word used over Noah, over Esther, and over Israel in Egypt on the morning they left. See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing spreads"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the moment he gave Joseph that charge, **the LORD blessed the Egyptian's household for Joseph's sake**; the LORD's blessing rested on everything he had, **in the house and in the field**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Potiphar gets blessed. He does not believe, he owns a slave, and his crops and his household prosper because of who is in his house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Potiphar stopped thinking about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Potiphar **left everything in Joseph's hands** and **gave no thought to anything but the food he ate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He hands over the accounts and stops looking at them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Joseph was **well-built and handsome**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses drops it in at the end of the paragraph without comment, and the next verse is *his master's wife cast longing eyes on Joseph.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence that closes the prosperity is the one that starts the disaster."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refuses her, she keeps his garment, he is accused, and he goes to prison — stripped of a coat for the second time in his life by someone who wanted something from him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the prison: *the LORD was with Joseph and showed him mercy, and He gave him favor in the sight of the keeper of the prison.* The same three words again."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter measures.** Not his circumstances — those get worse. Moses tracks one thing across the whole of it, and states it four times in the same words."}]}, "Genesis 39:7–12": {"title": "\"And sin against God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A slave in a foreign house, with nobody watching and nothing to lose, gives a reason nobody could have checked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his position was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen when his brothers sold him. No family, no rights, no legal standing, and no prospect of the story ever reaching Canaan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was, in every practical sense, unobserved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives three reasons and then a fourth, and the fourth is the one that matters."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*my master gives no thought to anything in this house*", "*he has entrusted everything he owns to me*", "*he has held nothing back from me **except you, because you are his wife***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *\"**How then could I do such a wicked thing and sin against God?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose wife"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *because I would be caught*, or *because it is against the law*, or *because I would lose my position*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names two people who would be wronged — Potiphar, and God — and Potiphar is the lesser of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same instinct is in David's psalm after Bathsheba: *against You, You only, have I sinned.* See the note on Psalms 51:3–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Day after day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Day after day she pressed him, but he would not listen; **he would not lie with her or even be near her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two refusals. The second is the practical one — he stopped being in the room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to be with her*. He removed the opportunity as well as declining it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day it came to a head"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One day he went into the house to do his work, and **none of the household servants were inside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator supplies that detail. Empty house, no witnesses, nobody to report anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She caught him by his garment.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But he **left the garment in her hand and fled outside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He runs, and he lets go of the coat to do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the second coat he has lost. His brothers stripped him of the long robe and dipped it in goat's blood to lie to his father with. Now a garment is left in a woman's hand and used to lie about him again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times a coat is the evidence in a false story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prison. Immediately, without a hearing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's last line: *the LORD was with Joseph and showed him mercy, and gave him favour in the sight of the keeper of the prison.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence that opened the chapter about Potiphar's house. Same clause, worse address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it gained"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing he could see for two more years. The cupbearer forgot him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Pharaoh's dream, and a man who could be trusted with everything in Egypt because he had already been trusted with everything in one house and not touched the one thing withheld."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says at the end.** *You meant evil against me, but God meant it for good.* Said to the brothers, about the pit — but it covers the prison too. Nothing in these six verses looks like it is going anywhere good, and Genesis takes another eight chapters to show what it was for."}]}, "Genesis 4:8–12": {"title": "Blood that cries out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first death in the Bible is a murder, and God's response introduces an image the rest of scripture keeps using."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Your brother's blood is crying out to me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew noun is plural — *bloods*, which the rabbis noticed and commented on for centuries: not one life only but every life that would have come from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verb is the ordinary word for a shout, a cry for help, the noise a wronged person makes. Blood that has been spilled is pictured as a voice, and what it says is *this was wrong and nothing has answered for it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what innocent blood does. It does not go quiet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question and the dodge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is your brother Abel?* — God asks a question He does not need answered, exactly as He did in the garden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Am I my brother's keeper?* is Cain's answer, and it is a good deal more brazen than Adam's. He does not hide; he throws the question back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to Cain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ground he worked will no longer yield to him, and he becomes a wanderer. But read on four verses: when Cain says the punishment is more than he can bear and that anyone finding him will kill him, God puts a mark on him to protect him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first murderer in scripture is not executed. He is marked so that nobody touches him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this comes back.** Hebrews says the blood of Jesus *speaks better than the blood of Abel* — and the comparison only works because of this passage. Abel's blood cried out for the guilty to answer. The other blood is described as saying something better, which does not mean louder. It means it speaks in the opposite direction. See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}]}, "Genesis 41:25–32": {"title": "\"God is showing Pharaoh what He is about to do\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner tells the king of Egypt what his dreams meant, and the first word out of his mouth is God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Both of Pharaoh's dreams are one and the same**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two dreams — seven fat cows eaten by seven lean ones, and seven full heads of grain swallowed by seven withered ones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt's magicians could not interpret either. Joseph says they are one dream told twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is doing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God is showing Pharaoh what He is about to do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already said it on the way in: *\"It is not in me; God will give Pharaoh an answer of peace.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he says it again in verse 28: *\"It's as I told Pharaoh — **God has shown Pharaoh what He is about to do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in four verses, to a king who was worshipped as a god himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interpretation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The seven good cows are seven years, and the seven good heads of grain are seven years — **it is one dream**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The seven thin, ugly cows… and the seven empty heads scorched by the wind: they are **seven years of famine**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wind is the **khamsin**, the desert wind that blasts standing grain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forecast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Seven years of great plenty are coming** to all of Egypt, but after them will come **seven years of famine**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The severity"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**All the plenty will be forgotten**, and the famine will **drain the land dry**. **The abundance will not even be remembered**, so severe will the famine be.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice. The seven good years will not register against the seven bad ones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the dream came to Pharaoh twice** because **the matter is firmly settled by God**, and **God will bring it about soon**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *established by God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The repetition is read as confirmation, not emphasis. Joseph explains the literary structure of the dream as part of the interpretation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody asked him for advice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now therefore, **let Pharaoh select a discerning and wise man**, and set him over the land of Egypt… and let them **gather all the food of those good years**… **then that food shall be a reserve for the land** for the seven years of famine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He interprets the dream and then proposes a national policy: a fifth of the harvest, stored under officers, for seven years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the dream contained the plan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The plan pleased Pharaoh and all his officials.* And: *\"**Since God has shown you all this, there is no one as discerning and wise as you**.\"* See the note on Genesis 41:37–45."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two Hebrew words Pharaoh uses — **chakam** and **navon** — are the ones Joseph had used in his own recommendation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph described the man needed and Pharaoh handed him the job."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been doing beforehand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirteen years — sold at seventeen, Potiphar's house, prison on a false charge, and two more years forgotten by a butler he had helped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had interpreted two dreams in that prison as well, and asked one man to remember him. *Yet the chief butler did not remember Joseph, but forgot him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole thing was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God sent me before you to preserve life**.\"* See the note on Genesis 50:15–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he opens.** Not with the interpretation. Three times in four verses he names who is doing the showing — standing in front of a man his own culture called divine."}]}, "Genesis 41:33–36": {"title": "Joseph's counsel", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joseph was asked to interpret a dream. He gives the interpretation and then, without being asked, offers a national economic policy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was in a dungeon an hour ago. *They brought him quickly out of the dungeon; and he shaved, changed his clothing, and came to Pharaoh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirteen years between the pit and this room — sold, enslaved, falsely accused, imprisoned, and forgotten by the cupbearer for two more years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An interpretation. Nothing else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives it: seven years of plenty, seven of famine, *and the famine will deplete the land.* And *the dream was repeated twice because the thing is established by God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So now, **let Pharaoh look for a discerning and wise man** and set him over the land.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody requested advice. He supplies it anyway, in front of the most powerful man on earth, straight out of a cell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plan"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["appoint **officers over the land**", "gather **a fifth** of the harvest during the seven good years", "**store up grain** under Pharaoh's authority", "**keep it safe in the cities**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A fifth — twenty per cent, set aside across seven years of surplus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *gather* is a taxation word. It is a levy, and it is planned before there is any sign of trouble."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That food will be **a reserve for the land** through the seven years of famine… **so the land will not perish**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *reserve* is a deposit, something laid up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point is stated as survival rather than profit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the plan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything mystical. No prayer meeting, no further revelation, no waiting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is given seven years' notice and proposes storage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Pharaoh said to Joseph, \"Since God has shown you all this, **there is no one as discerning and wise as you**. You shall be over my house.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He describes the qualifications for the job and is handed the job."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt survives, and so does the family that threw him in the pit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the surplus goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph gathered very much grain, as the sand of the sea, **until he stopped counting, for it was immeasurable**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when the famine came, *all countries came to Egypt to buy grain, because the famine was severe in all lands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A nation with a plan feeds the world for seven years."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says to his brothers about all of it.** *You meant evil against me, but **God meant it for good**, in order to bring it about as it is this day, to save many people alive.* The dungeon and the granary are the same story, and he says so."}]}, "Genesis 41:37–45": {"title": "\"One in whom the Spirit of God lives\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man goes from a prison cell to second in Egypt inside one morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Pharaoh sent and called Joseph, and **they brought him hastily out of the dungeon**; and he shaved, changed his clothing, and came to Pharaoh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirteen years — sold at seventeen, in Potiphar's house, then in prison on a false charge, then forgotten for two more years by a butler who had promised to remember him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had just said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh had told him *I have heard it said of you that you can understand a dream to interpret it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph's answer: *\"**It is not in me; God will give Pharaoh an answer of peace**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same disclaimer Daniel gives Nebuchadnezzar centuries later. See the note on Daniel 2:29–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The assessment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The plan pleased Pharaoh and all his officials.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph had not only interpreted. He had added an administrative proposal — a fifth of the harvest, stored through seven years — that nobody asked him for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pharaoh's question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Can we find anyone like this man, **one in whom the Spirit of God lives?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a man in whom is the Spirit of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pagan king says it, about a Hebrew slave, in front of his whole court."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reasoning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Since God has shown you all this**, there is **no one as discerning and wise as you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh attributes the ability to God and promotes the man anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appointment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will be over my house**, and all my people will do as you say. **Only in the throne will I be greater than you**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The insignia"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took **the signet ring from his own hand** and put it on Joseph's.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A signet ring was the seal of authority. Whatever it stamped was law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **dressed him in fine linen** and hung **a gold chain around his neck**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Linen and gold — the dress of an Egyptian official, and both appear in the tomb paintings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had him **ride in the chariot of his second-in-command**, and men went ahead of him calling out, \"**Bow down!**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am Pharaoh, but **without your word no one will lift a hand or foot in all of Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The new name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pharaoh gave Joseph a new name, **Zaphenath-Paneah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The meaning is disputed. Older readings take it as *revealer of secrets*; Egyptologists have proposed *God speaks and he lives*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel is renamed Belteshazzar in the same way, by the same kind of court."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wife"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And gave him **Asenath, daughter of Potiphera the priest of On**, to be his wife.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On is Heliopolis, the centre of Egyptian sun worship. Joseph marries into its priesthood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records it without comment, and their two sons — Manasseh and Ephraim — become two of Israel's tribes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His age"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph was **thirty years old** when he stood before Pharaoh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen when he was sold. Thirteen years between the pit and the palace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he names his sons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Manasseh** — *for God has made me forget all my toil and all my father's house*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ephraim** — *for God has caused me to be fruitful in the land of my affliction*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the second one after the place, not in spite of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole thing was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God sent me before you to preserve life**.\"* And: *\"**You meant it for evil against me, but God meant it for good**.\"* See the note on Genesis 50:15–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Pharaoh notices.** Not the administration, first. He asks his court whether they can find another man with the Spirit of God in him — and then hands over the ring."}]}, "Genesis 41:53–57": {"title": "\"Go to Joseph\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famine arrives on schedule, and the whole world ends up at one man's door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Exactly as he said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The seven years of plenty in Egypt came to an end, and the seven years of famine began, **just as Joseph had said**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven years earlier he was in a prison interpreting dreams for a butler and a baker. The dream he read for Pharaoh had a timetable in it, and Moses notes that the timetable held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The famine spread to **every land**, but **throughout Egypt there was bread**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famine is universal. The supply is in one country."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt had no more rain than anyone else. What Egypt had was seven years of somebody storing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pharaoh's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people cried out to **Pharaoh** for food, and Pharaoh told them all, **\"Go to Joseph. Do whatever he tells you.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They go to the king and the king redirects them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a strange sentence for a monarch. He has the title, the throne and the granaries in his name, and he sends the nation to a Hebrew ex-convict."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Do whatever he tells you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what he says to you, do*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary uses the same words at a wedding in Cana, about her Son, to the servants standing by six empty water pots. See the note on John 2:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The storehouses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph **opened the storehouses** and sold grain to the Egyptians.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were opened by the man who filled them. Nobody else had the keys or the knowledge of what was in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sold"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sells it. The chapters that follow record what the price eventually was — money, then livestock, then land, then the people themselves as Pharaoh's servants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses reports the whole transaction without editorial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Everyone came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And people came **from every country** to Egypt to buy grain from Joseph, because the famine was **severe over all the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That last line is the hinge of the book. The next verse of chapter 42 is Jacob saying to his sons, *why do you look at one another? I have heard that there is grain in Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten brothers who sold him will walk up to the man selling grain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been doing for seven years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gathered grain **as the sand of the sea, very much, until he stopped counting**, for it was immeasurable.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As the sand of the sea* is the phrase used of Abraham's descendants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did it during the good years, when nobody could see a reason to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he got there.** Sold at seventeen, a slave in Potiphar's house, accused and jailed, forgotten by a man who promised to remember him. Then one morning shaved, changed and standing in front of Pharaoh. Moses gives thirteen years to the descent and one chapter to the rise."}]}, "Genesis 42:29–38": {"title": "\"Everything is against me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man adds up his situation and reaches a conclusion that is wrong in every particular."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are robbing me of my children. Joseph is gone, Simeon is gone, and now you want to take Benjamin. Everything is against me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three losses counted, and then the summary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Item by item"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Joseph is gone.** Joseph is alive, second in Egypt, and is the man they have just been dealing with. He is the reason there is grain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Simeon is gone.** Simeon is being held by his own brother, in comfort, as surety. He is not in danger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Now you want to take Benjamin.** Benjamin is the one condition on which the whole family is about to be reunited and fed through five more years of famine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every fact in the list is true, and every conclusion drawn from it is false."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silver"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As they emptied their sacks, **there was each man's bundle of silver** in his sack. When they and their father saw the silver, **they were afraid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The money was put back by Joseph, as a gift. Grain paid for and the payment returned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They receive a kindness and are frightened by it, because they cannot imagine a reason for it that is not a trap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Everything is against me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *all these things are against me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the most quoted sentences in Genesis, and it is spoken by a man who is mistaken about all three of the things he has just listed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's line in Romans is the mirror of it: *we know that **all things work together for good** to those who love God.* See the note on Romans 8:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word about the same kind of situation, from the other side of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob could see"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing that was actually happening. His son was ruler of the greatest empire on earth and had wept in the next room; the silver was a present; the demand for Benjamin was the last step before the reveal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From inside the tent it looked like a family being dismantled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Reuben's offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You may put both my sons to death** if I don't bring him back.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a serious offer and a terrible one. Jacob does not even answer it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah's version, one chapter later, is the one that works: *I myself will be surety for him; from my hand you shall require him.* He offers himself rather than his children, and Jacob agrees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says last"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"His brother is dead, and **he alone is left**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sons of Rachel, and he speaks as though one is gone and one is all he has."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are alive, and within a chapter or two he will have them both."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says when he finds out.** *\"It is enough. **Joseph my son is still alive.** I will go and see him before I die.\"* And Genesis records that *the spirit of Jacob their father revived.*"}]}, "Genesis 43:1–7": {"title": "\"Why did you bring this trouble on me?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The grain runs out, and the argument the family had been avoiding has to happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pressure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The famine **still lay heavy on the land**. When they had **eaten all the grain** they'd brought from Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has been decided since chapter 42. Simeon is still in an Egyptian prison and Jacob has refused to send Benjamin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have simply been eating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go back and buy us a little more food**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob says it as though nothing else is in the way. *A little more* — as if a small trip were possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Judah's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Judah **reminded him**, \"The man warned us plainly, '**You will not see my face unless your brother is with you**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes it twice in four verses. There is no way round it and he says so by repeating the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you send our brother along, **we'll go down and buy food for you**. But if you won't send him, **we can't go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah puts the decision back where it belongs. He does not plead and does not decide for his father."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jacob's complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Israel said, \"**Why did you bring this trouble on me by telling the man you had another brother?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *why did you deal so wrongfully with me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is looking for somebody to blame for a famine and a stranger's condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The man questioned us closely** about ourselves and our family. '**Is your father still alive? Do you have another brother?**' We simply answered what he asked.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are telling the truth. Chapter 42 records the interrogation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they could not know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How could we know he'd say, 'Bring your brother down here'?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph had been asking about his father and his mother's other son. The brothers took it for a Egyptian official's suspicion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Judah offers next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I myself will be surety for him; from my hand you shall require him**. If I do not bring him back to you and set him before you, then let me bear the blame forever.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben had offered his own two sons in chapter 42 and Jacob did not answer him. See the note on Genesis 42:29–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah offers himself, and Jacob agrees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Judah does later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When the cup is found in Benjamin's sack, Judah makes the longest speech in Genesis and ends: *now therefore, please **let your servant remain instead of the lad as a slave to my lord**, and let the lad go up with his brothers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He keeps the offer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Judah had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The brother who proposed selling Joseph in the first place: *what profit is there if we kill our brother and conceal his blood? Come, let us sell him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis puts twenty-two years and one chapter about Tamar between the two speeches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jacob's surrender"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take double money in your hand… **take your brother, and arise, go back to the man**. And may God Almighty give you mercy before the man, that he may release your other brother and Benjamin. **If I am bereaved, I am bereaved**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**God Almighty** — **El Shaddai**, the name given to Abraham at ninety-nine. See the note on Genesis 17:1–2."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What breaks the deadlock.** Not persuasion. The food ran out, and Judah put the terms in front of his father twice and then offered to stand for the boy himself."}]}, "Genesis 43:15–25": {"title": "Received at Joseph's house", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They arrive expecting arrest and are shown to a dining room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the men took **the gift, double the silver, and Benjamin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things, and the third is what the whole trip was about. See the note on Genesis 43:1–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Joseph **saw Benjamin with them**, he said to his steward, \"**Bring these men into my house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is looking for one face. The instruction follows immediately on seeing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benjamin is the only full brother he has — the other son of Rachel, born after Joseph was sold, whom he has never met as an adult."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Slaughter an animal and prepare a meal; **they will dine with me at noon**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their reading of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the men were afraid** to be taken there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It's because of the silver** returned in our sacks the first time. He wants to **fall on us, overpower us, seize us as slaves, and take our donkeys**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An invitation to lunch, read as an ambush."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not being paranoid without cause — they had found money they had not paid back in their sacks, and they are in a foreign country with no standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **at the doorway of the house** they approached Joseph's steward.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They will not go in. They intercept the steward at the threshold and make their case outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We came down once before to buy food, and **each man's silver was right there in the mouth of his sack — the full amount**. We've **brought it back with us, and more silver besides**. **We don't know who put our silver in our sacks**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They produce the money before anyone accuses them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It's all right**,\" he said. \"**Don't be afraid**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shalom lakem** — peace to you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the steward says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your God, the God of your father, must have given you treasure in your sacks. I received your silver**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An Egyptian household official tells eleven Hebrews that their father's God put money in their bags."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second clause settles it: the account is closed. Whatever is in the sacks, the payment was made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Simeon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **brought Simeon out to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The brother left in prison at the end of chapter 42, produced without ceremony."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hospitality"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The steward took the men into Joseph's house, gave them **water to wash their feet**, and **fed their donkeys**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three courtesies: shelter, water for the feet, fodder for the animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The washing of feet was the first thing a guest received, and it is the one Simon the Pharisee skipped. See the note on Luke 7:44–50."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with the gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **laid out their gift**, for they'd heard they would eat there at noon.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balm, honey, spices, myrrh, pistachios and almonds — Jacob's list, the best of the land, sent to a man who owned Egypt's granaries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph comes in, asks after their father, sees Benjamin, and has to leave the room. See the note on Genesis 43:26–34."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they were braced for.** Slavery. What they got was a threshold conversation, a steward saying *peace to you*, their brother released, water for their feet and hay for the donkeys — and the debt already settled."}]}, "Genesis 43:26–34": {"title": "Five times as much, and a man who had to leave the room", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joseph gives a dinner for the brothers who sold him, and two things happen at it that they do not understand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room he went into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph hurried out, for his heart was overflowing at the sight of his brother, and he was about to weep. He went into his private room and wept there. Then he washed his face, came out, controlled himself, and said, \"Serve the meal.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been the second most powerful man in Egypt for years. He sees his full brother for the first time since he was seventeen and has to leave his own house to cry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records the washing and the composure. It is one of the most human paragraphs in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The brothers were seated in front of him in order, from the oldest to the youngest, and they looked at one another in amazement.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had told the steward their ages. Ten men are placed in birth order by a household they have never visited, and they cannot account for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator gives no explanation either. He simply reports them staring at each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Benjamin's portion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Benjamin's portion was five times larger than anyone else's.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph does it deliberately, and it is a test as much as a gift. These are the men who once could not stand their father's favouritism toward one son and threw him in a pit for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now, in front of them, the youngest is singled out again — and this time nobody objects. *So they drank and were merry together with him.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it is going.** The next chapter is the silver cup planted in Benjamin's sack, and Judah — the brother who proposed selling Joseph — offering himself as a slave in the boy's place. The dinner is the setup. Joseph is finding out whether they are the same men, and they are not."}]}, "Genesis 44:1–13": {"title": "The cup in Benjamin's sack", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last test, and Joseph builds it so that the brothers have every reason to walk away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Fill the men's sacks with **as much food as they can carry**, and put **each man's silver in the mouth of his sack**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same trick as the first trip, and it had terrified them then."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The addition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And put **my cup, the silver one, in the mouth of the youngest one's sack**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benjamin. The one their father would not let out of his sight, and the one Judah has personally guaranteed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The staging"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At dawn the men were sent on their way. **They hadn't gone far from the city** when Joseph said to his steward, \"**Go after those men**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not far. They are close enough to be brought back and far enough to have committed to the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why have you repaid good with evil?** Isn't this **the cup my master drinks from and uses for divination**? You've done a wicked thing.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Divination by cup was a known Egyptian practice — liquid poured and read."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph, who has told Pharaoh outright that interpretations belong to God, has the steward describe him in Egyptian terms. He is still in character."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your servants **would never do anything like that**. We even **brought back to you from Canaan the silver we found in our sacks**. Why would we steal silver or gold?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is a good one. Men who returned money they could have kept do not steal a cup."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they swear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If any of your servants is found with it, let him die, and the rest of us will become my lord's slaves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They offer more than is asked. They are certain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The steward's terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Very well. But **only the one found with it will be my slave; the rest of you are free**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condition is deliberately generous. Ten men can go home clean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole design of the test: an exit is provided."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The search"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each man **quickly lowered his sack to the ground** and opened it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are so confident they unload before being asked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The steward searched, **beginning with the oldest and ending with the youngest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order draws it out. Ten sacks come up clean, one after another, and the tension rises with each one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The find"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the cup was found in Benjamin's sack**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At this **they tore their clothes in grief. They loaded their donkeys and returned to the city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody takes the exit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-two years earlier these men sold a brother into Egypt and told their father a wild animal had eaten him. Offered the chance to leave Rachel's other son behind and go home free, all ten turn round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what Joseph was testing, and it is the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Judah says in the next chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The longest speech in Genesis — the whole story from their father's side, and then: *let your servant remain instead of the lad as a slave to my lord, and let the lad go up with his brothers. **For how shall I go up to my father if the lad is not with me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who proposed the sale offers to take the place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Joseph could not restrain himself… and he cried out, \"**Make everyone go out from me!**\" And he wept aloud.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am Joseph; does my father still live?**\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the test was for.** Not the cup. Joseph gave them a clean way to abandon Rachel's remaining son and go home — and every one of them tore his clothes and went back."}]}, "Genesis 45:21–28": {"title": "\"Don't quarrel on the way\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joseph sends his brothers home loaded with gifts, and the last thing he says to them is oddly practical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What each of them got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To each of them he gave a new set of clothing, but to Benjamin he gave three hundred pieces of silver and five sets of clothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just revealed himself and forgiven them outright. Then he hands out clothes — and gives Benjamin five times what anyone else received, exactly as he did at the dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not being careless. He knows what favouritism did to this family; he grew up in the middle of it. He does it anyway, in the open, having already told them the whole thing was God's doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parting instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Don't quarrel on the way.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew can mean do not be agitated, or do not fall out with one another. Both fit the situation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have a three-week journey ahead with a great deal to talk about — chiefly who suggested what, twenty-two years earlier, at a pit. Joseph, who has just told them not to be distressed or angry with themselves, adds one more sentence for the road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He forgave them and then anticipated the argument they were going to have about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What convinced Jacob"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jacob's heart went numb; he could not believe them.* Words did not do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when he saw the carts Joseph had sent to carry him, the spirit of their father Jacob revived.* The carts did — physical evidence that somebody in Egypt had made arrangements for an old man to travel."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says.** *It is enough. My son Joseph is still alive. I will go and see him before I die.* The Hebrew for *it is enough* is one word — rav, plenty, abundance. He has just been told he owns Egypt's best, and what he says is enough, and then names the only thing he actually wants."}]}, "Genesis 47:13–26": {"title": "The famine and the land", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four stages of a national bankruptcy, administered by one man, and Moses reports it without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was **no food anywhere**, for the famine was terribly severe. **Egypt and Canaan alike wasted away** because of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *wasted away* is *fainted* — the land itself is exhausted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both countries. The one Joseph was sold from and the one he was sold into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Stage one: silver"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph gathered up **all the silver in Egypt and Canaan** in payment for the grain… and he **brought it into Pharaoh's house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every coin in two countries, and none of it stays with Joseph. Moses notes where it went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Stage two: livestock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give us food. **Why should we die before your eyes** just because the silver is gone?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Bring your livestock, and I'll give you food in exchange.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Horses, flocks, herds, donkeys. *That year he **carried them through** with food in exchange for all their livestock.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *carried through* is **nahal** — to lead gently, to guide, the word in Psalm 23 for *He leads me beside the still waters*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is used of a man taking a nation's animals off it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Stage three: land and bodies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Nothing is left for our lord but **our bodies and our land**… **Buy us and our land** in exchange for food, and we and our land will serve Pharaoh.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They propose it. Joseph does not demand it — the people come with the offer, twice, in their own words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Joseph **bought all the land of Egypt for Pharaoh**… and the land became Pharaoh's.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only **the land of the priests he did not buy**, for the priests **received a fixed allowance from Pharaoh** and lived on it, so **they did not have to sell their land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one group in Egypt that keeps its property is the one already being provided for. Moses states the reason plainly: they had an allowance, so they never had to trade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph gives back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Here is **seed for you to sow**. When the harvest comes, **give a fifth of it to Pharaoh**. **The other four-fifths you may keep** as seed for the fields and food for yourselves, your households, and your children.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having taken everything, he hands back seed and sets the rate at twenty percent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four-fifths stays with the farmer. That was a light rate by ancient standards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have saved our lives**,\" they said. \"May we find favor in our lord's eyes; we will be Pharaoh's servants.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They call it salvation. Nobody in the chapter complains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Joseph made it **a law over the land of Egypt, still in force to this day**, that **a fifth belongs to Pharaoh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To this day* — Moses is writing centuries later and the arrangement is still running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The relocation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As for the people, **he moved them into the cities** from one end of Egypt to the other.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole rural population reorganised around the granaries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening in the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel is settled in Goshen with the best of the land, tax-free, provided for by the brother his sons sold. The contrast is deliberate and Moses puts them in the same chapter. See the note on Genesis 47:27–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The direction of the seven years.** The whole transaction runs on grain that was stored during the plenty by a man who believed a dream about the famine. See the note on Genesis 41:53–57."}]}, "Genesis 47:27–31": {"title": "\"Do not bury me in Egypt\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man's last request, and it is entirely about a location."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Israel settled in Egypt, in **the region of Goshen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The best of the land, given by Pharaoh, during a famine that had just cost every Egyptian their fields. See the note on Genesis 47:13–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to them there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **took possession of property** there, and they were **fruitful and increased greatly in number**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *took possession* is **achaz** — to grasp, to take hold of. It is the root of the word for an inheritance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Fruitful and increased** is the language of Genesis 1 — *be fruitful and multiply* — and of the promise to Abraham."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus opens by repeating it: *the children of Israel were fruitful and increased abundantly, multiplied and grew exceedingly mighty; and the land was filled with them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessing works in Egypt, and it is the reason a later Pharaoh becomes afraid of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jacob lived in Egypt **seventeen years**, and the years of his life came to **a hundred and forty-seven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen years. Joseph was seventeen when he was sold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy had seventeen years with his father at the start; the father gets seventeen with him at the end. Moses gives both numbers and lets the reader put them together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If I have found favor in your eyes**, place your hand under my thigh and promise to show me **kindness and faithfulness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father asks his son for a favour, in the language of a subject asking a ruler — which is what Joseph now is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kindness and faithfulness** is **chesed ve'emet** — covenant loyalty and truth. The pairing runs right through the Old Testament and turns up in John's prologue: *full of grace and truth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Place your hand **under my thigh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same gesture Abraham required of his servant when he sent him to find a wife for Isaac. It appears twice in Scripture and both times it concerns the promise and the line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not bury me in Egypt**. When I rest with my fathers, **carry me out of Egypt and bury me where they are buried**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goshen has been good to him. He has land, grandchildren, a son running the country, and seventeen prosperous years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His last instruction is to be taken out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where \"where they are buried\" is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cave of Machpelah at Hebron — the one field Abraham bought and paid four hundred shekels of silver for, the only piece of Canaan any of them ever owned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham and Sarah are there, Isaac and Rebekah, and Leah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Not enough to be promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Swear it to me**,\" Jacob said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph has already said *I will do as you say*, and it is not enough. The old man wants it under oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Joseph swore to him, and **Israel bowed in worship at the head of his bed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is called **Israel** in that clause, not Jacob."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews takes the moment up: *by faith Jacob, when he was dying… worshiped, leaning on the top of his staff.* See the note on Hebrews 11:17–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters later, at his own death, he gives the same instruction about his own bones — and his coffin waits in Egypt for four hundred years until Moses carries it out on the night of the exodus."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the request says.** He is prospering in Egypt and he calls somewhere else home. Everything he had was in Goshen; the only ground he wanted was a cave in a country he had left."}]}, "Genesis 49:1–2": {"title": "\"What will happen in the days to come\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man on a bed calls twelve grown sons around him, and what follows is the longest piece of poetry in Genesis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The days to come\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will tell you what will happen to you **in the days to come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **acharit ha-yamim** — the latter days, the end of the days. It is the standard prophetic phrase; Isaiah, Hosea, Micah and Daniel all use it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not handing out farewell advice. He announces prophecy and then delivers twenty-five verses of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come together and listen, sons of **Jacob**. Listen to **Israel** your father.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both names in one sentence, and Genesis never uses them carelessly. Jacob is the supplanter, the man who grabbed a heel; Israel is the name he was given at Peniel, after the wrestling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father speaking is both of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of blessings these are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the twelve and they are not uniformly pleasant. Reuben loses his birthright in three lines. Simeon and Levi are told their anger is cursed and they will be scattered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not flattering anybody. Whatever is in these verses, he is saying what he sees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the weight falls"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the twelve get far more than the rest — Judah, and Joseph. Judah gets the sceptre and the line: *the sceptre shall not depart from Judah… until Shiloh comes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what is said over the others is short. A single line each, in most cases — Gad, Asher, Naphtali, Issachar, Dan, Zebulun. Two dozen words apiece, and there is a great deal packed into them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this chapter is worth going slowly through"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the road to Emmaus, *beginning at Moses and all the Prophets, He expounded to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beginning at Moses. Genesis is Moses, and this is the last chapter of it but one. The blessings over the twelve sons were in that sermon. See the note on Luke 24:13–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each of these sons became a tribe, and each tribe was given a piece of ground — and Jesus walked that ground, town by town."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter ends.** *He drew his feet up into the bed, breathed his last, and was gathered to his people.* Twenty-five verses of prophecy and then a man pulling his feet up onto a bed. Genesis notices things like that."}]}, "Genesis 49:13": {"title": "Zebulun — a harbour for ships", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One line, about a coastline, and it was said two hundred years before the land was divided."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Zebulun will **live by the seashore**. He will be **a harbour for ships**, and his border will reach toward Sidon.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob is in Egypt, dying, with twelve sons and no territory anywhere. Nobody had surveyed anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two centuries later Joshua divides the land by lot, and Zebulun's portion runs up into the north country toward the coast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zebulun** means dwelling. Leah named him: *now my husband will dwell with me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He lived"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He left Nazareth and went to live in **Capernaum**, a town beside the sea in **the territory of Zebulun and Naphtali**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says it deliberately, and then quotes Isaiah over it:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali… **the people sitting in darkness have seen a great light**, and on those living in the land where death casts its shadow, light has dawned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nazareth is in Zebulun. Capernaum is on the border of Zebulun and Naphtali. He grew up in one and made His home in the other, and both are in the tribe whose name means dwelling. See the note on Matthew 4:12–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harbour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zebulun is *a harbour for ships*, and the lake it sits on is where the boats were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon's boat, pushed out a little from the land, used as a pulpit and then filled until it settled low in the water. The storm crossing. The walking on the sea. Levi's tax booth at the harbour of Capernaum."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And after the resurrection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Simon Peter said, \"I am going fishing\"… and all that night they caught nothing at all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Back on the same water, in the same trade, after everything. And He is on the beach at daybreak with a charcoal fire, bread and fish already on it, asking whether they have any food."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He fills their net, feeds them breakfast, and then restores Peter three times over a charcoal fire — the only other charcoal fire in John's Gospel is the one Peter warmed himself at while he denied Him. See the note on John 21:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Livelihood, a meal, and the thing Peter could not say. All three, in the tribe called dwelling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses added over Zebulun.** *Rejoice, Zebulun, in your going out… they shall call the peoples to the mountain; there they shall offer sacrifices of righteousness, for they shall partake of the abundance of the seas.* A tribe on the trade routes, calling the nations in."}]}, "Genesis 49:14–15": {"title": "Issachar — the donkey between the burdens", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The saddest of the twelve, and it describes a man who could have had rest and chose a load instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Issachar is a **strong donkey**, resting among the sheepfolds. When he sees how good his resting place is and how pleasant the land, **he will bend his shoulder to the load and submit to hard labour**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the logic of it. He sees the rest is good. He sees the land is pleasant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And that is the reason he takes up the burden — the Hebrew is that he becomes a servant to forced labour, a slave gang."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had the rest in front of him and put his shoulder under a load."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Issachar** means *there is hire*, or *he brings wages*. Leah named him that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man whose name means wages, ending up in forced labour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Issachar's portion was the Jezreel valley — some of the best farmland in the whole country, which is what makes the *pleasant land* line so exact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in that valley sits a town called **Nain**, which means pleasant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened at that gate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As He came near the town gate, a dead man was being carried out — the only son his mother had, and she was a widow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A funeral procession, in the tribe whose blessing is a donkey between two burdens. Two dead men in one town — the son on the frame, and the mother's whole future with him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He touched the frame, the bearers stood still, and He said *young man, I say to you, get up.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did not ask for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus **gave him back to his mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is requested. No follow, no serve, no condition. He raises the boy and hands him over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Issachar submitted to hard labour to keep a pleasant land. In the pleasant town in Issachar's own valley, a man is given his life back and is asked for nothing at all. See the note on Luke 7:11–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two burdens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older rendering has him *couching down between two burdens* — the two packs a donkey carries, one on each side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the Hebrew word is exactly, the picture is a strong animal lying down under weight it did not have to carry."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the tribe was later known for.** *The sons of Issachar, who had understanding of the times, to know what Israel ought to do.* Two hundred chiefs of them came to David at Hebron. The tribe that bowed its shoulder to a load became the tribe that could read a season."}]}, "Genesis 49:19": {"title": "Gad — the troop and the last word", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eleven words in English, and in Hebrew it is a piece of wordplay Jacob almost certainly meant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name inside the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gad** means a troop, a raiding band. And the Hebrew line runs *gad gedud yegudennu, ve-hu yagud aqeb* — the same consonants hammered five times over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A troop shall troop upon him, but he shall troop upon their heel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Gad will be **attacked by raiders**, but he will **turn and strike at their heels**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trouble is not denied. He gets raided, and the raiders are described as a troop — more than he can meet head-on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last clause is the last event. Whoever hits first, Gad has the final word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground Gad was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "East of the Jordan, on the frontier — the most exposed territory any tribe held, open to Ammon, Moab and every raiding party out of the desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's line was not decorative. It described the border they would live on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus walked into it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Across that lake, on the eastern shore, He met a man who could not be bound with chains, living among the tombs, cutting himself with stones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What is your name?\" \"**Legion**,\" he answered, because a great many demons had gone into him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman legion was some thousands of men. It is the largest concentration of the enemy named anywhere in the Gospels, and it is standing on Gad's side of the water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks, and it comes out. One word against a troop, and the man is found sitting, dressed and in his right mind. See the note on Luke 8:30–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will strike at their heels.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heel is where Genesis started. Chapter 3: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's fourteenth son-blessing uses the word from the promise made in the garden, and the man who grabbed his brother's heel at birth is the one saying it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shortest blessings carry the most.** Gad, Asher and Naphtali get one line each. That is the whole inheritance in words — a troop turned back, food fit for a king, and a doe set free — and every one of them turns up as ground Jesus stood on."}]}, "Genesis 49:20": {"title": "Asher — bread fit for a king", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One line, and it is about food."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Asher's food will be **rich**; he will provide **delicacies fit for a king**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rich* is **shamen** — fat, the choicest part. And the second phrase is *dainties of a king*, the food set on a royal table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Asher** means happy, blessed. Leah named him: *happy am I, for the daughters will call me blessed.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asher's portion was the coastal strip in the far north-west, running up toward Tyre and Sidon — the richest agricultural land in Israel, olive country."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses says it in Deuteronomy 33: *let Asher dip his foot in oil.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus went into it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus went out from there and departed to the region of **Tyre and Sidon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Asher's border, and it is the only recorded time He left Israel's territory altogether."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There a Canaanite woman came after Him about her daughter, and what she got from Him was a sentence about bread:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is not right to take **the children's bread** and toss it to the little dogs.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children's bread"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls it bread, and He calls it the children's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not argue with the arrangement. She takes the picture and finishes it: *\"Yes, Lord — and even the little dogs eat **the crumbs** that fall from their master's table.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A crumb off that table broke a demonic hold on a child in another house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asher's line said *rich food, delicacies fit for a king*, and the meeting that happens on Asher's ground is a conversation about whose table the bread comes from. See the note on Matthew 15:21–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Woman, your faith is great. Let it be done for you just as you desire.\"* And her daughter was healed from that hour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She asked for a crumb and it was enough."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses added over Asher.** *As your days, so shall your strength be.* Set beside Jacob's line about rich food, the two blessings on this tribe are about being fed and being strong for the length of the days you get. See the note on Deuteronomy 33:24–25."}]}, "Genesis 49:21": {"title": "Naphtali — a doe set free", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The gentlest line in the chapter, and it is about something being let go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Naphtali is **a doe set free**, that bears **beautiful fawns**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a hind loosed, released. Something that was tied is not tied any more, and what comes out of it is beautiful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half can be read as beautiful fawns or as beautiful words — the Hebrew carries both, and older translations render it *he gives goodly words*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Naphtali** means my wrestling. Rachel named him after the struggle with her sister."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naphtali's territory was the north-west shore of the Sea of Galilee — Capernaum, Bethsaida, Chorazin, the Mount of Beatitudes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where Jesus based Himself. *Leaving Nazareth, He came and dwelt in Capernaum, which is by the sea, in the regions of Zebulun and **Naphtali**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes Isaiah over it: *the land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali… the people who sat in darkness have seen a great light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most of the miracles happened on that shoreline, and the Sermon on the Mount was preached on that hillside. Beautiful words, on Naphtali's ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The loosing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a synagogue in that country there was a woman bent double for eighteen years, who could not straighten up at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Woman, you are **loosed** from your infirmity.\"* And He laid His hands on her, and immediately she was made straight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When the ruler of the synagogue objected about the Sabbath, He used the word again — *ought not this woman, being a daughter of Abraham, whom Satan has bound for eighteen years, be **loosed** from this bond on the Sabbath?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Loosed, twice, over a woman who had been tied for eighteen years, in the tribe whose blessing is a doe set free. See the note on Luke 13:10–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did the binding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it plainly in that synagogue: *whom Satan has bound.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a discipline, not a lesson. A binding, and He unties it in a sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses said over Naphtali.** *O Naphtali, satisfied with favour, and full of the blessing of the LORD.* The word is *ratson* — good pleasure, acceptance. Satisfied with favour, on the shore where He did most of His work."}]}, "Genesis 49:22–26": {"title": "Joseph — branches over the wall", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The longest of the twelve blessings, and Jacob spends most of it on titles for God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Joseph is a fruitful vine, a fruitful vine **beside a spring**, whose branches **climb over a wall**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things: fruitful, next to water, and not staying inside the boundary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *climb over* is to march over, to stride across. The wall does not stop it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The archers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"With bitterness archers attacked him; they shot at him and pressed him hard. **But his bow stayed steady**, and his arms were made strong **by the hands of the Mighty One of Jacob**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything true of Joseph's life is in that line — the pit, the slave sale, Potiphar's wife, the prison, the forgotten cupbearer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the strength is credited: *by the hands of the Mighty One.* His arms were made strong by somebody else's hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names for God, stacked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four in two lines, and Jacob is running out of ways to say it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the Mighty One of Jacob**", "**the Shepherd** — the first time in Scripture God is called this", "**the Rock of Israel**", "**the Almighty** who blesses you"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old man who wrestled at Peniel calls Him a Shepherd, and it is the first occurrence of the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Blessings of the skies above, blessings of the deep that lies below, blessings of the breast and womb… **greater than the blessings of the ancient mountains**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Above, below, and the family line. And then a comparison — greater than the mountains, which is Hebrew for greater than the oldest and most permanent things anyone can point at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the branches went over the wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph's portion was Samaria. And Jacob's well was on that ground — *near the piece of ground Jacob had given to his son Joseph.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A fruitful vine beside a spring.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was a wall between Jew and Samaritan, and it was absolute: *Jews have no dealings with Samaritans.* His disciples marvelled that He was talking with her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He went through Samaria, sat down at that well worn out from the journey, and asked a woman for a drink. See the note on John 4:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The branches ran over the wall on Joseph's own land."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *Let all these rest on the head of Joseph, on the brow of **the prince among his brothers**.* The Hebrew is the one separated from his brothers — the same word used of a Nazirite, one set apart. It is exactly what they did to him, turned into a title."}]}, "Genesis 49:27": {"title": "Benjamin — the morning and the evening", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last of the twelve, and it is one line about a wolf and two halves of a day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Benjamin is a **ravenous wolf**; **in the morning** he devours the prey, and **in the evening** he divides the spoil.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same animal, two different activities, split by the hours of one day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Morning: tearing. Evening: distributing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rachel died bearing him and called him **Ben-Oni**, son of my sorrow. His father renamed him **Benjamin**, son of the right hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only son of Jacob born in the land, and the only one whose name his father overruled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The most famous Benjamite"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, **of the tribe of Benjamin**, a Hebrew of Hebrews.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it about himself twice — in Philippians and in Romans."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Saul was consenting to his death… **he made havoc of the church**, entering every house, and dragging off men and women, committing them to prison.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *made havoc* is the word used of a wild animal mangling a body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *breathing threats and murder against the disciples of the Lord*, going to Damascus with letters, to bring back any he found *bound to Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the wolf in the morning, and Luke's vocabulary for it is not accidental."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same man, later, writing from a cell: *I have learned, in whatever state I am, to be content.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen letters distributed to churches across the empire. Collections carried from Macedonia and Achaia to the poor saints at Jerusalem. Timothy, Titus, Luke, Onesimus, Philemon, Priscilla and Aquila."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the evening he divides the spoil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what he divides is what he took: *what things were gain to me, these I have counted loss for Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Standing before Agrippa"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now I stand here on trial for **the hope of the promise God made to our fathers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who dragged people to prison is in chains himself, on trial, and calls it standing for a hope. He asks the king a question rather than mounting a defence: *why should it be judged something incredible by any of you that God raises the dead?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the end of that hearing he has told Agrippa he wishes every man in the room were *as I am, except for these chains.* See the note on Acts 26:4–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it took"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One morning on the Damascus road, and a light above the brightness of the sun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in Jacob's line says the wolf became a different animal. It says what the same animal did with the second half of the day."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other famous Benjamite.** King Saul — the first king of Israel, from this tribe, who hunted David and died on Gilboa. And then Saul of Tarsus, from the same tribe, who hunted the Church and was renamed. Two men called Saul, one tribe, and only one of them let go of the sword."}]}, "Genesis 49:3–4": {"title": "Reuben — unstable as water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The firstborn gets the longest list of titles in the chapter and loses everything in the next line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Reuben, you are **my firstborn, my might, the first sign of my strength** — first in rank and first in power.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four titles stacked, and every one of them is genuine. The double portion, the headship of the family, the priesthood of the household — all of it was his by right of birth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob lists it in full before taking it away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet** you are as unruly as the churning waters, and you will no longer hold first place.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older rendering is *unstable as water*, and it is the phrase the tribe is remembered by."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water takes whatever shape the container gives it. It has no form of its own and no way of holding a line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For you **climbed into your father's bed** and defiled it — you went up onto my couch.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 35 records it in one sentence and moves on: *Reuben went and lay with Bilhah his father's concubine, and Israel heard about it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is all. No confrontation, no punishment, no conversation — *Israel heard about it*, and then thirty-five chapters of silence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The silence ends here, on a deathbed, in front of all eleven brothers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the flesh at its best produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben is not a villain. He is the one who tried to save Joseph from the pit — *let us not kill him… throw him into this pit*, intending to come back for him. He is the one who offered his own two sons as security for Benjamin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He means well every time, and nothing holds. Good instincts, no strength to carry them through — which is the definition of the word Jacob uses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What became of the tribe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No judge came out of Reuben. No prophet, no king, no leader of any note."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deborah's song asks about them: *among the divisions of Reuben there were great searchings of heart… why did you sit among the sheepfolds?* They debated it at length and did not come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben first, and he loses the birthright. Simeon and Levi next, and their anger is cursed. Then Judah, and the sceptre."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three sons pass before the blessing lands anywhere, and the first three are what the natural man is: unstable, and then violent."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the birthright went.** *The birthright was given to the sons of Joseph* — 1 Chronicles 5 says so outright, and explains why: *because he defiled his father's bed.* The double portion went to Joseph, the kingship went to Judah, and the priesthood went to Levi. What Reuben held alone was split three ways and given to other men."}]}, "Genesis 49:5–7": {"title": "Simeon and Levi — the anger, not the men", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only curse in the chapter, and it is aimed carefully."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 34. Their sister Dinah was violated by Shechem, and the two brothers proposed a treaty: intermarriage, on condition that every male in the town be circumcised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it came to pass on the third day, when they were sore, that two of the sons of Jacob took each man his sword and came upon the city boldly, and slew all the males.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They waited until the men could not defend themselves, and killed the town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In their fury they crippled oxen\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In their anger they killed men, and in their fury they **crippled oxen**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to hamstring — cut the tendon so the animal can never work again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the detail Jacob picks out. Not just the killing. Destruction beyond any purpose, on animals that had done nothing, because the rage had to go somewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is cursed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cursed be their anger, so fierce, and their fury, so cruel.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it exactly. Not *cursed be Simeon and Levi*. Their anger is cursed, and their fury."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob separates the men from the thing in them. It is the only curse he pronounces in the whole chapter and it is laid on a temper rather than a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **scatter** them throughout Jacob and **disperse** them across Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had used their unity to do it — two brothers acting together. So they are separated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simeon's inheritance ended up as scattered towns inside Judah's territory, and the tribe faded; it is missing from the blessing of Moses in Deuteronomy 33 altogether."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to the other one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levi was scattered too — forty-eight cities among the tribes, with no land of their own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the scattering became their office. *The LORD is their inheritance*, and the tribe with no territory is the tribe that carried the tabernacle, taught the law and served at the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levi at Sinai was the tribe that rallied to Moses when the calf was made. The sword that had been drawn on Shechem was put at God's disposal, and a curse ended up a priesthood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three at the start"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben, unstable. Simeon and Levi, violent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first three sons in the chapter, and the whole natural man is in them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Judah."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jacob says he will not do.** *\"Let me not enter their council; let me not join their gathering.\"* He will not be associated with the decision. He had said nothing at the time except that they had made him stink among the inhabitants of the land — and he says the rest of it here, twenty chapters later, dying."}]}, "Genesis 49:8–12": {"title": "Judah — the sceptre and the vine", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth son, and the chapter changes register completely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Judah, your brothers will **praise** you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yehudah** means praise. Leah named him: *now I will praise the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first three sons produced instability and violence. The fourth is called praise and gets the throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What praise does in that tribe's history"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jehoshaphat, king of Judah, faced three armies with no plan, and was told: *you will not even need to fight this battle. Take your positions, stand still, and watch the salvation of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He appointed singers to go out **in front of the army**, and *when they began to sing and to praise, the LORD set ambushes* — and it was over before Judah reached the battlefield. See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:16–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Judah is a **young lion**… like a lion he crouches and lies down — who would dare rouse him?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three stages of one animal in two lines: the young lion that has come up from the prey, the mature lion, and the lioness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation names Him from this verse: *the Lion of the tribe of Judah has prevailed.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sceptre"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The sceptre will not depart from Judah, nor the ruler's staff from between his feet, **until he to whom it belongs shall come**, and the obedience of the peoples will be his.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of *he to whom it belongs* is **Shiloh** — a word that occurs nowhere else, and the oldest Jewish readings take it as a name of Messiah. Its root carries the sense of rest, tranquility."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the obedience of the peoples* — not Israel. The peoples, plural, the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The donkey and the vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will **tie his donkey to the vine**, his colt to the choicest branch.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody ties livestock to a vine. A donkey would strip it in an hour, and a vine is the most carefully tended plant a man owns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a picture of abundance so complete that the loss does not matter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a colt, tied, waiting for a king — *tell the daughter of Zion, behold, your King is coming to you, lowly, and sitting on a donkey, a colt, the foal of a donkey.* They found it *tied* at a doorway, and He rode it into Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will **wash his garments in wine**, his robes in **the blood of grapes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not spilled on. Washed in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 63 takes the same image the other way: *why is Your apparel red, like one who treads in the winepress? I have trodden the winepress alone.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vine, again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the vine; you are the branches… **apart from Me you can do nothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it in an upper room, on the night of the cup, and the branch's whole job in that passage is to stay attached. The fruit is not the branch's work. See the note on John 15:5–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How long the sceptre stayed.** Judah held the right to try capital cases until Rome took it away, and the old accounts record the rabbis lamenting that the sceptre had departed and Shiloh had not come. He was in Nazareth at the time."}]}, "Genesis 5:1–5": {"title": "\"And then he died\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first genealogy in Scripture, and it is built around a refrain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **the written record** of Adam's descendants.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sepher toledoth** — the book of the generations. Genesis is structured around this formula; it appears eleven times and divides the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restatement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the day God created humanity, **He made them in the likeness of God**. **Male and female** He created them, and **He blessed them** and called them \"Mankind.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses goes back to chapter 1 and repeats it before starting the list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between then and now: a garden, a command, a serpent, an expulsion, and a murder. He restates the original terms anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **called them \"Mankind\"** on the day they were made.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **adam** — the word for humanity and the man's name. God gives the same name to both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change of preposition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had a son **in his own likeness, in his own image**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 1 has Adam made in the likeness of **God**. Verse 3 has Seth in the likeness of **Adam**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two words — **demuth** and **tselem** — used twice in three verses, with a different origin each time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something is now being transmitted down a line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Seth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4 explains the name: *God has appointed another seed for me instead of Abel, whom Cain killed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line does not run through the firstborn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The numbers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Adam lived another **800 years**… Altogether Adam lived **930 years**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then **he died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes at the end of every entry in the chapter. Seth died. Enosh died. Cainan died. Mahalalel, Jared, Methuselah, Lamech — *and he died*, eight times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the drumbeat of the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Enoch walked with God; and he was not, for God took him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only man in the list the refrain is not said of. See the note on Hebrews 11:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said in the garden"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In the day that you eat of it **you shall surely die**.\"* See the note on Genesis 2:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *dying you shall die* — the doubled construction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not fall down that afternoon. He lived nine hundred and thirty years and the process ran the whole way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through one man sin entered the world, and death through sin, and **thus death spread to all men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For as in Adam all die, even so in Christ all shall be made alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The genealogy is the demonstration. Eight entries, eight identical endings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other genealogy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke 3 runs the same list backwards from Jesus, through Seth, and ends *the son of Adam, the son of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew starts at Abraham and comes down. Luke goes all the way back to this chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is doing.** Repeating one sentence. Long lives, sons and daughters, and then three words at the end of each entry — and one man in the list who does not get them."}]}, "Genesis 5:21–24": {"title": "The man who is missing from the list", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 5 is a genealogy with a refrain. Every entry ends the same way, and one does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lived so many years, he had sons and daughters, and then he died.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight times in the chapter, like a drumbeat. Even Methuselah, who outlives everyone, ends with those two words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Enoch: *Enoch walked with God, and then he was no more, because God took him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence everybody else gets is missing from his."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Walked with God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in four verses. The Hebrew form suggests a continuing, habitual walking — not a journey with a destination, but a way of going about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only one other person in Genesis is described this way: Noah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the detail worth noticing is when it started. *When Enoch was 65, he fathered Methuselah. And Enoch walked with God — closely, faithfully — for 300 years after that.* The walking begins when the child arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shortest life in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "365 years, in a list where the others run to eight and nine hundred. By the standards of the genealogy he barely got started."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter measures everyone in years. Enoch is the one it does not need to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hebrews adds.** *By faith Enoch was taken from this life, so that he did not experience death… for before he was taken he was commended as one who pleased God.* The commendation came first, and the taking followed. Whatever the walking was, God enjoyed it enough to end it by simply keeping him."}]}, "Genesis 50:15–21": {"title": "\"You meant it for evil\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen years after the reconciliation, the brothers are still not sure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What triggers it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Joseph's brothers saw that **their father was dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob has just been buried at Machpelah, with a state procession of Egyptian officials and chariots."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They waited until the funeral was over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What if Joseph still holds a grudge against us** and pays us back for all the wrong we did him?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what if Joseph hates us*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had wept on their necks in chapter 45, told them not to be grieved or angry with themselves, kissed them, and settled them in Goshen with the best of the land. He had fed them through five years of famine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen years of provision, and they think it was for their father's sake."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they **sent word** to Joseph.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not come. They send."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Before he died, **your father gave this command**: 'Say to Joseph: Please forgive your brothers' offense.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis never records Jacob saying it. Whether he did or not, they put the request in a dead man's mouth rather than their own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they call themselves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now please forgive the offense of **the servants of the God of your father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They reach for the strongest connection they have and it is still second-hand — *your* father's God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When their message came to him, **Joseph wept**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew says nothing more. It is the seventh time Genesis records him weeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He weeps because after seventeen years they sent a message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prostration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then his brothers came and **threw themselves down before him**. \"**We are your slaves**,\" they said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dream from chapter 37 — the sheaves bowing — completes itself here, and by then nobody in the room wants it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't be afraid. Am I in the place of God?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *am I in God's stead?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He declines the position. Punishing them would require an office he says he does not hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You meant it for evil against me, but God meant it for good**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew uses the same verb twice — **chashab**, to weave, to devise, to reckon. Both parties intended, and the intentions ran in opposite directions over the same event."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften what they did. *You meant it for evil* stays in the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the good was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**To bring about what is happening today — the saving of many lives**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not his promotion. The survival of a region through seven years of famine, and of the family the covenant ran through. See the note on Genesis 41:53–57."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So don't be afraid. **I will provide for you and your little ones**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *provide* is **kul** — to sustain, to nourish. The same word he had used in chapter 45."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **comforted them and spoke tenderly to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *spoke to their heart*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul says the same thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we know that **all things work together for good** to those who love God.* See the note on Romans 8:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jacob's version from the other side, when he could see none of it: *all these things are against me.* See the note on Genesis 42:29–38."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says twice.** *Don't be afraid* — at the start of his answer and again at the end. The men in front of him had been forgiven for seventeen years and had spent them waiting for the bill."}]}, "Genesis 6:1–4": {"title": "The hundred and twenty years, and what they probably count", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four of the strangest verses in the Bible, and the honest thing to say about them first is that nobody is certain what they describe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Their days will be a hundred and twenty years\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two readings have been argued for a very long time, and both are serious:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**a new limit on human lifespan** — from now on, people live at most 120 years", "**a countdown to the flood** — 120 years of reprieve before the waters come"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second fits the immediate context better. The sentence begins *My Spirit will not contend with humanity forever*, which sounds like a clock running out on a situation rather than a change to biology. And the flood is the next thing that happens in the narrative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first reading has a real problem: several people after this live far longer — Noah, Shem, Abraham at 175. If it were a hard limit, it did not take."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sons of God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sons of God saw that these daughters were beautiful, and they took wives from among them, whomever they chose.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three explanations have long competed: angelic beings, the line of Seth intermarrying with the line of Cain, or dynastic rulers taking women by right. The phrase *sons of God* elsewhere in the Old Testament usually means heavenly beings, which is why the first reading is the oldest — but nothing here settles it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage is doing.** Whatever the details, the direction is clear: the phrase *took… whomever they chose* is the language of power used without restraint, and it sits immediately before God looking at the earth and seeing that every inclination of the human heart was bent. Genesis is describing a world coming apart, and it does not stop to explain the mechanics."}]}, "Genesis 6:13–22": {"title": "Build an ark", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A set of building instructions, and almost every detail in them is doing two jobs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pitch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Coat it with **pitch, inside and out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kaphar** — and this is the only place in Scripture it is used of tar. Everywhere else it is the word for **atonement**: to cover, to make propitiation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the root of **kippur**, as in Yom Kippur, the Day of Atonement. The noun **kopher** is the ransom price — the word Elihu uses in Job 33: *I have found a ransom.* See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing keeping the water out is called covering, inside and out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measurements"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*300 cubits long, 50 wide, and 30 high.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly 450 feet by 75 by 45 — a displacement of some 43,000 tons, and proportions close to what modern naval architecture uses for stability in heavy seas. It is not built to sail anywhere. It is built to stay upright and float."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lower, middle, and upper decks.* Three levels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set **a door in its side**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One. Not one per deck, not one at each end. A single door, and it is in the side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the door,\"* Jesus said. *\"If anyone enters by Me, he will be saved.\"* See the note on John 10:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what was opened in His side was opened with a spear, after He was dead — *immediately blood and water came out.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The window"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Put a roof on it, **finished to a cubit from the top**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An opening at the very top, running round under the roof line. The only light comes from above, and there is no way to see the water from inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever was in that boat could look up and could not look out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"But with you I will establish my covenant\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first occurrence of **covenant** in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes before the flood, not after — while the ark is still timber and the sky is still dry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who comes in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You and your sons, your wife, and your sons' wives.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight people, on the strength of one man's standing with God. Nothing is recorded about the faith of the other seven."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the animals *will come to you* — Noah is not told to go and catch them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Noah did everything just as God commanded him — **every last thing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew simply repeats it: *thus did Noah; according to all that God commanded him, so he did.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and twenty years of building, on a warning about weather that had never happened."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the instructions.** A rudder, a sail, an oar, or a destination. The ark has no means of steering and nowhere to go. It was built to be shut into and carried."}]}, "Genesis 6:5–8": {"title": "Noah found grace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first time the word **grace** appears in the Bible, and it lands in the darkest paragraph before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every inclination of the human heart was **only evil, all the time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three absolutes stacked: every, only, all the time. The Hebrew leaves no room — *every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only evil continually.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *inclination* is **yetzer**, from the verb for a potter forming clay. It is the shaping impulse, what the heart is bent toward before any act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it did to God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it **grieved the LORD deeply** that He had made humankind; **His heart was filled with pain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **atsab** — to hurt, to wound, to cause sorrow. It is the same root used of the pain of childbirth in chapter 3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God is described as being in pain. Genesis does not soften it, and this is four chapters after *very good*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will wipe from the earth the human race I have created.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to blot out, to erase — the word for wiping a dish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Noah — **Noah found grace in the eyes of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chen**. This is its first occurrence in Scripture, and it arrives at the bottom of the worst page in Genesis."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Found.** Not earned, achieved or deserved. The verb is the one for coming across something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the verses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 8: *Noah found grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 9: *Noah was a just man, perfect in his generations, and Noah walked with God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace comes first, and the character is described afterwards. The book does not say Noah was righteous and therefore found favour; it says he found favour, and then tells you what he was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his name means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Noah** — rest, comfort. Lamech named him: *this one will comfort us concerning our work and the toil of our hands, because of the ground which the LORD has cursed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man whose name is rest is the man who found grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Perfect in his generations\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tamim** — whole, sound, without blemish. It is the technical word Leviticus uses of an animal fit for sacrifice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole passage is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in it is God's assessment and God's decision — He saw, He grieved, He said, He will wipe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then one man is exempted, and the reason given is not his record."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Noah had to build with.** No rain had ever fallen, according to the account in chapter 2, and he was told to build a boat inland for a flood. Hebrews puts it plainly: *by faith Noah, being divinely warned of things **not yet seen**, moved with godly fear, prepared an ark.*"}]}, "Genesis 6:9–12": {"title": "\"Noah walked with God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three things are said about Noah, and then three about the world he lived in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about him"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**righteous** — right, in the sense of standing straight with God", "**blameless among the people of his time** — the word is used of sacrificial animals without defect", "**he walked with God**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third phrase is used of only one other man in Genesis: Enoch, four chapters earlier. Both are described the same way, and both stand out from a genealogy of people who simply lived and died."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"blameless\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not mean sinless. Genesis will show Noah drunk and shamed within a chapter of leaving the ark, and it does not edit that out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blameless among the people of his time* is a comparison. In a world the next verses call corrupt and violent, he was the one who was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about the world"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The earth was corrupt in God's sight and full of violence. God looked at the earth and saw how ruined it was.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word for *corrupt* and the word for *ruined* are the same root — and it is the same root used a few verses later when God says He will destroy them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The judgement matches the diagnosis exactly. They had ruined it; it would be ruined. God is described as confirming a state of affairs rather than inventing a penalty for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What kept Noah afloat.** Hebrews says he built the ark *by faith*, moved with godly fear, having been warned about things not yet seen. And Genesis, before any of the building starts, says the thing the whole account rests on: *Noah found favour in the eyes of the LORD.* The favour comes first, in the verse before this paragraph."}]}, "Genesis 7:1–5": {"title": "\"You and all your household\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the two halves of it do not obviously match."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mismatch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go into the ark, **you and all your household**, for I have seen that **you alone** are righteous in this generation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is about one man. The invitation covers eight people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said about Noah's wife, his sons, or his sons' wives — not a word about their character in either direction. They go in on the strength of the verdict passed on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern this belongs to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It happens repeatedly. A household is spared for the sake of one man's standing: Noah's family here, Rahab's family in Jericho, Lot's family in Sodom, the Israelite houses at Passover reckoned by household rather than by person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Bible keeps counting people in units of *house*, and it keeps doing it at the moments of rescue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Come into the ark\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is *come*, not *go*. It is the word you use to invite someone toward where you already are — which means God is not sending Noah into the boat from outside. He is calling him in from somewhere He already is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *And Noah did everything the LORD commanded him.* It is the fourth time Genesis says it in two chapters. The obedience is recorded flatly, without commentary, and then the rain starts seven days later — which is how long Noah had to sit in a finished boat under a clear sky."}]}, "Genesis 7:11–16": {"title": "\"Then the LORD shut him in\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last line of this passage is five words long in Hebrew and it is the point of the whole chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the six hundredth year of Noah's life, on **the seventeenth day of the second month**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis dates it to the day. Whatever else this account is, it is written as a record rather than a legend."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the water came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **great deep burst open** and the **floodgates of the heavens were flung wide**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both directions at once — up from below and down from above. The Hebrew for the first is that the fountains of the great deep were split open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rain is mentioned second, and it is the lesser half."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who went in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Noah, his sons Shem, Ham, and Japheth, his wife, and his three sons' wives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight. And every kind of creature *coming to Noah two by two* — the animals come to him; he does not go after them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the LORD shut him in** — sealed the door with His own hand, safe inside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and the LORD shut behind him*. Noah did not close the door."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He built the ark, gathered the food, loaded the animals, and walked in. The last act — the one that made it watertight — was done from outside by somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which name is used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text switches. Through the building instructions it is **Elohim**, God — the name of power and creation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is **YHWH**, the LORD, the covenant name given at the burning bush. The shutting of the door is done under the personal name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a shut door means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody inside could open it, and nobody outside could either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The security of everyone in that boat had nothing to do with how tightly they were holding on. The door was sealed by the One who told them to come in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus uses the same picture: *neither shall anyone snatch them out of My hand.* See the note on John 10:27–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word for the boat is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tebah**. It appears in only one other place in Scripture — the basket of papyrus, sealed with tar and pitch, that Moses' mother laid among the reeds. See the note on Exodus 2:1–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two vessels, both waterproofed, both carrying a life through water that killed everything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ark had no way of doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no rudder in the specification, no sail, no oar. Once the door was shut, nobody aboard could influence where it went."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For a hundred and fifty days they were carried."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word Jesus used about that week.** *They knew nothing about it **until the flood came** and swept them all away.* Matthew 24 — eating, drinking, marrying, right up to the day Noah entered the ark. The judgement is not what He describes as the surprise. The ordinariness is."}]}, "Genesis 8:1–5": {"title": "\"God remembered Noah\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turning point of the flood account is a verb about memory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"God remembered\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **God remembered Noah**, and all the wild animals and livestock with him in the ark.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zakar**. It does not mean God had forgotten — it is the word for acting on a commitment, turning attention toward someone in order to do something about them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is used of God remembering His covenant with Abraham when Israel groaned in Egypt, and of Him remembering Hannah, and Rachel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time it appears, something moves immediately afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What moved"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And God sent **a wind** over the earth, and the waters began to subside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for wind is **ruach** — also breath, also Spirit. The same word as Genesis 1:2, where *the Spirit of God hovered over the face of the waters.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A world under water, and a wind moving over it. Genesis is deliberately repeating its own opening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The closing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The springs of the deep and the floodgates of the heavens **were closed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two sources that were split open in chapter 7 are shut. Both directions, in reverse order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **the seventeenth day of the seventh month**, the ark came to rest on the mountains of Ararat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hold that date. The flood began on the seventeenth day of the second month."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel's calendar was reset at the exodus — *this month shall be your beginning of months* — which moved the seventh month to first place. The seventeenth of that month is three days after Passover on the fourteenth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days after the lamb is killed, the ark comes to rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Came to rest\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is **nuach** — to rest, to settle. It is the root of **Noah**'s own name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man called rest, in a boat that finally rests, on the seventeenth day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ararat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mountains of Ararat, in the region of ancient Urartu."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark does not land in a valley or on a shore. It comes to rest on high ground, above everything the water had covered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Noah does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing, for months. The waters *kept receding until, on the first day of the tenth month, the tops of the mountains appeared* — and he waits, and sends a raven, and then a dove, three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not open the door. He did not close it either. See the note on Genesis 7:11–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first thing God did in this chapter.** Remembered. Not calculated, not relented, not reconsidered. The Hebrew word for what turns the flood is the word for keeping somebody in mind."}]}, "Genesis 8:13–19": {"title": "Coming out of the ark", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The ground has been dry for nearly two months and Noah is still inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does when he sees dry land"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Noah **removed the covering** from the ark and looked out — and the surface of the ground was dry.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He looks. He does not leave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first day of the first month. The next date given is the twenty-seventh day of the second month — fifty-seven days later — and only then does God speak."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been shut in by the LORD's own hand and he waited to be let out the same way. See the note on Genesis 7:11–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then God said to Noah, \"**Come out** of the ark.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He went in when God said *come in*, and he comes out when God says *come out*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The entire year is bracketed by two instructions, and Noah supplies neither of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You, your wife, your sons, and their wives.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the order. Going in, it was *you and your sons, your wife, and your sons' wives* — the men and then the women."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Coming out: *you, your wife, your sons, and their wives* — the couples together. A year of confinement and the households are paired."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So they can multiply and fill the earth\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same commission given to Adam in Genesis 1 — *be fruitful and multiply, and fill the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The world is being restarted with the original sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Every one that went in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every animal, every creeping thing, and every bird **came out of the ark, kind by kind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *by their families*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing that went in is missing. The account has already recorded that the animals came to Noah rather than being hunted, and it now records that all of them came out again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The count going in matched the count coming out, across a year, in a boat, with no port of call."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is under his feet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A world that had been scoured. Everything he had known was gone — every place, every person outside the eight, every landmark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He steps out onto ground that is completely dry and completely emptied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *Noah built an altar to the LORD, and took of every clean animal and every clean bird, and offered burnt offerings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been given the clean animals by sevens rather than twos — the extra ones, which had been fed and carried for a year, were for this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD smelled a soothing aroma*, and said in His heart *I will never again curse the ground for man's sake.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise not to do it again follows an altar."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How long it lasted.** He entered on the seventeenth day of the second month and came out on the twenty-seventh day of the second month the following year — a year and ten days, shut in."}]}, "Genesis 8:20–22": {"title": "Seedtime and harvest", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first thing built on the new earth is an altar, and God's answer to it holds the world's weather in place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first act"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Noah **built an altar to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first altar named in Scripture. Cain and Abel brought offerings; nobody had built one before this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing on a planet with eight people on it, and this is what he does first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Taking **some of every clean animal and bird**, he offered burnt offerings on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clean species had come aboard by sevens rather than twos — the extra ones are on this altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The surplus that would have looked like breeding stock on an empty earth is what he burns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The aroma"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **smelled the pleasing aroma**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a soothing savour* — **reyach nichoach**, a restful smell. It becomes the standard phrase for accepted sacrifice through the whole of Leviticus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for it once, about Christ: *an offering and a sacrifice to God for a sweet-smelling aroma.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says in His heart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And said **in His heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses records it as an interior resolution, not an announcement. The promise is made before Noah is told it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Never again\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Never again** will I curse the ground because of humankind… and **never again** will I destroy all living creatures as I have done.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, in one breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Even though** every inclination of the human heart is bent toward evil **from childhood**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is nearly word for word what God said before the flood — *every intent of the thoughts of his heart was only evil continually* — and there it was the reason for the judgment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the identical assessment is the thing the promise is made in spite of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the human heart has changed between chapter 6 and chapter 8. What has changed is that there is an altar in front of Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As long as the earth endures, **seedtime and harvest, cold and heat, summer and winter, day and night will never cease**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four pairs. Every one is a cycle, and the guarantee is that they keep turning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seedtime comes first and harvest second, in that order, permanently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is guaranteed by"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not by the stability of the climate and not by anything Noah does afterward — the next chapter has him drunk in a tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By a sentence God said to Himself over a burning altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rainbow comes in the next chapter, given as the visible token of this. See the note on Genesis 9:8–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it rests on.** God smelled a sacrifice and put the seasons beyond recall. The rhythm of the world's food supply is fastened to an altar."}]}, "Genesis 9:8–17": {"title": "The bow in the cloud", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God makes a promise and then puts up something to look at, and the thing He puts up is a weapon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have set **my rainbow** in the clouds.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qeshet** — and it is simply the word for a bow. The war bow, the hunting bow, the thing David sang about, the weapon Jonathan gave away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no separate Hebrew word for a rainbow. God says *I have set My bow in the cloud*, and the picture is of a warrior hanging up his weapon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is hung pointing away — the arc bends toward heaven, with the string toward earth. Whatever is left in it is aimed at Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who has to remember"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whenever I bring clouds over the earth and the rainbow appears, **I will remember my covenant**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said three times in the passage: *I will remember*, *I will see it and remember*, *this is the sign of the covenant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *when you see it, remember*. The sign is placed where He looks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **zakar** is the same verb as *God remembered Noah* a chapter earlier — it means acting on a commitment, not recovering a lost thought. See the note on Genesis 8:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the covenant is with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"With you and your descendants after you, and **with every living creature** that was with you — the birds, the livestock, and all the wild animals.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it four times over. The animals are named as parties to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the first covenant in Scripture and it is made with the whole earth, with no conditions attached to anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not required"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. Read the whole passage and there is no *if*, no clause about behaviour, no term for either side to keep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the only reason it is still standing — the generation it was made with included Ham, and Noah is drunk and naked in his tent within a few verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who brings the clouds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whenever I bring clouds** over the earth.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not promise there will be no more clouds. He says He is the one bringing them, and that when they come, the bow shows up in them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign only appears when there is rain and sun at once. You do not get the rainbow on a clear day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To Me this is like the waters of Noah; for as I have sworn that the waters of Noah would no more cover the earth, so have I sworn that **I would not be angry with you, nor rebuke you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54, which follows Isaiah 53 — the servant *wounded for our transgressions*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God stakes the certainty of one promise on the certainty of the other, and invites the comparison Himself. See the note on Isaiah 54:9–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is the sign of the covenant I have established between me and **all life on the earth**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other place the bow turns up.** Ezekiel 1, and Revelation 4: *there was a rainbow around the throne, in appearance like an emerald.* The sign hung in the sky over a wet world is described as encircling the throne itself."}]}, "Habakkuk 1:1–4": {"title": "\"How long?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only book in the Bible that opens as a complaint to God and never stops being a conversation with Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **the burden Habakkuk the prophet carried, the vision he saw**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **massa** — a burden, a load, and the standard term for a prophetic oracle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The vision he saw** — **chazon**, the word Proverbs uses: *where there is no vision the people cast off restraint*. See the note on Proverbs 29:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book calls its own contents a vision, and the vision starts with a question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is speaking to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the nation. Most prophets deliver a message from God to Israel; Habakkuk opens by addressing God about Israel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How long, LORD, must I cry for help before You hear me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *how long shall I cry, and You will not hear*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How long must I call to You over the violence, and still You do not save?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *violence* is **chamas** — brutality, wrong done to a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why do You make me stare at wrong? Why do You let me watch such trouble?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *why do You show me iniquity and cause me to see trouble*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not asking why it exists. He is asking why he has to look at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Ruin and cruelty are in front of me**. Everywhere there is fighting, and **the quarreling never stops**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The legal collapse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The law goes limp. Justice never wins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *goes limp* is **pug** — to be numbed, chilled, ineffective."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The wicked crowd around the good and **hem them in**, so that when a verdict comes, **it comes out crooked**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The courts are running and the outcomes are wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is unusual about the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The complaint is preserved. Nobody edits it out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk is the only prophet who argues with God through a whole book and is answered twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look among the nations and watch — be utterly astounded! For I will work a work in your days which you would not believe, though it were told you. **For indeed I am raising up the Chaldeans**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer makes the problem worse. Judah's corruption will be dealt with by Babylon — a nation Habakkuk regards as far worse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are of purer eyes than to behold evil… **why do You look on those who deal treacherously, and hold Your tongue when the wicked devours a person more righteous than he?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes back with a harder question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will take my stand at my watchpost.* See the note on Habakkuk 2:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops arguing and waits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer that comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Write the vision… **though it tarries, wait for it; because it will surely come**.* See the note on Habakkuk 2:2–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle of it: *the just shall live by his faith* — the sentence Paul quotes twice and Hebrews once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though the fig tree may not blossom, nor fruit be on the vines; though the labour of the olive may fail, and the fields yield no food; though the flock be cut off from the fold, and there be no herd in the stalls — **yet I will rejoice in the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six failures listed, and then that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never gets the answer he asked for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the book preserves.** The complaint, unedited. Habakkuk asks how long twice, why twice, and accuses God of making him watch — and it is filed under *the vision he saw*."}]}, "Habakkuk 2:1": {"title": "\"I will take my stand\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is a decision about where to stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two rounds of complaint. *How long, LORD, must I cry for help before You hear me?* — and then an answer about Babylon that made it worse, and a second complaint harder than the first. See the note on Habakkuk 1:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this. He stops arguing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The post"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **take my stand at my watchpost**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mishmarti** — my watch, my guard-post. The place a sentry is assigned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **plant my feet on the wall** and keep watch.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I will station myself on the rampart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A watchman on a city wall in that world had one job: to see something coming before anybody else did, and to say so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah: *I have set watchmen on your walls, O Jerusalem, who shall never hold their peace day or night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel is appointed one: *son of man, I have made you a watchman for the house of Israel; therefore hear a word from My mouth, and give them warning from Me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is watching for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **look to see what He will say to me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to see what He will speak in me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look, to see what He will say. Habakkuk mixes the senses deliberately — the thing he is watching for is a word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **what I will answer when I am corrected**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what I shall answer when I am reproved*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He expects to be corrected. He has just accused God twice of watching and doing nothing, and he assumes an answer will include a rebuke."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He plans what to say when it comes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What actually happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD answered me. **Write the vision**.* See the note on Habakkuk 2:2–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no rebuke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the verse is about effort. He goes to a post, plants his feet, and waits for something to arrive from outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else stands and waits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah at Horeb, after wind, earthquake and fire, coming out to the cave mouth for a whisper. See the note on 1 Kings 19:9–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel, three weeks in mourning, with the answer already dispatched on day one. See the note on Daniel 10:12–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the watchman's answer turns out to be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, the proud, his soul is not upright in him; **but the just shall live by his faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses later, and it is the sentence Paul quotes in Romans and Galatians and Hebrews quotes in chapter 10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most consequential line in the Reformation comes out of a man standing on a wall waiting to be told off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end of the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the LORD is in His holy temple. Let all the earth keep silence before Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet who opened by demanding an answer closes the chapter by calling for silence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he does after two rounds of arguing.** Stops. Goes to a post, stands on a wall, and watches to see what will be said — and prepares an answer for the correction that never comes."}]}, "Habakkuk 2:12–14": {"title": "\"As the waters cover the sea\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third of five woes, and the sentence that ends it belongs to a different scale entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk opens with a complaint: *O LORD, how long shall I cry, and You will not hear?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God answers by saying He is raising up the Babylonians — which makes the prophet's problem worse, and he says so: *You are of purer eyes than to behold evil… why do You look on those who deal treacherously?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The five woes in chapter 2 are the answer, pronounced over the empire that was about to swallow Judah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The woe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sorrow to the one who **builds a town with blood** and **founds a city on wrongdoing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babylon was built by deported labour. The bricks of it are still stamped with Nebuchadnezzar's name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is it not from the LORD of hosts that **the peoples labor only to feed the fire**, that **the nations wear themselves out for nothing**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses about wasted work. The Hebrew is *the people shall labour for the very fire, and the nations shall weary themselves for nothing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything built with blood is described as fuel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the earth will be filled with the knowledge of the glory of the LORD, as the waters cover the sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **for** connects it. The reason the empire's work comes to nothing is what is going to fill the earth instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As the waters cover the sea.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not water on the sea. The sea is water — the covering is total and there is nothing else there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is filling it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The knowledge of **the glory** of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has nearly the same line: *the earth shall be full of **the knowledge of the LORD** as the waters cover the sea* — at the end of the passage about the wolf and the lamb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk adds *the glory*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the woes go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five in the chapter: to the plunderer, to the man who builds his house by unjust gain, to the city built on blood, to the man who makes his neighbour drunk, and to the idol-maker."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last verse of the chapter: *but **the LORD is in His holy temple. Let all the earth keep silence before Him**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse the chapter is famous for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold the proud, his soul is not upright in him; but **the just shall live by his faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 4, eight verses before this. Paul quotes it in Romans and Galatians, and Hebrews quotes it too — the sentence the Reformation was built on comes out of a book about Babylon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though the fig tree may not blossom, nor fruit be on the vines… **yet I will rejoice in the LORD**, I will joy in the God of my salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk lists six failed harvests and then says that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He got no answer to his original question. He got this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The scale of the sentence.** It sits inside a woe over one empire, and it is about the whole earth — and what fills it is not power or peace but the knowledge of the glory."}]}, "Habakkuk 2:2–3": {"title": "\"Write it plain\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The answer to a man standing on a wall, and it begins with an instruction about handwriting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the LORD answered me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had gone to his post expecting a correction. See the note on Habakkuk 2:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets an assignment instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Write the vision, He said. **Carve it clear on tablets**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *inscribe it upon tablets* — **luchot**, the same word as the tablets of stone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a scroll. Something durable and public."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **anyone who reads it can run to tell it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *that he may run who reads it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have divided over whether it means a man running past can read it, or a man who reads it runs to spread it. The Hebrew allows both, and both need the same thing: it has to be legible at speed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plainness is the point of the instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The waiting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the vision still waits for its appointed time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mo'ed** — an appointed time, a set season. The word used of the feasts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delay is scheduled rather than accidental."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **racing toward the end**, and **it will not lie**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it hastens to the end and will not lie*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things said in one clause: it is hurrying, and it will not deceive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction about the wait"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If it **seems slow, wait for it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *though it tarries, wait for it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Seems** is the operative word. The vision has a set time and it is hurrying; the slowness is in the watching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guarantee"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It will surely come. **It will not be late**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles it — *coming it will come, it will not delay*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, the proud, his soul is not upright in him; **but the just shall live by his faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **emunah** — faithfulness, steadfastness, reliability."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it in Romans and Galatians. Hebrews quotes it in chapter 10, and adds the next lines: *now the just shall live by faith; but if anyone draws back, My soul has no pleasure in him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three New Testament citations of one sentence, spoken to a man waiting for something overdue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verse is answering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk had asked *how long*. The reply does not give a date. It says there is one, that it is fixed, and that the thing is moving faster than it looks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **yet a little while, and He who is coming will come and will not tarry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted directly after *you have need of endurance, so that after you have done the will of God, you may receive the promise*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The context there is a congregation that had lost property and was under pressure to go back. See the note on Hebrews 10:23–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five woes that follow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of Habakkuk 2 is five woes on the empire the prophet was complaining about — and in the middle of them: *for the earth will be filled with the knowledge of the glory of the LORD, as the waters cover the sea.* See the note on Habakkuk 2:12–14."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he is told to do about the delay.** Write it down, large enough to read at a run — and then the reason: the appointment exists, the thing is hurrying, and what looks slow is not late."}]}, "Hebrews 1:1–3": {"title": "\"He sat down\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening sentence of the letter, and it ends on a posture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How God used to speak"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In piece after piece, and in one way after another, and through the prophets.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in Greek, both beginning with the same sound — in many portions and in many manners. Fragments, over centuries, by dreams and visions and voices and signs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about that is criticised. It is simply described as partial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How He speaks now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has spoken to us **in a Son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No article in the Greek — not *through the Son* as one more prophet in the line, but in the manner of a Son. The mode of communication has changed, not just the messenger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God's own glory, blazing out. He is exactly what God is, pressed into view.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second image is from a seal pressed into wax — the exact impression, every feature reproduced. The word is **charakter**, which is where our word comes from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He carries the whole of it along on one powerful word.* Not holding the universe up like a pillar. Carrying it forward, by speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had cleansed our sins away Himself, **He sat down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priests in the tabernacle did not sit. There was no chair in it — the work never finished, because the sacrifices never finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This priest sat down. The letter comes back to it again and again, and it is the whole argument in one verb: the sitting proves the work is over."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where He sat.** *At the right hand of Majesty, in the heights.* Not merely resting — the position of authority beside the throne. Finished, and seated in the place of rule."}]}, "Hebrews 10:1–2": {"title": "Only a shadow", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument turns on a question he expects the reader to answer for themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law **threw a shadow** of the good that was coming; **it never showed the thing itself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skia** (shadow) against **eikon** — the image, the actual form."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shadow is not a lie. It is cast by something real, has the shape of it, and proves it is there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It also cannot be used. Nobody sits in the shade of a shadow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law was a shadow of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The good that was coming.* Paul says the same in Colossians about food, drink, feasts and sabbaths: *these are a shadow of things to come, **but the substance is of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **year after year**, with **the same sacrifices offered without end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement, every year, the same ritual. And the daily offerings, morning and evening, for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it could not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It could **never bring the people who came to worship all the way to completion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teleiosai** — to bring to the end, to perfect, to finish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem named is not that the sacrifices were fraudulent. It is that they never arrived anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If it could, **would not the offerings have stopped?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts the test in the reader's hands. A treatment that works is discontinued."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple was still running when this letter was written, and the smoke went up every day, which was the visible proof of his point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Once the worshipers had been cleansed, **they would not have gone on carrying any sense of their sins**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **suneidesin hamartion** — a consciousness of sins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the writer says the arrangement failed to remove. Not the guilt in the abstract. The awareness of it in the worshipper."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sacrifices did instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *but in those sacrifices **there is a reminder of sins every year**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anamnesis** — the same word Jesus uses of the bread: *do this in remembrance of Me.* See the note on Luke 22:19–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One annual reminder of sins; one ongoing remembrance of Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blunt statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is **not possible that the blood of bulls and goats could take away sins**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer says it outright, about a system God Himself commanded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into Psalm 40 in the mouth of Christ — *sacrifices and offerings You did not want, but a body You prepared for Me.* See the notes on Hebrews 10:5–7 and Hebrews 10:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the priests who stand daily and the one who sat down. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Jeremiah: *their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more.* See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The test he offers.** If it worked, it would have stopped. He does not argue the point further — he asks the question and moves on to what did stop."}]}, "Hebrews 10:11–13": {"title": "The priest who sat down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument of the letter turns on a piece of furniture that was not there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The old arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every priest **stands** ministering daily and offering **repeatedly the same sacrifices**, which **can never take away sins**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words doing the work: stands, daily, repeatedly, never."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was missing from the tabernacle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were no chairs in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstand, the table of showbread, the altar of incense, the ark — the specifications in Exodus are exhaustive, down to the tongs and the snuffdishes, and no seat is mentioned anywhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A priest's work was never finished, so there was nothing to sit on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But this Man, after He had offered **one sacrifice for sins forever**, **sat down** at the right hand of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**One**, and **forever**, and then the posture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sitting is the proof. You sit when the job is done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He is doing now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that time waiting till **His enemies are made His footstool**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110, which Jesus had quoted at the Pharisees Himself: *the LORD said to my Lord, sit at My right hand until I make Your enemies Your footstool.* See the note on Matthew 22:41–46."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For by **one offering He has perfected forever** those who are being sanctified.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two tenses in one sentence, and both are true at once. **Perfected forever** — done, complete, permanent. **Being sanctified** — in progress."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The finished thing and the ongoing thing are not in competition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what the Spirit witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer quotes Jeremiah 31: *their sins and their lawless deeds **I will remember no more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then his own conclusion: *now where there is remission of these, **there is no longer an offering for sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the debt is forgotten, there is nothing left to pay."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, having boldness to enter the Holiest by the blood of Jesus… **let us draw near**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sitting down is what makes the drawing near possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison the letter keeps making"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing priests, repeated sacrifices, and a room nobody could enter except one man once a year. See the note on Hebrews 9:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against one offering, one seat, and *let us draw near* addressed to everybody."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the same posture turns up.** *And He was received up into heaven, and **sat down** at the right hand of God.* Mark's last chapter. And in Revelation, John sees Him standing among the lampstands — seated at the throne, walking among the churches."}]}, "Hebrews 10:14": {"title": "Perfected forever, being made holy", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and it holds two tenses that most people would not put together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **that one offering** He has **brought to completion, forever**, the people **He is making holy**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brought to completion, forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teteleioken eis to dienekes** — a perfect tense, and a phrase meaning *continuously, for all time*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense: done, and standing done. Nothing is pending."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Teleioo** is the word the writer uses throughout — the same root as *it is finished*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people **He is making holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tous hagiazomenous** — a present passive participle. Being sanctified, ongoing, in progress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Completed forever, and being made holy — in one sentence, about the same people, by the same offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer sets them side by side and does not reconcile them. One describes standing and one describes process, and both are attributed to the single offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's argument about the old arrangement: sacrifices *year after year* that *could never bring the people who came to worship all the way to completion*. See the note on Hebrews 10:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb, negated. What the repetition could not do, one offering did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"One offering\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says it four ways in a few verses:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By that will we have been sanctified through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ **once for all***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But this Man, after He had offered **one sacrifice for sins forever**, sat down at the right hand of God.* See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ephapax** — once, finally, never repeated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the Holy Spirit also witnesses to us; for after He had said before, \"**This is the covenant that I will make with them**…\"* — and then Jeremiah, and *their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more*. See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **where there is remission of these, there is no longer an offering for sin**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument closes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the writer goes from here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, having boldness to enter the Holiest… **let us draw near**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after verse 18 is invitation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for the people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people He is making holy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the same root as **hagios**, holy, saint. The New Testament's usual word for believers is the participle of this verb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are called the sanctified — a name for something being done to them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two tenses.** Finished and continuing. The writer puts a perfect and a present in one sentence and attaches both to a single offering — one is what they are, and the other is what is happening."}]}, "Hebrews 10:15–17": {"title": "\"I will remember no more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer quotes Jeremiah for the second time in the letter, and this time he quotes it in two halves with a joint in the middle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is called the speaker"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Holy Spirit** tells us the same thing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah wrote it. The writer of Hebrews attributes it to the Spirit, in the present tense — *tells us*, not *told them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"First He says\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is the covenant I will make with them once those days are over, says the Lord. **I will put My laws inside their hearts**, and I will **write them on their minds**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is God's. He puts, He writes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Compare the first time: *written by the very finger of God*, on stone, handed down a mountain. Same author, same hand, and the surface has changed. See the note on Exodus 31:18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Then He adds\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer separates the two clauses deliberately — *first He says… then He adds* — so the second one lands on its own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ou me mnestho** — a double negative with the subjunctive, the strongest form of denial available in the language. Not *I will try not to*, and not *I will overlook*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a promise not to bring something to mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Omniscience and remembering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is not a claim about God's memory failing. To *remember* in Hebrew usage is to act on — God *remembered Noah*, and the wind passed over the water; God *remembered* Hannah, and she conceived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not remembering is not acting on. The record is not consulted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer concludes from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **where there is remission of these, there is no longer an offering for sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his argument, and it is an argument about sacrifices, not about feelings. If the sins are not remembered, there is nothing left for another offering to address."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement came round every year precisely because there was a remembering — *in those sacrifices there is a reminder of sins every year.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The old covenant's wording"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 34, the LORD passing before Moses: *forgiving iniquity and transgression and sin, **by no means clearing the guilty**.* See the note on Exodus 34:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are God speaking about Himself, three books and one covenant apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, **having boldness to enter the Holiest** by the blood of Jesus… let us draw near with a true heart in full assurance of faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for boldness is **parrhesia** — freedom of speech, the right to say anything in a place you belong. See the note on Hebrews 10:19–22."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the quotation comes from.** Jeremiah 31, written while Jerusalem was falling to Babylon for exactly the sins in question. The promise not to remember was made in the middle of the consequences."}]}, "Hebrews 10:18": {"title": "\"No offering left to make\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and it is the conclusion of ten chapters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **where those have been forgiven**, there is **no offering for sin left to make**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *now where there is remission of these, there is no longer an offering for sin*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"those\" refers to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The quotation immediately before it: *\"and their sins and their lawless deeds **I will remember no more**.\"* See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer quotes Jeremiah and then draws one inference from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The logic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the sins are not being remembered, there is nothing for another offering to be about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An offering addresses something. Remove the something, and the offering has no subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer does not argue that further sacrifice is forbidden, ineffective, or disrespectful. He says there is nothing left for it to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Remission** — the Greek is **aphesis**, a sending away, a release. It is the word used of prisoners being let go and of debts being cancelled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also the word in the Greek Old Testament for the Year of Jubilee."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has established"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["The law was a shadow, and the repetition proved it never finished the job. See the note on Hebrews 10:1–2.", "The sacrifices reopened the account annually rather than closing it. See the note on Hebrews 10:3–4.", "A body was prepared instead. See the note on Hebrews 10:5–7.", "The first arrangement was cancelled to establish the second. See the note on Hebrews 10:8–9.", "Every priest stands daily; this one sat down. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13.", "The Spirit testifies: *I will remember no more.*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this verse, and the argument is closed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word **therefore**, and the whole tone of the letter changes:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, having **boldness to enter the Holiest** by the blood of Jesus, by a new and living way which He consecrated for us, through the veil… **let us draw near**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this verse is invitation. Everything before it is argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three \"let us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us draw near… let us hold fast the confession of our hope without wavering… and let us consider one another.* See the note on Hebrews 10:23–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, in a row, and all three rest on this sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it mattered to the readers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple was standing. The offerings were being made daily, a few days' walk away, and the people reading this letter were under pressure to go back to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's answer is not that the temple is unpleasant or that they will be disappointed. It is that there is nothing there to make an offering about."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shortest verse in the argument.** Ten chapters of priesthood, covenant, oath and sacrifice, and it comes down to one clause: if the sins are not remembered, nothing remains to be offered."}]}, "Hebrews 10:19–21": {"title": "\"A road that is alive\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The letter's argument turns here from explanation to invitation, and it opens with a place nobody was allowed to enter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the way leads"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The way into the holiest place is open to us now, and we can walk in without a flinch.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The holiest place is behind the curtain — one man, one day a year, with blood and smoke. See the note on Leviticus 16:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word behind *without a flinch* is **parrhesia**, boldness, the freedom of somebody with the right to speak in the room. The same word the letter used at 4:16 about the throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What makes it open"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because of the blood of Jesus.* Not because of improvement, and not because the holiness of the place has been reduced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A road that is alive\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He opened up a road for us that no one had ever walked before, a road that is alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims. It is **new** — the Greek word originally meant freshly slaughtered, and came to mean recent, unused. Nobody has been down it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is **living**. A road is not usually alive. The writer is stretching the image on purpose, because the way is a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Through the curtain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It runs straight through the curtain, which is His own body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records the temple curtain torn in two from top to bottom at the moment Jesus died. Hebrews puts the two together: the torn veil and the torn body are one event, and what they produce is a road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Matthew 27:51–53."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The priest at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we have a great Priest set over God's household.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things are now in place — an open way, a road that lives, and a priest already inside. The next verse begins *let us draw near*, and the rest of the chapter is what to do about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What stood in front of the curtain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The golden altar of incense, immediately outside the veil, with smoke going over the top of it into the room beyond."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest carried a censer through on the Day of Atonement precisely so the cloud would cover the mercy seat *lest he die*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The incense was what made the approach survivable. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the room now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we have **a great Priest set over God's household**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not waiting outside once a year. Inside, permanently, and the way to Him is described as open."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why \"flinch\" is the right word.** Everything in the old arrangement taught people to hesitate: the curtain, the once-a-year, the incense so the priest would not die. The letter is not asking readers to feel casual. It is telling them the reason for the hesitation has been dealt with."}]}, "Hebrews 10:23–25": {"title": "Hold on to each other", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three instructions in a row, and the reason attached to the first one is not about the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word **therefore** in verse 19, resting on the whole argument that ends *there is no offering for sin left to make*. See the note on Hebrews 10:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three *let us* clauses follow: draw near, hold fast, and consider one another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Holding on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **not let go of what we openly believe**, and let us **not wobble**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *let us hold fast the confession of our hope **without wavering*** — **aklines**, unbending, not leaning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who made the promise **keeps His word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pistos** — faithful, reliable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The steadiness asked for is not generated by the person holding on. The reason given is about the character of the one who promised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has already spent a chapter on it: an oath, sworn on Himself, because there was nobody greater. See the notes on Hebrews 6:13–14 and Hebrews 6:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **give some thought to one another**, and **look for ways to spark love and good work** in each other.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *give thought* is **katanoeo** — to observe closely, to study."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *spark* is **paroxusmos** — a sharp stirring up, a provoking. Our word *paroxysm* comes from it. In Acts it describes the sharp disagreement between Paul and Barnabas over Mark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer uses a word for irritation and points it at love and good work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it requires"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Attention. You cannot provoke somebody toward anything without knowing what moves them, which is why the observing comes first in the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **drop out of gathering together**, the way **some have gotten into the habit** of doing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **episunagoge** — a gathering together, from the same root as synagogue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Some have gotten into the habit** — the writer names it as an existing pattern, not a hypothetical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were dropping out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter says elsewhere: *you had compassion on me in my chains, and joyfully accepted the plundering of your goods.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Meeting had cost them property and put them in reach of the authorities."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What to do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep **encouraging each other**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parakalountes** — from the same root as **parakletos**, the word for the Helper. See the note on John 14:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are asked to do for each other what the Spirit is called."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The frequency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the more **as you see the Day coming closer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pressure that made them stop is the reason given for doing it more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning about sinning wilfully after receiving the knowledge of the truth. See the notes on Hebrews 10:26–27 and Hebrews 10:28–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts *do not stop meeting* directly before it. The drifting he is warning against starts with people quietly not turning up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the three.** Draw near to God, hold on to the confession, and pay attention to each other — and the only reason supplied for any of them is that the One who promised keeps His word."}]}, "Hebrews 10:26–27": {"title": "No sacrifice left", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second great warning of the letter, and it uses the same argument as the good news eight verses earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being written to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew believers under pressure to return to the temple. The whole letter is written against that one move, and the temple was still standing when it was sent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we **choose to keep on sinning after we have received the truth and come to know it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *if we sin **wilfully*** — **hekousios**, deliberately, of one's own choice. It is a rare word, and it appears here and once in Peter, of shepherding willingly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After we have received the knowledge of the truth* — **epignosis**, full knowledge. Not ignorance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No **sacrifice for sins is left**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *there no longer remains a sacrifice for sins*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identical clause, eight verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And where those have been forgiven, **there is no offering for sin left to make**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same statement, in nearly the same words, and there it is the conclusion of the good news."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has argued that there is one sacrifice, offered once, and no other. That is a comfort to a man resting on it and a terror to a man walking away from it — and the fact is the same fact either way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is described instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only **a terror of waiting for judgment**, and **a fire, burning with His anger, that will swallow up everyone who sets himself against God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *a certain fearful expectation of judgment, and fiery indignation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase *swallow up the adversaries* comes from Isaiah 26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sin in view is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse defines it, and it is not an ordinary lapse: *walked all over the Son of God*, *treated the blood of the covenant as something common*, *insulted the Spirit of grace*. See the note on Hebrews 10:28–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three descriptions, and all three are about repudiating the sacrifice rather than falling short of a standard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not drop out of gathering together, the way some have gotten into the habit of doing.* See the note on Hebrews 10:23–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer sets the warning right after a description of people quietly ceasing to turn up. He is tracing where a road goes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **recall the former days** in which, after you were illuminated, you endured a great struggle with sufferings… **you had compassion on me in my chains**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **do not cast away your confidence**, which has great reward.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *\"**But we are not of those who draw back to perdition, but of those who believe** to the saving of the soul.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer includes himself and them in the second category, five verses after the warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does this every time. Chapter 6's warning is followed immediately by *but beloved, we are convinced of better things concerning you.* See the note on Hebrews 6:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Warning, then reassurance, in that order, twice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sentence that does both jobs.** *There is no sacrifice left.* The letter says it twice — once as the reason nothing more is needed, and once as the reason there is nowhere else to go."}]}, "Hebrews 10:28–29": {"title": "Three things done to the blood", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer defines the sin he has just warned about, and the definition is entirely about how the sacrifice is treated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who **threw out the law of Moses died with no mercy shown him**, on the word of **two or three witnesses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy's standard for a capital case. No conviction on one voice — the same rule that governed the cities of refuge. See the note on Numbers 35:16–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument's shape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How much **heavier a punishment** do you think a man will deserve.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer uses this form repeatedly — the lesser to the greater. If that was true under the old arrangement, how much more under this one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is about the Son and His blood:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Walked all over the Son of God.** The Greek is **katapateo** — to trample underfoot. It is the word used of salt that has lost its savour, and of pearls before swine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Treated the blood of the covenant as something common.** The Greek is **koinon** — common, ordinary, unclean. It is the word Peter uses on the rooftop: *I have never eaten anything common or unclean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To call the blood common is to rank it with the blood of bulls and goats, which the writer has just said could never take sins away. See the note on Hebrews 10:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Insulted the Spirit of grace.** The Greek is **enubrizo** — to treat with contempt, to outrage. It appears once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The blood **that made him holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by which he was sanctified*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer says it about the person being warned. Whatever else the sentence does, it does not describe someone the blood never touched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not on the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Failure, weakness, relapse, or a particular sin. Nothing in the three descriptions is about behaviour falling short."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three are about the standing of the sacrifice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is arguing against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Going back to the temple. The offerings were still being made when this was written, a few days' walk away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man returning to that altar is saying the one on Golgotha was not sufficient — which is what *treating the blood as common* amounts to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For we know Him who said, \"**Vengeance is Mine, I will repay**,\" says the Lord. And again, \"The LORD will judge His people.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **recall the former days** in which, after you were illuminated, you endured a great struggle with sufferings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer turns immediately to what they had already come through, and ends the chapter:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **we are not of those who draw back** to perdition, **but of those who believe** to the saving of the soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts himself and them in the second category."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in the letter: a hard warning, followed at once by a statement that he does not think it describes them. See the notes on Hebrews 6:4–6 and Hebrews 6:9–10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the three verbs have in common.** Trampling, calling common, insulting — every one is something done to the sacrifice rather than something done wrong by the person."}]}, "Hebrews 10:3–4": {"title": "A reminder of sins", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two short verses, and they say what the sacrifices actually accomplished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead, those sacrifices **drag the sins back into memory, year after year**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anamnesis** — a calling to mind, a remembrance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word Jesus uses of the bread: *do this **in remembrance** of Me.* See the note on Luke 22:19–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two remembrances, of two different things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the annual repetition mattered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument in the verses before is that a treatment which worked would have stopped. See the note on Hebrews 10:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement came round every year, which means the account was reopened every year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because there is **no way that the blood of bulls and goats can take sins away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **adunaton** — impossible. The same word the writer uses of God lying, of pleasing Him without faith, and of renewing an apostate. See the note on Hebrews 6:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bluntness of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it about a system God commanded, in Leviticus, in detail, with the species and the handling of the blood specified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not accusing the law of fraud. He is saying what it was for: *the law threw a shadow of the good that was coming.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the animals could not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Take away** — the Greek is **aphairein**, to lift off and remove."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They covered. The Hebrew **kaphar** is to cover over, and that is what the mercy seat did — the lid over the tablets, with blood on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Covered is not the same as gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John the Baptist said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Behold! **The Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek there is **airo** — to lift up and carry off. The same root the writer says the bulls and goats could not do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Straight into Psalm 40 in the mouth of Christ — *sacrifices and offerings You did not want, but a body You prepared for Me.* See the notes on Hebrews 10:5–7 and Hebrews 10:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the priest who sat down, and Jeremiah's *their sins I will remember no more*. See the notes on Hebrews 10:11–13 and Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two rememberings, side by side"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old arrangement produced an annual reminder of sins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The new covenant's terms are *I will remember no more*, and the meal instituted under it is a remembrance of a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter's argument ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And where those have been forgiven, **there is no offering for sin left to make**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sacrifices were for.** Not to clear the account. The writer says they reopened it, once a year, and that the impossibility was in the material."}]}, "Hebrews 10:5–7": {"title": "\"You shaped a body for Me instead\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts a psalm into Christ's mouth and dates it: *as He came into the world*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 40, written by David, quoted here as words spoken at the incarnation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sacrifices and offerings **were not what You wanted** — **You shaped a body for Me instead**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four categories"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sacrifices, offerings, whole animals burned, offerings made for sin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four of the five Levitical offerings, named one after another and all dismissed in the same breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one was commanded by God in Leviticus. The writer is not saying they were wrong. He is saying they were never the thing wanted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"None of it gave You any pleasure\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *You did not delight in them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophets had said the same for centuries — *I desire mercy and not sacrifice*, *to obey is better than sacrifice* — while the altar kept burning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The body"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of Psalm 40 reads *my ears You have opened* — literally *dug*. The Greek Old Testament the writer is quoting has *a body You have prepared for me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ear is the part of the body that receives the command; the writer's version names the whole."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in Exodus, the servant who refuses to go free has his ear pierced at the doorpost and serves forever. See the note on Exodus 21:2–6, and on Psalms 40:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Here I am\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So I said, **Here I am**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer Abraham gives, and Moses at the bush, and Samuel in the night, and Isaiah in the temple. It is the standard reply of a person reporting for a task."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It stands written about Me in the scroll\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in the volume of the book it is written of Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said before the ministry begins, about a book already finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have come to do what You want, God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That sentence is the entire point of the quotation. The writer says so in the next verse: *He takes away the first that He may establish the second.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is the sacrifices. The second is the will."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it buys"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By that will **we have been sanctified** through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ **once for all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ephapax** — once, finally, not to be repeated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the priests who stand daily, and the One who sat down. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is exchanged.** Animals were brought, killed and burned, year after year, and the writer says none of it was ever wanted. What was wanted was a body and somebody willing to say *here I am* in it."}]}, "Hebrews 10:8–9": {"title": "\"He cancels the first to put the second in its place\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer takes the psalm he has just quoted and separates it into two halves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*First He says… **then** He says.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has quoted Psalm 40 whole in the verses before. See the note on Hebrews 10:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now he goes back over it and splits it, because the order of the two statements is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sacrifices and offerings, whole animals burned, offerings made for sin — **You did not want them, and they gave You no pleasure**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four categories, listed twice in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause he adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **those are the very things the law requires**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer inserts this himself. It is not in the psalm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything just dismissed was commanded in Leviticus, by God, in detail — the species, the age, the handling of the blood, the portions burned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He wants the reader to notice that the psalm is dismissing what the law demands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He says, \"**Here I am. I have come to do what You want**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the standard reply of a person reporting for duty — the answer Abraham, Moses, Samuel and Isaiah all give."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **cancels the first arrangement** in order to **put the second one in its place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anairei** — takes away, removes, abolishes. It is also the word for putting a person to death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **histesin** — sets up, causes to stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and the second is the purpose of the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being replaced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sacrifice by nothing. Sacrifice by a will."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first arrangement was a system of offerings. The second is a person saying *I have come to do what You want*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it has to be one or the other"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer does not allow both to run. The first is removed **in order that** the second may stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek makes the purpose explicit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it accomplishes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *by that will **we have been sanctified** through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ **once for all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**By that will** — not by the blood alone, in the writer's phrasing. By the willingness that carried it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Once for all** — **ephapax**, once, finally, never repeated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking in the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote it. The writer of Hebrews puts it in Christ's mouth *as He came into the world*, and reads the whole thing as spoken at the incarnation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does this repeatedly in the letter — Psalm 2, Psalm 45, Psalm 110, Psalm 40."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the readers were being pulled back toward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple, which was still standing and still functioning. The whole letter is written to people considering going back to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument here is that the first arrangement was removed by the second, and not by neglect."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two halves are.** A rejection of offerings and a statement of willingness, in the same psalm, in that order. The writer says the second one is why the first one ends."}]}, "Hebrews 11:1–3": {"title": "The shape of faith", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The nearest thing to a definition of faith in Scripture, and it is built out of two legal words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What we are **hoping for**, faith **already has hold of**; what **no one has ever seen**, faith is **sure of**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *already has hold of* is **hupostasis** — literally *that which stands under*. In the papyri of the period it is a title deed, the document establishing ownership of property you may never have visited."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *sure of* is **elegchos** — the word for evidence, proof, the thing that convinces a court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is described as the deed and the evidence. Not a mood, and not optimism."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is attached to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two objects, and neither is available to the senses: what is hoped for, and what has never been seen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is only ever exercised toward something not yet in hand. Once you have it, the faith has finished its work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a leap in the dark, on this definition. A deed and a proof are both about certainty; they simply concern something not yet visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is what **the people of long ago were praised for**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the chapter is that list — Abel, Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Sarah, Isaac, Jacob, Joseph, Moses, Rahab, Gideon, David, and the ones at the end who were sawn in two and wandered in sheepskins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of them are commended for achievements. They are commended for believing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the chapter says of all of them: *these all died in faith, **not having received the promises**, but having seen them afar off.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example given first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why we understand that **everything there is came into being at God's word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of everything the writer could have opened with, he takes creation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody watched it. There were no witnesses to Genesis 1, and no experiment can be run. It is held by faith, and the writer treats that as the model case."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **what we can see did not come from anything that can be seen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The visible came out of the invisible — which is the same relationship this chapter says faith has to its object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Without faith it is impossible to please Him, for he who comes to God must believe that He is, and that He is a rewarder of those who diligently seek Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things to believe: that He exists, and what He is like toward those who look for Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the roll of faith ends.** *And all these, having obtained a good testimony through faith, **did not receive the promise**, God having provided something better for us, that they should not be made perfect apart from us.* The list waits for the reader."}]}, "Hebrews 11:17–22": {"title": "The test, and three deathbeds", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four generations, and three of the four entries are about dying men still talking about the future."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Abraham"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Abraham, **when he was put to the test, offered Isaac up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is perfect: he **has offered**. As far as the writer is concerned it was done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contradiction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man who **had been handed the promises** was offering up **his only son**, the son about whom he had been told, **It is through Isaac that your line will be named**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer states the problem baldly. The promise and the command are about the same boy, and they cannot both stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Only son** — the Greek is **monogenes**. Ishmael existed. Isaac is the only son of the promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham concluded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had **worked it out that God could raise the dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logizomai** — to reckon, to calculate. It is the same accounting word James uses. See the note on James 1:2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham did arithmetic. If the promise runs through Isaac and Isaac is to die, then there is one remaining possibility, and he settled on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said as much to the servants at the foot of the hill: *stay here with the donkey; the lad and I will go yonder and worship, **and we will come back to you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No resurrection had ever happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In a manner of speaking\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in a manner of speaking, **that is how he got him back**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in a parable* — **en parabole**. Isaac did not die, but the writer says Abraham received him back as from the dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days' journey with the boy as good as dead in his mind, and then the voice from heaven. See the note on Genesis 22:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Isaac"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Isaac blessed Jacob and Esau **with an eye on what was still to come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one blessing recorded of Isaac is the one he was tricked into. He gave it to the wrong son in the dark, and when he found out he *trembled exceedingly* — and then said *I have blessed him, and indeed he shall be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews calls that faith, and lists it without mentioning the deception."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jacob"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Jacob, **dying**, blessed **each of Joseph's sons**, and **worshiped bent over the head of his staff**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He crossed his hands deliberately and put the right hand on the younger — and when Joseph tried to correct him, refused."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bent over his staff** — the writer takes the detail from the Greek Old Testament. An old man in Egypt, blind, propped on a stick, disposing of land he did not own in a country he would not be buried in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Joseph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Joseph, at the end of his life, **spoke of Israel going out**, and **left instructions about his bones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Second in command of Egypt, who had been in the country for eighty years, whose sons were Egyptian-born."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His last recorded words are that they are leaving, and *you shall carry up my bones from here*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The coffin sat in Egypt for four hundred years, and Moses took it out on the night of the exodus. Joshua buried it at Shechem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the four have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is credited with an achievement. The list is: a man who nearly killed his son, a man who was deceived, a man who could not see, and a man giving instructions about a funeral."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All four are commended for what they said about a future they would not be in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the writer puts the emphasis.** Every entry contains a verb of speech or blessing. These are men handing something forward — and Hebrews calls the handing forward the faith."}]}, "Hebrews 11:23–29": {"title": "Moses, and the accounting he did", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six sentences about Moses, each beginning the same way — *faith is why* — and the middle one is a calculation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the parents did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Moses, newly born, was hidden by his parents for three months: they could see he was a beautiful child, and the king's order did not frighten them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter begins its Moses section with somebody else's faith. Two slaves defy an infanticide order and are credited with it before Moses does anything at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weighing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He weighed the disgrace of Christ against everything stored up in Egypt and found the disgrace worth more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is a commercial one — reckoning, estimating value. This is not described as a sacrifice made against his own judgement. It is a man doing the arithmetic and concluding that one column was larger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the thing he weighed against a kingdom's treasury is called *the disgrace of Christ*, written of a man who lived over a thousand years before the crucifixion. The writer is claiming the same reproach runs through both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He could see the One nobody can see\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line is a deliberate contradiction, and it explains how the arithmetic came out that way. Moses walked away from Egypt *without a thought for how furious the king would be* because the invisible was, to him, the more solid of the two."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The list ends in water.** *Faith is why they walked through the Red Sea as though it were dry ground — and when the Egyptians tried the same thing, the water swallowed them.* The same act, performed by two parties, with opposite outcomes. The writer's point across the whole chapter is that the difference was never in the walking."}]}, "Hebrews 11:30–31": {"title": "Rahab in the roll of honour", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two sentences, side by side, and the pairing is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The company she is in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is a list: Abel, Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Sarah, Isaac, Jacob, Joseph, Moses. Patriarchs and prophets. Then the walls of Jericho — and then Rahab."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is one of only two women named in the chapter, and the writer does not soften her description. *Rahab the prostitute.* He could have written \"Rahab of Jericho.\" He did not, and neither did James."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What her faith is said to consist of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since she had welcomed the spies in peace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a confession, not a sacrifice, not a long obedience. She let two men in and did not turn them over. The writer treats a single act of hospitality toward God's people as the visible form of faith in her case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Did not die with those who refused to believe.* The Greek word is about disobedience through unbelief — refusing to be persuaded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone in Jericho had the same evidence, as she says herself on the roof. The line the chapter draws is not between the informed and the ignorant. It is between those who were persuaded and those who were not."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**James, on the same woman.** *Was she not shown to be right by that?* James uses Rahab as his second example of faith proved by action — the first being Abraham. Between the two writers she is cited as an example of faith and as an example of works, and it is the same episode both times. The two arguments are not in conflict; see the note on James 2:17."}]}, "Hebrews 11:32–35": {"title": "The sentence that turns halfway through", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the passage people quote for triumph, and it is the passage that makes the chapter honest — because of where it stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brought down kingdoms, dealt out justice, got hold of promises, shut lions' mouths, put fires out, got clear of the sword, were made strong where they had been weak, turned fierce in battle, sent foreign armies running. Women got their dead back alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item is a deliverance. Faith that worked, visibly, in this life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And others were tortured and would not take the way out, so that they could rise to something better.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word — *others* — and the same faith produces the opposite outcome. The writer does not change vocabulary, does not explain, does not suggest the second group had less of it. He puts both under the same heading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read on past this block and it gets starker: mocked, flogged, chained, stoned, sawn in two, destitute, wandering in deserts. And then the verdict on all of them: *the world was not worthy of them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any teaching that treats deliverance as the proof of faith has to account for the back half of this sentence, where people with the same faith were not delivered and the writer says so approvingly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Would not take the way out* is the detail that settles it. They were offered release and refused it — which means their situation was not a failure of faith but an exercise of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The end of the chapter.** *None of them received what was promised.* All of them — the delivered and the destroyed alike — are described as still waiting. Whatever this chapter is doing, it is not sorting people by outcome."}]}, "Hebrews 11:4–7": {"title": "Abel, Enoch, Noah", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three men before the flood, and the chapter's refrain starts here: *faith is why*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Abel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Abel brought God **a better offering** than Cain did.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pleiona** — a fuller, greater sacrifice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records the difference without explaining it: Cain brought of the fruit of the ground, Abel brought of the firstborn of his flock and of their fat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the offering did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **that offering is what marked him out as righteous**, since **God Himself vouched for what he gave**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The righteousness follows the offering, and the testimony is God's rather than the neighbours'."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis says *the LORD respected Abel and his offering* — the man and the gift, in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He has not stopped speaking\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is **dead, and he has not stopped speaking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first man to die, and the writer puts him in the present tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis has God saying *the voice of your brother's blood cries out to Me from the ground.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer picks it up again in chapter 12 — the blood of sprinkling *that speaks better things than that of Abel*. Abel's blood asks for justice; the other asks for something else. See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Enoch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Enoch was **taken away without ever meeting death**; **nobody could find him**, because God had taken him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis gives him six verses. *And Enoch walked with God, and he was not, for God took him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is one of two men in Scripture who did not die. The other is Elijah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said of him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before he was taken, this had been said of him: **God was glad of him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *he pleased God*. That is the whole of the character reference."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule drawn out of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **nobody makes God glad without faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer stops the narrative to state the principle, and then defines what the faith consists of:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Anyone coming to God has to believe **He is really there**.*", "*And that **He pays back the ones who go after Him in earnest**.*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two items. The second is a statement about what God is like — that seeking Him is not wasted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *pays back* is **misthapodotes**, a rewarder, one who gives wages."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Noah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Noah, **warned by God about things no one could see coming**, was afraid and **built an ark so his household would live**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and twenty years of carpentry on dry ground, on the strength of a warning about weather that had no precedent — Genesis says the earth was watered by a mist."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Was afraid** is the Greek for reverent fear, and the writer names it as part of the faith rather than the opposite of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ark did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That ark was **his verdict on the world**, and what he inherited from it was **the righteousness faith brings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The building of it condemned everything around it, without a word being recorded from Noah in the whole of Genesis."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is called *a preacher of righteousness* in 2 Peter, and no sermon of his survives. The boat was the sermon."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What all three have in common.** Not one of them is credited with a result they lived to see. Abel was killed, Enoch vanished, and Noah's proof arrived a century after he started building."}]}, "Hebrews 11:8–12": {"title": "\"He went out without knowing where he was going\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three times in five verses the writer says *faith is why*, and each one is attached to something that looks like a disadvantage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faith is why he went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Abraham obeyed when he was called… **he went out without knowing where he was going**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 12 records the instruction: *get out of your country… to a land that I will show you.* Not a land I have shown you. Will show."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had a direction and no destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faith is why he camped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why he **lived as a foreigner in the very land he had been promised**, camping in tents.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the strange one. The land was his by promise, and he spent his life in it as a resident alien in a tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only piece of it he ever owned outright was a burial cave he paid four hundred shekels for, to bury Sarah in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With Isaac and Jacob, who were promised the same thing he was* — three generations in tents, in a country deeded to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was actually waiting for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **waiting for the city with real foundations, the one God designed and built**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is between a tent and a city, and between pegs and foundations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's explanation is that Abraham was not disappointed by the tent because he was not aiming at the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faith is why Sarah conceived"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why **Sarah herself was given the strength to conceive** and bore a child when her body was long past it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that she received power for the laying down of seed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason given: *because she **counted the One who promised as good for it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is a reckoning word — she assessed His credit and judged it sound. It is the same kind of accounting language Paul uses of Abraham."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Sarah had done at the tent door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She laughed. *Therefore Sarah laughed within herself, saying, \"After I have grown old, shall I have pleasure, my lord being old also?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then denied it: *I did not laugh* — *for she was afraid.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament puts her in the roll of faith anyway, and says nothing about the laughing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So from **one man, and one whose body was finished**, came descendants past counting — as many as the **stars** overhead, as many as the **grains of sand** along the shore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two images God used with Abraham himself — the stars in Genesis 15, the sand in Genesis 22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One dead body at the start of the sentence and an uncountable multitude at the end of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter says a few verses later.** *These all died in faith, **not having received the promises**, but having seen them afar off, and were assured of them, and embraced them.* Every one of them died still waiting, and the chapter calls it faith rather than failure."}]}, "Hebrews 12:1–4": {"title": "\"Do not take your eyes off Jesus\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The whole of chapter 11 was a list of names. Chapter 12 begins with *therefore*, and turns the list into a stadium."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look at the size of the crowd around us, **every one of them a witness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *so great a cloud of witnesses*. Abel, Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Sarah, Moses, Rahab, Gideon, Samson, David — and the ones at the end of the chapter who were sawn in two and wandered in sheepskins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **martus** is one who testifies. They are not spectators in a gallery; they are the ones whose evidence has already been given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things to drop"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strip off **whatever weighs us down**, and **the sin that keeps winding itself around our legs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two separate items. The first is a weight — not necessarily sinful, just heavy. The second is the Greek **euperistatos**, a word found nowhere else, built from *well* and *standing around*: something that surrounds you closely, like a long robe tangling your stride."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The track"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Run without quitting, all the way down **the track that has been staked out in front of us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The course is laid out. Nobody in the passage is choosing a route."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not take your eyes off Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **aphorao** — it means to look away *from* one thing in order to look *at* another. Both halves are in the single word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You cannot simply add Him to what you are already staring at. Something has to be looked away from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is where our faith **starts** and He is the One who brings it to the **finish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Archegos** — the originator, the pioneer, the one who goes first. And **teleiotes** — the completer, the one who finishes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both ends of it belong to Him. It is His faith from the starting line to the tape, and the instruction is simply to keep looking at the One holding both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How He ran"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A joy was waiting for Him, and to get to it **He went through the cross and counted the shame as nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joy was in front of Him, not behind. He was looking at something too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And now He has **taken His seat** at the right hand of God's throne.* Every other priest stood. He sat down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In your fight with sin **you have not yet had to bleed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written to people under real pressure who thought they had reached their limit. He puts their situation beside His and leaves it there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter goes next.** Straight into the passage about being brought up as sons — *paideia*, upbringing. Fix your eyes on Him, and then: you are being raised, not managed. See the note on Hebrews 12:5–11."}]}, "Hebrews 12:12–17": {"title": "The root of bitterness, and what it actually grows out of", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This passage gets quoted in two directions at once. One crowd reads *chase holiness; without it nobody is going to see the Lord* and hears a threshold to clear. Another reads *nothing bitter takes root* and hears a warning about holding grudges. Both are picking up something real, and both are reading past the sentence that ties them together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read the order of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning is one sentence with two halves, and the halves are joined:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Watch that nobody misses out on God's grace**", "**and that nothing bitter takes root**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *misses out* is a word for falling short of something, coming up behind it, being left out — the same word Paul uses in *all have sinned and fall short*. And the bitterness grows out of that. Not out of what somebody did to you. Out of somebody missing the grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole diagnosis. Bitterness is what grows in a person who is trying to get by on something other than grace. It is the fruit, not the root."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The root is a quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is not the writer's own image. He is reaching back to Deuteronomy, where Moses warns against a root among the people producing poison — someone who hears the covenant and decides privately he will be fine on his own terms. That is the person in the picture. Not a wounded person. A self-sufficient one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the next line lands where it does: *nobody sexually immoral or godless like Esau*. Esau is not introduced as an example of lust. He is introduced as a man who traded what was already his for something immediate, because he did not value it. Missing the grace comes first. The visible failure comes after."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other half: what \"holiness\" is doing here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Chase peace with everybody, and chase holiness; without it nobody is going to see the Lord.* Read alone, that reads like a bar to clear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But look where it sits. Seven lines earlier, the same writer says God disciplines us *so that we come to share His holiness* — His, given, shared. The word is the same one. Holiness here is not a standard the reader manufactures and presents; it is something being handed to them, that they are told to go after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image in the surrounding verses is medical, not judicial: hands gone limp, knees giving way, a limb about to be thrown out of joint — *healed instead*. The writer is treating an injury, not setting an entrance exam."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it looks like in practice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage never says \"watch that nobody sins.\" It says watch that nobody **misses the grace**, because that is where the trouble starts and everything else follows. It puts the attention one step upstream of where we usually put it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On Esau and \"no room for repentance.\"** This line frightens people badly. In the Greek the sentence is about *Isaac's* decision, not God's: Esau found no way to reverse the blessing his father had already spoken over Jacob, though he begged for it with tears. It is a story about a transaction that could not be undone, not a statement that a person can want God and be turned away."}]}, "Hebrews 12:18–24": {"title": "Two mountains, and which one you are standing on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is one of the most carefully built passages in the New Testament, and its whole argument is carried by two words that are easy to read straight past: **what you have come to**. He says it twice, and the second time reverses the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What you have come to is not a mountain a hand can touch* — fire, gloom, dark, howling storm, a trumpet, a voice. This is Sinai, where the law was given, and the writer describes it entirely in terms of what it did to the people standing at the bottom of it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["they begged for the voice to stop", "they could not bear the command that was given", "even Moses said he was terrified and shaking"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the description is about the law being wrong. Everything in it is about what happens to people who come to God that way. It is unbearable — and the one man in the story who had God's own attention is the one who says he is shaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule at the foot of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If even an animal so much as touches the mountain, **it is to be stoned**, or run through with a spear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus records the boundary: a line drawn round the base, and a death penalty for crossing it — applying to livestock, which cannot read a warning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the detail the writer picks to show what the approach was like. Not the fire or the trumpet. A cow that wandered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The angels"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Angels in numbers no one can count.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **muriasin** — myriads, tens of thousands, and the word behind *myriad*. It is the largest number the language had, used as a plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Sinai they were there too — Deuteronomy says *the LORD came from Sinai… He came with ten thousands of saints* — but the mountain the readers were kept off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here they are listed among the things the reader has already come to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What you have come to is Mount Zion* — and then a list that does not stop:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the city of the living God, the Jerusalem above", "angels in numbers nobody can count", "the great gathering, every name on record in heaven", "God the Judge of all", "righteous people brought to completion", "Jesus, standing between God and us", "blood that says something better than Abel's"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice the tense. Not *will come to*. **Have come to.** The writer is not describing a destination for later; he is telling people where they are already standing while they read."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail everyone misses: Abel's blood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage ends by comparing two kinds of blood, and it expects you to know the story. Abel's blood cried out from the ground — it called for justice against the man who spilled it. That is what innocent blood does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So when the writer says this blood *says something better*, he is not saying it speaks more loudly. He is saying it speaks in the opposite direction. Abel's blood asked for the guilty to answer. This blood answers for the guilty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole argument of the letter in one image, and it is placed at the end of the mountain comparison on purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two details from Genesis sharpen it. God says to Cain *your brother's blood is crying out to me from the ground* — the verb is the ordinary word for a shout for help, and the noun is plural, *bloods*, which readers have long taken to mean not one life but every life that would have come from it. Spilled blood is pictured as a voice that will not go quiet until something answers it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then notice what God actually does with Cain. He does not execute him. When Cain says the sentence is more than he can bear, God puts a mark on him so that nobody will touch him. Even in the passage that gives us blood crying for justice, the first murderer walks away marked for protection. See the note on Genesis 4:8–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters where you are standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two mountains are not two eras that a reader chooses between as a matter of taste. They are two ways of approaching God, and the passage says plainly that one of them terrified everybody who tried it, including Moses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A person can be at Zion by fact and still be living at Sinai by habit — still approaching on the basis of how the week went. The passage is written to move them, and it does it not by pressure but by telling them where they already are."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the fire at the end.** The next lines say our God is a fire that burns things up, and it is often read as a threat bolted onto the comfort. Look at what the fire is doing in context: it takes away *everything that can be shaken* so that *what cannot be shaken is left standing*. It is not aimed at the person. It is aimed at what will not hold them."}]}, "Hebrews 12:25–29": {"title": "What the shaking is for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Read on its own, this sounds like the letter ending on a threat: do not turn Him down, everything is going to be shaken, our God is a fire that burns things up. Read as the finish of the argument that has been building since the two mountains, it is doing almost the opposite."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose clause is the whole thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence about shaking has a *so that* in it, and everything depends on where it points:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "the taking away of everything that can be shaken … **so that what cannot be shaken is left standing**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shaking is not the goal. It is the method. The goal is what is still there afterwards. And the writer tells you immediately what that is: *the kingdom we are being given can never be shaken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek makes it plainer than English does. The things that go are described with an ordinary participle — things being shaken. What remains is *asaleutos*, a single word meaning unshakable, immovable. It is not that the kingdom survives the shaking. It is that it is not the kind of thing shaking touches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Being given, not built"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The kingdom we are being given.* Not earned, not held onto by strength, not kept by performance. Receiving is the only verb in the sentence, and it is passive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That matters for how the next line reads. *Let us hold on to grace, and by it worship God.* A person who thinks the shaking is aimed at them will hold on to their own record. The writer says hold on to grace — and then says it is *by* that grace that acceptable worship happens at all."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our God is a fire that burns things up.* This is the last line of the chapter and it is often read as the sting in the tail."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But it is a quotation from Deuteronomy, and it is the same image the passage has just spent four verses explaining. Fire is what removes what will not last. Set next to the sentence before it — a kingdom that cannot be shaken — the fire is not aimed at the person holding the kingdom. It is aimed at everything that could not have held them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this is for, practically"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone is standing on a mix of things: what God has said, and a quieter set of supports we would rather not test — how the year went, how disciplined we have been lately, what other people think. This passage says a day comes when everything of that second kind goes, and the point of saying so is not to frighten anybody. It is so that a reader knows now which of the things they are standing on will still be there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"how much less will we get away with turning our backs.\"** The warning is real and should not be softened. But notice its shape: it is about *refusing to hear the One who is speaking* — turning away from the voice. It is not a warning about failing. It is a warning about not listening, addressed to people the writer has just told they have already arrived at Zion."}]}, "Hebrews 12:5–11": {"title": "The word behind \"discipline,\" and what it is not", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the passage people reach for when something has gone wrong and they want to know whether God did it to them. That is a heavy question to bring to a text, and this one is usually read with the wrong word in mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word actually is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word running through the whole passage — five times in seven verses — is *paideia*. It is built on the word for a child, and in ordinary Greek it means the whole business of bringing a child up: training, teaching, forming. It is the word a Greek would use for an education."}, {"k": "p", "t": "English \"discipline\" has drifted toward punishment, and once that is the word in your head, the passage reads as God arranging misfortunes. Put *upbringing* where discipline stands and read it again. The subject is not what God sends. It is that He is raising sons rather than managing strangers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument the writer is making"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Follow the logic and notice what it is arguing *for*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["being brought up this way is how you know you are handled as a son", "being left out of it would mean you were not one", "our own fathers did it and we thought better of them for it, not worse"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point being pressed is not \"expect suffering.\" It is **you belong to Him.** The writer is comforting people who were beginning to wonder, and the evidence he offers them is that they are being brought up at all."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says about the pain, exactly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No discipline feels like a good thing while it is happening — it feels miserable.* The passage does not spiritualise that, and neither should any reading of it. It concedes the misery outright."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then it says the outcome is *a harvest of peace and righteousness* — and notice that both of those are given, not achieved. Two verses earlier the same writer says God does it *so that we come to share His holiness*. Share. His. Whatever this upbringing produces, the passage never describes it as the reader manufacturing something to present back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not say every hard thing that happens is sent by God to teach a lesson. The passage never makes that claim, and reading it in is how this text ends up being used to explain a diagnosis or a bereavement to somebody who is already on the floor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What it says is narrower and kinder: God is bringing you up as a son, and a son is not left to raise himself. That is a claim about your standing with Him, made to people who were losing their nerve, and it is meant to steady them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"He lays the lash on every son.\"** The line quotes Proverbs, and it is the hardest sentence here. In its own context the image is a father's correction, not a judge's sentence — and the writer's use of it lands on the word *son*, which is the noun the whole passage keeps returning to. The emphasis is on who is being addressed, not on the instrument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul uses the same word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **chastened** by the Lord, so that we would not be condemned along with the world* — 1 Corinthians 11:32, and the verb there is **paideuo**, from this same root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul puts the chastening and the condemning on opposite sides of the sentence. Whatever the upbringing is, it is what keeps a son out of the world's verdict, not a lighter version of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the same argument this passage is making at length. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:31–34."}]}, "Hebrews 13:1–6": {"title": "\"I will never let go of you\" — and what it is attached to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most quoted promises in the Bible, and it is quoted almost entirely out of its sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the promise is given as a reason for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep your way of life free of the love of money, and be satisfied with what you have, **because** He has said it Himself: I will never let go of you, and I will never walk away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the argument for contentment. The reasoning runs: you can stop grasping, because the one thing you cannot lose is Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a more useful promise than the free-floating version. It is not offered as general comfort — it is offered to a specific anxiety about money and security, as the ground for letting go of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strength of the Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence in Hebrews stacks negatives in a way English cannot reproduce — something like *I will never, no never, leave you; I will never, no never, forsake you*. Five negative particles in one line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer is quoting God's promise to Joshua, and he piles the language up because he wants it to be unmissable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So we can say it right out: The Lord is the One helping me — I will not be afraid of anything anyone can do to me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives the promise and then supplies the answer, quoting Psalm 118. The believer's confidence is not manufactured; it is a line handed to them to say back."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The company it keeps.** The list before it is entirely practical: love each other, take in strangers, remember prisoners as though chained with them, honour marriage. Then contentment, then the promise. Whatever else this chapter is, it is not abstract — and the promise about God never letting go sits among instructions about hospitality and prison visits."}]}, "Hebrews 13:15–19": {"title": "A sacrifice made of words", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The letter has spent twelve chapters explaining that the sacrifices are finished. Then it names three that are not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through Him let us keep bringing God an offering of praise, words out of our mouths thanking His name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is from Hosea — *the fruit of our lips*. Under the old system you brought an animal; here the offering is speech."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two details keep it from being sentimental. It is **through Him** — the same mediation the whole letter has been arguing for. And it is *continually*, a present-tense keeping on rather than an occasion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second and third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not forget to do good and to share what you have; those are the offerings God takes pleasure in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Praise, then generosity — and the writer uses sacrificial vocabulary for both. The word for *takes pleasure* is the one used of a sacrifice being accepted as a pleasing aroma."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the offerings that remain are: what you say about God, and what you do for people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction about leaders"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They stay awake over your souls, and they will have to give an account, so let it be a glad job for them and not a groaning one — groaning would do you no good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unusually practical note. The reason given for cooperating is not the leaders' authority but the readers' own benefit: a discouraged shepherd is no use to the sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pray for us… I ask it all the more urgently, so that I can be given back to you sooner.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of the most theologically dense letter in the New Testament ends by asking for prayer and admitting he wants to see them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why \"through Him\" carries the weight.** Praise offered by people who know their own record can only be brought if somebody else makes it acceptable — which is precisely what the gold plate on Aaron's forehead was for, and what the letter says Christ now is. See the note on Exodus 28:36–38."}]}, "Hebrews 2:14–16": {"title": "Slaves to a fear, and what was actually broken", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is one of the plainest statements in the New Testament about why the incarnation happened, and it gives a reason that has nothing to do with teaching or example."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since the children are made of flesh and blood, He took on the same flesh and blood Himself* — and then a purpose clause, which is the point of the sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**so that by dying He could break the one who held the power of death**", "**and set free the people who had spent their whole lives as slaves because they were afraid to die**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He became human in order to die. Not primarily to teach, not to demonstrate. The argument is that only someone with a body could do this particular thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Break\" is not \"destroy\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *katargeo* — the same word behind *cut off* in Galatians 5, and it means to make idle, to put out of work, to leave with nothing to do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That precision matters, because the devil is plainly still active in the rest of the New Testament. The claim is not that he was annihilated at the cross. It is that the specific thing he held — *the power of death* — was taken out of his hands and he was left with nothing to hold over anybody."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The slavery is a fear, not a fate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look closely at what the passage says people were enslaved by. Not death. **Fear of death** — and *their whole lives*, which is the phrase that gives it away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The slavery being described is not what happens at the end. It is what fear of the end does to the middle: the way a person lives who is quietly managing an outcome they cannot control. The letter treats that as a life in chains, and treats the cross as the thing that unlocks it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He takes hold of\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line uses a vivid verb — reaching out and seizing, taking a grip on. It is used elsewhere of catching hold of someone who is sinking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is not angels He takes hold of; it is Abraham's children.* The writer is answering an unspoken question about why the rescue took the form it did. It took that form because of who was being grabbed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What this does not promise.** The passage does not say a believer will not die; the same letter goes on to list people who died in faith without receiving what was promised. What it says was broken is death's *hold* — the fear that ran a person's whole life. That is a real freedom, and it is not the same claim as exemption."}]}, "Hebrews 3:1–2": {"title": "\"The High Priest of the faith we own out loud\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the title given to Jesus in them is unusual."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the readers are called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brothers **set apart as holy**, sharing in **a call that came down from heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before anything is asked of them, two things are said about them. They are holy, and their calling originated somewhere other than in themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *sharing* is **metochoi** — partners, partakers. The same word is used later of *partakers of Christ*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Think hard about **Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katanoeo** — to fix the mind on, to observe closely, to consider attentively. It is the word used of Peter looking into the tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole command in these two verses. Not do, not try. Look at Him carefully."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two titles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One God sent* — **apostolos**, the sent one. The only time in the New Testament the word is applied to Jesus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **High Priest** of the faith we own out loud.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An apostle comes out from God to men. A high priest goes in from men to God. Both directions, one Person, in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Of our confession\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **homologia** — from *homo* (same) and *lego* (say). To say the same thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the High Priest **of that**. Of what is said out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not of our feelings, and not of our private opinions. The word means speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter keeps saying about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word turns up twice more, and both times as something to keep hold of:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since we have a great High Priest who has passed through the heavens, Jesus the Son of God, **let us hold fast our confession**.* — 4:14"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **hold fast the confession of our hope** without wavering, for He who promised is faithful.* — 10:23"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hold fast, and hold it without wavering, and the reason given is not about the one holding. It is *He who promised is faithful.* See the note on Hebrews 4:14–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faithful as Moses was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was faithful to the God who appointed Him, **just as Moses was faithful in all God's house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is not disparaged. He is called faithful in the same clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the argument turns on a distinction: *this One has been counted worthy of more glory than Moses, inasmuch as He who built the house has more honour than the house.* Moses was faithful **in** the house, as a servant. Christ is faithful **over** it, as a Son."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who \"the house\" is.** *Whose house we are, if we hold fast the confidence and the rejoicing of the hope firm to the end.* The letter has just called them holy brothers, and now it calls them the building."}]}, "Hebrews 3:12–15": {"title": "\"While there is still a Today\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The application of the psalm, and the instruction in it is about each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Watch out, brothers, that **not one of you turns out to have a heart gone bad and unbelieving**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kardia ponera apistias** — an evil heart of unbelief."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two nouns are joined. The writer does not treat unbelief as an intellectual position; he puts it in the same phrase as the badness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A heart that **walks away from the living God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apostenai** — to stand off, to withdraw. The root of *apostasy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of stepping back rather than of falling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep at each other **every single day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parakaleite** — exhort, encourage, urge alongside. It is from the same root as **parakletos**, the Helper. See the note on John 14:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Every single day** — the Greek is *day by day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The remedy for a heart hardening is other people, applied daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer says the same thing in chapter 10: *do not drop out of gathering together, the way some have gotten into the habit of doing… keep encouraging each other.* See the note on Hebrews 10:23–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The window"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For as long as **there is still a Today to say it in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word from the psalm, turned into a deadline."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **none of you gets hardened by sin and its lying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apate tes hamartias** — the deceitfulness of sin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hardening is described as the result of being lied to rather than of choosing badly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it the same way: *sin found its opening in the commandment, **deceived me**, and through it killed me.* See the note on Romans 7:9–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have **come to share in Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metochoi** — partakers, sharers. The word is used earlier in the chapter of *partakers of the heavenly calling*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense: we have become and remain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we **hold what we were sure of at the start firmly to the finish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the beginning of our confidence steadfast to the end*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As long as it is still being said: \"**Today, if you hear His voice, do not let your hearts turn to stone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer repeats the psalm's opening for the second of three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each repetition presses a different word. See the notes on Hebrews 3:7–11 and Hebrews 3:16–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole chapter is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Comparing two houses — Moses' and Christ's — and then warning against repeating what happened to the generation Moses led."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the letter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew believers under pressure to go back to the temple. The whole letter is written against that one move, and this is the first of its warnings."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is prescribed against a hardening heart.** Not more effort. Other people, daily, for as long as the word *Today* is still in use."}]}, "Hebrews 3:16–19": {"title": "\"Unbelief was what kept them out\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three questions and an answer, and the writer makes the reader do the work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage is a cross-examination. Each question has an obvious answer and the writer supplies it, and the effect is that the conclusion is drawn rather than announced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Some of them heard and pushed Him to it anyway** — though **not all of the ones Moses brought out of Egypt did**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exception matters. Caleb and Joshua came out of Egypt and went in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer is careful not to say the whole generation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **who was it He was sick of for forty years?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Was it not **the ones who sinned, the ones whose bodies dropped in the desert?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *whose carcasses fell in the wilderness* — the phrase from Numbers 14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of walking, and a funeral every few days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **who did He swear about, that they would never enter His rest?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The ones who refused to believe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apeithesasin** — those who disobeyed, who refused to be persuaded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a word about the will rather than the intellect. The same root John uses: *whoever refuses to believe the Son will not see life*. See the note on John 3:31–36."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So we can see it plainly: **they could not get in. Unbelief was what kept them out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *because of unbelief* — **di' apistian**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One cause is named, and it is not the giants, the walls, or the Anakim."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had accurate intelligence. All twelve spies agreed on the facts. See the note on Numbers 13:26–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He turns it on the reader immediately. The next verse: *therefore, since a promise remains of entering His rest, **let us fear lest any of you seem to have come short of it**.* See the note on Hebrews 4:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis he gives there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The good news came to us **just as it came to them**. But **hearing it did them no good, because it never mixed with any faith** in the ones who heard it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunkerannumi** — blended, compounded into one substance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same message, same preaching. What was missing was the thing that combines with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the wilderness generation actually did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stood at Kadesh, heard twelve reports, listened to ten, and refused."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers records what they said: *let us select a leader and return to Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, told the sentence, they changed their minds and went up anyway — and were beaten, because *the ark of the covenant of the LORD and Moses did not depart from the camp*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other kind of unbelief"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's warning is not aimed at people who never heard. It is aimed at people who heard, were on the road, and stopped at the border."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There remains therefore a **Sabbath rest** for the people of God.* See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer outlives the generation that missed it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one cause the writer names.** Not the opposition and not the difficulty of the land. He asks three questions with obvious answers and then says it in four words: unbelief was what kept them out."}]}, "Hebrews 3:7–11": {"title": "\"Today, if you hear His voice\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer quotes Psalm 95 in full, and attributes it to the Holy Spirit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So — and **this is the Holy Spirit speaking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote the psalm. The writer of Hebrews attributes it to the Spirit, in the present tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same in chapter 10 with Jeremiah. See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Today**, if you hear His voice.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument of the next two chapters hangs on this word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's point is that the psalm was written four hundred years after Joshua brought Israel into Canaan — so when David says *today*, the offer is evidently still open. See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not let your hearts turn to stone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skleruno** — to harden. The root of *sclerosis*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The way they did when they **pushed Him to it**, out in the desert **on the day they put Him on trial**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two Hebrew place names underlie this: **Meribah**, contention, and **Massah**, testing. See the note on Psalms 95:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both were names Moses gave to a place where there was no water and the people picked up stones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That was where your ancestors **tried Me, and tested Me, and watched what I did for forty years**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *proved Me, and saw My works*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had the evidence. Forty years of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that generation **made Me sick at heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosochthisa** — was grieved, was disgusted with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Their hearts are always wandering off, and they have never learned My ways**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not His acts — they saw those daily. His ways."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **in My anger I swore an oath: they will never enter My rest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strongest negative construction in the language."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rest was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Canaan — the end of the marching. And the writer will argue in the next chapter that it is also something else, since God had rested on the seventh day long before there was a land to enter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes the psalm three times across two chapters, and each time he presses a different word:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Here, the whole passage.", "Then *Today*: *exhort one another daily, **while it is called Today***. See the note on Hebrews 3:12–15.", "Then the failure: *they could not enter in **because of unbelief***. See the note on Hebrews 3:16–19.", "Then the offer: *there remains therefore a rest.* See the note on Hebrews 4:1–3."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter began"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, holy brethren, partakers of the heavenly calling, **consider the Apostle and High Priest of our confession, Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the comparison with Moses: *this One has been counted worthy of more glory than Moses, inasmuch as He who built the house has more honour than the house.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Christ as a Son over His own house, whose house we are** if we hold fast the confidence and the rejoicing of the hope firm to the end.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word the argument stands on.** *Today.* The writer notices that David used it four hundred years after the land was taken, and builds two chapters on the fact that the offer had not closed."}]}, "Hebrews 4:1–3": {"title": "The rest still standing open", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument turns on a door that has not been shut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **let us be afraid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phobethomen** — let us fear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a strange way to open a paragraph about rest, and he means it: the danger is real enough to be afraid of missing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is open"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The promise of entering His rest **is still standing open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *while a promise remains*. It has not been withdrawn or used up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **not one of you should turn out to have missed it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *lest any of you seem to have come short* — **hustereo**, to fall short, to arrive too late."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The good news came to us **just as it came to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *we have had the gospel preached to us, as well as they*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wilderness generation had good news preached to them. The writer says so plainly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They stood at Kadesh, twelve spies came back, two said go up, and the nation refused. That was the sermon and that was the response."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it did not work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **hearing it did them no good, because it never mixed with any faith in the ones who heard it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not being mixed with faith in those who heard*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **sunkerannumi** — blended, compounded together, the way ingredients are combined into one substance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The message was not deficient. It sat in them without combining."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who enters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We who have believed **are the ones who enter that rest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present tense. The entering is described as happening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as He said, \"**So I swore in My anger: they will never enter My rest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 95, and the writer quotes it three times across these two chapters. See the note on Psalms 95:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oddity at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And that, **though the work had been finished ever since the world was laid down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest they were barred from had been ready since Genesis 2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer says as much in the next verse: *God rested on the seventh day from all His works.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had to be built. The rest existed before the people who missed it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There remains therefore **a Sabbath rest for the people of God**… let us **be diligent to enter that rest**.* See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An exertion aimed at a rest, and the writer puts both words in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seeing then that we have **a great High Priest** who has passed through the heavens… **let us therefore come boldly to the throne of grace**, that we may obtain mercy and find grace to help in time of need.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter about a rest that was missed ends at a throne with an open invitation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the failure was.** Not that the news was withheld or unclear. The writer says the same gospel reached them, and names the missing element as the thing that would have combined with it."}]}, "Hebrews 4:12–13": {"title": "Alive, and cutting", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four claims about the word, and then the sentence turns and looks back at the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Directly after two chapters on the wilderness generation who heard the good news and did not enter, and the instruction *let us be diligent to enter that rest*. See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word that did them no good is the subject of the next sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Alive"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God's word is **alive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zon** — living. It is the first word of the sentence in Greek, placed for emphasis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Force"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **force** in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **energes** — active, at work, operative. The root of *energy*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The edge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It has **a keener edge than any blade sharpened on both sides**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **distomos** — literally *two-mouthed*. Greek describes the edge of a sword as its mouth, and a double-edged sword as one with two."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew does the same: **peh**, mouth, is the word for a blade's edge — and Isaiah's threshing sledge has *mouths* for teeth. See the note on Isaiah 41:14–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it separates"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It goes in until it has separated **soul from spirit** and **joint from marrow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pairs, and each is a pair of things nobody can get between."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joint and marrow are not adjacent; marrow is inside the bone. The picture is of something reaching past the surface to what is sealed inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it reads"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it **reads the heart** — **every thought it ever had, every intention**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kritikos** — able to judge, discerning. The root of *critic*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two objects: **enthumeseon**, the thoughts and reflections, and **ennoion**, the intentions and purposes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nothing that was ever made **stays hidden from Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject changes mid-passage. Verse 12 is about the word; verse 13 is about a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer does not mark the transition, and the effect is that the two are hard to separate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything lies **stripped bare, throat exposed**, in front of the eyes of the One we have to answer to.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tetrachelismena** — from **trachelos**, the neck. It is the root of *trachea*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word was used of a wrestler forcing an opponent's head back to expose the throat, and of a sacrificial animal with the neck laid open for the knife."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Stripped bare** — **gumna**, naked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next verse is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seeing then that we have **a great High Priest** who has passed through the heavens, Jesus the Son of God, **let us hold fast our confession**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *for we do not have a High Priest who cannot sympathise with our weaknesses, but was **in all points tempted as we are**, yet without sin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us therefore **come boldly to the throne of grace**, that we may obtain mercy and find grace to help in time of need.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage about being laid open is followed directly by an invitation to walk in without hesitating — and by a priest described as knowing what it is like."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order the writer uses.** Everything exposed, and then a throne to come to boldly. He puts them in consecutive sentences and the second is the conclusion of the first."}]}, "Hebrews 4:14–16": {"title": "Walk right up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The letter has spent four chapters on rest and on the word that cuts. Then it turns to prayer, and the instruction is startlingly forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Walk right up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is about coming to, approaching — and the adverb attached is **parrhesia**: boldness, freedom of speech, the frankness of someone entitled to be there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not creep in. Not petition from a distance. Approach with the freedom of a citizen speaking in his own assembly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For readers who knew the tabernacle, this is a considerable sentence. The place where God sat was behind a curtain, entered once a year, by one man, with blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it can be said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is not a high priest who cannot feel it when we are weak. He was put to the test in every way we are, and He never sinned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once. He has been through it — the verb for *feel it* is about suffering along with, sympathising from the inside. And He came through clean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both matter. A priest who had not been tested could not sympathise; one who had failed could not represent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The throne"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The throne where grace sits.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a throne — the seat of a ruler with every right to judge. And what the writer says is on it is grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is waiting there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mercy, and grace arriving exactly when we need help.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase for well-timed help is one word, about being in season, at the right moment. Not grace stockpiled in advance. Grace that shows up on time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse before it.** *We have a great high priest who has gone right up through the heavens.* He went up; we walk up. The whole basis of the boldness is that somebody is already inside — see the note on Hebrews 9:23–24."}]}, "Hebrews 4:8–11": {"title": "\"Work hard at entering that rest\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer's whole case rests on one word in a psalm and on a name that means two things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **Joshua had given them rest**, God would **not have spoken later on about another day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua brought them into Canaan. The land was taken, the tribes settled, and the book ends *the LORD gave them rest all around*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years later David writes *Today, if you will hear His voice, do not harden your hearts… they shall not enter My rest.* See the note on Psalms 95:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's point: you do not offer a rest that has already been given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek, Joshua and Jesus are the same name — **Iesous**. The older English versions print *Jesus* here, which is why the verse used to confuse readers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both mean *the LORD saves*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **there is still a Sabbath rest waiting to be kept** by the people of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sabbatismos** — a Sabbath-keeping. The word appears once in the New Testament, here, and the writer appears to have coined it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it consists of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who has entered His rest **has stopped working himself**, the way **God stopped when His own work was done**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is Genesis 2. God rested on the seventh day, and He did it because the work was finished, not because He was tired."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the model given: rest as the response to completion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **let us work hard at entering that rest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **spoudazo** — be diligent, make every effort, hurry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An exertion, aimed at a rest. The writer puts the two words in one sentence and leaves them there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the effort is against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **nobody falls by copying the same refusal to believe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the same example of disobedience* — and the word for disobedience in this letter is consistently tied to unbelief. Three verses earlier: *they could not enter in because of unbelief.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing to work at is not falling into the pattern of the generation that would not go in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern being avoided"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They stood at the border, sent twelve spies, heard two of them, and would not cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of walking followed, and Numbers records what they said: *let us select a leader and return to Egypt.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the word of God is living and powerful, and sharper than any two-edged sword… piercing even to the division of soul and spirit, and is a discerner of the thoughts and intents of the heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, immediately: *seeing then that we have a great High Priest… **let us therefore come boldly to the throne of grace**, that we may obtain mercy and find grace to help in time of need.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter about rest ends at a throne with an invitation to come to it without hesitating."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the writer keeps returning to.** *Today.* He quotes it three times across two chapters. The whole argument is that the day the offer is open is the one the reader is in."}]}, "Hebrews 5:11–12": {"title": "Still on milk", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer breaks off mid-argument and explains why."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **a great deal to say about all this**, and **it is hard to get across**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just named Melchizedek for the fourth time and is about to open the argument. See the note on Hebrews 5:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops instead, and the digression runs to the end of chapter 6 before he picks it up again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the fault is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **you have gotten lazy about listening**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nothroi tais akoais** — sluggish, dull, in the hearing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say the subject is beyond them. He says the difficulty is at their end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nothros** appears twice in the letter, here and at the end of chapter 6: *that you do not become **sluggish**, but imitate those who through faith and patience inherit the promises.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The expectation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By this time **you ought to be the ones teaching**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enough time has passed. The letter is written to a congregation that had been going long enough to have produced teachers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 10 fills in what they had been through: *you had compassion on me in my chains, and joyfully accepted the plundering of your goods.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "These are not new converts. They had lost property for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they actually are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead **somebody has to start you over at the beginning**, with **the simplest things God has said**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta stoicheia** — the elements, the ABCs. It is the word for the letters of the alphabet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have ended up **needing milk, not solid food**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they have *come to have need* of it — a state arrived at, not a state never left."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been further on and had come back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next verses say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For everyone who partakes only of milk is unskilled in the word of righteousness, for he is a babe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **solid food belongs to those who are of full age**, that is, those who **by reason of use have their senses exercised to discern both good and evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *exercised* is **gegumnasmena** — trained, from the root of *gymnasium*. It is a word about practice rather than information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, **leaving the discussion of the elementary principles of Christ, let us go on to perfection**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refuses to go back over the foundations, lists six of them, and then delivers the warning of Hebrews 6:4–6 — followed immediately by *but beloved, we are convinced of better things concerning you*. See the notes on Hebrews 6:4–6 and Hebrews 6:9–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he returns to Melchizedek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 7, after the anchor behind the veil. He picks up the sentence he abandoned here and spends a whole chapter on it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is blamed.** Not the difficulty of the subject. The writer says he has a great deal to say and that the obstacle is dull hearing — and then takes two chapters to get them ready before saying it."}]}, "Hebrews 5:13–14": {"title": "Food you have to chew", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer explains the difference between the two diets, and the test he gives is not about knowledge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who is **still living on milk cannot handle what is said about righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apeiros logou dikaiosunes** — inexperienced in the word of righteousness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Apeiros** means untried, unskilled — the opposite of practised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that they have not been told. That they have not handled it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is **a baby**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nepios** — infant, literally *non-speaking*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Paul uses of the heir who is no different from a slave. See the note on Galatians 4:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other diet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Food you have to chew belongs to **grown men and women**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teleion** — the mature, the complete. From **telos**, the end a thing is aiming at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People who have **worked their senses hard enough, and long enough**, to **tell what is good from what is evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gegumnasmena** — trained, exercised. From the root of *gymnasium*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **hexin** — a settled habit, a state produced by repetition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What maturity is not, in this sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Information. The writer has just said he has *a great deal to say* and cannot say it. See the note on Hebrews 5:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gap is not on the input side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Discernment produced by use. The Greek is literally *having the faculties trained by practice for the distinguishing of good and evil*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A trained sense is not a fact learned. It is what a person develops by handling something repeatedly until they can tell the difference without stopping to think."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reproach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By this time **you ought to be the ones teaching**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 12, and the diagnosis is that they had gone backwards: *you have come to have need of milk*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, **leaving the discussion of the elementary principles of Christ, let us go on to perfection**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *perfection* is **teleioteta** — the noun of the word he has just used for the grown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refuses to go back over the foundations, lists six of them so they know what he is leaving, and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the solid food turns out to be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Melchizedek. He had named it four times and broken off each time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He picks it up in chapter 7 and spends a whole chapter there: the priest with no genealogy, the oath in Psalm 110, and the change of law that follows a change of priesthood. See the notes on Psalms 110:4 and Hebrews 7:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Milk, solid food, and then two chapters of the hardest argument in the letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer does not simplify. He tells them what they are missing and then delivers it anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The test he gives.** Not what a person knows. Whether their senses have been worked hard enough and long enough to tell one thing from another without being told."}]}, "Hebrews 5:9–10": {"title": "\"Carried all the way through to the finish\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses that finish one of the hardest paragraphs in the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who, in the days of His flesh, when He had offered up **prayers and supplications, with vehement cries and tears** to Him who was able to save Him from death, and was heard because of His godly fear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though He was a Son, yet **He learned obedience by the things which He suffered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gethsemane, in the writer's account of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The completion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had been **carried all the way through to the finish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teleiotheis** — perfected, brought to the end, completed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a moral word. The same root is used of a sacrifice being accepted and of a priest being consecrated — the Greek Old Testament uses it for the ordination of Aaron."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing was lacking in Him. What was completed was the qualification: He went the whole way through the experience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He became"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He became **the reason everyone who obeys Him is saved**, and **saved forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the author of eternal salvation* — **aitios**, the cause, the responsible party."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Forever** — the salvation is described by its duration."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God Himself **named Him High Priest** — Melchizedek's kind of priest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosagoreutheis** — designated, addressed by a title. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The appointment is God's. The writer has already made the point: *no man takes this honour to himself, but he who is called by God, just as Aaron was.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fourth mention of Melchizedek in the letter so far, and the argument keeps being deferred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer names it here and then breaks off, because his readers are not in a state to follow it. See the note on Hebrews 5:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He picks it up again at chapter 7 and spends the whole chapter on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn, and He will not take it back: \"You are a priest forever, in the order of Melchizedek.\"* See the note on Psalms 110:4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two priesthoods"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron's line was appointed by command and served until death. This one was appointed by oath and holds it *forever*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the tears have to do with the priesthood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument earlier in the chapter: *for every high priest taken from among men is appointed for men… **he can have compassion on those who are ignorant and going astray, since he himself is also subject to weakness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A high priest has to be able to sympathise. Gethsemane is in the letter because of the job description."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the writer says it directly in chapter 4: *we do not have a High Priest who cannot sympathise with our weaknesses, but was in all points tempted as we are, yet without sin.* See the note on Hebrews 4:14–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was completed.** Not His character. The Greek is the word for a priest being consecrated — and the writer says the consecrating ran through prayers, cries and tears before the naming."}]}, "Hebrews 6:13–14": {"title": "\"He swore by His own name\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the whole point is the absence of anybody higher."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When God made Abraham a promise, **there was no one greater for Him to swear by**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An oath works by staking something larger than yourself. A man swears by God because God outranks him and will hold him to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For God there is no such party."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **He swore by His own name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of Genesis 22 is *by Myself I have sworn*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The guarantee and the guarantor are the same, which sounds like a weakness and is the opposite. If God is the highest thing there is, an oath sworn on Himself is backed by everything that exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Bless you I will; multiply you I will**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek doubles both verbs — *blessing I will bless you, and multiplying I will multiply you*. It is the Hebrew intensive construction carried straight over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same doubling appears in Jabez's prayer: *bless me, really bless me*. See the note on 1 Chronicles 4:9–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was sworn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 22, on the mountain, immediately after the knife was stopped. See the notes on Genesis 22:11–12 and Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the call in Genesis 12, and not at the covenant in Genesis 15. After the son was laid on the wood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer says about Abraham next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so, **after he had patiently endured, he obtained the promise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **makrothumesas** — long-tempered, slow to burn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-five years between the promise of a son and Isaac's birth. And Abraham never saw the descendants as the stars of the sky."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 11 says so plainly: *these all died in faith, not having received the promises, but having seen them afar off.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the writer brings it up here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier he told them to *imitate those who through faith and patience inherit the promises*. See the note on Hebrews 6:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham is the worked example. The connective is *for* — this is why he said it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the oath is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument does not stop at Abraham. Four verses on: *God, determining to show more abundantly **to the heirs of promise** the immutability of His counsel, confirmed it by an oath.* See the note on Hebrews 6:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other oath in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn and will not relent: You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer makes the same move there — an oath over the priesthood that the Levitical line never had. See the note on Hebrews 7:20–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two oaths in the letter, one about a family and one about a priest."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is unusual about it.** God does not need to swear. The writer's point is that He did it anyway, and the reason given is not doubt on His side."}]}, "Hebrews 6:16–17": {"title": "\"So God went further\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer explains how human oaths work and then says God submitted to the procedure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it works among people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People **swear by someone greater than themselves**, and once an oath is given **to settle a matter, the argument is over**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *an oath for confirmation is to them an end of all dispute* — **antilogia**, contradiction, argument back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That was the legal function. Where evidence ran out, an oath closed the case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **God went further**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *God, wanting more abundantly* — **perissoteron**, over and above."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already promised. The oath is on top of a promise that was already given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He wanted **the people who would inherit what He promised** to be **in no doubt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tois kleronomois tes epangelias** — the heirs of the promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Abraham only. The writer has widened it, and the readers of the letter are inside the category."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were to be in no doubt about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **His mind on this would never change**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **to ametatheton tes boules autou** — the unchangeableness of His purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oath does not make the purpose firmer. It makes the firmness visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **He backed it with an oath**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *He interposed Himself with an oath* — **mesiteuo**, from the same root as **mesites**, mediator. He stepped in between."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is strange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God has nothing to gain from swearing and nobody to swear by. See the note on Hebrews 6:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's point is that the oath exists entirely for the benefit of the people reading — an accommodation to how humans settle things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That by **two immutable things**, in which **it is impossible for God to lie**, we might have **strong consolation**.* See the note on Hebrews 6:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two are the promise and the oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the paragraph ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This hope we have as **an anchor of the soul**, both sure and steadfast, and **which enters the Presence behind the veil**, where the forerunner has entered for us — even Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An anchor that goes upward and inward rather than down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Forerunner** is **prodromos** — the scout who goes ahead of a party, or the small boat that carried a ship's anchor into harbour when the ship itself could not yet get in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It opened with the sharpest warning in the letter and closes with an anchor inside the veil. See the note on Hebrews 6:4–6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the oath adds.** Nothing, to God. Everything, to the reader — the writer says outright that the reason it was given was so that the heirs would be in no doubt."}]}, "Hebrews 6:18": {"title": "Two things that cannot shift", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer builds a guarantee out of two items and names them both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now there are **two things that cannot shift**, and God cannot lie in either of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before identify them: God made a promise to Abraham, and then He swore an oath to confirm it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because He could swear by no one greater, **He swore by Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A promise, and an oath. Both from someone who cannot lie."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the oath at all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God, determining to show more abundantly to the heirs of promise the **immutability of His counsel**, confirmed it by an oath.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise was already sufficient. God's word does not require notarising."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oath was added — the text says so plainly — for the sake of the people receiving it, so that they would have two immovable things instead of one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He accommodated human uncertainty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refugees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we, **who ran for shelter** and grabbed hold of the hope held out in front of us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the language of fleeing for refuge — the image is of somebody running to the cities of refuge in Numbers 35, where a man who had killed accidentally could run and not be touched by the avenger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You did not have to argue your case at the gate. You had to get inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Grabbed hold\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to seize, to take firm hold of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And note what is grabbed: *the hope held out in front of us* — set before us, offered. It is extended first and taken second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have **something powerful to steady us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *strong encouragement* — **parakalesis**, the noun behind *Comforter*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anchor"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *this hope we have as **an anchor of the soul, both sure and steadfast**, and which enters the Presence behind the veil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An anchor is thrown ahead of the ship into ground the ship has not reached, and it holds the vessel by being somewhere else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this one is described as going *behind the veil* — into the holy of holies, the room nobody could enter except the high priest, once a year, with blood. See the note on Hebrews 9:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The anchor is not in the harbour. It is in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where the forerunner has entered for us, even Jesus, having become High Priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for **forerunner** is *prodromos* — the scout who goes ahead of the main body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A forerunner is meaningless unless others are following. The word implies the rest of the fleet."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter has come from.** Some of the hardest verses in the letter, about falling away — and the writer's own comment on them is *we are confident of better things concerning you.* Then Abraham, the oath, the anchor and the forerunner. The passage that frightens people ends in a room they cannot be got out of."}]}, "Hebrews 6:4–6": {"title": "The impossible thing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest paragraph in the letter, and the first thing to see is who it is written to and what they were about to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the letter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew believers under pressure to go back to the temple. The whole letter is one long argument that there is nothing to go back to — a better priest, a better covenant, a better sacrifice, and all of it *once for all*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple was still standing. The offerings were still being made daily."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **impossible**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **adunaton** — without power, incapable. The writer uses it four times in this letter:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*It is **impossible** for the blood of bulls and goats to take away sins.*", "*Without faith it is **impossible** to please Him.*", "*It is **impossible** for God to lie.* See the note on Hebrews 6:18.", "And this one."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have once been **given light**, have **tasted the gift** that came down from heaven, have been **given a share in the Holy Spirit**, have **tasted how good the word of God is** and **the powers of the age that is coming**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five, and two of them are the verb **tasted** — **geuomai**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exposure to the whole thing. Light, the heavenly gift, the Spirit, the word, and the powers of the coming age — these people had seen all of it at close range."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they then do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then **turn their backs on it all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parapesontas** — to fall away alongside, to defect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The specific defection in view is the one the whole letter is written against: going back to the sacrifices."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is impossible"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **impossible to make them new again so that they change their minds**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to renew them again to repentance* — **metanoia**, a change of mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are **crucifying the Son of God over again, on their own account**, and **putting Him on show for people to jeer at**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anastauroo** (crucify again) and **paradeigmatizo** (expose to public shame)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what returning to the altar amounted to in the writer's argument. If the sacrifice was not enough, then the cross has to be repeated — and the letter has just spent five chapters saying it cannot be and does not need to be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no second cross to go to. That is the impossibility."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not leave it there. Three verses on:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But dear friends, even when we talk like this, **we are convinced of better things where you are concerned** — the things that come with being saved.* See the note on Hebrews 6:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states the warning and then immediately says he does not think it applies to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On an oath sworn to Abraham, and two unchangeable things, and *a hope both sure and steadfast, which enters the Presence behind the veil.* See the notes on Hebrews 6:13–14, Hebrews 6:16–17 and Hebrews 6:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that contains the sharpest warning ends on the strongest guarantee in the letter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage is arguing.** Not that a person can be lost and re-lost, but that there is only one sacrifice and it has already happened. A man walking back to an altar is walking toward something the letter says was finished."}]}, "Hebrews 6:9–10": {"title": "\"We are convinced of better things\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses after the hardest warning in the letter, the writer says he does not think it applies to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **dear friends**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agapetoi** — beloved. It is the only time in the whole letter the writer uses it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saves it for the sentence directly after the warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even when **we talk like this**, we are **convinced of better things where you are concerned**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pepeismetha** — perfect passive, the same word Paul uses in Romans 8: *I am fully persuaded*. See the note on Romans 8:38–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A settled conviction, not a hope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the better things are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The things that **come with being saved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *things that accompany salvation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the category rather than listing it, and then gives his evidence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is **not unjust**. He will **not forget the work you did and the love you put into it** for His name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is about God's character, not their performance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Not unjust** — the writer puts it negatively. It would be an injustice for God to overlook it, so He will not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Looking after His people, **as you are still doing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *having ministered to the saints and still ministering*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two tenses. There is a record, and it is ongoing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 10 fills it in: *you had compassion on me in my chains, and **joyfully accepted the plundering of your goods**, knowing that you have a better and enduring possession for yourselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had visited prisoners and lost their property for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the warning was given at all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because they had defected, but because the letter has just described them as sluggish: *you have become dull of hearing… you need someone to teach you again the first principles… you have come to need milk and not solid food.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning is aimed at where drifting ends up, and the writer says plainly he does not think they are there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we desire that **each one of you show the same diligence** to the full assurance of hope until the end, that you do not become sluggish, but **imitate those who through faith and patience inherit the promises**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Full assurance** — the Greek is **plerophoria**, full carrying, complete certainty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then he names one of the imitators: Abraham. See the note on Hebrews 6:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Warning, then *but beloved*, then Abraham's oath, then two unchangeable things, then an anchor behind the veil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter with the sharpest edge in it ends with the strongest guarantee in the letter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the writer bases his confidence on.** Not their record — he has just called them dull of hearing. God's justice, and the fact that there is something already done that it would be unjust to forget."}]}, "Hebrews 7:20–22": {"title": "The oath", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer has been building a case out of Genesis 14 and Psalm 110, and here he adds one more piece: an oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being compared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Those other priests **were made priests with no oath sworn over them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron's line was appointed by command. Exodus says *take Aaron your brother, and his sons with him* — an instruction, not a vow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer has combed the Old Testament for a divine oath attached to the Levitical priesthood and found none."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath that does exist"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Lord has sworn it and will not change His mind: You are a priest forever — Melchizedek's kind of priest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110:4, the verse after the one about the footstool. See the note on Mark 12:35–37."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote it about a descendant of his. The order named is older than Aaron by four hundred years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Will not change His mind\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *will not repent*. The same construction appears in Numbers — *God is not a man, that He should lie, nor a son of man, that He should repent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An oath is a promise with a second layer under it. God swearing means the thing is placed beyond revision."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer makes the point outright a chapter earlier: *God, determining to show more abundantly to the heirs of promise the immutability of His counsel, **confirmed it by an oath**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forever"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis ton aiona** — into the age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Levitical priest served from thirty to fifty and died. The writer says as much: *there have been many priests, because they were prevented by death from continuing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oath's subject does not vacate the post."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**That is the measure of it: Jesus has become the guarantee of a better covenant**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *guarantee* is **engyos** — a surety, the person who stands good for somebody else's debt. It appears once in the New Testament, here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A surety is not a witness or a middleman. He is liable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction it runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elsewhere the writer calls Him **mesites**, mediator, standing between the two parties. **Engyos** is stronger: He is not holding the covenant open, He is standing behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant's security rests on the person sworn about rather than on the people covered by it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Melchizedek came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He walks out of Genesis 14 with bread and wine, blesses Abram, receives a tenth, and walks off the page. Four verses in the whole of the Old Testament, plus one line in a psalm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of Hebrews builds two chapters on him. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20 and on Hebrews 7:8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is unusual about the argument.** The writer's evidence is partly what is *not* in the text. No oath over Aaron, no genealogy for Melchizedek, no record of his birth or death — and he reads the silences as deliberate."}]}, "Hebrews 7:4–5": {"title": "\"Think how big a figure this must be\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer asks his readers to work out the rank of a man from a single transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **think how big a figure this must be**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *consider* — **theoreo**, to look at attentively, the root of *theory*. He is asking for deliberate observation rather than agreement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham himself, **the father of the whole family**, handed him a tenth of what he had taken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is entirely about who gave to whom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To his Jewish readers, Abraham is the top of the tree. There is no higher human authority in their history — the covenant, the promise and the nation all begin with him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Abraham gave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule the argument runs on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated three verses later: *beyond all contradiction, **the lesser is blessed by the better**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Melchizedek blessed Abraham and received a tenth from him. On that rule alone, Melchizedek outranks him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison with Levi"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law tells **Levi's sons** who take up the priesthood **to collect a tenth from the people** — from their own brothers, who came out of Abraham exactly as they did.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Levitical priests also received tithes. But note the differences the writer is setting up:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["they collected **because the law told them to** — Melchizedek received without any law existing", "they collected **from their brothers** — men of the same standing as themselves", "both parties *came out of Abraham* — nobody in that arrangement outranks anybody"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Melchizedek has no genealogy at all, and Abraham paid him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If perfection were through the Levitical priesthood… what further need was there that another priest should rise according to the order of Melchizedek?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter is establishing that a better priesthood was promised — in Psalm 110, a thousand years after Abraham and centuries after Levi — and that it is not Aaron's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn and will not relent: You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the clause that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here mortal men receive tithes, but there **He receives them, of whom it is witnessed that He lives**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is verse 8, and it is the writer's own contrast: the Levites died and were replaced, generation after generation. The other one is testified to be alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Hebrews 7:8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter concludes about Him.** *Therefore He is also able to save to the uttermost those who come to God through Him, since **He always lives to make intercession for them**.* The permanence of the priesthood is the whole point, and the argument for it started with one afternoon in a valley."}]}, "Hebrews 7:6–7": {"title": "\"The lesser is blessed by the greater\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer states a rule nobody disputes and uses it to settle a question about rank."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **nobody argues with this: the lesser is blessed by the greater**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *beyond all contradiction*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that world, blessing ran downhill. A father blessed a son, a priest blessed the people, a patriarch blessed the tribes. The direction of the blessing told you who outranked whom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is being applied to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This man is **not out of their line at all** — and he **took a tenth from Abraham** and **spoke a blessing over the man who held the promises**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two facts about Melchizedek, and both point the same way: he received from Abraham, and he blessed Abraham. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The man who held the promises\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer chooses the biggest possible description of Abraham before making him the lesser party."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *Abraham the shepherd*, or *Abraham the newcomer*. The man who held the promises — the whole covenant, the land, the nation, the seed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That man received a blessing, which means somebody greater gave it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not out of their line at all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Levites collected tithes because the law told them to, from brothers who came out of Abraham exactly as they did. Melchizedek has no genealogy in the record at all, and he collected from Abraham himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter is establishing that a priesthood exists which is not Aaron's, and which is better."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If perfection were through the Levitical priesthood… what further need was there that another priest should rise according to the order of Melchizedek?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110 promised one, a thousand years after Abraham and centuries after Levi."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same principle lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody blesses himself. Blessing is received from somebody higher, by definition — that is what the rule says."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the great blessings in Scripture are always given rather than obtained: Melchizedek to Abraham, Isaac to Jacob, Jacob to the twelve, the priests to Israel with the Aaronic blessing, and Jesus with His hands raised over the disciples as He was carried up. See the note on Acts 1:9–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chapter's conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore He is also **able to save to the uttermost** those who come to God through Him, since **He always lives to make intercession for them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *to the uttermost* is completely, to the end, all the way through."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one who is greater.** *Here mortal men receive tithes, but there He receives them, **of whom it is witnessed that He lives**.* One verse later, and it is the writer's own summary of the difference. See the note on Hebrews 7:8."}]}, "Hebrews 7:8": {"title": "The one receiving it now is alive", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Here, the men who collect the tenth are men who die. There, the one who collects it is the one the testimony says is alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the tense in it is the thing to see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two priesthoods, two conditions"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**here** — the Levites, receiving tithes and dying, generation after generation. Aaron's robes taken off one man and put on his son, over and over.", "**there** — Melchizedek, of whom the record says only that he lives. No genealogy, no death notice, no successor."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument is that Christ is a priest *in the order of Melchizedek*, and that this order does not run out of men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The present tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for receiving is present. The writer does not say tithes *were* received; he says they are, and that the one receiving them is testified to be living."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a striking thing to leave standing in a letter written decades after the cross. Whatever a reader concludes about how much or how often, the picture the sentence gives is of a living Priest still receiving what is brought to Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the argument needed Abraham"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham gave the tenth to Melchizedek before Levi was born — and the writer says Levi, still in his ancestor, paid it through him. The greater blesses the lesser; the lesser pays the tenth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the tenth in scripture is first an act of honour toward a priest-king who blesses, and only later a Mosaic arrangement for feeding a tribe. The order runs blessing first, response after, from the very first time it appears."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter lands.** *He is able to save completely those who come to God through Him, because He always lives to intercede for them.* The same word — He **lives** — carries both halves: the one who receives, and the one who never stops praying for the people who come."}]}, "Hebrews 7:9–10": {"title": "Levi paid it through Abraham", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer makes an argument about a man who was not born yet, and he knows how it sounds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You could even put it this way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He flags it himself — *so to speak*, in most translations. He is aware he is pressing something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then he presses it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Levi, **who collects the tenth, paid it himself** — paid it through Abraham. **He was still inside his forefather's body** when Melchizedek came out to meet him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levi is Abraham's great-grandson. He is four generations away and centuries from existing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer says he was there, in Abraham, and that what Abraham did that afternoon counted for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of Hebrews 7 is establishing that Melchizedek's priesthood outranks Levi's, and therefore that Christ's does — *You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The logic: the lesser is blessed by the greater. Abraham gave a tenth to Melchizedek and received a blessing from him. So Melchizedek is the greater — and Levi, still in Abraham's body, was on the paying end of that transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man's act reached forward into descendants who did not exist."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same structure for Adam — *by one man's disobedience many were made sinners* — and for Christ: *by one Man's obedience many will be made righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What one man did, a whole line was counted in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Melchizedek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*King of Salem, priest of God Most High* — Genesis 14, and he appears in three verses and vanishes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He brings out **bread and wine** to Abraham coming back from the slaughter of the kings, and blesses him. Then Abraham gives him a tenth of everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews notes what the record leaves out: no father, no mother, no genealogy, no beginning of days, no end of life — *made like the Son of God, he remains a priest continually.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the tithe stands in the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham gave it before the law existed. Sinai was four hundred years off; there was no commandment, no tabernacle and no Levitical system to fund."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gave it to a priest-king who came out with bread and wine, and the writer of Hebrews uses that afternoon to prove a priesthood that has no end."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Abraham refused the same day.** The king of Sodom offered him the goods and he would not take a thread or a sandal-strap — *lest you should say, \"I have made Abram rich.\"* He gave a tenth to the priest and turned down the king, on one evening."}]}, "Hebrews 8:10–12": {"title": "\"I will remember no more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The longest Old Testament quotation in the New Testament, and the writer of Hebrews reproduces it in full because of how it ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four promises, all first person"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clause is God's own undertaking. Nothing in the new covenant is stated as the people's contribution:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**I will set My laws inside their thinking and carve them into their hearts**", "**I will be their God, and they will be My own people**", "**all of them will know Me already**", "**I will show mercy… and their sins I will remember no more**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old covenant was an agreement with terms on both sides. Read this list and notice there is only one signatory doing anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Inside, not on stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will set My laws inside their thinking and carve them into their hearts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one used of engraving. The law is not abolished in this promise — it is relocated. Out of a box, off tablets, into the person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah wrote this while watching the temple system collapse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From the lowest of them to the highest\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the arrangement needs an intermediary to tell them who God is. In a world of priests and Levites and scribes, that is a considerable claim, and it is made about *all of them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not overlooked, not filed. God undertakes to not remember — which is not forgetfulness in a being who knows everything, but a decision about what will be brought up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of Hebrews quotes the whole passage twice, and both times he is building to that sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he concludes from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where these have been forgiven, there is no longer any offering for sin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his argument in a line. If the sins are not remembered, there is nothing left for a sacrifice to do — which is why the priest in this letter is sitting down. See the note on Hebrews 1:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"new.\"** *By calling this covenant new, He has made the first one obsolete.* The writer says it plainly one verse before this quotation, and it is the whole reason he is quoting Jeremiah at all."}]}, "Hebrews 8:8–9": {"title": "\"The days are coming\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer begins the longest Old Testament quotation in the New Testament, and the first thing he does is say where the fault lay."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **if that first covenant had been faultless, then no place would have been sought for a second**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An argument from the existence of the promise. A replacement was promised, so the first was not final."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the blame goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But God does **lay the blame on them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **memphomenos autois** — finding fault with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Them**, not it. The writer is careful. He has just spent chapters saying the law was given by God, and the fault named is in the party that could not keep it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it plainly elsewhere: *what the law could not accomplish, **weakened as it was by the old nature***. See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah 31, and Hebrews quotes it at greater length than any other Old Testament passage in the New Testament — six verses straight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look, **the days are coming**, says the Lord, when I will make **a new covenant** with the house of Israel and with the house of Judah.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kaine** — new in kind, not merely recent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah said it while Jerusalem was falling to Babylon, in a book otherwise full of judgment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both houses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The house of Israel **and** the house of Judah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The kingdom had been split for centuries and the northern tribes had been gone for over a hundred years when Jeremiah wrote. The promise names both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It will **not be like the covenant I made with their fathers** on the day I **took them by the hand and led them out of the land of Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenderness of the image is the point. *Took them by the hand* — the way you lead a child across a road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah does not describe the first covenant as harsh. He describes it as a hand held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What went wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**They did not stay inside that covenant of Mine**, and so **I turned away from them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of Jeremiah adds a clause the Greek renders differently: *though I was a husband to them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, the failure named is on the human side of the agreement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next in the quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will put My laws in their mind and write them on their hearts… **and their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more**.\"* See the note on Hebrews 8:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four promises, all in the first person, and nothing required in return."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The writer's conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In that He says, \"A new covenant,\" **He has made the first obsolete**. Now what is becoming obsolete and growing old **is ready to vanish away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peleroken** — made old. And **aphanismou** — disappearance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written while the temple was still standing and the sacrifices were still being made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes the same Jeremiah passage a second time in chapter 10, in two halves with a joint in the middle. See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's whole case rests on a promise made in the middle of a siege."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the fault is placed.** Not on the covenant and not on the giver. The Greek pronoun is plural and it points at the people — and the covenant it describes is one where God took them by the hand."}]}, "Hebrews 9:23–24": {"title": "\"There He is now, standing before God's face on our account\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer has spent a chapter on the tabernacle, and here he says what all of it was a model of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Copies and the real thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ did not walk into a holy place that hands had built, a stand-in for the real one. He walked into heaven itself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *stand-in* is **antitypos** — a corresponding figure, a copy made to match a pattern. Moses had been shown the pattern on the mountain and told to build to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the tent in the desert was never the original. It was a scale model of something that already existed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not into the model. *Into heaven itself* — the place the tent was copied from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument of the whole letter compressed: everything Israel did once a year in a tent, He did once, in the real one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The present tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there He is now, standing before God's face on our account.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *went and returned*. He is there, presently, in front of God — and the phrase is *on our behalf*, in our interest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for appearing is the one used of being made visible, presented. What is presented before God is Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does to prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gold plate on Aaron's forehead made Israel's gifts acceptable while he stood in the tent. Here the writer says the priest is inside the true holy place, permanently, and that He is there for us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the letter can tell its readers to walk right up to the throne. The boldness is not confidence in ourselves; it is knowledge of who is already standing there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line just after.** He did not go in *to offer Himself again and again*, as the high priest entered yearly with blood that was not his own. Once, at the end of the ages, to put away sin by the sacrifice of Himself. The visit is not repeated because it does not need to be."}]}, "Hebrews 9:27–28": {"title": "Once, and once", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two \"once\"s set side by side, and one of them answers the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People **die once**; that is set. And after that comes **the judgement**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *it is appointed* — laid down, assigned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two facts about everybody, stated without qualification, and the writer does not soften either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **Christ was offered one time**, to carry away the sins of many.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hapax** — once, once for all. The same word as in the first clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parallel is the argument: one death for people, one offering for Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the point the whole letter has been building. The priests *stand ministering daily, offering repeatedly the same sacrifices*, and He *sat down*. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To carry away\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anaphero** — to bear up, to carry. It is the verb the Greek Old Testament uses of the scapegoat carrying the sins away into the wilderness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second appearance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He will appear a second time** — **sin will have nothing to do with it** — to bring salvation to those who are watching for Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clause in the middle is the striking one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first appearance dealt with sin. The second is described as having no relation to it at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who are **watching for Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apekdechomai** — to await eagerly, to expect. Paul uses it of creation groaning and waiting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the Day of Atonement the high priest went into the holy place with blood, and the people waited outside to see him come out again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If he came out, the sacrifice had been accepted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has just spent a chapter on that ceremony. See the note on Hebrews 9:6–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any second offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter says it elsewhere in so many words: *He then would have had to suffer often since the foundation of the world; but now, **once at the end of the ages**, He has appeared to put away sin by the sacrifice of Himself.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the verse a chapter later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By one offering He has **perfected forever** those who are being sanctified.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two \"once\"s do together.** A man dies once and is judged. Christ was offered once and carries it. The writer puts them in one sentence so that the second is read as the answer to the first."}]}, "Hebrews 9:6–7": {"title": "\"Only one day a year, and never without blood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses describing an arrangement, and every clause in them is a restriction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outer room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priests were **in and out of the front room every day**, doing the work of the worship.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daily traffic. The lampstand to trim, the bread to change, the incense morning and evening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ordinary priests, ordinary access, ordinary work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inner room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the inner room — **only the high priest** went in there, and **only one day a year**, and **never without blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three limits stacked in one sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["one man", "one day", "and a required condition"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement, Yom Kippur. Once a year, and Leviticus 16 sets out the procedure in detail — the linen garments, the washing, the incense to cover the mercy seat in cloud, the blood sprinkled seven times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron is warned that he *shall not come at just any time into the Holy Place*, on pain of death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "For himself first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which he offered **for himself** and for the sins the people had blundered into.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest could not represent anybody until he had dealt with himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews makes the point again a few chapters later: *He does not need daily, as those high priests, to offer up sacrifices, first for His own sins and then for the people's.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The sins the people had blundered into\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agnoema** — sins of ignorance, things done without knowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the Day of Atonement covered. The system had provision for what people did not realise they had done, and nothing at all for deliberate, high-handed sin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer concludes from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Holy Spirit indicating this, that **the way into the Holiest of All was not yet made manifest** while the first tabernacle was still standing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The closed curtain was a message. As long as that room was shut, the point was being made that the way in was not open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what happened to the curtain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, behold, the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records it at the moment of the death. **From top to bottom** — nobody standing on the floor could have started it there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast Hebrews draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, having boldness to enter the Holiest by the blood of Jesus, by a new and living way which He consecrated for us, through the veil, that is, His flesh… **let us draw near**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man, one day, never without blood — and then *let us draw near*, addressed to everybody, with no day named. See the note on Hebrews 10:19–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was in the room.** The ark, and above it the mercy seat with the cherubim. And after the exile, according to the historians, the room was empty — the ark was gone. The high priest went in once a year, with blood, into an empty room, for centuries."}]}, "Hosea 1:8–9": {"title": "Lo-Ammi", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophet is told to name his son a sentence, and the child carries it around for the rest of his life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hosea's wife bears three, and each name is a message:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Jezreel** — God scatters", "**Lo-Ruhamah** — Not-Pitied, not shown mercy", "**Lo-Ammi** — Not-My-People"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet's household is the sermon. Every time he calls his son in for dinner he says it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For you are **not My people**, and **I am not your God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the second clause is closer to *I am not I-AM to you* — the name from the burning bush, withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it describes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the season of the exile and everything after it. No temple that mattered, no king on David's throne, no prophet after Malachi, no ark, and the visible dealings of God with that nation suspended."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book of Esther sits inside this. Ten chapters, a Persian empire, a death decree, and the name of God never once written down. That is what *Lo-Ammi* looks like from inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not gone. Esther is the proof of it — a sleepless king, an old record read at the right hour, a gallows turned round, and a nation alive at the end of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hidden face, and not one hand withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same chapter is already going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read what stands immediately before verse 9 and immediately after it. Hosea 1:10:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet the number of the children of Israel shall be as the sand of the sea, which cannot be measured or numbered; and in the place where it was said to them, \"You are not My people,\" there it shall be said to them, \"You are the sons of the living God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reversal is in the same chapter as the sentence. It is not a later correction; the promise is written on the other side of the same page."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the very place\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not somewhere else, and not to a different people. In the place where the words *not My people* were spoken."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes exactly that line in Romans 9 and applies it — and he applies it to Gentiles being brought in. See the note on Romans 9:25–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hosea is told to do in chapter 3.** Go and love her again. He buys his own wife back for fifteen shekels and a homer and a half of barley — the price of a slave. The names of the children are what the book says; the purchase is what the book is about."}]}, "Hosea 12:10–11": {"title": "\"Vision after vision\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God describes His own method of speaking, and then names what it was spent on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **spoke through the prophets**, gave **vision after vision**, told **parables through their mouths**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew gives three: spoke, multiplied visions, and used **similitudes** — **damah**, to liken, to compare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The multiplying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Vision after vision.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have multiplied visions* — **chazon**, the same word as Proverbs 29 and Habakkuk 2. See the note on Proverbs 29:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one vision. A supply of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Told parables through their mouths.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *by the ministry of the prophets I have used similitudes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God says He spoke in likenesses — pictures standing for something else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is exactly what the prophets do. Jeremiah's almond branch and boiling pot, Amos's plumbline and basket of summer fruit, Ezekiel's valley of bones, Zechariah's lampstand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Hosea's own life: he is told to marry Gomer, and the marriage is the message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with the same method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **without a parable He did not speak to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew adds that it fulfilled a psalm: *I will open My mouth in parables; I will utter things kept secret from the foundation of the world.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hosea's own life was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go, take yourself a wife of harlotry… **for the land has committed great harlotry by departing from the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three children with names that are messages: Jezreel, Lo-Ruhamah (*no mercy*), and Lo-Ammi (*not My people*)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the reversal: *I will have mercy on her who had not obtained mercy, and I will say to those who were not My people, \"You are My people.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes both in Romans 9. See the note on Romans 9:25–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is there **evil in Gilead**? Then **everything there has come to nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gilead was a Levitical territory east of the Jordan, and by Hosea's time a byword for bloodshed — chapter 6 calls it *a city of evildoers, defiled with blood*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The altars"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **Gilgal they sacrifice bulls**, and their **altars stand like heaps of stone in a plowed field**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gilgal was where Israel first camped after crossing the Jordan and where the twelve stones were set up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By Hosea's day it was a shrine centre, and he mentions it three times, never approvingly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Like heaps of stone in a plowed field.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *heaps in the furrows of the field*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A farmer clearing a field piles the stones at the edges. The altars are described as those piles — numerous, made of what was in the way, and worthless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is a pun underneath it: **Gilgal** and **gallim**, heaps."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two verses do together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first says God spoke in every way available — prophets, visions, pictures."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second says what the country did with it: multiplied altars."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By a prophet the LORD brought Israel out of Egypt, and by a prophet he was preserved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is not named. The point is the office."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God says about His own speaking.** Three methods and a plural: vision after vision, through the mouths of prophets, in likenesses — and the next sentence is about stone heaps in a ploughed field."}]}, "Hosea 2:14–15": {"title": "A door of hope in the valley of trouble", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before this is a divorce proceeding. Then one word changes everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has come before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hosea 2 opens with God laying out a case against an unfaithful wife — strip her, hedge her way with thorns, take back the grain and the wine and the wool, end the feasts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is that she went after other lovers and *did not know that I gave her grain, new wine and oil.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So now, listen. I will draw her back to Myself.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew opens with *therefore* — and it is the strangest *therefore* in the prophets. Everything before it argues for judgement, and the conclusion drawn is courtship."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is to allure, to entice, to persuade. It is the word for winning somebody over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Into the wilderness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **lead her out into the wilderness** and **speak softly to her heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the speaking is *speak upon her heart* — the idiom for wooing, used of a man courting a woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the destination is a desert. He takes her to the emptiest place available in order to talk to her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wilderness in Israel's memory is the honeymoon: *I remember you, the kindness of your youth, the love of your betrothal, when you went after Me in the wilderness.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vineyards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There I will give her back her vineyards.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the desert. He returns the fruitfulness in the place where nothing grows, and before she is out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The valley"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **turn the Valley of Achor, that valley of trouble, into a door of hope**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Achor** means trouble, and it is a place with a history."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua 7 — Achan took the devoted things from Jericho, Israel was defeated at Ai, and the valley where he was dealt with was named Achor because of it. *Why have you troubled us? The LORD will trouble you this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was the site of Israel's first defeat in the land, and the name preserved the shame."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God says He will make a **door** of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not remove it, not rename it, not compensate for it. Turn the place itself into an entrance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There she will answer Me **as she did when she was young**, as on the day she first came up out of Egypt.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that she will sing there — the same verb used of Miriam at the sea with a tambourine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a chastened response. The first one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Hosea was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet told to marry a woman who would be unfaithful, and to name his children *Not-Pitied* and *Not-My-People*. See the note on Hosea 1:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, in chapter 3, told to go and love her again — and he buys his own wife back for the price of a slave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's argument is made with his household before it is made with words."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the door leads.** *And it shall be, in that day, that you will call Me \"My Husband,\" and no longer call Me \"My Master.\"* Two Hebrew words, *ishi* and *baali*. The valley of trouble opens onto a change in what she calls Him."}]}, "Isaiah 1:18": {"title": "\"Come now, let us talk this through\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most generous sentence in the first chapter of Isaiah, and the chapter around it is one of the harshest in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A nation described as sick from head to foot, a country burned, worship God calls a burden He is weary of carrying, and hands full of blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *come now, let us talk this through.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The invitation arrives in the middle of the indictment, not after it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a legal term — to argue a case, to reason together, to settle a dispute. It is the language of a courtroom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God summons them to the argument, and then does not argue. He offers terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two colours"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though your sins are scarlet, they can turn white as snow. Though they are red as crimson, they can become soft and clean as wool.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both dyes named were the fastest, most permanent colours in the ancient world. Scarlet and crimson came from an insect and soaked into the fibre — cloth dyed that way did not fade, and could not be washed out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the point of choosing them. The stain being described is the kind that does not come out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they become"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not merely clean — **snow** and **wool**. Wool is what the cloth was before it was dyed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is not of a stain removed but of the material restored to its original state, as though it had never been in the vat."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows immediately.** *If you are willing and obedient, you will eat the good of the land.* The offer has a shape, and it belongs to the covenant Isaiah is prosecuting. What Christians have always heard in the verse is the colour chemistry: God naming the most permanent stain available and saying it can go."}]}, "Isaiah 11:1–3": {"title": "A shoot from the stump", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before this one ends with a forest being cut down. This one begins with what grows out of the stump."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stump"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A shoot will spring up **from the stump of Jesse**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from the tree — from the stump. The house of David is described as felled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is *Jesse*, not David. Back past the throne to the farmer in Bethlehem whose youngest son was out with the sheep. The royal line is reduced to the family it came from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 10 closes with God cutting down the high trees with an axe: *the thickets of the forest He will cut down with iron, and Lebanon will fall.* Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shoot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **netzer** — a sprout, a green shoot. Matthew's *He shall be called a Nazarene* has been read against this word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shoot out of a stump is what a tree does when it has been cut down and is not dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Spirit resting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of the LORD **will rest on him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to settle, to come to rest and stay. It is the same root as Noah's name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sevenfold list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it as it is written and it is three pairs under one heading:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the Spirit of the LORD**", "the Spirit of **wisdom** and **understanding**", "the Spirit of **counsel** and **might**", "the Spirit of **knowledge** and **the fear of the LORD**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven, and it is one Spirit named seven ways rather than seven Spirits."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation opens with *the seven Spirits who are before His throne*, and the lampstand in Exodus had seven lamps on one hammered stand. See the notes on Revelation 1:4–6 and Exodus 25:31–40."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"His delight will be in the fear of the LORD\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that His scent, His breathing, is in it — an unusual verb, related to smell. He finds it fragrant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How He judges"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **not judge by what his eyes see**, nor decide by what his ears hear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two things every human court runs on — testimony and appearance — are set aside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse says what He judges by instead: *with righteousness He shall judge the poor, and decide with equity for the meek of the earth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wolf with the lamb, the leopard with the young goat, the calf and the lion together, and a child leading them. A nursing infant playing at a cobra's hole."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They shall not hurt nor destroy in all My holy mountain, for the earth shall be full of the knowledge of the LORD as the waters cover the sea.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter ends with.** *In that day there shall be **a Root of Jesse**, who shall stand as a banner to the peoples; for the Gentiles shall seek Him.* He is the shoot out of Jesse in verse 1 and the root of Jesse in verse 10 — after him, and underneath him."}]}, "Isaiah 14:12–15": {"title": "\"I will\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A taunt-song over the king of Babylon, and five sentences in the middle of it that readers have always taken as describing something larger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter introduces it: *you will take up this **proverb against the king of Babylon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **mashal** — a taunt, a mocking poem. It is addressed to a human king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the language in these four verses goes beyond any human king, which is why the church has read it as describing the power behind him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How you have fallen from heaven, **O shining one, son of the dawn**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **helel ben-shachar** — shining one, son of the morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Latin Bible rendered *helel* as **Lucifer**, light-bearer, and the name entered English that way. It is a description of the morning star, not a proper name in the Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five \"I will\"s"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will** ascend to heaven. **I will** lift my throne above the stars of God. **I will** sit on the mount of assembly, in the far reaches of the north. **I will** rise above the tops of the clouds. **I will** make myself like the Most High.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five, in a row, and each one goes higher than the last."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last is the only one that names a person: *like the Most High.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any object outside himself. Every clause is *I* and a destination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set it beside the other great five-fold statement in Scripture — *behold, I have come to do Your will, O God*, and *not what I want, what You want.* See the notes on Psalms 40:6–8 and Luke 22:39–46."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sets of intentions, and they run in opposite directions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet you are brought down to the grave, to the depths of the pit.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five ascents, and one sentence in reply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Sheol**, and *the depths of the pit* — the lowest place named in the poem, answering the highest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel 28 does the same thing with the king of Tyre: *you were the anointed cherub who covers… you were perfect in your ways from the day you were created, till iniquity was found in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two prophets, two pagan kings, and both oracles reach past the man in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I watched Satan fall out of heaven like a bolt of lightning.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke 10, to the seventy coming back excited that the demons obeyed them. He speaks as an eyewitness. See the note on Luke 10:17–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning star, taken back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I, Jesus… am the Root and the Offspring of David, **the Bright and Morning Star**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation 22, and it is the last self-description in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title the shining one grasped at is claimed by somebody else at the end of the book."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the taunt-song is really about.** Verse 4 says it plainly: *the LORD has broken the staff of the wicked… the whole earth is at rest and quiet; they break forth into singing.* The poem is what the nations sing when an oppressor falls."}]}, "Isaiah 2:2–3": {"title": "Nations streaming uphill", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's second chapter opens with a picture that requires water to do something water does not do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Streaming upward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the nations will **stream toward it** like rivers running home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb **nahar** is built from the noun for a river. Rivers run down. These are running up a mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is deliberately impossible, and it is the first thing said about the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The highest of the mountains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The mountain of the LORD's house will stand as the highest of the mountains, lifted above the hills.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mount Zion is not a large hill. It is overtopped by the Mount of Olives across the valley, and by most of the ranges around it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is not measuring altitude."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the inviting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Many peoples will come and say, **Come, let us go up**…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is sent to fetch them in these two verses. The invitation is on the lips of the peoples themselves, and they say it to each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they want when they get there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will teach us His ways, and we will walk in His paths.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not protection, not prosperity, not a verdict on their enemies. Teaching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second clause commits them before the first is answered — *we will walk*. They ask to be taught by somebody they have already decided to obey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the teaching goes out from Zion, the word of the LORD from Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Teaching** is **torah**, which is instruction before it is legislation — the word a parent's guidance is called in Proverbs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next in the chapter.** Swords into ploughshares, spears into pruning hooks, and nations that *will not learn war any more*. The disarmament is placed after the teaching, not before it — in Isaiah's order, they stop fighting because they have been to the mountain."}]}, "Isaiah 26:1–6": {"title": "\"Whose mind is stayed on You\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A song about a city, with the best-known line in Isaiah in the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The walls"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have **a strong city**. **He makes salvation its walls** and its rampart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The defences are not masonry. Salvation is the wall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah is writing to people whose actual city walls were about to be tested by Assyria."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who comes in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Open the gates, that **the righteous nation may enter, the nation that keeps faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *keeps truth* — **emunim**, faithfulness, the root of *amen*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You keep him in **perfect peace** whose **mind is stayed on You**, because **he trusts in You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *perfect peace* is **shalom shalom** — peace, peace. The word twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew has no superlative form; doubling is how it says *complete*. Holy, holy, holy works the same way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Stayed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Samak** — to lean, to prop, to rest one's weight on. It is the verb used of a man laying his hand on the head of a sacrifice and pressing down. See the note on Leviticus 1:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *thinks about*. Leans on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Trust first, then the leaning, then the peace. Isaiah gives the causal chain in the sentence: *because he trusts in You.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Trust in the LORD forever, for **the LORD, the LORD Himself, is an everlasting Rock**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the Rock of Ages* — **tsur olamim**, and the hymn takes its title from here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does to the other city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **brought low those who lived on high**, the lofty city He lays low. He lays it low to the ground. He casts it into the dust.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs of bringing down, one after another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who tramples it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The foot tramples it down, **the feet of the poor, the steps of the needy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people with nothing walk over what the high city becomes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In that day this song will be sung* — a song written for a day that has not arrived, given to people to learn in advance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 24 to 27 is often called the little apocalypse: the earth broken open, the feast on the mountain, death swallowed up forever, and *the LORD will wipe away tears from all faces.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This song sits inside it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter says a few verses later.** *With my soul I have desired You in the night; yes, by my spirit within me I will seek You early.* The man singing about perfect peace says he was awake in the dark looking for God."}]}, "Isaiah 28:11–13": {"title": "\"This is the resting place\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A sentence about rest, sitting in the middle of one of the harshest chapters in Isaiah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens on *the drunkards of Ephraim* and moves to the priests and prophets of Jerusalem: *they have erred through wine… they err in vision, they stumble in judgment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people mishandling the word of God are drunk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had been mocking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their own words are quoted in verse 10: *tsav latsav, tsav latsav, qav laqav, qav laqav, zeer sham, zeer sham* — a sing-song jingle, mocking the prophet as though he were teaching toddlers their letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, with **stammering lips and a foreign tongue** He will speak to this people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They complained that the prophet's Hebrew sounded like baby talk. They will hear a language they genuinely cannot understand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Assyrian army spoke it. Isaiah is describing an invasion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer they refused"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones to whom He said, \"**This is the resting place, let the weary rest here, this is the place of repose**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **menuchah** — rest, a settled place, a stopping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It shares a root with the name **Noah**, and it is the word in Psalm 23: *He leads me beside the still waters* — literally, *waters of rest*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer had been made and it was for the weary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **they would not listen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, and the whole chapter turns on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the word of the LORD will become for them **command upon command, command upon command, rule upon rule, rule upon rule, a little here, a little there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their own mocking jingle, handed back to them as the shape of what they will get."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having refused the rest, what is left is the accumulation — line after line, rule after rule, endlessly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that they **go and fall backward, and are broken and snared and captured**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, each worse than the last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"With men of other tongues and other lips I will speak to this people, and even so they will not hear Me,\" says the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes this passage in 1 Corinthians 14 and draws his conclusion about tongues as a sign from it. See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:20–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore thus says the Lord GOD: \"**Behold, I lay in Zion a stone for a foundation, a tried stone, a precious cornerstone, a sure foundation. Whoever believes will not act hastily**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes it. Paul quotes it. In the middle of the chapter about the jingle and the foreign tongue, Isaiah lays a cornerstone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other rest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There remains therefore **a rest for the people of God**.\" Let us **be diligent to enter that rest**, lest anyone fall according to the same example of disobedience.* See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews uses the same argument shape: an offer of rest, a refusal, and the offer still standing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was on offer.** *This is the resting place, let the weary rest here.* The people it was said to were the ones who would not have it — and Isaiah records the sentence anyway."}]}, "Isaiah 28:20–22": {"title": "\"His strange work\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah calls judgement two things, and both of them mean *not His*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The bed is **too short** to stretch out on, the blanket **too narrow** to wrap yourself in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A proverb about inadequate arrangements. Whatever they had made for themselves would not cover them — a treaty with Egypt, in the chapter's context, described a few verses earlier as *a covenant with death*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You can lie on a short bed. You cannot rest on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two battles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For the LORD will rise up as He did at **Mount Perazim**. He will rouse Himself as in the **Valley of Gibeon**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are places where God fought **for** Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perazim is where David defeated the Philistines and said *the LORD has broken through my enemies before me like a breakthrough of water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gibeon is where the hailstones fell on the Amorites and the sun stood still for Joshua."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The threat in this verse is that He will do the same thing again — with His own people on the receiving end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To do **His work, His strange work**, to perform **His task, His alien task**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *strange* is **zuwr** — foreign, from somewhere else, not native. And *alien* is **nokri** — belonging to another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah names judgement twice, and both times as something out of character. Not that God does not do it — that it is not the work that comes naturally to Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Set beside what is called natural"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD, the LORD — a God tender and kind, gracious and full of compassion, slow to anger, overflowing with steadfast love.* That is God's own self-description, and none of it is called strange. See the note on Exodus 34:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His anger lasts only a moment, but His favour lasts a lifetime.* See the note on Psalms 30:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lamentations says it plainly: *He does not afflict willingly, nor grieve the children of men.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **do not scoff now**, or your chains will grow heavier.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that their bonds will be made strong. Mockery tightens what is already holding them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier is one of the great promises in Isaiah: *Behold, I lay in Zion a foundation stone, a tried stone, **a precious cornerstone**, a sure foundation. Whoever believes will not act hastily.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes it, and Paul quotes it. In the middle of a chapter about drunkards, short beds and strange work, a cornerstone is laid."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Isaiah is arguing against.** *We have made a covenant with death, and with Sheol we are in agreement* — the leaders' own words, quoted back at them. They had made an arrangement they thought would hold, and the bed was too short."}]}, "Isaiah 32:15–17": {"title": "The work of righteousness", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A chapter of desolation turns on one word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses just above are about a land stripped: *thorns and briers*, *the palace forsaken*, *the populous city deserted*, and the women of Jerusalem told to strip themselves and put on sackcloth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The forts and towers will become lairs forever, a joy of wild donkeys, a pasture of flocks.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word, and everything after it is reversal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pouring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until **the Spirit is poured on us from on high**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **arah** — to be emptied out, poured until nothing is left in the vessel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**From on high** — the source is named and it is not in the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three stages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the desert becomes a fruitful field**, and **the fruitful field seems like a forest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word for *fruitful field* is **karmel** — orchard land, the same word as the mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three levels: wilderness, orchard, forest. The desert does not merely recover; it goes two steps past where it started, and the good land is re-graded as ordinary next to what follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where justice lives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **justice will dwell in the desert**, and **righteousness will live in the fruitful field**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both places, not one. The word for *dwell* is the ordinary verb for settling down and staying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fruit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the fruit of righteousness will be peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the root. Righteousness first, peace as what grows on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul in Romans: *therefore, having been justified by faith, we have **peace** with God.* The order is the same. See the note on Romans 5:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the work of righteousness will be quietness and confidence forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *work* is **abodah** — labour, service, what a thing produces by operating."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what it produces is:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Quietness** — **shaqat**, stillness, undisturbed rest. It is the word used of a land that *had rest* from war.", "**Confidence** — **betach**, security, safety, from the root for trusting."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are states of not having to do anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oddity"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness works, and what its work amounts to is quietness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah puts an active noun and a passive result in the same clause and leaves them there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else Isaiah says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In returning and rest you shall be saved; **in quietness and confidence shall be your strength**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same two words, in chapter 30, and there the sentence ends *but you would not*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My people will dwell in a peaceful habitation, in secure dwellings, and in quiet resting places.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for the same thing, one after another."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it starts.** Not with reform. With something poured out from above onto a place described as thorns and briers — and everything in the paragraph after that is consequence."}]}, "Isaiah 38:1–3": {"title": "A word from God that did not turn out to be the last word", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophet arrives with a message that leaves no room in it — *you are going to die, you will not recover* — and by the end of the chapter it has not happened. This is one of the most theologically awkward passages in the Old Testament, and it is worth reading rather than smoothing over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah actually said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two statements, both flat, both introduced with *this is what the LORD says*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["put your house in order", "you are going to die, you will not recover"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no *unless* in it. Hezekiah is not offered terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hezekiah does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not accept it and he does not argue with Isaiah. He turns his face to the wall — away from the prophet, away from the room — and prays. Then he weeps bitterly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His prayer is short and it contains no request. He reminds God how he has walked, and he cries. What he asks for is never stated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before Isaiah has left the palace grounds, he is sent back with fifteen more years."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this passage is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a technique. Hezekiah does not do anything a reader could copy — there is no formula, no confession, no claim. And the same book is full of prayers that did not turn out this way; the psalms are half made of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nor does it make Isaiah a false prophet. The word was true of the situation as it stood. What changed was not God's honesty but the situation, once a dying man prayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a record that a word about the future is not always a locked door, and that God is not offended by a person who takes bad news to Him and weeps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth holding carefully in both directions. It is real permission to pray about an outcome that has been declared. It is not a promise that everyone who prays gets fifteen years — the same chapter has Hezekiah writing afterwards about how the reprieve felt, and the rest of his story is not a simple triumph."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The part people forget.** The extra fifteen years are not presented as an unmixed good. Manasseh, the worst king in Judah's history, is born in them, and the chapter after this has Hezekiah showing the treasury to Babylonian envoys — the visit Isaiah tells him will end in exile. The Bible gives him the years and then tells the truth about what he did with them."}]}, "Isaiah 40:21–24": {"title": "The circle of the earth", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah asks four questions and then says something about the shape of the world."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sits enthroned above **the circle of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chug** — a circle, a sphere, a compass, the shape drawn by swinging a fixed radius."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah wrote it around 700 BC. Every culture around him thought the earth was flat, and it stayed that way in the popular mind for two thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not know? Have you not heard? Was it not told to you from the beginning? Have you not understood since the earth was founded?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks them the way you ask someone who ought to know already."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grasshoppers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And its people are **like grasshoppers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The view described is from above the circle, looking down, and from there the people are insects."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a change of altitude in the middle of a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He stretches out the heavens **like a curtain**, spreads them **like a tent** to live in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is used elsewhere in Isaiah of the heavens being stretched out, and it is the word for pitching a tent — pulling fabric taut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The princes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He reduces princes to nothing… **No sooner are they planted, no sooner are they sown, no sooner do they take root** — than He breathes on them and they wither.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses to say how briefly. The whole career of a ruler is compressed into the time between sowing and the first root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He *breathes* on them. It takes nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 40 opens with *comfort, comfort My people*, and this passage is inside that comfort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point of the circle, the tent and the grasshoppers is not astronomy. It is that the people frightening you are seed that has not rooted yet, and the One above the circle knows your name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's last lines are the famous ones: *those who wait on the LORD shall renew their strength; they shall mount up with wings like eagles.* See the note on Isaiah 40:29–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where else the same word turns up.** Proverbs 8, the passage about Wisdom present at creation: *when He drew a **circle** on the face of the deep, I was there.* And Job says the same thing in his own words — *He drew a circle on the surface of the waters.* See the note on Job 26:5–10."}]}, "Isaiah 40:29–31": {"title": "The order the verbs come in", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*They will soar on wings like eagles. They will run and not grow weary. They will walk and not faint.* The list descends, and readers have wondered about that for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the sequence goes down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "You would expect a crescendo: walk, then run, then fly. Isaiah puts them the other way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The likeliest reason is that it ends where life mostly is. Soaring is the picture; running is the effort; walking is the long ordinary stretch — and *not fainting* on that stretch is the hardest of the three. The promise is arranged to finish on the least dramatic thing, because that is where it is needed longest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even the young grow tired and weary, even strong young men stumble and fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah names the strongest people available and says they run out. That is the setup: the promise is not that some people are naturally tireless. It is that strength comes from somewhere other than youth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"wait\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **qavah** carries the sense of a cord being twisted — strands drawn together under tension. From that comes waiting, expecting, hoping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not passive. It is closer to being wound tight around something. And the promise attached is not that strength returns but that it is **exchanged** — the verb means to change one thing for another, as you would change clothes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter starts.** *Comfort, comfort my people.* This is the opening of the section of Isaiah written to exiles, and the whole chapter builds toward these three verses: God measures the oceans in His hand, weighs mountains on scales, brings princes to nothing — and then gives strength to the one with nothing left. The size of Him is the argument for the promise."}]}, "Isaiah 40:3–5": {"title": "Every valley lifted, every hill brought down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Road-building language, used of a coming that has not happened yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the image is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the ancient world, when a king travelled, work parties went ahead to make the route passable — filling in the dips, cutting down the high ground, straightening what wandered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah applies that to God arriving. The road is not being built *to* Him. It is being made ready *for* Him to come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two directions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let every valley be lifted up, every mountain and hill made low.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both movements aim at the same result — level ground — and both are stated in the passive. The valley does not raise itself. The hill does not step down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That levelling shows up all through Scripture as the way God deals with people: the low lifted, the high brought down. Mary sings it in the Magnificat; James says the humble are exalted. Here it is described as the preparation for glory being seen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the opening of the second half of Isaiah, addressed to a people in exile, and it begins two verses earlier with *comfort, comfort my people*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The road being described leads home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All four Gospels quote these verses of John the Baptist. Luke quotes the longest stretch, and ends where Isaiah does — *all people together will see it.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *The mouth of the LORD has spoken.* Isaiah adds it as the guarantee. The chapter goes on to say that grass withers and flowers fall, but the word of our God stands forever — and this is one of the things that word has said."}]}, "Isaiah 41:11–13": {"title": "\"You will look for your enemies and not find them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A promise about opposition disappearing, and it ends with a hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to the opposition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All who rage against you will be ashamed and disgraced. Those who oppose you will be as nothing and perish. **You will look for your enemies and not find them.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle line is the striking one. Not that the enemies are defeated in front of you — that you go looking and they are not there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice the phrase *as nothing* appears. The Hebrew is a word for non-existence, nothingness. What was threatening is described as having no substance left to locate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I am the LORD your God, who takes hold of your right hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise is not grounded in Israel's strength or in the enemies' weakness. It is grounded in a grip."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is specific: the **right** hand — the working hand, the sword hand. God is described as holding the hand a fighter would need free."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says while holding it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not fear. I Myself will help you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The emphatic pronoun is in the Hebrew. Not *help will come* — I, Myself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah uses this construction repeatedly in these chapters, and each time the comfort is anchored in who is speaking rather than in the circumstances improving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it was said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exiles, or people about to be. The chapter opens with God summoning the nations to a courtroom and asking who has directed history, and it addresses Israel as *My servant, whom I have chosen, the offspring of Abraham My friend.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the standing from which the promise is made — a relationship named before a single enemy is mentioned."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse just before this passage.** *Do not fear, for I am with you; do not be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you and help you; I will uphold you with My righteous right hand.* His right hand holding theirs. The image runs through the whole section."}]}, "Isaiah 41:14–16": {"title": "\"You worm Jacob\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The address is an insult and the promise is a farm implement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be afraid, **you worm Jacob**, you **little people** of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tolaath** — a worm, a grub. It is the word used of the maggot in the manna that was kept overnight, and of the crimson dye made from a crushed insect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also the word in Psalm 22: *but I am a worm, and no man.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the address is there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is God against the idol-makers — *let them bring forth and show us what will happen*, *behold, you are nothing, and your work is nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that argument He turns to Israel and calls them a grub."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not flattering them into confidence. He states what they are and then says what He is going to do with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The help"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I Myself will help you**,\" says the LORD, \"**your Redeemer, the Holy One of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two titles. **Redeemer** is **goel** — the kinsman-redeemer, the relative obliged to buy back what a family member had lost. It is Boaz's role in Ruth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nearest relative and the Holy One, in one line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The transformation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"See, **I will make you into a threshing sledge, new and sharp, with many teeth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **morag** — a heavy wooden sled dragged over cut grain, its underside set with stone or iron teeth, to separate the wheat from the straw."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *many teeth* is **piphiyoth** — literally *possessors of mouths*, from the word for mouth. The same construction is used of a sword: *a sword with two mouths* is the phrase behind *two-edged*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The teeth of the sledge and the edge of a sword are described in Hebrew as mouths."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it threshes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will thresh the mountains and crush them**, and **reduce the hills to chaff**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sledge is for grain. This one is put over mountains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scale is deliberately wrong, and the results are the same either way: what was solid becomes chaff."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will **winnow them, and the wind will carry them off**, and a gale will scatter them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Winnowing is the step after threshing. You throw the crushed material into the air and the breeze takes the light part away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left to sweep up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose the outcome is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But you will rejoice in the LORD, and glory in the Holy One of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The worm ends the passage rejoicing, and not in what it did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is around it in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fear not, for I am with you; be not dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you, yes, I will help you, I will uphold you with My righteous right hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is verse 10, four verses earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after this passage: *I will open rivers in desolate heights, and fountains in the midst of the valleys… I will plant in the wilderness the cedar and the acacia tree.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The distance in three verses.** From a grub to a threshing sledge new and sharp, going over mountains — and the only thing between the two is *I Myself will help you*."}]}, "Isaiah 41:8–10": {"title": "\"Abraham My friend\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four titles handed out in three lines, and then five promises in a row."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The offspring of **Abraham My friend**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *Abraham who loved Me* or *My beloved* — the participle works both ways, and both readings have been held."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is said of nobody else. 2 Chronicles repeats it and James repeats it: *he was called the friend of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What earned it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing that Abraham did. The verse Scripture attaches to the title is the one about believing: *Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness — and he was called the friend of God.* James puts the two clauses in one sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had no law to keep. He lied about his wife twice, took Hagar, and laughed at the promise. And he is the friend. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls Israel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My **servant**… whom I have **chosen**… the **offspring** of Abraham.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chosen, and then the negative that goes with it: *I have chosen you **and not cast you away**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He found them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **took you from the ends of the earth**, I **called you from its farthest corners**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The initiative is entirely His in both verbs. Nobody in the passage came looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five promises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **do not fear**, for I am with you. **Do not be dismayed**, for I am your God. I will **strengthen** you. I will **help** you. I will **uphold** you with My righteous right hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two commands and three undertakings, and each command has a reason attached rather than an exhortation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Do not fear — *for I am with you*. Not because the situation is manageable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The right hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **My righteous right hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the right hand of My righteousness. What holds a man up in this passage is God's own righteousness, not his."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the same thing Isaiah 54 says: *you will be established in righteousness… their righteousness is from Me.* See the note on Isaiah 54:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls them a few verses later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Fear not, **you worm Jacob**, you men of Israel! I will help you,\" says the LORD and your Redeemer, the Holy One of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's own title. And in the next breath: *I will make you into a new threshing sledge with sharp teeth; you shall thresh the mountains and beat them small.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A worm, told it will thresh mountains. The transformation is announced in the same sentence as the insult, and the only thing between them is *I will help you.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is arguing.** It is a courtroom scene — God challenging the idols to predict anything at all. *Show the things that are to come hereafter, that we may know that you are gods.* And in the middle of the trial He turns aside to reassure a frightened people that He has hold of their hand."}]}, "Isaiah 45:22–23": {"title": "\"By My own self I have sworn\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An invitation shouted at the edges of the world, and then an oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turn to Me and be saved, **all the ends of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *turn* is to look, to face toward. The older rendering is *look unto Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same posture as the bronze serpent in the wilderness — a nation dying of snakebite, told to look at something lifted up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole instruction. Not climb, not fetch, not achieve. Turn your face this way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is addressed to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the ends of the earth.* Written to Israel, in exile, and aimed past them at everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I am God, and there is no other.* The chapter says it repeatedly — Isaiah 45 is the most concentrated statement of monotheism in the Old Testament, and it is addressed partly to Cyrus, a Persian king named a century and a half before he was born."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By My own self I have sworn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nobody higher to swear by. Hebrews makes the point: *because He could swear by no one greater, He swore by Himself.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was sworn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My mouth has spoken **righteousness**, a word that **will not come back empty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that it will not return — the same idea as chapter 55: *my word shall not return to Me void, but it shall accomplish what I please.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness is what the sworn word is about, and it is spoken out of His own mouth rather than required from anybody's hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before Me **every knee will bow**, by Me **every tongue will swear**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it twice — in Romans 14, of the judgement seat, and in Philippians 2, where it is the climax of the passage about Christ emptying Himself:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That at the name of Jesus every knee should bow… and every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes a verse where God swears by Himself and applies it, without comment, to Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knee and the tongue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the second clause is that every tongue will *swear* — will make its own declaration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two go together in the verse: a knee bent and a mouth open. And the next verse in Isaiah is what the mouth says: *surely in the LORD I have righteousness and strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in myself. In Him, and said out loud. See the note on Romans 10:5–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes right after.** *In the LORD all the descendants of Israel shall be justified, and shall glory.* Justified — the same word Paul builds Romans on, in a prophet writing seven centuries earlier."}]}, "Isaiah 48:17–18": {"title": "\"Teaches you what is best for you\" — and the sigh that follows", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the second one changes the tone of the first completely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God calls Himself here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your Redeemer, the Holy One of Israel* — and then two working descriptions:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**who teaches you what is best for you** — the Hebrew is the verb for profiting or benefiting. Teaching aimed at the pupil's advantage.", "**who leads you in the way you should go**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are stated as ongoing character, not as offers. This is what He is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the sigh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If only you had listened to My commands, your peace would have flowed like a river, your righteousness like the waves of the sea.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not a threat. It is regret — the kind of sentence a parent says looking at a situation that did not have to happen. And it is spoken to a people already in exile, already living with the consequences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The images are worth noticing: a river, and sea waves. Both are pictures of something that keeps coming. Not a reservoir that could run out — a supply with a source behind it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read on.** The very next verses do not end in the sigh. God goes straight to bringing them out, telling them to leave Babylon with a shout, and reminding them He led people through the desert without letting them go thirsty. The regret is real and it does not get the last word."}]}, "Isaiah 49:22–26": {"title": "\"I Myself will save your children\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The passage answers a question that is put in the middle of it, and the question is a good one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection, raised inside the text"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Can plunder be taken from a warrior? Can captives be rescued from the grip of the strong?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The expected answer is no. That is the point of asking — it is the objection of someone looking at a situation and concluding that whatever has hold of it is stronger than anything likely to intervene."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah puts the doubt in the text rather than talking past it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yes, even the captives of the warrior will be taken back… I Myself will fight those who fight you, and I Myself will save your children.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The emphatic pronoun appears twice. Not *your children will be saved* — **I Myself**. The rescue is claimed personally, by name, against a stronger captor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The imagery at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make your oppressors eat their own flesh. They will be drunk on their own blood as if it were sweet wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is violent, and it should not be trimmed. Isaiah is writing about empires that took children into captivity, and the language of judgement in the prophets is deliberately unbearable — it is meant to sound like what it describes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The setting.** This whole chapter is addressed to people who had concluded God had forgotten them; a few verses earlier is the famous *can a mother forget the child at her breast?* The promise about children is the answer to a complaint about abandonment, which is why it is stated in the first person twice over."}]}, "Isaiah 52:13–15": {"title": "\"Disfigured beyond that of any man\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth Servant Song begins here, three verses before the chapter break, and it opens with the ending."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will be raised up and lifted high and greatly exalted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs of exaltation, stacked, **before** a word about suffering. Isaiah gives away the outcome first and then tells you what it cost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three-fold lifting language is used elsewhere in Isaiah of God Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disfigurement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His appearance was disfigured beyond that of any man, his form beyond human likeness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is stronger than most translations manage — a marring so severe that what people were looking at no longer registered as a man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah wrote this centuries before crucifixion existed as a Roman practice. What he describes is a body damaged past recognition, in front of appalled onlookers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So he will startle many nations\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb has been debated for a long time. It can mean to sprinkle — the word used of a priest sprinkling blood — or to startle, to make them recoil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parallel in the sentence favours the second: *just as many were appalled at him… so he will startle many nations.* One reaction answering another. But the sprinkling sense sits underneath, and readers have heard both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Kings with nothing to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Kings will shut their mouths because of him. For what they were never told, they will see, and what they never heard, they will understand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The silence is not respect. It is people with authority having no reply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason given is that something arrives outside the report — not passed down, not heard about. Seen."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it goes next.** *Who has believed our message?* — the opening line of chapter 53. The song moves straight from kings shutting their mouths to the complaint that nobody believed it. Both are true of the same figure, and Paul quotes the second line in Romans 10."}]}, "Isaiah 53:10–12": {"title": "\"He will see the light and be satisfied\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last three verses of the song, and they are the only ones with a future in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hardest line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **it was the LORD's will to crush him** and cause him to suffer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it pleased the LORD to bruise him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has spent the chapter saying the servant was despised by men, rejected by men, oppressed and afflicted — and then names who was behind it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And though the LORD makes **his life an offering for guilt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **asham** — the guilt offering, the one in Leviticus 5 brought when restitution had to be made as well as atonement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the five offerings, this is the one about repayment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this is future tense, and all of it is gain:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*He will **see his offspring***", "*and **prolong his days***", "*and **the will of the LORD will prosper in his hand***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who has just been described as cut off out of the land of the living, without descendants, sees offspring and long days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Prosper\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsalach** — to advance, to succeed, to push through. The same word used of Joseph in Potiphar's house. See the note on Genesis 39:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The satisfaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **the anguish of his soul**, he will **see the light and be satisfied**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the travail of his soul*. **Satisfied** is **saba** — full, having had enough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something is on the far side of it that is worth what it cost, and He looks at it and is content."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the justifying works"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **his knowledge** my righteous servant **will justify many**, and **he will bear their iniquities**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves in one line. He justifies, and He bears — the second is how the first happens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Justify** is the legal word: to declare in the right. It is not the same as making somebody better."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The division"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore I will **give him a portion among the great**, and he will **divide the spoils with the strong**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Conqueror's language — the general dividing the plunder after the battle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four reasons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew gives four clauses, each stating why:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*because he **poured out his life even to death***", "*and was **counted in among the lawbreakers***", "*for he **bore the sin of many***", "*and **made intercession for the transgressors***"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I say to you that this which is written must still be accomplished in Me: **'And He was numbered with the transgressors.'** For the things concerning Me have an end.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes verse 12 about Himself, on the night of the arrest, and the next day two criminals are crucified either side of Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **made intercession for the transgressors**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's final word about the servant is not the death. It is the praying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records it from the cross: *Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they do.* And Hebrews puts it in the present tense: *He always lives to make intercession for them.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter ends up.** It opens *who has believed our report?* and closes with spoils divided and a man satisfied. The turn happens at the guilt offering."}]}, "Isaiah 53:4–6": {"title": "The verbs of exchange", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six lines, and almost every one of them is a transfer: something that was ours, moved onto him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What moves, and where it goes"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**took up** our pain — the Hebrew *nasa*, to lift and carry away", "**carried** our sorrows — *sabal*, to bear a load on the back", "pierced **for** our transgressions", "crushed **for** our iniquities", "the punishment that brought **us** peace was laid **upon him**", "the LORD has laid on him the guilt of **us all**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage is described as being fixed in place. Everything is moved from one party to another. That is the grammar of substitution, and it is why this chapter is quoted more often in the New Testament than almost any other in the prophets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet we thought him stricken by God, struck down by Him, and afflicted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the confession of the people speaking. They saw a man suffering and drew the ordinary conclusion — that God must be against him. They were exactly wrong, and the chapter is written from the far side of finding out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is worth remembering the next time suffering is read as a verdict."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"By his wounds we are healed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew *rapha* is the ordinary word for healing, used of bodies and of nations and of God healing a people's turning away. It is broad, and Isaiah does not narrow it here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the sentence does specify is the mechanism: the healing is *by his wounds*. Whatever is being healed, it is being healed at his cost and not at ours — which is the argument of the whole chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *We all, like sheep, have wandered off. Each of us has turned to his own way.* Note the two halves: a herd going astray together, and every single one going a different direction. Both are true at once, and the chapter puts the collective and the individual in the same sentence before laying all of it on one man."}]}, "Isaiah 54:1–3": {"title": "\"Make the tent wider\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens by telling a barren woman to sing, and then to build for children she does not have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sing, **you who never held a child**. Break into song and shout for joy, **you who never knew the pains of birth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that world this was the worst thing that could be said about a woman, and the chapter opens by naming it twice and then commanding singing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The singing comes first. Before anything has changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54 follows Isaiah 53 — *wounded for our transgressions… by His stripes we are healed… He shall see His seed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The barren woman sings because of what happened in the chapter before. The Servant's suffering is what produces her children."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **make the tent wider. Stretch out the curtains. Do not hold back. Lengthen your ropes and drive your stakes down deep.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five commands, and they are all construction work on an empty tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is told to prepare accommodation before there is anyone to accommodate — to buy the cot, in effect, while still childless."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lengthen your ropes* — the guy lines go further out, because the tent is getting bigger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Drive your stakes down deep* — and the Hebrew is *strengthen your stakes*. A wider tent takes more wind; the anchoring has to go deeper as the span increases."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both at once. Wider and better anchored, not one or the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will spread out to the right and to the left.** Your children will take over the nations and settle the emptied cities.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *spread out* is to break forth, to burst through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage tells her to produce a child, or to try. The enlargement is what she does; the children are what God does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the woman left alone will have more children than the woman with a husband, says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Further down: *you will be established in righteousness… their righteousness is from Me, says the LORD.* See the note on Isaiah 54:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the guarantee He gives for the whole thing is a rainbow: *as I have sworn that the waters of Noah would no longer cover the earth, so have I sworn that I would not be angry with you.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Paul says this is about.** He quotes verse 1 in Galatians 4, of *the Jerusalem above, which is the mother of us all* — the covenant of promise rather than the covenant of law. The barren woman with more children than the married one is, in his reading, grace outproducing the law."}]}, "Isaiah 54:13–14": {"title": "\"Established in righteousness\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and everything in the second one hangs off a single condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All your children will be **taught by the LORD**, and great will be their peace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *taught of the LORD* — not taught about Him. He is the teacher."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus quotes it in John 6: *it is written in the prophets, \"And they shall all be taught by God.\" Therefore everyone who has heard and learned from the Father comes to Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *great will be their peace* — **shalom**, which is not the absence of noise. Wholeness, soundness, nothing missing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be **established in righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *established* is to be set firm, fixed, made to stand — the word used of a foundation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what a person is established in is named: righteousness. Not effort, not vigilance, not track record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already said whose righteousness it is — verse 17: *this is the heritage of the servants of the LORD, and **their righteousness is from Me**, says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things, all of them consequences of the establishing:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Cruelty will be far from you*", "*you will have nothing to fear*", "*Terror will be far from you, and it will not come near*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *far* is distance — put away, removed to a distance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fear is not managed here. It is relocated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54 is the chapter immediately after Isaiah 53 — the suffering servant, wounded for our transgressions, by whose stripes we are healed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And 54 opens *Sing, O barren, you who have not borne!* The whole chapter is the result of chapter 53, and it is addressed to somebody who had nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For the mountains shall depart and the hills be removed, but **My kindness shall not depart from you**, nor shall My covenant of peace be removed,\" says the LORD, who has mercy on you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He names the guarantee: *this is like the waters of Noah to Me; for as I have sworn that the waters of Noah would no longer cover the earth, so have I sworn that I would not be angry with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He points at a rainbow and says the two promises are the same kind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last verse.** *No weapon formed against you shall prosper, and every tongue which rises against you in judgement you shall condemn.* It is the most quoted line in the chapter, and the sentence it ends on is *their righteousness is from Me.* The verse is anchored to whose righteousness it is."}]}, "Isaiah 54:15–16": {"title": "\"Both are Mine\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses in the middle of a chapter about a marriage, and they are about weapons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54 follows immediately after the suffering servant of chapter 53. It opens *sing, O barren, you who have not borne*, and it is addressed to a woman who has been left."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For your Maker is your husband… for the LORD has called you like a woman forsaken and grieved in spirit.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **anyone attacks you, it will not be My doing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *behold, they shall surely assemble, but not because of Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Attacks are not denied. Their origin is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same clause the chapter's most quoted verse depends on: *no weapon formed against you shall prosper* — formed, which grants that they get made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever comes against you **will fall before you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall fall for your sake*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blacksmith"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, **I created the blacksmith** who **fans the coals into flame** and **forges the weapon for its work**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses about the manufacture: the man, the fire, and the product."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God claims the craftsman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destroyer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **I created the destroyer to lay waste**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the waster to destroy*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Both are Mine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is emphatic. Isaiah has already said it of the whole business: *I form the light and create darkness; I make peace and create calamity; I, the LORD, do all these things.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is not about the weapons failing to exist. It is about who made the man who makes them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse draws the conclusion: *no weapon formed against you shall prosper, and every tongue which rises against you in judgment you shall condemn.* See the note on Isaiah 54:17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reasoning runs: the smith is Mine, so the product is not outside My reach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heritage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is **the heritage of the servants of the LORD**, and **their righteousness is from Me**,\" says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's final sentence, and it ends on where the righteousness comes from rather than on the weapons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised earlier in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For the mountains shall depart and the hills be removed, but **My kindness shall not depart from you**, nor shall My covenant of peace be removed.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**In righteousness you shall be established**; you shall be far from oppression, for you shall not fear.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *established* is **kun** — fixed, set firm. The same word used of the priests standing in the Jordan. See the note on Joshua 3:14–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter promises about children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**All your children shall be taught by the LORD**, and great shall be the peace of your children.\"* See the note on Isaiah 54:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus quotes that line in John 6: *it is written in the prophets, \"And they shall all be taught by God.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not claimed.** That nothing is made. Isaiah grants the smith, the fire and the finished weapon, and then says who made the smith."}]}, "Isaiah 54:17": {"title": "\"This is the inheritance\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last verse of the chapter, and the most quoted sentence in it depends on the clause after it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No **weapon** shaped against you will succeed, and every **tongue** that rises to accuse you, you will prove wrong.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A weapon and a tongue. Force and accusation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what is not promised: that the weapon will not be made. The verse before says outright *I have created the blacksmith who blows the coals in the fire and brings forth an instrument for his work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The forging happens. The prospering does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tongue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every tongue that **rises to accuse you**, you will prove wrong.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you shall condemn* — a courtroom verb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only active thing the person does in the whole verse, and it is answering an accusation rather than fighting a weapon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause everything rests on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **the inheritance of the servants of the LORD**. This is their **vindication, and it comes from Me**, says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *vindication* is **tsedaqah** — righteousness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last three words are the ones that hold the verse up: **from Me**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their righteousness is not theirs. The chapter says so plainly, and the promise about weapons is attached to it as its consequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "An inheritance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nachalah** — the family portion, what you receive because of who you belong to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody earns an inheritance, and nobody keeps it by performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter has come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54 follows Isaiah 53 — *He was wounded for our transgressions… by His stripes we are healed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens by telling a barren woman to sing, and to enlarge her tent for children she does not have. See the note on Isaiah 54:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *you will be **established in righteousness**… terror will be far from you.* See the note on Isaiah 54:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the guarantee in the middle: *as I have sworn that the waters of Noah would no longer cover the earth, **so have I sworn that I would not be angry with you**.* See the note on Isaiah 54:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A rainbow, offered as collateral."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sing. Enlarge. Be established in righteousness. And then, last: no weapon will prosper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famous line is the conclusion, not the premise."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the servants have.** Not immunity from being shot at. An inheritance, and a righteousness that came from somewhere else, and a promise about outcomes rather than about weather."}]}, "Isaiah 54:9–10": {"title": "The oath God swears about His own anger", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God does not often swear oaths. When He does, it is worth stopping to see what He binds Himself to — and here He binds Himself twice, on the same terms, about two different things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison is doing the work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is like the days of Noah to Me. I swore then that the waters would never again cover the earth. So now I swear that I will not be angry with you or rebuke you again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs through the flood. After it, God gave a sign in the sky and a promise that took the form of a permanent renunciation: never again by water. Everyone reading Isaiah knew that promise had held for centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the second oath is measured against the first. *That* is how settled this is. Whatever you believe about the reliability of the rainbow, believe this on the same footing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being sworn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that nothing hard will happen. The oath is specifically about **God's disposition**: I will not be angry with you or rebuke you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a strange thing for God to promise a people whose failures the same book catalogues at length. Isaiah is not saying they became blameless. He is saying something changed on God's side about how He will deal with them — and the chapter before this one is the reason, which is why 53 and 54 sit next to each other."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though the mountains walk away and the hills come loose and fall, My love for you will never walk away, and My covenant of peace will never come loose.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is deliberately absurd. Mountains are the standing metaphor for permanence in Hebrew poetry — the oldest, heaviest, least movable things anyone could point at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says: suppose those went. Suppose the most fixed thing you can see got up and left. Even then. He picks the strongest available comparison and then says this outlasts it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens by addressing a barren woman, a widow, someone abandoned and ashamed. That is the audience being sworn to. Not a people at their best — a people at their least deserving of a promise about God's permanent good will."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"covenant of peace.\"** *Shalom* is wider than the absence of conflict; it is wholeness, everything in its right place. The word God attaches to His oath is not \"truce.\" It is a settled state of things that He is committing Himself to maintain."}]}, "Isaiah 55:1–3": {"title": "\"Come, buy with no money\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An invitation shouted like a market trader's cry, and the price is the strangest thing about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come, all you who are **thirsty**… and you who **have no money**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two qualifications, and both are lacks. Thirst, and an empty purse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is required except the absence of what would normally be required."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The impossible instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come, **buy** and eat! Come, **buy wine and milk, with no money and no cost**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Buy, with nothing to buy with. The verb is the ordinary word for a commercial purchase, and Isaiah puts it next to *without money and without price*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is deliberately absurd. There is a transaction and there is no payment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water, wine, milk, and *the richest food* — the Hebrew is *fatness*, the choicest part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not rations. Wine and milk are what you serve at a feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why **spend your silver on what is not bread**, and your labour on **what never satisfies**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two currencies wasted: money and work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah does not say the things bought are evil. He says they are *not bread* — they do not do what food does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes ran the same experiment at length and reported the same finding. See the note on Ecclesiastes 3:11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen well… **eat what is good**… let your soul delight… **Turn your ear and come to Me. Listen, and your soul will live.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Listen** three times in two verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *incline your ear* — the Hebrew is to stretch the ear toward, to lean in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the listening produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen, and **your soul will live**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Life, in exchange for attention."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make **an everlasting covenant** with you, **the sure mercies promised to David**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the faithful, dependable mercies — **chasdei David**, the covenant loyalties sworn to David in 2 Samuel 7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes exactly this phrase at Antioch, and applies it to the resurrection: *God has fulfilled this for us… \"I will give you the sure mercies of David.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53 is the suffering servant. Isaiah 54 is the barren woman told to sing and enlarge her tent. Isaiah 55 is the free invitation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters in sequence: a price paid, a household enlarged, and a meal offered at no cost. See the note on Isaiah 54:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My word… shall not return to Me void, but it shall accomplish what I please.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *the mountains and the hills shall break forth into singing before you, and all the trees of the field shall clap their hands.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last word of the invitation.** *Seek the LORD **while He may be found**, call upon Him **while He is near**.* The offer is free and it is not indefinite — and Paul says the same in his own words: *now is the accepted time; now is the day of salvation.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 6:1–2."}]}, "Isaiah 55:10–11": {"title": "\"It will not come back to Me empty\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A comparison between rain and speech, and the comparison does more work than it first appears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What rain does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rain and snow come down from the sky and do not return there until they have soaked the earth, making it bud and bloom, giving seed to the sower and bread to the eater.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah traces the whole cycle: down, into the ground, up as a plant, out as seed and as bread. Nothing about it is instant, and nothing about it is wasted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rain does not look like bread on the day it falls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So it is with the word that goes out from My mouth. It will not come back to Me empty. It will do what I intend and finish the work I sent it to do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims: it will not return empty, and it will complete its errand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *empty* means without result, having achieved nothing — the same word used of coming back from somewhere with nothing in your hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The part the image supplies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rain that soaks into ground and disappears has not failed. It is at the beginning of its work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the comparison is for. A word that has been spoken and appears to have vanished is described here as being in the middle of doing something, on a timescale the passage has just spelled out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this are the famous ones — *my thoughts are not your thoughts, neither are your ways my ways.* And the invitation at the top of the chapter: *come, all who are thirsty… without money and without cost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the passage about a word that finishes what it was sent for sits inside an offer of something free."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The chapter's last image.** *You will go out in joy and be led forth in peace; the mountains and hills will burst into song, and all the trees of the field will clap their hands.* Isaiah keeps the agriculture running to the end — what the rain produces, in his telling, is a harvest that celebrates."}]}, "Isaiah 55:12–13": {"title": "\"Where the thornbush grew, the cypress will rise\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last two verses of the chapter, and they are the harvest of everything in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two exchanges"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where the thornbush grew, the cypress will rise. Where the briar grew, the myrtle will stand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thorns and briars are the first curse. When the ground was cursed in Genesis 3, what it produced was *thorns and thistles* — and they have stood for that ruin ever since."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cypress and myrtle are the opposite kind of plant: tall evergreen timber, and a fragrant shrub used at festivals. Neither is a crop. Both are things you would plant because they last and because they are good to be near."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exchange is not thorn-for-wheat. It is thorn-for-something-permanent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they grow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where the thornbush grew.* The same ground. Nothing is relocated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the specific promise: the site of the curse becomes the site of the cypress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The going out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will go out in joy and be led home in peace. The mountains and the hills will burst into song before you, and all the trees of the field will clap their hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written to exiles, so *go out* means leaving Babylon. The landscape is described as celebrating the road home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This will be a name for the LORD, a sign that lasts forever and is never cut off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trees are a monument. What the exchange is *for* is a name — a permanent, visible marker of who did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *cut off* is the word used of felling a tree, which is a deliberate touch in a sentence about trees."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the crown of thorns fits.** The Gospels record it without comment. Readers have long noticed that the first curse on the ground ends up pressed onto a head — and Isaiah, four chapters after his Servant is pierced, ends with thorn ground growing cypress."}]}, "Isaiah 55:8–9": {"title": "\"Higher than your ways\" — what the comparison is about", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The verse gets quoted at funerals and after disappointments, as a way of saying God's reasons are beyond us. Read in its own paragraph it is doing something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the wicked forsake his way, and the unrighteous man his thoughts; let him return to the LORD, and He will have mercy on him, and to our God, for **He will abundantly pardon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *for My thoughts are not your thoughts, and your ways are not My ways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *for* connects them. The subject of the comparison is the pardoning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Human beings forgive on a scale — a bit, grudgingly, up to a point, with conditions. God's forgiving is described as operating at a different altitude altogether."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *abundantly pardon* is emphatic: He will multiply to pardon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sentence is not primarily about God's mysterious purposes in suffering. It is about how far past our instincts His mercy runs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As high as the heavens are above the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The largest distance available to an ancient writer, and Isaiah uses it twice elsewhere for God's steadfast love and for the removal of sins — *as far as the east is from the west.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this reading matters pastorally"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted as a general statement about inscrutability, the verse can be used to shut down a grieving person's questions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as it stands, it says something warmer: whatever you would do with someone who had wronged you, His response is higher than that — and the gap is the size of the sky."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter opens.** *Come, all you who are thirsty… come, buy and eat, without money and without cost.* The whole chapter is an invitation to receive something free, and the verse about higher ways sits in the middle of it. See also the note on Isaiah 55:10–11."}]}, "Isaiah 58:8–9a": {"title": "\"Your healing will come quickly\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four promises in a row, and the chapter is careful about what they are attached to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Do this\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promises begin with a condition, and the chapter has just spelled it out at length. Israel had been fasting and complaining that God took no notice. His answer describes the fast He actually wants:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "loose the chains of injustice, share your bread with the hungry, bring the homeless poor into your house, clothe the naked, do not hide from your own flesh and blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do this*, and then these four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**your light will break forth like the dawn** — sudden, and unstoppable once begun", "**your healing will come quickly** — the Hebrew word is about new flesh sprouting over a wound; it is a recovery word, used of a nation's restoration and of a body's", "**your goodness will walk ahead of you**", "**the glory of the LORD will be your rear guard**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The formation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last two are military. Something goes in front and something covers the back — the same picture as the exodus, where the pillar of cloud moved from before Israel to behind them, between the camp and Egypt's army."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A person in that formation is covered at both ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then you will call, and the LORD will answer. You will cry out, and He will say, \"Here I am.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase is the same one Abraham and Moses and Samuel say to God — *hinneni*, here I am. Here it comes back the other way, from God to the person calling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the condition matters.** The chapter is not describing a technique for getting light and healing. It is God telling a religious people that the observance they were proud of had left the hungry hungry — and that the breakthrough they wanted was on the other side of feeding them."}]}, "Isaiah 59:18–20": {"title": "\"The Redeemer will come to Zion\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of a chapter about a nation with nothing to offer, and God supplies the whole answer Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your iniquities have separated you from your God… your hands are defiled with blood… we grope for the wall like the blind… we look for justice, but there is none.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, at verse 16: *He saw that there was no man, and **wondered that there was no intercessor**; therefore **His own arm brought salvation** for Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody was found, so He did it Himself. That is the setting for these verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repayment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **repay according to what has been done**, wrath to His foes and just return to His enemies.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the west**, people will fear the name of the LORD, and from **the east**, His glory.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *from the going down of the sun* and *from the rising*. Both directions, which is the Hebrew way of saying everywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famous line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **the enemy comes rushing in like a flood**, **the Spirit of the LORD will lift up a banner against him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew here is genuinely difficult, and translations divide over the punctuation. Some read it as the enemy coming in like a flood; others as *the Spirit of the LORD will lift up a standard against him* when *He shall come like a rushing stream*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the two elements are a flood and a raised banner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *banner* is **nes** — a standard, a signal pole. It is the word Moses used after Amalek: *the LORD is my Banner* — **Jehovah Nissi**. See the note on Exodus 17:8–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A standard was raised so scattered troops could see where to rally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Redeemer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Redeemer will come to Zion**,\" says the LORD, \"**to those in Jacob who turn away from their sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **goel** — the kinsman-redeemer, the relative obliged to buy back what a family member had lost. Boaz's role in Ruth, and Job's word: *I know that my Redeemer lives.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 11, at the end of three chapters about Israel: *\"**The Deliverer will come out of Zion, and He will turn away ungodliness from Jacob**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the Greek version, which has the Redeemer doing the turning rather than the people. See the note on Romans 11:5–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in Isaiah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for Me,\" says the LORD, \"**this is My covenant with them: My Spirit who is upon you, and My words which I have put in your mouth, shall not depart** from your mouth… from this time and forevermore.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then chapter 60 opens: *Arise, shine, for your light has come.* See the note on Isaiah 60:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The armour underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 17, between the *no man* and this passage: *for **He put on righteousness as a breastplate, and a helmet of salvation on His head**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the same pieces and puts them on the reader in Ephesians 6. See the note on Ephesians 6:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Isaiah, God wears it because nobody else was found."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter is built.** A list of what the people cannot do, a note that no intercessor could be found, and then everything from that point on is God's own arm, God's own armour, and a Redeemer coming to Zion."}]}, "Isaiah 59:21": {"title": "\"My Spirit, and My words\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is God stating the terms of an agreement in His own voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the end of the chapter about a nation with nothing to offer — *your iniquities have separated you*, *we grope for the wall like the blind* — and after the line that turns it: *He saw that there was no man, and wondered that there was no intercessor; therefore **His own arm brought salvation**.* See the note on Isaiah 59:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Redeemer comes to Zion in verse 20. This is the next sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **this is My covenant with them**,\" says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls it a covenant, and He is the only party who does anything in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is asked. There are no conditions in the verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My Spirit**, which rests on you, and **My words**, which I have put in your mouth.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are possessives — **My** Spirit, **My** words. Neither originates with the person holding them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Which I have put in your mouth** — the Hebrew is *which I have placed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah's call has the same picture: *then the LORD put forth His hand and touched my mouth, and the LORD said to me, \"Behold, **I have put My words in your mouth**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised about them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Will not leave your mouth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall not depart out of your mouth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three generations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Nor the mouths of **your children**, nor the mouths of **their children**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three generations named, and then:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**From now on and forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other \"shall not depart from your mouth\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua 1: *this Book of the Law **shall not depart from your mouth**, but you shall meditate in it day and night.* See the note on Joshua 1:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There it is an instruction to Joshua. Here it is a promise from God about what He has put there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next verse is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Arise, shine, for **your light has come**, and the glory of the LORD is risen upon you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 60 opens on it, with no break — the chapter division is a later addition. See the note on Isaiah 60:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant of Spirit and words is followed immediately by the light rising."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul quotes the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 11, at the end of three chapters about Israel: *\"The Deliverer will come out of Zion, and He will turn away ungodliness from Jacob. **For this is My covenant with them, when I take away their sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes verse 20 and the opening of verse 21 together, and completes it from Jeremiah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant Jeremiah described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will put My laws inside their hearts, and I will write them on their minds… and their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more.* See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word — covenant — and the same direction: God putting something inside a person."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is in the covenant.** Not obligations. A Spirit that rests and words that stay, both described as His, and a duration of three generations and then *forever*."}]}, "Isaiah 60:1–3": {"title": "Arise and shine — with a light that is not yours", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two imperatives, and then the reason for both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Arise. Shine. **Your light has come**, and the glory of the LORD rises upon you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commands come first and the reason second, and the reason is not an instruction to generate anything. The light *has come*. The glory *rises upon* you — the verb is the one used of the sun coming up over a horizon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is being told to produce light. They are being told to get up, because something has risen on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The darkness is not denied"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, darkness covers the earth, thick darkness over the peoples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second word is heavier than the first — a deep, enveloping dark. Isaiah puts it right between the command to shine and the promise of nations coming, and does not soften it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is the point: *but the LORD rises upon you*. Both things are true at once, and the light is not described as ending the darkness elsewhere. It is described as landing here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who ends up looking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nations will come to your light, and kings to the brightness of your dawn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The light attracts. That is the whole mechanism given — no campaign, no persuasion. Something is lit in a dark landscape, and people walk toward it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it lands in the New Testament.** Jesus tells a hillside crowd *you are the light of the world* and *a town built on a hill cannot be hidden* — the same picture of light in a dark place drawing eyes to it. Both passages put the attention that follows on someone else: nations coming to *your* light, and people giving glory to *your Father*. See the note on Matthew 5:13–16."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What arrives once the light is up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter does not stop at the dawn. The next two verses tell the city to look around at who is walking toward it — sons from far away, daughters carried close and safe, and then the wealth of the sea and the riches of nations streaming in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is worth holding on to. The light comes first and it is not the city's own; then people come to it; and only then does anything arrive that could be counted. Isaiah never presents the traffic as the point — it is the consequence of something having risen. See the note on Isaiah 60:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the darkness is not said to lift everywhere. *Darkness covers the earth, thick darkness over the peoples* stays true in the same breath as *the LORD rises upon you*. Whatever this chapter is promising, it is not a world with the lights back on. It is one lit place in a dark landscape, and a road toward it."}]}, "Isaiah 60:4–5": {"title": "\"Your heart will race and swell\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The light has risen in the verses before this. Now Isaiah tells the city to look up and see who is walking toward it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your sons come from far away, and your daughters are carried close and safe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before any mention of wealth, the returning crowd is family. Isaiah is writing to a people who had watched their children marched off into exile, and the first thing he describes coming back is them — the daughters carried, which is the detail a parent notices."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the wealth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wealth of the sea will be brought to you, and the riches of the nations will come streaming in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for wealth is a broad word — strength, force, resources, army. It can mean a nation's substance in every sense. And *the sea* means overseas trade, which for Judah meant the whole Mediterranean world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is of a city that had nothing becoming the destination everything arrives at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will see it, and your face will glow. Your heart will race and swell with joy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is physical: the heart *trembles and grows wide*. It is the body's response to news too large to take in — the same verb used elsewhere of fear, here turned to joy."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On reading this as a promise of transfer.** The passage has a long history of being applied to personal finances. What it actually describes is a restored city at the centre of a world that had scattered it, and Revelation picks it up that way — the kings of the earth bringing their glory into the new Jerusalem, with the gates never shut. It is worth reading first as the thing it says before it is applied to anything smaller."}]}, "Isaiah 61:1–3": {"title": "Where He stopped reading", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The passage Jesus read out in Nazareth, and the most important thing about it is where He closed the scroll."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of the Lord GOD is on me, because the LORD **has anointed me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mashach** — the root of *Messiah*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five things"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**bring good news to the poor**", "**bind up the brokenhearted**", "**proclaim freedom for the captives**", "**release from darkness for the prisoners**", "**proclaim the year of the LORD's favour**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is a verb of announcement or repair."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The year of favour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the year of the LORD's acceptance*, and it points at the jubilee — every fiftieth year, when debts were released, slaves went free and land returned to the family that lost it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…**and the day of our God's vengeance**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two clauses sit in one sentence: a **year** of favour, and a **day** of vengeance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the synagogue at Nazareth He read from this scroll — and Luke records the exact point He stopped:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To proclaim the acceptable year of the LORD.\" **Then He closed the book**, gave it back to the attendant, and sat down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops mid-sentence, at the comma."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He began to say to them, \"**Today this Scripture is fulfilled in your hearing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Luke 4:16–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The day of vengeance is not deleted. It is left unread, on the same line, in the same breath of the prophet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah wrote them together. Jesus read the first half and said *today*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in Isaiah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To comfort all who mourn… to give them **a crown of beauty instead of ashes**, the **oil of joy instead of mourning**, a **garment of praise instead of a spirit of despair**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three exchanges, each a swap of one thing for another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ashes were what mourners put on their heads. A crown goes where the ashes were."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will be called **oaks of righteousness**, a planting of the LORD, **for the display of His splendour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not saplings. Oaks — the slowest-growing, longest-standing tree in that country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened next in Nazareth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He mentioned two Gentiles — the widow at Zarephath and Naaman the Syrian — and the congregation took Him to the brow of the hill to throw Him off. See the note on Luke 4:28–30."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the scroll would have looked like.** He would have unrolled it to find the place, read aloud standing, rolled it up, handed it back, and sat down — sitting being the posture for teaching. Luke records every step, and the sermon is one sentence long."}]}, "Isaiah 62:1–5": {"title": "Hephzibah and Beulah", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two names are taken away and two are given, and the new ones are the vocabulary of a wedding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal to be quiet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For Zion's sake **I will not keep silent**. For Jerusalem's sake **I will not stay quiet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with God declining to say nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Until what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until her **righteousness shines out like the dawn**, her salvation like a blazing torch.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *shines out* is the ordinary verb for going out — as the sun goes out in the morning. Nothing is added to the dawn to make it bright; it arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who sees it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The nations will see **your righteousness**, and all the kings your glory.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her righteousness, on display to outsiders. The chapter never says she produced it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The new name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be given **a new name**, a name **the mouth of the LORD will choose**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a name she picks. Naming rights belong to whoever gives it, and in Scripture the one who names is the one with authority — Adam over the animals, God over Abram, Jacob, Simon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be **a crown of splendor in the hand of the LORD**, a royal diadem in the palm of your God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not on His head. In His hand — held up, turned in the light, shown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names removed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No longer will they call you **Forsaken**, no longer name your land **Desolate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Azubah** and **Shemamah**. Forsaken is what a wife is called when her husband has left; desolate is what land is called when nobody works it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be called **Hephzibah**, My Delight Is in Her, and your land **Beulah**, Married.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chephtsi-bah** — *my delight is in her*. **Beulah** — *married, owned, possessed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah supplies the translations inside the verse, which he rarely does. He wants the meanings heard, not just the sounds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hephzibah was also a real woman's name — Hezekiah's wife, the mother of Manasseh, in 2 Kings. Isaiah's readers knew it as an ordinary name a man gave his wife."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the LORD takes delight in you**, and your land will be joined to Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is given because the delight is already there. The naming reports a fact rather than creating one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wedding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As a young man marries a young woman, so will **your Builder** marry you. As a bridegroom rejoices over his bride, **so will your God rejoice over you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rejoices* is **sus** — to be glad, to exult. It is the word for the groom's face at the wedding, applied to God, with the reader in the bride's place."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is written over.** Jerusalem after the fall — walls down, the land unworked, the people gone. God renames it in the middle of the ruin, and the names He gives are about how He feels rather than how it looks."}]}, "Isaiah 65:21–23": {"title": "\"As the days of a tree\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Part of the picture Isaiah gives of the world made new, and every clause in it is about keeping what you made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For behold, **I create new heavens and a new earth**; and the former shall not be remembered or come to mind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John quotes the phrase at the end of Revelation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The building"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **build houses and dwell in them**. They will **plant vineyards and eat their fruit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two ordinary activities, each with its outcome attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The negative version"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No longer will they **build for another to live in**, or **plant for another to eat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the promise is set against."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy's curses had named it: *you shall build a house and not dwell in it; you shall plant a vineyard and not gather its grapes*. See the note on Deuteronomy 28:58–68."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Israel's history had run that way — invasions, deportations, and other people occupying the houses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **as the days of a tree, so will be the days of My people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Olives and oaks live for centuries. Some of the olives on the Mount of Olives are held to be a thousand years old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah picks the longest-lived thing that grows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The enjoyment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My chosen ones will **long enjoy the work of their hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall long enjoy* — literally *wear out*, in the sense of using something to the full."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The labour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **not labor in vain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **riq** — emptiness, for nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes' complaint answered: *what profit has a man from all his labour in which he toils under the sun?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or **bear children doomed to misfortune**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *bring forth for trouble*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Infant mortality in the ancient world ran near half. The clause is not abstract."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For they will be **a people blessed by the LORD**, **they and their descendants with them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two generations named, as in the covenant language."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wolf and the lamb shall feed together, the lion shall eat straw like the ox… **they shall not hurt nor destroy in all My holy mountain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *it shall come to pass that **before they call, I will answer**; and while they are still speaking, I will hear.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opens with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I was sought by those who did not ask for Me; I was found by those who did not seek Me. I said, '**Here I am, here I am**,' to a nation that was not called by My name.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it in Romans 10 of the Gentiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that ends with houses and vineyards begins with God saying *here I am* to people who were not looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the tree image does elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is like **a tree planted by the water**… **it never stops bearing fruit**.* See the note on Jeremiah 17:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Psalm 92: *those who are planted in the house of the LORD… **shall still bear fruit in old age**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the promise consists of.** Not spectacle. Living in the house you built, eating off the vine you planted, and lasting as long as a tree."}]}, "Isaiah 65:24–25": {"title": "\"Before they call, I will answer\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses about timing, and both of them run ahead of the request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Before they call, I will answer.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer precedes the asking. Not *as soon as*, and not *while* — before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**While they are still speaking, I will hear.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that He hears mid-sentence. There is no interval between the request leaving the mouth and its arriving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two lines together remove any lag at either end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same thing happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel, three chapters into his own vision: *\"At the beginning of your supplications the command went out, and I have come to tell you… for you are greatly beloved.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel says the order was issued when Daniel started praying, and the travel time accounts for the rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Abraham's servant at the well: *before he had finished speaking*, Rebekah came out. See the note on Genesis 24:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The animals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The wolf and the lamb will feed together**, and **the lion will eat straw like the ox**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The predator and the prey at the same trough, and the carnivore on a vegetarian diet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 11 has the same picture at more length — the wolf with the lamb, the leopard with the young goat, a child leading them, and an infant playing at a cobra's hole. See the note on Isaiah 11:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But dust will be the serpent's food.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else in the picture is reconciled. One creature is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 3: *on your belly you shall go, and **you shall eat dust** all the days of your life.* The sentence in the garden is the one thing this passage leaves standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**They will neither harm nor destroy** on all My holy mountain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that they will do no evil and no ruin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 65 opens with *I was sought by those who did not ask for Me; I was found by those who did not seek Me* — which Paul quotes in Romans 10 about the Gentiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle of it: *behold, **I create new heavens and a new earth**, and the former shall not be remembered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation 21 opens with the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is also promised in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They shall not build and another inhabit; they shall not plant and another eat… **they shall not labour in vain**, nor bring forth children for trouble.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same reversal Amos and Psalm 128 describe — a man eating what he grew. See the note on Amos 9:13–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is said about the length of a life.** *No more shall an infant from there live but a few days, nor an old man who has not fulfilled his days… **as the days of a tree, so shall be the days of My people**.*"}]}, "Isaiah 7:14–16": {"title": "\"The virgin will conceive\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A sign given to a frightened king, and a name that carries more than the moment it was spoken into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz, king of Judah, is facing two enemy kings and is about to buy Assyrian protection instead of trusting God. Isaiah offers him a sign — *ask for anything, as deep as the grave or as high as heaven* — and Ahaz refuses, in a show of false piety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sign is given anyway, over his refusal. *Therefore the Lord Himself will give you a sign.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **almah** — a young woman of marriageable age. It is used a handful of times in the Old Testament and never of a married woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two centuries before Christ, Jewish scholars translating into Greek chose **parthenos**, which means virgin specifically. That is the reading Matthew quotes, and it was made by translators with no Christian argument to advance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She will call his name Immanuel* — with us, God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes the verse and then translates the name for his readers, which is how he tells you what he thinks it means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Gospel closes on the same note: *I am with you always, to the very end of the age.* The name at the beginning and the promise at the end are the same claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The near horizon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before the boy knows enough to reject the wrong and choose the right, the land whose two kings you dread will be left empty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign had an immediate function — a timescale for Ahaz, measured in a child's early years. Prophecy in Isaiah often works on two horizons at once, and the near one does not cancel the far one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Ahaz did with it.** He went to Assyria anyway, sent the temple silver as tribute, and got the protection he wanted along with everything that came after. The sign of Immanuel was given to a man who had already decided not to need it."}]}, "James 1:13": {"title": "\"Nobody gets to say, God is doing this to me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James shuts a door, and he shuts it hard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is forbidden"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the pull toward evil comes, **nobody gets to say, \"God is doing this to me.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *let no one say, I am tempted by God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not correcting a misunderstanding gently. He rules the sentence out of the believer's mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it needed saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven verses earlier he told them to count it all joy when they land in trouble — using the noun **peirasmos**. See the note on James 1:2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here he uses the verb from the same root and says God does not do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader who has just been told that trials are worth something might reasonably ask whether God sends the pull toward evil as well. James answers before it is asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things, one word family"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Greek uses **peirazo** for both testing and enticing. English separates them into *trial* and *temptation*; the Greek does not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James separates them by argument instead. Circumstances that test faith come, and they work endurance. Solicitation to evil is a different thing with a different source, and he names the source in the next verse. See the note on James 1:14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Evil **has no hold on God whatsoever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apeirastos** — untemptable, incapable of being solicited by evil. The word appears once in the New Testament, here, and is rare in all Greek."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nothing in Him for it to fasten to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says the same about the ruler of this world: *he has nothing in Me.* See the note on John 14:28–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He pulls no one toward it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims: nothing pulls Him, and He pulls nobody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James closes it from both sides."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this rules out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oldest instinct in Scripture is to relocate the blame. Adam: *the woman whom You gave to be with me.* The pointing runs past Eve and lands on God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is writing to people under real pressure — scattered, poor, dragged into court by the rich — and the sentence he takes away from them is the one that would have been easiest to say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say the pressure is imaginary, or that suffering has no purpose, or that God is absent from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim is narrow and total: God is not the one doing the enticing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows the whole paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above, and comes down from the Father of lights, **with whom there is no variation or shadow of turning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses later, and it is his closing argument. The one who cannot be pulled toward evil is also the one who does not change."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape of the paragraph.** James removes one explanation, supplies the true one in the next verse, and then says where the good things come from instead."}]}, "James 1:14": {"title": "\"Here is what actually happens\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Having ruled out one explanation, James gives the real one in a single sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here is what actually happens.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *but each one is tempted…* — and the **each one** matters. It is not a description of humanity in general but of one person at a time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The previous verse removed God from the account. See the note on James 1:13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your **own** craving.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epithumia** — desire, longing, appetite. The word is not automatically negative; Jesus uses it of Himself: *with fervent desire I have desired to eat this Passover with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What makes it lethal here is the possessive. **His own.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hauls you off and **baits you in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are hunting and fishing words, and James chose them as a pair:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Exelko** — to drag out, to draw away. It is what you do to a fish, pulling it out of cover.", "**Deleazo** — from **delear**, bait. To lure with something on a hook."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is deliberate. First drawn out of position, then baited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No devil, and no God. James is at his most stripped-down here — he mentions the devil elsewhere in the letter (*resist the devil and he will flee from you*), but not in this diagnosis."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mechanism he describes needs no external agent at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, when desire has conceived, **it gives birth to sin**; and sin, when it is full-grown, **brings forth death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He switches metaphors mid-paragraph, from fishing to childbirth, and lays out a sequence: desire, conception, sin, maturity, death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chain is instantaneous. Every stage takes time, and each one is the parent of the next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The point of describing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is not writing a psychology. He is writing so that the thing can be recognised at the first stage rather than the last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse after the sequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be deceived, my beloved brethren.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, immediately, the good gifts and the Father of lights."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts the two accounts side by side: what comes up out of a man, and what comes down from above."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he uses again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Epithumia** turns up in the letter's fourth chapter: *you lust and do not have… you ask and do not receive, because you ask amiss, that you may spend it on your pleasures.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same root. James's diagnosis is consistent from one end of the letter to the other."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is honest about it.** The bait works because it is wanted. James does not pretend otherwise — he says the pull comes from the person's own appetite, and calls it theirs."}]}, "James 1:17": {"title": "\"He never swings around to throw a shadow\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James reaches for astronomy to say something about God's character, and the metaphor is doing precise work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim about gifts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anything good that ever came into your hands, anything that reached you whole and unspoiled, came down from up there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Greek — every good *giving* and every perfect *gift*. The act and the thing given. Both are traced to one source."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction is stated: **down from above**, from the Father of lights."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why lights"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the One who made every light burning in the sky.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heavenly bodies were the ancient world's picture of reliability — and also of variation. The sun moves through the year, the moon waxes and wanes, constellations rise and set. Everything in the sky changes by turning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James names God as the maker of all of them and then says the thing that separates Him from what He made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He never dims, never shifts, never swings around to throw a shadow He was not throwing before.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**no variation** — the astronomical word for change or alteration", "**no shadow cast by turning** — the word is *trope*, the root of *tropic*, used of the solstice, the point where the sun turns back"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything that gives light in the sky produces shadow as it moves. God does not turn, so there is no side of Him that is dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why James says it here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at the verse before: *do not be deceived, my beloved brothers.* He has just been saying that God tempts nobody, and that people are drawn away by their own desire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the correction to a suspicion — that God might be behind the trouble, or might have a colder side that shows sometimes. James says the light is unvarying, and everything good that ever reached you came from it."}]}, "James 1:2": {"title": "\"When you land in the middle of trouble\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first instruction in the letter, and it is about arithmetic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "***Count it** nothing but joy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hegeomai** — to reckon, to consider, to lead the mind to a conclusion. It is an accounting word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James does not say *feel* joy. He says work it out and put it in that column."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Nothing but\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Count it **nothing but joy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *all joy* — **pasan charan**. Not partly, not on balance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The landing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you **land in the middle of trouble**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peripipto** — to fall around, to fall among. It is the word Luke uses of the man on the Jericho road who *fell among thieves*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not walk into it deliberately. It happens on the way somewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"When\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *if*. James assumes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Every kind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every kind of it there is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poikilos** — many-coloured, variegated, of every shade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the identical word two verses apart in his letter: *though now for a little while you have been grieved by **various** trials*, and then *stewards of the **manifold** grace of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same adjective for the trouble and for the grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for trouble"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Peirasmos** — a trial, a test, an examination. It is also the word in *lead us not into temptation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James uses it here about circumstances that come at a person from outside, and eleven verses later he uses the related verb about being drawn toward evil — and says flatly that God does not do that. See the notes on James 1:13 and James 1:14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word family, two different things, deliberately separated inside one chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he can say it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because of the next verse, which starts *you know why*. See the note on James 1:3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joy is not asked for in the abstract. It is the conclusion of an argument James is about to make."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*James, a bondservant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ* — the Lord's brother, who did not believe in Him during His life, and who led the Jerusalem church through the persecutions in Acts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing to *the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad* — refugees."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not being asked for.** Not that the trouble be enjoyed, and not that it be called good. James asks for a calculation, and gives the working in the next sentence."}]}, "James 1:21": {"title": "What \"saved\" actually covers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Our text says the message has power to save your souls. Both of those words — saved and souls — are wider in Greek than they have become in English."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same word, in this same letter, means healed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is sozo. James uses it again at the end of his letter: the prayer offered in faith will sozo the sick man. Same verb. There it plainly means made well — a man getting up off his bed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So inside one short letter James uses this one word for the rescue of a person's whole life, and for a sick man being restored. That is not a stretch or a clever reading. It is simply how he uses it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "It is a rescue word, not a religious word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sozo means to bring someone safely through. It is used of a drowning man being pulled out, of a ship that survives a storm, of someone brought out of danger in one piece. Whatever the threat is, sozo is coming through it intact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "English saved has narrowed to mean one thing: not going to hell. The Greek word never narrowed like that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And \"souls\" is wider too"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is psyche. In English, soul makes us think only of the invisible part that outlives the body. In Greek it means the life, the self, the whole person — you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When Jesus asked what it profits a man to gain the whole world and lose his psyche, He was not talking about one compartment of a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So save your souls is not keep the spiritual part of you out of hell. It is nearer to: rescue the whole of you, and bring you through in one piece."}, {"k": "note", "t": "Do not overreach with this. Sozo genuinely does include eternal salvation — that is not being denied here, and this is not a case of everyone having had the word wrong. The claim that holds is that saved is WIDER than we usually hear it, not that it means something other than we thought. And the width of a word does not by itself promise a particular outcome in a particular case. What it tells you is the size of what God is dealing with: not a compartment of you, but you."}]}, "James 1:3": {"title": "\"You know why\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The reason attached to the instruction in the verse before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You know\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You know why.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ginoskontes** — knowing, being aware. James assumes they already have this; he is reminding, not informing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joy of verse 2 rests on a piece of knowledge, and James supplies it immediately. See the note on James 1:2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The testing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Testing your faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dokimion** — the noun for the process of assaying metal. The related adjective **dokimos** means *proved genuine*, and it is stamped on coinage that has been tested and found to be what it claims."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is about verification rather than damage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Works **endurance** into you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupomone** — from *hupo*, under, and *meno*, to remain. To stay under."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not passive. Older versions render it *patience*, which now sounds like waiting quietly; the Greek is the quality of a soldier holding a position, or a load-bearing wall under weight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is what is tested. Endurance is what comes out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James does not say the trouble produces the faith. The faith was already there — that is what makes the testing possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter says the same"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The genuineness of your faith, **being much more precious than gold that perishes, though it is tested by fire**, may be found to praise, honour and glory.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same word, **dokimion**, and the same picture of the refiner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gold goes into the fire because it is gold. The fire does not make it gold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We also glory in tribulations, knowing that **tribulation produces perseverance; and perseverance, character; and character, hope**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word for perseverance, and the same *knowing* at the front of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three writers, one chain of reasoning, and all three put a knowing before the rejoicing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let endurance have its full effect, that you may be **mature and complete, lacking nothing**.* See the note on James 1:4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, immediately: *if any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives to all liberally and without reproach.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James says *lacking nothing*, and the very next sentence tells them what to do if they lack something."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse does.** It answers the obvious objection to verse 2. James does not ask for joy and leave it there — he says *you know why* and then says why."}]}, "James 1:4": {"title": "\"No piece of you left unfinished\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the two words at the end of it are not about moral perfection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will come out grown up and whole, with no piece of you left unfinished.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**teleios** — brought to its end, complete, mature. Used of a full-grown adult as against a child, and of a thing that has reached what it was for.", "**holokleros** — literally *whole-lot*, having all its parts. It was used of a body with nothing missing, and of a stone fit for building because it had no defect."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither word means sinless. Both mean **nothing lacking**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James then says it a third way in case it was missed: *lacking in nothing*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What produces it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So let that endurance run all the way to the finish.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before names the mechanism — testing produces endurance — and this one says: now let the endurance finish its work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is not to generate anything. It is to let something already underway complete rather than cutting it short."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a person who has suffered and become tougher. A person with no piece missing — the parts that only get built under pressure now present."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why James opens the letter by telling scattered, harassed believers to count trials as joy. Not because the trial is good, but because of what it is in the middle of making."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The next verse.** *If any of you is short on wisdom, ask God.* It follows directly, and the connection is easy to miss: a person in the middle of an unfinished process, told to let it run, is exactly a person who will not understand what is happening. So the immediate provision is wisdom, given generously and without being made to feel small for asking. See the note on James 1:5."}]}, "James 1:5": {"title": "The two words that take the sting out of asking", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One short verse, and two Greek words in it are doing something the English has to work hard to carry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"With both hands\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **haplos**. It means singly, simply, without folds — and by extension, without a hidden second motive. It is the opposite of giving with one hand while keeping an account with the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the promise is not merely that God gives generously in quantity. It is that He gives *straightforwardly*: no strings running back, no ledger being kept, no part of Him withholding while another part hands something over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He never once makes you feel small for asking\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second phrase is **me oneidizontos** — not reproaching. *Oneidizo* is the word for upbraiding somebody, throwing something in their face, shaming them with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James puts it in the sentence because he knows exactly what stops people asking. It is not doubt that God can. It is the suspicion that the asking will be remembered — that the answer will arrive with a comment attached about why you needed it, or how often you have needed it lately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rules that out in four words."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits in his letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes immediately after James has told them to count it joy when they fall into various trials, and that endurance has a work to finish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the wisdom on offer is not academic. It is for people in the middle of something they do not understand, who need to know what to do next. That is when a person is least confident of a welcome, and it is precisely there that James puts the promise about not being shamed for asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no qualification about the asker's state. Not *if any of you is worthy*, not *if you have been faithful*. The condition is **short on wisdom** — the lack itself is the qualification."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the outcome is stated with equal plainness: *it will be put right into your hands*. Not considered. Given."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the verses that follow.** James goes on to talk about asking in faith and not being double-minded, and that is sometimes used to load the promise back up with conditions. Read what he actually describes: a person in two minds about whether to ask God at all, pulled one way and then the other. He is not setting a standard of confidence to reach. He is saying: decide who you are asking, and then ask him."}]}, "James 2:10": {"title": "Trip over one", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence about how the law works as a unit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep **every last piece of the law** and **trip over one**, and you are **answerable for the whole of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *whoever keeps the whole law and stumbles in one point is guilty of all*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ptaio** — to stumble, to trip. Not to abandon or to defy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James picks the mildest available word. A stumble is what happens to somebody who is walking in the right direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Enochos** — liable, answerable, under charge. It is a courtroom word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *has broken all of them*. Answerable for all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it works that way"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse after gives the reason: *for **He who said**, \"Do not commit adultery,\" **also said**, \"Do not murder.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is one lawgiver's word, not a list of separate requirements. Offending at one point is not failing a subsection; it is contradicting the person who spoke all of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James makes the argument about the speaker rather than the statute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is arguing against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The context is favouritism. The chapter opens with a rich man in fine clothes and a poor man in filthy ones walking into the same meeting, and the rich man being given the good seat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James calls that a breach: *if you show partiality, you commit sin, and are convicted by the law as transgressors.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody was evidently treating it as a minor lapse, and this verse is the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The royal law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you really fulfil **the royal law** according to the Scripture, \"You shall love your neighbour as yourself,\" you do well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls it royal — **basilikon**, the king's law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul says the same thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For as many as are of the works of the law are under the curse; for it is written, \"Cursed is everyone who does not continue in **all things** which are written in the book of the law, to do them.\"* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both writers land on the same word. Paul quotes Deuteronomy's *all things*; James says *guilty of all*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are usually set against each other on faith and works, and on this point they are identical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So speak and so do **as those who will be judged by the law of liberty**. For **judgment is without mercy to the one who has shown no mercy**. **Mercy triumphs over judgment**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph that contains the strictest statement of the law's unity ends on that sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *triumphs over* is **katakauchatai** — boasts against, exults over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old illustration is a chain over a pit: it does not matter which link goes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James's own argument is not about links. It is about one voice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How light the offence is in the sentence.** A stumble, at one point, by somebody keeping the rest. James chooses the smallest possible failure and attaches the largest possible liability to it."}]}, "James 2:17": {"title": "Does James contradict Paul?", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the verse people set against Paul. Paul says a person is put right with God through faith and not by works. James says faith without works is dead. Here are seven reasons they are not fighting each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "1. They are answering two different questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is answering: how does a guilty person get right with God? James is answering: how can you tell a living faith from a dead one? Same words, different question. Neither of them has to lose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "2. They are arguing with two different people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's opponent thinks doing good earns you standing with God. James's opponent thinks saying you believe is enough and nothing needs to follow. Neither man is attacking what the other one taught."}, {"k": "h", "t": "3. James tells you exactly which faith he is rejecting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses back, the man SAYS he has faith. It is a claim, not a life. And two verses on, the belief being tested is simply believing God exists — which, as James points out, the demons manage too, and it makes them shake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not attacking trust in God. He is attacking a counterfeit that he carefully describes first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "4. The little word \"that\" — the strongest point of all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek there is a small word, the, which James attaches to faith every single time he points at the counterfeit: 2:14, this verse, 2:20, 2:22 and 2:26. Your text shows it — that faith, this faith. He means the one he has just described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he drops that little word in exactly one place: 2:24, where he stops describing the fake and states the general rule."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So every time he means the counterfeit, the word is there. The one time he states the principle, it is gone. That is grammar, not opinion — it sits in the text whether anyone likes it or not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "5. Abraham's dates settle the order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham believed God and was counted righteous in Genesis 15. He put Isaac on the altar in Genesis 22 — chapters later, years later. James quotes the earlier moment and says the altar filled it full. The standing came first; the deed filled it out. There is more on this at 2:21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "6. Chapter 1 had already settled it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before this argument even begins James has said four things in order: God acted first and nobody talked Him into it (1:18); the message is already planted in you, and it is the message that saves you (1:21); now carry it out (1:22); and you are blessed IN the doing, not paid for it (1:25)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gift comes before the work — twenty verses before anyone mentions works."}, {"k": "h", "t": "7. The whole passage is about showing, not paying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Show me. I will show you. Do you need showing. Look at what happened. You see it now. Over and over. James is asking for evidence that the faith is alive, not for payment to make it count."}, {"k": "note", "t": "One thing not to claim: do not say Paul and James use different words. They use the same ones — faith, works, justified, righteousness, the same Abraham, and the same Old Testament verse about him. That is precisely why this passage has been argued over for centuries. The claim that holds up is not that they used different words, but that they used the same words to answer different questions."}]}, "James 2:21": {"title": "Shown to be right, or made righteous?", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This verse has been argued over for five hundred years. Here is why our text reads shown to be right rather than justified or made righteous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word itself means both"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is dikaioo. It carries two senses, and both are ordinary:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["made right — a verdict, the way a judge declares it", "shown to be right — proof of something that was already true"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither side can win this by pointing at the word. It will not choose between them. So the question has to be settled from the passage, not the dictionary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "James answers it with dates"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things happened in Abraham's life, a long way apart. In Genesis 15 he believed God, and God counted him righteous. In Genesis 22 he put Isaac on the altar. Those are chapters apart, and years apart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now the key move: in the next verse but one, James quotes the earlier moment — the believing — and says the altar filled it full."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So James himself puts the righteousness first and the altar second. If Abraham had been made righteous at the altar, the verse James quotes would be describing something that had not happened yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "A picture for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man passes his exams and is licensed as a doctor. Years later he saves a life in an emergency. Did the emergency make him a doctor? No. He already was one. The emergency showed it, in public, where everyone could see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Abraham. Righteous by believing, back in Genesis 15. The altar showed it, in Genesis 22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three reasons this rendering stands"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Shown to be right is a real, ordinary meaning of the word — nothing invented.", "James never says in front of God or in front of people. Those words are simply not in the chapter. Paul does add that kind of phrase when he writes about Abraham; James does not, and our text stays as quiet as he does.", "The whole passage runs on showing: show me, I will show you, do you need showing, look at what happened, you see it now."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What should not be claimed"}, {"k": "note", "t": "It would be too strong to say the Greek means shown and not made. The word genuinely carries both, and that claim can be knocked down in a moment — taking the good arguments with it. The honest statement is this: the word can mean either; we read it as shown because James's argument runs on showing, because he names no courtroom, and above all because he dates the righteousness to Genesis 15 and calls the altar its filling-full. That is a reading of the passage, not a fact about the word."}]}, "James 2:25": {"title": "Why James picks Rahab second", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James has just used Abraham. Then he reaches for the least likely person in the Old Testament to put beside him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pairing is deliberate"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Abraham** — the father of the nation, the man the whole argument about faith is usually conducted through", "**Rahab** — a Canaanite, a prostitute, an outsider with no covenant standing at all"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "If his point were that respectable people prove their faith by respectable behaviour, the second example destroys it. He chooses the two most distant points on the map and says the same thing happened in both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What her \"work\" was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She took the messengers in and sent them out another road.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire deed. She hid two men and pointed them at the hills. It is not charitable giving or moral improvement — it is one decision, made at real risk, that showed which side she had joined."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Shown to be right\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the same one under discussion throughout the chapter, and its range matters: it can mean *made* right, and it can mean *shown* to be right. Read the question James is asking — how can you tell a living faith from a dead one — and the second sense is the one carrying the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody made Rahab righteous by hiding spies. What she did made visible where she already stood."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**See also.** The note on James 2:17 works through why this is not a contradiction of Paul, and the note on Hebrews 11:30–31 shows the same episode being used as an example of *faith* by a different writer. Two arguments, one woman, one night."}]}, "James 3:17–18": {"title": "How to tell which wisdom you are hearing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James has just described a wisdom that is earthly and produces disorder. Here is the other kind, and he gives it a checklist."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wisdom that comes down from above is **clean, first of all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *first* deliberately — this one leads. The word is about being pure, unmixed, without a hidden agenda."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else on the list is relational, and the purity has to come first because the rest can be counterfeited by someone who simply wants to be liked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the list"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**it makes peace**", "**it goes easy on people** — gentle, considerate, the word for not insisting on the letter of your rights", "**it will hear you out and give ground** — willing to be persuaded, open to reason", "**packed with mercy and with good things that actually grow**", "**it does not play favourites and it never puts on a face**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it as a test and it becomes very practical. Not *is this clever*, but: does it make peace, does it go easy, will it listen, does it produce anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom that is technically correct and leaves wreckage behind it fails the test James sets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harvest line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People who make peace plant their seed in peace, and what comes up out of that ground is a life put right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is agricultural and the sequence matters — the peace is both the soil and the seed. Righteousness is the crop, not the method."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he is contrasting it with.** The verses just above describe wisdom that is earthly, unspiritual, and demonic, marked by bitter envy and selfish ambition — and James says where it shows: *disorder and every evil practice.* Both wisdoms are recognised by what grows out of them, which is the only test he offers for either."}]}, "James 3:6–8": {"title": "\"Nobody has ever broken in the tongue\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James has just used two pictures — a bit in a horse's mouth, a rudder on a ship — about small things steering large ones. Then he stops being gentle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the tongue is that fire… touching off the whole running wheel of your life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase behind *running wheel* is odd in Greek and has been discussed for centuries — something like the wheel of existence, the whole course of a life turning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is of a spark at the axle. Not damage in one place; damage that travels with the wheel wherever it goes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim about taming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People have broken in every sort of creature there is… but nobody has ever broken in the tongue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James surveys the whole animal kingdom — the four categories from Genesis — and says humanity has managed all of them and not this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It will not be held still.* That is a statement about human capacity, and it is absolute. Nobody. Ever."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he says something so bleak"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because the chapter opens by warning that not many should become teachers, since they will be judged more strictly. James is writing to people who talk for a living, and he is refusing to pretend it can be mastered by effort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The honest reading is that this is a diagnosis with no cure offered in the paragraph — which is deliberate. If nobody can tame it, self-improvement is not the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the letter goes for the answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wisdom that comes down from above is clean, first of all. Then: it makes peace, it goes easy on people, it will hear you out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight verses later, and the solution is something that comes **down from above** rather than something worked up. See the note on James 3:17–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul says about the same problem.** *If by the Spirit you go on putting the body's doings to death, you will live* — continuous, and by the Spirit, not by resolve. The two writers agree about the diagnosis and about where the remedy comes from. See the note on Romans 8:12–14."}]}, "James 5:1–3": {"title": "\"The last days are what you piled it up for\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The bluntest paragraph in the New Testament, and it is addressed to a specific group."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now you. **The rich.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the church. James is addressing a class of people from outside it — the next verse names what they did: *you kept back by fraud the wages of the labourers who mowed your fields.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unpaid wages. That is the charge, and it is the same charge Egypt had four hundred years of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three corrosions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your **wealth has gone rotten**. Your good clothes are **full of moth holes**. Your gold and your silver have **gone green with corrosion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three kinds of stored wealth in that world — grain, garments, and metal — and each is described decaying in its own way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gold does not corrode. James says it anyway; the point is not metallurgy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The corrosion as a witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That corrosion is going to **stand up and testify against you**, and eat its way through your flesh like fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rust becomes a witness in court. The evidence against them is produced by the hoard itself, sitting in the dark, doing what unused things do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You piled the stuff up — and **the last days are what you piled it up for**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you have heaped up treasure in the last days*, and it carries both senses: they did the heaping in the last days, and the last days are what the heap is for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pile has a destination that is not theirs. Ecclesiastes said the same: *to the one who misses the mark, He gives the task of gathering and piling up, only to hand it over to the one who pleases God.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 2:24–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James tells the believers to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing aggressive. Four verses later:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be patient, brethren, until the coming of the Lord. See how the farmer waits for the precious fruit of the earth, **waiting patiently for it until it receives the early and latter rain**. You also be patient. **Establish your hearts**, for the coming of the Lord is at hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A farmer, two rains, and a heart established. No scheming, no grasping, no hurry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs: *the stingy man hurries after wealth and never knows that want is coming for him.* See the note on Proverbs 28:21–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wages that cried out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The cries of the reapers have reached the ears of the Lord of Sabaoth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sabaoth** — the LORD of armies. James reaches for the military title in a paragraph about farm wages."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same thing happened in Egypt: *their cry came up to God because of the bondage, and God heard their groaning.* And on the way out, the back pay was settled. See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who James was.** The brother of Jesus, and the leader of the Jerusalem church — writing to Jewish believers scattered by persecution, most of them poor. He does not tell them to organise. He tells them to wait for the rain."}]}, "James 5:10–11": {"title": "\"What the Lord finally made of it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only mention of Job in the New Testament, and it tells you what the whole book was for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who James points at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Think about **the prophets**… take them as your pattern: what they had to take, and how long they took it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not offer them as examples of success. He offers what they had to take, and the length of time they took it for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The ones who hold out\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupomeno** — to remain under. Not to grit the teeth; to stay put under a weight rather than get out from under it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones who hold out under it are the ones we call fortunate.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict on Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have heard how Job held out, and you have seen **what the Lord finally made of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James assumes his readers know the story. What he tells them is where to look in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the boils. Not at the ashes, or the arguments, or the thirty-five chapters of speeches. At **the end** — the Greek is *the end of the Lord*, the outcome the Lord brought it to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the outcome shows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*— that **He feels for people deeply, and He is kind all the way through**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Greek, and the first is one James appears to have coined: **polusplagchnos**, literally *many-boweled*, from the word for the inward parts, where compassion was felt. Very compassionate. It occurs nowhere else in Greek literature before him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is **oiktirmon** — merciful, full of pity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the New Testament's one-sentence summary of the book of Job, and it is not about patience, endurance, or the mystery of suffering. It is a statement about what God is like, drawn from how the story ended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James is telling his readers to conclude"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If you want to know what the Lord is, read to the end of that book. He is deeply compassionate and kind all the way through — and the whole forty-two chapters are offered as evidence for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the middle of it looked like, the end of it is what tells you His character."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The famous phrase.** *The patience of Job* comes from the older English of this verse. The Greek word is not patience but staying under — and James's point is not really about Job at all. It is about what the Lord was, the whole time, and what He turned out to be at the end."}]}, "James 5:13–15": {"title": "\"Let him send for the elders\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three conditions and three responses, and the third one is the longest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is one of you **having a hard time**? Let him **pray**.* *Is one of you **in good spirits**? Let him **sing**.* *Is one of you **sick**? Let him **send for the elders**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every state gets an action, and none of them is to do nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two are things you do alone. The third is the only one that requires somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who sends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **him** send for the elders.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The initiative is the sick man's. Nobody in the passage is told to go looking for the sick."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let them **pray over him** and **put oil on him** in the name of the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two actions, in that order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *pray over* is literally that — praying over the person, and it implies being physically present."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **aleipho**, which is the ordinary word for rubbing oil on — what you do after a bath, or to an athlete, or to a wound."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not **chrio**, the ceremonial anointing word from which *Christ* comes. James uses the everyday one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Olive oil was the standard medical application of that world. The Samaritan poured oil and wine on the man's wounds on the Jericho road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else is happening, the elders are doing something ordinary and physical, in a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What does the healing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the prayer that trusts God** will make the sick man well, and **the Lord** will get him back on his feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the oil, and not the elders. The Greek is *the prayer of faith*, and then the Lord is named as the one who raises him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **if he has committed sins**, he will be forgiven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**If.** James does not assume it, and he does not connect the sickness to the sin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts the forgiveness in a conditional and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the twelve did it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They drove out many demons, and **they rubbed oil on many who were sick and healed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark 6, on the first mission, with no bread, no bag, no money and one stick. See the note on Mark 6:7–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, and it is the only occasion in the Gospels the disciples are recorded using oil."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next in James"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Confess your trespasses to one another, and pray for one another, that you may be healed. **The effective, fervent prayer of a righteous man avails much.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then Elijah, *a man with a nature like ours*, who prayed and it did not rain for three and a half years. See the note on James 5:16–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the letter is like.** James is the most practical book in the New Testament — the tongue, the poor, favouritism, patience, oaths. This paragraph is in character: somebody is ill, so send for the elders, and let them use oil."}]}, "James 5:16–18": {"title": "\"Made of exactly the same stuff we are\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James picks the most spectacular prayer in Israel's history and then goes out of his way to say the man who prayed it was nobody special."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elijah was made of exactly the same stuff we are.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is one word — **homoiopathes**, of like feelings, subject to the same things. Paul and Barnabas use it at Lystra when a crowd tries to sacrifice to them: *we are men of the same nature as you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is heading off the obvious objection before anyone makes it. Yes, but he was Elijah. No — he was not a different kind of creature."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the man James chose is one whose recorded low point is asking God to let him die under a broom tree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Greek word covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Homoiopathes** is built from *homoios*, alike, and *pathos*, feeling, suffering, what happens to a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a statement about ability. It is a statement about what he was subject to — fear, exhaustion, hunger, and the wish to be finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All four are in one chapter of 1 Kings, and James knew it. See the note on 1 Kings 19:1–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He prayed hard that the rain would stop… then he prayed again, and the sky let the rain down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *prayed hard* is a Hebrew-flavoured construction: he prayed with prayer. Not a technique — earnestness, the same thing James means when he says a righteous man's prayer works *when he means it all the way down*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What makes a prayer effective"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When a man who stands right with God prays… it puts real power to work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *stands right* is the righteousness word. In the New Testament that standing is a gift, received rather than achieved — which is what makes this verse usable by ordinary people rather than only by the very good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James has just spent a letter on practical faith, and he lands here: confession to one another, prayer for one another, healing, and an ordinary man's prayer shutting the sky."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the detail comes from.** The three and a half years is not stated in 1 Kings — James supplies it, and Jesus gives the same figure in Luke 4. The length of that drought was evidently well known, and both of them count it the same way."}]}, "Jeremiah 1:17–19": {"title": "\"A fortified city, an iron pillar, a wall of bronze\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the call, and God tells a frightened young man what he has been made into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So gather yourself, **Jeremiah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *gird up your loins* — tie up the long robe to be able to move."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had objected at the start of the chapter: *\"Ah, Lord GOD! **Behold, I cannot speak, for I am a youth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *do not say \"I am a youth,\" for you shall go to all to whom I send you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stand up and **tell them everything I command you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **fall apart before their faces**, or **I will let you fall apart in front of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *do not be dismayed before their faces, lest I dismay you before them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb both times, and it is uncomfortable. The protection is attached to the standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is made"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, **this very day I have made you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense. It is stated as done before he has preached a word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **fortified city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ir mivtsar** — a walled city. The one structure in that world designed to be attacked and not fall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*An **iron pillar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pillar holds a roof. Iron is the strongest metal they had."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **wall of bronze**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bronze does not rust and does not let water through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three images, and every one is defensive rather than offensive. He is not given a weapon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Against whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Against **the whole land, against the kings of Judah, its officials, its priests, and its people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four groups, from the throne down. Everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah is one man told he has been made a city against a country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **fight against you, but they will not win**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves. The fighting is guaranteed as much as the outcome."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not told he will be popular or believed. He was told at the start: *they will fight against you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **I am with you to rescue you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to deliver you* — **natsal**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same clause given at the start of the chapter: *do not be afraid of their faces, for **I am with you to deliver you**, says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in one chapter, opening and closing the call."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of preaching, and almost nobody listened. He was put in the stocks, thrown in a cistern and left in the mud, denounced as a traitor, and forced into Egypt at the end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw the city fall and wrote Lamentations over it. See the note on Lamentations 3:31–33."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wall did not stop the fighting. It held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the preaching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I said, \"I will not make mention of Him, nor speak anymore in His name.\" **But His word was in my heart like a burning fire shut up in my bones**; I was weary of holding it back, and I could not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tried to stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said before he was born"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Before I formed you in the womb I knew you**; before you were born I sanctified you; I ordained you a prophet to the nations.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he is given.** Not eloquence and not an easy hearing. A city wall, an iron pillar and bronze — three things whose only function is to be hit and stay up."}]}, "Jeremiah 15:15–18": {"title": "\"When Your words came, I ate them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophet's complaint, and in the middle of it one of the best lines in the book about Scripture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O LORD, **You know**. Remember me. Come for me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three short imperatives. He is not composing; he is asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Your words came, **I ate them**. Your words became **my joy, the delight of my heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he found them and consumed them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel is handed a scroll and told *eat this scroll*, and reports *it was in my mouth like honey in sweetness*. John does the same in Revelation 10 — sweet in the mouth and bitter in the stomach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eating is the standard biblical picture for taking in what is written, and Jeremiah uses it about a word that cost him everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **I bear Your name**, O LORD God of hosts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eating and the bearing of the name are given as one thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **never sat in the circle** of those who laugh and make merry. Because Your hand was on me, **I sat alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the prophet who was forbidden to marry, forbidden to go to funerals, and forbidden to go to feasts — see the note on Jeremiah 16:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The isolation was instructed, and he lists it here as a grievance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why is **my pain unending**, my wound grievous and **refusing to heal**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not asking rhetorically. He wants an answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Will You be to me **like a stream that fails**, like **waters that are not sure**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wadi — a desert streambed that runs in the rains and is dry when you need it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking God whether He is a seasonal brook."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the sharpest thing any prophet says to God's face, and it is preserved in the book without softening. Two chapters earlier God had called Himself *the fountain of living waters*; Jeremiah asks whether that is true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer he gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you return, then I will bring you back; you shall stand before Me. If you take out the precious from the vile, you shall be as My mouth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *I will make you to this people a fortified bronze wall… **for I am with you, to save you and deliver you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No explanation of the pain. A restoration, a commission, and a promise of company."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Keeps going for another thirty-seven chapters, through a cistern, stocks, a burned scroll he dictates again, and the fall of the city."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other thing he says in this book.** *His word was in my heart **like a burning fire shut up in my bones**; I was weary of holding it back, and I could not.* Said right after *I will not make mention of Him, nor speak any more in His name.* He resigned, and could not do it."}]}, "Jeremiah 16:5–7": {"title": "A prophet told not to mourn", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah is forbidden three ordinary human things, and each prohibition is a sermon he has to live rather than preach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not enter a house of mourning.** Do not go to grieve or to comfort them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture, staying away from a funeral was unthinkable. Mourning with the bereaved was a duty everybody owed everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told not to go, and his absence at every graveside in Jerusalem is the message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter forbids him three things in a row: do not marry or have children here, do not go to a funeral, do not go to a feast."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No wife, no mourning, no celebration. He is cut off from the beginning, the end and the middle of ordinary life, and the isolation is deliberate — it is what the nation is about to experience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being withdrawn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have taken My peace away from this people**, says the LORD, My steadfast love, My mercy.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three nouns: *shalom*, **chesed** — the covenant love — and mercy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same season Hosea named *Lo-Ammi*, not My people. See the note on Hosea 1:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cup of consolation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No one will **lift the cup of consolation** for a father or a mother.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was a customary cup handed to mourners after a burial. The chapter names it and says it will not be offered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody will break bread with the grieving. Nobody will cut himself or shave his head. Every rite that made a death bearable is stripped out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the other side of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Keep reading. Nine verses later:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Behold, the days are coming, says the LORD, that it shall no more be said, 'The LORD lives who brought up the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt,' but 'The LORD lives **who brought up the children of Israel from the land of the north and from all the lands where He had driven them**.' For I will bring them back into their land which I gave to their fathers.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the return will be a bigger event than the exodus — so much bigger that the nation will change the way it swears its oaths."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That was written around 600 BC, to people about to be deported, and the scattering that eventually followed lasted nineteen centuries."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for the sending.** *I will send for many fishermen, and they shall fish them… and afterward I will send for many hunters, and they shall hunt them from every mountain and every hill and out of the holes of the rocks.* Fishermen first, then hunters. Jeremiah says the gathering will reach the ones who do not come when they are called."}]}, "Jeremiah 17:5–6": {"title": "The bush in the desert", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first half of a pair, and everything in it is about where the roots are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is what **the LORD** says.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah attributes it directly. The comparison that follows is not the prophet's opinion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cursed is the one who **trusts in man**, who **leans on mere flesh for his strength**, whose **heart turns away from the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, and they run in sequence: trusting, leaning, and then the turning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heart's departure is listed third. It is the consequence rather than the cause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Mere flesh\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **basar** — flesh, meat. The word for what a body is made of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah uses it the same way: *the Egyptians are men, and not God; and their horses are flesh, and not spirit.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is like **a bush in the desert wastes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **arar** — a juniper or dwarf shrub of the wilderness. It survives; it does not thrive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he cannot do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **does not see when good comes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he shall not see when good comes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The good arrives and he misses it. Nothing in the sentence says the good is withheld."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he lives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the parched places of the wilderness**, in **a salt land where no one can live**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Salt land is the ground around the Dead Sea — white, crusted, and nothing grows on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah picks the most sterile landscape in the country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jeremiah was watching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah in its last decades, negotiating with Egypt against Babylon and then with Babylon against Egypt. The whole political programme of the period was leaning on flesh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told at his call: *they will fight against you, but they shall not prevail against you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse in between the two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses on, after the blessed man: *the heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked; who can know it?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah puts the two trees on either side of that sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **blessed is the one who trusts in the LORD**… He is like **a tree planted by the water**.* See the note on Jeremiah 17:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men, two root systems, one climate. The drought comes to both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the image comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 1, which opens the psalter with the same figure: *he shall be like a tree planted by the rivers of water… the ungodly are not so, but are like the chaff which the wind drives away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah keeps the tree and changes the opposite from chaff to a shrub."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter goes on to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, the LORD, **search the heart**, I test the mind, even to give every man according to his ways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, in Jeremiah's own voice: *Heal me, O LORD, and I shall be healed; save me, and I shall be saved, **for You are my praise**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two halves share.** The heat and the drought. Jeremiah does not describe one man in good weather and one in bad — the difference in the passage is entirely where the roots went."}]}, "Jeremiah 17:7–8": {"title": "The tree that never fails", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second half of the pair, and the weather in it is the same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is the one who **trusts in the LORD**, whose **confidence rests in Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs where the cursed man had three. The Hebrew for *confidence* is **mibtach** — a refuge, the thing you put your weight on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is like **a tree planted by the water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *transplanted* — **shathal**, set out deliberately, not self-seeded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody put it there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The roots"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sending its roots down **along the stream**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *spreading out its roots by the river*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The water is not falling on it from above. The tree is drawing from a source at its feet that does not depend on the weather."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole mechanism of the passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It has **no fear when the heat comes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heat is not prevented. It arrives, and the tree does not react."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah does not describe a sheltered life. He describes the same climate as verse 6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The leaves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its **leaves stay green**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *its leaf shall be green*. Not merely alive. The colour is the same as it was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The drought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **a year of drought it does not worry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall not be anxious*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A year, not a week. The word for *anxious* is the same root Jeremiah uses elsewhere for care and dread."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It **never stops bearing fruit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall not cease from yielding fruit*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not more fruit than usual and not fruit in season only. It does not stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison held"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both men in the pair face the same conditions — heat, drought, a year of it. See the note on Jeremiah 17:5–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One is in a salt land and cannot see good when it comes. One is by a stream and does not notice the drought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the image starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 1: *he shall be like a tree planted by the rivers of water, that brings forth its fruit in its season, whose leaf also shall not wither.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's man delights in the law. Jeremiah's trusts in the LORD."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the same chapter says two verses later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The heart is **deceitful above all things**, and desperately wicked; **who can know it?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer, immediately: *I, the LORD, search the heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah sets the two trees on either side of a sentence about how little a man can tell about his own inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the tree turns up at the end of the Bible"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the middle of its street, and on either side of the river, was **the tree of life**, which bore twelve fruits, each tree yielding its fruit every month.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tree by a river, bearing every month."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the water is.** Not falling on the leaves. Underground, at the roots, reached by a system the drought never touches — and Jeremiah says the tree does not even register the year."}]}, "Jeremiah 22:28–30": {"title": "The curse on Jeconiah, and the line that had to get past it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the strangest problems in the Bible is set up here, and it is not solved until Matthew's first chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Write this man down as childless, a man who will not prosper in his days. For none of his offspring will prosper, none will sit on the throne of David or rule again in Judah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Coniah — also called Jeconiah and Jehoiachin — was king of Judah for three months before Babylon carried him off. The curse is not that he has no children; he had seven. It is that **none of his descendants will ever sit on David's throne.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that is a problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God had also sworn to David that his throne would be established forever, and that the Messiah would come from his line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the promise runs through David, and the royal line runs through Jeconiah, and the royal line has just been declared permanently disqualified. Both statements are in the same Bible and neither is withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Matthew resolves it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's genealogy runs straight through Jeconiah — he names him — and traces the line down to *Joseph, the husband of Mary*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the wording changes. Every other link in that chapter says *was the father of*. This one does not:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*Jacob was the father of Joseph, the husband of Mary, and it was **from Mary** that Jesus was born.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph gives Jesus the legal claim to David's throne, as his adoptive father — which He needs. But the blood does not come through Joseph, so the curse on Jeconiah's descendants does not touch Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Luke's genealogy takes a different route entirely, through David's son Nathan rather than Solomon, bypassing the cursed line altogether."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What that means about the virgin birth.** It is usually argued from prophecy and from purity. This is a third reason, and it is legal: a Messiah descended physically from Jeconiah could not have taken the throne. The line had to arrive by another road, and Matthew's careful change of wording is where he shows you it did."}]}, "Jeremiah 29:10–14": {"title": "\"Plans to give you a future and a hope\" — and the seventy years in front of it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted verse in Jeremiah, and it begins with a number that never gets quoted with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence it belongs to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When seventy years are completed for Babylon, I will come to you and make good on My gracious promise to bring you back to this place.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy years. Most of the people reading the letter would die in Babylon. The promise is real, and its fulfilment is beyond the lifetime of nearly everyone being addressed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the setting for *I know the plans I have for you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who received it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exiles — and Jeremiah's letter tells them what to do in the meantime: build houses, plant gardens, marry, have children, and *seek the peace of the city where I have sent you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not endure it. Settle in it, and pray for the place holding you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the promise says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Plans for peace and not for harm, to give you a future and a hope.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *future* is literally an **end**, an outcome — the far side of something. Paired with hope, it means: there is a later, and it is good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Spoken to people whose visible situation was going to last seventy years, that is not a small thing. It is precisely the promise a person needs when the near view holds nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters that we read it whole"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted alone it can sound like a guarantee that plans will go well soon. In place it is stronger and harder: God's intentions are good, the timescale may be longer than your life, and you are told to plant gardens while you wait."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows.** *You will call on Me and come and pray to Me, and I will listen. You will seek Me and find Me, when you seek Me with all your heart.* The promise about the future is immediately followed by an invitation to talk to Him in the middle of the seventy years, not only at the end of them."}]}, "Jeremiah 3:19–20": {"title": "\"I thought you would call Me 'My Father'\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God quotes His own private thought, and it is about a name He hoped to be called."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The construction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I had said **to Myself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I said*, and what follows is an interior sentence. Jeremiah records it as overheard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The intention"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How gladly would I set you among My children** and **give you a pleasant land, the finest inheritance of any nation**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *how shall I put you among the children*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A pleasant land** — the Hebrew is *a land of desire*, **eretz chemdah**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The expectation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **I thought you would call Me 'My Father'** and **never turn away from Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you shall call Me My Father*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole weight of the passage is on a form of address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that is unusual"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God is called Father in the Old Testament, but rarely, and almost always of the nation rather than by an individual."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah: *doubtless You are our Father, though Abraham was ignorant of us*. And: *You, O LORD, are our Father, our Redeemer from Everlasting is Your name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a common form of address in those pages. Here God says it is what He was hoping for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **as a wife betrays her husband, so you betrayed Me**, house of Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bagad** — to act treacherously, to deal falsely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of Jeremiah 3 runs on this image: Israel as an unfaithful wife, and the chapter opens with the law about a man who cannot take back a wife who has married another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two metaphors together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage moves from father-and-child to husband-and-wife in one sentence, and does not smooth the transition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are used because both are covenant relations that can be broken from one side only."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Return, you backsliding children**, and I will heal your backslidings.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And two verses on: *\"Return, O backsliding Israel,\" says the LORD; \"**I will not cause My anger to fall on you**. For I am merciful,\" says the LORD; \"I will not remain angry forever.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *merciful* is **chasid** — from **chesed**, covenant loyalty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Only acknowledge your iniquity**, that you have transgressed against the LORD your God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One thing. The chapter does not set terms beyond it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the Father-name goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Our Father in heaven**, hallowed be Your name.\"* See the note on Matthew 6:9–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **Abba**, in Gethsemane and in two of Paul's letters, left untranslated both times. See the note on Galatians 4:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the Spirit puts it in the mouth: *God has sent forth the Spirit of His Son into your hearts, crying out, \"Abba, Father!\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Jeremiah was writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah in its last decades, before Babylon. He was told at his call that nobody would listen, and he preached for forty years anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God says He was hoping for.** Not obedience, in this sentence. A name — that they would call Him Father — and He says it as a thought He had to Himself."}]}, "Jeremiah 33:6–9": {"title": "\"They will tremble in awe at all the good\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A promise of restoration made to a city under siege, by a prophet in prison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jeremiah was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens by noting he was *still confined in the courtyard of the guard*. Babylonian siege ramps were against the walls. The city fell shortly after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything promised here is spoken into that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Health and healing… abundant peace and security… bring them back from exile… rebuild them as they were before… cleanse them of every sin… forgive all their sins of rebellion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six undertakings, and the last two are about guilt rather than circumstances. The rebuilding of walls and the cleansing of sin are in the same sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *abundant peace* is a phrase about an overflow of shalom and truth — wholeness, and reliability with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the nations do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then this city will bring Me joy and honour and praise before all the nations of the earth, when they hear of all the good I do for it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The restoration is public. The audience is everybody else, and what they hear about is not Judah's recovery but *the good I do*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line worth stopping on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will tremble in awe at all the good and all the peace I pour out on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Trembling is normally the response to judgement. Here the nations shake at **goodness**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is an unusual sentence, and Jeremiah means it: the sheer scale of the mercy is what unsettles the onlookers."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is promised three verses later.** *In this place, of which you say, \"It is a waste\"… there will be heard again the voice of joy and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride.* Jeremiah quotes the city's own despairing description back and reverses it."}]}, "Jeremiah 7:30–31": {"title": "\"It never even entered My mind\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most disturbing passages in the prophets, and one of the most revealing sentences about God in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They have built the high places of Topheth in the Valley of Ben Hinnom, and there they burn their own sons and daughters in the fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Child sacrifice, in a valley outside Jerusalem, by people who also kept the temple running. Jeremiah is not describing a foreign nation; this is Judah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The valley's name — Ge-Hinnom — comes through Greek into the New Testament as **Gehenna**, the word Jesus uses for hell. The place became the image."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three denials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God says three things about it in a row, and the third is the one that stops you:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["I never commanded it", "I never spoke of it", "**it never even entered My mind**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it did not come up on My heart*. Not merely that He forbade it — that the notion had never crossed His thoughts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it needed saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because they were doing it in His name, in a valley within sight of His temple, alongside sacrifices they believed He wanted. The whole passage is about worship that had become something God did not recognise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has to state that He never conceived of it, because the people doing it thought it was religion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He wanted instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the same chapter: *obey My voice, and I will be your God and you will be My people, and walk in all the way that I command you, that it may be well with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The alternative offered is not more sacrifice. It is listening."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the valley ends up.** Josiah later desecrated Topheth precisely to stop this, and the place became a refuse dump — which is how a name for a valley became a name for the fire. The worst thing people did to their children gave its name to the worst thing there is."}]}, "Jeremiah 9:23–24": {"title": "The three things not to boast in, and the one thing to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three prohibitions and one permission, and the permission is the surprise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let not the wise boast in their wisdom. Let not the strong boast in their strength. Let not the rich boast in their riches.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom, strength, wealth — the three things every ancient society ranked people by, and ours has not moved on much."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note that none of the three is condemned. Solomon was given wisdom and wealth by God directly. The prohibition is on boasting in them, which is a different thing from having them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if anyone would boast, let them boast in this alone, that they have the understanding to know Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Boasting is not forbidden. It is redirected. There is something a person is permitted to be loud about, and it is knowing God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *understanding* here is about insight gained — a knowing that has been worked at and arrived at, not merely information held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He wants known about Himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That I am the LORD, and that I show steadfast love, justice, and righteousness on the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He specifies. Of everything that could be said about God, three things are named: **hesed** — the covenant-keeping love — along with justice and righteousness. And all three are described as being done *on the earth*, not in the abstract."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *for in these I take delight.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes the last line twice — in 1 Corinthians 1 after saying God chose the foolish and weak things, and again in 2 Corinthians. Both times as *let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He treats it as settling the question of what a Christian may be proud of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When Jeremiah said it.** In a book largely about a nation about to lose its wise men, its soldiers and its wealth to Babylon. The three things he tells them not to boast in were all about to be taken away, and the one thing he leaves them was the one thing exile could not touch."}]}, "Job 1:1–5": {"title": "A blameless life", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens with a man nobody has anything on, and the very first habit it records about him is worth noticing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was a good man, **honest and whole all the way through**. He walked with God and turned away from evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, and the writer says them flatly. There is no irony in the sentence and no qualification after it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *honest and whole* is **tam** — complete, entire, without a crack running through it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seven thousand sheep, three thousand camels, five hundred yoke of oxen, five hundred donkeys… **the greatest man in all the East**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The numbers are given so the reader can measure the losses later, and so the doubling at the end of the book can be checked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one habit recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Job would gather his children and set them right before God. **Early in the morning** he would rise and offer a burnt offering for each one of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for himself. For them. Seven sons and three daughters, one offering each, after every round of feasting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he said to himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Perhaps** my children have sinned and cursed God in their hearts.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the reason he gives, and he gives it to himself — the text says he said it in his heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had happened. Nobody had done anything. There is a *perhaps*, and there is an early morning and ten burnt offerings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"This is what Job always did\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *thus did Job all the days*. A continuous, unbroken practice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's very first picture of its hero is a man rising before dawn, week after week, because of something his children *might* have thought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had no way of knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether the offerings were needed. Whether they worked. Whether God was pleased."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no priest in this book, no tabernacle, no Scripture, no covenant, and no word from God to Job about any of it. He is doing what he can think of, on his own, with a *perhaps*, and repeating it every time the feasting comes round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the man the rest of the book is about."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the New Testament says about that arrangement.** *Every priest stands daily ministering and offering repeatedly the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins. But this Man, after He had offered one sacrifice for sins forever, sat down.* Job stood up early. There is Somebody who sat down."}]}, "Job 1:6–8": {"title": "The adversary comes before God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The scene shifts to a place Job never sees and never learns about, and the whole book turns on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He got in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sons of God came to present themselves before the LORD, and **the adversary came among them too, the accuser**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ha-satan**, with the article — *the* adversary, *the* accuser. It is a job description before it is a name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the thing to see is that he is standing there. He has access. He walks into the throne room and is asked a question and answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"From **wandering the earth, roaming back and forth** across it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same picture: *your adversary the devil walks about like a roaring lion, seeking whom he may devour.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who brings up Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD said to him, \"**Have you noticed My servant Job?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The accuser did not name him. God did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what God says about him is the same four things the writer said in verse 1 — *blameless and upright, a man who walks with Me and turns away from evil* — with one addition: *there is no one on earth like him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is God's own verdict on Job, spoken out loud, before any of the trouble starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job never knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not told about this conversation. Not in chapter 1, not in chapter 42, not anywhere in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty-two chapters of arguing about why it happened, and the reader has the answer from page one while Job never gets it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has changed since"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing Job did not have is the one thing this scene depends on: nobody was standing between him and that accuser."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone sins, we have an Advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous.* *He always lives to make intercession for them.* And in Revelation the accuser of the brethren is *cast down* — the one who *accused them before our God day and night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job had no mediator in that room. He says so himself in chapter 9: *there is no umpire between us, who might lay his hand on us both.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole difference between his position and ours, and it is not a small one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the accuser wanted.** *Does Job fear God for nothing?* His entire case is that the man is bought. Take the hedge down, he says, and he will curse You to Your face. The wager was never really about Job's suffering. It was about whether anyone loves God for who He is."}]}, "Job 11:13–15": {"title": "\"Zophar, and the formula\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third friend, and he brings a procedure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If** you would only set your heart right… **if** there is sin in your grip, put it far away… **then** you could lift up your face without a stain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two conditions and a result. It is a formula, and it is the oldest one there is: do this, and God will do that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The steps"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*set your heart right*", "*stretch out your hands to Him*", "*if there is sin in your grip, put it far away*", "*let no wrong live in your tent*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four items on a checklist, handed to a man covered in boils scraping himself with a piece of broken pot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then you could **lift up your face without a stain**. You would stand firm and unafraid.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses on: *your life would be brighter than noonday; its darkness would become like the morning.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words are beautiful. The offer is genuine. And everything in it is conditional on Job's performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is the wrong word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clause depends on something Job has to produce, and the whole premise is that his darkness is being caused by sin he is holding on to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader knows it is not. Chapter 1 said so, and God Himself said so out loud in the throne room: *there is no one on earth like him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zophar is telling a man to clean up something that is not there, and then promising him light on the other side of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it sounds like now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your darkness is because of sin — deal with it, and the brightness will come back.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is still preached. And it still lands on people who are already on the floor, and it still asks them to produce a righteousness of their own before God will lift their face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The face is lifted at Calvary, and the sin was put far away by Somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Zophar had said just before this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Know then that God exacts from you **less than your iniquity deserves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his opening position. He begins by telling Job he is getting off lightly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does not have"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of the three friends has a word from God. They have observation, tradition, and a formula, and between them they cover every way of speaking to a sufferer that leaves him to sort himself out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verdict.** All three are named in chapter 42, and the same sentence is spoken over all three: *you have not spoken of Me what is right.* And what they have to do about it is bring seven bulls and seven rams and stand there while Job prays for them. See the note on Job 42:7–8."}]}, "Job 16:1–6": {"title": "Miserable comforters", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job tells his three friends exactly what they are, and then tells them what he would have done in their place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name he gives them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Comforters? You are a misery, every one of you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a play on words — *comforters of trouble*, comforters who are themselves the trouble. It is the phrase that gave English the expression *Job's comforters*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They came to comfort him. Chapter 2 says so: they *made an appointment together to come and mourn with him and comfort him.* That was the intention."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I have heard all this before\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have heard all this before, and heard it many times. **Is there no end to your windy words?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *windy* is words of wind — air."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And there is a question after it: *what is it that stirs you up to keep answering?* He is asking what is driving them. Nobody has asked them that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he could do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I could talk the way you talk, **if it were you sitting where I sit**. I could pile up fine speeches against you and shake my head over you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument collapses into that clause. If the positions were reversed, he could produce the same material."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their theology costs them nothing to hold. It costs him everything to be under."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says he would do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But no. I would use my mouth to steady you**, and the comfort of my lips would ease your pain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence. A man scraping himself with a piece of broken pot, having buried ten children, describing what he would have said to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *steady* is to strengthen, to make firm — and *ease your pain* is the verb for restraining, holding something back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is telling them the thing they are for, and none of the three does it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the honest line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yet whether I speak, my grief is not eased, and if I stay silent, it does not leave me either.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no position he can take that helps. Speaking does not relieve it; silence does not either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where he is, and the book leaves it on the page without softening it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they had already done right.** Seven days on the ground with him and not a word, *for they saw that his suffering was very great.* The best thing the three of them managed was the week before any of them opened their mouths."}]}, "Job 19:1–6": {"title": "\"How long will you crush me?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job counts, and then draws a line his friends have been crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What words do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How long will you keep **tormenting me and grinding me to pieces with words**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *grinding to pieces* is to crush, to break something small. It is used of pounding grain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is naming the weapon. Not the boils, not the losses — the talking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Ten times\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Ten times now** you have insulted me, and you are not ashamed to wound me so.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been keeping count. Ten is a round number in Hebrew for a full tally, the whole of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are not ashamed.* Not that they are wrong — that they feel nothing about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line he draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Even if I have gone wrong, **that is mine to carry, not yours**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a complete answer to all three of them. Suppose he is guilty. It is between him and God, and none of it is their business to prosecute."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my error remains with me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were really doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you really mean to **lift yourselves above me** and **use my disgrace as proof against me**…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things he names, and both are about them rather than him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are using his condition as evidence, and they are standing on it. A man on the ground is useful to a theology that says the upright are never on the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he concludes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then understand this. **It is God who has bent me down** and drawn His net tight around me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's settled conviction, and he holds it for the whole book: God did this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader knows better. Chapter 2 said in plain words that *the adversary went out from the presence of the LORD and struck Job* — and Job is never told. See the notes on Job 1:6–8 and Job 2:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone in the argument is working from the same wrong premise. The three friends and Job all agree God sent it; they only differ on whether He was right to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirteen verses further on, out of the middle of all this, comes the most famous sentence in the book:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know that my Redeemer lives, and He shall stand at last on the earth… and in my flesh I shall see God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Redeemer** is **go'el** — the kinsman-redeemer, the relative whose job was to buy back what a man had lost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is crushed, convinced God has netted him, and he says it anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asks for two verses before that.** *Oh, that my words were written… engraved on a rock forever.* They were."}]}, "Job 2:7–8": {"title": "Struck from head to foot", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the shortest of them names who did it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who struck him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the adversary** went out from the presence of the LORD and **struck Job** with painful boils.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence names the agent. It was not God who put the boils on him — the text says the accuser went out and did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth holding on to, because Job spends the next thirty-five chapters convinced it was God, and his friends spend the same thirty-five chapters agreeing with him about that and only arguing over why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader knows something the whole cast does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From the soles of his feet to the crown of his head\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is a merism — the two ends stated to mean everything in between. No part of him was untouched."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy uses the identical phrase for a specific curse: *the LORD will strike you with painful boils… from the sole of your foot to the top of your head.* Any reader who knew that verse would draw the conclusion Job's friends draw."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book puts that conclusion in the reader's hand and then spends forty chapters showing it is wrong in this case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pottery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Job took a **piece of broken pottery** to scrape himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greatest man in all the East, sitting with a shard of a broken pot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had seven thousand sheep a chapter ago. What he has now is a fragment of something that was already thrown away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ashes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **sat down among the ashes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ash heap outside a town was the rubbish dump, where the sweepings and the burnt refuse went. It is where the destitute sat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he still had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His wife tells him to curse God and die. His three friends arrive, do not recognise him from a distance, and sit with him on the ground for seven days without a word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And no one spoke a word to him, for they saw that his suffering was very great.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seven silent days are the best thing the three of them do in the entire book."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says at the end of chapter 2.** *Shall we accept good from God, and not trouble?* And the writer adds: *in all this Job did not sin with his lips.* He is holding, and he is holding without knowing one thing about why."}]}, "Job 20:1–5": {"title": "Zophar answers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third friend's second speech, and the reason he gives for making it is that he is upset."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Zophar from Naamah** answered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third of the three. The shortest-tempered of them, and the only one who does not get a third speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My uneasy thoughts drive me to reply**, because of **the turmoil churning inside me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states his own motive and it is agitation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a word about Job's condition, Job's argument, or anything he has learned. He is answering because he cannot sit still."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has stung"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have heard a rebuke that shames me**, and now my understanding urges me to speak.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job had just said, at the end of chapter 19: *be afraid of the sword for yourselves; for wrath brings the punishment of the sword, that you may know there is a judgment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zophar takes it personally, and the speech that follows is about how the wicked come to nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job had said just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most famous sentence in the book: *For **I know that my Redeemer lives**, and He shall stand at last on the earth; and after my skin is destroyed, this I know, that **in my flesh I shall see God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zophar's answer to that is *the triumph of the wicked is short*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is replying to a confession of faith with a lecture about the fate of the godless."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appeal to what everybody knows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Surely you know how it has always been**, ever since man was first set on the earth.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stock move of all three friends. They argue from the way things generally go and apply it to a case they have not understood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thesis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The triumph of the wicked is short. The joy of the godless lasts only a moment**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is true, and it is in Proverbs and the Psalms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also, in this room, an accusation. Job has lost everything, so on Zophar's reasoning the category is obvious."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book has already told the reader"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 1: *that man was blameless and upright, and one who feared God and shunned evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the conversation in heaven that none of the men on the ground know about. See the note on Job 1:6–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every speech of the friends is made by men working from a rule that does not fit the case in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says about them at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My wrath is aroused against you and your two friends, for you have not spoken of Me what is right**, as My servant Job has.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are told to bring seven bulls and seven rams, and that Job will pray for them — *for I will accept him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man they had been correcting is the one appointed to intercede for them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the speech does not contain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any question. Zophar does not ask Job anything in the whole chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The reason he gives for speaking.** The turmoil inside himself. Job's real answer, when it comes, is out of a whirlwind and consists almost entirely of questions."}]}, "Job 22:21–30": {"title": "\"Whatever you decide will hold firm\" — and who says it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Some of the loveliest promises in the Old Testament are in this speech, and the speaker is one of Job's three friends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is talking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliphaz the Temanite, in his third and harshest speech. A few verses earlier he has accused Job of stripping the clothes off the poor and sending widows away empty — charges the book has already told the reader are false."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the passage is a true thing said by a man who is wrong about the person he is saying it to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make peace with Him, Job, and be at rest. Then good things will find their way back to you… You will pray, and He will hear you… Whatever you decide will hold firm, and light will shine on every path you take.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Taken as statements about God, these are sound and the rest of Scripture backs them. Taken as Eliphaz means them — repent of the sins we have decided you committed and your fortunes will return — they are a cruelty, and God says so at the end of the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book settles it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's verdict on the three friends is unusually blunt: *you have not spoken of Me what is right, as My servant Job has.* They are told to bring sacrifices and have Job pray for them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which makes the last line here almost unbearable in hindsight. Eliphaz tells Job that God *will rescue even the guilty, and they will be delivered through the cleanness of your hands* — and that is precisely what happens, with Eliphaz as the guilty party and Job praying for him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How to use a passage like this.** The sentences are true of God; that is why they read so well on their own. What the book adds is the warning that true statements can be aimed at a suffering person as a weapon. Job's friends were at their best in the seven days they sat with him and said nothing."}]}, "Job 26:5–10": {"title": "\"He hangs the earth on nothing at all\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job's answer to Bildad, and in the middle of it he says something no one else in the ancient world said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He stretches the northern skies over **empty space** and **hangs the earth on nothing at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *upon nothing* — **beli-mah**, without anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job is probably the oldest book in the Bible. Every culture around it had the earth resting on something: on a turtle, on elephants, on pillars, on the shoulders of a giant, on the back of a great fish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it is hanging on nothing, and nobody could confirm it for three and a half thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The circle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **drew a circle on the surface of the waters**, a boundary line where light meets darkness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Hebrew word Isaiah uses — **chug**, a circle drawn with a fixed radius."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second clause is exact. The boundary where light meets darkness on the surface of a sphere is a circle, and it moves. It is called the terminator, and it is what you are looking at in every photograph of the earth from space."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clouds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **wraps up the waters in His thick clouds**, and the clouds do not burst beneath their weight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *wraps up* is to bind in a bundle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An average cumulus cloud carries hundreds of tonnes of water and does not fall. Job noticed that it does not, and said the not-falling was the remarkable part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is in the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is a man on an ash heap, covered in boils, whose three friends have been telling him for twenty chapters that he brought it on himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His reply is a description of the universe. Bildad had just said *how then can man be righteous before God?* — and Job's answer is four verses of cosmology."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And these are but the outer fringes of His ways. How faint the whisper we hear of Him! Who then can understand the thunder of His power?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *outer fringes* is the ends, the edges. He has just described empty space, a hanging earth, bundled oceans and a drawn circle, and calls it the hem of the garment."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who says the same thing later.** *In Him all things hold together* — Colossians 1. Job says the earth hangs on nothing; Paul names what nothing is."}]}, "Job 29:14–17": {"title": "\"I wrapped myself in righteousness\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job's longest speech, remembering what he used to be, and it is worth counting the pronouns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The garment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I wrapped myself** in righteousness, and it clothed me; justice was my robe and my crown.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He put it on himself. That is what the verb says, and the whole passage is built the same way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah describes a different arrangement: *He has clothed me with the garments of salvation, He has covered me with the robe of righteousness.* Somebody else does the dressing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's robe is one he wove and wrapped around himself, and every word of it is true — he really was that man. Chapter 1 said so and God said so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I was **eyes to the blind** and **feet to the lame**. I was **a father to the needy**, and when a stranger came with a case I did not know, **I searched it out for him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The record is extraordinary. He was not boasting about property or influence — he is listing the blind, the lame, the poor and the stranger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I broke the jaws of the wicked and pulled the prey from between his teeth.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the chapter as a whole and count. In chapter 29, Job refers to himself well over forty times and to God about five."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not how he began. Chapter 1: *the LORD gave and the LORD has taken away; blessed be the name of the LORD.* Chapter 2: *shall we accept good from God and not trouble?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between there and here, three men have spent twenty-five chapters telling him his suffering is his own fault. He has had to defend himself for so long that he has become his own subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what preaching from experience, tradition and a formula does to the man underneath it. It does not produce repentance. It produces a case for the defence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what it turns into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the next chapter he is looking down on men whose fathers he *would not have trusted even with my sheepdogs.* See the note on Job 30:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bitterness and the pride arrive together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is still in it at the end of chapter 31: *let the Almighty answer me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the Almighty does, out of a whirlwind, and Job's answer is *I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear, but now my eye sees You.* The robe he wrapped himself in comes off the moment he actually sees Him. See the note on Job 42:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the chapter is here.** It is not a villain's speech. Every claim in it is true, and God will publicly call him *My servant Job* four times over in chapter 42. It is what a genuinely good man sounds like after twenty-five chapters of being told to justify himself."}]}, "Job 29:21–25": {"title": "\"They waited for me as for the rain\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job remembering what his life used to be, and the memory is about how people listened to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They waited for me as for the rain, and opened their mouths wide as for the spring showers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **spring** showers — the latter rain, the one that filled out the grain just before harvest. See the note on Deuteronomy 11:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mouths open, upturned, waiting for something that only comes from above and cannot be summoned. That is how he says people used to receive what he said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his words did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My words fell gently on them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the dripping or distilling of dew — the same picture Moses uses in Deuteronomy 32 of his own teaching. Not a downpour. Something that settles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the effect: *after I had spoken they had nothing to add.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did with the standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I chose the road for them and sat at their head. I lived like a king among his troops, **like one who comforts those who mourn**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase turns the whole passage. He has been describing authority — men falling silent, princes deferring, a seat at the head — and he ends it by comparing himself to someone consoling the grieving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his own account of what the position was for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the chapter exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job is speaking in the past tense throughout. The next chapter begins *but now they mock me* — men younger than him, whose fathers he would not have put with his sheepdogs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is a man remembering, out loud, in the middle of losing everything."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was actually like.** The verses just above this passage are his own account: he rescued the poor, was eyes to the blind and feet to the lame, took up the case of the stranger, and broke the jaws of the wicked. The book's assessment of him at the start — blameless and upright — is not contradicted by anything he says here."}]}, "Job 30:1–4": {"title": "Laughed at by the lowest", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter turns on two words, and then Job says something he would not have said in chapter 1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"But now\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 29 was *oh, that I were as in months past*. Chapter 30 begins *but now*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two chapters are a hinge — everything he was, and everything he is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Those younger than I make sport of me, men whose fathers **I would not have trusted even with my sheepdogs**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the line. He is not describing what they did to him; he is placing them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their fathers were beneath the dogs that watched his flock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they actually are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the next three verses, because Job describes them himself:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Gaunt from want and hunger, they gnawed the dry ground… They picked salt herbs among the bushes, and the roots of the broom tree were their bread.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Starving people. Eating roots and weeds in waste ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who was *eyes to the blind and feet to the lame*, *a father to the needy*, is looking at the destitute and measuring them against his sheepdogs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has been done to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is chapter 30 of a book in which three men have spent twenty-five chapters telling him his ruin is his own fault."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man forced to defend himself for that long ends up with a case, and the case needs someone to be beneath him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bitterness in chapter 30 and the self-justification in chapter 29 are the same thing looked at from two sides, and both of them arrived after his comforters started talking. He did not sound like this in chapter 1 or 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The honesty of the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is tidied up. The writer records the greatest man in the East sneering at starving men and does not annotate it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job has to hear from God before this comes off him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the whole speech is going.** By the end of the chapter: *I cry out to You, and You do not answer me… I am a brother of jackals and a companion of ostriches… my harp is turned to mourning.* He is not enjoying the contempt he is handing out. He is a man in pieces, and the pieces cut in every direction."}]}, "Job 32:1–5": {"title": "The three fall quiet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-nine chapters of argument end because everyone has run out of words, and a young man who has been sitting there the whole time stands up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the three stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was nothing left to say, because **in Job's own eyes he was in the right**, and no argument of theirs could move him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the writer's own summary, not a friend's accusation. After everything they threw at him, Job's settled position is that he is right."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it himself in chapter 29 — *I wrapped myself in righteousness, and it clothed me* — and in that speech he refers to himself well over forty times and to God about five. See the note on Job 29:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three men's preaching did not humble him. It hardened him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Elihu"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But there was a young man sitting by, **listening to it all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been present since chapter 3 and this is the first the reader hears of him. Nine chapters of speeches, and he was in the room for every one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elihu, son of Barachel the Buzite, from the family of Ram.* The fullest genealogy given to anyone in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His name means *He is my God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is angry at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things, and they are stated separately:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*angry at **Job** for making himself out to be more in the right than God*", "*angry at **the three friends** too, because they had found no real answer, yet they had gone right on condemning Job anyway*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only person in the book who is against both sides of the argument. And his charge against the three is precise: they had no answer, and they kept condemning anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he waited"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All this time Elihu had **held his tongue, waiting**, because the others were older than he was and it seemed only fitting.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine chapters of silence out of respect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu speaks for six chapters. Nobody interrupts him. Job — who answered every one of the other three, at length, ten times over — does not say a word back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when Elihu finishes, the next verse is *then the LORD answered Job out of the whirlwind.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says he is not doing.** *I will not show partiality to anyone, nor flatter any man, for I do not know how to flatter — my Maker would soon take me away.* The three came to defend a system. He says at the outset that he has no side."}]}, "Job 33:14–18": {"title": "\"He speaks in a dream\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elihu's first claim is that the silence Job has been complaining about is not silence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, I go forward, but He is not there; and backward, but I cannot perceive Him.* And: *I cry out to You, but You do not answer me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty chapters of a man saying God will not speak to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Elihu's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The truth is, **God does speak**. He speaks **one way, then another**, and people simply **miss it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that God has spoken once and stopped. Repeatedly, in different modes — and the failure named is on the hearing end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that man does not perceive it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He speaks **in a dream, in a vision of the night**, when **deep sleep settles over everyone** and they are drowsing on their beds.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *deep sleep* is **tardemah** — the same word used of Adam when the woman was taken from his side, and of Abram when the covenant was cut and only the fire passed between the pieces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of those are occasions when God did something to a man who could not participate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That is when **He opens their ears** and presses His warning home.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He uncovers the ear* — the idiom for disclosing something privately, the way you lift a man's hair to speak to him quietly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ear is described as needing opening. It is not that the speech was too faint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three purposes, all protective:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*to **turn a person back from some reckless plan***", "*to **keep pride from swallowing him whole***", "*to **hold a soul back from the pit**, to keep a life from crossing over by the sword*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is punitive. Every clause is about stopping somebody before something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative Elihu offers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the first of two ways he says God speaks. The second is the sickbed:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Or He speaks through pain, laying a person low on his bed with an ache that never leaves his bones.\"* See the note on Job 33:19–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the messenger, and the ransom found. See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else was spoken to in the night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob, on a stone, with a stairway and the covenant. Solomon at Gibeon, given wisdom in a dream. Joseph, told four times in dreams what to do about a child. Paul, told *do not be afraid, but speak* and *this night the angel of God stood by me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scripture is full of it, and Elihu says it is a standing arrangement rather than a set of exceptions."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Elihu is doing that the three friends did not.** He tells Job that God is already talking. The others explained Job's suffering. He says the silence is not what it appears to be, and then names a mediator."}]}, "Job 33:19–22": {"title": "The man on the bed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elihu describes a man at the end of himself, and the passage exists to set up what comes next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it looks like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"An ache that never leaves his bones. Now **the sight of food turns his stomach**; even the finest dish is nothing to him. His flesh wastes away until you can hardly see it, and **the bones that were hidden now jut out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a clinical description, and it is unsparing. Constant pain, no appetite, visible wasting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever wrote this had watched somebody go through it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"His soul drifts toward the grave. His life inches toward **the messengers of death**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the destroyers, the ones who bring death. He is not dying quickly. He is being carried there by degrees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word the whole passage waits for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse begins: *\"**Yet even then**, if there is a messenger at his side…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in these four verses is the *before*. It is written to be the darkest possible position, so that the *yet even then* has something to stand against."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which messenger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice the two in the passage, and that they are the same Hebrew word."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["verse 22 — the **messengers** of death, carrying him toward the pit", "verse 23 — the **messenger** at his side, one in a thousand, who speaks for him"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same noun, and they are pulling in opposite directions over one man on a bed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that messenger does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell the man what he did wrong. He does not prescribe a remedy or take a history."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He shows him God's righteousness, and God answers: *I have found the price for his release.* See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then his flesh grows fresh again, softer than a child's.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From bones jutting out to skin like a child's, and the thing that stands between the two descriptions is a ransom found by God. See the note on Job 33:25–28."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why Elihu is describing this at all.** He is talking to a man scraping boils with a piece of broken pot. The three friends looked at Job and produced an accusation. Elihu looks at the same man and produces a mediator."}]}, "Job 33:23–24": {"title": "\"I have found the price for his release\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turning point of the whole book, spoken by a young man nobody had asked, and it is the answer to everything the three friends could not solve."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu has just described a man on a sickbed — flesh wasting, bones jutting out, his soul drifting toward the grave, his life inching toward the messengers of death. See the note on Job 33:19–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *Yet even then, **if there is a messenger at his side**…*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one in a thousand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One who **speaks for him**, one in a thousand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a **melitz** — an interpreter, a spokesman, a go-between. It is the word used of the interpreter standing between Joseph and his brothers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *one in a thousand* — there is one such person, and He is not common."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is exactly what Job had been crying out for. In chapter 9: *He is not a man, as I am, that I should answer Him… **there is no umpire between us**, who might lay his hand on us both.* In chapter 16: *my witness is in heaven, my advocate is on high.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job knew what was missing. Elihu names it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the messenger does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **remind this person of what is right and true**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to examine him. Not to search out his sin, prescribe steps, or diagnose the cause — which is what all three friends spent twenty-five chapters doing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to declare to the man His **uprightness** — God's righteousness, shown to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The messenger's job is to show a suffering man what God is like. That is the whole commission."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then God speaks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **God is gracious to him** and says, \"Spare him. Do not let him go down into the pit. **I have found the price for his release**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *gracious* is **chanan** — the verb behind *chen*, grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ransom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have found the price for his release.* The Hebrew is **kopher** — a ransom, a covering, the price paid to redeem a life that was forfeit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same root as **kippur**, the covering of the Day of Atonement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read who does the finding. Not the sick man. Not the friends. **I have found it.** God locates the ransom Himself, and announces it as the reason the man is spared."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no condition attached to it in Elihu's sentence. A messenger shows him God's righteousness; God says a price has been found; the man does not go down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the other side of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *then his flesh grows fresh again, softer than a child's. He returns to the strength of his young days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing follows the ransom. It does not produce it. See the note on Job 33:25–28."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the messenger is.** *There is one God and one Mediator between God and men, the Man Christ Jesus, who gave Himself a ransom for all.* One God, one Mediator, one ransom — and Elihu says it in a book that has no priest, no temple and no covenant in it anywhere."}]}, "Job 33:25–28": {"title": "\"Softer than a child's\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elihu is speaking — the young man who waited for the three friends to finish — and this is the best passage in his long speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restoration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then his flesh grows fresh again, softer than a child's. He returns to the strength of his young days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same picture as Naaman coming up out of the Jordan with skin like a little boy's. Not repaired. Made new."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second line adds the strength back — not merely healed, but restored to what he could once do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word \"then\" is attached to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then* is the first word of the passage, and it is pointing back at something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu has described a man brought to the edge — laid low on his bed, his flesh wasting until you can hardly see it, his life inching toward the messengers of death. Then a messenger comes and shows him God's righteousness, and God says: *I have found the price for his release.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Then** his flesh grows fresh again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is fixed and it does not run the other way. Ransom found, and the renewal follows it. Nothing in these four verses is produced by the sick man's effort — he is on a bed, unable to eat, and a price is found for him by Somebody else. See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *ransom* is **kopher**, a covering, the price paid to buy back a forfeited life — and it is what the New Testament is built on: *the Son of Man came to give His life a ransom for many.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the man then does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He prays to God, and God welcomes him with delight. He looks on God's face and shouts for joy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things: prayer, a welcome, and then his face lifted. The Hebrew for *welcomes him with delight* is the word for accepting a sacrifice as pleasing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confession he makes afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I sinned. I twisted what was straight, and it gained me nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes **after** the rescue, not before it — he is singing it to everyone as testimony. The order in Elihu's account is: ransom found, life restored, and then the honest account of what he had done."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Elihu was.** God's verdict at the end names Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar and tells them they have not spoken what is right. Elihu is never rebuked, never corrected, and never answered by Job — who argued with all three of the others and has nothing to say to this one. He is the only voice in the book that speaks of a mediator and a ransom, and the storm that brings God's own voice comes immediately after he has finished."}]}, "Job 35:9–13": {"title": "\"Who fills the night with songs\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elihu names a question nobody thinks to ask, and the description of God inside it is the best line in his speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People **groan under the weight of oppression**. They cry out under the crushing arm of the powerful.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crying is real and the oppression is real. Elihu is not disputing either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **no one thinks to say, \"Where is God my Maker?\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They cry out **because** of the pressure. Nobody cries out **to** anyone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the distinction the passage turns on — the difference between a noise pain makes and a question addressed to somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The One who **fills the night with songs**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *gives songs in the night*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not takes the night away. Not explains it. Gives something to sing in the middle of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Songs are what this book's God is said to hand out in the dark, and Elihu offers it as a reason to ask where He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Who **teaches us more than the beasts of the field** and **makes us wiser than the birds of the sky**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Animals cry when they are hurt. That much requires nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu's point is that people were given something the animals were not, and in trouble they use the part they share with the cattle and neglect the part that is theirs alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the answer does not come"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They cry out, but **no answer comes** — because of the arrogance of wicked people. **It is all empty noise**, and God will not listen to emptiness.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *vanity* — the same root as Ecclesiastes' *hebel*, vapour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu is describing a cry with nothing in it and nobody in mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu, the young man who waited through nine chapters for the three friends to finish, and who is never rebuked by God at the end of the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is also the one who says *if there is a messenger at his side, one who speaks for him, one in a thousand… I have found the price for his release.* See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where songs in the night turn up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul and Silas, backs opened by rods, feet in the stocks in the inner prison: *at midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the prisoners were listening to them.* See the note on Acts 16:29–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the psalm: *the LORD will command His lovingkindness in the daytime, and **in the night His song shall be with me**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the whole book has been about.** Job groaning, and Job's friends explaining it, and Job asking for an umpire. Elihu's contribution is that the groaning could be addressed to somebody — and God answers out of the whirlwind the moment he stops talking."}]}, "Job 36:8–12": {"title": "Elihu on the cords of affliction", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth speaker in the book, and the only one God does not rebuke by name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Elihu is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He appears in chapter 32, out of nowhere, after the three friends *ceased answering Job, because he was righteous in his own eyes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is young — *I am young in years, and you are very old; therefore I was afraid, and dared not declare my opinion to you* — and he has been waiting through thirty-one chapters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His anger is stated twice: against Job *because he justified himself rather than God*, and against the three friends *because they had found no answer, and yet had condemned Job*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks for six chapters and nobody answers him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is describing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And if any of them end up **bound in chains, caught tight in the cords of affliction**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *held in cords of affliction* — the picture of a man tied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says God does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then God **shows them plainly what they have done**, the way **their pride carried them too far**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He **opens their ears to correction** and **calls them to turn back**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He opens their ear to instruction*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs about hearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two outcomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If they listen and serve Him**, they will **live out their days in good things**, their years in **real contentment**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in pleasantness* — **neimim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **if they will not listen**, they **cross over by the sword** and **die still knowing nothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they die without knowledge*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elihu gets right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three friends had argued backwards from Job's suffering to his guilt. Elihu's argument is different: he says affliction can be instructional rather than punitive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He **delivers the poor in their affliction**, and **opens their ears in oppression**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And earlier: *\"For God may speak in one way, or in another, yet man does not perceive it.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elihu also does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Applies the general principle to a man the reader knows is not guilty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader has read chapter 1 and 2. Job's friends have not, and neither has Elihu."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every speech in the book is made by men working without the information the reader was given on the first page. See the note on Job 1:6–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To Eliphaz: *My wrath is aroused against you and your two friends, for **you have not spoken of Me what is right**, as My servant Job has.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Your two friends** — three men are named. Elihu is not among them, and he is not addressed in the epilogue at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book leaves him alone, which readers have argued about for a very long time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after his speeches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD answered Job **out of the whirlwind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu's last chapter is a description of a gathering storm — lightning, thunder, clouds turning — and then the storm speaks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God's answer consists of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Questions. Seventy of them, about wild goats, ostriches, the war horse, the hawk, the sea, snow, and Orion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is about suffering."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Elihu contributes.** A category the other three did not have — that affliction can open an ear rather than settle an account. And like all of them, he applies it to a man whose file he has not read."}]}, "Job 37:6–10": {"title": "\"By the breath of God the ice is formed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elihu's last speech, and he spends it on weather."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He says to **the snow**, 'Fall on the earth,' and to **the rain**, 'Pour down.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weather addressed as something under orders."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu is building toward the whirlwind — God answers Job out of one in the very next chapter, and Elihu is describing storms when it arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What snow does to work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He **seals up every hand from its work**, so that all people He has made **may stop and know it is His doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that He puts a seal on the hand of every man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A heavy snowfall stops the farming, the building, the travelling. Nobody works."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Elihu gives a purpose for it: so that people stop, and know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only place in Scripture that offers a reason for winter, and the reason is that it makes everyone put their tools down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The animals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The wild animals **slip into their dens** and stay in their shelters.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The observation is exact. Animals go to ground before weather, and Elihu notes it as part of the same obedience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The whirlwind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Out of its chamber comes **the whirlwind**, and the biting cold rides in on the driving winds.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *chamber* is an inner room, a storehouse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu speaks of a whirlwind having a room it comes out of, and then God speaks to Job *out of the whirlwind.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By the breath of God the ice is formed**, and the wide waters freeze solid.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *by the breath of God ice is given*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Freezing is a loss of heat, and Elihu attributes it to breath — which is counter-intuitive, since breath is warm. But the Hebrew **ruach** is also wind, and it is wind off cold ground that freezes standing water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elihu is doing with all this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends: *the Almighty — **we cannot find Him out**; He is excellent in power, in judgement and abundant justice; He does not oppress.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is arguing from snow, rain, ice and thunder to a God who is beyond working out, and whose power is not oppressive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the whirlwind arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where God takes the same subject"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Have you entered the treasury of the snow, or have you seen the treasury of the hail?\"* — Job 38, a chapter later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God asks about the same storehouses Elihu mentioned, and asks Job whether he has been inside them. See the note on Job 38:4–7."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last thing Elihu says before God speaks.** *Therefore men fear Him; He shows no partiality to any who are wise of heart.* And then, mid-storm, the LORD answers — and never rebukes Elihu, though He rebukes the other three by name."}]}, "Job 38:1–3": {"title": "God begins to speak", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-five chapters of silence, and then He speaks — and what He says is not what anyone in the book was expecting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Out of the storm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He spoke **out of the storm**, out of the wind and the wild weather.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the whirlwind, the tempest. It is how He came for Elijah too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer a single one of Job's questions. Not one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not explain the boils. He does not mention the throne room, the accuser, or the wager. He does not defend Himself against the charge Job has been laying for thirty chapters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He does not confirm the friends' diagnosis either. There is no list of Job's sins, because there is no list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have questions for you**, and it is your turn to answer Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks. Four chapters of questions — seventy-odd of them — and almost every one is about something Job has never had anything to do with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Where were you when I laid the earth's foundations. Have you entered the springs of the sea. Have you commanded the morning. Who provides food for the raven. Do you know when the mountain goats give birth. Can you bind the Pleiades."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is about Job's conduct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Brace yourself like a strong man\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *gird up your loins* — pull your robe through your belt so you can move."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not gentle and it is not a rebuke either. He is telling Job to stand up. Job has been on an ash heap for thirty-five chapters and God's first instruction is to get on his feet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Words that carry no knowledge\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Who is this who **muddies My counsel** with words that carry no knowledge?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *muddies* is to darken, to obscure — the same picture as silt stirred up in clear water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's answer, when it comes, is to agree: *it was me.* See the note on Job 42:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the questions are actually doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole speech is about the ostrich and the wild donkey and the storehouses of snow and the horse that laughs at fear. It is a tour of things being cared for that no one is watching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The raven's chicks are fed. The mountain goats calve without a midwife. The rain falls *on a land where no one lives, on a desert with no one in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers a man who thinks he has been abandoned by showing him how much is being looked after where nobody can see."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Job says he gets out of it.** *I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear, but now my eye sees You.* Not an explanation. He never gets one. He gets the One he had been asking to see."}]}, "Job 38:31–33": {"title": "The constellations in their seasons", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God asks Job about specific star groups by name, and asks whether he can steer them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Can you bind the beautiful cords of **the Pleiades**, or loosen **the belt of Orion**? Can you lead out **the constellations** in their seasons, or guide **the Bear with her cubs** across the sky?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four named groups, in a book generally reckoned the oldest in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever wrote Job knew the night sky well enough to name its features and expect the reader to recognise them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Pleiades"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bind the beautiful cords.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Kimah**, and the word rendered *cords* is a word for sweet influences or chains — a bond."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pleiades are a genuine cluster. The stars in it are gravitationally associated and move together through space, which is not true of most constellations, where the stars are unrelated and only appear near each other from here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question asks about binding something that is, in fact, bound."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Orion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Loosen **the belt** of Orion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Kesil**, and the word is *bands* — the cords that tie him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Orion's belt is the most recognisable line of stars in the sky, and the three are drifting apart. In tens of thousands of years the belt will not be a belt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bound, and loosening. The two questions are exact opposites and both are put to Job."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The constellations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Can you **lead out the constellations in their seasons**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Mazzaroth** — a word that appears once in Scripture, here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is generally taken as the twelve signs, the zodiac band the sun travels through across a year, and *in their seasons* is what they do: each one rises at its own time and gives way to the next."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They come out in order, and Job is asked whether he brings them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Bear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Or **guide the Bear with her cubs**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ayish** and her sons — the great bear, and the stars around her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is to lead, to guide, the word used of a shepherd with a flock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The laws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do you know **the laws that govern the heavens**? Could you **set their rule over the earth**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *laws* is **chuqqot** — statutes, fixed decrees. The same word used of the law given at Sinai."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heavens are described as running on statutes, and having a governing effect on the earth below — which, in the plainest sense, they do: seasons, tides, the length of a day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 38, out of the whirlwind, in the middle of four chapters of questions about the sea's doors, the storehouses of snow, the ostrich, the wild donkey and the horse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is about Job's conduct. See the note on Job 38:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sky is doing, according to Scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The heavens are telling the glory of God… there is no speech, there are no words… yet their sound goes out through all the earth.* See the note on Psalms 19:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Speech with no language, going out to everybody."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God says the stars did at the beginning.** *When the morning stars sang together and all the sons of God shouted for joy* — five verses earlier in this same speech. He asks Job about their orbits, and mentions that they sang. See the note on Job 38:4–7."}]}, "Job 38:4–7": {"title": "\"When the morning stars sang together\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God's first question out of the whirlwind, and it is about a construction site."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Where were you** when I laid the foundations of the earth? Tell Me, if you understand so much.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job has been demanding an audience for thirty chapters. He gets one, and it opens with a question he cannot begin to answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vocabulary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **marked out its measurements**? Who **stretched the line** across it and set it true? On what were **its footings** sunk? Who laid **its cornerstone**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every term is a builder's term — the measuring line, the footings, the cornerstone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God describes creation as a job of work with a plan and a surveyor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was watching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"…that morning when **the stars sang together and all the sons of God shouted for joy**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two groups, or one described twice in Hebrew parallel: the morning stars, and the sons of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sons of God** — **bene ha-elohim**. In Job the phrase means angels; the same expression opens chapter 1, where *the sons of God came to present themselves before the LORD, and the adversary came among them too.* See the note on Job 1:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were present at the founding of the earth, and they were singing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that dates"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Angels existed before the earth did. They watched it being laid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which places them before Genesis 1:1 in the order of things, and puts a spectator's gallery at the creation account."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sang** and **shouted**. The Hebrew for the second is the shout of triumph — the noise Israel made when the walls of Jericho fell, and when the ark came into the camp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage is quiet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is God's opening move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's friends had argued about causes for thirty chapters. God does not enter that argument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks about the morning of the world, and then goes on for four chapters about the sea's doors, the storehouses of snow, the ostrich, the wild donkey, and the horse that laughs at fear. See the note on Job 38:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of the questions is about Job's conduct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the singing means for the man on the ash heap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is being shown a world that was made deliberately, measured, footed, and cornerstoned, and cheered while it went up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer to *why is this happening to me* is never given. What is given is the architect."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the cornerstone turns up again.** *The stone which the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone.* Job hears about the cornerstone of the earth; the Psalms and the Gospels name one the builders threw out."}]}, "Job 4:7–11": {"title": "\"Eliphaz, and what he had seen\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first friend to speak, and the ground he stands on is his own observation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where his authority comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What I have seen** is this: those who plough trouble and plant sorrow harvest exactly that.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his source. Not a word from God, not a Scripture — what he has watched happen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same thing again a few verses later: *a word was secretly brought to me… a spirit passed before my face, and the hair of my flesh stood up.* A vision in the night, a voice he could not identify."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Experience is not the mother of wisdom. The Word of God is. A man who preaches from what he has personally seen will build a rule out of a sample, and then apply the rule to a man he does not understand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Name one honest person who was ever destroyed for nothing.** Where were the upright ever cut down?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds unanswerable. It is a rhetorical question with an obvious answer, put to a man sitting in ashes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer is sitting in front of him. Job is the one honest person destroyed for nothing, and Eliphaz cannot see it because his rule will not let him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rule does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It converts the suffering into evidence. Once you hold *the upright are never cut down*, then a man who is cut down cannot be upright, and every day he stays in the ashes is another day of proof."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the machine Job is now inside for twenty-five chapters, and all three friends are feeding it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sowing and reaping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Those who plough trouble and plant sorrow harvest exactly that.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a true principle. Paul states it — *whatever a man sows, that he will also reap.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "True, and applied to the wrong man, in the wrong situation, at the worst possible hour. The problem with Eliphaz is not that what he says is false in general. It is that it is false about Job, and he says it anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four lines about lions with their teeth broken and their cubs scattered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is describing the fate of the wicked, in poetry, to a man whose ten children were killed a fortnight ago."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says about him at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"My wrath is aroused against you and your two friends, for **you have not spoken of Me what is right**, as My servant Job has.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said directly to Eliphaz, by name, in chapter 42. He is the first of the three to speak and the one God addresses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else the three of them got wrong, God's own summary of the charge is that they misrepresented **Him**."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the friends thought they were defending.** God's justice. They spent twenty-five chapters protecting His reputation, and the verdict at the end is that they had not said one right thing about Him. See the note on Job 42:7–8."}]}, "Job 40:6–8": {"title": "\"Would you condemn Me so that you could be in the right?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second speech from the whirlwind, and it contains the only direct charge God lays against Job in the entire book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Would you really cancel My justice? Would you condemn Me so that you could be in the right?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is it. Forty-two chapters, and this is the whole indictment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the boils. Not any sin the friends dug for. Not one item off Eliphaz's, Bildad's or Zophar's lists — because none of them was there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is that Job put God in the wrong in order to be in the right himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"cancel My justice\" means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to break, to annul, to make void — the word used of breaking a covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's defence had required a verdict on God. If Job is innocent and God sent the suffering, then God is unjust. That is the only place the argument could go, and Job followed it there: *it is God who has bent me down and drawn His net tight around me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The choice in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There are two positions and only one of them can be occupied. Either God is right, or I am."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job had spent thirty chapters taking the second one — and every man does, by instinct. Being wrong is the harder of the two to say out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David took the other one: *against You, You only, have I sinned… **so You are right when You speak, You are clear when You judge**.* He wrote it after the worst thing he ever did. See the note on Psalms 51:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole difference. One man justifies himself and God ends up in the dock; the other agrees God is right and finds himself in the clear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the self-justifying came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job did not start here. Chapter 1: *the LORD gave and the LORD has taken away; blessed be the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It came out of him after twenty-five chapters of men telling him to account for himself. A man who is made to defend himself long enough ends up with a case, and a case needs a defendant on the other side. See the note on Job 29:14–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Brace yourself again\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same words as chapter 38 — *stand up now, brace yourself like a strong man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is being stood up a second time, not knocked down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I know that You can do all things… I spoke of things I did not understand. **It was me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the other position, and the book turns over in that sentence. See the note on Job 42:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where Paul lands in the same place.** *For I know that nothing good lives in me — that is, in the old nature.* It is not despair; it is the end of an argument. A man who has settled that stops trying to build a defence, and can take a righteousness that was given to him. See the note on Romans 7:18–20."}]}, "Job 42:1–3": {"title": "\"It was me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job's last speech, and it is six verses long. He has been talking for thirty chapters and this is what he has left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He quotes the question back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You asked, '**Who is this who hides My counsel without knowledge?**'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God had put it as a question in chapter 38 and Job repeats it word for word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It was me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words. There is no negotiation, no *but*, no explanation of the circumstances."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not plead the ash heap, the ten graves, the boils or the friends. He answers the question that was asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he admits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I spoke of things I did not understand, wonders far too great for me, things I did not know.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that he sinned in a way that caused the disaster — that was the friends' charge and it was never true, and God will say so in five verses' time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he concedes is that he talked about something he could not see, and formed a verdict on the One running it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he stops doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing he says in the passage that follows is *I abhor myself, and repent in dust and ashes*, and the Hebrew of that first phrase is difficult — it can be read *I retract*, or *I melt away*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the case is withdrawn. Chapter 29 was a man wrapping himself in his own righteousness; here he takes it off. See the note on Job 29:14–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now I know that **You can do all things**, and that **no purpose of Yours can ever be stopped**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has agreed God is right, and the first thing he says after it is about God's ability rather than his own failure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never learns why. Not one word about the accuser, the throne room, or the wager, then or ever."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What actually changed him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear, **but now my eye sees You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an argument. Four chapters of ostriches and rainstorms and wild goats calving, and a Person on the other side of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three friends had reasoned at him for twenty-five chapters and hardened him. He sees Him once and the whole defence drops."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes immediately after.** Not a rebuke. God turns to Eliphaz and calls Job *My servant* four times in two verses, and tells the three friends that Job is the one who spoke rightly of Him. See the note on Job 42:7–8."}]}, "Job 42:10": {"title": "\"When Job prayed for his friends\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and the turn of it is in the first four words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trigger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **when Job prayed for his friends**, the LORD restored everything he had lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when he repented — that was four verses earlier and nothing was returned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when he understood — he never did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When he prayed for **them**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliphaz, who told him no innocent man was ever destroyed. Bildad, who said his children got what they deserved. Zophar, who told him he was getting off lightly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-five chapters of it. He had called them *miserable comforters* and told them they were grinding him to pieces with words, and he was right about all of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those are the three he is told to pray for, and God will not forgive them without it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He still had nothing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the order of the chapter. Job prays while he is still on the ash heap. The sheep are still dead, the children are still buried, the boils are still on him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not praying out of an abundance. He has nothing to give but the prayer, and he gives it to the men who hurt him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **restored** everything he had lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *turned the captivity of Job* — the same phrase used of God bringing Israel back from exile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is release language. He had been held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And gave him **twice as much** as he had before.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not restored to level. Doubled, and the numbers in verse 12 confirm it item by item against chapter 1: seven thousand sheep become fourteen thousand, three thousand camels become six thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What did not wait"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the verse says Job's own situation had to improve before he could pray for theirs. It says the reverse — the praying came first, and the restoring followed it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He interceded for men who did not deserve it, out of a position where he had every reason not to, and that is the sentence the whole book turns on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing that is not doubled.** He had seven sons and three daughters, and he ends with seven sons and three daughters. The livestock doubles; the children do not. Readers have always noticed it, and the usual reading is the kind one — the first ten were not lost to him."}]}, "Job 42:12–15": {"title": "Twice as much as before", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last picture in the book, and the writer takes his time over the details."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Checked against chapter 1"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["7,000 sheep → **14,000**", "3,000 camels → **6,000**", "500 yoke of oxen → **1,000**", "500 donkeys → **1,000**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every figure exactly doubled. The numbers in chapter 1 were given so this could be counted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD blessed **the last part of Job's life even more than the first**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The daughters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He named the first daughter **Jemimah**, the second **Keziah**, and the third **Keren-happuch**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sons are counted; the daughters are named. In the whole of Job the only people given personal names besides Job, his friends and Elihu are these three girls."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The names are all about beauty and scent — **Jemimah** is dove, **Keziah** is cassia, the fragrant bark, and **Keren-happuch** is *horn of eye-paint*, the flask of dark cosmetic a woman kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man came out of the ashes and named his daughters after perfume and a dove."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No women as lovely as Job's daughters\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In all the land there were **no women as lovely** as Job's daughters.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer states it as fact, in the same breath as the livestock count. It is part of the blessing being listed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beauty is something God gives, and it is written into the ledger here alongside the sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inheritance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And their father gave them **an inheritance among their brothers, right alongside the sons**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not how it was done. Daughters inherited only where there were no sons — the case of Zelophehad's daughters in Numbers 27 had to be brought to Moses specially."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job had seven sons. He gave the girls a share anyway, as equals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who has lost everything and been given it back twice over is not careful about the rules for dividing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Job died, old and full of days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *satisfied with days*. He had enough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw four generations — *his children and their children to the fourth generation* — which is one hundred and forty more years after the ash heap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is still missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is never told why. Not in these verses, not anywhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets his life back doubled, four generations of grandchildren, three beautiful daughters with an inheritance, and no explanation at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What James says the end of it proves.** *You have heard how Job held out, and you have seen what the Lord finally made of it — that He feels for people deeply, and He is kind all the way through.* The New Testament's verdict on this chapter is not about endurance. It is about what God turned out to be. See the note on James 5:10–11."}]}, "Job 42:7–8": {"title": "\"You have not spoken of Me what is right\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The verdict, and it goes the opposite way from everything the previous forty chapters seemed to be building toward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is in trouble"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My anger burns against you and your two friends.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three men who spent twenty-five chapters defending God's justice are the ones He is angry with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliphaz is named. He spoke first and he is addressed first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **you have not spoken of Me what is right**, as My servant Job has.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice, once at the start and once at the end, framing the whole instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offence is not how they treated Job — though they crushed him with words for twenty-five chapters. It is what they said about **God**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had made Him the kind of God who breaks a man to punish hidden sin, who operates a formula, who has to be defended by accusation. Every sermon they preached misrepresented Him, and that is the thing He answers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As My servant Job has\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job, who said God had netted him, bent him down, and would not answer — Job is the one who spoke rightly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said hard things and he said them to God's face. They said correct-sounding things about Him behind His back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *My servant Job* is used four times in these two verses. God keeps repeating the title over the man his friends had been treating as a defendant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they have to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take **seven bulls and seven rams**, and go to My servant Job, and offer them up as a burnt offering for yourselves.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven and seven — the largest sacrifice named anywhere in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they have to carry it to Job. Three older men driving fourteen animals across to the ash heap of the man they had been lecturing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And who has to pray"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And My servant Job will pray for you. I will accept his prayer** and not deal with you as your foolishness deserves.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sacrifice is not enough on its own. God makes their forgiveness run through the man they wronged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job has to intercede for Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar. They cannot approach without him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that makes him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man standing between God and three guilty people, praying, with a burnt offering on the fire — which is precisely the office Elihu had described back in chapter 33: *a messenger at his side, one who speaks for him, one in a thousand.* See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what it does to Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verse: *when Job prayed for his friends, the LORD restored everything he had lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when he repented. Not when he understood. When he prayed for them. See the note on Job 42:10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the verdict.** Any answer. God rebukes the friends, vindicates Job, and never once explains the boils to anybody. The book ends with the question unanswered and the man restored."}]}, "Job 8:8–19": {"title": "\"Bildad, and the generations before\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second friend, and he stands on something older than himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where his authority comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Just ask the generations who came before**; consider what their ancestors learned.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his case. Not observation, like Eliphaz — inheritance. What has always been taught."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We were born only yesterday and know nothing.\"* He disqualifies the present in favour of the past, and then speaks with total confidence about a man in front of him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tradition does not mean truth. Something can be handed down for four hundred years and still be the wrong word for the man in the ashes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The papyrus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Can papyrus grow tall where there is no marsh?** Can reeds flourish without water?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A proverb, and a good one. Cut the reed off from the water and it withers faster than grass that was never green."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That is the way of everyone who forgets God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just called Job a man who forgot God, in a metaphor, without saying his name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spider's web"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What he leans on is fragile; what he trusts is **a spider's web**. He leans on his house, and it will not hold. He grabs it, and it will not last.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The house that will not hold. Job's ten children died when a house came down on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bildad had opened his speech in this chapter with *if your children have sinned against Him, He has cast them away for their transgression.* He is not being careless. He means it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The uprooted plant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He is like a well-watered plant in the sunlight, spreading its shoots… gripping the rocks. But **tear it from its place**, and that place disowns it: 'I never saw you.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most accurate description of Job's situation in the chapter, and it is delivered as an accusation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greatest man in the East, spread out over the hills, torn out. Bildad has the picture exactly right and the diagnosis exactly wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the traditional wisdom actually says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bildad's inherited system is simple: God blesses the righteous and destroys the wicked, visibly, in this life, every time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is neat, and much of the Old Testament reads that way at a distance. The book of Job exists because it is not the whole story, and because a man was sitting in ashes for whom it was not true at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verdict on him.** *You have not spoken of Me what is right.* He quoted the fathers, he used the proverbs correctly, and God's summary of his theology was that it misrepresented Him. See the notes on Job 4:7–11 and Job 42:7–8."}]}, "Job 9:25–35": {"title": "\"There is no umpire between us\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job asks for something that does not exist yet, and names it precisely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"My days **run faster than a courier**; they flee away and **never see anything good**. They slip by **like reed boats**, like **an eagle swooping down on its prey**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three images, each faster than the last — a runner, a papyrus skiff on a river, a stooping eagle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fastest things he could name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The attempt to cheer up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If I say, **I will forget my complaint, I will change my face and smile**, I still dread all my sufferings.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tries it and reports that it does not work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For I know **You will not treat me as innocent**. **I will be found guilty anyway**, so **why should I struggle for nothing?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *treat me as innocent* is the legal word for acquittal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His problem is not the suffering. It is that he cannot see a court in which the case could go his way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The washing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Even if I **washed myself with snow and scrubbed my hands with lye**, You would plunge me into the pit, and **my own clothes would loathe me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Snow water and lye were the strongest cleaning agents available."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is saying that self-cleaning does not reach the problem — and the image at the end is that even his clothes would find him disgusting afterward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The real difficulty"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He is not a man like me**, that I could answer Him, **that we could go to court together**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disparity is the whole obstacle. There is no shared ground, no common footing, no venue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There is no umpire between us, no one to lay his hand on us both**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mokiach** — an arbiter, a referee, one who decides between two parties."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the picture he attaches is physical: someone who can put one hand on each. To do that, a person would have to be able to reach both — to be at home on both sides."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job does not know of anyone who could."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he would do if there were one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If only He would **take His rod away from me**, so His terror would stop frightening me, **then I would speak up and not be afraid of Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He wants to speak freely, and says he cannot while afraid."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But that is not how things stand with me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He closes the speech by conceding it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament calls it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For there is **one God and one Mediator** between God and men, the Man Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mesites** — the go-between, the one standing in the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Hebrews adds the other half of Job's request — a High Priest *who can have compassion, since He Himself is also subject to weakness* — a man, who can be answered, and God, who can answer. See the note on Hebrews 7:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job says four chapters later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I know that **my Redeemer lives**, and He shall stand at last on the earth… **whom I shall see for myself, and my eyes shall behold, and not another**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who said there is no umpire says this in chapter 19."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asked for.** Not relief, not an explanation, and not vindication. Somebody to stand between with a hand on each — and he says outright that no such person is available to him."}]}, "Joel 1:1–4": {"title": "Wave after wave", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens with a question and then describes what happened in four stages."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Has anything like this ever happened** in your days, or in the days of your fathers before you?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks the oldest men in the room, and the expected answer is no."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Tell it to your children**, and let them tell their children, and let their children pass it down to the ones who come after.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four generations of transmission ordered for a locust plague."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something is being preserved deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What the **gnawing** locust left, the **swarming** locust ate. What the swarming locust left, the **hopping** locust ate. And what the hopping locust left, the **destroying** locust finished off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four Hebrew words — probably four stages of the same insect's life, or four kinds of devastation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The structure is what matters: each one starts where the last one stopped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wave after wave, until nothing was left.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What locusts do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A swarm strips everything green — leaf, bark, and the vine down to the wood. Joel describes it a few verses on: *he has laid waste My vine and ruined My fig tree; he has stripped it bare and thrown it away; its branches are made white.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trees are left white. The bark is gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grain offering and the drink offering **are cut off from the house of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nothing left to bring. The worship stops for lack of materials."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel calls the priests to *gird yourselves and lament… lie all night in sackcloth*, and the cattle groan because there is no pasture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same four words come back in chapter 2, in a promise:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **restore to you the years** the locusts have eaten, the swarming locust and the hopping locust, the destroying locust and the gnawing locust, **My great army that I sent among you**.\"* See the note on Joel 2:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book names the destruction in four stages and then names all four again as the thing being restored."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is asked for in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now, therefore,\" says the LORD, \"turn to Me with all your heart… **so rend your heart, and not your garments**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The visible sign of grief was tearing your clothes. He asks for the thing the sign stood for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Joel was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The son of Pethuel.* That is all anyone knows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No date, no king named, no city. The shortest biography of any prophet."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the book runs into.** Locusts in chapter 1, an army in chapter 2, and then *I will pour out My Spirit on all flesh* — the passage Peter stood up and quoted at Pentecost. The prophet nobody can date wrote the sermon that opened the church."}]}, "Joel 2:21–24": {"title": "\"Just as before\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joel has spent a chapter and a half describing a locust invasion that stripped the country bare. This is the turn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is told not to be afraid"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times, and the first two are addressed to things that cannot hear:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Do not be afraid, **land***", "*Do not be afraid, **you wild animals**, for the pastures of the wilderness are turning green again*", "*Be glad, **you children of Zion***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ground and the animals are addressed before the people. The devastation was total, so the reversal is announced to everything it touched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gives you the early rain in right measure, and He sends the showers down for you, the early rain and the late, **just as before**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both rains, in their seasons, restored to what they used to be. The Hebrew for *in right measure* carries the sense of according to what is right — the proper amount, neither withheld nor destructive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not more than before. **As** before — which, to a country that had watched everything eaten, is the whole promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The overflow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The threshing floors will be full of grain, and the vats will overflow with new wine and oil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three crops of Deuteronomy 11, named again: grain, wine, oil. The promise is being restated in the vocabulary of the original covenant blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famous line just after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will repay you for the years the locusts have eaten.* It comes two verses on, and this passage is the run-up to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note that Joel does not say the years were not lost. He says they will be repaid — which assumes the loss was real."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter ends.** *And afterward, I will pour out My Spirit on all people.* The same verb — pour — that has been used of rain the whole way through. Peter quotes it at Pentecost, and by then the harvest language has become something else. See the note on Acts 2:1–4."}]}, "Joel 2:25–27": {"title": "\"The years the locusts have eaten\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A promise to restore something nobody can restore."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is restored"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **restore to you the years** the locusts have eaten.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the crops. The **years**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Property can be replaced and a harvest can be regrown. Time is the one commodity in the passage that cannot be handed back, and it is the one named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The **swarming** locust and the **hopping** locust, the **destroying** locust and the **gnawing** locust.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four Hebrew words, probably stages of the same insect's life cycle, and Joel opens his book with them in the reverse order: *what the chewing locust left, the swarming locust has eaten; what the swarming locust left, the crawling locust has eaten.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wave after wave, each finishing what the last one started, until nothing is left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose army it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My great army that I sent among you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel does not soften it. The locusts are called God's army, and He says He sent them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same passage that promises restoration takes responsibility for the damage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will have **plenty to eat and be satisfied**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *eat in plenty and be satisfied* — and the book opened with a land stripped bare, the grain offering cut off from the house of the LORD, and the cattle groaning because there was no pasture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Said twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Never again will My people be put to shame.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 26 and verse 27, in the same words. Joel repeats it as a bracket."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then you will know that **I am in the midst of Israel**, that I am the LORD your God, and there is no other.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The restoration has a purpose beyond the food, and it is stated: so that they will know where He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And it shall come to pass afterward that **I will pour out My Spirit on all flesh**; your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter stands up at Pentecost and quotes exactly that: *this is what was spoken by the prophet Joel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The locust chapter runs straight into the outpouring, and the New Testament dates the fulfilment to a morning in Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the locusts and the promise, Joel's call: *\"Now, therefore,\" says the LORD, \"turn to Me with all your heart… **so rend your heart, and not your garments**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tearing your clothes was the visible sign of grief. He asks for the thing the sign stood for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The reason given for the whole promise.** *For He is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness.* Joel quotes God's own self-description from Exodus 34 as the grounds for turning back. See the note on Exodus 34:5–7."}]}, "Joel 2:28–29": {"title": "\"On all people\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The promise Peter reached for on the day it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And afterward\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and it shall come to pass afterward*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "After what: the chapter before this is the locust plague and the call to return — *rend your heart and not your garments*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the restoration: *I will restore to you the years that the swarming locust has eaten*, and *you shall eat in plenty and be satisfied*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pouring out comes after the restoring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **pour out** My Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shaphak** — to pour, to spill, the word used of blood being poured out at the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a portion measured out. A vessel emptied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it lands on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **all people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *all flesh* — **kol basar**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the old arrangement the Spirit came on individuals for tasks: Bezalel for the tabernacle, the seventy elders, judges, kings, prophets. Named men, for jobs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had wished for this: *oh, that all the LORD's people were prophets, that the LORD would put His Spirit upon them!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four groups"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Your **sons and your daughters** will prophesy*", "*Your **old men** will dream dreams*", "*Your **young men** will see visions*", "*Even on **the servants, both men and women***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Male and female, old and young, free and slave. Every division that mattered in that society appears and is crossed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The servants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even on the servants, **both men and women**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **avadim** and **shephachot** — male and female slaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel names them last and separately, which puts the emphasis on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priests. Levites. The temple. Any qualification at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing in front of a crowd in Jerusalem at nine in the morning, dealing with a heckle: *no, **this is what was spoken of through the prophet Joel**.* See the notes on Acts 2:14–16 and Acts 2:17–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes the whole passage, including the wonders and the blood and fire and smoke, and ends on the last line: *whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change Peter makes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel has *afterward*. Acts has *in the last days*. Peter says which afterward it was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the divisions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither slave nor free, there is neither male nor female; for you are all one in Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three pairs Joel crosses."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is unusual about the promise.** Not the Spirit — Israel knew about that. That the list of recipients has no qualification in it, and that Joel names the slaves twice."}]}, "John 1:1–5": {"title": "\"Before anything at all began\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John opens his Gospel with the first three words of the Bible and then goes back further."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before anything at all began, **the Word was already there**. The Word **was with God**, and the Word **was God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three statements about the same subject, and each one adds something the last did not have."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He existed. He was in company. He was God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second and third do not obviously fit together, and John does not soften either to make room for the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The Word\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **Logos** — a word with a long history in Greek philosophy, where it meant the rational principle ordering the universe, and in Jewish thought, where the word of the LORD does things: *by the word of the LORD the heavens were made.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John picks a term both audiences already had and attaches it to a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the beginning\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three words that open Genesis, in the Greek Old Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 1:1 starts at the beginning. John says that when the beginning began, the Word was already there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The universal claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything that exists came into being through Him, and **in all that has been made there is not one thing** that came into being **without Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated positively and then negatively, which is John's habit throughout this Gospel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left outside the claim."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same in Colossians: *all things were created through Him and for Him. And He is before all things, and **in Him all things hold together**.* See the note on Colossians 1:15–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Life, then light"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In Him was **life**, and that life was **the light** of everyone alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order matters. Life first, and the light is what the life is to people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The light **keeps shining** in the darkness, and the darkness has **never once been able to swallow it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is present tense — it goes on shining."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **katalambano** carries both senses: to overcome, and to grasp or comprehend. The darkness neither put it out nor worked out what it was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both readings are true in this Gospel, and translators have never fully settled which John meant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the prologue lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine verses later: *and the Word **became flesh** and dwelt among us.* The Greek is *pitched His tent* — **eskenosen**, the tabernacle word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one who was there before the beginning ends up camping."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What John does not include.** No genealogy, no Bethlehem, no shepherds, no manger. Matthew starts with Abraham, Luke goes back to Adam, and John goes back past the first sentence of Genesis."}]}, "John 1:14–18": {"title": "\"Moved in among us\" — and grace on top of grace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three phrases in this passage are doing more than the English can easily carry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Moved in among us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is built on the word for a **tent**. Literally: He pitched His tent among us, He tabernacled with us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every Jewish reader knew which tent. The tabernacle in the wilderness was where the glory settled — and John's next words are *we saw His glory with our own eyes*. He is saying the presence that filled the tent has taken a body and moved into the neighbourhood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Grace on top of grace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is odd in Greek too — literally *grace instead of grace*, or grace succeeding grace. The picture is of one wave arriving as the last recedes: nothing is used up, and what replaces it is more of the same."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not grace once, and then the reader's effort. Grace, replaced by grace, out of a fullness John says we have all been drawing from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The law was given through Moses; grace and truth came through Jesus Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different verbs, and the difference is deliberate. The law was *given* — handed over, delivered as a deposit. Grace and truth **came into being** through Jesus Christ: the verb John uses of things happening, coming to pass."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say the law was bad. He says one was delivered and the other arrived as an event, in a person."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *No one has ever seen God. The only Son… He is the One who has made Him known.* The verb behind *made known* is where the word *exegesis* comes from — to lead out, to explain, to set forth in full. Jesus is described as God's own exposition of Himself. John has spent seventeen verses building to that, and then the Gospel begins."}]}, "John 1:29–34": {"title": "\"Look — the Lamb of God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John the Baptist gives Jesus a title nobody had used before, and it carries a thousand years of Israel's worship in three words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which lamb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every lamb in Israel's memory is standing behind the phrase:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the ram caught in the thicket in place of Isaac", "the Passover lamb whose blood went on the doorposts", "the two lambs offered every morning and evening in the temple", "the lamb Isaiah described, led to the slaughter and silent"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not specify. He says *the Lamb of God*, and lets the whole system answer to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Takes away\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb means to lift and carry off. Not covers, not compensates for — removes, by carrying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the difference between the old arrangement and this one. The sacrifices covered sin year after year; this one is described as taking it away and going off with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sin offering looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 4 sets out the procedure, and the order of it is worth walking through:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the man brings the animal — **the animal is examined**, not the man", "he **lays his hand on its head**, and the Hebrew is to lean, to press down", "the animal is killed in his place", "the blood is applied by the priest", "*and it shall be forgiven him*", "**and the man goes home**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about his sincerity, his record, or his resolve enters the ceremony. The inspection falls on the lamb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why the four days of examination in Passion Week matter, and why every delegation that came at Him went away with nothing, and why Pilate's verdict is *I find no fault in Him.* The lamb was looked over in public and passed. See the note on Matthew 21:23–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Of the world\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not of Israel. John the Baptist, a Jewish prophet in the Judean wilderness, extends it to the whole world in his first recorded description of Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I did not know myself who He was\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice. They were relatives — Luke has their mothers meeting before either was born — so he knew the man. What he did not know was the identity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The confirmation came by a sign he had been told to watch for: the Spirit coming down and **staying**. The verb is the one John uses all through his Gospel for abiding."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he calls himself.** Nothing. Asked earlier who he is, he says he is a voice. Here he gives his whole testimony and it is entirely about somebody else — including the line *He was there long before I ever was*, said by a man six months older."}]}, "John 1:6–13": {"title": "\"He gave the right to become\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The prologue breaks off from eternity to introduce a man with a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There came **a man, sent from God, whose name was John**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five verses about the Word who was in the beginning, and then a person with a name and a job."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek shifts too: the Word **was** — imperfect, continuous — and John **came into being**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came **as a witness, to testify about the Light**, so that through him **everyone might come to believe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **martyria** — testimony, evidence. It is a legal word, and John's Gospel runs on it: the Baptist testifies, the works testify, the Father testifies, the Scriptures testify."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **not the Light himself**; he came **to point people to it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John the Evangelist says it twice about John the Baptist — here and again a few verses on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Baptist says it himself: *\"I am not the Christ\"*, and *\"He must increase, but I must decrease.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Light"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The true Light, **the Light that gives light to every person**, was coming into the world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**True** is **alethinos** — real, genuine, as against a copy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three rejections"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prologue states it three times, each closer in:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*He was **in the world**, and the world came into being through Him, and **the world never recognized Him**.*", "*He came **to what belonged to Him**.*", "*And **His own people did not take Him in**.*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek moves from *ta idia* (his own things, neuter) to *hoi idioi* (his own people)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He made it, He owned it, and it did not let Him in the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **to everyone who did receive Him**, to all who **put their trust in His name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *receive* is **elabon** — took, accepted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave them **the right to become the children of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exousia** — authority, the right to act. Not merely permission and not merely power."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**To become** — the Greek is a verb of coming into being, the same one used of John appearing in verse 6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three negatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Born **not of bloodline**, not out of **any human wanting**, not out of **a husband's decision** — but **born of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three ways a person normally comes into a family, and all three are excluded:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Bloodline** — the Greek is plural, *bloods*: descent, ancestry, the thing the crowd in chapter 8 insists on when they say *we are Abraham's descendants*.", "**Human wanting** — the will of the flesh.", "**A husband's decision** — the will of a man."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing on the list is sinful. They are the ordinary means, and John rules all three out before naming the actual one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the prologue goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the Word became flesh and dwelt among us**, and we beheld His glory.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *dwelt* is **eskenosen** — pitched His tent, tabernacled."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is given and what it is given for.** Not a status conferred, but the right to become — and the three usual routes into a family are named and excluded first."}]}, "John 10:11–15": {"title": "The shepherd and the hired hand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says *I am the good shepherd* twice in five verses, and between the two He describes the alternative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"good\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kalos** — not merely morally good but fine, admirable, the right one for the job. It is the word used of a good tree, a beautiful thing, a work well done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What makes him good"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The good shepherd lays down His life for the sheep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not his skill or his knowledge of the country. The definition offered is a willingness to die, and it is stated before anything else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ordinary shepherds risked their lives — David fought lions and bears. But risking and *laying down* are different, and John's Gospel keeps the distinction: no one takes it from Me, I lay it down of My own accord."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hired hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sheep are not his own, so when he sees the wolf coming he abandons them and runs… he is only hired, and the sheep mean nothing to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said about him being wicked. He is not accused of stealing — that was the thief, two verses earlier. His failure is ownership: they are not his, so at the moment it costs something he leaves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know My own, and My own know Me — just as the Father knows Me, and I know the Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is astonishing and easy to read past. The mutual knowledge between Christ and His people is set on the same scale as the knowledge between the Son and the Father."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *I lay down My life for the sheep* twice in the passage — once as the definition and once as the conclusion, with the knowing in between."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who was listening.** This follows the healing of the man born blind, whom the religious leaders had thrown out of the synagogue for defending Jesus. The hirelings who scatter and abandon are described with those men present. See the note on John 10:7–10."}]}, "John 10:16–18": {"title": "\"No one takes it from Me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is in the middle of the shepherd discourse, and He says the most surprising thing in it about His own death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other sheep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There are **other sheep of Mine that do not belong to this fold**. **I must bring them in** as well.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to a Jewish audience, in Jerusalem, at the Feast of Dedication."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *I have* — present tense. They are already His before they are brought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"They will listen to My voice\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *they might*. The whole discourse works this way: *My sheep hear My voice, and I know them, and they follow Me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "One flock, one shepherd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And there will be **one flock, one shepherd**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one fold. The fold is the enclosure — the pen, the boundary. The flock is the animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel had promised it: *I will set up one shepherd over them, and he shall feed them — My servant David.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul spends a chapter of Ephesians on the same sentence: *He has made both one, and has broken down the middle wall of separation.* See the note on Ephesians 2:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the Father loves Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is why the Father loves Me: I lay down My life** so that I may take it up again.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states a reason for the Father's love, and the reason is the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four times He says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In three verses He says *I lay it down* or *take it up* four times, and each time the verb is His own:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*I lay down My life so that I may take it up again*", "*No one takes it from Me*", "*I lay it down of My own accord*", "*I have the right to lay it down, and the right to take it up*"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No one takes it from Me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to men who were picking up stones in the same chapter, in a city where a Roman governor and a temple establishment would shortly do exactly that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's account of the death keeps the verse: *He bowed His head and **gave up** His spirit.* Not *expired*. He handed it over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate is told the same thing at the trial: *you could have no power at all against Me unless it had been given you from above.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exousia** — authority, the right to act. He has it twice: to lay it down and to take it up again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The taking up again is claimed by Him here, and elsewhere in the New Testament the resurrection is attributed to the Father and to the Spirit. All three, on the same morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is what My Father has charged Me to do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Commandment, in the Greek. The laying down is obedience as well as choice, and the chapter puts both in one sentence without deciding between them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the discourse goes next.** *And there was a division again among the Jews because of these sayings.* John records the reaction to the shepherd chapter twice, and both times it splits the room."}]}, "John 10:22–26": {"title": "Winter at the Dedication", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John gives the season, the feast and the exact colonnade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The feast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was winter, and **the Festival of Dedication** had come to Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hanukkah.** Not one of the feasts commanded in Leviticus — it commemorates the cleansing of the temple by Judas Maccabeus in 164 BC, after Antiochus Epiphanes had defiled it by sacrificing a pig on the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight days, in the ninth month, with lamps."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the feast is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A defiled temple, cleaned out and rededicated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same thing Hezekiah did in sixteen days, seven centuries earlier. See the note on 2 Chronicles 29:12–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It was winter\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says it and he does not need to. Hanukkah is always in winter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tends to do this — *and it was night*, when Judas goes out — and readers have long taken the weather as part of the scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was walking in the temple courts, in **Solomon's Colonnade**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A covered portico along the east side of the outer court, sheltered from the winter weather. It was where people gathered when it was cold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The early church met there daily after Pentecost, and the healed man at the Beautiful Gate ran into it holding on to Peter and John. See the note on Acts 3:9–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How long are You going to keep us in suspense? If You are the Messiah, tell us plainly**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *how long do You lift up our soul?* — hold us in tension."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The demand is for a plain statement, and it sounds reasonable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I did tell you, and you do not believe. The works I do in My Father's name — those are the witness, and they speak for Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two answers: He has already said it, and the works are saying it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's Gospel has recorded the works — water into wine, the official's son, the man at the pool, the five thousand, the walking on water, and a man born blind, one chapter back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But you do not believe, because you are not My sheep**, just as I said to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say they are not His sheep because they do not believe. The order in the sentence runs the other way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it in the same chapter: *My sheep hear My voice, and I know them, and they follow Me.* See the note on John 10:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I and My Father are one**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they pick up stones. When He asks which good work they are stoning Him for, the answer is: *for blasphemy, and because You, being a Man, make Yourself God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked Him to tell them plainly, and He did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in dispute"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the works. The charge they bring is about who He is saying He is, and both sides agree about what He said."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the feast in the background is.** A rededication — a building cleared out and given back to God. He walks through it in winter and tells them the works are already speaking."}]}, "John 10:27–30": {"title": "Two hands, and a double negative", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the passage people are quietly reaching for when they ask whether they can be lost. It answers, and the way it answers is worth seeing in detail, because Jesus stacks the assurance four times over in three sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four layers"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**I give them life that lasts forever** — given, not lent", "**they will never be lost** — the Greek uses a double negative, the strongest form of denial the language has: not ever, under any circumstance", "**no one will ever tear them out of My hand**", "**no one is able to tear them out of My Father's hand**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, having named two hands, He closes with *I and the Father are one*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The security is not asserted once and left. It is stated, strengthened, transferred to a second pair of hands, and then sealed by saying the two are one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"no one\" covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is unrestricted. It is not *no enemy* or *no other person*. The grammar does not carve out an exception for the sheep itself, and it would be strange to read one in, since the point of the sentence is the strength of the hand rather than the grip of the sheep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notice the word Jesus uses for what cannot happen: *tear out*, snatch, seize by force. It is the language of a predator — the same word John uses earlier in the chapter for the wolf."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read the first line again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My sheep listen to My voice. I know them, and they follow Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs. Only one of them is the sheep's, and it is *listen* — and even that is described as a recognition rather than an achievement. Sheep are not clever animals. Their whole competence in this picture is knowing which voice is theirs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already said why they can: the shepherd calls them by name and goes ahead of them. Recognition is possible because He speaks first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the passage is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I and the Father are one* is not an aside tacked onto a comfort. It is the conclusion of the argument, and the people listening understood it perfectly — the next verse has them picking up stones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the assurance and the claim about His identity are the same statement. Whether you can be held depends on who is holding you, and Jesus answers that question by saying whose hand it is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the sheep who wander.** Nothing here says a sheep never strays; the chapter next door in Luke is about one that does. What this passage rules out is the sheep being *taken*, and it rules it out with the strongest negative Greek can build. Straying and being snatched are not the same thing, and only one of them is addressed here."}]}, "John 10:7–10": {"title": "The gate, and the thief", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus calls Himself two things in this chapter — the gate and the shepherd — and the first one is the stranger claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I am the gate\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sheepfolds in the hill country were stone enclosures with a single gap in the wall. There was no door. At night the shepherd lay down across the opening and slept there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shepherd *was* the gate. Nothing came in or went out except over him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the gate is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever comes in through Me will be saved, and will go in and go out and find pasture.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two directions. A gate that only let you in would be a cage; this one is for coming and going. *Going in and out* is a Hebrew idiom for living freely and safely — the phrase used of Israel's leaders moving among the people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Safety and freedom in one sentence, and the pasture is outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thief's three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The thief comes for one reason only — to steal, to kill, to destroy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, and they escalate. Take what you have, end what you are, ruin what is left. Jesus gives no other motive: *for one reason only.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have come so that they may have life, and have it overflowing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *abundantly* — beyond the necessary measure, over and above. Not merely enough life to go on with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set beside three verbs of destruction, the answer is one verb of giving, with an adverb attached that means more than required."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the thieves were.** The chapter follows the healing of a blind man whom the religious authorities threw out of the synagogue for saying who healed him. Jesus' words about hirelings who abandon the sheep and thieves who come to destroy are said with those men in the room."}]}, "John 11:1–6": {"title": "\"And so He stayed two more days\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most uncomfortable *therefore* in the Gospels, and John puts it there deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the sisters sent word to Jesus: \"**Lord, the one You love is sick.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not ask for anything. No request, no instruction, no *please come*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They name the relationship and state the situation, and leave it there. It is one of the shortest prayers in Scripture and one of the most confident."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The love, stated three times"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the one **You love** is sick* — the sisters' words", "*Now **Jesus loved** Martha, and her sister, and Lazarus* — John's own note", "and later, at the tomb, the crowd: *see how **He loved** him!*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is making sure nobody can read what follows as indifference."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He heard that Lazarus was ill, **He stayed on where He was two more days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the Greek, the word joining the love to the delay is *therefore*. **Because** He loved them, He stayed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not the connective anybody would choose. John chose it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This sickness **will not end in death**. It is for the glory of God, so that the Son of God may be glorified through it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lazarus does die. So either the statement is wrong, or *end* means something other than the moment of death — and the rest of the chapter makes clear which."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Death happens and is not the end of the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic of the delay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethany was about two days' journey away. He waits two days, then travels two days, and Lazarus has been in the tomb four days when He arrives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jewish belief of the period held that the spirit lingered near the body for three days. Four days was past any possible ambiguity — and Martha says so at the tomb: *Lord, by this time he stinks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delay put the situation beyond every explanation except one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He told the disciples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lazarus is dead. **And I am glad for your sakes that I was not there**, that you may believe.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Glad. He says it plainly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Had He come at once, Lazarus would have been healed and the sisters would have had their brother back — which is what they wanted, and less than what they got."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the waiting was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not reluctance, not distraction, and not a failure to hear. He heard, He loved, and He stayed — in that order, in one sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does when He finally arrives.** Weeps. He knows exactly what He is about to do, He arranged the timing Himself, and He still stands at the grave and cries. See the note on John 11:33–37."}]}, "John 11:11–16": {"title": "\"Our friend Lazarus\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He tells them plainly, and then has to tell them plainly again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Our friend Lazarus** has fallen asleep.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Our** friend. He includes them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kekoimetai** — has fallen asleep. It is the standard New Testament word for a believer's death, and the root of *cemetery* — a sleeping-place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am going there to wake him up**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The misunderstanding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **if he is only sleeping, he will get better**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *he will be saved* — in the medical sense, he will recover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a reasonable inference. Sleep in a sick man is a good sign."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus had been speaking of his death, but **they thought He meant the rest of ordinary sleep**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John explains it for the reader, which he does throughout the Gospel — the temple of His body, the water the woman asked for, the food the disciples did not know about. See the note on John 4:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plain version"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then He told them **plainly**, \"**Lazarus is dead**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parresia** — openly, without figure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strangest clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **for your sake I am glad I was not there**, because **now you will believe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says He is glad about the timing of a death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is about the eleven men in front of Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it when the message first came: *this sickness is not unto death, but for the glory of God, that the Son of God may be glorified through it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *so when He heard that he was sick, **He stayed two more days** in the place where He was.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delay was deliberate and John says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But let us go to him**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that is dangerous"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this: they had just left Judea because *the Jews sought again to stone Him*. The disciples had said *Rabbi, lately the Jews sought to stone You, and are You going there again?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethany is two miles from Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Thomas"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Thomas, the one called the Twin, said to the other disciples, \"**Let us go as well, so that we may die with Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the first thing Thomas says in the Gospel, and it is usually read as pessimism."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also a decision to go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is remembered for the sentence he says at the end — *unless I see in His hands the print of the nails* — and for the one after that: *my Lord and my God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens at Bethany"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four days in the tomb. Martha meets Him on the road; Mary stays in the house until she is called."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the resurrection and the life.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the shortest verse in the Bible: *Jesus wept*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knew what He was about to do and He wept anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order at the tomb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take away the stone.\"* Then the prayer. Then *\"Lazarus, come forth!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**Loose him, and let him go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The raising is His and the unwrapping is theirs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He is glad about.** Not the death. The timing — and the reason He gives is that the men standing in front of Him are about to believe something they could not have otherwise."}]}, "John 11:23–27": {"title": "\"I am the resurrection and the life\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Martha has correct doctrine and a dead brother, and Jesus moves the thing she believes from a date to a Person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your brother will rise again.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deliberately ambiguous. He means in about twenty minutes. She hears something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I know he will rise again, **in the resurrection on the last day**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not wrong. That was standard Jewish belief and Jesus taught it Himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But she has taken a promise about *now* and filed it under a date far enough away to be no comfort at all. Her brother has been dead four days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am** the resurrection and the life.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *there will be* a resurrection. **I am** it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes an event she was waiting for and identifies it as Himself, standing in front of her, in a village outside Jerusalem, on a Thursday."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the fifth of the seven *I am* sayings in John, and the only one spoken at a graveside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever believes in Me **will live, even if he dies**. And everyone who is alive and believes in Me **will never die at all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different people are described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first has died — and lives anyway. Physical death happens and does not terminate anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is alive and believing, and of him the Greek is emphatic: *ou me apothane eis ton aiona* — a double negative, shall never ever die, unto the age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever death is for a believer in that sentence, it is not the thing the word normally means. The New Testament's habit of calling it **sleep** — *our friend Lazarus sleeps*, four verses of this chapter earlier — follows from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you believe this?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts it directly, to a grieving woman, before doing anything about the grave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yes, Lord. I believe that You are the Messiah, the Son of God, the One who was to come into the world.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not answer the question He asked. She answers a bigger one — not *do you believe this claim* but *I believe who You are*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her confession is nearly word for word Peter's at Caesarea Philippi, and John puts it in the mouth of a woman in a village, mid-funeral."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weeps. Then *Lazarus, come forth.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why He waited.** John records it plainly and it is uncomfortable: *when He heard that he was sick, **He stayed two more days** in the place where He was.* And to the disciples: *Lazarus is dead. And I am glad for your sakes that I was not there, that you may believe.*"}]}, "John 11:33–37": {"title": "\"Jesus wept\" — and the word before it that is stronger", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The shortest verse in the Bible, and the sentence before it is the one that carries the weight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Something moved deep in His spirit, and He was shaken\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two Greek verbs, and neither is gentle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first, **embrimaomai**, is used in classical Greek of a horse snorting. Where it appears elsewhere in the Gospels it describes a stern, forceful reaction. It is not sorrow — it is closer to indignation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second, **tarasso**, means to stir up, agitate, throw into turmoil. It is what John uses of water being churned, and of Jesus' own soul later in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So before the tears there is anger, and it is anger at something. He is standing in front of death with the man's sisters weeping beside Him, and He is about to undo it in ten minutes' time. The reaction is not grief at a loss He cannot fix. It is fury at the thing itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the tears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus wept.* The verb is different from the one used of the crowd — theirs is loud, ritual wailing; His is quiet weeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He cries at a funeral He is about to interrupt. Nothing about the outcome required it. The tears are simply what He does in the presence of what death does to people He loves."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two reactions in the crowd.** *Look how He loved him* — and, from others, *could He not have kept this man from dying?* John reports both without picking one. The second is the sharper question and it is left standing in the text, as it usually is in life. The answer, when it comes four verses later, is not an explanation. It is Lazarus walking out."}]}, "John 13:1–3": {"title": "\"Knowing that the Father had placed everything into His hands\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John spends three verses telling you what Jesus knew, and then tells you what He did with a towel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus knew that **His hour had come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John has been counting toward this since chapter 2 — *My hour has not yet come*, said four times through the Gospel. Here it arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All the way to the end\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had loved His own who were in the world, and **He loved them all the way to the end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis telos** — to the end, to the uttermost, completely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same root as the word He speaks from the cross: **tetelestai**, it is finished. The loving and the finishing share a root."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Judas, in the middle of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The devil had already put it into the heart of **Judas Iscariot** to hand Him over.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John places this between *He loved them to the end* and *knowing the Father had given all things into His hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas is in the room. His feet are among the ones about to be washed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things He knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus knew that **the Father had placed everything into His hands**, and that **He had come from God**, and **was going back to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Total authority, settled origin, certain destination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the fullest statement in the Gospels of what Jesus was conscious of about Himself, and John puts it here on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with that knowledge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *He rose from supper and **laid aside His garments**, took a towel and girded Himself. After that, He poured water into a basin and began to wash the disciples' feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Foot-washing was the job of the lowest slave in a household — a Gentile slave, in some accounts, because a Jewish servant could not be required to do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who knew everything was in His hands took off His outer clothing and knelt on the floor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which way round it runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not write *although* He knew all this. He writes *knowing* — the security is the cause, not the obstacle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody uncertain of their position takes the lowest job. It is the man who cannot be diminished who can afford to kneel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You shall never wash my feet!\"* And the answer: *\"If I do not wash you, you have no part with Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Peter asks for a bath, and is told *he who is bathed needs only to wash his feet, and is completely clean.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You call Me Teacher and Lord, and you say well, for so I am. **If I then, your Lord and Teacher, have washed your feet, you also ought to wash one another's feet.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not lower the titles. He confirms them, and then points at the basin."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does with the same shape.** *Let this mind be in you which was also in Christ Jesus, who, being in the form of God… **made Himself of no reputation**, taking the form of a bondservant.* Philippians 2 argues it exactly this way: the descent starts from a secure height, and Paul says think like that."}]}, "John 13:27–30": {"title": "\"And it was night\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four words end this paragraph, and John did not put them there for the weather."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bread"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As soon as **Judas took the bread**, Satan entered into him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The morsel had just been handed to him by Jesus, dipped and given — which at that table was a gesture of honour, offered to a favoured guest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing Judas receives from Him is a mark of friendship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What you are about to do, do quickly.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *don't*. Not an exposure in front of the others — John says nobody at the table understood it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knows exactly what is happening and releases him to go and do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the others assumed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some of them thought that, **because Judas kept the money bag**, Jesus was telling him to buy what they needed for the festival, **or to give something to the poor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven men, three years in, and not one of them suspects him. Judas is the treasurer, and the natural assumption about him leaving is that he has gone to give money away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter Jesus had said *one of you will betray Me*, and they looked at one another *perplexed*, and each asked *is it I?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "He went out at once"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Judas took the piece of bread and **went out at once**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He obeys immediately. It is the last recorded thing he does in the upper room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it was night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John writes the way a man writes who has been thinking about it for sixty years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could have written nothing. The time is not needed — nobody is going to be confused about the hour of the Last Supper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has spent his whole Gospel on light and darkness: *the light shines in the darkness*, *men loved darkness rather than light*, *I am the light of the world*, *he who follows Me shall not walk in darkness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man walks out of the room where the Light is sitting, and John records the weather of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens the moment he is gone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verse: *\"Now the Son of Man is glorified, and God is glorified in Him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *\"A new commandment I give to you, that you love one another as I have loved you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The door closes behind Judas and Jesus starts the longest and warmest passage in the Gospels — chapters 14 to 17, the upper room discourse, and the prayer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had done to Judas' feet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier the same evening He washed them. See the note on John 13:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knew, and He knelt down in front of him anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other side of the same night.** Peter denies Him before the rooster crows, warming himself at a charcoal fire — and is restored over another charcoal fire on a beach. Two men fail on the same night. Only one comes back to breakfast. See the note on John 21:1–3."}]}, "John 14:15–17": {"title": "Another Helper who stays", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is leaving, and He tells them what is coming instead — and the word He picks for it is the one He uses for Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you love Me, **keep My commands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order runs love first, keeping second. John says the same in his letter: *this is the love of God, that we keep His commandments. And His commandments are not burdensome.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **I will ask the Father**, and **He will give you** another Helper.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus asks and the Father gives. The whole arrangement is described as a request granted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Another\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **allos** — another of the same kind, as against **heteros**, another of a different kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a substitute of a lesser sort. The same kind of person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Parakletos** — from *para*, alongside, and *kaleo*, to call. One called to stand beside you: an advocate, a counsel for the defence, a supporter in court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "English versions render it Comforter, Helper, Advocate, Counsellor. No single word carries all of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses it once more, in his letter, of Jesus: *we have an **Advocate** with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Another** Helper. The first one is the man saying it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The duration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To be with you **forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *into the age*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been with them three years and was about to be arrested."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Spirit of truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses the title three times in these chapters. Later: *when He, the Spirit of truth, has come, **He will guide you into all truth**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the world cannot do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The world cannot receive Him**, because **it does not see Him and does not know Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *will not*. Cannot. The reason given is a lack of recognition rather than a refusal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they can"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But you know Him**, because **He lives with you and will be in you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two prepositions, and the change between them is the whole point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**With** — present tense, already true."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**In** — future tense, from Pentecost onward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will not leave you orphans; I will come to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **orphanos** — the word for a child left without a father, and also for disciples left without their teacher."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just told them He is going, and the next sentence denies the thing they would conclude from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The upper room, after the supper, before the arrest. The same discourse that ends *get up now; let us go from here*. See the note on John 14:28–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is promised.** Not a replacement and not an influence. Another of the same kind as Himself, permanently, and moving from beside them to inside them."}]}, "John 14:25–27": {"title": "The Helper's job description, and a peace unlike the world's", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is hours from arrest, and He spends the time telling frightened men what will happen after He is gone. Two promises, and both are narrower and more practical than they are usually quoted as."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Helper is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Parakletos** — from *para*, alongside, and *kaleo*, to call. One called to your side. In ordinary Greek it was used of an advocate: the person who stands with you when you are in trouble, and speaks for you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "English has never found a single word for it. *Comforter* is too soft, *Counsellor* too clinical, *Advocate* too legal. The picture underneath all of them is someone summoned to stand next to you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things He is said to do here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is unusually specific, and both jobs point in the same direction:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**teach you everything**", "**bring back to your mind everything I have said to you**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are about the words of Jesus. Not new material of the Spirit's own — He teaches what Jesus taught, and He reminds them of what Jesus said. Two chapters later Jesus makes it explicit: the Spirit will not speak on His own, and He will glorify Christ rather than Himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a useful measure. Something that pulls attention away from Christ and onto itself is not doing the work described here."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "A promise to men who were about to forget"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is something quietly kind in the second job. These are the men who would scatter that night. Peter is hours from denying he knows Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not say *remember everything I told you*. He says Someone will bring it back to their minds. He is making arrangements for their forgetfulness before it happens, without mentioning it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not the way the world gives\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peace I leave with you; My peace I give to you. I do not give to you the way the world gives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The world's peace is conditional and it is mostly the absence of something — quiet while nothing is going wrong, safety while the money holds. It is also given in a particular way: with expectation attached, and withdrawn when circumstances change."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus distinguishes His gift on *both* counts. It is a different peace, and it is handed over differently. And He says it in a room where nothing outward was about to improve for any of them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"do not let your hearts be troubled.\"** It sounds like an instruction to manage a feeling, which nobody can do on command. In the sentence it comes attached to a gift just handed over — peace given, then the instruction. He is not asking them to generate calm. He is telling them what they now have to settle themselves with."}]}, "John 14:28–31": {"title": "\"Get up now; let us go from here\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the first half of the upper room discourse, and it closes with Him standing up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks them to feel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you loved Me **you would be glad that I am going to the Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are grieving because He is leaving. He tells them the leaving is good news from His side of it, and asks them to think about where He is going rather than what they are losing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The Father is greater than I\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said in the same discourse as *I and My Father are one* and *he who has seen Me has seen the Father*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is speaking as the Son in the middle of the errand — sent, obeying, about to die. The greater is about position in the sending, not about nature; John has already settled the nature question in his first sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He is telling them early"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have told you now, **before it happens**, so that **when it does happen you will believe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does this repeatedly in these chapters. The advance notice is deliberate, and it is aimed at the moment afterward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ruler of this world"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The **ruler of this world** is coming. **He has nothing in Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *he has nothing in Me* — no hold, no claim, no matching part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything that happens over the next eighteen hours happens to a man who owes nothing and can be charged with nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But the world has to know that **I love the Father**, and that **I do exactly as the Father has commanded Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives His own reason for the cross, and states it as love running upward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's Gospel says both directions in the same book: *God so loved the world*, and this. See the note on John 10:16–18, where He says the Father loves Him because He lays His life down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last words of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up now; let us go from here**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *arise, let us go hence.* He stands, and tells them to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oddity"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 15 opens *I am the true vine*, and the discourse runs three more chapters before the prayer of chapter 17 and the crossing of the Kidron in chapter 18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *let us go* and then keeps talking. The likeliest reading is that they left the room here and the rest was said walking — through the city, past the temple with its great golden vine over the doors, down toward the valley."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at the end of the walk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went out with His disciples over the Brook Kidron, where there was a garden.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passover blood from the temple drained into that brook."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The words He uses again.** In Gethsemane, when the torches appear: *rise, let us be going. See, My betrayer is at hand.* The same instruction, at the other end of the walk, with the arrest party in view."}]}, "John 14:8–11": {"title": "\"Show us the Father\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Philip asks for one thing that would settle everything, and the answer is that he has been looking at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, show us the Father, and that will be enough for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a foolish thing to want. Moses asked to see God's glory. Philip is asking for the same and saying it would be sufficient."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pathos is in *that will be enough* — a man who thinks one more thing would do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have I been with you all this time, Philip, and you still do not know Me? Anyone who has seen Me has seen the Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years of walking, eating and watching. Jesus is not surprised at the question so much as at who is asking it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the claim is unqualified: seeing Him **is** seeing the Father. Not a resemblance, not a good representation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two grounds He offers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words I speak to you I do not speak on My own; it is the Father, living in Me, who is doing His work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *believe Me when I tell you… or else believe because of the works themselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives them two ways in — take His word for it, or reason back from what they have watched Him do. He does not insist on the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this settles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything a person guesses about God's character has to be checked against this face. If Jesus is what God looks like, then the disposition on display in the Gospels — toward lepers, tax collectors, the bent-over woman, the thief on the cross — is the Father's disposition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nothing behind Jesus that is different from Jesus."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter opens.** *Do not let your hearts be troubled… I am going to prepare a place for you.* Philip's question comes minutes later, on the same night, from a man who has just been told the way and asked where it goes. The whole conversation is Jesus explaining Himself to people about to lose Him."}]}, "John 15:1–4": {"title": "\"My Father the gardener\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two people are named in the first sentence, and neither of them is the branch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The true vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the true vine**, and **My Father is the gardener**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had been called a vine repeatedly, and every time the account ends in disappointment — Isaiah's vineyard that produced wild grapes, Jeremiah's noble vine gone degenerate, Psalm 80's vine brought out of Egypt and then broken down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**True** is **alethinos** — genuine, real, the authentic one. He is not correcting the image; He is claiming to be the thing the others were pictures of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gardener"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **georgos** — a farmer, a tiller of soil. The one who owns the vineyard and works it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both roles in the sentence belong to God. Nobody else is doing anything yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two operations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Every branch in Me that **bears no fruit He takes away**, and every branch that **does bear fruit He prunes clean**, so that it will bear more.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek does a thing here that translation struggles with. **Airo** means to lift up, and also to take away — and vinedressers do lift trailing branches off the ground so they can get light."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kathairo** is to clean, to prune. The two verbs are almost the same word, and John put them side by side deliberately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both operations happen to branches, and both are the gardener's. The branch does not prune itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What pruning is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that **it will bear more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated purpose is not correction. A vine is cut back hardest where it is producing, because unpruned wood makes leaves instead of grapes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Already clean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are already clean** because of the word I have spoken to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root as *prunes clean*, one verse later. He uses the pruning word about them and then says the work is done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the agent named is His word — not their effort, not the pruning yet to come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas has just left the room. John notes it at the end of chapter 13: *he then, having received the piece of bread, went out immediately. And it was night.* See the note on John 13:27–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Remain in Me**, and I will remain in you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meno** — abide, stay, remain. It appears eleven times in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not work, not produce, not strive. Stay attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A branch cannot bear fruit by itself**; it has to remain in the vine. **Neither can you**, unless you remain in Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is from horticulture. A branch on the ground does not make grapes more slowly; it makes none."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the sentence that follows in the next verse: *apart from Me you can do nothing.* See the note on John 15:5–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where He said it.** On the way from the upper room to Gethsemane, at night — possibly passing the temple, whose gate carried a great golden vine, or through vineyards on the way to the Kidron. He is hours from the arrest and talking about fruit."}]}, "John 15:12–15": {"title": "\"I no longer call you servants\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He changes what He calls them, and gives the reason — and the reason is about information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is My command: **love one another as I have loved you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard is not a level of effort. It is a comparison, and the second half of it has already happened in front of them — He washed their feet an hour earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greatest love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No one has greater love than this: **to lay down his life for his friends**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is hours from doing it, and He says it in the present tense while walking to the garden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I do not call you **servants** any longer.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **doulos** — a slave, an owned person. It is what these men had been calling themselves and what Paul would call himself for the rest of his life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **a servant does not know what his master is doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the definition offered. Not that a slave is mistreated, or that he works harder. He is not told."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A slave receives instructions. He does not receive the plan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason for the new name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have called you **friends**, because **everything I heard from My Father I have made known to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same test, the other way round. They are friends because they have been told."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The change of status is measured by disclosure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the supper and the arrest, in the middle of the longest continuous speech in the Gospels — the upper room discourse. He spends the last free hours of His life explaining things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That itself is the demonstration of the verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What went with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are My friends **if you do what I command you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command in question is stated in verse 12 and repeated in verse 17: love one another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The friend of God before this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham is the only person in the Old Testament called it — *the friend of God*, in James and in Isaiah — and the moment it is shown is Genesis 18, when God says *shall I hide from Abraham what I am doing?* and tells him about Sodom before it happens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same test again: he was told. See the note on Genesis 18:16–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You did not choose Me, but I chose you** and appointed you that you should go and bear fruit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after that, the warning that the world will hate them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the sentence.** He does not say they became friends and were then let into the plan. He says they are friends **because** everything He heard has been made known — the telling is what the friendship consists of."}]}, "John 15:5–8": {"title": "\"I am the vine; you are the branches\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last of the seven *I am* sayings in John, spoken on the way to Gethsemane."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a branch does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the vine; **you are the branches**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A branch does not manufacture fruit. It does not decide to produce grapes, work at producing grapes, or know how a grape is made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is attached, and the sap does the rest. Everything the fruit is made of comes up through the vine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever remains in Me**, and I in him, is the one who bears much fruit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meno** — to remain, to stay, to abide. It occurs eleven times in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not work, not strive, not produce. Stay where you are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **apart from Me you can do nothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *very little*. Nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is an absolute, and He states it as the reason the previous sentence is true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had been called a vine before. Isaiah 5: God planted a vineyard, cleared the stones, put in the choicest vine — *and He looked that it should bring forth grapes, and it brought forth wild grapes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 80: *You brought a vine out of Egypt.* Jeremiah: *I planted you a noble vine… how then have you turned into the degenerate plant of an alien vine?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, the vine fails."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, in an upper room: *I am the **true** vine.* The Greek is genuine, real, the authentic one — the vine the others were pictures of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says about the asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you remain in Me, and **My words remain in you**, you may ask whatever you want, and it will be done for you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condition is not a formula. It is remaining, and His words remaining in you — and out of that, the asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jacob's line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will tie his donkey to the vine, his colt to the choicest branch. He will wash his garments in wine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob said it over Judah on a deathbed in Egypt. Sixteen hundred years later a Man of Judah's tribe sat in an upper room in Jerusalem, on the night of a cup, and said *I am the vine.* See the note on Genesis 49:8–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says the fruit is for.** *This is how **My Father** is glorified: that you bear much fruit.* The branch is not producing to prove anything about itself. The whole crop goes to the vinedresser."}]}, "John 16:12–15": {"title": "A Spirit who does not speak about Himself", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The clearest thing Jesus ever says about how the Spirit works, and it is almost entirely about restraint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus withholds, and why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is much more I have to say to you, but you are not able to carry it now.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is about bearing a weight. He is not keeping anything back for the sake of mystery; He is not loading men who are already at their limit. It is the night before the arrest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That sets up everything after it. The Spirit's coming is described first as the means by which the rest arrives — later, when they can carry it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things He does not do"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**He will not speak on His own** — He speaks what He hears", "**He will glorify Me** — not Himself", "**He will take what is Mine and make it known**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says the second one outright: the Spirit's work is to make Christ look like what He is. That is a usable test. Something that draws attention to itself, or to the person carrying it, is not doing what is described here."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"Everything the Father has is Mine.\"** Jesus adds this as the explanation for why the Spirit taking what is His counts as taking from the Father too. In four lines all three are named and their work is shown to be one work — the Father's possession, the Son's to give, the Spirit's to deliver. It is one of the plainest trinitarian sentences in the Gospels, and it arrives as an aside."}]}, "John 16:23–24": {"title": "\"Until now you have not asked for anything in My name\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus tells the disciples that something is about to change in how they pray, and He is precise about what."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What changes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On that day you will not ask Me anything.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "For three years they had asked Him directly — He was standing there. After the cross and the ascension the arrangement alters: *whatever you ask the Father in My name, He will give you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a downgrade. The request now goes to the Father, and it carries the Son's name on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In My name\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a formula appended to the end of a prayer. A name in that world meant authority to act on someone's behalf — the way a servant buying at market in his master's name was doing the master's business with the master's credit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So praying in His name is asking for what He would ask for, backed by who He is rather than by who we are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that stings a little"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until now you have not asked for anything in My name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been with Him the whole time and had never once done this. It was not possible yet — the door He is describing opens at the resurrection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it without reproach. It is a statement about timing, and the next words are an invitation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ask, and you will receive, **so that your joy may be full**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated purpose of answered prayer here is not efficiency or provision. It is joy — filled up, brought to the brim."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it sits.** This is in the middle of the last conversation before Gethsemane, in a passage about sorrow turning to joy like a woman after childbirth. He is preparing men for the worst night of their lives by telling them how they will pray afterwards."}]}, "John 17:1–5": {"title": "\"This is eternal life\" — the definition Jesus gives", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of the longest prayer of His recorded, Jesus stops to define a term, and the definition is not what most people would guess."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And this is eternal life: that they know You, the only true God, and Jesus Christ, whom You sent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not endless duration. Not a destination. **Knowing a person** — and the Greek verb is the ordinary one for knowing by acquaintance and experience, not by information."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Duration is real and John says so elsewhere. But when Jesus supplies the definition Himself, He puts the emphasis on relationship rather than length."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The hour has come\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "John has been counting down to this since chapter 2, where Jesus tells His mother His hour has *not* come. It is said again in chapter 7, and again in chapter 8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it arrives, and what it means is the cross. The prayer that follows is spoken by a man who knows what the next eighteen hours contain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I have finished the work\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said before the arrest. The verb is the same root as *it is finished* on the cross — completed, brought to its end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks of the work as done while the hardest part is still ahead, which is consistent with everything else in John: the crucifixion is described throughout as a lifting up and a glorifying, not as a defeat that gets reversed later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The request.** *Glorify Me in Your own presence with the glory I had with You before the world began.* He does not ask for something new. He asks for the return of something He had and set down — which is what Paul describes in Philippians 2 as emptying Himself. The prayer assumes a life before Bethlehem."}]}, "John 17:13–16": {"title": "\"Not to take them out of the world\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of the prayer, He asks for one thing and specifically declines to ask for another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now **I am coming to You**, and I am saying these things **while I am still in the world**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prays out loud, in front of them, on purpose. John is recording a private conversation the disciples were meant to overhear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that they may have **My joy made full in them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His joy, not a joy of their own. The Greek is *fulfilled*, brought to the top."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said hours before the arrest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have given them Your word**, and the world has hated them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two clauses are joined as cause and effect. What He handed over is what draws the hostility."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason for the hatred"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **they are not of the world, just as I am not of the world**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice in four verses — once in verse 14 and again in verse 16, identically."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's Gospel repeats things when it wants them settled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request He does not make"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am not asking You to take them out of the world**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states the prayer He is declining to pray."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They stay. Everything that follows in Acts — the beatings, the shipwrecks, the prisons, the markets and synagogues and courtrooms — happens because of this sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request He does make"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But to keep them from the evil one**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be *from evil* or *from the evil one*; the same phrase ends the Lord's Prayer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Keep** is **tereo** — to guard, to watch over, to hold in custody. He has already used it of Himself in verse 12: *while I was with them, I kept them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is handing over a watch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things held together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not out of it, and kept inside it. The prayer asks for protection in the place rather than removal from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Sanctify them by Your truth**. Your word is truth. **As You sent Me into the world, I also have sent them** into the world.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sent in, not called out. The commission in the next breath repeats the decision."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who else is in the prayer.** *I do not pray for these alone, but also for those who will believe in Me through their word.* Two verses later, everybody reading it is named in it."}]}, "John 17:17–19": {"title": "Set apart by the truth", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three sentences in the prayer, and the same verb runs through all of them — including one He applies to Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Set them apart for Yourself by the truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hagiason** — sanctify, make holy, set apart for a purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used of the tabernacle vessels, the priests, and the Sabbath. Something ordinary designated for one use."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your word is truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, and no article on *truth* in the Greek — *your word **is** truth*, not merely true."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the instrument. Not effort, not separation from the world, not ritual."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just asked the Father *not to take them out of the world*. See the note on John 17:13–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As You sent Me into the world, so I have sent them into the world**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two sendings are put in the same sentence with the same verb, and the second is measured by the first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Into the world** — the second time in five verses He insists on the direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **for their sake I set Myself apart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, **hagiazo**, and now He is both the one doing it and the one it is done to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not being made holy. There is nothing in Him to purify — He had said so an hour earlier: *the ruler of this world is coming, and he has nothing in Me.* See the note on John 14:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sense is the older one: set apart for a purpose. A priest consecrating himself for what he is about to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was setting Himself apart for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The garden is on the other side of this prayer. John 18 opens *when Jesus had spoken these words, He went out with His disciples over the Brook Kidron.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that they too may be truly set apart by the truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *sanctified in truth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His consecration is stated as the ground of theirs. The order in the sentence runs one way only."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else is in the prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses later: *\"I do not pray for these alone, but **also for those who will believe in Me through their word**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody reading it is named in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks for them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That they all may be one, as You, Father, are in Me, and I in You.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end: *\"Father, I desire that they also whom You gave Me **may be with Me where I am**, that they may behold My glory.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is prayed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Out loud, in front of them, between the supper and the arrest. See the note on John 17:9–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The three uses of one verb.** Set them apart; I send them as I was sent; I set Myself apart for them. The middle clause is the errand, and the outer two are the same act done at both ends of it."}]}, "John 17:20–23": {"title": "\"Loved them just as You have loved Me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last section of the longest prayer of Jesus recorded anywhere, and it widens to include people who did not exist yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He is praying for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am **not praying for these alone**. I am praying also for **everyone who will believe in Me through their word**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven men in a room, and He prays past them to everybody who would believe on the strength of what they said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is every Christian who has ever lived, prayed for by name of category, hours before the arrest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**That they may all be one**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said four times in these verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The standard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Just as You, Father, are in Me and I am in You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one in the sense of an alliance or an agreement. The comparison offered is the union of the Father and the Son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that **the world may believe** that You sent Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And again in the next verse: *\"so that **the world may know** that You sent Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, and both times the reason for the unity is what people outside would conclude from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He gave them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The glory You gave Me I have given to them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense, said the night before the crucifixion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever that glory is, He describes it as already handed over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"…and have **loved them just as You have loved Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not loved them well, or loved them greatly. *Just as* — the same measure as the Father's love for the Son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks the world to know two things: that He was sent, and that these people are loved on that scale."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the prayer has come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 17 has three movements: He prays for Himself, then for the eleven, then for everybody who will believe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For the eleven: *I have kept them; not one of them is lost except the son of perdition.* See the note on John 17:9–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus had spoken these words, **He went out with His disciples over the Brook Kidron**, where there was a garden.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer ends and He walks straight to Gethsemane."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does not pray"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here asks for their circumstances to improve. Earlier in the chapter: *I do not pray that You should take them out of the world, but that You should keep them from the evil one.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says about being heard.** Standing at Lazarus's tomb: *Father, I thank You that You have heard Me. And I know that You always hear Me.* This prayer was prayed by somebody who had said that."}]}, "John 17:24–26": {"title": "\"That they may be with Me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last three verses of the prayer, and the first word of them is not a request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Father, **I want**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thelo** — I will, I desire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everywhere else in the prayer He asks: *I pray for them*, *keep them*, *sanctify them*. Here He states what He wants."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He wants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones **You have given Me** to be **with Me where I am**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Given Me** — the phrase runs through the whole prayer. He uses it seven times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Where I am** — the same words He used in the upper room: *I go to prepare a place for you… that where I am, there you may be also.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that they may **see My glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not share it, in this clause. See it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had asked earlier in the prayer for the other thing: *the glory which You gave Me I have given them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the glory comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The glory **You gave Me because You loved Me before the world was made**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *before the foundation of the world*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The loving is dated earlier than the creation, and the glory is attributed to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Righteous Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has said *Holy Father* earlier in the prayer, and *Father* throughout. This is the only *righteous* one, and it comes attached to a statement about the world not knowing Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three knowings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The world **has not known You**, but **I have known You**, and **these have known that You sent Me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses in descending scale — the world, Himself, and the eleven men in the room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the disciples know is the smallest of the three, and it is enough to put them on the right side of the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have **made Your name known to them**, and **I will go on making it known**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two tenses. Done, and continuing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it at the start of the prayer: *I have manifested Your name to the men whom You have given Me out of the world.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the name is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a sound. In Hebrew usage a name is what somebody is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the Gospels the disclosure has a specific shape: He teaches them to say **Father**, and He says **Abba** in Gethsemane — a word so particular that Paul and Mark both leave it untranslated. See the note on Galatians 4:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the love with which You loved Me may be in them, and I in them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the love with which You loved Me* — the same love, not a comparable one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last request of the prayer, and it asks for the love named in verse 24 — the one that predates the world — to be located inside people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last four words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I in them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer ends there. The next verse is *when Jesus had spoken these words, He went out with His disciples over the Brook Kidron.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was listening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven men, hours from scattering. One of them would deny Him before morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prays this over them out loud."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He asks for last.** Not protection and not success. That the love He was loved with before the world existed would be inside the people in the room — and then He goes out to the garden."}]}, "John 17:9–12": {"title": "\"Not one of them is lost\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus prays for a specific group of people, and the way He describes them is worth reading slowly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones You have given Me, because they are Yours… Everything that is Mine is Yours, and everything Yours is Mine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are described as a gift from the Father to the Son, and the ownership is mutual. Nothing in the passage describes them as having attached themselves to Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four times in these verses they are *the ones You have given Me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Holy Father, keep them safe by Your own name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not by their vigilance. By the Father's name — the same name Jesus says He had been keeping them safe by while He was with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request is for continuity: I am leaving, You keep doing what I have been doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those You gave Me I guarded, and not one of them is lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is hours from the cross, giving an account of the men He was given — men who are about to scatter, one of whom will deny Him three times before morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He counts Peter among the not-lost while knowing exactly what Peter is going to do that night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Except the one doomed to be lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not soften it and neither should any reading of it. Judas is named as an exception, and the sentence attaches it to Scripture being fulfilled rather than to a failure of keeping."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it matters that He prayed it out loud.** The disciples were in the room. Jesus could have prayed this privately; John records it because it was said in front of them. Men who were about to fail badly heard themselves described as given, guarded, and kept — before the failing happened."}]}, "John 19:28–30": {"title": "\"It is finished\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three words in English, one in Greek, and it was a word people used every day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Tetelestai"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **teleo** — to complete, to bring to its end. In the perfect tense: finished, and the result stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It turns up on business documents of the period stamped across a bill: **paid in full**. It was used of a task discharged, a debt settled, an obligation met."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I am finished*. **It** is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says was finished"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that everything had now been brought to completion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it before the word is spoken, and he uses the same root. The completion is stated as an accomplished fact, and the shout is Jesus announcing it rather than discovering it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hyssop"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They soaked a sponge in it, put it on a stalk of hyssop, and lifted it to His mouth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only John mentions hyssop, and it is a strange plant for the job — a small bushy herb, not an obvious tool for reaching a man on a cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the plant Israel was told to use to brush the lamb's blood on the doorposts at Passover, and the plant used in the cleansing rituals of Leviticus. John is writing his Gospel around a Passover, and he puts hyssop at the mouth of the Lamb of God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Gave up His spirit\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is active. John does not say He died; he says He handed it over — the same claim Jesus made in chapter 10: *no one takes it from Me. I lay it down of My own accord.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He was thirsty for.** John notes it was said *so that the Scripture would come true* — Psalm 69, in a psalm of suffering. The last thing recorded before the shout is a prophecy being deliberately completed. Even the thirst is part of what was being finished."}]}, "John 19:31–34": {"title": "The spear and the water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John records a piece of Roman procedure and one thing that did not go according to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were in a hurry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **the day of preparation**, and the Jewish leaders did not want the bodies left on the crosses through the Sabbath, **since that Sabbath was a high holy day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passover Sabbath. Deuteronomy required a hanged body to be taken down the same day — *his body shall not remain overnight on the tree… for he who is hanged is accursed of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men who arranged the execution are concerned about ceremonial defilement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The procedure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they asked Pilate to have **the legs of the men broken** and the bodies taken away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **crurifragium** — the legs smashed with an iron mallet. It killed by removing the ability to push up to breathe, and it was the standard way of finishing a crucifixion quickly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The soldiers came and did this to **the first man, and to the other one** crucified with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both robbers. The soldiers work along the row."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when they came to Jesus and **saw that He was already dead, they did not break His legs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They stop because the procedure is unnecessary, not out of any regard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why John records it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses on, he says: *for these things were done that the Scripture should be fulfilled, \"**Not one of His bones shall be broken**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the Passover law from Exodus 12 — of the lamb: *nor shall you break one of its bones*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Psalm 34: *He guards all his bones; not one of them is broken.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead, **one of the soldiers pierced His side with a spear**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enuxen** — jabbed, pierced. A professional's check."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **at once blood and water came out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John gives it as an observation, and in the next verse insists on his own presence: *and he who has seen has testified, and his testimony is true; and he knows that he is telling the truth, so that you may believe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops the narrative to swear to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second Scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And again another Scripture says, \"**They shall look on Him whom they pierced**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah 12, and Revelation quotes it too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rock that was struck"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strike the rock, and water will pour out for the people to drink*, and God's own clause: *I myself will stand there before you, on the rock.* See the note on Exodus 17:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's identification: *that Rock was Christ.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John writes later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is He who came **by water and blood** — Jesus Christ; not only by water, but by water and blood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He returns to the pair in his letter, decades on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph of Arimathea, a secret disciple, asks Pilate for the body. Nicodemus comes with a hundred pounds of myrrh and aloes — the man who first came at night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of them come out into the open once He is dead."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the soldiers decided.** Not to break the legs, because there was no point — and John says a Passover regulation about a lamb's bones was kept by a Roman working party who had never heard of it."}]}, "John 19:8–11": {"title": "\"Given to you from above\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The governor asks two questions and gets silence for one and a correction for the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is afraid"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Pilate heard that, he was **more afraid than ever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he had just heard: *we have a law, and according to our law **He ought to die, because He made Himself the Son of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**More afraid than ever** — the Greek is *he was the more afraid*, which means he was already."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His wife had sent word that morning: *have nothing to do with that just Man, for I have suffered many things today in a dream because of Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went back inside and said to Jesus, \"**Where do You come from?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *who are You*. Where from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman religion had categories for men descended from gods, and Pilate is asking inside them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Jesus gave him no answer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *gave him no answer*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had answered Pilate's earlier question about the kingdom at length. This one He does not take."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah: *He was oppressed and He was afflicted, **yet He opened not His mouth**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will not speak to me?** Do You not know that **I have the authority to release You and the authority to crucify You**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate states his position accurately. He did have both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already tried to use the first half — three times he says *I find no fault in Him* — and had scourged Him hoping it would be enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You would have no authority over Me at all if it had not been given to you from above**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exousia** — authority, the right to act."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not deny the authority. He locates its source, and the sentence puts Pilate inside an arrangement he did not set up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the general form of it: *there is no authority except from God, and the authorities that exist are appointed by God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That is why **the one who handed Me over to you carries the greater guilt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meizona hamartian** — greater sin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *no guilt* for Pilate. **Greater** for the other party, which means both are real and one is larger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Who handed Him over: Caiaphas and the council, in John's account — *the chief priests and officers delivered Him to Pilate*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Pilate does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From then on **Pilate sought to release Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tries again, and the crowd produces the sentence that ends it: *if you let this Man go, **you are not Caesar's friend***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tiberius's reign was not a good time to have that said about you. Pilate sits down on the judgment seat and hands Him over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wrote"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Pilate wrote a title and put it on the cross. And the writing was: **JESUS OF NAZARETH, THE KING OF THE JEWS**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In three languages, and when the chief priests objected: *what I have written, I have written.* See the note on Luke 23:32–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing he would not revise."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is conceded and what is corrected.** Jesus does not tell Pilate he lacks the power. He tells him where it came from — and then says the greater weight is on somebody else in the room."}]}, "John 2:1–5": {"title": "\"They have no more wine\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first sign in John's Gospel happens at a party, and it starts with an embarrassment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What running out meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jewish wedding feast could last a week, and providing for the guests was the family's responsibility. Running out of wine was not a catering inconvenience — it was a public humiliation the village would remember for years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody was in danger. Nobody was ill. The need was social, and Mary brings it to Him anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They have no more wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire request. No instruction, no suggestion, no argument about why He should act. She states the situation and stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Dear woman, what does this have to do with you and Me? My hour has not come yet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It reads sharply in English. The address is not harsh in Greek — it is the same word He uses to her from the cross. The idiom behind *what does this have to do with you and Me* is a way of saying the matter is not ours to be involved in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *My hour*, in John, always means the cross. He is saying the time for public disclosure has not arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His mother turned to the servants and said, \"Whatever He tells you, do it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not argue with Him and she does not withdraw the request. She hands the matter over and instructs the staff to obey whatever comes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the last recorded words of Mary in Scripture, and they are about doing what He says."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He then makes.** Water in six stone jars kept for ceremonial washing — the vessels of purification rites — turned into the best wine of the feast. The master of the banquet says the good wine has been saved till last, and has no idea where it came from. Only the servants who filled the jars knew."}]}, "John 2:18–22": {"title": "Forty-six years", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They ask for credentials and He gives them one they cannot use."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are asking about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just cleared the temple — the money changers, the tables, the doves, the whip of cords."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What sign will You show us** to prove You have the right to do all this?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A fair question by their lights. Somebody disrupting temple business ought to be able to say who authorised it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Tear this temple down, and in three days I will raise it up again.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He offers a sign that cannot be produced on demand, and can only be verified after the fact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is imperative — *destroy it* — which is not an instruction so much as a condition. Do that, and watch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It has taken **forty-six years** to build this temple, and You are going to raise it up in three days?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod began the reconstruction around 20 BC, and work on it continued for decades — it was finally finished in the AD 60s, a few years before the Romans destroyed it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their figure dates this scene to about AD 27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the temple He was speaking about was His body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John supplies the explanation, and he is the only one who does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for temple here is **naos** — the sanctuary, the inner house, not the whole complex."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same word of believers: *do you not know that you are the temple of God?* See the notes on 1 Corinthians 3:16–17 and 1 Corinthians 6:18–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When they understood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **when He was raised from the dead**, His disciples remembered that He had said this, **and they believed** the Scripture and the word Jesus had spoken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the time. Years later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says it about several things in this Gospel — *these things His disciples did not understand at first; but when Jesus was glorified, then they remembered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were in the room, heard it, and it meant nothing until Sunday morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else remembered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests did. At the trial: *we heard Him say, \"I will destroy this temple made with hands, and within three days I will build another made without hands.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the cross, passers-by shook their heads: *You who destroy the temple and build it in three days, save Yourself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His enemies quoted it back at Him for three years. His friends worked it out afterwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after the burial the chief priests went to Pilate: *we remember, while He was still alive, how that deceiver said, \"**After three days I will rise**.\"* They asked for a guard on the tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They took it more literally than the disciples did."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sign turned out to be.** He was asked for authority to clear a building and pointed at His own body. The one He was standing in came down forty years later, and has not been rebuilt."}]}, "John 2:9–11": {"title": "\"You have kept the best until now\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first miracle, and almost nobody at the party knows it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had **no idea where it had come from**, though **the servants who had drawn it knew perfectly well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John records both halves. The master of the feast, the man whose job was to know about the wine, does not know. The servants do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people who carried the water jars are the only ones in the room who saw anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six stone waterpots, standing there *according to the manner of purification of the Jews* — for washing hands and vessels, not for drinking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Fill the waterpots with water.\" And **they filled them up to the brim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty or thirty gallons each. Six of them. Somewhere between a hundred and a hundred and eighty gallons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The compression"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wine takes a vine, a season, a harvest, pressing, and time in a jar. Every stage of it is time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the drawing and the tasting there was none of that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the master says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Everyone puts out the good wine first**, and then brings on the cheaper wine once the guests have had plenty. But **you have kept the best until now**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is complaining, in the way a professional complains, that the order is wrong."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is speaking better than he knows. It is John's habit throughout the Gospel — Caiaphas does the same thing later, and John points it out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John calls it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This was **the first of His signs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **semeion** — a sign, something pointing beyond itself. John never calls them miracles; he counts seven signs and this is number one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He did it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At Cana in Galilee* — a village nobody outside that district had heard of, at a family wedding, for people who had run out of drink."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first sign is not in the temple, not in Jerusalem, and not in front of anybody who mattered. It solves a social embarrassment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **let His glory be seen**, and **His disciples put their trust in Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things. The glory was shown, and a handful of men believed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is recorded about the crowd, the bridegroom, or the master of the feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What His mother said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whatever He says to you, do it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her last recorded words in Scripture, spoken to the servants — the only people in the story who end up seeing anything. See the note on John 2:1–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The second sign.** A nobleman's son healed at Capernaum, twenty-odd miles away, at the exact hour Jesus spoke. The first sign compresses time; the second crosses distance. John records them as the first two, in that order. See the note on John 4:46–50."}]}, "John 20:11–13": {"title": "One at the head and one at the feet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter and John have looked into the tomb and gone home. Mary stays and cries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "She stayed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Mary stayed outside by the tomb, crying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two disciples had run there, looked in, seen the linen cloths, and *went away again to their own homes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has nowhere to go. The Greek for her weeping is the loud kind — wailing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And while she was crying **she bent down and looked into the tomb**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She looks in after the men had already looked in and found it empty. There is no reason to expect anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Two angels in white, seated where the body of Jesus had been laid, **one at the head and one at the feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the detail, and it is a description of a piece of furniture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mercy seat sat on top of the ark, with *two cherubim of gold… one cherub at one end, and the other cherub at the other end*, facing each other across it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Once a year the high priest went in and sprinkled blood on the space between them, and God said: *there I will meet with you, and I will speak with you from above the mercy seat, from between the two cherubim.* See the note on Exodus 25:17–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two angels, one at each end, and between them the place where a body had lain and blood had been shed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary is looking into the holiest place, from outside, and it is empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Woman, why are you crying?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Angels are asking a question they can hardly need the answer to, in a tomb that is empty because of good news."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**They have taken away my Lord, and I do not know where they have put Him.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She says almost the same words three times in this chapter — to Peter and John, to the angels, and then to the gardener."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her grief is not that He is dead. It is that she has lost the body and cannot perform the last service she came to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She turns round, sees Him, and takes Him for the gardener — in a garden, on the first day of the week."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says one word. *Mary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And she is the first person on earth to see Him alive, and the first commissioned to tell anybody: *go to My brethren and say to them…*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the angels are sitting.** *Where the body of Jesus had been laid.* John notes the position because it is the point. The room in the tabernacle that nobody could enter is now a place a weeping woman is leaning into, and the seat is empty."}]}, "John 21:1–3": {"title": "A night with nothing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "After the resurrection, seven men go back to work, and it is the same lake and the same result as the first time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By the **Sea of Tiberias*** — the Sea of Galilee, in the territory of Zebulun and Naphtali."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zebulun's blessing was *a harbour for ships*, and Zebulun's name means dwelling. He had lived there, taught there, and done most of His work on that shoreline. See the note on Genesis 49:13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Simon Peter, Thomas, Nathanael, the sons of Zebedee, and two others.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven of them. Peter first, and Thomas next — the one who had said he would not believe unless he put his hand in the side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I am going fishing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words in the Greek, and John gives no explanation for them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the trade Peter left when he pulled two boats onto the shore, full of fish, and walked away from the catch of his life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They said to him, \"We will come with you.\"* Nobody argues."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All that night they caught nothing at all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same lake, the same trade, the same night hours, the same empty nets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is exactly what Luke 5 recorded before any of them followed Him: *we worked hard all night long and came up with nothing at all.* See the note on Luke 5:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years of walking with Him, and the fishing goes the same way it always did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is waiting on the beach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the morning had now come, Jesus stood on the shore; yet the disciples did not know that it was Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks whether they have any food, tells them which side to cast on, and the net comes up with a hundred and fifty-three fish and does not break."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they had come to land, they saw a **fire of coals** there, and **fish laid on it, and bread**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had breakfast cooking before they got there. He did not need their catch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charcoal fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word for it, **anthrakia**, occurs twice in the whole New Testament, both in John."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other one is in the high priest's courtyard, where *the servants and officers had made a fire of coals… and Peter stood with them and warmed himself*, and denied Him three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's restoration happens over the same kind of fire, and he is asked three questions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He handled that morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The work — a net so full they could not haul it in. The body — bread and fish already on the coals. And the thing Peter could not say out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three, and He did not raise the third until the man had eaten."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does not mention.** The denial. Not once. He asks *do you love Me?* three times and never says why He is asking three times."}]}, "John 21:15–17": {"title": "\"Do you love Me?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three questions on a beach, and Peter had denied Him three times beside another fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they had **finished breakfast**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and fish, cooked on *a fire of coals* — the Greek is **anthrakia**, a charcoal fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word occurs twice in the New Testament. The other is the courtyard where Peter warmed himself and said *I am not*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John chose the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Simon, son of Jonas**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Peter. Three times, He uses the name the man had before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you love Me more than these do?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is in it. Peter had said, hours before the arrest, *even if all are made to stumble because of You, I will never be made to stumble*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asked about that claim once, and never again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two Greek verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus asks with **agapao** the first two times and **phileo** the third."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter answers with **phileo** all three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have divided over whether the change is significant since the early centuries. John uses the two words interchangeably elsewhere in the Gospel, and the whole conversation would have been in Aramaic."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not in doubt is that Peter does not raise his answer to meet the question, and that the third time Jesus comes down to Peter's word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter appeals to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yes, Lord, **You know** that I love You.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times he appeals to what Jesus knows rather than to what he can promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not make a claim about his own reliability. He had done that once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **it grieved Peter that He asked him a third time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **elupethe** — was pained, distressed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the account needs the number explained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **You know everything. You know that I love You**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three commissions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Feed My lambs**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take care of My sheep**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Feed My sheep**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different verbs — **bosko**, to feed, and **poimaino**, to shepherd — and two different nouns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each answer is met with a job, and the job is not about Peter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose sheep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My** lambs. **My** sheep. Three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter picks the word up in his own letter: *shepherd the flock of God which is among you… **and when the Chief Shepherd appears**, you will receive the crown of glory.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Most assuredly, I say to you, when you were younger, you girded yourself and walked where you wished; but **when you are old, you will stretch out your hands**, and another will gird you and carry you where you do not wish.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John adds: *this He spoke, signifying by what death he would glorify God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *\"**Follow Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two words He had said on the shore three years earlier. See the note on Mark 1:16–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter does immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Turns round, sees John, and asks *but Lord, what about this man?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If I will that he remain till I come, what is that to you? **You follow Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Back at the lake, fishing. *Simon Peter said to them, \"I am going fishing.\"* See the note on John 21:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had caught nothing all night."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not asked.** Whether he is sorry, and whether he will do better. Three questions about one thing, and Peter answers all three by pointing at what Jesus already knows."}]}, "John 21:20–23": {"title": "\"What is that to you?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just been restored — three questions, three answers, and a commission — and then he immediately looks over his shoulder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, and what about this man?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has just told Peter how he will die. Peter's response is to point at somebody else and ask what their arrangement is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is such an ordinary reaction that John, who was the man being pointed at, has no interest in softening it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I want him to remain until I come, what is that to you? You follow Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last two words are emphatic in Greek — *you*, as opposed to him. Jesus does not tell Peter what will happen to John, and He does not rebuke the curiosity at length. He redirects it in four words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction Peter is given is the same one he was given at the beginning, on the same shore, three years earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction John adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the word went around that this disciple would not die. But Jesus had not said that.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is writing decades later, presumably as the last of them alive, and he stops to correct a rumour that had grown up around a conditional sentence. *If I want* had become *he will*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a small, careful act of honesty, and it tells you something about the writer: given the chance to leave a flattering story standing, he prints the exact words instead."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it leaves the reader.** Each of them gets a different road — one martyred young, one apparently growing old — and neither is told the other's. The only instruction that fits both is the one Jesus gives: *you follow Me.*"}]}, "John 3:14–18": {"title": "The snake on the pole, and why it is attached to the famous verse", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John 3:16 almost never gets quoted with the sentence before it, and that sentence is the reason it says what it says."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison Jesus chose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as Moses lifted up the snake in the wilderness, so the Son of Man must be lifted up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The story is in Numbers 21. The people are dying of snakebite, and God's remedy is not to remove the snakes. It is a bronze snake on a pole — an image of the very thing killing them, raised up where everyone can see it. Anyone who looked at it lived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They did not have to understand it, deserve it, or do anything but look."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the picture Jesus reaches for to describe His own death, and the verse everybody knows follows immediately from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Lifted up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **hypsoo** means to raise up, and also to exalt. John uses it three times in this Gospel of the crucifixion, and each time both senses are live at once: lifted onto a cross, and lifted to glory. The word will not let you keep the two apart."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the famous verse actually claims"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For God loved the world so much that He gave His only Son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The love comes first and the giving follows from it. That order matters, because the sentence is often heard the other way round — as though the cross persuaded a reluctant God to love. John says the love was the reason for the gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the scope is *the world* — the same word John uses elsewhere for the world that does not know Him. Not the deserving part of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verses nobody quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God did not send His Son into the world to condemn the world, but so that the world might be rescued through Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is verse 17, and it exists because verse 16 raises the question. Jesus states the purpose negatively and positively in the same breath: not to condemn; to rescue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then verse 18 draws the line: whoever believes is not condemned; whoever does not *stands condemned already*. Not *will be* — already. The condemnation is the situation being rescued from, not a punishment for refusing the rescue."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the snake, once more.** Israel kept that bronze snake for centuries and eventually started burning incense to it, until Hezekiah broke it up. Even the right symbol becomes a problem when people look at the object instead of what it points to."}]}, "John 3:25–30": {"title": "\"He must increase\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John's disciples bring him a complaint, and his answer is the last thing recorded of him in this Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Rabbi, the man who was with you on the other side of the Jordan, **the one you testified about** — He is baptizing now, and **everyone is going to Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not use the name. *The man who was with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are indignant on their teacher's behalf, and the phrase they use — *the one you testified about* — makes the grievance worse in their telling: he vouched for the man taking his crowd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The premise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one can receive a single thing unless it is given to him from heaven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers the crowd question with a statement about supply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It applies both ways. Whatever Jesus is receiving was given; whatever John had was given too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reminder"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You yourselves can testify that I said, '**I am not the Messiah; I was sent on ahead of Him**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it plainly to the delegation from Jerusalem: *I am not the Christ*, and *I am not* to Elijah, and *no* to the Prophet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am the voice of one crying in the wilderness.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The one who has the bride is the bridegroom**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bride belongs to the groom. Nobody in a wedding is confused about this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The friend"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The bridegroom's friend stands nearby, listening for him**, and he is **flooded with joy the moment he hears the bridegroom's voice**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **shoshbin** — the groom's friend in Jewish custom, who arranged the wedding, managed the details, and had one job on the night: to stand at the door and wait."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When he heard the groom's voice he handed everything over and his work was finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His joy is stated as coming from a sound."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**That is my joy, and it is complete**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pepleromai** — filled full, perfect tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say he is coping with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He must become greater and greater; I must become less and less**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **auxanein** and **elattousthai** — to increase and to be diminished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Must** — the Greek is **dei**, it is necessary. He states it as the way things are arranged, not as something he is bracing for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was arrested by Herod for saying the marriage to Herodias was unlawful, and beheaded on a birthday."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From prison he sent two disciples to ask *are You the Coming One, or do we look for another?* — which is the question of a man in the dark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answered with a list of what was happening, and then turned to the crowd and said *among those born of women there has not risen one greater than John the Baptist*. See the note on Matthew 11:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said about his joy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He was the burning and shining lamp, and **you were willing for a time to rejoice in his light**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last words in the paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses that follow — *the One who comes from above is above everything* — read as either John continuing or the Evangelist taking over. The Greek gives no quotation marks. See the note on John 3:31–36."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the friend is for.** To stand at the door and listen. John's answer to losing his crowd is that his joy was never in having one — it was in hearing a voice, and he says it is now full."}]}, "John 3:31–36": {"title": "\"Not in measured amounts\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last paragraph of John 3, and it is hard to tell where the Baptist stops speaking and the writer starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's disciples had come to him complaining that everyone was going to Jesus instead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer: *a man can receive nothing unless it has been given to him from heaven… **He must increase, but I must decrease**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this, and no quotation marks in the Greek to say who is talking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The origin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The One who **comes from above is above everything and everyone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in three lines. John's Gospel builds on the contrast: *you are from beneath; I am from above*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two speakers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever **belongs to the earth is of the earth, and speaks as one who belongs to the earth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an insult. A statement about range. A man can only report from where he has been."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The testimony"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He **testifies to what He has seen and heard**, and **no one accepts His testimony**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the legal word — **marturia**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**No one** — and then, immediately, the exception."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What accepting it means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever does accept it has **put his own name to this: that God tells the truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *has set his seal that God is true*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A seal was a signature — pressed into wax to certify a document."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Believing is described as countersigning a statement about God's character."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says the reverse in his letter: *he who does not believe God has made Him a liar*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Since **God does not hand Him the Spirit in measured amounts**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not by measure* — **ou ek metrou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rabbis spoke of the Spirit being given to the prophets in measures. This clause removes the measuring cup."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Father has done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Father loves the Son and has put everything into His hands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both clauses in one line, and John repeats the pair through the Gospel: *the Father loves the Son and shows Him all things*, and at the supper *knowing that the Father had given all things into His hands*. See the note on John 13:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The love and the handing-over are always together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two outcomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever **believes in the Son has life that never ends**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present tense. Has."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever **refuses to believe the Son will not see life**; **the anger of God remains on him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *refuses to believe* is **apeithon** — disobeying, refusing to be persuaded. It is a word about the will rather than the intellect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Remains** — **menei**, the word John uses everywhere for abiding. Not *will come upon*. It is already there and stays."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty verses after *God so loved the world that He gave His only begotten Son*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same chapter contains both sentences, and John does not soften either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Baptist's last recorded words in this Gospel. The next time John mentions him he is in prison, and after that he is dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was the burning and shining lamp, and you were willing for a time to rejoice in his light.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause about the Spirit.** *Not by measure.* Everything else in the paragraph is about where a speaker has come from and what he is therefore able to report — and this is why the reporting is unlimited."}]}, "John 4:1–6": {"title": "Through Samaria", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six verses of travel arrangements, and every detail in them is load-bearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"His road took Him through Samaria\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *He had to pass through Samaria* — it was necessary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Geographically it was the direct route, and Jews going north routinely crossed the Jordan to avoid it rather than set foot in that country. The necessity in John's sentence is not about mileage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jews have no dealings with Samaritans*, John says a few verses later, and his disciples *marvelled that He talked with a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two walls in one conversation, and He goes through both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names it in Ephesians: *He has broken down the middle wall of partition.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose ground it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Near **the piece of ground Jacob had given to his son Joseph**, and **Jacob's well** was there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John names the owner. This is Joseph's portion, and Jacob's blessing over Joseph was:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph is a fruitful vine, **a fruitful vine beside a spring**, whose branches **climb over a wall**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A spring, and branches over a wall. He is sitting at the well on the land, and the wall He crosses is the one He is about to cross with a Samaritan woman. See the note on Genesis 49:22–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Worn out\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was **worn out from the journey**, and He sat down **just as He was** beside the well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *worn out* is to labour to the point of exhaustion. John, who opens his Gospel with *the Word was God*, writes this six verses into chapter 4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as He was* — no ceremony, nothing arranged. He sat down the way a tired man sits down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sixth hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noon. The hottest part of the day, and the wrong time to draw water — the women came at evening, together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She comes alone at midday because of what her life is, and He is there, tired, at exactly that hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks her for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give Me a drink.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing He says to her is a request. He has no bucket and the well is deep, and He opens the conversation from a position of need."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the end of the chapter she has left her water jar, gone into the town, and brought it out to meet Him. See the note on John 4:10–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He tells the disciples when they come back.** They had gone to buy food and He would not eat. *My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me.* Then: *lift up your eyes and look at the fields, for they are already white for harvest.* He is looking at a Samaritan town walking toward Him."}]}, "John 4:10–15": {"title": "\"If you knew the gift\" — a conversation He starts by asking for help", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus opens by asking a Samaritan woman for a drink, which is the least likely sentence in the chapter. He is thirsty and has nothing to draw with. The whole exchange runs from a position of need."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"If you knew the gift God is holding out\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things she does not know: *what* is being offered and *who* is doing the offering. He names both as missing, then says what she would do if she had them — *you would have asked Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not qualified, not prepared. Asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Living water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In ordinary speech *living water* meant running water — a spring or a stream, as opposed to still water in a cistern. Her reply takes it that way, which is why she points out He has no bucket and the well is deep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not being obtuse. She is answering the plain sense of the words, and John lets the misunderstanding stand for four verses because the second meaning arrives better after the first has been exhausted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He promises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who drinks this water will be thirsty again.* True of the well, and true of everything else that has to be gone back to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The water I give will become a spring inside him.* The location changes. Not a better well to keep returning to — a source relocated inside the person, welling up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Her answer.** *Give me this water, so that I will not be thirsty and will not have to keep coming all this way to draw.* She is still thinking about the walk, and He does not correct her or make her understand first. The next thing He says is about her husband, and the conversation goes somewhere else entirely. He answers people mid-misunderstanding."}]}, "John 4:31–34": {"title": "\"Food you know nothing about\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The disciples come back with lunch and find He is not hungry any more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Jacob's well, at the sixth hour, in Samaria. The disciples had *gone away into the city to buy food* — which is why the woman found Him alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The conversation with her has just ended and she has run back to the town leaving her waterpot behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The urging"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the meantime the disciples were **urging Him**, \"Rabbi, **eat something**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been *wearied from His journey* and sat down at the well because of it. John says so plainly in verse 6. The tiredness was real and so was the hunger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have food to eat that you know nothing about**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *food you do not know of*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The misunderstanding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the disciples said to one another, \"**Has somebody brought Him something to eat?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A running feature of John's Gospel. Nicodemus asks whether a man can enter his mother's womb; the woman at this same well asks for water so she will not have to come back and draw."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, a physical reading of a sentence meant otherwise, and every time it gives Jesus the opening to explain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the food is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My food is to do the will of the One who sent Me, and to finish the work He gave Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses: **do** the will, and **finish** the work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *finish* is **teleioo**, from the same root as the word He says from the cross — *it is finished*, **tetelestai**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names His food, and it is a completed assignment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Samaritan woman with five husbands had been told everything she ever did and had gone into the town saying *come, see a Man who told me all things that I ever did. Could this be the Christ?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the meal He is talking about. It had happened while the disciples were queueing in a shop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Lift up your eyes and look at the fields, for they are already white for harvest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said with the townspeople walking out toward the well across the open ground — John notes in verse 30 that *they went out of the city and came to Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The harvest He points at is arriving on the road while He speaks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other statements of the same thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have come down from heaven, **not to do My own will, but the will of Him who sent Me**.\"* See the note on John 6:35–40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I do exactly as the Father has commanded Me.\"* See the note on John 14:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it in nearly every discourse in the Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome in Samaria"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And many of the Samaritans of that city believed in Him because of the word of the woman… **and He stayed there two days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *now we believe, not because of what you said, for we ourselves have heard Him.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He did not do.** He does not refuse the food or make a point about fasting. He tells them there is a kind of eating they have not noticed, and then points at a field with people walking across it."}]}, "John 4:46–50": {"title": "\"The man took Jesus at His word and started for home\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A court official walks about twenty miles uphill to ask Jesus to come back down with him, and goes home alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He begged Him to come down and heal his son, who was at the point of death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A specific request with a method attached: come, and heal. The verb for *begged* is continuous — he kept asking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Capernaum is on the lake shore; Cana is up in the hills. He had made a hard journey with a dying child behind him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Unless you people see signs and wonders, you simply will not believe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is plural — *you people* — so it is not aimed only at this father. Jesus is saying something about the crowd's appetite for spectacle before He does anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man does not defend himself. He says: *Sir, please — come down before my little boy dies.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is given instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go on home. Your son lives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what he asked for. He wanted a journey; he gets a sentence. And the tense of *lives* is present — not *will live*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man took Jesus at His word and started for home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the hinge of the story. He had come for a person and left with a sentence, and he turned around and walked back down the hill on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verses have his servants meeting him on the road with the news, and the fever leaving at the exact hour Jesus spoke. John notes that the man worked it out and then *he himself believed, and his whole household.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Twice at Cana.** John points out that this is where the water became wine. Both signs are at the same village, and in both the person who acts on His word before seeing anything — the servants filling jars, and this father walking home — is the one who knows what happened."}]}, "John 4:7–9": {"title": "\"Would you give Me a drink?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first thing He says to her is a request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jewish man, alone, tired, sitting on the edge of a well at noon with no bucket."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was worn out from the journey, and He sat down just as He was beside the well.* See the note on John 4:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Would you give Me a drink?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an announcement, a rebuke, or an offer. A need, stated to a stranger, from a position of not having."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He begins the conversation by putting Himself in her debt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she hears in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How is it that You, a Jew, are asking me for a drink — me, a Samaritan woman?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She names three problems in one sentence: He is a Jew, she is a Samaritan, and she is a woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John adds the fourth as an aside: *for Jews and Samaritans have nothing to do with each other.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they do not use vessels in common — a Jew would not drink from a cup a Samaritan had handled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked her for a drink knowing whose jar it was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the disciples are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His disciples had **gone off into the town to buy food**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John notes it because it explains the privacy. Twelve men present and this conversation does not happen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When they come back they *marvelled that He talked with a woman*, and John says *yet no one said, \"What do You seek?\" or \"Why are You talking with her?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why she was there at noon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sixth hour, the hottest part of the day. Water was drawn in the cool of the morning and evening, by the women together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman with five husbands and a sixth man who is not her husband draws water when nobody else is at the well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is sitting there at exactly that hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with the request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never gets the drink. The passage does not record her giving Him one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Within a few exchanges He has moved from her water to *living water*, and then to *whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him will never thirst.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request was the way in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she leaves behind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The woman then **left her waterpot**, went her way into the city, and said to the men, \"Come, see a Man who told me all things that I ever did.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She came for water and walked off without the jar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what He tells the disciples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They come back with lunch and He will not eat. *\"My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *\"Lift up your eyes and look at the fields, for they are already white for harvest.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is looking at a Samaritan town walking toward Him because of a woman with no reputation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He said He would have given.** *\"If you knew the gift of God, and who it is who says to you, 'Give Me a drink,' you would have asked Him, and He would have given you living water.\"* The one asking for the drink was carrying the well."}]}, "John 5:1–5": {"title": "Thirty-eight years by the pool", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John sets the scene carefully, and everything in the setting is against the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Over by **the Sheep Gate**, there is a pool… called **Bethesda**, and it has **five covered walkways**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Sheep Gate is where the animals came in for sacrifice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bethesda** — house of mercy, or house of outpouring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Archaeologists uncovered a twin pool complex near St Anne's church in Jerusalem in the nineteenth century, with colonnades on four sides and one across the middle dividing it. Five porches, exactly as John describes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For a long time this verse was cited as evidence that John did not know Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A great crowd of people who could not get well — **the blind, the lame, the ones whose bodies had wasted away** — and they were **waiting for the water to move**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three categories, and a crowd of them lying under the roofs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The waiting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From time to time an angel would come down… and **the first one into the water** was made well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the tradition was, it had a cruel shape to it. Only the first got anything, which meant the sickest never could."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people least able to move were competing to move fastest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One man had been lying there a long while. He had been **an invalid for thirty-eight years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John gives the number, and it is a number with a history — Israel wandered thirty-eight years between Kadesh Barnea and crossing the brook Zered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses following: *\"**Do you want to be made well?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer it. He explains the system instead: *\"Sir, I have no man to put me into the pool when the water is stirred, but while I am coming, another steps down before me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-eight years of arriving second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Rise, take up your bed and walk.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No water, no stirring, no angel, and no race."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And immediately the man was made well, took up his bed, and walked.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one He picked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Out of a great crowd of blind, lame and paralysed people, John records one healing that day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jesus *had withdrawn, a multitude being in that place* — He leaves without a scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was the Sabbath, and the objection is about a man carrying a mat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, later, in the temple: *\"See, you have been made well. **Sin no more, lest a worse thing come upon you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the man goes and tells the authorities who it was."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other question in the chapter.** *\"Do you want to be made well?\"* — asked of a man who had been lying there for thirty-eight years. Nothing in the account suggests it was rhetorical."}]}, "John 5:19–23": {"title": "\"What the Son sees\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He has just healed a man on the sabbath and told him to carry his mat, and this is the answer to the objection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What provoked it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore the Jews sought all the more to kill Him, because He not only broke the Sabbath, but also **said that God was His Father, making Himself equal with God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They understood the claim correctly. John records their reading and does not correct it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The denial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The Son **can do nothing on His own**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which sounds like a retreat and is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He does **only what He sees the Father doing**, and **whatever the Father does, the Son does in the same way**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **homoios** — likewise, in like manner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first clause limits Him to what He sees. The second says the range is everything the Father does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Together they are a larger claim than the one being denied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Father does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **the Father loves the Son and shows Him everything He Himself is doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The love and the showing are one clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says the same pairing repeatedly: *the Father loves the Son and has given all things into His hand*. See the note on John 3:31–36."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise of more"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **He will show Him works greater than these, until you are left astonished**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the greater works are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Just as the Father raises the dead and gives them life, **so the Son also gives life to whomever He chooses**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *whom He wills*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Raising the dead was God's prerogative alone in Jewish thought. He assigns it to Himself in the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The judgment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the Father judges no one. He has handed the whole of judgment over to the Son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *has given all judgment to the Son*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that everyone will honor the Son just as they honor the Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathos** — in the same way, to the same degree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Anyone who does not honor the Son does not honor the Father who sent Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no third position in the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage is built on seeing. He does what He sees; the Father shows Him; and the works are the evidence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later in the same Gospel: *the words that I speak to you I do not speak on My own authority; but the Father who dwells in Me does the works.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says about the man at the pool"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing that started the argument had one odd feature. The pool at Bethesda was full of sick people — *a great multitude of sick people, blind, lame, paralysed*. See the note on John 5:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He healed one man and walked away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the account explains the selection, and this passage is the nearest thing to one: *what He sees the Father doing*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two resurrections"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses after: *the hour is coming, and now is, when the dead will hear the voice of the Son of God; and those who hear will live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *the hour is coming in which all who are in the graves will hear His voice and come forth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present and future, in consecutive paragraphs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape of the answer.** A denial that sounds like modesty — *the Son can do nothing on His own* — attached to a claim that He does everything the Father does, and that judgment has been handed over entirely."}]}, "John 5:24–27": {"title": "\"Has crossed over\" — the tense that settles it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three statements, stacked, and each one is stronger than the last. Jesus is not building toward a promise here; He is closing a question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read the three parts"}, {"k": "note", "t": "whoever hears My word and trusts the One who sent Me **has** eternal life. He **does not come up** for condemnation. He **has already crossed over** out of death and into life."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*has* — present tense. Not will have.", "*does not come up for condemnation* — the word is the courtroom one. He is not saying the verdict will be favourable. He is saying the person does not appear on that docket.", "*has already crossed over* — and this is the one that carries the weight."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense in that last phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is in the perfect. English does not have a good single word for it: it means a thing was done at a point in the past and the state it produced is still the case. Not \"crossed over and we shall see,\" but crossed, and standing on the other bank now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same tense as *it is finished* on the cross. A completed action whose result stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sentence is not describing something a person is working toward or could slip back out of. It describes a border already behind them."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is conditioned on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Worth being exact, because the condition is stated and it is short: *hears My word and trusts the One who sent Me.* Hearing and trusting. There is no third item, and nothing about sustaining it by effort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole entry requirement in a sentence that then hands over eternal life, immunity from the courtroom, and a completed crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strange middle of the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Jesus says a time is coming *and it is here now* when the dead hear His voice and live. Both at once — future and present in the same breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He means it in both senses, and John's Gospel goes on to show it: Lazarus called out of a tomb, and people all through the book crossing from death to life while still walking around. The resurrection at the end is not a different event from what happens when someone hears Him now. It is the same voice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why this passage gets used the way it does.** People reach for it when they are afraid of losing what they have. It answers that fear, but notice how — not by promising to hold on tightly enough, but by pointing out that the crossing is in the past tense. The question the verse settles is not *will I make it*. It is *where am I standing*, and it answers: on this side already."}]}, "John 6:1–4": {"title": "Across the Sea of Galilee", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses of setting, and every detail in them is doing something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crossing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After this Jesus **crossed to the far side of the Sea of Galilee**, which is also called **the Sea of Tiberias**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John gives both names. **Tiberias** was the Roman name, after the emperor — Herod Antipas had built a city on the shore and named it for him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John writes for readers who would know the Roman name and not the Jewish one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A large crowd **kept following Him**, because they had **seen the signs He was doing for the sick**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is imperfect — they were following, continuously."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John states the reason plainly and without flattery: the signs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the end of the chapter almost all of them are gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **went up on the mountainside and sat down there with His disciples**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sitting is the posture of a teacher."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Passover, the festival of the Jews, was near.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John marks three Passovers in his Gospel, and it is how the ministry is usually dated to about three years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This one matters for what follows. Passover is the feast of the lamb and of unleavened bread, and the chapter is about to be entirely about bread."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why John gives the date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The discourse that follows runs on Passover material: manna in the wilderness, bread from heaven, and *unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink His blood, you have no life in you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The feast is in the background of every line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five thousand men, besides women and children. Five barley loaves and two small fish from a boy in the crowd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jesus took the loaves, and **when He had given thanks** He distributed them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve baskets of fragments left over — one for each disciple who had said it could not be done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only miracle in all four Gospels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd tried to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore when Jesus perceived that they were about to **come and take Him by force to make Him king**, He departed again to the mountain by Himself alone.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does that night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walks out to the disciples on the water, three or four miles from shore, in a storm. See the note on John 6:16–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says the next day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Most assuredly, I say to you, **you seek Me, not because you saw the signs, but because you ate of the loaves and were filled**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells the crowd why they came, and it is not the reason John gave in verse 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is a hard teaching. Who can listen to it?\"* — and *from that time many of His disciples went back and walked with Him no more*. See the note on John 6:60–65."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with a large crowd following and ends with twelve men and a question: *do you also want to go away?*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the four verses set up.** A lake with two names, a crowd there for the healings, a teacher sitting on a hill, and Passover coming — and then a boy's lunch feeds five thousand and a discourse about bread empties the room."}]}, "John 6:10–13": {"title": "Twelve baskets of leftovers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only miracle in all four Gospels, and John keeps a detail the others share and nobody quite explains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As much as they wanted\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a ration. John says the fish were handed out *as much as they wanted*, and that everyone *ate their fill*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for eating their fill is a strong one — it was used of feeding animals to satisfaction. Five thousand men, plus the women and children the other accounts mention, all eating until they stopped wanting more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gather up the pieces that are left over, so that nothing is wasted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the sentence worth stopping on. The man who has just produced an unlimited supply out of five loaves tells His disciples to pick up the crumbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abundance and waste are treated as different things. The miracle does not make the fragments unimportant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Twelve baskets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They end with more bread than they started with, and the number is exact — twelve, one for each disciple. The men who had said *what are five loaves among so many* each end the day carrying a full basket."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not comment on that either. He just records the number."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happens next.** The crowd tries to make Him king by force, and He withdraws to a mountain alone. The following morning He tells them they are looking for Him because they ate the loaves, and then calls Himself the bread of life — a claim so hard that most of them stop following Him by the end of the chapter. The feeding is the beginning of a much longer argument."}]}, "John 6:16–21": {"title": "\"Immediately the boat was at the shore\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John tells the storm in six verses and adds an ending the other Gospels do not have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Started across **toward Capernaum**. It was dark by then, and **Jesus had not come to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John notes the absence. They set out expecting Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conditions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A strong wind was blowing, and **the water was rising rough** around them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they had rowed about **twenty-five or thirty stadia — three or four miles out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John gives the figure. The lake is roughly seven miles across at that point, so they are about halfway and have been at it for hours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only one who measures it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They saw Jesus **walking on the sea and coming near the boat**, and **they were afraid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not mention the ghost. Just that they were frightened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is I. Do not be afraid.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ego eimi** — two words, and John has been building his whole Gospel on that phrase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In John's hands it is never just *it's me*. He records seven sayings with a predicate — bread, light, door, shepherd, resurrection, way, vine — and several without one, including *before Abraham was, I am*, which nearly got Him stoned. See the note on John 8:57–59."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is spoken over water in the dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they were **glad to take Him into the boat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they were willing to receive Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says He was *about to go right past them* and stopped for their screaming. John records the moment they wanted Him aboard. See the note on Mark 6:45–52."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ending nobody else has"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **immediately the boat was at the shore they had been heading for**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three or four miles out, at night, into a headwind — and then at the destination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John states it flatly, in one clause, with no explanation and no reaction from anybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Mark both record the wind dropping. Only John records the distance disappearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd finds Him on the other side the following day, and He tells them *you seek Me, not because you saw the signs, but because you ate of the loaves and were filled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the bread of life discourse, ending with many disciples walking away. See the note on John 6:52–59."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two problems in the chapter.** Five thousand people and not enough bread, and then a lake and not enough time. John puts them on consecutive pages, and the second is solved in half a sentence."}]}, "John 6:35–40": {"title": "\"I will never turn away\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the seven great *I am* sayings in John, and what follows it is a run of promises with nothing conditional in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am the bread of life. Whoever comes to Me will never be hungry, and whoever believes in Me will never be thirsty again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and thirst in one sentence — He answers a need He was not asked about. The crowd had come looking for another meal after the five thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The double negative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who comes to Me I will never turn away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek stacks two negatives, which in that language intensifies rather than cancels: I will certainly not, under any circumstances, cast out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verb is *ekballo* — to throw out, put outside. It is the word used of driving out demons and of expelling someone from a synagogue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else is uncertain, the person who comes is not going to be shown the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Twice, the same will"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus states the Father's will twice in three verses, and both times it is about keeping:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*that of everything He has given Me I should **lose none of it**, but raise it up on the last day*", "*that everyone who looks to the Son and believes in Him should have eternal life, and I will raise him up*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for *lose* is the one used of a lost sheep and a lost coin. He describes His own commission as not losing anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone the Father gives Me will come to Me, and the one who comes to Me I will never turn away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are in the sentence and John does not resolve the tension. The giving comes first; the coming is real; and the welcome is unconditional."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The repeated line.** *I will raise him up on the last day* appears four times in this chapter. Whatever the bread of life turns out to mean, Jesus keeps attaching it to resurrection rather than to sustenance."}]}, "John 6:47–51": {"title": "\"The bread I will give is My flesh\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus compares Himself to manna and then says the comparison does not go far enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manna argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your ancestors ate manna in the wilderness, and **they died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd had raised the manna themselves, as a credential — Moses gave our fathers bread from heaven. Jesus accepts the fact and points at the graves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wilderness generation ate the miracle bread every morning for forty years and is buried in the desert. Whatever the manna did, it did not deal with death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the bread that comes down from heaven, so that a person may eat it and not die. I am the living bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The manna came down and was gathered. This bread is described as **living** — alive in itself, not merely supplied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the tense at the end is future: *the bread I **will** give is My flesh, and I will give it for the life of the world.* He is pointing forward to the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the crowd could not take it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verses have them arguing among themselves — *how can this man give us his flesh to eat?* — and many disciples turn back before the chapter ends."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not soften it or explain it away. He repeats it, harder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb He uses in the following verses is a blunt one — the ordinary word for chewing. Whatever else the language does, it insists on something taken in personally rather than admired from outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Manna had to be gathered by each household, every morning, and eaten. Nobody could gather it for you."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter ends.** *The words I have spoken to you are spirit and they are life.* Jesus Himself supplies the interpretive key after the hard sayings — and then asks the twelve whether they want to leave too. Peter's answer is about having nowhere else to go."}]}, "John 6:52–59": {"title": "\"Unless you eat\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest thing Jesus ever said in public, and He does not soften it when the room turns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can this man give us His flesh to eat?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a fair question, and the Greek says they were *arguing sharply* — fighting among themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything about the statement would have been abhorrent to that audience. Blood was forbidden absolutely under Leviticus 17, on pain of being cut off. See the note on Leviticus 17:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does about the objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Repeats it, four times, harder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Unless you **eat the flesh** of the Son of Man and **drink His blood**, you have no life in you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then *whoever feeds on My flesh and drinks My blood* three more times in five verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb shifts partway through from **phago** (eat) to **trogo** — to gnaw, to chew. It is a physical, unmistakable word, and He moves toward it rather than away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **My flesh is true food, and My blood is true drink**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **alethes** — real, genuine. Not a metaphor for something realer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, stated flatly:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*has **eternal life***", "*I will **raise him up on the last day***", "*remains in Me, and I in him*", "*will **live because of Me***"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As **the living Father sent Me and I live because of the Father**, so the one who feeds on Me **will live because of Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The relationship between the Father and the Son is offered as the pattern for the relationship between Christ and the one who eats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the largest comparison in the passage, and it is easy to read past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manna, again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **not like the bread your ancestors ate, and died**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd had opened this conversation by quoting the manna at Him. He grants that they ate it, and points out how the story finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the notes on Exodus 16:13–15 and John 6:47–51."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that time **many of His disciples went back and walked with Him no more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the next paragraph. He does not call them back or clarify."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Instead He turns to the twelve: *do you also want to go away?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter: *Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While He was teaching in **the synagogue in Capernaum**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not privately, not to the twelve. In the synagogue of the town He lived in, to people who knew His family."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter opened with.** Five thousand fed with five loaves. They came back the next day wanting more bread, and He told them *do not labour for the food which perishes.* The crowd that had eaten the miracle is the crowd that walked away from the sermon about it."}]}, "John 6:60–65": {"title": "A hard thing to hear", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The day after the feeding of the five thousand, and by the end of it almost everybody has gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bread of life discourse — *I am the living bread which came down from heaven*, and then the sentences that emptied the room: *unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink His blood, you have no life in you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is a hard teaching. Who can listen to it?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skleros** — hard, harsh, rough. Not *difficult to understand*. Hard to take."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is not the crowd saying it. *Many of **His disciples***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **knew within Himself** that His disciples were grumbling about it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gogguzousin** — murmuring. It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses for Israel in the wilderness, complaining about the manna."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just been fed miraculously and are murmuring about bread. John chose the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Does this trip you up?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skandalizei** — from **skandalon**, the trigger of a trap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harder thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **what if you were to see the Son of Man going up to where He was before?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the incarnation offends, the ascension will be worse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften the teaching. He raises it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The key"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is the Spirit who gives life; human strength on its own counts for nothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the flesh profits nothing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has spent the chapter talking about eating flesh, and now says flesh is worth nothing. It is the interpretive key handed over in one line — they had been hearing it at the level of meat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same contrast: *the letter kills, but the Spirit gives life.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What His words are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The words I have spoken to you are spirit, and they are life**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **there are some of you who do not believe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For Jesus had known **from the beginning** which of them did not believe, and **which one would betray Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas is in the room and has been for some time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is why I told you that **no one can come to Me unless it has been given to him by My Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it earlier in the chapter: *no one can come to Me unless the Father who sent Me draws him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He repeats it at the moment of the exodus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *from that time **many of His disciples went back and walked with Him no more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the largest recorded departure in the Gospels, and He does not call them back or adjust anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question to the twelve"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you also want to go away?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter: *\"**Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life**. Also we have come to believe and know that You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An answer with no enthusiasm in it and no alternative either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Did I not choose you, the twelve, and **one of you is a devil**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that opens with five thousand fed ends with a crowd gone, eleven staying, and one named."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does when the room empties.** Nothing. He does not explain further, soften the saying, or go after them — and the only question He asks afterward is put to the men who are left."}]}, "John 7:37–39": {"title": "Rivers of living water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He waits until the last day of the feast, and then He shouts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day He chose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the **last day of the festival, the great day**, Jesus **stood up and called out in a loud voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Feast of Tabernacles ran seven days, and every morning a priest carried a golden pitcher down to the pool of Siloam, filled it, and poured it out at the base of the altar while the people sang from Isaiah 12 — *with joy you will draw water from the wells of salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By most accounts the pouring did not happen on the eighth day. The pitcher stayed empty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that silence, a man stands up in the temple court and says: if anyone is thirsty, come to Me."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Let him come to Me and drink\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and both are simple. Not qualify, not prepare. Come, and drink."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The invitation is addressed to a condition — *if anyone is **thirsty*** — and thirst is not an achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the rivers come out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of his **innermost being** will flow **rivers** of living water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a trickle and not a cistern. **Rivers**, plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *out of his belly* — the deepest inside of a person, the part nothing reaches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And note the direction. He drinks, and what comes out of him is more than he took in, and it comes out for other people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As the Scripture has said\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not tell us which Scripture, and no single verse matches word for word. The picture runs through the prophets — water from the rock in the wilderness, Ezekiel's river out from under the temple threshold, Zechariah's living waters going out from Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is drawing on a whole current, not quoting a line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "John's own footnote"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was speaking about the Spirit, whom those who believed in Him were going to receive; for the Holy Spirit had not yet been given, **because Jesus had not yet been glorified**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John, writing decades later, explains it for us. The Spirit waits on the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the sequence is stated plainly: glorified first, then given. What was announced in a temple courtyard at a feast arrived in an upper room at Pentecost."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the water had been coming from.** The pool of Siloam — the same pool Jesus sends the blind man to wash in two chapters later. John keeps his images and comes back to them."}]}, "John 8:31–36": {"title": "\"We have never been anyone's slaves\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted line in this passage — *the truth will set you free* — is attached to a condition on one side and an argument on the other, and both get cut off when it is quoted alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you hold to My word, then you really are My disciples. And you will know the truth…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb means to remain, to stay put, to keep living somewhere. Freedom is promised to people who stay in what He said, not to people who encounter it once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim that gives the game away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are Abraham's descendants, and we have never been anyone's slaves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were speaking in a country under Roman occupation, as descendants of people who had been slaves in Egypt, and after Babylon. The sentence is historically false three times over, and they say it without blinking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole diagnosis. A person can be quite certain they are free while standing inside the thing that holds them, and the certainty is part of the condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says the slavery is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who commits sin is a slave of sin.* Not \"does wrong things occasionally\" — the construction is about a settled practice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the family image: *a slave does not stay in the house forever, but a son belongs there forever*. Slaves in a household could be sold; sons could not be un-sonned. He is contrasting two kinds of belonging, and only one of them is permanent."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"Free indeed.\"** The word is *ontos* — really, actually, in fact. Jesus adds it because He has just been told by the people in front of Him that they were free already. The adverb is aimed at that claim: not free in the way you think you are. Free in fact."}]}, "John 8:57–59": {"title": "\"Before Abraham was, I AM\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The shortest sentence He ever said about Himself, and the crowd reached for stones before He finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are not yet fifty years old**, and You have seen Abraham?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had just said *Abraham rejoiced to see My day, and he saw it and was glad.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their objection is about arithmetic. Two thousand years and one lifetime do not overlap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I tell you the truth, **before Abraham was, I am**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenses do not agree, and that is the whole sentence. Not *I was*. Not *I existed*. **I am.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before Abraham came into being — the Greek verb is one of becoming, of coming to exist — I **am**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ego eimi.* It is the Greek of Exodus 3, where God tells Moses to say *I AM has sent me to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name given at the burning bush, in the present tense, applied to Himself in the temple courts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Exodus 3:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At that they **picked up stones** to throw at Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was no misunderstanding. Stoning was the penalty for blasphemy, and nobody in that court had to have it explained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's earlier claims — that He was sent, that He does the Father's will, that they would die in their sins — had not produced this. This did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven \"I am\"s in John"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread of life. Light of the world. The door. The good shepherd. The resurrection and the life. The way, the truth and the life. The true vine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven with something after them, and this one, with nothing after it at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He walked into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jesus **hid Himself and went out of the temple, passing right through the middle of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Through the crowd holding the stones. John does not explain it and does not comment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same in Luke 4, when Nazareth takes Him to the brow of the hill to throw Him off — *passing through the midst of them, He went His way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They could not lay a hand on Him until the night He let them, and then in the garden, when the soldiers said they were looking for Jesus of Nazareth and He answered *I am*, they *drew back and fell to the ground.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name and what it does not specify.** *I AM WHO I AM* — the sentence has no predicate. It is not *I am strong* or *I am merciful*. It is the verb standing alone, and whatever the need in front of it, the name is large enough."}]}, "John 9:24–27": {"title": "\"One thing I do know\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man who has been blind from birth is interrogated twice by the religious authorities, and he wins."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they want him to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give the glory to God.** We know this man is a sinner.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a legal formula — an oath, roughly *tell the truth before God*. And they attach the verdict they want in the same breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not asking him a question. They are telling him what the answer is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he will not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whether He is a sinner, I do not know.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He declines to go past what he has. He is not a theologian and does not pretend to be one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does have"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**One thing I do know: I was blind, and now I see.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no argument against that sentence. They can dispute the doctrine; they cannot get at the fact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was blind on that street every day of his life — the neighbours in this chapter argue about whether it is even the same man. And he is standing there looking at them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second interrogation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What did He do to you? How did He open your eyes?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask him to go through it again. They had already heard it — verse 15 records the first telling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are looking for a discrepancy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have already told you, **and you would not listen**. Why do you want to hear it again? **Do you want to become His disciples too?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A beggar, unschooled, being examined by the Sanhedrin, and he asks them if they are signing up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the funniest line in John's Gospel and it is delivered by a man with everything to lose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they threw him out.* Excommunicated — put out of the synagogue, which in that world meant out of the community."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His own parents had already backed away: *he is of age, ask him*, because *they feared the Jews.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who came looking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus heard that they had cast him out, and **when He had found him**, He said, \"Do you believe in the Son of God?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes and finds him. The man had never seen His face — he had been sent to the pool to wash while still blind, and had not laid eyes on Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, I believe.\" And he worshipped Him.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says about the whole thing.** *For judgement I have come into this world, that those who do not see may see, and that those who see may be made blind.* The Pharisees ask whether He means them, and He answers: *if you were blind, you would have no sin. But now you say, \"We see.\" Therefore your sin remains.*"}]}, "Joshua 1:1–5": {"title": "\"Now it's your turn\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens with a funeral and a promotion in the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The death"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses, **the servant of the LORD**, had died.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title is given at the moment it ends. Joshua does not receive it here; he is called *Moses' assistant*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets it at the end of the book: *Joshua the son of Nun, the servant of the LORD, died.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Joshua is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man who had **stood at Moses' side for so long**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *Moses' minister* — **mesharet**, an attendant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had led the fight against Amalek, gone up the mountain with Moses, stayed in the tent of meeting when Moses left it, and been one of the two spies who said go. See the notes on Exodus 17:8–13 and Numbers 14:5–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of second place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Moses my servant is gone. So now it's your turn**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *Moses My servant is dead; now therefore arise*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no interval. The book's first paragraph moves from a death to a river crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, you and all these people, and cross over this Jordan River**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The river is in flood — Joshua 3 notes it overflows its banks through the harvest season. See the note on Joshua 3:14–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Into the land **I am giving them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present participle. Not *will give*, and not *would have given*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction runs through the book: *every place your foot touches, **I am handing over to you***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Every place your foot touches, I am handing over to you**, just as I promised Moses.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given, and walked on. The giving is God's and the walking is theirs, and both are in the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua 24 makes the same point at the end: *I gave you a land you did not toil for.* See the note on Joshua 24:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The boundaries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"From the wilderness in the south all the way to Lebanon, from the great Euphrates River across the land of the Hittites to the Great Sea in the west.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The full extent promised to Abraham in Genesis 15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise about opposition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one will be able to stand against you as long as you live**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The way I was with Moses, that's how I will be with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the sentence Joshua would have needed most. He had watched the sea part, the mountain smoke, and the water come out of a rock, and is now expected to lead the same nation with none of that behind him personally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two negatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will never fail you. I will never walk away from you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles the assurance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes this line and applies it to the reader: *He Himself has said, \"I will never leave you nor forsake you.\" So we may boldly say: \"The LORD is my helper; I will not fear.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in the next four verses: *be strong and of good courage*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the one instruction about method: *this Book of the Law shall not depart from your mouth, but you shall meditate in it day and night.* See the note on Joshua 1:6–9."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the book starts.** Moses is dead in the first clause and the crossing is ordered in the second. The only argument given for the change is that the same God is going with the next man."}]}, "Joshua 1:6–9": {"title": "What \"meditate day and night\" meant to a soldier", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous line here is about turning God's words over in your mind day and night. It is worth knowing who was told to do it and what he was about to do, because it was not a quiet man in a quiet room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Hebrew word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hagah.* It is not silent reflection. Its ordinary uses are physical and audible — a lion growling over its prey, a dove moaning, a person muttering under their breath. The same word is in Psalm 1, and both times it is paired with *day and night*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the instruction here begins with the mouth: *keep the words of this book always on your lips*, and then turn them over. It is closer to chewing than to studying — going over something aloud until it works into you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua, on the day he takes over from Moses, facing a river and a walled city. The book is about a military campaign, and the first thing he is given is not a strategy. It is a way of handling words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in four verses he is told to be strong and courageous, which is the tell: this is a man being steadied, and the muttering of the text is part of how he is steadied."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The promise attached.** *Then your path will open before you, and you will do well.* The Hebrew word behind it means to act with insight and so to succeed — it covers both the wisdom and the outcome. It is not a guarantee of ease; the book that follows has failures in it. It is the promise that a man soaked in what God said will know how to act."}]}, "Joshua 10:12–14": {"title": "\"A day when the LORD listened to the voice of a man\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joshua gives an order to the solar system, out loud, in front of everybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five Amorite kings had attacked Gibeon — the city that had tricked Israel into a treaty a chapter earlier. Israel is defending people who had deceived them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua marched all night from Gilgal to get there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua spoke to the LORD, and **in the sight of all Israel he cried aloud**: \"**Sun, stand still** over Gibeon; and **Moon**, over the Valley of Aijalon.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses the LORD, and then addresses the sun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stand still* is **dom** — be silent, be still, cease."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And note the setting: *in the sight of all Israel*, out loud. If nothing had happened, everybody would have watched nothing happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the sun stood still, and the moon stopped… **The sun halted in the middle of the sky and did not hurry to set for about a whole day**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The citation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is this not written in **the Book of Jashar**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A source the writer expects his readers to have. It is quoted once more in Scripture, for David's lament over Saul and Jonathan, and it has not survived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of Joshua is pointing at an external record."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There has never been a day like it, before or since — **a day when the LORD listened to the voice of a man**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the writer's summary, and it is not about astronomy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *a day when the sun stood still*. He says a day when God listened to somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the clause after it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the LORD Himself was fighting for Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are in the same verse. God listened to a man, and God was doing the fighting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua's shout was not what won the battle — the chapter has already recorded hailstones killing more of the enemy than the sword did. What the long day did was finish it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A treaty with people who had lied to them. Israel could have let Gibeon fall and been rid of an embarrassment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua marched all night instead, because he had given his word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same idea turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elijah was a man with a nature like ours, and **he prayed earnestly** that it would not rain, and it did not rain on the land for three years and six months.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James makes the point deliberately — the man was ordinary, and the sky obeyed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other thing that stood still.** *Jesus **stood still**, and commanded him to be called.* Mark 10, on the road out of Jericho, for a blind beggar shouting. The same word in Greek that the Old Testament used of the sun. See the note on Mark 10:46–52."}]}, "Joshua 10:22–27": {"title": "Feet on the necks of kings", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five kings are brought out of a cave, and what Joshua does with them is a lesson for the men watching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five Amorite kings had allied against Gibeon, which had made peace with Israel. Joshua marched all night from Gilgal, and the LORD *cast down large hailstones from heaven on them*, and the sun stood still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The five kings hid in a cave at Makkedah, and Joshua had great stones rolled over the mouth of it and men set to guard it while the army finished the pursuit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The kings of **Jerusalem, Hebron, Jarmuth, Lachish, and Eglon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The head of the alliance is Adoni-Zedek of Jerusalem — a name meaning *lord of righteousness*, and a variant of Melchizedek, *king of righteousness*, who had blessed Abram in the same city. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is called forward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua **called the men of Israel together** and told **the commanders who had gone with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole nation watches; the commanders act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come, **put your feet on the necks of these kings**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *place your feet upon the necks*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a standard ancient gesture of conquest, carved on reliefs across the Near East."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not asked of them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They did not capture these kings. The hailstones did more damage than the sword — Joshua 10 says so outright: *there were more who died from the hailstones than the children of Israel killed with the sword.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commanders are put on the necks of men they did not defeat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't be afraid or discouraged. Be strong and courageous**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same four given to Joshua himself at the start of the book, three times in the first chapter, and given to Joshua by Moses before that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He passes on what he was told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is what the LORD will do to every enemy you fight**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *this is what you will do*. The LORD is the subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gesture is a demonstration, and what it demonstrates is stated in the next sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The execution"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Joshua **struck the five kings down** and put them to death, and **hung their bodies on five trees until evening**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At sunset he ordered them **taken down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy required it: *his body shall not remain overnight on the tree… for he who is hanged is accursed of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same law the Jewish leaders invoked at the crucifixion. See the note on John 19:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they ended up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **thrown into the very cave where they had hidden**. Great stones were piled at the entrance — and they are there to this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hiding place becomes the tomb, sealed with the same stones that had trapped them alive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the picture goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sit here at My right hand **until I make your enemies a footstool under your feet**.* See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *the God of peace will crush Satan **under your feet** shortly.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the commanders did.** Walked forward and stood on five necks, after a battle decided by hail and a long day. Joshua tells them what it means before they step off."}]}, "Joshua 11:21–23": {"title": "\"The land had rest from war\" — and the three cities left standing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The summary at the end of the campaign, and it contains a detail the writer did not have to include."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua took the entire land, exactly as the LORD had promised Moses… And the land had rest from war.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is as complete as the book gets. The giants are cut off from the hill country — Hebron, Debir, Anab, the highlands — and *not one of the Anakim was left in the land of Israel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception, stated in the same breath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only in Gaza, Gath, and Ashdod did any remain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three Philistine cities on the coastal plain. The writer records the total victory and the surviving pocket in consecutive sentences, without appearing to notice a problem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It matters later. Gath is where Goliath comes from, four centuries on — a descendant of the people this chapter says were cleared out, living in one of the three cities this chapter says they were not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of claim it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Bible's histories are willing to say *the whole land* and then name what was left, and to do it on the same page. The summary is describing the campaign's outcome — the field armies broken, the hill country taken — not a census."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters later God says outright: *there is still so much land left to possess*. The book contains its own qualification."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The phrase that closes it.** *The land had rest from war* appears twice in Joshua, here and at the end of Caleb's chapter. It is the note the conquest narrative keeps landing on — not conquest as the goal, but rest as the point of it."}]}, "Joshua 13:1–7": {"title": "\"You've grown old, and there is still so much land left\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the bluntest sentences God says to anyone in the Old Testament, and He says it to the man who had just finished the conquest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You've grown old, and there is still so much land left to possess.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are true and neither cancels the other. Two chapters earlier the book said Joshua *took the entire land, exactly as the LORD had promised* — and it meant it, at the level of the campaign. Now God lists what is still out there by name: the Philistine cities, the Geshurites, the Sidonians, Lebanon up to Hermon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The victory was real. The occupation was not finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not rebuke Joshua. There is no suggestion the old man failed, and no call for one more campaign. The instruction that follows is administrative: *divide this land among the nine and a half tribes as their inheritance*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua's job changes. He is to allocate what has been promised, and the taking of it will belong to the tribes and to the years after him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the driving out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I myself will drive them out before my people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is stated in the middle of the list of what remains. The unfinished part is not handed over as a burden — it is handed over with the promise that the work is God's, and the tribes' part is to go and live in what is theirs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The tension the book leaves standing.** Joshua says the land was taken; Judges opens with a long account of what was not. Both are in the canon and neither is edited out. A promise can be entirely given and still not entirely entered, and this chapter is where the Bible says so out loud."}]}, "Joshua 14:6–15": {"title": "Caleb at eighty-five, and the hill he asked for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Forty-five years after the report that cost his generation the land, Caleb walks up and asks for the hardest piece of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic he does out loud"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["forty years old when he was sent to scout", "forty-five years of waiting since", "eighty-five now, *as strong now as the day Moses sent me out*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He counts it publicly. He is not claiming exemption from time; he is saying the promise held through it, and so did he."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a quiet valley. **Hebron** — and he names the problem himself: *You heard yourself that the Anakim are there, giants in great fortified cities.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The giants are the exact reason ten of the twelve scouts had said no, all those years earlier. Caleb asks for the place his generation refused, by name, and tells Joshua the giants are still in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase the chapter keeps repeating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He followed the LORD my God completely* — with a whole heart. It appears three times in these verses, twice from Caleb and once from the narrator, and it is what Moses' oath was hung on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *completely* means to fill up, to make full. Not flawlessly. Fully — nothing held back."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *And the land had rest from war.* The chapter about an eighty-five-year-old asking for a fight ends on rest. Both things are in the same paragraph, and the order matters: the rest comes after the hill country is taken, not instead of it."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Caleb was asking against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read this chapter beside the two around it and the request gets sharper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua 11 says the Anakim were cut off from the hill country and *not one was left in the land of Israel* — with three coastal cities named as the exception. Joshua 13 has God telling Joshua, now old, that *there is still so much land left to possess*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caleb is standing in that gap. The campaign is over, the summary has been written, and there is still a fortified hill country full of giants that nobody has moved into. He does not ask for something new. He asks for the part of the promise that was made to him personally and has been sitting unoccupied for forty-five years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what he did with it after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 15 records the follow-through in one line: *from there Caleb drove out the three sons of Anak* — named, individually — and then went on against Debir."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The asking and the taking are two different chapters, and the second one is short. See the notes on Joshua 11:21–23, Joshua 13:1–7, and Joshua 15:13–19, where his daughter asks him for springs in exactly the same spirit."}]}, "Joshua 15:13–19": {"title": "Achsah, who asked for the springs", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short domestic scene dropped into the middle of a long list of boundary lines, and it is there because of what a young woman says."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had been given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Land in the Negev — the dry south. It is a real inheritance and it is genuinely hers, and without water it is close to useless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has just described her father taking Hebron off three giants, so nobody in this family is short of nerve. Achsah is her father's daughter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give me a blessing. You've given me land in the dry Negev — now give me springs of water too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She names the gap precisely. Not more land, and not a complaint about what she received. The specific thing that would make what she already has work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the detail the narrator keeps: *as she climbed down from her donkey*. She dismounts to ask — the posture of someone making a formal request, and the sort of small physical detail the Bible records when someone was actually there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Caleb gave her the upper springs and the lower springs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. She asked for springs and he gave her the whole watershed, top and bottom. No hesitation is recorded, and no negotiation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the story is in the book at all.** Judges tells it a second time, almost word for word — which means it was remembered and repeated. In a chapter that is otherwise surveyors' work, someone thought this was worth keeping: a woman who looked at a good gift, saw what it lacked, and asked her father for the rest."}]}, "Joshua 2:12–14": {"title": "\"Kindness and truth\" — the two words she is given", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Rahab asks for a specific thing, in specific words, and the answer comes back in the same vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have shown you kindness — promise me you will show kindness to my family.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word is **hesed**: the loyal, covenanted, will-not-let-go kind of love that is Israel's central word for how God treats His own. She uses it of what she did, and asks for it back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *give me a sure sign*. She is not content with a promise; she asks for something she can point to. That is what the scarlet cord becomes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We will treat you with kindness and truth* — **hesed and emet**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That pairing runs right through the Old Testament as a description of God's own character: loyal love and dependability, the two together. It is what Moses hears when God passes by him on the mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two spies, in a compromised house, in a doomed city, hand a Canaanite prostitute the pair of words that describe what God is like."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the pair turns up again.** John writes that the Word became flesh, *full of grace and truth* — and that grace and truth *came through Jesus Christ*. Greek this time, but the same pairing, and John's readers would have heard the Hebrew behind it. What Rahab was promised on a rooftop is what John says arrived in a person. See the note on John 1:14–18."}]}, "Joshua 2:15–21": {"title": "The scarlet cord, and the terms she agreed to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman in a doomed city is given a set of conditions, and the conditions are worth reading closely, because every one of them turns up again in how the Bible talks about rescue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1. **the cord in the window** — the same rope she let them down by, tied where it can be seen 2. **everyone must be inside her house** — *if anyone steps outside your doors into the street, their blood is on their own head* 3. **say nothing about the arrangement**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The safety is not spread across the city or attached to her personally wherever she goes. It is located: this house, this mark, everyone in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where you have read this before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A household. A doorway. A red mark on the outside. Everyone who stays in is safe, everyone who goes out is not — and the destruction passes through the city regardless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 12 is the same shape, and Rahab's night is the only other place in scripture where the pattern appears whole. Whether she knew that is not the point; the text is built so that the reader does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rahab's own words, three verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know that the LORD has given you this land.* Everyone in Jericho had heard the same reports about the Red Sea and the two Amorite kings, and the whole city was melting with fear. She is not better informed than her neighbours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is the only one who acts on it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she was.** The text never softens her trade, and neither do the two New Testament writers who name her — Hebrews lists her among the faithful, James as an example of faith that did something. Matthew puts her in the genealogy of Jesus. Nobody in scripture is embarrassed about Rahab, which is itself worth noticing."}]}, "Joshua 2:8–11": {"title": "What the whole city knew, and what one person did about it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Rahab's speech is the most theologically loaded thing anyone says in the book of Joshua, and it comes from a Canaanite prostitute on a rooftop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "She names the evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We've heard how the LORD dried up the waters of the Red Sea… We've heard what you did to Sihon and Og.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Red Sea was forty years earlier. The two Amorite kings were recent. She has been following the news for a long time, and she has drawn a conclusion from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the city knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Terror of you has fallen on us. Everyone living here is melting with fear… there was no courage left in anyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the crucial detail. Rahab is not the only person in Jericho with the information. The whole city has it, and the whole city is terrified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She differs from her neighbours in exactly one respect: she acts. The rest of Jericho locks the gates."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line she ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD your God — He is God, in the heavens above and on the earth below.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not \"your God is strong.\" It is a statement about which God is God, in both realms, and it is close to the wording Moses uses in Deuteronomy. A woman outside the covenant, in a condemned city, states the central claim of Israel's faith more plainly than most Israelites in the previous forty years managed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Two verses on.** *I know that the LORD has given you this land* — she says *has given*, past tense, before a single stone of the wall has moved. Then she asks for her family. She is not gambling on an outcome; she is negotiating on the far side of one she has already accepted."}]}, "Joshua 24:11–13": {"title": "A land you did not toil for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joshua is old, the conquest is behind them, and he stands the nation at Shechem and tells them what happened — in the first person, as God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole recital is *I brought, I gave, I sent, I destroyed*. Joshua is quoting God speaking about the campaign Joshua himself led."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The general's own account of the war gives the credit to somebody else, all the way through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list of enemies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people of Jericho fought against you — as did the **Amorites, Perizzites, Canaanites, Hittites, Girgashites, Hivites, and Jebusites** — but **I gave them all into your hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven nations named, and one verb for the outcome. Not *you defeated them*. **I gave them.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hornet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I sent **the hornet** ahead of you, and it drove out the two kings of the Amorites before you — **not by your sword or your bow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsirah**, and it was promised back in Exodus: *I will send hornets before you, which shall drive out the Hivite, the Canaanite and the Hittite.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever it was, the point of the clause is the last six words. Their weapons were carried, drawn and used — and the verse says the ground was not taken by them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things given"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**A land you did not toil for**", "**Cities you did not build but now live in**", "**Vineyards and olive groves you did not plant but now eat from**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "An olive tree takes fifteen years to bear properly and a vineyard four. They walked into groves planted by men who never harvested them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Deuteronomy had said in advance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great and good cities which you did not build, houses full of all good things which you did not fill, hewn-out wells which you did not dig, vineyards and olive trees which you did not plant — when you have eaten and are full, **then beware, lest you forget the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The danger named is not poverty. It is amnesia."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Joshua is saying it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The recital ends with the demand: *choose for yourselves this day whom you will serve… **but as for me and my house, we will serve the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list of what was given is the argument. He sets out everything they received without earning, and then asks for a decision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they still had to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They crossed, they marched, they fought at Jericho and Ai and the Valley of Aijalon. Joshua does not pretend otherwise — the fighting is in the verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts the labour and the result in the same sentence and attributes the result to God."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they had done.** Nothing in the list. They received a settled country with the wells already dug — and Joshua's whole point in reciting it is that a people living off what they did not build are living off a gift."}]}, "Joshua 3:14–17": {"title": "The waters stand in a heap", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The crossing into the land, and the detail that matters is when the water stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of march"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **the priests carrying the ark of the covenant walking ahead of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the sea, forty years earlier, Moses stretched out a rod and the water divided before anyone moved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the ark goes first and nothing happens until it arrives at the edge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The season"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **the Jordan overflows its banks all through harvest season**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Spring, at the barley harvest, with snowmelt off Hermon coming down. The river is at its widest and fastest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator puts the clause in so nobody reads it as a summer crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the moment the priests who carried the ark reached the river and their feet touched the water's edge**, the water flowing down from upstream **stood still**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The water stopped when the feet went in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been told in advance: *when the soles of the feet of the priests rest in the waters of the Jordan, the waters shall be cut off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing was visible until somebody stepped into a flooded river."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It rose up in a heap, **far upstream, near a town called Adam, beside Zarethan**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew names the place. Adam is about sixteen miles upstream."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stopping happened out of sight of the people crossing. What they saw was a dry bed; the event itself was a day's walk north."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Hebrew for \"heap\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ned** — a heap, a mound. It is the same word used of the sea in the Song of Moses: *the waters were gathered together, the floods stood upright **like a heap**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the rest went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the water flowing down toward **the Salt Sea, the sea of the plain, was completely cut off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything downstream drained away and left the bed dry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they crossed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the people crossed over, **right in front of Jericho**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In full view of the city they were about to besiege."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rahab had already told the spies what the news was doing: *we have heard how the LORD dried up the water of the Red Sea for you… and as soon as we heard these things, our hearts melted.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The priests"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the priests who carried the ark **stood firm on dry ground in the middle of the Jordan**, and **all Israel crossed** on dry ground, **until the whole nation had finished passing over**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stood firm* is **kun** — to be fixed, established, made ready. It is the word for something set in place and holding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They went in first and came out last. The whole nation walked past them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve men, one from each tribe, carried twelve stones out of the riverbed where the priests' feet had stood, and Joshua set them up at Gilgal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And twelve more were set in the river itself, where the priests stood — *and they are there to this day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That this may be a sign among you when your children ask in time to come, \"**What do these stones mean to you?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened when they came up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it came to pass, when the priests who bore the ark came from the midst of the Jordan and the soles of their feet touched the dry land, that **the waters of the Jordan returned to their place and overflowed all its banks as before**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people came up from the Jordan **on the tenth day of the first month**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nisan 10 — the day the Passover lamb was selected and taken into the house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years earlier, on that day in Egypt, they were choosing lambs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What had to happen first.** The feet. The river was in flood and the promise was that it would stop when the soles of the priests' feet rested in it — so the first men into the water went in while it was still running."}]}, "Joshua 6:15–19": {"title": "The one house left standing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The city falls, and the exception is named out loud in the middle of the instruction to destroy it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the exception is placed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The city and everything in it are set apart to the LORD for destruction. Only Rahab and everyone in her house will live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an afterthought once the dust settled. Joshua announces it as part of the command, before the shout, before the wall goes down. The rescue was arranged in advance and spoken publicly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her house was in the wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 2 mentions it almost in passing: *her house was built into the city wall, so that she lived within the wall itself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means when the wall came down, the one structure that had to stand was the one built into the thing that fell. The text does not comment on it. It simply records the wall collapsing and Rahab surviving, and leaves the reader to put the two facts side by side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because she hid the messengers we sent.* Not because she was Israelite — she was not. Not because she had reformed — the narrative never says so, and both New Testament writers who mention her still call her by her trade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had made a covenant with the people of God, marked her house, and gathered her family inside it. That is the whole of her qualification."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows immediately.** The next paragraph is Achan taking what was devoted to destruction, and the trouble it brings on the whole camp. The chapter puts them together on purpose: an outsider who is spared because she was inside the terms, and an insider who is ruined because he ignored them."}]}, "Jude 1:5–7": {"title": "\"Angels who would not stay in the place that was theirs\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three examples, and the middle one is the strangest paragraph in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You know all of this already\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You know all of this already, but **I am going to walk you back through it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jude's method throughout the letter. He is not informing them; he is reminding them, and he says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Egypt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord brought a whole people up out of Egypt and rescued them — **and afterward He destroyed the ones among them who would not believe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Being brought out was not the end of the matter. The generation that walked through the sea is the generation that fell in the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes the same point in 1 Corinthians 10, from the same history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The angels"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there were angels who **would not stay in the place that was theirs; they walked out of their own home**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and both are about leaving a position."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *the place that was theirs* is **arche** — their rule, their domain, their principality. And *their own home* is **oiketerion**, a dwelling — the word Paul uses in 2 Corinthians 5 of the body as a habitation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something was abandoned that had been assigned to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has them now in **chains that will never break, down in the dark**, and He is **keeping them there for the judgement of the great day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present tense. They are not free and not yet sentenced — held."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says it in almost the same terms: *He handed them over to chains and to the dark, and there they are being kept until the judgement.* See the note on 2 Peter 2:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Sodom and Gomorrah did the same\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sodom and Gomorrah **did the same**, and so did the towns around them. They handed themselves over to sexual sin and **went after flesh that was not theirs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That connective is what makes the paragraph what it is. Jude says the cities did the same **as the angels** — and the phrase he uses of the cities is *strange flesh*, flesh of a different kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 19 records what the men of Sodom demanded, and who the visitors actually were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever Jude means, he means the two cases to be read together, and he has put them in that order deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exhibit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are **set out in plain sight as an example**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **deigma** — a specimen, something displayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Jude is doing this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's purpose is stated in verse 3: *contend earnestly for the faith which was once for all delivered to the saints*, because *certain men have crept in unnoticed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three examples are all cases of something that was inside a position and left it. That is the pattern he wants his readers to see."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the letter ends.** After all of that — chains, darkness, fire — the last two verses are the warmest doxology in Scripture: *now to Him who is able to keep you from stumbling, and to present you faultless before the presence of His glory with exceeding joy.* Kept, and presented faultless. Jude spends a page on people who did not stay, and closes on the One who keeps."}]}, "Judges 1:1–7": {"title": "\"Who should go up first?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens with a leaderless nation asking a question, and getting an answer that is already in the past tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question and the answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua was gone now, and the people were unsure where to turn. So they asked the LORD, \"Who should go up first?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer: *Judah will go. I have already handed the land to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the tense. Not *I will hand it over*. The giving is spoken of as done, and the going up is what remains. That order — given first, taken afterwards — runs through the whole conquest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Judah's first move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah does not go alone. He asks Simeon to come along, and offers the same in return: *come up with us into the land that's ours, and we'll fight side by side. Then we'll do the same for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simeon's allotted territory lay inside Judah's, so the two tribes had a natural reason to work together. Whatever else it is, it is a leader's first act being an invitation rather than a command."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grim episode"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They capture Adoni-Bezek and cut off his thumbs and big toes — mutilations that made a man unable to hold a weapon or run."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he says it himself: *seventy kings with their thumbs and big toes cut off once gathered scraps under my table. Now God has paid me back for what I did.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verdict is in the mouth of the man it falls on. Judges is a violent book and it rarely comments; here it lets the victim of the reprisal describe it as exactly what he had done to others."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seventy under the table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seventy kings with their thumbs and big toes cut off once **gathered scraps under my table**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy men who had once ruled cities, kept alive, maimed, and fed on what fell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had spent his reign collecting them, and he describes the arrangement without shame. It is the boast in the middle of the confession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other kings under a table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five Amorite kings brought out of a cave, and Joshua's commanders told to put their feet on their necks — with the promise attached: *this is what the LORD will do to every enemy you fight.* See the note on Joshua 10:22–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pictures of kings on the floor, one chapter of Scripture apart, and the second one is the model."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the name matters.** *Judah* means praise, and the tribe is sent up first. Genesis had already said the sceptre would not depart from Judah, and the line runs from here to David and to Christ. The book's first move puts that tribe at the front."}]}, "Judges 1:16–21": {"title": "\"The LORD was with Judah\" — and what stopped them anyway", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD was with Judah, and they took the hill country. But they could not drive out those in the valley, for they had iron chariots.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves, and the second one does not follow from the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Presence and the chariots"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse says God was with them. Then it explains a failure by pointing at enemy equipment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those two things cannot both be doing the work. If the LORD was with Judah, then iron was never the deciding factor — and the book itself proves it a few chapters later, when Deborah and Barak face Sisera's **nine hundred** iron chariots and the LORD routs every one of them in a river valley."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same terrain. Same technology. Opposite outcome. What changed was not the metal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Caleb was doing in the same paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Moses had promised, Hebron was given to Caleb, and he drove out the three sons of Anak from it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An eighty-five-year-old walks into a fortified hill town and clears out giants by name, in the same six verses where a whole tribe stops at flat ground because of chariots."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts them side by side without comment. He does not need to say anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is beginning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read on and the word *but* keeps returning: this tribe did not drive them out, and neither did that one, and neither did the next. Chapter 2 opens with the consequence spelled out — the peoples left behind become a snare."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land had been given. God had said so in the first paragraph of the book: *I have already handed the land to them.* What is being described here is not a promise falling short. It is a promise not fully entered."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the eyes went.** Judah looked at the valley and saw iron. Caleb looked at Hebron and said *if the LORD is with me, I will drive them out, exactly as He said.* One of them was reckoning with the chariots and one with the promise, and the chapter records what each of them ended up with."}]}, "Judges 11:30–31": {"title": "Jephthah's vow — a bargain nobody asked him to make", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the darkest passages in the Old Testament, and the first thing to notice is that God never speaks in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has already happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of the LORD came upon Jephthah.* That is the verse immediately before this one. He has already been equipped, already commissioned. The outcome is not in doubt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then he makes a vow anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If You will truly give the Ammonites into my hand… then whatever comes out of the door of my house to meet me when I return in peace — it will be the LORD's, and I will offer it up as a burnt offering.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is negotiating for something he has already been given. That is the shape of the tragedy: not too little faith in God's power, but a bargain offered to someone who had already said yes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law he lived under said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Torah is unambiguous. Human sacrifice is named repeatedly as the thing the nations did that God detests, and Leviticus provides a way to redeem a rash vow by payment. A priest could have released him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the text presents the vow as pious or the outcome as required. Judges records it; it does not commend it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book frames it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges is a downward spiral, and it ends with the line *everyone did what was right in his own eyes*. Jephthah's story sits inside that. The book is describing what happens to a people, not modelling behaviour."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On what became of his daughter.** The Hebrew is genuinely ambiguous, and readers have divided for centuries between a literal sacrifice and a life dedicated in perpetual virginity — the text dwells oddly on her never marrying. The honest position is that we do not know. What the passage does say plainly is that a man bound himself needlessly, and that a child paid for it."}]}, "Judges 14:5–7": {"title": "\"He told no one, not even his parents\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first recorded act of Samson's strength, and it happens where nobody can see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anointing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of the LORD came powerfully upon him, and he tore the lion apart with his bare hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is about rushing or advancing on someone — it is used of an army charging. It comes on Samson four times in his story, and every time something physical follows immediately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what it is not: not a mood, not an inward sense. Judges reports it as a force arriving and a thing being done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As easily as one might tear a young goat\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is deliberately domestic. Tearing a kid was something a farmhand did without thinking about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the strength was, the narrator describes it as effortless rather than strained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he told no one, not even his parents, what he had done.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence worth stopping on. Samson's whole story is otherwise a catalogue of showing off — riddles, boasts, a secret eventually talked out of him by a woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here, at the start, he says nothing. His parents were walking a little way ahead and never knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went on and spoke with the woman, and she was right in his eyes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is not presenting Samson as a model. He is on his way to a marriage his parents objected to, in Philistine territory, and the phrase *right in his eyes* is the book's own shorthand for what was wrong with that whole era — *everyone did what was right in his own eyes.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the lion becomes.** He goes back that way later, finds honey in the carcass, and builds a riddle out of it that starts the feud with the Philistines. The private victory turns into the public trouble. Judges keeps reporting both without comment."}]}, "Judges 15:18–20": {"title": "The spring called \"the one who cried out\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Samson has just won a battle single-handed, and the next thing recorded is that he nearly died of thirst."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have given Your servant this great victory. Must I now die of thirst and fall into the hands of these uncircumcised men?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things worth noticing. He credits the victory to God without hesitation — which, for Samson, is not guaranteed. And then he complains, bluntly, about the state he has been left in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a polite prayer. It is a man at the end of his strength saying so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So God split open the hollow place at Lehi, and water came pouring out. Samson drank, his strength returned, and he revived.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No rebuke. No lesson about attitude. Water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *revived* is the word for a spirit returning — the same idea used of someone coming round after a faint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is why the spring is called En-hakkore, the Spring of the One Who Cried Out — and it is there in Lehi to this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The place is named after the cry, not the victory. Not after the thousand Philistines or the jawbone, both of which the chapter has just described at length. After a man shouting for help because he was thirsty."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Samson was.** This is not a model believer. His story is a catalogue of appetite and broken vows, and the book knows it. The passage is worth reading precisely because of that: God answers a complaint from a man in the middle of a badly lived life, and the memorial left behind is of the asking."}]}, "Judges 4:17–24": {"title": "Jael and the tent peg", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The commander of nine hundred iron chariots is killed with two objects that belong to housework."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Sisera was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with him: twenty years of harsh oppression, nine hundred chariots of iron, an army Israel had no answer to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He ends the day on foot, in a woman's tent, asking for a drink."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jael took up a **tent peg** and a **hammer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pitching and striking the tents was women's work among the nomads. The peg and the mallet were the tools of her trade, in her hand every time the camp moved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not go looking for a weapon. She kills him with what she already had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The milk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He asked for a little water, and she opened a skin of milk and gave him a drink.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for less than she gave him. The milk is what puts him into the *exhausted sleep* the next sentence describes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges 5, Deborah's song, dwells on this: *he asked for water, she gave him milk; she brought him curds in a lordly bowl.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"At the hand of a woman\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the great commander died at the hand of a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator says it as a verdict, and it had been announced ten verses earlier. Deborah had told Barak that because of the way he was going about it, *the LORD will sell Sisera into the hand of a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barak arrives after it is over. *Come, and I'll show you the man you're looking for.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the story is told this way"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Iron chariots against a hammer and a tent peg. The book of Judges keeps doing this — an ox goad, a jawbone, three hundred men with jars and torches, a millstone dropped from a wall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same principle Paul states outright: *God chose the weak things of the world to shame the strong.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:26–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The small ordinary thing is not a lesser weapon. It is the one God keeps choosing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter closes.** *Israel's hand pressed harder and harder against Jabin, until at last they destroyed him.* One peg in one tent, and then twenty years of oppression unwinding."}]}, "Judges 4:4–10": {"title": "Deborah, Barak, and the honour that went elsewhere", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman is running the country, a general will not move without her, and the victory ends up credited to a third person nobody has met yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Deborah's position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A prophet was leading Israel — Deborah, a woman of fire and wisdom.* She judges under a palm tree, and Israel comes up to her for judgment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text states it without commentary or apology. She is the only judge in the book described as holding court, and the only one who is also a prophet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Barak's condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you go with me, I'll go. But if you won't go with me, I won't go.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just been given a direct command from God through a prophet, complete with the battle plan and the promised outcome. His answer attaches a condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges is a book of increasingly compromised leaders, and this is early in the slide — not a refusal, but not a straight yes either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it costs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because of the road you are taking, the honour will not be yours — the LORD will hand Sisera over to a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes anyway. The mission is not withdrawn and the victory is not smaller; what changes is whose name is on it. And the woman in question turns out not to be Deborah but Jael, who nobody in this conversation has mentioned."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hebrews does with him.** Barak is named in the roll of faith in Hebrews 11 — Deborah is not. The New Testament lists the man who hesitated among those commended, without mentioning the hesitation. Both facts sit in the canon: the honour redirected here, and the name kept there."}]}, "Judges 6:11–16": {"title": "\"Mighty man of valour\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An angel greets a man hiding in a hole with a title that describes nobody in the vicinity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gideon was there, **beating out wheat in a winepress** — hiding, really, keeping the grain out of Midian's sight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "You thresh wheat on a high, open floor, so the wind can carry the chaff away. A winepress is a pit cut into rock, for treading grapes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is doing the work in the worst possible place because it is the only place they cannot see him from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD is with you, mighty man of valour.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gibbor chayil** — a warrior, a man of force. It is the phrase used of David's mighty men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to a man crouched in a pit, threshing by hand, out of sight of an occupying army."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Illumination's note on it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was not the name of the man crouching in a winepress; **it was the name of the warrior grace would make him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title is spoken over what he will be, not over what is standing there — the same way Abram was called *father of a multitude* while childless. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Gideon says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If the LORD is with us, then why has all this come upon us?** Where are all His wonders that our fathers told us about?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He argues with the greeting. He has heard about the exodus and can see the winepress he is sitting in, and he says so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But now **He has abandoned us** and handed us over to Midian.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no faith in the reply, and the angel does not correct it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Go in this strength of yours\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go in this strength of yours**, and you will save Israel from Midian's hand. **Am I not the One sending you?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This strength of yours* — the strength he had just been given the name of, and shown none of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the credentials offered are not his. The question is about who is doing the sending."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My clan is the weakest** in all Manasseh, and **I am the least** in my father's house.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weakest tribe, smallest clan, youngest son. He gives three reasons and every one of them is true."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses said the same at the bush. So did Jeremiah, and Saul, and Solomon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But I will be with you**, and you will strike down Midian as though they were one man.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire response to the three objections. Not a correction of his self-assessment — a statement about company."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As though they were one man* — an army of a hundred and thirty-five thousand, counted as one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he ends up with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three hundred men, jars, torches and trumpets. Every weapon deliberately removed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lest Israel claim glory for itself, saying, \"My own hand has saved me.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the New Testament remembers him for.** *And what more shall I say? Time would fail me to tell of **Gideon**…* — Hebrews 11, in the roll of faith. The man who asked for a fleece twice, and then for the opposite sign the next night."}]}, "Judges 6:17–24": {"title": "The LORD is peace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Gideon asks for proof and then asks the visitor to wait while he cooks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give me **a sign that it is really You** speaking with me. Please, **don't leave until I return**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just been called *mighty man of valour* while threshing wheat in a winepress, and told to save Israel. See the note on Judges 6:11–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He wants confirmation, and he is not rebuked for wanting it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will wait until you come back.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words. Whatever Gideon takes to prepare a meal — and it turns out to be considerable — the visitor sits under the oak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he brings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **young goat**, and **from an ephah of flour** he made bread without yeast. He put the meat in a basket and **the broth in a pot**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ephah is something like twenty-two litres of flour. That is an enormous quantity — the same measure Abraham told Sarah to use for the three visitors at Mamre."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A young goat was a costly animal, and Israel was living on what Midian had not stolen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is under military occupation, hiding his grain in a winepress, and he cooks a feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is done with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take the meat and the bread and **lay them on this rock**, and **pour out the broth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not eaten. Put on a stone, and the liquid poured away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The meal becomes an offering, and the rock becomes an altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the angel of the LORD **reached out the staff** in His hand, and **its tip touched** the meat and the bread. **Fire blazed up from the rock** and consumed them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A touch of a staff tip, and fire out of stone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the angel of the LORD vanished from his sight.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Gideon says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Alas, Lord GOD! I have seen the angel of the LORD face to face!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is terrified, because the belief was that no one sees God and lives — *you cannot see My face; for no man shall see Me, and live.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Peace to you. Do not be afraid. You will not die.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three short sentences. The fear is addressed before anything else is said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The altar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Gideon built an altar there and named it **The LORD Is Peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew **Yahweh-Shalom**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names it after the sentence he was given, not after the fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the writer adds: *to this day it still stands at Ophrah.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does that night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next paragraph: told to tear down his father's altar to Baal and cut down the Asherah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did it **by night**, because he was too afraid of his father's household and the men of the town to do it by day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges records both — he obeyed, and he was frightened. The book does not tidy him up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the altar was for.** Gideon builds it before any battle, before the fleece, and before the three hundred. The first thing he does after being commissioned is make an altar and name it peace."}]}, "Judges 8:18–21": {"title": "\"Each one had the bearing of a king's son\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two captured kings describe men they killed, and their description is the striking thing in the passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What sort of men did you kill at Tabor?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gideon has run down Zebah and Zalmunna, the two Midianite kings, after the rout with the three hundred. He asks about an incident at Tabor that the book has not recorded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Men like you**,\" they answered. \"**Each one had the bearing of a king's son.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *according to the form of the sons of a king*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enemies, under no obligation to flatter, describing men they had killed — and the word they reach for is royal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the men were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Those were my brothers, my own mother's sons.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gideon's own family. The Midianites had killed his brothers at Tabor and he had not known how, until now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that says about Gideon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Men like you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two kings are looking at the man who defeated them, and they say his brothers looked like him — and that all of them looked like princes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set that beside where the book found him: *beating out wheat in a winepress — hiding, really, keeping the grain out of Midian's sight.* And beside what he said of himself: *my clan is the weakest in all Manasseh, and I am the least in my father's house.* See the note on Judges 6:11–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel had called him *mighty man of valour* while he was crouching in a pit. Two enemy kings, chapters later, independently say the same thing about his whole family."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was that all along. He was the last person to know it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jether"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He turned to **Jether, his firstborn**, and said, \"Get up, kill them.\" But **the boy did not draw his sword; he was afraid**, for he was only a youth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gideon hands the sword to his son, and the boy cannot do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges records the failure without comment, and the fear is given as the reason and the age as the excuse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the kings say to that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up yourself and do it — a man's strength is measured by the man.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *as the man is, so is his strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by men about to die, to the man who will kill them, and it is a taunt about the son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ornaments"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And took **the ornaments shaped like crescent moons** that hung on their camels' necks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crescents were Midianite religious symbols."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gideon takes the gold from that campaign and makes an ephod out of it, and *all Israel played the harlot with it there, and it became a snare to Gideon and to his house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that shows him told who he really is ends with him making an idol out of the proceeds."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two answers to \"what sort of men?\"** The enemy said *like a king's son*. Gideon had said *the least in my father's house*. Both statements are in the same book about the same man, and only one of them was ever true."}]}, "Lamentations 3:31–33": {"title": "\"He does not wound from His heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The centre of the book, and it is the only part that looks up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five poems over the ruins of Jerusalem, written by an eyewitness to the siege. The city is burned, the temple is gone, the walls are down, and the poems describe famine so severe that mothers ate their children."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 1, 2, 4 and 5 are unrelieved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 3 is the middle poem, and it is the longest — sixty-six verses, an acrostic with three lines to each Hebrew letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The turn comes at verse 21, and this passage is on the other side of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This I recall to my mind, therefore I have hope: **through the LORD's mercies we are not consumed, because His compassions fail not. They are new every morning; great is Your faithfulness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written by a man standing in the ash of his own city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the Lord **will not cast off forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zanach** — to reject, to cast away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not deny the casting off. He puts a limit on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though **He brings grief**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated plainly, in the same sentence. The poet does not attribute the disaster to accident or to Babylon alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **gather you back in compassion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **racham** — the verb from the word for a womb. Deep, physical mercy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For His **unfailing love is past all counting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chesed** — covenant loyalty — and *the multitude of His mercies*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two negatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **does not wound from His heart**, nor **bring grief to the children of men because He wills it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the first is *He does not afflict **from His heart***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two statements about motive, in a book whose subject is a judgment nobody disputes was deserved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The poet has already said God did it. Here he says where it did not come from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"children of men\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bene ish** — sons of man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *sons of Israel*. He widens it to the human race."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the poem says a few verses on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who is he who speaks and it comes to pass, when the Lord has not commanded it? Is it not from the mouth of the Most High that woe and well-being proceed?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *why should a living man complain, a man for the punishment of his sins? Let us search out and examine our ways, and turn back to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The poet holds both — that God did it, and that it is not what He wants."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jeremiah had spent his life doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Warning the city for forty years, being put in a cistern for it, and being told at the start that nobody would listen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the man who wept over it and the man who said it was coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other line from this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is good that one should hope and wait quietly for the salvation of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the sentence stands.** In the middle of five poems about a burned city, by somebody who saw it — and what he says is not that it did not happen, but that it did not come from the heart."}]}, "Leviticus 1:10–13": {"title": "On the north side of the altar", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The burnt offering from the flock, and the instructions include a compass point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A male without flaw\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let it be **a male without flaw**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tamim** — whole, sound, unblemished. The same word used of Noah, and the word Peter reaches for: *a lamb without blemish and without spot.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it dies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is put to death **on the north side of the altar** before the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction is specified, and Leviticus does not usually waste words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Golgotha lay north of the temple mount. The gospels place the crucifixion *outside the gate*, and Hebrews makes a point of it: *Jesus also, that He might sanctify the people with His own blood, suffered outside the gate.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the offerer does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read verse 4, from the paragraph before: *he shall put his hand on the head of the burnt offering, and it will be accepted on his behalf to make atonement for him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One gesture. He leans on the animal's head, and the transfer happens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The examination in this system falls on the animal, never on the man. See the note on John 1:29–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is burned"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priest arranges them **with the head and the fat** on the wood above the fire… Then the priest **offers it all**, burning it on the altar.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole animal. The burnt offering is the one sacrifice in which nothing is kept back — no portion for the priest, no portion for the giver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew name for it is **olah**, from the verb to go up. It all goes up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is washed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While **the inner parts and the legs are washed with water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two things nobody sees: what is inside, and what it walked on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A gift by fire, **a fragrance that pleases the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a soothing, restful aroma. The same phrase used after Noah's altar, when God said in His heart that He would not curse the ground again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes it into Ephesians: *Christ also has loved us and given Himself for us, an offering and a sacrifice to God for **a sweet-smelling aroma**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is first in the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus opens with this one, before the grain offering, the peace offering, the sin offering or the trespass offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burnt offering is not about a particular sin. It is about a whole life given up, entirely consumed, and the first thing the book describes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The graded provision.** A bull, or a sheep or goat, or — in the paragraph after this — *turtledoves or young pigeons*, for those who could afford nothing else. Mary and Joseph brought the birds. The system was written so that no one was excluded by price."}]}, "Leviticus 16:11–14": {"title": "Blood on the mercy seat, once a year", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement, and the only occasion in the year when anyone went behind the curtain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He deals with himself first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Aaron slaughters the bull that is his own sin offering, making atonement for himself and his household.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before he can carry anything in for the nation, the high priest has to be covered himself. The man doing the mediating needs a mediator."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews makes a great deal of this: *He has no need, like those high priests, to offer sacrifices daily, first for his own sins and then for the people's.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The incense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A cloud of sweet smoke rises and covers the mercy seat — **so that he may live and not die**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The smoke is not decoration or atmosphere. Leviticus says plainly what it is for: it veils the mercy seat so the priest is not destroyed by what is above it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even on the one day access is permitted, and even after the bull has died, there is a screen between the man and the glory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sprinkles it with his finger on the front of the mercy seat, and seven times before it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Once on the lid itself, seven times on the ground in front. Seven is the number of completeness — a full measure, not a token."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood is applied to the exact place God had said He would speak from. What was underneath the lid was the broken law; what went on top of it was blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The frequency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Once a year, one man, with blood, behind a curtain, in smoke, having first atoned for himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set that beside *let us walk right up to the throne where grace sits* and the distance the New Testament is describing becomes measurable."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The curtain.** Matthew records that at the moment Jesus died, the curtain of the temple was torn from top to bottom. Not opened. Torn — and from the top, which is the end no man could reach. See the notes on Hebrews 9:23–24 and Hebrews 4:14–16."}]}, "Leviticus 17:10–12": {"title": "\"The life given in your place\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most important verse in Leviticus about why any of the sacrifices worked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone… **eats blood in any form**, the LORD Himself will **set His face against that person** and cut him off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It applies to Israelites and to *any foreigner living among them* alike."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will set My face against* is the strongest formula of divine opposition in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the life of every creature is in its blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated as fact. Not a ritual convention — a claim about what blood is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus says it three times in this short passage, and again in chapter 17's hunting law. See the note on Leviticus 17:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD has **given that blood to you upon the altar** for one gracious purpose: **to make atonement for your souls**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the verb: **given**. The blood is described as a gift, assigned to a use."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why it may not be eaten. It has already been allocated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *atonement* is **kaphar** — to cover, to make propitiation. It is the same root as the pitch on Noah's ark and the ransom in Job 33. See the notes on Genesis 6:13–22 and Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is the blood — **the life given in your place** — that covers you and sets you free.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Substitution, stated in the law itself. A life instead of a life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews draws the conclusion: *without shedding of blood there is no remission.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the arrangement could not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same letter says it: *it is not possible that the blood of bulls and goats could take away sins*, which is why the sacrifices had to be repeated *year by year continually.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The system held the place for something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blood may never be taken in. That is the law, stated absolutely, with a penalty of being cut off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, in an upper room:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Drink from it, all of you. **This is My blood** of the new covenant, poured out for many for the forgiveness of sins.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one command Leviticus will not bend, He bends over a cup — and to the same men who had kept the food laws all their lives. See the note on Matthew 26:26–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost Him to say it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In John 6 He said it plainly in a synagogue: *unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink His blood, you have no life in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that time many of His disciples went back and walked with Him no more.* See the note on John 6:47–51."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is called.** Leviticus 17 is the chapter of the altar and the blood, and it sits immediately after the Day of Atonement in chapter 16. The book puts the explanation right after the ceremony."}]}, "Leviticus 17:13–14": {"title": "\"The life of every creature is its blood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A rule about hunting, and inside it a statement about biology that nobody on earth knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the life of every creature is its blood** — its blood is its very life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said three times in two verses. Leviticus does not usually repeat itself like that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nephesh** — life, soul, the living self. The life of the flesh is in the blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Around fifteen centuries before Christ."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Physicians did not understand what blood does until William Harvey worked out the circulation in 1628, and bleeding remained standard medical practice for another two hundred years after that. George Washington was bled to death by his doctors in 1799 — several pints, for a throat infection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Book said where the life was, and it was ignored for three thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He must **pour out its blood and cover it with earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pour it out. Not consume it, not use it, not keep it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For any animal taken in hunting, the blood goes into the ground and dirt goes over it. The life goes back where it came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the rule exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says it a few verses earlier: *I have given it to you upon the altar to make atonement for your souls, for it is the blood that makes atonement for the soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the reason blood is not food. It has been assigned to something else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Life, poured out, for a life. The whole sacrificial system runs on the sentence in verse 14, and the sentence in verse 14 happens also to be true anatomy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it points to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Without the shedding of blood there is no remission.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason the sacrifices had to be repeated is in the same passage: *the blood of bulls and goats* could not take sins away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One life was worth the exchange, and Leviticus is holding the place for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one exception.** The blood of every creature is to be poured out and never taken in. And then, at a table in an upper room: *drink from it, all of you. This is My blood of the new covenant.* The one command Leviticus never bends, He bends over a cup."}]}, "Leviticus 2:1–3": {"title": "Fine flour, oil, and frankincense", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one offering in Leviticus with no blood in it, and every element is specified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What goes in"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**fine flour** — the Hebrew is *solet*, the finest grade, sifted until there is no coarseness left anywhere in it. Nothing is uneven.", "**oil** — poured over it and mixed through it", "**frankincense** — the fragrant resin, laid on top"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the two things that are excluded, a few verses later: no yeast, and no honey. Nothing that ferments, and nothing that sweetens artificially."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The memorial portion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priest takes a handful of the flour and oil, together with **all** the frankincense, and burns this memorial portion on the altar.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A handful of the flour goes into the fire — and all of the incense. The fragrant part is entirely God's; none of it is kept back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest is food"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rest of the grain offering belongs to Aaron and his sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of this offering: a portion given, and the remainder becoming a meal for the priests. It is the only sacrifice in which the bulk of the gift feeds people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A grain offering was the produce of ordinary work — a farmer's crop, ground and brought. There is no death in it and no atonement language attached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set among the sin offering and the burnt offering, it stands for something different: a life given over, sifted fine, with oil through it and a fragrance that rises."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the details are worth reading.** Christian readers have long seen a portrait here — the flour with no unevenness in it, the oil mixed all the way through, no leaven and no artificial sweetness, and the whole of the fragrance going up to God. The chapter never says so. It simply specifies the recipe, and the recipe is exact."}]}, "Leviticus 2:11–13": {"title": "The salt of the covenant", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two things are kept off the altar and one thing is never allowed to be missing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is excluded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No grain offering you bring to the LORD is to be made with **yeast**, for **you must not burn any yeast or honey** as an offering by fire to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Yeast and honey. Both are ordinary, both are good, and both are barred from the fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why these two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have one thing in common: they both ferment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Yeast works through dough by spreading, and Scripture uses it that way throughout — *a little leaven leavens the whole lump*, in two of Paul's letters, and *beware of the leaven of the Pharisees*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Honey ferments as well, and sours in heat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both change on their own, and neither of them keeps."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are still good for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You may bring these as **an offering of firstfruits**, but they are **not to be burned on the altar** as a pleasing fragrance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They can be brought. They cannot go on the fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Pentecost, the wave loaves were *baked with leaven* — two of them, waved before the LORD. Leaven appears in the offering that represents the people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not on the altar; in the hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The requirement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Season **every** grain offering with salt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is emphatic. Every one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name given to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never leave out **the salt of the covenant of your God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the salt of seasoning. The salt of the covenant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a salt covenant was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that part of the world, sharing salt made an agreement binding. The phrase turns up twice more:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Numbers, of the priests' portion: *it is **a covenant of salt** forever before the LORD.*", "2 Chronicles, of David's throne: *the LORD God of Israel gave the dominion over Israel to David forever, to him and his sons, **by a covenant of salt**.*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times it means unbreakable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What salt does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The opposite of what yeast and honey do. Salt does not spread, ferment or change. It preserves, it stops decay, and it does not go off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two agents of change are kept off the altar and the agent of permanence is required on every offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction repeated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **all** your offerings, bring salt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus says it three ways in one verse — *season with salt*, *never leave out*, *with all your offerings*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the picture goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are the salt of the earth**; but if the salt loses its flavour, how shall it be seasoned?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *let your speech always be with grace, **seasoned with salt**.* See the note on Colossians 4:5–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else it is required"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the incense: *seasoned with salt, pure and holy*. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offering on the bronze altar and the incense on the golden one both have it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the rule amounts to.** Nothing that ferments goes on the fire, and nothing goes on the fire without the thing that keeps. Leviticus attaches the word *covenant* to the salt and to nothing else in the chapter."}]}, "Leviticus 2:4–10": {"title": "Oven, griddle, pan", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The same offering, three ways of cooking it, and the oil is in all three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three methods"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**baked in an oven** — loaves or thin wafers, the oil mixed in or spread on", "**cooked on a griddle** — an open plate over the fire, the hottest and most exposed method. This one is *broken into pieces with oil poured over it.*", "**prepared in a pan** — a covered vessel, the gentlest of the three"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Household kitchens had all three. Whatever equipment a family owned, there was a version of the offering they could bring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one that is broken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The griddle offering is the only one with the breaking specified: **broken into pieces**, and then oil poured on again afterwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fiercest heat, the most exposure, and the oil applied a second time — after the breaking rather than only before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is constant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fine flour in every case. Oil in every case. No yeast in any of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only the fire changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The memorial portion again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lifts out the memorial portion and burns it on the altar — a fragrance that pleases the LORD. The rest belongs to Aaron and his sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same pattern as the uncooked version: a portion into the fire, the remainder feeding the priests. However it was prepared, it ended up as somebody's meal."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the oil.** In Scripture oil is the standing figure for the Spirit — priests and kings were anointed with it, and the word *Messiah* means the anointed one. In this chapter the oil is mixed in, spread on, and poured over, and it is never optional. Whatever the fire does to the offering, the oil is already through it."}]}, "Leviticus 25:1–7": {"title": "The Sabbath year", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Every seventh year, an agricultural nation was commanded to stop farming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **Mount Sinai** the LORD said to Moses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus notes the location, which it rarely does. This is given at the mountain, before they have a field to rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you **come into the land I am giving you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command has no application in the wilderness. It waits for the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose Sabbath it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **land itself** is to keep a Sabbath **to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land is the subject. It is not a rest for the farmer, though he gets one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a sabbath of sabbaths* in verse 4 — the same intensive used of the Day of Atonement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For six years **sow your fields, prune your vineyards, and gather what they yield**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three activities, listed so the seventh year's prohibitions can match them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seventh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the seventh year is **a Sabbath of complete rest** for the land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not sow your fields or prune your vineyards. **Do not reap what grows on its own** or gather the grapes of your untended vines.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not only no planting. No harvesting of what comes up by itself either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything the ground produced that year was not to be brought in and stored."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A farmer stops in year seven. The last harvest was in year six. He has to eat through year seven, and then through year eight until the harvest he plants in year eight comes in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is roughly two years of food from one crop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later in the same chapter God raises the objection Himself: *and if you say, \"What shall we eat in the seventh year, since we shall not sow nor gather in our produce?\" **Then I will command My blessing on you in the sixth year, and it will bring forth produce enough for three years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, not two. The provision is stated with a margin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats in the seventh year"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever the land brings forth in that Sabbath **will still feed you** — **you and your servants and hired workers, and the foreigner living among you, and your livestock and the wild animals** of the land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six categories, in descending order of status, ending with wild animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever grows is unowned. Exodus puts it plainly: *let it rest and lie fallow, **so the poor among your people may eat from it***. See the note on Exodus 23:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For one year in seven, the fences come down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened when it was ignored"}, {"k": "p", "t": "2 Chronicles, on the exile: *and they went to Babylon, **until the land had enjoyed her sabbaths**. As long as she lay desolate she kept Sabbath, to fulfil seventy years.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy years of exile, and the chronicler counts them as the sabbaths owed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah's seventy years, which Daniel was reading when Gabriel came. See the note on Daniel 9:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after seven sevens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The jubilee — the fiftieth year, when debts were released, slaves went free and land returned to the family that had lost it. The rest of Leviticus 25 is about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah calls it *the year of the LORD's favour*, and Jesus read that line in Nazareth and said *today*. See the note on Isaiah 61:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the command asks.** That a farming people put down their tools for a year and eat from a harvest gathered two summers earlier — and the reason given is that the ground belongs to somebody."}]}, "Leviticus 7:11–18": {"title": "\"None may be left until morning\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The peace offering has a shelf life, and it depends on why it was brought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three occasions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus distinguishes them:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**thanksgiving** — brought because of something received", "**a vow** — brought to fulfil a promise", "**a freewill gift** — brought for no reason at all"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two time limits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The meat of **the thanksgiving** peace offering must be eaten **the same day**; **none may be left until morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the offering is made because of **a vow or as a freewill gift**, it may be eaten on the day it is offered **and on the next day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thanksgiving gets one day. The others get two."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most immediate of the three has the shortest window."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever is left over on the third day **must be burned**. If any meat is eaten on the third day, **it will not be accepted; it counts for nothing** to the one who brought it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **piggul** — foul, refuse, an offensive thing. Strong language for meat that is two days old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the effect is retroactive: the offering counts for nothing. It does not become a partial credit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the limit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage is about hygiene — the vow offering keeps a day longer under the same sun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distinction is in the reason for bringing it, and thanksgiving is the one that cannot be carried over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Yesterday's gratitude does not do for today. It has to be brought fresh."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bread that goes with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Loaves of bread **without yeast** mixed with oil, thin wafers spread with oil, and loaves of fine flour well-mixed with oil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three kinds, all unleavened, all with oil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, unexpectedly:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Along with these, present **loaves of bread made with yeast** as your peace offering of thanks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leaven is excluded from almost every offering in Leviticus — *no grain offering which you bring to the LORD shall be made with leaven.* It is the standing symbol of what corrupts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the peace offering it is commanded. Bread as people actually bake it, brought to the same table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of each kind is presented as a contribution to the LORD; **it belongs to the priest who sprinkles the blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest who handled the blood gets the bread. See the note on Leviticus 7:28–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the offering is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shelamim** — from **shalom**. The one sacrifice in the system the worshipper sat down and ate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was not brought to establish peace. It was eaten by somebody who already had it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul says about the leaven.** *Purge out the old leaven… **for indeed Christ, our Passover, was sacrificed for us.*** He uses it the usual way. And yet Leviticus puts a leavened loaf on the thanksgiving table — real bread, from a real kitchen, alongside the unleavened."}]}, "Leviticus 7:28–34": {"title": "The breast and the thigh", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two cuts of meat, and the instruction is specific about which two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the offerer brings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **his own hands** he brings the offerings made by fire — the fat, together with the breast.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew stresses it: his own hands. Nobody carries it in for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **waves the breast before the LORD as a wave offering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **tenuphah** — a waving, a moving to and fro. The priest and the offerer held it and moved it horizontally before the altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two portions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The breast belongs to **Aaron and his sons**… You are to give **the right thigh** to the priest as a contribution.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The breast** — the front of the animal, over the heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The right thigh** — the shoulder and haunch, the strongest part of the beast."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heart-part and the strength-part, and both go to the priest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which offering this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **peace offering** — Hebrew **shelamim**, from **shalom**. It is the only sacrifice in the system that the person bringing it got to eat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burnt offering went up entirely. The sin offering went to the priest or outside the camp. This one is a meal: God receives the fat, the priest receives the breast and thigh, and the offerer eats the rest with his family."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three parties at one table, over one animal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peace offering, and it is not brought to make peace. Leviticus places it after the burnt and grain offerings, and it is the sacrifice of somebody at peace already — brought for thanksgiving, or a vow, or freely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The time limit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The meat of **the thanksgiving** peace offering must be eaten **the same day** it is offered; **none may be left until morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A vow or freewill offering could be eaten on the second day. The thanksgiving one had to be eaten fresh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And on the third day, anything left *counts for nothing to the one who brought it. It has become unfit.* See the note on Leviticus 7:11–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Yesterday's thanksgiving does not keep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their **lasting share** from the people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a perpetual statute — a permanent arrangement for the priests, who had no land. See the note on Ezekiel 44:28–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes with the meat.** *Loaves of bread without yeast mixed with oil, thin wafers spread with oil, and loaves of fine flour well-mixed with oil* — and then, unusually, *loaves of bread made with yeast.* Leaven is banned from almost every offering in Leviticus. In the peace offering it is commanded."}]}, "Luke 1:18–20": {"title": "Silent until the day", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The question a priest asks an angel, and what it costs him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can I be sure of this?** I am an old man, and my wife is well along in years.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by what shall I know this*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for a sign, which is not in itself an unreasonable request. Gideon asked for one twice and got both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is talking to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am Gabriel. I stand in the presence of God**, and I was sent to speak with you and **hand you this good news**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel gives his own name and his position, which is rare — angels in Scripture usually decline to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel appears four times in the Bible: twice to Daniel, and twice in this chapter. The same messenger who explained the seventy weeks turns up to announce their fulfilment. See the note on Daniel 9:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hand you this good news** — the Greek is **euangelisasthai**, to evangelise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now, **because you did not believe what I told you**, you will be **silent — unable to say a word — until the day all of this happens**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **siopon** — silent — and the later verses show he was deaf as well: they *made signs to his father*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine months without speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it lands as it does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reply is not *you asked for a sign*. It is *you did not believe*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke sets up the comparison deliberately. Mary asks a question too, in the same chapter, and is not silenced. See the note on Luke 1:34–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her question is *how can this happen?* — a request for the mechanism."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His is *how can I be sure?* — a request for grounds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guarantee"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And it will happen. Every word of it will come true right on time**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *which shall be fulfilled in their season*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The unbelief does not cancel anything. The promise is restated in the same breath as the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the silence is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a sign. He asked for one and got one, and it is the thing he asked with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when he came out, **he could not speak to them**; and they perceived that he had seen a vision in the temple, for **he beckoned to them and remained speechless**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He should have come out and pronounced the priestly blessing — *the LORD bless you and keep you* — over the people who had been praying outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine months later, at the circumcision, they hand him a writing tablet and he writes *his name is John*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right then **his mouth was opened and his tongue was set loose**, and he began to speak — and the first thing that came out of him was praise to God.* See the note on Luke 1:59–67."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets his voice back the moment he agrees with what he was told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Benedictus — one of the three songs in Luke's opening chapters:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is the Lord God of Israel, for **He has visited and redeemed His people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who spent nine months unable to say anything opens with that."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sentence was.** Not a punishment for asking. He asked how he could be sure, and was given nine months in which he could do nothing but wait and watch it happen."}]}, "Luke 1:26–29": {"title": "The greeting that unsettled her", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An angel arrives in an unimportant town and says something that frightens a young woman — not because it is a threat, but because of what it implies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Gabriel actually says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice, you who have been so greatly favoured! The Lord is with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *greatly favoured* is one word, **kecharitomene** — a perfect passive participle built on *charis*, grace. Something like: you who have been graced, and stand in that state."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is passive. Nothing is said about what Mary has done. The word describes something done **to** her, already complete."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words unsettled her all the way through, and she turned them over, trying to understand what kind of greeting this could possibly be.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says the greeting disturbed her, not the angel. She is troubled by being addressed that way — and then she does what Luke will say she does twice more in two chapters: she thinks about it rather than answering quickly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the story is set"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A town in Galilee called Nazareth* — a village of a few hundred people, never mentioned once in the Old Testament. Luke has just come from the temple in Jerusalem, where the other annunciation in this chapter happened, to a priest, at the altar, at the hour of incense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is deliberate and Luke does not comment on it. He simply reports where the second angel went."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows.** Mary's question is not *why me* but *how* — a practical objection, and Gabriel answers it. Then: *let it be to me according to your word.* Luke gives her more speaking lines in this chapter than anyone else, and the longest of them is a song about God putting the powerful down and lifting the low."}]}, "Luke 1:30–33": {"title": "\"You have found favor\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The angel's second sentence to Mary, and it is a reassurance before it is an announcement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reassurance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid, Mary**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel says the same thing to Zechariah in the same chapter, and to Daniel twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has just recorded her reaction to the greeting: *she was troubled at his saying, and considered what manner of greeting this was.* See the note on Luke 1:26–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have found favor with God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **charin para to theo** — grace with God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greeting a few verses earlier used the related verb: **kecharitomene**, one who has been graced. The same verb appears once more in the New Testament, in Ephesians, of every believer: *He made us accepted in the Beloved*. See the note on Ephesians 1:3–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"found favour\" means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew equivalent is **matsa chen** — the phrase used of Noah before the flood, of Joseph in Potiphar's house, of Esther before the king. See the notes on Genesis 6:5–8 and Genesis 39:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In every case it is stated about the person and not earned by them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are going to **conceive and give birth to a son**, and you are to **name Him Jesus**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yeshua** — the LORD saves. Matthew gives the reason: *for He will save His people from their sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary is told the name. In that culture the father named the child, and Joseph is told the same name separately in Matthew's account."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four things said about Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He will be great**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of John, Gabriel had said *he will be great in the sight of the Lord*. Here the qualification is dropped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will be called **the Son of the Most High**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The Lord God will give Him **the throne of His father David**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And He will **reign over the house of Jacob forever**. **His kingdom will never come to an end**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The throne"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His father David.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise to David: *I will set up your seed after you… and I will establish the throne of his kingdom forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah had not had a king on that throne for six hundred years. The last one, Zedekiah, was blinded and taken to Babylon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two \"forevers\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Reign forever*, and *a kingdom that will never come to an end*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel says it twice in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the words come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 9: *of the increase of His government and peace **there will be no end**, upon the throne of David and over His kingdom, to order it and establish it with judgment and justice from that time forward, even forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel is quoting a prophecy seven hundred years old to a teenager in Nazareth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is hearing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A virgin betrothed to a man whose name was Joseph, of the house of David. **The virgin's name was Mary**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nazareth was a village of perhaps a few hundred people, in a region Judeans looked down on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can this happen?**\"* — and Gabriel answers with the mechanism rather than a rebuke. See the note on Luke 1:34–38."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is stated before anything is asked.** That she has found favour. The announcement follows it, and nothing in the passage presents the favour as a result of her answer."}]}, "Luke 1:34–38": {"title": "\"Let it happen to me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mary asks a question, gets an answer, and says one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can this happen? I have never been with a man**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *how shall this be, since I do not know a man*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is betrothed and not yet married. Betrothal in that world was legally binding — Joseph would have needed a divorce to end it — but the couple did not live together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this is not Zechariah's question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked *how can I be sure of this?* and was struck dumb. See the note on Luke 1:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She asks *how can this happen?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One asks for evidence; the other asks for the mechanism. Gabriel treats them differently and Luke sets them four hundred words apart so the comparison is unavoidable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Holy Spirit will come over you**, and **the power of the Most High will overshadow you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *overshadow* is **episkiasei** — to cast a shadow over, to envelop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses of the cloud covering the tabernacle: *the cloud covered the tent of meeting, and the glory of the LORD filled the tabernacle*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the word used at the transfiguration: *a bright cloud overshadowed them*. See the note on Matthew 17:1–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So the holy child who is born of you **will be called the Son of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And here is something else: **your relative Elizabeth is carrying a son in her old age**. The woman **everyone called childless** is **six months along**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel volunteers a sign she did not ask for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah asked for one and received nine months of silence. Mary does not ask and is handed one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **nothing at all is impossible for God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *no word from God shall be void of power* — **ouk adunatesei para tou theou pan rhema**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence, in the Greek Old Testament, is what the LORD says to Abraham when Sarah laughs behind the tent door: *is anything too hard for the LORD?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel quotes the Sarah passage to Mary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the Lord's servant. Let it happen to me exactly as you have said**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **doule** — a female slave, a bondservant. It is the feminine of the word Paul uses of himself at the head of his letters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Let it happen** — the Greek is **genoito**, an optative: may it be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same verb form as the *me genoito* Paul uses for his strongest denials, here without the negative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is agreeing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unmarried pregnancy in a village of a few hundred people, and a betrothed husband who would be entitled to end it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records what Joseph decided before he was told: *not wanting to make her a public example, was minded to put her away secretly*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the angel left her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing else is said. No further instruction, no assurance, and no plan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary got ready and **went quickly** up into the hill country.* See the note on Luke 1:39–45."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes to find the one other person on earth in a comparable position."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elizabeth says about her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How happy you are, because you believed** that what the Lord said to you would actually be done!\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two questions in one chapter.** A priest in the temple asking how he can be sure, and a girl in Nazareth asking how it will work. Luke records both and lets the answers speak."}]}, "Luke 1:39–45": {"title": "The baby leaped", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two pregnant women in a house in the hills, and the first person to recognise the Messiah has not been born yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The journey"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary got ready and **went quickly** up into the hill country, to a town in Judah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meta spoudes** — with haste."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nazareth to the Judean hills is roughly eighty miles. A young woman travelling that distance is not a small undertaking, and Luke does not say who went with her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes because Gabriel had told her about Elizabeth. See the note on Luke 1:34–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened at the door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She walked into Zechariah's house and **greeted Elizabeth** — and **the moment Elizabeth heard Mary's voice, the baby inside her leaped**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eskirtesen** — leapt, jumped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elizabeth is six months along; John is the one who moves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The baby leaps, and **then** Elizabeth is filled with the Spirit and speaks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the sequence deliberately. The recognition comes from inside her before she knows anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The volume"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **called out at the top of her voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anephonesen** — a loud cry. The word occurs once in the New Testament, and it is used in the Greek Old Testament of the Levites' shout at the ark's procession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are blessed among women**, and **blessed is the child you are carrying**!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two blessings, and the second one is what the first depends on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **why should this come to me — that the mother of my Lord should walk through my door?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My Lord.** An older woman, married to a priest, six months pregnant with the forerunner, calls her young relative's unborn child that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody has told her anything. Mary has said nothing beyond a greeting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The instant your greeting reached my ears, the baby inside me jumped for sheer joy**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She reports what her own body did and reads it correctly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel had said of John: *he will be filled with the Holy Spirit, even from his mother's womb.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How happy you are, because you believed** that what the Lord said to you would actually be done!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **makaria** — blessed, happy. It is the word that opens the Beatitudes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing Elizabeth commends is the believing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her own husband is standing somewhere in the house, unable to say a word, because he did not. See the note on Luke 1:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the two in the same building and does not comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mary says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Magnificat: *my soul magnifies the Lord, and my spirit has rejoiced in God my Saviour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is stitched almost entirely out of the Old Testament — Hannah's song in 1 Samuel 2 above all — and it is sung by a teenager."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has put down the mighty from their thrones, and exalted the lowly. He has filled the hungry with good things, and the rich He has sent away empty.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long she stayed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Mary **remained with her about three months**, and returned to her house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Long enough to be there for the birth, or nearly."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who recognises Him first.** Not a priest, not a prophet, and not an adult. An unborn child, at the sound of a voice through a doorway — and his mother says so out loud before anybody has explained anything."}]}, "Luke 1:46–55": {"title": "Mary's song", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A teenager in a hill-country house sings ten verses of Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elizabeth has just called out *blessed are you among women*, and *how happy you are, because you believed*. See the note on Luke 1:39–45."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary answers with this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My whole soul **lifts up the Lord**, and my spirit is **dancing in God my Saviour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **megalunei** — magnifies, makes great. It is where the song's Latin name, *Magnificat*, comes from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My Saviour** — she includes herself among those who need one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **He noticed me — a servant girl, nobody of consequence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tapeinosin** — low estate, humiliation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She uses **doule** of herself — a female slave, the same word she used to the angel: *I am the Lord's servant*. See the note on Luke 1:34–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prediction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From now on, **every generation will call me blessed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by a village girl in Nazareth, and it has been true for two thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the song is made of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost every line comes from the Old Testament. The closest model is Hannah's song, which it follows in structure and content. See the note on 1 Samuel 2:1–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two women, a thousand years apart, both told they would have a son, both singing about God turning the order of things over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary knows the Scriptures well enough to compose out of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **flexed the strength of His arm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *He has shown strength with His arm* — the standard Old Testament image for the exodus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six reversals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **scattered the proud**, tangled in the schemes of their own hearts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **pulled the powerful down off their thrones** and **lifted up the lowly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **filled the hungry with good things** and **sent the rich away with empty hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three pairs, and each is a swap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every verb is aorist — completed action."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened yet. She is a few weeks pregnant, no thrones have fallen, and the child will not be born for months."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She sings about it as done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last section"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **taken hold of Israel His servant**, remembering to be merciful, **just as He promised our fathers long ago — to Abraham, and to his children, forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The song ends where the covenant started."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything about herself after the first four lines. The personal note is brief and the rest is national."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *Mary got ready and went quickly up into the hill country.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had walked eighty miles to the one other person who would understand, and this is what she says when she arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long she stayed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Mary remained with her about three months, and returned to her house.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What waits at home"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unmarried pregnancy in a village of a few hundred people, and a betrothed husband entitled to end it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records what Joseph decided before the angel came to him: *not wanting to make her a public example, was minded to put her away secretly*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she sings about.** Not the child. Six reversals, in the past tense, before any of them has happened — and the personal part is four lines about being noticed."}]}, "Luke 1:59–67": {"title": "\"His name is John\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine months of silence end on a writing tablet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **the eighth day** they came to circumcise the child.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The day set in Genesis 17 and in Leviticus. Naming happened at the same ceremony."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The assumption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they were going to **name him Zechariah, after his father**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The family assumes. Nobody asks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mother"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **his mother spoke up: \"No. He is to be called John.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elizabeth has not been told by anyone in the room. Zechariah has been deaf and mute since the temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever passed between them, she knows the name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **there is no one in your family with that name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the whole argument they have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appeal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they **made signs to his father**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They gesture. He is deaf as well as silent — Luke shows it here rather than stating it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tablet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He asked for **a writing tablet**, and he wrote: **His name is John**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *John is his name* — a statement rather than a choice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not selecting. He is reporting what he was told nine months earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**John** — **Yochanan**, *the LORD is gracious*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The astonishment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **everyone was astonished**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two people who could not have consulted each other produced the same name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The release"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right then **his mouth was opened and his tongue was set loose**, and he began to speak.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The moment he agrees in writing, the speech comes back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel had said *until the day all of this happens*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first thing out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the first thing that came out of him was praise to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine months of stored-up speech, and Luke says what it was spent on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The effect"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **hush of awe settled over all the neighbors**, and **the story traveled through the whole hill country of Judea**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And people kept **laying it up in their hearts** and asking, \"**What is this child going to be?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *laying it up* is the same verb Luke uses of Mary twice: *Mary kept all these things and pondered them in her heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses it of ordinary neighbours here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the hand of the Lord was plainly on him**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And his father Zechariah was **filled with the Holy Spirit**, and he began to prophesy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Benedictus:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is the Lord God of Israel, for **He has visited and redeemed His people**… to give light to those who sit in darkness and the shadow of death, **to guide our feet into the way of peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And to the child: *and you, child, will be called the prophet of the Highest; for **you will go before the face of the Lord to prepare His ways**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man in the wilderness in camel's hair, saying *behold the Lamb of God*, and *He must increase, but I must decrease*. See the note on John 3:25–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And executed in a prison at a birthday party, on a girl's request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus on him: *among those born of women there has not risen one greater than John the Baptist*. See the note on Matthew 11:12–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What broke the silence.** Not the birth. Zechariah wrote down the name he had been given and had not believed — and the sentence about it opens with *right then*."}]}, "Luke 1:8–12": {"title": "The lot falls to Zechariah", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of silence end at an incense altar, and the man it happens to is terrified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rota"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Zechariah's division came up for **its turn of service**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David had divided the priests into twenty-four courses, each serving twice a year for a week. Luke says earlier that Zechariah was *of the division of Abijah* — the eighth of the twenty-four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the lot fell to him** — as the custom of the priesthood decided such things — to **go into the sanctuary of the Lord and burn the incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **elache** — obtained by lot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were thousands of priests. Burning the incense was the most desirable duty in the temple, and rabbinic tradition holds that a man who had done it once was not eligible again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For most priests it never came round. Zechariah is old."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Burning incense on the golden altar, immediately outside the veil. Four spices in equal parts, salted, unlawful to make for private use. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was outside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Outside, **at the hour of incense**, the whole gathered crowd **was praying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hour of incense was the hour of the morning or evening sacrifice — and the incense on the altar and the prayers of the people are the same thing in Scripture: *let my prayer stand before You like incense*. See the note on Psalms 141:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the crowd praying outside and the angel arriving inside, in the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the angel stood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Standing at **the right side of the incense altar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the position. The right side is the place of favour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Zechariah **went rigid at the sight of him**, and **fear came over him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **etarachthe** — troubled, agitated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the angel says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be afraid, Zechariah, **for your prayer is heard**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which prayer is not said. He and Elizabeth are *both well advanced in years*, and Luke has already recorded that *they had no child, because Elizabeth was barren*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer for a son had presumably been abandoned decades earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke says about them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they were **both righteous before God, walking in all the commandments and ordinances of the Lord blameless**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke clears the obvious inference before anyone draws it. The childlessness was not a verdict."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A son named John, who will *turn many of the children of Israel to the Lord their God*, and go before Him *in the spirit and power of Elijah*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Malachi's last words, four hundred years earlier: *behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah asks how he can be sure, and is struck dumb until the day it happens. See the note on Luke 1:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd outside waits, and when he comes out he cannot pronounce the blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Luke's Gospel ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He led them out as far as Bethany, and **He lifted up His hands and blessed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A priest who could not give the blessing at the start, and a blessing completed at the end."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the silence breaks.** In the one room a priest might enter once in a lifetime, at the hour the nation was praying outside, to a man old enough to have stopped asking."}]}, "Luke 10:17–20": {"title": "\"Your names are written down in heaven\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seventy men come back thrilled, and He redirects the excitement without dampening it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **even the demons do what we say when we use Your name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is emphatic — *even the demons*. It is more than they expected."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had sent them out to heal the sick and say *the kingdom of God has come near to you.* He had not mentioned demons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They came back with something they were not promised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Your name\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are careful about the mechanism. Not *we cast out demons* — *when we use Your name.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I watched Satan fall out of heaven like a bolt of lightning.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is imperfect — I was watching. He speaks as somebody who was present."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Like lightning** — sudden, visible, and from above to below."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The authority"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have **given you authority** to walk right over snakes and scorpions, and over **all the power of the enemy**, and nothing at all will harm you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *authority* is **exousia** — delegated right, permission. It is given, not developed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Snakes and scorpions* echoes Psalm 91: *you shall tread upon the lion and the cobra… you shall trample underfoot.* See the note on Psalms 91:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The redirection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But do not let your joy rest on the fact that the spirits obey you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not deny it happened, correct their report, or warn them about pride."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them what to be glad about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What to be glad about instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let your joy rest on this: your names are written down in heaven.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is perfect tense — have been written, and stand written."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ministry success is variable. Some days the demons come out and some days they do not; Jesus' own disciples failed at it in Matthew 17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name in the book does not fluctuate, and it was written before any of them cast out anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image runs through Scripture — *blot me out of Your book*, Moses said; *the book of life*, in Philippians, Daniel and Revelation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In that hour Jesus **rejoiced in the Spirit** and said, \"I thank You, Father… that You have hidden these things from the wise and prudent and revealed them to babes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records Jesus rejoicing exactly once, and it is here, immediately after this conversation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the seventy were.** Not the twelve. An unnamed larger group, sent out two by two ahead of Him, given no money, no bag and no sandals, and told *he who hears you hears Me.* They came back with authority over the enemy and were told to be glad about something else."}]}, "Luke 10:38–42": {"title": "\"Martha, Martha\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two sisters, one guest, and a correction that is gentler than it is usually read."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who opened the door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A woman named **Martha welcomed Him into her home**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is her house and her invitation. Nothing in the passage criticises that, and without her there is no scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Mary is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary **sat down at Jesus' feet** and listened to what He was saying.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sitting at a teacher's feet is the posture of a disciple — Paul says he was *brought up at the feet of Gamaliel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was not a woman's place in that culture. She has taken a student's seat in a room where women served."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was happening to Martha"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Martha was **pulled in every direction** by all there was to do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perispao** — to be dragged around, drawn away, distracted. It is a word about being pulled off course."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not lazy and she is not doing anything wrong. She is being torn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Lord, do You not care** that my sister has left me to do all the serving by myself? **Tell her to come and help me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things in one sentence: an accusation about His caring, a complaint about her sister, and an instruction to Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same accusation the disciples made in the boat — *do You not care that we are going down?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Martha, Martha.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubled name is how affection is spoken in Scripture. *Abraham, Abraham. Moses, Moses. Simon, Simon. Saul, Saul. Jerusalem, Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, it precedes something serious, and every time it is warm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are **worried and pulled about** by so many things.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words: anxious, and troubled — the second means to be in a tumult, disturbed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names her state before He names the remedy, and He does not tell her she should not have been cooking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Only one thing is really needed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is between *many things* and **one**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not many things done better, or more efficiently. One."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mary chose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Mary has chosen the good portion, and no one is going to take it away from her.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meris** — a portion, a share, the word for what you are allotted at a meal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Martha is preparing portions. Jesus says Mary has already taken hers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one is going to take it away* — the one thing about the afternoon that is permanent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Martha is not told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not told to stop serving, leave the kitchen, or be like her sister. She is told what she is being pulled apart by, and what the one necessary thing is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the sisters turn up next.** John 11, at Lazarus's grave. Martha runs out to meet Him and gets the sentence *I am the resurrection and the life* — spoken to her, and it is Martha who answers *I believe that You are the Christ, the Son of God.* See the note on John 11:23–27."}]}, "Luke 11:37–44": {"title": "\"Without letting go of the others\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is at a dinner party and says the hardest thing in Luke. The sentence at the centre of it is a correction of proportion, not a cancellation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He accuses them of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You carefully tithe your mint and your rue and every garden herb you grow, and you step straight over justice and the love of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The precision is the joke. The law asked for a tenth of the crop; these men were counting leaves out of the kitchen garden while walking over the two largest things in the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line He does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *stop tithing your herbs*. He says:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*These are the things you should have been doing, **without letting go of the others**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. Justice and the love of God taken up, and the giving not put down. Jesus had every opportunity in that sentence to abolish the practice and instead He kept it in place while re-ranking it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a correction of what had gone missing, not of what they were doing with the mint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You Pharisees scrub the outside of the cup and the plate, but inside you are full of greed and cruelty… Give away what is inside as a gift to the poor, and see — everything about you is clean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having diagnosed greed under a polished exterior, His prescription is not more careful measuring. It is generosity — giving the contents away. He treats open- handedness as the thing that reaches what scrubbing never could."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the meal setting matters.** This is said at a table, to a host, after Jesus declined to perform the ritual handwashing. Everything in the passage is about outsides and insides — clean hands and grasping hearts, polished dishes and unmarked graves. The tithing line belongs to that argument: a right practice, carried out by a heart that had never been dealt with."}]}, "Luke 11:45–54": {"title": "\"You have taken away the key\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A lawyer objects to being included, and gets three woes of his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of the experts in the law answered Him, \"Teacher, **when You say things like that, You are insulting us too**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just pronounced three woes on the Pharisees — over tithing mint and rue while passing over justice, over the best seats, and over being like unmarked graves people walk on without knowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lawyer's complaint is that the description reached further than intended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How terrible it is for you experts in the law as well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three more, addressed to them directly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **load people down with burdens too heavy to carry**, and you **will not lift a finger to help carry them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phortia dusbastakta** — loads hard to bear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves matter. The loading and the refusal to help are named as one offence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version: *they bind heavy burdens, hard to bear, and lay them on men's shoulders; but they themselves will not move them with one of their fingers.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other yoke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take My yoke upon you**… **for My yoke is easy and My burden is light**.\"* See the note on Matthew 11:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *burden* is the same word — **phortion**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **build fine tombs for the prophets** — and it was **your own ancestors who killed them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So you are **witnesses against yourselves**: **you approve of what your ancestors did**. They did the killing, and you do the building.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The monument is read as an endorsement. The argument turns on the fact that the same family is doing both jobs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The span"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"From **the blood of Abel** to **the blood of Zechariah**, who was killed **between the altar and the sanctuary**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abel is the first murder in Genesis. Zechariah is in 2 Chronicles 24, stoned in the temple court — and Chronicles is the last book in the Hebrew ordering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "First book to last book. He is naming the whole span of the Scriptures."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah's dying words there: *the LORD look on it, and repay*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have **taken away the key of knowledge**. **You did not go in yourselves**, and **you got in the way of the ones who were trying to**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the key of knowledge* — **ten kleida tes gnoseos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A key is for opening a door for somebody. This one has been removed from the lock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two failures again: not entering, and blocking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's parallel: *you shut up the kingdom of heaven against men; for you neither go in yourselves, nor do you allow those who are entering to go in.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the teachers of the law and the Pharisees began pressing Him hard, badgering Him with question after question**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enechein** — to hold a grudge, to be hostile — and **apostomatizein**, to interrogate, to press for answers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Waiting to **catch something out of His mouth** that they could use against Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a hunting word — lying in wait."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a meal. Verse 37: *a certain Pharisee asked Him to dine with him. So He went in and sat down to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage is dinner-table conversation, and it starts because He did not wash His hands first."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the three woes have in common.** All three are about what happens to other people: burdens laid on them, a monument that endorses their killing, and a door held shut in front of them."}]}, "Luke 13:10–17": {"title": "\"Should she not be untied on the Sabbath?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eighteen years bent double, and the argument afterwards is about whether the timing was appropriate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "She did not ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus saw her, He called her over.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every detail is His initiative. He sees her, calls her, speaks, and lays hands on her. Nothing in the account records her asking for anything — she had come to the synagogue, which is where she presumably went every week."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Woman, you are set free from what has been crippling you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is perfect passive — you **have been** released, and you stand released. He announces it as done before He touches her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words for tying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument turns on one image, and Jesus builds it deliberately:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Is there one of you who does not **untie** his ox or his donkey from the stall on the Sabbath and lead it out to water?*", "*And this woman… whom Satan has kept **tied up** for eighteen years — should she not be **untied** on the Sabbath day?*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb, three times. If a knot can be undone for an animal that has been tied overnight, what about a woman tied for eighteen years?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The country He was in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus based Himself in Capernaum, *in the regions of Zebulun and **Naphtali***, and most of His work was done along that shore."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's blessing over Naphtali is one line: *Naphtali is **a doe set free**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A doe let loose, and a woman untied after eighteen years, in the same territory. See the note on Genesis 49:21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A daughter of Abraham.* Not a phrase used elsewhere in the Gospels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives her the standing of an heir of the covenant, in a synagogue, in front of the man complaining about her — and He does it in the same sentence that names what had been holding her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reactions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who had been opposing Him was put to shame, and the whole crowd was overjoyed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports both, and the contrast is the point of the paragraph: the same event humiliated the officials and delighted everybody else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she did first.** *Right away she stood up straight, and she began to praise God.* Luke gives the standing and the praise in one clause. After eighteen years of looking at the ground, the first thing recorded is her voice."}]}, "Luke 14:1–6": {"title": "A Sabbath healing watched closely", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is invited to dinner and the dinner turns out to be a test."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One Sabbath Jesus came to eat at the house of **one of the leading Pharisees**, and **every eye in the room was on Him, waiting to see what He would do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they were watching Him closely* — **paratereo**, to observe from the side, to keep under surveillance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He accepts the invitation knowing what it is. Luke has Him at three Pharisees' tables and He goes every time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right there in front of Him was a man whose body was **badly swollen with fluid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hudropikos** — dropsical. It is a medical term, and Luke is the only New Testament writer who uses it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man is not said to have asked for anything, and he is not described as a guest. He is positioned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus turned to the experts in the law and to the Pharisees and asked them plainly, \"**Is it allowed to heal someone on the Sabbath, or not?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks first. The trap is sprung before they can spring it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not one of them answered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they kept silent*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They could not say no — nothing in the law forbids it. They would not say yes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So He **took hold of the man, healed him, and sent him on his way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *took hold* is **epilabomenos** — he takes the man by the arm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, and the last one removes him from the room. The man does not have to sit through what follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If your donkey, or your ox, **fell into a well on the Sabbath**, which one of you would **leave him down there**? **You would have him out that very moment**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their own practice. Rabbinic discussion of exactly this case survives, and the general ruling permitted rescue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He argues from what they would do for an animal, on the day in question, without hesitating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they had nothing at all to say to that**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it twice in six verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other Sabbath healing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before: a woman bent double for eighteen years, and the synagogue ruler objecting. Jesus answered with an ox and a donkey there too — *does not each one of you on the Sabbath loose his ox or donkey from the stall, and lead it away to water?* See the note on Luke 13:10–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same argument, same animals, and the same result: *all His adversaries were put to shame.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says next at the table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He watches the guests choose the best seats and tells them to take the lowest, and then tells the host to invite *the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind* — people who cannot return the invitation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the parable of the great supper, where the invited guests all make excuses and the servants are sent to the highways and hedges."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not asked of the man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. No question about his life, no instruction, no condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is taken by the arm, healed, and sent home."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the scene.** He asks the question first, gets no answer, heals the man, sends him out, and only then makes the argument — to a room that had come to watch."}]}, "Luke 14:15–20": {"title": "\"Please excuse me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three men turn down a dinner, and none of them has anything against the host."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What prompted it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How happy the one will be who sits down to eat in the kingdom of God!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pious remark from a fellow guest at a Pharisee's table, and it is the kind of thing nobody expects to be tested."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers it with a story about people who did not come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two invitations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **sent invitations out to a long list** of guests. When everything was ready, he **sent his servant around to tell them, \"Come now.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how it was done. A first invitation to reserve the day, and a second when the food was on the table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of these men had already accepted. The refusals come after they had said yes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is ready"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The table is set and it is all waiting for you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is asked of them. The cost, the preparation and the timing are the host's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three excuses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have just **bought a field** and I have to go out and look it over.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody buys a field unseen. The inspection happens before the purchase, not after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have just **bought five yoke of oxen** and I am on my way to try them in the harness.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten oxen, unexamined, already paid for. The same problem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have just **been married**. I cannot come.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third does not even say *please excuse me*. He states it as settled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the excuses have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Land, livestock, and a wife. All three are good — a field, working animals, a marriage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing sinful is named. The dinner is lost to ordinary property and ordinary happiness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And every excuse is in the past tense: *I have just bought, I have just bought, I have just been married.* Things already acquired, keeping them from something being given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one legal exemption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 24 does exempt a newly married man — from military service, for a year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third man may be quoting it. He is producing a valid reason for not going to war, about a dinner invitation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the master does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The master of the house **was angry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *\"Go out quickly into the streets and the back lanes… **bring in the poor and the crippled and the lame and the blind**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Luke 14:21–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Jesus was talking to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is at a Pharisee's house on the Sabbath, having just healed a man with dropsy and told the guests to stop taking the best seats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He had said, a few verses earlier: *when you give a feast, invite the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind — and you will be blessed, because they cannot repay you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then He tells a story in which the master does exactly that."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the first men lose.** *\"Not one of those men on the first list will ever taste this dinner of mine.\"* Not because they were refused. Because they were busy, and the food went to people who were not."}]}, "Luke 14:21–24": {"title": "\"There is still room\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The invited guests decline, and the food is already cooked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The master of the house **was angry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it plainly. Three men had accepted and then sent excuses when the meal was served."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first sweep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go out **quickly**, into **the streets and the back lanes of the town**, and bring in **the poor and the crippled and the lame and the blind**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four categories Jesus had listed a few verses earlier when telling the guests who to invite to their own feasts — *and you will be blessed, because they cannot repay you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The master does what Jesus had just recommended."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And these are people who could not have come to a normal dinner. Nobody invited the lame to a banquet; they had nothing to give back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sir, it is done — **and there is still room**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The best line in the parable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody the servant could find in the town has been brought in, and the house is not full."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second sweep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then go out on **the country roads and along the hedgerows**, and **press them to come in**, whoever you find.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outside the town now — travellers, and people sleeping rough under hedges."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *press them* is to compel, to constrain. It is urgency rather than force; a beggar in rags would need convincing that the invitation was real."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I want my house full.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the master's stated motive for the whole exercise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to punish the first list, and not to fill the seats out of pride. He wants the house full."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Not one of those men on the first list will ever taste this dinner of mine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not excluded. They excused themselves — and Luke's Greek in the previous paragraph has all three of them saying so politely. See the note on Luke 14:15–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Invited, declined. Town, brought in. Countryside, brought in. Room, still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's account of the same movement: *it was necessary that the word of God should be spoken to you first; but since you reject it… we turn to the Gentiles.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He was sitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a Pharisee's table on the Sabbath, as a guest, having watched the other guests choosing the best places."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells the room a story in which the people on the guest list miss dinner and the street gets fed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the servant did not do.** Argue with anybody, or lower the standard of the meal, or ask the master to reduce it. He went out twice and came back both times, and the second time the house filled."}]}, "Luke 15:11–16": {"title": "A son in a far country", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third of three parables told to answer one complaint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is being told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then all the tax collectors and the sinners drew near to Him to hear Him. And the Pharisees and scribes complained, saying, \"**This Man receives sinners and eats with them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke 15 is one answer in three parts: a lost sheep, a lost coin, and this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two end with the finder rejoicing. The third is longer and has a second half about somebody who does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Father, **give me my share of the property now**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture the estate passed at death. Asking for it early was as good as saying the father was dead to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no record in the parable of the father arguing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the father **divided what he had between them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **bion** — his living, his means of life. The father divides his own livelihood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The elder brother's share goes to him at the same moment, which matters for the second half of the story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not many days later the younger son **gathered up everything that was his, left home**, and traveled off **to a distant country**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *gathered up* is a word for converting property into cash. He liquidated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there he **threw the whole of it away on wild and reckless living**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **asotos** — from **a-sozo**, unsaved, without deliverance. It carries the sense of a life with no way of being kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not itemise. The elder brother does that later, and Luke is careful to attribute the details to him rather than to the narrator."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When it was all gone, **a hard famine settled over that country**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The money ran out first and then the famine came. Either alone he might have survived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So he **hired himself out** to one of the local men, who sent him **into the fields to feed pigs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekollethe** — he glued himself to, attached himself. It is the language of a man begging for work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jew feeding pigs is at the bottom of every category at once — foreign country, unclean animals, hired rather than owned, and hungry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He got so hungry that he **would gladly have eaten the pods the pigs were eating**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pods are carob — animal fodder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **nobody gave him anything at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That sentence ends the section, and it is the bottom of the story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when he came to himself* — and the speech he rehearses, and the father who sees him *while he was still a great way off* and runs. See the note on Luke 15:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the father's reaction is **esplanchnisthe** — the gut-word, the same one used of Jesus looking at the crowds. See the note on Matthew 9:35–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he rehearses and what he gets to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He plans: *make me like one of your hired servants.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets as far as *I am no longer worthy to be called your son* and the father cuts him off with instructions to the servants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line about hired servants is never delivered."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the story starts.** With a father who divides his living because he was asked to, and lets a son walk out with it. Nothing in the first half is done to stop him."}]}, "Luke 15:17–20": {"title": "\"He ran\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The son rehearses a speech in a pigsty, and his father ruins the plan by running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What brings him home"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How many hired men on my father's place have more bread than they can eat, and here I am starving to death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is honest about the motive. It is hunger, and a comparison — the servants at home eat better than this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is presented as a noble repentance. He *came to his senses*, which is the language of a man waking up rather than a man reforming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speech he prepares"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three parts: I have sinned against heaven, I have sinned against you, **take me on as one of your hired men**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third part is a proposal. He has worked out terms — a job, a wage, a way to be useful and stay fed without claiming anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the father sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While he was still a long way off, his father saw him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distance is the detail. To see someone that far away you have to have been looking, and to recognise him at that range you have to have been looking for a while."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The running"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His heart went out to the boy, and he ran — he ran down the road to meet him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture, an elderly man did not run. It required hitching up the robe and exposing the legs, and it was undignified in a way that would have been noticed by the whole village."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it anyway, and he does it before a word of the speech is spoken. The embrace and the kiss come first; the confession arrives afterwards, and the third part of it never gets said at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the speech never reached.** *Take me on as one of your hired men* is cut off by the father calling for the best robe. The bargain the son had worked out is not refused so much as never allowed into the conversation. See the note on Luke 15:21–24."}]}, "Luke 15:21–24": {"title": "The speech he never finished", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The son has a speech ready. He worked it out in the pigsty, and it had three parts: I have sinned against heaven, I have sinned against you, **make me one of your hired men.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Watch what happens to the third part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He gets two-thirds of the way through"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Father, I have sinned against heaven, and I have sinned against you. I am not fit to be called your son —*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the dash is doing real work. He is cut off. The father is already talking to the servants about a robe. The line the son rehearsed hardest — the arrangement he was going to propose, where he earns his keep and everyone's honour is satisfied — never gets said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Confession is welcomed. The bargain is not allowed to be spoken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the father calls for, and why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, and none of them are comfort items. Every one is a marker of standing in that household:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the best robe** — literally the first robe, the one belonging to the head of the house", "**a ring** — a signet: authority to act in the family name", "**sandals** — servants went barefoot; sons did not", "**the fattened calf** — the animal kept for the one occasion big enough to need it"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not get a bath and a probationary period. He gets dressed as a son in front of everybody, before he has done a single thing to prove himself, and the household is fed on the best animal on the property."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"This son of mine was dead\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father gives his reason twice, and it is not that the boy repented well:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "This son of mine was dead, and he is alive again. He was lost, and he is found."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dead and alive. Lost and found. Those are the two pairs the whole chapter has been building — the sheep, the coin, and now this. In all three the thing that was missing does nothing to recover itself. It is carried, swept up, run to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The son's repentance is real and it matters. But the story is careful about what it accomplishes: it gets him home. It does not buy the robe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is doing in this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is answering a complaint about the company He keeps. The answer is a father who runs, interrupts an apology, and throws a party — and the point of putting the interruption in is that the son's plan to work his way back was never on the table."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the celebration.** In all three stories the finder calls the neighbours together. The joy is not private, and it is never at the recovered thing's expense. Nobody in the chapter is welcomed home quietly and then reminded later what they cost."}]}, "Luke 15:29–32": {"title": "The older brother, and the word he never uses", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The story is usually named after the younger son, but Jesus did not stop where the party started. He told it to people who were annoyed that He ate with the wrong sort, and the last scene is aimed straight at them. It is the only one of the three stories in this chapter that does not end with something found."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Listen to how he talks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four sentences, and every one of them is worth slowing down for:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*all these years I have worked for you* — the word is the one used of a hired hand's labour, not a son's life at home", "*I have never once disobeyed an order of yours* — an order. Not a word from a father; an instruction from a master", "*you never gave me so much as a young goat* — a man who has never asked", "*this son of yours* — not *my brother*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has lived in the house for years and describes it as a job. The tragedy is not that he is wrong about his own record — the father never disputes a word of it — but that a faultless record has left him standing outside his own home, angry, on the one night the place is full of music."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My child, you are always with me, and everything I have is yours.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read that against the complaint. He asked for a goat. He is told he owns the estate. He has been *working for* what was already in his name. And the word the father uses is not the formal one for a grown son; it is affectionate, the word you would use for your own boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is being given to him in that sentence. He is being told what has been true the entire time he was earning it."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thing he never says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Track the vocabulary. The younger son comes home and says *father* — twice. The older son opens with *Look*, and never uses the word at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The younger one lost everything and knew what he was coming home to. The older one lost nothing and did not know where he lived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Jesus ends it unfinished"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The story stops. We are not told whether he came in. Every other thread in the chapter closes — the sheep is carried home, the coin is found, the son is embraced — and this one is left open, because the people Jesus was telling it to were standing in exactly that spot while He told it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the elder son's picture of his father.** Notice that his bitterness is not aimed at his brother first — it is aimed at a father he believes has been withholding a goat for years. That is not what the father is like, and the story has already shown us so. A person's grievance is usually built on a picture of God that the facts do not support, and the correction here is not a rebuke but a piece of information: *everything I have is yours*."}]}, "Luke 15:3–7": {"title": "\"Until he finds it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the three stories in this chapter, told to people who were complaining about the company Jesus kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He is telling it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with tax collectors and sinners crowding in to hear Him, and the Pharisees muttering: *this man welcomes sinners and eats with them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three parables answer that one sentence. The complaint is the reason the chapter exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Keep going until he finds it\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The searching has no stated limit. Not for a while, not until dark — until."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the ninety-nine are left *out in the open country*. Jesus does not pause to justify that. The shepherd's arithmetic is not the point of the story; the finding is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lifts it up onto his shoulders, and the whole way home he is rejoicing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sheep does nothing. It does not find its way back or follow him home — it is carried the entire distance, on the shoulders of the one who came out for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the mood is set on the road, not at the door. He is rejoicing before anyone else knows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The party"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Celebrate with me — I have found my sheep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of the three stories ends with neighbours being called together. The joy is public each time, and the celebration is never at the recovered thing's expense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*More joy over one sinner whose mind is turned around than over ninety-nine upright people who have no need of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase carries an edge. Jesus is describing people who see no need of repentance, and He is looking at some while He says it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the three stories add up to.** A sheep that wandered off, a coin that could not have moved itself, and a son who walked out deliberately. Different ways of being lost — and in every case somebody goes looking, and there is a party afterwards. See the notes on Luke 15:8–10, 15:17–20 and 15:21–24."}]}, "Luke 15:8–10": {"title": "The lamp and the broom", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The middle story of the three, and the shortest — but it changes one thing about the picture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The coin could not stray"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sheep wanders off. A son leaves on purpose. A coin does neither."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was lost in the house, by no fault and no decision of its own, and it could not have found its way anywhere. That is a third kind of lostness, and Jesus puts it deliberately between the other two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Does she not light a lamp and sweep out the whole house, going over every corner of it until she finds it?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three actions: light, sweep, search. Houses of that period had small high windows and packed earth floors — a coin could sit in the dust for a long time. The lamp is lit because you cannot find anything in that room without one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And again: **until**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was worth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A drachma, roughly a day's wage. Not a fortune — one tenth of what she had."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She turns the entire house over for it, which is the proportion the story wants you to notice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The joy is somewhere specific"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is like that among the angels of God. They are glad over one sinner whose mind is turned around.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first story said *in heaven*; this one says *among the angels*. Jesus is locating the celebration a little more precisely each time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why a woman.** Jesus tells three stories in a row and gives the middle one to a woman searching her own house — set beside a shepherd and a father. In a chapter arguing about who God is like, He uses all three without comment."}]}, "Luke 16:14–18": {"title": "\"The law and the prophets ran up to John\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four short sayings, and Luke sets them between two parables about money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is listening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Pharisees were listening to all this, and **because they loved money they sneered at Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exemukterizon** — literally *turned up the nose*. It occurs twice in the New Testament, here and at the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have just heard the parable of the unjust steward and *you cannot serve God and mammon*. See the note on Luke 16:9–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are the ones who **make yourselves look righteous in front of other people**, but **God knows what is inside you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you justify yourselves before men*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that they were failing to keep the standard. That the audience was wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What men hold up as impressive is detestable in the sight of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **bdelugma** — abomination. It is the word used in Daniel of the abomination of desolation, and in Leviticus of the things that defile."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The boundary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The law and the prophets ran up to the time of John**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same statement Matthew records: *all the Prophets and the Law spoke their prophecies right up to John.* See the note on Matthew 11:12–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both Gospels put John at the join."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has been happening since"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Since then the good news of the kingdom of God has been announced**, and **everyone is pushing to get in**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **biazetai** — the same force-word as Matthew's version."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But it would be **easier for heaven and earth to disappear** than for **the smallest stroke of a letter in the law to fail**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **keraia** — a horn, the little serif that distinguishes one Hebrew letter from another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the era has ended and in the next breath says not one pen-stroke will fail."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both stand. He says the same in Matthew 5: *do not think that I came to destroy the Law or the Prophets. I did not come to destroy but **to fulfil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing drops off. It is carried to its end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Anyone who **divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An abrupt landing after a statement about eras."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is put there as the demonstration: the Pharisees had built a permissive divorce practice out of a clause in Deuteronomy, and He applies the standard they claimed to hold to the thing they had loosened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just been told they justify themselves before men. This is what that looked like in practice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke does with the arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Parable about money — sneering — this — and then straight into the rich man and Lazarus, which is about a man in fine linen who fared sumptuously every day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke frames the sayings with two stories about wealth, and the men sneering are described as lovers of money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of the next parable"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If they do not hear Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded though one rise from the dead**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is where the chapter ends."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two halves nobody can drop.** The law and the prophets ran up to John, and not one serif will fail. He says both in consecutive sentences."}]}, "Luke 16:9–13": {"title": "\"Faithful with what belongs to someone else\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has just told the strangest parable in Luke — a dishonest manager who cooks the books and gets commended for shrewdness — and this is what He draws out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale He puts money on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read how He describes it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*a very small thing*", "*something as small as money*", "*what belongs to someone else*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times, and each one demotes it. Money is the small thing, the borrowed thing, the test case — not the prize."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set against it is *what is genuinely valuable* and *anything of your own*. The comparison is not between poverty and wealth. It is between two orders of property, and the one everybody worries about is the lesser."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strange logic of the first line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Use the money of this world to make friends for yourselves, so that when you finally fail, you will be welcomed into a home that lasts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase for money is literally *unrighteous mammon* — wealth belonging to a crooked world. Jesus does not tell them to avoid it; He tells them to spend it in a direction that outlasts it. The manager in the parable used his master's money to secure a future. Do that, He says, with better aim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that ends it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You cannot serve God and money both.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is to serve as a slave, and the point is not that money is evil but that it competes. Two owners cannot both have you. Everything before this — the smallness, the borrowed quality, the shrewd spending — is preparing for a sentence about who owns whom."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"faithful in little\" means here.** Not that careful budgeting earns spiritual promotion. The argument is about character being one thing: how a person handles the small borrowed thing is what they will do with the large permanent one, because it is the same person doing it."}]}, "Luke 17:5–6": {"title": "\"Give us more faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The apostles ask for an increase, and the answer they get is not more of what they had in mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the four verses before it. Jesus has just said that if your brother sins against you seven times a day and turns back seven times saying he repents, you must forgive him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give us more faith* is what they say next. It is not a request about mountains or miracles. It is a response to being told to forgive somebody seven times before dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If your faith were the size of a mustard seed, you could say to this mulberry tree, \"Pull yourself up by the roots and plant yourself in the sea,\" and it would obey you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not give them more. He tells them the quantity was never the issue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A mustard seed was proverbially the smallest thing anyone handled — and the mulberry, with its deep root system, was proverbially among the hardest trees to shift. Smallest possible faith, hardest possible object, and the tree obeys."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction inside it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You could **say** to this tree.* The faith in the illustration is exercised by speaking to the thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth noticing in a passage about forgiveness. The uprooting Jesus describes is not something felt into being; it is addressed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A parable about a servant who comes in from the field and serves dinner before eating — *we are unworthy servants; we have only done our duty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus keeps the two together: faith the size of a seed can uproot a tree, and the people exercising it are not to think of themselves as impressive for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"size.\"** The Greek is literally *if you have faith as a grain of mustard* — a comparison of kind as much as quantity. Something alive and small, which is what a seed is. Not a measured amount of a substance."}]}, "Luke 18:18–23": {"title": "\"One thing you still lack\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man with everything asks what he has to do, and the answer takes everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Good teacher, **what do I have to do to inherit eternal life?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An inheritance is the one thing in the world nobody does anything to get. It comes by birth, by relationship, by somebody's death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks what to **do** to get one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Jesus picks up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why do you call Me good?** No one is good except God alone.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not deny it. He asks the man whether he has thought about what he just said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man used *good* as a compliment between equals. Jesus takes it as a category with one occupant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commandments listed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not commit adultery, do not murder, do not steal, do not tell lies about people, honor your father and your mother.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five, and all five are from the second table — the ones about other people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first table, the commandments about God, is not read out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have kept every one of those**,\" the man said, \"ever since I was a boy.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it without hesitation and Jesus does not contradict him. Mark's account adds the line Luke leaves out: *then Jesus, looking at him, loved him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"One thing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There is **one thing you are still missing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a list. He has kept the five, and one item remains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the one thing is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go and **sell everything you own** and **give the money to the poor**, and you will have **treasure in heaven**. Then **come back and follow Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four instructions. The first two empty him, the third relocates what he had, and the fourth is the actual invitation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same invitation given free to fishermen and a tax collector, with a condition attached that was attached to nobody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commandment that was left out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first one. *You shall have no other gods before Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was asked to prove he had kept the table that was never recited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the man heard it, **sorrow came over him, because he was very rich**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke ends the scene there. The man does not argue, does not bargain, and is not recorded as leaving in Luke's wording — Matthew and Mark say he went away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only person in the Gospels who is invited to follow and does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How hard it is** for those who have riches to enter the kingdom of God! It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when they ask *who then can be saved*, the answer is *the things which are impossible with men are possible with God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next man in Luke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zacchaeus, in the very next chapter — rich, and he gives half his goods to the poor without being asked. Luke puts them one after another. See the note on Luke 19:1–10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he came for.** He asked how to inherit. He was told to sell, give, and come — and the treasure was named as already waiting in heaven before he took a step."}]}, "Luke 18:24–27": {"title": "\"What is impossible for people is not impossible for God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A rich man has just walked away sad, and Jesus watches him go before He says anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus saw the sorrow settle over him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comment that follows is not aimed at the man's back as a rebuke. Luke records Jesus watching the grief land, and Mark's account adds that He had looked at him and loved him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The camel and the needle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is easier for a camel to squeeze through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to get into the kingdom of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Attempts have been made to soften this — a small gate in Jerusalem called the Needle's Eye, or a rope rather than a camel. There is no evidence for the gate, and the rope theory rests on a variant reading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is meant to be impossible. That is why the disciples react the way they do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then who can possibly be saved?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the right response, and it tells you what they had assumed. Wealth was read as evidence of God's blessing; if the obviously blessed cannot get in, nobody can."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not correct the logic. He accepts it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What is impossible for people is not impossible for God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say it is difficult, or that rich people need to try harder. He moves the whole question out of the category of human capacity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the answer covers the disciples too — the ones who had just pointed out that they left everything."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who it turns out to be possible for.** Two chapters later Luke gives us Zacchaeus, a rich tax collector, up a tree, and Jesus inviting Himself to dinner. Luke tells the impossible saying and then tells the story of it happening."}]}, "Luke 19:1–10": {"title": "\"Zacchaeus, hurry down\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man climbs a tree to get a look at Jesus and ends up hosting Him for dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **chief tax collector**, and he was **rich**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **architelones** — a word that appears nowhere else in Greek literature. Not a tax collector but the head of a district's collectors, taking a cut of everything they took."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jericho sat on a major trade route. It was a lucrative posting, and it made him a collaborator with Rome and a thief in his neighbours' eyes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He could not get a look at Him over the crowd, **because he was a short man**. So he **ran on ahead and climbed up into a sycamore tree**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wealthy official in that culture did not run, and did not climb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sycamore-fig has a low, broad trunk and wide branches — easy to get into, and easy to be seen in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **looked up** and said, \"**Zacchaeus, hurry down. I am staying at your house today.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He looks up, uses the man's name, and invites Himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no record of anyone telling Him who it was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And nothing is asked first. No repentance is required as a condition of the visit, and no conversation about his profession precedes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who saw it started grumbling. \"**He has gone in to be the guest of a sinner**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses the word for muttering — the same verb used of Israel in the wilderness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Zacchaeus does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, here and now I am **giving half of everything I own to the poor**, and if I have cheated anyone, **I will pay it back four times over**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody asked him for either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law required a fifth added to restitution. Fourfold was the penalty for stolen livestock — the maximum in the law, and the figure David pronounced on the rich man in Nathan's parable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He volunteers the harshest rate available, on himself, having already given half away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dinner invitation comes first. The generosity comes after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Compare the rich young ruler two chapters earlier, who was told to sell what he had and *went away sorrowful, for he had great possessions.* Same subject, opposite sequence, opposite outcome."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Today salvation has come to this house** — for this man **too is a son of Abraham**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Too.* The crowd had written him out of the family. Jesus writes him back in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Son of Man came to seek and to save what was lost.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the mission statement of the Gospel in the mouth of Jesus, at the end of this story, about a man who climbed a tree."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who else was in Jericho that week.** A blind beggar on the roadside who would not stop shouting, and got his sight. Luke puts Bartimaeus in chapter 18 and Zacchaeus in chapter 19 — one man nobody would let speak, and one nobody would let near. See the note on Mark 10:46–52."}]}, "Luke 19:16–19": {"title": "\"Trustworthy with a very small thing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two servants report, and the arithmetic of the reward has nothing to do with the arithmetic of the return."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they made"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sir, **your one mina has earned ten more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sir, **your one mina has made five more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same starting capital — every servant got one mina. Different results."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose money it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of them say *your* mina. Neither claims it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is emphatic in the first: *your mina has earned* — the servant credits the money rather than himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the master says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Well done. You are a good servant.**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You proved trustworthy with **a very small thing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A mina was roughly three months' wages. Not nothing — and the master calls it *very small*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever scale he is working on, that sum is at the bottom of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **take charge of ten towns**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cities, in exchange for three months' wages well handled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second: *\"You take charge of five towns.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proportion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten minas earned → ten towns. Five earned → five towns. The reward tracks the return exactly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But the *scale* is absurd. Nothing about a small sum of money merits a province, and the master hands them out without comment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The money was a test, not a transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No comparison between the two servants. The one who made five is not asked why he did not make ten."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are rewarded on the same principle, and the master's pleasure is not divided between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has already recorded Jesus saying it plainly: *he who is faithful in what is least is faithful also in much… if you have not been faithful in the unrighteous mammon, who will commit to your trust the true riches?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Money, in that saying, is the small thing and the test. The *true riches* are something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The context"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable is told *because they were expecting the kingdom of God to appear at any moment*, and because He was near Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers an expectation of immediate arrival with a story about a long absence and what to do during it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one who did nothing.** A third servant hands back the same mina, wrapped in a cloth, and gives his reason: *I was afraid of you. You are a hard man.* See the note on Luke 19:20–23."}]}, "Luke 19:20–23": {"title": "The mina in a cloth", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third servant returns the money intact and gives his reason first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he hands back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sir, **here is your mina**. I kept it **wrapped up safe in a cloth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing lost. He is not a thief or a spendthrift. By his own accounting he has been careful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A mina was about three months' wages — the same amount given to each of the ten servants in this parable, unlike the talents in Matthew's version where the sums differ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because I was afraid of you. You are a hard man.** You collect what you never put down, and you harvest what you never planted.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason is stated before the excuse. He was afraid, and he was afraid because of what he thought the man was like."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *hard* is **austeros** — severe, exacting. It is the root of *austere*, and it was used of unripe fruit: sharp, not sweet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the master does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Out of your own mouth I will judge you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not accept the description or argue with it. He works inside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So you knew** I was a hard man…? Then **why did you not put my money on deposit**, so that when I came back I could at least have collected it with interest?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If that had really been his conviction, the bank was the obvious minimum. Fear that does nothing at all is not even consistent with itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was actually given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the instruction: *\"**Put this money to work** until I return.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a trading word — do business with this. It is an invitation to activity, handed out with the capital."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the master had not asked for ten minas back. The first servant produced ten, the second five, and both were commended without comparison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the others got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You proved trustworthy with **a very small thing**, so take charge of **ten towns**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months' wages, and cities in return. The reward is out of all proportion to the sum, which tells you the money was never the point. See the note on Luke 19:16–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other version"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew 25 tells a similar parable with talents and different amounts, and the third servant says nearly the same sentence: *I knew you to be a hard man… so I was afraid, and I went and hid your talent in the ground.* See the note on Matthew 25:24–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two parables, two servants, and the same self-justification in both — a description of the master, and fear following from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this parable is told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because He was **close to Jerusalem now**, and because they were expecting the kingdom of God **to appear at any moment**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the reason for the story. They thought it was about to happen; He tells them about a nobleman going away for a long time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the man believed, and what was true.** He said the master reaped where he had not sown. In fact the master had sown — he had put a mina in this man's hand himself. The one thing his description got wrong is the thing he was holding."}]}, "Luke 19:41–44": {"title": "\"You did not recognise the time\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is riding into Jerusalem to shouts of *Hosanna*, the crowds are spreading their cloaks on the road, and He is crying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When He came near enough to see the city, **He wept over it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **klaio** — to wail, to sob out loud. It is not the quiet word John uses at Lazarus's tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is at the top of the Mount of Olives, with the whole city and the temple spread out below in the sun, at the height of the only public acclamation He ever received."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody could tell Him anything about Himself from the size of a crowd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"On this day of all days\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you had only known, **even now, on this day of all days**, what would bring you peace.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a broken sentence. He does not finish it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**This day.** Nisan 10, the day the Passover lamb was taken into the house and set aside — four days before it was killed. Israel was choosing its lamb that morning, and the Lamb was coming down the hill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Hidden from your eyes\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But it is hidden from your eyes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The peace was there. The seeing was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He describes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your enemies will **throw up a wall of earth around you** and close you in and press you from every side… **they will not leave one stone standing on another**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman siege practice, described forty years before it happened. Titus built a wall of earth right round Jerusalem in AD 70 to stop anyone escaping, and Josephus, who was there, describes it — timber stripped from the countryside for miles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple came down and the stones were prised apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"— **because you did not recognise the time when God came to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the time of your visitation* — **episkope**, the moment of being looked in on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because of their sins in general. Because of a specific missed morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel had already said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After the sixty-two sevens, the Anointed One will be cut off… the people of the prince who is to come will destroy the city and the sanctuary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written in Babylon five hundred years earlier, in that order: the Messiah cut off first, the city destroyed after. See the note on Daniel 9:26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is weeping over a date that was already on paper."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does immediately after.** Rides down the hill, walks into the temple, and turns over the tables. The weeping and the whip are the same afternoon. See the note on Matthew 21:12–17."}]}, "Luke 2:25–28": {"title": "The old man who was told he would not die yet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four sentences, and the Holy Spirit is named in three of them — unusually dense even for Luke, who mentions the Spirit more than the other Gospel writers combined."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three movements"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the Spirit **was on him**", "it had been **shown to him** by the Spirit that he would not see death until he had seen the Messiah", "**led by the Spirit**, he came into the temple"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A standing presence, a specific word, and then a prompting on a particular morning. All three are needed for the scene to happen, and Luke lists them in order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as the parents carried the child Jesus in.* Simeon walks into the temple courts at the moment one poor family arrives with a baby to do what the law required — one of many families doing the same thing that week."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing marked them out. Luke gives no star, no crowd, no announcement. An old man crosses a courtyard and takes a child out of a stranger's arms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been promised, exactly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that he would be spared death. That he *would not see death until* — a deferral, not an exemption. The promise had an end built into it, and Simeon plainly knew it: the first thing he says after this is that his master can now let him go in peace."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was waiting for.** *The comfort of Israel* — the Greek is *paraklesis*, from the same root as the name Jesus gives the Spirit in John, the one called alongside. Simeon was waiting for the consolation of his people and was carrying it in his arms before he finished the sentence."}]}, "Luke 2:46–50": {"title": "\"My Father's business\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only recorded episode between infancy and the Jordan, and the first recorded words of Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they found Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the temple, sitting right among the teachers, listening to them and asking them questions.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what a student did — the rabbinic method was question and answer, and a learner sat among the teachers rather than in front of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is careful about the posture. He is not lecturing; He is sitting in the circle, listening first, then asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What astonished them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who heard Him was astonished at how much He understood and at the answers He gave.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are named: what He grasped, and what He said back. A twelve-year-old holding His end of a discussion with the temple teachers for three days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Mary's question and His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Child, why have You treated us like this? Your father and I have been searching everywhere for You, sick with worry.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why were you searching for Me? Did you not know that I had to be taken up with My Father's business?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She says *your father*. He answers *My Father*, and the contrast is unmistakable in the Greek. It is the first thing He is recorded as saying, and it distinguishes between the two."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase can be rendered *in My Father's house* or *about My Father's business* — the Greek is elliptical, and both are defensible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But they did not understand what He was telling them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it plainly about the two people who knew most about who He was — who had both had angelic visits. Neither the annunciation nor the shepherds nor Simeon had prepared them for this sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does next.** He goes home to Nazareth and lives under their care for eighteen more years. The boy who has just said He must be about His Father's business spends the next two decades in a carpenter's household. See the note on Luke 2:51–52."}]}, "Luke 2:51–52": {"title": "Eighteen years in one sentence", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the last thing the Gospels say about Jesus before He is thirty. Everything between the temple at twelve and the Jordan is covered by two verses, and what they choose to record is unexpected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went back down to Nazareth with them, and He lived under their care and did as they said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had just told Mary and Joseph He had to be about His Father's business. Then He goes home and spends eighteen years under theirs. Luke puts the two right next to each other without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Went on growing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is imperfect — continuous, over time, not a state He arrived at. And Luke lists four directions at once:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["in wisdom", "in stature", "in favour with God", "in favour with the people around Him"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is the one that stops people. Whatever it means, Luke was willing to write it: a real childhood, a real adolescence, real development. Not a divine adult in a small body waiting for the years to pass."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Mary does.** *She kept every one of these things treasured up in her heart* — Luke says something similar after the shepherds' visit. He seems to be telling his readers where parts of this account came from, and the answer is a woman who had been storing things up for decades."}]}, "Luke 2:6–7": {"title": "Wrapped, and laid in a feeding trough", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells the birth in two sentences and spends most of them on where the baby was put."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Wrapped Him around and around in strips of cloth\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ordinary practice — newborns were bound in bands of cloth, believed to help the limbs grow straight. Every mother in that world did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not ordinary is that Luke mentions it three times in this chapter. The angel gives it to the shepherds as the identifying sign: *you will find a baby wrapped in strips of cloth and lying in a feeding trough.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign by which the Messiah could be recognised was that He looked exactly like any other newborn, in a place no other newborn would be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trough"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phatne** — a manger, the box animals eat from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not describe a stable, and there is a good case that the setting was the lower room of a village house where animals were kept at night, since the word he uses for *inn* is the one he later uses for the upper room at the Last Supper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, the detail is the same: the first bed was a feeding trough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No room left for them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke states it without accusation. Nobody is blamed and no innkeeper appears in the text — that character comes from later tradition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethlehem was crowded because of the census. There was simply no space, and a young couple took what was available."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He is laid in.** A container that food is put in, in a town whose name means *house of bread*, by a mother who would later hear Him say *I am the bread of life.* Luke does not point at any of that. He just records where they put Him."}]}, "Luke 2:8–12": {"title": "Told to shepherds first", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The announcement of the birth goes to the people who were working the night shift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shepherds were not romantic figures. The work was dirty, the hours were antisocial, and their testimony was not accepted in a Jewish court — a shepherd could not have served as a witness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, who has just named Caesar Augustus and Quirinius governor of Syria in the opening verses, brings the news to men in a field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"For you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today, in the town of David, a Saviour has been born **for you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel does not say a Saviour has been born, and you should be pleased about it. The birth is announced as being on their account."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the titles stack: Saviour, Messiah, Lord — three of the heaviest words available, said to men holding sticks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is how you will know Him: you will find a baby wrapped in strips of cloth and lying in a feeding trough.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A strange sign. Every baby in Judea was wrapped in cloth; only one was in a manger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the identifying mark is ordinariness in an impossible place. Nothing about the sign suggests grandeur, and the shepherds are told to look for it in a town, not a palace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A great joy meant for everyone\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *all the people* — and Luke, who will end his Gospel with the good news going to all nations, starts widening it here in the second chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the glory did.** *The glory of the Lord blazed all around them, and they were terrified.* Then the first words: *do not be afraid.* Every angelic announcement in these two chapters begins the same way, because the reaction is always the same."}]}, "Luke 21:20–24": {"title": "The times of the Gentiles", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version of this discourse says something the other Gospels do not: armies, a siege, and a clock that starts running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you see **armies camped in a ring around Jerusalem**, then you will know that her ruin is close.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Mark have *the abomination of desolation standing where it ought not.* Luke, writing for a Gentile readership, gives the plain military fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let everyone in Judea **run for the hills**, and let those inside the city **get out of it**, and let those out in the countryside **not go in**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three directions, all outward. Nobody is told to defend the city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The historian Eusebius records that the Christians of Jerusalem left before the siege closed, and crossed the Jordan to Pella. They had been told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In AD 70 Titus surrounded Jerusalem, and the city and the temple were destroyed. Josephus, who was there, describes the famine inside the walls, and *it will be bitter in those days for a woman carrying a child* reads as understatement beside his account."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will fall by the sword, and they will be carried off as captives among all the nations.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scattering that followed lasted nineteen centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And Jerusalem will be **trampled under Gentile feet** until the times of the Gentiles **have run their course**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Until.** The trampling is given an end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The times of the Gentiles* is a defined period — Luke's phrase, and the only place it appears. The Greek for *run their course* is to be fulfilled, to be filled up, as a measure is filled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Daniel's statue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same period is what Nebuchadnezzar saw as a statue: gold, silver, bronze, iron, and feet of iron mixed with clay — a succession of Gentile empires running from the Babylon that burned the first temple onward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in Daniel's interpretation it ends the same way, with a stone *cut from the mountain but not by human hands*, which breaks all of it and stands forever. See the note on Daniel 2:44–45."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two passages share"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both put Jerusalem under Gentile rule for a stated stretch. Both put a term on it. Neither describes the arrangement as permanent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version of the same night ends with *you will not see Me again until you say, \"Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord.\"* The same word again. See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says immediately after.** Signs in the sun and moon and stars, and then *when these things begin to happen, look up and lift up your heads, for your redemption draws near.* The instruction in the siege was to run for the hills. The instruction at the end is to look up."}]}, "Luke 21:25–28": {"title": "The one instruction in the middle of the collapse", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A description of things falling apart, and then a command that runs against every instinct the description produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage describes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Signs in sun, moon and stars. Nations at their wits' end. The sea roaring. People fainting from fear and dread. *The powers that hold the heavens in place will be shaken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not soften any of it. The reaction he reports is collapse — people literally giving out from fear of what is coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When all of this begins to happen, stand up straight and lift up your heads.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two postures, and both are the opposite of what a frightened crowd does. The first word means to straighten up — used in this same Gospel of a bent woman being made straight. The second is lifting the head that fear drives down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the timing: *when all of this begins*. Not after it resolves. At the start, while it is still getting worse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because the day of your rescue is drawing near.* The word is **apolutrosis** — the ransom-release word, the same one in Ephesians 1:7 for being bought out of captivity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sign that terrifies everyone else is read by these people as the sound of the rescue arriving. Same events, opposite posture, because of a different expectation about what they mean."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"the powers of the heavens shaken.\"** This is the phrase people connect with modern weaponry, and the words behind it are the ordinary Greek for the starry host and for shaking — the same phrase Isaiah 34:4 already used centuries earlier of judgement on a nation. It is a stock prophetic image for the cosmos being disturbed, not a coded reference to anything later."}]}, "Luke 21:7–9": {"title": "\"Do not be frightened\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They ask for a timetable and He answers with a warning about people who will offer one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**When will all of this happen? What will the sign be** that it is about to take place?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just been admiring the temple stonework, and He had said not one stone would be left on another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two questions: when, and what is the sign."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be careful that no one leads you astray.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before any content. The first thing He says about the end is a warning about information regarding the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the deceivers say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Many will come using My name and saying, '**I am the one**,' and, '**The time has arrived**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims: an identity, and a date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not follow them.**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wars"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you hear of **wars and uprisings, do not be frightened**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *do not be terrified*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say they will not happen. He says they will, and tells them what to do with the news."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Such things **have to happen first**, but **the end will not come right away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once: these are necessary, and they are not the signal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence that undoes date-setting from both directions. The turbulence is expected, and it does not mean the timetable has moved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says a few verses later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There will be great earthquakes in various places, and famines and **pestilences**; and there will be fearful sights and great signs from heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **loimos** — plague, pestilence. Named alongside earthquakes and famines, as one of the things that are not the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He tells them to do about all of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By your patient endurance you will gain your souls.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And, at the end of the chapter: *when these things begin to happen, **look up and lift up your heads**, because your redemption draws near.* See the note on Luke 21:25–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two instructions in one chapter: do not be terrified, and look up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He also says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Settle it in your hearts **not to meditate beforehand on what you will answer**; for I will give you a mouth and wisdom.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them not to rehearse."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing He rules out entirely.** *\"It is not for you to know times or seasons which the Father has put in His own authority.\"* Said to the same men, in Acts 1, when they asked the question again — after the resurrection, and He still did not answer it."}]}, "Luke 22:19–20": {"title": "\"Do this to remember Me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs on the bread, and then a sentence about the cup that names a covenant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four things He does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **picked up** the bread, and **gave thanks** over it, and **broke** it, and **handed it** to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Took, blessed, broke, gave. Luke uses the same four in the feeding of the five thousand and again at Emmaus, where the two disciples recognise Him at the exact moment He does them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thanks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eucharisteo** — where *Eucharist* comes from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives thanks holding bread He has just said is His body, on the night He is betrayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Given for you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is My body,\" He said, \"**given for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not taken from Him. **Given.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same about His life in John: *no one takes it from Me. I lay it down of My own accord.* See the note on John 10:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Do this to remember Me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *remembrance* is **anamnesis**. Luke and Paul both record the instruction; Matthew and Mark do not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only ongoing practice He tells them to keep by saying *do this*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing of the cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in the same way, **after the supper**, He took the cup.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke marks it. The bread was in the meal; the cup came after it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a Passover seder four cups are drunk. The one after the meal is the third — the cup of redemption."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This cup is **the new covenant in My blood**,\" He said, \"**poured out for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase *new covenant* comes from Jeremiah 31, written six centuries earlier, where it ends: *I will forgive their iniquity, and **their sin I will remember no more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Hebrews 8:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two rememberings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *remember Me*. The covenant He is inaugurating says *I will remember their sins no more*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, running in opposite directions, in the same cup."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The old covenant's ratification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses took blood, threw it on the people and said *this is the blood of the covenant which the LORD has made with you*. Exodus 24, on the mountain, with the elders eating and drinking in God's presence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the same form of words, at a table, with the blood not yet shed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who was still at the table.** Luke puts the next sentence like this: *but behold, the hand of My betrayer is with Me on the table.* The order in Luke's account has Judas present for the bread and the cup."}]}, "Luke 22:31–34": {"title": "\"But I have prayed for you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four sentences at the supper table, and the middle one is the reason Peter has a rest of his life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Simon, Simon**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doubling a name in Scripture is how the serious things start — *Abraham, Abraham* at the knife; *Moses, Moses* at the bush; *Saul, Saul* on the Damascus road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He uses the old name. Not Peter, the rock, which He gave him. Simon, which he was born with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Satan has asked to have all of you**, to sift you like wheat.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exaiteomai** — to demand, to ask for the surrender of something, as a prosecutor demands a prisoner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked. Somebody had to answer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Job's courtroom again: *Have you considered My servant Job?* See the note on Job 1:6–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All of you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek pronoun in *have all of you* is plural. The whole table is asked for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next sentence goes singular."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sifting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To **sift you like wheat**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sieve is shaken hard. Everything in it is thrown about, and what falls through is thrown away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is violent, and it is also agricultural: sifting is what you do to keep the grain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But I have prayed for you, Simon**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Singular now. Satan asked for all eleven; Jesus names one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *prayed* is **deomai** — to beg, to make petition out of need."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the tense is aorist: it is already done, before the courtyard, before the fire, before the girl asks the question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He prayed for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That **your faith would not give out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that he would not fall. He does fall, three times, with an oath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer is for the thing underneath — that the collapse would not go all the way down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other side of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **when you have turned back**, strengthen your brothers.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**When**, not if. He is given his assignment after the failure while the failure is still in the future."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *strengthen* is **sterizo** — to fix firmly, to set in place. Peter uses it in his own letter about God doing it to them. See the note on 1 Peter 5:10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **I am ready to go with You anywhere — to prison, to death**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He means it. He draws a sword in the garden a few hours later and takes a swing at an armed party."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I tell you, Peter, **the rooster will not crow today before you have said three times that you do not know Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the name changes back — **Peter**, the rock, in the sentence predicting the denial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rooster crowed, *and the Lord turned and looked at Peter*, and he went out and wept bitterly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then a beach, a charcoal fire, and three questions. And at Pentecost the man standing up to preach to Jerusalem is Simon Peter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was decided before the denial.** The prayer, the outcome and the assignment. All three are stated at the table, while he is still insisting it will not happen."}]}, "Luke 22:39–46": {"title": "\"Not what I want. What You want.\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke was a physician, and his account of Gethsemane includes a detail no other Gospel records."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As He always did\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He went out, **as He always did**, to the Mount of Olives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes the habit. It is also how Judas knew where to find Him — John says *Judas also knew the place, for Jesus often met there with His disciples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes to the place His betrayer can find Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Pray that you do not walk into the testing.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said at the start and again at the end of the passage, bracketing the whole scene."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peirasmos** — testing, trial, temptation. It is the same word in the Lord's Prayer: *do not lead us into temptation.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "A stone's throw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **drew away from them, about as far as a stone could be thrown**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke measures it. Close enough to be seen; far enough to be alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Father, **if You are willing, take this cup away from Me. And yet — not what I want. What You want.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are real. He asks for it to be removed, and He submits."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request is not withdrawn — He asks, and then places the asking under something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image runs through the prophets — the cup of God's wrath, passed round and drunk to the dregs. Psalm 75: *there is a cup in the hand of the LORD… He pours it out, and the wicked of the earth drink it dry.* See the note on Psalms 75:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is looking at that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The angel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **an angel from heaven appeared to Him, strengthening Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Luke records this. The cup is not taken away and help is sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to strengthen, to put strength into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke records next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **in His anguish He prayed more earnestly still**, and **His sweat became like great drops of blood** falling to the ground.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *anguish* is **agonia** — the origin of the English word, and it carried the sense of a contest, a struggle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the sweat. The condition is called **hematidrosis** — under extreme stress the small vessels around the sweat glands can rupture, and blood mixes with sweat. It is rare, documented, and associated with acute terror or grief."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, of the four writers, is the one who mentions it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note also what the strengthening produced: *he prayed **more earnestly**.* The angel did not settle Him into calm. He went back in harder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the drops fall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Falling **to the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 3: *cursed is the ground because of you… in toil you will eat of it all the days of your life. It will bring you thorns and thistles. By the sweat of your brow you will eat your bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sweat and cursed ground, and a man in a garden. See the note on Genesis 3:17–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Found them **asleep, worn out with grief**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke alone supplies the reason. Matthew and Mark just say they were sleeping; Luke says why, and it is not indifference."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was in a garden the first time.** A man, a tree, and a choice, and the sentence *not what You want, but what I want.* This one is a garden, a man on his knees, and the sentence reversed."}]}, "Luke 22:54–62": {"title": "\"The Lord turned and looked straight at Peter\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Only Luke records the look, and it is the hinge of the scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter followed a long way behind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's phrasing is exact and it is not only geography. He is close enough to be in the courtyard and far enough to deny knowing Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three denials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They escalate, and they get shorter:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Woman, I do not know Him*", "*Man, I am not*", "*Man, I have no idea what you are talking about*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "An hour passes between the second and third. He had time to leave, and stayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And while the words were still in his mouth, the rooster crowed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the two in the same instant. No gap for reflection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The look"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Lord turned and looked straight at Peter.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus was inside the high priest's house being interrogated. For this to happen He was being moved through the courtyard, or was visible across it — bound, already struck, in the middle of His own trial."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb Luke uses is for a direct, deliberate look at someone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said. No word is recorded, and no rebuke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the look did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Peter remembered what the Lord had said to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The remembering is what breaks him — not the servant girl, not the rooster. Christ had told him this would happen, and had said in the same breath: *I have prayed for you, that your faith may not fail; and when you have turned back, strengthen your brothers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told the failure and the recovery in one sentence, before either happened."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it ends.** *He went outside and wept bitterly.* Luke leaves him there and does not resolve it in this chapter. The resolution is on a beach in John 21, over a charcoal fire — the only other charcoal fire in the Gospels is the one in this courtyard."}]}, "Luke 22:7–13": {"title": "The room already set out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men are sent to prepare a meal and find that most of it is done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The day the **Passover lamb had to be killed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nisan 14. Thousands of lambs were slaughtered in the temple that afternoon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes the date in a chapter that will end with a trial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is sent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus sent **Peter and John** ahead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two who will run to the tomb together on Sunday morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Where do You want us to prepare it?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A reasonable question in a city packed with pilgrims. Rooms in Jerusalem at Passover were impossible to find, and by custom nobody charged for them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As you go into the city, **a man carrying a jar of water** will meet you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Carrying water was women's work. A man with a water jar would stand out in a crowded street."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a marker chosen so that two men could pick him out without asking anybody anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The password"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Say to the owner of the house, '**The Teacher asks: where is the guest room** where I may eat the Passover with My disciples?'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No name is given. *The Teacher* is enough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever arrangement had been made, it had been made without the disciples' knowledge, and Judas is not in the room when the location is given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they find"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will take you upstairs and show you **a large room, already set out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *furnished* — spread with couches, laid out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were sent to prepare, and the preparing had been done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Get everything ready there* — the lamb, the bread, the wine. The room was not theirs to arrange."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The two of them went, and **found it just as He had told them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it flatly. He does the same with the colt on Palm Sunday — sent, described, found exactly so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in that room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Passover, the bread and the cup, the argument about who is greatest, the warning to Peter, the washing of feet in John's account, and the four chapters of the upper room discourse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have longed to eat this Passover with you before I suffer.\"* See the note on Luke 22:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other room in Luke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was no room for them in the inn.* Chapter 2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel opens with no space available and closes its last week in a large upper room, furnished and ready."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the man with the jar never knew.** He is not named, the householder is not named, and neither appears again. Somebody in Jerusalem gave up the best room in his house that week and is not recorded."}]}, "Luke 23:32–38": {"title": "\"Father, forgive them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke records the first sentence spoken from the cross, and it is a prayer for the people holding the hammer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The company"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Two others were led out with Him as well, **both of them criminals**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's word is **kakourgoi** — evildoers. Matthew and Mark call them robbers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One on His right and one on His left* — the position James and John had asked for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke does not describe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They crucified Him there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words. No description of the nails, the raising, or the pain. All four Gospels do this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus said, \"**Father, forgive them; they do not know what they are doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is imperfect — He **kept saying** it. Not once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who \"them\" is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not say. The soldiers driving the nails are nearest. The rulers who arranged it are standing there watching. The crowd is there too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He leaves it open, and the reason He gives applies to all of them: **they do not know**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The category"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ignorance is exactly the condition the cities of refuge were built for — a death caused *without meaning to*. See the notes on Numbers 35:9–15 and Numbers 35:16–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says it to Jerusalem a few weeks later: *\"I know that you did it **in ignorance**, as did also your rulers.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *\"had they known, they would not have crucified the Lord of glory.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clothes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they **divided His clothes among themselves, throwing lots** for them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 22: *they divide My garments among them, and for My clothing they cast lots.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written a thousand years earlier by a man describing an execution method that did not exist yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who watched"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people **stood there watching**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke separates the crowd from the mockers. The people watch; the rulers sneer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three mockeries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each has the same shape — *save Yourself*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*The **rulers** sneered: \"He saved other people. Let Him save Himself, **if He is the Messiah of God, the chosen One**.\"*", "*The **soldiers** mocked Him too… \"**If You are the king of the Jews**, save Yourself.\"*", "And one of the criminals, in the next verse: *\"**If You are the Christ**, save Yourself and us.\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times *if*, and it is the same word the wilderness temptation opened with: *if You are the Son of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rulers admitted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He saved other people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They concede it in the middle of the taunt. Nobody in the account disputes the works."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The notice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A notice fixed above Him, written in **Greek and Latin and Hebrew**: This is the King of the Jews.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three languages of the empire's east, its law, and its religion. Pilate wrote it, and when the chief priests objected he said *what I have written, I have written.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second criminal turns on the first, says *we receive the due reward of our deeds, but this Man has done nothing wrong*, and asks to be remembered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Assuredly, I say to you, today you will be with Me in Paradise.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of it.** He prays for their forgiveness before anyone asks, before anyone is sorry, and while the work is still being done to Him."}]}, "Luke 24:13–16": {"title": "A stranger on the road", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men walking away from Jerusalem on the day of the resurrection, and the man they are talking about falls into step beside them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A village called **Emmaus**, sixty stadia out from Jerusalem — a walk of about seven miles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means warm springs, warm baths. There were hot springs in that country and a village took its name from them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are walking away from the city, and they are on the road on the same day the tomb was found empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were talking the whole way about everything that had happened… **talking it over and turning it around** between them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the second is to debate, to dispute — they are going over it and getting nowhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have all the facts. Luke's next paragraph shows they know about the crucifixion, the third day, the women at the tomb, the angels, and the men who went and found it empty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Facts, and no sight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Their eyes were held\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus Himself came up and walked along with them. But **their eyes were held**, and they did not recognise Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is passive — something was holding them. Luke does not say what."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is physically present, close enough to walk seven miles with them, and it does nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what you would expect. He does not identify Himself, and He does not show them the marks — that comes later, in the room, with the others."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And beginning at Moses and all the Prophets, **He expounded to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives them a Bible study. Seven miles of it, from Genesis forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which He thought was better"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had the option of simply appearing. Instead He walked the whole way concealed and opened the Scriptures — and their own verdict afterwards is about the road, not the moment of recognition:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Were our hearts not burning inside us **while He talked with us on the road, while He was opening the Scriptures to us**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burning happened before they knew who He was. It happened while the Book was being opened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in that sermon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses and all the Prophets. Whatever else was in it, Genesis was, and Jacob's blessings over the twelve sons are in Genesis."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He calls them.** *Foolish ones, and slow of heart to believe in all that the prophets have spoken.* Not slow to believe the women, or the empty tomb. Slow to believe what was already written down."}]}, "Luke 24:17–24": {"title": "\"But we had hoped\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men explain the resurrection to the risen Christ and get every fact right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is this you are discussing** with each other as you walk along?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks. He knows, and He asks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they stopped, **their faces heavy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes the stopping. The question halts them in the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Are You **the only visitor in Jerusalem** who does not know what has happened there this week?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are addressing the one person in Jerusalem who knows more about it than anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"What things?\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and He lets them tell Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Run through their account. It is accurate on every point:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Jesus of Nazareth, *a prophet, powerful in what He did and what He said*", "handed over by the chief priests and rulers", "crucified", "*this is now **the third day** since it all happened*", "the women went early, found no body, and *came back saying they had seen a vision of angels who told them **He is alive***", "some of the men went and *found it exactly as the women had said*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have the timeline, the third day, the empty tomb, the angelic message, and independent corroboration."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But we had hoped** that He was the one who was going to redeem Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense. The saddest three words in the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hope is spoken of as something finished — and the man it was about is walking next to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"But they did not see Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their last line, and it is the complaint. They had the report and not the sight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not their information. Their conclusion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Foolish ones, and slow of heart to believe in all that the prophets have spoken.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is His response, and note what He faults. Not slow to believe the women, or the empty tomb, or the angels. Slow to believe **what was already written down**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beginning at Moses and all the Prophets, He **expounded to them in all the Scriptures** the things concerning Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven miles of it, still unrecognised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And their own verdict afterwards is about the road, not the moment of recognition: *were our hearts not burning inside us while He talked with us on the road, while He was **opening the Scriptures** to us?* See the notes on Luke 24:13–16 and Luke 24:25–27."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"redeem Israel\" meant to them.** A throne, an army, and the Romans out. He had done something larger and they could not see it, standing on the third day with all the evidence in hand."}]}, "Luke 24:25–27": {"title": "The best Bible study never written down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two people walking to Emmaus get a seven-mile exposition of the Old Testament from its subject, and Luke does not record a word of the content."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He rebukes them for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How slow you are, and how heavy of heart, when it comes to believing everything the prophets said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for failing to recognise Him — Luke says their eyes were kept from doing that. For not believing what was already written."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The material to work it out had been available the whole time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"had to\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Did the Messiah not have to suffer all of this and then come into His glory?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek carries necessity — it was *necessary*. Luke uses this word repeatedly of Jesus' path."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the order is fixed: suffering, then glory. That is the sequence the two on the road could not make fit, because a defeated Messiah made no sense to them. Isaiah 52 has the same two elements in the same relationship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Beginning with Moses\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And beginning with Moses and going on through all the Prophets, He opened up for them everything in the Scriptures that was about Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole Hebrew Bible, in order, with Himself as the thread. Later in the same chapter He names the third division too — the Psalms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every reader since has wanted the transcript. Luke gives the method and not the notes, which may be deliberate: the exercise is left for us to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they recognised Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not during the exposition. *When He was at the table with them, He took bread, gave thanks, broke it and began to give it to them. Then their eyes were opened.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Scriptures burned in them on the road. The recognition came at the breaking of bread."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they said afterwards.** *Were not our hearts burning within us while He talked with us on the road and opened the Scriptures to us?* They knew something had happened before they knew who had done it."}]}, "Luke 24:28–32": {"title": "Known in the breaking of bread", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven miles of Scripture, a meal, and then He is gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He made as if to go further on\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not invite Himself in. He keeps walking, and they have to stop Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stay with us.** The day is nearly over.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *urged* is strong — they constrained Him, pressed Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who becomes the host"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They invited Him into their house, and then:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When He had sat down at the table with them, **He took the bread** and gave thanks over it, and broke it, and handed it to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The guest takes the bread. That is the host's part, and He takes it without asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Took… gave thanks… broke… handed it to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same four, in the same order, as the upper room. The same four as the five thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What opened their eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **their eyes were opened and they knew Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same passive as verse 16 — *their eyes were held* — now reversed. Something had been holding them; something releases them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is at the breaking of the bread that it happens. Not during the seven miles of exposition, and not when He first appeared on the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And He was gone from their sight\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instant they recognise Him He vanishes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not stay for the meal He has just begun. They get one look, and then they are alone with an opened Bible and a broken loaf."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say to each other"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Were our hearts not burning inside us** while He talked with us on the road, while He was opening the Scriptures to us?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence, and note what they point at. Not the moment of recognition. The road. The Scriptures being opened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burning had already happened, before they knew who He was, while a stranger was walking them through Moses and the Prophets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They rose up that very hour and returned to Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven miles back, in the dark, immediately, after telling Him it was too late in the day to travel."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they tell the eleven.** *How He was known to them in the breaking of bread.* Of everything on that road — the exposition, the walk, the vanishing — that is the phrase they use for it."}]}, "Luke 24:44–49": {"title": "\"He opened their minds\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last scene of Luke's Gospel, and He gives them a reading lesson."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is what **I was telling you while I was still with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not new information. He had said it repeatedly — three times in Luke that the Son of Man must suffer, and each time Luke notes they did not understand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three divisions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Everything written about Me in **the law of Moses and in the Prophets and in the Psalms** had to be fulfilled.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard Jewish division of Scripture: Torah, Nevi'im, Ketuvim — the Psalms standing for the writings, as the largest book in them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is claiming all three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **He opened their minds to understand the Scriptures**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dienoixen** — to open thoroughly, to open up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses the same verb three times in this chapter: on the Emmaus road *He **opened** to us the Scriptures*; at the table *their eyes were **opened***; and here, the mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem was not that the texts were unclear. Something had to be opened at the reader's end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What stands written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That the Messiah would **suffer** and **rise from the dead on the third day**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And that **a change of mind and the forgiveness of sins would be proclaimed in His name** to all the nations, **beginning at Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three items, and the third is the mission. He includes the preaching in what the Old Testament said had to happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Beginning at Jerusalem\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city that had just executed Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts follows the order exactly: *in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are witnesses of all of this**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **martures** — the root of *martyr*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A witness reports what he saw. It is the only qualification named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **I am sending what My Father promised out upon you**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stay here in the city of Jerusalem until you are clothed with power from on high**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *clothed* is **endusesthe** — to be dressed in, to put on like a garment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Until.** They are told to do nothing until it arrives — and they do exactly that: eleven men and a hundred and twenty people in an upper room for ten days. See the note on Acts 1:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had been given and not given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Opened minds, an explanation of the whole Old Testament, the commission, and the title of witness — and they are still told to sit still."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verses of the Gospel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He led them out as far as Bethany, and He lifted up His hands and blessed them. Now it came to pass, **while He blessed them, He was parted from them** and carried up into heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing they saw was His hands up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they returned to Jerusalem **with great joy**, and were continually in the temple praising and blessing God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the Gospel opened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "With a priest, Zacharias, unable to give the blessing when he came out of the temple because he had been struck dumb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke ends with a blessing that is completed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was opened.** Not the book. Luke says He opened *their minds to understand the Scriptures* — the same scrolls they had been reading in synagogue their whole lives."}]}, "Luke 4:16–21": {"title": "Where He stopped reading", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus opens His public ministry by reading a passage aloud and stopping in the middle of a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He read"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 61, and the list is specific:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["good news to the poor", "healing for the brokenhearted", "release for the prisoners", "sight for the blind", "freedom for everyone being crushed", "**the year of the Lord's favour**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the job description He chose for Himself, out of the whole scroll, on the first day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's sentence does not end at *the year of the Lord's favour*. It continues: *and the day of vengeance of our God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus reads to the comma, rolls up the scroll, hands it back and sits down. The day of vengeance is not cancelled — it simply is not what He had come to announce that morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today, in your hearing, this Scripture has come true.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The year of favour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase points at the Jubilee — the fiftieth year in Leviticus 25 when debts were released, slaves went free and land returned to its family. As far as the record shows, Israel never once kept it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus announces it as arriving in Him, on that Sabbath, in that room."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the anointing was for.** *The Spirit of the Lord is on Me, because He has anointed Me* — and every item in the list that follows is something done for somebody else: the poor, the brokenhearted, the prisoners, the blind, the crushed. He states the anointing and then spends the rest of the Gospel spending it."}]}, "Luke 4:25–27": {"title": "A widow and a leper", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two examples from the same room in Nazareth, and they are what gets Him thrown out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The synagogue in Nazareth, immediately after He read Isaiah 61, closed the scroll and said *today this Scripture is fulfilled in your hearing.* See the note on Isaiah 61:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says the reaction to that was favourable: *all bore witness to Him and marvelled at the gracious words.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then He says this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There were **plenty of widows in Israel** in the days of Elijah, when the sky was shut up for three and a half years.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's figure of three and a half years matches James's. 1 Kings says *in the third year*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the prophet went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But Elijah was **not sent to a single one of them** — he was sent to **a widow in Zarephath, in the region of Sidon**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sidon is Phoenicia — Gentile territory, and specifically Jezebel's country. Her father was king of the Sidonians."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet who spent his life opposing Jezebel was fed for three years by a widow from her homeland. See the note on 1 Kings 17:8–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And there were **plenty of people with leprosy in Israel** in the days of the prophet Elisha, and **not one of them was made clean** — only **Naaman, the Syrian**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naaman was commander of the army of Aram, the nation Israel was at war with. He had been raiding Israelite territory; the girl who told him about the prophet was a slave taken in one of those raids."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was healed and Israel's own lepers were not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What both examples have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An Israelite prophet, a national crisis, a country full of people with the need — and the one person helped is a foreigner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both stories are in Israel's own Scriptures. Nothing He says is new information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it enraged them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So all those in the synagogue, when they heard these things, **were filled with wrath**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just asked *is this not Joseph's son?* and were expecting the local boy to do at Capernaum's miracles here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers by naming the two occasions when the prophets were sent past Israel altogether."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And rose up and thrust Him out of the city; and they led Him to the brow of the hill on which their city was built, **that they might throw Him down over the cliff**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **passing through the midst of them, He went His way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives no explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said before the examples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Assuredly, I say to you, **no prophet is accepted in his own country**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same theme goes in Luke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel is full of it — the good Samaritan, the ten lepers where only the Samaritan returns, the centurion whose faith is greater than any in Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Acts, by the same author, is the story of the message going out to exactly the people these two examples name."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is quoted at them.** Their own history. He does not tell them anything they had not read — he points at the two occasions in it when the help went elsewhere."}]}, "Luke 4:28–30": {"title": "Rage in the synagogue", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The town He grew up in tries to kill Him, minutes after admiring Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had just said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier: *all bore witness to Him and **marvelled at the gracious words** which proceeded out of His mouth. And they said, \"Is this not Joseph's son?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had read Isaiah 61 — good news to the poor, healing for the brokenhearted, liberty to the captives, sight to the blind — and sat down and said *today this Scripture is fulfilled in your hearing.* See the note on Luke 4:16–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were pleased."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What turned them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He brought up two Gentiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There were many widows in Israel in the days of Elijah… but to none of them was Elijah sent except to Zarephath, in the region of Sidon, to a woman who was a widow. And many lepers were in Israel in the time of Elisha the prophet, and none of them was cleansed except Naaman the Syrian.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two famines of blessing inside Israel, and both times the prophet went outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence that empties the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone in the synagogue heard that, and they were **filled with rage**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thumos** — a boiling over, fury."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From *marvelled at His gracious words* to this, in the space of two sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they tried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **drove Him out of the town**, and brought Him to the edge of the hill the town was built on, **meaning to throw Him off it headlong**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nazareth sits on a ridge. This is an attempted execution by mob, on the Sabbath, by His own neighbours — the people who had watched Him grow up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **He walked right through the middle of them** and went on His way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No struggle is described, no miracle announced, no rebuke. He walks through the crowd holding Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John records the same thing in the temple after *before Abraham was, I AM* — they picked up stones, *but Jesus hid Himself and went out, passing right through the middle of them.* See the note on John 8:57–59."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody laid a hand on Him until the night He let them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Nazareth got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's account of the same visit: *He could do no mighty work there, except that He laid His hands on a few sick people and healed them. And He marvelled because of their unbelief.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had opened the scroll and read out what He came to do, and offered it to them first. They took Him to the cliff instead, and He went elsewhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer was withdrawn from the town, not from the world."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two marvellings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says Jesus *marvelled* at Nazareth's unbelief. Matthew says He *marvelled* at a Roman centurion's faith, and said *I have not found faith like this anywhere in Israel.* See the note on Matthew 8:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only two things recorded as astonishing Him, and they are on opposite sides of the same question."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He had said just before the two examples.** *No prophet is accepted in his own country.* He knew what the room was going to do, and read the passage anyway."}]}, "Luke 5:1–3": {"title": "Two boats at the shore", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He needs a pulpit and there are two available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The crowd kept pressing in around Him, **hungry to hear the word of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a strong word — pressing, crushing. He is being backed toward the water by people who want to hear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two boats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He noticed **two boats** pulled up at the water's edge, **empty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both were there. Both were free. Neither owner was in them — the fishermen had climbed out and were washing their nets after a night that produced nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one He got into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He stepped into **the one that belonged to Simon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke names the boat by its owner. Of the two available, He picks one, and it is the boat of a man who had worked all night and caught nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon is not asked whether he minds. He is asked to *push out a little way from the land* — a small thing, an inconvenience at the end of a bad shift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Simon gave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The loan of a boat for an hour, and the trouble of pushing it out and holding it steady while somebody else talked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole of his contribution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he got back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next paragraph: *take her out into the deep water* — and the nets tear, and both boats fill until they are settling low in the water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other boat, still empty on the shore, ends up loaded too. Simon's partners are waved over and they share it. See the note on Luke 5:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He handed over an empty boat and it came back sinking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The teaching happens first. He sits down in the boat and *taught the crowds from the water* — and only after He has finished speaking does He say anything about fish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon heard the whole sermon from a few feet away, sitting in his own boat, before he was asked to let the nets down."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He was doing with a fishing boat.** Using the tool of a man's trade, on the water he had worked all night, to preach. And then filling it. Simon's business was not set aside for the ministry — it was pushed out a little way from the land and taught out of."}]}, "Luke 5:17–20": {"title": "\"When Jesus saw their faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four men dig through a roof, and the sentence that follows is one of the most remarkable in the Gospels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pharisees and teachers of the law were sitting there, men who had come in from every village of Galilee and from Judea and from Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is describing an inspection. Religious authorities have travelled from three regions to watch this man, and they are sitting down — the posture of people who intend to stay."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the same sentence: *the power of the Lord was present with Him to heal.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The roof"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Houses had flat roofs of beams, brushwood and packed mud, reached by an outside stair. Luke, writing for a Greek audience, says *tiles* — describing it in terms his readers would picture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, four men take somebody's roof apart with a room full of dignitaries underneath, and lower a man on a mat into the gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Their faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when Jesus saw **their** faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plural. Luke does not say the paralysed man believed anything. Whatever he thought, the faith Jesus responds to is the faith of the men on the roof."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth sitting with. Somebody else's conviction got him in front of Jesus, and it is credited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Friend, your sins are forgiven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what anybody in the room came for. They had dug through a ceiling for legs, and He addresses something nobody mentioned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is literally *man*, but warm — the Illumination's *friend* catches the tone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows.** The scribes reason that only God can forgive sins, and Jesus agrees with the premise: *so that you may know the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins* — He said to the paralysed man, get up. The healing is offered as evidence for the forgiveness, which tells you which of the two He considered the greater work."}]}, "Luke 5:4–7": {"title": "Out into the deep", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A carpenter tells a professional fisherman where the fish are, at the wrong time of day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Everything about it is wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fishing on that lake was night work, with nets, in the shallows. Jesus says daytime, deep water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Simon has just spent the entire night proving there was nothing there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Simon says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Master, **we worked hard all night long and came up with nothing at all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states his case first, and it is a good one. Experience, effort, and the evidence of the last twelve hours all agree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But because You say so**, I will let the nets down.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *at Your word*. That is the entire reason given, and it is set against everything he knows about his own trade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Net, singular"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon says he will let down *the net* — one. Jesus had said *let your **nets** down*, plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke keeps the difference. And the next sentence is *the **nets** began to tear apart in their hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He went out with less than he was told to take."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Such an **enormous shoal** of fish that the nets began to tear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same water. The same lake he had swept all night. The fish were not brought from somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both boats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They waved to their partners in the other boat to come and help them, and they came, and **the two boats were loaded so full** that they began to settle low in the water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other boat — the one He did not get into — is filled as well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The catch outruns the vessel that was lent. James and John, who had nothing to do with the loan, go home with a full boat too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The loan of an empty boat and one more cast of a net he had already washed and put away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon gave the small thing he had at the end of a wasted night, and the return nearly sank two boats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Depart from me, for I am a sinful man, O Lord.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not thank you. The size of the catch tells him who is in the boat with him, and his first instinct is to ask Him to leave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be afraid. From now on you will catch men.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they did with the haul.** *They brought their boats to land, left everything, and followed Him.* The biggest catch of Simon's life is sitting on the shore, and he walks away from it. It was never the point; it was the proof."}]}, "Luke 6:37–38": {"title": "\"Poured right into the fold of your coat\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous giving verse has three sentences in front of it, and they are all about something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not sit in judgment** on people, and you will not be judged. **Do not hand down sentences**, and no sentence will be handed down on you. **Forgive**, and you will be forgiven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses, and only then: *give, and it will be given back to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage is a run of four, and the first three are about how you treat people. Giving is the fourth item on a list about generosity of judgement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A full measure, pressed down, shaken together, spilling over the top**, poured right into the fold of your coat.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four stages, and they describe a grain merchant filling a container properly:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**a full measure** — filled to the brim", "**pressed down** — compacted so the air is out", "**shaken together** — settled so it packs tighter", "**spilling over the top** — and then still more, until it runs over"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "An honest dealer did the first. A generous one did all four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Into **the fold of your coat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The outer garment was belted, and the fabric above the belt formed a deep pouch — the way a man carried grain home when he had no sack."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a picture of somebody being given more than they came prepared to receive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whatever measure you use for others is the measure that will be used for you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence the whole passage runs on, and it applies to all four items — judgement, condemnation, forgiveness and giving alike."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The measure is not set by God's generosity, in this saying. It is set by yours, and then matched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who \"they\" are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It will be given back to you\"* — the Greek is *they will give*, an impersonal plural. Luke does not name the giver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same construction as *they will pour into your lap*. Something comes back, and the source is left unstated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in the sermon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Luke's Sermon on the Plain, and the paragraph before it is *love your enemies, do good, and lend, hoping for nothing in return… for He is kind to the unthankful and evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be merciful, just as your Father also is merciful.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the model given, and then these four instructions."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage is not.** A transaction. The three clauses in front of it — judging, condemning, forgiving — cannot be done for a return without becoming something else entirely, and the giving is fourth in the same list."}]}, "Luke 6:43–45": {"title": "A tree and its fruit", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three sentences about produce, and the last one moves from orchards to speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A good tree does not produce bad fruit**, and **a bad tree does not produce good fruit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *bad* is **sapron** — rotten, worthless, decayed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated both ways, so there is no exception in either direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Every tree is known by what it grows**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *each tree is known by its own fruit*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fruit is not what makes a tree good. It is what makes it identifiable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The examples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Nobody picks **figs off a thornbush**, and nobody gathers **grapes from a briar patch**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version has grapes from thornbushes and figs from thistles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are absurd on purpose. Nobody in the audience has ever tried it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The application"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"A **good person brings good things up out of the good stored away in the heart**, and an **evil person brings evil up out of the evil stored there**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thesaurou** — a treasury, a storehouse. The root of *thesaurus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something is laid up, and what comes out is drawn from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **what the mouth says is whatever the heart is full of**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks* — **ek tou perisseumatos**, out of the overflow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what the heart contains. What it has too much of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same saying in Matthew is aimed at the Pharisees, and he adds: *for **every idle word** men may speak, they will give account of it in the day of judgment. For by your words you will be justified, and by your words you will be condemned.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it in Luke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole Sermon on the Plain: love your enemies, judge not, give and it will be given to you, and the blind leading the blind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And immediately before this, the beam and the speck: *first remove the plank from your own eye*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tree saying follows a passage about seeing clearly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two houses — one on rock and one on earth without a foundation — and the flood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why do you call Me 'Lord, Lord,' and **not do the things which I say**?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the saying is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Diagnosis. Neither Gospel uses it to tell people to produce better fruit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thornbush cannot be improved into a fig tree, and the sayings are about identification rather than effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the tree image goes elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is like **a tree planted by the water**… **it never stops bearing fruit**.* See the note on Jeremiah 17:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tree there is *transplanted* — set out deliberately by somebody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the fruit of the Spirit is singular in Paul, and grows rather than being assembled. See the note on Galatians 5:22–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the last sentence measures.** Not what is in the heart, but what there is too much of. The Greek is *out of the overflow* — the mouth reports the surplus."}]}, "Luke 7:11–17": {"title": "The widow of Nain", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two crowds meet at a town gate, one coming in and one carrying a body out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Nain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means pleasant. It sits in the Jezreel valley, in the territory of Issachar — the tribe whose blessing was about seeing that *the resting place was good and the land was pleasant*, and taking up a load anyway. See the note on Genesis 49:14–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **only son** his mother had, and she was **a widow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the two facts that decide everything. No husband, and now no son. In that world she has no income, no legal standing and no protection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is burying her boy and her own future in the same procession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Nobody asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the point at which the story differs from almost every other healing in the Gospels. There is no request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not know who He is. She has not called out, touched a hem, sent friends, or torn open a roof. She is walking behind a bier, crying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the Lord saw her, **His heart went out to her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **splagchnizomai** — moved in the inward parts, the strongest word the language had for compassion. Luke says the compassion is for **her**, not for the boy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Please, do not cry\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "An impossible thing to say to a widow at her only son's funeral, unless you are about to do something about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The touch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He **stepped up and laid His hand on the frame** they were carrying, and the men bearing it **stood still**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Touching the bier made a man ceremonially unclean. He does it anyway, in public, in front of a great many people from the town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bearers stop. Nobody has said anything yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Young man, I say to you, get up.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five words to a corpse. Not a prayer — He does not ask God for anything. He speaks to the boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The dead man sat up and began to speak.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The handing over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus **gave him back to his mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last act, and it is the whole point of the story. He does not enlist him, instruct him, or ask anything of either of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy is put back into his mother's hands, and Jesus walks on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha needed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other raising in that valley is Elisha and the Shunammite's son, a few miles away over the hill."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That one took a shut door, a prayer, lying over the boy mouth to mouth, getting up and pacing the room, and stretching over him a second time. Then *call the Shunammite*, and *take your son* — she had to come and get him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here: one sentence, and He carries the boy across Himself. See the note on 2 Kings 4:32–37."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the crowd said.** *\"A great prophet has risen among us! God has come to help His people!\"* The Greek for *has come to help* is God visiting His people — the same word Zechariah used at the start of Luke, when *the Dayspring from on high has visited us.*"}]}, "Luke 7:36–39": {"title": "A woman at His feet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "She gets in without an invitation, and the host says nothing out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of the Pharisees invited Jesus to eat with him, so **He went** to the man's house and took His place at the table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He accepts. Luke's Gospel has Him at three different Pharisees' tables, and He goes every time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How they were sitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Took His place at the table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a formal meal guests reclined on couches around a low table, propped on the left elbow, feet pointing away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why she can be at His feet without being under the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A woman in that town who was **known for her sinful life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives her no name. The town knew her; the Gospel does not say for what."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not Mary Magdalene, and she is not Mary of Bethany. Luke introduces both of them elsewhere and does not connect them to this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How she got in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When she **learned that Jesus was eating at the Pharisee's house**, she came.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Formal meals in that world were semi-public — the door stood open and people came in to listen from the edges of the room. Getting in was not the hard part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Getting in as her was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*An **alabaster jar of perfume**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She came carrying it, which means she had decided what she was going to do before she left home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The jar had to be broken to be used."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **stood behind Him at His feet, weeping**, and her tears began to fall on His feet. She **wiped them with her own hair**, and **kissed His feet over and over**, and **poured the perfume on them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plan was the perfume. The tears were not planned — they start, and then she has to improvise a way to deal with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman's hair was bound up in public. She unbinds it in a room full of reclining men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kissed His feet over and over** — the Greek is intensive and continuous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the host thinks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said **to himself**, \"**If this man were really a prophet, He would know who is touching Him, and what kind of woman she is**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say it aloud. Luke records the interior sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the sentence is a test of prophecy: a real prophet would know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The irony Luke leaves standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers the thought. The next verse is *Simon, I have something to say to you* — and the host had never opened his mouth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The test was passed in the act of answering it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Simon had not done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water for the feet, a kiss of welcome, oil for the head. Three ordinary courtesies, and he had skipped all three. Jesus lists them a few verses later. See the note on Luke 7:44–50."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parable in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two debtors, five hundred denarii and fifty, both forgiven. *Which of them will love him more?* See the note on Luke 7:40–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon answers it correctly and does not see himself in it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she does not do.** Ask for anything, say anything, or explain herself. Luke records no words from her in the whole account — only the weeping, the hair, the kissing, and the jar."}]}, "Luke 7:40–43": {"title": "Two debts, one lender, and the question Simon answers correctly", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is at a Pharisee's dinner. A woman with a reputation has just come in and wept on His feet, and Simon has said nothing out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the story is told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon's objection was internal: *if this man were a prophet, He would know what kind of woman this is.* Luke tells us he said it to himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers the thought. *Simon, there is something I want to tell you* — which is itself the reply to the doubt about whether He knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two figures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five hundred denarii and fifty. A denarius was a day's wage, so roughly two years of work against two months."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gap is real and Jesus does not flatten it. One debt is genuinely ten times the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail that decides it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Neither of them had anything to pay with.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the equaliser, and it is easy to read past. The difference between the two men is the size of the debt, not their ability to clear it. Both are at zero."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten times nothing and one times nothing come to the same thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"forgave\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's Greek is **charizomai** — from **charis**, grace. To give freely, to grant as a favour, to cancel as a gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not the courtroom word for acquittal. It is what you do with a debt you have decided to hand over rather than collect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same verb of God in Colossians: *forgiving you every trespass.* See the note on Colossians 2:11–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which of the two will love him more?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon answers correctly, and slightly grudgingly — *I suppose*. Jesus says he has judged rightly, and then applies it: you gave Me no water, no kiss, no oil; she has not stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her many sins have been forgiven — for she loved much.* Read alone that can sound like love earning the forgiveness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable Jesus has just told settles the order. In the story, the cancelling comes first and the loving follows from it. The love is the evidence of the debt released, not the price of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last thing He says to her.** *Your faith has saved you. Go in peace.* Not your love. Luke has Him say the same words to the woman in the crowd who touched His clothes. See the note on Mark 5:30–34."}]}, "Luke 7:44–50": {"title": "\"Do you see this woman?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is at a Pharisee's table, and He turns His back on the host to address him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **He turned toward the woman** and said **to Simon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He faces her and speaks to him. Luke records the direction of the body, which he did not need to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you see this woman?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon has seen her. His whole objection was about seeing her — *if this man were a prophet, He would know **who and what sort of woman** this is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is not whether she is in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three comparisons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one is the same shape: what Simon did not do, and what she did."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"You gave Me **no water for My feet** — but she has **washed My feet with her tears** and dried them with her hair.\"*", "*\"You gave Me **no kiss of welcome** — but from the moment I walked in **she has not stopped kissing My feet**.\"*", "*\"You did **not pour oil on My head** — but **she has poured perfume on My feet**.\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water, a kiss, oil. All three were ordinary courtesies owed to a guest, and Simon had skipped every one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She supplied all three, and in each case at the feet rather than the head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her hair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman's hair was bound up in public. She unbound it in a room full of men to dry His feet, having already made a scene by crying on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So I tell you this: **her many sins have been forgiven, and that is why she has loved so much**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The forgiveness is stated first and the love is the consequence. Luke's Greek puts the forgiving in the perfect tense — already done, standing done — before she ever entered the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **the one who has been forgiven little loves little**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to a man who had just been told a parable about two debtors, one owing fifty denarii and one five hundred, and had answered correctly which would love more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gave the right answer about the story and missed himself in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says to her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He said to her, \"**Your sins are forgiven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is told directly, out loud, in front of the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who is this man, that He even forgives sins?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same question the scribes asked when the paralytic's sins were forgiven. It is the right question, asked by people who will not follow it anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dismissal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your faith has saved you. Go on your way in peace**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *your tears*, and not *your perfume*. Her faith."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *go in peace* is the standard Hebrew farewell — *lech leshalom*, go toward peace."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the perfume was.** An alabaster flask, which had to be broken to be used. She came carrying it, so she had decided what she was going to do before she reached the door."}]}, "Luke 8:30–33": {"title": "Legion and the herd", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The largest concentration of the enemy named anywhere in the Gospels, and it takes one word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eastern shore of the lake, the country of the Gerasenes — the old territory of Gad, out beyond the Jordan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gad's blessing was one line: *Gad will be attacked by raiders, but he will turn and strike at their heels.* The Hebrew hammers the word for *troop* five times over. See the note on Genesis 49:19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He crossed the water into the tribe of the troop, and the first thing that met Him on the beach was a legion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What is your name?\" \"**Legion**,\" he answered, because a great many demons had gone into him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman legion was some thousands of soldiers at full strength. The man does not give a name — he gives a headcount."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds *for we are many*, and the count of the pigs: about two thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is asking whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **begged** Him not to order them away into the abyss… the demons **begged** Him to let them go into the pigs instead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in three verses. Whatever they are, they cannot move without permission, and the whole exchange is them asking and Him deciding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The abyss** is the bottomless place, the same word John uses in Revelation. It is what they are afraid of, and they name it themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. No struggle, no formula, no repetition. *He gave them permission*, and they came out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has already told us what the man was before: no clothes, no house, living among the tombs, breaking chains and shackles, driven into the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after: *sitting at the feet of Jesus, clothed and in his right mind.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the town wanted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not more of it. *They asked Him to depart from them, for they were seized with great fear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He got into the boat and went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one who wanted to come"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man begged to go with Him — a third begging in the chapter — and this is the one request Jesus refuses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Return to your own house, and tell what great things God has done for you.\" And he went his way and proclaimed throughout the whole city.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who could not be bound is sent home to talk about it, and he is the first person commissioned to preach in Gentile territory."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Gad's line, and where it lands.** *He will turn and strike at their heels.* The heel is the word from Genesis 3 — *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.* Jacob used it over the son whose name means troop, and a legion went into the lake on that tribe's ground."}]}, "Luke 8:49–56": {"title": "\"She is asleep\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The news arrives while He is still talking to somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While He was **still speaking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is finishing the conversation with the woman who touched His clothes — telling her *daughter, your faith has made you well*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jairus has been standing in the street through all of it, with a dying child at home. See the note on Mark 5:30–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your daughter is dead. Do not trouble the Teacher any further**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skulle** — do not bother, do not harass."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is meant kindly. There is nothing left to ask for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jesus **overheard it** and said to Jairus, \"**Do not be afraid. Only believe, and she will be well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *having heard* — He was not addressed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Only believe** — the Greek is a single word, **pisteuson**, aorist imperative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who goes in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He would not let anyone go in with Him except **Peter and James and John**, and **the child's father and mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five people. The three He takes everywhere, and the parents."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is in the room, and thirty years later he does the same thing in an upstairs room in Joppa. See the note on Acts 9:36–43."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was happening in the house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone there was **weeping and wailing over her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Professional mourners were engaged immediately in that culture — flute players and wailing women. Matthew mentions the flutes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The house is already full of a funeral."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stop your crying. She is not dead. She is asleep**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they laughed at Him**, because **they knew she had died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kategelon** — laughed him to scorn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were right about the fact. Luke says so plainly: *knowing that she was dead*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sleep is the New Testament's usual word for a believer's death, and He uses it of Lazarus too — where the disciples make the same mistake in the opposite direction. See the note on John 11:11–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a denial of the death. It is a statement about how long it lasts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He **put them all outside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekbalon** — thrust out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mourners go. Only the five stay."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **took her by the hand** and called out, \"**My child, get up!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps the Aramaic: **Talitha koum**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **her spirit came back to her, and she stood up right then**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke the physician gives the sequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He told them to **give her something to eat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve years old, raised from the dead, and the instruction is dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark notes her age at exactly this point, and the woman on the road had been bleeding for twelve years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her parents **could hardly take it in**, and He told them **not to say a word to anyone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which was never going to hold. Matthew: *and the report of this went out into all that land.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who was right about the facts.** The mourners. She was dead and they knew it, and they laughed — and He put them outside and called her the way you would call a child in the morning."}]}, "Luke 9:28–36": {"title": "His face changed while praying", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's account of the transfiguration keeps three details the others leave out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason for going up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took Peter and John and James with Him and went up on a mountain **to pray**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke alone gives the purpose. Matthew and Mark say He led them up; Luke says why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does this throughout — he records Jesus praying at His baptism, before choosing the twelve, before Peter's confession, and in Gethsemane."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **while He was praying**, the look of His face was changed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The change occurs during the prayer, not after it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew uses **metamorphoo** — transformed. Luke avoids the word, which had associations with pagan myth for Greek readers, and describes the appearance instead. See the note on Matthew 17:1–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two men"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Two men appeared, talking with Him — **Moses and Elijah** — and they were **shining with glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lawgiver and the prophet. Moses died and was buried by God in a valley in Moab; Elijah went up in a whirlwind. See the note on 2 Kings 2:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they discussed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were speaking with Him about **His departure**, the thing **He was going to accomplish at Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Luke records the subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *departure* is **exodos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses, who led the first exodus, is standing on a mountain discussing the second one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Accomplish** — the Greek is **pleroun**, to fulfil, to complete."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter and the two with him were **heavy with sleep**, and **when they came fully awake** they saw His glory.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke alone says they were asleep. He notes the same thing in Gethsemane — *He found them sleeping from sorrow*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They nearly missed it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Master, it is good for us to be here. **Let us put up three shelters** — one for You, one for Moses, and one for Elijah.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, of equal size."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke adds: *he **did not know what he was saying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notes when he said it: *as Moses and Elijah were leaving*. He is trying to keep them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **while he was still speaking**, a cloud came and settled over them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is cut off mid-sentence, twice over — by the cloud and then by the voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The voice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is My Son, the One I love. Listen to Him.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version has *the One I love*; some manuscripts read *My Chosen One*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command is the same in all three Gospels: **listen to Him**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It answers the offer of three shelters. There are not three voices to attend to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the voice had finished, **Jesus was there alone**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they kept it to themselves**. In those days they told no one a word of what they had seen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter broke the silence eventually, in writing: *we were eyewitnesses of His majesty… we heard this voice which came from heaven when we were with Him on the holy mountain.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said eight days before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The Son of Man must suffer many things… and be killed, and be raised the third day.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *\"if anyone desires to come after Me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross daily.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The glory on the mountain comes eight days after the first announcement of the cross, and the conversation on it is about the same subject."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Luke keeps that nobody else does.** That He went up to pray, that the change happened while He prayed, that the three were asleep, and what the three of them were talking about."}]}, "Luke 9:51–56": {"title": "\"You have no idea what kind of spirit you are speaking out of\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two disciples propose burning a village down, and they have a Bible verse for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the village refused Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The village would not receive Him, because He was clearly heading for Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samaritans worshipped on Mount Gerizim and regarded Jerusalem's temple as illegitimate. A pilgrim on the road there was a walking insult."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refusal was religious, and it was rude."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, do You want us to call fire down out of heaven and burn them up, **the way Elijah did**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James and John are not inventing this. Elijah had called fire down on soldiers sent to arrest him, in that same region, and they knew the passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have precedent, provocation, and permission-seeking manners. Everything is in order except the request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have no idea what kind of spirit you are speaking out of. The Son of Man did not come to destroy people's lives. He came to save them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue about Elijah. He tells them they have misread which era they are standing in and what He came to do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the diagnosis is about the **source** of the suggestion, not its logic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who these two were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pair who a chapter later ask for the seats on His right and left. Jesus nicknamed them Sons of Thunder, and Luke keeps giving us evidence for why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John, who wanted to incinerate a village, is the one who ends up writing *God is love* and *we love Him because He loved us first.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they simply went on to another village.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No confrontation, no curse, no shaking of dust recorded. The response to being turned away is to keep walking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He had just done.** *He set His face to go to Jerusalem, and nothing would turn Him from it.* Luke puts that sentence three verses before this — the road they were refused passage on was the road to the cross. He was on His way to die for Samaritans, and would not let anyone burn one."}]}, "Malachi 3:1–4": {"title": "\"Who can endure the day of His coming?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last book of the Old Testament announces a messenger, and then asks a question nobody wants to answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two figures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am sending My messenger, and he will prepare the way before Me. Then suddenly the Lord you are seeking will come to His temple.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospels take the first as John the Baptist — all four quote or allude to this verse — and the second as Christ. *Suddenly* is the word Malachi chooses, and four hundred silent years followed before it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who can endure the day of His coming? Who can stand when He appears?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people of Malachi's day had been complaining that God was absent and that serving Him was pointless. The prophet's answer is to ask whether they have thought through what His arrival would mean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same reversal the prophets keep performing — Amos asking why anyone would long for the day of the LORD, since it is darkness and not light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two images"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are about cleaning, and both hurt:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**a refiner's fire** — the silversmith holds the metal in the heat until the impurities rise and can be lifted off", "**a launderer's soap** — a harsh alkaline lye, the strongest cleaning agent of the ancient world"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point of both is stated: *until they bring offerings to the LORD in righteousness*. The refining has an object, and it is worship that is actually worth something."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who gets refined.** *He will purify the Levites* — the priests, the professionals, the people running the temple. The chapter that goes on to charge a nation with robbing God opens by putting the clergy in the fire first."}]}, "Malachi 3:7–12": {"title": "The one place God says \"test Me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God forbids putting Him to the test everywhere else in scripture. Here He invites it, and the subject is giving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the passage begins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not with money. *Return to Me, and I will return to you.* The tithe question arrives inside an invitation to come back, and it is God's answer to their own question — *how are we to return?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That order matters. This is not a demand for revenue. It is a description of what turning back looks like for a people who had drifted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bring the whole tithe into the storehouse… **Test Me in this**, says the LORD Almighty, and see if I will not throw open the floodgates of heaven and pour out so much blessing that there won't be room enough to store it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three promises follow, and they are specific:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the floodgates opened — the same word used of the windows of heaven at the flood", "**the devourer rebuked** — the locust stopped before it reaches the crop", "the vine not dropping its fruit early"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the three are about **protection** rather than increase. What is already coming is guarded so it arrives."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tithe is older than the law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the point that decides how a Christian reads it. Abraham gave a tenth to Melchizedek in Genesis 14 — four hundred and thirty years before Sinai, with no commandment anywhere requiring it. Jacob promised a tenth at Bethel before there was a law to obey."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the tenth did not begin as a Mosaic regulation and does not stand or fall with one. It began as something a man did in response to a priest who blessed him, which is the order it still runs in: the blessing first, the tenth after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Hebrews says the priest receiving it now is the living one. See the note on Hebrews 7:8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this passage is not for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a lever for putting a believer in fear. Christ took the curse of the law at the cross, and a Christian giving under threat has misread which covenant they are standing in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it the way it is written and the fear drops out anyway. *Test Me* is an invitation from someone confident of the outcome, addressed to people who had stopped expecting anything from Him. The passage is trying to get them to find out that He gives."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"All the nations will call you blessed.\"** The promise does not stop at full barns. It ends with a people so evidently cared for that everyone around notices — *yours will be a land of delight*. What is being described is not a transaction but a reputation."}]}, "Malachi 4:1–3": {"title": "Healing in His rays", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last page of the Old Testament, and it ends with a sunrise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sun of righteousness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But for you who fear My name, the **sun of righteousness** will rise with **healing in its rays**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rays* is **kanaph** — wings. The same word used of the wings in Psalm 36, and the same word for the corner or hem of a garment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word behind the woman who touched the hem of His garment and was healed. The sun rising with healing in its wings, and a woman reaching for the wing of His robe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What rises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *a* sun. **The** sun of righteousness — righteousness itself coming up over the horizon like the morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order in the sentence is worth noticing: righteousness rises first, and healing is what is carried in its wings. The healing is not the sun. It is what the sun brings with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will go out and frolic like calves let loose from the stall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is to leap or skip. The picture is stall-fed calves — animals kept in and well fed — released into open ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the least solemn image in the prophets, and it is the note the Old Testament goes out on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The furnace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The day is coming, burning like a furnace… not a root or a branch will be left.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same day is a furnace and a sunrise. Malachi does not soften either half. Which one it is depends on the phrase *for you who fear My name*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next.** Four hundred years of silence, and then a man in the wilderness. The book's final lines promise Elijah before the great and dreadful day — and the first chapter of Luke says of John that he will go on before Him *in the spirit and power of Elijah*."}]}, "Mark 1:16–20": {"title": "Four fishermen by the sea", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mark's account of the first four, and it takes five verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He saw Simon and his brother Andrew **casting a net into the water**, because **fishing was their living**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds the clause. They are not out for the afternoon; it is the family trade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a circular casting-net, thrown by hand from the shallows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come with Me**,\" Jesus said to them, \"and **I will make you fishers of people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *come after Me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is His. He does not tell them to become anything; He says what He will do to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the thing they already are and says the object will change."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **dropped their nets right there** and went with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **euthus** — immediately, at once. Mark uses it more than forty times in sixteen chapters; it is the pulse of his Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A little farther on He saw **James son of Zebedee and his brother John, sitting in their boat putting their nets back in order**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *putting in order* is **katartizontas** — mending, repairing, setting right."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word Paul uses for restoring a fallen brother, and Peter uses of what God does after suffering: *will put you back together*. See the note on 1 Peter 5:10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And right away they **left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men** and walked away after Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark mentions the hired men, which tells you the business was large enough to employ staff. This is not destitution being walked away from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it mentions the father, sitting in the boat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John's Gospel adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John 1 has an earlier meeting — Andrew hears the Baptist say *behold the Lamb of God*, follows, spends a day with Jesus, and goes to find his brother: *we have found the Messiah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So this is not the first time they had met. It is the day the trade ended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they went back to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After the resurrection, in John 21: *Simon Peter said to them, \"I am going fishing.\" They said to him, \"We are going with you also.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They fish all night and catch nothing, and a man on the shore tells them which side to cast on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then a charcoal fire, breakfast, and three questions for Peter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the two pairs end up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, James and John become the three taken into Jairus's house, up the mountain, and into Gethsemane."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is the first of the twelve to be killed, in Acts 12, by Herod's sword. John is traditionally the last to die."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark records next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same day: the synagogue at Capernaum, the unclean spirit, Peter's mother-in-law, and the whole town at the door after sunset."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark 1 does not slow down."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says He will do.** *I will make you.* They are asked to come, and the making is stated as His work — and Mark says the nets went down immediately."}]}, "Mark 10:17–22": {"title": "\"He looked at him with love\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only person in the Gospels who comes to Jesus and leaves worse off, and Mark records a detail the others do not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he arrives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man came **running up** and **dropped to his knees** in front of Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Running and kneeling. Mark alone has both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about his approach is grudging. He is eager, public, and respectful."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Good Teacher, **what must I do** to inherit eternal life?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question has a verb in it that decides the whole conversation: **do**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An inheritance is not something you do anything for. He asks how to earn one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why do you call Me good? **No one is good except God alone.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a denial. A question about what the man meant by the word he used."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not commit adultery, do not murder, do not steal, do not lie about anyone, **do not cheat anyone**, honour your father and your mother.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six commandments, and only from the second table — the ones about other people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not cheat anyone* is not in the ten. It is closer to *do not covet*, and Mark alone has it in this form. Coveting is the one commandment nobody can verify from outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have kept every one of these since I was a boy.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not being flippant. Externally, it may well have been true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line only Mark has"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jesus **looked straight at him, and He loved him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Luke both tell this story and neither includes it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever follows is not a rebuke. Mark tells you the expression on His face first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There is one thing you are missing.** Go and sell everything you own, and give the money to the poor… **Then come, take up the cross, and follow Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for what the man will not give — and there is no evidence He asks anyone else in the Gospels for this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zacchaeus, a chapter later in Luke, gives away half voluntarily and is not asked for anything. See the note on Luke 19:1–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At those words **the man's face fell**, and he walked away with a heavy heart, **because he owned a great deal**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *face fell* is used elsewhere of the sky darkening before a storm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He came running and went away slowly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How hard it is for those who have riches to enter the kingdom of God!\" And the disciples were astonished.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were astonished because wealth was read as a sign of blessing. When they ask *who then can be saved?*, the answer is the point of the whole passage:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**With men it is impossible, but not with God; for with God all things are possible.**\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asked for and what he was offered.** He asked what to *do* to inherit. He was offered *treasure in heaven* and *come, follow Me* — and both are things you receive. The only sentence in the exchange with a *do* in it was his."}]}, "Mark 10:41–45": {"title": "A ransom for many", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Ten men are furious with two, and He uses the argument to say why He came."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What started it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James and John had asked for the seats on His right and left in glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked it immediately after the third and most detailed prediction of the crucifixion. Mark puts the request straight after *they will mock Him, and scourge Him, and spit on Him, and kill Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The indignation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the other ten heard about this, **they were indignant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because the request was wrong. Because two of them got in first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the world works"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You know how it works among the nations. Their rulers **lord it over** people, and the important ones **make sure everyone feels their weight**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two Greek verbs, both compounds built on *down* — to dominate from above, to exercise authority against."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He describes it accurately and without heat. That is simply how power operates."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It is not to be like that among you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is blunt: *but not so is it among you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not abolish greatness. The next sentence assumes people will want it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inversion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever wants to be **great** among you must be **the one who serves you**, and whoever wants to be **first** must be **the servant of all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and the second is stronger. **Diakonos** is a servant, a waiter at a table. **Doulos** is a slave — owned, with no standing at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Great → servant. First → slave of everyone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For even the Son of Man **did not come to be served, but to serve**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue the principle. He gives Himself as the case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ransom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **to give His life a ransom for many**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lutron** — the price paid to free a slave or a prisoner of war. It appears twice in the New Testament, here and in Matthew's parallel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the preposition is **anti** — in place of, instead of. Not *on behalf of* but *in the position of*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's friend Elihu had said it in his own words: *deliver him from going down to the pit; **I have found a ransom**.* See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark's whole Gospel is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shortest and fastest of the four, with *immediately* used more than forty times, and no genealogy or birth narrative — a book about a servant, and servants do not need pedigrees."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This verse is generally taken as its key sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He was going when He said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Up the road to Jerusalem, six days out. Bartimaeus is in the next paragraph, and the triumphal entry in the next chapter. See the note on Mark 10:46–52."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the ten and the two had in common.** All twelve wanted position. The difference between James and John and the other ten is that two of them asked out loud."}]}, "Mark 10:46–52": {"title": "Bartimaeus throws off his cloak", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last healing Mark records before the triumphal entry, and the man is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A blind beggar named **Bartimaeus, the son of Timaeus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark gives the name and then translates it — *bar Timaeus* means son of Timaeus, so he says the same thing twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most people Jesus heals in the Gospels are anonymous. This one is not, which readers have long taken to mean he was known in the early church afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he shouts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Son of David** is a messianic title, and this is the first time in Mark anybody uses it out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A blind beggar on a roadside has worked out what the disciples are still arguing about — two paragraphs earlier James and John were asking for the best seats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Plenty of people told him **sharply to be quiet**, but he **only shouted louder**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they rebuked him — the same verb used of Jesus rebuking demons and the storm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What stops Jesus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **stopped** and said, \"Call him over.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word. The whole procession into Jerusalem for the last week of His life halts on the shout of a man nobody wanted to hear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take heart. Get up. He is calling you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same people who were telling him to shut up are now helping him to his feet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cloak"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **threw off his cloak**, jumped to his feet, and came to Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A beggar's cloak was his licence and his livelihood — spread on the ground in front of him to catch coins, and his blanket at night. For a blind man it was also the one thing he could find again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He throws it away before he can see, on the strength of a summons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What do you want Me to do for you?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The identical question He had asked James and John a few verses earlier. They wanted thrones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Rabbi, **I want to see**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go. **Your faith has made you well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *your faith has saved you* — **sozo**, the wide word covering healing, rescue and salvation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with the *go*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And immediately he could see, and **he followed Him down the road**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told to go, and follows instead — down the road that leads to Jerusalem, and to the cross, six days away."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this sits in Mark.** The disciples have just failed to understand the third passion prediction and asked for positions. Then a blind man calls Him the Son of David, asks for one thing, and follows Him to Jerusalem. Mark puts the sighted disciples and the blind beggar side by side on purpose."}]}, "Mark 11:20–26": {"title": "\"Believe that you have received it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter points at a withered tree, and Jesus answers with three sentences about prayer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Have faith in God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first words, and they set the direction. The Greek can be rendered *have the faith of God* — faith that has God as its object and its source."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this hangs on where the faith is placed, not on how much of it there is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense that carries the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever you ask for in prayer, **believe that you have received it**, and it will be yours.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is aorist — believe you *received*, past. Not that you will receive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sequence is: ask, take it as given, and then it becomes yours in experience. That is an odd order by every ordinary measure, and it is what the sentence says."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone says to this mountain, \"Be lifted up and thrown into the sea.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were walking from Bethany to Jerusalem, over the Mount of Olives, with the Dead Sea visible in the distance. **This** mountain, and that sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notice the verb: *says to*. Twice. The faith in the illustration is exercised by speaking to the obstacle, not by talking to God about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Does not doubt in his heart\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word rendered *doubt* is **diakrino** — to separate, to divide, to make a distinction between two things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So doubting, in this sentence, is not weak feeling. It is being divided in yourself — one thing held in one hand and its opposite in the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the two things being divided are named in the passage itself. Peter is astonished at a dried-up fig tree; Jesus answers by talking about a mountain going into the sea. A small withering and a mountain moved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who separates those — who has faith for the tree and none for the mountain — will not see either. That is what the verb rules out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same word, at the table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses **diakrino** twice in 1 Corinthians 11: *not **discerning** the Lord's body*, and *if we **judged** ourselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are handed over at that table, the body and the blood, and dividing them is the failure Paul names. It is the same verb, doing the same work: a man divided between two things God gave together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the notes on 1 Corinthians 11:27–30 and 1 Corinthians 11:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition He attaches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And whenever you stand praying, if you are holding something against anyone, forgive it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus attaches one thing to the whole passage, and it is not effort or technique. It is forgiveness — and He makes it the immediate neighbour of the promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever unanswered prayer is about, He points at the person you have not forgiven before He points anywhere else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the fig tree is there.** The withering brackets Mark's account of Jesus clearing the temple — the tree is cursed on the way in and found dead on the way out, with the temple scene in between. Mark's sandwich construction is deliberate: a tree in leaf with no fruit, and a house of prayer with no prayer in it."}]}, "Mark 12:35–37": {"title": "\"David himself calls Him Lord\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He asks the question Himself, in public, and nobody answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While Jesus was **teaching in the temple courts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passion week. He has been questioned by four delegations in two days and each has gone away with nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now He asks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How is it that the teachers of the law say the Messiah is the son of David?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a denial. Every Jew held it, and it is true — Matthew opens his Gospel with the genealogy proving it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking why they stop there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"David himself, **speaking by the Holy Spirit**, said: '**The Lord said to my Lord**: sit at My right hand until I make Your enemies a footstool under Your feet.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110:1, the most-quoted Old Testament verse in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different words. The first **Lord** is YHWH. The second is **Adonai** — my Lord."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**David himself calls Him Lord — so how can He be David's son?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture the honour ran upward. A father does not address his own descendant as *my Lord*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unless the descendant existed before him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He credits with the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Speaking by the Holy Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He attributes the authorship in the middle of the argument, and the whole case depends on the wording being exact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Sit at My right hand until** I make Your enemies a footstool.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews builds a chapter on that posture: every priest *stands ministering daily*, and this one *sat down*. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sitting is not idleness. It is what you do when the work is finished, and the subduing that follows is somebody else's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who answered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody. Matthew's parallel adds: *no one was able to answer Him a word, nor from that day on did anyone dare question Him anymore.* See the note on Matthew 22:41–46."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the great crowd listened to Him with delight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone records this. The Greek is that they heard Him gladly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scholars were silenced and the crowd enjoyed it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Beware of the scribes, who **desire to go around in long robes**, love greetings in the marketplaces, the best seats in the synagogues… **who devour widows' houses**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then He sits down opposite the treasury and watches a widow put in two mites."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Psalm 110 says next.** *The LORD has sworn and will not relent: You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek.* A king on David's throne who is also a priest of a line older than Aaron's — and Hebrews takes two chapters over it. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}]}, "Mark 14:12–16": {"title": "The man carrying a jar of water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two disciples are sent into a crowded city with no address, and told to follow a man they have never seen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the first day of Unleavened Bread, **the day the Passover lamb was killed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jerusalem at Passover held a population many times its usual size. Every household needed a room, and rooms had been spoken for weeks earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Where do You want us to go and **get the Passover ready** for You?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are asking a practical question. He answers with a sign."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A man carrying a jar of water** will meet you. **Follow him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water-carrying was women's work in that culture. A man with a water jar on his shoulder was unusual enough to pick out of a festival crowd without a description, without a name, and without a street."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two are given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the location. A person to follow, and the next instruction only when they arrive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are told what to say at the door, and nothing about what happens after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words at the door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The Teacher asks: **where is the guest room** where I may eat the Passover with My disciples?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Teacher** — no name. The householder knows who is meant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *guest room* is **kataluma**, the same word Luke uses for the inn with no room in it at the beginning. The first *kataluma* had no room for Him. This one is kept ready."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will show you a **large room upstairs, already furnished and ready**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **estromenon** — spread, laid out, with the couches already in place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had to be arranged. It was furnished before the two disciples set out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The two of them went into the city and found it **exactly as He had told them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says it as flatly as that. Every detail landed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He is doing in the same week"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas has already been to the chief priests. The arrangement of this room keeps the supper's location out of Judas's hands until they are in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in that room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bread and the cup, the announcement of the betrayer, the new covenant in His blood, and the walk out to the Mount of Olives singing a hymn. See the note on Mark 14:22–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other errand like it.** Six days before, two disciples were sent for a colt nobody had ridden, told what they would find and what to say to the owner — *the Lord has need of it* — and found it exactly so. See the note on Mark 11:1–6."}]}, "Mark 14:22–26": {"title": "The bread and the cup", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mark's account of the table is the shortest, and the order of the sentences carries something the other accounts do not put as plainly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in Mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He took a cup, gave thanks, and passed it to them, **and every one of them drank from it**. \"This is My blood,\" He said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They drank first. The explanation comes after the cup is empty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark is the only one who reports it in that sequence. In Matthew the words come with the cup; here the drinking is a completed act, and then Jesus tells them what they have just done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Until it went into them, it was wine at a Passover table like any other. What made it His blood was not a formula said over the cup. It was His word, and the disciples had already swallowed it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Torn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He picked up the bread. He gave thanks over it, **tore** it, and put it into their hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is to break, and the Illumination renders it *tore* — the tearing of a flat loaf by hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53 says *He was wounded for our transgressions, He was bruised for our iniquities.* The bread is broken before it is given, and the giving is hand-to-hand: *put it into their hands.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things named, and what each is for"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the body** — *this is My body*", "**the blood** — *the blood of the new covenant, poured out for many*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood is given a stated purpose in the text: the covenant, and in Matthew's account, *for the forgiveness of sins.* The body is given without a stated purpose here — and Matthew 8:17 supplies it: *He took our infirmities and carried our diseases.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two elements, two provisions. Paul's warning in 1 Corinthians 11 about *not discerning the body* turns on holding them together. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:27–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Every one of them drank\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records the participation. Judas is at that table in Mark's account — the betrayal has already been announced in the paragraph above."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last supper is not the last"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will not drink of the fruit of the vine again until the day I drink it new in the kingdom of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it while the cup is still in His hand. The meal is left unfinished on purpose, with a date set for the rest of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they did next.** *Then they sang together, and went out to the Mount of Olives.* At Passover the closing hymn was the Hallel, Psalms 115–118 — which includes *I shall not die but live, and declare the works of the LORD*, and *the stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone.* He sang that on the way to Gethsemane."}]}, "Mark 14:27–31": {"title": "\"Even if all of them fall away, I will not\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter's most famous sentence before the courtyard, and what is striking is what he skips over to say it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise he walks past"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says two things. First, *every one of you is going to fall away tonight* — with a quotation from Zechariah about the shepherd struck and the sheep scattered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *but after I am raised, I will go on ahead of you into Galilee.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That second sentence is a plan for after the failure. Before anyone has denied anything, a meeting place has been arranged. Peter answers the first sentence and says nothing about the second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his answer reveals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even if all of them fall away, I will not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is the tell. Peter's confidence is measured against the other eleven, in front of the other eleven. And when Jesus tells him precisely how far he will go, he says it *again, harder*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark, whose Gospel the early church associated with Peter's own preaching, keeps all of it. Including the last line: *and every one of them said the same thing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail Mark alone records"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before the rooster has crowed twice.* Only Mark has the two crowings, and only Mark later notes the first one, which Peter did not hear or did not heed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the kind of detail a man remembers about the worst night of his life."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happened to the arrangement.** At the empty tomb the messenger says: go and tell the disciples *and Peter* that He is going ahead of you into Galilee. The plan made before the denial stands after it, and Peter's name is added to the message."}]}, "Mark 15:42–47": {"title": "Joseph of Arimathea", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The disciples have gone. A member of the council that condemned Him comes forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **respected member of the council**, a man who was himself **waiting for the kingdom of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The council is the Sanhedrin — the body that had tried Jesus that same morning. Luke adds that he *had not consented to their decision and deed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says he was rich. John says he was a disciple *secretly, for fear of the Jews*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It took courage to walk in there\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says it outright, and Mark is the shortest Gospel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes to Pilate — a Roman governor who has just executed a man for sedition — and asks for the body of the condemned. There is no way to do that anonymously."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he had spent a career protecting goes in that request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pilate's surprise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pilate was **surprised to hear that Jesus was already dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Crucifixion normally took days. Pilate sends for the centurion and checks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone records this exchange, and it settles the question of whether He was actually dead: the officer who ran the execution confirmed it to the governor before the body was released."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph did with his money"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph **bought** a length of linen, took Him down, wrapped Him in the cloth, and laid Him in a tomb that had been **cut out of the rock**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He paid for the linen. Matthew says the tomb was *his own new tomb, which he had hewn out of the rock*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wealthy man's grave, bought and cut for himself, given away on a Friday afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they made His grave with the wicked — but with the rich at His death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves. He was executed between two criminals and buried in a rich man's tomb, and Isaiah put the two clauses side by side seven hundred years earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "John's addition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nicodemus came with him — the other secret one, the man who had come by night in chapter 3 — carrying *a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about a hundred pounds*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a royal burial quantity, and an enormous expense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two members of the Sanhedrin who would not declare themselves while He was alive are the two men handling His body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The women watching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary Magdalene and Mary the mother of Joses were watching, and **they saw where He was laid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark notes it because it matters two days later. They knew which tomb."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was in the tomb by Sunday.** The linen lying there, and the cloth that had been around His head *folded together in a place by itself.* John looked in and reports the arrangement of the cloth before he reports anything else."}]}, "Mark 16:15–18": {"title": "\"Tell the good news to every person alive\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last instruction in Mark, and it has no limits on it anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go out into **the whole world**, and tell the good news to **every person alive**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to all creation*. Matthew's version has *all the nations*; Mark's is wider still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is excluded by geography, nation, or condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He said it to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven men who had run. The chapter has just recorded Him rebuking them for unbelief and hardness of heart because they would not believe the ones who had seen Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rebukes them, and in the next sentence hands them the world."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever **believes and is baptised** will be saved; whoever **refuses to believe** stands condemned.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves are not symmetrical. Believing and baptism on one side; on the other He does not say *whoever is not baptised* — only *whoever refuses to believe*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dividing line in the sentence is belief."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The signs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And these signs will **go along with** the ones who believe.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to follow after, to accompany. The signs follow the believers; the believers do not chase the signs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five are listed:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*in My name they will **drive out demons***", "*they will **speak in new tongues***", "*if they **pick up snakes** or **drink anything deadly**, it will do them no harm*", "*they will **lay their hands on the sick**, and the sick will get well*"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two that are not commands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here tells anyone to handle a snake or drink poison. Both are described as things that will not harm, not as things to attempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is bitten by a viper on Malta, shakes it off into the fire, and the islanders wait for him to swell up and die. He had not gone looking for it — he was picking up firewood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In My name\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The authority is stated once and covers the list. Not in their own name, and not by any technique."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the last verse of Mark says happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they went out and preached everywhere, **the Lord working with them** and confirming the word through the accompanying signs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark ends with the two parties named: they preached, and He worked with them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where He is while they are doing it.** *So then, after the Lord had spoken to them, He was received up into heaven, and sat down at the right hand of God.* Mark puts the ascension and the seat in one line, and then says He was working with them. Seated, and working."}]}, "Mark 2:1–5": {"title": "A roof torn open", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four men make a hole in somebody's house, and the first thing said to the man they lower through it is not about his legs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came back to **Capernaum**, and word got around that **He was home**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says *at home* — probably Peter's house, where He had healed Peter's mother-in-law and where the whole town had gathered at the door after sunset."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So many people packed in that **there was no room left, not even out by the doorway**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The door is blocked from the inside and the outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He was speaking the word to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark puts it in there so the reader knows what gets interrupted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Four men came carrying a paralyzed man **on a mat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone gives the number. One at each corner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obstacle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they **could not get anywhere near Jesus** because of the crush of people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They try the ordinary route first and it fails."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They went up on top of the house, **broke open the roof right above Him, dug their way through**, and lowered the mat down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Galilean roof was beams laid across, packed with branches, reeds and clay, rolled flat. An outside stair went up to it — it was usable space."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dug** is the right word. They are removing packed earth, and it is falling into the room the whole time, onto the crowd and onto Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody's roof. Nobody in the account asks permission, and Mark records no complaint from the householder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **saw their faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Their** — plural. The four, and the man on the mat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is described as visible. What He saw was a hole in a ceiling and a stretcher coming down on ropes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Son, your sins are forgiven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody asked for that. Five men went to enormous trouble about a pair of legs, and the first sentence addresses something else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Son** — the Greek is **teknon**, child. An affectionate word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark leaves out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any explanation. The paralysis is not said to be caused by sin, and the man is not questioned or told to confess."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forgiveness first, walking second. The healing comes seven verses later, and Jesus says outright that it happens *so that you will know the Son of Man has authority here on earth to forgive sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The legs are the evidence for the sentence about the sins. See the note on Mark 2:6–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the scribes make of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are in the room, sitting, and they conclude it is blasphemy — *nobody can forgive sins except God alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their logic is sound. It is the premise about who is speaking that they have wrong."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the four did.** Carried him, failed at the door, climbed, dug through a roof, and lowered him into a room full of people. Mark calls the whole sequence *their faith*."}]}, "Mark 2:15–17": {"title": "At the table with sinners", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The dinner that follows a tax collector's call, and the objection is about seating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was at the table **in Levi's house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levi is Matthew. Mark and Luke use the Levi name; the man's own Gospel uses Matthew and adds *the tax collector* to it in the list of the twelve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been sitting at the tax booth in Capernaum when Jesus walked past and said *follow Me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A whole crowd of **tax collectors and people with bad reputations** sat down and ate with Him and His disciples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tax collectors worked for Rome, could set their own margin, and were classed with thieves. A Jew who took the job was outside respectable society permanently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause Mark adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **there were many of them who followed Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a few curious guests. Mark says there was a crowd of them, and that they were already following."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why does He eat with people like that?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *why does He eat and drink with tax collectors and sinners*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask the disciples, not Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What eating meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shared table in that world was not a neutral act. It signified acceptance, and it made you ritually vulnerable to whatever the other party carried."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The complaint is not that He talks to them. It is that He is reclining at a meal with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same charge is made against Him repeatedly: *this Man receives sinners and eats with them* — which is what provokes the three parables of Luke 15. See the note on Luke 15:11–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **not the healthy who go looking for a doctor. It is the sick**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A proverb, and one that circulated in the ancient world in various forms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not defend the company. He explains the profession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I did not come to call **the ones who think they are already righteous**. I came to call **sinners**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version adds a quotation: *go and learn what this means: \"I desire mercy and not sacrifice.\"* — from Hosea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the call goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To bring them **to a whole new way of thinking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metanoia** — a change of mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark records around it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 2 is a run of four confrontations: the paralytic through the roof and the forgiveness of sins, this dinner, a question about fasting, and the disciples picking grain on the sabbath. See the note on Mark 2:6–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four scenes, four objections, and the opposition is assembled by the end of chapter 3: *then the Pharisees went out and immediately plotted with the Herodians against Him, how they might destroy Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doctor line explains why He is there. The second line says who He came for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is an apology."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the objection was about.** Not the teaching. A meal — and Mark notes in passing that there was a crowd of such people already following Him."}]}, "Mark 2:6–12": {"title": "\"Which is easier to say?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The objection is never spoken aloud, and it gets answered anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is in the room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some of the teachers of the law **were sitting there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's parallel says they had come *out of every town of Galilee, Judea, and Jerusalem*. This is not a casual audience; the scholars have travelled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They began **turning it over in their hearts**: \"Why does this man talk this way? **That is an insult to God. Nobody can forgive sins except God alone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reasoning is correct. A third party cannot forgive a debt owed to someone else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin is against God, so the pardon is God's to give — and they are right about that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus knew **immediately, deep in His spirit**, what they were reasoning **among themselves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had been said. Mark says twice in one sentence that it was interior."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Answering the unspoken thought is itself an answer to the question they were asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why are you turning such things over in your hearts?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Which is easier to say** to this man — 'Your sins are forgiven,' or 'Get up, pick up your mat, and walk'?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Easier **to say**. That is the precise wording."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saying *your sins are forgiven* is easier, because nothing visible follows and nobody can check it. Saying *get up and walk* is verifiable within seconds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So He says the checkable one, to establish the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But so that you will know the Son of Man has authority here on earth to forgive sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence breaks off mid-way — Mark inserts *and here He turned to the paralyzed man* — and then finishes as a command to somebody else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing is inside a sentence addressed to the scribes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Here on earth\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *on the earth*. Their objection was that forgiveness belongs in heaven. He puts it on the ground, in a house in Capernaum, in the afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The Son of Man\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "His usual way of referring to Himself. It comes from Daniel 7: *One like the Son of Man, coming with the clouds of heaven… to Him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds modest and is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three commands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, pick up your mat, and go home**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mat is included. He is to carry the thing that carried him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the man **stood right up, took his mat, and walked out in front of them all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's Greek is immediate. Through the crowd, past the scribes, out the door that had been too full to get in by."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were beside themselves, and they **praised God** and kept saying, \"**We have never seen anything like this**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they were amazed out of their minds*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was and was not proved"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The legs were the visible half. The forgiveness was the point, and He said so before the man moved."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on offer in the passage.** The scribes came to assess a claim about authority. They watched a man walk out carrying the bed he was brought in on, and the claim it was offered as proof of was the one about sins."}]}, "Mark 3:1–6": {"title": "\"Angry and grieved\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only place in the Gospels where Mark tells us Jesus was angry, and he tells us what it was aimed at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were **watching Him closely** to see whether He would heal the man on the Sabbath, **so that they would have something to accuse Him of**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *watching closely* is to observe with sinister intent — to lie in wait."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the room is asking about the man's hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with the staging"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come and stand here where everyone can see you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts him in the middle. Whatever happens next is going to happen in public, and Jesus arranges it that way deliberately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could have healed him quietly after the service. He calls him out to the front first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is it right on the Sabbath to do good, or to do harm? To save a life, or to destroy one?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pairs, and the second is sharper than it looks. While He is asking about destroying a life on the Sabbath, they are in the process of planning His death — as the last verse shows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But they would not say a word.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they were silent. There is no answer to the question and they know it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He looked around at them, **angry and grieved at how hard their hearts had gone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two emotions in one clause. The Greek for *angry* is **orge**; the participle for *grieved* means being pained together with, deeply distressed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Luke both record this scene and neither mentions the anger. Only Mark does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was directed at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the man. Not sinners. Not the crowd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The hardness of their hearts* — the Greek is **porosis**, a medical word for a callus, or bone forming over a break. Tissue that has gone hard and lost feeling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is angry at the calcification, and the same sentence says He is grieved by it. Anger and grief over the same object, at the same time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The healing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stretch out your hand.**\" He stretched it out, and the hand was made completely whole.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing was done to him. No touch is recorded, no word over the hand, no prayer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told to do the one thing a withered hand cannot do, and does it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the healing produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Pharisees went straight out and **began plotting with Herod's men how they might destroy Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees and the Herodians had nothing in common and detested each other. A restored hand united them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records the first plot against His life immediately after this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where Jesus' anger is not recorded.** With the woman caught in adultery. With Zacchaeus, or Matthew at the tax booth, or the woman at the well, or the thief on the cross. The Gospels show Him angry at hardness and at a temple market, and nowhere else."}]}, "Mark 3:13–19": {"title": "\"So that they would be with Him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The appointment of the twelve, and Mark gives the job description in three parts with an unexpected first item."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He went up **on the mountainside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke adds what happened first: *He went out to the mountain to pray, and continued all night in prayer to God. And when it was day, He called His disciples to Him, and from them He chose twelve.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A night of praying, and then the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **called to Himself the ones He wanted**, and they came to Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *whom He Himself willed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody applies. He says it later in John: *you did not choose Me, but I chose you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He appointed twelve of them, **so that they would be with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first item, and it is not a task."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hina osin met' autou** — that they might be with Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second and third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so that He could **send them out to preach**, with **authority to heal the sick and to drive out demons**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sending comes second, and the power comes attached to the sending."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark puts them in that order and does not reverse it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"apostle\" means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **apostolos** — one sent out. The name describes the second purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve names, and Mark notes what was distinctive about several:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Simon, to whom He gave the name Peter** — **Petros**, a stone", "**James and John, whom He called Boanerges, Sons of Thunder** — the two who wanted to call fire down on a Samaritan village. See the note on Luke 9:51–56.", "**Simon the Zealot** — from the party committed to armed resistance against Rome", "**Matthew** — who collected taxes for Rome"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A zealot and a tax collector in the same group of twelve."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Judas Iscariot, the one who would hand Him over**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Mark names the last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every list of the twelve in the New Testament ends with Judas, and every one attaches the same clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was on the mountain, called by name, and included in the sending and the authority."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve. Nobody in that world would have missed it — twelve tribes, twelve sons of Jacob."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark records around it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately before: crowds pressing so hard He had to have a boat kept ready, and unclean spirits crying out that He was the Son of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after: *then the multitude came together again, so that they could not so much as eat bread.* And His family coming to take Him home, *for they said, \"He is out of His mind.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's ordering has been noticed for a long time. The men are appointed to be with Him first, and everything else in the sentence follows from that clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are with Him for roughly three years before Acts opens."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes first in the job description.** Not preaching and not power. That they would be with Him — and Mark puts it at the head of the sentence with the other two hanging off it."}]}, "Mark 4:13–20": {"title": "What the story means", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one parable Jesus explains in full, and He says it is the key to the rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You do not understand this story? Then how will you understand any of them?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone keeps it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This parable is treated as foundational. If the mechanism here is missed, none of the others will work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being sown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The farmer is sowing the word**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a message about morality or effort. The seed is the word, and everything that follows describes what happens to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first soil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Some people are like **the seed along the path**: the word is sown, and **Satan comes right away and snatches it out of them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **euthus** — immediately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Path soil is not bad soil. It is soil that has been walked on until it is hard, and the seed sits on top of it where the birds can see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Some are like **the seed on rocky ground**: **the moment they hear the word they welcome it with joy**, but **it has no root in them and does not last**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joy is real and Mark says so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem is depth. Rocky ground in that country is a shelf of limestone under a thin layer of soil — the seed germinates fast because the shelf holds the heat, and dies fast because the root has nowhere to go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So when **trouble or persecution comes because of the word**, they fall away at once.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Because of the word.** The pressure is named as arriving on account of the thing sown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Others are like **the seed sown among thorns**: **they hear the word**, but…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things crowd in:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"**the worries of this life**\"*", "*\"**the pull of money**\"* — the Greek is *the deceitfulness of riches*", "*\"**the craving for other things**\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version says *pleasures of life*; Matthew has the first two."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of the three is a sin in itself. Worry, money, and wanting other things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All crowd in and **choke the word**, and **it never comes to anything**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sumpnigousin** — choke, throttle, crowd out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plant is alive and produces nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And some are like **the seed sown on good soil**: they **hear the word and welcome it**, and **it bears fruit in them — thirty times what was sown, or sixty, or a hundred**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs: hear and welcome. The Greek is **paradechontai** — receive, accept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The yields"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three different figures, and all three are called good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The good soil is not uniform. Mark keeps the range."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Normal yields in that agriculture ran to seven or eight times. Thirty is exceptional and a hundred is extraordinary — the number Isaac reaped in a famine year. See the note on Genesis 26:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What all four have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same seed and the same sower. Nothing in the parable varies except the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Is a lamp brought to be put under a basket or under a bed? Is it not to be set on a lampstand?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**Take heed what you hear**. With the same measure you use, it will be measured to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has *take heed **how** you hear*. Between them, both the content and the manner."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What varies in the story.** Not the seed. One sower, one word, four grounds — and the third one is choked by three things none of which are wrong in themselves."}]}, "Mark 4:24–25": {"title": "\"Be careful how you listen\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses about hearing, tucked between the parables."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be careful how you listen.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *what* you listen to — Luke's version has *how you hear*, and Mark's is *what you hear*, and between them they cover both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point is that hearing is something that can be done well or badly, and that the manner of it has consequences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The measure you use will be the measure used for you**, and more besides.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same saying appears in Luke 6 about judging, condemning, forgiving and giving. See the note on Luke 6:37–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is applied to listening. However much attention you bring is the amount you get back — *and more besides.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hard sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The one who has will be given more**, and from the one who has nothing, even the little he has will be taken away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It reads as unfair until you notice what is being measured."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The context is the parable of the sower, which Mark has just finished — four soils, the same seed, and the difference is entirely in the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the sowing varies. What a hearer ends up with depends on what he does with what he heard, and the man who does nothing with it loses even the memory of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately before: *is a lamp brought to be put under a basket or under a bed? Is it not to be set on a lampstand?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after: the seed growing secretly, and the mustard seed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sequence is — the lamp is meant to be seen, so pay attention to how you hear, because the seed grows on its own once it is in the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lamp and the hearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark puts a lamp and an instruction about listening in consecutive breaths."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstand in the tabernacle was the only light in a windowless room, and the priest's daily job at it was trimming the wicks so it kept burning. See the note on Exodus 25:31–40."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unavoidable disclosure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For there is nothing hidden which will not be revealed, nor has anything been kept secret but that it should come to light.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the verse in between. Whatever is put under the basket does not stay there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line Mark repeats.** *If anyone has ears to hear, let him hear.* He uses it twice in this one chapter — before the explanation of the sower and again here. Every occurrence of it in the Gospels is attached to something that had just gone over somebody's head."}]}, "Mark 4:35–41": {"title": "\"Asleep on a cushion\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps a detail the other Gospels leave out, and it is the kind of thing an eyewitness remembers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cushion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was in the stern, asleep on a cushion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the* cushion — the definite article. It is the steersman's seat cushion, a fixed part of a fishing boat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Mark records it, and Mark's Gospel is the one the early church associated with Peter's preaching. Peter owned a boat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us cross over to the other side.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence before the storm. Everything that happens next takes place inside that instruction — they are going where He said they were going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question they ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teacher, do You not care that we are going down?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *save us*. It is an accusation, and it is about His caring rather than His power. Fishermen who have been on that lake all their lives are certain they are sinking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says to the sea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Peace. Be still.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *be still* is the word used for muzzling — put a muzzle on it. It is the same word Mark used a chapter earlier when Jesus silenced an unclean spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks to the storm the way He speaks to a demon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two fears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why are you so frightened? Do you still have no faith?\" And a deep awe came over them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark is precise: they were afraid of the storm, and after it stopped they were more afraid. The word for their reaction afterwards is the great fear reserved for the presence of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask *who is this?* — and the answer, for anyone who knew the Psalms, is that stilling the sea is something God does."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the disciples had already been told.** He had just spent the afternoon teaching in parables from that boat. The storm follows immediately, and Mark places it there deliberately: the men who had heard the word all day are the ones asked whether they still have no faith."}]}, "Mark 5:11–13": {"title": "They had to ask", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and the striking thing is who is doing the requesting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The begging"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The spirits begged Him.* Mark uses the verb twice in this scene — earlier they had begged not to be sent out of the region."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever they are, they cannot move without permission. The whole exchange is them asking and Him deciding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the request reveals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Send us into the pigs. Let us go into them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They want somewhere to be. The parallel account in Luke has them begging not to be sent into the abyss."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same thing the returning spirit is looking for in Matthew 12 — somewhere to settle, a house to occupy. See the note on Matthew 12:43–45."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He let them go\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and Mark does not explain them. The permission is granted without a recorded reason."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have argued about the pigs for centuries — whose they were, what the loss meant, why Jesus allowed it. Mark says nothing about any of that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he does record is the count: *about two thousand of them*. It is the only indication in the passage of the scale of what had been living in one man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in the man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter he gives his name as Legion, *for we are many* — a Roman legion being some thousands of soldiers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody could bind him, not even with chains. He had been breaking irons and cutting himself with stones among the tombs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The pigs stampeded down the steep bank into the lake and were drowned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The destruction the spirits had been working on the man goes into the herd, and the man is left sitting, dressed, in his right mind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the region asks for afterwards.** Not more healings. They *begged Jesus to leave*, and He got into the boat. The third begging in the chapter comes from the townspeople, and it is the only one about which He does what is asked without a word. See the note on Mark 5:18–20."}]}, "Mark 5:18–20": {"title": "The one man Jesus told to stay", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man who has just been set free asks to come along, and it is the one request of that kind in the Gospels that gets turned down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Jesus was getting back into the boat, the man who had been set free asked if he could come along with Him. But Jesus would not let him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone else is called to follow. This man asks to and is sent home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been living among the tombs, cutting himself with stones, so strong that chains would not hold him. Now he is dressed and in his right mind and wants to get in the boat, and Jesus says no."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is sent to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go home, to your own people, and tell them what the Lord has done for you, and how much mercy He had on you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words carry the commission: **what the Lord has done**, and **mercy**. No training, no doctrine, no instruction on what to say beyond his own story."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been an eyewitness for about an hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the man went off and began telling it all through the Decapolis, the ten towns of that region.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was told to go home to his own people. He covered ten cities."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Gentile territory — the region where the pigs were, where the locals had just asked Jesus to leave. The first missionary to the Decapolis is a man with no qualifications except a before and an after."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happened later.** When Jesus returns to that area in Mark 7 and 8, the reception is completely different — crowds come to Him and He feeds four thousand there. Nobody says why. But somebody had been telling that region what the Lord had done for him for the intervening months."}]}, "Mark 5:25–29": {"title": "Twelve years and no help", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "She is in a crowd on the way to somewhere else, and she interrupts the story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is on his way to Jairus's house. A synagogue ruler's daughter is dying and the whole crowd is moving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark puts this inside that journey — one story cut open and another set in the middle of it. He does this deliberately, and the two are connected by a number."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Twelve years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A woman in that crowd who had been **bleeding for twelve years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The girl he is walking toward is twelve years old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For as long as one family has had a daughter, this woman has been bleeding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What her condition meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under Leviticus 15 a woman with a discharge is unclean, and so is anything she sits on and anyone who touches her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve years of that means twelve years without the temple, without a synagogue, and without being touched by anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had tried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She had been through **a great deal at the hands of many doctors**, and she had **spent every last coin she had**, and **none of it had done any good** — she had **only grown worse**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four clauses, and each one is worse than the last. Mark takes his time over it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke — a physician — records this scene and leaves out the line about the doctors."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She had **heard the reports about Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is all she has. No introduction, no invitation, and no conversation with Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How she came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She came up **behind Him** in **the crush of people** and reached out for **His clothes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Behind Him, and at the level of the hem. She is legally unclean in a packed street, and everyone she brushes past becomes unclean too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not ask permission because asking would have ended it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she kept saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If I can only touch what He is wearing**,\" she kept telling herself, \"**I will be well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is imperfect — she **was saying** it, over and over, while pushing through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word she uses is **sozo**: saved, made whole, rescued."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The edge of the garment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers commands a tassel on the four corners of the garment with a blue thread — **tzitzit**. Malachi's promise reads *the Sun of Righteousness shall arise with healing in His wings*, and *wings* is the same word used for those corners."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She reaches for the corner. See the note on Mark 6:53–56, where whole villages do the same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And she touched Him, and **at that very moment** she **felt in her own body** that the bleeding had stopped and **she was healed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately, and she knew it internally before anyone said anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she expected next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing good. When He asks who touched Him she comes *fearing and trembling* and falls down and tells Him the whole truth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What she gets is a word she had not heard in twelve years: *Daughter, your faith has made you well. Go in peace, and be healed of your affliction.* See the note on Mark 5:30–34."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The law she broke.** Under Leviticus her touch made others unclean. In this case the traffic ran the other way — she touched Him, and it was the uncleanness that stopped."}]}, "Mark 5:30–34": {"title": "He stopped a crowd to find her", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman gets what she came for without permission, and Jesus stops everything to make sure she knows she has it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He felt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus knew at once, in Himself, that power had gone out from Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is **dunamis** — power, ability, the word behind our *dynamite*. Mark says Jesus registered it going out, immediately, and turned round in the middle of a crush of people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was on His way to a dying child, with a synagogue ruler beside Him. He stopped anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples' objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look at this crowd pressing in on You from all sides, and You ask who touched You?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are right about the crowd. Hundreds of people were making contact with Him. Their point is that the question is unanswerable — and Mark's point is that one touch was different from all the others."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But He kept looking around to find her.* He would not move on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it mattered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had been bleeding twelve years — which under the law meant twelve years of being ceremonially unclean, unable to touch or be touched without passing it on. She had come up behind Him deliberately, hoping to take the healing and slip away unnoticed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He would not let her leave anonymous. And when she came forward *shaking and afraid*, He gave her the one word that ended the twelve years socially as well as physically:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Daughter.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only time in the Gospels He calls anyone that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your faith has made you well. Go in peace, and be free of this trouble for good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He credits her — not the hem, not the accident of proximity. Her faith. And He sends her away with a blessing rather than a rebuke for taking without asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Go in peace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *go into peace* — **eis eirenen** — which is the standard Greek rendering of the Hebrew farewell *lech leshalom*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shalom** is not the absence of trouble. It is wholeness, everything in its place, nothing missing and nothing broken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had been telling herself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before any of this, pushing through the crowd: *\"If I can only touch what He is wearing, I will be well.\"* Mark's Greek has her saying it repeatedly. See the note on Mark 5:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers with the same word she had been using: **sozo** — saved, made whole."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The interruption cost something.** While He was talking to her, the message came that Jairus' daughter had died. Jesus had stopped for one woman and a child died in the delay — and then He raised her. Mark tells both stories inside each other on purpose."}]}, "Mark 5:38–43": {"title": "\"Talitha koum\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A house full of professional mourners, and Jesus clears them all out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The noise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus heard the noise of it — the weeping and the wailing and all the commotion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Funeral wailing was hired. Even a poor family was expected to provide mourners and flute players, and the volume was the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says, and what they do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The child is not dead. She is asleep.\" And they laughed in His face.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps the laughter in. The people making the noise switch to mockery instantly, which tells you what the grief was worth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is not denying the death — He uses *asleep* the way the New Testament uses it everywhere, of a state that ends. See the note on 1 Thessalonians 4:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is allowed in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He put them all outside.* Then five people: the father, the mother, and Peter, James and John."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parents are in the room. Whatever happens next, they see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Talitha koum\" — which means, \"Little girl, I am speaking to you. Get up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark preserves the Aramaic, which he does only a handful of times. This is what He actually said, in the language He said it in — a household phrase, the sort of thing a parent says in the morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No formula, no gesture. He takes her hand and speaks to her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said they should give her something to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark ends the raising of a dead child with a note about breakfast. The practicality is the tell — the girl is not a vision or a spirit. She is a twelve-year-old who has not eaten."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The detail Mark saves for the end.** *She was twelve years old.* And the woman healed on the way to this house had been bleeding twelve years. Mark deliberately interleaves the two stories, and gives the same number to both. See the note on Mark 5:30–34."}]}, "Mark 6:35–41": {"title": "\"You give them something to eat\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only miracle in all four Gospels, and Mark keeps the details a witness would remember."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples' proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Send them off** so they can go to the farms and villages around here and buy themselves something to eat.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reasonable. It is late, the place is deserted, and five thousand men plus women and children have nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their solution is dispersal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You give them something to eat.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pronoun is emphatic in the Greek — *you*, yourselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He hands the problem back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The costing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Are we supposed to go and spend **two hundred days' wages** on bread?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is two hundred denarii. A denarius was a day's pay for a labourer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight months' wages, and they have done the arithmetic on the spot. Their objection is not lazy; it is accurate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The audit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How many loaves do you have? Go and see.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer the objection. He asks what is in hand and sends them to count."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Five — and two fish.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John adds that it was a boy's lunch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grass"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sit down in groups **on the green grass**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark is the only one who mentions the colour, which places it in spring, after the rains — and Passover was near, as John notes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the kind of detail somebody remembers because they were sitting on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people **settled in, group after group, some of a hundred and some of fifty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the groups is a word used of garden beds laid out in rows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is standing. Before anything is distributed, five thousand men are seated in ordered blocks on the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **took** the five loaves and the two fish, looked up to heaven, **gave thanks**, and **broke** the loaves apart. Then He **kept handing them** to His disciples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Took, gave thanks, broke, gave. The same four, in the same order, as the upper room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The imperfect"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **kept handing them** to His disciples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek tense is continuous. The multiplication is not described as a flash; it happens in His hands, over and over, as fast as twelve men could carry it away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the disciples ended up doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exactly what He told them to. *You give them something to eat* — and they did, all evening, from a supply that did not stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve baskets. One each."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Mark says about it a page later.** *They had not yet grasped what the loaves had meant.* Written about the same men, screaming at a figure on the water, nine hours after this. See the note on Mark 6:45–52."}]}, "Mark 6:45–52": {"title": "\"He was about to go right past them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mark tells the same night as Matthew and keeps two details nobody else has."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He made them go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right away He **had His disciples get into the boat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is compelled — He made them. They are on that water because He put them there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went up the mountainside **to pray**. Night came on. The boat was out in the middle of the lake, and **Jesus was alone on the shore**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark separates them deliberately: the boat in the middle, and Him alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **could see that they were straining hard at the oars**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From a mountainside, at night, on a lake several miles across."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever Mark means by it, he says Jesus was watching the whole time. They could not see Him; He could see them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *straining* is being tormented, harassed — the word for being tortured."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the wind was blowing straight against them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Contrary. They had been rowing since evening and were about halfway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At about **the fourth watch of the night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three to six in the morning. Nine hours or so of rowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He comes at the end of the night, not the beginning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He was about to go right past them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Mark has this, and it is the strangest sentence in the passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not going to get into the boat. He was going to pass by."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have long connected it to Exodus 33, where God tells Moses *I will make all My goodness **pass before you***, and to Elijah at Horeb, where *the LORD passed by*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are occasions when God shows Himself by going past somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What made Him stop"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when they saw Him… they thought He was a ghost, and **they screamed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops for the scream."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passing-by would have been a revelation. The screaming turned it into a rescue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take heart. It is I. Do not be afraid.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle two words are **ego eimi** — the ordinary *it's me*, and also the name from the bush."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were utterly stunned. **They had not yet grasped what the loaves had meant.** Their hearts were still slow and closed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's own comment, and it is severe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched five thousand men fed from five loaves that same afternoon and collected twelve baskets of leftovers. See the note on Mark 6:35–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says the storm should not have surprised them, because of the bread."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Matthew adds and Mark leaves out.** Peter walking on the water and sinking. Mark's Gospel is traditionally associated with Peter's preaching, and it is the one account that does not include the part where Peter tried."}]}, "Mark 6:53–56": {"title": "The edge of His clothes", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A summary passage, four verses long, and Mark packs a whole region into it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they land"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They crossed over and came to land at **Gennesaret** and tied up the boat there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had spent the night on the lake in a storm, and Jesus had walked out to them on the water. See the note on Mark 6:45–52."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the morning after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Recognised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the moment they stepped out, people recognized Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No announcement and no preaching. He is identified getting off a boat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the region did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **ran all through the countryside** and began **carrying the sick to Him on their mats**, wherever they heard He was.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whole villages mobilised. People are carried on the pallets they were lying on, over open country, to intercept a man who kept moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Every place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every **village**, every **town**, every **farm** He came to.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark lists three sizes of settlement to say the same thing three ways: everywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they put them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **laid the sick out in the marketplaces**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The market was the one place in a village everyone passed through. They put the sick in the road He would have to walk down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **begged Him to let them touch even the edge of His clothes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kraspedon** — the tassel on the corner of the garment, the **tzitzit** commanded in Numbers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are asking for the least possible contact: not His hand, not a word, not a visit to the house. The hem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they got the idea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters earlier a woman had done exactly this in a crowd and been healed, and Jesus had stopped the procession to find out who it was and tell her *your faith has made you well.* See the note on Mark 5:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had been meant to stay anonymous. Because He would not let her, a whole district now knows what to reach for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **everyone who touched Him was made well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **esozonto** — imperfect, continuous: they *were being* made whole, one after another, as long as it went on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records no exceptions and no failures in this passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast a page earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In his own town, *He could do no mighty work there, except that He laid His hands on a few sick people and healed them. And He marvelled because of their unbelief.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same chapter. Nazareth got a few; Gennesaret got everyone who touched Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had just done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fed five thousand men with five loaves, sent the disciples ahead, prayed alone on a mountain, walked on the sea, and now this. Mark 6 has no gaps in it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was on offer.** Not a service, not a queue, not a set of conditions. A man walking through a marketplace with people lying along the edges of it, reaching for a tassel."}]}, "Mark 6:7–13": {"title": "Sent out with a stick", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twelve men are sent out with an inventory of what they may not take."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two by two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Began sending them out **in pairs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody goes alone. The law required two witnesses, and the practice runs right through Acts — Peter and John, Paul and Barnabas, Barnabas and Mark, Paul and Silas."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He gave them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave them **authority over unclean spirits**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole equipment list on the credit side. One item, and it is not material."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they may take"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take **nothing for the road but a walking stick** — no bread, no traveling bag, no coins tucked in your belt. **Wear your sandals**, and **do not carry a second shirt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A stick and the sandals on their feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things forbidden: food, luggage, money, and a change of clothes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is what a sensible traveller packs. The instruction removes any possibility of self-sufficiency for the length of the journey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stick and the sandals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are allowed to keep what they are already holding and already wearing. Nothing is to be acquired for the trip."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whatever house you go into, **stay there until you leave that town**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No moving to better accommodation. Whoever takes you in first, you stay with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It rules out working up through a village's households, and it puts them at the mercy of the first person who says yes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dust"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If there are any who will not welcome you… **shake the dust off your feet** as a witness against them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jewish traveller shook off Gentile dust on re-entering the land. The gesture said: your ground is not ours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are told to do it to Jewish towns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they preached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They went out and **called people to change their minds and turn to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metanoeo** — to think differently afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **drove out many demons**, and they **rubbed oil on many who were sick and healed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only place in the Gospels where the disciples use oil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **aleipho** — the everyday word for rubbing oil on, as you would after a bath or onto a wound. Not the ceremonial anointing word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James gives the same instruction to elders: *let them pray over him and put oil on him in the name of the Lord.* Same verb. See the note on James 5:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened when they got back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The apostles gathered to Jesus and told Him all things, both what they had done and what they had taught.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And His response: *come aside by yourselves to a deserted place and rest a while* — *for there were many coming and going, and they did not even have time to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The debrief is a rest."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He said about the sending later.** *\"When I sent you without money bag, knapsack and sandals, **did you lack anything?**\" So they said, \"Nothing.\"* Luke 22, on the last night, and He asks them to check their own record."}]}, "Mark 7:24–30": {"title": "\"The scraps the children drop\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mark's version of the Syrophoenician woman, and he keeps details Matthew leaves out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went into a house and **did not want anyone to know He was there, but there was no hiding Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Mark records this. He had crossed the border into Gentile territory to get away from the crowds, and it did not work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **Greek, born in Syrian Phoenicia**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark identifies her by culture and birthplace. Matthew calls her a Canaanite — the old covenant word, the nation Israel was told to drive out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between them: a pagan by religion, a Greek by culture, a Canaanite by ancestry. Every category that excludes her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **came and fell at His feet**… and **pleaded** with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *pleaded* is imperfect — she kept on asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let the children at the table be **fed first**. It is not right to take **the children's bread** and throw it to the little dogs.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone keeps **first**. It is a word about order, not exclusion — and she hears it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Little dogs** — the Greek **kunarion** is a diminutive: the household pets, not the scavengers in the street. It is a word from inside a house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yes, Lord** — and even **the little dogs under the table** eat the scraps the children drop.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She concedes everything. She does not argue with the order, the priority, or the term."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And she picks up the household picture and finishes it: *under the table.* If the dogs are indoors, then she is talking about a place at the family's feet, and food that is already falling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For an answer like that**, you may go home.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *because of this word* — because of what you said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's version credits the reply. Matthew's credits the faith: *O woman, great is your faith.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The healing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The spirit has left your daughter.**\" And she went back and found her child lying on the bed, and the spirit was gone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense before she moves. It was done while she was still standing there, and the child was miles away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before this in Mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole first half of chapter 7 is the argument about clean and unclean — washing hands, traditions, and Jesus' verdict: *there is nothing that enters a man from outside which can defile him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds his own note: *thus He declared all foods clean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, in the very next paragraph, He crosses into unclean territory and heals an unclean woman's daughter of an unclean spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark puts the sermon and the demonstration side by side."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does next.** Goes further into Gentile country, to the Decapolis, and heals a deaf man with an impediment — *Ephphatha, be opened.* Then feeds four thousand people there. The bread the children could not finish is being handed out across the border within two chapters."}]}, "Mark 8:1–10": {"title": "The second feeding, and the question they should not have asked", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four thousand people, seven loaves, and disciples who have already watched this happen once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What starts it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have compassion for the crowd, because they have already been with Me three days and have nothing to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He raises it, not them. And the reason given is the length of time they have stayed — three days without provisions, some of them a long way from home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I send them home hungry, they will collapse on the way.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where could anyone get enough bread to feed these people out here in this lonely place?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been present at the five thousand. Mark has told that story two chapters earlier, with twelve baskets left over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He records the question without comment, which is more damning than an explanation would be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The differences from the first feeding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps the numbers distinct, and they are not the same story told twice:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| | five thousand | four thousand | |---|---|---| | loaves | five | seven | | baskets left | twelve, *kophinoi* | seven, *spyrides* | | location | Jewish side of the lake | Gentile region, the Decapolis |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even the word for basket changes. The second is the large hamper — the kind Paul was later lowered over a wall in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the Gentile setting matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the region where the man freed from the legion had been telling people what the Lord had done for him. See the note on Mark 5:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd that stays three days to listen is in the country that once asked Jesus to leave."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jesus says about it later.** In the boat He asks them: when I broke five loaves for five thousand, how many baskets? Twelve. And the seven for four thousand? Seven. *Do you still not understand?* He treats the two feedings as a single lesson they had failed twice."}]}, "Mark 8:34–38": {"title": "\"What could a man possibly offer in exchange for his soul?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus calls the crowd over — not just the disciples — and says the hardest thing in Mark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just confessed Him as the Messiah, and then rebuked Him for saying He would be killed. Jesus has answered that sharply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then He calls the crowd, so that what follows is said to everybody rather than to the twelve."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Pick up his cross\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image was not yet a metaphor. Crucifixion was a Roman execution his hearers had seen, and a condemned man carried the crossbeam to the place through public streets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone in that crowd knew what a man carrying a cross was: someone already sentenced, walking to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever tries to hold on to his life will lose it, and whoever gives up his life for My sake and for the sake of the good news will save it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *life* here is **psyche** — life, self, soul. The same word runs through the next two sentences, which is why they belong together:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What good does it do a man to gain the whole world and lose his own soul? What could a man possibly offer in exchange for his soul?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commercial language"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gain*, *lose*, *exchange* — the vocabulary is a merchant's, and the question is about price."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second question is the sharper one. Suppose the trade has already been made and the world is in hand: what is left to buy the soul back with? The answer the question expects is nothing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whose loss is being described.** *For My sake and for the sake of the good news.* Jesus does not commend self-denial in general — Mark's Gospel is not asking for austerity. The letting go is for a person, and He names Himself."}]}, "Mark 9:14–24": {"title": "\"Help me with the part of me that does not\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most honest sentence anyone says in the Gospels, and Jesus answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father's careful wording"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If there is anything You can do, have pity on us and help us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been living with this for years — *since he was small* — and he has just watched the disciples fail. The *if* is not rudeness. It is a man who has stopped expecting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice also *help **us***. He is not only asking for the boy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with the \"if\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you can believe — everything is possible for the one who believes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He picks up the father's own word and hands it back. The man had put the *if* on Jesus' ability; Jesus moves it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is worth reading carefully, because it can be made cruel. Jesus is not saying the healing depends on the father generating enough certainty — the next thirty seconds rule that out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, I do believe. Help me with the part of me that does not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves at once, and the man does not pretend. He has faith and he has unbelief, and he says so, and he asks for help with the second."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus heals the boy immediately. No further requirement, no waiting for the doubt to clear."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What that settles.** Whatever \"believing\" means in this passage, it evidently includes a man who admits mid-sentence that he does not fully believe. The faith that works here is not a quantity achieved. It is a request made to the right person, doubt and all."}]}, "Matthew 1:12–16": {"title": "The one link in the chain that is worded differently", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-nine times in this chapter Matthew writes the same formula. On the fortieth he changes it, and the change is the point of the whole genealogy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel. Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Down through the list, one word doing the work each time — the ordinary verb for begetting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The break"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*Jacob was the father of Joseph, **the husband of Mary**, and it was from Mary that Jesus was born, the One who is called the Messiah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph is not said to be the father of Jesus. He is identified by his marriage, and the birth is attributed to Mary. In Greek the relative pronoun before *from whom* is feminine singular — it can only refer to her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has just spent forty-one verses establishing a legal line and then, at the last link, declines to say the thing the whole form was built to say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the legal line still matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus needed a claim to David's throne, and in that culture it came through the legal father. Joseph, descended from David through Solomon and the kings, gives Him that claim by adoption."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What Joseph does not give Him is blood — which is exactly as well, because that line runs through Jeconiah, and Jeremiah had declared that none of Jeconiah's descendants would ever sit on David's throne. See the note on Jeremiah 22:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title comes down the legal line. The life does not."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who else is in the list.** Four women are named before Mary, and Matthew did not have to name any of them: Tamar, who posed as a prostitute; Rahab, who was one; Ruth, a Moabite; and *the wife of Uriah*, whom he pointedly does not call Bathsheba. A genealogy designed to impress would have left all four out. This one puts them in."}]}, "Matthew 10:16–20": {"title": "Snakes and doves", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two animals, chosen for one quality each, and neither is chosen for the obvious reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pairing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be as wise as snakes and as harmless as doves.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the snake** — not for cunning in the sinister sense. The word is *phronimos*, shrewd, practically intelligent, the same word Jesus uses of the wise man who built on rock and the wise servant left in charge.", "**the dove** — the word is *akeraios*, literally *unmixed*. Pure in the sense of nothing added, no hidden second motive."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the instruction is: think clearly about what you are walking into, and have nothing in you that needs concealing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either quality without the other goes wrong quickly. Shrewd without unmixed is just cunning; unmixed without shrewd is walking into a wolf pack blindfolded — which is what He has just said He is sending them into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look at what I am doing: I am sending you out like sheep into the middle of wolves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the danger before He gives the instruction, and He does not pretend the odds are good. Sheep among wolves is not a fair fight, and nothing in the sentence suggests otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He promises instead of safety"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not fret about how to speak or what to say. What you are to say will be given to you at that hour, because it is not you speaking; it is the Spirit of your Father speaking through you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The provision is not protection from the councils and the floggings — He says plainly those are coming. It is words, supplied at the hour they are needed, by Someone else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The turn in the middle.** *That will be your chance to testify.* Being dragged in front of governors and kings is described as an opportunity rather than a disaster. The whole passage assumes the trouble and then repurposes it."}]}, "Matthew 11:1–6": {"title": "John's question from prison", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The man who identified Him sends to ask whether he got it right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **John, sitting in prison**, heard about the things the Messiah was doing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is John the Baptist. He had seen the Spirit descend and remain, and had said *behold, the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world.* He had refused to baptise Him at first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is in Herod's fortress at Machaerus, and he will be beheaded there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Are You the One who was coming, or should we keep watching for someone else?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not asking a general question about the Messiah. He is asking about the man he pointed at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records it without any embarrassment and without any explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he might ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had preached a Messiah with an axe and a winnowing fan: *the axe is laid to the root of the trees… He will thoroughly clean out His threshing floor, and burn up the chaff with unquenchable fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he is hearing about instead is healings and preaching in villages, and no axe anywhere — and he is in a cell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go back and tell John what you are hearing and seeing.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a rebuke, not a reassurance, and not a direct yes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Evidence, and John is left to draw the conclusion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*blind people are seeing*", "*crippled people are walking*", "*lepers are being made clean*", "*deaf people are hearing*", "*dead people are being raised*", "*and **the poor are having the good news brought to them***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a quotation. Isaiah 35 and Isaiah 61, stitched together — the passages that describe what happens when God comes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers a question about His identity by listing what is going on and letting the prophets do the identifying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not on the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The axe. The fire. The threshing floor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And one more thing: Isaiah 61 says *to proclaim liberty to the captives, and the opening of the prison to those who are bound* — and Jesus had read that scroll aloud in Nazareth. He leaves that clause out of the message He sends to a man in prison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is not getting out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **blessed is the one who does not stumble over Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skandalizo** — to be tripped up, to take offence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a beatitude, and it is addressed to a man whose expectations were not being met and who was going to die in a cell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says about him afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The moment the messengers leave, He turns to the crowd:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?… Among those born of women there has not risen one greater than John the Baptist.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one word of criticism, said behind his back, about a man who had just asked whether He was real."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for the doubt.** John does not stop believing in God. He asks a question, in the dark, of the person he is asking about — and gets an answer, and a character reference he never heard."}]}, "Matthew 11:12–15": {"title": "\"Right up to John\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus places John at a boundary and says which side of it everything falls on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The occasion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is in Herod's prison and has sent two disciples to ask *are You the Coming One, or do we look for another?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers by listing what is happening — the blind see, the lame walk, the poor have the gospel preached to them — and then turns to the crowd and speaks about John."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pressing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"From the days of John the Baptist until this very hour, **the kingdom of heaven has been advancing under force, and forceful people are seizing hold of it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **biazetai** and **biastai** — words about violence and force. They can be read as the kingdom suffering violence or as it forcefully advancing; the grammar allows both, and readers have divided over it since the beginning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not in doubt is the timing: **from the days of John**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The boundary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**All the Prophets and the Law spoke their prophecies right up to John**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *prophesied until John*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version is blunter: *the law and the prophets were until John. **Since that time the kingdom of God has been preached**, and everyone is pressing into it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both put John at the join."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that makes John"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last man on one side of the line. Jesus says it two verses earlier: *among those born of women there has not risen one greater than John the Baptist* — and then, immediately, *but he who is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greatest of the old order, and smaller than the least of the new."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **if you are willing to receive it, he is the Elijah who was to come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Malachi's last words before four hundred years of silence: *behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you are willing to receive it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus attaches a condition to His own identification."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John had denied it himself when the priests asked: *\"Are you Elijah?\" He said, \"I am not.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel had told Zacharias he would go *in the spirit and power of Elijah*, which is the resolution — not the man, the office."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The closing formula"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever has ears, let him listen**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it after the parables and Revelation uses it to each of the seven churches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It marks something that will not be understood by everyone hearing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Elijah stands with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the mountain, eight days after the first announcement of the cross, Moses and Elijah appear and talk about the departure. See the note on Luke 9:28–36."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law and the prophets, in person, discussing the thing that ends their era."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when the cloud lifts, *Jesus was there alone*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the line is drawn.** *The Law and the Prophets… right up to John.* Everything before is one thing, and Jesus says a man alive in the same generation was standing at the end of it."}]}, "Matthew 11:20–24": {"title": "The towns that saw the most", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus names three towns and says they are worse off than the cities everyone in His audience would have named as the worst."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Chorazin and Bethsaida** measured against **Tyre and Sidon** — the Phoenician cities the prophets denounced for centuries", "**Capernaum** measured against **Sodom**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in each case the verdict runs the same way: those places would have turned if they had seen what these towns saw."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Capernaum had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was His base of operations. Peter's house was there, and the synagogue. Matthew was called at its tax booth. The centurion's servant, Peter's mother-in-law, Jairus' daughter — the concentration of miracles in that one town is remarkable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were lifted up to the sky* is a quotation aimed originally at the king of Babylon in Isaiah 14, and the second half of the sentence comes with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had not done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because they had not changed their minds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because they attacked Him, and not because they drove Him out — Nazareth did that and is not on this list. The charge is that nothing changed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They watched, and stayed the same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "More evidence means more responsibility. Jesus says it elsewhere in plainer terms — to whom much is given, much will be required."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That cuts toward anyone with easy access to Scripture, sermons, and a Bible on their phone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What surrounds this passage.** Immediately before, John the Baptist's question and Jesus' defence of him. Immediately after, *come to Me, all of you who are worn out* — the gentlest invitation in the Gospels. The hardest words Matthew records and the kindest are four verses apart, spoken in the same breath. See the note on Matthew 11:28–30."}]}, "Matthew 11:28–30": {"title": "Whose yoke it is, and what \"easy\" actually says", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted invitation in the Gospels, and the picture in the middle of it is the part most likely to be missed by a reader who has never worked an animal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "A yoke is for two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **zygos** is the wooden beam across the necks of a pair of oxen. A single ox does not wear one. The whole point of the device is that two animals pull together and the load is shared between them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So *take My yoke on your shoulders* is not an offer of a lighter burden to carry alone. It is an offer to be paired with Him. That is why the sentence can hold two things that sound contradictory — come to Me and rest, and take up a yoke — without any strain. The rest is not the absence of work. It is the end of pulling alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"My yoke\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The emphasis in the Greek falls on the possessive, and it matters, because everybody He was speaking to already had a yoke on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rabbis of the period spoke of the *yoke of the law* — the teaching a disciple took on from his teacher. Jesus is not asking people to pick up a burden they had been free of. He is telling them to swap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is also why the next line is *learn from Me*. It is the language of apprenticeship, and the reason He gives for coming is not that He is powerful: *because I am gentle, and I am humble in heart*. He recommends Himself on His manner, which is not how anyone recruits disciples."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word behind \"fits easily\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chrestos.** It means kind, good, serviceable — and it was used of things that were well made, and of wine that had mellowed. In the Greek Old Testament it is the word in *taste and see that the LORD is good*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Translating it as *easy* has done some damage, because it sounds like a promise that nothing will be demanding. The word is nearer to *this one fits*. A badly made yoke rubs an animal raw over a day's work. A well-made one is shaped to the neck wearing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim is not that there is no work. It is that this yoke will not take the skin off you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is invited"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of you who are worn out and carrying too much.* The two words describe being exhausted by labour and being loaded up by someone else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no other entry condition in the sentence. Not repentance first, not understanding first. Tiredness is the qualification, which is a strange basis for an invitation and is exactly what makes this one memorable."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where else this word turns up.** *Rest* here is the same idea running through Hebrews 3 and 4 — a rest already prepared, entered by coming rather than by achieving. Matthew's version puts a person and a yoke at the centre of it: the rest is not a place so much as a partnership."}]}, "Matthew 12:15–21": {"title": "The bruised reed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees have just decided to destroy Him. Matthew's next sentence is what Jesus does about it, and then the longest Old Testament quotation in his Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He withdrew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing this, Jesus **withdrew** from that place. Large crowds followed Him, and **He healed all of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not confront the plot and He does not stop healing. He moves, and the healing goes with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of them* — Matthew's habit. He does not report a percentage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the quotation is here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew reaches for Isaiah 42 to explain a man walking away from a fight, and the prophecy he chooses is about a servant who does not raise His voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servant does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will not argue or shout. No one will hear His voice raised in the streets.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three negatives before a single positive. The portrait is drawn by what is absent from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reed and the wick"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **bruised reed** He will not break, and a **smoldering wick** He will not put out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A reed grew by the Jordan and was used for measuring, for writing, and cut into a simple pipe. Bruised, it is worthless — you throw it away and cut another, because reeds cost nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wick with no flame left is smoke and smell. You pinch it out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are of something that has stopped doing the one thing it exists for, and is worth less than the effort of keeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is discarded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Until"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…**until He carries justice through to victory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gentleness has a destination. It is not resignation or a mild temperament; it is restraint held on purpose, and the sentence says where it is going."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *justice* in Isaiah is **mishpat** — the right ordering of things, what ought to be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in His name the nations will place their hope.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew, writing to Jewish readers, ends the quotation on the Gentiles — placed here, immediately after Israel's teachers have resolved to kill Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why Matthew put it here.** The chapter's argument is about the Sabbath and a withered hand. A man whose hand had stopped working was, by any practical measure, a bruised reed. He was not broken; he was told to stretch it out. See the note on Matthew 12:9–14."}]}, "Matthew 12:33–37": {"title": "\"The mouth speaks out of whatever fills the heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus explains where words come from, and it is not the mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Make the tree good and its fruit will be good**… because **a tree is known by its fruit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not fix fruit. You cannot improve an apple by working on the apple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fruit is diagnostic — it tells you what the tree is — and the remedy in the sentence is applied to the tree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The mouth speaks out of whatever fills the heart.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *out of the abundance of the heart* — out of the overflow, what is in excess."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Speech is described as spillage. What comes out under pressure, without editing, is simply the level of what was already in there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The storehouse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"A good person brings good things out of **the good stored up inside**, and an evil person brings evil things out of **the evil stored up inside**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thesauros** — a treasury, a storehouse, the word behind *treasure*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both people are described as bringing out. Nobody manufactures on the spot; they withdraw from a store, and the store was filled earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which is why guarding it matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Above all else, guard your heart, for from it flow the springs of life.* Proverbs puts the heart first and everything else downstream — mouth, eyes, feet, in that order. See the note on Proverbs 4:20–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon's word for *guard* is a sentry's word. It assumes something is trying to get in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Every careless word\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"On the day of judgment people will give an account for **every careless word** they have spoken.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **argos** — idle, inactive, unemployed. Not wicked words. Words that were doing no work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say they carry no weight. He says they will be accounted for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By your words you will be declared right, and by your words you will be condemned.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb in the first half is the justification word Paul uses all through Romans."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Words are treated here not as a report on the heart but as evidence in a hearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He is saying it to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees, who had just said He cast out demons by Beelzebub. He calls them a *brood of snakes* in the middle of this passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched a blind and mute man healed and produced that sentence about it — which is exactly his point. Their words came out of a store, and the store is what he is naming."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other place this principle runs.** Ten men looked at Canaan and said *we are not able*, and two looked at the same country and said *we are well able*. Same forty days, same giants. And God's answer to the first group was *I will do to you the very things you spoke in My hearing.* See the notes on Numbers 13:26–33 and Numbers 14:26–35."}]}, "Matthew 12:43–45": {"title": "\"Empty, swept out and neatly arranged\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short, strange parable about a house, and the problem with the house is not that it is dirty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When it arrives it finds the place **empty, swept out and neatly arranged**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them sounds like an improvement. The house is clean. Nothing is out of place. Somebody has clearly done work on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And that is exactly the condition in which it gets taken over. The trouble is the first word: **empty**. Nobody moved in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wandering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It wanders through waterless places looking for rest and finds none.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail is offered without explanation and Matthew does not develop it. What the parable does say is that the spirit's preference is to return to what it knows: *I will go back to the house I left.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Reform is not the same as occupancy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument. A life can be tidied — habits stopped, behaviour corrected, the place genuinely swept — and still be unoccupied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable's ending is blunt: *that person ends up worse off than before.* A cleaned house with nobody in it is more vulnerable than a lived-in one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Jesus was talking about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is exactly how it will be for this evil generation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He applies it to a generation, not to an individual. The context is a demand for a sign from scribes and Pharisees who had just accused Him of casting out demons by Beelzebul — religious people, morally scrupulous, whose reform had never included receiving Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The alternative in the same chapter.** *Whoever does the will of My Father in heaven is My brother and sister and mother.* Jesus says it moments before this, pointing at His disciples. The empty house and the family are the two options Matthew 12 puts side by side."}]}, "Matthew 12:9–14": {"title": "Stretch out your hand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man with a withered hand, a room full of men watching to see what Jesus will do, and an instruction to do the one thing he cannot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were there for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Looking for something to accuse Him of, they asked Him, \"Is it permitted to heal on the Sabbath?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew tells us the motive before he tells us the question. Nobody in the room is asking about the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sheep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Suppose one of you has a single sheep, and it falls into a pit on the Sabbath. Will you not take hold of it and lift it out?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers with their own practice. Not a theory about the Sabbath — what they would in fact do, that same day, for one animal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And a human being is worth so much more than a sheep.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is from the lesser to the greater, and the whole weight of it sits on the value of the man standing in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Stretch out your hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hand is withered. Stretching it out is exactly what it cannot do — that is the definition of the condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is offered first. No touch is recorded, no word over the hand. He is told to do the impossible thing, and Matthew's next clause is *He stretched it out.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As sound as the other one\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The restoration is measured against something. There was a good hand on the other arm the whole time, and the healed one comes back matching it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the healing produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the Pharisees went out and began making plans against Him, to find a way to destroy Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew puts the plotting immediately after the healing, without a gap. The first recorded plan to kill Jesus in this Gospel follows a restored hand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He said before the healing.** *So yes, it is permitted to do good on the Sabbath.* He does not argue that the Sabbath is suspended. He rules on what it was for."}]}, "Matthew 13:1–9": {"title": "The sower goes out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first parable Matthew records at length, and the one Jesus says the others depend on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He sat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus went out of the house and **sat down by the sea**… He **stepped into a boat** and sat there, while all the people stood on the shore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A boat as a pulpit, and the water carrying the voice up the slope of the shore. It is an acoustic arrangement as much as a practical one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records the same use of Simon's boat in chapter 5, and it ends with a net tearing. See the note on Luke 5:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What varies, and what does not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sower. One kind of seed. Four soils."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the parable suggests the seed differed, that some was thrown carelessly, or that the sower ran out. The variable is entirely the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the path** — the seed never gets in; birds take it", "**rocky ground** — it springs up *quickly* and dies *quickly*, having no root", "**among thorns** — it grows and is choked", "**good soil** — a harvest, *a hundred times what was sown, or sixty, or thirty*"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second soil is the fast one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It **sprang up quickly**, because the soil was shallow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shallowness is why it was fast. Rock a few inches down warms early and the shoot comes up before anything else in the field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The quickest response in the parable is the one with no root."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What killed it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **the sun climbed high** it was scorched.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sun is not the villain — it is the same sun the good soil needed. Heat exposes what the root system is, and does not create the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thorns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They *grew up and choked it out.* Jesus explains them later as *the cares of this world and the deceitfulness of riches.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing dramatic. Nothing hostile. The plant is not attacked; it is crowded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three numbers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A hundred times what was sown, or sixty, or thirty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even the good soil varies, and thirtyfold is still an extraordinary return by ancient farming standards — sevenfold to tenfold was a decent harvest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this parable matters most"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records what He said about it: *do you not understand this parable? **How then will you understand all the parables?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the key one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The closing line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever has ears, let them listen.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses on, the instruction about how: *be careful how you listen. The measure you use will be the measure used for you.* See the note on Mark 4:24–25."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He was doing that day.** Matthew says *that same day* — the day the Pharisees had accused Him of casting out demons by Beelzebub, and His mother and brothers came asking for Him. He walks out of that house, sits in a boat, and starts talking about soil."}]}, "Matthew 13:18–23": {"title": "The four kinds of soil", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version of the explanation, and one word in it is not in Mark's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first soil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When anyone **hears the message of the kingdom and it does not take hold in him**, the evil one comes and **snatches away what was sown in his heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *does not take hold* is **me sunientos** — not understanding, not putting it together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says only that Satan comes and takes it. Matthew names the gap the theft goes through. See the note on Mark 4:13–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sown in his heart** — Matthew locates the seed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who **hears the word and welcomes it right away with joy**, but **it has no root in him and does not last**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proskairos** — temporary, for a season."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As soon as **trouble or persecution comes because of the word**, he falls away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Because of the word.** The pressure is named as arriving on account of the thing sown, not despite it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who hears the word, but **the worries of this life and the false promises of wealth crowd in and choke it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *false promises* is **apate** — deceitfulness. Riches are not called evil; they are called deceptive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things do the choking here. Mark has three; Luke has *pleasures of life* as well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **it never comes to anything**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *it becomes unfruitful*. The plant is alive and produces nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who **hears the word and takes it in and understands it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs where the others have one or two. Matthew's good soil is defined by understanding — the same word that was missing from the first soil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The explanation is framed by it: **sunientos** at the start, **sunieis** at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The yields"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **hundred times** what was sown, or **sixty**, or **thirty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew runs them downward; Mark runs them up. Both keep the range."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The good soil is not uniform, and all three figures are called good."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before the explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Therefore I speak to them in parables, because seeing they do not see, and hearing they do not hear, nor do they understand**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the quotation from Isaiah 6, given in full — *lest they should understand with their hearts and turn, so that I should heal them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says to the disciples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **blessed are your eyes for they see, and your ears for they hear**; for assuredly I say to you that **many prophets and righteous men desired to see what you see, and did not see it**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven parables in one chapter — the sower, the wheat and tares, the mustard seed, the leaven, the hidden treasure, the pearl, and the dragnet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew notes: *all these things Jesus spoke to the multitude in parables; and without a parable He did not speak to them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one word Matthew adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Understanding, at both ends of the explanation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first soil loses the seed because it is not understood. The fourth bears because it is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Matthew supplies that Mark does not.** The gap the birds come through. He names it at the top of the explanation and again at the bottom, and it is the same word both times."}]}, "Matthew 14:13–14": {"title": "His heart went out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses between a murder and a miracle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus **heard it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: Herod's birthday, the dancing girl, the request put up to her by her mother, and John the Baptist beheaded in prison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And his disciples came and took away the body and buried it, **and went and told Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what He heard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who John was to Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His cousin. The man who baptised Him and said *behold the Lamb of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said of him: *among those born of women there has not risen one greater than John the Baptist.* See the note on Matthew 11:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **withdrew from that place by boat to a quiet spot, to be alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anechoresen** — withdrew, retired. And **kat' idian**, privately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes looking for a deserted place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the crowds heard where He was going and followed Him on foot from the towns**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They walk round the lake and get there first. Mark says *they ran there on foot from all the cities, arrived before them, and came together to Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He lands and the place is full."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He came ashore and **saw the great crowd waiting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *coming out, He saw a great multitude*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What moved"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His **heart went out to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **esplanchnisthe** — from **splanchna**, the inward parts. The gut-word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb Matthew uses of Him looking at the crowds in chapter 9. See the note on Matthew 9:35–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He healed their sick**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any note that this was difficult, that He was owed the afternoon, or that the crowd was inconsiderate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records the news, the withdrawal, the crowd arriving first, and the compassion, in that order, and offers no comment on the sequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When it was evening, His disciples came to Him, saying, \"This is a deserted place, and the hour is already late. **Send the multitudes away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jesus said to them, \"**They do not need to go away. You give them something to eat**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five loaves, two fish, five thousand men besides women and children, twelve baskets over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only miracle in all four Gospels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And after that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately Jesus made His disciples get into the boat and go before Him to the other side, while He sent the multitudes away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had sent the multitudes away, **He went up on the mountain by Himself to pray**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets the solitude at the end of the day, after everyone has been fed — and then walks out on the water to the boat."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the paragraph.** He hears his cousin has been executed, gets in a boat to be alone, finds the shore crowded when he lands, and the word Matthew uses for what happened next is the one for being moved in the gut."}]}, "Matthew 14:22–27": {"title": "The fourth watch", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He sends them into the storm, and then comes to them at the worst hour of the night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who put them there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right away Jesus **had the disciples get into the boat** and go on ahead of Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is stronger than *had* — He compelled them, made them get in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are on that lake because He put them there. It is not a wrong turn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He climbed the mountainside **by Himself to pray**. Evening fell, and He was there alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the evening after the feeding of the five thousand. He dismisses the crowd, sends the disciples off, and goes up alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The boat was already out in the middle of the sea, **thrown about by the waves, for the wind was against it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that the wind was contrary — against them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds that He *saw them straining at rowing*, from the mountain, in the dark. They could not see Him; He could see them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth watch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the fourth watch of the night** Jesus came toward them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Romans divided the night into four watches. The fourth runs roughly from three until six in the morning — the last stretch, the coldest, the darkest before it lightens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been rowing since evening. He comes at the end of it, not the beginning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What frightens them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the disciples saw Him walking on the water **they were terrified**. \"**It is a ghost!**\" they said, and they cried out in fear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The storm had not made them scream. He did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing that had come to rescue them was the most frightening thing that happened that night, because it did not arrive in a shape they recognised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take heart. It is I. Do not be afraid.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three short sentences, and the middle one is two words in Greek: **ego eimi** — *I am*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the ordinary way of saying *it's me*. It is also the name from the burning bush, and John's Gospel builds seven sayings on it. See the note on Exodus 3:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was going to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records something Matthew leaves out: *He came toward them, walking on the sea, **and would have passed them by**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever that means, He was not going to force Himself into the boat. They *cried out* — and then He spoke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter asks to come, walks, looks at the wind, sinks, and *immediately Jesus stretched out His hand and caught him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *when they got into the boat, the wind ceased.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where He had just been.** Praying, alone, on a mountain, all night — and the chapter opens with Him hearing that John the Baptist had been beheaded, and trying to withdraw to a deserted place before the crowds followed Him."}]}, "Matthew 14:28–33": {"title": "A hand reaching out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Only Matthew records this, and every detail in it is worth its own line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **if it is You**, tell me to come to You on the water.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for a command rather than permission. *Tell me to come.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else Peter is doing, he is not proposing to try it on his own initiative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Peter **climbed down out of the boat, walked on the water**, and started toward Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only person besides Jesus recorded as doing it, and the fact usually gets lost behind what happened next."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven men stayed in the boat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What changed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when he **felt the force of the wind** he was afraid.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wind had been blowing the whole time. It did not get worse when he stepped out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The variable is what he was attending to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sinking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **beginning to sink** he cried out, \"**Lord, save me!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words. It is one of the shortest prayers in the Bible, and it works."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not swim for the boat. He calls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately Jesus **reached out His hand and caught hold of him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *immediately*, and the verb is to take hold of firmly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not let him go under first to make a point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You of little faith, why did you doubt?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Oligopistos** — Matthew's word, used five times, always to disciples."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Greek for *doubt* is **distazo**, to be pulled two ways, to waver between."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said while holding him up out of the water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the faith failed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the asking, and not in the stepping out. He walked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is asked of a man who is being lifted back on to the surface, about the part in the middle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when **the two of them climbed into the boat, the wind died away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew notes that they got in together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the boat did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the men in the boat **worshipped Him** and said, \"**Truly You are the Son of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first time in Matthew that the disciples say it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched five thousand fed that afternoon and concluded nothing. A man sinking and being caught produced the confession."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Peter got wrong and what he got right.** He asked, he got out, he looked at the weather, and he sank. Of the twelve men on that lake, he is the only one who was ever on the water — and the only one who ever needed to be pulled out of it."}]}, "Matthew 15:21–28": {"title": "\"Even the little dogs eat the crumbs\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest conversation Jesus has with anyone who came to Him for help, and it ends with the highest compliment He pays anybody in Matthew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus went out from there and departed to the region of **Tyre and Sidon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the far north-west coast — the border of **Asher**, whose whole blessing in Genesis 49 is one line about food:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Asher's food will be rich; he will provide **delicacies fit for a king**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only recorded time He left Israel's territory, and the conversation He has there is about bread and a table. See the note on Genesis 49:20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three obstacles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She meets a wall three times:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*He did not answer her a word*", "*I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel*", "*It is not right to take the children's bread and toss it to the little dogs*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Silence, then a limit, then what sounds like an insult. And in between, the disciples asking Him to get rid of her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with each one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She keeps coming. After the silence she comes closer; after the limit she kneels; after the dogs remark she takes the image and finishes it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yes, Lord — and even the little dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not dispute the order He has stated. She accepts it entirely and points out that it does not exclude her: if the children are being fed, there is something under the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word He used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kunarion** — a diminutive. Not the scavenging street dogs, but the little ones inside a house. It is a household word, and she hears the household in it — *their master's table*, not somebody else's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a crumb did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She asked for a crumb and she got what she asked for. *Let it be done for you just as you desire* — and a demon left her daughter that hour, at a distance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what fell off the edge of the table, unnoticed by the people eating at it: enough to break a demonic hold on a child in another house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The children's bread is what the crumb broke off. Whoever is at that table is not reaching for fragments — they are handed the loaf, torn and put into their hands, and told to eat it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Woman, your faith is great.* He says this to exactly two people in Matthew, and both are Gentiles; the other is the centurion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let it be done for you just as you desire.* And her daughter was healed from that hour, at a distance, with the mother still standing in front of Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she called Him first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have mercy on me, Lord, Son of David.* A Canaanite woman, from the country Israel was told to drive out, addressing Him by His messianic title."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had the doctrine right before the conversation started."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the delay.** Nothing here suggests reluctance in Him — Matthew has already shown Him healing a Roman's servant on request. What the exchange produced was an answer from her that has been quoted ever since, and a public verdict on her faith in front of disciples who had just asked Him to send her away."}]}, "Matthew 16:13–20": {"title": "\"Who do you say I am?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus asks two questions in a place chosen for the asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He asks it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Caesarea Philippi* — as far north as He ever took them, at the foot of Mount Hermon. The town sat beside a cave sacred to the Greek god Pan, with a temple to Caesar Augustus built nearby."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Surrounded by the shrines of other lords and other sons of gods, He asks who they say He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who do people say the Son of Man is?* Then: *But **you** — who do you say I am?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is a survey and they answer it easily. The second is emphatic in Greek — *you*, plural, as against everybody else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reported opinions are all complimentary: John the Baptist, Elijah, Jeremiah, one of the prophets. Every one of them is a category He does not fit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are the Messiah, the Son of the living God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves matter. Messiah is the office; Son of the living God is the identity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No human being revealed this to you. My Father in heaven showed it to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not congratulate Peter on his insight. He identifies the source, and it is not Peter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are Peter, and on this rock I will build My church.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wordplay is deliberate — *Petros*, a stone, and *petra*, rock. Christians have argued for centuries over whether the rock is Peter himself or the confession he has just made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not in dispute is the builder and the outcome. *I* will build, and *the gates of the realm of the dead will not hold out against it.* Gates are defensive. The picture is of the church advancing on death's stronghold, not enduring a siege."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happens four verses later.** The same man, who has just been told the Father revealed this to him, is called Satan for objecting to the cross. Matthew puts the two scenes side by side without softening either. See the note on Matthew 16:21–23."}]}, "Matthew 16:21–23": {"title": "\"A stone in My path\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses after being told the Father revealed the truth to him, Peter is called Satan. Matthew puts the two scenes back to back on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From that time on\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that time on Jesus began to make it plain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The confession changes what He tells them. Once they know who He is, He starts explaining what that means — and what it means is Jerusalem, suffering, death, and the third day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew notes it as a turning point in the Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter took Him aside and began to correct Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one used for rebuking — the same word Jesus uses on demons and on a storm. Peter is not raising a question. He is telling Him off, privately, to spare Him the embarrassment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never, Lord! This will never happen to You.* In Greek it is emphatic: by no means, not ever."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Get behind Me, Satan. You are a stone in My path.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **skandalon** — the trigger of a trap, the thing that makes you stumble. Peter has just been called a rock to build on; now he is a rock to trip over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you are not thinking the thoughts of God but the thoughts of men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the charge, and it is worth being precise about. Peter is not accused of malice. He is accused of ordinary human reasoning — a friend who does not want the man he loves to be killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the most natural sentiment in the passage, and Jesus identifies its source as the same one that offered Him the kingdoms of the world without a cross."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pattern.** Both temptations come from someone who cares. The devil in the wilderness offered a route to the crown with no suffering in it; Peter offers the same route in the voice of a friend. Jesus answers both with the same refusal. See the note on Matthew 4:1–4."}]}, "Matthew 17:1–8": {"title": "\"Listen to Him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six days after telling them He is going to die, He takes three of them up a hill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Six days later.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Mark both date it. Luke says *about eight days*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six days after what: Peter's confession, the first announcement of the cross, and Peter being told *get behind Me, Satan*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men who go up the mountain include the one who had just been rebuked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter, James, and John the brother of James.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three at Jairus's house and in Gethsemane. Matthew names them the same way each time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there **He was transformed in front of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metamorphoo** — the root of *metamorphosis*. Changed in form."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same verb of the reader: *be **transformed** by the renewing of your mind*, and *we are being **transformed** into the same image from glory to glory*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His face **shone like the sun**, and His clothes turned **as white as the light**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' face shone after forty days on Sinai, and he had to veil it — but that was reflected. Mark says of the clothes *such as no launderer on earth can whiten them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who arrives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Moses and Elijah** appeared to them, **talking with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lawgiver and the prophet. One died and God buried him; the other never died at all. See the note on 2 Kings 2:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says what they were discussing: *they spoke of **His decease** which He was about to accomplish at Jerusalem* — the Greek is **exodos**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **it is good for us to be here**. If You want, **I will put up three shelters** — one for You, one for Moses, and one for Elijah.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, equally sized. Mark adds the note: *he did not know what to say, for they were greatly afraid.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While he was still speaking, **a bright cloud covered them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is cut off mid-sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cloud is the **shekinah** — the one that filled the tabernacle and the temple, and that led Israel through the wilderness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the voice says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is My Son, whom I love. My delight is in Him. Listen to Him.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two sentences are word for word what was said at the Jordan. See the note on Matthew 3:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is new, and it is the only command in the passage: **listen to Him**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It answers the offer of three shelters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the disciples heard it, they **fell facedown, terrified**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the shining face. At the voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The touch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Jesus came over and touched them**. \"**Get up**,\" He said. \"**Do not be afraid**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He crosses to them. The Greek is *having come and having touched*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they lifted their eyes they **saw no one but Jesus Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses gone, Elijah gone, the cloud gone. The Greek is *Jesus only*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command was to listen to Him, and when they look up He is the only one there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was said on the way down.** *Tell the vision to no one until the Son of Man is risen from the dead.* Peter kept it for decades, and then wrote it down: *we were eyewitnesses of His majesty… we heard this voice which came from heaven when we were with Him on the holy mountain.*"}]}, "Matthew 18:15–20": {"title": "\"Where two or three come together\" — the setting of a much-loved verse", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Where two or three come together in My name, I am there among them* is quoted at small gatherings everywhere, and it is a true and warm thing to say. It is also the last line of a paragraph about something else entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the paragraph is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject is a Christian who has wronged another Christian, and the process is laid out in stages:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["go to him **privately** first", "if that fails, take one or two others", "if that fails, tell the church", "if that fails, treat him as an outsider"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every step is designed to keep the circle as small as possible for as long as possible. The aim is stated at the start: *you have won your brother back*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two or three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The number is not chosen for its smallness. It comes from the previous sentence — *every matter may be settled on the word of two or three witnesses* — which is the standard of evidence from Deuteronomy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So when Jesus says *where two or three come together in My name, I am there*, He is promising His presence to the hardest thing a church ever does: the conversation where somebody is confronted about a wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does to the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not shrink it. It makes it braver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comfort of a handful of believers meeting with Christ among them is real and the verse carries it. But it was given first to people about to sit down in a room and try to win somebody back, and it says that Christ is in the room for that."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On binding and loosing.** The phrase belongs to the same setting — the language is from rabbinic practice, where binding and loosing meant declaring what was forbidden and permitted. In context it is about the church's judgement in exactly the situation being described, not a general formula."}]}, "Matthew 2:1–2": {"title": "The men who came the wrong way", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Foreigners arrive in Jerusalem asking a question that nobody in Jerusalem had thought to ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Magi** — the word covers astrologers, court advisers and priests of the eastern kingdoms. Not kings, and Matthew never says how many; the three comes from the number of gifts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were Gentiles, from outside the covenant, practising a discipline Israel's own law forbade. And they are the first people in Matthew's Gospel to worship Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is the child who has been born King of the Jews?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask it in the capital, in the reign of a king who was famously paranoid about his throne — a man who had already killed his wife and three of his own sons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They walk into Herod's city and ask where the real king is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they went wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They went to a palace. It is the obvious place to look for a royal birth, and it is the wrong one — a detour that puts Herod on the scent and leads to the soldiers being sent to Bethlehem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The star had brought them a long way. It did not take them to Jerusalem; their own reasonable assumption did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in Jerusalem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests and scribes answer Herod's question immediately: Bethlehem, and they quote Micah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They knew the address. Five miles down the road, and there is no record of any of them going to look."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two groups had.** The scholars had the Scripture and stayed home. The foreigners had a light in the sky, an incomplete map, and enough interest to travel for months. Matthew reports both without comment, in the second chapter of a Gospel written to Jewish readers."}]}, "Matthew 2:16–18": {"title": "Rachel crying for her children", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The part of the Christmas story that does not appear on cards, and Matthew does not soften it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every boy two years old and under was put to death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethlehem was a small town; historians estimate the number of children killed in the low dozens. That is not a comfort, and Matthew does not offer it as one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod's record makes the account entirely credible — he executed his own sons and had ordered notable men killed at his death so the country would mourn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A voice is heard in Ramah… Rachel crying for her children, and she will not be comforted, because they are no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew reaches into Jeremiah 31, where Rachel — buried near Bethlehem, mother of the northern tribes — is pictured weeping as her descendants are marched into exile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what Matthew does **not** do. He does not say the mothers should be comforted. He quotes a verse in which comfort is explicitly refused."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the quotation comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read on in Jeremiah 31 and the next verse is God's answer: *restrain your voice from weeping and your eyes from tears, for your work will be rewarded — they will come back from the land of the enemy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses further, the same chapter contains the promise of the new covenant — the law written on hearts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes the grief and leaves his readers to know where it is going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is in the Gospel at all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because it happened, and because Matthew is telling the truth about the world Jesus was born into. A king who kills children is part of the Christmas story."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one detail of mercy.** Joseph is warned in a dream and takes the child to Egypt. Jesus survives — and Matthew, who has already had Him called out of Egypt like Israel, is showing a nation's story replayed in one family: down into Egypt, and out again."}]}, "Matthew 20:24–28": {"title": "\"Not to be served, but to serve\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two brothers have just asked for the best seats, the other ten are furious about it, and Jesus responds by redefining the whole idea of position."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says about the world"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You know how it works among the nations. Their rulers throw their weight around, and the great ones make their authority felt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs in the Greek are compounds with *kata* — lording it **down** over, exercising authority **against**. The preposition does the work: it is power exerted downward onto people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *it must not be that way among you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inversion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever wants to be great among you must be the one who serves you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice He does not say wanting to be great is wrong. He does not rebuke the ambition — He redirects it. The route to greatness is not blocked; it runs the other way from what everyone assumes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for servant is **diakonos**, the ordinary word for someone who waits at a table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern is not offered as a principle but as a biography. He is describing what He is doing, in a chapter where He has just told them for the third time that He is going to Jerusalem to be killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ransom** is *lutron* — the price paid to release a captive or free a slave. It is the noun behind the redemption word in Ephesians 1:7."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the brothers had actually asked for.** Seats at His right and left in His kingdom. When Matthew's Gospel finally shows two people at His right and left, it is at the crucifixion, and they are criminals. The Gospel answers the request without comment, several chapters later."}]}, "Matthew 20:8–12": {"title": "Paid last first", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A landowner hires men all day at different hours and pays every one of them the same. The detail that makes the parable work is the order of the queue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Beginning with the last ones hired\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could have paid the first group first, sent them home content, and settled with the rest quietly. Nobody would have known."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He deliberately arranges it so the men who worked twelve hours watch the men who worked one receive a full day's wage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not carelessness. He wants them to see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the first group then expects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the ones hired first came up, they assumed they would get more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had been said to suggest it. They had agreed a denarius at dawn — the standard day's wage, a fair rate, exactly what they asked for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The expectation is generated entirely by watching somebody else be treated generously."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have made them equal to us, who carried the weight of the whole day and the burning heat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The grievance is not that they were underpaid. It is the word **equal**. They object to the last men being brought level with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sharp end of the parable: generosity to somebody else felt like an injury, in men who had received precisely what they were promised."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The landowner's answer.** *Am I not allowed to do what I want with what is mine? Or is your eye evil because I am good?* The Greek idiom is about a grudging, envious eye. And the parable ends where the chapter before it ended — the last will be first, and the first last. Jesus tells it to disciples who had just asked what they would get for having left everything."}]}, "Matthew 21:12–17": {"title": "The temple cleared", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One man clears a courtyard the size of several football pitches, and nobody stops Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He objected to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The money changers and the dove sellers were operating in the Court of the Gentiles — the only part of the temple a non-Jew was permitted to enter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one space in the building where the nations could come and pray had been turned into a livestock market with an exchange rate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is written, '**My house will be called a house of prayer**,' but you have turned it into **a hideout for robbers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first half is Isaiah 56, and Mark keeps the rest of the line: *a house of prayer **for all nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half is Jeremiah 7 — the temple sermon, where the people were chanting *the temple of the LORD, the temple of the LORD* while robbing each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **hideout** is where thieves go after the theft. It is not where the robbery happens; it is where they feel safe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did not damage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He drove out people and overturned furniture. Nobody is reported hurt, and no animal is reported killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He would not allow anyone to carry wares through the temple*, Mark adds. He shut the shortcut down as well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened next in the same courtyard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the blind and the lame came to Him right there in the temple, and He healed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Matthew records this, and the placement is the point. The tables go over and the room fills with people who could not have got near it before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the old rule, *the blind and the lame shall not come into the house.* They came in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The children in the temple courts **shouting, \"Hosanna to the Son of David!\"** And they were furious.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests object to the children, and He answers them with Psalm 8: *out of the mouths of children and nursing infants You have brought forth praise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of that psalm says *You have ordained **strength**.* He quotes the Greek, which has praise. Both are in the line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He slept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He left them, went out of the city to **Bethany**, and spent the night there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He cleared the temple and then walked two miles out of the city to sleep at Lazarus's house. Every night that week."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What week this is.** Nisan 10, the day the Passover lamb was brought into the house and kept until the fourteenth. He comes into the temple on the day the lamb comes in, and the next three days are spent being questioned. See the notes on Matthew 21:23–27 and Exodus 12:1–13."}]}, "Matthew 21:23–27": {"title": "By whose authority", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The examination begins, and it runs for three days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening this week"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamb was brought into the house on the tenth of Nisan and kept until the fourteenth — *you shall keep it up until the fourteenth day* — and in those days it was watched for any blemish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus rode in on the tenth. From this point until the Passover, one delegation after another comes at Him with questions."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the chief priests and elders** — by what authority?", "**the Pharisees and Herodians** — is it lawful to pay taxes to Caesar?", "**the Sadducees** — the woman with seven husbands, whose wife is she?", "**a lawyer** — which is the great commandment?"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four inspections in two chapters, from four groups who agreed on almost nothing else, and every one of them goes away with nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate's verdict at the end of the week is the standard formula: *I find no fault in Him.* Herod finds none. Pilate's wife, Judas, and the thief on the cross all say the same thing in their own words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamb was examined and passed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question they ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By what authority** are You doing these things? And **who gave You** this authority?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are asking about the tables. Nobody licensed Him to clear the court."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will ask you one question. If you answer it for Me, I will tell you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not refuse to answer. He puts a condition on it, and the condition is a question they cannot afford to answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question He asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Where did John's baptism come from? From heaven, or from men?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is exactly parallel to theirs. They asked where His authority came from; He asks where John's did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If John was from heaven, then John's testimony about Him settles their question already — *behold, the Lamb of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their working"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records their deliberation, which is the most damning thing in the passage:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If we say, 'From heaven,' He will ask us why we did not believe him. But if we say, 'From men,' **we have the crowd to fear**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one clause about what is true. Both options are weighed entirely by consequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer they give"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We do not know.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men whose office was to rule on exactly this kind of question — that was the Sanhedrin's job — say in public that they cannot tell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And His"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Then I will not tell you** by what authority I do these things.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He keeps the terms of the deal precisely."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He tells them instead.** Three parables, straight afterwards, all aimed at them: the two sons, the wicked tenants, and the wedding feast. He gives them no answer about authority and then describes them to their faces for the rest of the chapter."}]}, "Matthew 22:23–33": {"title": "An argument on a present tense", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Sadducees bring a riddle designed to make resurrection look absurd, and He answers it from a verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Sadducees — **the ones who say there is no resurrection**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew defines them in the same breath as introducing them. They were the priestly aristocracy, they ran the temple, and they accepted only the five books of Moses as binding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That last detail matters for what He quotes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The riddle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven brothers, one widow, the levirate law from Deuteronomy 25. *So at the resurrection, whose wife will she be?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a genuine question. It is a stock debating trap, designed to make the doctrine collapse under its own arithmetic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are wrong, and it is because **you do not know the Scriptures or the power of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two failures named, and both are shortages rather than errors. They knew a great deal of Scripture professionally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"At the resurrection people **do not marry** and are not given in marriage. They are **like the angels** in heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The premise of the riddle is wrong, so the riddle does not arise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Have you never read what God said to you: '**I am** the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob'? He is **not the God of the dead, but of the living**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 3, at the burning bush — from the books the Sadducees accepted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument rests on a tense. God did not say *I was* their God. He said **I am**, and He said it centuries after all three men were buried."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that requires"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If God is presently the God of Abraham, then Abraham presently is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument would fail entirely on a past tense, and Jesus builds the resurrection of the dead on the present one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"What God said to you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *what God said to Moses*. He tells them the sentence was addressed to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the crowds heard this, they were **astounded** at His teaching.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Luke adds that some of the scribes said *Teacher, You have spoken well* — the Pharisees, who did believe in resurrection, enjoying the Sadducees being beaten with their own five books."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this week is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passion week. Four delegations come at Him in two chapters — the chief priests and elders, the Pharisees and Herodians, these Sadducees, and a lawyer — and each goes away with nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And from that day on no one dared ask Him another question.* See the note on Matthew 22:41–46."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How much a single word can hold.** Paul does the same thing in Galatians 3 with a singular noun: *it does not say \"and to seeds,\" as though to many, but as of one.* Two arguments, one on a verb tense and one on a plural, and both treat the text as exact. See the note on Galatians 3:15–18."}]}, "Matthew 22:34–40": {"title": "\"Everything hangs on these two\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A lawyer sets a trap and Jesus answers by quoting two verses that were not usually put together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question behind the question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which is the greatest commandment in the law?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a live rabbinic debate — the tradition counted 613 commands, and ranking them was a standing argument. Any answer would offend somebody, which is why it is described as a test."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two He picks"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Deuteronomy 6:5** — love the LORD your God with all your heart, soul and mind. This is the Shema, recited twice daily by every observant Jew. Nobody could object to it.", "**Leviticus 19:18** — love your neighbour as yourself. A single line, buried in a chapter of miscellaneous laws about gleaning, wages and honest weights."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first was the obvious answer. Pulling the second up beside it was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The second is like it\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word means *of the same kind*. He does not rank them as first and runner-up; He says they are the same sort of thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the move the lawyer would not have expected. Love of God and love of the person next to you are put in one category."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Hangs on\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one used of hanging something on a peg — and of a body hanging on a cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else, in this image, is suspended from these two. Take them away and the rest has nothing to hold it up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the commanding runs from.** Jesus is answering a question about the law, and the answer is a command. What the New Testament adds is where the capacity comes from: *we love Him because He loved us first.* The order is not a softening of the command — it is the only thing that makes it possible. See the note on 1 John 4:19."}]}, "Matthew 22:41–46": {"title": "Whose Son is He?", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "After three days of being questioned, He asks one of His own, and it ends the week's interrogation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is He?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only question in the Gospels He puts to His enemies about Himself, and it is the one everything hangs on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"David's.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Correct, and it is what every Jew would have said. The Messiah comes from David's line — 2 Samuel 7, Isaiah 11, Jeremiah 23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not wrong. They are short."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then how is it that **David, speaking by the Spirit, calls Him Lord**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Psalm 110:1 — *the LORD said to my Lord: sit at My right hand until I make Your enemies a footstool.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different words. The first is **YHWH**. The second is **Adonai**, my Lord — and David is the greatest king Israel ever had, with nobody above him but God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls someone else *my Lord*, and that someone is his own descendant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If David calls Him Lord, how can He be David's son?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture the line ran one way. A father does not address his descendant as *my Lord*; every honour flows upward through the generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unless the descendant existed before the ancestor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other time He said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Before Abraham was, **I AM**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They picked up stones for that one. See the note on John 8:57–59."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same claim, two forms. Once to a hostile crowd in plain words, and once here, as a question, from their own songbook — and neither time does anybody misunderstand what is being said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Psalm 110 goes on to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn and will not relent: **You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king on David's throne who is also a priest, and not of Aaron's line. Hebrews spends two chapters on that verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one could answer Him a word, and **from that day on no one dared ask Him another question**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The questioning stops. Matthew reports it as the end of it — after this the Sanhedrin stop debating and start planning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does immediately after.** Turns to the crowds and the disciples and delivers the seven woes, and finishes with *Jerusalem, Jerusalem, how often I have longed to gather your children.* The last public words of His ministry, and they end in tears. See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}]}, "Matthew 23:13–15": {"title": "Doors shut in people's faces", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the seven woes, and the charge is about a door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **shut the door of the kingdom of heaven in people's faces**. You will not go in yourselves, and you will not let anyone else go in either.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of men standing in a doorway. Not outside it and not inside it — blocking it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version says they *took away the key of knowledge*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who this is said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Teachers of the law and Pharisees. The most careful, most scrupulous, most observant men in the nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people who shut the kingdom are not the ones you would expect to. They are the religious experts, and what shuts it is what they preach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Pretenders\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupokrites** — an actor, one who speaks from under a mask. It was the ordinary word for a stage performer before it became an insult."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus uses it seven times in this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The widows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **eat up widows' houses**, and to cover it you make long prayers.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things stacked: what they take, and what they use to hide it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *cover* is a pretext, a screen. The praying is the cover story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The convert"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You cross land and sea to win a single convert, and when you have won him, **you make him twice as fit for hell as you are**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The effort is real — land and sea, for one person. Nobody accuses them of laziness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What they hand him at the end of it is the problem. A man taught to stand on his own performance ends up further from the door than the man who never went looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same thing happens in Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three men travelled to sit with a friend in ashes, and what they preached to him — experience, tradition, a formula — produced bitterness and a defence rather than rest. And God's verdict on all three was *you have not spoken of Me what is right.* See the notes on Job 4:7–11, Job 8:8–19 and Job 11:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Preaching that puts a man back on his own resources shuts the door on him, whatever it costs the preacher to deliver it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter ends.** After seven woes: *Jerusalem, Jerusalem… how often I have longed to gather your children together.* The harshest chapter in the Gospels finishes in tears over the city, and with the word *until*. See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}]}, "Matthew 23:37–39": {"title": "\"How often I have longed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the harshest chapter in the Gospels, and it finishes in tears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Jerusalem, Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubled name is how you address someone you love. *Abraham, Abraham.* *Moses, Moses.* *Simon, Simon.* *Saul, Saul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven woes have just been pronounced over the scribes and Pharisees. Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"How often\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How often** I have longed to gather your children together.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's Gospel records perhaps three visits to Jerusalem. *How often* is the speech of somebody who has been reaching for that city for a great deal longer than three years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The One saying it is the One who sent the prophets they killed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The way a **hen** gathers her chicks under her wings.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is not lion, eagle, or shepherd. A hen — an ordinary farmyard bird with no weapons at all, whose one method of protection is to open her wings and let her chicks run under her, and to stay there and take whatever comes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wings language is the Psalms' — *under the shadow of Your wings* — and He puts Himself in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And you were not willing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refusal is theirs and He says so plainly. He does not say they could not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you did not want to*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Your house is left to you empty\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look, **your house** is left to you empty.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your* house. Two chapters earlier He had called it *My house*, when He turned over the tables — *My house shall be called a house of prayer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The possessive has changed, and the word *empty* is *desolate*, abandoned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He then walks out of the temple, and the next verse of chapter 24 has the disciples pointing at the buildings and Him saying not one stone will be left on another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For I tell you, **you will not see Me again until** you say, 'Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until.* It is not a closing of the door; it is a term."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words He names are from Psalm 118, the crowd's shout at the triumphal entry five days earlier. He is saying the city will say them again, and that when it does, they will see Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last word of the harshest chapter in Matthew is a word about a reunion."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The season this describes.** Hosea's *not My people* — the face hidden, the house empty, the prophets gone. And even then it is *until*, with the words of the return already written out for them. See the notes on Hosea 1:8–9 and Romans 9:25–26."}]}, "Matthew 24:1–3": {"title": "\"Not one stone left\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The disciples point at a building and He tells them it is coming down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He has just come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **left the temple** and was walking away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before ends with the seven woes and *Jerusalem, Jerusalem… your house is left to you desolate.* See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He walks out, and the disciples change the subject to architecture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were pointing at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod's temple was one of the wonders of the ancient world. Josephus describes stones of enormous size, and some of those still in the retaining wall weigh several hundred tons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been working on it for forty-six years and were not finished. See the note on John 2:18–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records what one of them said: *Teacher, see what manner of stones and what buildings are here!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Not one stone here will be left sitting on another. Every one of them will be thrown down.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In AD 70 Titus took the city. The temple burned, and the gold in it melted down between the stones — which, according to the accounts, is why they were prised apart afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He sits down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As He was sitting on **the Mount of Olives**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Across the Kidron valley, facing the temple. They are looking at the building He has just described being demolished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah says His feet will stand on that hill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**When will these things happen?** And **what will be the sign of Your coming**? And **of the end of the age**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask three things and assume they are one event."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That assumption is what makes the discourse that follows difficult — Jesus answers all three, and the answers are interleaved rather than sorted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He starts with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a date. *\"Take heed that no one deceives you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same opening as Luke's account: *be careful that no one leads you astray.* See the note on Luke 21:7–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And where He ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But of **that day and hour no one knows**, not even the angels of heaven, but My Father only.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then three parables about being ready rather than being informed. See the note on Matthew 24:36–41."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing they got right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked privately. Matthew notes it — *the disciples came to Him privately.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else this is, it is a conversation with four men on a hillside rather than a public announcement."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the building meant to them.** Not a landmark. The temple was where God had said He would put His name, where sin was dealt with, and where a nation's identity was held. He told them it would be rubble, and then sat down opposite it to explain."}]}, "Matthew 24:32–35": {"title": "Learn from the fig tree", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short parable set inside the longest answer Jesus gives about the end, and it is about reading a season rather than fixing a date."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lesson"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As soon as its branch turns soft and puts out its leaves, you know that summer is near.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *turns soft* is about the branch becoming tender — sap moving in wood that was dry all winter. It happens before the leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is told to calculate. They are told to notice what a farmer notices."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Right at the door\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you see all these things, you know that He is near, right at the door.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be read *it is near* or *He is near* — the verb has no stated subject. The Illumination takes the personal reading, and the phrase *at the door* elsewhere in the New Testament is always used of someone standing there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James uses it: *the Judge is standing at the door.* Revelation uses it: *I stand at the door and knock.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The generation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This generation will certainly not pass away until all these things have happened.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **genea**. It has been read as the people alive then, as the Jewish people as a race, and as the generation that sees the signs begin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is left as He said it, and it has been argued about for two thousand years without being softened by anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Heaven and earth will pass away, but My words will never pass away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most permanent things a person can point at are named, and then set below His own sentences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *will never pass away* is a double negative — the strongest form of denial available in the language."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this sits.** The disciples had asked two questions on the Mount of Olives: when will these things be, and what will be the sign. He answers the second at length and, a few verses on, says of the first that no one knows the day or the hour."}]}, "Matthew 24:36–41": {"title": "\"As it was in the days of Noah\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He rules out date-setting in the first sentence and then describes what the time will feel like instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Nobody knows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"About that day and hour **no one knows** — not even the angels of heaven — **only My Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strongest statement in Scripture against working it out. Angels do not have it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As it was in the days of Noah**, so it will be at the coming of the Son of Man.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of everything He could have reached for, He picks the flood — and He treats it as history, not a story. His argument depends on it having happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the days of Noah looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"People were **eating and drinking, marrying and being given in marriage**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the list. Four ordinary things, and not one of them is a sin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not describe the violence Genesis reports, or the corruption, though both were there. He describes normal life continuing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point of the comparison is not wickedness. It is unawareness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"They knew nothing about it\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And they knew nothing about it** until the flood came and swept them all away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they did not know, did not perceive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noah had been building for a very long time in plain sight, and 2 Peter calls him *a preacher of righteousness*. The information was available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Right up to the day Noah entered the ark\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The door stayed open through the whole of the building. It shut on one particular day, and the LORD shut it. See the note on Genesis 7:11–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "One taken, one left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Two men will be in the field; **one will be taken and the other left**. Two women will be grinding at the mill; one will be taken and the other left.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pairs, doing identical work, side by side. Nothing visible separates them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have long divided over which is the better outcome — taken, or left. In the Noah comparison just made, the ones *swept away* were taken by the flood and Noah was left; in the Greek of verse 40 the word for *taken* is the one Jesus uses in John 14 for *I will come again and receive you to Myself*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage does not settle it, and He does not pause to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He tells them to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not calculate. The next verse: *\"Watch therefore, for you do not know what hour your Lord is coming.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then three parables in a row about it — the householder and the thief, the servant left in charge, the ten virgins with their lamps. All three are about being ready rather than being informed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says about His own knowledge.** Mark's version keeps a clause Matthew also has: *nor the Son, but only the Father.* He states the limit about Himself, out loud, and then goes straight on teaching about the day anyway."}]}, "Matthew 24:4–8": {"title": "The beginning of birth pains", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first thing He says in answer to the disciples' question is a warning about being misled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had shown Him the temple buildings and He had said *not one stone shall be left here upon another*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, on the Mount of Olives, privately: *when will these things be? And what will be the sign of Your coming, and of the end of the age?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three questions. The chapter is the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be careful that no one leads you astray**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **planese** — deceive, cause to wander. The root of *planet*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before any sign is named, the first thing said is about deception."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He returns to it: *many false prophets will rise up and deceive many*, and *false christs and false prophets will rise and show great signs and wonders, so as to deceive, if possible, even the elect*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in one chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Many will come using My name**, saying, '**I am the Messiah**,' and they will lead many astray.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not opponents. People using the name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wars"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will **hear of wars and rumors of wars**, but **do not let yourselves be alarmed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **throeisthe** — be frightened, be startled into panic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause that governs the paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**These things have to happen, but the end is still to come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves. They must occur, and they are not the marker."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again at the end of the section: *all of this is only the beginning*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning is against reading the news as a countdown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom**, and there will be **famines and plagues and earthquakes in place after place**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in various places* — **kata topous**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All of this is only **the beginning of the birth pains**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **odinon** — labour pains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is precise, and Jesus chose it. Labour pains are real, they intensify, they come in a pattern, and they are not the disaster they feel like — they are the beginning of something arriving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same word of creation: *the whole creation groans and labours with birth pangs together until now.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says will be happening at the same time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifteen verses later: *and **this gospel of the kingdom will be preached in all the world as a witness to all the nations**, and then the end will come.* See the note on Matthew 24:9–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two run together in the same chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But of **that day and hour no one knows**, not even the angels of heaven, but My Father only.\"* See the note on Matthew 24:36–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then three parables about being ready — the servant, the ten virgins, and the talents — and the sheep and the goats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole discourse repeats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Watch therefore, for you do not know what hour your Lord is coming.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He was sitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Opposite the temple He had just walked out of, two days before the arrest."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first instruction of the chapter.** Not to watch the news. To make sure nobody misleads you — and then a list of alarming things with *do not be alarmed* attached to it."}]}, "Matthew 24:9–14": {"title": "\"The love of most people will grow cold\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Part of the answer to a question the disciples asked on the Mount of Olives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had asked three things: *when will these things be? And what will be the sign of Your coming, and of the end of the age?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter is the answer, and it moves between the fall of Jerusalem and something further out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The handing over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then they will **hand you over to be persecuted and put to death**, and you will be **hated by all nations because of My name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *hand over* is **paradosousin** — the same verb used of Judas, and of Christ being delivered up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Because of My name** — the reason given is not conduct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The falling away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"At that time **many will fall away**, and they will **betray one another and hate one another**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skandalisthesontai** — tripped up, made to stumble. From **skandalon**, the trigger of a trap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The betraying is internal — *one another*, twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The false prophets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Many false prophets will appear and **lead many astray**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says it three times in the chapter: at the start, here, and again at verse 24 — *false christs and false prophets will rise and show great signs and wonders*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because lawlessness will spread**, the love of most people will grow cold.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *lawlessness* is **anomia** — from *a-nomos*, without law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Will spread** — the verb is to multiply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus gives a cause and an effect, and the effect is about temperature."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cooling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The love of most people will grow cold**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **psugesetai** — to be chilled, to cool by blowing on. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for love is **agape**, the highest of the four Greek words for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that love is replaced by hatred. That it drops in temperature."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Most people** — the Greek is *the many*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one who holds on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **the one who holds on to the end will be saved**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupomeinas** — from **hupomeno**, to remain under. The endurance word, the same one James says testing produces. See the note on James 1:3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proclamation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **this good news of the kingdom will be proclaimed in the whole world as a testimony to all nations**, and then the end will come.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *inhabited world* is **oikoumene** — the whole populated earth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**As a testimony** — the Greek is **marturion**, evidence. The preaching is described as the depositing of testimony rather than as a result achieved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Persecution, falling away, betrayal, false prophets, lawlessness multiplying, love cooling — and then, in the middle of all of it, the message going everywhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not present the two as alternatives. Both run at once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is sitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now as He sat on the Mount of Olives, the disciples came to Him privately.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two days before the arrest, opposite the temple He had just walked out of, having said *not one stone shall be left here upon another*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one sentence about the reader.** Everything else in the paragraph is about what conditions will do. *The one who holds on to the end* is the only clause with a person in it, and the verb means to stay under the weight."}]}, "Matthew 25:24–30": {"title": "\"I knew you to be a hard man\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third servant explains himself, and his first sentence is about the master rather than the money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I knew you to be a hard man**, harvesting where you did not plant and gathering where you scattered no seed.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his opening line, and it is the whole problem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *hard* is **skleros** — harsh, stiff, rough. It is the root of *sclerosis*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So I was afraid**, and I went and hid your talent in the ground.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So.* He states the cause himself, and it is a straight line: this is what I thought you were like, therefore I was afraid, therefore I buried it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the amount. The man with two talents had a small share too, and doubled it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was actually given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the first half of the parable. *To one he gave five talents, to another two, and to another one, **each according to what he was able to handle**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A talent was an enormous sum — years of a labourer's wages. He was not slighted; he was given what he could carry, by a master who had assessed him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the master handed over *his property* and left. It is an act of trust from a man about to be absent for a long time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Here is what belongs to you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He returns it intact. Nothing lost, nothing stolen, nothing wasted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By his own accounting he has done nothing wrong. That is what fear produces — carefulness that looks like integrity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The master's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So you knew** that I harvest where I have not planted?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not concede the description. He argues from inside it — *if that is what you believed, then at the very least the bank.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rebuke is that the man did not even act consistently with his own fear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take the talent away from him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other two are told *well done, good and faithful servant… enter into the joy of your lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference between them was not ability, opportunity or amount. It was what each believed about the man who gave it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For everyone who has will be given more… but from the one who has nothing, even what he has will be taken away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same saying Mark attaches to how you listen. See the note on Mark 4:24–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus never says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not correct the servant's picture of God by softening the master. He lets the parable stand and puts the burden where the servant put it — on what he thought he was dealing with."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the other two did first.** *Went **at once** and put them to work.* No deliberation is recorded. They knew the man, and knowing him is what made them quick."}]}, "Matthew 26:26–30": {"title": "\"This is My body, this is My blood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's account adds one phrase the others do not have, and it is the phrase about what the blood is for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is My blood — the blood of the new covenant, poured out for many **for the forgiveness of sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark and Luke stop at *for many*. Matthew names the purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the Gospel that opened with an angel saying *you shall call His name Jesus, for He shall save His people from their sins*. Matthew closes the circle at the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Every one of you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Every one of you**, drink from it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Matthew it is a command, not just a report. Mark records that they all drank; Matthew records that they were all told to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody at that table had earned it. The betrayer is present and the man who will deny Him three times before morning is holding a cup."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two elements, two things given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cup is given a stated purpose — forgiveness. The bread is given without one: *take this and eat it. This is My body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has already told us what the body did. In chapter 8 he quotes Isaiah over a night of healings: *He took our infirmities and bore our diseases.* The same writer, the same book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever eats the bread and drinks the cup is receiving two things, not one. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:27–30, where Paul's warning is about failing to distinguish them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Thanks, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **gave thanks** over it… and when He had **given thanks** He passed it to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eucharisteō** — the word the meal has been named after ever since."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gave thanks over bread He was about to call His broken body, hours before the whipping post. The thanks came first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unfinished cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will not drink of the fruit of the vine again until the day I drink it new with you in My Father's kingdom.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**With you.** Matthew keeps the pronoun. The next cup has a guest list."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The hymn.** *Then they sang a hymn together and went out to the Mount of Olives.* The Passover ended with the second half of the Hallel — Psalm 118 among them, which says *the LORD is on my side, I will not fear; what can man do to me?* He walked from that hymn into the garden."}]}, "Matthew 26:36–39": {"title": "Gethsemane", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The garden, and Matthew does not hide any of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came to a place called **Gethsemane**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means **oil press** — **gat shemanim**. An olive grove on the slope of the Mount of Olives, with a press in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fruit is crushed to get what is in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Sit down here** while **I go over there and pray**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eight are left at the entrance. Three go further."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took **Peter and the two sons of Zebedee**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three from Jairus's house and the mountain. See the note on Matthew 17:1–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had seen Him transfigured. Now they see this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came over Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **grief came over Him, and a heaviness He did not hide**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lupeisthai** and **ademonein** — the second is a rare word for distress, anguish, being overwhelmed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark has an even stronger word: **ekthambeisthai**, appalled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He tells them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My soul is crushed with sorrow**,\" He told them. \"**It is almost more than I can bear**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perilupos estin he psuche mou heos thanatou** — sorrowful to the point of death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them. He does not manage it privately and does not perform composure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stay here, and keep watch with Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gregoreite** — stay awake. The same word Peter later uses in his letter. See the note on 1 Peter 5:8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They fall asleep three times, and He comes back three times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **He went on a little farther**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says *about a stone's throw*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Near enough to be seen and far enough to be alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **fell with His face to the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not kneeling. Face down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark preserves the Aramaic: **Abba**. It is the word Paul says the Spirit puts in the believer's mouth. See the note on Galatians 4:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If there is any way, let this cup pass from Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *if it is possible*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cup is the Old Testament image for what a person is given to drink — and in the prophets it is usually the cup of wrath. Isaiah: *you have drunk the cup of His fury*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause after it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet not what I want — what You want**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records three prayers. The first has *if it is possible*. The second changes: *if this cup cannot pass away from Me unless I drink it, Your will be done.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request shifts once and the last clause never does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says about the same night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who, in the days of His flesh, when He had offered up **prayers and supplications, with vehement cries and tears** to Him who was able to save Him from death, **and was heard** because of His godly fear.* See the note on Hebrews 5:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**And was heard.** The writer says the prayer was answered — and the cup was not taken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then an angel appeared to Him from heaven, strengthening Him. And being in agony, He prayed more earnestly. Then His sweat became like great drops of blood falling down to the ground.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says when He comes back the last time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Rise, let us be going. See, My betrayer is at hand.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same words He used in the upper room. See the note on John 14:28–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does not do.** Hide it. He tells three men He is crushed, asks them to stay awake, goes face down, and asks — twice with an *if* and every time with the same last clause."}]}, "Matthew 27:27–31": {"title": "A crown of thorns", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A whole garrison assembles to make fun of one prisoner, and every prop they use means something they do not know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The company"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Called **the whole company together** around Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a cohort — the tenth part of a legion, several hundred men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not busy. This is entertainment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The robe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **stripped Him** and put **a scarlet robe** on Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark and John call it purple; Matthew calls it scarlet. It is a soldier's cloak, faded red, used as a stand-in for imperial purple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They dress Him as a king in order to laugh at Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **twisted thorn branches into a crown** and pressed it onto His head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thorns entered the world in Genesis 3, in the sentence on the ground: *cursed is the ground for your sake… **both thorns and thistles** it shall bring forth for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the visible sign of the curse on the earth, and the soldiers press a circle of them into His scalp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And put **a reed in His right hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A mock sceptre. Then: *they took the reed out of His hand, and **struck Him on the head with it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing they gave Him as a joke is used to drive the thorns further in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The kneeling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they **knelt down in front of Him** and jeered, \"**Hail, king of the Jews!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every gesture is correct. The robe, the crown, the sceptre, the bent knee, the acclamation — the full ceremony of homage to a monarch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are performing, with total accuracy, something that is true."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philippians says every knee will bow eventually. A cohort of Roman soldiers got there first, and did it as a joke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **spat on Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah had it: *I gave My back to those who struck Me, and My cheeks to those who plucked out the beard; **I did not hide My face from shame and spitting**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then they dressed Him again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they were finished mocking Him **they took the robe off, put His own clothes back on Him**, and led Him away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mock robe comes off and His own clothes go back on — the ones that will be divided among four soldiers within hours, with lots cast for the seamless tunic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Pilate had already said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I find no fault in Him at all* — three times in John's account."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The examination had passed. What follows is not judicial; it is a barracks with nothing to do."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the thorns were made of.** The curse on the ground, worn on His head — and Paul: *Christ has redeemed us from the curse of the law, having become a curse for us.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}]}, "Matthew 27:32–34": {"title": "Simon of Cyrene", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of logistics, and two details in them are worth stopping on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the way out they **came across a man from Cyrene named Simon**, and they **forced him to carry the cross**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Cyrene** is in North Africa, in what is now Libya. There was a large Jewish community there, and Cyrenians are on the list of nationalities at Pentecost. See the note on Acts 2:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is in Jerusalem for Passover, a long way from home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Forced\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **angareuo** — a Persian loan-word for the right of a courier or soldier to commandeer a civilian for a task."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same verb in the Sermon on the Mount: *whoever **compels** you to go one mile, go with him two.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon is conscripted. He did not volunteer and could not refuse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it was needed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condemned man carried the crossbeam to the site. John records that Jesus went out *bearing His cross*, so this happens partway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been scourged, and the Gospels do not explain further."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Simon, **the father of Alexander and Rufus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark names the sons, which only makes sense if the readers knew them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul closes Romans with *greet **Rufus**, chosen in the Lord, and his mother and mine.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came to a place called **Golgotha**, which means **the Place of the Skull**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is **gulgolta**. Matthew translates it for his readers, as he does with a few other terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The drink"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There they **offered Jesus vinegar mixed with gall**, and **He tasted it and would not drink it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says wine mixed with myrrh. It was a sedative, offered to dull what was coming — tradition holds that the women of Jerusalem prepared it as an act of mercy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two actions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **tasted** it* — so He knew what it was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **would not drink it**.* The Greek is *He was not willing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He declines the anaesthetic knowingly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They also gave me **gall for my food**, and for my thirst they gave me **vinegar to drink**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 69, and Matthew's wording follows it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other drink"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later, at the end: *\"I thirst\"… immediately one of them ran and took a sponge, filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed, and offered it to Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That one He takes. John says *when Jesus had received the sour wine, He said, \"It is finished!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refuses the drink that would dull it and accepts the one that lets Him speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dividing of the garments, the sign over His head, the two robbers, the darkness from the sixth hour, the cry, and the veil torn from top to bottom. See the note on Matthew 27:51–53."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Simon got.** A conscription notice, in a foreign city, on a feast day. Mark assumes his sons are known to the church in Rome thirty years later."}]}, "Matthew 27:51–53": {"title": "Torn from the top", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four things happen at the moment Jesus dies, and the first one is reported with a detail that carries the whole meaning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The curtain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The curtain of the temple was torn in two, **from the top all the way down to the bottom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The veil separated the Most Holy Place from everything else. Behind it was the place a single man entered once a year with blood, in smoke, having first atoned for himself. See the note on Leviticus 16:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was not opened. It was **torn**, and torn from the end nobody could reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew does not explain it. He records the direction and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The earthquake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ground shook and the rocks split apart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has a pattern of noting the earth's reaction — there is another quake at the resurrection two chapters on, and it is the centurion's response to this one that produces the confession *truly this was the Son of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tombs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tombs broke open, and the bodies of many of God's people who had died were raised to life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strangest paragraph in the Gospel, and Matthew alone reports it. He is careful about the timing: the tombs open at the death, but the people do not come out until **after** the resurrection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even here, Christ is kept as the firstfruits — nobody precedes Him out of a grave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He offers no interpretation of any of it. Four events, reported flat, in two sentences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader is left to notice that at the moment the sacrifice was finished, the barrier came down, the ground broke, and death started letting people go."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the letter to the Hebrews takes it.** *We have confidence to enter the holy places by the blood of Jesus, by the new and living way that He opened for us through the curtain, that is, through His flesh.* The torn veil and the torn body are put together, and the conclusion drawn is an invitation to come in. See the note on Hebrews 4:14–16."}]}, "Matthew 27:54–56": {"title": "The centurion and the women", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first confession of faith after the death comes from the officer in charge of the execution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **centurion and the soldiers keeping watch with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail assigned to the crucifixion. They had done the nailing, cast lots for the clothes, and offered Him the sour wine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Professionals. They had seen men die before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they experienced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Felt **the earth shake** and **saw everything that happened**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has just recorded it: the darkness from the sixth hour, the cry, the veil torn from top to bottom, and an earthquake. See the note on Matthew 27:51–53."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they were **filled with fear**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they feared greatly*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This man really was the Son of God**,\" they said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark gives it to the centurion alone. Luke has a different sentence: *certainly this was a righteous Man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek has no article — *a son of God* is a possible rendering, and Roman ears would have heard it that way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records what they said, in their vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The irony Matthew builds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title has been thrown at Him all day as a taunt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passers-by: *if You are the Son of God, come down from the cross.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests: *He trusted in God; let Him deliver Him now if He will have Him; for He said, \"I am the Son of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one man who says it and means it is the officer who killed Him, and he says it because He did not come down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other centurions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every centurion in the New Testament comes off well. The one at Capernaum whose faith Jesus called greater than any in Israel; this one; Cornelius; and Julius, who kept Paul alive on the ship. See the notes on Matthew 8:10–13 and Acts 10:1–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The women"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A number of women were there as well, **watching from a distance** — women who had **followed Jesus all the way from Galilee and cared for Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *cared for* is **diakonousai** — served, ministered. The root of *deacon*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke names some of them earlier and says they *provided for Him from their substance*. They had been funding the ministry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Joses, and the mother of Zebedee's sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last one had asked, not long before, for her two boys to sit at His right and left in the kingdom. See the note on Matthew 20:24–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is at the cross, and two men are at His right and left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is not there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eleven. Matthew has already recorded it: *then all the disciples forsook Him and fled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's Gospel puts John himself at the cross with Mary. Matthew's list is women."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the women do next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Follow Joseph of Arimathea to the tomb and sit opposite it. Then come back after the sabbath, first light, and find it open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the last at the cross and the first at the tomb, and Matthew keeps them in every scene."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who says it first.** The officer commanding the execution squad, after an earthquake, about a man he had just watched die — and the reason it lands is that the taunt all day had been *come down*."}]}, "Matthew 3:16–17": {"title": "What was said before He had done anything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Father speaks aloud twice in Matthew's Gospel. This is the first, and the timing is the whole point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has not preached a sermon, healed anybody, called a disciple or told a parable. His public ministry begins in the next chapter, after forty days in the desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words come first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is My Son, the One I love. My delight is in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three statements: identity, love, and pleasure. Nothing is conditional and nothing refers to output."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *my delight is in Him* is a verb about being well pleased, taking pleasure in something — and it is in a tense that reads as a settled state rather than a reaction to a recent event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two texts underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is stitched from two places every listener would have known:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*You are my Son* — Psalm 2, spoken of the anointed king", "*in whom my soul delights* — Isaiah 42, the opening of the first Servant Song"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "King and servant, in one line. Matthew's readers would have heard both, and the second one leads to the servant who is pierced for transgressions."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where else the words turn up.** On the mountain at the transfiguration, the same voice says nearly the same thing and adds three words: *listen to Him.* The Father's public statements about the Son are remarkably consistent, and neither of them is about performance."}]}, "Matthew 4:1–4": {"title": "\"If You are the Son of God\" — the word the tempter starts with", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Read what happened immediately before this. At the end of chapter 3, the Father's voice says *this is My Son, the One I love. My delight is in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the first word out of the tempter's mouth is **if**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The attack"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If You are the Son of God, tell these stones to turn into bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not attack the hunger. He attacks the sonship — and he attacks it by suggesting it needs demonstrating. Do something, and the question will be settled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus had nothing to prove. The Father had already said it out loud, in public, before a single miracle had been performed. See the note on Matthew 3:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He answers with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is written: a person does not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes from the mouth of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Deuteronomy 8 — Moses telling Israel why God let them hunger in the wilderness and fed them with manna."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the deliberate parallel. Israel spent forty years in the desert and failed the tests. Jesus spends forty days in one and does not, and He answers each time with a line from the book about the first forty years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word He lives on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every word that comes from the mouth of God.* He has one in hand already: *this is My Son, the One I love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temptation is answered by holding onto what has been said rather than producing evidence for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who led Him there.** *Then the Spirit led Jesus up into the wilderness.* The desert is not a detour or a failure of guidance. Matthew puts the Spirit's leading and the testing in the same sentence, immediately after the dove descends."}]}, "Matthew 4:12–17": {"title": "Light in Galilee", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records a change of address and treats it as a fulfilled prophecy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He left **Nazareth** and went to live in **Capernaum**, a town beside the sea in the territory of **Zebulun and Naphtali**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew names both tribes, which is not how anyone in the first century gave directions. The tribal boundaries had not mattered for centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names them because Isaiah did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali, along the road to the sea, beyond the Jordan, **Galilee of the nations** — the people sitting in darkness have seen a great light.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 9, seven hundred years earlier. The chapter that goes on to say *for unto us a Child is born… and His name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, the Mighty God, the Everlasting Father, the Prince of Peace.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why those two tribes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were the first to go. When Assyria came, 2 Kings records that Tiglath-Pileser took *Gilead and Galilee, all the land of Naphtali*, and carried them away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first territory to fall into the dark is named as the first to see the light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Galilee of the nations\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *Galilee of the Gentiles*. It had been mixed country for centuries, and the Judeans looked down on it — *can anything good come out of Nazareth?* and *search and look, for no prophet has arisen out of Galilee.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where He moved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Zebulun and Naphtali"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their blessings in Genesis 49 are one line each:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Zebulun** — *he will be a harbour for ships.* His name means dwelling, and this is where He dwelt.", "**Naphtali** — *a doe set free.* This is where the woman bound for eighteen years was loosed."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the notes on Genesis 49:13 and Genesis 49:21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Sitting in darkness\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not walking. Sitting — settled in it, no longer trying to get out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the land where death casts its shadow*. Isaiah's word is the same one in Psalm 23: *the valley of the shadow of death.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He began to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Change your minds and turn to God**, because the kingdom of heaven has come near.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metanoeo** — to change the mind, to think differently afterwards. And the reason given is not a threat. It is proximity: because the kingdom has come near."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the other side of Isaiah's sentence.** *On those living in the land where death casts its shadow, **light has dawned**.* The verb is a sunrise. Nobody in the dark did anything to produce it; the morning simply came up over them."}]}, "Matthew 4:5–7": {"title": "The temptation that quotes Scripture", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second attempt, and this time the tempter comes with a Bible verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will give His angels orders concerning You, and they will lift You up in their hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Psalm 91, and it is quoted accurately as far as it goes. The promise is real; the psalm says it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is left out is the line in the middle — *to guard you in all your ways.* The keeping is promised along the road God has for a person, not on a jump the person invents."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The highest point of the temple*, over the Kidron valley — a drop of well over a hundred feet, in full view of the courts below."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The suggestion is a spectacular, public, self-authenticating demonstration. It would have settled the question of who He was in an afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is also written: do not put the Lord your God to the test.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not dispute the psalm. He puts another Scripture beside it, from Deuteronomy 6 — the passage about Massah, where Israel demanded water and asked whether the LORD was among them or not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A promise is not a lever. Testing God is what you do when you are not sure of Him and want proof; and demanding proof is the opposite of the trust the promise was given to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this settles about quoting Scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A verse can be quoted correctly and used wrongly. The correction is not less Bible but more of it — Jesus answers all three temptations from Deuteronomy, and here He answers a quotation with a quotation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read alongside Psalms 91:5–8.** The psalm's promises are real and this app takes them as such. What this scene rules out is treating them as a mechanism to be operated, which is exactly what was being suggested."}]}, "Matthew 5:13–16": {"title": "\"You are\" — before anyone has done anything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two images, and the grammar under both of them is the thing to notice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Not \"become\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **are** the salt of the earth. You **are** the light of the world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present indicative, stated as fact. Jesus is not setting a target. He is telling a crowd — who at this point in Matthew have done nothing but climb a hill and sit down — what they already are."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything He says afterwards follows from the statement rather than earning it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What can actually go wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two images fail in different ways, and neither failure is about quantity."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**salt** can go flat — lose the thing that made it useful. What is left is not weaker salt; it is something that gets walked on", "**light** can be hidden — nobody in the illustration puts out the lamp. They put a bucket over it"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the warnings are about losing distinctiveness and about concealment. Not about shining harder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the attention goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that when they see the good you do, they will give the glory to your Father in heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The visibility is deliberate — a town on a hill, a lamp on a stand, light filling a house. But the sentence ends somewhere other than where it started: what people see is the good; who they credit is the Father."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**A page later.** In chapter 6 Jesus warns against doing good *to be seen by people*. It looks like a contradiction of this and is not — the difference is in the last clause of each. Let them see the good so they glorify God; do not perform it so they admire you. Same visibility, opposite destination."}]}, "Matthew 5:17–20": {"title": "Not torn down, but filled full", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Early in the Sermon on the Mount, He answers a charge nobody has made yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The denial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not imagine that I have come to tear up the Law or the Prophets**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katalusai** — to demolish, to pull down. It is used of a building being taken apart, and of the temple: *destroy this temple*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He raises the accusation before anyone brings it, which means He knew what the sermon would sound like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have come to **fill it full**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plerosai** — to fill, to complete, to bring to its intended fullness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to keep it, in the first instance. To fill it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guarantee"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Until heaven and earth pass away, **not the smallest letter, not the least stroke of a pen, will drop out of the Law**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **iota** — the smallest Hebrew letter, **yod** — and **keraia**, a horn: the tiny serif that distinguishes one Hebrew letter from another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He picks the two smallest marks on a page."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The term"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Until **every last bit of it has been accomplished**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **genetai** — until all comes to pass."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two *untils* in one sentence: until heaven and earth pass, and until it is all accomplished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version of the same saying sits directly beside *the law and the prophets ran up to the time of John*. See the note on Luke 16:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are His, in the same breath, in both Gospels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ranking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever **sets aside one of the smallest of these commands**, and teaches others to do the same, will be **called least in the kingdom of heaven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Least in the kingdom, not outside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The standard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Unless **your righteousness goes further and deeper than the righteousness of the teachers of the law and the Pharisees**, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven at all.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perisseuse** — exceed, overflow, go beyond."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that meant to the hearers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees were the most scrupulous people in the country. They tithed garden herbs and counted paces on the sabbath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Telling a crowd on a hillside that they needed to exceed that is not a call to try harder. Nobody in the audience could out-Pharisee a Pharisee."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sermon does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six paragraphs, each beginning *you have heard that it was said… but I say to you*:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not murder, but anger. Not adultery, but the look. Not divorce by certificate. Not oaths, but plain speech. Not an eye for an eye, but the other cheek. Not love your neighbour and hate your enemy, but love your enemies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one goes inward, past what a court can reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the filling full."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Therefore **you shall be perfect, just as your Father in heaven is perfect**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teleioi** — complete, brought to the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's summary of the same thing: *Christ is the end of the law for righteousness to everyone who believes.* See the note on Romans 10:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Telos** — the end, and also the goal."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two words to hold together.** Not one letter drops out, and not one of them is what He came to keep — He came to fill. And the standard He sets in the same paragraph is higher than the most careful men alive."}]}, "Matthew 5:21–26": {"title": "\"But I say to you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He takes a commandment everyone in the crowd had kept, and moves it somewhere nobody can keep it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The formula"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have heard that it was said**… **But I say to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it six times in this chapter. Nobody had ever spoken that way about the law — the scribes taught by citing older rabbis, and He cites nobody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commandment was *do not murder*. Almost every man on that hillside had a clean record on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But I say to you, anyone who **nurses anger** against his brother… anyone who says 'You are worthless'… anyone who says 'You fool'.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offence moves from the hand to the temper, and from the temper to a word said under the breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which direction the standard is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Sermon on the Mount does not lower the bar to something manageable. It raises it past the reach of anybody in the audience — and He is only getting started; adultery goes the same way four verses later. See the note on Matthew 5:27–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the passage is for. The law was never a ladder; it is a mirror. Paul says so: *by the law is the knowledge of sin*, and *the law was our tutor to bring us to Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who thinks he has kept it has not read it closely enough. Jesus reads it closely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift at the altar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you are bringing your gift to the altar, and right there you remember that **your brother has something against you**, leave your gift where it is.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the direction. Not that you have something against him — that he has something against you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You are the one who is owed nothing, and you are the one told to leave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Leave your gift where it is, in front of the altar. Go.* The offering waits on the stone while a man walks out of the temple to find somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Settle quickly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Settle it with him quickly, **while the two of you are still on the road together**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is two men walking to court. There is a stretch of road before the judge, and He says use it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this leaves the hearer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line is *you will not get out of there until you have paid the very last coin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no relief anywhere in the passage. That is deliberate — the Sermon closes with a house falling flat, and the whole of it drives to one place: a man who cannot produce this righteousness, and needs one given."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He said just before starting.** *Unless your righteousness exceeds the righteousness of the scribes and Pharisees, you will by no means enter the kingdom of heaven.* The most meticulous men alive, and He says you need better than theirs. Then six paragraphs showing what better looks like."}]}, "Matthew 5:27–30": {"title": "\"Already, in his heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second of the six, and it does the same thing to adultery that the first did to murder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have heard… 'Do not commit adultery.' **But I say to you**, anyone who looks at a woman with wanting in his eyes **has already committed adultery with her in his heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seventh commandment concerned an act. He relocates it to a glance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Already.** The Greek is emphatic — it is done, past tense, before anything happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is left standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the question the passage is designed to raise, and the answer is nobody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first paragraph took murder down to a temper. This one takes adultery down to a look. Between them they clear the hillside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anyone who leaves the Sermon on the Mount feeling he has done reasonably well has not been listening. That is the whole point of it: *by the law is the knowledge of sin*, and the law was *our tutor to bring us to Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not making the law harder to keep. He is showing what it always said, and what it always said cannot be kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eye and the hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If your right eye is what trips you up, **tear it out**… if your right hand is what trips you up, **cut it off**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verbs are violent and specific."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the arithmetic is stated: *better to lose one part of you than to have your whole body thrown into hell.* On His own terms that is simply correct — if a hand could be traded for a life, the trade would be worth making."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But a blind man can still want, and a one-handed man can still hate. The surgery does not reach the organ that is actually the problem, and the passage has already named it: *in his heart.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has to be dealt with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing removable is the trouble. That is why the sentence about the heart comes first and the amputations after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel had the only remedy that reaches it: *I will give you a new heart, and put a new spirit within you; I will take the heart of stone out of your flesh and give you a heart of flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An operation nobody can perform on himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Sermon ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two houses, and one of them *fell — and great was its fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He finishes His most demanding sermon with a collapse, and the crowds are *astonished at His teaching, for He taught them as one having authority.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this leaves a reader.** Exactly where it left the hearers: with a standard that is God's own and no way to reach it, and a Man on the hillside who will go on to keep it entirely and then hand what He earned to people who did not. That is the sequence the Sermon is written into."}]}, "Matthew 6:19–21": {"title": "\"Where your treasure is\" — the direction of the sentence", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Wherever your treasure is, that is where your heart will be living.* Most people quote it the other way round — put your heart somewhere and your money follows. Jesus says the reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Treasure first, heart after. The heart is described as taking up residence wherever the valuables already are."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That makes it a diagnosis and a lever at once. A diagnosis, because a person can find out what they actually treasure by looking at where their resources have gone. A lever, because moving the treasure is presented as the thing that moves the affection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three thieves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moths eat it and rust eats it and thieves break in and carry it off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three ways to lose it, covering the three forms wealth took: cloth, metal, and anything storable. The Greek behind *rust* is broader than iron corrosion — it is eating away, decay of any kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is not moralising about wealth here. He is making a practical argument about where things keep."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse after it.** He goes straight on to the eye being the lamp of the body, and then to serving two masters. The passage is a run: where your money is, what your eye is fixed on, who you actually work for. All three ask the same question from a different angle, and Luke's version of the third one is in the note on Luke 16:9–13."}]}, "Matthew 6:25–34": {"title": "Look at the birds", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three times He says do not worry, and in between He points at two things anybody in that field could see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The birds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **do not plant, they do not harvest, they do not store anything away in barns**, and your heavenly Father feeds them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, all denied. And they are the three stages of agriculture — the whole economy of that hillside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Birds are not idle; a bird works hard all day. What it does not do is the thing the crowd was doing: plan the year, secure the surplus, guarantee the future."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are you not worth far more than they are?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The flowers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look how the wildflowers grow out in the open field. **They do not work at it, they do not spin a thread**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs again, and they are women's work — the crowd is being addressed in halves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not even Solomon in all his splendour was dressed like one of these.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The richest man Israel produced, out-dressed by a weed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If God clothes **the grass of the field** like that — grass that is here today and **tossed into the oven tomorrow** — how much more will He clothe you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dried grass was fuel. He is comparing a person to kindling, and arguing from the lesser to the greater."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You of little faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is one word, **oligopistos**, and Matthew uses it five times, always to disciples. It is not a rebuke about doctrine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He attaches it to worry, which tells you what He thinks worry is a shortage of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What worry cannot do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which of you, **by worrying, can add a single hour** to your life?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue that worry is wrong. He argues that it is ineffective, which is harder to dispute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who chases"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People who **do not know God** chase after all of that, and **your heavenly Father knows perfectly well that you need it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and the second is the answer to the first. The need is not denied — it is granted, and the point is that it is already known about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seek first the kingdom of God and **His righteousness**, and **all these other things will be given to you as well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note whose righteousness is to be sought. Not one of your own — that is the whole argument of the Sermon, which has just spent a chapter showing nobody has one. See the note on Matthew 5:27–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All these other things* — the food, the drink, the clothes. Not withheld and not despised; put second, and added."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *will be given* is to add on top, to throw in besides. They arrive as an extra."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Today"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will worry about itself. **Today has enough trouble of its own**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say tomorrow has no trouble in it. He says today's ration is sufficient and there is no provision for carrying a second day's."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse before all this.** *No one can serve two masters… you cannot serve God and mammon.* Then *therefore I say to you, do not worry.* The passage about birds is an answer to a question about who owns you."}]}, "Matthew 6:9–13": {"title": "The prayer, line by line", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "It is the most said prayer in the world, which is exactly why it is worth slowing down over. Jesus gives it as a pattern — *pray like this* — immediately after telling people not to heap up words as though volume helped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Our Father in heaven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in tension, and both matter. **Father** — relationship, nearness, a family word. **In heaven** — and He is not one of us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice it is *our*, not *my*. The whole prayer is plural: our bread, our debts, lead us, deliver us. Jesus taught it to be prayed by someone who has other people in mind, even when they are alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "May Your name be held holy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a request that God become holy. The verb asks that His name be *treated* as what it is — set apart, not used as small change."}, {"k": "h", "t": "May Your kingdom come"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing asked for is not a personal need. And the line that follows tells you what a kingdom coming looks like: *may what You want be done, here on the earth just as it is done in heaven.* Two lines, one request. God's rule arriving is God's will being done."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Give us today the bread we need for today"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek adjective here — **epiousios** — is famous for being almost unknown. It appears in no other surviving Greek text of the period apart from this prayer. Every translation is a considered guess: bread for the coming day, bread that is needed, daily bread."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever it means precisely, the request is small and immediate. Today's bread, today. Nobody is asked to pray for a storehouse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forgive us our debts, as we ourselves have forgiven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Debts, not sins — Matthew uses the commercial word, something owed. And the second half has already happened: *as we ourselves have forgiven*, past tense. It is not a bargain being struck. It is a person praying who has already let somebody off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the only line Jesus goes back and comments on after the prayer ends, so He evidently expected it to be the sticking point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Do not lead us into the time of testing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word covers both testing and temptation. Given James says plainly that God tempts nobody, the request is best read as it stands: *keep us out of the trial* — a prayer of ordinary self-knowledge, from people who would rather not find out how they would do."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the last line.** *For the kingdom is Yours, and the power, and the glory* — it is absent from the earliest manuscripts of Matthew and was almost certainly added early, from church use. It is not a corruption; it is a congregation's Amen that ended up in the text, and it echoes David's prayer in 1 Chronicles. The app prints it because it is what most readers know."}]}, "Matthew 7:7–11": {"title": "\"How much more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three commands, three promises, and then an argument from the worst father in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Ask**, and it will be given to you. **Seek**, and you will find. **Knock**, and the door will be opened to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verbs are present imperatives — continuous. Keep asking, keep seeking, keep knocking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each is more active than the last: asking is words, seeking is movement, knocking is arriving at a specific door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Everyone** who asks receives, and the one who seeks finds, and to the one who knocks the door is opened.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says all three again, as statements of fact rather than instructions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Everyone** — the Greek is **pas**. No category is excluded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Which of you, if your son asked you for **a loaf of bread**, would hand him **a stone**? Or if he asked for **a fish**, would hand him **a snake**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both pairs are things that look alike. A flat stone from the Galilee shore looks like a loaf; an eel-like fish and a water snake are hard to tell apart in a net."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The absurdity is not a father giving something useless. It is a father giving something that looks right and is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The premise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **if you, who have plenty of wrong in you**, know how to give good gifts to your children.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *if you, being evil* — **poneroi**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it to the crowd on the hillside without softening it, and then uses their fatherhood as the good half of the comparison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How much more** will your Father in heaven **give good things to the ones who ask Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **posso mallon** — the standard rabbinic form of argument from lesser to greater."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gap between a bad human father and a good one is small. The gap between any human father and this one is the size of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke has instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How much more will your heavenly Father **give the Holy Spirit** to those who ask Him!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says *good things*; Luke names one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Near the end of the Sermon on the Mount, after *do not judge* and immediately before the golden rule: *therefore, whatever you want men to do to you, do also to them, for this is the Law and the Prophets.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **therefore** connects them. Because your Father gives like that, treat people like that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has already been said about asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters earlier, in the same sermon: *your Father knows the things you have need of before you ask Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *do not worry, saying, \"What shall we eat?\"… **your heavenly Father knows that you need all these things**.* See the note on Matthew 6:25–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about how much faith, how long, or how well. The conditions in the sentence are asking, seeking and knocking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The argument's weakest link is deliberate.** He builds the case on a father He has just called evil, and says the conclusion holds *how much more* at the other end."}]}, "Matthew 8:1–4": {"title": "\"If You are willing\" — and the answer that settles it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The leper's question is the one everybody actually asks, and Jesus answers it in two words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the man was sure of, and what he was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, if You are willing, You can make me clean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has no doubt about the power. **Can** is not in question — he says it flatly. What he does not know is whether Jesus *wants* to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where most people stand. Not wondering whether God is able. Wondering whether He is willing, in their case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus reached out His hand and touched him. \"I am willing,\" He said. \"Be clean.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the man's own word and hands it straight back. The uncertainty is not left hanging for a moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He touches him first. Under the law a leper was untouchable, and anyone touching one became unclean until evening. Jesus reaches out before He speaks. The cleanness travels the other way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the touch says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the first healing Matthew records after the Sermon on the Mount, and he places it deliberately. Jesus has just finished teaching with authority; now He puts a hand on the one person in Israel nobody would touch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instantly the leprosy left him.* No process, no waiting."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why He sends him to the priest.** Leviticus 14 sets out a whole ceremony for cleansed lepers that had, so far as anyone knew, never been performed — two birds, cedar, scarlet, hyssop. Jesus tells the man to go and present himself *so they can see it for themselves*. The priests were about to have to open a chapter of the law that had lain unused."}]}, "Matthew 8:10–13": {"title": "\"As you have believed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one time in Matthew that Jesus is recorded as astonished, and it is by a Roman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He marvelled"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus heard that, **He marvelled**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to wonder, to be amazed. Mark records Him marvelling at unbelief in Nazareth; here it goes the other way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times it is caused by people's faith — the absence of it, and this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the officer had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *only speak a word, and my servant will be healed. For I also am a man under authority… I say to this one, \"Go,\" and he goes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a career soldier and he has worked out the whole thing from military structure. Orders travel. The officer does not have to be present for the order to take effect, only to have the authority behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not ask Jesus to come. He asked Him to speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have **not found faith like this anywhere in Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said out loud, to the crowd following Him, about a Gentile officer of the occupying army."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Many will come **from the east and from the west** and sit down at the table with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob… while **those who thought the kingdom belonged to them by birth** will be put outside.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes one Roman's faith and extends it to a table filled from every direction, and warns the people who assumed the seats were theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word to the officer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go home. Let it be done for you just as you have believed.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Matthew's next clause: *the servant was healed at that very hour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a distance. Jesus never went to the house and never saw the servant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as you have believed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *because you deserved it*, or *because your servant was worth it*, or *because you are a good master* — though the passage shows he was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing named as the measure is what the man believed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records the same formula twice more in his Gospel: to the two blind men, *according to your faith let it be to you*, and to the Canaanite woman, *let it be done for you just as you desire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times, three different people, and every one of them an outsider of some kind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the officer thought he was.** *Lord, I am not worthy that You should come under my roof.* A commander of a hundred men, in a country he was occupying, saying that to a village carpenter. Jesus never contradicts the self-assessment — He simply speaks the word."}]}, "Matthew 8:14–17": {"title": "Matthew tells you which prophecy the healings were", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An evening of healing, and then Matthew stops the narrative to tell you what he thinks was happening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This was to fill out what the prophet Isaiah had said: \"He took up our weaknesses and carried our diseases away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line is from Isaiah 53 — the Servant chapter, the one about being pierced for our transgressions and crushed for our iniquities."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew, writing under inspiration and knowing exactly which chapter he is reaching into, applies it to **an evening of physical healings**. Not to the forgiveness of sins, which is what the chapter is usually preached as. To fevers and demons and bodies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Hebrew he is drawing on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53:4 in Hebrew reads *surely he has borne our sicknesses and carried our pains*. The words are the ordinary ones for illness and physical suffering; they are used elsewhere in the Old Testament of disease."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So Matthew is not stretching the verse. He is quoting it at its plain sense, and attaching it to what he had just watched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Healed everyone who was sick\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scope is worth reading slowly. Not many. Not those with sufficient faith. *Everyone.* Matthew says the same in several other places — great crowds, and all of them healed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in these scenes is turned away, and no one is told they did not qualify."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The fever \"let go of her.\"** Matthew's verb is the one used of releasing a prisoner or forgiving a debt. He touches her hand and the fever lets go — and the next thing she does is get up and serve. Restored, and immediately useful."}]}, "Matthew 8:23–27": {"title": "\"Where is your trust?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew tells the storm in five verses and puts the emphasis in a different place from Mark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who got in first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **He stepped into the boat, and His disciples went with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's order. He gets in; they follow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever happens on that lake happens to men who are where they are because they were following."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for the storm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Without warning **a violent squall** came down on the lake.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **seismos** — the word for an earthquake. Matthew uses it of the ground shaking at the crucifixion and at the tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls this weather an earthquake at sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And Jesus was asleep\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words, set against the earthquake, in the same sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boat is *buried* by the waves — the Greek is being covered over — and He is asleep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds the detail about the cushion in the stern. Matthew leaves it out and lets the contrast stand bare. See the note on Mark 4:35–41."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Lord, save us! We are going down!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version is a prayer. In Mark it is an accusation — *do You not care that we are going down?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has them asking Him to do something. Mark has them asking whether He cares."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why are you so frightened? Where is your trust?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before the sea. Matthew is precise about the order: He addresses them, and then He addresses the storm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you of little faith* — **oligopistos**, the word Matthew uses five times, always to disciples, and always about worry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the sea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **stood up and spoke sharply** to the winds and to the sea, and everything went **completely still**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *completely still* is a great calm — the same adjective as the *great* tempest a few verses earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The size of the calm matches the size of the storm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they conclude"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What kind of man is this**, that even the winds and the sea do what He tells Him?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not answer their own question. Matthew leaves it hanging."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anyone who knew the Psalms had the answer: *He calms the storm, so that its waves are still* — Psalm 107, about the LORD."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had already seen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Matthew 8, and by this point in the chapter He has cleansed a leper, healed the centurion's servant at a distance, healed Peter's mother-in-law, and cast out spirits with a word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched all of it, and still asked what kind of man He was."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the other side of the lake.** Two men out of the tombs, so violent that nobody could pass that way. The storm was on the route to them. See the note on Luke 8:30–33."}]}, "Matthew 8:5–9": {"title": "The centurion's argument", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A Roman officer stops Jesus in the street and makes an argument about authority that Jesus calls the greatest faith in Israel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A centurion — roughly eighty to a hundred men under him, the backbone of the Roman army, and an occupier in a country that hated the occupation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the person he is asking about is *his servant*. A man with the power to replace a slave is out in the road pleading for one, describing the condition in detail: paralysed, in terrible pain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he refuses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, I am not worthy to have You come under my roof.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has already said He will come. The centurion turns down the visit — partly because entering a Gentile home would make a Jewish teacher ceremonially unclean, and he knows it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for less access than he is being offered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only say the word… I am a man under orders myself, and I have soldiers under me. I say to one, \"Go,\" and he goes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here is the reasoning: I know how authority works. I do not need to be present for my orders to be carried out, because the authority is not in my presence. It is in my position."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has understood that Jesus operates the same way — and, crucially, that Jesus is *under* authority too, which is why the word carries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never asks Jesus to do anything except speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have not found such great faith, not even in Israel.* And then the servant is healed at that moment, at a distance, without a word being spoken over him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he built.** Luke's account adds that the Jewish elders vouched for him: *he loves our nation and he built our synagogue.* An occupying officer paid for the local place of worship. That is the man who says he is not worthy to have Jesus in his house."}]}, "Matthew 9:1–8": {"title": "\"Take heart, son\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version of the paralytic, and it is stripped down to the exchange."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew leaves out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd at the door, the four carriers, the roof, the digging, the ropes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark gives all of it. See the note on Mark 2:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew keeps the faith and the words and cuts everything else — which is how he handles most miracles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus got into the boat, crossed over, and came to **His own town**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Capernaum. Matthew calls it His own city; He had moved there from Nazareth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus saw **their faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plural, as in Mark. Whatever it took to get the man in front of Him, Jesus reads it as faith and says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take heart, son. Your sins are forgiven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *take heart* is **tharsei** — be of good cheer, courage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Son** — **teknon**, child."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had raised the subject of sins. The men came about a body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some of the teachers of the law said among themselves, \"**This man is insulting God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their reasoning is correct: sin is against God, so the pardon is God's to give."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **knew what they were thinking** and said, \"**Why are you turning over such dark thoughts in your hearts?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had been said aloud. Answering the interior objection is itself the answer to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Which is easier to say** — 'Your sins are forgiven,' or 'Get up and walk'?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Easier **to say**. Forgiveness is unverifiable; legs are checkable in seconds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the checkable one to establish the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The broken sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **so that you will know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins**\" — and here **He turned to the paralyzed man** — \"**get up, pick up your mat, and go home**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is addressed to the scribes and finishes as a command to somebody else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing happens inside a clause about authority."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the man **got up and went home**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew gives it in five words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the crowds saw it, they were **filled with wonder**, and they **praised God, who had given such authority to human beings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to men* — **tois anthropois**, plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew alone records that detail, and it is the strange one. The crowd does not conclude that a unique person is present. They conclude that this kind of authority has been given to human beings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's Gospel ends with *all authority has been given to Me… **go therefore***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in the next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Jesus passed on from there, He saw a man named **Matthew** sitting at the tax office, and He said to him, \"Follow Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts his own call immediately after this scene, and then a dinner at his house with *many tax collectors and sinners* — and the Pharisees asking why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer: *those who are well have no need of a physician, but those who are sick… **I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners, to repentance**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He addressed first.** Not the legs. Five men went to great trouble about a body, and the first sentence spoken over the man is *take heart* — and then something nobody had asked about."}]}, "Matthew 9:18–22": {"title": "A dying girl, a desperate woman", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew tells both stories in five verses. Mark takes twenty-three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While He was still speaking.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is answering a question about fasting. The ruler comes in mid-sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of **the leading men of the town** came and **knelt in front of Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark and Luke name him: Jairus, a ruler of the synagogue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A synagogue official kneeling in public to a travelling teacher is a considerable thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My daughter has just died. But come and lay Your hand on her, and she will live**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has her already dead. Mark and Luke have her dying, with the news of the death arriving during the delay."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew compresses — he does this throughout, and it is why his miracle accounts are half the length."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come, lay Your hand on her.* One gesture, and a statement about what will follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The journey"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Jesus **stood up and went with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No question asked and no condition set."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption inside the interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **on the way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second story happens inside the first. Both Matthew and Mark keep the structure, and it is the point: a man whose daughter is dead has to stand in the street while somebody else is dealt with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The woman"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A woman who had been **bleeding for twelve years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The girl is twelve years old — Mark records it. For as long as one family has had a daughter, this woman has been bleeding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve years of ceremonial uncleanness under Leviticus 15: unable to touch or be touched, excluded from the temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she touched"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Came up **behind Him** and touched **the edge of His cloak**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kraspedon** — the tassel on the corner of the garment, the **tzitzit** commanded in Numbers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She reaches for the lowest, furthest part of him, from behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she kept saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because she **kept saying to herself**, \"If I can only touch His clothes, **I will be well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is imperfect — she was saying it, repeatedly, while pushing through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**I will be well** is **sothesomai** — saved, made whole."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **turned and saw her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had planned to take it and leave. He will not let her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take heart, daughter. Your faith has made you well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Daughter** — the only time in the Gospels He calls anyone that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier He had called a paralysed man **son**. See the note on Matthew 9:1–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Take heart** — **tharsei**, the same word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He credits her faith, not the tassel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **from that moment the woman was well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says it happened when He spoke. Mark says she felt it when she touched. See the note on Mark 5:25–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He arrives at the house, tells the flute players and the wailing crowd that the girl is not dead but sleeping, and they laugh at Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when the crowd was put outside, He went in and **took her by the hand, and the girl arose**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the delay cost.** A man with a dead daughter waited in a street while a woman who had planned to slip away unnoticed was found, spoken to, and given a name."}]}, "Matthew 9:27–31": {"title": "\"According to your faith\" — and what the question was", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two blind men follow Jesus indoors, and He asks them one question before doing anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you believe that I am able to do this?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *do you have enough faith*, and not *how strong is your faith*. The object of the question is His ability, not their capacity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth holding on to when the next line arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"According to your faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let it be done for you according to your faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read alone, it sounds like a measurement — as much healing as you have faith for. Read as the answer to the question He just asked, it means something narrower: according to what you have just said you believe about Me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their faith had exactly one item in it. They believed He could. That was enough to be going on with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they called Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have mercy on us, Son of David.* It is the first time in Matthew anybody uses that title, and it is a messianic one — two blind men see who He is before most of the people watching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew, who is building a case throughout his Gospel that Jesus is the promised son of David, gives the line to them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The instruction they ignore.** *See that nobody hears about this* — and they went out and spread it everywhere. Matthew records the disobedience plainly and without approval. It is the reaction of people who have just received their sight, and the Gospel does not tidy it up."}]}, "Matthew 9:35–38": {"title": "Sheep without a shepherd", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A summary of everything He had been doing, and then a sentence about what He felt looking at the crowd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teaching in their synagogues, **telling the good news of the kingdom**, and **healing every disease and every weakness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew gives the same three-part summary in chapter 4, before the Sermon on the Mount. This one closes the section, and the two summaries bracket everything in between."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All through **the towns and the villages**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every disease and every weakness* — the Greek is **pasan noson kai pasan malakian**, and Matthew repeats *every* twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What moved"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He looked at the crowds, **something moved deep inside Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **esplanchnisthe** — from **splanchna**, the inward parts, the guts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the strongest word for compassion in the language, and in the Gospels it is used almost exclusively of Jesus. The exceptions are three parable characters: the father seeing his son a long way off, the Samaritan seeing the man in the road, and the master forgiving the debt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because they were **worn ragged and thrown down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eskulmenoi** — flayed, harassed, torn — and **errimmenoi**, thrown down, cast aside like something discarded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Like **sheep with no shepherd anywhere**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is old. Moses used it asking for a successor: *that the congregation of the LORD may not be **like sheep which have no shepherd***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Ezekiel, on Israel's leaders: *My sheep wandered through all the mountains… and no one was seeking or searching for them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had shepherds. There were priests, scribes, elders and Pharisees in every town he walked through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says He looked at the crowd and saw no shepherd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The harvest is enormous, but the workers are few**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He changes the picture mid-sentence — from sheep to a field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sheep need a shepherd; a harvest needs hands. The crowd is both things in two verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **beg the Lord of the harvest to send workers out into His harvest field**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *beg* is **deomai** — to plead out of need."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Send out** is **ekbale** — to thrust out, drive out. It is a forceful word; Matthew uses it of casting out demons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 10, verse 1: *and when He had called His twelve disciples to Him, He gave them power.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are told to pray for workers and are immediately sent as the answer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter break obscures it — the sending follows the instruction with nothing in between."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose harvest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His harvest.* The field belongs to the one being asked."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He was looking at.** Crowds that had teachers, courts, synagogues and a temple — and the word Matthew uses is that they were thrown down, like sheep with nobody over them."}]}, "Micah 5:2": {"title": "\"But you, Bethlehem\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, seven hundred years early, and it names the village."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which Bethlehem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you, **Bethlehem Ephrathah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were two towns of that name. Micah specifies the one in Judah, using its older name, so there is no confusion with the Bethlehem in Zebulun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophecy is precise enough that it could only be fulfilled in one place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Too little to be counted\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Small among the clans of Judah, **too little to be counted**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethlehem did not make the list. When Joshua divided the land and the towns of Judah were enumerated, Bethlehem is not in the Hebrew text of that list at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the village David came from, and even that had not made it a place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of you will come **one for Me**, a ruler over Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For Me.* The ruler is produced for God's own purposes before He is produced for Israel's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whose beginnings reach back **into the far distance, into ancient days, into times older than memory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *from of old, from everlasting* — **mi-qedem, mi-mei olam**. The same phrase is used in Scripture of God's own eternity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A ruler with a birthplace and no beginning. Micah says both in one sentence and does not explain how."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who read it out loud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod asked the chief priests and scribes where the Christ was to be born, and they answered immediately: *in Bethlehem of Judea, for thus it is written by the prophet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They knew the verse, quoted it accurately from memory, and did not go the five miles to look."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it took to fulfil it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph and Mary lived in Nazareth, ninety miles north. A Roman emperor ordered a census of the whole empire, and it required every man to return to his ancestral town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caesar Augustus moved the population of the Roman world to get one pregnant woman to a village too little to be counted."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is beside it in the chapter.** The verse before speaks of a judge of Israel struck on the cheek with a rod; the verses after describe Him standing and shepherding *in the strength of the LORD*, and *He shall be their peace.* Micah puts the striking and the shepherding on the same page."}]}, "Micah 6:8": {"title": "\"He has shown you what is good\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The answer to a question somebody asks in a panic three verses earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Micah 6 is written as a courtroom scene. God calls the mountains as witnesses and puts His case: *O My people, what have I done to you? In what have I wearied you? Testify against Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then He recites what He did — brought them out of Egypt, redeemed them from the house of bondage, sent Moses, Aaron and Miriam."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The panicked question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people answer, and the answer escalates:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With what shall I come before the LORD? **With burnt offerings, with calves a year old**? Will the LORD be pleased with **thousands of rams**, with **ten thousand rivers of oil**? Shall I give **my firstborn** for my transgression?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four bids, each bigger than the last, ending at child sacrifice — which the law forbade absolutely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the mood the verse answers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He has shown you**, mortal, **what is good**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He has told you* — it is already stated, already given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question assumed the requirement was unknown and had to be guessed at with increasingly extravagant offers. The answer says it was never hidden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Mortal\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **adam** — man, human being, the word for the first one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Only this: **to act justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with your God**.\"*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Act justly** — *mishpat*, right judgment. Do the right thing.", "**Love mercy** — the Hebrew is **chesed**, covenant loyalty, steadfast love. Not *do* mercy. **Love** it.", "**Walk humbly with your God** — one Hebrew word, **hatsnea**, found only twice in the Old Testament. It carries the sense of walking carefully, modestly, in step with somebody."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is brought. No rams, no oil, no calves, no firstborn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question was about what to carry to the altar and the answer is about how to walk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three items against *ten thousand rivers of oil*. The reply is deliberately smaller than the question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same word turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Chesed* is the word the psalms translate *mercy* and *lovingkindness* — *His mercy endures forever*, twenty-six times in Psalm 136."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word describing what God is like is the one the people are told to love."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"With your God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last two words. Not *before* Him and not *for* Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enoch is the man Genesis says *walked with God*, and Hebrews explains it as faith. See the note on Hebrews 11:4–7."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being answered.** A man in a panic, bidding upward, asking what will be enough. The reply names three things, none of which cost anything, and opens by saying he was told long ago."}]}, "Nehemiah 13:10–14": {"title": "The singers had gone back to their fields", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah comes back from a trip to Persia and finds the temple system quietly collapsed. What he notices first is where people are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail that tells the story"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Levites and singers who did the work had gone back, each to his own field.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody rebelled. Nobody made a decision. The support stopped arriving, and men with families went home to grow food, because that is what a person does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah's question is aimed at the officials, not at the Levites: *why is the house of God neglected?* He puts the failure on the people responsible for the arrangement, not on the men who left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he actually fixes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The interesting part is the administration. He does not merely restart the collection — he appoints four named men over the storerooms, *for they were counted faithful*, whose job is to distribute fairly among their kinsmen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The previous arrangement had been compromised: a few verses earlier, a priest had given a storeroom over to an official's private use. The problem was not only supply. It was that the system had stopped being trustworthy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah's answer to a giving problem is accountable handling of what is given."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The prayer at the end.** *Remember me for this, O my God, and do not wipe away the good I have done.* Nehemiah prays this four or five times in his book, always about specific administrative work — walls, gates, storerooms, rosters. It is the closing note of a man who did unglamorous jobs and wanted them noticed by the only person whose notice he cared about."}]}, "Nehemiah 13:4–5": {"title": "Tobiah in the storeroom", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The enemy who could not get over the wall ends up living inside the temple, and a priest gives him the keys."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now before this, **Eliashib the priest**, who **had charge of the storerooms of the house of our God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest. The man who, in chapter 3, is named first in the list of builders — *Eliashib the high priest rose up with his brethren the priests and built the Sheep Gate.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He helped build the wall. He also opened the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The connection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And was **closely tied to Tobiah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *near to* — allied, related. Tobiah's family had married into Jerusalem's priestly households, and the alliance is stated as the reason for what follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Had **made ready for Tobiah a large room**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a great chamber*. Not a corner. The largest storeroom in the temple complex."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An **Ammonite** — and the chapter opens with the law being read that day: *the Ammonite and Moabite should never come into the assembly of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book puts the reading and the room three verses apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the room was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **the very place where they used to keep**:*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the grain offerings**", "**the incense**", "**the vessels**", "**the tithes of grain, new wine, and oil** set aside for the Levites, singers, and gatekeepers", "**the offerings for the priests**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah lists every item that had to be moved out to make space."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clearing was not incidental. Somebody carried the vessels of the house of God out of that room and put a man's furniture in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it belonged to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Levites, the singers and the gatekeepers. Their entire living came from those stores."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses on: *I also realised that **the portions for the Levites had not been given**, so that the Levites and the singers who did the work **had gone back to their own fields**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The music stopped and the temple staff went home to farm. Nobody voted on it and nobody announced it. The room was reallocated and the consequence followed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*During all this **I was not in Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah had gone back to Artaxerxes. It happened while the governor was away, and it took him going back to Persia and returning to find out. See the note on Nehemiah 13:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Tobiah was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Ammonite official who mocked the wall — *if a fox climbed up on it, he'd knock the stones right down.* See the note on Nehemiah 4:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His name means *the LORD is good*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he got in.** Not by force. He was invited by the high priest, on the strength of a family connection, into a room that had to be emptied of the tithes first."}]}, "Nehemiah 13:6–9": {"title": "Everything thrown out of the room", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He comes back, finds out what has happened, and empties the room himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the **thirty-second year of Artaxerxes** king of Babylon I **had returned to the king**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said he would. Back in chapter 2 the king asked how long he would be gone and *I set him a time*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve years as governor, and then he went back to his post at court, exactly as promised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The leave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After some time I **asked his leave and came back to Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks a second time. The whole enterprise runs on permissions correctly obtained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There I learned **the wrong that Eliashib had done for Tobiah**, making him a room in the courts of the house of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *wrong* is **ra'ah** — evil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the priest and he names the beneficiary. No euphemism for either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It **grieved me deeply**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction used of Sanballat and Tobiah when they heard he was coming — *it grieved them exceedingly*. See the note on Nehemiah 2:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book uses the identical phrase for both sides."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I **threw all of Tobiah's belongings out of the room**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I cast out*. Personally, by hand, into the courtyard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No hearing, no negotiation with Eliashib, no committee. The furniture goes out the door."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does the same thing to the same courts five centuries later, with a whip of cords and the tables of the money changers. See the note on John 2:13–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cleansing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I gave orders, and **they cleansed the rooms**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the ritual word — **taher**, the term for purifying what has been defiled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Occupation by an Ammonite made the storeroom unclean, and it had to be made fit before anything could go back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he brought back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I brought back into them **the vessels of the house of God, with the grain offering and the incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same items listed in verse 5 as having been displaced, restored in the same order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He reverses the transaction item by item."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He finds the Levites gone to their fields, confronts the officials — *why is the house of God forsaken?* — gathers the people back, and *all Judah brought the tithe of the grain and the new wine and the oil to the storehouse.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he appoints treasurers over the stores, *for they were considered faithful*, so it cannot happen again while he is away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Remember me, O my God, concerning this, and **do not wipe out my good deeds** that I have done for the house of my God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says a version of it four times in this chapter. It is how the book ends."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the whole chapter turns on.** A room. Not the wall, not an army, not a siege — a storeroom reassigned while the governor was out of the country, and the singers going home because there was nothing in it."}]}, "Nehemiah 2:5": {"title": "The sentence he had been waiting four months to say", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is the request the whole book comes out of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then I answered the king.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah is cupbearer to Artaxerxes — the man who tastes the wine first. It put him in the king's presence daily and made him, of necessity, someone the king trusted with his life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king has just noticed his face is sad, and Nehemiah says he *was very much afraid*. Sadness in front of a Persian king was a serious matter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gap between the chapters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The news from Jerusalem came in the month of Chislev. This conversation happens in Nisan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four months. He wept, fasted and prayed through the whole of chapter 1 and said nothing to the king until asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The half-second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the question and this verse there is one clause: *so I prayed to the God of heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing in front of the king, mid-conversation, with no time to withdraw. It is the shortest recorded prayer in the Old Testament and nothing of its content is given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wording"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If it pleases the king, and if your servant has found favor with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Careful, formal court language. He does not demand and he does not explain his theology to a Persian."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Send me to Judah**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not permission to resign. Not funds. He asks to be sent — to go on the king's business, with the king's authority behind him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he names the place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To **the city where my ancestors are buried**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say Jerusalem. He says the city of his fathers' tombs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Persian religion held the graves of ancestors in high regard, and a king could understand a man wanting to repair a family burial ground even if he had no interest in the God of Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah says something entirely true in terms his hearer would feel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that I may rebuild it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated flat, at the end, with nothing after it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was actually asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rebuilding of Jerusalem's walls had been stopped by royal decree, in this same king's reign, recorded in Ezra 4 — *give the command to make these men cease, that this city may not be built.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking Artaxerxes to reverse Artaxerxes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king asks how long he will be gone, and Nehemiah *set him a time*. He had the answer ready."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then letters to the governors, timber from the king's forest, and an escort of army officers and horsemen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had thought through the whole logistics of it during four months of praying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says about the outcome.** *And the king granted them to me, **according to the good hand of my God upon me**.* The letters are in his bag and the credit goes elsewhere. See the note on Nehemiah 2:9–10."}]}, "Nehemiah 2:9–10": {"title": "The two men who were displeased", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He arrives with an escort, and two men in the region are unhappy about it before he has laid a stone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I came to the governors of the province **Beyond the River**, and **I gave them the king's letters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beyond the River* is the official Persian name for the satrapy west of the Euphrates — Nehemiah uses the imperial term, as a court official would."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letters are produced immediately. His authority is established at the border, in writing, before anyone can question it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escort"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king had also sent **army officers and horsemen** with me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezra, going the same road a few years earlier, had deliberately refused an escort — *I was ashamed to request of the king an escort of soldiers, because we had spoken to the king saying, \"The hand of our God is upon all those who seek Him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah takes the cavalry. Ezra declines it. The book records both without ranking them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when **Sanballat the Horonite** and **Tobiah the Ammonite official** heard of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sanballat** — governor of Samaria; a Babylonian name meaning something like *Sin has given life*, after the moon god. Papyri from Elephantine name him as governor, so he is a documented figure outside the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tobiah** — a Hebrew name meaning *the LORD is good*. He is called *the Ammonite servant*, and he keeps turning up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man called The-LORD-is-good is the standing opponent for thirteen chapters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The connections Tobiah had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is married into a Jerusalem family, his son married the daughter of another, and nobles in the city were *sending letters to him*. In chapter 13 he is given a room in the temple itself. See the note on Nehemiah 13:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never attacks from outside. He is inside the correspondence the whole time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What displeased them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were **deeply displeased** that someone had come **to seek the good of the people of Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it grieved them exceedingly with a great grief*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offence named is not the wall, the money, or the royal authority. It is that somebody had come to seek Israel's good."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Nehemiah does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells nobody. Three days after arriving he goes out at night with a few men and one animal, and rides round the ruins in the dark: *I told no one what my God had put in my heart to do at Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he calls the assembly, and only then does he say *let us build.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply when they mock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The God of heaven Himself will prosper us**; therefore we His servants will arise and build, **but you have no heritage or right or memorial in Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Short, and not defensive."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What arrives with him.** Letters, timber, cavalry, and two enemies. All four are in the same paragraph, and Nehemiah lists them without treating the last as a surprise."}]}, "Nehemiah 4:1–3": {"title": "\"If a fox climbed up on it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opposition changes tactics. Chapter 2 was displeasure; this is ridicule."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escalation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It made him **furious**, and his anger **spilled over into scorn**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has him burning with anger and then mocking. The mockery is what the anger turns into when there is nothing else available."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sanballat has no legal move — Nehemiah holds the king's letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The audience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In front of **his companions and the army of Samaria** he sneered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He assembles a crowd first. This is a performance rather than a confrontation; he never speaks to Nehemiah, only about him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four questions"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"**What are these feeble Jews even doing?**\"*", "*\"**Do they think they can rebuild this?**\"*", "*\"**Will they offer sacrifices and finish in a single day?**\"*", "*\"**Can they breathe life back into stones that are nothing now but burned rubble?**\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is a question, and none of them is asked for an answer. It is the standard shape of contempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Feeble** in the Hebrew is *withered, languishing* — the word for a plant going limp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Burned rubble.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an exaggeration. Nebuchadnezzar's men had burned the city a hundred and forty years earlier and the debris was still lying where it fell. Nehemiah's night ride found places where the animal could not get through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sanballat's sneer is factually accurate about the material. The builders are using the burnt stones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Tobiah's line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**That little wall of theirs**,\" he said, \"**if a fox climbed up on it, he'd knock the stones right down**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lightest weight he can name. Not an army, not a battering ram. A fox."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tobiah's contribution is the joke, and he delivers it standing beside Sanballat while the army laughs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Nehemiah does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer them. He turns to God mid-chapter — *hear, O our God, for we are despised* — and then:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So we built the wall, and **the entire wall was joined together up to half its height**, for **the people had a mind to work**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the people had a heart to work*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reply to the mockery is the next verse of the construction report."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When ridicule fails, the opposition moves to a plot to attack, and the builders work with a weapon in one hand and a tool in the other: *every one of the builders had his sword girded at his side as he built.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long it took"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-two days, and the last verse of the chapter about it says the surrounding nations *were very disheartened in their own eyes; for they perceived that this work was done by our God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fox joke was made about a wall that went up in under two months."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the mocking was aimed at.** Not the wall — the builders. *Feeble Jews.* The book answers it by recording the height reached in the same paragraph."}]}, "Nehemiah 7:4–5": {"title": "\"My God put it into my heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The wall is finished, and Nehemiah looks at a city that is still mostly empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The city was **large and spacious, but few people lived in it**, and the houses had not yet been rebuilt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has spent fifty-two days rebuilding the walls against organised opposition, with a trowel in one hand and a weapon in the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And now there is a defended city with almost nobody inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **my God put it into my heart** to gather the nobles and the rulers and the people to be registered by families.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my God gave into my heart*. It is Nehemiah's characteristic phrase — he uses it earlier in the book too, of the work on the wall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not report a vision, a prophet or a voice. He reports a thought arriving, and he says where it came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it looked like from inside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Like his own idea. Like an administrative instinct — a governor deciding a census is the sensible next step."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah's habit through the whole book is to name the source of his instincts as he goes. *The good hand of my God was upon me.* *What my God had put in my heart to do for Jerusalem — I told no one.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **I found the record of the genealogy** of those who had come back first.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He set out to organise a registration and found an existing document — the list of the first return under Zerubbabel, ninety years earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that follows is that list, name by name, family by family, down to the singers, the gatekeepers and the temple servants. The same list appears in Ezra 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the record mattered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The genealogies decided who could serve as a priest — the chapter goes on to describe men who *sought their listing among those who had been registered by genealogy, but it was not found, so they were excluded from the priesthood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thought in a governor's heart, and a census that recovered a lost document and settled who could stand at an altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 8. *All the people gathered together as one man… and Ezra the priest brought the Law before the assembly*, and read it from morning until midday, and the Levites *gave the sense, and helped them to understand the reading.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The registration led straight to the Book being opened in the square."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was before this.** Cupbearer to Artaxerxes — a servant with a wine cup, in a Persian palace, four months' journey from a ruined city he had never seen. The first thing recorded of him is that he asked how Jerusalem was, and sat down and wept."}]}, "Numbers 11:31–35": {"title": "The graves of craving", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They got exactly what they asked for, in a quantity nobody could have wanted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A wind went out from the LORD and **drove quail in from the sea**, dropping them all around the camp — **a day's walk in every direction, lying about three feet deep**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quail migrate across that region in vast flocks and come down exhausted. The mechanism is ordinary; the timing and the scale are not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three feet deep, for a day's journey in each direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gathering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All that day and night and the next day the people gathered quail; **even the one who gathered least brought in heaps of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-six hours of collecting without stopping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Compare the manna: *he who gathered much had nothing left over, and he who gathered little had no lack.* With the manna nobody could get ahead. With the quail everybody did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **while the meat was still between their teeth, before it was even chewed**, the LORD's anger blazed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The judgement lands at the first mouthful."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They named the place **Kibroth-hattaavah — \"the graves of craving\"** — because there they buried the people who had given in to their craving.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **taavah** is desire, longing, craving — the same root as the word in verse 4, where *the mixed crowd was seized with a craving.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The place is named after the appetite rather than the plague."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He gave them their request, **but sent leanness into their soul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 106, and it is one of the more frightening sentences in the Psalms. The request was granted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the camp moves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From Kibroth-hattaavah the people traveled to **Hazeroth**, and they stayed at Hazeroth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A flat travel note, and the writer records it without comment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next thing that happens at Hazeroth is Miriam and Aaron speaking against Moses, and Miriam coming out leprous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now these things became our examples, to the intent that **we should not lust after evil things as they also lusted**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1 Corinthians 10, and Paul lists the wilderness failures one by one as written down *for our admonition.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was actually wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that they wanted meat. God had given them quail once before, in Exodus 16, without any anger attached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter locates the problem elsewhere: *you have wept in the hearing of the LORD, saying, \"Who will give us meat to eat? **For it was well with us in Egypt.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rejection of the present provision, and the rewriting of slavery as the good years. See the note on Numbers 11:4–9."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other thing that happened in this chapter.** Seventy elders received the Spirit, and two of them — Eldad and Medad — prophesied in the camp instead of at the tent. Joshua wanted them stopped. Moses said *would that all the LORD's people were prophets.* The best line in the chapter is spoken by the man who had just asked to die."}]}, "Numbers 11:4–9": {"title": "\"Nothing to look at but this manna\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A complaint about food, and the writer interrupts it to describe the food."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **mixed crowd** traveling with them was seized with a craving, and soon **all Israel was weeping** again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *mixed crowd* is the rabble — the non-Israelites who came out of Egypt with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It starts at the edge of the camp and spreads to everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for the craving is intense: they lusted a lusting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they remembered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We remember **the fish we ate for free in Egypt**, the cucumbers and melons, the leeks and onions and garlic.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six items, listed with relish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**For free.** They were slaves. Nothing in Egypt was free — it cost them four hundred years, the bricks without straw, and their sons in the Nile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Memory has edited the price out and left the menu."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But now **our appetite is gone. There's nothing to look at but this manna**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *our soul is dried up; there is nothing at all except this manna before our eyes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**This manna** — the definite article, dismissive. The bread of angels, and it has become *this*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops the narrative to describe it:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The manna was **like coriander seed, pale like resin**. The people would **gather it, grind it or crush it, boil it in pots and shape it into cakes**; it tasted like something baked with oil. It **fell with the dew each night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is an author's interjection, and it functions as a defence of the food."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He also notes there were six ways to prepare it. It was not monotonous; they were."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A wind went out from the LORD and **drove quail in from the sea**… **lying about three feet deep** across the ground, a day's walk in every direction.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked for meat and got a month of it. God is quoted earlier in the chapter: *until it comes out of your nostrils and becomes loathsome to you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The graves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While the meat was still between their teeth… the LORD's anger blazed, and He struck them with a severe plague. They buried the dead there, and named the place **Kibroth-hattaavah — \"the graves of craving.\"***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A place name preserving what killed them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 106 sums it up: *He gave them their request, but sent leanness into their soul.* See the note on Numbers 11:31–35."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What familiarity had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the manna changed. It fell the same way, tasted the same, and arrived every morning wrapped in dew."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What changed was that they had been eating it long enough to stop seeing it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses does in the middle of this chapter.** Asks God to kill him. *If You treat me like this, please kill me here and now — if I have found favour in Your sight — and do not let me see my wretchedness.* The complaint about the food broke the man leading them."}]}, "Numbers 12:4–9": {"title": "\"Not so with My servant Moses\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A family quarrel, settled by God appearing at the tent door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What started it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Miriam and Aaron **spoke against Moses because of the Ethiopian woman whom he had married**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the real grievance: *\"**Has the LORD indeed spoken only through Moses? Has He not spoken through us also?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron is the high priest. Miriam is a prophetess, and had led the women with tambourines after the sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their claim is not baseless. God had spoken through both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. The narrator supplies the only comment:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the man Moses was **very humble, more than all men who were on the face of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written by Moses, according to the traditional authorship, which readers have found awkward for a very long time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suddenly the LORD said to Moses, Aaron, and Miriam, \"**The three of you, come out to the meeting tent**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *suddenly*. There is no delay and no hearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD came down **in a pillar of cloud**, stood at the entrance of the tent, and called out, \"**Aaron and Miriam**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two names. Moses is summoned with them and then left out of the address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says about prophets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When there is **a prophet among you**, I reveal myself to him **in visions** and speak to him **in dreams**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mar'ah** and **chalom**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the clearest statement in the Old Testament of how prophetic revelation normally worked. God names His own method."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are indirect — something seen, and something dreamed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But not so with My servant Moses**. He is **faithful in all my house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he is entrusted with all My house*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes it: *Moses indeed was faithful in all His house as a servant… but Christ as a Son over His own house.* See the note on Hebrews 3:7–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The difference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"With him I speak **face to face, plainly and not in riddles**, and **he sees the very likeness of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *mouth to mouth*, and *not in dark sayings* — **chidot**, riddles, enigmas."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus says the same: *the LORD spoke to Moses face to face, as a man speaks to his friend.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **why were you not afraid to speak against my servant Moses?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The departure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD's anger burned against them, **and he departed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that He went. The cloud lifts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when the cloud departed from above the tabernacle, suddenly **Miriam became leprous, as white as snow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron is not struck. He is the high priest, and a leprous high priest would have ended the priesthood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Aaron does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Oh, my lord! Please **do not lay this sin on us**… please do not let her be as one dead.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls Moses *my lord*, which is the whole argument conceded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Moses cried out to the LORD, saying, \"**Please heal her, O God, I pray!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words in the Hebrew. The shortest prayer in the Old Testament, and it is for the woman who had just attacked him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven days shut out of the camp, and *the people did not journey till Miriam was brought in again*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole nation waits a week for her."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God says about His own method.** Visions and dreams, to prophets, as the norm — and then names one exception, and the exception says nothing in his own defence."}]}, "Numbers 13:21–25": {"title": "The valley of the cluster", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twelve men walk the length of the country for forty days and bring back one branch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How far they went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the wilderness of Zin** as far as **Rehob, near the pass to Hamath**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The southern desert to the far north. They covered the whole land, on foot, in enemy territory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they saw at Hebron"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where **Ahiman, Sheshai, and Talmai** lived — **descendants of Anak**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The giants are named. Three of them, individually, which means somebody got close enough to learn who they were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebron is where Abraham, Sarah and Isaac were buried. The patriarchs' graves are in the town the Anakim were holding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parenthesis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*(Hebron had been built **seven years before Zoan in Egypt**.)*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An aside about comparative city dating, dropped into a military report."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zoan was a major Egyptian centre. Whoever wrote this expected readers who would find the comparison meaningful."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cluster"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They cut down **a single branch with one cluster of grapes so heavy that two men carried it between them on a pole**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One cluster. Two men. A pole."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only physical evidence they bring back, and it is beyond argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What else"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Along with some **pomegranates and figs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three of the seven species Deuteronomy uses to describe the land — *a land of wheat and barley, of vines and fig trees and pomegranates.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They named that place **the Valley of Eshcol — \"the valley of the cluster\"** — because of the cluster they cut there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The place is renamed after the fruit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forty days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **forty days** of searching the land, they made their way back.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That number becomes the sentence: *for each of the forty days you explored the land, you will bear your guilt a year.* See the note on Numbers 14:26–35."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A day for a year, measured against the walk they took and would not finish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with the evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next paragraph: *they showed them the fruit of the land. \"It truly does flow with milk and honey — here is its fruit. **But** the people who live there are strong.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They hold up the grapes and then put a *but* after them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten men saw the cluster and reported that the land *devours those who live in it.* See the note on Numbers 13:26–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caleb. He walked the same forty days, saw the same three giants, and carried the same pole."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us go up at once and take it. We can certainly do it.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the fruit came from.** Not the coastal plain or the easy country. Eshcol is beside Hebron, in the hill country, where the Anakim were. The best grapes in the land were growing where the giants lived."}]}, "Numbers 13:26–33": {"title": "\"In our own sight, and so we looked to them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twelve men see the same country for forty days and come back with two reports."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What everybody agreed on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It truly does **flow with milk and honey** — here is its fruit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All twelve say that. The cluster of grapes took two men and a pole to carry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And all twelve agreed on the difficulties too: strong people, vast cities, towering walls, the descendants of Anak, and four named nations holding the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The facts are not in dispute anywhere in this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that changes everything"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But** the people who live there are strong.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten men have the same information as Caleb and put a *but* in the middle of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ephes ki** — a strong adversative: *only, however, except that*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word, and the report changes direction. Everything before it is true and everything after it is true; the word is what joins them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Caleb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Caleb **quieted the people** before Moses and said, \"**Let's go up at once and take it. We can certainly do it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is emphatic — *we are well able*. It is not bravado about Israel's army; the chapter has already listed the giants and he is not disputing them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We can't attack those people — they're far stronger than we are.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then it escalates into something else entirely:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The land we explored devours those who live in it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is no longer a report. Forty days earlier it was a land flowing with milk and honey; now it eats people. The text calls it what it is — *a bad report*, and the Hebrew word is the one for slander."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Next to them we felt like grasshoppers, and that's how we looked to them too.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the two halves in order. First: *in our own sight*. Then: *and so we were in their sight*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They decided what they were, and then reported that the giants had reached the same conclusion. Nobody in Canaan had told them that. It is a guess about somebody else's opinion, made out of their own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The self-assessment came first, and the world's assessment is what they projected out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the giants actually thought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "We find out forty years later. Rahab, in Jericho: *I know that the LORD has given you the land, and that **the terror of you has fallen on us**… our hearts melted, and there remained no courage in anyone because of you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She says it had been that way *when you came out of Egypt* — the whole time the ten spies were feeling like grasshoppers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The giants were terrified. The grasshoppers were the ones who reported otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Caleb and Joshua say the next day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The land we passed through is **an exceedingly good land**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**Do not fear the people of the land, for they are our bread**; their protection has departed from them, and the LORD is with us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Our bread** — the Hebrew is *they are our food*. The same men, the same walls, and the obstacle described as the provision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Another spirit\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **My servant Caleb, because he has a different spirit in him and has followed Me fully**, I will bring into the land where he went.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *another spirit*. See the note on Numbers 14:20–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty-five years later, at eighty-five, he asks Joshua for the hill country with the fortified cities in it, names the same Anakim, and takes it. See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it cost.** The next chapter: *as surely as I live, I will do to you the very things you spoke in My hearing.* They said *if only we had died in this wilderness*, and that is precisely what was granted. See the note on Numbers 14:26–35."}]}, "Numbers 14:1–4": {"title": "The night they wept", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A whole nation cries all night over a country they have not seen, on the strength of a report from ten men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That night the whole assembly broke into loud weeping, and **the sound did not fade until morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hours of it. Two million people, according to the census figures, weeping in the dark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened. No battle has been fought, no one has been hurt. They have received information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If only we had died in Egypt — or here in this wilderness!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hold that sentence. It is the exact fate God grants them twelve verses later, quoted back word for word: *as surely as I live, I will do to you the very things you spoke in My hearing… in this wilderness your bodies will fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked for it in so many words, in the dark, and it was heard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why is **the LORD bringing us into this land only to fall by the sword**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have redescribed the rescue as a trap. The God of the plagues, the sea and the manna is now a God who brought them out to kill them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Our wives and children will be taken as plunder.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the fear that undoes them, and it is the one God answers most directly: *as for your children — the ones you said would be taken as plunder — **I will bring them in**, and they will know the land you rejected.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very thing they were afraid of losing is the thing that inherits. Their fear was not only wrong; it was inverted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Let's go back to Egypt\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they said to one another, \"**Let's choose a leader and go back to Egypt.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They get as far as a leadership plan. Not a moment of panic — an organised proposal to reverse the exodus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt is where they were slaves, where the boys were thrown in the Nile, where they groaned until God heard. Two years out, it is the safe option."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at the door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are camped at Kadesh Barnea, on the southern edge of the land. The journey from Sinai had taken about eleven days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are within walking distance of everything promised to Abraham, and they spend the night asking to go back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses and Aaron do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *they fell on their faces before all the assembly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Joshua and Caleb tear their clothes and plead — *the land is exceedingly good… only do not rebel against the LORD.* The assembly's answer is *stone them with stones.* See the note on Numbers 14:5–10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The forty years.** *For each of the forty days you explored the land, you will bear your guilt a year.* One year in the wilderness for each day the spies were out. The wandering is measured against the walk they would not take."}]}, "Numbers 14:20–25": {"title": "\"A different spirit\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The forgiveness and the consequence are in the same paragraph, and one man is pulled out of it by name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I have forgiven them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD said, \"**I have forgiven them, as you asked.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had just interceded — *pardon the iniquity of this people, according to the greatness of Your mercy* — and the answer is granted in five words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not withheld and it is not partial. And the next sentence is still *not one of them will see the land.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ten times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet tested Me these ten times** and would not listen to My voice.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten. The rabbis counted them: at the Red Sea, at Marah, twice over the manna, at Rephidim, the golden calf, Taberah, Kibroth-hattaavah, and now this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He kept a count and states it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**None who treated Me with contempt** will see it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to spurn, to despise. It is a strong word, and it is aimed at what they did with the report, not at their conduct generally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Caleb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But My servant Caleb has a different spirit in him and has followed Me wholeheartedly.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things said about him, and each is worth its own line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My servant** — the title used of Moses, Abraham, David, and of the Servant in Isaiah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A different spirit** — the Hebrew is *another spirit*, a spirit other than the one in everybody else. He had walked the same forty days, seen the same walls, and measured himself against the same giants, and something in him was of a different kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Followed Me wholeheartedly** — literally *filled after Me*, followed fully, all the way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will bring him into the land he explored, and his descendants will inherit it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land he explored. The specific ground he walked as a spy is deeded to him personally, forty-five years before he collects it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does collect it, at eighty-five, and he asks for the part with the giants on it. See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Turn back tomorrow and set out into the wilderness, toward the Red Sea.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Back the way they came. Toward the sea they had walked through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened the next morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They changed their minds — *we have sinned; we will go up.* And Moses told them not to, because the LORD was not among them. They went anyway and were beaten back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A day late, and doing it on their own strength, which is a different thing from faith even when the action looks the same."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two who came out.** Of everyone twenty and over who left Egypt, Joshua and Caleb are the only two who entered. Two out of a nation, and the thing that separated them was not courage in battle — none had been fought. It was what they said about a country after looking at it."}]}, "Numbers 14:26–35": {"title": "\"The very things you spoke in My hearing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God answers the complaint, and the answer is the complaint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have heard every murmur they raise against Me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *murmur* is the grumbling word used all through the wilderness accounts. It is not shouted; it is muttered among themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He reports having heard all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As surely as I live, declares the LORD, **I will do to you the very things you spoke in My hearing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then He quotes them: *\"In this wilderness your bodies will fall.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Go back one page. Their words the night before were *if only we had died in Egypt — or here in this wilderness!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives them exactly what they asked for, in their own phrasing, and says so explicitly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is excepted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Except Caleb son of Jephunneh and Joshua son of Nun.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two who said something else. Caleb: *let's go up at once and take it — we can certainly do it.* Joshua, with him, tearing his clothes: *the land is exceedingly good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men out of a nation, and the difference between them and everybody else is what came out of their mouths about the same country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for your children — **the ones you said would be taken as plunder** — I will bring them in, and they will **know the land you rejected**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He repeats their own fear back to them and reverses it in the same sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The generation that was afraid for their children lost the land; the children got it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For each of the forty days you explored the land, you will bear your guilt a year** — forty years.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A day for a year. The wandering is measured against the reconnaissance — the walk they took and then would not finish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forty years of shepherding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your children will be shepherds here for forty years, bearing the weight of your unfaithfulness **until the last of your bodies lies in the wilderness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The young ones spend their twenties and thirties burying the generation that would not go in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any suggestion the giants got stronger, or that the land became harder to take. It was as takeable the next morning as it had been the day before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua and Caleb walked into it forty years later, and Caleb at eighty-five asked for the hill country with the Anakim on it — *give me this mountain.* See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same giants. A different man's report about them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God said before all this.** *\"I have forgiven them, as you asked.\"* Moses had interceded and the forgiveness is granted in verse 20, five verses before the sentence. Forgiven, and still not going in — the consequence and the pardon are both in the same paragraph."}]}, "Numbers 14:5–10": {"title": "\"The shade that sheltered them is gone\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men against a whole camp, and the argument they make is not the one you would expect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joshua and Caleb do not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not say the giants are small, or that the ten scouts exaggerated. The report was accurate — the people were strong and the cities were fortified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What they dispute is the conclusion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strange image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We will swallow them up. The shade that sheltered them is gone, but the LORD is with us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is literally *their shadow has departed from them*. In that world, shade meant protection — the shade of a rock in the desert, the shadow of a king's hand, the shadow of God's wings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the argument is: whatever was covering these people has been withdrawn, and what is covering us has not. It is not a claim about relative strength. It is a claim about who still has cover."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it costs them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The whole assembly talked of stoning them.* That is the immediate response to the most faith-filled speech in the chapter, and the glory of the LORD appears at the tent precisely as the stones are being picked up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"bread.\"** Joshua and Caleb's phrase for swallowing the enemy is the ordinary Hebrew for eating — the giants will be *bread* for us. It is a vivid, almost crude reversal of the ten scouts' report, which had said the land devoured its inhabitants. One party says the land eats people. The other says we will eat them. Same country, two readings."}]}, "Numbers 16:1–15": {"title": "\"You take too much on yourselves\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The complaint is made in the language of a compliment to everybody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who leads it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Korah, **a Levite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an outsider. Korah is Kohathite — the branch of Levi that carried the ark, the table, the lampstand and the altar on their shoulders. He had the most sacred haulage job in Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he was Moses' first cousin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who joins him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Dathan and Abiram and On, and with them **two hundred and fifty leaders of Israel, men of name and standing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *men of renown*, called of the assembly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is not a mob. It is the leadership."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You take too much on yourselves**,\" they said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you have gone too far*, *enough from you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The whole community is holy, every one of them, and the LORD is among them all. Why do you lift yourselves above the assembly of the LORD?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clause of that is true. God had said it at Sinai — *you shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Korah takes an accurate statement about the nation and uses it to conclude that no particular calling exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Moses' first response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Moses heard it, **he fell facedown**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer. Numbers records him doing this repeatedly — at Kadesh, at Taberah, here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man accused of lifting himself up goes down on the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test he proposes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In the morning **the LORD will show who is His** and who is holy… **Take censers**, all of you, and put fire and incense in them before the LORD tomorrow, and **we will see whom He chooses**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not defend the appointment. He hands the question over and sets a time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Incense was priest's work exclusively. Two hundred and fifty men are invited to do it, and the offer is not a trick — it is the test they asked for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question back to Korah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You Levites — **is it a small thing that God has drawn you close** to serve at His dwelling? **Yet you reach for the priesthood too**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *drawn you close* is **qarab** — brought near. It is the word for approaching the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He already had the nearness. The complaint is about the office."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Dathan and Abiram"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sent for Dathan and Abiram, but **they refused to come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And their answer inverts the whole exodus:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You brought us out of a land flowing with milk and honey** to die in this desert.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That phrase describes Canaan, in every other place it appears in the Torah. They apply it to Egypt — the country of the brick quotas and the river of dead babies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now you would **lord it over us**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Moses' defence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses was **deeply grieved** and said to the LORD, \"**I have not taken so much as a donkey from them, nor wronged a single one**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only thing he answers is the charge of profiteering, and he answers it to God rather than to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel says almost the same words at the end of his life: *whose ox have I taken, or whose donkey have I taken?*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the whole quarrel is about.** Access. Korah, who carried the holy things, wanted to burn the incense — and Moses answers by handing out two hundred and fifty censers and letting God decide."}]}, "Numbers 16:16–35": {"title": "The ground opens", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The test Moses proposed is carried out, and twice in the middle of it he tries to stop what is coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Korah and all his company came with their censers, **fire and incense burning**, and stood at the entrance of the tent, **the whole assembly gathered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They turn up. All two hundred and fifty go through with it, in front of the nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Korah has assembled the crowd as an audience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The glory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The glory of the LORD appeared.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He told Moses and Aaron to **step away from the people**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **they fell on their faces and pleaded**, \"**O God, will You be angry with the whole community when one man sins?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Told to get clear, they go down on the ground and argue for the people who had spent the morning accusing them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the second time in the chapter Moses hits the dirt. Both times it is on behalf of his accusers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham did the same over Sodom, and Moses himself had done it at Sinai over the calf."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD had them **warn the people to move back** from the tents of Korah, Dathan, and Abiram.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The intercession works. The judgment narrows from the community to the three households that led it, and a warning is issued so the rest can get clear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people drew away.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By this you will know the LORD has sent me**: if these men die **an ordinary death**, He has not. But **if the LORD does a new thing**, you will understand they scorned Him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *a new thing* is *if the LORD creates a creation* — **bara**, the verb of Genesis 1, used only of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses stakes his entire commission on it. If they die of natural causes, he was never sent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As he finished speaking, **the ground beneath the rebels split open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately. He had said *by this you will know*, and the sentence had not cooled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they and their households went down alive**. **It closed over them**, and they were gone from among the assembly.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **fire came out from the LORD** and took the **two hundred and fifty men who had offered the incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different judgments in one paragraph. Dathan and Abiram's households by the ground; the two hundred and fifty by fire, at their censers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same fire that had consumed the offering at the tabernacle's dedication."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Israel **fled at the sound of it**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did not die"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next chapter names *the sons of Korah* — they were not swallowed with their father. Numbers 26 says so plainly: *nevertheless the children of Korah did not die.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven psalms carry their name in the title, including *as the deer pants for the water brooks* and *how lovely is Your tabernacle*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The family of the man who wanted to be nearer to God ended up writing Israel's songs about wanting to be nearer to God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was done with the censers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bronze was hammered into a covering for the altar, *as a sign to the children of Israel* — a permanent reminder, bolted to the place where the offerings were made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens the next day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nation blames Moses and Aaron for it, and a plague starts. Aaron runs into the middle of it with a censer. See the note on Numbers 16:41–50."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses did twice.** Fell on his face for the people accusing him — before the test and during it. The narrowing of the judgment happens because of the second one."}]}, "Numbers 16:41–50": {"title": "Between the dead and the living", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man runs into a plague carrying a fire pan, and stops it by standing still."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The next day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the next day** the whole community murmured again against Moses and Aaron.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One day after the ground opened and fire fell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have killed the people of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They watched the earth swallow three households and fire take two hundred and fifty men at their censers, and concluded that Moses did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they call the rebels *the people of the LORD*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As they gathered in anger, **the glory of the LORD appeared**, and **a plague began to move among them**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses said to Aaron, \"**Quickly — take your censer, put fire from the altar and incense in it, and hurry to the people** to make things right, for **wrath has gone out and the plague has begun**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two details in it are exact:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Fire from the altar.** Not any fire. The fire off the bronze altar where the sacrifice burned. Korah's men had brought their own; two of Aaron's own sons had died for offering *strange fire*.", "**Incense.** The same substance the two hundred and fifty were killed holding. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The identical act, done by the right man with the right fire, does the opposite thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The run"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Aaron ran. **He ran into the midst of the assembly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Toward it. Into a moving plague, into the crowd that had spent the morning accusing him of murder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is in his eighties."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there **he stood — between the dead and the living** — holding up the incense, **and the plague was stopped**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he stood between the dead and the living*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plague was running through the camp in a direction. He put himself on the line it was travelling and it did not pass him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The plague had killed **14,700 people**, besides those who had died in the affair of Korah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen thousand seven hundred died. The text does not say how many were behind him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **stood in the gap**, an old man with a censer, and **mercy held the line**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel later uses the same picture, looking for somebody to do it: *I sought for a man among them who would make a wall, and **stand in the gap** before Me on behalf of the land, that I should not destroy it — **but I found no one**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The return"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **returned to Moses at the entrance of the tent, the plague at an end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He walks back. Nothing is said about what the people said to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in his hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a sword, not an argument, not a defence of himself. A censer with coals off the altar and a handful of spice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in the next chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The twelve rods laid up before the LORD overnight, and Aaron's alone *sprouted and put forth buds, produced blossoms and yielded ripe almonds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dead stick with fruit on it, kept in the ark as the final answer to Korah's question about who was chosen."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the priest ends up.** In the middle of the people who had just accused him, facing the wrong way — with his back to the safety and the incense held up."}]}, "Numbers 18:21–32": {"title": "A tithe of the tithe", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one tribe with no land, and the arrangement that fed it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after Korah's rebellion and Aaron's rod that budded. Korah's complaint was about who gets to come near; this chapter sets out what the nearness cost and what it paid."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Levites get"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To the Levites **I give the tithe of Israel** as their share, **in return for the work they do at the tent**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is stated as wages. The Hebrew is *for their service which they serve*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do not get"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They **will not have land either**, so this is their living.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every other tribe received territory in Canaan. Levi received forty-eight cities scattered through everyone else's allotments, and no block of its own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua records the reason: *the LORD God of Israel was their inheritance.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second tithe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the Levites too must give**: **a tithe of the tithe**, the best of what they receive, lifted up to the LORD and given to Aaron the priest.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tribe that lives on other people's tithes tithes on what it receives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten percent of the ten percent goes to the priests — Aaron's household within Levi."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The best of what they receive\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set aside **the finest part** as holy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the fat*, **cheleb** — the choicest portion, the part of the sacrifice that always belonged to God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a tenth of whatever. A tenth off the top."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the rest is yours to eat freely, wherever you are**, you and your households, for **it is your wages for your service**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in that sentence do a lot: **freely**, and **wherever**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest carried no restrictions. It could be eaten anywhere, by anyone in the household, with no ceremony attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do this rightly, and you will not bear guilt** over the holy gifts.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The protection runs to the Levites themselves. Handling holy things carelessly was the danger the previous two chapters had just demonstrated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened when it stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah, coming back from Persia, found the storerooms emptied for Tobiah and the Levites gone home to their fields because their portions had not been given. The temple music stopped. See the note on Nehemiah 13:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The arrangement in this chapter is what kept the tent staffed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument goes in Hebrews"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who are of the sons of Levi, who receive the priesthood, **have a commandment to receive tithes** from the people according to the law… But he whose genealogy is not derived from them **received tithes from Abraham**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's point is that the Levitical arrangement was commanded, and the older one — Abraham to Melchizedek — was not. See the notes on Genesis 14:17–20 and Hebrews 7:8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The direction of the whole chapter.** A tribe that owns nothing, paid from what everyone else brings, and required to give the first tenth of that away before eating any of it."}]}, "Numbers 18:8–20": {"title": "\"I am your portion\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The tribe that gets no land is told what it gets instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Directly after Korah's rebellion and Aaron's rod that budded. Korah's complaint was about who gets to come near; this chapter sets out what the nearness costs and what it pays. See the note on Numbers 16:1–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And I give you a portion**,\" the LORD said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have given you the charge of My heave offerings*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The offerings the people bring Me — **the grain gifts, the sin offerings, the guilt offerings, the holy things** — these are yours.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The first ripe fruits, the first oil and wine and grain, the firstborn of the flocks, the things devoted to Me** — I give them to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The firsts. Not what is left over after the altar; the first of each crop and each flock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yours and your sons' and daughters'** to eat.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole household, sons and daughters both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The redemption price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Every **firstborn son and unclean animal is redeemed at a set price**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five shekels, according to the verses that follow. A firstborn son belonged to God from the night of the Passover, and was bought back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is your bread, **a covenant of salt, unbreakable, forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Salt because salt does not change, ferment or spoil. It is the term for an agreement that cannot be dissolved. See the note on Leviticus 2:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase is used of David's throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do not get"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will have **no land of your own among Israel, no inheritance of fields**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every other tribe received territory. Levi received forty-eight towns scattered through everyone else's allotments — including the six cities of refuge. See the note on Numbers 35:9–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man fleeing a death ran into a Levite town and lived off the priests' provision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **I am your portion and your inheritance**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *portion* is **cheleq** — a share, an allotted piece of ground. It is a surveying word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God uses the land word about Himself and gives it to the tribe with no land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When others count their acres, **you will count Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up in the psalms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is **the portion of my inheritance** and my cup; You maintain my lot. The lines have fallen to me in pleasant places.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David, who owned a kingdom, uses the Levite's line about himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Asaph: *my flesh and my heart fail, but God is the strength of my heart and **my portion forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah in the ruins: *\"**The LORD is my portion**,\" says my soul, \"therefore I hope in Him.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Levites lived on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tithe, and they tithed on that in turn — a tenth of the tenth, the best of it, up to the priests. See the note on Numbers 18:21–32."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The trade.** No fields, no boundary stones, no inheritance to pass on. In place of all of it, one sentence: *I am your portion.*"}]}, "Numbers 19:1–10": {"title": "The red heifer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The strangest ordinance in the law, and everything about it runs backwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD gave Moses and Aaron **a strange and gracious provision**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew calls it **chuqqat ha-torah** — the statute of the law. Jewish tradition classes it among the **chuqqim**, the decrees given without a stated reason, and rabbinic literature treats it as the one even Solomon could not work out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The animal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **red heifer**, **perfect and unblemished**, one that has **never worn a yoke**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three requirements:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Red** — the Hebrew is **adummah**, from the root for red, blood, earth, and **adam**.", "**Without blemish** — the standard sacrificial requirement.", "**Never worn a yoke** — never put to work. Nothing had been got out of it."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A female, which is unusual; most offerings were male. And red, which is specified for nothing else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is killed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lead it **outside the camp** and let it be slaughtered there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the altar. Outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews takes it up: *the bodies of those animals, whose blood is brought into the sanctuary by the high priest for sin, are burned **outside the camp**. Therefore Jesus also, that He might sanctify the people with His own blood, **suffered outside the gate**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Eleazar is to **sprinkle some of its blood toward the tent seven times**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven, toward the tent, from outside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleazar, not Aaron — the deputy, so the high priest does not become unclean."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is burned"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the heifer is **burned whole — hide, flesh, blood, and all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is kept back and nothing is eaten. The entire animal, including the blood, goes into the fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things thrown in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **cedar wood, hyssop, and scarlet wool** are cast into the fire with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three used in the cleansing of a leper in Leviticus 14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cedar is the tallest tree in that country and hyssop the smallest bush — the pair Solomon is said to have spoken of, from the cedar to the hyssop. And scarlet, the colour of the crimson dye."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hyssop is what the blood was applied with at the Passover, and what was lifted to the cross with the sour wine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ashes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ashes are **gathered by a clean man** and **kept outside the camp in a clean place**, to be **mixed into the water of cleansing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ash of one animal, kept in store, mixed with running water as needed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jewish tradition holds that very few red heifers were ever prepared across the whole history of the temple. One animal supplied the nation for generations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 19 goes on: contact with a dead body. Anyone who touched a corpse, or a grave, or was in a tent where someone died, was unclean seven days — and this water was the only remedy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unavoidable defilement, with a provision waiting for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inversion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who **handle it must wash and wait until evening**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who gathers the ashes becomes unclean. The priest who sprinkles the blood becomes unclean. The man who burns it becomes unclean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone who touches the thing that makes people clean is made unclean by it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A lasting statute **for Israel and for the stranger among them**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Hebrews takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For if the blood of bulls and goats, and **the ashes of a heifer**, sprinkling the unclean, sanctifies for the purifying of the flesh, **how much more shall the blood of Christ**… cleanse your conscience from dead works to serve the living God?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer names the heifer specifically, and the thing he says is cleansed is the conscience."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is odd about it.** Everyone who administers it is defiled by it, the animal is killed outside the camp, and the whole of it — blood included — goes into the fire. The ashes of one animal, kept in a clean place, waiting for whoever touched death."}]}, "Numbers 20:10–11": {"title": "He struck it twice", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses does three things he was not told to do, and the water comes anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gathering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses and Aaron **gathered the assembly in front of the rock**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That part was the instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Listen now, you rebels** — **must we bring you water out of this rock?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in one sentence, and neither was in the instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**You rebels** — the Hebrew is **hammorim**. It is the only recorded time Moses addresses the nation like that, and the man described in Numbers 12 as *very humble, more than all men who were on the face of the earth* is the one saying it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Must we** — the pronoun is first person plural. God had said *you will bring water out of the rock for them*, and Moses says *must **we***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The striking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Moses **lifted his hand and struck the rock twice** with his staff.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was told to speak to it. He hits it, and then hits it again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubling is recorded deliberately. Numbers could have said he struck it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **water came gushing out, more than enough**, and **the people drank, and their animals too**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *water came out abundantly*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is withheld. The people get everything that was promised, the animals are included as specified, and the supply is described as more than sufficient."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The failure is Moses'. The provision is unaffected by it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the earlier instruction had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Rephidim, thirty-eight years before: *strike the rock, and water will pour out.* See the note on Exodus 17:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is doing what worked last time. The instruction had changed. See the note on Numbers 20:7–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the striking said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verdict in verse 12 names the problem as failing to *honor Me as holy in the sight of the people of Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nation was watching. What they saw was a furious man calling them rebels and beating a rock, and what they were told to see was a word spoken and water coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name of the place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This was the water of **Meribah** — contention — because the children of Israel contended with the LORD, and He was hallowed among them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same name as Rephidim carried. Two places called Quarrelling, thirty-eight years apart, over the same commodity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says the rock was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They drank from a spiritual Rock that went with them, and **that Rock was Christ**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses says about it later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Deuteronomy he mentions it three times, and each time he attaches it to the people: *the LORD was angry with me for your sakes*, and *because of you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never disputes the ruling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not affected.** The water. Numbers puts the disobedience and the abundance in the same sentence — struck twice, and it came out more than enough."}]}, "Numbers 20:12": {"title": "\"It was a heavy word, and it was final\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse ends the careers of both men who led Israel out of Egypt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD said **to Moses and Aaron**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. Aaron was standing there and is included in the ruling; he dies on Mount Hor before the chapter is over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because you did not trust Me enough to honor Me as holy in the sight of the people of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *because you did not believe Me, to sanctify Me in the eyes of the children of Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and the second is the one that names the setting: **in the sight of the people**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not water — that came. See the note on Numbers 20:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nation had watched. What they saw was a man calling them rebels and hitting a rock twice, and what they were meant to see was a word spoken and water arriving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is about the picture given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will not bring this assembly into the land I am giving them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of leading, and the last stretch is withheld."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets to the border. Deuteronomy 34 has him on Nebo, shown the whole land from Gilead to the Western Sea, and he dies there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The closing line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **a heavy word, and it was final**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked. Deuteronomy records the prayer: *\"I pleaded with the LORD at that time, saying, 'O Lord GOD… **let me cross over and see the good land beyond the Jordan**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *\"**Enough of that! Speak no more to Me of this matter**. Go up to the top of Pisgah, lift your eyes westward, northward, southward and eastward, and see it with your eyes, **for you shall not cross over this Jordan**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asked once, refused, and told not to raise it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in Deuteronomy he attaches it to the people: *the LORD was angry with me **for your sakes***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never disputes the ruling and never mentions the rock in those passages."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was not taken away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He writes Deuteronomy after this. He commissions Joshua, blesses the tribes, sees the land from the mountain, and God buries him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the man who was told he would not enter the land is standing in it in Matthew 17, on a mountain, talking with Jesus. See the note on Matthew 17:1–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The epitaph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But since then **there has not arisen in Israel a prophet like Moses, whom the LORD knew face to face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written after the ruling, in the same book, as the last word on him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the charge is.** Not temper and not disobedience in the abstract. The verse says *in the sight of the people of Israel* — the nation was watching, and what they were shown was wrong."}]}, "Numbers 20:2–5": {"title": "The old complaint, forty years on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The same argument they had at the beginning of the journey, made again at the end of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kadesh, in the fortieth year. Miriam has just died and been buried there — the verse immediately before this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The generation that came out of Egypt is nearly gone. Most of the people complaining were children when the last water crisis happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shortage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **there was no water for the people**, and they **gathered against Moses and Aaron**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *gathered against* is the same verb used of Korah's company. It is the word for assembling in opposition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wish"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If only we had died with our brothers before the LORD!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are referring to the fourteen thousand seven hundred who died in the plague after Korah, and to the two hundred and fifty at the censers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They wish they had been among them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why did you bring the LORD's assembly out into this wilderness to die here** — us and our animals?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why did you drag us up out of Egypt into this terrible place?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both questions are addressed to Moses and Aaron. Neither mentions that the forty years were the consequence of a decision the nation made at this same place, Kadesh, when the spies came back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they miss"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **no place for grain or figs or vines or pomegranates**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four crops, and every one of them is on the list the spies brought back **from Canaan**: *they came to the Valley of Eshcol and cut down a branch with one cluster of grapes… they also brought some of the pomegranates and figs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are describing the promised land and attributing it to Egypt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had in Egypt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 11 has the earlier version: *we remember the fish which we ate freely in Egypt, the cucumbers, the melons, the leeks, the onions, and the garlic.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No mention of the brick quotas or the drowned sons, either time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The old complaint rose again, sharp and bitter\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the fifth or sixth water-and-food crisis in the book. Marah, Rephidim, Taberah, Kibroth Hattaavah, the fiery serpents, and this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses and Aaron do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They walk away from the crowd, go to the tent door, and fall on their faces. See the note on Numbers 20:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same thing Moses did during Korah's rebellion, twice. See the note on Numbers 16:16–35."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Deuteronomy says about the same forty years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD your God has blessed you in everything your hands have done**… These forty years the LORD your God has been with you, and **you have lacked nothing**.\"* See the note on Deuteronomy 2:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses says that in his last sermon, about the period the people are complaining about here."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is the same and what is not.** The complaint is nearly word for word what was said at Rephidim thirty-eight years earlier. What is different is that the God they are complaining to has fed them every morning for the whole intervening time."}]}, "Numbers 20:25–29": {"title": "The garments taken off one man and put on another", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Aaron's death is unlike any other in the Old Testament. He is told where, told when, and walks up the mountain to it in front of the whole nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of events"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take off Aaron's priestly garments and put them on Eleazar his son… Aaron will be gathered to his people, and he will die there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The transfer comes **before** the death. Moses strips his brother of the robes on the mountain top, dresses the son in them, and only then does Aaron die."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priesthood is not interrupted for a moment. There is never a point at which Israel has no high priest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it was done in public"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They went up the mountain in the sight of the whole community.* Three men climb; two come down, one of them wearing the vestments everybody recognised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had to be explained afterwards. The nation watched the office change hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it exposes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A priest dies and the robes go to somebody else, over and over, generation after generation. The system depends on replacement because the men wearing it keep running out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The comment the New Testament makes.** Hebrews puts its finger on exactly this: there had to be many priests, *because death kept stopping them from staying in office* — but He continues forever, so His priesthood does not pass to anyone else. The writer had scenes like this mountain in mind. See the note on Hebrews 2:14–16, where the same argument runs the other way: He took a body in order to die."}]}, "Numbers 20:6": {"title": "They turned away and fell on their faces", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is entirely made of movements."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Moses and Aaron **turned away from the crowd**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not answer. The assembly has just accused them of dragging the nation out of Egypt to die. See the note on Numbers 20:2–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither man argues."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And went to **the door of the tent of meeting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **ohel moed** — the tent of appointment, the place God had said He would meet them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Away from the people and toward the tent. That is the whole geography of the verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **fell on their faces**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses does this repeatedly in Numbers. At Korah's rebellion he does it twice — once when the accusation is made and once when the judgment is announced, both times on behalf of the people accusing him. See the notes on Numbers 16:1–15 and Numbers 16:16–35."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the glory of the LORD appeared to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kavod** — weight, glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase appears in chapter 16 and again in chapter 14, both times in the middle of a rebellion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any words. Numbers does not give a prayer, a request, or a complaint. Two men walk to a door and lie down, and the glory appears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern this establishes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before has the accusation. The verse after has the instruction: *take the staff… speak to the rock.* See the note on Numbers 20:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the problem and the answer there is one verse, and it consists of leaving the crowd and going to the tent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Miriam. She died and was buried at Kadesh in the verse before this chapter's crisis begins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron dies at the end of the same chapter, on Mount Hor. And Moses is told in verse 12 that he will not enter the land. See the note on Numbers 20:12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three leaders are gone or under sentence by the end of Numbers 20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses does elsewhere with the same posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the golden calf: *I fell down before the LORD, as at the first, forty days and forty nights.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When the spies' report caused the rebellion: *then Moses and Aaron fell on their faces before all the assembly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it more than anyone else in Scripture."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sequence.** Accused, they turn their backs on the crowd, walk to a door, and go face down. And the next thing recorded is that the glory showed up."}]}, "Numbers 20:7–9": {"title": "\"Speak to the rock\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is given in four parts, and one word in it is different from last time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Take the staff"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take the staff**, and gather the people together, you and your brother Aaron.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has the definite article — **the** staff, and the next verse says Moses took it *from before the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where Aaron's rod was kept. It had been laid up in front of the Testimony after Korah's rebellion, and it had budded, blossomed and produced almonds overnight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dead stick with fruit on it, kept as the answer to the question of who was chosen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Speak"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **speak to the rock in front of them all**, and **it will give up its water**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *speak* is the ordinary verb. Nothing is to be done to the rock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Rephidim, thirty-eight years earlier, the instruction was **strike it**. See the note on Exodus 17:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same nation, same problem, different instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who brings the water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will bring water out of the rock for them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is given the credit in advance, in the instruction itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And give **the community and their animals** something to drink.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The animals are included. The people had complained about them in verse 4 — *us and our animals* — and the answer covers them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Moses **took the staff from before the LORD, just as He had told him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first half is exact. He does what he is told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gathers the assembly, calls them rebels, asks *must we bring you water out of this rock*, and strikes it twice. See the note on Numbers 20:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The water comes anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two rods"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' rod is the one that struck the Nile and turned it to blood, struck the rock at Horeb, and was stretched over the sea. It is a rod of judgment throughout Exodus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron's rod is the one that budded — the dead branch that produced almonds and was kept in front of the ark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says the staff was taken *from before the LORD*, which is where the budded one was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says the rock was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They drank from **a spiritual Rock that went with them, and that Rock was Christ**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because you did not trust Me enough to honor Me as holy** in the sight of the people of Israel, **you will not bring this assembly into the land**.\"* See the note on Numbers 20:12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verdict is about representation — what the action said about God in front of the nation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The change in the instruction.** Struck once, and after that spoken to. Numbers gives the new instruction plainly and then records that it was not followed."}]}, "Numbers 21:5": {"title": "\"We loathe this miserable food\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse of complaint, and the last clause of it is the writer's own comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they speak against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They spoke **against God and against Moses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, and God first. Numbers is precise about the order elsewhere too — the grumbling is aimed past the leader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three complaints"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why did you bring us up out of Egypt **to die in the wilderness**? **There is no bread. There is no water.** And **we loathe this miserable food**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read them together and the last two contradict each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no bread — and we are sick of the bread. They complain about the absence of food and the quality of the food in the same breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *miserable* is **qeloqel** — worthless, contemptible, light. It is a made-up-sounding reduplicated form, and it occurs nowhere else in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They coined a word for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The narrator's line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The manna that had kept them alive — **they had come to despise even that**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the verdict on the sentence, and it names what the food actually did: **kept them alive**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of a substance falling every morning, wrapped in dew, that nobody had to plant or store or pay for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it had been called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The bread of angels*, in Psalm 78. *The grain of heaven*. See the note on Psalms 78:21–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about it changed between Exodus 16 and here. The description in Numbers 11 — coriander seed, resin-pale, tasting of oil — still stood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had simply been eating it long enough to stop seeing it, and the same complaint appears in chapter 11 and again here, ten chapters and many years apart. See the note on Numbers 11:4–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD sent fiery serpents among the people, and they bit the people, and many of the people of Israel died.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the pole. See the note on Numbers 21:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were when they said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *they journeyed from Mount Hor by the Way of the Red Sea, **to go around the land of Edom**, and the soul of the people became very discouraged on the way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A detour. Edom had refused them passage, so they had to go the long way round, and the complaint comes out of the extra miles."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the same chapter ends.** With a song. *Spring up, O well! All of you sing to it* — sung over a well at Beer, a few verses after the snakes. Numbers puts the loathing and the singing on the same page."}]}, "Numbers 21:8–9": {"title": "\"He only had to look\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A nation is dying of snakebite and the remedy is a piece of metal on a stick."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the remedy is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The snakes are not removed. Nothing in the passage says the serpents left."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They keep biting, and the arrangement God gives is for people who are still being bitten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Make **a fiery serpent** and set it up **on a pole**. And it will be that everyone who is bitten, **when he looks at it, will live**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An image of the very thing killing them, raised where everybody can see it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *pole* is a standard, a banner-pole — the thing you lift so an army can see it from a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is asked of the dying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did not have to fix himself, or reach the wounds his own hands could not reach. **He only had to look** — to turn his eyes away from the poison in him toward the thing God had lifted up. **Whoever looked, lived.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No treatment, no ritual, no payment, no confession recorded. No quantity of looking is specified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man too weak to stand could do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were bitten"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **spoke against God and against Moses**… \"There is no bread. There is no water. And **we loathe this miserable food**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The manna again. Forty years on and they still call it miserable — the Hebrew is *this worthless bread*. See the note on Numbers 21:5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Just as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of Man be lifted up**, that whoever believes in Him should not perish but have eternal life.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John 3:14, and then verse 16 follows straight out of it. He chose this incident to explain His own death, in a night conversation with Nicodemus. See the note on John 3:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lifted up** is **hupsoo** — raised, and also exalted. John uses it three times of the crucifixion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why a serpent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul: *He made Him who knew no sin **to be sin for us**, that we might become the righteousness of God in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing on the pole is the image of what was killing them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What became of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They kept it. Seven hundred years later, Hezekiah *broke in pieces the bronze serpent that Moses had made, for until those days the children of Israel burned incense to it* — and he called it **Nehushtan**, a piece of bronze."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The right remedy became an idol once people looked at the object instead of what it pointed at."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the Israelites asked for first.** *Pray to the LORD that He take away the serpents from us.* They asked for removal. They got a pole, and a look, and their lives."}]}, "Numbers 23:18–20": {"title": "\"He has blessed, and I cannot reverse it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The man being paid to curse Israel opens his mouth and this comes out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Balaam**, hired by Balak king of Moab to curse Israel as they camped on the plains opposite Jericho."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not an Israelite, not a prophet of the LORD by office, and is described elsewhere in Scripture as a man who loved the wages of unrighteousness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing on a hilltop with seven altars behind him, paid to say the opposite of this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Rise up, Balak**, and hear; listen to me, son of Zippor.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He makes the king stand for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God is not a man, that He should lie**; He is not a son of man, **that He should change His mind**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *nor a son of man that He should repent*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel says nearly the same words to Saul: *the Strength of Israel will not lie nor relent, for He is not a man, that He should relent.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Does He speak, and then **not act**? Does He promise, and then **not fulfill**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are rhetorical, and both are about the distance between saying and doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The admission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have received **a command to bless**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not permission. A command."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He has blessed, and I cannot reverse it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He has blessed, and I cannot turn it back*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man with the fee in his hand states plainly that the thing he was hired to do is outside his power."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How many times he tries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four oracles, from three different hilltops, with seven altars and seven bulls and seven rams built each time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balak keeps moving him — *come to another place from which you may see them; you shall see only the outer part of them* — as though a different angle would help."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, a blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Balak says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I called you to curse my enemies, and look, you have kept on blessing them these three times!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Balaam's answer: *\"If Balak were to give me his house full of silver and gold, **I could not go beyond the word of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Israel was doing at the time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. They are in their tents at the bottom of the hill and do not know any of this is happening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole episode takes place over their heads."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next oracle says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He sees no wrong in Jacob, no trouble laid up against Israel**.\"* See the note on Numbers 23:21–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Balaam ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not well. Numbers 31 records his death, and the counsel he gave Balak afterward — to seduce Israel into idolatry at Peor, since cursing had not worked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament names it three times: the way of Balaam, the error of Balaam, the doctrine of Balaam."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could not reverse the blessing, so he went after the people instead."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the sentence comes from.** Not from a prophet, a psalm, or a friend of Israel. From a hired man on a hill, standing in front of the king who paid him, saying he cannot take it back."}]}, "Numbers 23:21–24": {"title": "\"He sees no wrong in Jacob\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second half of the oracle, and the first line of it is the one to stop on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He sees no wrong in Jacob, no trouble laid up against Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He has not beheld iniquity in Jacob, nor has He seen trouble in Israel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 23. By this point in the book Israel has made a golden calf, refused to enter the land, grumbled about the manna, rebelled under Korah, complained at Meribah, and been bitten by serpents."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses has spent chapters recording all of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a man on a hill, looking down at their tents, says God sees no wrong in Jacob."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the view is from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balaam is above them, looking down at a camp arranged in four squares around a tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He cannot see individuals from there. He sees the arrangement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God is doing in the camp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD their God is with them**, and **the shout of the King is among them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *shout* is **teruah** — the blast of the trumpet, the shout that goes up when a king arrives among his people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exodus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God brought them out of Egypt**; they have **the strength of a wild ox**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **reem** — the wild ox, an animal nobody could domesticate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balaam repeats this line in the next oracle as well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two negatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There is no spell against Jacob, no divination against Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew words are the technical terms of Balaam's own profession — enchantment and augury."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing there as a professional diviner, hired for exactly this, reporting that his trade does not work here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about them instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now it is said of Jacob and of Israel, '**See what God has done!**'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what has God wrought* — the sentence Samuel Morse chose for the first telegraph message."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The report about Israel is a report about God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look — **a people rising like a lioness, lifting itself like a lion**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lion image belongs to Judah, from Jacob's blessing in Genesis 49: *Judah is a lion's whelp… he bows down, he lies down as a lion; who shall rouse him?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fourth oracle says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I see Him, but not now; I behold Him, but not near. A Star shall come out of Jacob; a Scepter shall rise out of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing the hired diviner says about Israel is about a star."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he could not curse them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oracle before gives the reason: *He has blessed, and I cannot reverse it.* See the note on Numbers 23:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because the people were blameless. Because of what had been spoken over them and could not be taken back."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sentence is not.** A statement that nothing happened in the camp. Numbers records it all. It is what God says He sees, spoken by a man who was paid to say otherwise."}]}, "Numbers 26:52–56": {"title": "Measured out, and drawn by lot", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The land is about to be divided, and God gives two instructions that look as though they cancel each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two rules"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**by size** — *to a larger group give a larger inheritance, to a smaller group a smaller one, each receiving in proportion to those who were counted*", "**by lot** — *the land itself is to be assigned by lot… whether large or small, every inheritance is to be settled by the drawing of lots*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "How much you get is proportionate. **Where** you get it is drawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why both"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The size rule makes the allocation fair. The lot takes the choosing out of human hands entirely — no tribe lobbying for the fertile valleys, no favouritism, no Moses deciding who gets the coast."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs says it directly: the lot is cast into the lap, but its every decision is from the LORD. The Illumination's line here says the same thing plainly — *God's own hand would set the boundaries.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the second census, taken at the end of forty years. The first generation is gone; these are the people who will actually cross. The counting is done specifically so the land can be divided among them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the whole exercise is forward-looking. They are being numbered for an inheritance, before a single boundary stone has been placed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next.** The very next paragraph is the daughters of Zelophehad coming forward — because a distribution had just been announced that had no place for them in it. The chapters belong together: the inheritance is described, and then somebody who was left out of the arrangement asks. See the note on Numbers 27:1–4."}]}, "Numbers 27:1–4": {"title": "Five women who asked", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five sisters walk to the front of the camp and ask for something the law had not provided for. It is one of the boldest scenes in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they stood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At the entrance to the tent of meeting, before Moses, before Eleazar the priest, before the leaders and the whole assembly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a private word. They took it to the most public place in Israel and stood in front of every layer of authority at once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they are named — Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milcah, Tirzah. The chapter records all five, and it records them again three more times in the books that follow. Scripture does not lose their names."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How they argued"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their case is careful, and they clear the ground before they ask:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["our father died in the wilderness", "**he was not among Korah's followers** — they head off the obvious objection before it is raised", "he died for his own sin, and he left no sons"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the question: *why should our father's name disappear from his clan simply because he had no son?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not complain about their treatment. They argue from their father's name and from what the inheritance was *for*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They asked, and they were not afraid to ask.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole scene. The land had not yet been distributed; the promise was still in front of everyone. They looked at what was about to be handed out, noticed they were not in the arrangement, and came forward anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where boldness like this is aimed.** They were not asking for something outside the promise. They were asking for their share *of* it — the portion that belonged to their father's name. That is the shape of a good request: not inventing an entitlement, but coming forward for what the promise already contained."}]}, "Numbers 27:5–7": {"title": "\"The daughters of Zelophehad are right\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses does not rule on it. He takes it upstairs, and the answer that comes back is unambiguous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "God's verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The daughters of Zelophehad are right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, and they settle it. Not *let them have a portion as an exception*, and not *this is irregular but permitted*. **Right** — their case is correct."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the instruction: *you must surely give them property as an inheritance among their father's relatives. Turn their father's inheritance over to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles the verb for emphasis — *giving you shall give*. It is the same construction used in Genesis 2 for eating freely from every tree. There is nothing grudging in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it changed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is not merely a favourable outcome for one family. Read on: it becomes **statute**. *If a man dies and leaves no son, turn his inheritance over to his daughter* — permanent law in Israel, from that day, because five women asked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request did not bend the rule. It made one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses did right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He *brought their case before the LORD*. He did not decide it himself, and he did not dismiss it for being unprecedented."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given what the same book records about Moses' patience running out elsewhere, this is worth noticing: presented with a claim no one had made before, his instinct was to take it to God rather than to defend the existing arrangement."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line the Illumination adds.** *What the overlooked had asked, God Himself upheld.* It is the translator summarising, and it is a fair reading of the scene. The people with no standing in the system got the ruling, and the ruling came from the top."}]}, "Numbers 30:10–15": {"title": "\"He bears the responsibility for her guilt\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A law about vows, and the last verse of it is the one to stop on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Vows and binding pledges — **neder** and **issar**. A vow was voluntary, and once made it was absolute: *when you make a vow to God, do not delay to pay it, for He has no pleasure in fools. **Pay what you have vowed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had to make one. Everybody who made one was held to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The husband's hearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a woman living with her husband **makes a vow** or binds herself with a pledge, and her husband **hears of it and says nothing**, forbidding her nothing, then **all her vows and pledges stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Silence confirms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cancellation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if **he nullifies them on the day he hears**, then **nothing that came from her lips — vow or pledge — will hold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he has made them void*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**On the day he hears.** The window is one day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who releases her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **her husband has canceled them, and the LORD will release her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses. The husband cancels; the LORD forgives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *release* is **salach** — the word used throughout Leviticus for God forgiving. It is used of God alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has broken a vow made to God, and the text says God releases her because a man cancelled it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence rule, stated again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If he says nothing to her **from one day to the next, he confirms all her vows and pledges by his silence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doing nothing is a decision, and the law says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If he cancels them **some time after he has heard**, then **he bears the responsibility for her guilt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he shall bear her iniquity* — **nasa avon**, to lift and carry the guilt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the phrase used of the scapegoat: *the goat shall **bear on itself all their iniquities** to an uninhabited land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And of the servant in Isaiah: *He shall **bear their iniquities**.* See the note on Isaiah 53:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law arranges"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A vow is made and cannot be unmade. A man hears it, cancels it late, and the obligation does not vanish — it transfers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes free and he carries it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 30, on the plains of Moab, near the end of the wilderness years, alongside the laws about inheritance and the cities of refuge. See the note on Numbers 35:9–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word in Galatians"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ has redeemed us from the curse of the law, **having become a curse for us**.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **genomenos** — having become."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not paid on our behalf from outside it. Became it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The mechanism in the law.** The obligation is real, the breaking of it is real, and the guilt does not evaporate. Numbers 30 says where it goes."}]}, "Numbers 32:1–5": {"title": "\"Do not make us cross the Jordan\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two tribes look at good grazing land on the wrong side of the river and ask to stop there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the request is framed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every part of it sounds reasonable:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["they genuinely have enormous herds", "Jazer and Gilead genuinely are good cattle country", "they ask politely — *if we have found favour with you*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the sentence is a rebellion. It is a sensible business decision made by men who could see what was in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one line that gives it away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not make us cross the Jordan.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Jordan is the whole point. Everything from Egypt onward has been aimed at crossing it — the promise to Abraham, the plagues, the sea, the forty years. The land on the other side is the land God swore to give them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not asking for less than they were promised because they were refused more. They are asking to be excused from receiving it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What made it look wise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The herds were real, and the grass was real. The decision was driven entirely by what suited the livestock — good land, immediately, without a river crossing or a war."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what makes the chapter worth reading slowly. Nothing here is obviously sinful. It is a preference for the visible good over the promised one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Moses' first reaction.** He hears it as the same failure as Kadesh — *are your brothers to go to war while you sit here?* — and reminds them what happened when ten spies discouraged the people forty years earlier. He treats it as serious immediately, and the rest of the chapter is the negotiation. See the note on Numbers 32:20–27."}]}, "Numbers 32:20–27": {"title": "\"Your sin will find you out\" — what it actually refers to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous line is quoted as a general warning about secret sin. In its own chapter it means something much more specific."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses agrees to the request, with terms:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["arm yourselves and cross the Jordan with everyone else", "stay until the LORD has driven out His enemies and the land is subdued", "**then** come back, free of obligation, and this land is yours"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The settlement on the east bank is allowed. What is not allowed is settling there while the rest of the nation fights."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the warning is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if you fail to do this, you will be sinning against the LORD, and know for certain your sin will find you out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sin named is **not going**. Leaving your brothers to take the land while you graze cattle in safety. That is the thing that would find them out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a promise-keeping verse, not a hidden-sin verse. Moses is telling men who have just made a commitment that failing to keep it will catch up with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of us who can bear arms will cross over to fight before the LORD, as you have said.* Wives, children, flocks and herds stay; the fighting men go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they kept it. Joshua 22 records Joshua sending them home years later with a blessing, having done exactly what they said — *you have not deserted your brothers over this long time.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it cost them anyway.** Their land, being east of the river, was the first territory Israel lost. Reuben and Gad are the tribes carried off earliest in the Assyrian invasions. They kept the promise and they still ended up living on the exposed side of the border, which is the quiet ending the chapter never spells out."}]}, "Numbers 32:6–15": {"title": "\"Are your brothers to go to war while you sit here?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two tribes ask to settle on the wrong side of the river, and Moses hears it as something he has heard before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben and Gad had *a very great multitude of livestock*, and the land east of the Jordan was good grazing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If we have found favour in your sight, let this land be given to your servants as a possession. **Do not take us over the Jordan.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a reasonable request about pasture, and Moses does not hear it that way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Are your brothers to go to war while you sit here?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the charge. Not that the land is bad, or that they are wrong about the grazing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine and a half tribes are about to cross a river and fight for a country, and two of them have found somewhere comfortable short of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why would you discourage the people** from crossing into the land the LORD has given?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to break the heart, to turn the will aside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His concern is not the two tribes. It is what the sight of them staying will do to everybody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The precedent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is just what your fathers did** when I sent them from Kadesh-barnea to see the land.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is standing in front of the children of the men who refused, thirty-eight years later, telling them they are doing it again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They lost heart and **turned the whole community away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten men came back with a report and a nation stayed in the desert. See the note on Numbers 13:26–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cost, recited"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That whole generation wandered forty years until it passed away, **except Caleb and Joshua, who followed the LORD wholeheartedly**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men out of a nation, and the phrase used of them is *filled after the LORD* — followed fully."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now here you are, doing the same, **kindling His anger against Israel again**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses fears will happen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you turn away from following Him, **He will leave this people in the wilderness, and you will be the ruin of them all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not warning them about their own loss. He tells them they will be the reason everybody else stays out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They propose a solution, and it is a good one: *we will build sheepfolds here for our livestock and cities for our little ones, **but we ourselves will be armed, ready to go before the children of Israel** until we have brought them to their place.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They will settle their families and cross over to fight anyway, and not come home until every other tribe has its inheritance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses accepts it. See the note on Numbers 32:20–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They kept it. Joshua 22 records him sending them home years later: *you have not left your brethren these many days, up to this day, but have kept the charge.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they were the first tribes carried away when Assyria came."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being negotiated.** Not whether they can have the land. Whether they will go over with everybody else first. Moses' whole objection is about the order of things, and the two tribes end up agreeing with him."}]}, "Numbers 35:16–21": {"title": "When blood is shed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The refuge is real, and so is the line drawn around it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **justice must still be clear**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cities of refuge are not a loophole. The passage that establishes them is immediately followed by the test for who does not qualify."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three materials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If someone strikes another down with **something deadly — iron, stone, or wood used to kill** — and the person dies, **that is murder**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law works by instrument. Iron, stone or wood — the three things a weapon could be made of in that world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Picking one up is treated as evidence of intent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three actions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If **out of hatred** someone **shoves** another, or **hurls something**, or **strikes with the hand**, and the person dies, **that too is murder**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the test moves from the object to the motive: **out of hatred**, **be'ivah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No weapon is needed. A shove is enough if the hatred was there first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two lists do together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between them they cover both ways a death can be deliberate — by what was in the hand, and by what was in the heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else falls into the other category, and the six cities are for that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The avenger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The avenger of blood may hold the murderer responsible when they meet**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **goel haddam**. The law does not abolish him; it regulates him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is permitted to act on a murderer, and forbidden to touch a man inside a refuge until the trial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the trial decided"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The congregation heard the case and put the man into one category or the other. If manslaughter, he returned to the city and stayed. If murder, *the avenger of blood himself shall put the murderer to death.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter adds a rule: *whoever kills a person, the murderer shall be put to death **on the testimony of witnesses**; but **one witness is not sufficient** testimony against a person to cause death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No conviction on one voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "No ransom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Moreover you shall **take no ransom for the life of a murderer** who is guilty of death, but he shall surely be put to death.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Money could settle almost anything else in that legal system. Not this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **blood defiles the land**, and no atonement can be made for the land, for the blood that is shed on it, except by the blood of him who shed it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land itself is treated as a party to the case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the other side of the line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who did it by accident, and everyone who ran. See the note on Numbers 35:9–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said from the cross"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Father, forgive them; they do not know what they are doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exact category the cities were built for — the man who caused a death without knowing. See the note on Luke 23:32–38."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the second list exists.** So that the refuge means something. A shelter with no boundary is not a shelter; the chapter draws the line first and then leaves the door open on the other side of it."}]}, "Numbers 35:9–15": {"title": "Cities of refuge", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six towns are set aside before the land is even entered, for people who have not yet done anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**When you cross into Canaan**, choose cities of refuge.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel is still on the plains of Moab. The land has not been taken, the tribes have not been allotted, and the provision is already made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody is going to need this, and God legislates for them in advance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they are for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that **a person who kills someone by accident, without meaning to**, may flee there.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *who kills any person unintentionally* — **bishgagah**, in error, by mistake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy gives the example: two men chopping wood, the axe head flies off the handle, and the neighbour dies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"These will be **a shelter from the avenger**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **goel haddam** — the avenger of blood, the nearest kinsman, whose duty it was to pursue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word **goel** is the kinsman-redeemer: Boaz in Ruth, and the *my Redeemer lives* of Job."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nearest relative both redeems and avenges, depending on which side of the death you are on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the refuge does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that the one who caused a death **is not struck down before he can stand trial before the community**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not an acquittal. It buys a trial."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The runner is protected long enough to be heard, and the congregation decides which category he is in. See the note on Numbers 35:16–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number and the layout"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Set apart **six such cities — three on the east side of the Jordan and three in Canaan**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua names them: Kedesh, Shechem and Hebron in the west; Bezer, Ramoth and Golan in the east."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are spread deliberately, so that no part of the country is far from one. All six were Levitical cities."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later Jewish tradition held that the roads to them were kept in repair and signposted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who could use them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They will be a refuge for **the Israelite, for the foreigner, and for anyone living among you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three categories, and two of them are not Israelites."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is unusual in extending identically to the resident alien."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the runner had to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Get there, and stay. The city held him until the death of the high priest, and then he went home free."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man's death released everybody in all six cities at once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the priests come in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cities of refuge were Levite towns, so a man fleeing a death ran into a place full of priests and lived off the priests' portion. See the note on Numbers 18:8–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The provision, described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here is God's tenderness, **built right into the map**: a door left open, a place of safety for the frightened and the innocent.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the language turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We, **who ran for shelter** and grabbed hold of the hope held out in front of us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek in Hebrews 6 is the vocabulary of fleeing for refuge. See the note on Hebrews 6:18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the law provides for.** Not the guilty and not the safe. The man who did it, did not mean to, and is being chased — and the arrangement was made before anyone crossed the river."}]}, "Philippians 1:21–24": {"title": "A man weighing up his own death, in a letter about joy", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is in custody, the outcome is genuinely uncertain, and he does something unusual: he thinks out loud about which he would prefer, and admits he cannot decide."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words are both nautical"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I long to cast off and be with Christ* — the verb is **analuo**, used of loosing a ship from its moorings and of striking camp to move on. Not ending. Departing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Staying on in the body* — **epimeno**, to remain at a place, to stay put."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the choice is not between existing and not existing. It is between casting off and staying in port a while longer, which tells you what Paul thought death was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He genuinely cannot pick"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which of the two I would choose, I honestly cannot say. I am caught between them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image behind *caught between* is of pressure from both sides at once, like a man wedged. And the two pressures are not fear and hope — they are two goods:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["being with Christ, which he calls **better by far**", "being useful to people who need him, which he calls **more needed**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is resigned, and nothing is morbid. He is weighing a gain against a gain."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"For me, life means Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famous line is doing structural work. If life means Christ, then living is not a stretch of time to be got through, and if dying means gain, then death is not a loss to be feared. Both halves are needed for the paragraph to make sense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is also why the paragraph does not read as depression. A man for whom living has no content might long to depart; Paul says living is full and departing is better, and that is why he cannot choose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he settles it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He settles it on somebody else's account: *for your sake, staying on in the body is what is needed more.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **being confident of this, I know that I shall remain and continue with you all** for your progress and joy of faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *I know* is **oida** — settled knowledge, not a hope. He states the outcome of a capital case as a fact, and the reason he gives is their progress."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it happened. He was released, travelled again, and wrote to Timothy years later from a second imprisonment — where the language changes: *the time of my departure is at hand.* See the note on 2 Timothy 4:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same nautical word, **analuo**, in both letters. Here he weighs it and stays. There he says it has come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What decided it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the tribunal, and not the charge. He weighs two goods, and lands on the one that serves the people he loves."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this sits in the letter.** Philippians is the joy letter — the word runs through all four chapters — and it was written from prison by a man openly discussing his possible execution. The two facts belong together. It is not joy despite the situation being described; it is joy from a source the situation cannot reach."}]}, "Philippians 2:12–13": {"title": "\"For it is God who is at work in you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses that are almost always quoted apart, and the second is the reason the first is possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Work out\" is not \"work for\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Work out your own salvation with fear and trembling.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb means to bring to completion, to work something through to its end — what you do with something you already have. It was used of working a mine: extracting what is in there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not telling them to earn it. The letter has already called them saints and said God began a good work in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"for\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is God who is at work in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything turns on that connective. The reason to work it out is that Someone else is working in you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verb is **energeo** — the word behind our *energy*. God is the one supplying the power; the working out is what that power produces on the outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both halves of the willing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Both the willing and the doing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not merely the strength to do what you already wanted. The wanting itself is described as His work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence that takes the pressure out of the first verse. If even the desire is being supplied, then *fear and trembling* is not anxiety about performance — it is the seriousness appropriate to God working inside a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Paul says it here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what precedes it: the hymn about Christ emptying Himself, taking the form of a servant, obedient to death on a cross, and being exalted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so, my beloved friends* — the instruction flows out of that. The working in is by the One who already did the emptying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The purpose clause.** *For the sake of what pleases Him.* The whole operation, willing and doing alike, is aimed at His good pleasure — which is where Paul located the reason for everything back in Ephesians 1. See that note."}]}, "Philippians 3:17–19": {"title": "\"With tears running down\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks them to copy him, and then describes the people he is warning them about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brothers, all of you together, **follow the way I have walked**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **summimetai** — joint-imitators. Paul appears to have coined it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same elsewhere: *imitate me, just as I also imitate Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds immodest until you read what he has just written about himself — everything he had counted as gain, counted as loss, and *not that I have already attained*. See the note on Philippians 3:2–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **keep your eye on those who walk by the pattern you have seen in us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *pattern* is **tupos** — a stamp, a mould, the impression a die leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not only him. Anyone walking the same way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For there are **many who walk otherwise**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The frequency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Of whom **I have told you often**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it before, in person, more than once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And now tell you **with tears running down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *weeping*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul cries in three places in his letters and speeches: over these people, over the Ephesian elders, and in the letter to Corinth *out of much affliction and anguish of heart I wrote to you, with many tears*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is describing opponents and weeping while he does it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name he gives them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are **enemies of the cross of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not enemies of God, or of the church, or of him. Of the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The end of that road is **ruin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apoleia** — destruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their **god is their appetite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **koilia** — the belly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their **glory is in what should shame them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their **whole mind is fixed on the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *who mind earthly things* — **ta epigeia phronountes**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not identify them. The letter has already warned about *the dogs… the knife-cutters* in the same chapter, which points at the circumcision party."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four descriptions here read more like indulgence than legalism, and readers have divided over whether these are the same people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next sentence: *for **our citizenship is registered in heaven**. And it is from there that we are watching eagerly for the Saviour.* See the note on Philippians 3:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their whole mind is fixed on the earth* against *our citizenship is in heaven*. The two verses are built as a pair."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the paragraph ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who will **change these lowly bodies** of ours so that they are **formed like His own glorious body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The belly-god and the transformed body, in consecutive verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, my beloved and longed-for brethren, **my joy and crown**, so stand fast in the Lord, beloved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls them his crown in the sentence after describing enemies in tears."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the warning is delivered.** Weeping. Paul gives four descriptions, each harder than the last, and tells the readers he is crying while he writes them."}]}, "Philippians 3:2–3": {"title": "Watch for the three", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three warnings in a row, and then a definition that takes the other side's word away from them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The break in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philippians is the warmest thing Paul wrote — *I thank my God upon every remembrance of you*, and *rejoice in the Lord always*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 3 opens *finally, my brethren, rejoice in the Lord*, and then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep your eyes open for **the dogs**. Keep your eyes open for **the workers who do harm**. Keep your eyes open for **the knife-cutters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek repeats **blepete** three times — look, watch, beware."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dogs.** In that world, not pets. Scavengers in the street, and a standard Jewish term of contempt for Gentiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul turns it round and applies it to the party insisting on Jewish observance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Workers who do harm** — the Greek is **kakous ergatas**, evil workers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They would have called themselves workers. Paul keeps the noun and changes the adjective."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Knife-cutters** — the Greek is **katatome**, a cutting-down, a mutilation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a pun. The word for circumcision is **peritome**, a cutting-around. Paul coins a near-identical word meaning butchery."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus forbids cutting the flesh: *you shall not make any cuttings in your flesh.* Paul puts the practice in that category."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **we are the circumcision**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the term itself. The people insisting on the operation are the mutilation; the people not having it are the thing it was meant to signify."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had already located it: *circumcise the foreskin of your heart, and be stiff-necked no longer.* And Jeremiah, and Paul in Romans: *circumcision is that of the heart, in the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three marks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **who serve God by His Spirit**, who **make our boast in Christ Jesus**, and who **put no trust at all in what human flesh can offer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses answering the three warnings — a positive for each negative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He lists what he would have had to boast about if the third clause were not true:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, of the tribe of Benjamin, a Hebrew of the Hebrews; concerning the law, a Pharisee; concerning zeal, persecuting the church; concerning the righteousness which is in the law, blameless.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven items — four by birth and three by achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict on them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **what things were gain to me, these I have counted loss for Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *I also count all things loss for the excellence of the knowledge of Christ Jesus my Lord… and count them as **rubbish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skubala** — refuse, dung. It is a coarse word and Paul chose it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wants instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And be found in Him, **not having my own righteousness, which is from the law, but that which is through faith in Christ** — the righteousness which is from God by faith.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our citizenship is registered in heaven.* See the note on Philippians 3:20–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does with the word.** Takes it. The party demanding circumcision is renamed the cutting — and the people refusing it are called the circumcision, defined by three things none of which involve a knife."}]}, "Philippians 3:20–21": {"title": "\"Our citizenship is registered in heaven\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses a piece of civic vocabulary that Philippi in particular would have felt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our **citizenship** is registered in heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **politeuma** — a colony, a commonwealth, the body of citizens whose register you are on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philippi was a Roman colony. Its people held Roman citizenship though most of them had never seen Rome, lived under Roman law, and were governed as though the city were a piece of Italy transplanted into Macedonia."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to people who already knew what it meant to be enrolled somewhere you do not live."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it is **from there** that we are **watching eagerly** for the Saviour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apekdechomai** — to await with the head craned forward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A colony's business was to look toward the capital. Paul does the same with the direction of expectation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who will **change these lowly bodies** of ours so that they are **formed like His own glorious body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *change* is **metaschematizo** — to alter the outward form; and *formed like* is **summorphos**, sharing the same inner form."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both words, in one sentence, about the same event."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lowly** is *the body of our humiliation* — Paul does not flatter it. He is writing from prison, in a body that had been beaten five times with thirty-nine lashes and three times with rods."}, {"k": "h", "t": "By what power"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By the working of that power of His **which can bring all things under Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same energy that will subdue everything is what is applied to a body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul had just said about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *not that I have already attained, or am already perfected; but I press on… **forgetting those things which are behind**, and reaching forward to those which are ahead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *brethren, join in following my example.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had also just listed what he had thrown away — circumcised the eighth day, of the tribe of Benjamin, a Pharisee, blameless concerning the righteousness in the law — and called all of it loss."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast in the paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately before this: *many walk… **whose god is their belly**, and whose glory is in their shame — **who set their mind on earthly things**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two citizenships, two horizons. See the note on Philippians 3:17–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written from a Roman cell, with a capital case pending, to a church that had been sending him money since a riverside prayer meeting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice in the Lord always. Again I will say, rejoice.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The next sentence.** *Therefore, my beloved and longed-for brethren, my joy and crown, **so stand fast in the Lord**.* The citizenship is stated, and the instruction attached to it is to stand still."}]}, "Philippians 3:9": {"title": "Which righteousness a man is wearing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just listed his own credentials — circumcised the eighth day, tribe of Benjamin, a Pharisee, blameless as to the law — and called the whole file rubbish. This verse is what he wants instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two righteousnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not wearing a righteousness of my own that comes from the law, but the one that comes through faith in Christ, the righteousness that comes from God and rests on faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sets them side by side:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| his own | the other | |---|---| | from the law | from God | | produced | received | | worn as his own | found in Him |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say his own record was fake. By the law's own measure he says he was blameless. He says it is the wrong kind of thing to be standing in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Found in Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about being discovered in a place — where you turn out to be when somebody looks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's ambition is that whenever the inspection comes, he is located inside Christ rather than inside his own record. That is a statement about position, not about effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for the credentials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The term he used two verses earlier is deliberately coarse — refuse, what is thrown to dogs. He is not saying his advantages were worthless in themselves; he is describing what they became once he had something to compare them with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that I may gain Christ* is the sentence they were traded for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he wants next.** The verse immediately after: *that I may know Him and the power of His resurrection, and share in His sufferings.* Having settled that the standing is received rather than earned, the thing he then reaches for is not more standing. It is knowing the person."}]}, "Philippians 4:17–18": {"title": "The fruit to your account", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is thanking them for money, and he stops to say what he is actually pleased about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disclaimer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is not the gift itself that I am after**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just told them he has learned to be content in any state — *I know how to be abased, and I know how to abound.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not want them thinking the thank-you is a hint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What I am after is **the fruit that keeps mounting up to your account**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is commercial: **karpos** (fruit, yield) and **logos** (account, ledger entry). *Mounting up* is **pleonazo** — to increase, to abound."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is using the language of a bank ledger, and the account in question is theirs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The benefit he names is not his."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had received"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have everything I need, and more besides. I am amply supplied**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three statements of sufficiency in a row. The Greek for *I have everything* is **apecho** — the word used on receipts: *paid in full*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing from a Roman prison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who brought it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now that **Epaphroditus has put your gift into my hands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philippi sent a man as well as money. Epaphroditus nearly died on the errand — *he was sick almost unto death; but God had mercy on him* — and Paul sends him home with the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things he calls it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A fragrance of sweetness, a sacrifice that is welcomed, a delight to God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is temple vocabulary, and none of them is about Paul."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**A fragrance of sweetness** — the phrase from Leviticus for accepted sacrifice, the same one used over Noah's altar. See the note on Genesis 8:20–22.", "**A sacrifice that is welcomed** — **dekten**, acceptable, received.", "**A delight to God** — well-pleasing."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Money handed to a prisoner is described as smoke going up off an altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To an inmate's food and expenses in Rome. Paul says God took it as an offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Philippi had done before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Even in Thessalonica you sent aid once and again** for my necessities… **no church shared with me concerning giving and receiving but you only**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been supporting him since the beginning — since a riverside prayer meeting, a seller of purple, and a night in the town jail."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence after this one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And my God shall supply all your need according to His riches in glory by Christ Jesus**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes immediately after the receipt. See the note on Philippians 4:19–20."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whose ledger.** He says twice that he is not writing about his own supply. The subject of the paragraph is what is accumulating on the other side of the transaction."}]}, "Philippians 4:19–20": {"title": "\"According to His riches\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most quoted promises in Paul, and it is a thank-you note."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Philippians had just sent him a gift, carried by Epaphroditus, while he was in prison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses immediately before: *you sent aid once and again for my necessities… **I have all and abound. I am full**, having received from Epaphroditus the things sent from you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise is Paul's response to people who had given him something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My God shall supply all your need **according to His riches in glory** by Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The preposition is the whole verse. **According to**, not *out of*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A rich man giving *out of* his wealth might hand over a coin. Giving *according to* it means the size of the gift is set by the size of the fortune."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All your need\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chreia** — need, necessity, what is required. Not desire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had just distinguished the two in the same paragraph: *I have learned, in whatever state I am, **to be content**… I know how to be abased, and I know how to abound.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man promising supply for every need had already said he had learned to do without."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In glory\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The riches are located. Not in a national economy or a treasury, and not in the generosity of the Philippians, who were poor themselves — 2 Corinthians describes the Macedonian churches giving *in a great trial of affliction… their deep poverty abounded in the riches of their liberality.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The channel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything Paul promises anywhere runs through that phrase."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doxology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now to **our God and Father** be glory **forever and ever**. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not end the sentence with the supply. He ends it with praise, and the possessive is *our*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul had said four verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I can do all things through Christ who strengthens me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Also usually quoted alone, and also written in a passage about contentment in poverty. The *all things* he names in context are *being full and being hungry, both abounding and suffering need.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman prison, with a case pending, writing the most joyful letter in the New Testament. *Rejoice in the Lord always. Again I will say, rejoice.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the Philippians in particular.** *No church shared with me concerning giving and receiving but you only. For even in Thessalonica you sent aid once and again.* They had been funding him since the beginning — since a riverside prayer meeting and a purple merchant's house. See the note on Acts 16:14–15."}]}, "Proverbs 10:1–7": {"title": "\"A diligent hand quietly makes him rich\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is where the long run of two-line proverbs begins, and the pattern is fixed from here on: one line, then its opposite. Each verse is a small before-and-after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pair about hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A lazy hand leaves a person poor. A diligent hand quietly makes him rich.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for **diligent** is a sharp word — it is used of a threshing sledge, the tool that cuts through grain. Not busyness; something decisive that gets through work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination's *quietly* is picking up the sense of the verb, which is about steady accumulation rather than a sudden windfall. Nothing dramatic is being promised. Something is being built by a hand that keeps going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one nobody quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wealth won by wrong does no good in the end. Righteousness pulls a soul back from death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It arrives before the industry proverbs and sets the terms for them: the book is not simply in favour of getting more. Two verses later comes gathering in summer versus sleeping through harvest — timing, not just effort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On reading these as promises.** They are observations about how life generally runs, not contracts. The same book says elsewhere that the poor can be swept away by injustice regardless of how hard they work, and Ecclesiastes exists partly to press on the exceptions. Proverbs tells you where to put your weight; it does not promise the floor never gives."}]}, "Proverbs 10:22–27": {"title": "\"He adds no sorrow along with it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*It is the blessing of the LORD that makes rich, and He adds no sorrow along with it.* One of the most argued-over lines in Proverbs, because the second half can be read two ways in Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two readings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is compressed, and the disagreement is about what *toil* or *sorrow* attaches to:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**He adds no sorrow with it** — what God gives does not arrive with grief attached", "**and toiling adds nothing to it** — no amount of extra labour increases what the blessing gives"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are defensible from the grammar, and the second has an old pedigree. The first is the reading most English versions take, and the one this translation follows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the contrast is with the verse a few lines earlier — *wealth won by wrong does no good in the end* — and with the sluggard two verses later. The chapter is not against work. It is against believing work is the source."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What the wicked dreads will fall upon him. What the righteous long for will be given.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pair, set deliberately: one man's fear arrives, the other's longing does. Proverbs states these as the general run of things, in the confident style the book uses throughout."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How to read the run of promises here.** Chapter 10 is a rapid series of contrasts, and it does not pause for exceptions — that is not what this genre does. Job and Ecclesiastes exist partly as the counterweight, and the Bible keeps all three in the same collection without embarrassment. Proverbs tells you which way the world generally leans; it does not promise that it never tips the other way."}]}, "Proverbs 11:8–14": {"title": "\"In many wise counselors there is safety\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous line closes a run of sayings that are almost all about the mouth, and that is not an accident."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read what it is sitting with"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*with his mouth the godless would ruin his neighbor*", "*by the blessing of the upright a city is lifted high; by the mouth of the wicked it is torn down*", "*whoever belittles a neighbor has no sense at all*", "*a gossip goes about giving secrets away; a faithful spirit keeps the matter quiet*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *where there is no guidance, a people falls. In many wise counselors there is safety.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the proverb about counsel arrives at the end of a paragraph about how much damage talk can do — which is a caution built into the promise. The counsellors being commended are the sort just described as trustworthy with what they are told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Guidance** is a navigation word — the same root is used of steering a ship, and elsewhere in Proverbs of a person of understanding acquiring *direction*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Safety** is literally deliverance, rescue. Not \"a better outcome.\" Getting out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the proverb assumes.** Not that a crowd is wiser than one person — this book has plenty to say about crowds. It assumes *wise* counsellors, plural, who can keep a confidence. A proverb is a general truth well put, not a mechanism, and this one carries its own quality control in the verses above it."}]}, "Proverbs 12:22–28": {"title": "\"The lazy man never roasts what he caught\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A run of contrasts, and one of them is a picture rather than a principle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunter who does not cook"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The lazy man never roasts what he caught, but the diligent hold their gain as precious.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is difficult in Hebrew and translators have long divided over it. The sense most take is the one here: he went out, he caught something, and then he never got round to doing anything with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The failure is not in the hunt. It is in what happened after — the thing obtained and not used, the catch left on the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the few proverbs about laziness that concerns something already acquired rather than something never attempted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line worth stopping on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Worry weighs a heart down low, but a kind word makes it glad.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb means to bow down or make stoop. Anxiety is described physically — it bends a person over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the remedy offered is not advice or resolution. It is *a good word* from somebody else. In a chapter full of proverbs about hard work and honest speech, this one says that a heart bent by worry is straightened by a kindness spoken to it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *In the way of righteousness there is life, and along that path there is no death.* It is the strongest thing said in the chapter, and it reads oddly among proverbs about lying lips and lazy hands — which is how Proverbs works. The small practical sayings keep being punctuated by something much larger, without transition."}]}, "Proverbs 13:17–25": {"title": "\"Walk with the wise and grow wise\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The proverb everyone knows from this passage is about the company you keep, and the Hebrew behind it is more physical than the English."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb is \"walk\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *admire*, not *listen to*. **Halak** — to walk, to go along with, to travel in someone's company. It is the ordinary word for how you spend your days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is not of picking mentors. It is of who is beside you when nothing special is happening, and the proverb claims that wisdom transfers by proximity rather than by instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half is deliberately blunt: *keep fools for friends and come to harm*. Same mechanism, opposite direction. Whatever you walk with, you become."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A longing fulfilled is sweet to the soul. Fools would rather die than leave their evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Odd pairing, until you see it. The verse before speaks of a desire finally satisfied; the verse after describes people who will not let go of what is destroying them. Then: walk with the wise. The proverb about company is set between something worth wanting and an example of wanting the wrong thing badly enough to die of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On reading a proverb.** These are general truths, sharply put — not guarantees. The same chapter says the fields of the poor could yield plenty but injustice sweeps the harvest away, which is the book itself acknowledging that the pattern does not always hold. A proverb tells you which way to lean, not what is certain to happen."}]}, "Proverbs 13:9–16": {"title": "\"Money gathered slowly grows\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eight couplets, and one of them is about how money behaves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two ways it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Money **grabbed fast slips fast away**. Money **gathered slowly grows**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the first is wealth from vanity, from nothing, from vapour — the same root as Ecclesiastes' *vanity of vanities*. The older rendering is *wealth gotten by vanity shall be diminished.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second is literally *gathered by the hand* — a handful at a time, by labour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb is not against money. It is about the manner of its arrival, and it says the manner determines whether it stays."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes fast, goes fast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb in the first line is to become small, to dwindle. It does not say the money is taken away — it says it shrinks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Hope stretched thin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hope **stretched thin** makes the heart sick. A longing filled is a **tree of life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *hope deferred*. Something delayed past the point the heart can hold it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer is not a lowered expectation. It is a longing **filled** — and the image chosen for it is the tree in the garden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lamp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The light of the upright **burns clear and glad**. The lamp of the wicked **flickers out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first verb carries rejoicing. A light that is glad."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the wisdom comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The teaching of the wise is a **spring of life**, turning your feet from the snares of death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A spring, not a cistern. Something with its own source."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one about advice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pride only breeds more quarrels. **Wisdom listens, and takes advice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *with the well-advised is wisdom*. It is not the clever who are wise in Proverbs; it is the ones who let themselves be counselled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the flag"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The clever act on what they know. **The fool spreads his folly like a flag**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he spreads it out, unfurls it, makes a display."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter says a few verses later.** *A good man leaves an inheritance to his children's children, and the wealth of the sinner is stored up for the righteous.* Slowly gathered, and it outlasts him by two generations. See the note on Proverbs 13:17–25."}]}, "Proverbs 15:1–7": {"title": "\"A gentle answer turns away wrath\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted proverb about speech, and the word behind *gentle* is not what most readers assume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **rak** — soft, tender. It is used of tender meat, of a delicate child, and of Leah's eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not weak, and not evasive. Soft in texture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turns away wrath.* The verb is about causing something to go back the way it came — deflection rather than absorption."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb is not saying a soft answer makes you feel better about the abuse. It says the anger goes back down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second half gives the alternative: *a harsh word stirs the anger up.* The Hebrew for *harsh* means painful, hurtful — a word that causes grief."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves describe an answer. The choice is not between speaking and staying silent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the paragraph sits with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every proverb in the run is about the mouth:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the tongue of the wise makes knowledge sweet*", "*a healing tongue is a tree of life; a twisted tongue breaks the spirit*", "*the lips of the wise scatter knowledge like seed*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tree of life image is the strongest. It appears four times in Proverbs, and one of them is a way of speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The eyes of the LORD are everywhere, watching the evil and the good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An odd insertion in a run about speech — until you notice what it does to the rest. All these exchanges happen in front of somebody."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where a soft answer is hardest.** The examples in Scripture are unflattering. Peter, told the cross was coming, argued. James and John proposed fire. Paul called a high priest a whitewashed wall and then apologised when told who he was. The proverb is not describing a personality type — it is describing a choice made in the second after being provoked."}]}, "Proverbs 15:16–23": {"title": "\"A word in season, how good it is\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The run ends on a line about timing, and everything before it is about what makes a household bearable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two \"betters\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Better a little with the fear of the LORD than great riches and trouble with it. Better a plate of greens where love is than a fattened calf and hatred at the table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both compare a small good thing with a large bad one, and both put the difference in the atmosphere rather than the menu."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A fattened calf was the best meal available — the thing killed for the prodigal's homecoming. Proverbs says vegetables beat it if the room is hostile."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The temper proverbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A hot temper stirs up strife. A patient one calms the quarrel down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for patient is *long of nostrils* — slow to flare. The idiom is the same one used of God being slow to anger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is joy in giving the right answer, and **a word in season**, how good it is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves. The right answer, and the right moment — and Proverbs treats them as separate achievements."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A true thing said at the wrong hour is not counted here as a success. The verse just before it makes the same point from the other side: *the one who understands walks straight ahead* rather than blurting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it sits with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Plans fall apart without counsel, but with many advisers they stand.* The season-word proverb comes directly after it — which suggests the good answer is often somebody else's, arriving when you needed it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How to use a run like this.** Proverbs 15 is a chapter of contrasts about speech: a gentle answer turning away wrath at the top, a soothing tongue as a tree of life in the middle, and the word in season at the end. Read the chapter whole and the subject is not wisdom in the abstract. It is what comes out of a person's mouth in a house."}]}, "Proverbs 16:1–7": {"title": "\"The LORD weighs the spirit\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven proverbs, and five of them name the LORD as the one who actually decides."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plans"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **plans of the heart belong to us**, but **the answer of the tongue comes from the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves, and the division is between what a person arranges inwardly and what comes out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs says this repeatedly — *a man's heart plans his way, but the LORD directs his steps*, eight verses later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man's ways **all seem clean in his own eyes**, but **the LORD weighs the spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *weighs* is **takan** — to measure, to gauge, to test the weight of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb does not say the man is lying. He genuinely thinks so, and the assessment is being made elsewhere on a different instrument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commitment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Commit your work to the LORD, and **your plans will find firm ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *commit* is **galal** — to roll. Roll your works onto the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Find firm ground** is **kun** — established, fixed, made to stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before said the plans are ours. This one says what to do with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has **made everything for its purpose**, even the wicked for the day of trouble.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for its own answer* — everything has a matching end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pride"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **hates every proud heart**. Be sure of this: **it will not go unpunished**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *an abomination to the LORD is everyone proud of heart*, and then *hand to hand* — a gesture that meant a thing was as good as settled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs returns to it two chapters on: *pride goes before destruction.* See the note on Proverbs 18:9–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **love and faithfulness sin is covered over**, and by **the fear of the LORD one turns from evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chesed ve'emet** — covenant loyalty and truth. The same pairing Jacob asks Joseph for on his deathbed, and the pairing John puts in his prologue: *full of grace and truth*. See the note on Genesis 47:27–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Covered over** is **kaphar** — the atonement word. The verb behind **kapporeth**, the mercy seat, and behind Yom Kippur."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin is not said to be excused or overlooked. It is atoned for, and the agents named are love and faithfulness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves of that verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Atonement in the first clause, and turning from evil in the second — in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The enemies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **a man's ways please the LORD**, even **his enemies are made to be at peace with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He makes even his enemies to be at peace with him* — God is the one doing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The peace is not negotiated by the man and is not conditional on the enemies changing their minds. It is made."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter puts the initiative.** The heart plans, the eyes approve, the man commits — and in every one of the seven verses the outcome is attached to the LORD: the answer, the weighing, the establishing, the purpose, the covering, and the peace."}]}, "Proverbs 17:15–21": {"title": "Both alike", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven proverbs, and the first one names two opposite errors and gives them the same verdict."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Clearing the guilty and condemning the just, **the LORD hates them both alike**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he who justifies the wicked and he who condemns the just* — the two verbs are **tsadaq** and **rasha**, the courtroom pair."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are miscarriages, and they run in opposite directions. Proverbs says the LORD hates them equally."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *both alike* is *even both of them* — the phrase exists to close off any ranking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same words in the law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one **God declares guilty** repays his neighbour double* — **rasha** again. See the note on Exodus 22:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Deuteronomy: *the judges will decide, **clearing the innocent and condemning the guilty**.* See the note on Deuteronomy 25:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law uses the two verbs the right way round. This proverb names what it looks like when they are reversed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fool with money"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why should a fool **hold money to buy wisdom**, when **he has no heart to learn**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a price in the hand of a fool*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has the means and not the appetite. Proverbs treats the second as the binding constraint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The friend"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A friend **loves at all times**, and **a brother is born for the hard days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a brother is born for adversity*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses that are usually read as a contrast and can be read as a pair: constancy in one, and a relation that exists for the bad stretch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Shaking hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man with no sense **shakes hands on a deal** and **takes on his neighbor's debt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs returns to surety repeatedly and never approves of it — *he who is surety for a stranger will suffer*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one exception in Scripture is Judah offering himself for Benjamin, and Paul offering to cover Onesimus's debt: *if he owes you anything, put that on my account*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quarrel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **loves a quarrel loves sin**. Whoever **builds a high gate invites ruin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the second is *he who exalts his gate seeks destruction*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A gate higher than the wall around it is display rather than defence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crooked heart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A crooked heart **never stumbles onto good**, and a twisted tongue **falls headlong into trouble**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *crooked* is **iqqesh** — twisted, perverse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody arrives at good by accident from there. Proverbs makes it a statement about direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To father a fool brings only sorrow. **The parent of a fool has no joy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book says the reverse as often: *a wise son makes a glad father*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the first verse lands in the New Testament"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But to him who does not work but believes on Him **who justifies the ungodly**, his faith is accounted for righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes that in Romans 4, using the same construction Proverbs condemns — and the whole of Romans 3 is his account of how it is not a miscarriage: *that He might be **just and the justifier** of the one who has faith in Jesus.* See the note on Romans 3:23–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the opening verse rules out.** Both directions. Calling a guilty man innocent and calling an innocent man guilty are named in the same line and given one verdict."}]}, "Proverbs 18:17–24": {"title": "\"Death and life are in the power of the tongue\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most quoted proverbs about speech, and the two lines it sits between are what keep it in proportion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man eats his fill from the fruit of his mouth; the harvest of his lips is what satisfies him. Death and life are in the power of the tongue, and those who love it will eat its fruit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is agricultural and it runs across both verses: words are a crop, and the person who grew them is the one who has to eat the harvest. What you say comes back to your own table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *power* is literally **hand** — death and life are in the hand of the tongue. It is a small organ given a large grip."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the surrounding verses are about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at the company it keeps in this chapter:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the first to plead his case seems right, until another comes and questions him*", "*a brother offended is harder to win than a fortified city*", "*the poor plead for mercy, but the rich answer with harsh words*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is about speech between people — testimony, a quarrel that has hardened, an answer given roughly to someone with no power. That is the field this proverb grows in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On how it gets used.** The verse is often taken to mean that spoken words shape outcomes by their own force. What the proverb states is narrower and harder to dismiss: your words do real damage or real good to other people, and you will live with the result. James says the same at length — a small rudder, a small fire — and both writers are talking about the tongue's effect on human beings, not on circumstances."}]}, "Proverbs 18:9–16": {"title": "The tower and the wall", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two fortifications in consecutive verses, and only one of them is real."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tower"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The name of the LORD is **a strong tower**. The righteous **run into it and are safe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *migdal oz* — a fortified tower, the strongpoint inside a walled city that people retreated to when the outer defences fell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs: they **run**, and they are **set on high** — the Hebrew is to be lifted up out of reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what does the protecting. Not their record. The **name**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wealth of the rich is his fortified city, a high wall, **in his own imagining**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse, and it is built as the deliberate opposite."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same picture — a city, a high wall — and one clause added at the end that pulls the whole thing down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in his own conceit*, in his imagination. The wall exists in the mind of the man behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs sets the two side by side and lets the reader compare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crushed spirit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man's spirit **can carry him through sickness**, but **a crushed spirit, who can bear it?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *carry through* is **kul** — to sustain, to hold up, to provide for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse observes something ordinary: a person's inner state affects what their body can bear, and there is a state past which nothing carries them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second half is a question, not an answer. Proverbs leaves it open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pride and honour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before destruction **a heart grows proud**, but **humility comes before honour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pairing appears at the end of chapter 15, and again at 16:18 in its most famous form — *pride goes before destruction, and a haughty spirit before a fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book says it three times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Answering too early"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **answer before you have listened** is folly, and it shames you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he who answers a matter before he hears it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A gift **opens the way** before a man and ushers him into the presence of the great.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs is describing how the world works rather than recommending it. The book does the same with bribes elsewhere and condemns them plainly: *he who hates a bribe will live.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The slack workman"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who is slack in his work is **brother to the one who destroys**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a lesser fault, in this verse. Family."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being contrasted.** A tower you run into, and a wall you have built out of your own resources and your own opinion of them. Proverbs puts them in consecutive lines and never says the second one falls — it just tells you where it is."}]}, "Proverbs 19:22–29": {"title": "\"What a person longs for is steady love\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first line of this passage names what everybody actually wants, and the word it uses is the covenant word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The longing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What a person longs for is **steady love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chesed** — loyal love, covenant kindness, the word used of God's own faithfulness some two hundred and fifty times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older rendering is *the desire of a man is his kindness*, and it has been read both ways: what a man desires is kindness, and a man's desirability is his kindness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the quality named is **chesed** — and it is not niceness. It is the kindness of somebody keeping faith with you when they need not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **better to be poor than a liar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pairing is the argument. Set a man's kindness against a man's means, and the proverb says which one is worth having."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same measure the whole book keeps applying: *better is a dinner of herbs where love is, than a fattened calf with hatred.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The satisfied life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fear of the LORD leads to life, and one who has it **rests satisfied, untouched by harm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he abides satisfied* — lodges, stays the night, remains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lazy man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The lazy man **buries his hand in the dish and will not even lift it to his mouth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs is funny about laziness in a way it is about nothing else. He has got as far as the food and cannot complete the journey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Correction, and who it works on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strike a scoffer, and **the simple learn caution**; correct a wise one, and **he gains knowledge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three characters and three outcomes, and note who benefits from what."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Striking the scoffer does nothing for the scoffer. It teaches the onlooker."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wise man is the only one in the verse who profits from correction directed at him — which is Proverbs' definition of wisdom throughout: not knowing more, but being correctable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning about drifting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stop listening to instruction, my son, and you will stray** from the words of knowledge.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing dramatic is described. Just stopping listening, and then drift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end of the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Judgements are ready for the scoffers, and beatings for the backs of fools.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that they are prepared, already established. Nothing has to be arranged; the consequence is standing by."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse in the middle of it all.** *A child who assaults his father and drives out his mother brings shame and disgrace.* Proverbs sits its bluntest sentence about family violence between a joke about a lazy man's dinner and a note on correction — the book keeps ordinary life and serious things in the same column."}]}, "Proverbs 19:8–14": {"title": "\"A sensible wife comes from the LORD\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven couplets, and the last one draws a line between two kinds of provision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two sources"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*House and wealth are **handed down from parents**, but **a sensible wife comes from the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole point of the verse, and it is a contrast, not a comparison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Property comes down a family line. It can be inherited, arranged, negotiated, insured. The other cannot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **maskeleth** — from **sakal**, to be prudent, to have insight, to act with understanding that produces good outcomes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same root in Joshua 1:8, where meditating on the book day and night makes a man's way prosperous and gives him *good success*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the word used of Abigail: *a woman of good sense* — **sekel** — listed before her looks. She talked a future king out of a massacre and saved a household in an afternoon. See the note on 1 Samuel 25:2–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same chapter says the opposite runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A foolish child is his father's ruin, and **a quarrelsome wife is a leaking roof that never stops dripping**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs uses that image three times. It is not a storm. It is the drip that will not stop — and the book's complaint about it is always its relentlessness rather than its force."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The glory of overlooking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Good sense makes a person **slow to anger**, and **his glory is to overlook an offence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *overlook* is to pass over, to let go by."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is called his **glory** — his beauty, his adornment. Not his loss, and not his weakness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king's two moods"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A king's rage is like the roar of a lion, but **his favour is like dew on the grass**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are natural and neither is negotiable — you cannot argue with a lion or manufacture dew. The verse simply reports what proximity to power is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What opens the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever gains sense **loves his own soul**; whoever keeps understanding **will find good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Getting wisdom is described as an act of self-love, which is not the usual framing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the one about luxury"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Luxury does not suit a fool.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that delicate living is not fitting for him — it does not sit right, like good clothes on the wrong man."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Proverbs says about finding one.** *Whoever finds a wife finds a good thing, and obtains favour from the LORD.* The verb is to find, which is what you do with something you were not able to manufacture — and the favour is credited to the same source as the wife."}]}, "Proverbs 2:6–11": {"title": "\"From His mouth come knowledge and understanding\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom in this chapter is not a skill acquired. It is something given, and the passage says where from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The source"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is the LORD who gives wisdom; from His mouth come knowledge and understanding.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is spoken — out of His mouth. Which puts the search for wisdom in the same place as the search for what He has said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses just before this describe looking for it like silver and digging for it like hidden treasure. Then the reason that search works: the thing being dug for is being handed over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does once you have it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the run of verbs — every one of them is protective:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["He is a **shield** to those who walk with honest hearts", "He **guards** the paths of the just", "He **watches over** the way of those who love Him", "good sense will **keep watch** over you", "understanding will **guard** your steps"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five in six verses. In this chapter wisdom is not primarily about being clever. It is about being kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it settles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wisdom will settle into your heart, and knowledge will be a delight to your soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *come into* your heart — it takes up residence rather than being consulted. And the result is described as pleasure, not duty: knowledge becoming something a person enjoys."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows immediately.** *Then you will understand what is right and fair, every good path will open before you.* The understanding of what is right comes after the wisdom is given, not before. In Proverbs the discernment is a consequence of the gift, which is why the chapter opens by telling you to go looking for it."}]}, "Proverbs 22:1–7": {"title": "\"Train up a child in the way he should go\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will not turn from it.* A promise with a long fuse, and the Hebrew has more in it than the English lets through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"According to his way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is **al pi darko** — literally *on the mouth of his way*. There is no word for *should* in the Hebrew at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The natural sense is *according to his way*: this child, his bent, his grain, the particular shape of him. It turns the instruction from a method applied to children in general into an act of attention paid to one child in particular."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You train the child you actually have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chanak** is used elsewhere of dedicating a house or a temple — setting something apart for the purpose it was made for. The related noun gives us Hanukkah, the feast of dedication."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the training is not primarily drilling. It is dedicating: putting a child on the path he was made for, early."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he is old he will not turn from it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The timescale is the striking part. Not next year. When he is old. The promise reaches past the years a parent has any influence at all, and past the stretch where it may look as if nothing took."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what makes it worth having. A promise that only covered the compliant years would be no comfort to anybody."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter opens with.** *Choose a good name over great riches.* The chapter that contains the great parenting promise begins by saying what is worth handing on, and it is not money. Four verses later: *the reward of humility and the fear of the LORD is riches and honour and life.* The inheritance being described runs through character."}]}, "Proverbs 28:21–28": {"title": "\"The stingy man hurries after wealth\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eight couplets about money, and the sharpest of them is about a man in a hurry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hurry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **stingy man hurries after wealth** and never knows that want is coming for him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *stingy* is *a man with an evil eye* — the idiom for a grudging, tight, envious way of looking at things. Jesus uses the same expression: *if your eye is bad, your whole body will be full of darkness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are said about him: he hurries, and he does not know what is coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is coming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Want.* The Hebrew is lack, poverty — and the verb has it arriving at him, coming upon him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is running toward money with the thing he is running from already on the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Trust and enrichment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **greedy heart stirs up strife**, but **whoever trusts in the LORD will be enriched**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves are the alternative to each other. A grasping heart produces conflict; trust produces increase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *enriched* is to be made fat — the word for prosperity and fullness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift that does not empty"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **gives to the poor will lack nothing**, but whoever shuts his eyes to them gathers many curses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall not lack*. Nothing at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the opposite is not *will be poor* — it is *gathers many curses*. The man who shuts his eyes is still gathering; the chapter simply names what he is collecting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rebuke and flattery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **rebukes** a man will find more favour at last than the one who **flatters** with his tongue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At last* — afterward, in the end. The flatterer is preferred now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set that beside a king who *never once crossed* his son, never asked *why do you do this?* See the note on 1 Kings 1:5–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Trusting your own heart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **trusts his own heart is a fool**, but whoever walks in wisdom will be kept safe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the flat contradiction of the usual advice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last couplet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the wicked rise, people go into hiding, **but when they perish, the righteous increase**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says it twice — the same line appears seven verses earlier."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the same chapter says about interest.** *Whoever swells his wealth by interest and gain **gathers it for one who is kind to the poor**.* He is still gathering. The proverb only disagrees with him about who the pile is for. See the note on Proverbs 28:7–13."}]}, "Proverbs 28:7–13": {"title": "\"Gathers it for one who is kind to the poor\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A proverb about interest that turns out to be about who ends up holding the money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **swells his wealth by interest and gain gathers it for one who is kind to the poor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not stopped, and he is not impoverished. He goes on swelling the pile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb simply tells him whose pile it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law said about interest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall not lend him your money for interest, nor lend him your food at a profit* — Leviticus 25, about a brother who has become poor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Taking interest from the destitute was the specific practice the law forbade, and it is the practice in view here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who receives it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One who is **kind to the poor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the one who shows favour, who is gracious to the poor — the root of **chen**, grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who squeezed the poor for interest is described as a collector working on behalf of a man who gives to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same idea elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wealth of the sinner is stored up for the righteous* — Proverbs 13:22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the one who misses the mark, God gives the task of gathering and piling up, only to hand it over to the one who pleases God* — Ecclesiastes 2:26. See the note on Ecclesiastes 2:24–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three places, same claim. The gathering and the keeping are two different jobs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer that offends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a man **turns his ear from hearing the law**, even his prayer becomes an offence.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that his prayer is an abomination. He is still praying — the proverb does not say he stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever leads the upright down an evil road **falls into his own pit**, but the **blameless will inherit good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pit is his. He dug it for somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rich man's eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rich man is **wise in his own eyes**, but a poor man with sense **sees right through him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that the poor man searches him out. Money buys the appearance of judgement, and it does not fool a discerning man with nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **hides** his sins will not prosper, but whoever **confesses and forsakes** them will find mercy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs on each side, and the outcome named at the end is mercy."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What connects the chapter.** Hiding. The man hides his sins, the wealthy man hides behind his own good opinion, the people hide when the wicked rise. And the one line about not hiding ends in mercy."}]}, "Proverbs 29:14–18": {"title": "\"Where there is no vision\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five proverbs, and the last one is the famous one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A king who **judges the poor with truth** will have **his throne made sure forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *judges the poor in truth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stability of a throne is attached to how it treats the people with no leverage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rod"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rod and reproof give wisdom, but **a child left to himself shames his mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a child sent away* — left to his own devices."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mother is named rather than the father, which Proverbs does more than once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wicked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the wicked increase, **so does wrongdoing**, but the righteous will watch their downfall.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Discipline your child, and **he will give you rest**; he will **bring delight to your soul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *delight* is **maadanim** — delicacies, dainties."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famous line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where there is **no vision**, **the people cast off restraint**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *vision* is **chazon** — a prophetic vision, the disclosed word. It is the word used of Isaiah's *vision*, and of Nathan and Samuel receiving one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not imagination or planning. Revelation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *cast off restraint* is **para** — to let loose, to run wild, to go unbridled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used of Israel at the golden calf: *Moses saw that the people were unrestrained*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older rendering — *the people perish* — comes from an older reading of the verb. The Hebrew is about a people breaking ranks, not about death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the second half says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **happy is the one who keeps the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ashrei** — blessed, happy. The word that opens Psalm 1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is a pair: no revealed word, and a people running loose; the law kept, and a person content."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are about what a word does to conduct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the word appears elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD answered me and said: \"**Write the vision** and make it plain on tablets, that he may run who reads it. For **the vision is yet for an appointed time**… though it tarries, wait for it; because **it will surely come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk, and the same noun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And 1 Samuel 3, of the period before Samuel: *the word of the LORD was rare in those days; **there was no widespread revelation**.* See the note on 1 Samuel 3:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the condition the proverb describes, stated as history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened in that period"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges — a book whose refrain is *in those days there was no king in Israel; **everyone did what was right in his own eyes***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the proverb's first half, described from the inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*An unjust man is an abomination to the righteous, and he who is upright in the way is an abomination to the wicked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last verse of Proverbs 29, and it works both ways."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse is about.** Not ambition or planning. **Chazon** is a word received — and the proverb's claim is that when it is absent a people does not die, it comes unbridled."}]}, "Proverbs 3:1–4": {"title": "\"Tie them around your neck\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father tells his son to keep two things close, and describes where to keep them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let love and faithfulness leave you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew pair is **hesed and emet** — covenant love and dependability. It is one of the standard descriptions of God's own character, the words He speaks over Himself when He passes by Moses on the mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the son is being told to wear what God is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two places"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tie them around your neck, write them on the tablet of your heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outside and inside. Around the neck is visible — an ornament, or the cord a seal hung on. On the tablet of the heart is where nobody else sees."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, and the order matters: the visible one is named first, but the inward one is the permanent one. Jeremiah later promises a covenant written on hearts rather than stone, using the same image."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will add to your days, long years and a peaceful life… you will find favour and good name in the sight of God and everyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Favour with both — God and people. Luke uses almost exactly this phrasing of Jesus growing up in Nazareth: increasing in favour with God and with people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not forget my teaching, let your heart hold on to my commands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *learn* — do not forget. The instruction assumes it has already been given. Much of Proverbs is a father repeating what a son has heard before, on the understanding that knowing something and keeping hold of it are different jobs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes three verses later.** *Trust in the LORD with all your heart and do not lean on your own understanding.* This paragraph is the run-up to it, and it is worth reading them together: hold on to what you were taught, and do not rely on your own reading of the situation."}]}, "Proverbs 3:13–20": {"title": "What is in wisdom's two hands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom is described as a person holding two things, and the contents of each hand are listed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is the one who **finds** wisdom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Finding, not manufacturing. It is the verb for coming across something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparisons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Worth more than **silver**… yields more than **gold**… more precious than **jewels**, and **nothing you desire compares to her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three commodities, escalating, and then a clause that removes the possibility of any further comparison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Long life is in her **right hand**, riches and honour in her **left**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The right hand in that world was the hand of the primary gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the arrangement is: **length of days** is what she is chiefly holding out, and **riches and honour** are in the other hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are there. The passage does not disparage the second pair — it ranks them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The paths"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her ways are **ways of pleasantness**, and all her paths are **peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *pleasantness* is **noam** — agreeableness, delight, beauty. And peace is **shalom**, wholeness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She is **a tree of life** to those who take hold of her, and blessed are all who **will not let her go**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first mention of that tree since Genesis 3, where the way to it was barred by a flaming sword."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs of grip: take hold, and do not let go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What wisdom built"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **wisdom** the LORD laid the earth's foundations, by **understanding** He set the heavens in place. By His **knowledge** the deep waters burst open.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three words the passage has been recommending are used of the creation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever wisdom is, the argument runs, it is the thing the universe was made with — which is why a man who gets it is working with the grain of things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs 8 develops it at length: *the LORD possessed me at the beginning of His way… when He prepared the heavens, I was there.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Solomon asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give to Your servant an understanding heart to judge Your people.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *because you have asked this thing, and have not asked long life for yourself, nor riches… **I have also given you what you have not asked**: both riches and honour.* See the note on 1 Kings 3:5–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for what was in the right hand. What was in the left came anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul locates it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ, **in whom every treasure of wisdom and knowledge lies hidden**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Colossians 2, and the Greek is *hidden* — stored up, concealed, the way treasure is. See the note on Colossians 2:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter says four verses earlier.** *Honour the LORD with all you have, with the first and best of everything you gather.* And four verses later, *do not despise the LORD's discipline.* Proverbs 3 puts firstfruits, correction and wisdom on the same page. See the note on Proverbs 3:9–12."}]}, "Proverbs 3:9–12": {"title": "\"The first and best of everything\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses, and the second pair is not what the first pair leads you to expect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Honour the LORD with all you have, with **the first and best of everything you gather**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **reshith** — the beginning, the first, the choicest. It is the word for firstfruits, and it is the first word of the Bible: *in the beginning*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are specified: that it is **first**, and that it is **best**. Not what is left over, and not the ordinary portion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then your **barns will overflow**, your **vats will brim with new wine**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *overflow* is to be filled to bursting. And the vats *burst out* with new wine — the verb is used of a wall breaking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grain and wine, the two staples of that economy, and both described as more than the container can hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, without any transition:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My son, **do not despise the LORD's discipline**, and do not grow weary of His correction.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the next verse. Overflowing barns, and then a word about correction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs sets them side by side deliberately. Both are how a father treats a son — the provision and the correcting come from the same relationship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for discipline"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Musar** — instruction, chastening, the whole business of forming a child. It is the Hebrew equivalent of the Greek **paideia** that runs through Hebrews 12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes this exact passage: *my son, do not despise the chastening of the LORD, nor be discouraged when you are rebuked by Him; for whom the LORD loves He chastens.* See the note on Hebrews 12:5–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD corrects **the one He loves**, as a father the son **in whom he delights**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves name affection. The correction is evidence of the relationship, not a withdrawal of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the two halves belong together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who reads only the first two verses gets a formula: give the first and best, receive overflow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter will not let him stop there. The same Father who fills the barns also corrects, and the son is told not to despise either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opens with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Trust in the LORD with all your heart, and lean not on your own understanding; in all your ways acknowledge Him, and He shall direct your paths.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five verses before this one. The honouring with firstfruits is one instance of the acknowledging."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Proverbs says elsewhere about the arithmetic.** *There is one who scatters, yet increases more; and there is one who withholds more than is right, but it leads to poverty.* The book keeps insisting the sums do not work the way they look like they should."}]}, "Proverbs 4:10–19": {"title": "Two paths", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father's instruction, and it ends by comparing two roads by how much you can see on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen, **my son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs 4 opens *hear, my children, the instruction of a father*, and then does something unusual — Solomon quotes his own father teaching him:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When I was my father's son, tender and the only one in the sight of my mother, he also taught me, and said to me, \"**Let your heart retain my words**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three generations in one chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take my words to heart, and **the years of your life will be many**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you **walk**, nothing will hold you back; when you **run**, you will not stumble.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *hold back* is about a stride being cramped — the picture is of a path wide enough to move on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walking and running are separated deliberately: the first is about obstruction, the second about speed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grip"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hold on to my instruction, **do not let it go**, guard it well, **for it is your life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four imperatives in one line, and the last clause gives the reason: not *it will improve your life*. It **is** your life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four ways of avoiding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **set foot** on the path of the wicked, do not **walk** in the way of evil. **Avoid** it, do not travel on it, **turn away** from it and pass on by.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five clauses saying one thing. Proverbs rarely repeats itself this hard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The counsel is not about resisting on the road. It is about not being on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the wicked cannot do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For they **cannot sleep unless they do wrong**, they **lose their rest unless they trip someone up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *their sleep is taken away unless they cause someone to fall*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An appetite that has to be fed before the day can end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their diet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **feed on the bread of wickedness**, they **drink the wine of violence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Staple food and drink. It is not an occasional thing; it is what they live on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first road"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the path of the righteous is like the dawn, growing brighter and brighter until full day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the light of dawn that shines ever brighter unto the perfect day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a floodlight switched on. First light, and then more of it, on a curve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The measure of the road is how much can be seen from it, and it increases."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second road"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The way of the wicked is **like deep darkness**; they **cannot even see what makes them fall**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **aphelah** — thick darkness, gloom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second clause is the sting. Not that they fall — that they never find out what over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two comparisons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both roads are described by visibility rather than by comfort or outcome. One gets brighter; on the other the cause of the fall stays invisible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep your heart **with all diligence**, for out of it spring the issues of life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then a run of instructions about the parts of a body: the mouth, the eyes, the eyelids, the feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ponder the path of your feet, and let all your ways be established.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *established* is **kun** — fixed, set firm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the dawn image goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until the day dawns and **the morning star rises in your hearts**.* — Peter, of the prophetic word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Malachi: *the Sun of Righteousness shall arise with healing in His wings.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the two roads are told apart.** Not by where they lead. Proverbs measures them by the light on them — one brightening toward noon, and one where a man cannot see what tripped him."}]}, "Proverbs 4:20–27": {"title": "\"Health to the whole body\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father tells his son what to do with words, and then works down the body part by part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the words are called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are life to those who find them, and **health to the whole body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *health* is **marpe** — healing, a cure, and by extension soundness. It is used of a physician's remedy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the phrase is literally *to all his flesh*. Not to his mind or his character. His flesh."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How to keep them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs in two lines: *pay attention… lean your ear in close… do not let them slip from your sight*, and then the fourth, which is the destination — *keep them deep within your heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ear, eye, and then inside. The route is specified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the body, in order"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the heart** — *above all else, guard it, for from it flow the springs of life*", "**the mouth** — put away twisted speech", "**the eyes** — look straight ahead", "**the feet** — give careful thought to where you step"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Heart first, and everything after it flows from that. The Hebrew for *springs* is the word for the outgoings or sources of a thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Guard your heart\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is a sentry's word — to watch over, to keep under guard. It assumes something is trying to get in, or out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not sentimental. It is hydraulic: whatever is in there is what will come out everywhere else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the passage ends.** *Do not turn to the right or the left.* The same phrase Joshua was given about the book of the law. The chapter is not describing a mood or an atmosphere — it is describing a road, walked deliberately, by somebody watching where their feet go."}]}, "Proverbs 5:15–23": {"title": "\"Drink water from your own cistern\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father's warning to a son, and the middle of it turns into something warmer than a warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Drink water from **your own cistern**, fresh water from **your own well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water rights were the most valuable thing a household owned in that country. A cistern was cut into rock at real expense and belonged to the family who dug it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage runs on the metaphor without ever dropping it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Should **your springs overflow in the streets**, your streams of water **in the public squares**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water spilled into a street is water lost. The image is of something valuable running away where anybody can use it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let them be **yours alone**, never to be shared with strangers.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May **your fountain be blessed**, and may you **rejoice in the wife of your youth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *let your fountain be blessed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The wife of your youth** — the phrase turns up in Malachi as well, in the passage about God hating divorce: *she is your companion and your wife by covenant*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The animals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **loving doe**, a **graceful deer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a loving hind, a graceful doe*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs is not usually tender. This is the exception, and it sits in the middle of a chapter about avoiding an adulteress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let her tenderness **fill you always**, be **forever captivated by her love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shagah** — to be intoxicated, to reel, to go astray in the sense of being carried away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word used two verses later of the man who is captivated by somebody else's wife. Proverbs uses one verb for both and lets the direction decide."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why be **captivated, my son, by another woman**? Why **embrace the arms of a stranger**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb. The question is not whether a man will be carried away — the passage assumes he will — but by whom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **a man's ways are in full view of the LORD**, and **He watches every path he takes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He ponders all his goings* — **palas**, to weigh, to make level."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chapter is framed as a secret that might be discovered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wicked are **trapped by their own sins**, held fast **in the cords of their own guilt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody else does the tying. The image is of a man caught in rope he made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's friend Elihu uses the same picture of cords. See the note on Job 36:8–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **die for want of discipline**, led astray **by their own great folly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *led astray* is **shagah** again — the third use in the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Carried away by the wife, carried away by the stranger, and carried away to death. One verb, three times, and the whole chapter turns on where the man lets it take him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opened with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the lips of an immoral woman drip honey, and her mouth is smoother than oil; **but in the end she is bitter as wormwood**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm puts the same picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your wife within your house will be like a fruitful vine.* See the note on Psalms 128:3–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word the chapter is built on.** *Shagah* — to be carried away. Proverbs uses it of the wife, the stranger, and the ruin, and never suggests the son will avoid being carried away by something."}]}, "Proverbs 9:10–12": {"title": "\"Where wisdom begins\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence the whole book is built on, stated here for the second time — it opened the collection back in chapter 1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Begins\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fear of the LORD is where wisdom begins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word can mean the starting point or the chief part, and both senses are usually intended. It is the first thing and the principal thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the verse rules out is treating wisdom as a technique that could be acquired by someone starting anywhere. There is a door, and this is it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second line defines the first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And knowing the Holy One is understanding.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew poetry works by pairing lines that illuminate each other. So *fear of the LORD* is set beside *knowing the Holy One* — which keeps the fear from collapsing into terror."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the awe of someone who knows who they are dealing with. You cannot be in awe of someone you do not know at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who carries the result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you are wise, your wisdom rewards you. If you mock, you carry the weight alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unusually stark note to end on. Wisdom is described as paying its owner — and the mocker is not threatened with punishment so much as told he will bear it by himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both outcomes land on the person who chose them. Nobody else absorbs either."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it sits in the chapter.** Proverbs 9 stages two dinner invitations — Wisdom has built her house and set her table, and Folly sits at her door calling to passers-by. Both call the same people. This verse is at the hinge, and the chapter is asking which house you eat in."}]}, "Psalms 1:1–3": {"title": "Three verbs going downhill, and a tree that does not", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm that opens the whole book is built on a progression most readers walk straight past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Walk, stand, sit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Won't walk in step with the wicked… won't stop and stand where sinners loiter… won't settle into the seat of the scornful.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three postures, and they descend: moving alongside, then stopping, then sitting down. The company gets worse as the posture gets more settled — wicked, then sinners, then scoffers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a description of how a thing that starts as company ends as identity, and it is arranged so you can see the slope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for meditating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turning it over softly, day and night.* The Hebrew is **hagah** — the same word in Joshua 1:8, and it is audible: a lion growling, a dove moaning, a person muttering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set against the three verbs above, it is what fills the space they left. The happy person is not defined by avoidance; something else has their attention."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rooted beside flowing streams… offers its fruit right on time, whose leaves never wither.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two details are easy to miss. The tree is **planted** — the Hebrew is passive, transplanted by someone. And the fruit comes *in its season*, which is a real qualification: not constantly, not on demand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"Whatever that one does comes into bloom.\"** The line has been used to promise success in any undertaking, which the psalm does not support — it is a tree image, and trees do not prosper by trying. What the psalm claims is that a life rooted in the right place bears what it is meant to bear, on time, and does not dry out when the heat comes. Jeremiah 17 uses the same picture and spells that out: it does not fear when heat comes, and in drought it does not stop bearing."}]}, "Psalms 103:1–2": {"title": "\"O my soul\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David opens by talking to himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bless the LORD, **O my soul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not God, and not the congregation. He turns to his own inner life and gives it an instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does this four times — twice at the start and twice at the end, closing the whole thing in the same words it opened with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Barak** — to bless, and the root sense is to kneel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To bless God is not to confer something on Him. It is a posture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Everything"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **all that is within me**, bless His holy name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *all my inward parts*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing exempted. He is calling the whole interior to do one thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bless **His holy name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not His gifts. The psalm gets to those in the next verse and spends the rest of itself on them, but it starts at the name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bless the LORD, O my soul, and **do not forget a single one of His kindnesses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *forget not all His benefits* — **gemul**, a dealing, a recompense, what someone has done for you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it needs saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forgetting is the Old Testament's standing charge against Israel. Deuteronomy warns of it repeatedly: *beware, lest you forget the LORD who brought you out of the land of Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 106 states it flatly: *they soon forgot His works.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David does not assume he is immune. He instructs his own soul against it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It lists them, so the forgetting has something to work against:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **forgives** all your iniquities, who **heals** all your diseases, who **redeems** your life from destruction, who **crowns** you with lovingkindness and tender mercies, who **satisfies** your mouth with good things.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five participles in a row, all present tense, all God's. See the note on Psalms 103:3–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As far as the east is from the west, so far has He removed our transgressions from us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As a father pities his children, so the LORD pities those who fear Him. For **He knows our frame; He remembers that we are dust**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm that begins by telling the soul not to forget says that God remembers what we are made of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bless the LORD, all you His angels… Bless the LORD, all you His hosts… Bless the LORD, all His works, in all places of His dominion. **Bless the LORD, O my soul!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It widens out to the angels, the hosts and the whole creation, and then comes back to the one soul it started with."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who gives the order.** He does, to himself. The psalm's first move is a man deciding what his own inside is going to do."}]}, "Psalms 103:3–5": {"title": "\"Your youth is renewed like the eagle's\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five things listed in three verses, and David tells himself not to forget any of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *Bless the LORD, O my soul, and **forget not all His benefits**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is talking to himself, and the list that follows is what he is refusing to forget."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*He **forgives** all your wrongdoing*", "*He **heals** all your sicknesses*", "*He **redeems** your life from the pit*", "*He **crowns** you with steadfast love and mercy*", "*He **fills** your life with good things*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "**All**, twice. All your wrongdoing and all your sicknesses, and the two sit in consecutive lines as a matched pair — the same *all* over both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forgiveness and healing together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew poetry pairs lines deliberately. These two are not a list of unrelated items; they are set side by side because they belong together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pairing turns up when the paralysed man is let down through the roof: *your sins are forgiven* first, and then *rise, take up your bed.* And in Isaiah 53, *wounded for our transgressions* and *by His stripes we are healed* are two clauses of one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Redeems your life from the pit\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ga'al** — the kinsman-redeemer's word, the relative whose duty was to buy back what a family member had lost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Crowns you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is to encircle, to surround. **Steadfast love** is **chesed**, the covenant word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Crowned with it — worn on the head, visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The renewal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **fills your life with good things**, so **your youth is renewed like the eagle's**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *fills* is to satisfy, and the older rendering is *satisfies your mouth with good things*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Youth** is **na'ar** — the word for a young man, a lad. The season of natural strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb *renewed* is **chadash**, to make new, to restore. It is the same root as the new moon, and the new covenant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eagle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah uses the same bird for the same idea: *those who wait on the LORD shall renew their strength; they shall **mount up with wings like eagles**.* See the note on Isaiah 40:29–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An eagle is long-lived and, in the ancient picture, moults and comes back with new feathers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says a few lines on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As far as the east is from the west, so far has He removed our transgressions from us. As a father pities his children, so the LORD pities those who fear Him. For He knows our frame; **He remembers that we are dust**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same psalm that promises renewed youth also says He has not forgotten what we are made of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David says about the source.** *Who satisfies…* — every verb in the list has the same subject, and it is never the reader. Five things, one actor."}]}, "Psalms 105:12–15": {"title": "\"Do not touch My anointed ones\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm looks back at the patriarchs and describes how small and exposed they were."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they were still **only a handful, few, and strangers** in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for it: a handful, few, and foreigners."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham, Isaac and Jacob were nomads with tents and livestock, owning no land except a burial cave, in a country full of established kingdoms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wandering **from nation to nation, from one kingdom to another people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Constant movement, through territory belonging to other people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of those crossings was an occasion to be robbed, absorbed or killed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What did not happen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **let no one do them harm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that He did not permit any man to oppress them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How He did it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For their sake **He warned kings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm says the protection worked by God speaking to the men who could have harmed them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records two occasions. Abimelech, warned in a dream about Sarah — *you are a dead man because of the woman you have taken.* And Laban, overtaking Jacob: *God had come to Laban the Syrian in a dream by night and said, \"Be careful that you speak to Jacob neither good nor bad.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times the protection is a conversation the patriarch never hears about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not touch My anointed ones. Do My prophets no harm.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two titles for men who held neither office in any formal sense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Anointed ones** — *meshichai*, the plural of the word behind Messiah. Nobody had poured oil on Abraham."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Prophets** — and Genesis does use it once, of Abraham, in God's own words to Abimelech: *he is a prophet, and he will pray for you and you shall live.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were being protected for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's next verses go straight into Joseph, sold as a slave, *his feet hurt with fetters, he was laid in irons* — *until the time that his word came to pass.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The protection did not mean an easy passage. Joseph is in the same psalm, in chains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and forty-five verses of history in Psalms 105 and 106, and this one is the positive telling — a catalogue of what God did, verb after verb, with almost nothing about what Israel did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Its purpose is stated at the top: *remember His marvellous works which He has done.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it ends up.** *He brought His people out with silver and gold, and not one of their tribes stumbled.* The handful of wandering strangers become a nation walking out of Egypt. See the note on Psalms 105:37–38."}]}, "Psalms 105:37–38": {"title": "\"Not one of their tribes stumbled\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is a retelling of Israel's history, and this is its summary of the exodus in two lines."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it records"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He brought His people out with silver and gold, and not one of their tribes stumbled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims. They left with wealth — the payment described in Exodus 11 and 12 — and the whole company got out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *stumbled* means to totter or be feeble. The psalmist is describing a mass departure of perhaps a very large number of people, including the old and the very young, in which nobody fell behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the whole of Psalm 105 and the pattern is unmistakable: it is a catalogue of things God did, verb after verb, with almost nothing about what Israel did. He sent, He turned, He spoke, He brought out, He spread a cloud, He opened the rock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's purpose is stated at the start — remember, give thanks, tell what He has done. This verse is one line in a very long list of that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had done the night before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is not describing a crowd that happened to be in good health. It is describing the morning after Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every household in that nation had, the previous evening, eaten a lamb — *roasted with fire… his head with his legs and with the appurtenance thereof*, the whole animal, and the instruction was to eat it, not merely to kill it. The blood went on the doorframe and the lamb went inside them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ate the lamb, and they came out the next morning with nobody feeble among them. The psalm puts the two facts side by side because they belong side by side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "However many they were, the number included the elderly, the newborn, and everyone in between — a whole population that had been in hard labour under Egyptian taskmasters for generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them stumbled. Not one lagged behind. That is what is being recorded, and the psalm records it as a thing God did."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The lamb and the table.** Paul calls Him *Christ our Passover*, sacrificed for us, and the table He left His disciples is the table of that lamb. The bread is His body, broken. What the night of the exodus produced in Israel is what the body was given for. See the notes on 1 Corinthians 11:23–26 and Matthew 8:14–17."}]}, "Psalms 107:1–3": {"title": "\"Let the rescued ones say it out loud\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm opens by telling a particular group of people to speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give thanks to the LORD, **for He is good. His steadfast love never runs out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chesed** — the covenant word, loyal love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never runs out* is the Hebrew *endures forever*, the refrain of Psalm 136, said twenty-six times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is instructed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **the rescued ones** say it out loud, **the ones He bought back**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the redeemed of the LORD* — **ga'al**, the kinsman-redeemer's word, the relative whose duty was to buy back what a family member had lost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is not addressed to everybody. It is for people who have been got out of something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Say it out loud\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is simply *let them say so*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's entire method rests on this line: what follows is four accounts of rescue, and the reader is told at the top to be one of the voices."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gathered from the countries, **from sunrise and sunset, from the far north and the sea in the south**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four directions, and the phrasing is not the usual compass list — east and west by the sun, and then north and the sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are being brought back from everywhere they were scattered to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four stories, each with the same structure:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the wanderers** — lost in a desert, hungry and thirsty, no city to live in", "**the prisoners** — in darkness and irons, because they rebelled", "**the sick** — fools, afflicted because of their transgressions, near the gates of death", "**the sailors** — in a storm, with every skill swallowed up. See the note on Psalms 107:25–27."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And each one gets the same two refrains: *then they cried out to the LORD in their trouble, and He delivered them*, and *oh, that men would give thanks to the LORD for His goodness.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the four have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the four are explicitly their own fault — the prisoners *rebelled against the words of God*, and the sick are *fools, because of their transgression*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not sort them. All four cry, and all four are brought out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is placed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 107 opens Book Five of the Psalter, straight after the end of Book Four, and it reads as a homecoming song for people back from exile."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *Whoever is wise will observe these things, and **they will understand the lovingkindness of the LORD**.* The psalm says the way to understand **chesed** is to look at four rescues and notice what they have in common."}]}, "Psalms 107:19–20": {"title": "\"He sent out His word and healed them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third of four rescues in the psalm, and all four have the same shape."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm's pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 107 tells four stories: travellers lost in a desert, prisoners in iron chains, sick men who could not eat, and sailors in a storm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one repeats the same two lines:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they **cried to the LORD in their trouble**, and He **saved them out of their distress**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *Oh, that men would give thanks to the LORD for His goodness, and for His wonderful works to the children of men!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four groups, four crises, one response, one deliverance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third group"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before describe them: *fools, because of their transgression, and because of their iniquities, were afflicted. **Their soul abhorred all manner of food, and they drew near to the gates of death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not soften how they got there. It says their own way brought it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they cried to the LORD **in their trouble**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not after sorting themselves out. In it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the ordinary verb for shouting for help."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was sent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **sent out His word** and healed them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **davar** — word, matter, thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a doctor, a remedy, or an instruction. A word, sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm uses the same construction elsewhere for creation: *by the word of the LORD the heavens were made.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lifting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **lifted them clear of the pit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *delivered them from their destructions* — **shechitah**, the pit, the grave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had drawn near the gates of death in verse 18, and this is the reverse motion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Healed, and lifted out. The psalm gives both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sending goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He sent His word to Jacob**\"*, and John's opening: *In the beginning was the Word… **and the Word became flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's phrase is what John's Gospel makes a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the centurion said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Only **speak a word**, and my servant will be healed.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus called it faith greater than any He had found in Israel — and the man's argument was that he understood how orders work at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chorus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After each rescue: *Oh, that men would **give thanks** to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after this one specifically: *let them **sacrifice the sacrifices of thanksgiving**, and declare His works with rejoicing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, give thanks to the LORD, for He is good! For His mercy endures forever. **Let the redeemed of the LORD say so**, whom He has redeemed from the hand of the enemy.* See the note on Psalms 107:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever is wise will **observe these things**, and they will understand the lovingkindness of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing all four groups do.** Cry out. The psalm does not distinguish between the ones who were lost, imprisoned, sick by their own fault, or caught in weather — it gives them the same sentence, four times."}]}, "Psalms 107:25–27": {"title": "\"Every skill they had was swallowed up\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth of four rescue stories in the psalm, and this one is at sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who sent it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He spoke, and **a storm wind rose up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm attributes the storm to a word. It does not describe weather that happened to arrive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The motion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Up to the sky they went, then **down into the depths**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that they go up to the heavens and down to the deeps."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What melts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their **courage melting** in the danger.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *their soul melted because of trouble* — the same verb used of hearts melting before an army."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sailors"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **reeled and staggered like drunk men**, and **every skill they had was swallowed up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last clause is the point of the passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "These are professionals — verse 23 says *they that go down to the sea in ships, that do business in great waters.* Seamanship is what they have."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm says their competence was swallowed. Not overwhelmed by fear; used up, and insufficient."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they **cried out to the LORD in their trouble**, and He brought them out of their distresses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the refrain, and it appears four times in this psalm — after the wanderers in the desert, the prisoners in irons, the fools who were sick because of their transgressions, and now the sailors."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four different kinds of trouble, one sentence in the middle of each."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **calms the storm, so that its waves are still**. Then they are glad because they are quiet; **so He guides them to their desired haven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before this in the same psalm is the one the disciples had every reason to recall on a lake at night. See the notes on Mark 6:45–52 and John 6:16–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately the boat was at the shore they had been heading for.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain that follows each rescue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, that men would give thanks to the LORD for His goodness, and for **His wonderful works to the children of men**!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Also four times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give thanks to the LORD, for He is good… **let the rescued ones say it out loud**, the ones He bought back.* See the note on Psalms 107:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole psalm is an instruction to people who have been got out of something: say so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever is wise will observe these things, and they will understand the lovingkindness of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The four groups.** Lost in a desert, sitting in darkness and irons, sick and near the gates of death, and going down in a storm. The psalm gives each of them the same three lines: they cried, He brought them out, let them give thanks."}]}, "Psalms 110:1": {"title": "\"Sit here at My right hand\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is quoted or alluded to more often in the New Testament than any other line in the Old."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two Lords"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **LORD** says to **my Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has two different words. The first is **YHWH**, the covenant name. The second is **Adoni** — my lord, my master."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote it, and the second Lord is somebody David calls his superior."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question Jesus asked about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**David himself calls Him Lord — so how can He be David's son?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He put it to the scribes in the temple during passion week, and nobody answered. See the note on Mark 12:35–37."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Sit here at My right hand**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The right hand of a throne is the seat of the co-regent — the place of delegated authority."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is asked for. He is told to sit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What sitting means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews builds a chapter on the posture: every priest *stands ministering daily, offering repeatedly the same sacrifices*, and this one *sat down*. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You sit when the work is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Until\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Until** I make your enemies a footstool under your feet.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seating and the subduing overlap. Both are happening in the same window, and the one seated is not the one doing the subduing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes the same point: *He must reign **until** He has put all enemies under His feet.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:23–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The footstool"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A footstool is what a seated man rests on. The picture is not a battle in progress; it is furniture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua's commanders were told to put their feet on the necks of five kings, and told *this is what the LORD will do to every enemy you fight.* See the note on Joshua 10:22–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the making"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I** make your enemies a footstool.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject is the first Lord. The one sitting is not described as fighting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the New Testament uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, at Pentecost, quotes it to close his sermon: *\"For David did not ascend into the heavens, but he says himself: 'The LORD said to my Lord…'\"* See the note on Acts 2:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 1 uses it as the closing argument about angels: *to which of the angels has He ever said, \"Sit at My right hand\"?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen sees it: *\"I see the heavens opened and the Son of Man **standing** at the right hand of God\"* — and the change of posture in that one sentence has been noticed ever since."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn and will not relent: **You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king on David's throne who is also a priest of a line older than Aaron's. Hebrews takes two chapters over it. See the notes on Genesis 14:17–20 and Hebrews 7:20–22."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is unusual about the verse.** It is a report of a conversation David overheard — one Lord speaking to another — and the whole New Testament reads it as the seating after the cross."}]}, "Psalms 110:4": {"title": "\"The LORD has sworn\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and the whole of Hebrews 7 comes out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **has sworn**, and **He will not take it back**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the LORD has sworn and will not repent*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An oath from God is unusual — He has nothing higher to swear by, so He swears on Himself. The only other place in the Old Testament He does it about a person is Genesis 22, after the knife. See the note on Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews makes the point twice: the Levitical priests were made priests *with no oath sworn over them*, and this one was. See the note on Hebrews 7:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are** a priest forever.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Second person. The psalm's first verse has David overhearing one Lord speak to another — *the LORD said to my Lord, sit at My right hand*. See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the same conversation, three verses on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two offices"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king on David's throne, seated at the right hand — and a priest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the law those could not be the same person. Kings came from Judah and priests from Levi, and the wall between them was absolute. Uzziah went into the temple to burn incense and came out a leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm puts both on one man, and David wrote it about a descendant of his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Forever\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **le'olam**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levitical priests served from thirty to fifty and died. Hebrews says it plainly: *there have been many priests, because they were prevented by death from continuing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In the order of **Melchizedek**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *after the manner of*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Melchizedek appears in four verses of Genesis, brings out bread and wine, blesses Abram, receives a tenth, and is never mentioned again in the Old Testament until this line. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years before Aaron, and a thousand before David."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Genesis is silent about him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No father, no mother, no genealogy, no birth, no death. Hebrews notices all four omissions and treats the silence as deliberate: *made like the Son of God, remaining a priest continually.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a book that gives genealogies for everybody, the absence is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is used for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the most-quoted Old Testament verse in the New Testament, and Hebrews quotes this line five times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If a different order of priesthood was promised under oath, after the Levitical one was running, then the Levitical one was not final: *if perfection were through the Levitical priesthood… what further need was there that another priest should rise according to the order of Melchizedek?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a change of priesthood requires a change of law: *for the priesthood being changed, of necessity there is also a change of the law.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument of Hebrews 7 to 10 is built on one verse in a psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord is at Your right hand; He shall execute kings in the day of His wrath.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it ends: *He shall drink of the brook by the wayside; therefore He shall lift up the head.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is sworn.** Not a promise about what He will do. A statement about what He is — and Hebrews says the oath exists because the office was never going to be reviewed."}]}, "Psalms 112:1–3": {"title": "\"Their goodness stands forever\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A psalm about a person, and it is deliberately built to echo the one before it, which is about God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 111 describes the LORD: His work is honour and majesty, His righteousness endures forever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 112 describes the person who fears Him — and uses the same phrases. *Their goodness stands forever* is word for word what the previous psalm said about God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two are twin acrostics in Hebrew, each running through the alphabet. Read together, the second is a portrait of someone taking after the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it begins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Happy is the one who holds the LORD in awe, who delights, deep down, in what He asks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *obeys* first. **Delights** — the Hebrew is about taking pleasure in something, wanting it. The commands are described as a pleasure before they are described as a duty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their children will be strong across the land… Their house holds plenty and wealth, and their goodness stands forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three generations of effect: the person, the children, and something that outlasts both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last clause is the one that does not fit the pattern of the other two. Plenty and wealth are in the house; the righteousness stands *forever* — it is the only item on the list that time cannot reach."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read on for the harder verses.** The psalm goes on: *even in darkness light dawns for the upright*, and *they will have no fear of bad news; their heart is steady, trusting in the LORD.* It does not describe a life without darkness or bad news. It describes someone who has light in the first and a steady heart in the second."}]}, "Psalms 116:12–14": {"title": "The cup of salvation", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalmist asks what he can give back, and the answer he arrives at is that he will take something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What can I **give back** to the LORD for all His goodness to me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what shall I return to the LORD for all His benefits toward me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the instinct of anybody who has been given something large. You start looking for what to send in the other direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **lift the cup of salvation** and call on the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks what to give and answers by taking a cup."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing he can do about the goodness is receive more of it, publicly, and say whose it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the Passover meal, cups were lifted and blessed at set points, and this psalm is part of the Hallel — Psalms 113 to 118, sung at Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus sang this psalm in the upper room, on the night He took a cup and said *this is My blood of the new covenant.* See the note on Matthew 26:26–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kos yeshuot** — the cup of salvations, plural."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **keep my promises to the LORD in front of all His people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Public. Twice in the psalm he says it that way — *in the presence of all His people*, and again in the verses following, *in the courts of the LORD's house, in the midst of you, O Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm has come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The opening: *I love the LORD, because He has heard my voice and my supplications… The pains of death surrounded me, and the pangs of Sheol laid hold of me; I found trouble and sorrow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle: *I believed, even when I said, \"I am badly shaken.\"* — the line Paul quotes as *the spirit of faith*. See the note on Psalms 116:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man asking what he can give back is a man who has just been rescued from something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse in the middle of the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of His saints.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he calls himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O LORD, truly I am Your servant; I am Your servant, the son of Your maidservant. **You have loosed my bonds.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *servant* three times in one verse, and then names what was done to his chains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the language of Exodus 21 — a man freed, who stays. See the note on Exodus 21:2–6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm ends.** *I will offer to You the sacrifice of thanksgiving.* The peace offering that had to be eaten fresh, the same day, with none left until morning. See the note on Leviticus 7:11–18."}]}, "Psalms 116:8–11": {"title": "\"I believed, even when I said, I am badly shaken\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The verse Paul builds *the spirit of faith* on, and it is not spoken from a comfortable place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three rescues"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You rescued **my life from death**, **my eyes from tears**, **my feet from stumbling**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, and they cover three different things: the body, the emotions, the walk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the middle one is that He delivered his eye from tears. Not that the tears were forbidden — that they were stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **walk before the LORD in the land of the living**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the temple, not in retreat. In the land of the living — ordinary daylight, among people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line Paul quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I believed, even when I said, \"**I am badly shaken**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are in the sentence at once. He believed **and** he said he was in pieces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I was greatly afflicted*. Nothing is being minimised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes this exact line into 2 Corinthians — *we have the same spirit of faith as the one who wrote, \"I believed, and so I spoke\"* — while listing beatings, shipwrecks and being hard pressed on every side. See the note on 2 Corinthians 4:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So whatever the spirit of faith is, both men exercised it while stating plainly how bad things were."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In my alarm I said, \"**Every person is a liar.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in my haste* — said hurriedly, in panic. He is quoting himself at his worst and flagging that it was hasty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm keeps his panic on the record rather than editing it out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the psalm from the top and it opens with *the pains of death surrounded me, and the pangs of Sheol laid hold of me; I found trouble and sorrow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *I love the LORD, because He has heard my voice.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Further down the same psalm: *what shall I render to the LORD for all His benefits toward me? **I will take up the cup of salvation** and call upon the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 116 is one of the Hallel psalms, sung at Passover. The cup of salvation is in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus sang this psalm with the disciples in the upper room, on the night He took a cup and said *this is My blood of the new covenant.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse in the middle of it.** *Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of His saints.* Written by a man who has just said God rescued his life from death — and who still calls the other outcome precious."}]}, "Psalms 119:105–112": {"title": "\"A lamp for the ground beneath my feet\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best-known verse of the longest psalm, and the two halves describe two different distances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Lamp and light"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your word is a **lamp for the ground beneath my feet**, a **light laid down along the path** I walk.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An oil lamp of that period was small — a clay dish you carried in one hand or tied at the ankle. It threw enough light for the next step and no further."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second image widens it: light along the path. Near and far, in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is floodlighting. You get the next step, and enough sense of the road to keep going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not use the lamp verse as a comfortable saying. Read the eight lines it belongs to:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*I am worn thin, brought low almost to nothing*", "*my life is always cupped here in my hands* — an idiom for constant danger", "*the wicked set their snare along my way*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp is being carried by a man in trouble, at night, with traps on the road. That is the setting the verse belongs to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I've sworn an oath, and I intend to keep it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He binds himself twice in the passage — the oath here, and at the end *I've bent my heart to living out Your statutes, forever, faithful, all the way to the end.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *bent my heart* is about inclining or stretching something toward a direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inheritance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your steady words I've taken as my heirloom, my heart's own gladness, treasure without end.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Heirloom** is the word for an inherited portion — the family land that passed down. A man with nothing else says the words are the estate he was left."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The whole psalm.** 176 verses, eight per Hebrew letter, and almost every one mentions God's word under one name or another — law, precepts, statutes, promises, judgments. It is the longest sustained meditation on Scripture inside Scripture, and it was written by somebody being hunted."}]}, "Psalms 119:129–136": {"title": "\"The opening of Your words lets in the light\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The **Pe** stanza — eight lines, every one beginning with the same Hebrew letter, and the middle of it says what happens when the words are opened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unfolding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **opening** of Your words lets in the light; it gives plain folk the sense they never had.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a noun built from the verb *to open* — an entrance, an unfolding, a doorway. The word itself carries the picture: something shut that is opened, and light comes through the gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It gives **plain folk** the sense they never had.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **pethi** is the simple one — not the fool, and not the scoffer, but the untaught, the person with no advantage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the one specified as the beneficiary. Not the learned. The opening of the words is what levels the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The panting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I open wide my mouth and pant for breath, so hungry, longing for Your good commands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is an undignified image and the psalmist chose it. The Hebrew is the gasping of someone who has been running, or of an animal in heat and thirst."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The requests in between"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*steady my footsteps by the word You gave*", "*let no wrong thing hold mastery over me*", "*rescue me from the men who crush and cheat*", "*let Your bright face shine warm upon Your servant*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Let Your face shine** is the Aaronic blessing, quoted back to God by a man asking for what was already promised over him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the stanza ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rivers of tears go running from my eyes, because the people will not keep Your teaching.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *channels of water* — the runnels that carry a stream. Not for himself. For everybody else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The stanza's shape.** It opens with the words being wonderful and closes with weeping over people who ignore them. The distance between those two lines is the whole burden of the psalm."}]}, "Psalms 119:161–168": {"title": "\"Like one who finds a heap of hidden spoil\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The **Shin** stanza, near the end of the psalm, and the man is still being hunted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rulers have **hunted me for no good reason**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *princes have persecuted me without cause*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm never explains who they are or what the accusation was. It refers to them repeatedly — *the proud have forged a lie against me*, *the wicked have laid a snare for me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **my heart stands in awe before Your words**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my heart stands in awe of Your word*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Princes on one side and a sentence on the other, and the awe goes to the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I take a joy in all the promise You made **like one who finds a heap of hidden spoil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *as one who finds great spoil*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plunder after a battle — the thing a soldier hopes for and does not expect. Judah spent three days collecting it after Jehoshaphat's battle. See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is to a windfall rather than to a wage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **hate the lie, I cannot bear the falsehood**, but oh, **the teaching You have given, I love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does this constantly — a hatred and a love in one verse, and the hatred is always of falsehood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seven times a day **I stop to praise You** for all the fair decisions of Your justice.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven — completeness rather than a schedule, though later Jewish and Christian practice took it as one and built the daily offices on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famous line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great peace belongs to those who **love Your teaching**; and **there is nothing left to make them stumble**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *nothing shall offend them* — **mikshol**, a stumbling block."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek Old Testament renders it **skandalon**, the trigger of a trap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man saying it is being hunted by rulers. The peace is not an absence of pressure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The waiting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*LORD, **I have waited, hoping for Your rescue**, and I have lived out all Your good commands.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My soul has kept the steady words You gave, and oh, **I love them, love them past all telling**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and I love them exceedingly*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I've kept Your precepts and the words You keep, for **all my ways lie open, LORD, before You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for all my ways are before You*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the reason given for the keeping, and it is not fear. He is describing a life lived in sight of somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two stanzas later, at the very end:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have gone astray like a lost sheep; **seek Your servant**, for I do not forget Your commandments.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The longest chapter in the Bible, one hundred and seventy-six verses about loving God's word, ends with a man saying he is lost and asking to be looked for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The comparison the stanza turns on.** Not a wage and not a duty. A heap of spoil — what a man finds after a battle he expected to lose."}]}, "Psalms 119:25–32": {"title": "\"My soul lies flat in the dust\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The **Daleth** stanza, and it is the lowest point in the psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My soul **lies flat and clinging to the dust**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **davqah le'aphar naphshi** — my soul clings to the dust."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dust** is where the dead go, and where the serpent was told to travel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever is happening to this man, he begins the stanza face down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Revive me, **breathe me back, the way You promised**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chayyeni** — make me live."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb runs through the whole psalm — it appears nine times, always as a request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The way You promised** — the request is grounded in something already said. He is not asking for a new arrangement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The account"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **told You all my roads, and You answered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I declared my ways and You heard me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The teaching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **teach me, walk me through the ways You keep**. Help me to **understand the path You've marked**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three requests for instruction in two verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The melting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My soul is **melting down from sheer heaviness**; **lift me and hold me steady**, as You said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my soul melts from grief*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**As You said** — the second appeal to a promise in eight verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two roads"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep **the deceiving road far off from me**… I've **chosen out the faithful, truthful way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the way of falsehood* against *the way of faithfulness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grip"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **hold on tight to all Your steady words**; O LORD, **don't let me stand here put to shame**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have clung to Your testimonies* — the same verb as verse 25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His soul clings to the dust, and he clings to the word. The stanza is built on one verb used twice in opposite directions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I'll **run the road of Your commands wide open**, for **You have set my cramped heart free to move**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for You shall enlarge my heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stanza opens with a man in the dust and closes with him running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in between explains the change. There is no rescue narrated, no circumstance altered, and no answer described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What happens between verse 25 and verse 32 is eight requests."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for enlarge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Rachab** — to widen, to make room. It is the root behind **Rehoboth**, the well Isaac named when he finally dug one nobody fought him for: *now the LORD has made room for us*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same movement appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I waited patiently for the LORD; and He inclined to me, and heard my cry. He also brought me up out of a horrible pit, out of the miry clay, **and set my feet upon a rock**, and established my steps.* See the note on Psalms 40:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dust and clay at the start, and a firm footing at the end."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What changes across the stanza.** Not the situation. He is clinging to dust in the first line and running in the last, and the only thing that happens in between is that he asks — twice on the ground of something already promised."}]}, "Psalms 119:97–104": {"title": "\"All day long I turn it over\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The **Mem** stanza — eight verses, every one beginning with the same Hebrew letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 119 is the longest chapter in the Bible: twenty-two stanzas of eight verses, one for each letter of the Hebrew alphabet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every verse in a stanza begins with that letter, and almost every verse in the psalm names God's word by one of eight synonyms — law, testimonies, precepts, statutes, commandments, judgments, word, promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a work of enormous craft, and this stanza is usually reckoned the finest of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, **how I love the teaching that You gave!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *how I love Your law*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm says this repeatedly, and it is startling every time. Nobody expects affection for a legal code."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The practice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All day long **I turn it over and over**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sichah** — musing, meditation, going over something in the mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The related verb in Joshua 1:8 is **hagah** — to mutter, to murmur under the breath. See the note on Joshua 1:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ancient reading was aloud. Meditation in the Old Testament is a sound."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three comparisons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your good commands have made me **wiser than my enemies**, for they are always mine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I understand **far more than all my teachers**, because Your steady words are my long study.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I see with **clearer eyes than aged men**, because I've kept the precepts You have given.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three groups, each with more claim to know than the last: opponents, teachers, and the old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reasons given are different each time — possession, study, and keeping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restraint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I've **held my feet back from each evil road**, so I might **keep the word** that You have spoken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is deliberate. He stays off the road in order to keep the word, not the reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The teacher"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have not turned aside from Your fair judgments, for **You Yourself have been the one who taught me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three comparisons are all undercut by this line. He outstrips his teachers because of who taught him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The taste"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How **sweet Your words taste when I let them linger**, sweeter than honey dripping in my mouth!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *how sweet to my palate*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The manna tasted *like wafers made with honey*. See the note on Exodus 16:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Ezekiel is given a scroll to eat: *it was in my mouth like honey in sweetness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of Your precepts **I have gathered wisdom**; so now **I hate each false and crooked road**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The love and the hatred are in the same stanza and joined by a *so*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who wrote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unknown. The psalm gives no author and no occasion, and the voice in it is of somebody under pressure — princes persecuting him, the proud digging pits, and *my soul melts from heaviness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse everyone knows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From the **Nun** stanza: *Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And from **Beth**: *Your word I have hidden in my heart, that I might not sin against You.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The claim in the middle.** More understanding than his teachers and clearer sight than the elders — and the reason given at the end of the stanza is that God taught him Himself."}]}, "Psalms 121:7–8": {"title": "Your going out and your coming in", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of a short psalm, and one Hebrew verb is repeated through the whole of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shamar** — to keep, to guard, to watch over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It appears six times in eight verses, and every occurrence has God as the subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used of Adam in the garden — *to tend and **keep** it* — and of the priestly blessing: *the LORD bless you and **keep** you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm's shape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It opens as a question: *I will lift up my eyes to the hills — **from whence comes my help**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And answers itself: *my help comes from the LORD, who made heaven and earth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before these verses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will not allow your foot to be moved; **He who keeps you will not slumber**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, **He who keeps Israel shall neither slumber nor sleep**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is your keeper; the LORD is your shade at your right hand. The sun shall not strike you by day, nor the moon by night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sun and moon — day and night, the whole of a day covered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD will keep you **from all harm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *from all evil* — **kol ra**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will keep **your very life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nephesh** — soul, life, the person themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm moves from the foot in verse 3 to the life in verse 7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two directions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD will keep **your going out and your coming in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Hebrew merism — naming the two ends to mean everything between."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the standard phrase for a whole life's activity. Moses uses it of himself at the end: *I can no longer go out and come in*. And of Joshua: *he shall go out before them and come in before them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **this moment** and **forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *from now and unto everlasting*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the Songs of Ascents — Psalms 120 to 134, sung by pilgrims walking up to Jerusalem for the feasts. See the note on Psalms 128:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hills in the opening line are the ones on the road. Bandits worked them, and the shrines on the high places were on them too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question *from whence comes my help* is asked by somebody looking at a hill country they have to cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That nothing happens. The psalm is about a keeper rather than about a smooth road, and it is sung by people on a journey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one who does not sleep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice: *will not slumber*, and *shall neither slumber nor sleep*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah's taunt on Carmel — *perhaps he is asleep and must be awakened* — was about a different god. See the note on 1 Kings 18:25–29."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb the psalm is built on.** *Keep*, six times in eight verses, and God is the subject every time — over the foot, the head, the life, the going out and the coming in, now and forever."}]}, "Psalms 127:1–2": {"title": "\"He gives sleep to His beloved\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short psalm attributed to Solomon — the man who built the actual house and watched over the actual city — and it begins by saying so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two examples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Unless the LORD builds the house, its builders labour in vain. Unless the LORD watches the city, the watchman keeps awake for nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Building and guarding: making something, and keeping it. Between them they cover most of what anyone spends a life doing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not say the builders are idle or the watchmen asleep. It says their work is *in vain* without something else — the labour is real and the result is not in their hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The bread of anxious toil\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is no use rising early, sitting up late, eating the bread of anxious toil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is literally the bread of *pains* or *sorrows* — the same root used of the toil laid on the ground in Genesis 3. It is a picture of work eaten as misery: early starts, late nights, and food taken standing up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For to His beloved He gives sleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew here is compressed and can be read two ways: *He gives sleep to His beloved*, or *He gives to His beloved while they sleep*. Translators have split on it for centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, sleep is the image, and it is offered as the alternative to the anxious bread. The psalm's argument is not that work is pointless — it is that the outcome does not depend on staying awake."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next.** The rest of the psalm is about children being a heritage from the LORD. The two halves belong together: a house is built and a city is kept, and what actually endures out of a life is described as something received rather than achieved."}]}, "Psalms 128:1–2": {"title": "\"You will eat the fruit of your labour\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the Songs of Ascents — the fifteen psalms pilgrims sang walking up to Jerusalem — and it is about a house rather than a temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is happy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Happy is **everyone** who fears the LORD, who walks in His ways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ashrei** — the word Psalm 1 opens with, and the word behind *blessed are* in the Beatitudes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Everyone.** Not the priest, the Levite, or the notable. The psalm is deliberately ordinary from the first line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eating what you grew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will **eat the fruit of your labour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds unremarkable until you set it beside the curse in Deuteronomy 28: *you shall build a house and not dwell in it; you shall plant a vineyard and not eat of it… your ox shall be slaughtered before your eyes and you shall not eat of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of the curse — working and not getting to keep it. The blessing here is the plain reversal: you eat what your own hands produced."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says the same thing about the world put right: *they shall not build and another inhabit; they shall not plant and another eat.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be **happy**, and **it will go well with you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles it — good for you, and well with you. Inside and outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rest of the psalm shows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your wife shall be like a fruitful vine in the very heart of your house, your children like olive plants all around your table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A vine in the middle of the house and young olive trees round a table. Both are slow-growing plants that take years before they yield, and both go on yielding for generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is a meal. That is the whole vision of blessing in this psalm — a man eating what he grew, at a table, with his family around it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the blessing comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **bless you out of Zion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not out of the field. The blessing on the house is sourced at the sanctuary, and the psalm is sung by people walking toward it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And how far it reaches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May you see your children's children. Peace be upon Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grandchildren, and then the nation. The psalm starts with one man's fear of God and ends with peace on a whole people."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it sits.** Psalm 127 is the one before it — *unless the LORD builds the house, they labour in vain who build it… He gives His beloved sleep.* The two are a pair: one says the building is His, and then this one describes the house."}]}, "Psalms 128:3–4": {"title": "A vine and young olive shoots", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two images from a smallholding, applied to a household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the Songs of Ascents — Psalms 120 to 134, sung by pilgrims going up to Jerusalem for the feasts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Short psalms, meant to be walked to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is every one who fears the LORD, who walks in His ways. **When you eat the labour of your hands, you shall be happy, and it shall be well with you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eating what your own hands produced was not guaranteed in that world. Deuteronomy's curses include *you shall plant a vineyard and not gather its grapes* and *another shall eat it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your wife **within your house** will be like **a fruitful vine**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in the innermost parts of your house* — the private quarters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A vine is not a decorative plant. It takes four years before it bears, it needs pruning and staking, and it produces for generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel is called a vine repeatedly, and Jesus calls Himself the true one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The olive shoots"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your children **around your table** will be like **young olive shoots**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *olive plants* — the suckers that come up around the base of a mature olive tree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An olive takes fifteen years to bear properly and can live for centuries. The shoots at the base are the next tree, growing from the same root, already there while the old one is still producing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two locations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Within your house* and *around your table*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are interior. The psalm's picture of blessing is a room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, **this is how the man is blessed who fears the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *behold, thus shall the man be blessed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm opened with the same clause and closes the first half by repeating it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD bless you **out of Zion**, and may you **see the good of Jerusalem all the days of your life**. Yes, may you **see your children's children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peace be upon Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm widens from a table to a city to a nation in three lines, and the last word is **shalom**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 127 is directly before it — *unless the LORD builds the house, they labour in vain who build it*, and *children are a heritage from the LORD*. See the note on Psalms 127:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two are usually read together: one says the building is God's, and this one describes what is in the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything spectacular. A wife, children, a table, food you grew yourself, and grandchildren before you die."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Songs of Ascents were sung by people walking to a feast, and this is the one about what they had left at home."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two plants have in common.** Both take years, both come from a root, and both outlast the man they belong to. The psalm chooses the two slowest crops in the country."}]}, "Psalms 129:3–4": {"title": "\"The plowmen plowed across my back\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the shorter Songs of Ascents, and one of its images is unusually brutal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The plowmen plowed across my back and cut their long furrows there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A plough opening ground, used of a human back. The Hebrew for *furrows* is the agricultural term — the parallel cuts a blade leaves in a field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel is speaking as one person here, describing centuries of oppression from Egypt onward: *many a time they have afflicted me from my youth, yet they have not prevailed against me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is a harvest image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is one of the pilgrim songs, sung by farmers walking up to Jerusalem for the festivals. A ploughed field was not a picture of ruin to them — it was what you did before sowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That tension is left standing. A back opened like a field, in a psalm sung on the way to a harvest feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the LORD is just. He has cut me free from the ropes of the wicked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs in tension: they cut the back, He cuts the ropes. The same action word answering itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is just* is stated as the reason. Not that Israel endured well — that God is righteous, and therefore the cords did not hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where readers have taken it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scourging of Jesus is described in the Gospels in a single word and no detail. Christian readers have long heard this verse behind it, and it sits naturally beside Isaiah's servant whose back was given to those who struck him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not say that. It is Israel's own account of survival — and it is worth noticing that the language it reaches for is the language of a body taking what a field takes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm ends.** With the wicked as grass on a roof — sprouting fast in shallow soil, withered before anyone can gather it. Set against a ploughed field, the contrast is between ground that gets worked and ground nothing grows in."}]}, "Psalms 133:1": {"title": "\"When brothers dwell together as one\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses in the whole psalm, and this is the first. The other two are the pictures."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look how **good**, look how **pleasant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, and they are not the same thing. Some good things are not pleasant and some pleasant things are not good. The psalm says unity is both at once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Dwell together\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is about settling, staying, living in one place — not visiting. It is the word used of a family sharing an inheritance rather than dividing it and moving apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is compared to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm goes on to two images, and both are about something running downward:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**oil poured on Aaron's head**, running down his beard, down onto the collar of his robes. The anointing oil of a priest, and the psalm follows it all the way down.", "**the dew of Hermon** falling on the mountains of Zion. Hermon is the great snow peak in the far north; Zion is two hundred kilometres south. The image is of moisture from the highest place arriving somewhere else entirely."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are liquid, both come from above, and both go down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For there the LORD commanded the blessing — life for evermore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There* — in the place where brothers dwell together. The blessing is located rather than merely described."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What kind of psalm it is.** One of the Songs of Ascents, sung by pilgrims walking up to Jerusalem for the festivals — people from scattered towns arriving in one place three times a year. The unity being celebrated is not theoretical. It is a road full of families converging."}]}, "Psalms 136:1–3": {"title": "\"His steadfast love endures forever\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm with one line in it, repeated twenty-six times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The structure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every verse in Psalm 136 has two halves. The first varies; the second never does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His steadfast love endures forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-six verses, twenty-six repetitions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it was used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The form is antiphonal. A leader sang the first half and the congregation answered with the second."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is built to be said back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chesed** — covenant loyalty, steadfast love, mercy. It is one of the hardest words in the Old Testament to translate, and English versions render it a dozen ways."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It means the kind of love that holds because of an agreement rather than because of a feeling — and then exceeds the agreement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three titles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give thanks to **the LORD**, for He is good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give thanks to **the God of gods**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give thanks to **the Lord of lords**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **YHWH**, then **Elohei ha-elohim**, then **Adonei ha-adonim** — a Hebrew superlative construction, the same shape as *king of kings* and *holy of holies*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm goes on to list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Creation — the heavens, the earth on the waters, the great lights, the sun for the day and the moon and stars for the night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the exodus — the firstborn of Egypt, the outstretched arm, the sea divided *into parts*, Israel through the middle, Pharaoh overthrown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the wilderness and the kings — Sihon, Og, the land given as a heritage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *who **remembered us in our lowly state**… and **rescued us from our enemies**… who **gives food to all flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cosmology, national history, and dinner, and after every one of them the same line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the repetition does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The listed events are all different. The refrain is the constant, which is the point of the form."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the verse is about, the answer is the same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up in the history"}, {"k": "p", "t": "2 Chronicles, at the dedication of Solomon's temple: the trumpeters and singers *as one, to make one sound to be heard in praising and thanking the LORD… saying, \"**For He is good, for His mercy endures forever**\" — that the house was filled with a cloud.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at Jehoshaphat's battle, where the singers went out in front of the army with the same line. See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the laying of the second temple's foundation, in Ezra — where the old men who remembered the first one wept while everyone else shouted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name it carries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jewish tradition calls Psalm 136 **the Great Hallel**, and it is sung at Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means it was very likely sung in the upper room. Matthew records the end of the supper: *and when they had sung a hymn, they went out to the Mount of Olives.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm is.** One sentence, said twenty-six times, with the whole of creation and the whole of Israel's history hung between the repetitions."}]}, "Psalms 141:1–2": {"title": "\"Let my prayer stand before You like incense\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David asks for his words to be treated as temple furniture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The urgency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*LORD, **I call to You. Come quickly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *hasten to me*. It is how a lot of David's psalms open, and he never apologises for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen to my voice **when I cry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when he composes. When he cries out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let my prayer **stand before You like incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *let my prayer be set forth* — **kun**, to be established, fixed, made ready. It is the word used for setting a thing in its proper place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking for his words to be positioned where the incense was positioned: in front of the veil, on the golden altar, directly outside the room where God said He would meet them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that is a large request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David was not a priest. He was of Judah, not Levi, and the incense altar was off limits to him entirely — Uzziah, a king of his own line, tried it and came out a leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for his prayer to go where his body could not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **my lifted hands** be **the evening offering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evening sacrifice was at the ninth hour — three in the afternoon — a lamb on the bronze altar, every day without fail."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the hour Elijah prayed on Carmel, the hour Gabriel reached Daniel, and the hour Jesus died."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two altars"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse names both. Incense for the golden altar inside, and the offering for the bronze altar in the court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David asks for one prayer to count as both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lifted hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard posture for prayer in the Old Testament, and the standard posture of the priest lifting the offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul keeps it: *I desire that the men pray everywhere, lifting up holy hands.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he prays next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set a guard, O LORD, over my mouth; keep watch over the door of my lips.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having asked for his words to be accepted as incense, the next thing he asks is a sentry on his mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer given later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of them had a harp, and a gold bowl full of burning incense — **and the incense is the prayers of God's people**.* See the note on Revelation 5:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David asked for it. John saw the bowls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the incense was made of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four spices, equal parts, salted, compounded by a perfumer, and unlawful to make for private use. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he is asking to be counted.** A voice, and a pair of raised hands. He puts them in the place of the two things the priesthood did every day and asks God to take them as such."}]}, "Psalms 141:3–4": {"title": "\"Keep watch at the door of my lips\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Having asked for his prayer to be received as incense, David asks for a sentry on the place it comes out of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set **a guard**, LORD, **over my mouth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shomrah** — a watch, a guard post. The word appears once in the Bible, here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not asking to be silenced. He is asking for a sentry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep watch at **the door of my lips**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *door* is **dal** — the leaf of a door, the part that swings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A door is not a wall. It opens and shuts, and the point of a guard on it is that some things go through and some do not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not himself. *Set a guard, LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before, in Psalm 140, he is describing men *who sharpen their tongues like a serpent*. He knows what a mouth does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let my prayer stand before You **like incense**, let my lifted hands be the evening offering.* See the note on Psalms 141:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is deliberate. He asks for his words to be treated as temple offering, and the next thing he asks is that a guard be posted where they come from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let **my heart turn toward evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mouth in verse 3, the heart in verse 4. He moves inward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *do not incline my heart* — the same verb used of stretching out or bending toward something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is afraid of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or **busy itself with wrongdoing**, **sharing the feast of those who do harm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to practise wicked deeds with men who work iniquity*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The danger named is not solitary. It is joining in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let me **never taste their delicacies**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *delicacies* is **manammim** — dainties, pleasant things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not describe their company as repulsive. He calls the food good and asks not to eat it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for instead, in the next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the righteous strike me; it shall be a kindness. And let him rebuke me; it shall be excellent oil; let my head not refuse it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He would rather be hit by an honest man than fed by a dishonest one, and he calls the blow oil on the head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm was written from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title is *a psalm of David*, and the last verses are a man asking to be kept out of a net set for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let your words always be gracious, with salt in them.* See the note on Colossians 4:5–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And James: *if anyone does not stumble in word, he is a perfect man, able also to bridle the whole body.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being asked.** Not for the door to be sealed. For somebody to be standing at it — and the request comes from a man who had just asked for what goes through it to be received as incense."}]}, "Psalms 147:1–3": {"title": "\"He heals the brokenhearted\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm puts two things side by side that do not obviously belong together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three actions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **rebuilds Jerusalem**. He **gathers the scattered** of Israel. He **heals the brokenhearted** and **binds up their wounds**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A city, a nation, and a person — and they are in one breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scale drops from a skyline to an individual without any change of tone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Binds up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chabash** — to bandage, to wrap. It is what you do to a wound with cloth, and it is what a physician does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *wounds* is the word for pain, hurt, a sore place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is metaphorical in the Hebrew. It is field-dressing vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **counts the number of the stars**; He **calls them all by name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is verse 4, and it is the line the passage is best known for pairing with verse 3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same God binding up one person's wounds is counting a sky nobody has finished counting, and naming every one of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then verse 5: *Great is our Lord, and mighty in power; His understanding is infinite.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stars are there to establish the size of the One doing the bandaging."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stars, and Abraham"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb behind counting the stars is **saphar** — to number, and to recount, to tell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word God used when He took Abram outside: *count the stars, if you can even number them.* See the note on Genesis 15:4–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the psalm is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He rebuilds Jerusalem. He gathers the scattered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written for people coming home from exile to a ruined city. Not an untroubled congregation — a returning one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says God is not impressed by"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Further down: *He does not delight in the strength of the horse; He takes no pleasure in the legs of a man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two fastest things in that world, and both are dismissed in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD takes pleasure in those who fear Him, in those who hope in His mercy.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hallelujah! How **good** it is to sing to our God, how **pleasant**, how **fitting** the praise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for it. The psalm argues that praise is good, that it is enjoyable, and that it is appropriate — and then gives four verses of reasons."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm ends on.** *He declares His word to Jacob, His statutes and His judgements to Israel. He has not dealt thus with any nation.* The final boast of the psalm is not the stars or the city. It is that He talked to them."}]}, "Psalms 16:5–8": {"title": "\"Even in the night my heart instructs me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David describes what he was allotted, using the vocabulary of a land survey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The portion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is **all I have, my cup, my share**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the language of inheritance — *the portion of my inheritance and my cup.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what the Levites were told: *I am your portion and your inheritance among the children of Israel.* They got no territory and were given Him instead. See the note on Ezekiel 44:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David was a king with a kingdom, and he uses the landless tribe's language about himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measuring line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The lines have fallen for me in **lovely places**; yes, **the inheritance I have is beautiful**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *lines* are surveyor's cords — how land was measured and allotted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote this having spent years in caves, hunted by Saul, and later driven out of his own capital by his son. He calls the plot he was given lovely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will bless the LORD who counsels me; **even in the night my heart instructs me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my reins instruct me in the night seasons* — the kidneys, which in Hebrew idiom are the seat of the deepest inner life, below thought."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something in him is being taught while he is awake in the dark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The counsel comes first in the verse and the night instruction second. What he has been given in the day is what surfaces at three in the morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Set at the right hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have **set the LORD always before me**; **He is at my right hand**, I will not be shaken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *set* is deliberate — placed, positioned. It is something David does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the right hand is the position of the advocate, or of the strong man who defends you. In Zechariah 3 it is where the accuser was standing. See the note on Zechariah 3:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is occupied by somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter takes this psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Pentecost, in his first sermon, he quotes the verses that follow:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore my heart is glad and my glory rejoices; my flesh also will rest in hope. For You will not leave my soul in Sheol, nor will You allow Your Holy One to see corruption.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says David *spoke concerning the resurrection of Christ, that His soul was not left in Hades, nor did His flesh see corruption.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it too, at Antioch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will show me the path of life; in Your presence is fullness of joy; at Your right hand are pleasures forevermore.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm is titled.** *A Michtam of David* — a word nobody has definitively translated, possibly from a root meaning to engrave, or gold. Six psalms carry it, and every one of them was written in trouble."}]}, "Psalms 18:16–24": {"title": "\"He brought me into a wide open place\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David wrote this the day the LORD delivered him from all his enemies and from Saul. It is the longest psalm of rescue in the book, and this is the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Reaching down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **reached down from on high** and took hold of me. He **drew me up** out of the deep waters.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *drew me up* is the verb in Moses' name — *because I drew him out of the water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David uses the language of a baby lifted out of a river to describe his own rescue from Saul."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From those too strong for me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From my mighty enemy, from those who hated me, **from those too strong for me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He concedes the point without embarrassment. They were stronger. That is the premise of the rescue, not an objection to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came at me **on my day of ruin**, but the LORD was my support.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the day of my calamity*. He does not say there was no such day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage takes for granted that the trouble happened, that he went under, and that the deep waters were deep. He is not describing an escape from the valley. He is describing being drawn up out of the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wide place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **brought me into a wide open place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Merchab** — a broad place, open ground, room. It is the opposite of the Hebrew word for trouble, which is built on the idea of being pressed and narrow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Distress is being squeezed; deliverance is space. And note the verb: He brought him *into* it — out of one place and into another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He rescued me **because He delighted in me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the reason stated in the text, and it is stated before any of the lines about clean hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a word of pleasure and desire. He wanted to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Clean hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD rewarded me for my right living. For clean hands He repaid me… I was blameless before Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David says this in a psalm written after Saul had hunted him for years and he had twice refused to put out his hand against the LORD's anointed when he had him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delight comes first in the passage, and the record follows it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the psalm appears twice.** It is Psalm 18 and it is also 2 Samuel 22 — the same song printed once in the songbook and once in the history. Whoever put the books together thought the day David was drawn out of deep water was worth recording in both."}]}, "Psalms 19:1–4": {"title": "The speech with no words", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sky is described as talking, and then as having nothing to say it with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The heavens are **telling** the glory of God. The skies **pour out** the work of His hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *telling* is **saphar** — to count, to recount, to narrate. It is the same verb God used when He took Abram outside: *count the stars, if you can even number them.* See the note on Genesis 15:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbering and narrating are the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Day and night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Day after day they keep **speaking**. Night after night they keep **teaching**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verbs are continuous — pouring forth, and declaring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two shifts, and neither one stops. Whatever is being said is being said around the clock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contradiction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **no speech**, there are **no words**. Their voice is **never heard**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three negatives in a row, immediately after four verbs of speaking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It says nothing and it will not stop saying it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet their sound goes out **through all the earth**, their words **to the end of the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No language, and universal reach. The one message on earth that needs no translation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul puts this verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 10, in the middle of his argument about preaching:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But I say, have they not heard? Yes indeed: **\"Their sound has gone out to all the earth, and their words to the ends of the world.\"***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes this psalm as evidence that the message has been available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the heavens **He pitched a tent for the sun**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that He set a tent for it — the sun has an address."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verses following describe it *like a bridegroom coming out of his chamber, and rejoicing like a strong man to run its race.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Halfway through, without warning, it changes subject entirely:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law of the LORD is perfect, converting the soul; the testimony of the LORD is sure, making wise the simple.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six statements about Scripture, matching the six about the sky."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two books, in one psalm — the heavens, and the word — and David sets them side by side without explaining the join."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the words of my mouth and the meditation of my heart be acceptable in Your sight, O LORD, my strength and my Redeemer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sky, Scripture, and then a man asking that his own speech be acceptable."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Job is asked about the same sky.** *Can you bind the beautiful cords of the Pleiades, or loosen the belt of Orion? Can you **lead out the constellations in their seasons**?* God asks about specific star groups by name, and asks whether Job can steer them. See the note on Job 38:31–33."}]}, "Psalms 22:1–5": {"title": "\"My God, my God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening line of this psalm is the sentence Jesus cried from the cross, in Aramaic, at the ninth hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My God, my God, **why have You abandoned me?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not compose it there. He quoted it. And in that culture, naming the first line of a psalm called up the whole psalm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the rest of it and it is a description of a crucifixion written a thousand years before the Romans invented one: *they pierced my hands and my feet… I can count all my bones… they divide my garments among them and cast lots for my clothing… all who see me mock me, they shake their heads.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"My God\" — twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even in the abandonment the possessive holds. He says *my* God, twice, to the One He is saying has left Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is different here from every other psalm of distress"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I cry out **by day, but You do not answer**, by night, but I find no rest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's psalms of trouble almost always turn on a rescue that comes. This one records the silence and does not explain it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **You are holy**, enthroned on the praises of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word *yet* carries it. Nothing is being accused. In the middle of the forsaking, holiness is affirmed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs about the fathers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In You our fathers **trusted**. They **trusted**, and You **delivered** them. They **cried** to You and were **rescued**. They trusted in You and were **not put to shame**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone before Him trusted and was delivered. That is what makes the first line what it is: the pattern held for all of them, and it did not hold here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the one exception in the history of the psalm. He trusted, and He was not delivered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not stay there. From the middle it turns: *You have answered me.* And it closes on a proclamation to a people not yet born — *they shall come and declare His righteousness to a people who will be born, that **He has done it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three Hebrew words at the end. The Greek of the New Testament renders the same thought in one: *tetelestai*. It is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three psalms together"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**22** — the cross, forsaken, *He has done it*", "**23** — the shepherd, the valley, the table, goodness and mercy following", "**24** — *lift up your heads, O gates… the King of glory shall come in*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Death, then care, then the King coming in through the gates. They stand in that order in the book. See the notes on Psalms 23:1–3 and Psalms 24:7–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the psalm's opening.** Any answer to the question. He asks *why*, and no reply is recorded. The answer is on the other side of the psalm, in the last line, and it is not an explanation — it is a completed work."}]}, "Psalms 22:16–18": {"title": "\"They have pierced My hands and My feet\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David wrote this around 1000 BC. Crucifixion had not been invented."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When the method appeared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Persians began impaling people some five centuries after David. The Romans developed crucifixion into what it became, and it was not in use anywhere in David's world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel's own method of execution was stoning. Nothing in David's experience involved piercing a man's hands and feet and leaving him where people could stand and stare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They have **pierced my hands and my feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, written by a man describing something he had no category for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What else is in the passage"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Dogs surround me* — the term Jews used for Gentiles, and Romans did the work", "*a band of evildoers has closed in*", "*I can **count all my bones*** — what hanging by the arms does to the ribcage", "*they **stare and gloat** over me* — the crowd standing and watching", "*they **divide my clothes** among them and **cast lots** for my garment*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "That last one is two separate actions, and John records both: the soldiers divided His outer garments four ways, and then came to the tunic, *woven in one piece from the top*, and said *let us not tear it, but cast lots for it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They divided some and gambled for one, because of how the tunic was made. David wrote both halves of it a thousand years earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Not one bone broken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I can count all my bones.* And in the same psalm's world, Exodus 12 said of the Passover lamb: *nor shall you break one of its bones.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soldiers came to break the legs of the crucified men to finish them, and found Him already dead, and did not break His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm opens with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My God, my God, why have You abandoned me?* — the sentence He quoted from the cross at the ninth hour. See the note on Psalms 22:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naming the first line of a psalm called up the whole psalm. He was pointing at this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And how it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has done it.* Three Hebrew words at the close, and the Greek for the same thought is one: **tetelestai**. It is finished."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the psalm.** Any hint that David is describing his own life. Nothing recorded of him matches it. He wrote a first-person account of an execution that would not exist for five hundred years, in a country that never practised it."}]}, "Psalms 23:1–3": {"title": "\"He lays me down\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best-known psalm in the world, and the verbs in its first three lines are all His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lays me down… He leads me… He restores… He guides me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, one subject. The sheep in this psalm is not described as following well, finding pasture, or keeping up. Everything active belongs to the shepherd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing said about the sheep is *I lack nothing* — a state, not an achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Lying down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lays me down in green pastures.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sheep do not lie down when they are hungry, frightened, or bothered by other animals. A resting flock is the sign that every one of those has been dealt with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has the shepherd causing it. He does not merely permit the rest; he brings the conditions about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Still waters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is *waters of rest* — quiet water, water that has stopped moving. Sheep will not drink from a fast stream; the noise and movement frighten them, and a wet fleece will drown them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shepherd in that country dammed a pool or found a still place. The provision is shaped to what the animal can actually use."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He restores my soul\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he brings my soul back* — the verb for returning, turning around. It is used elsewhere of bringing back a wanderer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not merely refreshment. Retrieval."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He guides me along right paths **for the sake of His name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The motive is stated, and it is not the sheep's merit. The shepherd's reputation is bound up with where the flock ends up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm does not skip.** Verse 4 goes straight into the valley of the shadow of death, and the shepherd is still there — *You are with me.* The green pastures and the valley are on the same path, led by the same person."}]}, "Psalms 23:4": {"title": "Through the valley", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and everything in it turns on a single preposition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Through\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even when I walk **through** the valley of the shadow of death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not into it, not down to it, and not around it. Through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word assumes an entrance and an exit. Nobody is promised a route that avoids the valley; the promise is about what the valley is — a passage, not a destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I walk\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not run, not flee. The pace does not change."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The valley"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsalmaveth** — a compound of *shadow* and *death*, and it is used of deep darkness, the places where light does not reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shadow requires a light source and an object, and it can frighten but it cannot touch. David is a shepherd writing about the ravines he walked his flock through, where the walls are high and the sun does not get in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm changes person"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the psalm from the beginning and watch the pronouns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is **my** shepherd… **He** makes me lie down… **He** leads me… **He** restores my soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is being talked about. Then the valley:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **You** are with me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The moment the darkness comes, the psalm stops speaking about Him and starts speaking to Him. That is where He stops being the subject of a sentence and becomes the person being addressed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I will fear no evil\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not that there is no evil in the valley. It is *for You are with me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rod and the staff"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two implements, and they do different jobs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **rod** is the club — a shepherd's weapon, carried against what comes at the flock. The **staff** is the crook, for guiding and lifting a sheep out of a place it has got itself into."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One is used on the enemy, one on the sheep, and both are listed as comfort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the other side of the valley.** *You prepare a table before me in the presence of my enemies.* He does not clear the room first. The enemies are still there and the meal is served anyway."}]}, "Psalms 23:5": {"title": "A table in front of them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four lines, and the shepherd has become a host."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm has got to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verses 1 to 4 are pasture, water, paths and a valley — shepherd's country."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the imagery changes without warning. There is a table, oil, and a cup. This is a house, and the reader is a guest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **set a table before me** in the presence of my enemies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to arrange, to lay out in order. It is unhurried."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the presence of my enemies\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are still there. Not defeated, not removed, not converted — present, and watching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not describe a rescue from the room. It describes a meal served in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **anoint my head with oil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things this could be, and both are in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A host poured oil on a guest's head at a feast — ordinary hospitality, and a mark of honour. Jesus notes its absence at Simon the Pharisee's table: *you did not anoint My head with oil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a shepherd poured oil on a sheep's head against flies and infection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm has been about a shepherd for four verses and is about a host in this one. Both readings sit inside the same line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My cup **overflows**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my cup is saturation* — running over the top."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A host who fills a cup to the brim has been generous. A host who keeps pouring after it is full is making a point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the enemies get"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. They are not fed, not addressed, and not asked to leave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are made to watch, and the watching is part of what the verse is describing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Surely **goodness and mercy will pursue me** all the days of my life, and I will dwell in the house of the LORD forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *pursue* is a hunter's word — the verb used of Pharaoh's chariots chasing Israel. See the note on Psalms 23:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The table is in a house, and the last line says the guest is moving in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the whole psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pasture, water, valley, table, house. It moves outdoors to indoors, and from *He* to *You* at the valley and never goes back."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the enemies were in David's life.** Saul with three thousand men, the Philistines, and eventually his own son. The man who wrote about a table set in front of enemies had eaten a good many meals looking over his shoulder."}]}, "Psalms 23:6": {"title": "The two that follow", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last verse of the psalm, and the verb in it is not a gentle one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Pursue\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Surely goodness and mercy will **pursue** me all the days of my life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **radaph** — to chase, to hunt, to run after. It is the word used for an army in pursuit, and for Pharaoh's chariots after Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most translations soften it to *follow*, which sounds like something trailing along behind. The verb is a chase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are hunting him down, and they are goodness and mercy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tov** — goodness. **Chesed** — the covenant word, steadfast love, loyal kindness. It is the word repeated twenty-six times in Psalm 136, once per verse: *His mercy endures forever.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All the days\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one. Not the good ones, not the ones he handled well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The valley is in verse 4 and the enemies are in verse 5, and verse 6 says *all the days of my life* without excluding either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a shepherd's psalm ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm has moved from pasture to water to valley to a table with enemies watching, and then to a house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I will **dwell in the house of the LORD forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to sit, to remain, to settle — the word for taking up residence, not visiting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote this. He was a man who spent his life in tents and caves and on the run, and who was never allowed to build the house he wanted to build."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The whole psalm in its place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set between the cross and the coming King:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Psalm 22** — forsaken, pierced, *He has done it*", "**Psalm 23** — the shepherd, the valley, the table, and the house", "**Psalm 24** — *lift up your heads, O gates, that the King of glory may come in*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the middle one. It is the psalm for now: the walking, the days, the pursuit. See the notes on Psalms 22:1–5 and Psalms 24:7–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first verse and the last.** *The LORD is my shepherd, I shall not want* — and *I will dwell in the house of the LORD forever.* It opens with a want that does not exist and closes with an address that does not change."}]}, "Psalms 24:7–8": {"title": "Lift up your heads, O gates", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A command shouted at architecture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lift up your heads, **O gates**! Be lifted up, **you ancient doors**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the people. The gates."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm tells the doorways to make themselves higher, because what is coming through will not fit under them as they stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Ancient doors\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *doors of old*, everlasting doors. Whatever gates these are, they are older than the city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question and the answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who is this King of glory?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm asks it, and answers it, and then asks again a few lines later and answers again. It is written to be sung antiphonally — one voice at the gate, one voice inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD, **strong and mighty**, the LORD, **mighty in battle**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer is military. He is identified by a fight He has been in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which battle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm before it is the shepherd. The psalm before that is the cross — *forsaken*, *pierced*, and *He has done it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the time the gates are told to lift their heads, the battle referred to is over. He is not going out to it; He is coming back from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three psalms as one sequence"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**22** — the Saviour dying, forsaken, the work finished", "**23** — the Shepherd, present, walking the days and the valley with His own", "**24** — the King of glory coming in through the gates"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past, present, and what is still ahead. They stand in that order in the book and they have always been read that way. See the notes on Psalms 22:1–5 and Psalms 23:6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The earth is the LORD's, and everything in it, the world and all who live in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim is made before the King arrives at the gate. He owns the city He is being asked to be let into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who goes up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle of the psalm asks who may ascend the hill of the LORD, and answers: *he who has clean hands and a pure heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then it names the generation that seeks Him, and the last line of that section is *they will receive blessing from the LORD, and righteousness from the God of their salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness is listed as something received from Him, in the same breath as the blessing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line of the psalm.** *The LORD of hosts — He is the King of glory.* The title is Yahweh Sabaoth, the LORD of armies. The gates are opened for the commander, coming home."}]}, "Psalms 30:4–5": {"title": "\"Weeping may stay for the night\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two lines built as a pair, and the pairing is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **His anger lasts only a moment**, but **His favour lasts a lifetime**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *a moment* is the blink of an eye — the shortest measure the language has."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against it: **chayyim**, life, a lifetime."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not the same size, and the psalm is not saying they balance. It is saying one is a blink and the other is the whole of the days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Weeping may **stay for the night**, but **joy comes with the morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stay* is to lodge, to spend the night — the word for a traveller taking a room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weeping is a guest. It checks in, and it does not have a permanent address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the Hebrew day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Evening, then morning — the way Genesis 1 counts every one of the six days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the night is, the direction of travel in that calendar is always toward the light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sing to the LORD, **O you His faithful**. Give thanks to His holy name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two lines about anger and weeping are addressed to His own people. It is not a general description of how life goes; it is what the covenant faithful are told about their own experience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David had been through"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's heading is *a song at the dedication of the house of David*, and the body of it is a man recounting a rescue:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O LORD my God, I cried out to You, and You healed me. O LORD, You brought my soul up from the grave; You have kept me alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And earlier: *in my prosperity I said, \"I shall never be moved\"… You hid Your face, and I was troubled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been confident, then undone, then healed, and this psalm is written from the far side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have turned for me **my mourning into dancing**; You have put off my sackcloth and clothed me with gladness — to the end that my glory may sing praise to You and not be silent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *turned* is the word for overturning, converting a thing into something else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not mourning followed by dancing. Mourning made into it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word Isaiah uses for judgement.** *His strange work… His alien task* — foreign, not native to Him. This psalm is the same claim in a song: the anger is a blink, the favour is the lifetime. See the note on Isaiah 28:20–22."}]}, "Psalms 36:7–9": {"title": "In Your light we see light", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses in the middle of a psalm that opens with a description of wickedness. David turns from that to this without transition, and the change of temperature is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shadow of the wings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People take shelter under the shadow of Your wings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for shelter is the word for taking refuge — running to a rock or a stronghold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wings are the cherubim wings over the mercy seat, and also a bird's over its young. Both pictures are in the Psalms, and both are about a small thing being covered by something larger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Feasting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **feast on** the rich abundance of Your house, You give them drink from Your river of delights.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one for being saturated, drinking to the full. The noun behind *rich abundance* is *deshen* — the fat, the richest portion, the part of the offering that was burned as the choice part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Delights** is **adanim** — the plural of the word Eden is built from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two \"withs\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For with You is the fountain of life, and in Your light we see light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not near You. **With** You. The source is not somewhere He points to; it is where He is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Fountain** is *maqor* — a spring, the origin of running water, as against a cistern that only holds what was poured in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Your light we see light\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is circular on purpose. You do not bring a light to God in order to find Him. You see by His."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same logic as the lamp in Psalm 119 and the lamp in 2 Peter 1 — the seeing is done in borrowed light, and the borrowing is not a deficiency."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David was looking at.** The verses immediately before this describe a man who has *no fear of God before his eyes*, who plots on his bed. David does not argue with him. He looks up instead, and the psalm changes register in a single line: *Your unfailing love, O LORD, reaches to the heavens.*"}]}, "Psalms 40:6–8": {"title": "\"My ears You have opened\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David says God did not want the sacrifices, in the middle of a system built on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sacrifice and offering You did not desire… **Burnt offering and sin offering You did not require**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four of the five Levitical offerings named and set aside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that they were wrong — they were commanded in the same law David lived under. The psalm is saying they were never the thing wanted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel had said it to Saul: *to obey is better than sacrifice.* Hosea: *I desire mercy and not sacrifice.* Micah, Isaiah and Jeremiah all say versions of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My ears You have opened.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **karah** — to dig, to bore through. *Ears You have dug for me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not the word for opening a door. It is the word for making a hole."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the law it points back to is Exodus 21: a servant who could go free, saying *I love my master… I do not want to go free*, brought to the doorpost, and his ear pierced with an awl. See the note on Exodus 21:2–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sacrifice was not wanted. A bored ear was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I said, **see, I have come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *behold, I come* — the response of somebody who has heard an order and is presenting himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An opened ear, and then an arrival."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scroll"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the scroll of the book **it is written of me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever David meant by it, Hebrews takes it as spoken by Christ, and the writer of Hebrews is not being subtle about it:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, when He came into the world, He said: \"Sacrifice and offering You did not desire, but **a body You have prepared for Me**… Behold, I have come — in the volume of the book it is written of Me — to do Your will, O God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes the Greek Old Testament, which renders *ears You have dug* as *a body You have prepared* — the part standing for the whole."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion Hebrews draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He takes away the first that He may establish the second. By that will **we have been sanctified through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ once for all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four offerings God did not require, replaced by a body and a will."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The delight"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **delight** to do Your will, O my God. **Your law is here within my heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not carried, not consulted. Inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah's new covenant promise is the same: *I will put My law in their minds, and write it on their hearts.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the psalm starts.** *I waited patiently for the LORD… He brought me up out of a horrible pit, out of the miry clay, and set my feet upon a rock.* The man writing about the pierced ear had been in a pit first."}]}, "Psalms 46:1–3": {"title": "\"A help always near\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three things said about God in one line, and then the worst thing the psalmist can imagine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is our **refuge** and our **strength**, **a help always near** the moment trouble comes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Refuge** — *machseh*, a shelter you run into from a storm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Strength** — the word for might, and it is His rather than borrowed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A help always near** — the Hebrew is *a help in troubles, very much found*. He is described as easily located."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The moment trouble comes\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not say trouble might come. It assumes it, and the whole claim is about timing: found, and found immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he imagines"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even if **the earth gives way**, even if **the mountains topple into the heart of the sea**, though its waters roar and foam, though the mountains shake.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most permanent things anybody in that world could point at — the ground and the mountains — going into the water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the language of creation coming undone. The psalmist reaches for the end of the world and says *even if*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **we will not be afraid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Placed before the list, not after it. The fearlessness is stated and then the catastrophes are named underneath it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Selah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm pauses here — and again twice more. Nobody is certain what the word directs, but it marks a break."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the middle of the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **a river** whose streams shall make glad the city of God… **God is in the midst of her, she shall not be moved**; God shall help her, **just at the break of dawn**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seas roar and the mountains fall into them in verse 3. In verse 4 there is a river, and it is quiet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jerusalem had no river. It had one spring, Gihon, and a tunnel Hezekiah cut through rock to bring the water inside the walls before a siege."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line everybody knows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be still, and know that I am God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is stronger than *be quiet* — it means let go, cease, drop your hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is not addressed to the anxious believer in the usual reading. Look at the verse before: *He makes wars cease to the end of the earth; He breaks the bow and cuts the spear in two.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command is addressed to the nations. **Stop.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD of hosts is with us; the God of Jacob is our refuge.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, at the end of the two halves. **LORD of hosts** is the military title, and *the God of Jacob* is the God of the man who cheated his brother and wrestled all night."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was written on Luther's tombstone territory.** He wrote *A Mighty Fortress Is Our God* out of this psalm, in the middle of the plague and the Diet of Speyer. The psalm has a long record of being reached for when the mountains are moving."}]}, "Psalms 46:10–11": {"title": "\"Be still\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted line in the psalm, and it is usually read as being addressed to the wrong people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He makes wars cease to the end of the earth; **He breaks the bow and cuts the spear in two**; He burns the chariot in the fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the immediate context. Weapons being destroyed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be still, and know that I am God.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **raphah** — to let go, to sink down, to drop, to cease. It is not primarily about quietness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is used of hands going slack, and of abandoning an effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it with verse 9 attached and the addressee changes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just broken the bow and the spear and burned the chariot, and then says **stop**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command is addressed to the nations who were making the war — *the nations raged, the kingdoms were moved* in verse 6. It is a cease-fire order shouted at combatants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That does not make it useless to an anxious reader. It means the calm being offered is the calm of somebody who has watched the weapons being broken, not the calm of somebody being told to relax."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will be lifted high among the nations. I will be lifted high in the earth.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, and both are His own intention rather than anything anybody does for Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD of hosts is with us. **The God of Jacob is our high tower.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two lines close the first half of the psalm as well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**LORD of hosts** — Yahweh Sabaoth, the God of armies, in a psalm about wars ceasing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The God of Jacob** — not the God of Abraham, the friend of God, or Israel the prince. Jacob: the supplanter, the man who cheated his brother and wrestled all night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm has said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble. Therefore we will not fear, **even though the earth be removed**, and though the mountains be carried into the midst of the sea.* See the note on Psalms 46:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle: *there is a river whose streams shall make glad the city of God… **God is in the midst of her, she shall not be moved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roaring seas and toppling mountains in verse 3, and a quiet river in verse 4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Selah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm pauses three times, and one of them is right here at the end."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Luther made of it.** *A Mighty Fortress Is Our God* is this psalm, written in the 1520s during plague and upheaval. Psalm 46 has a long record of being reached for when things are moving."}]}, "Psalms 51:3–4": {"title": "\"You are right when You speak\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Written after the worst thing David ever did, and the astonishing thing is the direction he faces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heading on the psalm says it: *when Nathan the prophet came to him, after he had gone in to Bathsheba.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adultery, and then a man murdered by arrangement to cover it. A year of silence before the prophet arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Never leaves my sight\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I know my rebellion, **my sin never leaves my sight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that it is *before me continually*. He is not confessing something he had forgotten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Against You, You only\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Against You, **You only**, have I sinned, and done what is evil in Your sight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Uriah is dead. Bathsheba's life is wrecked. A child will die. And David says *against You only*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not dismissing them. He is naming who the offence is finally against — the psalm is addressed to God and he refuses to spread the account across other defendants."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **You are right when You speak, You are clear when You judge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the move. He does not argue for a mitigating circumstance. He rules in God's favour before God has said anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that God is *justified* in His speaking and *clear* in His judging."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Set against Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's one charge against Job, out of the whirlwind: *would you condemn Me so that you could be in the right?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two positions and only one can be occupied. Job took his own for thirty chapters and God had to come out of a storm about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David takes the other one at the top of the psalm, before anything is asked of him. He agrees God is right and puts himself in the wrong, and everything he receives in the rest of the psalm comes down that channel. See the note on Job 40:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Purge me with hyssop, and I shall be clean. Wash me, and I shall be whiter than snow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hyssop is the little bunch of herb used to put blood on the doorframes at Passover, and to sprinkle a cleansed leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks to be handled the way a leper is handled — by an application of blood he does not administer himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what he does not offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You do not delight in sacrifice, or I would bring it… **the sacrifices of God are a broken spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king with a treasury and a tabernacle says the one offering that would work is not something he can fund."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asks not to lose.** *Do not cast me from Your presence, and do not take Your Holy Spirit from me.* Under the old covenant the Spirit came and went — he had watched it happen to Saul. It is a prayer no believer since Pentecost has had to pray."}]}, "Psalms 75:6–8": {"title": "Promotion comes from one direction", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm rules out three directions and leaves one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three that are excluded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For no one is lifted up **from the east or the west, or from the wilderness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "East, west, and the desert to the south. The one direction the psalm does not name is up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever is left after those three is the answer, and the psalmist leaves the reader to say it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to lift up, to raise, to exalt — and it is the word used for promotion, for a head being raised, for a man being set high."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of the compass points can do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **God who judges**. He brings one down and lifts another up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are His. The bringing down and the lifting up are the same hand doing two things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *judges* is not primarily about a courtroom. It is the word for governing, deciding, ruling a matter — the verb the book of Judges is named after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two examples in one sermon's reach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Uzziah** — helped until he was strong, and struck at the altar the day his heart got proud. Fifty-two years, and the last of them in a separate house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Haman** — promoted above every official for no stated reason, and hanged on his own gallows within the day. And **Mordecai**, sitting at a gate, given the ring that had been on Haman's hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither man's rise came from the east or the west. Neither man's fall did either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is a **cup in the hand of the LORD**, foaming with wine, well mixed, and He pours it out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of a cup passed round — the prophets use it repeatedly, and it is the cup Jesus names in Gethsemane."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Down to the very dregs the wicked of the earth will drink it dry.* The dregs are the sediment at the bottom, the last and worst of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is holding it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage is about whose hand things are in. The cup is in His hand; the lifting is His; the bringing down is His."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the psalm arranges their own position."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm opens.** *We give thanks to You, O God, we give thanks, for Your name is near.* The name is *near* — which is what the book of Esther demonstrates without once writing it down."}]}, "Psalms 76:10–12": {"title": "Even human rage will end up praising You", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One line, and it settles what happens to everything thrown at God's people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even **human rage** will end up **praising You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not tolerated. Not survived. It ends up as praise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rage* is the hot word — burning anger, fury. It is what men do at their worst, and the psalm says the finish of it is God's glory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **what rage is left, You put on like a belt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *You gird Yourself with the remainder of wrath*. Whatever anger does not end up as praise is strapped on as a belt — held, restrained, and worn by Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A belt is what you fasten so nothing swings loose. What is left over is not running free; it is buckled to Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Haman"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of Esther is this verse in narrative."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["his **rage** at one man not bowing produced the decree", "the decree produced the fast and Esther's approach to the throne", "his **rage** built a seventy-five-foot gallows, and it is what he was hanged on", "his **boast** to his wife and friends brought out the advisers' verdict against him", "his **pleading** for his life across a couch is what the king read as assault"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every furious thing Haman did was load-bearing in his own destruction, and the Jews came out of it with the ring, the house and a feast that is still kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of those steps required a miracle, and God is not named in any of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says about kings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **cuts down the pride of rulers**; He is a terror to the kings of the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *cuts down* is to cut off, as of a vine being pruned — the word used of a grape harvest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahasuerus, who ruled from India to Cush, spent the whole book being moved by his own temper and never knew who was moving him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make your vows to the LORD your God and **keep them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set between the rage that turns to praise and the kings who are cut down, the psalm's one command to the reader is small and domestic: say what you will do, and do it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm begins.** *In Judah God is known; His name is great in Israel.* Known, and named. The book of Esther is the same God at work in a place where He was neither."}]}, "Psalms 78:21–25": {"title": "\"Mortals ate the bread of angels\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm reports God's anger and what He did about it, and the two are in the wrong order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD heard, and **He was furious**… **because they did not believe in God**, they did not trust Him to save them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is unbelief, and it is named twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is a long retelling of Israel's history, and it does not soften this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after that conjunction is provision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **commanded the skies above** and **opened the doors of heaven**. He **rained down manna** for them to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, all of them about the sky being opened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *doors* is the word for the doors of a house. The heavens are described as a building with entrances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave them **the grain of heaven**. Mortals ate **the bread of angels**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the second is **lechem abbirim** — bread of the mighty ones. Young's Literal keeps it that way: *food of the mighty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some read it as angels' bread, some as the bread of the strong. Either way the point is the same: it is not food that belongs to the eaters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men ate what came from somewhere above them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To the full\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sent them **food to the full**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to satisfaction*. Not rations, not a subsistence measure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sequence in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unbelief. Anger. And then the doors of heaven opening and bread falling on the camp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm puts the provision immediately after the complaint about the unbelief, and does not explain the order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the manna was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Something fine and flaky, delicate as frost.* They asked *what is it?* — **man hu** — and the question became the name. See the note on Exodus 16:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It fell six mornings a week for forty years and stopped the day they ate the produce of the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with this psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, and are dead… **I am the living bread which came down from heaven**. If anyone eats of this bread, he will live forever.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He grants the comparison and then says what is wrong with it: they ate, and they died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd had quoted this very psalm at Him — *He gave them bread from heaven to eat.* See the note on John 6:47–51."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm says a few verses later.** *In spite of all this they still sinned, and did not believe in His wondrous works.* The psalm's whole method is to lay unbelief and provision side by side, over and over, for seventy-two verses."}]}, "Psalms 78:40–43": {"title": "\"They did not remember His hand\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A long psalm retelling Israel's history, and this is its diagnosis of what went wrong in the desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They defied Him… they grieved Him… they tested God… they wounded the Holy One of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third and fourth are the striking ones. **Tested** — they put God through a trial, repeatedly. And the last, often rendered *limited*, has the sense of marking out a boundary or causing pain; the older translation *they limited the Holy One of Israel* is where the familiar phrase comes from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the psalm attributes something to God that is rarely said aloud: He was grieved. Not inconvenienced. Pained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cause given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They did not remember His hand, the day He set them free.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forgetting is the root, and it is a strange thing to forget — a sea opening, a nation walking out of Egypt in a night. The psalm's charge is not that they disbelieved a promise they had never seen kept. It is that they stopped recollecting one they had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole psalm is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The opening verses say it: these things were handed down *so that the next generation would know them*, and set their hope in God rather than repeating the pattern."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a psalm written to be taught to children, and its method is to tell the story honestly, failures included."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"limiting\" God.** The phrase has been used to mean that unbelief restricts what God is able to do. The Hebrew is disputed and the psalm's own explanation is simpler and sadder: they forgot what He had already done. Whatever the verb means precisely, the sentence beside it is the one the psalmist chose to explain it."}]}, "Psalms 8:5–6": {"title": "\"A little lower than the angels\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses in the middle of a psalm about the night sky."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question they answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When I consider Your heavens, the work of Your fingers, the moon and the stars, which You have ordained — **what is man that You are mindful of him, and the son of man that You visit him?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David is looking up and asking why anybody down here registers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet You made him **just a little lower than the angels**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **me'at me'elohim** — a little lower than **Elohim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is genuinely difficult. The word normally means God. The Greek Old Testament renders it *angels*, and Hebrews follows that reading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Older English versions have *angels*; several modern ones have *God* or *the heavenly beings*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A little** — the Hebrew can also mean *for a little while*, which is how Hebrews takes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **crowned him with glory and honor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kavod** and **hadar** — weight, and majesty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both words are used elsewhere of God and of kings. They are applied here to humanity as such."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You gave him **charge over the works of Your hands**. You **laid everything beneath his feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Genesis 1 restated: *let them have dominion over the fish of the sea, over the birds of the air, and over the cattle, over all the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm lists them in the next verse — sheep and oxen, beasts of the field, birds, fish, and *whatever passes through the paths of the seas*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the observable world looks like this. Man does not visibly have everything under his feet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer quotes all of it and then says exactly that:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For in that He put all in subjection under him, He left nothing that is not put under him. **But now we do not yet see all things put under him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He concedes the gap in the middle of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **we see Jesus**, who was made a little lower than the angels, for the suffering of death crowned with glory and honour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer applies the psalm to Christ — and the phrase *a little lower* becomes *for a little while lower*, which the Greek allows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commission given to humanity in Genesis, unfulfilled in observation, is read as fulfilled in one man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For He has put all things under His feet.* — 1 Corinthians 15, about the reign that continues until the last enemy. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:23–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Ephesians: *and He put all things under His feet, and gave Him to be head over all things to the church.* See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three New Testament writers use this psalm, and all three read it messianically."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is bracketed by"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same line, opening and closing:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O LORD, our Lord, **how excellent is Your name in all the earth**!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who quotes verse 2"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of the mouth of babes and nursing infants You have ordained strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus quotes it in the temple, to the chief priests objecting to children shouting *Hosanna to the Son of David*. See the note on Matthew 21:12–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm claims and what Hebrews concedes.** Everything under his feet — and the writer of Hebrews quotes it, admits nobody can see it, and points at one man."}]}, "Psalms 90:11–12": {"title": "\"Teach us to number our days\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turn in Moses' psalm. Everything before it is description; this is where it becomes a request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teach us to count our days.* Not *let us live longer*, and not *explain why*. Teach us to reckon them — the verb is arithmetical, the ordinary word for counting a number of things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The premise is that a person does not naturally do this. Days are spent as though the supply were unlimited, and the psalm asks for the ability to hold the real figure in mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we may get a heart of wisdom.* The counting is not the goal. It is the instrument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is literally *bring in* a heart of wisdom — like a harvest gathered into a barn. Numbering the days is how the crop gets in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It follows immediately on the verse about God's anger and the fear owed to Him, and it precedes a series of requests that get steadily warmer: *satisfy us in the morning with Your unfailing love… make us glad for as many days as You have afflicted us… establish the work of our hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm moves from lament to petition, and this verse is the hinge. A man watching a generation die in the desert asks first to be taught how to count."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read with the verses above it.** The famous *seventy years, or eighty if we are strong* is four lines earlier, and it is description, not decree — see the note on Psalms 90:7–10. This request is what Moses does with that observation: not resignation, but a prayer to be taught how to spend what there is."}]}, "Psalms 90:7–10": {"title": "\"Seventy or eighty years\" — spoken from inside the wrath", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best-known number in the Bible about lifespan, and it sits in the middle of a paragraph about being under God's anger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read the verses it lives in"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Our short lives feel Your fire; **in Your anger** we grow weary and afraid*", "*You set our failings before You, our secret faults lit up in the light of Your face*", "*Our days pass under Your gaze and end like a sigh*", "**then** — *our years may reach seventy, and if we are strong, eighty, yet even the best of them are toil and sorrow*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The number is the last line of a lament about living under displeasure. It is description, not decree — what life measured out to that generation, in those conditions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking, and about whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heading calls this *a prayer of Moses*, and the wilderness is all over it. He is watching the generation that refused at Kadesh die off across forty years, exactly as had been said they would."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the seventy and eighty. Not a limit God set on human beings in general — the span of a people under judgement, written down by the man watching it happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the same Bible records elsewhere"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Moses himself**, at a hundred and twenty, *his eyes were not weak and his strength had not left him*", "**Caleb**, at eighty-five, asking for a hill country full of giants and saying he was as strong as the day Moses sent him out", "**Hezekiah**, given fifteen years after being told to put his house in order"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who wrote the seventy-year line outlived it by fifty years. That is worth holding in mind before handing anyone the number as though it were an appointment."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses does next.** He does not settle for the description. The psalm turns into a request — *teach us to count our days… satisfy us in the morning with Your unfailing love… make us glad for as many days as You have afflicted us.* He is asking for something better than what he has just described. See the note on Psalms 90:11–12."}]}, "Psalms 91:1–2": {"title": "The secret shelter", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses at the head of the psalm, and they name four things about God in the space of a breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Makes a home\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **makes a home** in the secret shelter of the Most High.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to sit, to remain, to dwell — the word for taking up residence, not visiting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything the rest of the psalm promises runs off this verb. It is not a place you go to in an emergency; it is an address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The secret place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sether** — a covering, a hiding place, somewhere concealed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is secret because it is hidden, not because it is hard to find. Nobody outside can see you there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shadow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Will **rest in the shadow** of the Almighty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shadow requires being close. You cannot be in the shade of something at a distance from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Hebrew for *rest* here is to lodge overnight — to spend the night. The first line is about living there; the second is about sleeping there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four names, in order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm stacks them:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Most High** — *Elyon*, the highest, the one above all", "**Almighty** — *Shaddai*, the all-sufficient", "**the LORD** — *YHWH*, the covenant name from the burning bush", "**my God** — *Elohim*, and the possessive is the point"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "It moves from the highest and most distant title down to *my*. The psalm ends the sentence closer than it started it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will say of the LORD, \"**You are** my refuge and my fortress.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first verse is a statement about anybody. The second is spoken, in the first person, and addressed to Him directly — *You are*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm changes from third person to second person inside two lines. The saying of it is part of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Refuge and fortress"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words that are not the same. **Machseh** is a shelter you run into from a storm. **Metsudah** is a stronghold — a fortified place, a mountain hold, the word used of David's strongholds in the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somewhere to run, and somewhere that holds."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who quotes this psalm.** The devil, in the wilderness, and he quotes verses 11 and 12 accurately — *He shall give His angels charge over you.* He leaves out the line *to keep you in all your ways*. The one place in Scripture where Psalm 91 is cited by name, it is being misused, and the answer is a different verse."}]}, "Psalms 91:11–13": {"title": "The verses the devil quoted", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best-known lines in the psalm, and the most famously misused."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **give His angels orders about you**, to guard you **in all your ways**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that He will command them concerning you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angels are described as under instruction rather than acting on their own initiative, and the assignment is specific to a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To guard you **in all your ways**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the clause the devil left out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the wilderness he quoted verses 11 and 12 accurately — *He shall give His angels charge over you… in their hands they shall bear you up, lest you dash your foot against a stone* — and stopped before *in all your ways*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jumping off the temple is not one of a man's ways. It is a stunt, and the psalm's protection is over a road being walked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus answered with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a correction of the psalm. *It is written again: you shall not tempt the LORD your God* — Deuteronomy 6, about Massah, where Israel demanded proof that God was among them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers a real quotation with another real quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **lift you in their hands**, so you will not strike your foot against a stone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A stubbed toe. The smallest injury the psalm could have named."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the angels are doing, the passage puts them at ankle height."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four animals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will walk over **the lion and the cobra**; **the young lion and the serpent** you will tread beneath your feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pairs, and each pair has one that attacks openly and one that attacks hidden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lion is visible and loud; the cobra is underfoot and unseen. The same division as verse 3 — the snare you cannot see and the plague you cannot hear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the treading turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have given you authority to **walk right over snakes and scorpions**, and over all the power of the enemy.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke 10, to the seventy — and the sentence after it is *do not let your joy rest on that. Let your joy rest on this: your names are written down in heaven.* See the note on Luke 10:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Genesis 3:15: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.* The serpent under a heel is where the Bible's first promise ends up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verbs are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walk, and tread. Neither is a fight."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm is doing here.** Three voices run through it — a teacher describing the shelter, a believer confessing *You are my refuge*, and then God Himself in the last three verses. These lines are the teacher's, and the devil borrowed them."}]}, "Psalms 91:14–16": {"title": "The three verses where God speaks for Himself", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm changes speaker at the end, and most readings walk straight past it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is talking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verses 1 to 13 are *about* God — He will cover you, you will not fear. Someone is describing Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From verse 14 the voice switches to the first person and stays there. Seven promises in three verses, every one of them God's own speech:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**I** will set you free", "**I** will lift you high", "**I** will answer", "**I** will be with you in trouble", "**I** will rescue you", "**I** will honour you", "**I** will fill your hands with long life"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not end with a description of God. It ends with God taking over the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you have held onto Me in love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb means to cling, to be joined to — the word used of a man joined to his wife, and of Israel told to hold fast to the LORD. It is not achievement and it is not blamelessness. It is attachment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the seven promises are hung on. Someone who has taken hold and will not let go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"With long life I will fill your hands\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *satisfy* — filled up, having enough. Not merely a long span of years but a fullness of them, and then the last line: *I will let you see how deep My rescue runs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm ends on sight. Not only rescued, but shown the rescue — allowed to see how far down it goes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The trouble is named, not denied.** *I will be with you in trouble* is one of the seven, and it is worth holding on to. This is not a psalm promising a life nothing ever happens in. It promises a Presence in the middle of what happens, and a rescue out the other side, spoken by God about Himself."}]}, "Psalms 91:3–4": {"title": "\"Under His wings\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two dangers named, and both of them are the kind you do not see coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The snare"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will lift you free of **the hidden snare**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the snare of the fowler* — a bird-catcher's trap, laid on the ground and covered, with bait on top."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A bird walks into it because nothing about it looks like a trap. That is the whole design."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sickness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Free of **the sickness that stalks in silence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **deber** — pestilence, plague. The older rendering is *the noisome pestilence*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both dangers in the verse share one feature: they are concealed. A snare you cannot see and a sickness you cannot hear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The feathers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **cover you with His feathers**, and **under His wings** you will find your hiding place.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is a bird over a nest. Not a fortress or a shield — the softest covering available."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy uses it of the exodus: *as an eagle stirs up its nest, hovers over its young, spreading out its wings, taking them up, carrying them on its wings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Boaz says it to Ruth: *the LORD repay your work, and a full reward be given you by the LORD God of Israel, **under whose wings you have come for refuge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jesus, over Jerusalem: *how often I wanted to gather your children together, **as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings**, but you were not willing.* See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hen is not a fighter. Her one method is to open her wings and stay put over what is underneath her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His faithfulness is **a shield around you**, a wall you can lean your whole life against.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *shield* here is **tsinnah** — the large body shield, the one that covered a soldier entirely, as against the small round buckler."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the image changes without warning. Feathers in one line and armour in the next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the shield is made of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His **faithfulness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not His power, and not your faith. The Hebrew is **emeth** — truth, reliability, steadiness. The root behind *amen*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the verses sit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after the opening two, which name the condition: *whoever makes a home in the secret shelter of the Most High.* See the note on Psalms 91:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then these — the snare, the plague, the feathers, the shield — and then *a thousand may fall at your side.* See the note on Psalms 91:5–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God says at the end of the psalm.** The last three verses switch to first person: *because he has set his love upon Me, therefore I will deliver him… **I will be with him in trouble**.* In it, not instead of it. See the note on Psalms 91:14–16."}]}, "Psalms 91:5–8": {"title": "Around the clock, seen and unseen", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four dangers named in two lines, and the way they are arranged tells you the psalm means all of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The terror of the night, the arrow that flies in the day, the plague that moves in the dark, the ruin that strikes at noon.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two at night, two by day. Two hidden, two out in the open. It is a Hebrew way of saying *everything* by naming the poles — the whole clock, and both kinds of threat, the sort you can see coming and the sort you cannot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left outside the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It will not come near you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A thousand may fall at your side, ten thousand at your right hand, but it will not come near you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not pretend the danger is imaginary. People are falling — that is in the verse. What it says is that the thing sweeping the ground around you does not arrive where you are standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *come near* is the ordinary word for approach. It is a promise about proximity, spoken over someone in the middle of a field where others are going down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is spoken over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 1 sets the terms for everything that follows: *the one who lives in the shelter of the Most High, who stays in the shadow of the Almighty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dwelling is the posture. Not visiting in an emergency — living there. The promises in this psalm are addressed to somebody already at home in that place, and the rest of the psalm reads as a description of what that address is like."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it ends.** God takes over the sentence at verse 14 and speaks in the first person — *I will set you free… I will answer… I will be with you in trouble… with long life I will fill your hands.* The One making the promises stops being described and starts speaking. See the note on Psalms 91:14–16."}]}, "Psalms 91:9–10": {"title": "\"No plague will come near your tent\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the first one is the condition the second one rests on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you have made the LORD **your refuge**, the Most High **your dwelling place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and they are not the same."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Refuge** — *machseh*, a shelter you run into from a storm. Somewhere you go when something is happening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dwelling place** — *maon*, a habitation, a lair, a home. Somewhere you live."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's opening line says the same: *whoever **makes a home** in the secret shelter of the Most High.* See the note on Psalms 91:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No evil will overtake you, **no plague will come near your tent**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *plague* is **nega** — a stroke, a blow, a mark. It is the word Leviticus uses of the mark of leprosy, and the word for the strokes God said would fall on Egypt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *your tent* — the older rendering is *your dwelling*. The protection is described around a residence rather than a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A thousand may fall at your side, and ten thousand at your right hand; **but it shall not come near you**.* See the note on Psalms 91:5–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not describe an untroubled world. It describes falling on every side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same phrase turns up in Exodus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The blood shall be a sign for you on the houses where you are. And when I see the blood, I will pass over you, and **the plague shall not be on you to destroy you** when I strike the land of Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same noun, over doorframes, on a night when it was on everybody else. See the note on Exodus 12:1–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who quoted this psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The devil, in the wilderness, and he quoted the two verses immediately following:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He shall give His angels charge over you… in their hands they shall bear you up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He got the words right and left out the clause *to keep you in all your ways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jesus did not correct the psalm. He answered from Deuteronomy: *you shall not tempt the LORD your God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last three verses are the only ones spoken by God rather than about Him:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because he has set his love upon Me, therefore I will deliver him… **He shall call upon Me, and I will answer him; I will be with him in trouble**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**In** trouble. Not instead of it. See the note on Psalms 91:14–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm is doing structurally.** It has three voices — a teacher describing the refuge, a believer saying *I will say of the LORD, \"You are my refuge\"*, and God Himself at the end. The promises are stated, confessed, and then confirmed by the One making them."}]}, "Psalms 92:10–15": {"title": "\"Even in old age they still bear fruit\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two trees, chosen for two different qualities, and a promise about the far end of a life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The palm and the cedar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The righteous will flourish like the palm tree, they will grow tall like a cedar of Lebanon.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the palm** — grows in the desert, fruits for decades, and its trunk bends in a storm without snapping. It also bears its best dates late in life.", "**the cedar** — the great timber tree of the ancient world, slow, enormous, and used to build the temple itself."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "One thrives where nothing should; the other lasts and towers. Between them they cover fruitfulness and endurance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are planted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Planted in the house of the LORD, they will bloom in the courts of our God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *transplanted* — moved from somewhere else and put here deliberately. Neither tree grew where it happened to land."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the location is the point of the psalm: not planted near the house, or visiting it. In it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise about age"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even in old age they still bear fruit; they stay fresh and green.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two words are *fat* and *fresh* — full of sap, still green. It is a horticultural picture, and it is set against everything people expect of the last stretch of a life: drying out, thinning, stopping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fruit is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Singing out that the LORD is upright. He is my Rock, and there is no wrong in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm names its own harvest. The old man still bearing fruit is bearing testimony — still able to say what God is like, out of a long life of finding out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The psalm's title.** *A song for the Sabbath day.* This is the rest-day psalm, and it opens with how good it is to give thanks in the morning and at night. The flourishing it describes is the fruit of a life lived at rest, not of a life spent striving."}]}, "Psalms 95:8–11": {"title": "\"They will never enter My rest\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm turns halfway through, and God speaks in the first person for the rest of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh come, let us sing to the LORD! Let us shout joyfully to the Rock of our salvation… Oh come, let us worship and bow down; let us kneel before the LORD our Maker.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven verses of invitation. Then, without warning, the voice changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not harden your hearts** as at **Meribah**, as on that day at **Massah** in the desert.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two place names, and both are nicknames rather than map locations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Meribah** — *quarrelling, contention*. **Massah** — *testing, proving*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses named the place after what happened there: *he called the name of the place Massah and Meribah, because of the contention of the children of Israel, and because they tempted the LORD, saying, \"Is the LORD among us or not?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the question at the bottom of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rephidim, in Exodus 17. No water, the people ready to stone Moses, and God saying *strike the rock, and water will come out of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They got the water. The place kept the name of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Where **your ancestors tested Me**, tried Me, **though they had seen My work**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they proved Me, they saw My work*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had seen the plagues, the sea, the pillar, the manna. The complaint is not ignorance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forty years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **forty years I grieved over that generation**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *grieved* is to be disgusted with, to loathe. It is a strong word and it is in the first person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And I said, '**Their hearts keep wandering, they do not know My ways**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a people who err in heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**They do not know My ways** — not His acts. They had seen the acts for forty years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **I made a promise in My anger**, '**They will never enter My rest**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I swore in My wrath*, and the form of the sentence is the strongest negative available."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My rest** — **menuchah**, the same word Isaiah uses. See the note on Isaiah 28:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rest was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Canaan. The land, the end of the marching, the place with the wells already dug."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the generation that came out of Egypt did not go in. Only Caleb and Joshua."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer quotes these verses twice, in full, and builds two chapters on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His argument: the word *Today* in the psalm means the offer was still open when David wrote it, four hundred years after Joshua brought them in — so the rest in question is not only the land. See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today, if you will hear His voice, do not harden your hearts.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pivot word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today.* It is in the verse immediately before this passage, and Hebrews hangs the whole case on it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm is built.** An invitation to come and kneel, and then God's own voice naming the last time they were invited and would not. The warning is inside the call to worship."}]}, "Psalms 96:10–13": {"title": "\"Say among the nations, the LORD reigns\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm gives an announcement to make and then hands the singing over to creation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Say among the nations, \"**The LORD reigns.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in Hebrew. It is not a wish or a prayer — it is a statement of what is currently the case, to be said out loud among people who do not know it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The world **stands firm, it cannot be shaken**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is established, fixed, made to stand. The stability of everything is placed downstream of who is reigning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the rejoicing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the **heavens** be glad and the **earth** rejoice; let the **sea** and all it holds resound. Let the **fields** be joyful, and all that is in them; then all the **trees of the forest** will sing for joy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five subjects, and not one of them is a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sky, ground, sea, field, forest. The psalm moves outward and downward and the whole of creation is put in the choir."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they are glad"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before the LORD, for **He is coming, He is coming to judge the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice. And the reason creation rejoices is the judgement — which is not how the word usually lands on us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *judge* is the governing word: to put right, to set in order, to rule a matter. Creation is described elsewhere as *groaning*, waiting; here it is told the One who will set it right is on His way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will judge the world with **righteousness**, and the peoples with **His faithfulness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness and faithfulness — **emunah**, the steady, dependable word, the root of *amen*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first line of it is *sing to the LORD a new song, sing to the LORD, all the earth.* Then *declare His glory among the nations, His marvellous works among all the peoples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a psalm about telling. The whole thing is instruction on what to say and to whom."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it was first sung.** 1 Chronicles 16 records almost this entire psalm as the song David gave Asaph on the day the ark was brought into Jerusalem. It was written for the day the presence came home."}]}, "Revelation 1:12–13": {"title": "Among the lampstands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John turns to look at a voice and sees furniture from the tabernacle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To look at the voice\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I turned around **to look at the voice** that was talking to me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek says it that way — to see the voice. John is not tidying his grammar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Seven lampstands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There they were: **seven lampstands, all of them gold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Exodus there is one lampstand, hammered out of a single talent of gold, with six branches from a central shaft. See the note on Exodus 25:31–40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here there are seven separate stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus identifies them in the same chapter: *the seven lampstands which you saw are the seven churches.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In among them stood a figure like **the Son of man**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not above them, not in front of them. **In the middle of** them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstands in the holy place were the only light in a room with no windows, and He is standing among these."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses later He is described as holding the seven stars in His right hand — but the lampstands He walks among."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Like the Son of man\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is Daniel 7: *one like the Son of Man, coming with the clouds of heaven… and to Him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also the title Jesus used of Himself more than any other, some eighty times in the Gospels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The robe and the band"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His robe ran **the whole length of Him, down to His feet**, and there was **a band of gold around His chest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The full-length robe is priestly — the Greek word is used in the Greek Old Testament for the high priest's garment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the golden band across the chest, rather than at the waist. A working man belted at the waist to move; a band across the chest is not for labour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The work is finished, and He is dressed as the priest who is not hurrying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with the lampstands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 2 opens: *these things says He who holds the seven stars in His right hand, **who walks in the midst of the seven golden lampstands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walking among them. And in the temple, the priest's daily task at the lampstand was trimming the wicks and topping up the oil so the light kept burning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letters that follow are addressed to seven churches, most of them with something seriously wrong, and He is among them all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first thing He says to John.** *Do not be afraid. I am the First and the Last. **I am He who lives, and was dead, and behold, I am alive forevermore.*** John had fallen at His feet as dead, and the hand that holds the seven stars is laid on him."}]}, "Revelation 1:4–6": {"title": "\"He made us kings and priests\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Before a single vision, before the seals and the trumpets, John states what the reader already is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He loved us. With His own blood He washed our sins off us. He made us kings and priests serving God, His own Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three short clauses, all in the past. Loved, washed, made. Nothing here is pending."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the order runs from the inside out: love first, then the cleansing, then the office."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Kings and priests"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Israel those two were kept strictly apart. Priests came from Levi, kings from Judah, and when King Uzziah went into the temple to burn incense he came out a leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Melchizedek held both, and Hebrews makes much of it. Here both are given to ordinary believers in seven small congregations in Asia, some of which the next two chapters will criticise sharply."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to spiritual heroes. Read on and these are the churches that have left their first love, tolerated false teaching, and gone lukewarm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The status is stated at the front, before any of that, and it is never withdrawn during the letters that follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description of Christ"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The witness who never lies, the first to be born out of the dead, the ruler the kings of the earth answer to.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three titles, and they map onto the whole book: the one who tells the truth about what is coming, the one who has already been through death, and the one to whom every earthly power is answerable — which is the thing a persecuted church most needed to hear."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"The One who is here now, who was here before, and who is on His way.\"** John's version of the divine name, stretched across all three tenses, and he puts the present first. Not *was, is, and will be* — but **is**, was, and is coming."}]}, "Revelation 12:10–11": {"title": "The accuser thrown down, and the three things that beat him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A loud voice in heaven announces a defeat, and it names both what the enemy did for a living and what put him out of business."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His job description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who accused our brothers… the one who stood in front of our God accusing them, day and night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what he was doing. Not tempting, in this passage — **accusing**, in front of God, without stopping. The word behind it is a courtroom word, and it is where the title Satan comes from: the accuser, the adversary in a legal sense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job 1 and Zechariah 3 both show him at that work. Zechariah's scene is the clearest: the high priest standing in filthy clothes with Satan at his right hand to accuse him — and God rebuking the accuser and re-clothing the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What beat him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things, and the order is worth keeping:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the Lamb's blood** — the ground of it. Accusation has nothing to work with once the charge has been answered.", "**the message they told** — their testimony, spoken. What was true was said out loud.", "**they would not cling to their own lives even with death in front of them** — people who had stopped being controlled by the last threat available."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is what was done for them. The second and third are what they did with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Now it has arrived\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Salvation, and strength, and the kingdom of our God, and the power of His Christ.* The announcement of the accuser's fall is made in the same breath as the announcement that the kingdom has come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two are treated as one event."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the accusing stopped.** Romans asks *who is going to lay a charge against the ones God Himself has chosen?* and answers by pointing at the one at God's right hand — not accusing, but interceding. Revelation shows the same thing from the other side: the seat the accuser used to occupy is empty. See the note on Romans 8:31–34."}]}, "Revelation 14:1–2": {"title": "The Lamb on Mount Sion", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "After the darkest chapter in the book, John looks and sees somebody standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 13: the beast out of the sea, the beast out of the earth, the mark on the right hand or the forehead, and nobody able to buy or sell without it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the chapter break, and: *then I looked*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was **the Lamb, standing** on Mount Sion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hestos** — standing. The same participle used in chapter 5, where the Lamb stands *as though it had been slain*. See the note on Revelation 5:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not enthroned in this scene. Standing, on a hill, with a crowd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mount Sion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple hill. The psalm says *beautiful in elevation, the joy of the whole earth, is Mount Zion*, and Hebrews uses it as the destination: *you have come to Mount Zion and to the city of the living God.* See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two mountains in that letter — one that could be touched and burned with fire, and this one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **a hundred and forty-four thousand** were with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve squared, times a thousand. The number is introduced in chapter 7, sealed *out of every tribe of the children of Israel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **His Father's name written on their foreheads**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before had a mark on the forehead or the hand, without which nobody could trade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the same location and a different name. Revelation sets the two marks opposite each other by putting them one chapter apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sound"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I heard a sound out of heaven **like a great weight of water falling**, like **a huge roll of thunder**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *like the voice of many waters and like the voice of loud thunder*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are already in the book: chapter 1 describes the voice of the risen Christ as *the sound of many waters*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the sound I heard was **harpists playing their harps**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something with the weight of a waterfall and the force of thunder turns out, on hearing, to be music."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Harps are in the throne room in chapter 5, held by the elders alongside the bowls of incense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The song"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *they sang **as it were a new song** before the throne… and **no one could learn that song except the hundred and forty-four thousand** who were redeemed from the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A song with a restricted membership. Not because it is secret — because it is theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are the ones who follow the Lamb wherever He goes. These were **redeemed from among men, being firstfruits to God and to the Lamb**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Firstfruits** — **aparche**, the first sheaf, which is a guarantee rather than a sample. Paul uses it of Christ. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:23–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three angels with three announcements — the everlasting gospel to every nation, the fall of Babylon, and a warning — and then the harvest of the earth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The placement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the two beasts and the bowls of wrath, John inserts a hill with a Lamb on it and a sound like water."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the two foreheads.** A mark that lets you buy and sell, and a name. The chapters are consecutive, and the second opens with the Lamb standing."}]}, "Revelation 16:11": {"title": "\"They did not turn away\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is a report on what pain did not accomplish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fifth bowl. The bowl judgments are the last series in the book — seven angels, seven bowls, poured on the earth, the sea, the rivers, the sun, the throne of the beast, the Euphrates, and the air."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fourth bowl scorched men with fire; the fifth brought darkness on the beast's kingdom, *and they gnawed their tongues because of the pain*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **cursed the God of heaven** over their pains and their sores.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eblasphemesan** — blasphemed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They know who is doing it. The verse says they cursed **the God of heaven**, which means the source is not in doubt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **did not turn away from the things they were doing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they did not repent of their deeds* — **metanoeo**, to change the mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not the only time. The chapter says it three times, and the sixth trumpet in chapter 9 says it too:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*They **did not repent** to give Him glory.*", "*And they **did not repent** of their deeds.*", "*And men blasphemed God because of the plague of the hail… and they **did not repent**.*", "Chapter 9: *the rest of mankind, who were not killed by these plagues, **did not repent** of the works of their hands.*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four times, after four different judgments."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is reporting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The judgments land, the pain is real, the source is correctly identified, and nobody changes their mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation states it as an observation each time, without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says produces repentance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you despise the riches of His goodness, forbearance and longsuffering, **not knowing that the goodness of God leads you to repentance?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agei** — leads, draws along."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names a different agent from the one operating in Revelation 16, and Revelation records what the other one achieved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one exception in the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 11, after the two witnesses are raised and an earthquake kills seven thousand: *and the rest were afraid and **gave glory to the God of heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only time in the judgment sequences that anyone does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the bowls are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seven golden bowls full of the wrath of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word for bowl — **phiale** — as the golden bowls full of incense held by the elders in chapter 5, which John says are the prayers of God's people. See the note on Revelation 5:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same vessel, two contents."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse records.** Pain, correctly attributed, and no change. Revelation says it four times across two judgment cycles and never once presents it as a surprise."}]}, "Revelation 2:2–3": {"title": "\"I know what you have been doing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the seven letters, and it opens with a page of commendation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *these things says **He who holds the seven stars in His right hand, who walks in the midst of the seven golden lampstands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every letter opens with a description of the speaker taken from the vision in chapter 1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I know what you have been doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oida** — settled knowledge. It opens six of the seven letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven things are commended, and they are all real:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"I know **how hard you have worked**\"* — **kopos**, labour to the point of exhaustion", "*\"and **how much you have taken without giving way**\"* — **hupomone**, remaining under", "*\"You **cannot stomach wicked men**\"*", "*\"When people turned up claiming to be apostles, you **put them to the test and caught them out as liars**\"*", "*\"You have **carried the weight**\"*", "*\"You have **held out**\"*", "*\"You have **worn yourself down for the sake of My name**, and **you have not run out of strength**\"*"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of church this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doctrinally careful, tireless, intolerant of frauds, and still standing after decades of pressure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had spent three years there and warned the elders that *savage wolves* would come in and that *from among your own selves men will rise up*. See the note on Acts 20:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They did what he told them to. They tested and they caught them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The endurance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **hupomone** appears twice in two verses. Peter uses it of the same period, and James says testing produces it. See the note on James 1:3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nevertheless I have this against you, that you have left your first love**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aphekes** — you left, you let go. Not *lost*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole commendation is intact and this is added to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Remember therefore from where you have fallen; repent and do the first works**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three imperatives: remember, repent, do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The first works** — not new ones. The ones they were doing at the start."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Or else I will come to you quickly and **remove your lampstand from its place** — unless you repent.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstand is the church itself; chapter 1 says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they still had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But this you have, that **you hate the deeds of the Nicolaitans, which I also hate**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He agrees with them again, in the same breath as the warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To him who overcomes I will give **to eat from the tree of life**, which is in the midst of the Paradise of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tree nobody has reached since Genesis 3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Praise, one thing, instruction, warning, praise again, promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The complaint occupies one verse out of seven."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the commendation contains.** Work, endurance, doctrinal vigilance, and stamina — all confirmed by the speaker. And the one thing named against them is not on that axis at all."}]}, "Revelation 2:4–5": {"title": "\"Do again what you did then\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The church at Ephesus is commended for a great deal and then told one thing is wrong, and the one thing outweighs the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the verses just before: hard work, perseverance, intolerance of evil, they had tested false apostles and found them out, they had endured hardships for His name and not grown weary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By any external measure this is a strong church. Nothing on that list is withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That love you had at the beginning — you have walked away from it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *your first love*, and the verb is to leave or abandon. Not that it cooled. That they walked off from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doctrine intact, effort intact, discernment intact — and the thing that made it all worth doing has been left somewhere behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three imperatives, and the middle one is the practical one:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**remember** how high you stood before you fell", "**turn back**", "**do again what you did then** — the first works"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell them to work up a feeling. He tells them to do the things they did when the love was fresh. The affection is recovered by returning to the actions that belonged to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *remember* is present continuous — keep remembering, not one act of recollection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will take your lampstand away from where it stands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstand is the church itself, as chapter 1 explains. Not a threat to individuals' salvation — a warning that a congregation which keeps everything except the love stops being a light in that place."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being asked for.** The whole letter is written by the One who chapter 1 describes as walking among the lampstands. He is not asking for more output from a church that is already working hard. He is asking to be loved by it."}]}, "Revelation 22:1–2": {"title": "The river and the tree", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last chapter of the Bible, and the two things in it are the two things Genesis 2 opened with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The river"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he showed me **a river of the water of life**, pure, **clear as crystal**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 2: *now a river went out of Eden to water the garden, and from there it parted and became four riverheads.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Bible opens with a river in a garden and closes with one in a city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Running out from **the throne of God and of the Lamb**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One throne, two occupants. The construction is deliberate and John uses it again in the next verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The river has a source rather than a catchment. It does not collect; it proceeds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The street"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Down the middle of the city's street, **with the river on both sides of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The geometry is difficult to picture, and John does not help. A street with a river on either side, and a tree in the middle of both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel saw something similar — water from under the temple threshold, deepening as it went, *and on the bank of the river, on this side and that, will grow all kinds of trees for food*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stood **the tree of life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 2: *the tree of life was also in the midst of the garden.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 3 closes the access: *He drove out the man; and He placed cherubim at the east of the garden of Eden, and a flaming sword which turned every way, **to guard the way to the tree of life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing between Genesis 3 and Revelation 22 gets back to it. Proverbs uses the phrase figuratively — wisdom is *a tree of life to those who take hold of her* — and the letters to the seven churches promise it: *to him who overcomes I will give to eat from the tree of life*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the guard is gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fruit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It carries **twelve kinds of fruit** and gives its fruit **every month**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve, and monthly. There is no season in which it is not producing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The leaves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the leaves of the tree are for the healing of the nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **therapeian** — the root of *therapy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The nations** — **ethnon**, the peoples. The word that runs through the whole Bible as the outsiders, from the promise to Abraham that *in you all the nations shall be blessed* to the great multitude *from every nation, tribe, people and tongue*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next verse says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **there shall be no more curse**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katathema**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The curse in Genesis 3 fell on the ground: *cursed is the ground for your sake; in toil you shall eat of it all the days of your life.* See the note on Genesis 3:17–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What else is gone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There shall be **no night there**: they need no lamp nor light of the sun, for the Lord God gives them light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And earlier: *God will wipe away every tear from their eyes; there shall be **no more death, nor sorrow, nor crying**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Spirit and the bride say, \"Come!\"… **and let him who thirsts come. Whoever desires, let him take the water of life freely**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dorean** — freely, as a gift, for nothing. The same word Paul uses of being justified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Bible's last offer is a drink, at no cost."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two chapters share.** A river and a tree. Genesis puts a sword between the man and one of them; Revelation puts it in the middle of a street with leaves for the nations."}]}, "Revelation 3:14–15": {"title": "\"The Amen\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last of the seven letters, and the speaker names Himself with a word nobody had used as a title before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Write this to **the angel of the church of the Laodiceans**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laodicea was a banking centre in the Lycus valley, known for a black wool industry and a medical school that produced an eye ointment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three turn up in the letter: *buy from Me gold refined in the fire… white garments… and anoint your eyes with eye salve*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks to a city in its own vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here is who is speaking: **the Amen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Amen** is a Hebrew word meaning *firm, sure, so be it*. It is what a congregation says back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had used it as a name. Here it is a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah comes closest: *he who blesses himself in the earth shall bless himself in **the God of truth*** — and the Hebrew there is *the God of Amen*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second and third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The witness who is **faithful**, the witness who **tells the truth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho martus ho pistos kai alethinos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Martus** — witness, the root of *martyr*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **beginning of everything God made**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **he arche tes ktiseos tou theou** — the beginning, the origin, the ruling principle of God's creation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Arche** carries both senses: what something starts from, and what governs it. Colossians uses the same idea: *He is before all things, and in Him all things consist… **the beginning**, the firstborn from the dead.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul takes the Amen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **all the promises of God in Him are Yes, and in Him Amen**, to the glory of God through us.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 1:15–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God says Yes; the Amen comes back through the people who trust it. Paul puts both halves in one sentence, and here the Amen is a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The assessment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I know what you have been doing**. You are **not cold and you are not hot**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **psuchros** and **zestos** — cold, and boiling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of the seven letters opens *I know*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The preference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I wish you were cold, or else hot**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is a startling sentence. Cold is named as preferable to what they are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The local detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laodicea had no water of its own. It was piped in — hot mineral water from Hierapolis six miles north, cold spring water from Colossae ten miles east."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the time either arrived it was lukewarm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hot springs were medicinal; the cold water was refreshing. Tepid water is the one that is good for nothing, and the next verse says what it provokes: *I will vomit you out of My mouth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said about themselves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because you say, '**I am rich, have become wealthy, and have need of nothing**' — and do not know that you are **wretched, miserable, poor, blind, and naked**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said last"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Behold, **I stand at the door and knock**. If anyone hears My voice and opens the door, **I will come in to him and dine with him**, and he with Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Addressed to a church, from outside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The harshest of the seven letters ends with a meal being offered through a door."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The title He opens with.** The Amen — the word people say back. Paul says every promise gets its Yes in Him and its Amen through us; here the Amen is the one doing the speaking."}]}, "Revelation 5:11–12": {"title": "Every voice there is", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The circle widens, and John gives up trying to count."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Lamb took the scroll, and the four creatures and twenty-four elders went down in front of Him with harps and golden bowls of incense — *and the incense is the prayers of God's people*. See the note on Revelation 5:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They sang the new song: *You are worthy… for You were slain, and have redeemed us to God by Your blood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then John looks again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were standing **all the way around the throne and the creatures and the elders**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Concentric. The elders and creatures are already round the throne; the angels are round all of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The redeemed are nearer than the angels in this scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There were **tens of thousands of tens of thousands** of them, and **thousands upon thousands** more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **muriades muriadon kai chiliades chiliadon** — myriads of myriads and thousands of thousands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel has the same construction at the Ancient of Days: *a thousand thousands ministered to Him, and ten thousand times ten thousand stood before Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not arithmetic. It is the largest phrase the language has."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The volume"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And what they were saying, **at full volume**, was this.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *with a loud voice*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lamb **deserves it all** — the Lamb **who was killed** — the **power**, the **wealth**, the **wisdom**, the **strength**, the **honor**, the **glory**, the **blessing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven items, and one article governing all of them in the Greek — they come as a single package rather than a list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven is the number Revelation uses for completeness throughout: seals, trumpets, bowls, churches, spirits, lampstands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lamb **who was killed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the perfect participle — slain, and standing slain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what John saw when he turned to look at the Lion: *a Lamb as though it had been slain*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The worthiness is attached to that and to nothing else, twice in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the elders sang and what the angels sing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The elders' song is *You have redeemed **us**… and have made **us** kings and priests*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angels' song has no *us* in it. They are not the redeemed, and they do not claim to be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says they want to look into it: *things which angels desire to look into*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **every creature which is in heaven and on the earth and under the earth and such as are in the sea, and all that are in them**, I heard saying:*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Blessing and honour and glory and power be to Him who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb, forever and ever!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The circle widens a third time, to everything that exists."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the four living creatures said *Amen*, and the elders fell down and worshipped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The structure of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sealed scroll and nobody worthy. John weeping. The Lion who is a Lamb. The scroll taken. The elders. The angels. Then all creation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four expanding rings, and the object of every one is the same."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the count breaks down.** John reaches for myriads of myriads and thousands of thousands and stops. The seven things they name are given as one package — and the reason attached is a single perfect participle: slain."}]}, "Revelation 5:5–6": {"title": "The Lion, and the Lamb", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John is told to look at a Lion and sees a Lamb, and the whole book turns on the substitution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he was crying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: a scroll sealed with seven seals, and *no one in heaven or on the earth or under the earth was able to open it*, and John *wept much*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not weeping over a book. Whatever the scroll is, its unopened state is what undoes him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stop crying. Look — the Lion out of Judah's tribe, the Root of David, has won.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two titles, and both are old promises."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Lion of Judah** is Jacob's blessing in Genesis 49: *Judah is a young lion… who would dare rouse him?* See the note on Genesis 49:8–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Root of David** is Isaiah 11: *there shall come forth a Rod from the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Has won\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enikesen** — He conquered, aorist tense. A completed victory, already achieved, reported as news."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John actually sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I looked, and there in the middle of it all… **stood a Lamb**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told Lion. He sees Lamb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a correction — the same Person. But the conquering was done in the other form."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He had been killed, and He was standing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a lamb *as though slain* — bearing the marks of slaughter, and on its feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both facts held together in one clause: the wounds are visible and He is standing. It is the only description of the risen Christ in Scripture that keeps the killing in the picture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven horns and seven eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Horns** are the Old Testament image for power, **eyes** for seeing and knowing, and **seven** is completeness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the eyes are the seven Spirits of God, sent out into every part of the earth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the story started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 3:15, in the garden, to the serpent: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A heel bruised and a head crushed — a wound taken in the winning. That is the Lamb standing with the marks on Him, and it is the first promise in the Bible arriving at its end. See the note on Genesis 3:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The woman's seed at the beginning, the Lion of Judah at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They sing a new song: *You are worthy to take the scroll, for **You were slain**, and have redeemed us to God by Your blood out of every tribe and tongue and people and nation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given for His worthiness is not the Lion. It is the slaying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The scroll.** Nobody in the vision could open it, and John's grief was proportionate to that. Whatever it holds — the title deed of the earth, the unrolling of what is to come — the point of the chapter is that the seals came off in the hands of somebody who had been killed."}]}, "Revelation 5:7–8": {"title": "\"The incense is the prayers of God's people\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He takes the scroll, and the whole throne room goes down — carrying two things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sealed scroll in the right hand of the One on the throne, and an angel asking who is worthy to open it. Nobody in heaven, on earth or under it could."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I wept much, because no one was found worthy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then an elder says *do not weep — the **Lion** of the tribe of Judah has prevailed*, and John turns to look and sees *a **Lamb** as though it had been slain*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence before this passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The taking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **walked up and took the scroll** out of the right hand of the One on the throne.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No permission is asked and none is recorded as given. The Lamb walks up to the throne and takes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the moment He took it**, the four creatures and the twenty-four elders **went down in front of Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately. The taking is what triggers the worship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What each one carried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of them had **a harp**, and **a gold bowl full of burning incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two objects, and both are temple furniture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gold bowls are the **phiale** — the shallow libation dishes from the tabernacle service, listed among the vessels Nehemiah carried back into the storerooms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the incense is the prayers of God's people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says it flatly, and it is the only time in Scripture the equation is made outright."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the incense was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four spices in equal parts, salted, blended by a perfumer, and forbidden for private use. Three of the four give off nothing until they are crushed or burned. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the bowls are full of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is holding them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the people who prayed. The four living creatures and the twenty-four elders — the nearest beings to the throne."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayers have been collected, kept, and are in gold when they arrive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let my prayer **stand before You like incense**, let my lifted hands be the evening offering.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for it as a request. See the note on Psalms 141:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else the bowl appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters on: *another angel, having a golden censer… **and the smoke of the incense, with the prayers of the saints, ascended before God** out of the angel's hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the angel fills the same censer with fire from the altar and throws it to the earth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they sing next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are worthy to take the scroll and to open its seals, for **You were slain, and have redeemed us to God by Your blood** out of every tribe and tongue and people and nation.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The worthiness is stated as a fact about the slaying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is in the bowls.** The prayers themselves — not a report of them, not a record. John sees them in front of the throne, in gold, at the moment the scroll changes hands."}]}, "Romans 1:16–17": {"title": "The power of God for everyone", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The thesis of the whole letter, in two sentences, before Paul has argued anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not ashamed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **not ashamed** of the good news of Christ — not for a moment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing to the capital of the empire, about a Jewish carpenter executed by Rome on a slave's cross. The message had every social reason to be embarrassing, and he opens by naming the embarrassment and dismissing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **the power of God** to rescue everyone who believes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunamis**. Not that the gospel describes God's power, or points to it, or asks for it. It **is** it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same in 1 Corinthians: *the message of the cross is… the power of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To rescue\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **soteria** — the standard word for salvation, and wider in that language than the English suggests."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root is used in the Gospels of a bleeding woman made well, a blind man healed, Peter pulled out of the water, and a girl raised from the dead. Rescue, healing, wholeness, preservation, safety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one word covering all of it, and Paul says the gospel is God's power toward all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who believes — **the Jew first, and also the Greek**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only qualification in the sentence is believing. Not merit, not nationality, not standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being revealed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In it **the righteousness of God is being revealed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not demanded. Revealed — uncovered, disclosed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the hinge of the letter. The law revealed a righteousness God requires; the gospel reveals one God supplies. Paul returns to it in chapter 3: *the righteousness of God apart from the law is revealed… through faith in Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From faith to faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A right standing that comes **from faith and leads on to faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *ek pisteos eis pistin* — out of faith, into faith. It starts there and it goes on there. Nothing switches over to another basis later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The one who is right with God will live by faith.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk 2:4 — five words in Hebrew, quoted three times in the New Testament (here, Galatians 3, Hebrews 10), and the sentence that set Luther off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It can be read *the righteous shall live by faith* or *the one who is righteous by faith shall live*. Paul's argument holds either way, and the letter goes on to make both true."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When Habakkuk said it.** Standing on a watchtower, having complained twice that God was doing nothing about violence and injustice, and being told the answer was coming but not yet. *Though it tarries, wait for it.* The verse the Reformation turned on was written by a man who could not see what God was doing."}]}, "Romans 10:1–4": {"title": "A zeal that runs ahead of knowledge", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul says what he wants, and it is not an argument won."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The desire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **desire of my heart** and **my prayer to God** for Israel is **that they may be saved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *desire* is **eudokia** — good pleasure, the thing he would choose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just written three chapters about Israel's unbelief, and he opens the fourth by saying what he prays."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had said a chapter earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have great sorrow and continual grief in my heart. For **I could wish that I myself were accursed from Christ for my brethren**, my countrymen according to the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not arguing against people he dislikes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The credit he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I can **testify about them that they have a zeal for God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zelos** — zeal, fervour. Paul is not accusing them of indifference, and he knew the zeal from the inside: *concerning zeal, persecuting the church.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But it is **a zeal that runs ahead of knowledge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not according to **epignosis*** — full or accurate knowledge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The energy is real and the information is missing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis, in three clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not **recognizing the right standing that comes from God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And busy **establishing one of their own**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **did not submit themselves** to the righteousness of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *establishing* is **stesai** — to set up, to make stand. It is construction work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"submit\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hupetagesan** — to place oneself under, to be subordinated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Receiving a righteousness that is not yours is described as a submission. Building one of your own is described as the alternative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the three clauses is about immorality."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **Christ is the end of the law**, so that there is **a right standing with God for everyone who believes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **telos** — the end, and also the goal, the finish line, the point a thing was aiming at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both senses are live. Where the road stops, and what it was going toward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"everyone\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul repeats it through the chapter: *everyone who believes*, *whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved*, and *there is no distinction between Jew and Greek, for the same Lord over all is rich to all who call upon Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage about the word being near: *the word is near you, in your mouth and in your heart… **if you confess with your mouth the Lord Jesus and believe in your heart** that God has raised Him from the dead, you will be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which he quotes out of Deuteronomy 30 — the law testifying to a righteousness apart from it. See the note on Romans 3:21–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How then shall they call on Him in whom they have not believed? And how shall they believe in Him of whom they have not heard? And how shall they hear without a preacher?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that opens with a prayer ends by asking who is going to tell them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is wrong in the passage.** Not the zeal, and not the sincerity. The clause Paul keeps circling is *establishing one of their own* — and he calls receiving the other one a submission."}]}, "Romans 10:14–15": {"title": "The chain of questions", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four questions, each one taking a step back from the last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How are they to **call** on the One they have not **believed** in?* *How are they to **believe** in the One they have never **heard** of?* *How are they to **hear** without someone **preaching**?* *How is anyone to **preach** unless they are **sent**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Call ← believe ← hear ← preach ← sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is working backwards from the promise he has just quoted — *whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved* — to find out what has to happen first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chain ends at a sending, and the sending is God's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is arguing this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is about Israel: *my heart's desire and prayer to God for Israel is that they may be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is establishing that the message went out and was heard, so that the failure to believe was not for want of hearing. Three verses later: *have they not heard? Yes indeed — their sound has gone out to all the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He then quotes Psalm 19, about the sky. See the note on Psalms 19:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How beautiful are the feet** of those who carry the good news of peace, who carry the good news of good things!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 52:7, and the setting there is a runner coming over the hills toward a besieged city with news that the siege is lifted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Feet* is the odd part of the compliment. It is not what anybody normally praises."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But if you are waiting for news, the feet are what you see first — coming over the ridge, before you can make out the face or hear the shout."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before it in Isaiah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your God reigns!\"* — that is the message the beautiful feet are carrying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verses after are the beginning of Isaiah 53. The runner with the good news is the paragraph immediately preceding *He was wounded for our transgressions.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Romans 10 says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then **faith comes by hearing**, and hearing by the word of God.* See the note on Romans 10:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **rhema** — the spoken word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what it said just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The word is near you, in your mouth and in your heart… **if you confess with your mouth** the Lord Jesus and **believe in your heart**… you will be saved.* See the note on Romans 10:5–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter runs on speech — mouth, heart, hearing, preaching, and a sound going out to the ends of the world."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Paul is quoting the chain against.** Himself, twenty years earlier. He was the man with the letters to Damascus, and what stopped him was a voice."}]}, "Romans 10:16–17": {"title": "\"Faith comes by hearing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just run a chain of questions — how can they call on someone they have not believed in, believe in someone they have not heard, hear without a preacher — and this is where it lands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So faith comes by hearing, and hearing comes through the word of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two steps, and the direction is fixed. Faith is not summoned up from inside a person; it arrives by a route, and the route starts with something said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in this chain generates their own believing. Something is spoken, it is heard, and faith follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"word\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses **rhema** — a word spoken, an utterance, rather than *logos*. It is the living, said thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why the chain has a preacher in it at all. The gospel in this chapter is something that travels by voice: *how beautiful are the feet of those who bring good news.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The present tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs are present. Faith *comes*, hearing *comes* — ongoing, repeatable, not a single event in the past."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the mechanism does not stop working after conversion. Faith arrives the same way at every stage: something is heard."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse before it.** *But not all of them welcomed the good news.* Paul is honest that the same word is heard and refused. Hearing in the physical sense is not the whole of it — Isaiah's complaint, which Paul quotes, is that the message was announced and not believed. The route is reliable; the response is still a response."}]}, "Romans 10:5–8": {"title": "\"The word is near you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul lets the two righteousnesses speak in their own voices, and they sound nothing alike."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses describes the righteousness that comes from the law like this: \"**The person who does these things will live by them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 18. One verb, and everything hangs on it: **does**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a harsh statement. It is simply the arrangement, stated plainly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second one speaks differently"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the righteousness that comes from faith **speaks a different way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul personifies it. Faith-righteousness has a voice, and the first thing out of its mouth is a prohibition on two questions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two forbidden questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not say in your heart, 'Who will go up into heaven?'**\" — that is, to bring Christ down — \"**or, 'Who will go down into the deep?'**\" — that is, to bring Christ up from among the dead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are attempts to fetch. One reaches up, one reaches down, and both assume the thing needed is somewhere else and has to be retrieved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul rules out both, and gives the reason in each case as a completed event. Christ has already come down. He has already come up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nothing to go and get."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The word is near you, in your mouth and in your heart.**\" That is the word of faith that we preach.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is quoting Deuteronomy 30, where Moses says the commandment *is not too mysterious for you, nor is it far off. It is not in heaven… nor is it beyond the sea… but the word is very near you, in your mouth and in your heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two locations. The mouth first, then the heart — and Paul keeps Moses' order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you **confess with your mouth** the Lord Jesus and **believe in your heart** that God has raised Him from the dead, you will be saved. For with the heart one believes unto righteousness, and **with the mouth confession is made unto salvation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states it twice, mouth and heart both times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *confess* is **homologeo** — to say the same thing, to agree out loud. It is the verb behind *the High Priest of our confession* in Hebrews 3. See the note on Hebrews 3:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"saved\" covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sozo** and **soteria** are wider than the English. The same words are used in the Gospels of a woman made well, a blind man healed, a sinking man pulled out of water, and a girl raised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rescue, healing, wholeness, preservation. It is one word doing all of that work."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter goes.** *For whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved* — Joel — and then Paul's chain of questions: how shall they call on Him in whom they have not believed, and how shall they believe in Him of whom they have not heard, and how shall they hear without a preacher? The whole passage is about speech, from beginning to end."}]}, "Romans 10:9–13": {"title": "The mouth and the heart", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two organs, named twice, in two different orders — and Paul does that on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order changes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If your **mouth** confesses the Lord Jesus, and your **heart** believes that God raised Him from the dead, you will be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then immediately:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is with the **heart** that a person believes… and it is with the **mouth** that confession is made.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "First mouth then heart; then heart then mouth. He is not being careless. He is tying them together so tightly that neither can be lifted out alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Why the first order at all? Because he is quoting Deuteronomy 30 two verses earlier — *the word is near you, in your mouth and in your heart* — and he keeps the prophet's sequence before explaining it in his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is confessed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord Jesus.* In a Roman province where *Caesar is Lord* was the civic formula, saying it about a crucified Galilean was not a private sentiment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what is believed is specific: *that God raised Him from the dead.* Not a general trust in God. One historical claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is open to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is no difference here between Jew and Greek, for the same Lord is Lord of all, and He is generous to all who call on Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *generous* is literally *rich toward* — the wealth language Paul uses everywhere. And then the quotation that closes it: *everyone who calls on the name of the Lord will be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone. It is from Joel, and Peter used the same line at Pentecost."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the list.** No requirement to clean up first, no probation, no ceremony. A confession and a belief, from anyone, of any nation, calling on a name. Paul spends the next verses insisting that someone therefore has to go and tell them."}]}, "Romans 11:1–4": {"title": "\"He pleads with God against Israel\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks the hardest question of the letter and answers it with himself, then with a prophet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Has God **pushed His people away**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aposato** — thrust away, repudiated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters of argument about Israel's unbelief have led here, and Paul puts the obvious conclusion as a question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Certainly not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me genoito** — Paul's strongest negative, which he uses ten times in this letter. *May it never be.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am an Israelite myself, from the seed of Abraham, **from the tribe of Benjamin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the exhibit. If God had cast off Israel, Paul would not be writing the letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Benjamin** — the smallest tribe, and the one Saul the king came from. Paul had been named after him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whom **He knew beforehand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proegno** — foreknew. Paul has used it in chapter 8 of individuals; here it is of a nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not know what the Scripture says **in the passage about Elijah**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in Elijah* — the rabbinic way of citing a section by its subject, the way one might say *in the bush* for Exodus 3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How **he pleads with God against Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **entunchanei kata** — intercedes **against**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for intercession, pointed the wrong way. That is Paul's own description of what Elijah is doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elijah said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **they have killed Your prophets and torn down Your altars, and I am the only one left**, and they are after my life.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said it twice at Horeb, word for word, before and after the wind, the earthquake, the fire and the whisper. See the note on 1 Kings 19:9–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every fact in it is true except the last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **what was God's answer to him?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chrematismos** — a divine response, an oracle. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks what the answer was, which means he expects the reader to have noticed that one was given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven thousand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have kept for Myself seven thousand men** who have not bowed the knee to Baal.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *I have left for Myself* — the reserving is God's act, stated in the first person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been there the whole time, through the drought, through Jezebel's purge, while Elijah was on Carmel and while he was under the broom tree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Obadiah's hundred prophets in two caves were among them. See the note on 1 Kings 18:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with it next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the same way, at this present time also, there is **a remnant chosen by grace**.* See the note on Romans 11:5–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the number and turns it into a doctrine."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul notices about the passage.** Not that Elijah was wrong about the facts. That the direction of his intercession was *against* — and that God answered by naming seven thousand men Elijah had never met."}]}, "Romans 11:13–16": {"title": "The first handful, and the root", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two images in one verse, and both come from the same place in the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first handful of dough"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the first handful of dough is holy, so is the whole lump.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 15 required the first of the ground meal to be offered as a contribution. Once that portion was given, the rest of the batch was reckoned holy — set apart, usable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's logic runs from the part to the whole: what is true of the first is true of what follows it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The root and the branches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the root is holy, so are the branches.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same argument, different picture — and this one he will spend the next ten verses on: an olive tree, natural branches broken off, wild branches grafted in, and a warning to the wild ones not to be arrogant about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are making his point about Israel: the patriarchs are the root, and what happened at the root is not undone by what happened to some branches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage is actually about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject is not personal blessing. It is whether God has finished with Israel, and Paul's answer is a flat no — *their being received back* would be *life from the dead*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He also says something disarming about his own motives: he makes much of his work among Gentiles partly hoping to provoke his own people to jealousy and save some. An apostle admitting to an ulterior motive is not something the New Testament does often."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On lifting the first line out.** *If the first handful is holy, so is the whole lump* is sometimes applied to giving — set apart the first portion of income and the rest is blessed. The image comes from an offering, so the association is understandable, but Paul's own use of it here is about Israel and the patriarchs, and that is what the sentence is arguing."}]}, "Romans 11:5–6": {"title": "\"Otherwise grace would not be grace at all\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes Elijah's seven thousand and turns them into a definition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the same way, **at this present time also**, there is a remnant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah's seven thousand were a live example, not an illustration. Paul says the same thing is running now. See the note on Romans 11:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **remnant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **leimma** — what is left over. The Hebrew equivalent runs through Isaiah: *a remnant shall return*, which Isaiah made his son's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's own point is that a remnant is not a failure. It is what survives, and it is always there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who chose it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Chosen **by grace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to the election of grace*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a remnant who chose better. A remnant selected, and the selecting agent is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The either/or"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **if it is by grace, then it is no longer on the basis of works**; otherwise **grace would not be grace at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **if it is on the basis of works, then it is no longer grace**; otherwise **work would not be work**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states it in both directions, symmetrically, in two clauses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is arguing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that works are bad. That the two words mean incompatible things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Charis** is a gift; **ergon** is a wage. A gift that has to be earned is a wage under another name, and a wage that is given free is not a wage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mixing them destroys both words, and Paul says so about each in turn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts it the same way in Romans 4: *now to him who works, the wages are not counted as grace but as debt.* See the note on Romans 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elijah did not know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said *I am the only one left*, twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven thousand had not bowed. He had no way of knowing and had not been told until he asked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's point is that the reserving was going on invisibly, and that this is what grace does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The olive tree — branches broken off, a wild branch grafted in, and the warning: *do not boast against the branches… **you do not support the root, but the root supports you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *for God has committed them all to disobedience, that **He might have mercy on all**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, the depth of the riches both of the wisdom and knowledge of God! How unsearchable are His judgments and His ways past finding out!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For of Him and through Him and to Him are all things, to whom be glory forever. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters on the hardest question in the letter, and it ends in a doxology rather than a conclusion."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The test in the sentence.** Whether the word still means what it means. Paul does not argue that grace is better — he argues that if anything is added, there is no longer anything to call grace."}]}, "Romans 15:1–4": {"title": "What strength is for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent a chapter on Christians who disagree about food and holy days — the strong who can eat anything, the weak who cannot. This is his conclusion, and it is addressed to the winners of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those of us who are strong have something to do with that strength, and it is not to please ourselves. It is to carry the weaknesses of those who are not strong.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul counts himself among the strong. He agrees with them on the substance — he has already said no food is unclean in itself. And then he tells them that being right is not the end of the matter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is to *bear* — to pick up and carry. Not tolerate. Carry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ Himself never lived to please Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the proof he offers is a quotation from Psalm 69 — *the insults of those who insulted You fell on Me*. It is a strange verse to choose until you see the logic: the one with every right to be pleased took what belonged to others instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything that was written in earlier days was written to teach us, so that through the patience and the comfort the Scriptures give, we might go on holding to hope.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just quoted the Old Testament to settle a first-century church dispute about dinner, and then says why he can: the old writings were for our instruction. Note what he says they produce — patience, comfort, and hope. Not primarily information."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it sits here.** This is Paul's last word on a quarrel about secondary things, and his solution is not a ruling. He tells the people who are right to use their freedom on behalf of the people who are not, and points at Christ for the pattern. It is the most practical thing in the chapter."}]}, "Romans 16:17–20": {"title": "\"Under your feet\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last warning in Romans, and the last promise, in four verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who to watch for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mark out those who **cause divisions and lay down stumbling blocks** contrary to the teaching you have learned, and **stay away from them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things they do: split, and trip."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *stumbling blocks* is **skandalon** — the trigger stick of a trap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How to recognise them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are **serving their own appetites**, and with **smooth words and flattering speech** they deceive the hearts of the unsuspecting.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two marks: what they are actually serving, and how they talk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *smooth words* is **chrestologia** — literally *good-speech*, plausible talk. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they deceive *the unsuspecting* — the Greek is the innocent, those who think no evil of anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two kinds of knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be **wise about what is good**, and **innocent about what is evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *innocent* is **akeraios** — unmixed, unadulterated. It is used of undiluted wine and unalloyed metal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says the same thing with different animals: *be wise as serpents and harmless as doves* — the same word for harmless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Expertise in one direction and not the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the God of peace will be quick to crush Satan under your feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew original is in Genesis 3:15 — *He shall bruise your head* — and Paul is plainly reaching for it. See the note on Genesis 3:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But note what he does with the pronouns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The head is crushed **under your feet**, and the one doing the crushing is **God**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not you, and not without you. Both are in the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The God of peace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title chosen for a verse about crushing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it six times, and it is the name he attaches to this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Quick\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en tachei** — shortly, speedily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul was writing to a church in the capital of an empire that would kill many of them, and the Roman church waited a long time for anything that looked like this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A warning about divisive men, a promise about Satan underfoot, and then grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter is otherwise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 16 is mostly a list of names — twenty-six of them greeted personally, ten of them women, including Phoebe who probably carried the letter, and Priscilla and Aquila who *risked their own necks* for Paul."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning sits in the middle of the warmest chapter he wrote."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whose feet.** *The God of peace will crush Satan **under your feet**.* Paul could have written *under His feet* and been entirely orthodox. He wrote the other one."}]}, "Romans 16:25–27": {"title": "\"Able to hold you steady\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last sentence of Romans, and it is one long clause held open until the final word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now to Him who is **able to hold you steady**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sterixai** — to fix firmly, to establish, to set in place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word Jesus used to Peter — *when you have turned back, **strengthen** your brothers* — and the word Peter uses of what God does after suffering. See the notes on Luke 22:31–34 and 1 Peter 5:10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul opened the letter wanting to do it himself: *I long to see you, that I may impart to you some spiritual gift, **so that you may be established**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixteen chapters later he attributes the establishing to God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "According to what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In keeping with **the good news I carry** and **the preaching of Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The steadying is measured by a message. Not by effort, and not by conditions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My gospel** — Paul calls it his twice elsewhere. He means the one he was given to carry, not one of his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mystery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*According to the revelation of **the mystery that was held in silence through ages beyond counting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **musterion** — not a riddle, but something that could not be worked out and had to be disclosed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Held in silence** — the Greek is *kept secret*. Paul uses the same idea in Ephesians and Colossians: *the mystery which has been hidden from ages and from generations, but now has been revealed*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disclosure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But is **now brought out into the open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phanerothentos** — made manifest, brought into the light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "By what means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And, **through the writings of the prophets**, according to the commandment of the everlasting God, **made known to all nations for the obedience of faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The odd clause is *through the writings of the prophets*. A secret kept silent through the ages is now published — and the publishing runs through documents that had been available the whole time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent the letter doing exactly that: Habakkuk, Genesis, the Psalms, Isaiah, Hosea, Deuteronomy, quoted at every turn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase that bookends the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the obedience of faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He used it in the opening paragraph: *through whom we have received grace and apostleship for **obedience to the faith among all nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 1 and chapter 16, the same phrase, closing the circle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "To whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **God, the only wise One**, be glory **through Jesus Christ** forever. Amen.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter has been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin, law, faith, Abraham, no condemnation, the Spirit, Israel, the olive tree, living sacrifices, the weak and the strong, and a chapter of greetings naming twenty-six people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it ends on a doxology about being held steady."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the doxology sits in the manuscripts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some early copies place it at the end of chapter 14 or chapter 15 rather than here. The variation is old and has been noted since antiquity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wherever it is placed, it is the last word Paul gives the letter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being celebrated at the end.** Not the argument. Paul closes sixteen chapters of the tightest reasoning in the New Testament by pointing at the One able to hold the reader steady, and attributing the ability to the message rather than to the reader."}]}, "Romans 2:1–5": {"title": "\"It is God's kindness that is leading you to a change of mind\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent chapter 1 describing a society in freefall, and every reader nodding along is the person chapter 2 is written to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So you have no defence, whoever you are, when you sit in judgment on someone else. In the very act of judging another person you condemn yourself, because you who judge are doing the same things.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first word is *therefore*. Everything in chapter 1 was setting this up. Paul built a list his audience would agree with, and then turned and said: the agreeing is the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you think so lightly of the riches of His kindness and patience and restraint?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for God's forbearance, and each is a treasury word — *riches* of them. Kindness, patience, and holding back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is not that the judgmental person has broken more rules. It is that they have **misread what the kindness was for**, treating a delay in judgement as either approval or absence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the kindness is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not realising that it is God's kindness that is leading you to a change of mind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb means to lead by the hand, to conduct somewhere. Kindness is described as the thing doing the leading, and the destination is repentance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth stating plainly, because it is the reverse of how the process is usually imagined. Not: repent, and then God will be kind. His kindness is what brings a person round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But with a hard and unchanged heart you are storing up wrath for yourself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same treasury image, turned over. Both the kindness and the wrath are being accumulated; which one a person ends up drawing on depends on what they did with the first."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the argument goes.** Paul is on his way to *all have sinned*, and he gets there by closing every exit — the pagan in chapter 1, the moralist here, the religious in the rest of chapter 2. Nobody is left standing, which is exactly the ground he needs for chapter 3."}]}, "Romans 3:19–20": {"title": "Every mouth stopped", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul closes the prosecution and states what the law was actually for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters of indictment. The Gentile world in chapter 1, the moralist in chapter 2, the Jew in chapter 3 — and then a chain of six Old Testament quotations:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is none righteous, no, not one… their throat is an open tomb… their mouth is full of cursing and bitterness… there is no fear of God before their eyes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most of the quotations are about the mouth. Then Paul draws his conclusion about mouths."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the law addresses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever the law says, **it says to those who live under the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to those in the law*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is heading off the obvious objection. The quotations he has just stacked up are from Israel's own Scriptures, addressed to Israel — so the people most confident of their standing are the ones being described."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The point is that **every mouth would be closed** and **the whole world would stand accountable to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *closed* is **phrasso** — to stop up, to fence, to muzzle. It is used of damming a stream and of stopping the mouths of lions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Accountable** is **hupodikos** — a legal term for a defendant liable to judgment, with no defence left to enter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is a courtroom where the case has been made and nobody has anything to say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **no human being will be put right with Him by doing what the law requires**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by works of the law no flesh shall be justified in His sight*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul repeats it in Galatians in almost identical words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law does instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What the law does is **show us what sin is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *through the law is the knowledge of sin* — **epignosis**, full recognition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the cure. The diagnosis."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it again in chapter 7: *I would not have known sin except through the law. For I would not have known covetousness unless the law had said, \"You shall not covet.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law is not accused of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything. Paul defends it repeatedly — *the law is holy, and the commandment holy and just and good*, and *do we then make void the law through faith? Certainly not! On the contrary, we establish the law.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is about function, not quality."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The very next word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But now.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But now **the righteousness of God apart from the law is revealed**… even the righteousness of God, through faith in Jesus Christ, **to all and on all who believe**. For **there is no difference**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters of prosecution, one sentence of *every mouth stopped*, and then *but now*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Being **justified freely by His grace** through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *freely* is **dorean** — as a gift, without cause, for nothing. It is the word used of hating *without a cause*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where boasting goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is boasting then? **It is excluded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same idea as the closed mouth, four verses on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the silence is for.** Paul does not stop the mouths to leave the room quiet. The next thing in the chapter is *but now* — and the sentence after the courtroom is about a gift."}]}, "Romans 3:21–22": {"title": "\"But now\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two words turn the letter around."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they come"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After three chapters of indictment, six quoted verses about throats and tongues and mouths, and a courtroom where every mouth is stopped and the whole world stands accountable. See the note on Romans 3:19–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *but now*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nuni de** — but now, at this present moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a change of era as much as a change of subject. Paul uses the same phrase at the turns of his arguments elsewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is apart from what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Apart from **the law altogether**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **choris nomou** — separately from law, without reference to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not law softened, satisfied by degrees, or graded on a curve. Set aside as the mechanism entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has appeared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **rightness from God** has been **brought out into the open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dikaiosune theou** — the righteousness of God. It is the theme Paul announced in chapter 1: *in it the righteousness of God is revealed from faith to faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Brought out into the open** — the verb is perfect passive, made manifest and standing manifest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the law and the prophets have been testifying to it all along**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having said *apart from the law*, Paul immediately says the law testifies to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law cannot produce it and has been pointing at it the whole time. He proves the point in the next chapter with Abraham and David, both from the Old Testament. See the note on Romans 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The channel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is a rightness from God that comes **through faith in Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not through effort, descent, or ritual."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two prepositions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it comes **upon all** and **to all** who believe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it twice with different prepositions, which is not an accident of translation — the Greek has both **eis** and **epi**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The levelling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **no distinction between one person and another**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ou gar estin diastole** — there is no difference."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it here about who can receive it, and he has just said it in verse 22–23 about who needs it: *for all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase does both jobs. No difference in the need, no difference in the offer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Being **justified freely by His grace** through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus.* See the note on Romans 3:23–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Freely** is **dorean** — as a gift, gratuitously, for nothing. It is the word used of being hated *without a cause*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is boasting then? **It is excluded**. By what law? Of works? No, but **by the law of faith**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What has changed at \"but now.\"** Not the verdict on anybody — Paul restates it in the next verse. What has appeared is a righteousness that was never on offer through the mechanism he has just closed."}]}, "Romans 3:23–24": {"title": "Two sentences, and the word between them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted diagnosis in the Bible, and it is only half of the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it usually stops"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone has sinned. Everyone falls short of the glory of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted alone, that is a verdict. But in Greek it is not the end of the sentence — the participle that follows carries straight on into the next clause without a full stop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And everyone is put right with Him as a gift, freely, by His grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same *everyone* governs both halves. Paul builds the universal charge and then applies the universal remedy in the same breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Falls short\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about coming up behind, lacking, being left wanting. It is the word used in Hebrews for someone missing out on God's grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *broke a rule*. Failed to reach — which is a description of distance rather than of crimes committed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three words for how it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul stacks them because one is not enough:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**as a gift** — the word means gratuitously, without cause in the receiver. It is the same word Jesus uses in John for being hated *without reason*.", "**freely**", "**by His grace**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is closing every door through which a contribution might be smuggled back in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ransom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through the ransom paid in Christ Jesus that bought us out of slavery and set us loose.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Apolutrosis** again — the release accomplished by paying a price. See the note on Ephesians 1:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gift is free to the person receiving it. It was not free."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next.** Paul says God set Christ forth publicly as the mercy seat — the same word the Greek Old Testament uses for the lid of the ark. What was hidden behind a curtain and approached once a year with blood, Paul says was displayed in the open. See the note on Exodus 25:17–22."}]}, "Romans 4:13–15": {"title": "Two ways to be an heir, and why they cannot both be true", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is arguing about Abraham, and his case rests on a point of chronology anyone can check."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise to Abraham came in Genesis 15. The law came at Sinai, four hundred and thirty years later. Circumcision itself came a chapter after the promise, not before it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So whatever made Abraham an heir, it cannot have been law-keeping — the law did not exist yet. Paul makes this argument at length through the whole chapter, and it is unanswerable on its own terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the two cannot be combined"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the people of the law are the heirs, then faith has been drained of meaning and the promise has been cancelled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two strong verbs. Faith is *emptied* — made hollow, void of content. The promise is *abolished*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's point is not that law is bad but that the two systems cannot run at once. A promise received by faith and an inheritance earned by performance are different kinds of thing, and running the second empties the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"What the law brings about is anger\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is wrath. And Paul gives the mechanism in the same breath: *where there is no law there is no breaking of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is not accused of causing sin. It is described as the thing that turns wrongdoing into transgression — a crossing of a marked line — and so brings the matter under judgement. He develops this at length three chapters later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the promise actually was.** *That he would inherit the world.* Genesis promised Abraham a land, descendants, and blessing to all nations. Paul reads the whole thing as one inheritance and states it at its widest — which is the scale he thinks the promise was always working at."}]}, "Romans 4:16–17": {"title": "\"Calls things that do not exist as though they already do\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul explains why God set the whole thing up on faith rather than on performance, and then describes the God being believed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it rests on faith"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is why it rests on faith — **so that it can be a matter of grace**, and so that **the promise stands guaranteed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two reasons, and the second is the practical one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A promise conditional on my keeping is only as certain as my keeping. Move it onto faith and it moves onto grace, and a promise resting on grace has nothing shaky underneath it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *guaranteed* is **bebaios** — firm, valid, the word used of a legally binding document."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not only for those who have the law, but **for those who share the faith of Abraham**. He is **the father of us all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line is drawn by faith, not by descent. Paul has already pointed out that Abraham was declared righteous while still uncircumcised — before the mark, before the law, before anything that would exclude anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things God does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The God who **gives life to the dead** and **calls things that do not exist as though they already do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul picks these two out of everything he could have said about God, because both are what Abraham's situation required."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gives life to the dead** — Abraham's body *as good as dead*, Sarah's womb *dead as well*, in Paul's own words four verses later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Calls things that do not exist as though they already do** — the Greek is to call, to name, to summon. God speaks of a thing in the present tense while it is still nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where God did that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have made you a father of many nations**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Genesis 17, and the tense is the point: *I have made* — past, completed, said to a childless man of ninety-nine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the same breath God changed his name. **Abram** means exalted father. **Abraham** means father of a multitude."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had one son by a servant girl and no son by his wife. From that day everybody in the camp had to call him *father of a multitude*, every time they wanted his attention, for a year before Isaac was born. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God said it first. The name came before the child, the promise before the evidence, and Abraham's part was to answer to the name he had been given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is our father **in the sight of the God he believed**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Genesis 1 sounds like beside this.** *Then God spoke: \"Let there be light.\" And light was.* The same pattern, and Paul reaches for it as a description of who Abraham was dealing with. See the note on Genesis 1:3–5."}]}, "Romans 4:18–19": {"title": "Hoping against hope", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul describes what Abraham did with the facts, and it is not what the facts suggested."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **nothing left to hope for**, Abraham went on **hoping and believing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *against hope, in hope he believed* — **par' elpida ep' elpidi**. Two kinds of hope in one clause, and they are set against each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was no hope available from the situation. He held a different one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he refused to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **refused to dwell on the facts stacked against him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **katanoeo** — to fix the mind on, to consider closely, to study. It is the same word used in Hebrews 3 for *think hard about Christ Jesus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So it is not that Abraham was unaware. Paul is precise about what he did with the information: he would not stare at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The facts he would not stare at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists them, and he does not soften them either:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*his own body **as good as dead**, since he was about a hundred years old*", "*and **Sarah's womb dead as well***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the first is **nekroo** — to put to death, to make a corpse of. Paul's own vocabulary is blunter than most translations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two dead things, named. That is the raw material."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he looked at instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before tells you: *the God who gives life to the dead and calls things that do not exist as though they already do.* See the note on Romans 4:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had two dead things in his hand and a promise about them, and he attended to the One who made it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So he became the father of many nations\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Exactly as he had been told: \"**That is how many your descendants will be.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Genesis 15 — the night God took him outside and showed him the stars."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the name he had been given, *father of a multitude*, was already true in the telling before it was true in the household. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not claimed here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul never says Abraham felt confident, or that his circumstances improved, or that he stopped being a hundred years old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His body stayed as it was until it did not. What changed first was where he was looking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hebrews says Sarah did.** *Sarah herself was given the strength to conceive… **because she counted the One who promised as good for it**.* The Greek is a reckoning word — she assessed His credit and found it sound. She had laughed at the door of the tent, and the New Testament puts her in the roll of faith anyway. See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}]}, "Romans 4:20–22": {"title": "\"He grew strong in faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four things said about Abraham in two verses, and each one is worth its own line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He did not waver"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **did not waver at God's promise in unbelief**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diakrino** — the same verb behind *discerning the body* in 1 Corinthians 11 and *does not doubt* in Mark 11. It means to be divided, to be in two minds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not split. He was not holding the promise in one hand and the arithmetic of his own age in the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He grew strong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **grew strong in faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a passive — he was strengthened. Something was done to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And note the direction. Twenty-four years passed between the first promise and this chapter, and his faith did not wear down over them; it was built up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He gave glory to God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **gave glory to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before the son. Before any evidence at all. Glory is given for something not yet received."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He was fully convinced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fully convinced that **what God had promised, God was able to do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plerophoreo** — carried full, filled to capacity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And look at what he was convinced of. Not that it would be easy, or when it would happen, or how. That God was **able**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is why **it was credited to him as rightness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul comes back to Genesis 15:6, which is the sentence this whole chapter has been unpacking. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness was entered on his account — and the thing entered against it was believing that God could do what God said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was not on the ledger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not his obedience, which was real but patchy. Not his record — he lied about his wife to two kings, and took Hagar because the promise seemed slow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's whole argument is that this is the point: *to him who does not work but believes on Him who justifies the ungodly, his faith is accounted for righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Justifies the ungodly.** Paul's phrase, three verses into the chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who else it was written for.** *Now it was not written for his sake alone that it was imputed to him, but for us also.* Paul's next sentence. The ledger entry made in Genesis 15 was recorded as a precedent, and the account it applies to is the reader's. See the note on Romans 4:23–25."}]}, "Romans 4:23–25": {"title": "\"Raised so that we could be put right\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent the chapter on Abraham, and here he says why he bothered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not written down for his sake alone\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words \"**it was credited to him**\" were not written down for his sake alone. They were written **for ours as well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence Paul is quoting is Genesis 15:6 — *Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *credited* is **logizomai**, a bookkeeping word: to reckon into an account, to enter on a ledger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham had nothing to put in. It was entered anyway, and Paul says the entry was recorded in Scripture as a precedent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What we believe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It will be credited **to us who believe in the One who raised Jesus our Lord from the dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham believed God could bring life out of a dead womb — *he did not consider his own body, already dead… or the deadness of Sarah's womb.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The object of faith in both cases is a God who raises the dead. Paul makes them the same faith."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **handed over because of our wrongdoing**, and He was **raised so that we could be put right with God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two events, two prepositions, and both are *because of* in the Greek."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Handed over **because of** our sins — they were the reason He went to the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Raised **because of** our justification — and that is where the sentence gets interesting. Young's Literal keeps it: *raised up because of our being declared righteous.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which way round"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *raised so that we might one day be justified*. **Because** we were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The debt was settled at the cross, and the resurrection is what happened after the settlement — not the means of it but the proof of it. He went in carrying the sins and came out without them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If anything had been left owing, the tomb would still be shut. The empty tomb is God's receipt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is in this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 4 is Paul's case that righteousness has always been credited rather than earned — from Abraham, who was declared righteous *while still uncircumcised*, four hundred and thirty years before the law existed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends by extending the same entry to the reader, and then chapter 5 opens: *therefore, having been justified by faith, we have peace with God.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Abraham did not have.** A law, a temple, a priesthood, a Bible, or a synagogue. He had a promise and a night sky, and the ledger entry was made on the spot."}]}, "Romans 4:6–8": {"title": "The blessing David sang", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul calls in a second witness, and the witness is a psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why David is here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's argument runs on Abraham: *Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul then reaches for a second case, four hundred years later, from a different kind of man. Abraham was the founder; David was a king who had a man killed to cover an adultery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The person **to whose account God puts righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logizomai** — to reckon, to credit, to enter in a ledger. Paul uses it eleven times in this one chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a bookkeeping word, and the entry is made by somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualifier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Apart from **anything accomplished**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *apart from works*. Paul has already set it up in verse 4: *now to him who works, the wages are not counted as grace but as debt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wages are owed. This is not that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 32, and Paul quotes it in full — three clauses, and every one is passive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Whose wrongs are lifted off\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nasa** — to lift, to carry away. The word used of the scapegoat bearing the iniquities into the wilderness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Whose sins are buried out of sight\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kasah** — covered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Against whom the Lord counts no wrong\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is the one Paul is really after, because it uses his word: *the Lord does not **impute** iniquity*. Same verb, **logizomai**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses about what is done with the sin, and one about what is not written down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The happiness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, **the happiness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **makarios** — blessed, happy. It is how the Beatitudes open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in two verses, at the front of each sentence, which is the Hebrew word order of the psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Psalm 32 is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's own account of the year he said nothing:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When I kept silent, my bones grew old through my groaning all the day long. For day and night Your hand was heavy upon me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I acknowledged my sin to You, and my iniquity I have not hidden… **and You forgave the iniquity of my sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the opening of it, and the psalm behind the quotation is a man describing what the silence cost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The point Paul makes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Does this blessedness then come upon the circumcised only, or upon the uncircumcised also?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His whole use of the two witnesses turns on chronology: Abraham was counted righteous in Genesis 15, and circumcised in Genesis 17. Fourteen years apart, in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul concludes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now it was not written for his sake alone… **but also for us**. It shall be imputed to us who believe in Him who raised up Jesus our Lord from the dead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ledger entry is described as transferable, and the reason given is the resurrection."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the three clauses have in common.** Nobody in them does anything. Lifted off, covered, not counted — and Paul picks the psalm precisely because the man singing it had nothing to plead."}]}, "Romans 5:1–2": {"title": "Peace with God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The verse turns on its first word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Therefore\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four chapters of argument stand behind it — all have sinned, none is righteous, the law shuts every mouth, and then righteousness credited to Abraham and to everybody who believes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in this sentence is the conclusion of that, not a fresh claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Having been put right\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since we **have been put right with God** through faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is aorist passive — done, in the past, to us. Not being put right, and not hoping to be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent chapter 4 on the ledger entry: *it was credited to him as righteousness*, and *not written for his sake alone, but for ours also.* See the note on Romans 4:23–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peace with God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **at peace with God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not peace *from* God, or the feeling of peace. **Peace with** — the ending of a hostility."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses war language about the previous state without embarrassment: *when we were enemies we were reconciled to God through the death of His Son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The treaty was signed at the cross and this verse reports its terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Through our Lord Jesus Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The peace is not a mood that can be lost on a bad morning. It runs through a Person, and Paul names Him as the channel in the same breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Brought in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through Him we have also been **brought in**, by faith, **to this grace in which we now stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *brought in* is **prosagoge** — access, introduction. It is the word for being presented at a royal court by somebody with the standing to do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody introduces himself to a king. The word assumes an escort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In which we now stand\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense — we have stood and are standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace is described as a place you are on your feet in, not a favour you keep applying for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The overflow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **our joy overflows in the hope of the glory of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to boast, to exult."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the object is *the glory of God* — the very thing chapter 3 said everybody had fallen short of. Paul turns the deficit into the expectation, and the distance between the two is what the first four chapters were for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any condition, any maintenance, any qualification. Justified, at peace, brought in, standing, and exulting — five settled things in two verses, and every one of them arrives through the same Person."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes immediately after.** *And not only that, but our joy holds even in our sufferings.* Paul establishes the ground before he mentions the pressure, and the pressure never gets a vote on any of the five. See the note on Romans 5:3–5."}]}, "Romans 5:12–14": {"title": "Two men and two acts", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul traces everything back to one man and then names who the man was a picture of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sin made its way into the world **through one man**, and **death came in on sin's heels**, and so death came through to everyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three steps: one man, sin enters, death follows, and it reaches everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *made its way in* is to enter alongside — sin came in as a gate-crasher."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proof he offers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sin was in the world **before the law was given**, but **sin is not charged to anyone's account where there is no law**. **Yet death reigned from Adam to Moses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Paul's argument and it is worth following slowly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between Adam and Sinai there was no written law. On his own accounting, nothing could be formally charged to anybody's account in that period."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And people died anyway, for two and a half thousand years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So death was not being handed out for individual breaches of a code. Something else was in operation, and Paul says what it was: one man's act, reaching everybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Even over those who had not sinned in the same way Adam disobeyed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam broke a stated command with a stated penalty. Nobody between him and Moses was in that position, and they died too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Adam is a pattern of the One who was coming**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **typos** — a stamp, an impression, a die."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam is described as the mould, and Christ as what it was made for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the comparison goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the chapter runs it out: *as by one man's disobedience many were made sinners, so also **by one Man's obedience many will be made righteous**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mechanism is the same in both directions. Adam's act reached people who were not consulted; Paul says Christ's works the same way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole argument, and it is why he needed the Adam-to-Moses point first — to establish that an act of one man can be credited to people who did not perform it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Much more\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five times in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul never argues that grace merely balances the damage. Every comparison is weighted: *much more the grace of God and the gift by the grace of the one Man, Jesus Christ, abounded to many.* See the note on Romans 5:15–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where sin increased, grace overflowed all the more.* See the note on Romans 5:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the objection Paul knows is coming: *shall we keep on sinning, so that there will be all the more grace?*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one word the chapter turns on.** *Reigned.* Death reigned from Adam to Moses; sin reigned in death; and then *those who receive abundance of grace and the gift of righteousness **will reign in life** through the One, Jesus Christ.*"}]}, "Romans 5:15–17": {"title": "\"Reign in life\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul compares Adam and Christ and then insists the comparison does not balance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not two of a kind\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The free gift and the trespass are not two of a kind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just drawn the parallel and now he breaks it. The two sides look symmetrical and are not — the second is larger, and he says so three times with *much more*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the asymmetry is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sharpest line is the middle one:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The judgment followed a **single** trespass and brought condemnation, but the free gift followed **many** trespasses and brought a verdict of righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sin was enough to bring the verdict down. The gift had to deal with an accumulation — every trespass since — and it still came out the other side with an acquittal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The switch nobody expects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If by the trespass of the one man **death reigned**… much more will those who receive the overflow of grace and the gift of being put right with God **reign in life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The symmetry would require: death reigned, so life will reign. Paul does not write that. He says **they** will reign — the people, not the abstraction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Death is a monarch in his sentence. Grace does not merely replace it with a better monarch; it puts the formerly condemned on the throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Those who receive\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one active verb on the human side of the passage. Not those who achieve or maintain — those who **receive**, and what they receive is described twice as overflowing and as a gift."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"in life\" means.** Not after it. Paul says reign *in life* — the sphere is this one. The whole argument since chapter 3 has been that the standing is given now, which is why he can put the reigning in the present tense of a Christian's existence."}]}, "Romans 5:18–19": {"title": "One man, and then one Man", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's argument here runs on a single comparison, and everything depends on the two sides being the same shape."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| through one man | through the One | |---|---| | one trespass | one act of righteousness | | condemnation to everyone | the setting-right that is life | | the many were **made sinners** | the many will be **made righteous** |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb sits on both sides — *kathistemi*, to appoint, constitute, place into a category. Not treated as. **Made.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the symmetry matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody chose to be made a sinner by Adam's act. It arrived by connection, not by decision, and the effects were real long before anyone understood them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul insists the second half works identically. The righteousness comes by the obedience of the One, into a group described in exactly the same terms. If the first transfer was real, so is the second — that is the whole force of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"One act of righteousness\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is singular and concrete: one righteous deed. Paul does not point at Christ's whole life of obedience in general but at a single act, and in this letter that is the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against one trespass, one obedience. The books are balanced by matching pieces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So also through the obedience of the One the many will be made righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The righteousness on offer is not a reward for the reader's obedience. It comes by *His* obedience, credited across the same way the trespass was."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that follows.** Paul says the law came in so that the trespass would increase — *but where sin increased, grace increased all the more.* He is not trying to make sin look small. He builds it as high as it goes and then says grace went higher."}]}, "Romans 5:20–21": {"title": "\"Grace overflowed all the more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul says what the law was for, and it is not what most people assume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law **came in alongside**, so that **the trespass would increase**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *came in alongside* is **pareiserchomai** — to slip in beside, to enter as a secondary thing. It is not the main line of the argument; it comes in next to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the purpose clause is startling: *so that the trespass would increase.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Paul's own account of what the law was given to do. Not to reduce sin — to make it abound, to bring it out into the open where it could be seen for what it is. He says the same thing in chapter 7: *sin, taking opportunity by the commandment, produced in me all manner of evil desire.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But where sin increased, **grace overflowed all the more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huperperisseuo** — Paul stacks *huper* (over, beyond) on top of a word that already means to abound. Superabounded. He appears to have built the word for this sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever height sin reached, grace is described as clearing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reigns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as **sin reigned in death**, **grace might reign through righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs are the same — to reign as a king. Paul is describing two thrones, and only one of them can be occupied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin's reign operated *in death*. Grace's reign operates *through righteousness*, and its outcome is *eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what grace reigns **through**. Not through leniency, and not by overlooking anything. Through righteousness — which had to be supplied, and was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question this provokes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul knows exactly what the next objection is, and he puts it himself at the top of the very next paragraph:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What shall we say, then? Shall we keep on sinning, so that there will be all the more grace?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody asks that question of a message about trying harder. It is the question grace always draws, and the fact that Paul has to answer it is a sign he had preached grace the way he says he did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer is not a rule. It is a funeral: *we died to sin; how can we go on living in it any longer?* See the note on Romans 6:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter has been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of Romans 5 is two men — *by one man's disobedience many were made sinners, so also by one Man's obedience many will be made righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam's act reached everyone born after him without their doing anything. Paul's argument is that Christ's act works the same way, and *much more*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The phrase he keeps using.** *Much more.* Five times in this chapter. Paul is not arguing that grace matches what Adam did. He argues at every step that it exceeds it."}]}, "Romans 5:3–5": {"title": "The chain that ends in hope", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four links, and the last one is the point of the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our joy holds **even in our sufferings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *sufferings* is **thlipsis** — pressure, being squeezed. It is a word about weight from outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say the pressure is good, or sent, or enjoyable. He says the joy holds inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suffering works **endurance** into us, and endurance works **a character proved in the testing**, and a character proved that way works **hope**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**endurance** — *hupomone*, remaining under, staying put under weight", "**proven character** — *dokime*, the word for metal that has been through the assay and passed. Tested and found genuine.", "**hope**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chain runs one way and each link produces the next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Because we know\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **we know** that suffering works endurance into us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a settled knowledge. What makes the joy possible is not a feeling about the pressure but information about where the pressure leads."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hope that does not embarrass"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And that hope **does not put us to shame**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that it does not disappoint, does not humiliate. Hope that turns out to be misplaced makes a fool of the person who held it — Paul says this one will not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the love of God has been poured out and has flooded our hearts**, through the Holy Spirit who was given to us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what secures the hope. Not the character built in the previous verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chain of endurance and proven character is real, and it is not the guarantee. The guarantee is love already poured out — the Greek is a flooding, and the tense is completed and standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word \"hope\" means here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Elpis** — not wishing. It is confident expectation of something good, and it is the New Testament's normal word for what a Christian has about the future."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul defines the object of it in the verse before: *our joy overflows in **the hope of the glory of God**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the paragraph is anchored"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 1: *since we have been put right with God through faith, **we are at peace with God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 2: *through Him we have also been brought in, by faith, **to this grace in which we now stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The suffering paragraph sits on top of a settled peace and a standing position, and Paul establishes both before he mentions pressure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what comes three verses later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While we were still sinners, Christ died for us.* See the note on Romans 5:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proof of the love that floods the heart is dated to the cross, not to how the suffering turns out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul had been through by the time he wrote this.** Stoned and left for dead at Lystra, run out of three cities, beaten with rods at Philippi. The chain in these verses is not theory he arrived at from a desk."}]}, "Romans 5:6–8": {"title": "Love proved at the cross", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul dates God's love by when it acted, and the timing is the whole argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While we were **still without strength**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **asthenes** — weak, powerless, sick. It is the word used of invalids in the Gospels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *while we were doing our best*. Without strength."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At **just the right time**, Christ died for the ungodly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to the season* — at the appointed moment, on schedule."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about it was a rescue improvised when things got bad enough. It happened at the hour it was set for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the ungodly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul chooses the strongest word he has. Not the struggling, not the sincere. The ungodly — those without reverence for God at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same word in chapter 4 about the God *who justifies the ungodly*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The human comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is a rare thing for anyone to die even for **a righteous person**, though for **a good person** someone might possibly dare to die.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is careful here, and slightly wry. For a merely upright man — the sort you respect and do not warm to — almost nobody would. For a genuinely good man, someone might just about work up the nerve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the top of what human love manages, and Paul says even that is uncommon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But God **puts His own love for us on display** in this: **while we were still sinners**, Christ died for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is present tense and continuous — He goes on demonstrating it. The cross is described as an exhibit that stays up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While we were still sinners.* Not after we repented, not once we improved. Still."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the demonstration is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is making a case about certainty. The verses either side are about hope:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hope does not disappoint, because the love of God has been poured out in our hearts by the Holy Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after: *much more then, having now been justified by His blood, **we shall be saved from wrath through Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs from the harder thing to the easier. If He did that for enemies, the rest follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase Paul keeps using"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Much more.* Five times in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never argues that grace merely matches the damage. At every step he says it exceeds it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the cross settles about God's disposition.** Not that He loves us now that something has changed — that He loved us when nothing had. The demonstration is dated to the worst possible moment on purpose, so that no later moment can be worse."}]}, "Romans 5:9–11": {"title": "\"How much more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul builds an argument twice, in the same shape, and the shape is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If, while we were enemies, we were reconciled to God through the death of His Son, **how much more**, now that we have been reconciled, shall we be saved by His life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is an argument from the harder case to the easier one. If the difficult thing has already been done under the worst conditions, the lesser thing is not in doubt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it twice in three verses, and it runs all through this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the harder case was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While we were enemies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already said it two verses earlier — *while we were still sinners, Christ died for us* — and he keeps escalating the description: weak, ungodly, sinners, enemies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reconciliation happened at the worst point of the relationship. That is the foundation the whole argument stands on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Saved by His life\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is deliberate. The reconciling was accomplished by His **death**; the saving that follows is by His **life**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not dead now, and Paul treats that as the guarantee of everything still to come. Hebrews makes the same move — *He always lives to intercede for them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The odd word at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We rejoice in God through our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the boasting word. Paul has spent two chapters demolishing every ground for human boasting, and then permits exactly one: boasting **in God**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the salvation, or the security, or the change in circumstances. In Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What has already been said in this chapter.** *Since we have been put right by faith, we have peace with God… and we stand in this grace.* Standing is the posture Paul assigns to a person who has received it — which is why the *how much more* argument can be made at all."}]}, "Romans 6:1–2": {"title": "\"Shall we go on sinning?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks the question his own preaching provokes, and the fact that he has to ask it tells you what he had been preaching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shall we **keep on sinning, so that there will be all the more grace**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just written *where sin increased, grace overflowed all the more.* This is the obvious next move, and Paul makes it himself before anyone else can."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody ever asks this question of a message about behaving better. It only arises if the grace preached was extravagant enough to sound dangerous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is accused of it elsewhere too: *why not say — as we are slanderously reported and as some claim that we say — \"Let us do evil that good may come\"?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the charge, and Paul reports that people were making it about him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Certainly not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me genoito** — the strongest negative available, roughly *may it never be*. Paul uses it ten times in Romans."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the answer is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *because you will lose your salvation*. He does not say *because God will punish you*. He does not reintroduce a rule, or a threat, or a condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks a different question back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **died to sin**; how can we go on living in it any longer?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *we should die to sin*. Not *we are dying to sin daily*. Past tense, done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is about impossibility of a sort — the absurdity of a dead man continuing to keep house in the place he was carried out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul spends the rest of the chapter on that one fact: buried with Him, raised with Him, the old man crucified, freed from sin. And only after all of it does an instruction appear — *count yourselves dead indeed to sin, but alive to God.* See the note on Romans 6:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reckoning comes after the reality, and is based on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which way round the chapter runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every command in Romans 6 arrives after a statement of what is already true. Paul never asks anyone to die to sin in order to become dead to it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says at the end of the chapter.** *For sin shall not have dominion over you, **for you are not under law but under grace**.* The reason given for sin not ruling is the very thing the objector thought would let it rule."}]}, "Romans 6:12–14": {"title": "\"Sin shall not be your master\" — a promise, not a warning", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Almost everyone reads this backwards, and the grammar is what gives it away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb tense nobody points out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For sin shall not be your master, because you are not under law but under grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek that is a **future indicative** — a plain statement of what will be the case. It is not an imperative. Paul is not commanding *do not let sin master you*; he already gave that instruction two verses earlier, and he used a different form to do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sentence is not a rule with a penalty. It is a promise, and it comes with a reason attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason is the whole point"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what he gives as the ground of the promise:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["not *because you are trying hard*", "not *because you have dealt with that sin*", "**because you are not under law but under grace**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means Paul is claiming something most people find hard to believe: being under grace is what breaks sin's power, and being under law is what leaves it in place. He says it plainly enough that the next verse has him answering the obvious objection — *shall we sin because we are under grace?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That objection only makes sense if he really did say what he seems to have said. Nobody raises it against a preacher of rules."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"master\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Kurieuo* — the verb from *kurios*, lord. To have lordship over, to be the one whose word settles things. Paul is not talking about whether a believer ever sins. He is talking about who is in charge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That distinction matters when the verse is read by someone who has just failed. The sentence does not say sin will never touch you. It says sin will not be the one giving orders — and it locates the reason for that entirely outside their performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it asks of the reader"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction in the two verses before this one is worth reading in the same breath: *hand yourselves over to God as people brought from death to life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *become* people brought from death to life. As people who already are. Everything Paul asks here is asked on the basis of something he says has already happened, and the promise at the end is the reason he can ask it at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"under grace.\"** It is a position, not a mood — the same construction as *under law*. Paul is describing which of two authorities a person lives under, and the entire argument of chapters 5 to 8 turns on the claim that a Christian has changed jurisdictions. That is why the promise can be stated as fact."}]}, "Romans 6:22–23": {"title": "The wage and the gift", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two nouns, and the whole difference is in which of them you are owed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sin **pays a wage**, and the wage is death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **opsonia** — a soldier's pay, the rations and coin issued at the end of a stint. It is what a man has earned and can demand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is given death. It is drawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God **gives a gift**, and the gift is life that has no end.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **charisma**, from **charis** — grace. A thing handed over free, with no work behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul deliberately does not use the same word twice. He could have written *the wages of sin is death, and the wages of God is life*, and the sentence would have collapsed. The two halves are not the same kind of transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zoe aionios** — and it is not primarily about duration. The New Testament's word for endless time is doing something else here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus defines it in John 17: *this is eternal life, that they may know You, the only true God, and Jesus Christ whom You have sent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is God's own kind of life, given to a man, and the fact that it does not run out is one of its properties rather than its definition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Christ Jesus\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last three words locate it. The gift is not posted separately; it is in a Person, and it comes with Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Having been **set free from sin** and made slaves of God, you have your fruit, and it leads to being made holy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the state Paul is describing the gift arriving into. Freed first, and then the fruit follows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *made slaves of God* is the strong word — bondservants. Paul's picture is not neutrality; a man is owned either way, and the question is by whom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the chapter exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 6 opens with the objection: *shall we continue in sin that grace may abound?* — which is the question grace always provokes, and Paul's answer is not to add rules."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a funeral: *we died to sin; how shall we live any longer in it?* You cannot go back to a house you were carried out of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The most quoted half.** *The wages of sin is death* is preached far more often than the clause after it, and the clause after it is where the sentence is going. Paul wrote one sentence with a *but* in the middle."}]}, "Romans 6:8–11": {"title": "\"Once for all and never again\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes his case about the believer by first making it about Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is true of Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ, having been raised from the dead, **dies no more**; **death no longer has any mastery over Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two negatives, both permanent. Whatever death had a claim on, that claim is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The death He died"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The death He died was **a death to sin, died once for all and never again**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ephapax** — once, once and for all, decisively. Hebrews uses the same word repeatedly: *once for all He offered up Himself*, *by one offering He has perfected forever*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not die to sin repeatedly, and there is no arrangement by which He does it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The life He lives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the life He lives is a life lived to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present tense. That is what He is doing now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the same way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the same way, **count yourselves** dead indeed to sin, but alive to God through Jesus Christ our Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything said about Him in the three verses before is what the reader is told to reckon about himself. The pattern is not aspirational — it is the same set of facts, applied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Count\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logizomai** — the bookkeeping word. To reckon, to enter into the account, to regard as so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not mean to pretend, and it does not manufacture the fact. An accountant reckons what is already in the ledger. Paul has spent seven verses putting the figures there before asking anyone to add them up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the reckoning sits in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's order through Romans 6 is fixed and never reverses:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1. *we died to sin* — stated 2. *buried with Him… raised* — stated 3. *our old man was crucified with Him* — stated 4. *Christ dies no more* — stated 5. **then**: *count yourselves dead indeed to sin* 6. **then**: *do not let sin reign in your mortal body*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command in verse 12 is the first instruction in the chapter, and it arrives eleven verses after the facts it rests on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Through Jesus Christ our Lord\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase locates it. Not dead to sin in yourself, examined on your own terms — dead to sin *in Him*, which is the position the whole chapter has been describing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"dead to sin\" does not mean.** Not that sin has stopped existing or knocking. Paul says *do not let it reign* in the very next verse, which would be a strange thing to say to someone it could no longer approach. Dead to sin is about jurisdiction — who has a claim — not about whether the old master still calls round."}]}, "Romans 7:1–3": {"title": "A marriage ended by death", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops and makes sure of one legal principle before he builds anything on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law has authority over a person **only for as long as that person is alive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it flatly, and he says it to people who *know how law works* — Jewish readers in Rome who would grant the point without argument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A law can speak to the living. It has nothing to say to a corpse. No court prosecutes a dead man, and no statute binds one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The illustration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By law she is bound to her husband while he lives; but if her husband dies, she is released from the law that bound her to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law that bound her did not change. It was not repealed, softened, or amended. It is exactly as binding as it was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She simply is no longer inside its jurisdiction, because a death occurred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No adulteress at all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She is **no adulteress at all** if she marries another man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul presses the same case twice — once with the husband living, once with him dead — so that nobody can mistake which fact does the work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same act is a crime in one paragraph and blameless in the next. Nothing about the woman changed. A death changed everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse applies it: *you were made dead to the law through the body of Christ, so that you could belong to Another.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note who died in Paul's application. Not the law — us. *Through the body of Christ*, which is the phrase he will use again over a table three letters later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is still the law, holy and just and good, and Paul says so a few verses on. It simply has no claim on somebody who died and was raised."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why he pauses to establish this.** Chapter 6 has just been answering *shall we go on sinning that grace may abound?* Paul's answer is not tighter rules. It is a funeral: you died with Him, and what you are joined to now is a Person, not a code. See the note on Romans 7:4–6."}]}, "Romans 7:13": {"title": "Sin shown for what it is", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it answers the second objection in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **did something good become death to me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just said the commandment *meant for life* turned out to bring death, and then called the law *holy and just and good*. See the note on Romans 7:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two statements sit awkwardly, and Paul knows it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Certainly not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Me genoito** again — the second time in seven verses, and the tenth in the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The culprit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word, and the whole weight of the chapter rests on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What sin was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sin **showing itself for exactly what it is**, producing death in me **through what is good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *that it might appear sin*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commandment was the instrument, and Paul says so plainly — **through what is good**. He does not soften the mechanism to protect the law; he protects the law by naming the other party."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **through the commandment sin might be exposed as utterly, immeasurably sinful**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kath' huperbolen hamartolos** — sinful beyond measure, hyperbolically sinful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul stacks the intensifiers, as he does elsewhere: *far beyond anything we ask*, and *the weight of it was far past anything our strength could carry*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law is for, in this verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exposure. Not correction, not enablement, and not condemnation of the person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commandment is described as the thing that makes sin visible in its true size — and Paul says that was the intent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said it in chapter 5: *the law entered that the offence might abound.* See the note on Romans 5:20–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words for what the law reveals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Epignosis** in chapter 3 — full recognition: *by the law is the knowledge of sin*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And here, that it might *appear* — **phane**, be shown, come to light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is not arguing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That the law made him worse. He is arguing that it made something already there identifiable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *I would not have known covetousness unless the law had said, \"You shall not covet.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **we know that the law is spiritual, but I am carnal, sold under sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the long passage about doing what he does not want, and the cry. See the note on Romans 7:18–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the chapter exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of it had been *blameless* concerning the law's righteousness by his own account, and describes himself here as a man who found out what was underneath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the exposure is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in Romans 7 offers a remedy. That is the point of the chapter — it ends *O wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *I thank God — through Jesus Christ our Lord!*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on trial in the verse.** Not the law. Paul takes the charge, refuses it, names sin instead, and says the exposure was the object — that sin be shown at full size, using something good to do it."}]}, "Romans 7:18–20": {"title": "The most quoted description of failure in the Bible", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*The good I want to do, I do not do; the evil I have no wish to do is the evil I go on doing.* Anyone who has tried to live a good life recognises the sentence immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence it starts from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I know that **nothing good lives in me — that is, in the old nature**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it about himself, flatly, and he had more to boast about than most: *circumcised the eighth day, of the tribe of Benjamin, a Hebrew of Hebrews, concerning the law a Pharisee… concerning the righteousness which is in the law, blameless.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That man says there is nothing good in his flesh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not despair and it is not fishing for reassurance. It is a settled verdict, and it is the most freeing sentence in the chapter — once it is agreed, there is nothing left to defend and nothing left to produce."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it puts a man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job spent thirty chapters building a case for his own righteousness, and God's one charge out of the whirlwind was *would you condemn Me so that you could be in the right?* Two positions, and only one of them is habitable. See the note on Job 40:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already taken the other one. A man who has stopped expecting good out of his own flesh can be handed a righteousness that is not his — which is the whole argument of the letter: *the gift of righteousness*, *received*, *apart from works*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **wanting** is present and the **doing** is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wanting is right there within reach; the doing of it is not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a claim about willpower. The problem being described is not ignorance of what is right or reluctance to want it. It is the gap between the wish and the act — which is exactly the gap the law cannot close, because a rule can tell you what to do and cannot make you do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends in a cry — *who will rescue me?* — and an answer: *thanks be to God, through Jesus Christ our Lord.* And then chapter 8 opens with *there is now no condemnation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 7 is not a resting place in Paul's argument. It is the wall he builds so that the door in chapter 8 has something to be a door in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What chapter 8 says about the same man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you are not living out of the old nature; you are living in the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes it eight verses into the next chapter, about the same people who have just read chapter 7. It is a statement of location, not a description of how the week has gone. See the note on Romans 8:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The flesh is still there — Paul never says otherwise — and he says it is not where they are."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is not.** It is not a description of the normal Christian life. It is the sound of a man trying, in his own strength, to keep something holy — *I delight in the law of God according to the inward man*, and losing every time. Paul writes it to bring you to the end of that, so that the first line of the next chapter can land: *there is therefore now no condemnation to those who are in Christ Jesus.*"}]}, "Romans 7:4–6": {"title": "Released to belong to Another", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul applies the marriage illustration, and the application does not go where the illustration seemed to be heading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who died"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were made **dead to the law** through the body of Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the picture, the husband died and the wife went free. In the application, the one who dies is **you**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not saying the law was abolished. He says the party under obligation was removed from its reach by death — a death that happened *through the body of Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So that you could belong to Another\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not so that you could belong to nobody. The release is not into independence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him who was raised from the dead* — and the language is marital, carried straight over from the three verses before. The point of the funeral is the wedding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sinful passions **that the law kept stirring up** were at work in every part of us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Paul's most uncomfortable sentence about the law and he states it without softening: the passions were stirred **by** the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He explains it later in the chapter — *I would not have known what coveting was if the law had not said, do not covet* — and defends the law itself as holy. The fault is not in the statute. But a prohibition, read by the old nature, works like a lit match."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And what they produced was death.* That is the fruit of the first marriage. The second one bears fruit *for God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two fruits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same verb for both — **karpophoreo**, to bear fruit — and the difference is in the preposition:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*fruit **to death*** (verse 5)", "*fruit **to God*** (verse 4)"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither marriage is barren. The question in the passage is not whether there will be fruit but who it goes to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the fruit-bearing in verse 4 is stated as the purpose of the union, not as a condition of it: *that you should be married to another… **that** we should bear fruit to God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"That which once held us fast has been put to death\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb behind *held us fast* is to hold down, to confine — the word used for a prisoner in custody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something that had a grip on us was killed, and the release follows from the killing. Nobody wriggled free."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two ways of serving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we serve in the **newness of the Spirit** and not in the **old way of the written code**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Serving is in both halves. Paul never offers a release from service; he distinguishes what the serving runs on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *written code* is **gramma**, letter — the same contrast he draws in 2 Corinthians 3: *the letter kills, but the Spirit gives life.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter does next.** It gets worse before it gets better — the whole of *what I want to do I do not do*, and *who will deliver me from this body of death?* Then the answer, and then chapter 8: *there is therefore now no condemnation.* Paul takes the long way round on purpose."}]}, "Romans 7:7–8": {"title": "What the commandment uncovered", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks the question his argument invites, and defends the law in the same breath as describing what it did to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is **the law itself sin**? **Certainly not**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me genoito** — may it never be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just said the passions were *stirred up by the law* and that believers are *delivered from the law*. Somebody was going to ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the contrary, **I would never have recognized sin except through the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *I would not have known sin*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *would not have sinned*. Would not have known."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I would not have known **what wanting-what-is-not-mine was**, if the law had not said, **\"You shall not covet.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenth commandment. Paul reaches for the only one of the ten that is entirely internal — no court can try it, and nobody else can see it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who said he was *blameless* concerning the righteousness in the law names the commandment that got past his record."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **sin found its opening in that very commandment** and **stirred up in me every kind of craving**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *opening* is **aphorme** — a base of operations, a bridgehead. It was a military term for the place a campaign is launched from. See the note on Romans 7:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prohibition became the staging ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dormancy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Apart from the law, **sin lies there as good as dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *sin is dead* — inert, not roused."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not saying it is absent. He says it is lying still until something wakes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the paragraph protects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law, every time. He says it twice more before the chapter turns:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **the law is holy, and the commandment holy and just and good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *we know that **the law is spiritual**, but I am carnal, sold under sin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three defences in a dozen verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he puts the fault"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **sin — sin showing itself for exactly what it is**.* See the note on Romans 7:13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul repeatedly personifies sin in this chapter as an agent with intentions: it finds an opening, it deceives, it kills, it works."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the parallel in Genesis is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A command, a suggestion about the command, and a death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses **exapatao** for what sin did — *deceived me* — and it is the verb used of the serpent: *the serpent deceived me, and I ate*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For what I am doing, I do not understand… the good that I will to do, I do not do.* See the note on Romans 7:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the cry, and then chapter 8: *there is no condemnation now — none at all.* See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says the law does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By the law is **the knowledge of sin**.* See the note on Romans 3:19–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same conclusion, stated flatly in the earlier chapter and worked out autobiographically here."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two things held together.** The commandment is holy, and it is what sin used as a bridgehead. Paul states both and puts the blame on the second party — not on the statute, and not on the giver of it."}]}, "Romans 7:9–12": {"title": "When the commandment came", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells the story of his own encounter with one commandment, and the ending is a defence of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was a time when I was **alive apart from the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *I was alive without the law once*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not describing innocence. He is describing a period when the commandment had not yet landed on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrival"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the commandment came**, and **sin sprang to life**, and **I died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses in sequence, and the middle one is the surprise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *sprang to life* is **anezesen** — revived, came back to life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin is described as the thing that woke up. Paul does not say the commandment created it; he says it was there and dormant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which commandment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names it two verses earlier: *I would not have known covetousness unless the law had said, \"**You shall not covet**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenth. The only one of the ten that is entirely internal — nobody can see it, and no court can try it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The very commandment that was **meant for life** turned out, **in my case**, to bring death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the commandment which was unto life, this I found to be unto death*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus had said it: *you shall therefore keep My statutes and My judgments, **which if a man does, he shall live by them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer was life. Paul reports the outcome in his own case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For sin **found its opening in the commandment**, **deceived me**, and through it **killed me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *opening* is **aphorme** — a base of operations, the place an expedition starts from. It was a military word for a bridgehead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin used the prohibition as its launching point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Deceived me** — **exapatesen**, thoroughly deceived. It is the word used of the serpent in the garden: *the serpent deceived me, and I ate.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern is the same. A command, a suggestion about it, and a death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the law is holy**, and **the commandment is holy and right and good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just said it killed him, and this is his conclusion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three adjectives, and the whole point of the paragraph is that none of them is being withdrawn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again in the next verse: *has then what is good become death to me? Certainly not! But **sin**, that it might appear sin, was producing death in me through what is good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fault is located and it is not in the statute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For what I am doing, I do not understand… the good that I will to do, I do not do.* See the note on Romans 7:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the cry: *O wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then chapter 8: *there is no condemnation now — none at all.* See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul had been before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Concerning the righteousness which is in the law, blameless.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who says *I died* had a perfect external record."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the paragraph protects.** The law. Paul describes it doing him lethal damage and then spends the last verse insisting it is holy, right and good — and puts the blame on what used it as a bridgehead."}]}, "Romans 8:1–4": {"title": "\"None at all\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence that follows the worst chapter in Paul's letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 7 ends *O wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me from this body of death?* — and then *I thank God — through Jesus Christ our Lord!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 8 opens on the next line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, **there is no condemnation now — none at all** — for those who are in Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katakrima** — the sentence passed after a verdict. Not the accusation and not the trial. The punishment handed down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Now\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nun** — at this present moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not eventually, and not at the judgment. Now, while the struggle described in chapter 7 is still going on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For those who are **in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The location is the qualification. Paul's whole letter turns on the phrase, and it does not describe a standard of behaviour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two laws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For in Christ Jesus **another law is at work** — **the law of the Spirit, the law of life** — and that law has **set me free from the law of sin and death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the law repealed. A second one operating, and the stronger of the two winning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *set free* is aorist — it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law could not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What **the law could not accomplish**, **weakened as it was by the old nature**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul locates the failure precisely, and it is not in the law. He has already said *the law is holy, and the commandment holy and just and good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The weakness is in the material it was working on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God did instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God accomplished Himself: **He sent His own Son in the likeness of that sin-claimed humanity**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in the likeness of sinful flesh* — **homoioma**. Paul chooses the word carefully: real humanity, and not sinful."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentencing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there, **dealing with sin, He passed sentence on sin right where sin had made its home**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *He condemned sin in the flesh* — the same root as the **condemnation** in verse 1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condemnation that is not on the believer went somewhere. Paul says where."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did this so that **everything the law rightly asks might be fully met in us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the righteous requirement of the law might be fulfilled **in** us* — passive. Not *by* us."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The walk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **walk not according to the old nature but according to the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It appears twice, at the end of verse 1 and again at the end of verse 4, framing the paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Neither death nor life, neither angels nor rulers nor powers… has the strength to cut us off from the love of God.* See the note on Romans 8:38–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No condemnation at the top, no separation at the bottom, and the whole chapter in between."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What has changed between the chapters.** Nothing about the man. Chapter 7's *what I hate, that I do* is not resolved by improvement — Paul answers it with a location, and then says there is no sentence there."}]}, "Romans 8:12–14": {"title": "A debt Paul says you do not owe", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul opens with a strange half-sentence, and the strangeness is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence he does not finish"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are in debt — but not to the old nature, as though we owed it a life lived on its terms.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says who they are **not** indebted to, and then never says who they are. The sentence is left open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read what he has just spent eleven verses on and the missing half is obvious: if anyone is owed, it is the God who did what the law could not. Paul leaves the reader to finish it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"putting to death\" is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If by the Spirit you go on putting the body's doings to death, you will live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in the grammar keep this from becoming grim self-effort. It is continuous — *go on* doing it, not a single crisis. And it is **by the Spirit**, which names who is doing the work through them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already ruled out the alternative at length: the law could not manage it, and neither could he, as chapter 7 records in painful detail."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line that follows.** *All who are led by the Spirit of God are the sons of God* — and then Paul goes immediately to adoption, Abba and inheritance. He raises the leading in order to prove sonship, not to test it. See the note on Romans 8:15–17, which is the same argument continuing."}]}, "Romans 8:15–17": {"title": "Abba, and the word Paul made up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two words in this passage are worth stopping on. One is Aramaic, and one Paul appears to have coined himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Paul built"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit who places you as a son with full standing* — behind that phrase is one Greek word, **huiothesia**. It is not a normal word. Paul takes the word for son and the word for placing and puts them together, and it appears almost nowhere in Greek literature before he uses it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It describes the Roman legal act of adoption, which was not what we mean by the word. A Roman adoption transferred a person into a new family completely: old debts cancelled, old obligations void, full inheritance rights, and it could not be undone. It was more permanent than being born into a family, because a natural son could be disinherited and an adopted one could not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the standing Paul says the Spirit gives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Jesus used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By Him we cry out, Abba, Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abba* is Aramaic, not Greek — and Paul is writing to Rome, where hardly anyone spoke it. He leaves it untranslated anyway, then puts the Greek beside it. He does the same in Galatians. It is the word Jesus used in Gethsemane, and it survived into Greek-speaking churches as a word people kept saying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not baby-talk, as it is sometimes described. It is family speech — the word a grown son would use of his own father, in the language Jesus prayed in."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is set against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You did not receive a spirit that makes you a slave again, dragging you back into fear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the alternative Paul rules out, and notice what he calls it: fear, and slavery, and *again* — a return to something. He is describing a relationship run on anxiety about standing, and he says flatly that this is not what was given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evidence he offers is not a feeling. It is the cry itself: the fact that a person calls God Father at all is presented as the Spirit's own testimony, witnessing *together with* our spirit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about suffering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If indeed we suffer with Him, so that we may also be glorified with Him.* This reads to many people like a condition attached at the end that could void the whole thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But the sentence it belongs to is about inheritance — heirs of God, heirs together with Christ. Paul's point is that being a co-heir means sharing what Christ has, and what Christ has includes both a road that went through suffering and a glory on the far side of it. It is a description of the inheritance, not an entrance fee for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"led by the Spirit\" in the verse before.** The passage just above says all who are led by the Spirit are sons of God — and it is often read as a test of whether someone qualifies. Paul's next sentence is this one, and it goes straight to adoption, Abba and inheritance. He raises leading in order to say what it proves about a person's standing, not to make them anxious about whether they are being led well enough."}]}, "Romans 8:26–27": {"title": "The Spirit prays within us", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul admits an inability and then says who covers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the same way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hosautos** — likewise, in the same manner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It connects to the verses before, about creation groaning and about waiting *with perseverance* for what we do not see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three groans in the paragraph: creation groans, we groan, and the Spirit groans."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit also **comes alongside our weaknesses and takes hold with us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunantilambanetai** — a triple compound: *sun* (with) + *anti* (opposite) + *lambano* (take hold)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To take hold of a load together with somebody, from the other end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word occurs twice in the New Testament. The other is Martha's complaint: *tell her to **help** me.* See the note on Luke 10:38–42."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weakness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our **weaknesses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **astheneia** — the general word for infirmity, used of illness and of any lack of strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it about the readers and includes himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The specific failure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **do not know what we ought to pray for**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that we do not know how. That we do not know **what** — the Greek is *what we should pray as we ought*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, who wrote the prayers in Ephesians and Colossians, says this about himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The intervention"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the Spirit Himself steps in and pleads for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huperentunchanei** — to intercede on behalf of. Another compound, and another word that appears once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **groans too deep to be put into words**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **stenagmois alaletois** — unspoken, unutterable groanings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who hears it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the One who searches our hearts knows the Spirit's mind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Father searches the heart, and what He finds there is the Spirit's own mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *searches* is **eraunon** — to examine, to investigate. The same word John uses of the risen Christ in Revelation: *I am He who searches the minds and hearts.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because the Spirit **intercedes for God's holy people according to the will of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to God* — in line with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the three persons are involved on the reader's behalf, and Paul says the third is listening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other intercession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight verses on, in the same chapter: *it is Christ who died, and furthermore is also risen, who is even at the right hand of God, **who also makes intercession for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One inside, one at the right hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews puts it the same way: *He always lives to make intercession for them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we know that **all things work together for good** to those who love God.* See the note on Romans 8:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famous verse follows directly from the two about not knowing what to pray for. Paul puts the ignorance first and the confidence second."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is conceded.** That we do not know what to ask for. Paul does not treat it as a failure to be corrected — he names it and then says who takes the other end of the load."}]}, "Romans 8:28–30": {"title": "What the \"good\" in \"works together for good\" is", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the most quoted sentence in the letter and the most often quoted half-sentence in the Bible. Almost nobody reads the next line, and the next line is where Paul says what he means by *good*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He defines his own term"}, {"k": "note", "t": "everything, all of it together, is working toward good … **for** those God knew beforehand, He also marked out beforehand **to be shaped into the likeness of His Son**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *for* is doing the work of a definition. The good being worked toward is not a comfortable outcome, a resolved situation, or a happy ending. It is a person coming to look like Christ."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why the verse holds up in situations where a promise of good outcomes would be an insult. Paul is not saying the thing that happened was good. He is saying nothing is wasted in what God is making."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Shaped into the likeness\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is *symmorphous* — sharing a form with. It is not imitation from the outside. Paul uses the same root when he speaks of a body being transformed, and of being conformed to Christ's death. The picture is of something taking a shape rather than copying a model."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the purpose given is not the reader's improvement: *so that His Son would be the firstborn among many brothers and sisters.* The goal of the whole process is a family with Christ at the head of it. The reader's transformation is how that family comes to exist, not a self-improvement project with a spiritual label."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five links, and where they land"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul then does something unusual: he runs the whole thing end to end in one breath."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["knew beforehand", "marked out beforehand", "called", "put right with Himself", "**glorified**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every verb is past tense, including the last one. Glorified — a thing no reader has experienced yet — is spoken of as done. Paul is not confused about the timeline. He is describing the chain from God's side, where it is finished, and that is why he can start the paragraph with *we know*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is safe to say with this verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not: everything that happens is good. Paul does not say that, and the chapter around it is full of groaning, weakness and suffering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he says is that all of it together is being worked toward one end, that the end is Christ's likeness in a person, and that the sequence which gets them there is already complete in the mind of the One running it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"those who love God.\"** It reads like a qualifier a person might fail, and anxious readers use it that way. But Paul immediately restates the group by another name — *those who have been called according to His purpose* — and everything after that is God's action, not theirs. The love is how he describes them, not a threshold they maintain."}]}, "Romans 8:31–34": {"title": "Four questions, and nobody answers them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul finishes the argument of eight chapters by asking questions instead of making statements, and the silence where the answers should be is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*If God is for us — who is there left to stand against us?*", "*Who is going to lay a charge against the ones God Himself has chosen?*", "*Who is left to condemn?*", "and, a few lines on, *who shall separate us from the love of Christ?*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are courtroom questions — the language of accusation, charge and condemnation. Paul invites anyone who wants to prosecute to step forward, and nobody does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument from the greater to the lesser"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did not spare His own Son but handed Him over for us all — how will He not, along with Him, freely give us everything else?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the logic the whole paragraph rests on. If the hardest thing has already been given, nothing smaller is in doubt. Paul does not argue that God is generous in general; he argues from one specific act that has already happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase *did not spare* echoes Genesis 22, where Abraham does not withhold his son. Paul is putting the two scenes beside each other deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is at the right hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is Christ who died — and more than that, who was raised — who is now at God's right hand, and who is pleading there for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only figure the passage places in a position to condemn is the one described as interceding. That is the answer to the questions: the seat the prosecutor would have to occupy is already taken by the defence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"freely give.\"** The verb is built on *charis* — grace. Paul does not say God will *provide*; he says He will **grace** us with all things, alongside the Son. The giving of everything else is described in the same vocabulary as the giving of Christ."}]}, "Romans 8:38–39": {"title": "\"Nothing can cut us off\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul empties the vocabulary of everything he can think of, in pairs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **I am fully persuaded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pepeismai** — perfect passive. He has been convinced, and stands convinced."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I hope* and not *I feel*. A settled state."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pairs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item comes with its opposite, so that the space between them is covered:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**death** and **life** — the two conditions a person can be in", "**angels** and **rulers** — beings above the human order", "**things present** and **things to come** — time, both directions", "**height** and **depth** — space, both directions"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the catch-all: *nor **anything else anywhere in the whole of creation**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing from the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item is external. Paul does not include failure, doubt, sin, or the person's own performance — and he has just spent seven chapters on exactly those."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Powers\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunamis** — the word for power, ability, force. Paul includes them alongside angels and rulers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Colossians has the same categories, disarmed and led in a procession. See the note on Colossians 2:11–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Has **the strength to cut us off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chorisai** — to separate, to put a space between. It is the word used of divorce: *what God has joined together, let not man **separate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is denying that anything has the strength to do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is separation from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the love of God that is in Christ Jesus our Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from salvation, not from heaven, not from blessing. From the love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is located: **in Christ Jesus**. The same phrase that opens the chapter — *no condemnation for those who are **in Christ Jesus**.* See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph is built out of five questions, each answered by silence:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If God is for us, who can be against us? He who did not spare His own Son… how shall He not with Him also freely give us all things? **Who shall bring a charge** against God's elect? **Who is he who condemns? Who shall separate us** from the love of Christ?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not answer the first four. He answers the last one, and this is the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tribulation, or distress, or persecution, or famine, or nakedness, or peril, or sword?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven, and Paul had personally had six of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he quotes a psalm: *for Your sake we are killed all day long; we are accounted as sheep for the slaughter.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften it. He includes the psalm and then says *yet in all these things we are more than conquerors.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"More than conquerors\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "One Greek word — **hupernikao**. Over-conquering. Paul appears to have made it up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sentence is not.** A statement that nothing bad happens. The list of things that do happen is in the paragraph, and the psalm about sheep for the slaughter is quoted in full. What Paul denies is that any of it can get between."}]}, "Romans 8:5–8": {"title": "The mind set on the old nature", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul describes two kinds of people by what their minds are set on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have their **minds set on** what that nature wants.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phronousin** — from **phroneo**, to have a mindset, to be disposed toward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a word about thinking occasionally. It describes a settled orientation, the direction a mind faces by default."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the noun **phronema** four times in these four verses and almost nowhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two groups"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People who **live according to the old nature** have their minds set on what that nature wants; people who **live according to the Spirit** have their minds set on **what the Spirit wants**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The living and the mindset are described as one thing. Paul does not say the mindset produces the life or the reverse — the sentence puts them together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two outcomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A mind set on the old nature **ends in death**; a mind set on the Spirit is **life and peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is simply *is death* and *is life and peace*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *leads to*. Paul equates them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Peace** is added and not paralleled. There is no opposite named for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hostility"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the mind set on the old nature is **hostile toward God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **echthra** — enmity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not indifferent, and not weak. Set against."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It **does not submit to God's law**, and **in fact it cannot**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *neither indeed can be*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul goes past unwillingness to inability. It is the sharpest thing he says about the flesh, and he says it twice in one line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who are **living out of that nature cannot please God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the end of the road for any programme of self-improvement, and Paul has spent Romans 7 demonstrating it from the inside. See the note on Romans 7:18–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you are not living out of the old nature; you are living in the Spirit**, if indeed the Spirit of God lives in you.* See the note on Romans 8:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole paragraph is diagnostic, and the next sentence tells the readers it does not describe them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does this repeatedly — states the condition in full and then names where his readers stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter opened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, **there is no condemnation now — none at all** — for those who are in Christ Jesus.* See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason: *what the law could not accomplish, weakened as it was by the old nature, God accomplished Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The inability in verse 7 is the same inability named in verse 3. Paul is consistent about where the failure is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is not describing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two kinds of Christian. The categories in the paragraph are people in whom the Spirit dwells and people in whom He does not — verse 9 makes it explicit: *if anyone does not have the Spirit of Christ, that person is not His.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the Spirit of Him who raised Jesus from the dead lives in you, then the One who raised Christ from the dead **will bring life to your mortal bodies as well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then sonship, the Spirit's intercession, and *all things work together for good*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word the paragraph runs on.** Not what a mind thinks about — what it is set toward. Paul uses the noun four times in four verses and attaches death to one direction and life and peace to the other."}]}, "Romans 8:9–11": {"title": "\"You are living in the Spirit\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells them what they are, and the sentence is in the indicative rather than the imperative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you are not living out of the old nature; you are living in the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *you should*. He is describing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you are not in the flesh but in the Spirit* — a statement of location, the same construction as *in Christ Jesus* at the top of the chapter. See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualifier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If indeed **the Spirit of God lives in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **eiper** can be read *if indeed* or *since indeed*. Paul is not raising a doubt; he is naming the ground of what he has just said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if anyone does not have the Spirit of Christ, **that person is not His**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plainest test in the letter, and it is not about behaviour, background, or observance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent seven chapters dismantling the alternatives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In two verses the same Spirit is called **the Spirit of God**, **the Spirit of Christ**, and then simply **the Spirit**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul moves between them without explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The body and the Spirit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if Christ is in you, then **the body may be dead because of sin, but the Spirit is life because of righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things held together. The body is not exempted from the situation, and the Spirit is called life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that the Spirit **is** life — not gives it, in this clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The long title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if **the Spirit of Him who raised Jesus from the dead** lives in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul could have written *the Spirit*. He spells out the resurrection, because the whole next clause depends on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same thing in chapter 4 about Abraham: *God, who **gives life to the dead** and calls those things which do not exist as though they did.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the One who raised Christ from the dead will bring life to your mortal bodies as well** by His Spirit who lives in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mortal bodies** — the Greek is **thneta**, subject to death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same power, applied to the same kind of object. Paul's argument is that the Spirit already living in a person is the link between the two events."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says elsewhere in the same terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who will **transform** our lowly body that it may be conformed to His glorious body, according to the working by which He is able even to subdue all things to Himself.* See the note on Philippians 3:20–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, **we are debtors — not to the flesh**, to live according to the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the sonship: *you received the Spirit of adoption by whom we cry out, \"Abba, Father.\" The Spirit Himself bears witness with our spirit that we are children of God.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage settles.** Where a person is, rather than how they are doing. Paul states the location first and the consequences after — including one about a body he has just called mortal."}]}, "Romans 9:25–26": {"title": "Called My people", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Hosea twice, and both quotations are the reversal of a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is quoting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hosea's third child was called **Lo-Ammi** — Not-My-People — and God said over Israel *you are not My people, and I am not your God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the two verses that reverse it and sets them side by side. See the note on Hosea 1:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will call those who were **not My people** 'My people,' and her who was **not loved** 'beloved.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of Hosea's negative names, both undone. *Lo-Ammi* becomes *Ammi*. *Lo-Ruhamah* becomes *Ruhamah*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prefix is simply dropped. Nothing else changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the calling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will call.* The naming is His in both directions — He named them Not-My-People, and He is the one who renames them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in either verse describes them earning the second name. They are called it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**In the very place** where it was said to them, 'You are not My people,' there they will be called **sons of the living God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not somewhere else. The same place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the title is not restored to what it was. They were *My people*; they are now *sons of the living God*. The reversal overshoots the starting point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is arguing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's subject is God's freedom in mercy — *I will have mercy on whom I have mercy* — and Paul applies Hosea's words about Israel to Gentiles being brought in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is showing from the prophets that a people who were not a people becoming God's own is not a new idea invented by the church. It was written down centuries earlier, about Israel, and God's method has not changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"called\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "It runs through the whole chapter. *Not of works but of Him who calls… the children of the promise are counted as offspring… even us whom He called.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Calling is what makes the difference in Romans 9, and it belongs to Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the argument ends up.** Two chapters later: *God has not cast away His people whom He foreknew… for the gifts and the calling of God are irrevocable.* Paul spends three chapters on this and does not conclude that anybody was discarded."}]}, "Ruth 2:1–3": {"title": "\"Though it looked like plain chance\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of setup, and the narrator interrupts himself in the last one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The introduction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Naomi had **a relative on her husband's side** — a man of standing, **wealthy and well respected**, whose name was **Boaz**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gibbor chayil** — a mighty man of valour. The phrase is used of soldiers, and here of a landowner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator introduces him before Ruth has met him, so the reader knows what she does not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A relative** — the Hebrew notes the family connection, which is the hinge of the whole book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethlehem, at the start of the barley harvest. Naomi has come back from Moab with nothing — *I went out full, and the LORD has brought me home again empty*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ruth's proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let me go out to the fields and pick up the leftover grain** behind anyone who will **show me kindness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gleaning was a legal right. Leviticus: *when you reap the harvest of your land, you shall not wholly reap the corners of your field… **you shall leave them for the poor and the stranger***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ruth is both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The right existed and enforcing it was another matter, which is why she adds *anyone who will show me kindness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ruth the Moabite. Deuteronomy: *an Ammonite or Moabite shall not enter the assembly of the LORD, even to the tenth generation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator calls her *the Moabite* five times in the book, well after everyone knows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Naomi's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go ahead, my daughter**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So she went, and **she gleaned in the fields behind the harvesters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes out to work in a country she has never lived in, for a mother-in-law with no legal claim on her, having said *your people shall be my people, and your God my God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **as it turned out — though it looked like plain chance** — she found herself **in a field belonging to Boaz**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayiqer miqreha** — literally *her chance chanced*. It is a doubled construction, and it is the narrator drawing attention to itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book of Ruth never records a miracle. God is spoken about constantly and never seen to act."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This sentence is the closest it comes to a comment: the writer says *chance*, twice, in a way nobody could read as chance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens the same day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Boaz arrives from Bethlehem, greets his reapers *the LORD be with you*, asks who she is, and tells her to stay in his field, drink from his water jars, and not go elsewhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *the LORD repay your work, and **a full reward be given you by the LORD God of Israel, under whose wings you have come for refuge***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Boaz turns out to be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Goel** — the kinsman-redeemer. The relative obliged to buy back what a family had lost, and to marry a childless widow to raise up a name for the dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator told us in verse 1 and Ruth finds out in verse 20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Boaz took Ruth, and she became his wife… and she bore a son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they called his name **Obed**. He is the father of Jesse, **the father of David**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Moabite woman gleaning in a stranger's field is the great-grandmother of the king, and Matthew puts her in the first chapter of the New Testament."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The narrator's one aside.** *Her chance chanced.* A book with no miracles in it stops once, in a field, to say that what looked like luck was the field of the one man in Bethlehem who could redeem her."}]}, "Song of Songs 8:6–7": {"title": "\"Many waters cannot quench love\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The high point of the Song, and the only place in the book where the name of God appears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set me as a seal upon your heart, as a seal upon your arm.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A seal was a personal signet — pressed into wax to mark what belonged to you, and worn on a cord at the chest or as a ring on the hand. It was identity and ownership in one object."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two placements: the **heart**, where affection is, and the **arm**, where strength acts. She is asking to be stamped on both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The very flame of the LORD\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its flashes are flashes of fire, the very flame of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shalhevetyah* — a flame with the shortened divine name attached to the end. It is the only occurrence of the name in the book, and it turns up here, in a description of what love is like."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim being made is not that love is intense. It is that this fire comes from God's own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things that cannot touch it"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Many waters cannot quench love, neither can the floods drown it* — the deep waters are the Bible's standing image for chaos and death. They cannot put this out.", "*If a man offered all the wealth of his house for love, it would be utterly scorned* — and it cannot be bought either."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Strong as death, unquenchable by the flood, not for sale. Three of the largest forces anyone knows, and love outlasts all three."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the church has read it.** The Song is a love poem and it is not embarrassed about that. It has also been read from the earliest times as describing God's love for His people — and this passage is why: language this absolute, with His own name burning at the centre of it, is doing more than describing an engagement."}]}, "Titus 2:11–12": {"title": "Grace takes us in hand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and grace is the subject of every verb in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grace of God **has come out into the open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epephane** — appeared, shone out. The root of *epiphany*, and it was used of a god showing himself or of a king arriving in a city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace is described as an event that happened, at a time, in public."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bringing **rescue** with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **soterios** — saving, bringing salvation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who saw it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **plain sight of everyone alive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to all men*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And that same grace **takes us in hand and teaches us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paideuousa** — from **pais**, a child. It is the whole business of raising one: teaching, training, correcting, disciplining."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Hebrews uses of the Father's discipline: *whom the Lord loves He chastens*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace is the teacher in the sentence, and the teaching is not gentle in the Greek. It trains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it teaches, negatively"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **turn our backs on a life with no room for God**, and on **the wanting that belongs to a world like this one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **arnesamenoi** — to deny, to disown. It is the verb used of Peter in the courtyard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things disowned: **asebeia**, ungodliness, and **kosmikas epithumias**, worldly desires."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it teaches, positively"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three adverbs in the Greek:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Sophronos** — with clear heads, self-controlled, sound-minded.", "**Dikaios** — rightly, justly. Toward other people.", "**Eusebos** — turned toward God."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Toward oneself, toward others, toward God. The three directions a life runs in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here, now, **in the middle of the age we are actually living in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in the present age* — **nun aioni**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in retreat from it and not in the age to come. The training is for the place they are standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Looking for the blessed hope and glorious appearing of our great God and Saviour Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek uses the same root — **epiphaneia**. Grace appeared once and will appear again, and the verses are held together by the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace is not described as the reason a standard can be lowered. It is named as the teacher of the standard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does the same thing in Romans: *shall we continue in sin that grace may abound? Certainly not.* See the note on Romans 6:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being written to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Titus, left in Crete *to set in order the things that are lacking*. The chapter before this quotes a Cretan poet on his own countrymen and says the description is true."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that Paul writes that grace has appeared to everyone and is training them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The subject of every verb.** Grace appears, grace brings, grace takes in hand, grace teaches. Paul does not make it a disposition God has — he makes it the thing doing the work in the sentence."}]}, "Titus 2:13–14": {"title": "\"A people who belong to Him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second half of the sentence that begins with grace appearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the grace of God has come out into the open**, bringing rescue with it… and that same grace **takes us in hand and teaches us**.* See the note on Titus 2:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the same sentence continuing. The teaching has an object in view."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the while we are **watching the road ahead**, **glad about what is coming**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosdechomenoi** — looking for, waiting to receive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The blessed hope** — Paul calls the waiting itself blessed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is coming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The day **the glory of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ comes into full view**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epiphaneia** — appearing, manifestation. The same root as the verb in verse 11, where grace *appeared*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two appearings, one past and one future, and the sentence is held together by the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our **great God and Savior Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek construction — one article governing both nouns — is one of the clearest statements in the New Testament that the two titles belong to one person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the identical construction: *our God and Saviour Jesus Christ*. See the note on 2 Peter 1:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The self-giving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **gave Himself up for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **edoken heauton** — gave Himself. Paul uses it repeatedly: *who loved me and gave Himself for me*, and *gave Himself for the church*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **buy us out from under every kind of lawlessness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lutrosetai** — to ransom, to redeem by paying a price. From **lutron**, the ransom money."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word Jesus used of Himself: *to give His life a **ransom** for many.* See the note on Matthew 20:24–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Every kind of lawlessness** — **anomia**, without law. Not one kind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And to **wash us clean**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katharise** — to purify. The word for ritual cleansing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **make us a people who belong to Him and to nobody else**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **laon periousion** — a people for His own possession."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase comes straight out of Exodus: *you shall be **a special treasure** to Me above all people.* And Deuteronomy: *the LORD has chosen you to be a people for Himself, a special treasure.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul applies Israel's title to a mixed congregation on Crete."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **an appetite for doing good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zeloten kalon ergon** — zealots for good works."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, who spent his letters arguing that works do not justify, ends this sentence with zeal for them — and puts them at the far end of a chain that begins with grace appearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the whole sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace appeared → it teaches → we watch the road → He gave Himself → to ransom, to cleanse, to make a people → with an appetite for good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chain is asked of the reader before the giving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Speak these things, exhort, and rebuke with all authority. **Let no one despise you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line of the chapter, to a young man left on Crete."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the good works land.** At the end. Paul runs the whole sentence from an appearing, through a ransom and a washing, to a people who belong to somebody — and the appetite is the last item, not the first."}]}, "Zechariah 10:1": {"title": "\"Ask the LORD for rain in the springtime\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is an instruction about timing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The season named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ask the LORD for rain **in the springtime**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word is the one for the **latter** rain — the spring rain that comes just before harvest and fills out the grain. See the note on Deuteronomy 11:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So this is not a general encouragement to pray about weather. It is: when the season for the last rain arrives, ask for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why ask for something already promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy had promised both rains to a people who kept the covenant. Zechariah, centuries later, tells them to ask anyway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise does not remove the asking. It is what makes the asking sensible — you request what has already been undertaken, in the season it belongs to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is that gives it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is the LORD who makes the storm clouds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line matters because of what the verse before it says. Zechariah has just described people consulting household idols and diviners, who *speak nonsense and give empty comfort*, which is why the flock wanders like sheep with no shepherd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set against that, this is a redirection: not the teraphim, not the diviner. Ask the One who actually makes the clouds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gives showers of rain **to all**, and green plants in the field to everyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rain falls across a district, not on one field. What is asked for privately arrives publicly, over everybody's ground."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it sits in the book.** Zechariah 9 and 10 are full of restoration — a king coming humble on a donkey, prisoners released, the house of Judah strengthened. The instruction to ask for rain belongs to that: a people being told to expect a season and to say so out loud."}]}, "Zechariah 12:9–10": {"title": "\"The one they have pierced\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophecy about a city under siege, and in the middle of it the speaker says something about Himself that does not fit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"On that day I will set out to **destroy all the nations that come against Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah 12 to 14 is one long passage about a final assault on the city and what happens to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pouring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And I will **pour out on the house of David and on the people of Jerusalem a spirit of grace and of pleading**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ruach chen v'tachanunim** — a spirit of grace and of supplications."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chen** is favour, grace — the word used over Noah, over Esther, over Joseph in Potiphar's house. See the note on Genesis 39:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tachanunim** is entreaty, pleading for mercy. The same root as the word for the Israelite begging in the cities of refuge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are poured out. Neither is worked up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that does not fit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They will look on **Me, the one they have pierced**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they shall look upon **Me** whom they have pierced*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The speaker through the whole chapter is the LORD. And the pronoun changes mid-clause in the Hebrew — *upon Me*, and then *they shall mourn for **Him***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oddity is in the text and translators have wrestled with it for centuries. Some ancient copies smooth it to *upon him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Daqar** — to pierce, to thrust through. It is used of a spear or sword going into a body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mourning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And they will **mourn for him as one mourns for an only child**, and **grieve bitterly for him as one grieves for a firstborn son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two comparisons, and both are the loss of a son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**An only child** — the Hebrew is **yachid**, the word used of Isaac on the mountain: *your son, your only son*. See the note on Genesis 22:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where John quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the cross, after the spear: *and again another Scripture says, \"**They shall look on Him whom they pierced**.\"* See the note on John 19:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John cites it as the second of two fulfilled Scriptures in that scene — the unbroken bones and this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Revelation quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, He is coming with clouds, and every eye will see Him, **even they who pierced Him**. And all the tribes of the earth will mourn because of Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's opening page, joining this to Daniel 7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Zechariah says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**In that day a fountain shall be opened** for the house of David and for the inhabitants of Jerusalem, **for sin and for uncleanness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first verse of the next chapter, and it follows directly. The looking and the mourning are followed by a fountain being opened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is poured and what is opened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A spirit of grace and pleading, poured out. Then a fountain, opened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is described as obtained."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause everything turns on.** *They will look on Me, the one they have pierced.* The speaker is God, the wound is His, and what follows the looking is a fountain rather than a judgment."}]}, "Zechariah 13:7": {"title": "\"Strike the shepherd\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and Jesus quotes it about Himself on the last night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the sword is called against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Awake, sword, against My shepherd, against **the man who stands close to Me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the second phrase is unusual — it means an associate, a companion, someone of the same kind. It is used in Leviticus of a man's neighbour, his fellow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God calls the shepherd His own, and He calls him a companion, and He tells the sword to wake up against him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who wields it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Declares the LORD Almighty.* The command is God's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the difficult centre of the verse, and the New Testament does not soften it — Isaiah 53 says the same: *it pleased the LORD to crush him.* The striking is not something that happened to God's shepherd against His will."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the way to Gethsemane: *every one of you is going to fall away tonight. It stands written: I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will scatter.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes it to explain what His disciples are about to do — and then, in the same breath, tells them where to meet Him afterwards. See the note on Mark 14:27–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in Zechariah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will turn My hand against the little ones.* The Hebrew phrase can carry the sense of turning a hand **to** someone — and the same chapter goes on to describe a remnant refined and brought through fire, who call on God's name and are answered: *they are My people, and they will say, the LORD is my God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scattering is not the end of the sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the shepherd language goes.** Zechariah's struck shepherd stands behind John 10, where Jesus calls Himself the good shepherd who lays down His life for the sheep — *no one takes it from Me.* Both are true at once: the sword is commanded, and the shepherd lays it down."}]}, "Zechariah 3:1–5": {"title": "The filthy clothes", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A courtroom scene, and the defence never says a word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three figures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua the high priest, standing before the angel of the LORD, and **Satan standing at his right hand to accuse him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ha-satan** — the accuser, with the article. The right hand was the prosecutor's position in a court of that period."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua is the high priest of the returned exiles, standing in filthy clothes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the accuser had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything. The Hebrew for the clothes is the strongest word available — garments covered in excrement. It is not a poetic way of saying stained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest of Israel, in the presence of God, in that condition. There is no suggestion anywhere in the passage that the accusation was false."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD rebuke you, Satan. The LORD who has chosen Jerusalem rebuke you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No argument about the evidence. No defence of Joshua's record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rebuke rests on one thing: *the LORD **who has chosen** Jerusalem.* The choosing is the whole case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The brand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is this not a burning stick snatched from the fire?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A charred piece of wood pulled out of the flames — half burnt, blackened, and out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is God's own description of the man being accused. He does not deny the scorching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of what happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take off his filthy clothes.**\" Then he said to Joshua, \"**See, I have taken away your sin, and I will clothe you in fine garments.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clothes come off, and only then is the sin declared taken away, and only then does the new clothing go on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua does nothing in the entire scene. He does not repent, protest, promise, or speak. Others undress him and others dress him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *fine garments* is a rare word for rich festal robes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turban"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Put **a clean turban** on his head.\" So they placed a clean turban on his head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah himself interrupts to ask for this — *and I said, put a clean turban on his head.* The prophet joins in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest's turban carried a gold plate engraved *HOLINESS TO THE LORD*. It is the item that marked him as fit to enter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is standing there through all of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While **the angel of the LORD stood by**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one who rebuked the accuser stays present for the whole re-clothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Hear now, O Joshua… I am bringing forth **My Servant, the BRANCH**… and **I will remove the iniquity of that land in one day**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vision ends with a promise of one day on which iniquity is removed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jude does with the same phrase.** *Michael the archangel, contending with the devil… dared not bring against him a reviling accusation, but said, \"**The Lord rebuke you.**\"* The same sentence, quoted centuries later, and still the whole answer."}]}, "Zechariah 4:6–10": {"title": "\"Grace, grace to it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A governor with a half-built temple and an empty treasury gets one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zerubbabel had laid the foundation of the second temple sixteen years earlier. Then the work stopped — opposition, discouragement, a letter to the Persian court, and a building site standing open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old men who remembered Solomon's temple had wept when they saw the foundation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Not by might nor by power, but by My Spirit**, says the LORD Almighty.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Might** is *chayil* — an army, a force, collective strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Power** is *koach* — individual strength, the capacity of one man. It is the same word in Deuteronomy 8: *He gives you the power to gain wealth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are ruled out — the group's resources and the man's own — and one thing is put in their place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What are you, mighty mountain?** Before Zerubbabel you will become level ground.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mountain is addressed directly, not prayed about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the obstacle was — Persian bureaucracy, hostile neighbours, no money — it is spoken to and told what it will become."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The capstone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And he will bring out the capstone while the people shout, '**Grace, grace to it**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The final stone, the one that completes a building."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the shout that goes up as it is set is not *well done*, or *at last*, or a tribute to Zerubbabel. It is one word, said twice: **chen, chen** — grace, grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The house began with grace and is finished with the same word shouted over the last stone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The hands of Zerubbabel have laid the foundation** of this house, and **his hands will complete it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pair of hands at both ends. Sixteen years of stoppage in between, and the promise is that the man who started it finishes it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same of a different work: *He who has begun a good work in you will complete it.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day of small things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who dares despise the day of small things?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is literally *who has despised the day of small beginnings*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is asked as a rebuke, and the answer in context is: the people who saw the foundation and wept because it was not Solomon's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For they will rejoice when they see **the plumb line** in the hand of Zerubbabel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A plumb line is a weight on a string. That is the small thing — one man holding a builder's tool."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"These seven are **the eyes of the LORD, which range through the whole earth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's vision is a lampstand with seven lamps, and this is what the seven are."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation picks it up: the Lamb with *seven eyes, which are the seven Spirits of God sent out into all the earth.* See the note on Revelation 5:5–6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What actually happened.** *The elders of the Jews built, and the work went well in their hands, carried along by the words of Haggai the prophet and **Zechariah son of Iddo**.* Ezra 6 records the finish, and names the prophet who gave this word as one of the reasons it got done. See the note on Ezra 6:13–15."}]}, "Zechariah 6:9–15": {"title": "A priest on his throne", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophet is told to make a crown and put it on the wrong head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the metal comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take silver and gold from **the exiles Heldai, Tobijah, and Jedaiah, who have arrived from Babylon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three men just off the road from exile, carrying what they had. The crown is made out of what the returning poor brought back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose head it goes on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set it on the head of **Joshua son of Jozadak, the high priest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not on Zerubbabel, who was the governor and of David's line. On the priest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the law, that is not allowed. Crowns belonged to Judah's kings; the priesthood belonged to Levi. The two lines never met, and Uzziah was struck with leprosy for crossing between them. See the note on 2 Chronicles 26:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah is instructed to do it anyway, and told what it means."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Here is the man whose name is **the Branch**, and he will branch out from his place and **build the temple of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Branch** — **tsemach**, a sprout, a shoot. It is a title the prophets use of Messiah: Jeremiah's *Branch of righteousness* raised up to David, and Isaiah's shoot out of the stump of Jesse. See the note on Isaiah 11:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua the high priest is wearing a crown as a stand-in for somebody else, and Zechariah says so out loud while doing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **he will be a priest on his throne**, and **there will be harmony between the two**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the counsel of peace shall be between them both* — between the two offices."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Crown and censer, throne and altar, in one Person, at peace with each other rather than in tension."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110 had already said it: *the LORD has sworn and will not relent, \"You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek\"* — addressed to somebody David calls *my Lord*, sitting at God's right hand. See the note on Matthew 22:41–46."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who builds it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yes, he will build the temple of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in two verses, for emphasis, to people standing in front of a half-finished building site."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**Those who are far away will come and help build** the temple of the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Distant people, brought in to build. It is what Paul says happened: *you who once were far off have been brought near by the blood of Christ… in whom the whole building, being fitted together, grows into a holy temple in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crown afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The crown will be **a memorial in the temple** of the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes off Joshua's head and is hung up as a reminder. He wore it for an afternoon, as a sign."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The One it was made for wears it permanently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other Joshua"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest here is the same man Zechariah saw in chapter 3, standing in filthy clothes with the accuser at his right hand, and heard *take off his filthy clothes… I have taken away your sin.* See the note on Zechariah 3:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Re-clothed in chapter 3, crowned in chapter 6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Peter calls his readers.** *A royal priesthood* — the combination the law kept apart, and the thing this crown was made to picture. See the note on 1 Peter 2:9."}]}, "Zechariah 9:11–12": {"title": "Prisoners who still have hope", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the phrase in the middle of them is one nobody would have thought to put together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the release rests on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because of the blood of My covenant with you**, I will set your prisoners free.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because of their record, their repentance, or their usefulness. Because of blood, and because of a covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the blood of your covenant* — the blood by which the covenant was made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus uses the same phrase over the cup: *this is My blood of the new covenant, poured out for many.* See the note on Matthew 26:26–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"From **the pit that has no water in it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a cistern — a bottle-shaped hole cut into rock for storing rain, and a dry one was the standard improvised prison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph went into one: *the pit was empty; there was no water in it.* So did Jeremiah, and he *sank in the mire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dry cistern is not a place you climb out of. The sides curve inward and the neck is above your reach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come home, **you prisoners who still have hope**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prisoners **of hope**, in the older rendering — the Hebrew is *prisoners of the hope*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Being a prisoner and having hope do not normally belong in the same description. A man in a dry cistern is defined by what he cannot do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah names them by the thing they are holding rather than by the hole they are in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stronghold"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Return to your stronghold.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stronghold* is a fortified place, a rock hold. The prophet is calling them out of a pit and into a fortress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The double"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This very day I declare it. I will pay you back double.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *even today* — dated, immediate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Double is the law's word for restitution. A thief who was caught restored twofold — and here it is God doing the restoring rather than the thief."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says the same to Israel: *instead of your shame you shall have double, and instead of confusion they shall rejoice in their portion; therefore in their land they shall possess double.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Job, at the end: *the LORD gave Job twice as much as he had before.* See the note on Job 42:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes just before this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier is one of the best-known lines in the prophets:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion! Behold, your King is coming to you… **lowly, and riding on a donkey**, a colt, the foal of a donkey.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes it on Palm Sunday. The prisoners-of-hope promise is in the same paragraph as the donkey."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the prisoners are told to do.** Come home. Return. Both verbs are movement toward, and neither is about getting themselves out. The blood does the releasing; they do the walking."}]}};
